export const PROMPTS: Record<string, string> = {
  "ab-layer-crossfade": "# Build: A/B Layer Ping-Pong\n\nA double buffer for DOM content. Incoming content is written into whichever of two absolutely-positioned layers is currently invisible, then the two swap opacity. Because the outgoing content is still on screen, every change is a real crossfade rather than a swap with a fade bolted on — and running two of these at different durations is how a hover preview gets its lag between thumbnail and full-bleed background.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTwo stacked layers alternate so any change crossfades, however fast you move between rows.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Two absolutely positioned layers share a stacking context. A `front` slot (`\"a\"` or `\"b\"`) records which one is currently visible; the other is always at opacity 0.\n2. When `contentKey` changes, the new children are written into the BACK slot and `front` flips. The back layer is already transparent, so the new content mounts invisibly — writing into the front layer instead would replace the outgoing content before it had faded.\n3. `gsap.killTweensOf([inEl, outEl])` runs before every transition. Moving across a list faster than the duration otherwise leaves an unfinished fade-out driving the same node a fade-in is now driving up, and the layers flicker.\n4. Two symmetric tweens — `opacity: 1` on the incoming layer, `opacity: 0` on the outgoing — with `power2.inOut`, which keeps the midpoint of the fade at roughly equal luminance instead of dipping through a visible trough.\n5. The first commit is a `gsap.set`, not a tween, so the initial content is simply present; only subsequent key changes animate.\n6. The last `contentKey` acted on is held in a ref, so a parent re-rendering with the same key (new inline children, a hover on the row that is already active) does not restart the fade.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration (background) = 1`\n  Seconds. A full-bleed area needs a long fade or the whole frame appears to jump brightness; at 0.5s a large gradient change reads as a cut.\n- `duration (thumbnail) = 0.6`\n  The small element under the cursor answers first. Both at 1s and the interface feels unresponsive; both at 0.6s and the background flickers on fast passes. The 0.4s difference is the effect.\n- `ease = 'power2.inOut'`\n  Symmetric ease on both halves keeps their crossover near 50/50. With `power2.out` on the way in and `power2.in` on the way out, both layers are dim at the midpoint and the panel visibly darkens mid-fade.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ABLayerCrossfade(props: { contentKey: string; children: ReactNode; duration?: number; ease?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `contentKey: string`\n  Identity of the current content. A change starts a crossfade.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content for the current key. Captured into a layer at the moment the key changes.\n- `duration: number` — default `1`\n  Crossfade length in seconds. Use a shorter one for the smaller element in a pair.\n- `ease: string` — default `'power2.inOut'`\n  GSAP ease applied to both halves.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the positioned wrapper. Give it a size — the layers are absolute.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ABLayerCrossfade({\n  contentKey,\n  children,\n  duration = 1,\n  ease = 'power2.inOut',\n  className,\n}: ABLayerCrossfadeProps) {\n  const aRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const bRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [front, setFront] = useState<Slot>('a');\n  const [layers, setLayers] = useState<{ a: ReactNode; b: ReactNode }>({\n    a: children,\n    b: null,\n  });\n\n  const shownKey = useRef(contentKey);\n  const frontRef = useRef<Slot>('a');\n  const first = useRef(true);\n\n  // Write into the BACK layer, then flip. The back layer is already at\n  // opacity 0, so the new content mounts invisibly — no flash of it at full\n  // strength before the tween starts.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (shownKey.current === contentKey) return;\n    shownKey.current = contentKey;\n    const next: Slot = frontRef.current === 'a' ? 'b' : 'a';\n    frontRef.current = next;\n    setLayers((l) => ({ ...l, [next]: children }));\n    setFront(next);\n  }, [contentKey, children]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const inEl = front === 'a' ? aRef.current : bRef.current;\n    const outEl = front === 'a' ? bRef.current : aRef.current;\n    if (!inEl || !outEl) return;\n\n    if (first.current) {\n      first.current = false;\n      gsap.set(inEl, { opacity: 1 });\n      gsap.set(outEl, { opacity: 0 });\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // Kill first, always. Two rapid changes otherwise leave an old fade-out\n    // running on the layer the new fade-in is driving up.\n    gsap.killTweensOf([inEl, outEl]);\n\n    if (reduced) {\n      gsap.set(inEl, { opacity: 1 });\n      gsap.set(outEl, { opacity: 0 });\n      return;\n    }\n\n    gsap.to(inEl, { opacity: 1, duration, ease });\n    gsap.to(outEl, { opacity: 0, duration, ease });\n  }, [front, duration, ease, reduced]);\n\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      gsap.killTweensOf([aRef.current, bRef.current]);\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** An unmount effect calls `gsap.killTweensOf` on both layer elements, so an in-flight fade cannot write opacity to a detached node after React has removed it.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The layers still ping-pong, but the swap is a `gsap.set` rather than a tween: content changes instantly with no fade. The double-buffer structure is unchanged, so nothing else in the component behaves differently.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Crossfading one element by swapping its content at opacity 0. There is nothing to fade out of, so it reads as a blink; the two-layer buffer is the whole point.\n- Writing incoming content into the front layer. The old content is replaced before it has faded, which is a hard cut with a pointless fade on top.\n- Skipping `killTweensOf`. Hovering four rows in a second stacks eight opacity tweens across two nodes, and the layers end up at whatever the last-finishing pair leaves behind — usually both partly transparent.\n- Giving both layers the same duration in a paired preview. The lag between the small element and the full-bleed one is what makes the interaction feel like it is answering you; without it the whole frame changes at once and reads as a page change.\n- Restarting the fade when the parent re-renders. Any inline `children` array is a new object every render, so the effect must compare `contentKey`, not identity, or a hover crossfades continuously.\n- Leaving tweens running past unmount. They keep writing opacity onto detached nodes; the unmount cleanup kills both layers.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Layers hold whatever you give them — a gradient, a canvas, a `<video>`, an image. Nothing here is image-specific.\n- Content is captured per key, so if the same key needs to show new children you must change the key. That is deliberate: it is the only way to know when a transition is warranted.\n- App Router wiring: pass `contentKey={usePathname()}` and render the route content as children for a whole-page crossfade with no layout thrash.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove down a list at a normal pace and each change is a smooth blend with no dip in brightness at the midpoint. Sweep the cursor across every row as fast as you can and the layers never flicker or settle half-faded. In a paired setup, the small preview visibly finishes before the background does.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "accent-swatch-picker": "# Build: Accent Swatch Picker\n\nThe colour row under a composer preview: round, glossy swatches with an iridescent first option, chosen like radio buttons. `AccentScope` is the other half: it writes the chosen colour to one custom property, `--accent`, derives the kit's whole accent ramp from it with color-mix, and re-declares the tokens that read that ramp, so primary buttons, switches, focus rings and the swatch outline inside it all change colour together. The demo wires it to the kit Composer Loader, whose light band follows the swatch.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nGlossy colour spheres in one keyboard radio group; the pick becomes one --accent property that re-tints kit controls in its scope.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Render a react-aria `RadioGroup` (`orientation=\"horizontal\"`) whose `Radio`s are the swatches: one Tab stop, arrow keys move and select, `aria-label` names each colour. Each radio sets `--swatch` to its colour in inline style.\n2. Paint the sphere as a stacked background: a white radial highlight at 32% 26% fading out by 42%, over a radial body that runs from the colour mixed 70% with white, to the colour at 52%, to the colour mixed 72% with black at the rim. A swatch with `fill` (the iridescent one) swaps the body for its own conic gradient. An inset shadow made from the colour darkens the bottom edge.\n3. Show state on the sphere itself: a 2px outline at 2px offset is transparent until `data-selected`, then takes `var(--swatch)`; focus-visible switches it to `text-primary` so keyboard focus is never the same colour as the selection; hover scales to 1.05 and press to 0.95 over 150ms.\n4. `AccentScope` puts `--accent: <colour>` on a div and, through arbitrary-property classes, sets `--color-accent-50…400` as color-mix toward white (8, 16, 32, 52, 78%), `500` to the accent, and `600…950` toward black (86, 70, 56, 42, 28%).\n5. Because the kit's semantic tokens (`--gradient-button-primary-*`, `--color-border-focus-ring`, `--color-switch-on-chip-*`, `--color-button-ghost-*`, `--color-badge-new-*`) are resolved at :root, the scope re-declares each one with the same formula as the theme, with `dark:` variants where dark mode maps the ramp differently.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `sphere sizes = sm 24px · md 36px · lg 36px, 48px from 640px`\n  The compact pill matches the floating 24px row; lg drops to 36px on phones so seven spheres still fit one line at 360px.\n- `ramp mixes = 50→400: 8/16/32/52/78% accent in white; 600→950: 86/70/56/42/28% accent in black`\n  Roughly tracks Tailwind's blue ramp lightness, so swapping the hue keeps the kit's contrast (ghost text on ghost fill, white on the button gradient).\n- `selection outline = 2px at 2px offset (1px offset at sm)`\n  A gap between sphere and ring reads as \"selected\" on any colour, including the iridescent one, without a check mark.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AccentSwatchPicker(props: { swatches?: AccentSwatch[]; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (id: string) => void; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; variant?: \"plain\" | \"pill\"; label?: string; isDisabled?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction AccentScope(props: { color: string; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype AccentSwatch = { id: string; label: string; color: string; fill?: string }\n```\n\n- `swatches: AccentSwatch[]` — default `DEFAULT_ACCENT_SWATCHES`\n  Iridescent, blue, violet, pink, red, green, orange: id, label, CSS colour and optional sphere fill.\n- `value: string`\n  Controlled selected swatch id.\n- `defaultValue: string` — default `swatches[0].id`\n  Initial id when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the newly selected id.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Sphere size: 24px, 36px, or 36px rising to 48px from the sm breakpoint.\n- `variant: \"plain\" | \"pill\"` — default `\"plain\"`\n  `pill` places the row on a floating rounded surface with a border and shadow.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Accent colour\"`\n  Accessible name of the radio group.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Disables every swatch.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the group.\n- `AccentScope.color: string`\n  Any CSS colour; written to `--accent` and expanded into the accent ramp for the subtree.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * The ramp: 50–400 mix the accent toward white, 600–950 toward black, 500 is\n * the accent itself. Then every semantic token that reads the ramp is\n * re-declared here so it resolves against the scoped ramp (dark mode keeps\n * the kit's own dark mapping for the ghost button and NEW badge).\n */\nexport const accentScopeClassName = [\n  \"[--color-accent-50:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_8%,white)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-100:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_16%,white)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-200:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_32%,white)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-300:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_52%,white)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-400:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_78%,white)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-500:var(--accent)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-600:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_86%,black)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-700:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_70%,black)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-800:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_56%,black)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-900:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_42%,black)]\",\n  \"[--color-accent-950:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--accent)_28%,black)]\",\n  \"[--color-border-focus-ring:var(--color-accent-500)]\",\n  \"[--gradient-button-primary-default:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--color-accent-500)_0%,var(--color-accent-600)_100%)]\",\n  \"[--gradient-button-primary-hover:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--color-accent-400)_0%,var(--color-accent-500)_100%)]\",\n  \"[--gradient-button-primary-active:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--color-accent-600)_0%,var(--color-accent-700)_100%)]\",\n  \"[--color-switch-on-chip-start:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--color-accent-500)_63%,var(--color-accent-600))]\",\n  \"[--color-switch-on-chip-end:color-mix(in_oklab,var(--color-accent-600)_16%,var(--color-accent-700))]\",\n  \"[--color-button-ghost-background:var(--color-accent-100)] dark:[--color-button-ghost-background:var(--color-accent-900)]\",\n  \"[--color-button-ghost-hover:var(--color-accent-200)] dark:[--color-button-ghost-hover:var(--color-accent-800)]\",\n  \"[--color-button-ghost-active:var(--color-accent-300)] dark:[--color-button-ghost-active:var(--color-accent-700)]\",\n  \"[--color-button-ghost-foreground:var(--color-accent-700)] dark:[--color-button-ghost-foreground:var(--color-accent-300)]\",\n  \"[--color-badge-new-background:var(--color-accent-100)] dark:[--color-badge-new-background:color-mix(in_srgb,var(--color-accent-950)_50%,transparent)]\",\n  \"[--color-badge-new-text:var(--color-accent-600)] dark:[--color-badge-new-text:var(--color-accent-400)]\",\n].join(\" \");\n\nexport interface AccentScopeProps {\n  /** Any CSS colour: a palette variable, hex, oklch()… */\n  color: string;\n  children?: ReactNode;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\n/** Re-tints every kit control inside it from one `--accent` value. */\nexport function AccentScope({ color, children, className }: AccentScopeProps) {\n  return (\n    <div style={{ \"--accent\": color } as CSSProperties} className={cx(accentScopeClassName, className)}>\n      {children}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n/** Glossy sphere: a light spot up-left, the colour, a darker rim. */\nfunction sphereBackground(s: AccentSwatch) {\n  const gloss =\n    \"radial-gradient(circle at 32% 26%, color-mix(in oklab, white 85%, transparent) 0%, color-mix(in oklab, white 0%, transparent) 42%)\";\n  if (s.fill) return `${gloss}, ${s.fill}`;\n  return `${gloss}, radial-gradient(circle at 40% 35%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--swatch) 70%, white) 0%, var(--swatch) 52%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--swatch) 72%, black) 100%)`;\n}\n\nexport function AccentSwatchPicker({\n  swatches = DEFAULT_ACCENT_SWATCHES,\n  value,\n  defaultValue,\n  onChange,\n  size = \"md\",\n  variant = \"plain\",\n  label = \"Accent colour\",\n  isDisabled,\n  className,\n}: AccentSwatchPickerProps) {\n  return (\n    <RadioGroup\n      aria-label={label}\n      orientation=\"horizontal\"\n      value={value}\n      defaultValue={value === undefined ? (defaultValue ?? swatches[0]?.id) : undefined}\n      onChange={onChange}\n      isDisabled={isDisabled}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-fit flex-wrap items-center justify-center\",\n        GAP[size],\n        variant === \"pill\" &&\n          \"rounded-full border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-1.5 shadow-md\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {swatches.map((s) => (\n        <Radio\n          key={s.id}\n          value={s.id}\n          aria-label={s.label}\n          style={{ \"--swatch\": s.color } as CSSProperties}\n          className={cx(\n            \"group relative shrink-0 cursor-pointer rounded-full outline-none\",\n            \"data-disabled:cursor-not-allowed data-disabled:opacity-50\",\n          )}\n        >\n          <span\n            aria-hidden\n            style={{ background: sphereBackground(s) }}\n            className={cx(\n              \"block rounded-full\",\n              SPHERE[size],\n              \"shadow-[inset_0_-2px_4px_color-mix(in_oklab,var(--swatch)_55%,black),0_1px_2px_color-mix(in_oklab,var(--swatch)_30%,transparent)]\",\n              \"outline-2 outline-offset-2 outline-transparent\",\n              \"transition-[scale,outline-color] duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              \"group-data-hovered:scale-105 group-data-pressed:scale-95\",\n              \"group-data-selected:outline-(--swatch)\",\n              \"group-data-focus-visible:outline-text-primary\",\n              size === \"sm\" && \"outline-offset-1\",\n            )}\n          />\n        </Radio>\n      ))}\n    </RadioGroup>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: no timers, listeners or observers; state is the selected id.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 150ms hover/press scale and outline colour change on each sphere; it carries `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so selection snaps. The re-tint itself is instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Overriding only `--color-accent-500` on a wrapper. Tokens like the primary-button gradient were already resolved from the root ramp, so nothing inside changes until they are re-declared in the scope.\n- Using the focus-ring token for the swatch focus state inside a scope: it now equals the selected colour, so focus becomes invisible. Use a neutral (text-primary) outline for focus.\n- Making each swatch its own button with aria-pressed. A single choice is a radio group; that gives roving focus and arrow keys for free.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Swatch colours default to kit palette variables (`var(--color-violet-500)`…), so they follow the theme file; any CSS colour works.\n- Composer Loader takes hex stops for its SVG band, so the demo keeps a four-stop band per swatch next to the picker.\n- Two pickers can share one state, as the demo's big row and compact pill do.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe iridescent sphere starts selected with an outline. Clicking \"Violet\" outlines it, turns the send button gradient and the \"Agent working\" switch violet, and the caption reads \"Violet · --accent: var(--color-violet-500)\"; the compact pill shows the same selection. With focus on the group, ArrowRight selects the next sphere. Toggling the switch off fades the composer light and swaps the Stop button for Send.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "accordion": "# Build: Accordion\n\nStacked sections for FAQs, settings groups and order details. `Accordion` is React Aria's `DisclosureGroup` and each `AccordionItem` is a `Disclosure`, so every header is a real button inside a heading with `aria-expanded`, and a collapsed panel stays searchable by find-in-page. Opening is pure CSS: React Aria measures the panel into `--disclosure-panel-height`, the panel eases to that height over 300ms while the body fades in, drops 4px and sharpens from a 4px blur, and the indicator turns with it. Three looks share the code: hairline-divided rows, the landing FAQ's tray of rounded cards, and one bordered card per item.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSections that expand in place in divided, contained and separated styles, with an eased height and a blur-in body, on React Aria.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Accordion` renders `AriaDisclosureGroup` with all props passed through (`defaultExpandedKeys`, `expandedKeys`, `allowsMultipleExpanded`, `onExpandedChange`) and puts `{ variant, indicator }` in a context, so items style themselves without prop drilling.\n2. `AccordionItem` renders `AriaDisclosure` with `group/item`, then an `AriaHeading` (level 3 by default) holding an `AriaButton slot=\"trigger\"`: optional icon, title in `text-headline-medium`, optional description, and the indicator icon. Keyboard focus shows an inset 2px focus ring.\n3. The indicator is `RiArrowDownSLine` or `RiAddLine`. On `group-data-[expanded]/item` it rotates 180deg (chevron) or 45deg (plus turns into a close) and darkens from tertiary to primary icon colour, over 300ms.\n4. `AriaDisclosurePanel` gets `h-(--disclosure-panel-height) overflow-clip transition-[height] duration-300`. React Aria's `useDisclosure` writes the measured content height into that custom property, so the height eases between 0 and the real size.\n5. The body inside starts at `opacity-0` and, when motion is allowed, `-translate-y-1 blur-[4px]`. On expand it goes to `opacity-100 translate-y-0 blur-[0px]` with the same 300ms curve.\n6. Variant classes come from a `sortCx` map: `divided` uses `divide-y divide-separator-border`; `contained` is a `rounded-[26px]` tray with 3px gaps holding `rounded-[23px]` cards (surfaces swap in dark mode); `separated` is `gap-2` between `rounded-2xl` bordered cards with `shadow-xs`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `EASE = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), 300ms`\n  A fast-out, soft-landing curve shared with the agent limits card and Tree View, used on the height, the body fade and the indicator turn so they move as one.\n- `body entrance = opacity 0 → 1, translate-y -4px → 0, blur 4px → 0`\n  The text settles into place as the panel opens instead of being revealed by the clip edge alone.\n- `contained radii = tray rounded-[26px] p-[3px]; card rounded-[23px]`\n  The card radius is the tray radius minus its 3px padding, so the curves stay concentric.\n- `indicator turn = chevron rotate-180; plus rotate-45`\n  A chevron points back up; a plus rotated 45deg reads as a close mark.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype AccordionVariant = \"divided\" | \"contained\" | \"separated\"\ninterface AccordionProps extends Omit<DisclosureGroupProps, \"className\" | \"children\"> { variant?: AccordionVariant; indicator?: \"chevron\" | \"plus\"; className?: string; children: ReactNode }\ninterface AccordionItemProps extends Omit<DisclosureProps, \"className\" | \"children\"> { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; icon?: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6; className?: string; children: ReactNode }\nfunction Accordion(props: AccordionProps): JSX.Element\nfunction AccordionItem(props: AccordionItemProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `variant: \"divided\" | \"contained\" | \"separated\"` — default `\"divided\"`\n  Accordion: hairline rows, a tray of cards, or one bordered card per item.\n- `indicator: \"chevron\" | \"plus\"` — default `\"chevron\"`\n  Accordion: chevron that flips, or plus that turns into a close.\n- `defaultExpandedKeys / expandedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Accordion: uncontrolled or controlled open items (React Aria).\n- `allowsMultipleExpanded: boolean` — default `false`\n  Accordion: let several items stay open (React Aria).\n- `id: Key`\n  AccordionItem: key matched by the expanded keys.\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  AccordionItem: header text inside the trigger button.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  AccordionItem: supporting line under the title.\n- `icon: ComponentType`\n  AccordionItem: Remix Icon shown before the title.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6` — default `3`\n  AccordionItem: heading level wrapping the trigger.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  AccordionItem: dims to 50% and blocks toggling (React Aria).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AccordionItem({\n  title,\n  description,\n  icon: Icon,\n  headingLevel = 3,\n  className,\n  children,\n  ...props\n}: AccordionItemProps) {\n  const { variant, indicator } = useContext(AccordionContext);\n  const Indicator = indicator === \"plus\" ? RiAddLine : RiArrowDownSLine;\n\n  return (\n    <AriaDisclosure\n      {...props}\n      className={cx(\"group/item data-[disabled]:opacity-50\", styles.item[variant], className)}\n    >\n      <AriaHeading level={headingLevel} className=\"flex\">\n        <AriaButton\n          slot=\"trigger\"\n          className={({ isFocusVisible }) =>\n            cx(\n              \"group/trigger flex w-full cursor-pointer items-center gap-3 text-start outline-none\",\n              \"disabled:cursor-not-allowed\",\n              isFocusVisible && \"ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-inset\",\n              styles.trigger[variant],\n            )\n          }\n        >\n          {Icon ? <Icon className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" aria-hidden /> : null}\n          <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n            <span className=\"text-headline-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</span>\n            {description ? <span className=\"text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{description}</span> : null}\n          </span>\n          <Indicator\n            aria-hidden\n            className={cx(\n              \"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-tertiary transition-[rotate,color] duration-300 motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              EASE,\n              \"group-hover/trigger:text-foreground-icon-secondary group-data-[expanded]/item:text-foreground-icon-primary\",\n              indicator === \"plus\" ? \"group-data-[expanded]/item:rotate-45\" : \"group-data-[expanded]/item:rotate-180\",\n            )}\n          />\n        </AriaButton>\n      </AriaHeading>\n      <AriaDisclosurePanel\n        className={cx(\"h-(--disclosure-panel-height) overflow-clip transition-[height] duration-300 motion-reduce:transition-none\", EASE)}\n      >\n        <div\n          className={cx(\n            \"text-body-regular text-text-secondary\",\n            \"opacity-0 transition-[opacity,translate,filter] duration-300 motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            \"motion-safe:-translate-y-1 motion-safe:blur-[4px]\",\n            \"group-data-[expanded]/item:translate-y-0 group-data-[expanded]/item:opacity-100 group-data-[expanded]/item:blur-[0px]\",\n            EASE,\n            styles.content[variant],\n          )}\n        >\n          {children}\n        </div>\n      </AriaDisclosurePanel>\n    </AriaDisclosure>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. React Aria owns the expanded state and the panel measurement; the component adds no listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Every transition carries `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and the body's 4px drop and blur are applied only under `motion-safe:`. With reduced motion the panel snaps open, the text appears in place, and the indicator jumps to its turned angle.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Give every `AccordionItem` a stable `id`. `defaultExpandedKeys` and `expandedKeys` match on it.\n- Do not animate the panel with `max-height` or JS. The height comes from `--disclosure-panel-height`, which React Aria keeps up to date when the content changes.\n- The group allows one open item at a time by default. Pass `allowsMultipleExpanded` for settings groups where several can stay open.\n- Pick `headingLevel` to fit the page outline; the default h3 is wrong under an h1-only page.\n- Do not put interactive controls inside `title` or `description`. They render inside the trigger button.\n\n## Notes\n\n- A collapsed panel uses `hidden=\"until-found\"` (set by React Aria's `useDisclosure`), so the browser's find-in-page can reach hidden text and expand its section.\n- The `separated` card uses `bg-background-full` (the modal ground) rather than the card surface, so inputs and switch tracks inside keep an edge in dark mode.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA contained accordion with `indicator=\"plus\"` and `defaultExpandedKeys={[\"billing\"]}` shows three rounded cards on a tray with the first open and its plus turned into a close. Clicking the second header closes the first and opens the second: its height eases over about 300ms and its text fades in from a slight blur while dropping 4px. Each header is a button with `aria-expanded` inside an h3, Enter and Space toggle it, and find-in-page for text in a closed panel opens that panel. With reduced motion everything snaps.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "activity-rings-card": "# Build: Activity Rings Card\n\nA grey dashboard card with one value tile per goal (Move, Exercise, Running by default) above a stack of concentric SVG progress rings. Each ring starts at twelve o'clock and sweeps clockwise to its share of the goal over a tinted track, the outer ring first. Hovering or focusing a ring or its tile dims the others and puts the percentage in the centre, and clicking a tile pins that focus. Under reduced motion the rings are simply there at their final length.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nWatch-style concentric goal rings in SVG with value tiles: the rings draw in on mount, and hovering a ring or tile focuses it.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each ring is a pair of circles rotated by -90° so the path starts at twelve o'clock: a track and a progress stroke. The track colour is the ring colour mixed 22% into `--color-chart-track` with `color-mix(in oklab, …)`, so it reads as a pale tint in light mode and a dark tint in dark mode.\n2. The progress circle has `pathLength={100}`, so the dash maths is in percent regardless of radius: `stroke-dasharray: 100 100` and `stroke-dashoffset: 100 - pct` leave exactly pct percent of the lap visible, with round caps on both ends. Progress is clamped to 0–100 for the arc.\n3. Radii step inward: the outer ring sits half a stroke inside the edge and each next one moves in by one 18px band plus a 4px gap, which leaves a 60px hole at the default 184px size. More than three rings get thinner bands so they still fit.\n4. On mount every offset is 100 (nothing drawn). Two `requestAnimationFrame`s later `drawn` flips to true and the offsets move to their targets through a CSS `transition-[stroke-dashoffset]` of 1100ms with an expo-out curve, delayed 140ms per ring from the outside in. A remount (the demo's Replay) draws them again.\n5. Focus is `preview ?? pinned`. Pointer enter on a ring group (hit-tested on the stroke only, so the gaps do not count) or on a tile sets the preview; leaving clears it; clicking a tile toggles the pinned ring, which can also be controlled with `focused` and `onFocusedChange`. Unfocused rings fade to 30% and unfocused tiles to 50%.\n6. The centre readout is a polite live region that shows the focused ring's percentage and label, keyed by ring so `.animate-number-fade` replays on each change. The SVG itself carries an `aria-label` listing every ring's percentage.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `STROKE / RING_GAP = 18px / 4px at 184px`\n  Fat bands with a hairline of card between them read as separate rings at a glance; both scale with `size`.\n- `draw = 1100ms, cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  The expo-out curve spends most of the time near the end, so the rings sweep in fast and settle gently on the exact value.\n- `STAGGER_MS = 140ms`\n  Enough to see the rings draw one after another from the outside in without making the last one feel late.\n- `track mix = 22%`\n  Keeps the unfinished part of the lap visibly the ring's colour but much lighter than the stroke, in both themes.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ActivityRingsCard(props?: ActivityRingsCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype ActivityRing = { id: string; label: string; value: number; goal: number; display?: string; color: string }\nconst ACTIVITY_RINGS: ActivityRing[]\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Activity\"`\n  Header label.\n- `caption: string`\n  Right-hand header text, such as the selected day.\n- `rings: ActivityRing[]` — default `ACTIVITY_RINGS`\n  Rings from the outside in, each with its value, goal, display text and colour.\n- `size: number` — default `184`\n  Diameter of the ring stack in px; stroke and gap scale with it.\n- `focused: string | null`\n  Controlled pinned ring id.\n- `onFocusedChange: (id: string | null) => void`\n  Called when a tile click pins or unpins a ring.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Each ring is a circle with pathLength 100, so a dash of p units is p percent of the lap.\n// The outer radius leaves half a stroke of room; each inner ring steps in by one band plus the gap.\nconst stroke = rings.length > 3 ? Math.max(8, (size / 2 - 20) / rings.length - RING_GAP) : (STROKE * size) / SIZE;\nconst geometry = rings.map((ring, i) => {\n  const r = size / 2 - stroke / 2 - i * (stroke + RING_GAP);\n  const pct = ring.goal > 0 ? (ring.value / ring.goal) * 100 : 0;\n  const shown = drawn || reduced ? Math.min(100, Math.max(0, pct)) : 0;\n  return { ring, r, pct, offset: 100 - shown, delay: i * STAGGER_MS };\n});\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The mount effect cancels both pending animation frames on unmount. Everything else is CSS transitions and React event props.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` renders the rings at their final offsets from the start and `motion-reduce:transition-none` removes the draw transition, so nothing sweeps. The focus fades and the centre readout fade are also instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating `stroke-dasharray` or computing `2πr` per ring. `pathLength={100}` makes every ring use the same 0–100 numbers, so one transition rule covers them all.\n- Starting the transition in the same frame as the first paint. Setting `drawn` straight from `useEffect` can be batched with the initial render and the rings appear without animating; waiting two animation frames guarantees the browser has painted the empty state first.\n- Letting progress past 100% wrap the dash. The arc is clamped to a full lap; the centre readout still shows the real percentage, such as 112%.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `ACTIVITY_RINGS` and the `ActivityRing` type are exported. A ring is `{ id, label, value, goal, display?, color }`.\n- Pass `caption` to show the day on show at the top right; the Most Active Days Card demo uses this to pair the two cards.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe default card reads \"Activity\" with three tiles, Move 1,592 kcal (pink), Exercise 1h 45m (lime) and Running 5.2 km (sky), and three rings that draw clockwise from the top to about 88%, 81% and 76%. Hovering the Exercise ring or tile dims the other rings and tiles and shows \"81%\" and \"Exercise\" in the centre; leaving restores them. Replay draws the rings again.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-chat": "# Build: Agent Chat\n\nThe chat starter's home screen: the dashboard sidebar, a rounded chat card with an overlaid header, a streaming transcript and a pill composer, and a 260px rail of past chats. Messages go through the AI SDK's `useChat` to `/api/chat`, or to a scripted `DemoTransport` when the runtime has no key or `offline` is set. Assistant replies soften in a paragraph at a time, and threads are kept in localStorage, so history survives a reload without a database.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full AI chat screen on the AI SDK: sidebar, a chat card whose replies blur in line by line, and a local thread history rail.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @ai-sdk/react@^4.0.166, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, ai@^7.0.162, motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A single transport is created in a `useState` initializer so its identity never changes: `new SwitchingTransport(new DefaultChatTransport({ api: \"/api/chat\" }), new DemoTransport())`. `useChat({ transport })` drives `messages`, `status`, `sendMessage`, `stop` and `setMessages`. `submit` trims the input, stamps `activeUpdatedAt` with `Date.now()` and calls `sendMessage({ text }, { body: { demo: demoActive } })`. `SwitchingTransport.sendMessages` reads `body.demo` and hands the call to the canned transport when it is true, otherwise to the live one; `reconnectToStream` always goes live.\n2. On mount the screen probes the runtime with `fetch(\"/api/chat\", { method: \"GET\" })`. Only an explicit `configured: false` marks it \"unconfigured\"; a failed or unreadable probe counts as \"ready\" so a network fault never hides the composer. The probe's `providerLabel` and `model` feed the composer's status row. With no key, no messages and no demo flag, `SetupNotice` replaces the transcript with the AI_API_KEY steps and a \"Skip, show me the demo\" button that sets `demo` and stores `\"on\"` under `kinetic:agent-chat-demo`. `offline` skips the probe entirely and forces `demoActive`, so nothing touches the network.\n3. `DemoTransport.sendMessages` picks a reply with `pickDemoAnswer` (five regex-matched answers and a fallback) and returns a `ReadableStream<UIMessageChunk>`. It enqueues `start` and `start-step`, waits 700ms, sends `text-start`, splits the reply with `/(?<=\\s)/` so each word keeps its trailing space, and enqueues one `text-delta` every 28ms before `text-end`, `finish-step` and `finish`. The waits listen to the `AbortSignal`, so `stop()` clears the pending timer and the stream ends with an `abort` chunk.\n4. `busy` is `status === \"submitted\" || status === \"streaming\"`. `AgentThinking variant=\"wave\"` shows while `busy` and the last assistant message has no text yet, rather than only while `submitted`, because reasoning models flip the status to `streaming` on their first non-text chunk.\n5. `AgentMessage` renders the user turn as a right-aligned `max-w-[75%] rounded-2xl` bubble. The assistant turn goes through `StreamedText`, which splits on `\"\\n\"` and renders each line as a memoised `motion.p` that animates once from `{ opacity: 0, filter: \"blur(5px)\", y: 4 }` to `{ opacity: 1, filter: \"blur(0px)\", y: 0 }` over 0.42s with ease `[0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1]`. Lines are keyed by index, so a line mounts once and then only grows. Copy, read-aloud (`speechSynthesis`) and a relative timestamp sit under the reply and stay hidden while it streams.\n6. Threads live in component state and localStorage under `kinetic:agent-chat-threads`. A mount effect assigns a fresh thread id (`crypto.randomUUID()` with a fallback) and restores up to 30 stored threads. The open thread is derived with `useMemo` (its title is the first user message cut at 48 characters plus \"...\"), merged into the list and sorted by `updatedAt`. The list is written back only once `restored` is true and the stream is idle. `messageAt` records when each message id first appeared, for the hover timestamps.\n7. The header is `absolute` and 48px tall (`h-12`) over the scroll area, and the column starts at `pt-[72px]`. A `ResizeObserver` on the scroller checks whether `title.right + 16 > column.left`. Only when the transcript is scrolled and the title would clash does the header show its border and frost to `bg-white/20 backdrop-blur-[20px]` (`dark:bg-black/20`). Each change to `messages` or `busy` scrolls the transcript to `scrollHeight` with `behavior: \"smooth\"`.\n8. The root is `flex gap-4 p-3 bg-background-full`, `h-dvh` by default or `h-[var(--template-preview-height)]` when `contained`. `DashboardSidebar` with `starterNav(useStarterBase())` is `hidden lg:flex`; below `lg` a menu button opens it as an overlay drawer (`absolute` when contained, else `fixed`). `AgentChatHistory` is a 260px card, `hidden xl:flex`, with \"New chat\", rename, mark-unread and delete on each row, an account menu and a JSON export.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `THINK_MS / WORD_MS = 700ms, then 28ms per word`\n  The demo stream pauses long enough for the thinking indicator to read as a real wait, then releases whole words on a steady tick so the line-level blur-in looks like a live model rather than a paste.\n- `LINE_TRANSITION = { duration: 0.42, ease: [0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1] }, from blur(5px), opacity 0, y 4`\n  Animating once per line instead of per token avoids the stutter of a fragment blurring in and then mutating into a finished word. The objects sit at module scope so Motion gets a stable target on every token re-render.\n- `MAX_THREADS = 30`\n  Enough history to be useful without letting localStorage grow unbounded. `orderThreads` sorts newest first and slices to this count on every merge.\n- `header and column offset = header h-12 (48px) absolute, column pt-[72px]`\n  The header overlays the transcript instead of stacking above it, so text runs up to the card's rounded top. The 72px top padding (48px header plus 24px) clears the title when nothing is scrolled.\n- `clash buffer = 16px (title.right + 16 > column.left)`\n  On a wide screen the centred column never reaches a short title, so the border and frost only appear when the measured geometry says the text would actually run under the title.\n- `title cut = 48 characters plus \"...\"`\n  A derived title has to fit the header and a 260px rail row. Renaming sets `activeTitle`, so the derived title cannot overwrite a chosen name.\n- `composer pill = h-[52px] rounded-full p-2 gap-2.5, 36px (size-9) controls`\n  Matches the Pro composer's geometry, so 8px padding seats the 36px circles flush inside a 52px pill and the starter reads as the same product.\n- `feedback reset = 1600ms`\n  How long the share and copy buttons show their check before reverting. Both timers are cleared if the state changes or the button unmounts.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AgentChat({ className, contained = false, offline = false }: { className?: string; contained?: boolean; offline?: boolean })\nclass DemoTransport implements ChatTransport<UIMessage>\nclass SwitchingTransport implements ChatTransport<UIMessage> { constructor(live: ChatTransport<UIMessage>, canned: ChatTransport<UIMessage>) }\nfunction pickDemoAnswer(prompt: string): string\n```\n\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root with tailwind-merge. Pass `h-full` to fill a host instead of the viewport.\n- `contained: boolean` — default `false`\n  Fill `--template-preview-height` instead of `h-dvh`, and keep the phone nav drawer `absolute` inside the frame.\n- `offline: boolean` — default `false`\n  Skip the `/api/chat` probe and answer every message from the scripted `DemoTransport`. No network requests.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Demo mode: the visitor skipped the key notice. Only meaningful while the\n// runtime reports no key; the moment a key exists, the real route answers.\nconst [demo, setDemo] = useState(false);\nconst demoActive = offline || (demo && probe.status === \"unconfigured\");\nconst [transport] = useState(\n  () => new SwitchingTransport(new DefaultChatTransport({ api: ENDPOINT }), new DemoTransport()),\n);\nuseEffect(() => {\n  try {\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/set-state-in-effect -- restoring from localStorage after mount; there is no render-time source for it\n    setDemo(window.localStorage.getItem(DEMO_KEY) === \"on\");\n  } catch {\n    // Storage can be blocked; the notice simply shows again.\n  }\n}, []);\nconst enterDemo = () => {\n  setDemo(true);\n  try {\n    window.localStorage.setItem(DEMO_KEY, \"on\");\n  } catch {\n    // Nothing to remember without storage.\n  }\n};\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage, setMessages, status, stop, error } = useChat({\n  transport,\n});\n\nconst busy = status === \"submitted\" || status === \"streaming\";\n\n// Reasoning models stream their thinking before any text, and the SDK flips\n// status to \"streaming\" on that first non-text chunk. Gating the indicator on\n// \"submitted\" alone therefore hides it seconds before a word appears, leaving\n// an empty transcript. It stays up until real text exists.\nconst streamedText = useMemo(() => {\n  const last = messages[messages.length - 1];\n  if (!last || last.role !== \"assistant\") return \"\";\n  return last.parts\n    .filter((part) => part.type === \"text\")\n    .map((part) => part.text)\n    .join(\"\");\n}, [messages]);\nconst showThinking = busy && streamedText.length === 0;\n\n/** The open chat as plain text, for the share control. */\nconst transcript = useMemo(\n  () =>\n    messages\n      .map((message) => `${message.role === \"user\" ? \"You\" : \"Assistant\"}: ${messageText(message)}`)\n      .filter((line) => !line.endsWith(\": \"))\n      .join(\"\\n\\n\"),\n  [messages],\n);\n\n// Ask the runtime what it has before the first message, so a deployment with\n// no key renders setup steps instead of letting someone type into a dead box.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (offline) return;\n  let cancelled = false;\n  fetch(ENDPOINT, { method: \"GET\" })\n    .then((res) => (res.ok ? res.json() : null))\n    .then((data: { configured?: boolean; provider?: string; providerLabel?: string; model?: string } | null) => {\n      if (cancelled) return;\n      // Only an explicit `configured: false` means \"no key\". A failed or\n      // unreadable probe says nothing about the key, and treating it as\n      // missing would hide the composer behind setup steps for a fault that\n      // has nothing to do with configuration.\n      setProbe({\n        status: data && data.configured === false ? \"unconfigured\" : \"ready\",\n        provider: data?.provider ?? null,\n        label: data?.providerLabel ?? null,\n        model: data?.model ?? null,\n      });\n    })\n    .catch(() => {\n      // A failed probe shouldn't lock the composer — let the send attempt\n      // surface the real error rather than guessing the cause here.\n      if (!cancelled) setProbe({ status: \"ready\", provider: null, label: null, model: null });\n    });\n  return () => {\n    cancelled = true;\n  };\n}, [offline]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The probe effect sets a `cancelled` flag so a late response never sets state. The header `ResizeObserver` is disconnected. The 1600ms copy and share timers are cleared, and `speechSynthesis.cancel()` runs when a message unmounts. Calling `stop()` aborts the demo stream's pending wait. The export blob URL is revoked a tick after the download starts, and the React Aria menus remove their portals when they close.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `StreamedText` passes `initial={false}`, so each line appears settled with no blur, fade or rise. `AgentThinking` falls back to static rendering on its own. The smooth auto-scroll to the newest message is not gated.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Generating the thread id during render. It would differ between server and client and break hydration, which is why `activeId` starts as \"\" and the mount effect assigns it.\n- Persisting before the restore has run. On the first render `threads` is empty, and writing that would erase stored history on every load. The writer waits for `restored` and for the stream to go idle.\n- Constructing the transport inline. A new `SwitchingTransport` per render hands `useChat` a new transport each time. Keep it in a `useState` initializer.\n- Treating a failed probe as \"no key\". Only an explicit `configured: false` shows the setup notice. Anything else lets the send attempt surface the real error.\n- Gating the thinking indicator on `status === \"submitted\"` alone. Reasoning models switch to `streaming` before any text arrives, which leaves an empty transcript for seconds.\n- Animating each word of the reply. Tokens arrive as fragments and the blur-in plays on a fragment that then changes, which reads as a stutter. Animate whole lines and memoise them.\n- Revoking the export blob URL in the same turn as `click()`. The browser may not have read it yet; the URL is revoked on a `setTimeout(..., 0)`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The route handlers live in `registry/lib/agent-chat-route.ts` and no file of this item imports them. A host must re-export them from `app/api/chat/route.ts` and declare `export const maxDuration = 30` there, because Next reads route config only from the route file.\n- `offline` is a Kinetic addition for previews. It skips the GET probe and routes every send to `DemoTransport`, so the status row reads \"Demo mode\" and the empty state says the answers are scripted.\n- Sidebar links come from `starterNav(useStarterBase())` in `app-shell`. Wrap the screen in `StarterBaseContext.Provider value={STARTER_SITE_PREFIX}` to point them into the hosted starter.\n- Every subcomponent returns its JSX through `useTemplateCopy()`, which is the identity without a `TemplateCopyProvider`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe screen shows the sidebar, a chat card reading \"What can I help with?\" with three suggestion chips, and the history rail. Clicking \"Explain what this starter does\" adds a right-aligned user bubble, shows the wave \"Thinking\" indicator for about 700ms, then streams a scripted reply word by word with each paragraph blurring in. The header title becomes the prompt, the composer row reads \"Demo mode\" and \"2 messages\", and the thread appears under \"Recent\" in the rail.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-creator": "# Build: Agent Creator\n\nAn agent setup card. The top half is a paper-cut avatar ringed by sixteen face options, with nine shapes on an arc underneath that rotates to bring the chosen one into its socket, and a colour pill that recolours every piece of paper. Below it, grouped fields collect the name, label, description, model, tools, language, notifications, voice and playback speed. Create validates the draft, awaits onCreate, and swaps to a created summary with Edit details and Create another.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA create-an-agent card: paper avatar with a face ring and shape arc, colour pill, profile, tools and voice fields, then a created summary.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Faces sit on a circle of radius 104px around the avatar at 22.5° steps; shapes sit on a 162px arc below it, each tile placed by its wrapped offset k from the selected index (θ = 90° − 24°·k), so picking a shape slides the arc with a CSS transform transition.\n2. Faces, shapes and colours are radiogroups with a roving tabindex: arrow keys and Home/End move the selection and focus together, so a keyboard user can turn the arc without a pointer.\n3. The avatar is SVG paper: the silhouette drawn twice (a 28% darker copy offset 2.5/3.5 units for the cut edge), a feTurbulence speckle filter clipped to the shape alpha for grain, and ink features drawn in a fixed dark tone because they sit on paper, not on the page.\n4. validateAgent(draft) returns per-field errors. They appear only after the first Create, update live while typing, and the first invalid field receives focus; a footer line counts what is left to fix.\n5. Submit sets a submitting state, awaits onCreate, and guards against unmount with a mounted ref; a rejection keeps the form and shows its message, success swaps to the summary and focuses its heading.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `FACE_R = 104`\n  Clears the 136px avatar with room for 32px face discs without touching the shape arc.\n- `SHAPE_R = 162`\n  Hangs the arc under the face ring; five visible tiles span about 270px, inside the 320px stage.\n- `SHAPE_STEP_DEG = 24`\n  Spacing between neighbouring shapes on the arc; wider steps push the outer tiles off the card.\n- `SHAPE_VISIBLE = 2`\n  Tiles with |k| greater than 2 fade out and stop taking pointer input.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\n<AgentCreator defaultValue={{ name: \"Cloud engineer\" }} onCreate={async (draft) => save(draft)} onReset={() => {}} />\n```\n\n- `value: AgentDraft`\n  Controlled draft. Pair with onChange.\n- `defaultValue: Partial<AgentDraft>`\n  Uncontrolled starting draft merged over DEFAULT_AGENT_DRAFT; Reset restores it.\n- `onChange: (draft: AgentDraft) => void`\n  Called on every field, face, shape or colour change.\n- `onCreate: (draft: AgentDraft) => void | Promise<void>`\n  Called with a valid draft. A promise shows the creating state; a rejection shows its message.\n- `onReset: () => void`\n  Called after Reset or Create another.\n- `models / tools / languages / voices / speeds: readonly AgentOption[]`\n  Option lists for the selects, tool chips and speed control.\n- `colors: readonly AgentColor[]` — default `AGENT_COLORS`\n  Preset paper colours; a custom colour input sits at the end.\n- `submitLabel: string` — default `\"Create agent\"`\n  Primary button text.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the card, e.g. a fixed height so the body scrolls.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Field-level problems with a draft; an empty object means it can be created. */\nexport function validateAgent(draft: AgentDraft): AgentErrors {\n  const errors: AgentErrors = {};\n  const name = draft.name.trim();\n  if (!name) errors.name = \"Give the agent a name.\";\n  else if (name.length < 2) errors.name = \"Use at least 2 characters.\";\n  else if (name.length > 40) errors.name = \"Keep the name under 40 characters.\";\n  const label = draft.label.trim();\n  if (!label) errors.label = \"Add a short label.\";\n  else if (label.length > 16) errors.label = \"Labels fit 16 characters.\";\n  if (draft.description.trim().length < 12) errors.description = \"Say what it helps with (12+ characters).\";\n  if (draft.tools.length === 0) errors.tools = \"Pick at least one tool.\";\n  return errors;\n}\n\n/** Place shape `index` on the arc relative to the selected one; k wraps to -n/2..n/2. */\nfunction arcSlot(index: number, selected: number, count: number) {\n  let k = (index - selected + count) % count;\n  if (k > count / 2) k -= count;\n  const theta = ((90 - k * SHAPE_STEP_DEG) * Math.PI) / 180;\n  const visible = Math.abs(k) <= SHAPE_VISIBLE;\n  return {\n    k,\n    visible,\n    x: CX + SHAPE_R * Math.cos(theta) - TILE / 2,\n    y: CY + SHAPE_R * Math.sin(theta) - TILE / 2,\n    scale: k === 0 ? 1 : visible ? 0.86 : 0.5,\n  };\n}\n\n/** Roving-tabindex arrow handling shared by the face ring and shape arc. */\nfunction rovingIndex(event: KeyboardEvent, index: number, count: number): number | null {\n  switch (event.key) {\n    case \"ArrowRight\":\n    case \"ArrowDown\":\n      return (index + 1) % count;\n    case \"ArrowLeft\":\n    case \"ArrowUp\":\n      return (index - 1 + count) % count;\n    case \"Home\":\n      return 0;\n    case \"End\":\n      return count - 1;\n    default:\n      return null;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The only async work is the awaited onCreate; a mounted ref drops its result after unmount. Motion loops stop with their elements. The demo clears its pending save timers on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The avatar stops floating and blinking, the pop on pick is skipped, the shape arc and face hover snap without transitions, and the created summary appears without its rise.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Ink stays dark in both themes on purpose; it is drawn on the coloured paper, not on the page background.\n- Each PaperAvatar builds its grain filter id from useId, stripped of colons, so several avatars on one page never share a filter.\n- The shape arc wraps: a tile moving from k = −4 to k = 4 crosses the arc while invisible, so only visible tiles take pointer input.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass value + onChange to drive the draft yourself; defaultValue seeds the uncontrolled draft and is what Reset returns to.\n- Export validateAgent to run the same checks on the server.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClearing the name and pressing Create shows \"Give the agent a name.\" and focuses the field; a valid draft shows Creating… then \"<name> is ready\"; Create another restores the starting draft; arrow keys on the face ring and shape arc change the avatar.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-detail-panel": "# Build: Agent Detail Panel\n\nThe side panel of a multi-agent workspace for the agent you picked: avatar and name, a live status pill, the task it is working on, a progress rail with \"n of m\" steps, optional stat tiles and a vertical step log. The footer drives the run: Pause turns into Resume, Stop asks for confirmation, and a finished, failed or stopped run offers Run again. The step log re-reads itself from the run status, so a paused run shows a paused step and a stopped run strikes out what will never happen.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSelected-agent panel: status pill, task, progress, step log and Pause / Resume / Stop actions that change the run state.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. useAgentRunStatus keeps the run status controlled (status) or uncontrolled (defaultStatus) and returns a dispatcher: pause → paused, resume and restart → running, stop → stopped; every change calls onStatusChange(next, action), and restart also calls onRestart so the owner can reset its steps.\n2. stepLook(stepState, runStatus) folds the run status into each step: the active step renders a spinner while running, a pause chip when paused, a question chip when waiting, a stop chip when stopped and an error chip when failed; pending steps become \"Skipped\" (struck through, detail hidden) once the run is stopped or failed.\n3. The progress rail is the kit ProgressTrack at doneCount / steps.length, recoloured through its text colour: accent while running, orange paused, neutral stopped, red failed, green done.\n4. The footer swaps between three layouts: Pause or Resume plus Stop while live, an inline \"Stop this run?\" group with Keep running / Stop run when confirmStop is on, and a status line with Run again once the run is terminal.\n5. When the active step id changes and followActive is on, an effect measures that row inside the scrolling body (offsetTop against the relative body) and, only if it is out of view, scrolls the body so its bottom sits 16px above the fold; the page itself never scrolls.\n6. The status pill has role=\"status\" so the new label (\"Paused\", \"Stopped\") is announced; every step carries an sr-only state word next to its title.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `step chip = 20px circle, 12px icon`\n  Wide enough for the 16px kit spinner glyph so the working step does not shift when it turns into a check.\n- `connector = 1px at start-2.5, from top-6 to bottom-1`\n  Centres the rail under the 20px chip and leaves a gap above the next chip.\n- `follow margin = 16px`\n  Leaves a sliver of the next pending step visible under the live one, so the log reads as continuing.\n- `stats grid = 3 columns`\n  Three short metrics (elapsed, files, tokens) fit a 360px-wide panel without wrapping.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AgentDetailPanel(props: { name: string; subtitle?: string; avatar?: ReactNode; task?: ReactNode; steps: AgentStep[]; stats?: AgentDetailStat[]; status?: AgentRunStatus; defaultStatus?: AgentRunStatus; onStatusChange?: (status: AgentRunStatus, action: AgentRunAction) => void; onRestart?: () => void; onClose?: () => void; confirmStop?: boolean; followActive?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `name: string`\n  Agent name in the header.\n- `subtitle: string`\n  Role or label under the name.\n- `avatar: ReactNode`\n  40px avatar; defaults to a seeded monogram.\n- `task: ReactNode`\n  The instruction the agent is working on.\n- `steps: AgentStep[]`\n  { id, title, detail?, time?, state: \"done\" | \"active\" | \"pending\" | \"failed\" }.\n- `stats: { label: string; value: ReactNode }[]`\n  Optional stat tiles under the progress rail.\n- `status: \"running\" | \"waiting\" | \"paused\" | \"done\" | \"stopped\" | \"error\"`\n  Controlled run status.\n- `defaultStatus: AgentRunStatus` — default `\"running\"`\n  Initial status when uncontrolled.\n- `onStatusChange: (status, action: \"pause\" | \"resume\" | \"stop\" | \"restart\") => void`\n  Called by every footer action.\n- `onRestart: () => void`\n  Shows Run again on terminal runs; reset your steps here.\n- `onClose: () => void`\n  Shows the header close button.\n- `confirmStop: boolean` — default `true`\n  Ask before stopping.\n- `followActive: boolean` — default `true`\n  Scroll the panel body to keep the active step in view.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the panel surface (width, height).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function stepLook(step: AgentStepState, run: AgentRunStatus): StepLook {\n  if (step === \"done\") return \"done\";\n  if (step === \"failed\") return \"failed\";\n  if (step === \"active\") {\n    if (run === \"running\") return \"working\";\n    if (run === \"waiting\") return \"waiting\";\n    if (run === \"paused\") return \"paused\";\n    if (run === \"error\") return \"failed\";\n    if (run === \"done\") return \"done\";\n    return \"stopped\";\n  }\n  return run === \"stopped\" || run === \"error\" ? \"skipped\" : \"pending\";\n}\n\nexport function nextRunStatus(action: AgentRunAction): AgentRunStatus {\n  if (action === \"pause\") return \"paused\";\n  if (action === \"stop\") return \"stopped\";\n  return \"running\";\n}\n\n/** Controlled-or-uncontrolled run status plus the action dispatcher behind the footer buttons. */\nexport function useAgentRunStatus({\n  status,\n  defaultStatus = \"running\",\n  onStatusChange,\n  onRestart,\n}: Pick<AgentDetailPanelProps, \"status\" | \"defaultStatus\" | \"onStatusChange\" | \"onRestart\">) {\n  const [innerStatus, setInnerStatus] = useState<AgentRunStatus>(defaultStatus);\n  const run = status ?? innerStatus;\n  const act = (action: AgentRunAction) => {\n    const next = nextRunStatus(action);\n    if (status === undefined) setInnerStatus(next);\n    onStatusChange?.(next, action);\n    if (action === \"restart\") onRestart?.();\n  };\n  return [run, act] as const;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The panel owns no timers or listeners; the demo clears its step-advance interval whenever the run leaves running and on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The running ping on the status dot is hidden, colour and confirm-row transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none, and the kit spinner slows to a 2.4s turn instead of stopping, and following the active step jumps (behavior auto) instead of smooth-scrolling; state changes are instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not mutate steps when the run stops: the panel derives Stopped and Skipped from the run status, and the owner keeps the real step states for a later resume.\n- Restart only resets the status; reset your steps in onRestart or the log still shows the old run as finished.\n- In controlled mode the panel never changes its own status: update status from onStatusChange or the buttons appear dead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass onClose to show a close button in the header; pass avatar to replace the seeded monogram (for example with a blob avatar).\n- Set the panel height through className; the body scrolls while the header and footer stay put.\n- The panel is a size container: below 22rem wide the status pill collapses to its coloured dot and keeps the label for screen readers, so the name has room.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith a running run, Pause sets the pill to \"Paused\" and shows Resume; Resume returns to \"Running\"; Stop shows \"Stop this run?\" and Stop run sets \"Stopped\", turns pending steps into \"Skipped\" and shows Run again, which calls onRestart and sets \"Running\"; the \"n of m\" count equals the done steps.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-limits-card": "# Build: Agent Limits Card\n\nA usage card for an agent session. The top row reads used / capacity with a percent, over a thin bar split into one coloured segment per token bucket. Opening the row grows the card to list each bucket with its tokens and share, free space, deferred buckets that do not count, and collapsible member lists. Below it, each plan limit shows its reset time, percent used and a progress bar, and the plan heading links to plan settings.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn agent budget card: a context window bar split by token bucket, an expandable breakdown, and plan limits with reset times.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. summarizeContext sums only non-deferred buckets, so deferred tools are listed with a dash but never move the bar, the total or free space; segment widths divide by max(capacity, used) so an overflowing window still ends at the bar edge.\n2. Bucket colours come from the chart tokens in a fixed order (blue, purple, pink, yellow, green, sky, teal, lime, indigo); the counter skips deferred buckets, which take the neutral chart colour, and a bucket can pass its own colour.\n3. The breakdown and every group list open with a grid-template-rows 0fr → 1fr transition on a wrapper with min-h-0 overflow-hidden, so the card grows to its natural height without measuring; closed content is inert and turns visibility-hidden once the close transition ends.\n4. Hovering a bar segment or a breakdown row fades every other segment and row, linking the two views.\n5. Each limit is a role=\"meter\" with aria-valuetext carrying the reset copy; its bar switches to the warning tone at 75% and the error tone at 90%.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `WARN_AT = 0.75`\n  A limit three-quarters used is worth a glance; the bar turns the warning colour.\n- `DANGER_AT = 0.9`\n  Past 90% the bar and the percent switch to the error colour.\n- `BUCKET_PALETTE = chart-6, 5, 3, 8, 7, 4, 1, 2, 9`\n  Neighbouring buckets get colours far apart on the wheel, so thin segments stay distinguishable.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\n<AgentLimitsCard context={{ capacity: 1_000_000, buckets, groups }} plan=\"Max (5x)\" limits={[{ label: \"5-hour limit\", used: 0.38, resets: \"Resets in 2 hr 46 min\" }]} planHref=\"/settings/plan\" />\n```\n\n- `context: { capacity: number; buckets: ContextBucket[]; groups?: ContextGroup[] }`\n  Window size, token buckets ({ label, tokens, color?, deferred? }) and collapsible member groups.\n- `plan: string`\n  Plan name shown after the limits title.\n- `limits: PlanLimit[]` — default `[]`\n  Rows of { label, used (0–1), resets }.\n- `planHref: string`\n  Makes the plan heading a link.\n- `onManagePlan: () => void`\n  Makes the plan heading a button when there is no planHref.\n- `expanded / defaultExpanded / onExpandedChange: boolean / boolean / (open: boolean) => void` — default `false`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled breakdown.\n- `maxBreakdownHeight: number`\n  Caps the breakdown height in px; longer lists scroll inside the card.\n- `contextTitle / limitsTitle: string` — default `\"Context window\" / \"Plan usage limits\"`\n  Section headings.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Splits the window into what counts and what does not. Deferred buckets are\n * listed but never summed, so they cannot push the bar past the window. If\n * the counted tokens overflow the capacity, segment widths scale by the\n * larger number so the bar still ends at its right edge.\n */\nexport function summarizeContext({ capacity, buckets }: ContextUsage) {\n  const safeCap = Math.max(1, capacity);\n  let used = 0;\n  for (const bucket of buckets) if (!bucket.deferred) used += Math.max(0, bucket.tokens);\n  const scale = Math.max(safeCap, used);\n  let paletteIndex = 0;\n  const views: BucketView[] = buckets.map((bucket) => {\n    const auto = bucket.deferred ? DEFERRED_COLOR : BUCKET_PALETTE[paletteIndex++ % BUCKET_PALETTE.length]!;\n    const tokens = Math.max(0, bucket.tokens);\n    return {\n      ...bucket,\n      color: bucket.color ?? auto,\n      share: bucket.deferred ? null : (tokens / safeCap) * 100,\n      width: bucket.deferred ? 0 : (tokens / scale) * 100,\n    };\n  });\n  const free = Math.max(0, safeCap - used);\n  return { views, used, free, percent: Math.round((used / safeCap) * 100) };\n}\n\n/** Clamps a limit share and picks its tone. */\nexport function limitTone(used: number) {\n  const value = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, used));\n  const tone = value >= DANGER_AT ? \"danger\" : value >= WARN_AT ? \"warning\" : \"normal\";\n  return { value, tone } as const;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers; all motion is CSS transitions on state changes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The breakdown and group lists open and close instantly, chevrons snap, and bar segments and limit fills jump to their new widths without transitions.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Deferred buckets are excluded from the used total; pass them for the listing only, not as part of the capacity maths.\n- With maxBreakdownHeight the breakdown scrolls, so focus rings inside it are inset to avoid being clipped.\n- Share percentages use the capacity even when the window overflows, so they can add up past 100%.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass planHref for a link or onManagePlan for a button; with neither, the plan heading is plain text.\n- formatTokens and summarizeContext are exported for use in tooltips or server summaries.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe header reads 820k / 1M (82%); clicking it reveals Messages 520k 52.0%, the deferred rows with a dash and Free space 180k 18.0%; opening MCP tools lists its four members; the plan heading calls onManagePlan; sending a message widens the Messages segment and raises the 5-hour limit.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-list-panel": "# Build: Agent List Panel\n\nThe left rail of a multi-agent workspace: a pill toolbar (search, optional extra buttons, a blue \"+\"), a three-way All / Running / Done filter with live counts, and a list of agents. Each row shows a 32px avatar with a status dot on its corner, the agent name with a relative time, and its last message on one truncated line. Rows select like a listbox with the pointer or the arrow keys, and the selection can be controlled or left to the panel.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAgent sidebar list with avatar, live status dot, last message and time, single selection, search and All/Running/Done tabs.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Counts for every tab are computed once per agents array with matchesAgentFilter: Running covers status running or waiting, Done covers done, All covers everything.\n2. The visible list is the agents array filtered by the active tab and by the search query (case-insensitive match on name or last message), memoised on agents, filter and query.\n3. The filter is a React Aria ToggleButtonGroup with selectionMode=\"single\" and disallowEmptySelection, so one tab is always pressed; filter/defaultFilter/onFilterChange make it controlled or uncontrolled.\n4. The list is a React Aria ListBox (single selection, disallowEmptySelection): arrow keys, Home/End and typeahead move focus, Enter/Space or a click select, and handleSelection maps the key back to the agent before calling onSelect.\n5. The search pill toggles an autofocused input under the toolbar; Escape or the pill (now an X) clears the query and closes it.\n6. Avatars default to AgentMonogram, a 32px rounded tile whose tint is picked by hashSeed(name) from five calendar-event palettes, so a name always keeps its colour; any node can replace it per agent.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `avatar = 32px tile, 10px radius`\n  Matches the reference rows; the status dot (10px with a 2px ring) sits on its bottom corner without covering the initials.\n- `toolbar pills = h-8, rounded-full, flex-1`\n  Search, extra buttons and \"+\" share the width evenly like the reference toolbar.\n- `filter rule = running = running | waiting; done = done`\n  An agent waiting on your input is still in progress; failed and idle agents only appear under All.\n- `tones = 5 (lime, blue, pink, purple, emerald)`\n  calendar-event tokens carry their own dark values, so monograms stay legible in both themes.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AgentListPanel(props: { agents: AgentListItem[]; selectedId?: string | null; defaultSelectedId?: string | null; onSelect?: (id: string, agent: AgentListItem) => void; filter?: AgentFilter; defaultFilter?: AgentFilter; onFilterChange?: (filter: AgentFilter) => void; onCreate?: () => void; createLabel?: string; toolbar?: ReactNode; searchable?: boolean; footer?: ReactNode; emptyLabel?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `agents: AgentListItem[]`\n  { id, name, lastMessage?, time?, status?, avatar?, unread? } per agent.\n- `selectedId: string | null`\n  Controlled selected agent id.\n- `defaultSelectedId: string | null` — default `null`\n  Initial selection when uncontrolled.\n- `onSelect: (id: string, agent: AgentListItem) => void`\n  Called when a different agent is chosen.\n- `filter: \"all\" | \"running\" | \"done\"`\n  Controlled filter tab.\n- `defaultFilter: \"all\" | \"running\" | \"done\"` — default `\"all\"`\n  Initial filter when uncontrolled.\n- `onFilterChange: (filter: AgentFilter) => void`\n  Called when a tab is pressed.\n- `onCreate: () => void`\n  Shows the blue \"+\" toolbar button and runs on press.\n- `createLabel: string` — default `\"New agent\"`\n  Accessible label of the \"+\" button.\n- `toolbar: ReactNode`\n  Extra toolbar buttons between search and \"+\".\n- `searchable: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the search toggle.\n- `footer: ReactNode`\n  Content pinned below the list.\n- `emptyLabel: string` — default `\"No agents here yet.\"`\n  Shown when a tab has no agents.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Agents\"`\n  Accessible name of the list.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the panel surface (set its width and height here).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst counts = useMemo(() => {\n  const out: Record<AgentFilter, number> = { all: 0, running: 0, done: 0 };\n  for (const a of agents) {\n    out.all += 1;\n    if (matchesAgentFilter(a.status, \"running\")) out.running += 1;\n    if (matchesAgentFilter(a.status, \"done\")) out.done += 1;\n  }\n  return out;\n}, [agents]);\n\nconst visible = useMemo(() => {\n  const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();\n  return agents.filter(\n    (a) =>\n      matchesAgentFilter(a.status, activeFilter) &&\n      (!q || a.name.toLowerCase().includes(q) || (a.lastMessage ?? \"\").toLowerCase().includes(q)),\n  );\n}, [agents, activeFilter, query]);\n\nconst setFilter = (next: AgentFilter) => {\n  if (filter === undefined) setInnerFilter(next);\n  onFilterChange?.(next);\n};\n\nconst handleSelection = (keys: Selection) => {\n  if (keys === \"all\") return;\n  const [first] = keys as Set<Key>;\n  if (first === undefined) return;\n  const id = String(first);\n  const agent = agents.find((a) => a.id === id);\n  if (!agent || id === activeId) return;\n  if (selectedId === undefined) setInnerSelected(id);\n  onSelect?.(id, agent);\n};\n\nconst closeSearch = () => {\n  setQuery(\"\");\n  setSearchOpen(false);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers of its own; React Aria owns the listbox and toggle-group handlers and removes them on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The running dot drops its ping halo (motion-reduce:hidden), and row, tab and search transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none; selection and filtering still work instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not render the list as buttons in a div: the ListBox gives one tab stop, arrow navigation and aria-selected for free.\n- When the selected agent is filtered out it stays selected; keep showing its conversation instead of clearing it.\n- The status dot ring is drawn in the panel background colour; on a different surface pass a matching ring-* class or the dot looks cut out.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass footer for Support / Settings links or an account card; it pins under the scrolling list.\n- AgentStatusDot and AGENT_STATUS_META are exported so the conversation header can show the same status.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPressing Running leaves only running and waiting agents and its count matches the rows shown; Done leaves only done agents; clicking a row (or arrowing to it and pressing Enter) gives it aria-selected=\"true\" and fires onSelect once; the search toggle shows an input that filters rows by name as you type and Escape closes and clears it; \"+\" runs onCreate.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-log": "# Build: Agent Log\n\nThe shared machinery behind BoardUI's Task List and Web Search: a line of agent work drawing itself down the page. `useRevealTicker` paces how many units are visible, `LogRow` blurs each row in as its height opens, softening the clipping edge with an animated mask, and draws its own piece of a curved tree guide so the line reads as one pen stroke. `WorkingRow` is the loose \"still going\" indicator at the bottom, and `ShimmerText` marks a line in progress. It says nothing about what a row contains; that is the consumer's job.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nStreaming agent-log parts: a reveal ticker, rows that blur in under a soft clipping edge, and a curved tree guide that draws itself.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `useRevealTicker({ total, stepInterval = 850, startDelay = 320, delayFor, revealed, run, onComplete })` returns how many units to show. Uncontrolled, it schedules one `setTimeout` per step (`startDelay` before the first, `stepInterval` after, or `delayFor(index)` per unit) and clears it on cleanup. Passing `revealed` disables the timer so real events drive the log. `onComplete` fires once per completion, guarded by a ref.\n2. `LogRow` is a `motion.li` animating from `UNIT_INITIAL` (`opacity 0, height 0, y 4, blur(6px)`) to `UNIT_ANIMATE` (`opacity 1, height auto, y 0, blur(0px)`). Height runs 0.38s and the rest 0.42s, all on `SOFT_EASE` [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1], so the row has made space just before the text sharpens.\n3. Growing from height 0 behind `overflow-hidden` would cut a hard line through the text, so `useRevealMask` applies a `mask-image` gradient whose solid part ends `--bui-reveal-fade` pixels short of the bottom. Motion animates that custom property from 22px to 0 over 0.44s; on `onAnimationComplete` the mask is removed so nothing is composited at rest.\n4. `RowConnector` draws the row's piece of the guide: an SVG branch (down the trunk, a 6px quadratic corner at y = 14, out 12px) animating `pathLength` 0 → 1, and, unless the row is last, a 1px trunk below it scaling from its top edge.\n5. The pieces run in sequence like one pen: the tail (trunk) of the row above draws for 0.12s, then the new row's branch draws for 0.14s after a 0.12s delay (no delay for the first row). Both are `linear` so the stroke keeps one pace through each junction.\n6. `WorkingRow` uses the same blur-in to show `AgentThinking variant=\"stars\"` with a label, off the guide. `GuideBridge` fills the gap between a parent row's glyph and a nested list's trunk. `useLogMotion` reads `prefers-reduced-motion` once for the whole log.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SOFT_EASE = [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1]`\n  Steeply front-loaded, so text has settled by about 0.2s of its 0.42s blur-in.\n- `UNIT_TRANSITION = height 0.38s; opacity, filter, y 0.42s; --bui-reveal-fade 0.44s`\n  The container settles first, then the words arrive, which reads as smooth rather than as a jump.\n- `--bui-reveal-fade = 22px → 0px`\n  Longer than a 20px text line, so a growing row is always faintest exactly where it is being clipped.\n- `branch geometry = BRANCH_Y 14, BRANCH_RADIUS 6, BRANCH_WIDTH 12`\n  The elbow sits at half of the 20px line plus the 4px top padding, fixed from the top, so a wrapped label still branches at its first line.\n- `guide timing = tail 0.12s, branch 0.14s after 0.12s, linear`\n  Lengths over one pen speed of about 160px/s, so the handoff between pieces is invisible.\n- `ticker defaults = startDelay 320ms, stepInterval 850ms`\n  A short wait before the first row, then a steady pace that `delayFor` can override per unit.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useRevealTicker(options: { total: number; run?: boolean; stepInterval?: number; startDelay?: number; revealed?: number; delayFor?: (index: number) => number; onComplete?: () => void }): number\nfunction LogRow(props: { first: boolean; last: boolean; reduce: boolean; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction WorkingRow(props: { label: string; reduce: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ShimmerText(props: { children: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction GuideBridge(props: { height: number; offset?: number; reduce: boolean }): JSX.Element\nfunction RowConnector(props: { first: boolean; last: boolean; reduce: boolean }): JSX.Element\nfunction useLogMotion(): boolean\nfunction useRevealMask(reduce: boolean): { style?: CSSProperties; onAnimationComplete: () => void }\n```\n\n- `total: number`\n  useRevealTicker: how many units the log has.\n- `stepInterval / startDelay: number` — default `850 / 320`\n  useRevealTicker: wait between units and before the first, in ms.\n- `delayFor: (index: number) => number`\n  useRevealTicker: per-unit wait; overrides both defaults.\n- `revealed: number`\n  useRevealTicker: controlled count; disables the internal timer.\n- `run: boolean` — default `true`\n  useRevealTicker: pause and resume the timer.\n- `onComplete: () => void`\n  useRevealTicker: fires once when the last unit lands.\n- `first / last: boolean`\n  LogRow: position in the list; controls the branch delay and whether a trunk is drawn.\n- `reduce: boolean`\n  LogRow, WorkingRow, GuideBridge: skip the entrance and draw animations (from useLogMotion).\n- `label: string`\n  WorkingRow: text next to the stars indicator.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Each unit blurs in as it lands: 6px of blur and a 4px lift resolving over\n * 0.42s, the same language the AI Chat thread uses for a streaming line. The\n * height runs a touch shorter on the soft curve so the row has finished making\n * space slightly before the text finishes sharpening — the container settles\n * first, then the words arrive, which is what reads as smooth rather than as a\n * jump.\n *\n * Growing a row from height 0 means `overflow-hidden` cuts a hard line through\n * the text while it emerges. `--bui-reveal-fade` softens that edge: the row is\n * masked with a gradient whose solid portion ends that many pixels short of the\n * bottom, and the reveal animates it to 0, at which point the mask is a no-op\n * and nothing pops. The fade is longer than a text line, so a row is always\n * faintest exactly where it is being clipped.\n */\nconst REVEAL_FADE_VAR = \"--bui-reveal-fade\";\n\n// The transparent stop sits just past the box: with both stops at exactly 100%\n// the gradient is degenerate and browsers soften the final pixel row, which\n// shows up as breaks in the guide where one row meets the next.\nconst REVEAL_GRADIENT = `linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 calc(100% - var(${REVEAL_FADE_VAR}, 0px)), transparent calc(100% + 1px))`;\n\nconst REVEAL_MASK: CSSProperties = {\n  WebkitMaskImage: REVEAL_GRADIENT,\n  maskImage: REVEAL_GRADIENT,\n};\n\nexport const UNIT_INITIAL = {\n  opacity: 0,\n  height: 0,\n  y: 4,\n  filter: \"blur(6px)\",\n  [REVEAL_FADE_VAR]: \"22px\",\n};\n\nexport const UNIT_ANIMATE = {\n  opacity: 1,\n  height: \"auto\",\n  y: 0,\n  filter: \"blur(0px)\",\n  [REVEAL_FADE_VAR]: \"0px\",\n};\n\nexport const UNIT_TRANSITION = {\n  height: { duration: 0.38, ease: SOFT_EASE },\n  opacity: { duration: 0.42, ease: SOFT_EASE },\n  filter: { duration: 0.42, ease: SOFT_EASE },\n  y: { duration: 0.42, ease: SOFT_EASE },\n  [REVEAL_FADE_VAR]: { duration: 0.44, ease: SOFT_EASE },\n};\n\n/**\n * The soft edge only earns its keep while a unit is growing. Once the reveal\n * finishes, drop the mask entirely: no compositing layer at rest, and the guide\n * meets pixel-exactly at every row boundary.\n */\nexport function useRevealMask(reduce: boolean) {\n  const [revealing, setRevealing] = useState(!reduce);\n  return {\n    style: revealing ? REVEAL_MASK : undefined,\n    onAnimationComplete: () => setRevealing(false),\n  };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRevealTicker` clears its pending `setTimeout` whenever the count, options or component change, and on unmount. Motion stops its animations when rows unmount. No global listeners are added.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `reduce` true, every `initial` is `false`: rows mount at full height and opacity with no blur, the mask is never applied, and the branch and trunk appear fully drawn. The ticker still paces rows, since that is content arriving rather than decoration. `ShimmerText` drops its sweep through the theme's reduced-motion rule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Give each row content a 4px top padding and a 20px line (the demo uses `py-1` with body text). The branch elbow is fixed at 14px and will not meet the label otherwise.\n- Pass `last` correctly: only the final visible row omits its trunk. When a new row arrives the previous one gains its trunk, which is what draws the tail first.\n- Keep `delayFor` and `onComplete` stable or cheap. `delayFor` is an effect dependency, so an inline function restarts the pending timer on every render.\n- Change the parent's `key` to replay a log. Toggling `run` only pauses and resumes from the current count.\n- Do not render a log with motion on the hydration pass if the server cannot know the reduced-motion preference. `initial` differs between the two, so start revealing after mount.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Real agent steps do not take equal time. Drive the log with `revealed` from real events as soon as you have them, or use `delayFor` to dwell on the slow steps.\n- `ShimmerText` uses the theme's `.agent-progress-loading-text`: a 3.4s linear gradient sweep through `background-clip: text`, replaced by plain secondary text under reduced motion.\n- `agent-log` imports `AgentThinking` for the working row, so installing it brings that item in.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA log of five steps with the default ticker shows its first row about 320ms after mount, then one row roughly every 850ms (or per `delayFor`). Each row opens its height and sharpens from a 6px blur while lifting 4px, with no hard line cutting the text as it grows. The guide draws as one continuous stroke: the previous row's trunk extends down, then the new row's curved branch draws out to its label. The last row has no trunk hanging below its corner. `onComplete` fires exactly once. With reduced motion, rows appear instantly with the guide already drawn.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-progress": "# Build: Agent Progress\n\nA compact block that runs an ordered list of steps. Each step runs for stepDuration with its own ring filling, then checks off with a strike-through while the next one lights up; the header ring tracks the whole run and counts the steps left. The block can be minimized without losing its place, and after the last check it holds for completionDelay before calling onFinished.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA minimizable step list for multi-step agent work: an overall ring, a timed ring on the running step, check-offs and a completion hand-off.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. useStepClock stamps performance.now() when a step becomes active and samples the elapsed fraction in a requestAnimationFrame loop; at 1 it calls advance(step + 1), which changes the effect key and starts the next clock.\n2. The fraction is stored with the step it belongs to, so a new step reads 0 on its first render without a synchronous reset in the effect.\n3. Rings are a 16-unit SVG circle with stroke-dasharray equal to its circumference; dashoffset = C·(1 − fraction). The header ring uses (done + fraction) / total.\n4. Rows enter with a 60ms stagger (opacity, 6px rise, 2px blur) through motion; finished steps swap their ring for a check that springs in.\n5. Minimizing collapses the list with a grid-template-rows 0fr transition and makes it inert; the clock keeps running, so reopening shows the current step.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `stepDuration = 2400ms`\n  Long enough to read a step label and watch its ring fill, short enough that five steps finish in about 12s.\n- `completionDelay = 1000ms`\n  Lets the last check and \"All steps done\" register before the caller swaps in the reply.\n- `STAGGER_S = 0.06`\n  Rows arrive as a quick cascade; slower reads as lag on a five-step list.\n- `REDUCED_TICKS = 4`\n  Under reduced motion the rings step in quarters instead of moving every frame.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\n<AgentProgress steps={[\"Analyze the request\", \"Generate the component\"]} stepDuration={2400} completionDelay={1000} onFinished={() => reply()} />\n```\n\n- `steps: readonly string[]`\n  Ordered step labels.\n- `step / defaultStep / onStepChange: number / number / (step: number) => void` — default `0`\n  Index of the running step, controlled or uncontrolled. steps.length means done.\n- `stepDuration: number` — default `2400`\n  Milliseconds each step runs.\n- `completionDelay: number` — default `1000`\n  Milliseconds to hold the done state before onFinished.\n- `onFinished: () => void`\n  Fires once after the final step and the completion delay.\n- `minimized / defaultMinimized / onMinimizedChange: boolean / boolean / (minimized: boolean) => void` — default `false`\n  Collapses the list to the header.\n- `remainingLabel: (remaining: number) => string` — default `\"{n} steps left\"`\n  Header copy while running.\n- `doneLabel: string` — default `\"All steps done\"`\n  Header copy once finished.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the block.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** done / running / pending for row i while step `active` runs. */\nexport function stepState(index: number, active: number): AgentStepState {\n  if (index < active) return \"done\";\n  return index === active ? \"running\" : \"pending\";\n}\n\n/**\n * Drives one step: measures elapsed time from the moment `step` became active\n * and reports the fraction. A frame loop paints the rings smoothly; under\n * reduced motion a 4-tick interval moves them in quarters. When the fraction\n * reaches 1 the step is handed to `advance`, which re-keys this effect.\n */\nfunction useStepClock(\n  step: number,\n  total: number,\n  duration: number,\n  reduced: boolean,\n  advance: (next: number) => void,\n) {\n  const [tick, setTick] = useState({ step: -1, fraction: 0 });\n  const advanceRef = useRef(advance);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    advanceRef.current = advance;\n  });\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (step < 0 || step >= total) return;\n    const started = performance.now();\n    const span = Math.max(1, duration);\n    let raf = 0;\n    let interval: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | undefined;\n    const sample = () => {\n      const fraction = Math.min(1, (performance.now() - started) / span);\n      setTick({ step, fraction });\n      if (fraction >= 1) advanceRef.current(step + 1);\n      return fraction >= 1;\n    };\n    if (reduced) {\n      interval = setInterval(() => {\n        if (sample() && interval) clearInterval(interval);\n      }, span / REDUCED_TICKS);\n    } else {\n      const loop = () => {\n        if (!sample()) raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);\n      };\n      raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop);\n    }\n    return () => {\n      cancelAnimationFrame(raf);\n      if (interval) clearInterval(interval);\n    };\n  }, [step, total, duration, reduced]);\n\n  return tick.step === step ? tick.fraction : 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The clock effect cancels its requestAnimationFrame or interval whenever the step, duration or motion preference changes and on unmount; the completion timeout is cleared the same way.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Rows appear without the stagger, checks appear without the spring, the list opens and closes instantly, the label shimmer stops, and the rings advance in four discrete quarters per step instead of every frame. Steps still advance on time.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Restarting at the same step index does not restart the clock; remount with a new key to replay the stagger and the timer from zero.\n- In controlled mode the clock only advances through onStepChange; if the parent ignores it, the running ring stops full.\n- onFinished is read through a ref, so an inline callback does not restart the completion timer.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass step + onStepChange to skip ahead or sync with real agent events; steps.length means every step is done.\n- The running label uses the theme agent-progress-loading-text shimmer, which already falls back to plain text under reduced motion.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe header reads \"5 steps left\" then counts down as each step checks off; Skip step jumps to the next row; minimize hides the list and the count keeps moving; after the last step the header reads \"All steps done\" and onFinished fires about a second later; Restart replays from the first step.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agent-thinking": "# Build: Agent Thinking\n\nThe status line an agent shows while it works on a reply. Four indicators share one layout: a 3×3 dot grid with a diagonal wavefront (`wave`), the same grid with a bright head orbiting it (`spin`), five sparkles twinkling in a stagger (`stars`) and a comet lapping a figure-eight (`infinity`). Each sits next to a label with a travelling text shimmer and an optional elapsed-seconds counter, inside a `role=\"status\"` row whose colour comes from one of four tones.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe \"thinking\" line above a chat composer: dot wave, dot spin, stars or infinity, with a shimmering label and an elapsed timer.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The dot variants are driven from JavaScript. Each of the nine cells gets a scalar in [0, 1) along the direction of travel: for `wave` it is `(col + row) / 4` compressed by 3/4, so the front leaves the grid before the phase wraps; for `spin` it is the cell's `atan2` angle around the centre, which is naturally cyclic.\n2. A `setInterval` every 80ms advances the phase by 1/8 (one lap per 640ms) and recomputes opacities as a comet: `behind = (phase - s + 1) % 1`, `lit = max(0, 1 - behind / 0.3) ** 1.5`, mapped onto 0.12–1. A 220ms CSS opacity transition on every cell blends the 80ms steps into a glide.\n3. `stars` is pure CSS: five 4-point sparkle paths placed at fixed percentages and scales, each running the `bui-agent-thinking-star` keyframes (scale 0 → 1 → 0.8 → 0 with fade) over 1.4s, with `animation-delay` staggered by `i × 1.4 × 0.7 / 5`s.\n4. `infinity` draws the figure-eight twice: a 15%-opacity track, and a copy with `pathLength={100}` and `strokeDasharray=\"11 89\"` whose `stroke-dashoffset` animates to -100 every 1.2s, so an 11%-long dash laps the loop at constant speed.\n5. The root sets `color` and `--bui-agent-thinking-tone` to the tone colour. The indicators paint with `currentColor`; the label's `.bui-agent-thinking-label` paints a 300%-wide gradient (tone at 55% → 100% → 55%) through `background-clip: text` and slides it with the theme's `ai-chat-text-shimmer` keyframes over 2.6s.\n6. `ElapsedTimer` records `performance.now()` on mount and re-renders every 100ms with `elapsed.toFixed(1)` seconds in a tabular mono caption.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DOTS_GRID / DOTS_SIZE / DOTS_GAP = 3 / 4px / 2px`\n  Three 4px cells and two 2px gaps make a 16px square indicator beside the label.\n- `DOTS_TICK_MS / DOTS_PHASE_STEP = 80ms / 1/8`\n  Eight steps per lap gives a 640ms cycle: lively without reading as a flicker.\n- `DOTS_FADE_MS = 220ms`\n  Longer than the 80ms tick, so each cell is always mid-transition and the stepped updates read as continuous motion.\n- `DOTS_TRAIL / DOTS_MIN_OPACITY = 0.3 / 0.12`\n  The tail covers 30% of the lap with a power-1.5 falloff; unlit cells keep 12% so the grid shape never disappears.\n- `DOTS_SEED = [0.55, 0.3, 0.15, 0.85, 0.55, 0.3, 1, 0.85, 0.55]`\n  A fixed first frame shared by server and client, and the resting state under reduced motion.\n- `STAR_PERIOD_S / STAR_SIZE / STAR_COUNT = 1.4s / 14px / 5`\n  The box is 21px (1.5 × size); the largest sparkle is 14px and the others are scaled 0.4–0.55 around it.\n- `INFINITY_TRAIL / INFINITY_STROKE / INFINITY_DURATION_S = 11 of 100 / 2.75 / 1.2s`\n  An 11% dash is long enough to read as a comet but short enough to stay on one lobe of the eight at a time.\n- `TONE_COLORS = subtle text-tertiary, default text-secondary, primary text-primary, accent blue-500`\n  Every variant defaults to `default` except `stars`, which defaults to `subtle`.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype AgentThinkingVariant = \"wave\" | \"spin\" | \"stars\" | \"infinity\"\ntype AgentThinkingTone = \"subtle\" | \"default\" | \"primary\" | \"accent\"\nfunction AgentThinking(props: { variant?: AgentThinkingVariant; label?: string; tone?: AgentThinkingTone; shimmer?: boolean; showTimer?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `variant: \"wave\" | \"spin\" | \"stars\" | \"infinity\"` — default `\"wave\"`\n  Which indicator sits before the label.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Thinking\"`\n  Status text, e.g. \"Searching the docs\".\n- `tone: \"subtle\" | \"default\" | \"primary\" | \"accent\"`\n  Colour of the indicator and label. Defaults to `default`, or `subtle` for `stars`.\n- `shimmer: boolean` — default `true`\n  Animated highlight travelling across the label.\n- `showTimer: boolean` — default `true`\n  Elapsed seconds since mount, shown after the label.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root flex row.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * How far along the pattern's travel direction each cell sits, in [0, 1).\n * The wave scalar is compressed below 1 so the phase wrap reads as the front\n * leaving the grid and re-entering; the spin angle is naturally cyclic.\n */\nfunction dotScalar(variant: \"wave\" | \"spin\", col: number, row: number) {\n  const m = DOTS_GRID - 1;\n  if (variant === \"wave\") {\n    return ((col + row) / (2 * m)) * (DOTS_GRID / (DOTS_GRID + 1));\n  }\n  const center = m / 2;\n  return (Math.atan2(row - center, col - center) / (2 * Math.PI) + 1) % 1;\n}\n\nfunction dotOpacities(variant: \"wave\" | \"spin\", phase: number) {\n  return Array.from({ length: DOTS_GRID * DOTS_GRID }, (_, i) => {\n    const s = dotScalar(variant, i % DOTS_GRID, Math.floor(i / DOTS_GRID));\n    // Comet: bright head at the phase front, tail fading behind it.\n    const behind = (phase - s + 1) % 1;\n    const lit = Math.max(0, 1 - behind / DOTS_TRAIL) ** 1.5;\n    return DOTS_MIN_OPACITY + (1 - DOTS_MIN_OPACITY) * lit;\n  });\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The dot interval and the timer interval are each cleared by their effect cleanup on unmount; the dot interval is also restarted when `variant` changes. The stars and the infinity comet are CSS animations and stop with the element. No global listeners are added.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The dot variants check `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` on mount and, if it matches, never start their interval, so the grid rests on `DOTS_SEED`. The theme's reduced-motion block removes the label gradient and shows it in the plain tone, stops the comet (leaving the dash still), and shows the stars at 70% opacity instead of frozen at scale 0. The elapsed timer keeps counting, since it is information rather than decoration.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Only the dot variants check `prefers-reduced-motion` in JavaScript, and only on mount. Changing the OS setting while one is mounted does not stop its interval until it remounts or its `variant` changes.\n- The timer starts when the component mounts. To restart it for a new request, remount with a new `key`; changing `label` or `variant` keeps counting.\n- The root is `role=\"status\"` (a polite live region) and the timer text inside it changes every 100ms. If screen readers in your audience re-announce it, pass `showTimer={false}` or show the time elsewhere.\n- The label span carries `aria-label={label}` without a role, so assistive technology reads its text content in practice. Do not rely on the attribute to change what is announced.\n- The shimmer paints the label with `color: transparent` and a gradient. A parent that sets `background` on text or overrides `color` with `!important` will hide or flatten it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The infinity SVG has about 4px of empty viewBox on each side, so it uses `margin: 0 -4px` to keep the label at the same 10px gap as the other variants.\n- In light mode the stars are softened to 75% of the tone through `color-mix`; in `.dark` they use the full tone.\n- BoardUI's own preview stacks all four variants with timers off, because four clocks in one card is noise.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith `variant=\"wave\"` a bright band sweeps the 3×3 grid from top-left to bottom-right about every 0.64s, with a fading tail and no cell ever fully invisible. `spin` shows a head circling the grid clockwise. `stars` shows five sparkles popping in and out in a stagger; `infinity` shows a short dash lapping a faint figure-eight every 1.2s. The label shimmers left to right in the same tone as the indicator, and the timer counts up in tenths of a second from 0.0s. Remounting resets it. Under reduced motion nothing moves except the timer.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "agents-bar-card": "# Build: Agents Bar Card\n\nThe agents card of an AI profile. A grey card shows \"Agents\", the number of agents that ran this month and a bordered month switcher, then a row of thin purple bars, one per agent. Agents that did not run sit on the baseline as short grey stubs. A readout row above the bars shows an avatar, name and value: the busiest agent by default, the hovered or focused one while you explore.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne purple bar per agent for a month, grey stubs for idle ones; hover or arrow keys highlight a bar and show its avatar, name and value.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The bars are plain flex columns, no chart library. Each column fills the 160px chart height as its hit area, and the bar inside is sized as a percentage of the month's maximum with a 6px floor, so small values stay visible. Idle agents render a fixed 3px stub in `chart-neutral`; busy ones use `chart-agents-bar`, and the active one switches to `chart-agents-bar-active`.\n2. Height changes are a 300ms CSS transition, so paging months makes the bars grow and shrink in place; the highlight colour follows the pointer in 120ms. `motion-reduce:transition-none` turns both off.\n3. The bar row is a single `role=\"slider\"` over the agent index: one tab stop instead of thirty. ArrowLeft and ArrowRight step, Home and End jump, Escape clears; `aria-valuetext` reads \"Nova: 1,250 runs\". Pointer enter on a column sets the same active index, and leaving the row or blurring it clears it, so mouse and keyboard highlight identically.\n4. The readout uses the kit Avatar (24px, tinted by the agent's colour), the name, the value and a rank caption (\"#3 of 30\"); with nothing active it shows the busiest agent labelled \"Most active\". It is `aria-hidden` because the slider already announces the same text.\n5. The month switcher is a bordered pill of two ghost icon Buttons around the month name, disabled at either end; the name is `aria-live` so a screen reader hears the new month. The card works controlled (`month`) or uncontrolled (`defaultMonth`, the last month by default).\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `chart height = 160px (h-40), bars from a 6px floor to 100% of the month max`\n  Tall enough to rank thirty agents by eye; the floor keeps a 1% agent from vanishing into the baseline.\n- `gaps = 3px below sm, 6px from sm, 3px top radius`\n  Thirty bars still fit a 360px phone at about 7px each, and open up into the reference rhythm on wider cards.\n- `idle stub = 3px chart-neutral`\n  Shows the agent exists but did nothing, rather than leaving a gap that reads as missing data.\n- `transition = 300ms ease-out on height, 120ms on colour`\n  Long enough to follow which agents rose or fell between months; the highlight is quicker so it keeps up with a sweeping pointer.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AgentsBarCard(props: AgentsBarCardProps): JSX.Element\ninterface AgentsBarCardProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>, \"children\"> { title?: string; months: AgentMonth[]; month?: string; defaultMonth?: string; onMonthChange?: (id: string) => void; unit?: string; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; startLabel?: string; endLabel?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }\ninterface AgentMonth { id: string; label: string; agents: AgentUsage[] }\ninterface AgentUsage { id: string; name: string; initials?: string; color?: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\"; value: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Agents\"`\n  Caption above the headline and part of the chart label.\n- `months: AgentMonth[]`\n  Months in order, each with its label and per-agent values.\n- `month: string`\n  Controlled month id.\n- `defaultMonth: string` — default `last month`\n  Initial month when uncontrolled.\n- `onMonthChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the switcher pages to another month.\n- `unit: string` — default `\"runs\"`\n  Word after values.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `en-US grouping`\n  Formats values in the readout and label.\n- `startLabel: string`\n  Caption under the first bar.\n- `endLabel: string`\n  Caption under the last bar.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst max = agents.reduce((m, a) => Math.max(m, a.value), 0);\nconst activeCount = agents.filter((a) => a.value > 0).length;\nconst topIndex = agents.reduce((best, a, i) => (a.value > (agents[best]?.value ?? -1) ? i : best), 0);\nconst shownIndex = activeIndex ?? topIndex;\nconst shown = agents[shownIndex];\nconst rank = shown ? agents.filter((a) => a.value > shown.value).length + 1 : 0;\n\nconst goMonth = (step: -1 | 1) => {\n  const next = months[monthIndex + step];\n  if (!next) return;\n  setActiveIndex(null);\n  if (month === undefined) setInnerMonth(next.id);\n  onMonthChange?.(next.id);\n};\n\n// One tab stop for every bar: arrows step, Home/End jump, Escape clears.\nconst onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n  const last = agents.length - 1;\n  if (last < 0) return;\n  let next: number | null | undefined;\n  if (e.key === \"ArrowLeft\") next = activeIndex === null ? last : Math.max(0, activeIndex - 1);\n  else if (e.key === \"ArrowRight\") next = activeIndex === null ? 0 : Math.min(last, activeIndex + 1);\n  else if (e.key === \"Home\") next = 0;\n  else if (e.key === \"End\") next = last;\n  else if (e.key === \"Escape\") next = null;\n  if (next === undefined) return;\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setActiveIndex(next);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, observers or global listeners. Pointer and keyboard handlers live on the chart element and go away with it.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the bars jump to their new heights and colours without the 300ms and 120ms transitions. Nothing else moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Making every bar a button. Thirty tab stops to cross one chart is hostile to keyboard users; one slider with arrow keys is the same information in one stop.\n- Highlighting on hover only. Keyboard users then never see the readout; the active index is shared by pointer and keys.\n- Scaling bars to a fixed maximum across months. A quiet month then looks empty; each month scales to its own busiest agent, and the readout gives the absolute numbers.\n- Dropping idle agents from the row. Bars then shift position between months and the same agent lands in a different place; keep every agent and stub the idle ones.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Order agents by creation date (as the demo does, with \"Oldest\" and \"Newest\" captions) so a bar keeps its place month to month.\n- Use `unit` and `formatValue` for tokens or minutes instead of runs.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Agents\", \"22 agents\", a \"December\" switcher and thirty bars with eight grey stubs; the readout shows Cipher, 1,400 runs, \"Most active\". Hovering a bar turns it the darker purple and the readout shows that agent and its rank. Focusing the chart and pressing Home selects Atlas (620 runs). The previous button goes to November and October and is disabled there. At 360px all thirty bars still fit.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "ai-chat": "# Build: AI Chat\n\nA full AI coding chat for a 1280 × 800 frame. The left card holds the account menu, quick search and chats grouped by repository; the middle pane is the open chat with a glowing composer and a status bar for branch, project, mode and context; the right pane lists uncommitted changes with their code. Sending a message runs a simulated agent with timers only: a step checklist ticks through, a thinking indicator counts up, then the reply streams in and the run leaves a new file in the changes panel.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA coding-agent workspace: chat history sidebar, a thread with a live step checklist and streamed replies, and a changes panel with diffs.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. All state lives in `AiChat`: chats, the open chat, the view, files, terminal lines, mode, branch, project, context use and the single active run. The sidebar, thread and changes panel are controlled components in their own files.\n2. A run is `{ steps, step, phase, full, shown }`. One effect keyed on the run arms one timeout: in the working phase it advances `step` every 1300ms, holds 600ms on the finished list, then switches to streaming; in streaming it appends three characters every 24ms. Cleanup clears the timeout, so Stop, deleting the chat or unmounting never leaves a callback.\n3. The run lives in the page, not the thread, so it keeps going while another chat is open. The sidebar shows a working dot on that chat and the composer of other chats will not send until it ends.\n4. `AgentProgress` gets the parent's `step` as a controlled prop with no `onStepChange`, so its own clock only animates the bar between steps while the page decides when a step is done.\n5. Mode changes the run: Agent runs three steps and writes a file, Ask skips the checklist and only thinks, Plan returns a plan without touching files. Turning off \"Run lint and a production build\" in Customize drops the lint step and its terminal lines.\n6. The changes panel is real: selecting a file shows its tokenised code, Revert removes one file, Discard all asks first, and Commit empties the list and reports the commit. The Browser tab keeps a back and forward history over three local routes.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `STEP_MS = 1300ms`\n  Long enough to read each checklist line as it completes; the reference's five steps take about seven seconds.\n- `stream tick = 3 characters every 24ms`\n  About 125 characters a second reads as typing without making a two-sentence reply wait long.\n- `CONTEXT_LIMIT = 200,000 tokens, opening at 114,000`\n  Opens at 57% like the reference ring and climbs with each finished run.\n- `panel width = 400px, 560px when widened`\n  At 1280px the narrow panel leaves about 600px for the chat column next to the 248px sidebar.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AiChat(props: AiChatProps): JSX.Element\ninterface AiChatProps { folders?: AiChatFolder[]; threads?: AiChatThread[]; files?: AiChatFile[]; defaultThreadId?: string; autoStart?: boolean; className?: string }\n```\n\n- `folders: AiChatFolder[]` — default `AI_CHAT_FOLDERS`\n  Repositories shown as folders in the sidebar.\n- `threads: AiChatThread[]` — default `AI_CHAT_THREADS`\n  Seed chats; each names its folder and holds its turns.\n- `files: AiChatFile[]` — default `AI_CHAT_FILES`\n  Uncommitted files listed in the changes panel at load.\n- `defaultThreadId: string` — default `\"coding\"`\n  Chat open on first render.\n- `autoStart: boolean` — default `true`\n  If the opening chat ends with a user message, run the agent on it at load.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root, which is h-full w-full.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The agent timeline: one timer at a time walks the steps, holds on the\n// finished checklist, then streams the reply a few characters per tick.\n// Every state change re-arms the effect, and cleanup clears the old timer,\n// so stopping, deleting or unmounting never leaves a callback behind.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!run) return;\n  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;\n  if (run.phase === \"working\") {\n    const total = run.steps.length;\n    const done = run.step >= total;\n    timer = setTimeout(\n      () => {\n        if (done) {\n          setRun({ ...run, phase: \"streaming\" });\n          return;\n        }\n        log(`✓ ${run.steps[run.step] ?? \"\"}`);\n        setRun({ ...run, step: run.step + 1 });\n      },\n      total === 0 ? THINK_MS : done ? DONE_HOLD_MS : STEP_MS,\n    );\n  } else {\n    timer = setTimeout(\n      () => {\n        const shown = reduced ? run.full : run.full.slice(0, run.shown.length + CHUNK);\n        if (shown.length >= run.full.length) finish(run);\n        else setRun({ ...run, shown });\n      },\n      reduced ? 0 : TICK_MS,\n    );\n  }\n  return () => clearTimeout(timer);\n  // finish/log only call state setters, so the run and motion setting are the real inputs.\n  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n}, [run, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The run timeout is cleared on every run change and on unmount. The dictation timer, the share copied-state timer and the browser loading timer each clear on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under reduced motion the reply appears at once instead of streaming, the composer glow stops pulsing, the thinking label drops its shimmer, and hover and panel-width transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none. The step checklist still advances so the run stays readable.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Running the timeline in the thread component. Switching chats would unmount it and lose the run; the page owns it and passes it down only to the chat it belongs to.\n- Letting `AgentProgress` advance itself while the page also advances. Two clocks drift apart; pass a controlled `step` and leave `onStepChange` unset.\n- Sending on Enter during IME composition. The composer checks `isComposing` so confirming a Japanese or Chinese candidate does not send a half-typed message.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The \"coding scenario\" chat opens mid-run with the reference's five steps, so the first reply streams in about eight seconds after load.\n- Support and Upgrade are local stand-ins: Support copies an address and Upgrade switches the plan name on the sidebar card.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280 × 800 a sidebar, a chat and a changes panel fill the frame. The coding scenario chat ticks through five steps, then a reply streams in and a fourth file appears under Uncommitted changes. Typing a message and pressing Enter adds a bubble and starts a new run; Stop ends it. Selecting another chat, Automations or Customize switches the middle pane. The sidebar collapses to a rail, the panel hides, widens and switches to Browser.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "ai-image-generation": "# Build: AI Image Generation\n\nA full image-generation workspace for a 1280 × 800 frame. The left card lists repositories and their threads, the grey middle pane holds the selected thread and a pill composer, and the right pane is the gallery. Sending a prompt adds a user bubble and a generating tile that counts up with the halftone loader, reveals its picture, then turns into a finished card with copy, like, dislike, regenerate and download actions while the picture is filed at the top of the gallery. Automations and Customize are real views inside the block, and Customize settings change how the next image is made.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThree-pane image studio: repo sidebar, prompt thread with a pill composer and live generating tiles, and a masonry gallery with styles.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. All state lives in `AiImageGeneration`: repositories, a `Record<sessionId, ThreadEntry[]>` of threads, the gallery list, the running jobs, the composer draft and styles, and the Customize defaults. The sidebar, thread, composer and panels are controlled components in their own files.\n2. Running generations are a `Record<entryId, Job>` with a `progress` and a per-tick `step`. One `setInterval` at 100ms exists only while at least one job does. It reads the jobs from a ref, advances each, removes those that reach 100, prepends their pictures to the gallery when Save to gallery is on, and raises an \"Image ready\" toast. Each tile gets its progress and seconds left as props, so `ImageGeneratingLoader` runs controlled.\n3. A job's step is `100 × 100ms / duration`. The duration is `generationMs`, halved for the Fable 5 Mini model and halved again in Draft mode, so the model picker and the mode picker change real behaviour.\n4. Pictures come from `itemFromPrompt`: an FNV-1a hash of the entry id and prompt picks one of twelve inline-SVG artworks and one of six palettes, the title is the prompt with its leading verb stripped, the ratio comes from the Customize aspect setting and the style line is the chosen styles or the model name. The same prompt and id always draw the same picture.\n5. The composer is a form: Enter or the round send button submits, and the button is disabled while the field is empty. The + menu and the Styles tab toggle the same style list, which shows as removable chips and is cleared after sending. Dictate is a pressed toggle that, after 1.8s, writes a phrase into the field.\n6. Thread actions are real: Share copies a label to the clipboard, the more menu duplicates the finished entries into a new thread, clears the thread or deletes it (hidden for the last thread), and Stop generating removes a running tile. Clearing or deleting also cancels that thread's jobs.\n7. The sidebar collapse button hides the sidebar and the thread header shows a button to bring it back; the gallery's panel button does the same on the right. Below 1024px both panes become drawers over a backdrop, opened from the thread header and closed by the backdrop, Escape or their own close buttons.\n8. Quick Search opens the kit Command palette (also on ⌘L) with actions and every thread; Settings opens the kit Settings modal.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `frame = 12px padding and gaps; sidebar 240px; gallery 372px, 500px when widened`\n  At 1280px this leaves about 620px for the thread, enough for a 640px message column to sit centered with room for a 200px loader card.\n- `tick = 100ms`\n  Ten updates a second keeps the percentage and the halftone reveal smooth without re-rendering the page every frame.\n- `generationMs = 7000ms default`\n  Long enough to watch the loader stages and the reveal, short enough that a demo visitor sees the result land in the gallery.\n- `context = 114k of 200k tokens, +3200 plus 4 per character per prompt`\n  Opens at 57% like the reference ring and climbs visibly with each send.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AiImageGeneration(props: AiImageGenerationProps): JSX.Element\ninterface AiImageGenerationProps { generationMs?: number; defaultSessionId?: string; className?: string }\n```\n\n- `generationMs: number` — default `7000`\n  Milliseconds one generation takes with the default model and mode.\n- `defaultSessionId: string` — default `\"image-generation\"`\n  Thread selected on first render.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root, which is h-full w-full.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// One interval drives every running generation. It only exists while a job\n// does, reads the latest jobs from a ref, and files finished pictures in\n// the gallery (unless Customize turned that off) before announcing them.\nconst running = Object.keys(jobs).length > 0;\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!running) return;\n  const id = window.setInterval(() => {\n    const next: Record<string, Job> = {};\n    const done: Job[] = [];\n    for (const [key, job] of Object.entries(jobsRef.current)) {\n      const progress = Math.min(100, job.progress + job.step);\n      if (progress >= 100) done.push(job);\n      else next[key] = { ...job, progress };\n    }\n    jobsRef.current = next;\n    setJobs(next);\n    if (done.length === 0) return;\n    if (saveRef.current) {\n      setGallery((prev) => [...done.map((job) => job.item), ...prev.filter((item) => !done.some((job) => job.item.id === item.id))]);\n    }\n    for (const job of done) {\n      toast(\"Image ready\", {\n        id: `ready-${job.item.id}`,\n        description: saveRef.current ? `“${job.item.title}” was added to the gallery.` : `“${job.item.title}” is in the thread.`,\n        status: \"success\",\n      });\n    }\n  }, TICK_MS);\n  return () => window.clearInterval(id);\n}, [running]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The generation interval is cleared when the last job ends and on unmount. The copied-state, dictation and focus effects clear their timers, and the Escape listener for the drawers is removed when they close.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Hover and press transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none and the dictation pulse is motion-reduce:animate-none. The generating loader drops its ripple under reduced motion while progress keeps counting, the gallery stops zooming on hover, and the kit palette, modal and toasts follow their own reduced-motion rules.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Doing side effects inside a `setJobs` updater. React may call updaters twice in development, which would double the gallery insert and the toast. The interval computes the next jobs from a ref, then sets state and fires effects once.\n- Keying a running tile by its array index. Regenerate, Stop and Clear change the list while the timer runs; every entry has its own id and jobs are keyed by it.\n- Unmounting the Automations view when switching away. Its rows are local state, so it stays mounted and is hidden with `display: none` instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The opening thread starts with one generation in flight, so the first image lands in the gallery about seven seconds after load.\n- Downloads, Support and Upgrade are local stand-ins that report through toasts; wire them to a real endpoint in an app.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280 × 800 a sidebar, a grey thread pane and a gallery fill the frame. The \"image generation\" thread shows a user prompt and a loader that counts to 100% and then becomes a finished card while the picture appears first in the gallery with an \"Image ready\" toast. Typing a prompt and pressing Enter adds a bubble and a new loader. Picking a style chip, a model, Draft mode or a new aspect changes the next image. Selecting another thread, Automations or Customize switches the middle pane. The sidebar and gallery hide and come back. Below 1024px both become drawers.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "ai-profile": "# Build: AI Profile\n\nA full AI usage profile for a 1280 × 800 frame. A workspace sidebar sits on the left; the main pane holds a cover header with the owner's name, handle, badge, inline stats, Share and Edit, and four tabs. Overview shows a contributions headline with four selectable lifetime tiles and a Weekly / Monthly / Yearly activity heatmap. Agents ranks the month's active agents, Images is a masonry gallery and Activity is a timeline. Under the header an Agents bar chart steps through months and a Tokens area chart switches between 7, 30 and 90 days. Every sidebar entry opens a real view.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAI usage profile page: cover header with tabs, stat tiles, an activity heatmap, and agent and token charts beside a workspace sidebar.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. All page state lives in `AiProfile`: the view, the profile tab, the sidebar and drawer flags, the open overlays, the editable profile, the agent month, the token period, the selected tile, the unread count and the team. The sidebar, panels, workspace pages and edit form are controlled components in their own files.\n2. The agent month is shared. `AgentsBarCard` steps it through `onMonthChange`, and the same month drives the Agents tab count, the Agents panel ranking and the header's agents stat, so they always describe one month.\n3. The heatmap is a CSS grid with `grid-flow-col` and inline row and column templates, so one component draws the 7 × 38 weekly grid, the 4 × 12 monthly grid and the 3 × 12 yearly grid from column-major values. Each cell's level is `ceil(value / max × 4)`, capped at 4, with 0 for idle cells. One `pointerover` handler on the grid reads the cell's `data-cell` index and the readout under the grid names that cell and its runs.\n4. All series come from a seeded mulberry32 generator and hard-coded day labels, so the server and client render the same numbers and the demo never reads the clock.\n5. Edit opens a kit Modal with a form. Name and handle are validated (the handle must be 2 to 24 letters, numbers, dots or underscores, with a leading @ stripped), and Save writes the name, handle, bio and cover tone back to the header and the sidebar account button.\n6. The sidebar collapse button hides the sidebar and a Show sidebar button brings it back. Below 1024px the sidebar becomes a drawer over a backdrop, opened from the menu button and closed by the backdrop, Escape, a nav choice or the collapse button.\n7. Quick Search opens the kit Command palette (also on ⌘L) with every page and the profile actions; Settings opens the kit Settings modal. Share, Support, team switches and gallery downloads report through toasts.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `frame = 12px padding and gaps; sidebar 248px; content column up to 720px`\n  At 1280px the profile column sits centred in the main pane with room for the two charts side by side.\n- `heat levels = 5 steps: empty, then violet at 20%, 40%, 70% and 100%`\n  Quartiles of the busiest cell keep the grid readable whatever the range's scale.\n- `agents shown = top 6, then Show all`\n  Six rows fit the tab panel without pushing the charts below the fold.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AiProfile(props: AiProfileProps): JSX.Element\ninterface AiProfileProps { defaultView?: ProfileView; defaultMonth?: string; className?: string }\n```\n\n- `defaultView: ProfileView` — default `\"profile\"`\n  View selected on first render: \"home\", \"board\", \"marketing\", \"calendar\", \"finance\", \"medical\", \"chat\", \"images\" or \"profile\".\n- `defaultMonth: string` — default `\"dec\"`\n  Month the Agents chart and tab open on: \"oct\", \"nov\" or \"dec\".\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root, which is h-full w-full.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The month is shared: the Agents chart steps it and the Agents tab and its\n// count read it, so both always describe the same month.\nconst month = AGENT_MONTHS.find((m) => m.id === monthId) ?? AGENT_MONTHS[AGENT_MONTHS.length - 1]!;\nconst activeAgents = month.agents.filter((a) => a.value > 0).length;\nconst span = MONTH_SPAN[month.id] ?? [\"\", \"\"];\nconst tile = PROFILE_TILES.find((t) => t.id === tileId);\n\nconst go = (next: ProfileView, nextTab?: ProfileTabId) => {\n  setView(next);\n  if (nextTab) setTab(nextTab);\n  setDrawer(false);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The only listener the page adds is the Escape handler for the drawer, which exists while the drawer is open and is removed when it closes or the page unmounts. The kit overlays and toasts clean up their own timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Hover and selection transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none. The token chart turns its Recharts animation off under reduced motion, the agent bars stop easing their height, and the kit tabs, modal, palette and toasts follow their own reduced-motion rules.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Giving the Agents chart and the Agents tab their own month state. They drift apart as soon as the chart steps; the month lives in the page and both read it.\n- Seeding the series from `Math.random()` or the clock. The server and client then render different grids and hydration fails; the data uses a fixed-seed generator and fixed labels.\n- Attaching a listener to every heatmap cell. The weekly grid alone has 266 cells; one delegated `pointerover` on the grid reads a `data-cell` index instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The workspace pages (Home, Project board, Marketing and so on) are light stand-ins with seeded rows; Home's Mark all read clears the sidebar badge.\n- Share copies a profile link to the clipboard when the browser allows it. Support and downloads are local stand-ins that report through toasts.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280 × 800 a sidebar and a profile column fill the frame. The cover shows Mara Ellison, @maraellison, a PRO badge, Share and Edit. Overview shows a contributions headline, four tiles that toggle a detail line, and a heatmap that switches between Weekly, Monthly and Yearly and names the hovered cell. The Agents, Images and Activity tabs each show their panel. Stepping the Agents chart month changes the Agents tab count. The Tokens chart switches between 7D, 30D and 90D. Editing the profile updates the header. Every sidebar item opens its view, the sidebar hides and comes back, and below 1024px it becomes a drawer.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "analytic-disc-picking": "# Build: Analytic Disc Picking\n\nA slow ring of camera-facing discs that answers \"what is under the pointer\" without a raycaster. Because every disc is parallel to the image plane, the pick reduces to intersecting the pointer ray with one plane per disc and comparing the hit against the radius — four arithmetic operations, no traversal, no allocation, and exact rather than approximate. It is also the only correct answer here: the billboard happens in the vertex shader, so `Raycaster` intersects a square that is not on screen.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nHit-test camera-facing discs by intersecting the pointer ray with their plane. Exact, allocation-free, and correct for shader billboards.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The vertex shader billboards in view space: `vec4 mv = modelViewMatrix * vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0); mv.xy += position.xy * uScale;` takes only the translation out of the model-view matrix and adds the quad offset afterwards. The mesh's rotation and scale never reach a vertex, so the disc is always exactly parallel to the image plane and its view-space radius is exactly `uRadius * uScale`.\n2. Because the disc is parallel to the image plane, its plane normal is the camera's view direction for every disc in the scene. `camera.getWorldDirection(viewDir)` is called once per frame and `plane.setFromNormalAndCoplanarPoint(viewDir, disc.position)` then differs only in its constant.\n3. `raycaster.setFromCamera(ndc, camera)` builds the pointer ray and nothing else is used from the Raycaster — `intersectObjects` is never called. Constructing the ray is the cheap half of raycasting; the traversal, the per-object matrix inverse, and the per-triangle intersection are the half being skipped.\n4. `ray.intersectPlane(plane, hit)` returns `Vector3 | null`, and the null case is a free depth rejection: `Ray.distanceToPlane` returns null for `t < 0`, so anything behind the camera drops out before any distance work.\n5. The winner is the nearest hit along the ray whose distance from the disc centre is within the radius, both compared squared. Depth is tested FIRST — `if (along >= bestAlong) continue;` — so a disc that cannot beat the incumbent never runs its own radius test.\n6. The pick radius is `uRadius * uScale`, the same two numbers the vertex shader multiplies. Hover eases `uScale` from 1 to 1.14, and the pick reads the eased value in the same frame, so a disc is never clickable over area it has not grown into and never stops being clickable over area it still occupies.\n7. Every scratch object — ray, plane, hit vector, view direction, NDC vector — is allocated once into the stage object and reused. A pick loop that allocates a `Vector3` per disc per frame produces 780 garbage vectors a second at 60 Hz on a ring of thirteen.\n8. Rendering is gated on a dirty flag. The flag is set by the spin, by pointer movement, by a hover easing that is still in flight, and by resize. Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the spin never sets it, so the renderer idles at zero draw calls until the pointer moves.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `FOV = 42`\n  Camera vertical field of view in degrees. Wide enough that the near discs are visibly larger than the far ones — which is what makes an analytic test worth having — and narrow enough that the ring does not distort into an oval at preview aspect ratios.\n- `CAM_Z / ringRadius = 5.6 / 2.15`\n  A ratio of about 2.6. Closer and the front disc overruns the frame at preview width; further and the perspective flattens until a cheap screen-space approximation would have been good enough, which defeats the demonstration.\n- `discRadius = 0.46`\n  World units, and simultaneously the pick radius. A single source for both is the entire point: two constants that mean \"how big is this disc\" is how a hit region drifts away from the pixels it is supposed to cover.\n- `WAVE = 0.34`\n  Vertical amplitude of the sin(2a) offset. Enough that the ring reads as a ribbon rather than a flat carousel; past ~0.6 the discs at the crossings overlap and the nearest-along-the-ray tie-break starts doing visible work the user has no way to predict.\n- `HOVER_SMOOTHING = 0.0006`\n  Fraction of the hover gap remaining after one second, as `damp` defines it — about 12% of the gap per frame at 60 Hz, settling in roughly 0.2 s. A per-frame constant instead would converge twice as fast on a 120 Hz display and the growth would feel like a different component.\n- `HOVER_GROWTH = 0.14`\n  A hovered disc grows 14%. Large enough to read at the back of the ring where a disc is a third of its front size, small enough that the grown disc does not swallow its neighbours and make the pick ambiguous.\n- `FEATHER = 0.035`\n  Rim gradient width in units of the radius — roughly 1.5 px at preview scale. This is why the renderer is built with `antialias: false`: MSAA would pay for an entire extra sample buffer to solve a problem one smoothstep already solved.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AnalyticDiscPicking(props: { count?: number; period?: number; discRadius?: number; ringRadius?: number; color?: string; accent?: string; seed?: number | string; compare?: boolean; onPick?: (index: number) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `13`\n  Discs around the ring.\n- `period: number` — default `30`\n  Seconds for one revolution.\n- `discRadius: number` — default `0.46`\n  Disc radius in world units. Also the pick radius — the two cannot drift apart.\n- `ringRadius: number` — default `2.15`\n  Ring radius in world units.\n- `color: string` — default `'#7d848e'`\n  Base plate colour. Any value `THREE.Color` accepts.\n- `accent: string` — default `'#e8e4dc'`\n  Colour of the picked disc.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'analytic-disc-picking'`\n  Seeds the per-disc plate pattern. Same seed, same ring every reload.\n- `compare: boolean` — default `false`\n  Also run a real `Raycaster` each frame and report what it thinks was hit.\n- `onPick: (index: number) => void`\n  Fired when a disc is clicked, with its index.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nuseRaf((dt) => {\n  const s = stage.current;\n  if (!s) return;\n\n  // The ring phase is integrated from dt, not read from a wall clock, so\n  // freezing it for reduced motion is a matter of not adding anything.\n  if (!reduced) {\n    spin.current = (spin.current + (TAU / period) * dt) % TAU;\n    dirty.current = true;\n  }\n\n  // -- place the discs -----------------------------------------------------\n  for (const disc of s.discs) {\n    const a = disc.phase + spin.current;\n    disc.mesh.position.set(\n      Math.sin(a) * ringRadius,\n      Math.sin(a * 2) * WAVE,\n      Math.cos(a) * ringRadius,\n    );\n    // `cos(a)` is +1 at the front of the ring and -1 at the back. Remapped\n    // to 0..1 it is the depth fade, and it costs nothing extra here.\n    disc.uniforms.uDepth.value = Math.cos(a) * 0.5 + 0.5;\n  }\n  // No `updateMatrixWorld` here on purpose. The discs are direct children of\n  // the scene at identity, so `mesh.position` IS the world position, and the\n  // camera's matrix was made current in `resize`. The only consumer of the\n  // full matrix graph is `render`, which updates it itself.\n\n  // -- the analytic pick ---------------------------------------------------\n  let best = -1;\n  const p = pointer.current;\n  if (p) {\n    s.ndc.set(p.x, p.y);\n    // The Raycaster is used for its RAY and nothing else. Building an\n    // NDC-to-world ray by hand means duplicating `unproject`; the expensive\n    // half of raycasting is `intersectObjects`, which is never called.\n    s.raycaster.setFromCamera(s.ndc, s.camera);\n    const ray = s.raycaster.ray;\n    // Every disc is parallel to the image plane, so they all share one\n    // normal — the camera's view direction, already unit length. Only the\n    // plane's constant differs from disc to disc.\n    s.camera.getWorldDirection(s.viewDir);\n\n    let bestAlong = Infinity;\n    for (let i = 0; i < s.discs.length; i += 1) {\n      const disc = s.discs[i];\n      if (!disc) continue;\n\n      s.plane.setFromNormalAndCoplanarPoint(s.viewDir, disc.mesh.position);\n      // `intersectPlane` returns null when the plane is behind the ray\n      // origin, which rejects anything behind the camera for free. Note that\n      // `Ray.distanceToPlane` returns null too but is TYPED `number` in\n      // @types/three — the honest signature is on this method.\n      if (ray.intersectPlane(s.plane, s.hit) === null) continue;\n\n      // Nearest along the ray wins. Checked first so a disc that cannot\n      // beat the incumbent skips its own radius test.\n      const along = s.hit.distanceToSquared(ray.origin);\n      if (along >= bestAlong) continue;\n\n      // The drawn radius, hover growth included. `uScale` was eased last\n      // frame and is applied by the vertex shader this frame, so the region\n      // tested is the region rasterised.\n      const r = disc.uniforms.uRadius.value * disc.uniforms.uScale.value;\n      if (s.hit.distanceToSquared(disc.mesh.position) > r * r) continue;\n\n      bestAlong = along;\n      best = i;\n    }\n  }\n\n  // -- ease the hover, which the next frame's pick will read ---------------\n  for (let i = 0; i < s.discs.length; i += 1) {\n    const disc = s.discs[i];\n    if (!disc) continue;\n    const target = i === best ? 1 : 0;\n    const next = damp(disc.hover, target, HOVER_SMOOTHING, dt);\n    if (Math.abs(next - disc.hover) > 0.0005) dirty.current = true;\n    disc.hover = next;\n    disc.uniforms.uHover.value = next;\n    disc.uniforms.uScale.value = 1 + HOVER_GROWTH * next;\n    disc.uniforms.uPicked.value = i === pickedRef.current ? 1 : 0;\n  }\n\n  if (best !== hovered.current) {\n    hovered.current = best;\n    setHover(best);\n    dirty.current = true;\n  }\n\n  // -- what a real raycaster would have said -------------------------------\n  if (compare) {\n    let g = -1;\n    if (p) {\n      // The raycaster reads `matrixWorld`, which `render` would not refresh\n      // until the end of this frame. The analytic path needed no such call —\n      // it reads `position` directly — so this traversal is a cost the\n      // comparison introduces, not one it inherits.\n      s.scene.updateMatrixWorld();\n      // Reused array, cleared by hand: `intersectObjects` pushes into the\n      // target and sorts it, but never empties it.\n      s.intersects.length = 0;\n      s.raycaster.intersectObjects(s.meshes, false, s.intersects);\n      const first = s.intersects[0];\n      if (first) g = s.meshes.indexOf(first.object as THREE.Mesh);\n    }\n    if (g !== ghost) setGhost(g);\n  }\n\n  if (dirty.current) {\n    dirty.current = false;\n    s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n  }\n}, ready && !unsupported);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The effect removes every disc from the scene and disposes its `ShaderMaterial`, disposes the one shared `PlaneGeometry`, then calls `disposeRenderer` — which stops the animation loop, disposes the renderer, calls `forceContextLoss()`, and zeroes the drawing buffer — and finally removes the canvas it appended. The canvas is created by the renderer inside the effect and never handed to React, so a StrictMode double-invoke rebuilds a fresh context rather than reusing a killed one. The frame loop unsubscribes with `useRaf`; the shared rAF parks once nothing is subscribed. The `ResizeObserver` unobserves with its hook.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The ring holds still: the phase is integrated from delta time, so freezing it is a matter of adding nothing. The dirty flag is then only set by the pointer, which means the renderer draws zero frames until the pointer moves. Picking, hover growth and selection are unaffected — the technique is about where things are, not about them moving.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reaching for `THREE.Raycaster` against a shader billboard. The vertex shader runs on the GPU; the `BufferGeometry` in main memory is an unrotated square lying in world XY. The raycast intersects that square and reports hits on a shape nobody can see — turn on `compare` and the two readouts disagree the moment the camera is not staring down the world Z axis.\n- Using `Ray.distanceToPlane` and testing its result for null. It does return null at runtime for a plane behind the ray origin, but `@types/three` declares it as `number`, so the null check is a type error and removing the null check is a crash. `intersectPlane` has the honest `Vector3 | null` signature; use it.\n- Approximating the disc as a screen-space circle whose NDC radius is `halfDiagonal / (tanHalfFov * camDist)`. That is only correct for a sphere. For a flat disc it over-reports near the edges of frame, where perspective stretches the footprint, so the hover boundary is visibly not the disc boundary. The plane intersection is exact for the same cost.\n- Deriving the pick radius from a second constant. Hover growth then applies to the pixels but not the hit region, and the disc becomes clickable on a ring of its own body it has already grown past.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` on. The culler tests the geometry bounding sphere at the mesh transform, but the vertices are moved by the shader, so a disc vanishes the instant the untransformed square leaves the frustum.\n- Allocating in the pick loop. `new THREE.Vector3()` per disc per frame is invisible in a profile and unmistakable in a GC trace; reuse a scratch object on the stage.\n- Enabling depth test on the discs. They are square quads with the corners discarded, so the depth buffer records the quad, not the disc, and neighbouring rims punch holes in each other. Painter's-algorithm sorting is correct here because the billboard leaves the object position — the key three sorts on — untouched.\n- Handing three's renderer a React-rendered `<canvas ref>`. StrictMode double-invokes the effect, the first cleanup calls `forceContextLoss()`, and the second run is given the same permanently dead element. Let the renderer make its own canvas inside the effect and remove it in cleanup.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The same reduction works for any primitive with a closed-form ray test: a sphere is `ray.intersectSphere`, an axis-aligned card is `ray.intersectBox`, a ground plane is one `intersectPlane`. Raycasting against triangles is the fallback for arbitrary meshes, not the default for known shapes.\n- A square quad plus `discard` beats a `CircleGeometry` fan on both counts — two triangles instead of thirty-two, and a rim anti-aliased by a smoothstep instead of a polygon approximated by segments.\n- The `compare` prop exists to be switched on once. Leave it on in production and you pay for the traversal you set out to avoid, plus an `intersectObjects` call whose result you throw away.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover a disc at the front of the ring and it brightens and grows, and the readout says `plane hit NN`. Follow one disc as it travels to the back: it stays pickable over exactly its drawn silhouette, smaller but never offset. Set `compare` and hover a disc anywhere off the centre of frame — the `raycast` readout says something different, usually nothing, because it is testing an unrotated square in world XY. Click and the disc under the pointer takes the accent colour. With reduced motion the ring is static and every one of those behaviours still works.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "announcement": "# Build: Announcement\n\nAn onboarding or \"what's new\" card sized for the 260px sidebar footer: a 20px icon, a title, an optional description and an optional full-width secondary button. Dismissal is managed inside the card. The close button plays a 250ms blur, fade and scale-down exit, then the card unmounts and `onClose` fires. An opt-in `introDelay` plays the reverse as an entrance once the surrounding UI has settled.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA compact sidebar card with an icon, title, note and full-width call to action that blurs and shrinks away when dismissed.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The card is a `motion.div` inside `AnimatePresence`, rendered while a local `dismissed` flag is false. The close button sets the flag, `AnimatePresence` keeps the node mounted for its `exit` animation, and `onExitComplete={onClose}` calls the consumer only after the card has actually left.\n2. The exit animates `opacity: 0`, `scale: 0.85` and `filter: \"blur(6px)\"` over 0.25s with `easeInOut`. Blur together with scale reads as the card receding rather than being cut out.\n3. `introDelay` turns on the entrance. With it set, `initial` is `{ opacity: 0, y: 15, scale: 0.85, filter: \"blur(6px)\" }` and `animate` returns everything to rest over 0.25s `easeOut`, starting after `introDelay` seconds. Without it, `initial={false}` and `animate={undefined}`, so the card renders in its final state on the first frame with no animation work.\n4. The layout follows the Figma frame: a `flex-col gap-3 p-3` card with `rounded-xl`, a 1px `border-border-button-default` and `bg-background-primary-default`, and no shadow. Inside it a `gap-1` column holds the 20px icon (`text-foreground-icon-secondary`) and a `gap-0.5` text block with the title in `text-body-medium text-text-primary` and the description in `text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary`. That totals 150px with every part present.\n5. The close control is the kit's `CloseButton` at `size=\"xs\"` (a 20px round button), absolutely placed at `end-3 top-3` so it mirrors in RTL. It takes `aria-label={closeLabel}`, which defaults to \"Dismiss\".\n6. The action is a `Button` with `variant=\"secondary\"`, `size=\"small\"` and `className=\"w-full\"`, rendered only when `actionLabel` is set. It calls `onAction` directly and does not dismiss the card. Leaving out `description`, `actionLabel` or `dismissible` removes that part and the card shrinks to fit.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `exit = scale 0.85, blur 6px, opacity 0 over 0.25s easeInOut`\n  The card leaves visibly without dragging. At 0.85 the shrink reads as dismissal rather than a glitch, and the 6px blur hides the hard edge as it fades so the card never looks clipped.\n- `entrance = y 15px, scale 0.85, blur 6px → rest over 0.25s easeOut, after introDelay`\n  The mirror of the exit plus a small rise from below, so the card arrives from where the eye is not looking yet. `easeOut` lands it softly. The delay lets the sidebar settle first so the card draws attention on its own.\n- `card metrics = p-3, gap-3, rounded-xl, 20px icon, 150px tall`\n  Taken 1:1 from the Figma \"Event card\": 12 + 20 + 4 + (20 + 2 + 36) + 12 + 32 + 12 = 150. It fills its container (236px inside the 260px sidebar) instead of fixing a width.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Announcement(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"title\" | \"onDrag\" | \"onDragStart\" | \"onDragEnd\" | \"onAnimationStart\" | \"onAnimationEnd\"> & { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; actionLabel?: ReactNode; onAction?: () => void; dismissible?: boolean; onClose?: () => void; closeLabel?: string; introDelay?: number; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Required headline, set in Body 1 Medium (14/20).\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Supporting line in Body 2 Medium (13/18). Omit for a title-only card.\n- `icon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>` — default `RiShieldStarFill`\n  Leading icon component, drawn at 20px.\n- `actionLabel: ReactNode`\n  Label for the full-width secondary button. No button is rendered without it.\n- `onAction: () => void`\n  Click handler for the action button. Does not dismiss the card.\n- `dismissible: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows the 20px close button in the top-end corner.\n- `onClose: () => void`\n  Called once, after the exit animation finishes.\n- `closeLabel: string` — default `\"Dismiss\"`\n  Accessible name of the close button.\n- `introDelay: number`\n  Seconds before the entrance plays. Leave it out to skip the entrance.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Announcement({\n  title,\n  description,\n  icon: Icon = RiShieldStarFill,\n  actionLabel,\n  onAction,\n  dismissible = false,\n  onClose,\n  closeLabel = \"Dismiss\",\n  introDelay,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: AnnouncementProps) {\n  const [dismissed, setDismissed] = useState(false);\n  const hasIntro = introDelay !== undefined;\n\n  return (\n    <AnimatePresence onExitComplete={onClose}>\n      {!dismissed && (\n        <motion.div\n          ref={ref}\n          initial={hasIntro ? { opacity: 0, y: 15, scale: 0.85, filter: \"blur(6px)\" } : false}\n          animate={\n            hasIntro\n              ? {\n                  opacity: 1,\n                  y: 0,\n                  scale: 1,\n                  filter: \"blur(0px)\",\n                  transition: { duration: 0.25, ease: \"easeOut\", delay: introDelay },\n                }\n              : undefined\n          }\n          exit={{\n            opacity: 0,\n            scale: 0.85,\n            filter: \"blur(6px)\",\n            transition: { duration: 0.25, ease: \"easeInOut\" },\n          }}\n          className={cx(styles.card, className)}\n          {...props}\n        >\n          <div className={styles.content}>\n            <Icon className={styles.icon} aria-hidden />\n\n            {dismissible ? (\n              <CloseButton\n                size=\"xs\"\n                aria-label={closeLabel}\n                onClick={() => setDismissed(true)}\n                className={styles.close}\n              />\n            ) : null}\n\n            <div className={styles.text}>\n              <p className={styles.title}>{title}</p>\n              {description ? <p className={styles.description}>{description}</p> : null}\n            </div>\n          </div>\n\n          {actionLabel ? (\n            <Button\n              variant=\"secondary\"\n              size=\"small\"\n              onClick={onAction}\n              className=\"w-full\"\n            >\n              {actionLabel}\n            </Button>\n          ) : null}\n        </motion.div>\n      )}\n    </AnimatePresence>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release by hand. Motion stops any running entrance or exit animation when the card unmounts, and the component adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** There is no reduced-motion branch in the component, and Motion's default `reducedMotion` is `\"never\"`, so the 250ms exit (and the entrance, when enabled) plays as written. Under `<MotionConfig reducedMotion=\"user\">` Motion removes the scale and `y` transforms and keeps the short opacity and blur fades.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Unmounting the card yourself on close. The card owns its `dismissed` state. If the parent removes it immediately, `AnimatePresence` goes with it and the exit never plays. Listen to `onClose`, which fires after the exit finishes.\n- Passing an icon element (`icon={<RiSparklingFill />}`). The prop takes the component and renders it at `size-5` with `aria-hidden`.\n- Expecting the action button to dismiss the card. `onAction` only runs your handler. Hide the card from `onAction` yourself, for example by changing its `key` or rendering it conditionally after your own exit.\n- Setting `introDelay={0}` and expecting no animation. Any number, including 0, turns the entrance on. Leave the prop out entirely for a card that simply appears.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The card is uncontrolled. To show it again after a dismissal, remount it with a new `key`.\n- The component does not read the reduced-motion preference itself. In an app wrapped in `<MotionConfig reducedMotion=\"user\">`, Motion drops the scale and `y` transforms and keeps the opacity and blur fades.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nInside a 260px sidebar the card fills the column, shows the icon, title, description and a full-width \"See what's new\" button, and with a two-line description it is 150px tall. Clicking the close button blurs and shrinks the card away in about a quarter of a second, and `onClose` fires once the card is gone. Clicking the action runs `onAction` and leaves the card in place. A remount with `introDelay={0.1}` rises in from 15px below while un-blurring.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "antialiased-disc-fragment": "# Build: Anti-Aliased Point Discs\n\nA point sprite is a square; this is the pair of shader tricks that makes it a dot. The fragment shader carves a disc with one smoothstep across the outer few percent of the sprite and discards everything below a visibility threshold, and the vertex shader clamps the point size to the hardware floor and then divides the alpha by the area that clamp invented. The field is laid out as a phyllotaxis spiral with a size ramp so both ends of the problem — sub-pixel dots and twenty-pixel dots — are on screen at once.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA phyllotaxis field of dots that stay the same weight at every pixel ratio, with no MSAA and no texture.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Dots are placed by golden-angle phyllotaxis: `t = sqrt((i + 0.5) / count)` for the normalised radius and `i * 137.507°` for the angle, so spacing is even rather than crowded at the centre. A seeded jitter of ±0.06 rad is added to break the moiré a pure spiral shows at small radii.\n2. The vertex shader rotates differentially — `angle + uElapsed * uSpin * (0.6 + t)` — so the rim turns 1.6x faster than the core and the arms shear instead of the field spinning rigidly.\n3. Point size ramps as `mix(uMinRadius, uMaxRadius, t * t)`, quadratic so most of the field stays small and the large discs are confined to the rim where the anti-aliasing is easiest to inspect.\n4. The size is then clamped: `clamped = max(2.0, radius * 2.0 * uPixelRatio)` against `unclamped = max(1.0, …)`, and `vAlphaScale = unclamped² / clamped²` is passed to the fragment shader. A dot the driver forced to be twice as wide comes out four times fainter, so total emitted light is unchanged.\n5. The fragment shader computes `d = length(gl_PointCoord - 0.5)` and `disc = 1.0 - smoothstep(uEdgeInner, 0.5, d)` where `uEdgeInner = 0.5 - softness`, so the falloff always ends exactly at the sprite edge and the disc stays inscribed in its square.\n6. Anything whose final alpha lands below 0.003 is discarded rather than blended, which removes a framebuffer read-modify-write per pixel across the sparse outer field.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `GOLDEN_ANGLE = Math.PI * (3 - Math.sqrt(5)) ≈ 2.39996 rad`\n  The only angle whose successive multiples never repeat a rational fraction of a turn. Any nearby value produces visible spokes within a few hundred points.\n- `softness = 0.07`\n  Anti-aliased band as a fraction of the sprite radius, giving `smoothstep(0.43, 0.5, d)`. Below ~0.05 a 2px dot aliases back into a square; above ~0.15 every dot reads as blurred rather than small.\n- `MIN_POINT_SIZE = 2.0`\n  Hardware floor in device pixels. Below roughly 1px most GPUs rasterise nothing, so the smallest dots would be absent on some machines and present on others.\n- `ALPHA_EPSILON = 0.003`\n  Discard threshold, just under 1/255. Anything fainter cannot be shown by an 8-bit framebuffer, so blending it is pure fill cost.\n- `radius ramp exponent = t * t`\n  Quadratic rather than linear. Linear puts mid-size discs everywhere and the field turns into a solid disc; quadratic keeps the interesting large sprites at the rim.\n- `count = 4000`\n  One buffer, one draw call, and about 4000 sprites of up to 20px — roughly the point where fill rate rather than vertex count starts to dominate on integrated graphics.\n- `differential spin factor = (0.6 + 1.0 * t)`\n  Rim-to-core angular speed ratio of 1.6. At 1.0 the field spins rigidly and reads as a rotating image; past ~2.5 the arms wind into a solid ring within a minute.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AntialiasedDiscFragment(props: { count?: number; minRadius?: number; maxRadius?: number; fieldRadius?: number; spin?: number; softness?: number; opacity?: number; colorInner?: string; colorOuter?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `4000`\n  Number of discs. One draw call regardless.\n- `minRadius: number` — default `0.5`\n  Disc radius in CSS pixels at the centre — deliberately sub-pixel at DPR 1.\n- `maxRadius: number` — default `10`\n  Disc radius in CSS pixels at the rim.\n- `fieldRadius: number` — default `0.92`\n  Field diameter as a fraction of the container short side.\n- `spin: number` — default `0.08`\n  Base angular speed in radians per second; the rim runs 1.6x faster.\n- `softness: number` — default `0.07`\n  Anti-aliased band width as a fraction of the sprite radius.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied before the area compensation.\n- `colorInner: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Tint at the centre of the field.\n- `colorOuter: string` — default `'#6f7680'`\n  Tint at the rim.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'antialiased-disc-fragment'`\n  Seeds the angular jitter so the layout is stable across reloads and matches on the server.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AntialiasedDiscFragment({\n  count = 4000,\n  minRadius = 0.5,\n  maxRadius = 10,\n  fieldRadius = 0.92,\n  spin = 0.08,\n  softness = 0.07,\n  opacity = 1,\n  colorInner = '#ffffff',\n  colorOuter = '#6f7680',\n  seed = 'antialiased-disc-fragment',\n  className,\n}: AntialiasedDiscFragmentProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0.1, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const positions = new Float32Array(count * 3); // all zeros; the shader places them\n    const disc = new Float32Array(count * 2);\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      // sqrt spacing, not linear: linear spacing crowds the centre, which is\n      // exactly where the sub-pixel dots are and where crowding is least\n      // forgiving.\n      const t = Math.sqrt((i + 0.5) / count);\n      // A little seeded angular jitter breaks the moiré that a pure golden\n      // spiral shows at small radii on a low-DPR screen.\n      disc[i * 2] = t;\n      disc[i * 2 + 1] = i * GOLDEN_ANGLE + (rng() - 0.5) * 0.12;\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aDisc', new THREE.BufferAttribute(disc, 2));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uExtent: { value: 1 },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uSpin: { value: spin },\n      uFieldRadius: { value: fieldRadius },\n      uMinRadius: { value: minRadius },\n      uMaxRadius: { value: maxRadius },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uColorInner: { value: new THREE.Color(colorInner) },\n      uColorOuter: { value: new THREE.Color(colorOuter) },\n      // The band always ENDS at 0.5 — the sprite's own edge. Softening inward\n      // keeps the disc inscribed in the sprite; softening outward would clip\n      // the falloff square at the corners.\n      uEdgeInner: { value: 0.5 - softness },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: ANTIALIASED_DISC_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: ANTIALIASED_DISC_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      // Additive would blow the overlapping core to white. Normal blending\n      // with a discard keeps the density readable where the dots overlap.\n      blending: THREE.NormalBlending,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.left = -w / 2;\n      camera.right = w / 2;\n      camera.top = h / 2;\n      camera.bottom = -h / 2;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n      uniforms.uExtent.value = Math.min(w, h);\n      // Re-read rather than assume: moving a window between a retina and a\n      // non-retina display changes devicePixelRatio without changing the\n      // canvas size, and a stale value breaks the whole compensation.\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    count,\n    minRadius,\n    maxRadius,\n    fieldRadius,\n    spin,\n    softness,\n    opacity,\n    colorInner,\n    colorOuter,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed), ready && !reduced && !unsupported);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref, disposes the geometry and material via `disposeObject` plus an explicit `material.dispose()`, calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed drawing buffer, then removes the canvas. `useRaf` releases the frame subscription itself.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No rAF subscription is opened. A single frame is drawn at `uElapsed = 6`, which is far enough into the rotation that the arms are already sheared, so the composition is the interesting one rather than the unrotated starting pose. The field is fully visible; only the spin stops.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Softening the disc outward from 0.5 instead of inward to it. The sprite has no pixels past 0.5, so the outer half of the falloff is clipped by the sprite boundary and the dot shows four square corners.\n- Clamping `gl_PointSize` and leaving the alpha alone. The clamp does nothing at DPR 3 and doubles the covered area at DPR 1, so the identical component looks twice as heavy on a non-retina monitor — a bug that is invisible on the machine most people develop on.\n- Caching the pixel ratio at construction. Dragging the window from a retina to a non-retina display changes `devicePixelRatio` without changing the canvas size, so the compensation silently goes stale; it is re-read on every resize.\n- Reaching for `antialias: true` when the dots look jagged. MSAA allocates a multisample buffer the size of the canvas and does not help a point sprite whose edge is defined by alpha — the smoothstep is the fix.\n- Blending fully transparent fragments instead of discarding them. At 4000 sprites with a 20px radius the outer field is mostly empty pixels, and each one still costs a read-modify-write of the framebuffer.\n- Linear radial spacing instead of `sqrt`. It crowds the centre, which is precisely where the sub-pixel dots live, and the core turns into an undifferentiated smear.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The camera is orthographic and 1:1 with CSS pixels, so `uMinRadius` and `uMaxRadius` are literal pixel radii rather than scene units.\n- The same two tricks apply to any `THREE.Points` material. The spiral is only a way to show the whole size range at once.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA spiral of dots that grows from barely-visible at the centre to clearly round discs at the rim, with soft edges and no square corners at any size. Zoom the browser between 100% and 200% and the field keeps the same apparent weight instead of getting denser or lighter. Nothing shimmers along the arms as they turn. With reduced motion the spiral renders in full and holds still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "app-shell": "# Build: App Shell\n\nThe page standard every BoardUI template follows: a full-height frame with the dashboard sidebar on large screens (an overlay drawer below `lg`), and a main column of at most 1300px holding a breadcrumb trail, the page heading with its actions, and the page content sitting directly on the page background. The starter's navigation is generated from one base path read off the URL, so the sidebar only links to pages the app has. The default header actions are a notification bell with an unread badge that opens the notification center in a popover, plus Filters and Create ticket buttons.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPage frame for the chat starter: sidebar, phone drawer, breadcrumb and heading over a 1300px column, with a notification bell.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `AppShell` renders a `relative flex h-dvh w-full gap-4 overflow-hidden bg-background-full p-3` row: `DashboardSidebar` (hidden below `lg`) and a `<main>` that is the only scroller (`flex-1 overflow-y-auto overflow-x-hidden`, `sm:pt-3`) centring a `max-w-[1300px] flex-col gap-2.5` column. `columnClassName` overrides the column width.\n2. Navigation comes from `starterNav(base)`: Chat, Dashboard, Components and Blocks, Sign in, Sign up, each `href` prefixed with `base`. `useStarterBase()` returns a pinned base from `StarterBaseContext` when a host provides one, else matches `pathname` against `/templates/chat-starter` (with an optional language prefix) and falls back to \"\" in a deployed copy. `useStarterSelected()` maps the rest of the path to \"dashboard\", \"components\" or \"chat\".\n3. Below `lg`, an `IconButton` beside the heading toggles `navOpen`; when true a `fixed inset-0 z-50 lg:hidden` layer renders a full-bleed `<button aria-label=\"Close navigation\">` backdrop (`bg-black/40`) and the sidebar in `mobile` mode with `onClose`. The toggle swaps its glyph between `RiMenuLine` and `RiCloseLine`.\n4. The header is a `Breadcrumb` (team avatar, member avatar, then the current page with `icon`) over a wrapping row: the `h1` (`text-title-2-medium`, `heading` defaulting to `title`) on the left and the actions on the right. `actions` undefined renders the defaults; `null` renders none.\n5. `NotificationBell` owns `isOpen` and two refs: an `IconButton` trigger with `aria-expanded` / `aria-haspopup=\"dialog\"` and a React Aria `Popover` (`placement=\"bottom end\"`, `offset={8}`, `isNonModal`, `w-[440px] max-w-[calc(100vw-24px)]`) holding a `Dialog` with `NotificationCenter`. Outside presses close it via `useDismissOnOutsidePress`. The unread count (items with `unread`) shows in a 16px red badge at `top-0.5 start-[18px]` that loses its 1.5px ring on hover and press.\n6. Both the shell and the bell run their JSX through `useTemplateCopy()`'s `localize`, so localized templates translate the fixed copy without the components taking strings as props.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `content column = max-w-[1300px]`\n  The width every BoardUI template page uses, so cards and tables line up across pages; the catalogue overrides it (e.g. `max-w-[964px]`) through `columnClassName`.\n- `sidebar breakpoint = lg (1024px)`\n  Below it the sidebar would squeeze the content column, so it becomes an overlay drawer opened from the header.\n- `notification popover = 440px wide, max-w-[calc(100vw-24px)], bottom end, offset 8`\n  Wide enough for a notification row with inline actions; end alignment keeps it under the bell at the right of the header.\n- `STARTER_SITE_PREFIX = \"/templates/chat-starter\"`\n  Where the starter lives on the hosted site; reading it off the URL lets the same files run hosted or deployed at the root untouched.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AppShell(props: { title: string; heading?: string; icon?: IconComponent; actions?: ReactNode; children: ReactNode; className?: string; columnClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\nconst StarterBaseContext: Context<string | null>\nconst STARTER_SITE_PREFIX = \"/templates/chat-starter\"\nfunction useStarterBase(): string\nfunction starterNav(base: string): DashboardNavItem[]\nfunction useStarterSelected(): string\nfunction NotificationBell(props?: { notifications?: NotificationCenterItem[] }): JSX.Element\nconst STARTER_NOTIFICATIONS: NotificationCenterItem[]\n```\n\n- `title: string`\n  The page's name, shown as the current breadcrumb item.\n- `heading: string` — default `title`\n  The `h1` over the content.\n- `icon: IconComponent` — default `RiDashboardLine`\n  Icon on the current breadcrumb item.\n- `actions: ReactNode` — default `bell + Filters + Create ticket`\n  Header actions on the right; `null` for none.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the outer frame, e.g. `h-full` instead of `h-dvh` inside a host.\n- `columnClassName: string`\n  Overrides the 1300px content column.\n- `NotificationBell.notifications: NotificationCenterItem[]` — default `STARTER_NOTIFICATIONS`\n  The inbox shown in the popover; unread items drive the badge.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * The page standard every BoardUI template follows, free or Pro: content sits\n * directly on the page background inside a 1300px column, so cards and tables\n * carry their own surfaces; above it a breadcrumb trail (team, member, page)\n * and the page heading. Pages pass what differs and get the rest.\n */\nexport function AppShell({\n  title,\n  heading = title,\n  icon: Icon = RiDashboardLine,\n  actions,\n  children,\n  className,\n  columnClassName,\n}: {\n  /** The page's name, shown as the current breadcrumb item. */\n  title: string;\n  /** The heading over the content. Defaults to the title. */\n  heading?: string;\n  /** Icon on the current breadcrumb item. */\n  icon?: IconComponent;\n  /** Header actions on the right; the Pro dashboard's set by default, `null` for none. */\n  actions?: ReactNode;\n  children: ReactNode;\n  className?: string;\n  /** Overrides the 1300px content column, e.g. `max-w-[964px]` for the catalogue's grids. */\n  columnClassName?: string;\n}) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const [navOpen, setNavOpen] = useState(false);\n  const items = starterNav(useStarterBase());\n  const selected = useStarterSelected();\n\n  return localize((\n    <div\n      className={cx(\n        \"relative flex h-dvh w-full gap-4 overflow-hidden bg-background-full p-3\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <DashboardSidebar items={items} selected={selected} className=\"hidden lg:flex\" />\n\n      {/* Below lg the sidebar rides in as an overlay drawer. */}\n      {navOpen && (\n        <div className=\"fixed inset-0 z-50 flex lg:hidden\">\n          <button\n            type=\"button\"\n            aria-label=\"Close navigation\"\n            onClick={() => setNavOpen(false)}\n            className=\"absolute inset-0 cursor-pointer bg-black/40\"\n          />\n          <div className=\"relative flex h-full p-3\">\n            <DashboardSidebar\n              mobile\n              items={items}\n              selected={selected}\n              onClose={() => setNavOpen(false)}\n              className=\"flex\"\n            />\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      )}\n\n      <main className=\"relative flex min-h-0 min-w-0 flex-1 justify-center overflow-x-hidden overflow-y-auto bg-background-full sm:pt-3\">\n        <div className={cx(\"flex w-full max-w-[1300px] flex-col gap-2.5\", columnClassName)}>\n          <header className=\"flex w-full flex-col gap-2\">\n            <Breadcrumb>\n              <BreadcrumbItem href=\"#\">\n                <Avatar size=\"xs\" color=\"blue\" initials=\"A\" />\n                Acme team\n              </BreadcrumbItem>\n              <BreadcrumbItem href=\"#\">\n                <Avatar size=\"xs\" color=\"neutral\" initials=\"J\" />\n                Jordan\n              </BreadcrumbItem>\n              <BreadcrumbItem current icon={Icon}>\n                {title}\n              </BreadcrumbItem>\n            </Breadcrumb>\n            <div className=\"flex w-full flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-2\">\n              <div className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5\">\n                <IconButton\n                  icon={navOpen ? RiCloseLine : RiMenuLine}\n                  size=\"medium\"\n                  aria-label=\"Open navigation\"\n                  onClick={() => setNavOpen((open) => !open)}\n                  className=\"lg:hidden\"\n                />\n                <h1 className=\"px-1 text-title-2-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary\">\n                  {heading}\n                </h1>\n              </div>\n              <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-end gap-2.5\">\n                {actions !== undefined ? actions : (\n                  <>\n                    <NotificationBell />\n                    <Button variant=\"secondary\" size=\"medium\" leadingIcon={RiFilter3Fill}>\n                      Filters\n                    </Button>\n                    <Button variant=\"primary\" size=\"medium\" leadingIcon={RiAddFill}>\n                      Create ticket\n                    </Button>\n                  </>\n                )}\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </header>\n          <div className=\"flex w-full flex-col gap-4 pb-4\">{children}</div>\n        </div>\n      </main>\n    </div>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The bell's capture-phase outside-press listener exists only while its popover is open and is removed on close or unmount. The shell holds only `navOpen` state; nothing else to release.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The shell itself has no motion: the phone drawer appears and disappears without a transition. Motion inside it (the notification center, buttons) follows those components' own reduced-motion rules.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not configure the base path in two places. It is read off the URL (or pinned with `StarterBaseContext` by a host whose path has no prefix), so the sidebar never links to a page the app lacks.\n- Do not let the whole frame scroll. The outer row is `h-dvh overflow-hidden` and only `<main>` scrolls, so the sidebar stays put.\n- Do not make the bell popover modal to get outside dismissal; it is `isNonModal` and closed by `useDismissOnOutsidePress`, so opening it does not lock page scroll.\n- Do not pass `actions={undefined}` expecting no actions. `undefined` means \"use the defaults\"; pass `null` to render none.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `STARTER_NOTIFICATIONS` is the sample inbox: five items over Today and Yesterday, three unread, matching the sidebar badge and the bell. `STARTER_UNREAD_COUNT` is derived from it.\n- The shell's sample breadcrumb (Acme team, Jordan) is fixed copy; real apps replace it with their own trail.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px wide the sidebar sits on the left with \"Dashboard\" selected, and the main column shows the breadcrumb, the heading and the bell, Filters and Create ticket buttons. The bell shows a red 3; pressing it opens the notification list below it, and pressing outside closes it. Only the main column scrolls. Below 1024px the sidebar is gone and a menu button beside the heading opens it as an overlay drawer; pressing the dimmed backdrop closes it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "appointments-list-card": "# Build: Appointments List Card\n\nA patient's upcoming appointments in the medical profile's grey card: a label, a big count of visits that are not cancelled, then one white tile per visit with a month/day/weekday chip, the visit type, its time window and mode, the doctor's initials and a status chip. Reschedule opens a menu of open slots and moves the visit, Cancel strikes it through and swaps the actions for an Undo that restores the status it had.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nUpcoming visits as inner tiles: date chip, time window, mode, doctor initials and status, with reschedule slots, cancel and undo.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The list is controlled (`appointments`) or uncontrolled (`defaultAppointments`); every change goes through `commit`, which updates internal state only when uncontrolled and always calls `onAppointmentsChange` with the whole list. Tiles render sorted by the `date`T`time` string, so a moved visit slides to its new place.\n2. Dates are parsed with `parseDate` / `parseDateTime` from @internationalized/date and printed with `DateFormatter` in the `locale` (en-US by default) on `toDate(\"UTC\")` with `timeZone: \"UTC\"`, so the chip and the \"9:30 AM – 10:00 AM\" window never shift with the viewer's timezone. The end time adds `durationMinutes` (default 30) with `.add({ minutes })`.\n3. Reschedule is a kit `Dropdown` whose open state is held per card (`openMenu` = the visit id), because `DropdownItem` does not close the menu itself. Its items come from `getSlots`, which defaults to `defaultSlots`: the next three weekdays after the visit (`getDayOfWeek` skips Saturday and Sunday) at the same time, 14:30 and 10:00. Picking one patches `date`, `time` and `status: \"rescheduled\"`, closes the menu and calls `onReschedule`.\n4. Cancel stores the current status in `previousStatus` keyed by id, then sets `status: \"cancelled\"`; the tile dims to 60% with a line-through and shows Undo, which restores the stored status (or \"confirmed\") and calls `onRestore`.\n5. The count and the date chip are keyed by their value (`active` count, `date`T`time`), so the kit `animate-number-fade` keyframe replays when a visit is cancelled, restored or moved. A visually hidden `aria-live=\"polite\"` line announces each change.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `default duration = 30 minutes`\n  Most outpatient visits; the end of the time window is start + durationMinutes.\n- `default slots = next 3 weekdays at same time, 14:30, 10:00`\n  Gives a morning and an afternoon option without a scheduling backend.\n- `cancelled opacity = 60%`\n  Keeps the cancelled visit readable for the Undo while clearly out of the schedule.\n- `date chip width = 48px`\n  Fits a three-letter month, a two-digit day and a weekday at caption size.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function AppointmentsListCard({ appointments, defaultAppointments = [], onAppointmentsChange, onReschedule, onCancel, onRestore, getSlots = defaultSlots, title = \"Upcoming appointments\", countLabel = \"scheduled\", locale = \"en-US\", className }: AppointmentsListCardProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `appointments: Appointment[]`\n  Controlled list of visits.\n- `defaultAppointments: Appointment[]` — default `[]`\n  Initial list when uncontrolled.\n- `onAppointmentsChange: (appointments: Appointment[]) => void`\n  Called with the whole list after a reschedule, cancel or undo.\n- `onReschedule: (appointment: Appointment, slot: AppointmentSlot) => void`\n  Called when a slot is picked, with the visit before the move.\n- `onCancel: (appointment: Appointment) => void`\n  Called when a visit is cancelled.\n- `onRestore: (appointment: Appointment) => void`\n  Called when Undo restores a cancelled visit.\n- `getSlots: (appointment: Appointment) => AppointmentSlot[]` — default `defaultSlots`\n  Open slots offered in the Reschedule menu.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Upcoming appointments\"`\n  Card label and accessible name.\n- `countLabel: string` — default `\"scheduled\"`\n  Words after the count of visits that are not cancelled.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for month, weekday and time formatting.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AppointmentsListCard({\n  appointments,\n  defaultAppointments = [],\n  onAppointmentsChange,\n  onReschedule,\n  onCancel,\n  onRestore,\n  getSlots = defaultSlots,\n  title = \"Upcoming appointments\",\n  countLabel = \"scheduled\",\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  className,\n}: AppointmentsListCardProps) {\n  const [inner, setInner] = useState<Appointment[]>(defaultAppointments);\n  const [previousStatus, setPreviousStatus] = useState<Record<string, AppointmentStatus>>({});\n  const [openMenu, setOpenMenu] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [announcement, setAnnouncement] = useState(\"\");\n\n  const list = appointments ?? inner;\n  const sorted = [...list].sort(byStart);\n  const active = list.filter((a) => a.status !== \"cancelled\").length;\n  const f = makeFormatters(locale);\n\n  const toDate = (date: string) => parseDate(date).toDate(\"UTC\");\n  const toTime = (date: string, time: string, minutes = 0) =>\n    f.time.format(parseDateTime(`${date}T${time}`).add({ minutes }).toDate(\"UTC\"));\n  const slotLabel = (slot: AppointmentSlot) => `${f.slotDay.format(toDate(slot.date))} · ${toTime(slot.date, slot.time)}`;\n\n  const commit = (next: Appointment[]) => {\n    if (appointments === undefined) setInner(next);\n    onAppointmentsChange?.(next);\n  };\n  const patch = (id: string, change: Partial<Appointment>) =>\n    commit(list.map((a) => (a.id === id ? { ...a, ...change } : a)));\n\n  const reschedule = (appointment: Appointment, slot: AppointmentSlot) => {\n    patch(appointment.id, { ...slot, status: \"rescheduled\" });\n    setOpenMenu(null);\n    setAnnouncement(`${appointment.type} moved to ${slotLabel(slot)}`);\n    onReschedule?.(appointment, slot);\n  };\n\n  const cancel = (appointment: Appointment) => {\n    setPreviousStatus((prev) => ({ ...prev, [appointment.id]: appointment.status }));\n    patch(appointment.id, { status: \"cancelled\" });\n    setAnnouncement(`${appointment.type} cancelled`);\n    onCancel?.(appointment);\n  };\n\n  const restore = (appointment: Appointment) => {\n    const status = previousStatus[appointment.id] ?? \"confirmed\";\n    patch(appointment.id, { status });\n    setAnnouncement(`${appointment.type} restored`);\n    onRestore?.(appointment);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      aria-label={title}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-3 rounded-[20px] bg-background-secondary-default px-2.5 pt-4 pb-2.5\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-0.5 px-1.5\">\n        <h3 className=\"text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">{title}</h3>\n        <p className=\"flex items-baseline gap-1.5\">\n          <span\n            key={active}\n            className=\"animate-number-fade text-title-1-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums motion-reduce:animate-none\"\n            data-appointments-count=\"\"\n          >\n            {active}\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary\">{countLabel}</span>\n        </p>\n      </div>\n\n      {sorted.length === 0 ? (\n        <p className=\"rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default px-3 py-6 text-center text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">\n          No upcoming appointments\n        </p>\n      ) : (\n        <ul aria-label={title} className=\"flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-2 overflow-y-auto\">\n          {sorted.map((a) => {\n            const cancelled = a.status === \"cancelled\";\n            const day = toDate(a.date);\n            const mode = a.mode ? MODE[a.mode] : undefined;\n            const status = STATUS[a.status];\n            return (\n              <li\n                key={a.id}\n                data-appointment={a.id}\n                className=\"flex gap-3 rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default p-2.5\"\n              >\n                <div\n                  key={startOf(a)}\n                  aria-hidden\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"flex w-12 shrink-0 animate-number-fade flex-col items-center justify-center self-start rounded-lg bg-background-secondary-default py-1.5 motion-reduce:animate-none\",\n                    cancelled && \"opacity-60\",\n                  )}\n                >\n                  <span className=\"text-caption-2-semibold text-text-secondary uppercase\">{f.month.format(day)}</span>\n                  <span className={cx(\"text-title-2-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums\", cancelled && \"line-through\")}>\n                    {parseDate(a.date).day}\n                  </span>\n                  <span className=\"text-caption-2-medium text-text-tertiary\">{f.weekday.format(day)}</span>\n                </div>\n\n                <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-2\">\n                  <div className=\"flex items-start justify-between gap-2\">\n                    <div className={cx(\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\", cancelled && \"opacity-60\")}>\n                      <p className={cx(\"line-clamp-2 text-body-medium break-words text-text-primary\", cancelled && \"line-through\")}>\n                        {a.type}\n                      </p>\n                      <p className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-1.5 gap-y-0.5 text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">\n                        <span className=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1 whitespace-nowrap tabular-nums\" data-appointment-time=\"\">\n                          <RiTimeLine className=\"size-3.5 shrink-0\" aria-hidden />\n                          <span className=\"sr-only\">{f.slotDay.format(day)}, </span>\n                          {toTime(a.date, a.time)} – {toTime(a.date, a.time, a.durationMinutes ?? 30)}\n                        </span>\n                        {mode ? (\n                          <span className=\"inline-flex items-center gap-1\">\n                            <mode.icon className=\"size-3.5 shrink-0\" aria-hidden />\n                            {mode.label}\n                          </span>\n                        ) : null}\n                      </p>\n                    </div>\n                    <Chip variant=\"caption\" color={status.color} className=\"shrink-0\" data-appointment-status=\"\">\n                      {status.label}\n                    </Chip>\n                  </div>\n\n                  <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2\">\n                    <span className={cx(\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5\", cancelled && \"opacity-60\")}>\n                      <Avatar size=\"sm\" color={a.doctorColor} initials={a.doctorInitials ?? initialsOf(a.doctor)} aria-hidden />\n                      <span className=\"truncate text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">{a.doctor}</span>\n                    </span>\n                    <div className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1\">\n                      {cancelled ? (\n                        <Button\n                          variant=\"secondary\"\n                          size=\"xs\"\n                          leadingIcon={RiArrowGoBackLine}\n                          aria-label={`Undo cancel ${a.type}`}\n                          onClick={() => restore(a)}\n                        >\n                          Undo\n                        </Button>\n                      ) : (\n                        <>\n                          <Dropdown\n                            isOpen={openMenu === a.id}\n                            onOpenChange={(open) => setOpenMenu(open ? a.id : null)}\n                          >\n                            <DropdownTrigger\n                              aria-label={`Reschedule ${a.type}`}\n                              className={cx(\n                                buttonStyles.base,\n                                buttonStyles.size.xs,\n                                buttonStyles.variant.ghost,\n                                \"gap-1\",\n                              )}\n                            >\n                              <RiCalendarScheduleLine className=\"size-3.5 shrink-0\" aria-hidden />\n                              Reschedule\n                            </DropdownTrigger>\n                            <DropdownPopover\n                              aria-label={`New time for ${a.type}`}\n                              placement=\"bottom end\"\n                              className=\"w-[232px]\"\n                            >\n                              <DropdownGroup label=\"Open slots\">\n                                {getSlots(a).map((slot) => (\n                                  <DropdownItem\n                                    key={startOf(slot)}\n                                    className=\"px-2 py-1.5 tabular-nums\"\n                                    onSelect={() => reschedule(a, slot)}\n                                  >\n                                    <RiCalendarScheduleLine className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-text-secondary\" aria-hidden />\n                                    <span className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{slotLabel(slot)}</span>\n                                  </DropdownItem>\n                                ))}\n                              </DropdownGroup>\n                            </DropdownPopover>\n                          </Dropdown>\n                          <Button\n                            variant=\"secondary\"\n                            size=\"xs\"\n                            leadingIcon={RiCloseLine}\n                            aria-label={`Cancel ${a.type}`}\n                            onClick={() => cancel(a)}\n                          >\n                            Cancel\n                          </Button>\n                        </>\n                      )}\n                    </div>\n                  </div>\n                </div>\n              </li>\n            );\n          })}\n        </ul>\n      )}\n      <p className=\"sr-only\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        {announcement}\n      </p>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers of its own; the kit Dropdown removes its outside-press listener when it closes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The count and date chip fades use motion-reduce:animate-none, so values swap instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not format a `CalendarDate` through the viewer's local timezone: convert with `toDate(\"UTC\")` and format with `timeZone: \"UTC\"`, or the chip can show the previous day west of UTC.\n- Do not leave the Reschedule menu uncontrolled: kit `DropdownItem` only runs `onSelect`, so the menu would stay open over the moved tile.\n- Do not key the tile by its date: the `li` keeps the id as key so focus and the menu survive a re-sort, and only the date chip is keyed by start time to replay the fade.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Status colours: confirmed lime, pending yellow, rescheduled blue, cancelled rose (kit `Chip` caption variant).\n- Mode icons are Remix `RiHospitalLine` (in person), `RiVideoChatLine` (video call) and `RiPhoneLine` (phone).\n- The tile list is a flex-1 `overflow-y-auto` column, so a `max-h-*` class on the card keeps it to a fixed height and scrolls the visits (the demo uses max-h-[500px]).\n- Doctor initials come from `doctorInitials` or the first letters of the first two words after a \"Dr.\" prefix.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPicking a slot in a Reschedule menu closes it, moves the visit to that date and time with a blue Rescheduled chip; Cancel shows a rose Cancelled chip, lowers the count by one and offers Undo, which restores the previous status.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "area-chart-card": "# Build: Area Chart Card\n\nA grey dashboard card that charts monthly visitors by channel as gradient-filled Recharts areas, stacked by default. The header shows the period total with its change against the period before and a This year / Last year switch that swaps the whole data set. Hovering a month draws a dashed cursor, a pinging dot on each area and a tooltip with the split, while the headline counts to that month and the tiles under the chart show its per-channel values.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dashboard card of stacked gradient areas with a period switch, a hover tooltip and tiles that follow the hovered month.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each series is an `Area` with a 2px stroke in its tone's `-active` colour and a fill of `url(#<id>-<key>)`: a vertical `linearGradient` in the chart `<defs>` from `--color-chart-N` at 0.55 opacity to 0.05. Ids are `useId()` plus the series key, so cards never share gradients.\n2. `view` decides stacking. `stacked` gives every Area `stackId=\"total\"`; `overlap` gives none, so each area starts at zero; `percent` keeps the stack and sets `stackOffset=\"expand\"` on the chart, with the Y axis fixed to `[0, 1]` and ticks formatted as whole percents.\n3. `onMouseMove` stores `state.activeTooltipIndex` in `hovered` when `isTooltipActive` is true and the index is finite, and `onMouseLeave` clears it. With a month hovered the headline is that month's sum across series against the month before (no chip for the first month); otherwise it is the period total against `previousTotal`.\n4. The `Tooltip` keeps a dashed `--color-chart-cursor` cursor and renders `ChartTooltipCard` from `activeIndex` (mapped from `null` to -1 first): the month and its total, then one row per series from the top of the stack down, with each share in brackets in percent view.\n5. Every Area's `activeDot` is the shared `PulseDot`, so each stacked band gets a ringed dot with a ping behind it at its own height.\n6. `ChartStatTiles` lists the series under the chart with square colour keys. Each tile shows the hovered month's value or, with nothing hovered, the series total, so the tiles double as the legend.\n7. Choosing a period clears `hovered`, updates internal state only when `period` is uncontrolled, and calls `onPeriodChange`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `area gradient = chart-N at stopOpacity 0.55 → 0.05`\n  Stacked bands need more tint than a single area to stay distinct where they meet; keeping 0.05 at the bottom stops lower bands from looking empty.\n- `plot height = 248px`\n  The card height follows its content (header, plot, tiles). A fixed plot box gives `ResponsiveContainer` a real height without fixing the whole card.\n- `axes = YAxis width 44, tickCount 4 (5 in percent view), XAxis preserveStartEnd, minTickGap 8`\n  44px fits \"13.5K\" at 12px. The minimum gap lets Recharts drop month labels on phones instead of overlapping them.\n- `tile columns = 3 series: grid-cols-2, grid-cols-3 from a 384px card (@sm); 2 series: grid-cols-2`\n  Three tiles fit side by side at preview width; on narrow cards the third wraps instead of truncating the numbers.\n- `motion = areas 450ms, tooltip 160ms, count-up 320ms, number-fade 220ms`\n  Short enough to follow a pointer across twelve months; all of it is off under reduced motion.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AreaChartCard(props?: AreaChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype AreaChartCardProps = { title?: string; series?: AreaSeries[]; periods?: AreaPeriod[]; period?: string; defaultPeriod?: string; onPeriodChange?: (id: string) => void; view?: \"stacked\" | \"overlap\" | \"percent\"; shape?: \"curved\" | \"sharp\"; periodControl?: \"segmented\" | \"select\"; className?: string }\ntype AreaSeries = { key: string; label: string; tone: ChartTone }\ntype AreaPeriod = { id: string; label: string; data: AreaPoint[]; previousTotal?: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Visitors\"`\n  Header label; while hovering it becomes \"Visitors · <month>\".\n- `series: AreaSeries[]` — default `AREA_SERIES`\n  Categories from the bottom of the stack up; also the tile order.\n- `periods: AreaPeriod[]` — default `AREA_PERIODS`\n  Data sets the switch moves between; `previousTotal` feeds the headline chip.\n- `period: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultPeriod: string` — default `first period id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onPeriodChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the chosen period id.\n- `view: \"stacked\" | \"overlap\" | \"percent\"` — default `\"stacked\"`\n  Stack the areas, draw each from zero, or stretch each month to 100%.\n- `shape: \"curved\" | \"sharp\"` — default `\"curved\"`\n  Monotone curves or straight segments.\n- `periodControl: \"segmented\" | \"select\"` — default `\"segmented\"`\n  Segmented switch, or a calendar pill that opens a listbox.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AreaChartCard({\n  title = \"Visitors\",\n  series = AREA_SERIES,\n  periods = AREA_PERIODS,\n  period,\n  defaultPeriod,\n  onPeriodChange,\n  view = \"stacked\",\n  shape = \"curved\",\n  periodControl = \"segmented\",\n  className,\n}: AreaChartCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const gradientBase = useId();\n  const [innerPeriod, setInnerPeriod] = useState(defaultPeriod ?? periods[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const periodId = period ?? innerPeriod;\n  const active = periods.find((p) => p.id === periodId) ?? periods[0];\n  const data = active?.data ?? [];\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState<number | null>(null);\n\n  const point = hovered !== null ? data[hovered] : undefined;\n  const sumOf = (p: AreaPoint | undefined) => series.reduce((sum, s) => sum + valueAt(p, s.key), 0);\n  const totals = series.map((s) => data.reduce((sum, p) => sum + valueAt(p, s.key), 0));\n  const grandTotal = totals.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\n\n  const headline = point ? sumOf(point) : grandTotal;\n  const delta = point\n    ? hovered !== null && hovered > 0\n      ? describeDelta(headline, sumOf(data[hovered - 1]))\n      : null\n    : describeDelta(grandTotal, active?.previousTotal);\n  const curve = shape === \"sharp\" ? \"linear\" : \"monotone\";\n  const percent = view === \"percent\";\n\n  const selectPeriod = (id: string) => {\n    setHovered(null);\n    if (period === undefined) setInnerPeriod(id);\n    onPeriodChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section aria-label={`${title} chart`} className={cx(CHART_CARD_FRAME, \"gap-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 px-2 pt-2 @md:flex-row @md:items-start @md:justify-between\">\n        <ChartHeadline\n          label={point ? `${title} · ${point.label}` : title}\n          value={headline}\n          delta={delta}\n          swapKey={`${periodId}-${hovered ?? \"total\"}`}\n          reducedMotion={reduced}\n        />\n        {periods.length > 1 ? (\n          <ChartPeriodControl\n            options={periods}\n            value={periodId}\n            onChange={selectPeriod}\n            variant={periodControl}\n            label={`${title} period`}\n            className=\"self-start\"\n          />\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"h-[248px] w-full pe-2\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" initialDimension={{ width: 520, height: 248 }}>\n          <ComposedChart\n            data={data}\n            stackOffset={percent ? \"expand\" : \"none\"}\n            margin={{ top: 8, right: 8, bottom: 0, left: 0 }}\n            onMouseMove={(state) => {\n              const next = Number(state?.activeTooltipIndex);\n              if (state?.isTooltipActive && Number.isFinite(next)) setHovered(next);\n            }}\n            onMouseLeave={() => setHovered(null)}\n          >\n            <defs>\n              {series.map((s) => (\n                <linearGradient key={s.key} id={`${gradientBase}-${s.key}`} x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\">\n                  <stop offset=\"0%\" stopColor={toneColor(s.tone)} stopOpacity={0.55} />\n                  <stop offset=\"100%\" stopColor={toneColor(s.tone)} stopOpacity={0.05} />\n                </linearGradient>\n              ))}\n            </defs>\n            <CartesianGrid vertical={false} stroke={GRID_STROKE} strokeDasharray=\"4 4\" />\n            <YAxis\n              width={44}\n              domain={percent ? [0, 1] : [0, \"auto\"]}\n              tickCount={percent ? 5 : 4}\n              tickFormatter={(v: number) => (percent ? `${Math.round(v * 100)}%` : formatCompact(v))}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <XAxis\n              dataKey=\"label\"\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tickMargin={10}\n              interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"\n              minTickGap={8}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <Tooltip\n              cursor={CHART_CURSOR}\n              offset={16}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={160}\n              wrapperStyle={{ outline: \"none\", zIndex: 10 }}\n              content={({ active: isActive, activeIndex }) => {\n                const at = data[activeIndex == null ? -1 : Number(activeIndex)];\n                if (!isActive || !at) return null;\n                const total = sumOf(at);\n                return (\n                  <ChartTooltipCard\n                    title={`${at.label} · ${formatNumber(total)} total`}\n                    rows={[...series].reverse().map((s) => {\n                      const v = valueAt(at, s.key);\n                      const share = total > 0 ? ` (${Math.round((v / total) * 100)}%)` : \"\";\n                      return { key: s.key, label: s.label, tone: s.tone, value: `${formatNumber(v)}${percent ? share : \"\"}` };\n                    })}\n                  />\n                );\n              }}\n            />\n            {series.map((s) => (\n              <Area\n                key={s.key}\n                type={curve}\n                dataKey={s.key}\n                name={s.label}\n                stackId={view === \"overlap\" ? undefined : \"total\"}\n                stroke={toneActiveColor(s.tone)}\n                strokeWidth={2}\n                fill={`url(#${gradientBase}-${s.key})`}\n                activeDot={<PulseDot color={toneActiveColor(s.tone)} pulse={!reduced} />}\n                isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n                animationDuration={450}\n              />\n            ))}\n          </ComposedChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n\n      <ChartStatTiles\n        columns={series.length === 2 ? 2 : \"3-wrap\"}\n        tiles={series.map((s, i) => ({\n          key: s.key,\n          label: s.label,\n          tone: s.tone,\n          value: formatNumber(point ? valueAt(point, s.key) : (totals[i] ?? 0)),\n        }))}\n      />\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame on target change and unmount, `useReducedMotion` unsubscribes its media-query listener, and `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer. The card adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` turns off `isAnimationActive` on the Areas and the Tooltip, the headline shows its value without counting, the number fade is `motion-reduce:animate-none`, and the active dots drop their ping circle.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sharing one gradient id across series. Each series needs its own `<linearGradient>` id, and ids must be unique per card; the id is `useId()` plus the series key.\n- Using a Y domain of `[0, \"auto\"]` in percent view. With `stackOffset=\"expand\"` the stacked values run 0 to 1, so the domain is pinned to `[0, 1]` and ticks are multiplied by 100.\n- Comparing the hovered month with `undefined` for January. The first month has no month before it, so the chip is hidden rather than showing \"New\".\n- Reading `activeIndex` with `Number()` directly. Recharts passes `null` when inactive and `Number(null)` is 0, which would pin the tooltip to January.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `AREA_SERIES`, `AREA_PERIODS` and the `AreaSeries`, `AreaPoint`, `AreaPeriod` and `AreaChartView` types are exported. This year adds up to 94,700 (Organic 50,500, Referral 25,900, Paid 18,300) against 87,520, shown as \"+8.2%\"; last year is 87,520 against 80,940, \"+8.1%\".\n- The frame, headline, period switch, tooltip card, tiles and pulse dot come from `line-chart-card-parts.tsx`, so this card matches the line chart card beside it.\n- BoardUI's preview shows the period as a calendar pill; `periodControl=\"select\"` gives that pill and the default is a segmented switch.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Visitors\", \"94,700\" and a lime \"+8.2%\" chip with This year selected, three stacked lime, blue and purple gradient areas over dashed rules, and tiles Organic 50,500, Referral 25,900, Paid 18,300. Hovering a month shows a tooltip with its total and each channel, a dot on every band, and the headline and tiles switch to that month. Choosing Last year shows \"87,520\" and \"+8.1%\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "asymmetric-stagger": "# Build: Asymmetric In/Out Staggers\n\nA list whose entrance and exit are deliberately different animations. In: 24px up, 0.5s each, 0.06s apart, after a 0.25s hold that lets the outgoing view clear. Out: 16px up, 0.3s each, 0.03s apart, running from the last item back to the first. The exit is roughly half the total time of the entrance, which is what makes a view swap feel responsive without making the arrival feel rushed.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA list that arrives item by item after a hold and leaves twice as fast, from the last row backwards.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every direct child is wrapped in a `[data-stagger-item]` div, so the caller writes ordinary list markup and the component owns the animated node — no cloneElement, no required ref forwarding.\n2. Entering runs `gsap.fromTo(items, { autoAlpha: 0, y: 24 }, { autoAlpha: 1, y: 0, duration: 0.5, ease: \"power2.out\", stagger: 0.06, delay: 0.25 })`. The delay is not decoration: it is the window in which whatever this list replaces finishes leaving.\n3. Leaving runs `gsap.to(items, { autoAlpha: 0, y: -16, duration: 0.3, ease: \"power2.in\", stagger: { each: 0.03, from: \"end\" } })`. Halving the stagger and reversing its origin is what stops the exit from reading as the entrance rewound.\n4. `autoAlpha` rather than `opacity`: at zero it also writes `visibility: hidden`, so a faded row cannot swallow a click aimed at the view underneath it.\n5. Both tweens carry `overwrite: \"auto\"`, and the whole thing lives in a `useGsap` context keyed on `show`, so toggling twice in quick succession cancels the in-flight direction instead of layering two opposing tweens on the same nodes.\n6. A layout effect hides the items before first paint when the component mounts closed, and an internal `hasShown` flag makes sure `onExited` can only fire after a real entrance.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `inStagger = 0.06`\n  Seconds between arriving items. Below ~0.04 a five-item list reads as one block; above ~0.1 the last row lands well after the eye has moved on and the list feels slow to load.\n- `outStagger = 0.03`\n  Half the entrance value. Exits are acknowledgement, not presentation — the wave still reads as a direction, but the whole list is gone in under 200ms.\n- `inDelay = 0.25`\n  Seconds of hold before the first item arrives. Sized to the outgoing view's own fade (~0.3-0.4s) so the two never overlap; at 0 both views are half-opaque at the same time and the frame looks like a rendering bug.\n- `inDuration / outDuration = 0.5 / 0.3`\n  Per-item. The exit is shorter for the same reason its stagger is: nobody is reading rows that are leaving.\n- `inDistance / outDistance = 24 / 16`\n  Pixels. The entrance comes up from below (from where the content is arriving); the exit continues upward and travels less, because a long exit path is time spent watching nothing.\n- `stagger.from = 'end'`\n  Reverses the exit wave. Same-direction staggers make the exit look like a backwards entrance; from the end it looks like the list is being pulled off the screen.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AsymmetricStagger(props: { children: ReactNode; show: boolean; inStagger?: number; outStagger?: number; inDelay?: number; inDuration?: number; outDuration?: number; inDistance?: number; outDistance?: number; onExited?: () => void; className?: string; itemClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  List items. Each direct child is wrapped and animated on its own.\n- `show: boolean`\n  True enters, false exits. The component stays mounted through both.\n- `inStagger: number` — default `0.06`\n  Seconds between entering items.\n- `outStagger: number` — default `0.03`\n  Seconds between leaving items.\n- `inDelay: number` — default `0.25`\n  Hold before the entrance, sized to the outgoing view.\n- `inDuration: number` — default `0.5`\n  Per-item entrance length in seconds.\n- `outDuration: number` — default `0.3`\n  Per-item exit length in seconds.\n- `inDistance: number` — default `24`\n  Pixels travelled on the way in.\n- `outDistance: number` — default `16`\n  Pixels travelled on the way out.\n- `onExited: () => void`\n  Called once the last item has left. Safe place to unmount the data.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the flex column wrapper.\n- `itemClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every generated item wrapper.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AsymmetricStagger({\n  children,\n  show,\n  inStagger = 0.06,\n  outStagger = 0.03,\n  inDelay = 0.25,\n  inDuration = 0.5,\n  outDuration = 0.3,\n  inDistance = 24,\n  outDistance = 16,\n  onExited,\n  className,\n  itemClassName,\n}: AsymmetricStaggerProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const exited = useRef(onExited);\n  exited.current = onExited;\n\n  const startedHidden = useRef(!show);\n  /* An exit only makes sense after an entrance. Without this, mounting with\n     `show={false}` plays an invisible exit and fires `onExited` — and under\n     StrictMode the double-invoked effect fires it a second time. */\n  const hasShown = useRef(show);\n  /* One exit per hide. `reduced` is in the effect's dep list because the OS\n     setting is live, so flipping it while the list is already hidden re-entered\n     the exit branch and fired `onExited` a second time — at which point a\n     parent that unmounts its data on that callback unmounts it twice. */\n  const exitPlayed = useRef(false);\n\n  // Before paint, not after. A passive effect can land on the far side of the\n  // first frame, so a list that starts closed would flash its items once.\n  useLayoutEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el || !startedHidden.current) return;\n    gsap.set(el.querySelectorAll('[data-stagger-item]'), { autoAlpha: 0 });\n  }, []);\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      if (!el) return;\n      const items = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-stagger-item]'));\n      if (items.length === 0) return;\n\n      if (!show && !hasShown.current) return;\n\n      // Re-entering the exit branch for a list that has already gone. The\n      // `revert()` this hook runs before every re-execution has just undone the\n      // exit tween's styles, so the list is visible again and has to be put\n      // back — but it must not animate and must not report a second exit.\n      if (!show && exitPlayed.current) {\n        gsap.set(items, { autoAlpha: 0 });\n        return;\n      }\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        // No travel and no stagger, but the state change still has to happen\n        // or the list is stuck in whichever position it was left in.\n        gsap.set(items, { autoAlpha: show ? 1 : 0, y: 0 });\n        if (show) {\n          hasShown.current = true;\n          exitPlayed.current = false;\n        } else {\n          exitPlayed.current = true;\n          exited.current?.();\n        }\n        return;\n      }\n\n      if (show) {\n        hasShown.current = true;\n        exitPlayed.current = false;\n        // autoAlpha, not opacity: at 0 it also sets visibility:hidden, so a\n        // faded-out row cannot still swallow a click meant for what is\n        // underneath it.\n        gsap.fromTo(\n          items,\n          { autoAlpha: 0, y: inDistance },\n          {\n            autoAlpha: 1,\n            y: 0,\n            duration: inDuration,\n            ease: 'power2.out',\n            stagger: inStagger,\n            delay: inDelay,\n            overwrite: 'auto',\n          },\n        );\n        return;\n      }\n\n      exitPlayed.current = true;\n      // `fromTo`, not `to`, and the start state is stated rather than read off\n      // the element. This hook reverts its context before every re-run, and\n      // revert restores the inline styles that were on the node BEFORE the\n      // entrance tween — which, for a list that mounted with `show={false}`,\n      // are the `visibility: hidden; opacity: 0` written by the pre-paint set\n      // above. A `to()` therefore tweened a list that was already invisible:\n      // it vanished on the frame the hide was requested and `onExited` still\n      // arrived `outDuration` later. Reaching here at all means the entrance\n      // has run, so fully visible is the honest place to leave from.\n      gsap.fromTo(\n        items,\n        { autoAlpha: 1, y: 0 },\n        {\n          autoAlpha: 0,\n          y: -outDistance,\n          duration: outDuration,\n          ease: 'power2.in',\n          // `from: 'end'` reverses the wave. Same-direction staggers on the way\n          // out read as the list re-entering backwards.\n          stagger: { each: outStagger, from: 'end' },\n          overwrite: 'auto',\n          onComplete: () => exited.current?.(),\n        },\n      );\n    },\n    root,\n    [show, reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` reverts the context on unmount and on every `show` change, killing both tweens and restoring the inline styles GSAP wrote. `onExited` is held in a ref so a completion callback cannot reach a stale closure after the parent has re-rendered.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No travel and no stagger. Items are set to their visible or hidden state in one `gsap.set`, and `onExited` still fires, so the surrounding state machine behaves identically — only the choreography is dropped.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reusing the entrance timeline with `reverse()`. That gives you power2.out played backwards (so it decelerates into nothing), the entrance delay stranded at the end, and the same stagger direction — three separate reasons the exit feels heavy.\n- Unmounting the list when `show` flips false. React removes the nodes on the same frame, so the exit never runs; the component stays mounted and reports completion through `onExited`.\n- Animating `opacity` alone. A finished row sits at opacity 0 with full hit area and eats clicks intended for the view underneath. `autoAlpha` writes `visibility: hidden` at zero.\n- Omitting `overwrite: \"auto\"`. Toggling twice inside 300ms leaves the entrance tween running under the exit tween, and the rows settle at whatever opacity the two happen to agree on.\n- Setting the initial hidden state in a passive effect. Mounting with `show={false}` paints the list once, then hides it — a visible flash of content that was never meant to be seen. It is set in `useLayoutEffect` instead.\n- Firing `onExited` on mount. With `show={false}` at mount there is nothing to exit, and under StrictMode the double-invoked effect would call it twice; a `hasShown` flag gates it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The same numbers apply outside lists — modals, drawers, route content. Any pair of entrance/exit values that are equal is worth questioning.\n- App Router wiring: drive `show` from a pathname comparison in a layout, and do the actual `router.push` from `onExited` if you want the route to change only once the list has gone.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSwitch to the list: the previous view fades, a quarter-second of nothing, then rows arrive top to bottom. Switch back: the bottom row leaves first and the whole list is gone in about half the time it took to arrive. Toggle rapidly and rows never settle at a partial opacity.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "asymmetric-typewriter": "# Build: Asymmetric Typewriter\n\nA phrase erases itself and a new one is typed in its place, cycling forever. Deleting runs at exactly half the interval of typing, because backspacing is a held key with a mechanical auto-repeat while typing is composition — give both phases the same interval and the result reads as a ticker tape rather than as somebody at a keyboard. A quarter-second pause at the empty string turns the turnaround from a dropped frame into a decision.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRotating phrases that delete at twice the speed they type, with a beat of nothing at the empty string.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Timing is a chain of `setTimeout` calls, not a `setInterval`: the four phases (type, hold, delete, empty) each have their own interval, and the boundaries between them are pauses that a fixed interval cannot express without a counter and a modulo.\n2. The state machine is four closures over a `text` local — `type` slices one more character off the target and reschedules at `typeMs`; on reaching full length it schedules `beginErase` at `holdMs`. `erase` slices one character off and reschedules at `deleteMs`; at length zero it schedules `advance` at `emptyMs`. `advance` moves the phrase index modulo the list length and re-enters `type`.\n3. Typing slices the *target* string (`target.slice(0, text.length + 1)`) rather than concatenating a looked-up character. Under `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` an index read is `string | undefined`, and slicing also means the visible text can never desync from the source phrase.\n4. A single `timer` ref holds the one outstanding timeout id, so cleanup is a single `clearTimeout` regardless of which phase was in flight.\n5. The effect is keyed on `phrases.join(\"\\u0000\")` rather than on the array itself. An array prop is a fresh reference every parent render, which would tear the machine down and restart it, pinning the typewriter to its first character forever. NUL is the delimiter because real phrases contain spaces and commas.\n6. The caret is the `step-end-caret` component, driven with `paused={busy}`: while characters are being added or removed the caret holds solid, and it resumes blinking only during the hold and empty beats.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TYPE_MS = 70`\n  Milliseconds per character while typing. Around 14 characters a second — brisk human typing. Below ~45ms the reader cannot follow the word forming; above ~110ms the phrase takes longer to appear than anyone will wait for.\n- `DELETE_MS = 35`\n  Exactly half of `TYPE_MS`. This ratio is the whole component: deleting is a held key on auto-repeat, typing is composition. Equal intervals make both directions read as machine output.\n- `HOLD_MS = 1500`\n  How long a completed phrase stays up. It has to exceed the time to read four or five words. Under ~900ms the phrase is gone before it has been parsed and the component reads as flickering.\n- `EMPTY_MS = 250`\n  The beat at zero characters. Without it the string bottoms out and immediately rebounds, which the eye reads as a dropped frame rather than as a turnaround.\n- `START_DELAY_MS = 1500`\n  Longer than a normal hold, so the first phrase is legible while the rest of the page is still settling. Starting the erase immediately makes the component look like it is correcting a mistake.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AsymmetricTypewriter(props: { phrases: string[]; typeMs?: number; deleteMs?: number; holdMs?: number; emptyMs?: number; startDelayMs?: number; caret?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `phrases: string[]`\n  Cycled in order, forever. Index 0 is also the server-rendered string.\n- `typeMs: number` — default `70`\n  Milliseconds between characters while typing.\n- `deleteMs: number` — default `35`\n  Milliseconds between characters while deleting.\n- `holdMs: number` — default `1500`\n  Milliseconds a completed phrase is held.\n- `emptyMs: number` — default `250`\n  Milliseconds held at the empty string.\n- `startDelayMs: number` — default `1500`\n  Milliseconds before the very first deletion.\n- `caret: boolean` — default `true`\n  Render the blinking caret after the text.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer span via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TYPE_MS = 70;\nconst DELETE_MS = 35;\nconst HOLD_MS = 1500;\nconst EMPTY_MS = 250;\nconst START_DELAY_MS = 1500;\n\nexport function AsymmetricTypewriter({\n  phrases,\n  typeMs = TYPE_MS,\n  deleteMs = DELETE_MS,\n  holdMs = HOLD_MS,\n  emptyMs = EMPTY_MS,\n  startDelayMs = START_DELAY_MS,\n  caret = true,\n  className,\n}: AsymmetricTypewriterProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // An array prop is a fresh reference on every parent render. Keying on the\n  // joined string is what stops the effect tearing down and restarting each\n  // time — which would pin the typewriter to its first character forever.\n  // NUL is the delimiter because real phrases contain spaces and commas, and\n  // a separator that occurs in the data silently splits one phrase into two.\n  const key = phrases.join('\\u0000');\n  const list = useMemo(() => key.split('\\u0000'), [key]);\n\n  const [shown, setShown] = useState(() => list[0] ?? '');\n  const [busy, setBusy] = useState(false);\n  const timer = useRef(0);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const first = list[0] ?? '';\n    if (reduced || list.length < 2) {\n      setShown(first);\n      setBusy(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let index = 0;\n    let text = first;\n    setShown(text);\n\n    const at = (fn: () => void, ms: number) => {\n      timer.current = window.setTimeout(fn, ms);\n    };\n\n    const erase = () => {\n      if (text.length === 0) {\n        // The beat. Caret goes back to blinking because nothing is happening.\n        setBusy(false);\n        at(advance, emptyMs);\n        return;\n      }\n      text = text.slice(0, -1);\n      setShown(text);\n      at(erase, deleteMs);\n    };\n\n    const advance = () => {\n      index = (index + 1) % list.length;\n      setBusy(true);\n      at(type, typeMs);\n    };\n\n    const type = () => {\n      const target = list[index] ?? '';\n      if (text.length >= target.length) {\n        setBusy(false);\n        at(beginErase, holdMs);\n        return;\n      }\n      // Slice the target rather than concatenating a looked-up character:\n      // `target[i]` is `string | undefined` and the slice cannot desync from\n      // the source string if the phrase list changes underneath.\n      text = target.slice(0, text.length + 1);\n      setShown(text);\n      at(type, typeMs);\n    };\n\n    const beginErase = () => {\n      setBusy(true);\n      erase();\n    };\n\n    at(beginErase, startDelayMs);\n\n    return () => {\n      window.clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    };\n  }, [list, reduced, typeMs, deleteMs, holdMs, emptyMs, startDelayMs]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** A single `window.clearTimeout(timer.current)` in the effect teardown. Only one timeout is ever outstanding, so no phase can survive unmount and call `setState` on a dead component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The machine never starts. The first phrase is rendered in full and stays — the final text state, immediately, rather than an empty slot or a sped-up cycle. The caret is still rendered but its own reduced-motion rule holds it solid.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Driving the whole cycle from one `setInterval`. Hold and empty pauses then have to be faked with a frame counter, and every change to a phase length becomes an arithmetic edit rather than a constant.\n- Depending on the `phrases` array identity in the effect. Every parent render gives a new array, the effect restarts, and the typewriter is stuck showing the first character of the first phrase.\n- Joining phrases with a comma or a pipe as the effect key. Both occur in real copy, so one phrase silently becomes two.\n- Letting the element size itself from the current string. It grows and shrinks by tens of pixels several times a second and drags every neighbouring element with it — a transparent copy of the longest phrase reserves the box instead.\n- Using normal whitespace collapsing. A phrase erased back to \"Product \" loses its trailing space, so the caret jumps one character left and then right again on the next tick.\n- Blinking the caret while characters are being typed. Real cursors hold solid during input; a caret blinking mid-word makes the text look like it is being deleted by something else.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No text-splitting library is involved: the component owns a single string in React state and never mutates the DOM. Adding a split dependency here would buy nothing, since there are no per-character targets to animate.\n- React state at ~14 updates a second is a deliberate tradeoff. The subtree is one span, and holding the string in state is what keeps the server-rendered first phrase and the client in agreement.\n- A list of fewer than two phrases renders the first one statically and never starts the machine.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWatch one full cycle: the phrase erases visibly faster than the next one types — time it and the ratio is 2:1. At the empty string there is a perceptible pause before the next phrase starts. The caret holds solid while characters move and blinks while the phrase is held. Nothing to the right of the component moves at any point during the cycle. With reduced motion enabled the first phrase is present on first paint and never changes.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "auth-card": "# Build: Auth Card\n\nThe shell around a sign-in screen. One card covers three steps: `signin` (email, password, remember me, forgot password), `signup` (name, email with a hint, password, terms line) and `verify` (OTP boxes and a resend action). Social providers always sit below the email form, behind an \"or continue with\" divider, in one of three layouts from the same `providers` array. Passing `media` turns it into an 880px split card with artwork on the right, and `AuthMediaCarousel` is the ready-made option: full-bleed images that shrink into cards and swing away on a drum.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSign-in, sign-up and code-verify cards with social providers stacked, inline or in a grid, and an optional split layout with artwork.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `mode` picks a copy set from `COPY` (title, description, CTA and footer line) and which fields render. `verify` swaps the email and password inputs for `InputOtp` (6 digits by default, split into two groups when the length is even), drops the providers, shows a mail icon as the mark and turns the footer link into a resend button.\n2. The form is uncontrolled. On submit it calls `preventDefault` and hands `new FormData(form)` to `onSubmit`, with fields named `name`, `email`, `password` and `confirmPassword`, so it can be wired to any auth library.\n3. Providers map to `SocialButton`s with the white appearance. `stacked` gives full-width labelled buttons in a column; `inline` gives icon-only squares in a wrapping, centred row; `grid` gives icon-only buttons in `grid-flow-col auto-cols-fr`, stretched with `w-full`, so a small set reads as one row of controls. Icon-only buttons get `aria-label=\"Continue with <Provider>\"`.\n4. Without `media` the card is a single 400px column. With it, the card becomes a two-column grid (`md:grid-cols-2`, max 880px) and the media cell is `hidden` below `md`, so a phone gets the form alone instead of a tall image above it. `footnote` renders small print centred below the card, outside its border.\n5. `AuthMediaCarousel` runs a four-beat `setTimeout` state machine: `idle` for `interval` (3200ms), `shrink` to `scale(0.82)` with a 24px radius for 450ms plus a 200ms hold, `slide` for 700ms, then a 20ms `enter` frame before growing back to full bleed over 500ms.\n6. Each slide is placed by `position = (slideIndex − index + count) % count`. During `slide` the current one moves to `translateX(−100%) translateZ(−220px) rotateY(−48deg)` inside an 850px perspective, while the next one travels from the mirrored spot (`translateX(100%)`, `rotateY(48deg)`) to the centre at `scale(0.8)`. Only the slides moving in a beat get a transition, so a slide wrapping from the left to the back of the queue jumps without streaking. The sign flips in RTL.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card widths = 400px single, 880px split`\n  A sign-in column stays narrow enough to scan; the split halves the wider card between form and art.\n- `default providers = [\"google\", \"apple\", \"github\"]`\n  Three is a full row in `grid` and a short stack in `stacked`.\n- `CARD_SCALE / ENTER_SCALE / CARD_RADIUS = 0.82 / 0.8 / 24px`\n  The image visibly detaches into a card before it moves, and the incoming card arrives slightly smaller so the grow reads as a separate beat.\n- `PERSPECTIVE / DEPTH / TILT = 850px / 220px / 48deg`\n  Cards bank away from the middle as if on a drum turning past the viewer, which gives the movement a front and a back.\n- `SHRINK_MS / HOLD_MS / SLIDE_MS / GROW_MS = 450 / 200 / 700 / 500`\n  The hold makes the shrink read as deliberate; the slide is the longest beat because it carries the most distance.\n- `interval = 3200ms`\n  Time at full bleed before each change: atmosphere, not a slideshow to watch.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype AuthLayout = \"stacked\" | \"inline\" | \"grid\"\ntype AuthMode = \"signin\" | \"signup\" | \"verify\"\nfunction AuthCard(props: { mode?: AuthMode; email?: string; codeLength?: number; onComplete?: (code: string) => void; onResend?: () => void; layout?: AuthLayout; providers?: SocialProvider[]; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; media?: ReactNode; logo?: ReactNode; centered?: boolean; confirmPassword?: boolean; footnote?: ReactNode; onSubmit?: (data: FormData) => void; onProvider?: (provider: SocialProvider) => void; switchHref?: string; headingLevel?: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction AuthMediaCarousel(props: { slides: { src: string; alt?: string }[]; interval?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `mode: \"signin\" | \"signup\" | \"verify\"` — default `\"signin\"`\n  Which step the card shows.\n- `layout: \"stacked\" | \"inline\" | \"grid\"` — default `\"stacked\"`\n  How the provider buttons are arranged under the form.\n- `providers: SocialProvider[]` — default `[\"google\", \"apple\", \"github\"]`\n  Provider keys from `SOCIAL_PROVIDERS`; an empty array hides the divider and buttons.\n- `onSubmit: (data: FormData) => void`\n  Called with the form's FormData on submit.\n- `onProvider: (provider: SocialProvider) => void`\n  Called when a provider button is pressed.\n- `email: string`\n  `verify` only: the address shown in the description.\n- `codeLength: number` — default `6`\n  `verify` only: how many OTP boxes.\n- `onComplete / onResend: (code: string) => void / () => void`\n  `verify` only: fires when the last box is filled, and the resend action.\n- `media: ReactNode`\n  Artwork for the right half; switches to the split layout.\n- `logo: ReactNode`\n  Mark above the title.\n- `centered: boolean` — default `false`\n  Centre the mark and heading.\n- `confirmPassword: boolean` — default `false`\n  Sign-up only: two password fields instead of name and password.\n- `footnote: ReactNode`\n  Small print below the card; also replaces the in-card terms line on sign-up.\n- `title / description: ReactNode`\n  Override the copy for the current mode.\n- `switchHref: string` — default `\"#\"`\n  Target of the footer link to the other mode.\n- `headingLevel: 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6` — default `1`\n  Heading element used for the title.\n- `slides: AuthMediaSlide[]`\n  AuthMediaCarousel: images to cycle; `alt` makes a slide non-decorative.\n- `interval: number` — default `3200`\n  AuthMediaCarousel: ms a slide rests at full bleed.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst social = (\n  <div\n    className={cx(\n      layout === \"stacked\" && \"flex flex-col gap-2.5\",\n      // Wraps rather than scrolls: six providers on a narrow card should\n      // fall to a second row, not clip.\n      layout === \"inline\" && \"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-2\",\n      // Equal columns that share the full width, so three providers read as\n      // one row of controls rather than three loose squares.\n      layout === \"grid\" && \"grid grid-flow-col auto-cols-fr gap-2.5\",\n    )}\n  >\n    {providers.map((provider) => (\n      <SocialButton\n        key={provider}\n        brand={provider}\n        appearance=\"white\"\n        iconOnly={layout !== \"stacked\"}\n        fullWidth={layout === \"stacked\"}\n        // `iconOnly` fixes a square width; the grid needs it to stretch,\n        // and `className` is last into `cx` so it wins.\n        className={layout === \"grid\" ? \"w-full\" : undefined}\n        aria-label={layout !== \"stacked\" ? `Continue with ${SOCIAL_PROVIDERS[provider].label}` : undefined}\n        onClick={() => onProvider?.(provider)}\n      >\n        {`Continue with ${SOCIAL_PROVIDERS[provider].label}`}\n      </SocialButton>\n    ))}\n  </div>\n);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The carousel keeps exactly one pending `setTimeout`, cleared whenever the phase, slide count or interval changes and on unmount. The card adds no timers or listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The card itself has no motion beyond its controls' own hover and focus styles. `AuthMediaCarousel` checks `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` when its effect runs and, if set, never schedules a beat, so it holds on the first slide at full bleed. It also does nothing with fewer than two slides.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- `headingLevel` defaults to 1 because on a real sign-in screen the card is the page. Set it to 2 or lower wherever the card is embedded in a page that already has an `<h1>`.\n- The \"Remember me\" checkbox is internal state with no `name`, and `InputOtp` has no `name` either, so neither appears in the submitted `FormData`. Read the code from `onComplete`.\n- `confirmPassword` replaces the \"Full name\" field with a second password field rather than adding one, since the name input only renders for `signup && !confirmPassword`.\n- \"Forgot password?\" is hard-coded to `href=\"#\"` and `switchHref` defaults to `\"#\"`. Point them at real routes, or wrap the card and replace them.\n- The signup description mentions \"Acme\" and the copy is in English. Pass `title` and `description`, or provide a `TemplateCopyProvider`, for your own product.\n- `AuthMediaCarousel` is absolutely positioned (`absolute inset-0`) and keys slides by `src`. Give it a positioned parent with a height (the card's media cell already is one) and use distinct `src` values.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `auth-card` re-exports `AuthMediaCarousel` and `AuthMediaSlide`, so the carousel installs alongside the card even though the card never renders it itself.\n- The carousel uses a plain `<img>` (first slide eager, the rest lazy) so it does not impose `next/image` on the installing project. Slides are decorative (`alt=\"\"`, `aria-hidden`) unless you pass `alt`.\n- The carousel has no controls on purpose: next to a sign-in form, artwork is atmosphere, and arrows would invite visitors away from the form.\n- In dark mode both card variants switch to the secondary background.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nIn `signin` the card shows the title, email and password fields, \"Remember me\" (checked) and \"Forgot password?\", a full-width \"Sign in\" button, the divider and the providers, and a \"New here? Create an account\" footer. `inline` shows icon-only squares that wrap; `grid` stretches them across one row; `stacked` labels each one. Submitting calls `onSubmit` with the form's FormData. `verify` shows OTP boxes in two groups of three, no providers and a \"Send a new one\" button. With `media`, the card splits in two at `md` and above and shows only the form below it. The carousel rests on an image for about 3.2s, shrinks it into a rounded card, swings it left into depth while the next card comes in from the right, then grows the new one back to full bleed.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "auto-advance-filmstrip": "# Build: Auto-Advance Filmstrip\n\nPanels are a fixed number of container-width units wide and the whole track is one `translateX`. The active panel sits centred, its neighbours are dimmed and scaled down, and the strip steps every few seconds until you touch it. All motion is a CSS transition on a value React already recomputes when the index changes — there is no tween and no animation loop.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA centred strip that steps one panel every few seconds, neighbours dimmed and shrunk, on one CSS transition.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every panel is `W` container-width units wide (`width: 46cqw`) and the track is translated to `(100 - W) / 2 - W * index` of the same unit. The first term centres one panel — `(100 - 46) / 2 = 27` — and the second steps exactly one panel per index. It is one expression with no measurement and no resize handler, because both terms are already relative to the container.\n2. `cqw` rather than `vw`: the strip is sized against its own container, so it stays centred inside a card, a modal or a split layout instead of only when it happens to be full-bleed. The root sets `container-type: inline-size` to establish that container.\n3. The transition is `transform 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)` on the track. For a component where React already re-renders on index change, a CSS transition is fewer moving parts than a tween and it interpolates on the compositor.\n4. Inactive panels get `opacity: 0.35` **and** `scale(0.8)`, transitioning on the same curve and duration as the track so the three properties land together.\n5. Auto-advance is `setTimeout` re-armed on every index change, not `setInterval`. Manual navigation therefore restarts the dwell rather than inheriting whatever was left of the current tick.\n6. The timer is gated on four conditions — `autoPlay`, not reduced-motion, `document.visibilityState === \"visible\"`, and not currently hovered or focused within — so it never advances behind the user's back or in a background tab.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `panelWidth = 46`\n  Panel width in `cqw`. Under 50 means two neighbours are partly visible, which is what makes it a filmstrip rather than a slideshow. At 46 the centred panel leaves 27 units of peek on each side — enough to read the neighbour as a real panel, not a sliver.\n- `interval = 3000`\n  Milliseconds per panel. Below ~2500 a caption cannot be read before it leaves; above ~5000 the strip looks broken rather than paced on a page where nothing else is moving.\n- `duration = 500`\n  Slide transition. Roughly a sixth of the dwell, which keeps the strip reading as a series of held frames with a transition between them rather than as continuous motion.\n- `EASE = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  Expo-out. It covers about 80% of the distance in the first 30% of the duration, so the strip appears to arrive early and settle — the same reason it is the default curve for interface movement that should feel decided rather than mechanical.\n- `inactiveOpacity / inactiveScale = 0.35 / 0.8`\n  Both, together. 0.35 alone reads as a loading state; 0.8 alone reads as a layout bug. Applied together they read as depth, which is the only interpretation a viewer will land on.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AutoAdvanceFilmstrip(props: { children: ReactNode; panelWidth?: number; interval?: number; duration?: number; autoPlay?: boolean; inactiveOpacity?: number; inactiveScale?: number; className?: string; panelClassName?: string; 'aria-label'?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One panel per child.\n- `panelWidth: number` — default `46`\n  Panel width in container-width units (`cqw`).\n- `interval: number` — default `3000`\n  Milliseconds a panel is held before the strip advances.\n- `duration: number` — default `500`\n  Slide transition duration in milliseconds.\n- `autoPlay: boolean` — default `true`\n  Whether the strip advances on its own.\n- `inactiveOpacity: number` — default `0.35`\n  Opacity applied to every panel that is not active.\n- `inactiveScale: number` — default `0.8`\n  Scale applied to every panel that is not active.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root, which is also the query container.\n- `panelClassName: string`\n  Merged onto each panel cell.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Filmstrip'`\n  Label for the carousel group.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// (100 - W) / 2 centres a panel of width W; W * index steps one panel per\n// index. Both terms are in the same unit as the panel width, so the strip\n// stays centred at every container size with no measurement at all.\nconst offset = (100 - panelWidth) / 2 - panelWidth * index;\n\nconst running = autoPlay && !reduced && visible && !held && count > 1;\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!running) return;\n  // setTimeout keyed on `index`, not setInterval: any manual navigation\n  // re-arms a full dwell. With an interval, tapping \"next\" one tick before\n  // it fires advances twice in a row.\n  const id = window.setTimeout(() => {\n    setIndex((current) => wrapIndex(current + 1, count));\n  }, interval);\n  return () => window.clearTimeout(id);\n}, [running, index, interval, count]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The pending `setTimeout` is cleared by the effect's own teardown, which also runs on every index change and whenever the strip pauses. `useVisibility` and `useReducedMotion` unsubscribe their listeners. There is nothing else to release: all motion is CSS on nodes React owns.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Auto-advance never starts, so nothing moves unless the user presses a control. When they do, `transition` is set to `none` on both the track and the panels, so the strip cuts to the new panel in one frame instead of sliding. The dot pager and arrows behave identically; only the interpolation is gone.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using a percentage in the track's `translateX`. Percentages on `transform` resolve against the *element's own* width, and the track is as wide as all its panels — so `translateX(-46%)` moves it by 46% of the whole strip, not of one panel. The arithmetic only works in a unit that refers to the container: `cqw` here, `vw` if the strip is genuinely full-bleed.\n- Using `setInterval` for the auto-advance. Tap \"next\" a hundred milliseconds before the tick fires and the strip advances twice; the second jump has no cause the user can see. A `setTimeout` keyed on the index gives every panel a full dwell.\n- Leaving the timer running while the tab is hidden. The user comes back to a strip that has silently walked eleven panels forward, and every intervening transition ran on a throttled timer.\n- Not pausing on hover or focus. A pointer resting on a panel and a keyboard user tabbing into a control both mean \"I am reading this\"; advancing underneath either one is the single most common complaint about auto-advancing carousels.\n- Only setting `containerType` on an ancestor that has no definite inline size. `cqw` then resolves against the viewport-sized fallback and the arithmetic silently reverts to the `vw` behaviour it was meant to replace.\n- Fading inactive panels to `opacity: 0`. They are still in the layout and still hit-testable, so a click intended for the active panel can land on an invisible neighbour that happens to be under the cursor.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Cheapest of the three carousels here and the right default when the set is short and each panel needs a moment on screen. Unlike `wrapping-drum-carousel` it does not wrap continuously — it jumps from the last index back to the first, and that jump is a full-width slide, which is visible. Keep the set small enough that the loop point is rare.\n- Every panel stays mounted and in the layout at all times. For twenty panels that is twenty subtrees painting; virtualise or switch technique past roughly a dozen.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe strip steps one panel roughly every three seconds with the neighbours visible, dimmed and smaller on both sides. Hovering anywhere over it stops the advance and moving away resumes it with a full dwell. Switch tabs for ten seconds and come back: it is on the panel it was on, not ten further along. Resize the container from 320 px to full width and the active panel stays exactly centred.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "avatar": "# Build: Avatar\n\nA circular `<span>` that shows a photo when `src` is set and centred initials on a tinted disc otherwise. Four sizes match the places the dashboard uses avatars (breadcrumb marks, table rows, sidebar cards, people cards), and each size carries its own initials type style so the letters always fit the disc.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRound photo or initials avatar in four sizes (20 to 36px) and four tints, with a lazy-loaded image that fills the disc.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Keep every class in a `sortCx` map. `base` is `inline-flex shrink-0 select-none items-center justify-center overflow-hidden rounded-full text-center align-middle`: a flex-centred disc that clips its photo and never shrinks inside a row.\n2. Map `size` to a box and an initials style together: xs `size-5 text-[10px] leading-[15px] font-semibold`, sm `size-6 text-caption-1-semibold tracking-normal`, md `size-8 text-headline-semibold`, lg `size-9 text-[18px] leading-6 font-semibold`.\n3. Map `color` to a fill and text pair: `neutral` uses the semantic `bg-avatar-neutral-background` with `text-text-secondary`; `blue`, `lime` and `pink` use fixed palette pairs (`bg-blue-300 text-blue-900`, `bg-lime-200 text-lime-700`, `bg-pink-200 text-pink-500`).\n4. If `src` is set, render `<img className=\"size-full object-cover\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">` with `alt ?? \"\"`; otherwise render `initials` as the span's text. A photo wins over initials.\n5. Add `transition-[width,height,font-size] duration-200` to the base, so a list that switches density resizes its avatars smoothly instead of snapping.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `sizes = xs 20px, sm 24px, md 32px, lg 36px`\n  One per Figma context: breadcrumb workspace marks, table rows, sidebar workspace / team cards and people cards. Lg matches the kit's 36px controls so an avatar sits level with a button.\n- `initials type = 10/15, 12/16, 16/22, 18/24 px, all weight 600`\n  Roughly half the disc diameter. A single size-agnostic style either overflows the 20px disc or looks lost in the 36px one.\n- `neutral fill = light neutral-300, dark background-primary-default`\n  The neutral tint is the only theme-aware one, because it is the default and appears on every surface; the coloured tints are fixed Figma pairs.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ninterface AvatarProps extends HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement> { size?: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; color?: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\"; src?: string; alt?: string; initials?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement> }\nfunction Avatar(props: AvatarProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `size: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  20, 24, 32 or 36px disc, each with its own initials type style.\n- `color: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\"` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  Fill and initials colour when no photo is shown.\n- `src: string`\n  Photo URL. Rendered as a lazy, async-decoded `<img>` that covers the disc. Wins over `initials`.\n- `alt: string` — default `\"\"`\n  Alt text for the photo.\n- `initials: string`\n  Fallback text shown when `src` is not set, e.g. \"M\".\n- `className: string`\n  Merged last through `tailwind-merge`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  base: \"inline-flex shrink-0 select-none items-center justify-center overflow-hidden rounded-full text-center align-middle transition-[width,height,font-size] duration-200 ease\",\n  size: {\n    xs: \"size-5 text-[10px] leading-[15px] font-semibold\",\n    sm: \"size-6 text-caption-1-semibold tracking-normal\",\n    md: \"size-8 text-headline-semibold\",\n    lg: \"size-9 text-[18px] leading-6 font-semibold\",\n  },\n  color: {\n    neutral: \"bg-avatar-neutral-background text-text-secondary\",\n    blue: \"bg-blue-300 text-blue-900\",\n    lime: \"bg-lime-200 text-lime-700\",\n    pink: \"bg-pink-200 text-pink-500\",\n  },\n});\n\nexport function Avatar({\n  size = \"md\",\n  color = \"neutral\",\n  src,\n  alt,\n  initials,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: AvatarProps) {\n  return (\n    <span\n      ref={ref}\n      className={cx(styles.base, styles.size[size], styles.color[color], className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      {src ? (\n        <img\n          src={src}\n          alt={alt ?? \"\"}\n          loading=\"lazy\"\n          decoding=\"async\"\n          className=\"size-full object-cover\"\n        />\n      ) : (\n        initials\n      )}\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component itself only has a 200ms size transition when its `size` prop changes. It is not gated on `prefers-reduced-motion`; any hover fan-out of a group belongs to the caller.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not drop `overflow-hidden` on the disc. Without it a non-square photo pokes out of the circle.\n- Do not remove `shrink-0`. In a table cell or a flex row with long text, the avatar squeezes into an oval.\n- Do not leave `alt` unset on a photo of a named person when no name sits next to it. The component falls back to `alt=\"\"`, which marks the image decorative.\n- Do not pass three or more letters as `initials`. The xs and sm discs only fit one or two characters.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Overlapping groups are composed outside the component: wrap each avatar in a `rounded-full ring-2 ring-background-primary-default` element and pull it left with a negative margin, so the ring cuts a gap against the previous avatar.\n- The default is `size=\"md\"` and `color=\"neutral\"`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWithout `src`, `<Avatar size=\"lg\" color=\"blue\" initials=\"M\" />` renders a 36px blue-300 disc with an 18px semibold blue-900 \"M\" centred in it. The four sizes measure 20, 24, 32 and 36px and stay circular inside a flex row. With `src`, the photo fills and is clipped to the circle, and initials are not shown.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "avatar-stack": "# Build: Avatar Stack\n\nThe assignee cluster from the project board cards: kit avatars overlap by a quarter of their width, each wearing a 2px ring in the surface colour so the stack reads as cut-outs. Past `max`, the remaining people collapse into a \"+N\" disc whose tooltip lists them. Every disc is a tab stop with a name tooltip, and the group announces everyone in its label.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOverlapping assignee discs with surface-coloured rings, a +N overflow disc and a name tooltip on every avatar.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. People render left to right as kit `Avatar`s with negative inline-start margins (`-ms-1.5` for xs/sm, `-ms-2` for md, `-ms-2.5` for lg) on every disc after the first. The root is `isolate`, so the hover/focus `z-10` lift stays inside the stack instead of leaking over neighbouring UI.\n2. The ring is `ring-2` plus `ringClassName` (default `ring-background-primary-default`). Matching it to the surface underneath is what makes the overlap look like a cut-out rather than a border; pass the column colour when the stack sits on a non-white surface.\n3. Overflow rule: with `limit = max(1, max)`, the stack only collapses when `people.length > limit + 1`. Replacing the last avatar with \"+1\" saves no space and hides a face, so four people at `max = 3` show all four, while five show three discs plus \"+2\".\n4. Each disc is wrapped as `TooltipTrigger` (150ms delay) → RAC `Focusable` → `Avatar` with `role=\"img\"` and `aria-label` set to the name. `Focusable` makes the disc keyboard reachable and wires hover/focus to the kit `Tooltip` (placement top, name plus optional `description` line). `tooltips={false}` drops the triggers, so discs stop being tab stops.\n5. The \"+N\" disc is a `button` when `onOverflowPress` is set (e.g. to expand the list or open a member popover) and a `span role=\"img\"` otherwise. Both carry `aria-label=\"N more: names\"`; its tooltip lists the hidden names one per line.\n6. Without an explicit `color`, a person's initial tint comes from a djb2 hash of the name over blue, lime, pink and neutral (`tintOf`), so the same person keeps the same colour on every card. `initialsOf` takes the first letters of the first two words.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `overlap = xs/sm 6px, md 8px, lg 10px`\n  About a quarter of the disc width, matched by eye to the board card's 20–24px avatars. More overlap hides initials; less stops reading as one group.\n- `ring = 2px, surface colour`\n  Wide enough to separate two discs of the same tint, thin enough that the initials stay centred in the visible area.\n- `collapse threshold = people.length > max + 1`\n  Never shows \"+1\": a counter that hides one person takes the same room as that person's avatar.\n- `tooltip delay = 150ms`\n  Fast enough to scrub across the stack and read names, slow enough not to flash when the pointer crosses a card.\n- `hover lift = -translate-y-0.5 over 150ms ease-out`\n  A 2px nudge plus `z-10` brings the hovered face above its neighbour so its full disc is visible while its tooltip shows.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AvatarStack(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"children\"> & { people: readonly AvatarStackPerson[]; max?: number; size?: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; tooltips?: boolean; ringClassName?: string; label?: string; onOverflowPress?: () => void; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\ninterface AvatarStackPerson { id?: string; name: string; initials?: string; color?: AvatarProps[\"color\"]; src?: string; description?: string }\nfunction initialsOf(name: string): string\nfunction tintOf(name: string): AvatarProps[\"color\"]\n```\n\n- `people: readonly AvatarStackPerson[]`\n  Who to show, in order. `id` is the key (falls back to `name`).\n- `max: number` — default `3`\n  Discs shown before the rest collapse into \"+N\". Collapses only when two or more would hide.\n- `size: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"sm\"`\n  Kit avatar size; overlap and the \"+N\" disc scale with it.\n- `tooltips: boolean` — default `true`\n  Name tooltips on hover and focus. Off removes the tab stops too.\n- `ringClassName: string` — default `\"ring-background-primary-default\"`\n  Ring colour; set it to the surface the stack sits on.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Assignees\"`\n  Prefix of the group's accessible name, followed by every name.\n- `onOverflowPress: () => void`\n  Turns the \"+N\" disc into a button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AvatarStack({\n  people,\n  max = 3,\n  size = \"sm\",\n  tooltips = true,\n  ringClassName = \"ring-background-primary-default\",\n  label = \"Assignees\",\n  onOverflowPress,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: AvatarStackProps) {\n  // Showing \"+1\" in place of the last avatar saves no room, so only collapse\n  // when at least two people would hide.\n  const limit = Math.max(1, max);\n  const visibleCount = people.length > limit + 1 ? limit : people.length;\n  const shown = people.slice(0, visibleCount);\n  const hidden = people.slice(visibleCount);\n  const names = people.map((p) => p.name);\n\n  const discClass = (index: number) => cx(styles.disc, ringClassName, index > 0 && styles.overlap[size]);\n\n  const overflowDisc = hidden.length > 0 && (\n    <OverflowDisc\n      count={hidden.length}\n      names={hidden.map((p) => p.name)}\n      className={cx(\n        discClass(shown.length),\n        styles.overflow[size],\n        \"inline-flex shrink-0 select-none items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-background-tertiary-default text-text-secondary\",\n      )}\n      tooltips={tooltips}\n      onPress={onOverflowPress}\n    />\n  );\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={ref}\n      role=\"group\"\n      aria-label={people.length ? `${label}: ${names.join(\", \")}` : `${label}: none`}\n      className={cx(\"inline-flex shrink-0 items-center isolate\", className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      {shown.map((person, i) => {\n        const disc = (\n          <Avatar\n            key={person.id ?? person.name}\n            size={size}\n            color={person.color ?? tintOf(person.name)}\n            src={person.src}\n            alt=\"\"\n            initials={person.initials ?? initialsOf(person.name)}\n            role=\"img\"\n            aria-label={person.name}\n            className={discClass(i)}\n          />\n        );\n        if (!tooltips) return disc;\n        return (\n          <TooltipTrigger key={person.id ?? person.name} delay={150}>\n            <Focusable>{disc}</Focusable>\n            <Tooltip placement=\"top\">\n              <span className=\"block\">{person.name}</span>\n              {person.description && <span className=\"block text-text-secondary\">{person.description}</span>}\n            </Tooltip>\n          </TooltipTrigger>\n        );\n      })}\n      {overflowDisc}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No effects, timers or listeners of its own. Tooltip open/close timers belong to the kit Tooltip (React Aria) and are released when a trigger unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The 2px hover lift and its transform transition are removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none` and `motion-reduce:hover:translate-y-0`; the z-index raise and tooltips still work.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving the ring on its white default when the stack sits on a grey column or a dark tinted surface: the rings then read as white outlines. Pass `ringClassName` for that surface.\n- Dropping `isolate` from the root. The raised `z-10` disc can then paint over sticky headers or popovers that share the stacking context.\n- Using array indexes as keys. People join and leave cards; keys come from `id` and fall back to `name`.\n- Hiding names behind colour only. Each disc keeps `role=\"img\"` and its name as `aria-label`, and the group label lists everyone, so screen readers do not depend on the tooltip.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Kanban Card uses this for the top-right assignee cluster at `size=\"xs\"` with `max={3}`.\n- Photos work through `src`, but the demo uses initials only so it ships without remote images.\n- Below 640px the demo shows only the ticket and its assignee list so it fits a phone screen; the Sizes panel joins it from `sm` up.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFive assignees at max 3 render three overlapping discs plus \"+2\"; four render all four with no counter. Hovering or tabbing to a disc raises it and shows a tooltip with the name and role. In the demo, unchecking teammates shrinks the stack and updates \"N assigned\"; pressing \"+N\" expands the stack to show everyone and \"Collapse\" folds it back. Rings match the card in both light and dark themes.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "badge": "# Build: Badge\n\nA `<span>` with two colour tones and one fixed shape, 12px semibold type in a 4px-radius pill. It exists so every count in the kit (sidebar items, tab counts, unread totals) has the same metrics. The tone tracks the selection state of whatever holds it, not the meaning of the number.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe small numeric counter on nav items and unread totals: a 16px pill in a selected (blue) or resting (neutral) tone.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The shape is one class string: `inline-flex` centred content, `px-1 py-px`, `rounded-sm` and the kit's `text-caption-1-semibold` type style (12px over a 16px line). With one pixel of vertical padding the pill is 18px tall, short enough to sit inside a 32px nav row without pushing its height.\n2. `whitespace-nowrap` keeps \"1,204\" or \"99+\" on one line. Combined with `inline-flex` the pill grows with its content, and there is no fixed width that a three-digit count could overflow.\n3. `primary` uses `bg-accent-400` with white text and is meant for a badge inside the selected nav item, where the row itself is tinted. `neutral` uses the `badge-neutral-background` token with secondary text for every other row. The neutral token is redefined in `.dark`, so the same class reads correctly on both themes.\n4. All other attributes spread onto the `<span>`, so `aria-label=\"12 unread\"` or a `title` pass straight through. `className` merges through `tailwind-merge`, so `rounded-full` replaces `rounded-sm` instead of both applying.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `padding = px-1 py-px (4px / 1px)`\n  Figma's counter spec. Horizontal padding of 4px keeps a single digit almost square. Larger vertical padding makes the badge taller than the label text it sits beside, and the nav row starts to look uneven.\n- `type = text-caption-1-semibold, tracking-normal`\n  The smallest tier in the kit's type scale. `tracking-normal` overrides the negative tracking the scale applies at larger sizes, because tight tracking at 12px makes adjacent digits such as 11 merge into one shape.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Badge(props: HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement> & { color?: \"primary\" | \"neutral\"; ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `color: \"primary\" | \"neutral\"` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  `primary` (blue fill, white text) inside a selected row; `neutral` everywhere else.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The count or short label. Keep it to a few characters.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge`, so conflicting utilities replace the defaults.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  base: \"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-sm px-1 py-px text-caption-1-semibold tracking-normal whitespace-nowrap\",\n  color: {\n    primary: \"bg-accent-400 text-white\",\n    neutral: \"bg-badge-neutral-background text-text-secondary\",\n  },\n});\n\nexport function Badge({ color = \"neutral\", className, ref, ...props }: BadgeProps) {\n  return (\n    <span\n      ref={ref}\n      className={cx(styles.base, styles.color[color], className)}\n      {...props}\n    />\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component renders a styled span with no state, effects or listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates. The badge is static, so there is no motion to reduce.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Choosing the tone by meaning (\"red for errors\"). Badge has no status palette on purpose: its tone follows selection. For status use `Chip` or `StatusDot`, which do carry status colours.\n- Using a bare number as the only label. A screen reader hears \"12\" with no context. Pass `aria-label` (\"12 unread messages\"), or make sure the surrounding link text explains the count.\n- Giving the pill a fixed `w-*` so counts line up. Three digits then clip or wrap. Align the column with the parent's layout instead, for example `ms-auto` in a flex row.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The sidebar, tabs and notification centre all import this file, so changing its metrics changes every count in the kit at once. That shared source is the reason it is its own item.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA neutral badge reads clearly on the page background in both light and dark themes, and a primary badge is legible white-on-blue inside a selected nav row. \"7\", \"48\" and \"1,204\" all sit on one line with even side padding, and the pill is no taller than the 16px label line beside it. A consumer `rounded-full` turns it into a full pill without leaving `rounded-sm` in the computed styles.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "banded-scrollspy-rail": "# Build: Banded Scrollspy Rail\n\nA section rail that never flickers, because only one section can be \"active\" at a time by construction. The IntersectionObserver root is shrunk to a 7%-tall band across the middle of the scroller, so a section registers only while it crosses that line. The active tick grows from 16 to 32 pixels — a size change, legible in peripheral vision, rather than a tint change that is not. No animation library: one observer, one CSS width transition.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA tick rail whose active mark widens as a section crosses a 7% band through the middle of the screen.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One IntersectionObserver watches every `[data-scrollspy]` element with `rootMargin: \"-38% 0px -55% 0px\"`. Trimming 38% off the top and 55% off the bottom leaves a 7%-tall detection band (100 − 38 − 55), positioned just below centre so a section becomes active while you are reading it rather than while it is still arriving.\n2. Entries maintain a `Set` of ids currently inside the band. The active id is resolved by scanning the authored `sections` order for the first member of that set — not by reading `entries[0]`, whose order depends on how the observer batched the callback and therefore on scroll direction.\n3. When the set is empty — a gap between sections, or a header taller than the band — the previous answer is held rather than cleared, so the rail never blanks between sections.\n4. A second observer watches the element named by `endId` with `rootMargin: \"0px 0px -25% 0px\"`, which makes \"intersecting\" mean exactly \"its top has climbed above 75% of the root\". That retires the whole rail, so it is not hovering over a footer with nothing left to navigate.\n5. The indicator itself is a `<span>` whose inline `width` flips between 16 and 32 px with a 260 ms CSS transition. The only JavaScript per scroll event is a `Set` mutation and at most one `setState`.\n6. Section elements are collected into an id → element `Map` by walking the attribute once, rather than running a `[data-scrollspy=\"id\"]` selector per section — that form needs escaping for any id containing punctuation and silently matches nothing when the escaping is wrong.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `bandTop / bandBottom = 38 / 55`\n  Leaves a 7% band. Widen it and two sections are inside at once on a tall screen, which is the flicker this design exists to remove. Narrow it below about 4% and a fast flick can jump the band entirely between two observer callbacks, leaving the rail on the previous section. The asymmetry (38 vs 55) puts the band below centre, where the eye is while reading.\n- `ACTIVE_WIDTH / IDLE_WIDTH = 32 / 16`\n  A 2x length change is detectable without looking directly at it. Equal widths with only a colour change is invisible in peripheral vision, which defeats the point of a rail you are not supposed to be looking at.\n- `TRANSITION_MS = 260`\n  Long enough that the width change reads as movement and draws the eye, short enough that it has finished before the next section can reach the band during normal scrolling.\n- `hideAt = 0.75`\n  Fraction of the root the end marker must pass before the rail retires. At 1.0 the rail is still on screen while the footer is being read; below about 0.6 it disappears while the last section is still active.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BandedScrollspyRail(props: { sections: { id: string; label: string }[]; rootRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; bandTop?: number; bandBottom?: number; endId?: string; hideAt?: number; attribute?: string; onActiveChange?: (id: string | null) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `sections: { id: string; label: string }[]`\n  Ordered list. Each `id` must match a `data-scrollspy` attribute inside the root.\n- `rootRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container holding the sections. Omit for the document.\n- `bandTop: number` — default `38`\n  Percent of the root trimmed from the top to form the band.\n- `bandBottom: number` — default `55`\n  Percent trimmed from the bottom. The two must sum to under 100.\n- `endId: string`\n  Section after which the rail retires. Usually the footer.\n- `hideAt: number` — default `0.75`\n  Fraction down the root the end marker must pass before the rail hides.\n- `attribute: string` — default `'data-scrollspy'`\n  Attribute used to find sections inside the root.\n- `onActiveChange: (id: string | null) => void`\n  Fires once per active-section change. Held in a ref, so it needs no memo.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the nav element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst ACTIVE_WIDTH = 32;\nconst IDLE_WIDTH = 16;\nconst TRANSITION_MS = 260;\n\nexport function BandedScrollspyRail({\n  sections,\n  rootRef,\n  bandTop = 38,\n  bandBottom = 55,\n  endId,\n  hideAt = 0.75,\n  attribute = 'data-scrollspy',\n  onActiveChange,\n  className,\n}: BandedScrollspyRailProps) {\n  const [active, setActive] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [retired, setRetired] = useState(false);\n  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Held in refs so a new array literal or a fresh callback on every parent\n  // render does not tear down and rebuild the observers.\n  const sectionsRef = useRef(sections);\n  sectionsRef.current = sections;\n  const notify = useRef(onActiveChange);\n  notify.current = onActiveChange;\n\n  const order = sections.map((s) => s.id).join('|');\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const root = rootRef?.current ?? null;\n    const scope: ParentNode = root ?? document;\n\n    // Build id -> element by walking the attribute once, rather than running a\n    // `[attr=\"id\"]` selector per section: that needs escaping for any id with\n    // punctuation in it, and silently matches nothing when the escaping is\n    // wrong.\n    const elements = new Map<string, Element>();\n    for (const el of Array.from(scope.querySelectorAll(`[${attribute}]`))) {\n      const id = el.getAttribute(attribute);\n      if (id) elements.set(id, el);\n    }\n    if (elements.size === 0) return;\n\n    const inBand = new Set<string>();\n\n    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(\n      (entries) => {\n        for (const entry of entries) {\n          const id = entry.target.getAttribute(attribute);\n          if (!id) continue;\n          if (entry.isIntersecting) inBand.add(id);\n          else inBand.delete(id);\n        }\n\n        // Resolve in authored order, not entry order: callbacks arrive in\n        // whatever order the observer batched them, so picking `entries[0]`\n        // makes the active section depend on scroll direction.\n        const next = sectionsRef.current.find((s) => inBand.has(s.id))?.id;\n\n        // Nothing in the band — a gap between sections, or a header taller\n        // than the band. Hold the last answer instead of blanking the rail,\n        // which would read as the nav breaking.\n        if (next === undefined) return;\n\n        setActive((prev) => {\n          if (prev === next) return prev;\n          notify.current?.(next);\n          return next;\n        });\n      },\n      {\n        root,\n        // The band. 100 - bandTop - bandBottom percent of the root's height,\n        // centred low enough that a section is \"active\" while you are reading\n        // it rather than while it is still arriving.\n        rootMargin: `-${bandTop}% 0px -${bandBottom}% 0px`,\n        threshold: 0,\n      },\n    );\n\n    for (const el of elements.values()) observer.observe(el);\n\n    // Second observer: retire the rail once the end marker has climbed past\n    // `hideAt`, so it is not hovering over a footer with nothing left to\n    // navigate. Shrinking the bottom margin by (1 - hideAt) makes\n    // \"intersecting\" mean exactly \"its top is above hideAt of the root\".\n    let endObserver: IntersectionObserver | null = null;\n    const endEl = endId === undefined ? undefined : elements.get(endId);\n    if (endEl) {\n      endObserver = new IntersectionObserver(\n        (entries) => {\n          const entry = entries[entries.length - 1];\n          if (entry) setRetired(entry.isIntersecting);\n        },\n        { root, rootMargin: `0px 0px -${Math.round((1 - hideAt) * 100)}% 0px`, threshold: 0 },\n      );\n      endObserver.observe(endEl);\n    }\n\n    return () => {\n      observer.disconnect();\n      endObserver?.disconnect();\n    };\n  }, [rootRef, attribute, bandTop, bandBottom, endId, hideAt, order]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Both IntersectionObservers are disconnected on unmount and on any option change. An observer holds a strong reference to every element it watches, so skipping this pins the unmounted section tree in memory and keeps firing `setState` on a dead component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rail still tracks the active section and still changes width — that is state, not decoration. Every `transition` is set to `none`, so the tick snaps between widths and the labels appear instantly, and click-to-navigate uses `behavior: \"auto\"` rather than a smooth scroll.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Observing with the default full-height root. Three sections intersect at once on a tall screen and the active tick flickers between them on every callback. Debouncing hides the symptom and adds lag; shrinking the root removes the ambiguity.\n- Taking the active section from `entries[0]`. The callback batches entries in an order that depends on which boundaries were crossed, so the same scroll direction can yield a different winner between two frames. Resolve against the authored order instead.\n- Clearing the active state when nothing is intersecting. Any gap taller than the band — a spacer, a full-bleed image, a header — blanks the rail mid-scroll and it reads as broken.\n- Signalling the active section with colour only. On a hairline tick in the corner of the eye a tint change is imperceptible; a width change is not, and it also survives a monochrome or high-contrast rendering.\n- Rebuilding the observers on every render. `sections` is usually a fresh array literal from the parent, so keying the effect on it disconnects and reconnects two observers on every keystroke elsewhere in the page. Key on the joined id list and read the array from a ref.\n- Forgetting `observer.disconnect()`. IntersectionObserver keeps a strong reference to every observed element, so an undisconnected observer pins the whole unmounted section tree in memory and keeps calling `setState` on a dead component.\n- Scrolling with `window.scrollTo` on click when the sections live in a container. `scrollIntoView` walks up and scrolls whichever ancestor actually holds the overflow, which is the only version that works in both cases.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No GSAP and no ScrollTrigger: the whole component is two IntersectionObservers and a CSS transition, which is why it is classed `css` cost and can be used dozens of times on a page.\n- The labels are always in the DOM at `opacity: 0` and reveal on hover or keyboard focus, so the rail is operable and readable without a pointer.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll slowly: the active tick changes exactly once as each section crosses the middle of the screen, with no flicker at the boundary and no blank state in the gaps. The active tick is visibly twice as long as the others. Scroll to the end and the whole rail fades out. Hover it and the labels appear; tab into it and they appear too. With reduced motion on, the tick still moves but snaps rather than eases.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "bar-list-card": "# Build: Bar List Card\n\nAn analytics breakdown card: each row is a label and icon sitting on a pale bar whose width is the item against the largest one, with the metric on the right. Underline tabs switch between lists, and the caption doubles as a switch between shares of the list total and raw counts. Lists longer than `limit` end in a faded peek row with a small chevron pill that unfolds the rest. Hovering a row brings in the other number.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRanked analytics rows on proportional bars behind underline tabs, with a share / count switch and a pill that unfolds long lists.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each list is filtered to finite values of 0 or more, then sorted high to low. Every item gets `share = value / total` and `width = value / max`, so the top row always spans the full bar and hidden rows never change the visible ones.\n2. A bar is an absolutely positioned span behind the row content, `width: width × 100%` with an 8px minimum so a tiny item still shows a rounded nub. `useGrown` holds the width at 0 for two animation frames after a list mounts, then a 500ms ease-out width transition grows every bar to its size.\n3. The kit `Tabs` render one `TabPanel` per list. Only the selected panel is mounted, so switching tabs mounts the new list and its bars grow in. The tab strip sits in a sideways scroller with a 24px fade on the right, and `-mb-px` lays the blue underline over the header divider.\n4. Rows past `limit` live in a second list inside a clipping box. Collapsed, the box is 30px tall: a 4px gap plus 26px of the first hidden row, masked from opaque to transparent, and `inert` so the hidden rows are neither focusable nor announced.\n5. The chevron pill toggles the box between that peek and `hidden rows × 40px + 26px`, over a 300ms height transition. The extra 26px leaves a band for the pill under the last row, and the chevron turns 180°. Only one list is open at a time, and switching tabs folds it again.\n6. The caption button flips the metric between `share` and `value`; its % / # marker shows which is live. Each row's number is keyed by the metric, so it replays the kit's `animate-number-fade` on every switch. On row hover the other number (count or share) fades in beside it in tertiary text.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `row = 36px tall (h-9), 4px gap, 8px radius`\n  Gives the 40px pitch of the preview and lets the unfold height be computed exactly instead of measured.\n- `PEEK = 30px`\n  Shows enough of the next row (its icon, label and number) to read as \"there is more\" under the fade, while the pill sits on top of it.\n- `PILL_SPACE = 26px`\n  Room under the last row for the 18px pill when the list is open, so the pill never covers a number.\n- `BAR_MIN = 8px`\n  One radius wide: a 1% item still shows a rounded nub instead of a hairline.\n- `grow = 500ms ease-out width, after two animation frames`\n  Two frames guarantee the zero width is painted before the transition starts, even when a click mounts the panel synchronously.\n- `unfold = 300ms ease-out height`\n  Fast enough to follow the click, long enough to show the list growing rather than jumping.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BarListCard(props: { tabs?: BarListTab[]; items?: BarListItem[]; title?: string; tab?: string; defaultTab?: string; onTabChange?: (id: string) => void; metricLabel?: string; metric?: \"share\" | \"value\"; defaultMetric?: \"share\" | \"value\"; onMetricChange?: (metric: \"share\" | \"value\") => void; metricToggle?: boolean; limit?: number; mono?: boolean; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype BarListItem = { label: string; value: number; icon?: ReactNode }\ntype BarListTab = { id: string; label: string; items: BarListItem[] }\n```\n\n- `tabs: BarListTab[]` — default `TRAFFIC_TABS`\n  Lists behind underline tabs. Ignored when `items` is set.\n- `items: BarListItem[]`\n  One list without tabs, headed by `title`.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Breakdown\" (tabs) or `metricLabel` (items)`\n  Header of a single list; the card's accessible name in both modes.\n- `tab: string`\n  Controlled tab id.\n- `defaultTab: string` — default `first tab id`\n  Initial tab when uncontrolled.\n- `onTabChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when a tab is selected.\n- `metricLabel: string` — default `\"Visitors\"`\n  Uppercase caption over the numbers.\n- `metric: \"share\" | \"value\"`\n  Controlled metric: share of the list total, or the raw value.\n- `defaultMetric: \"share\" | \"value\"` — default `\"share\"`\n  Initial metric when uncontrolled.\n- `onMetricChange: (metric) => void`\n  Called when the caption switch flips the metric.\n- `metricToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Turns the caption into a share / count switch with a % / # marker.\n- `limit: number` — default `5`\n  Rows shown before the rest fold behind the peek row and pill.\n- `mono: boolean` — default `false`\n  Grey chart-track bars instead of pale blue.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `en-US integer`\n  Formats counts in the rows and the hover detail.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction BarRows({\n  name,\n  items,\n  metric,\n  metricLabel,\n  limit,\n  mono,\n  expanded,\n  onExpandedChange,\n  formatValue,\n}: RowsProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const grown = useGrown(reduced);\n  const moreId = useId();\n  const ranked = useMemo(() => rank(items), [items]);\n\n  const cap = Math.max(1, Math.floor(limit));\n  const shown = ranked.slice(0, cap);\n  const rest = ranked.slice(cap);\n  // Shares are of the tab's total and widths of its largest item, so hidden\n  // rows never change the visible bars when they expand.\n  const openHeight = rest.length * ROW_PITCH + PILL_SPACE;\n  const row = (item: RankedItem) => (\n    <BarRow\n      key={item.label}\n      item={item}\n      metric={metric}\n      metricLabel={metricLabel}\n      mono={mono}\n      grown={grown}\n      formatValue={formatValue}\n    />\n  );\n\n  if (ranked.length === 0) {\n    return <p className=\"px-2.5 py-2 text-body-regular text-text-tertiary\">No data yet</p>;\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex flex-col\">\n      <ul aria-label={`${name} by ${metricLabel.toLowerCase()}`} className=\"flex flex-col gap-1\">\n        {shown.map(row)}\n      </ul>\n      {rest.length > 0 && (\n        <div className=\"relative\">\n          <div\n            id={moreId}\n            inert={!expanded}\n            data-expanded={expanded || undefined}\n            className=\"overflow-hidden transition-[height] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n            style={{\n              height: expanded ? openHeight : PEEK,\n              maskImage: expanded ? undefined : 'linear-gradient(to bottom, black, transparent)',\n              WebkitMaskImage: expanded ? undefined : 'linear-gradient(to bottom, black, transparent)',\n            }}\n          >\n            <ul aria-label={`More ${name.toLowerCase()}`} className=\"flex flex-col gap-1 pt-1\">\n              {rest.map(row)}\n            </ul>\n          </div>\n          <button\n            type=\"button\"\n            aria-expanded={expanded}\n            aria-controls={moreId}\n            aria-label={expanded ? 'Show fewer' : `Show ${rest.length} more`}\n            onClick={() => onExpandedChange(!expanded)}\n            className=\"absolute bottom-1 left-1/2 flex h-4.5 w-9 -translate-x-1/2 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default text-foreground-icon-primary shadow-xs outline-none transition-colors duration-150 ease-out after:absolute after:-inset-x-2 after:-inset-y-3 hover:bg-background-secondary-default focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\"\n          >\n            <RiArrowDownSLine\n              aria-hidden\n              className={cx(\n                'size-3.5 transition-transform duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n                expanded && 'rotate-180',\n              )}\n            />\n          </button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGrown` cancels both of its animation frames on unmount, so switching tabs mid-grow leaves nothing running. Everything else is React state and CSS transitions; the kit `TabList` disconnects its own observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useGrown` is skipped, so the bars render at their final widths. The width, height, chevron, colour and fade transitions are removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and the number fade with `motion-reduce:animate-none`. The list unfolds and folds at once.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Scaling bar widths to the list total. The top item of a flat list then stops halfway across, and every bar looks short. Widths are against the largest item; shares are against the total.\n- Hiding folded rows with `display: none` or opacity alone. Neither gives the peek, and opacity leaves the rows focusable. A clipping box with a mask, plus `inert`, does both.\n- Putting the tab underline inside a scroll container without the strip. The underline would stay put while the tabs scroll. The whole `TabList` (with its underline) scrolls together inside one wrapper.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `TRAFFIC_TABS`, `BarListItem`, `BarListTab` and `BarListMetric` are exported. With neither `tabs` nor `items`, the card shows the traffic sample.\n- `icon` takes any 16px node. The sample countries use small text code chips rather than flag images, so the card works offline.\n- Pass `metricToggle={false}` for a plain caption, or control `metric` from outside.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe left card shows Devices, Browsers, Operating systems and Countries tabs and a \"VISITORS % #\" caption. Under Devices are Desktop 61%, Mobile 31% and Tablet 8%, with blue bars at full, about half and about an eighth of the width. Browsers shows four rows, then a faded Opera row under a chevron pill. Clicking the pill unfolds Opera, Samsung Internet and Other, and the chevron turns up. Back on Devices, clicking the caption changes Desktop to 3,120. Hovering a row shows the other number. The right card lists Pages in views: / 12,480, /pricing 6,210, /components 4,880, then /blog/launch peeking. Mono turns every bar grey.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "batched-blur-reveal": "# Build: Batched Blur Reveal\n\nA page-wide entrance that reveals elements in groups rather than one at a time. Everything that crosses the start line inside the same 100 ms window is handed to one tween, so a real stagger runs across a row of cards that entered simultaneously. Opacity, rise, scale and blur move together, which reads as the content focusing rather than sliding.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCards that enter together focus in as one staggered group instead of arriving as a slab.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. On mount, every `[data-reveal]` descendant of the container — queried from the container element, never from `document` — is set to `{ opacity: 0, y: 90, scale: 0.94, filter: \"blur(12px)\" }` plus a `will-change` hint for transform, filter and opacity.\n2. `ScrollTrigger.batch(targets, { start: \"top 88%\", interval: 0.1, batchMax: 6 })` replaces the usual one-trigger-per-element loop. Elements that cross the start line within `interval` seconds of each other are collected and delivered to `onEnter` as a single array.\n3. That array is animated by one `gsap.to` with `stagger: 0.14`, so the offset is computed across the whole group. Individual triggers cannot do this: each one starts at its own t=0 and knows nothing about its neighbours.\n4. The tween carries `overwrite: true` so a fast flick that re-enters an element mid-reveal restarts it cleanly instead of interpolating from a half-finished state.\n5. `onComplete` calls `gsap.set(batch, { clearProps: \"filter,willChange\" })`. A `filter` that survives the animation keeps the node on its own composited layer for the life of the page, which is exactly the cost the reveal was supposed to pay once.\n6. Under `prefers-reduced-motion`, no trigger is created at all: the targets are `gsap.set` to their final values and the content is already correct on first paint.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `start = 'top 88%'`\n  The element begins revealing when its top reaches 88% down the scroller — a sliver above the fold. At `top bottom` the reveal happens off-screen and the user sees nothing; below about 80% the element is already well into view before it starts, which reads as a late load.\n- `BATCH_INTERVAL = 0.1`\n  Seconds of grace before a batch is flushed. Too small and a row of three cards splits into three batches with no stagger between them — the exact failure this component exists to avoid. Too large and the first element visibly waits before anything moves.\n- `batchMax = 6`\n  Cap on group size. With `stagger: 0.14`, a batch of 20 puts 2.7 s between the first and last element, so the tail is still blurred long after the user has read the head. Six keeps the whole group inside roughly a second.\n- `stagger = 0.14`\n  Offset within a batch. Below ~0.08 the group reads as simultaneous and the batching is invisible; above ~0.2 it reads as a queue rather than a wave.\n- `blur = 12`\n  Starting blur in pixels. This is the expensive property — it forces a full repaint of the layer every frame — and it is what turns a slide into a focus-pull. Past ~20px the text is unreadable for long enough that the reveal feels broken.\n- `distance = 90`\n  Pixels of rise. Paired with `scale: 0.94` it reads as depth. Much more and the element travels further than its own height, which looks like a layout jump rather than an entrance.\n- `duration = 1.1`\n  Long for an entrance, and deliberately so: the blur needs time to resolve or the focus-pull reads as a flicker.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BatchedBlurReveal(props: { children: ReactNode; selector?: string; start?: string; distance?: number; blur?: number; scale?: number; duration?: number; stagger?: number; once?: boolean; batchMax?: number; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Section content. Anything matching `selector` inside it is revealed.\n- `selector: string` — default `'[data-reveal]'`\n  Which descendants reveal. Queried against the container, not the document.\n- `start: string` — default `'top 88%'`\n  ScrollTrigger start position for every batched element.\n- `distance: number` — default `90`\n  Pixels the element rises through.\n- `blur: number` — default `12`\n  Starting blur radius. Set 0 to drop the focus-pull and keep the rise.\n- `scale: number` — default `0.94`\n  Starting scale.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.1`\n  Seconds per element.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.14`\n  Seconds between elements within one batch.\n- `once: boolean` — default `true`\n  Reveal once and never replay.\n- `batchMax: number` — default `6`\n  Largest group a single callback may receive.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst BATCH_INTERVAL = 0.1;\n\nexport function BatchedBlurReveal({\n  children,\n  selector = '[data-reveal]',\n  start = 'top 88%',\n  distance = 90,\n  blur = 12,\n  scale = 0.94,\n  duration = 1.1,\n  stagger = 0.14,\n  once = true,\n  batchMax = 6,\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: BatchedBlurRevealProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      if (!el) return;\n\n      // Query from the container, never `document`. Two instances of this\n      // component on one page would otherwise each claim the other's targets\n      // and fight over the same tweens.\n      const targets = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(selector));\n      if (targets.length === 0) return;\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        // Not \"duration 0\" — no trigger at all. The content is simply already\n        // in its final state on first paint.\n        gsap.set(targets, { opacity: 1, y: 0, scale: 1, filter: 'none' });\n        return;\n      }\n\n      gsap.set(targets, {\n        opacity: 0,\n        y: distance,\n        scale,\n        filter: `blur(${blur}px)`,\n        // Promoting up front matters more than usual here: an animated\n        // `filter` repaints the layer every frame, and without the hint the\n        // first two or three frames of every batch are the browser deciding\n        // whether to promote at all.\n        willChange: 'transform, filter, opacity',\n      });\n\n      ScrollTrigger.batch(targets, {\n        start,\n        once,\n        batchMax,\n        interval: BATCH_INTERVAL,\n        scroller: scrollerRef?.current ?? undefined,\n        onEnter: (batch) => {\n          gsap.to(batch, {\n            opacity: 1,\n            y: 0,\n            scale: 1,\n            filter: 'blur(0px)',\n            duration,\n            ease: 'power3.out',\n            stagger,\n            // A fast flick can re-enter an element while its own reveal is\n            // still running. Without overwrite the second tween interpolates\n            // from wherever the first had got to and the two fight.\n            overwrite: true,\n            onComplete: () => {\n              // A permanent `filter` keeps the element on its own composited\n              // layer for the life of the page. Drop both hints once settled.\n              gsap.set(batch, { clearProps: 'filter,willChange' });\n            },\n          });\n        },\n      });\n    },\n    root,\n    [selector, start, distance, blur, scale, duration, stagger, once, batchMax, reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` runs the whole setup inside a `gsap.context` and calls `ctx.revert()` on unmount and on any prop change. That kills every batched ScrollTrigger and every in-flight tween, and restores the inline styles the initial `gsap.set` wrote, so an unmount mid-reveal leaves no opacity or blur behind on a node React may reuse.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No ScrollTrigger is created. The targets are set to their final state — full opacity, no offset, no blur — during the first effect, so the section renders as finished content and never moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Creating one ScrollTrigger per element. Sixty targets means sixty sets of start/end calculations on every refresh, and — the visible part — two cards entering in the same frame both begin at t=0, so the row lands flat. `stagger` cannot cross independent triggers.\n- Querying targets with `document.querySelectorAll`. Two instances of the component on one page each claim the other's elements, so both apply the initial state and both tween it, and elements reveal twice at different speeds.\n- Leaving `filter` on the node after the reveal. A non-`none` filter keeps the element on its own composited layer permanently; on a page with sixty of them the compositor memory cost outlives the animation by the whole session. Clear it in `onComplete`.\n- Omitting `overwrite: true`. Flick-scrolling re-enters an element while its reveal is running and the second tween starts from the partial state, so opacity and blur end up somewhere between the two.\n- Animating `filter: blur()` without a `will-change` hint. The first frames go to the browser deciding whether to promote the layer, so every reveal starts with a visible hitch.\n- Registering ScrollTrigger inside the component body. It runs on every render and touches `document`, which throws during server rendering. Register once at module scope behind a `typeof window` guard.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `scrollerRef` when the content scrolls inside a container rather than the document — ScrollTrigger measures against the window by default and every start position will be wrong.\n- `once: true` is the default because these are entrances, not a narrative. Set it false when the section is something the user is expected to scroll back through.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll a grid of cards into view: the cards in the same row lift and focus one after another with a visible offset, not all at once. Scroll back up and they stay revealed. Inspect a settled card and it has no `filter` or `will-change` left in its inline style. With reduced motion enabled the section is fully visible before any scrolling happens.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "bayer-plasma-hero": "# Build: Bayer-Quantised Plasma\n\nA full-screen plasma built from sixteen passes of iterated domain warping, then reduced to eleven grey levels through an 8x8 ordered dither matrix. The quantisation is the whole point: a smooth plasma is a screensaver, and the same plasma printed in eleven tones is a texture that type can sit on. The pointer displaces the plasma's own coordinate space so the field leans into the cursor rather than growing a highlight under it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn iterated domain-warped plasma crushed to eleven grey levels, so the hero reads as a dot-matrix proof.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each of the `iterations` passes rotates the sample point by `rotate` radians, then folds it: `p += warp * sin(p.yx * 1.31 + t + i*0.7) / (1 + i*0.35)`. The `p.yx` swap is what couples the two axes into marbling — with `p.xy` the same code produces stripes.\n2. Each pass also accumulates `amp * sin(p.x + p.y + t*0.6)` with `amp` decaying by 0.86, and the sum is divided by the amplitude actually accumulated rather than by a constant, so lowering `iterations` for a weak GPU changes the detail without changing the exposure.\n3. The result is remapped to 0..1, pushed through `clamp((lum - 0.5) * contrast + 0.5, 0, 1)`, then multiplied by an elliptical vignette centred at `(0.5, 0.4)` — the optical centre, not the geometric one; at 0.5 a hero reads bottom-heavy.\n4. Quantisation happens on a gamma-spaced ramp: `encode = pow(lum, 1/gamma)`, add the dither offset, `round` to one of `levels-1` steps, `decode = pow(q, gamma)`. The reference implementation carried an eleven-entry grey table and found the nearest entry with a branchless ladder of ten mix/step pairs; a monotone ramp collapses that search into encode, round, decode.\n5. The dither offset `(bayer8(fragCoord / cell) - 0.5) / (levels - 1)` is added in the ENCODED space. Applied after decoding it would be the wrong physical size in the shadows, where the ramp is compressed, and the dark half of the frame would band anyway.\n6. `iterations` is a uniform read against a constant `MAX_ITERATIONS = 48` loop bound with a `break`, so quality can be lowered at runtime without relinking a program or triggering a driver recompile.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MAX_ITERATIONS = 48`\n  Compile-time loop ceiling. A fully dynamic bound is legal in ES 3.00 but stops the compiler bounding register pressure, and some mobile drivers recompile the shader when the uniform changes — a visible hitch at exactly the moment you were trying to avoid one.\n- `iterations = 16`\n  Runtime pass count. Below about 6 the field is a plain ripple with no interference; past about 24 each extra pass adds detail finer than one dither cell, so it is thrown away by the quantiser and costs a full sin/rot per fragment for nothing.\n- `warp = 1.5`\n  How far each pass folds the coordinate. Under ~0.5 the passes barely disturb each other and the plasma is concentric; over ~2.5 the folding self-intersects and produces hard creases that survive quantisation as jagged black shapes.\n- `amplitude decay = 0.86`\n  Per-pass amplitude falloff. At 1.0 the late passes carry as much weight as the first and the field turns to grey mush; below ~0.6 only the first three passes contribute and the iteration count stops mattering.\n- `phase offset = float(i) * 0.7`\n  Decorrelates the passes. Without it every pass warps in lockstep and sixteen iterations look identical to one.\n- `levels = 11`\n  Grey steps. Eleven is the count that still reads as print: enough to hold the shape of the plasma, few enough that every transition is visibly dots rather than a ramp. Past ~20 the dither becomes invisible and you have paid for it anyway.\n- `gamma = 1.49`\n  Exponent the levels are spaced on. At 1.0 the steps are uniform in code and non-uniform to the eye, so the shadows show obvious banding while the highlights look smooth.\n- `vignette centre = (0.5, 0.4)`\n  The optical centre. Vignetting around the geometric centre makes a hero read as bottom-heavy, because the eye already places the middle of a frame slightly above halfway.\n- `cellSize = 2`\n  CSS pixels per dither cell, so the 8x8 tile is 16 CSS px. Indexing the matrix by device pixels instead makes the texture twice as coarse on a 1x monitor as on a 2x one.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BayerPlasmaHero(props: { iterations?: number; warp?: number; frequency?: number; rotate?: number; contrast?: number; gamma?: number; levels?: number; cellSize?: number; vignette?: number; ink?: readonly [number, number, number]; paper?: readonly [number, number, number]; speed?: number; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `iterations: number` — default `16`\n  Plasma warp passes. Clamped to 1..48, the shader's compiled ceiling.\n- `warp: number` — default `1.5`\n  How far each pass folds the coordinate through its own sine.\n- `frequency: number` — default `2.2`\n  Base spatial frequency of the plasma.\n- `rotate: number` — default `-0.1`\n  Radians of rotation applied to the coordinate each pass.\n- `contrast: number` — default `1.35`\n  Contrast pivoted on mid grey, applied before quantisation.\n- `gamma: number` — default `1.49`\n  Exponent the output levels are spaced on.\n- `levels: number` — default `11`\n  Output grey levels, both ends included.\n- `cellSize: number` — default `2`\n  CSS pixels per dither cell. Multiplied by DPR internally.\n- `vignette: number` — default `0.55`\n  Strength of the elliptical vignette at the optical centre.\n- `ink: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.03, 0.032, 0.038]`\n  Darkest output colour, linear 0..1 RGB.\n- `paper: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.88, 0.92, 0.9]`\n  Lightest output colour, linear 0..1 RGB.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier on plasma evolution.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Upper bound on device pixel ratio.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TRIANGLE = new Float32Array([-1, -1, 3, -1, -1, 3]);\nconst MAX_ITERATIONS = 48;\nconst ITERATIONS = 16;\nconst WARP = 1.5;\nconst FREQUENCY = 2.2;\nconst ROTATE = -0.1;\nconst CONTRAST = 1.35;\nconst GAMMA = 1.49;\nconst LEVELS = 11;\nconst CELL_SIZE = 2;\nconst VIGNETTE = 0.55;\nconst INK: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.03, 0.032, 0.038];\nconst PAPER: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.88, 0.92, 0.9];\n\ninterface Gpu {\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext;\n  program: WebGLProgram;\n  vao: WebGLVertexArrayObject;\n  buffer: WebGLBuffer;\n  locations: Map<string, WebGLUniformLocation | null>;\n}\n\nfunction build(gl: WebGL2RenderingContext): Gpu | null {\n  const compile = (type: number, src: string): WebGLShader | null => {\n    const shader = gl.createShader(type);\n    if (!shader) return null;\n    gl.shaderSource(shader, src);\n    gl.compileShader(shader);\n    if (gl.getShaderParameter(shader, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) return shader;\n    console.error('[bayer-plasma-hero]', gl.getShaderInfoLog(shader));\n    gl.deleteShader(shader);\n    return null;\n  };\n\n  const vert = compile(gl.VERTEX_SHADER, BAYER_PLASMA_VERT);\n  const frag = compile(gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER, BAYER_PLASMA_FRAG);\n  const program = gl.createProgram();\n  const vao = gl.createVertexArray();\n  const buffer = gl.createBuffer();\n  if (!vert || !frag || !program || !vao || !buffer) return null;\n\n  gl.attachShader(program, vert);\n  gl.attachShader(program, frag);\n  gl.linkProgram(program);\n  gl.deleteShader(vert);\n  gl.deleteShader(frag);\n  if (!gl.getProgramParameter(program, gl.LINK_STATUS)) {\n    console.error('[bayer-plasma-hero]', gl.getProgramInfoLog(program));\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.bindVertexArray(vao);\n  gl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, buffer);\n  gl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, TRIANGLE, gl.STATIC_DRAW);\n  const attrib = gl.getAttribLocation(program, 'a_pos');\n  if (attrib >= 0) {\n    gl.enableVertexAttribArray(attrib);\n    gl.vertexAttribPointer(attrib, 2, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);\n  }\n  gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n\n  return { gl, program, vao, buffer, locations: new Map() };\n}\n\nfunction loc(gpu: Gpu, name: string): WebGLUniformLocation | null {\n  const cached = gpu.locations.get(name);\n  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;\n  const found = gpu.gl.getUniformLocation(gpu.program, name);\n  gpu.locations.set(name, found);\n  return found;\n}\n\nexport function BayerPlasmaHero({\n  iterations = ITERATIONS,\n  warp = WARP,\n  frequency = FREQUENCY,\n  rotate = ROTATE,\n  contrast = CONTRAST,\n  gamma = GAMMA,\n  levels = LEVELS,\n  cellSize = CELL_SIZE,\n  vignette = VIGNETTE,\n  ink = INK,\n  paper = PAPER,\n  speed = 1,\n  maxDpr = 2,\n  className,\n}: BayerPlasmaHeroProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n  const gpuRef = useRef<Gpu | null>(null);\n  const dprRef = useRef(1);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.03 });\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n\n  const paramsRef = useRef({\n    iterations, warp, frequency, rotate, contrast, gamma,\n    levels, cellSize, vignette, ink, paper, speed,\n  });\n  paramsRef.current = {\n    iterations, warp, frequency, rotate, contrast, gamma,\n    levels, cellSize, vignette, ink, paper, speed,\n  };\n\n  const drawRef = useRef<(elapsed: number) => void>(() => {});\n  drawRef.current = (elapsed: number) => {\n    const gpu = gpuRef.current;\n    if (!gpu) return;\n    const { gl } = gpu;\n    const q = paramsRef.current;\n    const p = pointer.current;\n\n    gl.useProgram(gpu.program);\n    gl.uniform2f(loc(gpu, 'u_resolution'), gl.drawingBufferWidth, gl.drawingBufferHeight);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_time'), elapsed * q.speed);\n    gl.uniform2f(loc(gpu, 'u_pointer'), p.nx, 1 - p.ny);\n    // Clamped to the shader's compiled ceiling: a value above MAX_ITERATIONS\n    // would silently do nothing, which is worse than being clamped.\n    gl.uniform1i(\n      loc(gpu, 'u_iterations'),\n      Math.max(1, Math.min(MAX_ITERATIONS, Math.round(q.iterations))),\n    );\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_warp'), q.warp);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_freq'), q.frequency);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_rotate'), q.rotate);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_contrast'), q.contrast);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_gamma'), Math.max(0.05, q.gamma));\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_levels'), Math.max(2, Math.round(q.levels)));\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_cell'), Math.max(1, q.cellSize * dprRef.current));\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_vignette'), q.vignette);\n    gl.uniform3f(loc(gpu, 'u_ink'), q.ink[0], q.ink[1], q.ink[2]);\n    gl.uniform3f(loc(gpu, 'u_paper'), q.paper[0], q.paper[1], q.paper[2]);\n\n    gl.bindVertexArray(gpu.vao);\n    gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLES, 0, 3);\n    gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    /* The canvas is created here, not rendered by React, because the cleanup\n       below hands the context back through `WEBGL_lose_context` — and\n       `getContext` on an element that has been through that returns the SAME\n       dead context, not a new one. Keeping React's element across the cycle\n       therefore made the loss permanent. This is not a dev-only StrictMode\n       concern: the preview host hides components with `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">`,\n       which unmounts effects while keeping the DOM, so scrolling a preview out\n       of view and back is exactly that cycle. */\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.className = 'block h-full w-full';\n\n    const gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', {\n      antialias: false,\n      alpha: false,\n      depth: false,\n      stencil: false,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n    });\n    if (!gl) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const gpu = build(gl);\n    if (!gpu) {\n      loseContext(gl);\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    el.append(canvas);\n    canvasRef.current = canvas;\n    gpuRef.current = gpu;\n    setSupported(true);\n\n    // A hero that loses its context and is never told about it shows an empty\n    // rectangle for ever; flipping to the fallback keeps something on screen.\n    // `preventDefault` is mandatory — without it no restore is ever offered.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      // Detached as well: the fallback renders as a sibling in normal flow, so\n      // a canvas left in place would cover it.\n      canvas.remove();\n      setSupported(false);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      gpuRef.current = null;\n      canvasRef.current = null;\n      gl.deleteVertexArray(gpu.vao);\n      gl.deleteBuffer(gpu.buffer);\n      gl.deleteProgram(gpu.program);\n      loseContext(gl);\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const canvas = canvasRef.current;\n    const gpu = gpuRef.current;\n    if (!canvas || !gpu || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n\n    const dpr = pixelRatio(maxDpr);\n    dprRef.current = dpr;\n    const w = Math.max(1, Math.round(width * dpr));\n    const h = Math.max(1, Math.round(height * dpr));\n    if (canvas.width !== w || canvas.height !== h) {\n      canvas.width = w;\n      canvas.height = h;\n    }\n    gpu.gl.viewport(0, 0, w, h);\n    // At t = 0 every sine in the warp chain is in phase and the plasma is a\n    // near-flat field, so the still frame is taken from a developed moment.\n    drawRef.current(reduced ? 7.5 : 0);\n  }, [width, height, maxDpr, supported, reduced]);\n\n  const running = supported && visible && !reduced && width > 0 && height > 0;\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => drawRef.current(elapsed), running);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Deletes the VAO, vertex buffer and program, then `loseContext(gl)` to hand the context back. The rAF subscription and the pointer listener unsubscribe with their hooks.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No rAF subscription. A single frame is drawn at a fixed `u_time` of 7.5 seconds and redrawn on resize — not at t=0, where every sine in the warp chain is still in phase and the plasma is a nearly flat field. The dithered texture, the vignette and the level structure are all fully present; only the evolution stops.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Dividing the plasma sum by a hard-coded normaliser. Drop from 16 iterations to 8 on a weak GPU and the whole image gets darker, so the low-quality path looks like a different component rather than the same one with less detail.\n- Applying the dither offset after decoding the gamma ramp. In the shadows the ramp is compressed, so a fixed offset spans much less than one output step there and the dark half of the frame bands exactly as if there were no dither at all.\n- Writing `sin(p.xy * ...)` instead of `sin(p.yx * ...)`. The axes stop being coupled, every pass warps x by a function of x, and the marbling degenerates into diagonal stripes.\n- Making the iteration count a `const int`. Every quality level then needs its own compiled program, and switching between them stalls the pipeline; a uniform plus a constant loop bound with `break` costs one comparison per pass.\n- Centring the vignette at (0.5, 0.5). It is subtle and it is real — the frame reads as sagging, and the usual reaction is to reduce the vignette strength instead of moving it.\n- Rendering at native DPR. This shader is 16 iterations of transcendentals per fragment; at DPR 3 that is 2.25x the fragments of DPR 2 for no perceptible gain, and it is the difference between 60fps and 25 on a phone.\n- Leaving the context alive on unmount. Raw GL has no `dispose()` — the VAO, buffer and program are deleted explicitly and `loseContext(gl)` returns the context, or the page starts blanking its oldest canvases after roughly sixteen mounts.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The 8x8 matrix is derived from `gl_FragCoord` with six integer operations rather than sampled from a texture. The derivation is documented in full in `bayer8-ordered-dither`; the function is duplicated here so this component installs standalone.\n- The entire look is data: `iterations`, `warp`, `frequency`, `contrast`, `gamma` and `levels` are the only things separating newsprint from oil-slick.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEvery tonal transition is made of visible ordered dots — put your eye close and there is no smooth ramp anywhere in the frame. Set `iterations={6}` and the field simplifies without getting darker. Move the cursor and the plasma shears toward it rather than a bright spot appearing under it. The frame is darkest at the corners and brightest slightly above the middle.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "bayer8-ordered-dither": "# Build: Bit-Math Bayer8 Dither\n\nOrdered dithering with the threshold matrix computed from the fragment coordinate instead of sampled from a texture. Six integer operations reproduce the classic 8x8 Bayer matrix exactly, which turns a banding-prone gradient into a stable newsprint texture that holds at any zoom and costs less than the texture fetch it replaces.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA smooth gradient crushed to five levels through an 8x8 ordered matrix derived from six integer ops.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The Bayer matrix is defined recursively as `M2 = [[0,2],[3,1]]` and `M2n(x,y) = 4*Mn(x mod n, y mod n) + M2(x div n, y div n)`. Writing M2 in binary (0=00, 2=10, 3=11, 1=01) collapses it to the closed form `M2(a,b) = 2*(a^b) + b`.\n2. Unrolling that recursion three times gives `M8(x,y) = 16*M2(x0,y0) + 4*M2(x1,y1) + M2(x2,y2)` where xN is bit N of x, and substituting the closed form yields the whole matrix as one expression: `32*(x0^y0) + 16*y0 + 8*(x1^y1) + 4*y1 + 2*(x2^y2) + y2`.\n3. Read as bits that is the low three bits of `x ^ y` and of `y`, interleaved and reversed — the LEAST significant coordinate bit drives the MOST significant threshold bit, which is precisely what makes the pattern maximally dispersed instead of clustered. In GLSL it is `int x = c.x & 7; int y = c.y & 7;` followed by those six terms, divided by 64.\n4. `gl_FragCoord.xy` is divided by `cellSize * dpr` before the matrix lookup, so the dither cell is a fixed physical size and the texture does not get four times finer on a retina display.\n5. Quantisation is `floor(clamp(c + (threshold - 0.5) / steps, 0, 1) * steps + 0.5) / steps` with `steps = levels - 1`. Centring the threshold on zero and scaling it to exactly one output step is what makes the perturbation cancel out on average.\n6. A `pow(g, 1.35)` gamma is applied before quantisation, because the output levels are evenly spaced in the encoded value while the eye is not — quantising the linear value alone dumps all the visible banding into the shadows.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `divisor = 64.0`\n  The matrix holds 0..63, so dividing by 64 keeps the thresholds in [0, 1). Dividing by 63 puts the top cell at exactly 1.0, which pushes one cell in every 8x8 tile a full step brighter and stamps a visible dot grid across flat areas.\n- `levels = 5`\n  Output steps per channel. At 2 it is a pure 1-bit print and the gradient becomes texture only. Above about 12 the eye can no longer see the individual steps and the dither is doing nothing but adding noise — use it to fix banding, not as a look.\n- `cellSize = 2`\n  CSS pixels per dither cell, so the 8x8 tile is 16 CSS px. At 1 the pattern is at the Nyquist limit of most displays and shimmers when the page scrolls; above about 6 it stops reading as print texture and starts reading as a deliberate checkerboard.\n- `threshold scale = (t - 0.5) / (levels - 1)`\n  The perturbation must span exactly one output step. Larger and the dither adds contrast of its own — flat areas visibly sparkle. Smaller and it cannot push a value across a level boundary, so the bands survive untouched.\n- `gamma = 1.35`\n  Applied before quantisation. Without it the evenly spaced levels bunch perceptually in the highlights and spread in the shadows, so the dark half of the frame shows obvious steps while the bright half looks smooth.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Bayer8OrderedDither(props: { levels?: number; cellSize?: number; speed?: number; dark?: readonly [number, number, number]; light?: readonly [number, number, number]; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `levels: number` — default `5`\n  Output steps per channel, both ends included. 2 is pure 1-bit.\n- `cellSize: number` — default `2`\n  CSS pixels per dither cell. Multiplied by DPR internally.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier on the sweep that animates the underlying gradient.\n- `dark: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.035, 0.038, 0.045]`\n  Darkest output colour, linear 0..1 RGB.\n- `light: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.9, 0.94, 0.92]`\n  Lightest output colour, linear 0..1 RGB.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Upper bound on device pixel ratio.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TRIANGLE = new Float32Array([-1, -1, 3, -1, -1, 3]);\nconst LEVELS = 5;\nconst CELL_SIZE = 2;\nconst DARK: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.035, 0.038, 0.045];\nconst LIGHT: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.9, 0.94, 0.92];\n\ninterface Gpu {\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext;\n  program: WebGLProgram;\n  vao: WebGLVertexArrayObject;\n  buffer: WebGLBuffer;\n  locations: Map<string, WebGLUniformLocation | null>;\n}\n\nfunction build(gl: WebGL2RenderingContext): Gpu | null {\n  const compile = (type: number, src: string): WebGLShader | null => {\n    const shader = gl.createShader(type);\n    if (!shader) return null;\n    gl.shaderSource(shader, src);\n    gl.compileShader(shader);\n    if (gl.getShaderParameter(shader, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) return shader;\n    console.error('[bayer8-ordered-dither]', gl.getShaderInfoLog(shader));\n    gl.deleteShader(shader);\n    return null;\n  };\n\n  const vert = compile(gl.VERTEX_SHADER, BAYER8_DITHER_VERT);\n  const frag = compile(gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER, BAYER8_DITHER_FRAG);\n  const program = gl.createProgram();\n  const vao = gl.createVertexArray();\n  const buffer = gl.createBuffer();\n  if (!vert || !frag || !program || !vao || !buffer) return null;\n\n  gl.attachShader(program, vert);\n  gl.attachShader(program, frag);\n  gl.linkProgram(program);\n  gl.deleteShader(vert);\n  gl.deleteShader(frag);\n  if (!gl.getProgramParameter(program, gl.LINK_STATUS)) {\n    console.error('[bayer8-ordered-dither]', gl.getProgramInfoLog(program));\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.bindVertexArray(vao);\n  gl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, buffer);\n  gl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, TRIANGLE, gl.STATIC_DRAW);\n  const attrib = gl.getAttribLocation(program, 'a_pos');\n  if (attrib >= 0) {\n    gl.enableVertexAttribArray(attrib);\n    gl.vertexAttribPointer(attrib, 2, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);\n  }\n  gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n\n  return { gl, program, vao, buffer, locations: new Map() };\n}\n\nfunction loc(gpu: Gpu, name: string): WebGLUniformLocation | null {\n  const cached = gpu.locations.get(name);\n  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;\n  const found = gpu.gl.getUniformLocation(gpu.program, name);\n  gpu.locations.set(name, found);\n  return found;\n}\n\nexport function Bayer8OrderedDither({\n  levels = LEVELS,\n  cellSize = CELL_SIZE,\n  speed = 1,\n  dark = DARK,\n  light = LIGHT,\n  maxDpr = 2,\n  className,\n}: Bayer8OrderedDitherProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n  const gpuRef = useRef<Gpu | null>(null);\n  const dprRef = useRef(1);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.004 });\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n\n  const paramsRef = useRef({ levels, cellSize, speed, dark, light });\n  paramsRef.current = { levels, cellSize, speed, dark, light };\n\n  const drawRef = useRef<(elapsed: number) => void>(() => {});\n  drawRef.current = (elapsed: number) => {\n    const gpu = gpuRef.current;\n    if (!gpu) return;\n    const { gl } = gpu;\n    const params = paramsRef.current;\n    const p = pointer.current;\n\n    gl.useProgram(gpu.program);\n    gl.uniform2f(loc(gpu, 'u_resolution'), gl.drawingBufferWidth, gl.drawingBufferHeight);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_time'), elapsed * params.speed);\n    gl.uniform2f(loc(gpu, 'u_pointer'), p.nx, 1 - p.ny);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_levels'), Math.max(2, Math.round(params.levels)));\n    // Cell size is authored in CSS pixels and converted here, so the dither\n    // grain is the same physical size on a 1x monitor and a 3x phone.\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_cell'), Math.max(1, params.cellSize * dprRef.current));\n    gl.uniform3f(loc(gpu, 'u_dark'), params.dark[0], params.dark[1], params.dark[2]);\n    gl.uniform3f(loc(gpu, 'u_light'), params.light[0], params.light[1], params.light[2]);\n\n    gl.bindVertexArray(gpu.vao);\n    gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLES, 0, 3);\n    gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    /* The canvas is created here, not rendered by React, because the cleanup\n       below hands the context back through `WEBGL_lose_context` — and\n       `getContext` on an element that has been through that returns the SAME\n       dead context, not a new one. Keeping React's element across the cycle\n       therefore made the loss permanent. This is not a dev-only StrictMode\n       concern: the preview host hides components with `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">`,\n       which unmounts effects while keeping the DOM, so scrolling a preview out\n       of view and back is exactly that cycle. */\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.className = 'block h-full w-full';\n\n    const gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', {\n      antialias: false,\n      alpha: false,\n      depth: false,\n      stencil: false,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n    });\n    if (!gl) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const gpu = build(gl);\n    if (!gpu) {\n      loseContext(gl);\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    el.append(canvas);\n    canvasRef.current = canvas;\n    gpuRef.current = gpu;\n    setSupported(true);\n\n    // Unhandled, a lost context is a canvas that stays blank for the rest of\n    // the page's life with nothing rendered in its place, so swap in the CSS\n    // fallback. `preventDefault` is what keeps the loss recoverable at all.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      // Detached as well: the fallback renders as a sibling in normal flow,\n      // so a canvas left in place would cover it.\n      canvas.remove();\n      setSupported(false);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      gpuRef.current = null;\n      canvasRef.current = null;\n      gl.deleteVertexArray(gpu.vao);\n      gl.deleteBuffer(gpu.buffer);\n      gl.deleteProgram(gpu.program);\n      loseContext(gl);\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const canvas = canvasRef.current;\n    const gpu = gpuRef.current;\n    if (!canvas || !gpu || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n\n    const dpr = pixelRatio(maxDpr);\n    dprRef.current = dpr;\n    const w = Math.max(1, Math.round(width * dpr));\n    const h = Math.max(1, Math.round(height * dpr));\n    if (canvas.width !== w || canvas.height !== h) {\n      canvas.width = w;\n      canvas.height = h;\n    }\n    gpu.gl.viewport(0, 0, w, h);\n    drawRef.current(0);\n  }, [width, height, maxDpr, supported, reduced]);\n\n  const running = supported && visible && !reduced && width > 0 && height > 0;\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => drawRef.current(elapsed), running);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Deletes the VAO, vertex buffer and program, then `loseContext(gl)`. The rAF subscription and pointer listener are removed by their hooks.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF loop never subscribes; the sweep is frozen at t=0 and the pointer light sits at its seeded centre. One frame is drawn on mount and on every resize, so the dithered gradient is fully rendered — the texture is the point, and it is static.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Uploading the matrix as an 8x8 texture. It needs a texture unit, a sampler uniform, GL_NEAREST filtering and CLAMP wrapping set correctly on a non-power-of-two-safe path, and a resource to dispose — all to replace six integer instructions that are cheaper than the fetch.\n- Dividing the matrix by 63 instead of 64. The top cell lands on exactly 1.0, that one cell per tile is pushed a full level brighter than its neighbours, and flat regions grow a faint regular dot grid that only shows up on a real screen.\n- Comparing against the raw matrix value with `step(0.0, x)`. The matrix contains a genuine zero cell, so the comparison passes there even when it should not, and every 8x8 tile gets one stuck pixel. Bias the comparison by about 0.001 if you use a threshold form rather than the quantise form here.\n- Indexing the matrix with `gl_FragCoord` directly. The cell then measures one device pixel, so the same component looks twice as coarse on a 1x monitor as on a 2x one. Divide by `cellSize * dpr` and the texture is stable.\n- Quantising the linear value with no gamma. The steps are uniform in code and non-uniform to the eye, so the shadows band visibly while the highlights look fine and the fix gets misdiagnosed as \"not enough levels\".\n- Leaving the raw GL objects behind on unmount. There is no `dispose()` here — the VAO, buffer and program are deleted by hand and `loseContext(gl)` returns the context, without which a page that mounts this repeatedly starts blanking its oldest canvases.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The alternative implementation, a nested `mix`/`step` ladder, is branchless and works on WebGL1. This one needs WebGL2 for integer bit operators but is roughly half the instruction count.\n- The same six lines dither any signal — swap the gradient in `main` for a scene texture and this becomes a post-process.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe gradient shows no smooth ramp anywhere — every transition is a field of ordered dots resolving between five flat levels. Zoom the browser to 200% and the dot pitch doubles with the page instead of staying at one device pixel. Screenshot two consecutive frames with the pointer still: the dot pattern is byte-identical, because the matrix depends only on position.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "blend-difference-cursor": "# Build: Blend-Difference Cursor\n\nA white circle replaces the pointer and inverts everything under it via `mix-blend-mode: difference`, so it is legible over any background without a single contrast rule. It has three sizes — 16px idle, 44px over a link, 150px over a `data-cursor=\"view\"` target, the last carrying a text label — and the size change is CSS while the position is GSAP, deliberately kept on different properties.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA white dot in difference blend mode inverts whatever it crosses, growing 16 to 44 to 150px on hover.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Difference blending subtracts the backdrop from the source per channel. A pure white source therefore outputs `1 - backdrop`: black over white, white over black, the complement over any colour. That is the entire contrast strategy — no theme awareness, no luminance sampling, no per-section overrides.\n2. Position is two `gsap.quickTo` setters created once, each backed by a single tween whose end value is mutated per event. At 0.32s with `power3` the dot trails the pointer by a few frames — enough to read as a physical object, short enough that clicking still feels direct.\n3. `xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50` centres the dot on its own box once at mount, so the handler can pass raw pointer coordinates and, critically, the centring survives the width and height changing from 16px to 150px. A pixel offset would have to be recomputed for every size.\n4. Size transitions list `width`, `height` and `background-color` explicitly. `transform` is excluded because GSAP writes it every frame; a CSS transition on the same property would interpolate toward a value GSAP has already replaced, and the result is a cursor that lags by a constant distance no tween duration accounts for.\n5. Hover state is resolved by hit-testing `e.target.closest(...)` on each move rather than binding listeners to interactive children. React can add, remove and reorder those children freely and the cursor keeps working, with no subscription bookkeeping.\n6. The dot is `display: none` in markup and only switched to `flex` after the `(pointer: fine)` check passes, so nothing renders before JS confirms there is a cursor to replace — no stray blob flashes during hydration on a phone.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SIZE_IDLE / SIZE_HOVER / SIZE_VIEW = 16 / 44 / 150`\n  16px reads as a cursor rather than a dot of dust. 44px is roughly the tap-target minimum and clearly signals \"this is interactive\" without hiding the word underneath. 150px is enormous on purpose: it has to hold a 15px label with breathing room, and at that size the inverted region becomes a design element rather than a pointer.\n- `FOLLOW_DURATION = 0.32`\n  Seconds of catch-up. Below ~0.15 the dot is welded to the pointer and the blend looks like a rendering artefact; above ~0.5 clicking feels disconnected because the visual lands after the press.\n- `ease: 'power3' = power3 (in-out)`\n  In-out rather than `.out`. quickTo remutates the end value continuously, so the tween is always restarting mid-flight; the symmetric curve absorbs direction changes instead of snapping into a fresh outward ramp on every event.\n- `size transition timing = 0.4s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  A strong ease-out. The dot reaches most of its new size in the first ~120ms so the hover feels instant, then settles. Linear at this scale looks mechanical; a longer duration means the dot is still growing when the pointer has left the target.\n- `label scale = 0.5 -> 1`\n  The label grows with the dot rather than fading in at full size, so at the halfway point of the transition the text is proportionate to the circle around it instead of overflowing it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BlendDifferenceCursor(props: { children?: ReactNode; label?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content the cursor inverts. Mark targets with `data-cursor=\"view\"` or use plain `a`/`button`.\n- `label: string` — default `'View'`\n  Text shown inside the 150px state. Must fit at 15px inside the circle.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** The three states. Diameters in px, not scales — see the note above. */\nconst SIZE_IDLE = 16;\nconst SIZE_HOVER = 44;\nconst SIZE_VIEW = 150;\n\nconst FOLLOW_DURATION = 0.32;\n\nexport function BlendDifferenceCursor({\n  children,\n  label = 'View',\n  className,\n}: BlendDifferenceCursorProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const dot = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const text = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const node = dot.current;\n    const caption = text.current;\n    if (!el || !node || !caption) return;\n\n    // Never on touch: there is no hover state to grow into, and `pointermove`\n    // only fires mid-drag, so the dot would appear under the finger already\n    // covering it and then be stranded on lift.\n    if (!window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) return;\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;\n\n    /* The dot is `display: none` until JS confirms a fine pointer, so nothing\n       renders before this line — no stray blob flashes during hydration. */\n    node.style.display = 'flex';\n    /* Hiding the native cursor from JS, not CSS, for the same reason: a\n       stylesheet rule would blank the cursor on touch devices too, where this\n       component draws nothing to replace it. */\n    const previousCursor = el.style.cursor;\n    el.style.cursor = 'none';\n\n    gsap.set(node, { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, autoAlpha: 0 });\n\n    const duration = reduced ? 0 : FOLLOW_DURATION;\n    // Two tweens for the lifetime of the component, end values mutated in\n    // place. `gsap.to` per event would allocate ~120 tweens a second, each one\n    // overwriting a tween that had not finished.\n    const xTo = gsap.quickTo(node, 'x', { duration, ease: 'power3' });\n    const yTo = gsap.quickTo(node, 'y', { duration, ease: 'power3' });\n\n    let entered = false;\n    let state: 'idle' | 'hover' | 'view' = 'idle';\n\n    const apply = (next: typeof state) => {\n      if (next === state) return;\n      state = next;\n      const size = next === 'view' ? SIZE_VIEW : next === 'hover' ? SIZE_HOVER : SIZE_IDLE;\n      // Width and height only. `transform` is GSAP's.\n      node.style.width = `${size}px`;\n      node.style.height = `${size}px`;\n      caption.style.opacity = next === 'view' ? '1' : '0';\n      caption.style.transform = next === 'view' ? 'scale(1)' : 'scale(0.5)';\n    };\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const x = e.clientX - rect.left;\n      const y = e.clientY - rect.top;\n\n      if (!entered) {\n        entered = true;\n        // Second argument is quickTo's START value: this teleports instead of\n        // easing, so the dot does not sail across the panel on every entry.\n        xTo(x, x);\n        yTo(y, y);\n        gsap.to(node, { autoAlpha: 1, duration: reduced ? 0 : 0.2, overwrite: 'auto' });\n      } else {\n        xTo(x);\n        yTo(y);\n      }\n\n      // Hit-testing the event target beats a listener per interactive child:\n      // it survives children being added, removed or re-rendered by React.\n      const target = e.target instanceof Element ? e.target : null;\n      if (target?.closest('[data-cursor=\"view\"]')) apply('view');\n      else if (target?.closest('a, button, [data-cursor=\"hover\"]')) apply('hover');\n      else apply('idle');\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      entered = false;\n      apply('idle');\n      gsap.to(node, { autoAlpha: 0, duration: reduced ? 0 : 0.2, overwrite: 'auto' });\n    };\n\n    /* Listening on the container, not the dot. A listener on the dot would see\n       its own `pointerleave` as it slides out from under the pointer and chase\n       itself. `pointer-events: none` on the dot is the other half of that. */\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      el.style.cursor = previousCursor;\n      node.style.display = '';\n      gsap.killTweensOf(node);\n      gsap.set(node, { clearProps: 'all' });\n    };\n  }, []);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes both container listeners, restores the container's original inline `cursor` value, resets the dot to `display: none`, then `gsap.killTweensOf` and `clearProps: \"all\"` to drop the two quickTo tweens and the centring transform. Nothing is left on the element for a remount to inherit.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The cursor still appears, still inverts and still changes size — those are affordances, not motion. Both quickTo durations drop to 0 so the dot is welded to the pointer, and the fade in and out is instant. The CSS size transition is left in place; it is a 400ms state change, not continuous movement.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- An ancestor that creates a stacking context. A `transform`, `filter`, `opacity` below 1, `will-change`, `isolation: isolate`, `contain: paint`, a `position: fixed` parent or a `backdrop-filter` anywhere between the intended blend root and the dot silently confines the blend to that subtree — the dot inverts its immediate parent and nothing else. This is the single most common reason this effect \"does not work\", and it produces no error, no warning and no console output.\n- Transitioning `all` on the dot. It catches `transform`, which GSAP is writing at 60-120fps, and CSS interpolates toward values that are already stale. The cursor then trails the pointer by a fixed distance that changing the tween duration does not fix, because the tween is not the thing lagging.\n- Scaling instead of resizing. `scale()` is a transform, so it collides with the follow tween AND it scales the label's stroke weight and letter-spacing along with the circle. Animating `width`/`height` keeps typography at its designed size.\n- Listening for `pointermove` on the dot itself. It sits under the pointer, so it receives its own `pointerleave` as it slides, and the cursor chases itself out of the panel. Listen on the container and keep `pointer-events: none` on the dot.\n- Hiding the native cursor from CSS. A `cursor: none` rule applies on touch devices too, where this component renders nothing to replace it, so links become untappable-looking with no visible affordance. Set it from JS after the `(pointer: fine)` check and restore it on cleanup.\n- Centring with a negative margin instead of `xPercent`/`yPercent`. The margin is a fixed pixel value and the dot changes size by a factor of nine, so it visibly slides off the pointer as it grows.\n- Leaving the effect on over navigation. A 150px inverted blob over link text is genuinely worse than an arrow; a real deployment needs an escape hatch that restores the native cursor over the nav.\n- Using a theme token for the dot colour. Difference blending only produces a clean inversion from pure white; a near-white token yields a muddy tint over mid-greys.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch nothing mounts, the native cursor is never hidden, and the markup stays `display: none` — there is no hover to grow into and `pointermove` only exists mid-drag, which would put the dot under the finger already covering it.\n- Scoped to its own container with `position: absolute` inside a `relative isolate` root rather than `position: fixed`. A production site-level cursor is `fixed` at the document root; the isolate here is what makes the blend stop at the component edge instead of inverting the whole page.\n- Reduced motion keeps the cursor but sets every duration to 0, so the affordance and the inversion survive while the drift does not.\n- The label is `#000` and the dot `#fff` as raw literals, not tokens. Difference blending is defined on the actual channel values; a token would break the inversion the moment the theme shifted.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove across the panel and a white dot follows a few frames behind, inverting the text and background under it — dark text goes light where the dot crosses it. Hover a link and it grows to 44px; hover a `data-cursor=\"view\"` target and it grows to 150px with a label that scales up inside it. During the growth the dot stays centred on the pointer and never lags further behind than it did at rest. Leave the panel and it fades out. On touch there is no dot at all and the native cursor is untouched.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "blob-avatar": "# Build: Blob Avatar\n\nA friendly avatar for agents and bots: a soft coloured body with a darker edge and a pair of eyes. Seven body shapes (blob, squircle, star, flower, diamond, shield, cloud), six faces and eight colours. Give it only a name and the name picks all three, always the same way; set any of them to override. While motion is allowed the outline slowly breathes between three wobbled versions of itself and the eyes blink, each avatar on its own timing so a list never moves in step.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSeeded SVG blob with eyes: a name picks its shape, colour and face, the body gently morphs and blinks, six sizes.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. blobLook(seed) hashes the string once (FNV-1a via hashSeed) and reads colour, shape and face from different bits of the hash, so changing one letter of a name gives a new avatar but the same name is stable across renders, servers and reloads.\n2. Each shape is a unit outline of 40 points: polar radius functions for squircle (superellipse, p = 4), star (raised cosine of 5θ), flower (|cos 3θ|^0.6), cloud (five bumps with a flat base) and blob (three seeded cosine lumps), and arc-length samples along a polygon for diamond and shield.\n3. A seeded tilt of up to ±8° rotates the outline, then toPath turns the points into a closed Catmull-Rom spline written as cubic Béziers (control points at ±1/6 of the neighbour chord), which rounds polygon corners and keeps curves smooth.\n4. blobPaths builds the rest path and two wobbled frames by scaling every point along its radius by 1 + 5.5% × (0.6 sin(2θ + φ) + 0.4 sin(3θ − 2φ)), with φ advanced a third of a turn per frame. All frames share the same command list, so SMIL <animate attributeName=\"d\"> can interpolate rest → A → B → rest with eased keySplines.\n5. The same animate element drives the edge path (offset 2.5, 3.5), the body and the clip path of a soft top-left highlight, so light and edge follow the morph. Eyes are drawn around their own origin and blink with an animateTransform scale to 12% height for 4% of a 3.5 to 6.5 s cycle.\n6. Morph length (5.5 to 8 s), blink length and a negative begin offset are drawn from a second seeded generator, so every avatar starts mid-cycle and neighbours drift apart.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `samples = 40 per outline`\n  Divides by 5 and 8, so star tips land exactly on a sample; fewer points made the diamond and shield corners mushy.\n- `wobble = 5.5% of the radius`\n  Reads as breathing at 32px without changing the silhouette enough to look like a different shape.\n- `edge offset = 2.5, 3.5 in a 100 viewBox`\n  A thin darker rim on the lower right gives the flat blob a bit of thickness, like a cut-out sticker.\n- `sizes = xs 24 · sm 32 · md 40 · lg 56 · xl 80 · 2xl 128 px`\n  Matches list rows (32), headers (40) and the large editor preview (128); any number also works.\n- `blink = scaleY 0.12 for 4% of the cycle`\n  About 200 ms closed, quick enough to read as a blink rather than a squint.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BlobAvatar(props: { name: string; seed?: string; shape?: BlobShape; color?: BlobColor; face?: BlobFace; size?: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\" | \"xl\" | \"2xl\" | number; animated?: boolean; decorative?: boolean; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `name: string`\n  Accessible name and default seed.\n- `seed: string`\n  Seed for look and wobble when it should not follow the name (for example a stable id).\n- `shape: \"blob\" | \"squircle\" | \"star\" | \"flower\" | \"diamond\" | \"shield\" | \"cloud\"`\n  Overrides the seeded shape.\n- `color: \"lime\" | \"mint\" | \"sky\" | \"violet\" | \"peach\" | \"butter\" | \"pink\" | \"coral\"`\n  Overrides the seeded colour.\n- `face: \"dots\" | \"happy\" | \"sleepy\" | \"wink\" | \"surprised\" | \"none\"`\n  Overrides the seeded face.\n- `size: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\" | \"xl\" | \"2xl\" | number` — default `\"md\"`\n  Preset or pixel size.\n- `animated: boolean` — default `true`\n  Morph and blink; forced off under reduced motion.\n- `decorative: boolean` — default `false`\n  aria-hidden instead of role=\"img\" with the name.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Overlay such as a status dot.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the avatar box.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Shape, colour and face picked from a string, so the same name always gets the same blob. */\nexport function blobLook(seed: string): { shape: BlobShape; color: BlobColor; face: BlobFace } {\n  const h = hashSeed(seed);\n  return {\n    color: COLOR_KEYS[h % COLOR_KEYS.length] ?? \"lime\",\n    shape: BLOB_SHAPES[(h >>> 4) % BLOB_SHAPES.length] ?? \"blob\",\n    face: BLOB_FACES[(h >>> 8) % 4] ?? \"dots\",\n  };\n}\n\nfunction polar(radius: (angle: number) => number): Pt[] {\n  return Array.from({ length: SAMPLES }, (_, i) => {\n    const a = -Math.PI / 2 + (i / SAMPLES) * Math.PI * 2;\n    const r = radius(a + Math.PI / 2); // 0 points straight up\n    return [Math.cos(a) * r, Math.sin(a) * r] as const;\n  });\n}\n\n/** Even arc-length samples along a closed polygon; the spline later rounds its corners. */\nfunction polygon(verts: Pt[]): Pt[] {\n  const lens = verts.map((v, i) => {\n    const w = verts[(i + 1) % verts.length] ?? v;\n    return Math.hypot(w[0] - v[0], w[1] - v[1]);\n  });\n  const total = lens.reduce((sum, l) => sum + l, 0);\n  const out: Pt[] = [];\n  let seg = 0;\n  let start = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < SAMPLES; i++) {\n    const d = (i / SAMPLES) * total;\n    while (seg < verts.length - 1 && start + (lens[seg] ?? 0) < d) start += lens[seg++] ?? 0;\n    const v = verts[seg] ?? ([0, 0] as const);\n    const w = verts[(seg + 1) % verts.length] ?? v;\n    const t = (d - start) / (lens[seg] || 1);\n    out.push([v[0] + (w[0] - v[0]) * t, v[1] + (w[1] - v[1]) * t]);\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\n/** Unit outline (radius about 1, y down) for a shape; `rand` adds the per-seed lumps. */\nfunction outline(shape: BlobShape, rand: () => number): Pt[] {\n  switch (shape) {\n    case \"squircle\":\n      return polar((a) => 0.84 * (Math.abs(Math.cos(a)) ** 4 + Math.abs(Math.sin(a)) ** 4) ** -0.25);\n    case \"star\":\n      return polar((a) => 0.58 + 0.42 * ((1 + Math.cos(5 * a)) / 2) ** 1.4);\n    case \"flower\":\n      return polar((a) => 0.74 + 0.26 * Math.abs(Math.cos(3 * a)) ** 0.6);\n    case \"diamond\":\n      return polygon([[0, -1], [0.9, 0], [0, 1], [-0.9, 0]]);\n    case \"shield\":\n      return polygon([[-0.84, -0.8], [0, -0.94], [0.84, -0.8], [0.8, 0.18], [0, 0.98], [-0.8, 0.18]]);\n    case \"cloud\":\n      return polar((a) => 0.8 + 0.2 * Math.abs(Math.cos(2.5 * a)) ** 0.5).map(([x, y]) => [x * 1.06, Math.min(y, 0.66)] as const);\n    case \"blob\": {\n      const lumps = [2, 3, 4].map((k) => ({ k, amp: randRange(rand, 0.025, 0.075), phase: randRange(rand, 0, Math.PI * 2) }));\n      return polar((a) => 0.92 + lumps.reduce((sum, l) => sum + l.amp * Math.cos(l.k * a + l.phase), 0));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nconst round = (v: number) => Math.round(v * 10) / 10;\n\n/** Closed Catmull-Rom spline through the points, as cubic Béziers in viewBox units. */\nfunction toPath(points: Pt[]): string {\n  const p = points.map(([x, y]) => [CX + x * RADIUS, CY + y * RADIUS] as const);\n  const n = p.length;\n  const at = (i: number): Pt => p[((i % n) + n) % n] ?? [CX, CY];\n  let d = `M${round(at(0)[0])} ${round(at(0)[1])}`;\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const [p0, p1, p2, p3] = [at(i - 1), at(i), at(i + 1), at(i + 2)];\n    d += `C${round(p1[0] + (p2[0] - p0[0]) / 6)} ${round(p1[1] + (p2[1] - p0[1]) / 6)} ${round(p2[0] - (p3[0] - p1[0]) / 6)} ${round(p2[1] - (p3[1] - p1[1]) / 6)} ${round(p2[0])} ${round(p2[1])}`;\n  }\n  return `${d}Z`;\n}\n\n/**\n * The resting path of a shape plus two wobbled frames. A seeded tilt keeps two\n * blobs of the same shape from looking stamped; the frames push each sample\n * in and out along its own radius with two out-of-phase waves.\n */\nexport function blobPaths(shape: BlobShape, seed: string): { rest: string; frames: [string, string, string, string] } {\n  const rand = createRng(`${shape}:${seed}`);\n  const tilt = randRange(rand, -0.14, 0.14);\n  const base = outline(shape, rand).map(([x, y]) => [x * Math.cos(tilt) - y * Math.sin(tilt), x * Math.sin(tilt) + y * Math.cos(tilt)] as const);\n  const phase = randRange(rand, 0, Math.PI * 2);\n  const wobble = (k: number) =>\n    toPath(\n      base.map(([x, y], i) => {\n        const theta = (i / SAMPLES) * Math.PI * 2;\n        const ph = phase + (k * Math.PI * 2) / 3;\n        const f = 1 + WOBBLE * (0.6 * Math.sin(2 * theta + ph) + 0.4 * Math.sin(3 * theta - 2 * ph));\n        return [x * f, y * f] as const;\n      }),\n    );\n  const rest = toPath(base);\n  return { rest, frames: [rest, wobble(1), wobble(2), rest] };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or rAF: the morph and blink are SMIL animations owned by the SVG and stop when the element unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under prefers-reduced-motion the SVG animate and animateTransform elements are not rendered at all, so the avatar shows its resting outline with open eyes; shape, colour and face are unchanged.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Morph frames must have the same number of points and commands; mixing shapes inside one animate snaps instead of morphing.\n- Round path numbers before rendering (here to 0.1): long floats bloat the markup and can differ between the server and the browser, which breaks hydration.\n- SMIL ignores prefers-reduced-motion, so the animate elements are only rendered when motion is allowed; do not rely on a CSS media query to stop them.\n- Pass decorative when the name already sits next to the avatar, otherwise screen readers hear every agent name twice.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Children are overlaid on the avatar box, so a status dot can sit on its corner.\n- BLOB_COLORS holds plain artwork colours (fill and edge) that stay the same in light and dark themes; they are only used inside the SVG.\n- blobLook and blobPaths are exported for pickers and for drawing the same blob in a canvas or an OG image.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe same name renders the same shape, colour and face on every load; shape, color and face props override them; data-shape, data-color and data-face reflect the result; with reduced motion no animate element is in the DOM; sizes render at 24, 32, 40, 56, 80 and 128 px.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "board-empty-state": "# Build: Board Empty State\n\nWhat an empty project board column shows instead of a blank grey strip: a small illustration of three ticket cards stacked on a translucent panel, a short title such as \"Nothing in progress\", one line of help and an \"Add ticket\" button. The art is built from plain elements filled with the kit's `project-board-empty-*` tokens, so it flips to the neutral-700 family in dark mode with no images. On hover, keyboard focus or an `active` flag the back and middle cards fan out to either side and the front card lifts. A larger `board` size covers a board with no tickets at all.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nEmpty board or column: three ticket panels stacked on a soft surface that fan out on hover, with copy and a create button.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The illustration is a `relative` box with the `--color-project-board-empty-panel` fill and the 20px column radius. Three absolutely positioned spans sit inside it: the back card (narrowest, highest, `-empty-back`), the middle card (`-empty-middle`) and the front card (widest, lowest, `-empty-front`). Each is inset more from the sides the further back it is, so the stack reads as depth without any shadows on the back layers.\n2. The front card is a flex column holding the detail bars, all `rounded-full` and filled with `--color-project-board-empty-detail` (white in light, neutral-500 in dark): a tag pill and an assignee dot on the first row, then two title lines pushed to the bottom with `mt-auto`, the second shorter and at 70% opacity.\n3. The root carries `group/board-empty`. Every layer transitions `translate`, `rotate` and `opacity` over 300ms ease-out with `origin-bottom`, and the fan is three variant stacks on the same values: `group-hover`, `group-has-focus-visible` (Tab onto the button) and `group-data-active` (the `active` prop). The back card turns -6° and slides left, the middle card turns 4° and slides right, and the front card rises 4px.\n4. Mounting plays an entrance through `@starting-style` (`starting:translate-y-3 starting:opacity-0`). The middle and front layers carry 50ms and 100ms delays, so the cards settle back to front whenever a column empties.\n5. Copy and action are ordinary content under the art: a heading (level set by `headingLevel`), a `text-pretty` description, and either `action` or a primary Button with a plus icon built from `onCreate` / `createLabel`. The button is described by the description text. The root is `role=\"group\"` labelled by the heading so the action is announced in context.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `art size = 160×112 column, 224×144 board`\n  The column size leaves 50px of air on either side inside a 260px column; the board size reads at a glance in a wide empty area without competing with the heading.\n- `layer insets = back 32px, middle 20px, front 12px from the sides (column)`\n  Each layer is about 12px wider than the one behind it, which is enough to see three distinct cards at a small size.\n- `fan = back -6° / -16px, middle +4° / +16px, front -4px`\n  Opposite turns spread the stack like a hand of cards; small angles keep the back cards inside the panel corners.\n- `entrance = 300ms ease-out from 12px below, 0 / 50 / 100ms delays`\n  Short enough to finish before a reader reaches the button; the stagger shows that the art is a stack, not one shape.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BoardEmptyState(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"title\"> & { size?: \"column\" | \"board\"; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; onCreate?: () => void; createLabel?: string; action?: ReactNode; active?: boolean; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element; function BoardEmptyIllustration(props: { size?: \"column\" | \"board\"; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `size: \"column\" | \"board\"` — default `\"column\"`\n  Art size, spacing, heading style and button size.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"No tickets yet\"`\n  Heading under the art; also labels the group.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  One muted line of help; describes the create button.\n- `onCreate: () => void`\n  Renders the primary create button.\n- `createLabel: string` — default `\"New ticket\"`\n  Label of the create button.\n- `action: ReactNode`\n  Custom actions in place of the default button.\n- `active: boolean` — default `false`\n  Holds the cards fanned out, for example while something is dragged over.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4` — default `3`\n  Heading element for the title.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Three ticket-shaped panels stacked on the translucent empty-panel surface:\n * back and middle peek out above the front one, which carries the detail bars\n * (tag pill, assignee dot, two title lines). Inside a `group/board-empty`\n * the back and middle panels fan out to either side and the front one lifts.\n */\nexport function BoardEmptyIllustration({ size = \"column\", className }: BoardEmptyIllustrationProps) {\n  const detail = \"rounded-full bg-(--color-project-board-empty-detail)\";\n  return (\n    <div\n      aria-hidden\n      data-board-empty-art\n      className={cx(\n        \"relative isolate shrink-0 rounded-2-5xl bg-(--color-project-board-empty-panel)\",\n        styles.art[size],\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <span data-layer=\"back\" className={cx(layer, fan.back, styles.back[size], \"bg-(--color-project-board-empty-back)\")} />\n      <span\n        data-layer=\"middle\"\n        className={cx(layer, fan.middle, styles.middle[size], \"bg-(--color-project-board-empty-middle) delay-50\")}\n      />\n      <span\n        data-layer=\"front\"\n        className={cx(\n          layer,\n          fan.front,\n          styles.front[size],\n          \"flex flex-col bg-(--color-project-board-empty-front) shadow-xs delay-100\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <span className=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n          <span className={cx(detail, styles.pill[size])} />\n          <span className={cx(detail, styles.dot[size])} />\n        </span>\n        <span className={cx(detail, styles.line[size], \"mt-auto w-4/5\")} />\n        <span className={cx(detail, styles.line[size], \"w-1/2 opacity-70\")} />\n      </span>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface BoardEmptyStateProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"title\"> {\n  /** \"column\" fits a 272px board column; \"board\" fills an empty board or filtered view. */\n  size?: BoardEmptyStateSize;\n  title?: ReactNode;\n  description?: ReactNode;\n  /** Shows the default primary button with a plus icon. */\n  onCreate?: () => void;\n  createLabel?: string;\n  /** Replaces the default button, e.g. a pair of buttons or a link. */\n  action?: ReactNode;\n  /** Holds the panels fanned out, e.g. while a card is dragged over the column. */\n  active?: boolean;\n  headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4;\n  ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement>;\n}\n\nexport function BoardEmptyState({\n  size = \"column\",\n  title = \"No tickets yet\",\n  description,\n  onCreate,\n  createLabel = \"New ticket\",\n  action,\n  active = false,\n  headingLevel = 3,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: BoardEmptyStateProps) {\n  const titleId = useId();\n  const descriptionId = useId();\n  const Heading = `h${headingLevel}` as const;\n  const button =\n    action ??\n    (onCreate ? (\n      <Button\n        size={size === \"board\" ? \"medium\" : \"small\"}\n        leadingIcon={RiAddLine}\n        onClick={onCreate}\n        aria-describedby={description ? descriptionId : undefined}\n      >\n        {createLabel}\n      </Button>\n    ) : null);\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={ref}\n      role=\"group\"\n      aria-labelledby={titleId}\n      data-board-empty={size}\n      data-active={active ? \"\" : undefined}\n      className={cx(\n        \"group/board-empty flex flex-col items-center justify-center text-center\",\n        styles.root[size],\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <BoardEmptyIllustration size={size} />\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col items-center gap-1\">\n        <Heading id={titleId} className={cx(\"text-text-primary\", styles.title[size])}>\n          {title}\n        </Heading>\n        {description && (\n          <p id={descriptionId} className={cx(\"text-pretty text-text-secondary\", styles.description[size])}>\n            {description}\n          </p>\n        )}\n      </div>\n      {button && <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-2\">{button}</div>}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: no effects, timers or listeners. The entrance is CSS `@starting-style`, which runs once per mount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The entrance and the fan are both written behind `motion-safe:`, so with reduced motion the stack renders still and in place. Hover, focus and `active` change nothing in the art, and the heading and button work the same.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing `bg-project-board-empty-front`: the empty tokens are plain theme variables with no utility behind them. Use the variable shorthand `bg-(--color-project-board-empty-front)`.\n- Putting the fan behind a bare `hover:` on the art. The art is `aria-hidden` and small, so the fan never runs for keyboard users; hang it on the root group and include `group-has-focus-visible`.\n- Applying `motion-reduce:rotate-none` next to `group-hover:-rotate-6`: both are variants and the later one in the stylesheet wins. Gate the fan and the entrance with `motion-safe:` instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Drop it into Kanban Column's `empty` prop; while a card is dragged over an empty column the column shows its drop slot in place of the empty state.\n- The demo starts with one ticket in In progress, so To do (first on a phone) and Review show the column size. \"Clear board\" empties everything and swaps the board for the large size, whose \"New ticket\" button brings the board back.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith one ticket in In progress, the To do and Review columns each show the three-card stack, a title (\"Nothing queued\", \"Nothing to review\"), one line of help and an \"Add ticket\" button. Hovering a column's empty state fans the back and middle cards apart and lifts the front card. Clicking \"Add ticket\" in Review puts \"New ticket 1\" there and the empty state goes. \"Clear board\" swaps the board for the large empty state; its \"New ticket\" button brings back the board with one ticket in To do. In dark mode the cards are neutral-700 shades on a neutral-800 panel with neutral-500 detail bars.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "breadcrumb": "# Build: Breadcrumb\n\nA `<nav>` holding an ordered list of ancestor pages, with a small chevron placed between items automatically. Each `BreadcrumbItem` decides its own element: a Next `Link` when it has `href`, a `<button>` when it has `onClick`, and a non-interactive `aria-current=\"page\"` label when it is `current`. Ancestors are quiet tertiary text that lifts to secondary on a soft hover pill; the current page sits one step darker and never reacts.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAncestor-page trail with auto-inserted chevrons; each item is a link, a button or the current page, with optional 16px icons.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Breadcrumb` runs `Children.toArray(children).filter(isValidElement)`, which drops `null`, `false` and stray strings, so conditional items can be written inline. It maps the survivors into a `<Fragment key={item.key ?? index}>` and prepends a `ChevronRightSmall` (`size-3 shrink-0 text-text-tertiary`, `aria-hidden`) before every item except the first. Consumers never place separators by hand.\n2. The wrapper is `<nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\">` (override with `aria-label`) set to `flex w-full items-center overflow-x-auto` with the scrollbar hidden (`[scrollbar-width:none]` plus `[&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden`). Inside, an `<ol>` lays items out with `gap-2.5` and `px-1`. A long trail scrolls sideways instead of wrapping, and every item is `whitespace-nowrap`.\n3. `BreadcrumbItem` builds one `content` fragment: an optional `icon` component at `size-4 shrink-0` with `aria-hidden`, then `children`. The icon inherits the item colour.\n4. With `current`, the item is an `<li aria-current=\"page\">` in `text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary`, with no hover or focus styles.\n5. Otherwise the `<li>` wraps a Next `Link` (when `href` is set) or a `<button type=\"button\">` (when it is not) sharing one class string: `-mx-1 rounded-md px-1 py-0.5`, `text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary`, `hover:bg-background-primary-hover hover:text-text-secondary`, a 150ms colour transition and a `focus-visible:ring-2` focus ring. The `-mx-1` cancels the `px-1`, so the hover pill grows outward and the text stays exactly on the 10px rhythm.\n6. The chevron gets `rtl:rotate-180` from the shared chevron helper, so the trail reads right-to-left with arrows pointing the right way without any prop.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `item gap = gap-2.5 (10px)`\n  The Figma breadcrumb (node 3731:3011) spaces chevrons 10px from each label. The interactive items use `-mx-1 px-1` so their hover pill does not change this spacing.\n- `chevron = size-3 (12px), 12×12 viewBox, 1.5px stroke`\n  Small enough to read as punctuation rather than a control at the 12px caption type size. It uses `currentColor`, so `text-text-tertiary` sets its colour.\n- `type style = text-caption-1-medium`\n  Caption 1 Medium (12/16) is the Figma spec. A breadcrumb sits above a page title and must stay visually subordinate to it.\n- `hover transition = 150ms transition-colors`\n  Matches the kit's other ghost surfaces, so moving the pointer along the trail feels like the rest of the UI.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Breadcrumb(props: { children: ReactNode; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction BreadcrumbItem(props: { children?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; href?: string; onClick?: () => void; current?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `Breadcrumb.aria-label: string` — default `\"Breadcrumb\"`\n  Accessible name of the `<nav>` landmark.\n- `BreadcrumbItem.href: string`\n  Renders the item as a Next `Link`.\n- `BreadcrumbItem.onClick: () => void`\n  Used when there is no `href`: the item renders as a `<button type=\"button\">`.\n- `BreadcrumbItem.current: boolean` — default `false`\n  Marks the active page with `aria-current=\"page\"`. Not interactive, darker text.\n- `BreadcrumbItem.icon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  16px leading icon component that inherits the item colour.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Breadcrumb({\n  children,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Breadcrumb\",\n  className,\n  ref,\n}: BreadcrumbProps) {\n  const items = Children.toArray(children).filter(isValidElement);\n\n  return (\n    <nav\n      ref={ref}\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-full items-center overflow-x-auto [scrollbar-width:none] [&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <ol className=\"flex items-center gap-2.5 px-1\">\n        {items.map((item, index) => (\n          <Fragment key={item.key ?? index}>\n            {/* An <ol> may only hold <li>s; aria-hidden keeps the separator out of\n                the item count screen readers announce. */}\n            {index > 0 && (\n              <li aria-hidden className=\"flex shrink-0\">\n                <ChevronRightSmall className=\"size-3 text-text-tertiary\" />\n              </li>\n            )}\n            {item}\n          </Fragment>\n        ))}\n      </ol>\n    </nav>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. The only animation is a 150ms colour transition on hover, which is not motion and is left as is.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not put separators in the children. `Breadcrumb` inserts a chevron before every item after the first, so a hand-written \"/\" would show up as an extra crumb with a chevron in front of it.\n- Do not mark the current page with an `href`. When `current` is true the item ignores `href` and `onClick`. That is intentional: a link to the page you are on is noise for screen-reader users.\n- Do not wrap the trail to a second line. It is designed to scroll horizontally with a hidden scrollbar, so keep the `whitespace-nowrap` on items when restyling them.\n- Do not pass an icon element (`icon={<RiHome5Line />}`). The prop takes the component so the item can size it to 16px.\n\n## Notes\n\n- An item with neither `href` nor `onClick` and without `current` still renders a `<button>` that does nothing. Use `current` for the active page.\n- The file imports `next/link`. Outside Next, swap it for a plain `<a>` or your router's link.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThree items render on one line with a chevron between each pair and none before the first. Hovering an ancestor darkens its text and shows a rounded tinted background without moving any text. The last item is darker, has `aria-current=\"page\"` and does not react to hover or clicks. Tab reaches only the ancestor items, each showing a focus ring. In an RTL container the chevrons point left. A trail wider than its container scrolls sideways with no visible scrollbar.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "browser-chrome-frame": "# Build: Browser Chrome Frame\n\nA title bar plus a content well, drawn entirely in CSS, wrapped around anything you want to present as a live site rather than a picture of one. The address pill copies the URL and confirms with a checkmark that draws along its own path and holds for 1600 ms. Every state change happens inside a fixed-width pill, so nothing in the bar ever reflows.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA macOS window frame with hover-revealed traffic lights and an address pill whose check draws itself on copy.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The frame is a flex column: a `shrink-0` title bar and a `min-h-0 flex-1` content well. `min-h-0` is what lets the well actually shrink inside a flex parent — without it a tall child pushes the frame past its container and the rounded bottom corners leave the viewport.\n2. The three window buttons are `11px` circles filled with the real macOS values (`#ff5f57`, `#febc2e`, `#28c840`) holding a `7px` SVG glyph at `opacity: 0`. The cluster is one Tailwind `group`, so hovering anywhere in the title bar fades all three glyphs in together — which is how the system chrome behaves, and hovering one button to reveal only that button is the tell of a reimplementation.\n3. Copying goes through `navigator.clipboard?.writeText?.(url)`. That returns `undefined` outside a secure context and a rejecting promise when the document is not focused, so both branches resolve to an explicit `failed` state rather than a swallowed catch. A pill that silently does nothing reads as a broken button.\n4. The confirmation checkmark carries `pathLength=\"1\"` and `strokeDasharray=\"1\"`, so `strokeDashoffset` moves between exactly `1` and `0` regardless of the path coordinates, and a single 320 ms `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)` transition draws it. The link icon it replaces crossfades over 180 ms in an absolutely-positioned 12 px box, so the icon slot never changes size.\n5. Both text labels — the URL and the confirmation — sit in the same CSS grid cell (`col-start-1 row-start-1`) and swap by opacity. The pill therefore cannot resize between states, so the URL never shifts sideways under the cursor that just clicked it.\n6. A single `setTimeout` ref holds the return to idle. Every entry point clears it first, so clicking the pill three times in a row extends one confirmation rather than queuing three overlapping ones.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `COPIED_MS = 1600`\n  How long the confirmed state holds. Deliberately long: at the 600-800 ms most implementations use, a user who looks back at the pill after moving their eyes to the clipboard target has already missed it, and clicks again. 1600 ms survives a glance away and back.\n- `DRAW_MS = 320`\n  Stroke-draw duration for the check. Below ~200 ms the draw is indistinguishable from a hard appear, which throws away the only cue that says \"this just happened now\"; past ~450 ms the confirmation is still arriving after the user has moved on.\n- `FADE_MS = 180`\n  Crossfade for the icon and label swap, and the traffic-light glyph reveal. Shorter than the draw on purpose — the check should finish last, so the eye lands on the completed mark rather than on a half-drawn one under settled text.\n- `traffic light fills = #ff5f57 / #febc2e / #28c840`\n  The actual macOS window-button values. This component reproduces a specific piece of system chrome, so these are content, not theme: recolouring them to the site palette turns the frame from \"a browser\" into \"a diagram of a browser\" and the recognition is gone.\n- `dot size = 11px`\n  Matches the system button diameter at 1x. At 8px the glyphs are unreadable; past ~14px the bar starts to read as a toy illustration rather than chrome.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction BrowserChromeFrame(props: { url: string; children: ReactNode; label?: string; onCopy?: (url: string) => void; copiedDurationMs?: number; className?: string; bodyClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `url: string`\n  Shown in the address pill and written to the clipboard on click.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The framed content. Sized by the frame, not by itself.\n- `label: string`\n  Display text for the pill. Defaults to `url` with the scheme stripped, which is what a browser shows.\n- `onCopy: (url: string) => void`\n  Fires after a successful clipboard write, never on failure.\n- `copiedDurationMs: number` — default `1600`\n  How long the confirmed state holds before returning to idle.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer frame.\n- `bodyClassName: string`\n  Merged onto the content well below the title bar.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst COPIED_MS = 1600;\nconst DRAW_MS = 320;\nconst FADE_MS = 180;\n\ntype CopyState = 'idle' | 'copied' | 'failed';\n\nexport function BrowserChromeFrame({\n  url,\n  children,\n  label,\n  onCopy,\n  copiedDurationMs = COPIED_MS,\n  className,\n  bodyClassName,\n}: BrowserChromeFrameProps) {\n  const [state, setState] = useState<CopyState>('idle');\n  const timer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const notify = useRef(onCopy);\n  notify.current = onCopy;\n\n  const reset = useCallback(() => {\n    if (timer.current !== null) {\n      clearTimeout(timer.current);\n      timer.current = null;\n    }\n  }, []);\n\n  // A pending timer outlives the component unless it is cleared. Left alone it\n  // calls setState on an unmounted tree — React 19 no longer warns about that,\n  // which makes it quieter, not less of a leak.\n  useEffect(() => reset, [reset]);\n\n  const copy = useCallback(() => {\n    // `navigator.clipboard` is undefined outside a secure context and its\n    // promise rejects when the document is not focused. Both are ordinary, so\n    // the failure has a state of its own rather than a swallowed catch: a pill\n    // that silently does nothing reads as a broken button.\n    const write = navigator.clipboard?.writeText?.(url);\n    if (!write) {\n      reset();\n      setState('failed');\n      timer.current = setTimeout(() => setState('idle'), copiedDurationMs);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    void write.then(\n      () => {\n        reset();\n        setState('copied');\n        notify.current?.(url);\n        timer.current = setTimeout(() => setState('idle'), copiedDurationMs);\n      },\n      () => {\n        reset();\n        setState('failed');\n        timer.current = setTimeout(() => setState('idle'), copiedDurationMs);\n      },\n    );\n  }, [url, copiedDurationMs, reset]);\n\n  const copied = state === 'copied';\n  const failed = state === 'failed';\n\n  // Reduced motion keeps the state change and drops only the tweening: the\n  // check is already fully drawn on the frame it appears, and the two labels\n  // hard-swap instead of crossfading. The confirmation is still legible for\n  // the same 1600 ms — the information was never the animation.\n  const drawMs = reduced ? 0 : DRAW_MS;\n  const fadeMs = reduced ? 0 : FADE_MS;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** One `clearTimeout` on the single pending reset timer, via an effect that returns the same `reset` callback every entry point already uses. There are no listeners, no rAF and no tweens — all motion is CSS transitions on mounted nodes, which the browser discards with the node.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The state machine is untouched: clicking still copies, the check still replaces the link icon, and the confirmation still holds for the full 1600 ms. What goes to zero is `DRAW_MS` and `FADE_MS`, so the check appears already fully drawn and the labels hard-swap instead of crossfading. The traffic-light glyphs still reveal on hover, without the fade.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Swapping the icon element instead of stacking both. Replacing a link icon with a check changes the rendered width by a pixel or two, the flex row re-solves, and the URL text jumps sideways at the exact moment the user is reading the confirmation.\n- Letting the label change the pill width. If the URL and \"Copied\" are the same node with different text, the pill resizes and the whole title bar reflows. Stacking them in one grid cell keeps the width pinned to the longer of the two, permanently.\n- Hard-coding the measured path length for the dash animation. The first time anyone nudges a coordinate in the checkmark, the offset no longer matches the path and the stroke either starts partly drawn or never completes. `pathLength=\"1\"` renormalises so the animation is coordinate-independent.\n- Assuming `navigator.clipboard` exists. On `http://` origins and inside some embedded webviews it is `undefined`, and calling it throws a TypeError that takes the click handler down with it. On a secure origin it still rejects if the document is not focused — a case that happens constantly in dev tools.\n- Not clearing the reset timer before starting a new one. Rapid clicks queue several timeouts and the confirmation flickers off at the first one's deadline rather than the last one's, so the state disappears while the user is still clicking.\n- Leaving the timer pending on unmount. It fires into a disposed tree; React 19 no longer warns, which makes it quieter, not less of a leak.\n- Reading the reduced-motion state and disabling the confirmation itself. The check is information, not decoration. Only the tweening is dropped.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The `group-hover` glyph reveal has no touch equivalent, and correctly so: there is no hover on touch, and firing the reveal on press would light up three buttons the user cannot click.\n- The content well sets `overflow-hidden` and `bg-bg`, which is what gives the embed its edge. Framing exists as much to bound the content as to decorate it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover the title bar and all three glyphs fade in together. Click the pill: the link icon fades out, a checkmark draws itself left to right, \"Copied\" replaces the URL, the pill does not change width by a single pixel, and the state returns to the URL 1600 ms later. Click three times quickly and you get one confirmation that ends 1600 ms after the last click. On an `http://` origin the pill shows \"Press to copy\" instead of a silent no-op. With reduced motion the check is fully drawn on the first frame it appears.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "burst-audio-sync": "# Build: Chord-Synced Particle Burst\n\nA burst is N notes a fixed interval apart, each with a visual flare that has to appear on the same beat as its note. Audio is scheduled on `AudioContext.currentTime`, which is sample-accurate; the flares are driven by a rAF loop, which is not. This hook schedules both from one set of offsets and converts the audio times into wall-clock times via `getOutputTimestamp()`, so a flare is never more than one frame from its note however long the burst runs.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nFires an arpeggio and its visual flares off one schedule, so each flash lands on its own note.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Notes are scheduled ahead of time on the audio clock: for step `i`, `at = ctx.currentTime + LEAD + step * i`, then `gain.setValueAtTime(peak, at)` and `gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0.001, at + decay)`. Nothing is timed by `setTimeout`, so a busy main thread cannot skew a note.\n2. `audibleAt(ctx, at)` converts that audio time into a `performance.now()` millisecond value. `ctx.getOutputTimestamp()` returns a matched pair — the sample frame currently leaving the output device and the wall-clock instant it left — so the mapping is `ts.performanceTime + (at - ts.contextTime) * 1000`. Hardware output latency is already inside that pair; adding `outputLatency` on top double-counts it.\n3. When `getOutputTimestamp` is unavailable, or has not rendered a frame yet and returns `contextTime: 0`, the fallback is `performance.now() + (at - ctx.currentTime) * 1000 + min(outputLatency, 0.05) * 1000`. `currentTime` is the write head, not the speaker, so the latency has to be added back in this branch only.\n4. Each step is pushed onto a queue sorted by wall-clock time, and a rAF loop pops every entry whose time has passed and calls `onStep(index, total)`. Overlapping bursts merge into one sorted queue rather than one loop per burst.\n5. The loop is subscribed only while the queue is non-empty — `useRaf(tick, running)` — and unsubscribes itself on the frame it drains the last step, so an idle instrument holds no animation frame.\n6. The `AudioContext` is constructed inside `fire()`, which the caller invokes from a click handler, and `resume()` is called if the browser still hands it back suspended. Constructing it at mount produces a suspended context whose scheduled notes are silently discarded.\n7. `fire(steps)` caps at `chord.length` and returns the count actually scheduled, so a caller that has fewer flares available than the chord has notes hears exactly as many notes as it shows.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `step = 0.46`\n  Seconds between notes, and the same number the visuals use. Around 130 bpm in eighths — fast enough to read as one gesture rather than four separate events, slow enough that each flare is individually visible. Below ~0.2s the flares smear into one flash; above ~0.7s the burst stops sounding like a chord and starts sounding like four taps.\n- `gain = 0.15`\n  Peak amplitude of a single sine. Four notes overlapping at 1.0 would sum past full scale and hard-clip the destination; 0.15 leaves headroom for the tails of earlier notes still decaying under later ones.\n- `SILENCE = 0.001`\n  -60 dB, the ramp target. `exponentialRampToValueAtTime` is mathematically undefined at 0 and throws; ramping to a literal 0 is the single most common Web Audio error. -60 dB is inaudible.\n- `decay = 0.4`\n  Seconds from peak to silence. Slightly shorter than the 0.46s step so a note has essentially finished before the next arrives — overlap past about 0.6s turns the arpeggio into a chord and the individual attacks stop being audible.\n- `TAIL = 0.1`\n  Extra seconds before `osc.stop()`. Stopping an oscillator exactly at the end of its ramp truncates the last samples and can produce a faint click; 0.1s of -60 dB signal costs nothing and guarantees the stop is inaudible.\n- `LEAD = 0.02`\n  Seconds of lead before the first note. One render quantum at 44.1 kHz is 128 samples, about 2.9 ms; 20 ms covers several quanta plus the jitter in getting from the click to the scheduling call. Scheduling at exactly `currentTime` means the first note is already partly in the past and its attack is clipped.\n- `MAX_LATENCY = 0.05`\n  Ceiling on the fallback latency compensation, in seconds. Bluetooth output can report 150-300 ms; delaying the visual by that much to match makes the click itself feel broken. Capping trades exact sync on pathological hardware for a UI that still responds.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useBurstAudioSync(options?: { chord?: readonly number[]; step?: number; gain?: number; decay?: number; onStep?: (index: number, total: number) => void; muted?: boolean }): { fire: (steps?: number) => number; unlocked: boolean }\n```\n\n- `chord: readonly number[]` — default `[261.63, 329.63, 392, 523.25]`\n  One frequency in Hz per step. Caps how many notes a single `fire` can schedule.\n- `step: number` — default `0.46`\n  Seconds between steps. Shared by the audio schedule and the visual queue.\n- `gain: number` — default `0.15`\n  Peak gain of one note. Divided by the note count when the stagger collapses.\n- `decay: number` — default `0.4`\n  Seconds for the exponential ramp from `gain` down to -60 dB.\n- `onStep: (index: number, total: number) => void`\n  Called on the frame nearest each note. `index` is the position in the chord.\n- `muted: boolean` — default `false`\n  Skips audio entirely. Visual steps still fire on the same schedule, so the UI is unchanged.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** `exponentialRampToValueAtTime` is undefined at 0, so silence is -60 dB. */\nconst SILENCE = 0.001;\n/** Seconds of dead air after the ramp before the oscillator is stopped. */\nconst TAIL = 0.1;\n/** Seconds of lead before the first note. Absorbs one graph render quantum. */\nconst LEAD = 0.02;\n/** Ceiling on latency compensation, in seconds. */\nconst MAX_LATENCY = 0.05;\n\n/**\n * Wall-clock time at which a sample scheduled at `contextTime` is heard.\n *\n * The `getOutputTimestamp()` pair is measured *at the output device*, so the\n * hardware latency is already folded into it — adding `outputLatency` on top\n * would double-count it and push every flare late.\n */\nfunction audibleAt(ctx: AudioContext, contextTime: number): number {\n  if (typeof ctx.getOutputTimestamp === 'function') {\n    const ts = ctx.getOutputTimestamp();\n    // Before the graph has rendered anything both fields are 0. Trusting that\n    // pair maps every future note to wall-clock zero, and the whole burst\n    // flashes at once on the very next frame.\n    if (\n      ts.contextTime !== undefined &&\n      ts.performanceTime !== undefined &&\n      ts.contextTime > 0\n    ) {\n      return ts.performanceTime + (contextTime - ts.contextTime) * 1000;\n    }\n  }\n  // Fallback. `currentTime` is the time of the sample being *written*, which\n  // runs ahead of the speaker by roughly the output latency, so add it back.\n  const reported = Number.isFinite(ctx.outputLatency)\n    ? ctx.outputLatency\n    : ctx.baseLatency;\n  const latency = Number.isFinite(reported) ? Math.min(reported, MAX_LATENCY) : 0;\n  return performance.now() + (contextTime - ctx.currentTime) * 1000 + latency * 1000;\n}\n\nexport function useBurstAudioSync(options: UseBurstAudioSyncOptions = {}): BurstAudioSync {\n  const { chord = C_MAJOR, step = 0.46, gain = 0.15, decay = 0.4 } = options;\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [unlocked, setUnlocked] = useState(false);\n  const [running, setRunning] = useState(false);\n\n  const ctxRef = useRef<AudioContext | null>(null);\n  const queue = useRef<QueuedStep[]>([]);\n\n  // Everything `fire` reads lives in one ref, so `fire` keeps a stable\n  // identity and can be handed to a memoised child without re-binding it on\n  // every render.\n  const config = useRef({ chord, step, gain, decay, reduced, options });\n  config.current = { chord, step, gain, decay, reduced, options };\n\n  const fire = useCallback((steps?: number): number => {\n    const cfg = config.current;\n    const wanted = steps ?? cfg.chord.length;\n    const total = Math.min(Math.max(Math.trunc(wanted), 0), cfg.chord.length);\n    if (total === 0) return 0;\n\n    // Reduced motion collapses the arpeggio into a single chord: the feedback\n    // survives — it was asked for by a click, so it is not decoration — but\n    // nothing travels across the screen.\n    const stagger = cfg.reduced ? 0 : cfg.step;\n\n    let ctx: AudioContext | null = null;\n    if (!cfg.options.muted) {\n      ctx = ctxRef.current;\n      if (!ctx) {\n        // Constructed here and only here: we are inside the gesture handler\n        // that called `fire`, which is the one moment the autoplay policy\n        // lets a context start.\n        ctx = new AudioContext();\n        ctxRef.current = ctx;\n        setUnlocked(true);\n      }\n      if (ctx.state === 'suspended') void ctx.resume();\n    }\n\n    let baseMs = performance.now();\n\n    if (ctx) {\n      const start = ctx.currentTime + LEAD;\n      // Four sines at full gain sum to 4x the peak and clip the destination.\n      // They only ever overlap when the stagger is gone, so divide then.\n      const peak = stagger === 0 ? cfg.gain / total : cfg.gain;\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < total; i++) {\n        const freq = cfg.chord[i];\n        if (freq === undefined) continue;\n        const at = start + stagger * i;\n\n        const osc = ctx.createOscillator();\n        const env = ctx.createGain();\n        osc.type = 'sine';\n        osc.frequency.value = freq;\n        env.gain.setValueAtTime(peak, at);\n        // `cfg.decay`, not the closed-over `decay`. This callback is memoised\n        // with an empty dep array — deliberately, so a parent re-render never\n        // changes its identity — which freezes every prop it closes over at\n        // first render. Every other value here already comes through the ref;\n        // these two lines read the prop directly, so changing `decay` after\n        // mount left the envelope on whatever the first render happened to be.\n        env.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(SILENCE, at + cfg.decay);\n        osc.connect(env);\n        env.connect(ctx.destination);\n        osc.start(at);\n        osc.stop(at + cfg.decay + TAIL);\n        // Without this every burst leaves two live nodes attached to the\n        // destination. A few hundred bursts and the graph audibly degrades.\n        osc.onended = () => {\n          osc.disconnect();\n          env.disconnect();\n        };\n      }\n\n      baseMs = audibleAt(ctx, start);\n    }\n\n    const q = queue.current;\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i++) {\n      q.push({ at: baseMs + stagger * i * 1000, index: i, total });\n    }\n    // Two bursts can overlap; the drain below assumes the head is the\n    // earliest step, so keep the queue sorted rather than scanning it.\n    q.sort((a, b) => a.at - b.at);\n    setRunning(true);\n\n    return total;\n  }, []);\n\n  // The loop only exists while something is queued. An always-on rAF for a\n  // widget that fires twice a minute keeps the browser's animation frame\n  // pipeline alive for nothing.\n  useRaf(() => {\n    const q = queue.current;\n    const now = performance.now();\n    while (q.length > 0) {\n      const head = q[0];\n      if (head === undefined || head.at > now) break;\n      q.shift();\n      config.current.options.onStep?.(head.index, head.total);\n    }\n    if (q.length === 0) setRunning(false);\n  }, running);\n\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      queue.current.length = 0;\n      const ctx = ctxRef.current;\n      ctxRef.current = null;\n      // close() stops every scheduled note and releases the hardware. Chrome\n      // caps a document at ~6 live contexts and throws on the seventh.\n      if (ctx && ctx.state !== 'closed') void ctx.close();\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The unmount cleanup empties the pending-step queue and calls `ctx.close()`, which cancels every note still scheduled in the future and releases the hardware. The rAF subscription is owned by `useRaf` and released when `running` goes false or the component unmounts. Each oscillator disconnects itself and its gain node in `onended`.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The stagger collapses to zero: all notes are scheduled at the same audio time and every `onStep` fires on one frame, so the burst is a chord rather than a sequence travelling across the screen. Audio is not muted — it is direct feedback for a click, not ambient motion — but the per-note gain is divided by the note count because the notes now overlap.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Constructing the `AudioContext` at mount. Outside a user gesture the browser hands back a `suspended` context; `osc.start(at)` on it neither throws nor plays, so the bug presents as \"audio just does not work\" with a clean console.\n- Ramping the gain to 0. `exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0, t)` throws a `RangeError` — the target must be strictly positive. Ramp to 0.001 instead.\n- Using `setTimeout` for the notes so audio and visuals share a timer. Both then inherit the main thread's jitter, and on a frame that takes 80 ms the note lands 80 ms late while the sample-accurate alternative would not have moved.\n- Adding `outputLatency` on top of a `getOutputTimestamp()` mapping. That pair is measured at the output device and already contains the latency, so adding it again pushes every flare a full hardware buffer behind its note — the exact defect the conversion exists to prevent.\n- Trusting `getOutputTimestamp()` immediately after construction. Before the graph has rendered a quantum both fields read 0, which maps every future note to wall-clock zero and dumps the entire burst onto the next frame.\n- Never disconnecting the oscillator and gain. Both stay attached to the destination after the note ends; a few hundred bursts in one session and the graph is carrying hundreds of dead nodes, which shows up as rising CPU and eventually distortion.\n- Keeping a rAF loop running so the flare queue can be polled. The queue is empty almost all the time — subscribe on `fire`, unsubscribe on the frame the last step drains.\n- Letting the notes overlap at full gain when reduced motion collapses the stagger. Four sines at 0.15 sum to 0.6 and the summed peak clips; the per-note gain has to be divided by the note count in the collapsed case.\n- Forgetting `ctx.close()` on unmount. Chrome allows roughly six live `AudioContext` objects per document and throws on the next one, so a page that mounts this in a list silently stops producing sound after the sixth item.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The hook deliberately exposes no `play()` that can be called from an effect. `fire` is the only entry point and its contract is \"call me from a gesture handler\" — encoding the autoplay policy in the API rather than in a comment.\n- Two clocks, two policies. Reduced motion collapses the stagger but does not mute anything: the sound is feedback for a click the user made, not ambient decoration, and silencing it removes information.\n- `chord` is any frequency list. A minor triad is `[261.63, 311.13, 392]`; a single frequency makes it a one-shot tick with the same scheduling guarantees.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick a string: four flares appear 0.46s apart and each one coincides with a note — watch the fourth flare and hear the fourth note land together, not a beat apart. Click a string at either end and only one flare appears and only one note sounds. Nothing plays before the first click. Toggle sound off and the flares keep exactly the same timing. With reduced motion on, all four strings light in the same frame and you hear a chord. Unmount mid-burst and the remaining notes do not play.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "button": "# Build: Button\n\nA native `<button>` (and an `<a>` twin, `ButtonLink`) whose look is four style maps indexed by `variant` and `size`. The component draws its own icons from a component reference, so the consumer cannot pass the wrong icon size. The gradient fills of the primary and danger variants live in two `@utility` classes in the theme sheet, which fade a hover gradient in on a `::before` layer because CSS cannot interpolate `background-image`.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPrimary, secondary, ghost and danger buttons in three sizes, with gradient fills that fade on hover and a soft press.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every class comes from one `sortCx` object: `base` (layout, focus ring, press motion), `size` (height, padding, radius and type style per tier), `iconOnlySize` (the square override), `icon` (glyph size per tier), `label` (inner padding) and `variant`. `cx` is a `tailwind-merge` instance extended with the kit's custom type scale, so a consumer `className` such as `h-10` replaces the size tier's `h-9` rather than both landing in the class list.\n2. Icons are props that take a component (`leadingIcon={RiAddLine}`), not an element. The button renders it with the tier's size class — 20px at medium, 18px at small, 14px at xs — plus `aria-hidden`. Navigation glyphs (left/right arrows, sidebar fold) are listed in `directionalIconClass` and get `rtl:rotate-180`, so a \"Next\" arrow points the reading direction in Arabic or Hebrew without the caller knowing.\n3. Primary and danger paint with `bg-button-primary` and `bg-button-danger`. Each sets the default gradient as `background-image`, then a `::before` with `inset: 0`, `z-index: -1` and `border-radius: inherit` holds the hover gradient at opacity 0. Hover fades that layer to 1 over `--button-transition-ms`. `isolation: isolate` on the button is what keeps the negative-z layer above the button's own background and below its label.\n4. The press is `button-press-motion`: colours transition in 150ms while `transform` takes 420ms back to rest. `:active` scales to 0.98 and drops the transform duration to 220ms, so the button goes down fast and comes back slowly. Hover never touches `transform`, so hover colour changes never inherit the long press timing.\n5. Disabled works for both `disabled` and `aria-disabled=\"true\"`. The second matters when a disabled button must stay focusable so a tooltip can explain why. Every variant styles both selectors and the press motion turns itself off for both.\n6. `type` defaults to `\"button\"`. A bare `<button>` inside a `<form>` is a submit button, so a Cancel or \"Add row\" button would otherwise submit the form it sits in.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `heights = 36 / 32 / 24 px (medium / small / xs)`\n  Taken 1:1 from the Figma variant matrix. Medium matches the kit's inputs (also 36px), so a button next to a field lines up with no wrapper. Xs exists for dense surfaces such as calendar event cards and is the only tier that switches to the 12px caption type style.\n- `small icon-only size = size-8 p-0`\n  An 18px icon plus 8px padding on each side gives 34px, but Figma pins the small square at 32. Removing the padding and forcing the size keeps an icon button the same height as a small labelled button beside it. Medium needs no override because 8 + 20 + 8 is already 36.\n- `--button-transition-ms = 150ms`\n  Shared by the hover-gradient fade and the colour transitions, so the fill and the label change together. Much shorter and the gradient fade looks like a snap; much longer and fast pointer sweeps across a toolbar leave a trail of half-lit buttons.\n- `press = scale(0.98), 220ms down / 420ms back`\n  Two percent is enough to feel without visibly moving the label off its baseline. The asymmetric duration is the point: press feedback has to be immediate, and the return can be soft because nothing is waiting on it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Button(props: ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> & { variant?: \"primary\" | \"secondary\" | \"ghost\" | \"danger\"; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\" | \"xs\"; iconOnly?: boolean; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; trailingIcon?: IconComponent; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction ButtonLink(props: AnchorHTMLAttributes<HTMLAnchorElement> & { …same visual props }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `variant: \"primary\" | \"secondary\" | \"ghost\" | \"danger\"` — default `\"primary\"`\n  Gradient fill (primary, danger), bordered surface (secondary) or no surface until hovered (ghost).\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\" | \"xs\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36, 32 or 24px tall. Sets padding, radius, icon size and type style together.\n- `iconOnly: boolean` — default `false`\n  Square button showing only `leadingIcon`. Children and `trailingIcon` are ignored, so pass `aria-label`.\n- `leadingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon component drawn before the label at the tier's size. Directional glyphs mirror in RTL.\n- `trailingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon component drawn after the label.\n- `type: \"button\" | \"submit\" | \"reset\"` — default `\"button\"`\n  Native button type. Defaults to `button` so it never submits a surrounding form by accident.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge`, so conflicting utilities replace the defaults instead of fighting them.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Button({\n  variant = \"primary\",\n  size = \"medium\",\n  iconOnly = false,\n  leadingIcon: Leading,\n  trailingIcon: Trailing,\n  children,\n  className,\n  type = \"button\",\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: ButtonProps) {\n  return (\n    <button\n      ref={ref}\n      type={type}\n      className={cx(\n        styles.base,\n        styles.size[size],\n        styles.variant[variant],\n        iconOnly && styles.iconOnlySize[size],\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      {Leading ? <Leading className={cx(styles.icon[size], directionalIconClass(Leading))} aria-hidden /> : null}\n      {!iconOnly && children !== undefined && children !== null && (\n        <span className={styles.label[size]}>{children}</span>\n      )}\n      {!iconOnly && Trailing ? (\n        <Trailing className={cx(styles.icon[size], directionalIconClass(Trailing))} aria-hidden />\n      ) : null}\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects. All motion is CSS transitions that end on their own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The press scale and every transition on `button-press-motion` are removed under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, so states change instantly and the button never moves. The hover-gradient layer still swaps, because colour feedback carries meaning.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Swapping the gradient in `:hover` with another `background-image`. It snaps, because `background-image` does not interpolate. The fade only works because the hover gradient sits on its own layer and only its `opacity` changes.\n- Removing `isolation: isolate` from the gradient utilities. The `::before` sits at `z-index: -1`, and without a new stacking context it slips behind the button's parent background, so the hover state disappears on any card with a fill.\n- Passing an icon element (`leadingIcon={<RiAddLine />}`). The prop takes the component, so the button can size it. An element bypasses the size class and the glyph renders at Remix Icon's default 24px, which breaks the 36px height.\n- Shipping an icon-only button without `aria-label`. The glyph is `aria-hidden` and there is no text, so screen readers announce just \"button\".\n- Relying on the browser default `type`. Inside a form it is `submit`. The component defaults to `\"button\"`, so pass `type=\"submit\"` explicitly on the button that should submit.\n- Putting `transition-all` on a consumer `className`. It overrides the split timing of `button-press-motion` and gives hover colour changes the slow 420ms press-return curve.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The style maps are exported as `buttonStyles`, so another control (a toolbar toggle, a file input label) can share the exact variant classes without wrapping a `<button>` in a `<button>`.\n- The kit's `IconButton`, `CloseButton` and `LinkButton` are separate items with their own shapes. `Button iconOnly` is the square form of a full button, with the same gradient and border.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFour variants render side by side at the same height. Hover the primary button and its gradient fades smoothly to the lighter hover gradient (no snap). Press any button and it dips slightly, then eases back more slowly than it went down. Tab to a button and a 2px focus ring appears offset from the edge. Disabled buttons, including `aria-disabled` ones, show the muted palette, do not dip on press and show a not-allowed cursor. An icon-only small button is exactly as tall as a small labelled one. A button inside a form does not submit it unless `type=\"submit\"`.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "button-group": "# Build: Button Group\n\nA `role=\"group\"` container that owns the border, radius and shadow, with plain `<button>` items inside it divided by 1px hairlines. Only the container is rounded; `overflow-hidden` clips the first and last items to its corners, so the items themselves stay square and never need first/last-child logic. A `selected` item keeps the hover fill and announces itself with `aria-pressed`, which makes the same markup work as an action row, a single-select range picker or a multi-select formatting bar.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSecondary buttons fused into one bordered strip with hairline dividers, for toolbars, range pickers and pressed toggles.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The container carries every outer surface: `rounded-2lg` (10px), a 1px `border-border-button-group`, `shadow-xs` and the primary background. `divide-x divide-border-button-default` draws a left border on every item except the first, so the dividers come from the parent and adding or removing items never leaves a doubled or missing line.\n2. `overflow-hidden` on the container is what rounds the outer items. A hover or pressed fill on the first item would otherwise paint square corners over the container's rounded border. Clipping at the container means the items carry no radius at all.\n3. Item heights are 34px (medium) and 30px (small) because the container's 1px top and bottom border add 2px, so the whole strip lands on the kit's 36 / 32px button heights and lines up with a `Button` or input beside it.\n4. Focus uses an inset ring (`ring-inset`) plus `focus-visible:relative z-10`. An outset ring would be clipped by the container's `overflow-hidden`, and without the z-index lift the next item's divider paints over the right edge of the ring. `isolate` on the container keeps that z-index local to the group.\n5. `selected` maps to the hover fill (`bg-background-primary-hover`) and sets `aria-pressed={selected || undefined}`. Unselected items omit the attribute entirely, so a plain action row is not announced as a set of toggles.\n6. Icons are component props rendered by the item at 20px (medium) or 18px (small) with `aria-hidden`; arrow and sidebar glyphs get `rtl:rotate-180` from `directionalIconClass`. With `iconOnly` the item becomes a 34 or 30px square and drops its label, so it needs an `aria-label`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `item height = 34px / 30px (medium / small)`\n  The container border adds 1px above and below, so the visible strip is exactly 36 or 32px, matching Button's secondary sizes. Sizing items at 36px would make the group 2px taller than every control next to it.\n- `horizontal padding = 12px / 10px`\n  Same as Button's secondary tiers, so a labelled item and a standalone button with the same text are the same width.\n- `transition = background-color, color 150ms ease`\n  Only colour changes. Sweeping the pointer along a toolbar lights each item briefly without a trail, and no transform is involved, so items never shift inside the clipped strip.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ButtonGroup(props: { size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; children: ReactNode; className?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction ButtonGroupItem(props: Omit<ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>, \"children\"> & { size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; selected?: boolean; iconOnly?: boolean; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; trailingIcon?: IconComponent; children?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  On `ButtonGroupItem`: 36 or 32px overall strip height, with 20 or 18px icons. Pass the same size to every item in a group.\n- `selected: boolean` — default `false`\n  On `ButtonGroupItem`: keeps the hover fill and sets `aria-pressed`. Drive it from state for single or multi select.\n- `iconOnly: boolean` — default `false`\n  On `ButtonGroupItem`: square item showing only `leadingIcon`. Needs `aria-label`.\n- `leadingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon component drawn before the label at the tier's size.\n- `trailingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon component drawn after the label. Ignored when `iconOnly`.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  On `ButtonGroup`: names the group for assistive tech, for example \"Range\".\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge` on either element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  group: [\n    \"inline-flex items-stretch isolate\",\n    \"rounded-2lg border border-border-button-group bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs\",\n    // Hairlines between items, outer corners rounded on the first/last item\n    \"divide-x divide-border-button-default overflow-hidden\",\n  ].join(\" \"),\n\n  item: [\n    \"inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-1 whitespace-nowrap\",\n    \"bg-background-primary-default text-text-primary font-sans select-none cursor-pointer\",\n    \"transition-[background-color,color] duration-150 ease\",\n    \"outline-none focus-visible:relative focus-visible:z-10 focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-inset focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n    \"hover:bg-background-primary-hover active:bg-background-primary-active\",\n    \"disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:bg-background-primary-disabled disabled:text-text-tertiary\",\n  ].join(\" \"),\n\n  size: {\n    medium: \"h-[34px] px-3 text-body-medium\",\n    small: \"h-[30px] px-2.5 text-body-medium\",\n  },\n\n  iconOnlySize: {\n    medium: \"w-[34px] px-0\",\n    small: \"w-[30px] px-0\",\n  },\n\n  icon: {\n    medium: \"size-5 shrink-0\",\n    small: \"size-[18px] shrink-0\",\n  },\n\n  selected: \"bg-background-primary-hover\",\n});\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. Both components are stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. The only motion is a 150ms colour transition on hover, press and selection, which is not spatial and stays as is.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Giving items their own radius or border. The container already draws both, so you get a doubled outline and visible notches where the item corners do not match the 10px outer radius.\n- Removing `overflow-hidden` from the container. The first and last items' hover and selected fills then paint square corners over the rounded border.\n- Switching the focus ring to an outset `ring-offset`. The container clips it, so keyboard users see no focus indicator at all on the end items.\n- Using `selected` without updating it from state. It is purely visual plus `aria-pressed`, so clicking an item does nothing until your handler moves the selection.\n- Shipping an `iconOnly` item without `aria-label`. The glyph is `aria-hidden`, so the item is announced as an unnamed button.\n\n## Notes\n\n- For a single-choice switcher with an animated sliding thumb, use a segmented control instead. Button Group is for rows of equal actions where the selected state just stays lit.\n- Pass `aria-label` on `ButtonGroup` (for example \"Range\" or \"Text alignment\") so screen readers announce what the group of buttons controls.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nDay, Week and Month render as one 36px strip with hairline dividers and rounded outer corners only. Clicking Month moves the lit fill from Week to Month and screen readers report it as pressed. Hovering the first item fills it right into the rounded corner without square edges poking out. Tabbing through shows an inset focus ring that is not clipped and not overlapped by the neighbouring divider. A small group is 32px tall, and icon-only items are square.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "calendar": "# Build: Calendar\n\nThe Calendar template as one working page. A Day / Week / Month switch and a stepper move through time; the month grid shows up to three chips a day with \"+N more\", the week and day views place timed events on a 24-hour grid with overlaps side by side and a now line, and pressing any event opens an anchored popover with guests, meeting code, RSVP, Join and Delete. Empty days and hours start a New event, and the inbox holds invitations to accept.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full calendar page: Day, Week and Month views, anchored event popovers with guests and RSVP, new events, and an invitation inbox.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. All dates are `@internationalized/date` values. `monthWeeks(anchor)` starts at `startOfWeek(startOfMonth(anchor), \"en-US\")` and always returns six Sunday-first weeks so the grid keeps its height; `weekDays(anchor)` is the seven days from `startOfWeek(anchor)`. Today and the now line come from fixed constants, never the clock.\n2. One `anchor` date drives every view. The stepper calls `stepAnchor(view, anchor, ±1)`, adding a month, a week or a day (month steps clamp 31 Aug to 30 Sep), and `rangeLabel` prints \"August 2026\", \"16 – 22 Aug 2026\" or \"Thursday, 20 Aug 2026\". The header title is always the anchor month.\n3. `eventsOn(events, date)` keeps events whose start and end days bracket the date, all-day first and then by start time. A month cell shows them all when there are three or fewer, otherwise two plus a \"+N more\" button that opens that day.\n4. `layoutDay` walks a day's timed events in start order, grouping chains of overlaps into clusters. Each event takes the first column whose last block has ended, and every event in a cluster shares the cluster's column count, so the 10:00 Roadmap review and the 10:30 Vendor call sit side by side at half width.\n5. There is one standalone kit `Popover` for every event. Pressing a chip, block or agenda row stores that element in `anchorRef` and sets `openId`, and the popover anchors to it through `triggerRef`. Deleting closes the popover and toasts with Undo, which puts the event back.\n6. Each month cell and each hour slot has a full-size background button (`tabIndex=-1`, labelled \"New event on …\") under pointer-transparent content, so clicking empty space opens New event pre-filled with that day and hour. The dialog remounts with a fresh `key` each time so its fields start from that draft.\n7. Sidebar rows are `href=\"#\"` links. One `onClickCapture` on the root maps the link's `aria-label` to a `DASHBOARD_NAV` key and switches the view in state, and any other key shows a placeholder with \"Back to Calendar\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TODAY = 2026-08-20 (Thursday)`\n  Fixed day for the today ring, the Today button and the default anchor, so renders never read the clock.\n- `NOW = 10:40`\n  Where the red now line sits in today's column in the Week and Day views.\n- `HOUR_PX = 48px`\n  A 30-minute event is 24px tall, enough for its title; the full day is 1152px and scrolls inside the grid, opening at 07:00.\n- `MIN_BLOCK_MIN = 28 minutes`\n  Shortest drawn block, so a 15-minute stand-up still shows its title. Overlap detection uses the drawn length too.\n- `month rows = 6`\n  Always six weeks, so stepping between months never makes the grid jump in height.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CalendarTemplate(props: { events?: CalendarEvent[]; invites?: CalendarInvite[]; defaultNotifications?: BellNotification[]; defaultCalendarView?: \"day\" | \"week\" | \"month\"; today?: CalendarDate; defaultView?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `events: CalendarEvent[]` — default `CALENDAR_EVENTS`\n  Events on the calendar (28 samples, July to September 2026). Creating, accepting, RSVP and deleting change a local copy.\n- `invites: CalendarInvite[]` — default `CALENDAR_INVITES`\n  Pending invitations in the inbox. Accepting one adds its event.\n- `defaultNotifications: BellNotification[]` — default `CALENDAR_NOTIFICATIONS`\n  Initial bell contents; unread items drive the badge count.\n- `defaultCalendarView: \"day\" | \"week\" | \"month\"` — default `\"month\"`\n  View shown first.\n- `today: CalendarDate` — default `TODAY (2026-08-20)`\n  Day the calendar opens on and marks as today.\n- `defaultView: string` — default `\"calendar\"`\n  Sidebar key shown first. Any other key shows the placeholder view.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Sunday-first weeks, as the month grid and week view draw them. */\nconst LOCALE = \"en-US\";\n\n/** Six full weeks starting on the Sunday on or before the 1st, so the grid never changes height. */\nexport function monthWeeks(anchor: CalendarDate): CalendarDate[][] {\n  const first = startOfWeek(startOfMonth(anchor), LOCALE);\n  return Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, w) => Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, d) => first.add({ days: w * 7 + d })));\n}\n\nexport function weekDays(anchor: CalendarDate): CalendarDate[] {\n  const first = startOfWeek(anchor, LOCALE);\n  return Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, d) => first.add({ days: d }));\n}\n\n/** One step of the stepper. Month steps clamp the day (31 Aug -> 30 Sep). */\nexport function stepAnchor(view: CalendarView, anchor: CalendarDate, dir: 1 | -1): CalendarDate {\n  if (view === \"month\") return anchor.add({ months: dir });\n  if (view === \"week\") return anchor.add({ weeks: dir });\n  return anchor.add({ days: dir });\n}\n\n/** \"August 2026\" · \"16 – 22 Aug 2026\" · \"30 Aug – 5 Sep 2026\" · \"Thursday, 20 Aug 2026\" */\nexport function rangeLabel(view: CalendarView, anchor: CalendarDate): string {\n  if (view === \"month\") return `${monthName(anchor)} ${anchor.year}`;\n  if (view === \"day\") return `${weekdayName(anchor)}, ${anchor.day} ${shortMonth(anchor)} ${anchor.year}`;\n  const days = weekDays(anchor);\n  const a = days[0] ?? anchor;\n  const b = days[6] ?? anchor;\n  if (a.year !== b.year) return `${a.day} ${shortMonth(a)} ${a.year} – ${b.day} ${shortMonth(b)} ${b.year}`;\n  if (a.month !== b.month) return `${a.day} ${shortMonth(a)} – ${b.day} ${shortMonth(b)} ${b.year}`;\n  return `${a.day} – ${b.day} ${shortMonth(b)} ${b.year}`;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, observers or global listeners. The time grid sets its scroll position once in an effect, and the popover anchor is a ref. Overlays and the Toaster clean up their own listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Hover tints on slots, stepper buttons and agenda rows carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`. The popover, modal and segmented control use the kit's own reduced-motion handling; nothing else animates.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Formatting dates with `Intl` in the host time zone. The labels here are built from the `CalendarDate` fields directly, and the chip times format in UTC, so the server and the browser print the same text.\n- Laying out overlaps per pair instead of per cluster. A chain A–B–C where A and C do not touch still needs one shared column count, or the blocks have different widths and cover each other.\n- Anchoring a popover to state captured at render time. The anchor element is stored in a ref at press time, so the popover follows the chip that was actually pressed, even in a scrolled time grid.\n- A row button that unmounts when pressed (Accept or Decline in the inbox) leaves focus on the body, so Escape no longer reaches the popover. Focus moves to the dialog first.\n- Leaving the mini calendar in the Day view on its first month. It is keyed by year and month so it follows the stepper.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Composed from kit items: `DashboardSidebar`, `DashboardPageHeader`, `NotificationBellBadge`, `SegmentedControl`, `CalendarEventChip`, `Popover`, `Modal`, `DatePicker`, `DateCalendar`, `Select`, `Input`, `Checkbox`, `Avatar` and `Toaster`. The grids, popover body, inbox and dialog are this item's own files.\n- Week starts on Sunday (`en-US`) and times use a 24-hour clock, as in the preview.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280x800 the sidebar shows Calendar selected, the title reads \"August 2026\", the month grid starts on Sun 26 Jul and 20 Aug is ringed as today with Stand-up, Team lunch and \"+2 more\". Pressing Stand-up opens a popover with four guests, the meeting code and Join meeting. \"+2 more\" opens the Day view for Thursday, 20 Aug 2026 with a now line at 10:40. In Week view, Roadmap review and Vendor call on 18 Aug sit side by side. Next week reads \"23 – 29 Aug 2026\". New event with a title adds it and toasts. Accepting \"Design system sync\" in the inbox adds it on 21 Aug. Delete removes an event and Undo restores it. Clicking Finance in the sidebar shows the placeholder and \"Back to Calendar\" returns.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "calendar-event-chip": "# Build: Calendar Event Chip\n\nThe event pill of the calendar template as a native button. Each colour is a background, title and time token triple, so light and dark come from the theme. The compact variant is a 22px line whose title truncates while the start time stays; the full variant is a block with a coloured start edge, a two-line title and the time range, made to fill an absolutely positioned slot in a week grid. The file also exports the shared `CalendarEvent` model and `formatEventTime`, which the other calendar pieces import.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTinted calendar event pill in five colour tokens: one-line compact chip for month cells, block variant for time grids, selected ring.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `calendarEventColorStyles` maps each colour to complete class names (`bg-calendar-event-<c>-background`, `text-calendar-event-<c>-title`, `text-calendar-event-<c>-time`, plus border, ring and dot classes built from the title token), so Tailwind sees every class statically and nothing is concatenated at runtime.\n2. Compact is a single flex row: the title span takes `min-w-0 flex-1 truncate` and the time span takes `shrink-0`, so a narrow month cell eats the title first and never the time. All-day chips omit the time.\n3. Full is a column with `h-full w-full overflow-hidden`, a 3px logical start border in the title colour, `line-clamp-2` on the title and a truncated \"start – end\" line. The parent sets the height, so a 15-minute slot shows only the title and a 2-hour slot shows both lines.\n4. `formatEventTime` formats a fixed UTC instant built from the wall-clock hour and minute with a cached `DateFormatter` (`hourCycle: \"h23\"` and 2-digit hours for 24h, `h12` for 12h). Server and client therefore print the same string whatever their time zones.\n5. `selected` adds `ring-2` in the event colour and `data-selected`; keyboard focus uses the kit focus ring. Remaining button props are spread last, so the chip works as a `PopoverTrigger` child and accepts `aria-pressed`, handlers and refs.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `compact height = 22px`\n  Three chips plus a \"+N more\" line fit under the day number of a ~120px month cell.\n- `start border = 3px`\n  Reads as the colour key of the block at a glance without eating the narrow width of a week column.\n- `hover = brightness 0.97 light, 1.25 dark`\n  The tinted backgrounds are light in light mode and deep in dark mode, so the hover nudge must go in opposite directions to stay visible.\n- `time format = HH:mm (h23) by default`\n  Matches the template and keeps every time the same width with `tabular-nums`.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype CalendarEventColor = \"blue\" | \"emerald\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\" | \"purple\"\nfunction formatEventTime(time: { hour: number; minute: number }, options?: { locale?: string; hourCycle?: 12 | 24 }): string\nfunction CalendarEventChip(props: ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> & { title: string; color?: CalendarEventColor; start?: { hour: number; minute: number }; end?: { hour: number; minute: number }; allDay?: boolean; variant?: \"compact\" | \"full\"; selected?: boolean; locale?: string; hourCycle?: 12 | 24; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: string`\n  Event title; truncates (compact) or clamps to two lines (full).\n- `color: CalendarEventColor` — default `\"blue\"`\n  Token triple used for background, title and time.\n- `start / end: { hour: number; minute: number }`\n  Wall-clock times. Compact shows start; full shows the range.\n- `allDay: boolean` — default `false`\n  Hide the time.\n- `variant: \"compact\" | \"full\"` — default `\"compact\"`\n  One-line month chip or time-grid block.\n- `selected: boolean` — default `false`\n  Colour ring and `data-selected`.\n- `hourCycle: 12 | 24` — default `24`\n  \"09:30\" or \"9:30 AM\".\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for the time formatter.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CalendarEventChip({\n  title,\n  color = \"blue\",\n  start,\n  end,\n  allDay = false,\n  variant = \"compact\",\n  selected = false,\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  hourCycle = 24,\n  className,\n  type = \"button\",\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: CalendarEventChipProps) {\n  const c = calendarEventColorStyles[color];\n  const startLabel = !allDay && start ? formatEventTime(start, { locale, hourCycle }) : null;\n  const endLabel = !allDay && end ? formatEventTime(end, { locale, hourCycle }) : null;\n\n  const base = cx(\n    \"relative min-w-0 cursor-pointer select-none text-start outline-none\",\n    \"transition-[box-shadow,filter] duration-150 ease-out hover:brightness-[0.97] dark:hover:brightness-125\",\n    \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n    selected && cx(\"ring-2\", c.ring),\n    c.background,\n  );\n\n  if (variant === \"full\") {\n    return (\n      <button\n        ref={ref}\n        type={type}\n        data-selected={selected || undefined}\n        data-variant=\"full\"\n        className={cx(\n          base,\n          \"flex h-full w-full flex-col items-start gap-0.5 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border-s-[3px] py-1 ps-1.5 pe-1\",\n          c.bar,\n          className,\n        )}\n        {...props}\n      >\n        <span className={cx(\"line-clamp-2 w-full text-body-2-medium break-words\", c.title)}>{title}</span>\n        {startLabel ? (\n          <span className={cx(\"w-full truncate text-caption-1-regular tabular-nums opacity-90\", c.time)}>\n            {startLabel}\n            {endLabel ? ` – ${endLabel}` : null}\n          </span>\n        ) : null}\n      </button>\n    );\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button\n      ref={ref}\n      type={type}\n      data-selected={selected || undefined}\n      data-variant=\"compact\"\n      className={cx(base, \"flex h-[22px] w-full items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-1.5\", className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <span className={cx(\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-body-2-medium\", c.title)}>{title}</span>\n      {startLabel ? (\n        <span className={cx(\"shrink-0 text-caption-1-regular tabular-nums opacity-90\", c.time)}>{startLabel}</span>\n      ) : null}\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: no effects, timers or listeners. Formatters are cached in a module-level map.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only a 150ms colour/filter transition on hover; there is no movement. It is short and non-spatial, so it stays under reduced motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building class names like `bg-calendar-event-${color}-background` at runtime. Tailwind only generates classes it can find in source; keep the full-name map.\n- Formatting times with `toLocaleTimeString` on a local `Date`. The server and the browser can be in different zones and print different hours, which breaks hydration.\n- Forgetting `min-w-0` on the compact title. Without it the flex item refuses to shrink and the time is pushed out of the cell instead of the title truncating.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `aria-pressed` when the chip toggles a selection, or wrap it in the event detail popover trigger, which supplies `aria-expanded`.\n- The shared `CalendarEvent` type stores `CalendarDateTime` start and end; pass `{ hour, minute }` objects (or the `CalendarDateTime` itself) as `start`/`end`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFive compact chips render in blue, emerald, lime, pink and purple, each with its title and a 24h start time such as \"15:00\". In a 104px cell \"Coffee with Lena\" truncates with an ellipsis while \"09:30\" stays whole. Full blocks show the title and \"13:00 – 14:00\"; a 36px block shows only the title. Clicking a chip adds the colour ring and sets `data-selected`; clicking it again removes both. Both themes keep the title readable on the tinted background.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "calendar-toolbar": "# Build: Calendar Toolbar\n\nThe header row of the calendar template, assembled from kit pieces: the month as a heading with a Today button on the left, and the month stepper, a Day / Week / Month segmented control, a search field and the primary New event button on the right. Month, view and search are each independently controlled or uncontrolled. The row wraps into a second line, then stacks, as its container narrows.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCalendar page header: month title, Today, month stepper, Day/Week/Month segments, event search and a New event button.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Month state is a `CalendarDate` normalised with `startOfMonth`. `month` controls it; otherwise `defaultMonth`, then `today`, then the local current date seed internal state. Both the stepper and the Today button write through one `setMonth` that updates internal state and calls `onMonthChange`.\n2. The heading defaults to `DateFormatter(locale, { month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\", timeZone: \"UTC\" })` of the month and carries `aria-live=\"polite\"`, so stepping the month is announced. Passing `title={null}` removes it; any other node replaces it.\n3. Today sets the month to `startOfMonth(today)` and calls `onToday(today)` so the parent can also select or scroll to the day. `today` is a prop so server-rendered pages can pin it and avoid a hydration mismatch.\n4. The view switch is the kit `SegmentedControl` with `selectedKeys={[view]}`. `onSelectionChange` reads the single key out of the Set, ignores anything that is not a known view, then updates internal state and calls `onViewChange`. `views` picks and orders the segments.\n5. The search is the kit `SearchField` passed `value`/`defaultValue`/`onChange` straight through, so Escape clears it. Layout is two flex groups in a `flex-wrap` row: from `sm` up the right group is `flex-1 justify-end flex-wrap`. Below `sm` both groups take `w-full` (title and Today pushed apart), and the stepper and search also take `w-full`, so on a phone they each get their own line.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `control height = 36px everywhere`\n  Button medium, MonthStepper medium, SearchField medium and the solid SegmentedControl (4px track padding + 28px segment) all land on 36px, so the row reads as one baseline without per-control fixes.\n- `stepper width = w-60 (240px) from sm up`\n  Narrower than the stepper's 320px default so all five controls fit in one 1100px row, still wide enough that \"September 2026\" does not truncate.\n- `search width = w-52 (208px) from sm up`\n  Fits the \"Search events\" placeholder plus icon and clear button with room for a short query.\n- `gaps = 12px between groups, 8px inside the control group`\n  The tighter inner gap groups the controls visually, and the wider outer gap separates them from the title.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype CalendarView = \"day\" | \"week\" | \"month\"\nfunction CalendarToolbar(props: { month?: CalendarDate; defaultMonth?: CalendarDate; onMonthChange?: (month: CalendarDate) => void; today?: CalendarDate; onToday?: (today: CalendarDate) => void; view?: CalendarView; defaultView?: CalendarView; onViewChange?: (view: CalendarView) => void; views?: CalendarView[]; searchValue?: string; defaultSearchValue?: string; onSearchChange?: (value: string) => void; showSearch?: boolean; searchPlaceholder?: string; onNewEvent?: () => void; newEventLabel?: string; title?: ReactNode; actions?: ReactNode; locale?: string; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `month / defaultMonth: CalendarDate`\n  Shown month, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `onMonthChange: (month: CalendarDate) => void`\n  Called with the first day of the month from the stepper or Today.\n- `today: CalendarDate` — default `local today`\n  Target of the Today button.\n- `onToday: (today: CalendarDate) => void`\n  Called after Today moved the month.\n- `view / defaultView: \"day\" | \"week\" | \"month\"` — default `\"month\"`\n  Selected segment.\n- `onViewChange: (view: CalendarView) => void`\n  Called when a segment is chosen.\n- `views: CalendarView[]` — default `[\"day\", \"week\", \"month\"]`\n  Segments to show, in order.\n- `searchValue / defaultSearchValue / onSearchChange: string / string / (value: string) => void`\n  Search text, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `showSearch: boolean` — default `true`\n  Render the search field.\n- `onNewEvent: () => void`\n  Press handler of the primary button.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `formatted month`\n  Left heading; `null` hides it.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Extra controls before New event.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CalendarToolbar({\n  month: controlledMonth,\n  defaultMonth,\n  onMonthChange,\n  today,\n  onToday,\n  view: controlledView,\n  defaultView = \"month\",\n  onViewChange,\n  views = [\"day\", \"week\", \"month\"],\n  searchValue,\n  defaultSearchValue = \"\",\n  onSearchChange,\n  showSearch = true,\n  searchPlaceholder = \"Search events\",\n  onNewEvent,\n  newEventLabel = \"New event\",\n  title,\n  actions,\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  className,\n  ref,\n}: CalendarToolbarProps) {\n  const [uncontrolledMonth, setUncontrolledMonth] = useState<CalendarDate>(() =>\n    startOfMonth(defaultMonth ?? today ?? todayIn(getLocalTimeZone())),\n  );\n  const month = startOfMonth(controlledMonth ?? uncontrolledMonth);\n  const setMonth = (next: CalendarDate) => {\n    setUncontrolledMonth(next);\n    onMonthChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const [uncontrolledView, setUncontrolledView] = useState<CalendarView>(defaultView);\n  const view = controlledView ?? uncontrolledView;\n\n  const titleFormatter = useMemo(\n    () => new DateFormatter(locale, { month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\", timeZone: \"UTC\" }),\n    [locale],\n  );\n  const heading = title === undefined ? titleFormatter.format(month.toDate(\"UTC\")) : title;\n\n  const goToday = () => {\n    const target = today ?? todayIn(getLocalTimeZone());\n    setMonth(startOfMonth(target));\n    onToday?.(target);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={ref} className={cx(\"flex w-full flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex w-full min-w-0 items-center justify-between gap-3 sm:w-auto sm:justify-start\">\n        {heading !== null ? (\n          <h2 aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"truncate text-title-3-medium text-text-primary\">\n            {heading}\n          </h2>\n        ) : null}\n        <Button variant=\"secondary\" onClick={goToday}>\n          Today\n        </Button>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex w-full min-w-0 flex-wrap items-center gap-2 sm:w-auto sm:flex-1 sm:justify-end\">\n        <MonthStepper value={month} onChange={setMonth} locale={locale} className=\"w-full sm:w-60\" />\n        <SegmentedControl\n          aria-label=\"Calendar view\"\n          selectedKeys={[view]}\n          onSelectionChange={(keys) => {\n            const [next] = [...keys];\n            if (typeof next !== \"string\" || !(next in VIEW_LABELS)) return;\n            setUncontrolledView(next as CalendarView);\n            onViewChange?.(next as CalendarView);\n          }}\n        >\n          {views.map((v) => (\n            <SegmentedControlItem key={v} id={v}>\n              {VIEW_LABELS[v]}\n            </SegmentedControlItem>\n          ))}\n        </SegmentedControl>\n        {showSearch ? (\n          <SearchField\n            aria-label={searchPlaceholder}\n            placeholder={searchPlaceholder}\n            value={searchValue}\n            defaultValue={defaultSearchValue}\n            onChange={onSearchChange}\n            className=\"w-full min-w-0 sm:w-52\"\n          />\n        ) : null}\n        {actions}\n        <Button leadingIcon={RiAddLine} onClick={onNewEvent}>\n          {newEventLabel}\n        </Button>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing of its own. The composed kit components clean up their own effects and observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The toolbar adds no motion of its own. The month stepper skips its label slide and the segmented control's thumb follows the kit control's own behaviour.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reading `onSelectionChange` keys as an array. React Aria passes a `Set<Key>`; spread it before destructuring.\n- Defaulting `today` with `new Date()` on the server. Pass a fixed `CalendarDate` when the toolbar is server rendered, or the heading and Today target can disagree between server and client around midnight.\n- Letting the segmented control allow an empty selection. The kit control sets `disallowEmptySelection`; keep a view selected or the grid under the toolbar has nothing to render.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Use `actions` for the template's notification and inbox icon buttons; they render just before New event.\n- The toolbar only reports state. Filtering events by the search text and switching between grid components is the parent's job.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith month August 2026, the heading reads \"August 2026\". Pressing the stepper's next chevron changes the heading to \"September 2026\"; pressing Today returns it to \"August 2026\". Choosing \"Week\" in the segmented control marks it selected and reports `\"week\"`. Typing \"sync\" in the search reports the text and Escape clears it. New event calls `onNewEvent`. At 360px wide every control remains visible and nothing scrolls sideways.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "campaigns-table": "# Build: Campaigns Table\n\nA bordered card with a result count, a status segmented control and a channel select over the kit Table. Each row shows the campaign and its objective, the channel with its brand icon, a delivery status pill with a coloured dot, spend against budget as a figure, a percent and a thin progress bar, the click-through rate and the last edit date. Every column sorts through React Aria, and the kit Pagination pages the result.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA marketing campaigns table with channel icons, delivery status, budget-spent progress bars, CTR and sortable columns.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Keep the sort in a React Aria `SortDescriptor` (controlled or local, newest edit first) and hand it to the kit `Table`; every column has `allowsSorting`, so a header click or Enter toggles it and sets `aria-sort`.\n2. Derive rows with `sortCampaigns`: filter by status and channel, then compare per column (channel by label, status by rank active, paused, draft, budget by `spent / budget`, dates as ISO strings) times -1 for descending, falling back to the id so ties keep their order.\n3. Compute CTR as `clicks / impressions` and return `null` with no impressions; the CTR sort puts null rows last in both directions instead of letting the sign flip them to the top.\n4. Draw the budget cell as spend \"of\" budget, a percent, and the kit `ProgressTrack` at `size=\"sm\"` with the percentage clamped to 100; over budget the fill switches to chart-3 and the percent to the rose status text, so 108% still reads at a glance.\n5. Clamp the stored page to the page count and send every sort or filter change back to page 1; dates go through `parseDate(iso).toDate(\"UTC\")` and a UTC formatter so server and client agree.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pageSize = 6 (demo uses 5)`\n  Two-line rows are about 56px; six plus the toolbar and pager keep the card on one dashboard fold.\n- `table min width = 860px`\n  The budget cell needs about 200px for \"$30,884 of $40,000\"; below this the kit Table wrapper scrolls instead of squeezing.\n- `status rank = active 0, paused 1, draft 2`\n  Status sorts by delivery stage, not alphabetically.\n- `CTR precision = 2 decimals`\n  Ad CTRs mostly sit between 0.5% and 8%, where a whole percent hides real differences.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype Campaign = { id: string; name: string; channel: \"google\" | \"meta\" | \"x\" | \"linkedin\" | \"email\" | \"tiktok\"; objective: string; status: \"active\" | \"paused\" | \"draft\"; budget: number; spent: number; impressions: number; clicks: number; updated: string }\nfunction CampaignsTable(props: CampaignsTableProps): JSX.Element\nfunction sortCampaigns(rows: Campaign[], sort: SortDescriptor, status?: CampaignStatus | \"all\", channel?: CampaignChannel | \"all\"): Campaign[]\n```\n\n- `campaigns: Campaign[]`\n  All rows; filtering, sorting and paging happen inside.\n- `pageSize: number` — default `6`\n  Rows per page.\n- `sortDescriptor / defaultSortDescriptor / onSortChange: SortDescriptor` — default `{ column: \"updated\", direction: \"descending\" }`\n  Sort state, controlled or uncontrolled. Columns: name, channel, status, budget, ctr, updated.\n- `currency: string` — default `\"USD\"`\n  ISO 4217 code for spend and budget.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for money, percents and dates.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Campaigns\"`\n  Accessible name of the card and the table.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Click-through rate as a fraction, or null before the first impression. */\nexport function ctrOf(c: Pick<Campaign, \"clicks\" | \"impressions\">): number | null {\n  return c.impressions > 0 ? c.clicks / c.impressions : null;\n}\n\n/** Share of budget spent; a zero budget counts as nothing spent. */\nexport function spentRatio(c: Pick<Campaign, \"spent\" | \"budget\">): number {\n  return c.budget > 0 ? c.spent / c.budget : 0;\n}\n\nfunction compare(a: Campaign, b: Campaign, column: Key): number {\n  switch (column) {\n    case \"name\":\n      return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);\n    case \"channel\":\n      return CHANNEL[a.channel].label.localeCompare(CHANNEL[b.channel].label);\n    case \"status\":\n      return STATUS[a.status].rank - STATUS[b.status].rank;\n    case \"budget\":\n      return spentRatio(a) - spentRatio(b);\n    case \"updated\":\n      return a.updated < b.updated ? -1 : a.updated > b.updated ? 1 : 0;\n    default:\n      return 0;\n  }\n}\n\n/** Filter by status and channel, then sort. CTR is special-cased so rows\n *  without impressions stay at the bottom in both directions; every other\n *  tie falls back to the id for a stable order across pages. */\nexport function sortCampaigns(\n  rows: Campaign[],\n  sort: SortDescriptor,\n  status: CampaignStatus | \"all\" = \"all\",\n  channel: CampaignChannel | \"all\" = \"all\",\n): Campaign[] {\n  const sign = sort.direction === \"descending\" ? -1 : 1;\n  return rows\n    .filter((c) => (status === \"all\" || c.status === status) && (channel === \"all\" || c.channel === channel))\n    .sort((a, b) => {\n      if (sort.column === \"ctr\") {\n        const x = ctrOf(a);\n        const y = ctrOf(b);\n        if (x === null || y === null) return x === y ? a.id.localeCompare(b.id) : x === null ? 1 : -1;\n        return sign * (x - y) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id);\n      }\n      return sign * compare(a, b, sort.column) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id);\n    });\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component adds no timers or listeners. The kit SegmentedControl and Pagination disconnect their ResizeObservers on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The sort chevron rotates over 150ms and the kit ProgressTrack slides its fill over 500ms; both carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` they change instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not multiply null CTRs by the sort sign. Treat \"no impressions\" as missing and pin it last, or drafts jump to the top of every descending CTR sort.\n- Clamp the bar fill to 100% but keep the true percent in the text, otherwise an overspent campaign looks exactly like a fully spent one.\n- The progress bar is decorative next to the written figures, so it is `aria-hidden`; giving it a role would make screen readers announce each row twice.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `ctrOf`, `spentRatio` and `sortCampaigns` are exported for server-side sorting with the same rules.\n- The status pill is static. Swap it for the kit Select to edit delivery inline.\n- Spend and budget take whole currency units; amounts are formatted without decimals.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo card reads \"16 campaigns\", newest edit first with \"Founder story video · US\" on top. Sorting CTR descending puts \"Brand search · US\" (7.50%) first and ascending puts \"Creator collab · EU\" (0.80%) first, with the three drafts showing \"–\" at the bottom both ways. \"Newsletter promo · EU\" shows 108% in rose with a full pink bar. Choosing \"Draft\" leaves 3 rows; choosing the Meta channel leaves 4 and hides the pager. At 360px the table scrolls inside the card. Light and dark both read correctly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "canvas-handoff-transition": "# Build: Canvas Handoff Transition\n\nThe usual way to put a shader on more than one page is to mount it on each of them, and the price is a new context, a new compile and a new first frame every time the route changes. No easing hides that: a field restarting from t = 0 is visibly a different object. Here one canvas is created imperatively, outside React's ownership, and moved into the destination page with `appendChild` during the layout phase — no paint happens in between, so the context never blinks. What the viewer sees is the surface they were already looking at, travelling from one rectangle to another.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe live WebGL canvas is handed to the next page and flies to its new slot, instead of being destroyed and rebuilt.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1, gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Four steps in a fixed order. MEASURE first, inside the click handler, because one `setState` later the outgoing rectangle no longer exists. FLUSH one frame, so the pixels about to travel are current. MOVE in a `useLayoutEffect`, which shares its task with React's DOM mutations and therefore straddles no paint. RESIZE last, on the tween's completion.\n2. The move is a plain `target.appendChild(canvas)`. `appendChild` on a node that already has a parent relocates it rather than copying it, and a WebGL context belongs to the canvas element, not to its position in the document — so the context, the compiled program and the drawing buffer all survive being detached and re-attached.\n3. The flight is FLIP: `scaleX = first.width / last.width`, `scaleY = first.height / last.height`, and the translation is the difference between the two centre points. Scale, never `width`/`height` — a width tween is 0.72 s of continuous reflow, and writing the canvas's width attribute would reallocate the drawing buffer on every frame of it.\n4. The buffer resize is deferred to `onComplete` and the `ResizeObserver` is muted for the duration by a `flying` flag. Assigning `canvas.width` is what reallocates and CLEARS the backing store; do it while the element is mid-flight and the object being animated goes black for a frame. During the flight the old buffer is simply scaled by CSS, which is slightly soft and completely invisible at this duration.\n5. Measuring inside the handler also makes interruption correct for free. If a flight is already running, `getBoundingClientRect()` returns the TRANSFORMED rect — where the canvas visually is, mid-air — so a second click continues from that point instead of snapping back to the slot it was heading for.\n6. The destination slot has its `overflow` forced to `visible` inline for the length of the flight and restored afterwards. Inline, because the clipping usually comes from a utility class on the consumer's own markup that a rule in our stylesheet would lose to.\n7. A `uHandoff` uniform is tweened 0 to 1 and back on the same timeline, shearing the field's sampling coordinate. It disturbs the picture rather than fading it: a fade would hide precisely the frames that prove the surface is the same one.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `HANDOFF_DURATION = 0.72`\n  Seconds for the flight. It is long for a page transition, deliberately — the whole point is that you can watch a specific fold of the field travel and see that it never restarts. Under about 0.45 s the eye cannot track it and the effect degrades to a cut; over about 1 s the route change starts to feel withheld.\n- `HANDOFF_EASE = 'power3.inOut'`\n  Symmetric, because the two rectangles are equally important — the canvas is not leaving somewhere for somewhere, it is the same object being re-placed. An `out` ease would put the acceleration at the origin and imply the old page was the one that mattered.\n- `SURGE_EASE = 'sine.inOut'`\n  The `uHandoff` shear runs half the flight forward and half back (`yoyo: true, repeat: 1`). Sine, because it is the only common ease with zero slope at both ends, so the disturbance grows out of stillness and returns to it with no visible corner at the turnaround.\n- `LEVELS = 6`\n  Tones the field is crushed to. Low enough that the quantisation edges are hard and locked to the drawing buffer, which is what makes the mid-flight scaling legible as \"these are the pixels that were already there\". Above about ten the picture is a smooth gradient again and the proof is lost.\n- `CELL = 2`\n  Dither cell size in DRAWING-BUFFER pixels, so on a 2x display the pattern is one CSS pixel per cell — fine enough to read as tone, coarse enough to survive the scaling. Measured in buffer pixels rather than CSS pixels on purpose: it is the buffer that stretches during the flight.\n- `MAX_DPR = 2`\n  Device pixel ratio ceiling. Above 2 the visual gain on a quantised field is nil and the fill-rate cost is quadratic — and this surface is on screen on every page rather than on one hero, so its cost is paid continuously.\n- `INK / PAPER = [0.42, 0.65, 1] / [0.035, 0.037, 0.045]`\n  The two ends of the quantisation ramp, as raw 0..1 RGB. Passed as `THREE.Vector3` rather than `THREE.Color` because three applies working-colour-space conversion to a Color and none to a Vector3, and a `ShaderMaterial` emits `gl_FragColor` without the output-encoding chunk — Vector3 is the only pairing where the numbers written in the source are the numbers that reach the screen.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CanvasHandoffTransition(props: { controller: CanvasHandoff; children: ReactNode; duration?: number; ease?: string; ink?: readonly [number, number, number]; paper?: readonly [number, number, number]; levels?: number; cell?: number; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction CanvasHandoffSlot(props: { controller: CanvasHandoff; id: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction useCanvasHandoff(initialId: string): { active: string; fallback: boolean; handoff: (id: string, commit?: () => void) => void }\n```\n\n- `controller: CanvasHandoff`\n  From `useCanvasHandoff(initialSlotId)`. Shared by the host and every slot.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The pages. Each renders one `CanvasHandoffSlot` for its own landing place.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.72`\n  Seconds for the flight. `0` moves the canvas instantly with no tween.\n- `ease: string` — default `'power3.inOut'`\n  Easing for the flight.\n- `ink: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.42, 0.65, 1]`\n  Bright end of the quantisation ramp, raw 0..1 RGB.\n- `paper: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.035, 0.037, 0.045]`\n  Dark end of the quantisation ramp, raw 0..1 RGB.\n- `levels: number` — default `6`\n  How many tones the field is crushed to.\n- `cell: number` — default `2`\n  Dither cell size in drawing-buffer pixels.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Device pixel ratio ceiling.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host root via `cn`.\n- `id: string`\n  `CanvasHandoffSlot` only. Stable identity for this landing place, unique within one controller.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   BUILD. Depends on the motion preference and the DPR ceiling and nothing\n   else — emphatically not on the active slot, or a route change would tear\n   down the very context the component exists to preserve.\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const rootEl = root.current;\n  if (!rootEl) return;\n\n  /* Probed in an effect, never during render: `hasWebGL()` needs `document`,\n     and branching the markup on it would be a hydration mismatch rather than\n     a graceful degradation. */\n  if (!hasWebGL()) {\n    setFallback(true);\n    return;\n  }\n  setFallback(false);\n\n  let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n  try {\n    renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n      antialias: false,\n      alpha: false,\n      powerPreference: 'low-power',\n    });\n  } catch {\n    // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n    // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n    // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n    // page with it.\n    setFallback(true);\n    return;\n  }\n  renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n\n  /* three constructs the canvas; React never sees it. This is the whole\n     strict-mode story: the second invocation gets a brand new element and a\n     brand new context, because the first invocation's cleanup removed and\n     released its own. */\n  const canvas = renderer.domElement;\n  canvas.className = 'kx-handoff-canvas';\n  canvas.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');\n\n  const l = look.current;\n  const uniforms: HandoffUniforms = {\n    uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1, 1) },\n    uTime: { value: 0 },\n    uHandoff: { value: 0 },\n    uLevels: { value: l.levels },\n    uCell: { value: l.cell },\n    /* Vector3, not Color: three applies working-colour-space conversion to a\n       Color and none to a Vector3, and a `ShaderMaterial` emits `gl_FragColor`\n       without the output-encoding chunk. Vector3 is the only pair of ends\n       where the numbers written here are the numbers that reach the screen. */\n    uInk: { value: new THREE.Vector3(l.ink0, l.ink1, l.ink2) },\n    uPaper: { value: new THREE.Vector3(l.paper0, l.paper1, l.paper2) },\n  };\n\n  const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n    vertexShader: canvasHandoffVertexShader,\n    fragmentShader: canvasHandoffFragmentShader,\n    uniforms,\n  });\n  // A 2x2 plane under an identity ortho camera is a full-screen quad with no\n  // projection maths and no camera to keep in sync on resize.\n  const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material));\n  const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1);\n\n  const render = () => renderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n  const resize = () => {\n    const host = canvas.parentElement;\n    if (!host) return;\n    const w = host.clientWidth;\n    const h = host.clientHeight;\n    if (w === 0 || h === 0) return;\n    // `false` leaves the CSS size alone; the stylesheet's 100%/100% is what\n    // keeps the canvas glued to whichever slot currently owns it.\n    renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n    uniforms.uResolution.value.set(w, h);\n    render();\n  };\n\n  // Whatever slot is active right now. `rootEl` is the safety net for a\n  // controller whose slots have not registered — the canvas is visible and\n  // full-bleed rather than orphaned.\n  const home = slots.current.get(activeRef.current) ?? rootEl;\n  home.appendChild(canvas);\n  resize();\n\n  /* Observed on the CANVAS, not the slot: the observation follows the node\n     across the handoff, so one observer covers every slot it will ever live\n     in. `setSize(..., false)` never writes the CSS box, so this cannot feed\n     back on itself. */\n  const observer = new ResizeObserver(() => {\n    // The flight deliberately runs at the OLD buffer size, scaled. Honouring\n    // the resize here is the black frame the deferral exists to prevent.\n    if (engine.current?.flying) return;\n    resize();\n  });\n  observer.observe(canvas);\n\n  if (reduced) {\n    // One frame, then nothing. A field that drifts forever is motion, and\n    // motion is what was asked not to happen.\n    render();\n  } else {\n    // The timestamp is absolute, so elapsed time survives the handoff with\n    // no accumulator to carry across and nothing to reset.\n    renderer.setAnimationLoop((time) => {\n      uniforms.uTime.value = time / 1000;\n      render();\n    });\n  }\n\n  engine.current = { canvas, uniforms, render, resize, flying: false };\n\n  return () => {\n    observer.disconnect();\n    gsap.killTweensOf(canvas);\n    gsap.killTweensOf(uniforms.uHandoff);\n    // Stops the loop before releasing the context; a queued frame rendering\n    // into a lost context throws out of the cleanup.\n    disposeRenderer(renderer);\n    material.dispose();\n    geometry.dispose();\n    // The effect appended it, the effect removes it. React has no idea this\n    // node was ever here and will not try to reconcile it.\n    canvas.remove();\n    engine.current = null;\n  };\n}, [reduced, maxDpr, slots, engine, activeRef, setFallback]);\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   HAND OVER. A LAYOUT effect, because React's mutations and its layout\n   effects share one task: the canvas leaves the old parent and arrives at\n   the new one with no paint in between. The same code in `useEffect` shows a\n   frame with a hole where the canvas used to be.\n   ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\n  const e = engine.current;\n  const target = slots.current.get(active);\n  if (!e || !target) return;\n\n  const from = first.current;\n  first.current = null;\n\n  // First run after the build already parked it correctly.\n  if (e.canvas.parentElement === target) return;\n\n  // `appendChild` on a node that already has a parent MOVES it. No clone, no\n  // re-creation, and the WebGL context rides along untouched.\n  target.appendChild(e.canvas);\n\n  /* An interrupted flight leaves an inline transform behind. Clear it before\n     measuring or Last is read through the previous flight's scale, and the\n     inversion comes out compounded — the canvas leaps somewhere neither slot\n     has ever been. `clearProps` rather than a set to identity so the instant\n     paths below leave the element genuinely untouched. */\n  gsap.killTweensOf(e.canvas);\n  gsap.set(e.canvas, { clearProps: 'transform' });\n\n  if (!from || reduced || duration <= 0) {\n    e.resize();\n    return;\n  }\n\n  const to = e.canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n  if (to.width === 0 || to.height === 0) {\n    e.resize();\n    return;\n  }\n\n  // FLIP: invert the measured difference, then animate the inversion away.\n  // Scale rather than width/height — width is a layout property and a layout\n  // property tweened for 0.7 s is 0.7 s of reflow.\n  const scaleX = from.width / to.width;\n  const scaleY = from.height / to.height;\n  const x = from.left + from.width / 2 - (to.left + to.width / 2);\n  const y = from.top + from.height / 2 - (to.top + to.height / 2);\n\n  /* For the length of the flight the canvas is bigger than, and outside, the\n     slot it now lives in — so the slot must not clip. Written INLINE and\n     restored afterwards, because the thing being overridden is usually a\n     utility class on the consumer's own markup, and a rule in our stylesheet\n     would lose to it. Skip this and the transition is invisible: the canvas\n     is cropped to its destination rectangle from the first frame, which\n     looks exactly like no transition at all. */\n  const clipped = target.style.overflow;\n  target.style.overflow = 'visible';\n  const unclip = () => {\n    target.style.overflow = clipped;\n  };\n\n  e.flying = true;\n  const tl = gsap.timeline({\n    onComplete: () => {\n      e.flying = false;\n      unclip();\n      gsap.set(e.canvas, { clearProps: 'transform' });\n      // The ONE buffer resize, now that nothing is moving.\n      e.resize();\n    },\n  });\n\n  tl.fromTo(\n    e.canvas,\n    { x, y, scaleX, scaleY, transformOrigin: '50% 50%' },\n    { x: 0, y: 0, scaleX: 1, scaleY: 1, duration, ease },\n    0,\n  ).fromTo(\n    e.uniforms.uHandoff,\n    { value: 0 },\n    { value: 1, duration: duration / 2, ease: SURGE_EASE, yoyo: true, repeat: 1 },\n    0,\n  );\n\n  return () => {\n    tl.kill();\n    unclip();\n    e.flying = false;\n  };\n}, [active, duration, ease, reduced, engine, slots, first]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `disposeRenderer` stops the animation loop before releasing the context, so a queued frame cannot render into a lost one and throw out of the cleanup; it then calls `dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()` and zeroes the canvas dimensions to release the backing store immediately. The `ResizeObserver` is disconnected, both the canvas transform tweens and the `uHandoff` tween are killed, the material and geometry are disposed, and the canvas element is removed by the same effect that appended it. The flight effect's own cleanup kills its timeline and restores the destination slot's original `overflow`, so an unmount mid-flight cannot leave a slot permanently unclipped.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No animation loop is started: one frame is drawn when the context is built, and one more after each move and each resize, so the field is a still image rather than a drifting one. The handoff still moves the canvas — that is a DOM relocation, not motion — but the FLIP tween and the `uHandoff` surge are both skipped, and the drawing buffer is resized immediately instead of on a completion that will never fire. `prefers-reduced-motion` is read by the component through `useReducedMotion`, never inferred from the page.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Rendering the canvas from JSX. React unmounts the outgoing page's subtree on a route change, and a `<canvas ref>` inside it is destroyed with it — context, program, buffer and all. That is the exact failure this component exists to avoid, and it cannot be worked around from inside the page: the canvas has to be created by the effect and owned by nobody React can see.\n- A React-owned canvas under `reactStrictMode`. The strict-mode remount reuses the SAME element across both effect invocations, so the first cleanup's `forceContextLoss()` hands the second invocation a dead context and the component renders permanently blank — in development only, which is a genuinely expensive way to lose an afternoon. Creating the element inside the effect gives the second run a clean one.\n- Doing the move in `useEffect` instead of `useLayoutEffect`. Passive effects run after the browser has painted, so there is one composited frame in which the outgoing page is gone and the canvas has not arrived. It reads as a flash of empty box at exactly the moment the component is supposed to be proving continuity.\n- Measuring First inside the layout effect. By then React has already removed the outgoing subtree, so there is nothing left to measure and the FLIP either animates from the wrong rectangle or is skipped. The measurement has to happen in the handler, before `setState`.\n- Calling `renderer.setSize()` during the flight. Writing the canvas's width or height reallocates and clears the drawing buffer, so the object mid-animation flashes black for a frame. The `ResizeObserver` fires exactly then, because moving into a differently sized slot changes the border box — which is why it is muted by a `flying` flag rather than trusted.\n- A destination slot that clips. `overflow: hidden` on the slot crops the inbound canvas to its final rectangle from the first frame, so the flight is invisible and the result looks identical to no transition. Forcing `overflow: visible` inline for the duration is the only override that reliably beats a utility class.\n- Cloning instead of moving. `cloneNode(true)` on a canvas copies the element and NOT its bitmap or its context; `outerHTML` round-trips have the same effect. The clone is blank and the original is gone, so the page shows an empty box with no error anywhere.\n- Tweening `width` and `height` instead of `scale`. Besides reflowing on every frame, they are the two properties that reset the drawing buffer, so the animation would clear the surface sixty times a second.\n- Mounting the host inside the routed subtree. If the host unmounts on navigation the context goes with it and there is nothing left to hand over — the component silently degrades to the mount-a-new-one behaviour it replaces. It belongs in the layout, above the router outlet.\n- Assuming the canvas paints where you expect. It is `position: absolute; z-index: 1` so it can lift over static page content on the way past, which means anything meant to sit ON TOP of the field needs its own stacking context. Text without `relative z-10` disappears under the canvas for the length of the flight.\n- Letting the shader carry any state that could reset. Everything visible is a function of the absolute `setAnimationLoop` timestamp, so there is no accumulator to hand over. Drive the field from a per-frame increment instead and it will still restart on the handoff — the exact artefact the component was built to eliminate, hidden one layer deeper.\n\n## Notes\n\n- In Next.js App Router: mount `CanvasHandoffTransition` in `app/layout.tsx`, put a `CanvasHandoffSlot` in each page with an id of its own, and replace `<Link>` with a button calling `handoff(slotId, () => router.push(href))`. The component imports nothing from `next/navigation` — the route change is a callback, so the same host works under React Router or, as in the preview, plain `useState`.\n- The slot renders no children while WebGL is live. That is not cosmetic: React only reconciles children it created, so an empty child list is the guarantee that it will never touch the canvas sitting inside. Content that belongs over the field goes in a sibling, not inside the slot.\n- One context for the whole site is the real prize. Chromium allows roughly sixteen live WebGL contexts per renderer process and evicts the oldest without warning when you exceed it — a per-page canvas on a site with a persistent background is how an unrelated component three routes away turns black.\n- The field is deliberately slow, about eight seconds per visible cycle. Anything faster and the eye cannot hold a specific fold across the transition, which is the only direct evidence the viewer gets that the surface was not rebuilt.\n- Under reduced motion there is no animation loop at all — a single frame is drawn on build and again after each move — so the component costs nothing per frame for the people most likely to be on the weakest hardware.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWatch one bright fold of the field and click a nav item: the same fold is still there when the canvas lands, offset only by the time the flight took. Nothing blanks, blinks, or restarts. The canvas visibly changes proportion in flight — wide banner to tall column — with the dither cells stretching along with it, then snapping to square at the end when the buffer is finally resized. Click twice in quick succession and the second flight starts from wherever the canvas is in the air rather than jumping to the first destination. In React DevTools the canvas element has no owner. Under `reactStrictMode` in development the preview renders on first load, not blank. With reduced motion the field is a still image and the canvas relocates without a flight.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "capture-phase-click-swallow": "# Build: Capture-Phase Click Swallow\n\nThrow a drag carousel and let go over a card, and the browser dispatches a `click` on it — `mousedown` and `mouseup` landed in the same subtree, so that is exactly what the spec asks for. The result is that flinging the track navigates somewhere. One listener on the drag surface in the capture phase, which runs before any descendant handler, calls `stopPropagation` and `preventDefault` and the click is gone. The work is in the two edges: it has to swallow exactly one, and it has to let keyboard activation through, because Enter and Space fire a click with no pointer sequence behind it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCancels the single click a finished drag would fire, in the capture phase, without eating the next real click or keyboard activation.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The listener is registered with `addEventListener('click', handler, true)` on the drag surface. The third argument is the technique: capture runs root-down, so this handler sees the click before the card's own `onClick` and before a router's link interception. In the bubble phase the navigation has already happened.\n2. It calls both `stopPropagation()` and `preventDefault()`. They do different jobs and neither covers the other: propagation stops listeners deeper in the path, while the default action — following an `<a href>`, submitting a form, toggling a `<label>` — is not a listener and is only cancelled by `preventDefault`. A swallow with only `stopPropagation` still navigates.\n3. `arm()` is called by the gesture when it ends *having moved*, which `drag-deadzone-capture` reports as `onDragEnd(state, { moved })`. Arming on a stationary press is how the click a user actually meant gets eaten.\n4. The arm is consumed inside the handler before the event is cancelled, so exactly one click is swallowed. A flag that stays set is the bug this replaces: the drag is suppressed, and so is the deliberate click that follows it.\n5. Two independent stand-downs cover the drag that produces no click at all — one that ends over empty space, or that the browser cancelled. A `pointerdown` on the surface disarms, because a fresh press means the awaited click never came; and a 250 ms timer disarms as a backstop, since the click that follows a `pointerup` is dispatched in the same turn and anything later is not it.\n6. `event.detail` is the keyboard test. For a pointer click it is the click count, 1 or more; for Enter, Space, and `element.click()` it is 0. Those return early and pass through untouched, so the widget stays operable from the keyboard.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TIMEOUT_MS = 250`\n  How long an arm survives with no click. The click after a `pointerup` arrives in the same turn, so one frame would do for a mouse; the margin is for the delayed synthetic click some touch browsers still emit. The ceiling is the time a person needs to press and release again — go much past ~400 ms and the arm can outlive its own gesture and reach into the next one, which is exactly the bug the expiry exists to prevent.\n- `event.detail threshold = 0`\n  Not a tuned number but a specified one: `UIEvent.detail` is the click count for a pointer-driven click and exactly 0 for a keyboard-driven or scripted one. Testing `detail === 0` is the only cross-browser tell available on the click event itself — `pointerId === -1` is only present where `click` is a `PointerEvent`, and the `screenX === 0 && screenY === 0` heuristic misfires on a genuine click in the top-left corner of the screen.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useClickSwallow(ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>, options?: { timeoutMs?: number; enabled?: boolean; onSwallow?: (event: MouseEvent) => void }): { arm: () => void; disarm: () => void; armed: RefObject<boolean>; swallowed: RefObject<number> }\n```\n\n- `timeoutMs: number` — default `250`\n  How long an arm survives with no click arriving. Backstop for a drag that ends over nothing clickable.\n- `enabled: boolean` — default `true`\n  Detach the listeners and stand down when false.\n- `onSwallow: (event: MouseEvent) => void`\n  The click that was cancelled. Already stopped and prevented by the time this runs.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Milliseconds an arm survives if no click follows. The click that follows a\n * `pointerup` is dispatched in the same turn, so anything over one frame is\n * already generous; the margin is for the delayed click some touch browsers\n * still emit. It has to stay well under the time a person needs to press and\n * release again, or the arm outlives its own gesture.\n */\nconst TIMEOUT_MS = 250;\n\n/**\n * Suppress the one `click` that a finished drag is about to produce.\n *\n * Pairs with any gesture that reports whether it moved — `drag-deadzone-capture`\n * exposes exactly that as `onDragEnd(state, { moved })`. Arm on `moved`, and\n * only on `moved`: arming after a stationary press is how the click a user\n * meant gets eaten.\n */\nexport function useClickSwallow(\n  ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>,\n  options: UseClickSwallowOptions = {},\n): ClickSwallowHandle {\n  const { timeoutMs = TIMEOUT_MS, enabled = true } = options;\n\n  const armed = useRef(false);\n  const swallowed = useRef(0);\n  const timer = useRef<number | null>(null);\n\n  const config = useRef({ timeoutMs, options });\n  config.current = { timeoutMs, options };\n\n  const disarm = useCallback(() => {\n    armed.current = false;\n    if (timer.current !== null) {\n      window.clearTimeout(timer.current);\n      timer.current = null;\n    }\n  }, []);\n\n  const arm = useCallback(() => {\n    disarm();\n    armed.current = true;\n    // An expiry, not a standing flag. A drag that ends over empty space, or\n    // one the browser cancelled, produces no click at all — and an arm left\n    // standing swallows the user's next real click, whenever that arrives.\n    timer.current = window.setTimeout(() => {\n      armed.current = false;\n      timer.current = null;\n    }, config.current.timeoutMs);\n  }, [disarm]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = ref.current;\n    if (!el || !enabled) return;\n\n    const onClickCapture = (e: MouseEvent) => {\n      if (!armed.current) return;\n\n      // Enter and Space on a focused link or button dispatch a click with\n      // `detail === 0` and no pointer sequence behind it, as does\n      // `element.click()`. There is no drag for those to be the tail of, and\n      // swallowing them is what makes a drag carousel keyboard-inoperable.\n      if (e.detail === 0) return;\n\n      // Consumed here, so exactly one click is swallowed per arm.\n      disarm();\n      swallowed.current += 1;\n\n      // Both, always. stopPropagation keeps it from the card's own handler and\n      // from the router's link interception; preventDefault cancels the\n      // default action, which is the part that actually follows an href.\n      e.stopPropagation();\n      e.preventDefault();\n\n      config.current.options.onSwallow?.(e);\n    };\n\n    // A fresh press means the previous gesture is finished and the click it\n    // was going to produce never arrived. Second safety net under the timer,\n    // and the one that matters — it is deterministic rather than timed.\n    const onPointerDown = () => disarm();\n\n    // The third argument is the entire technique. In the bubble phase the\n    // card's own handler has already run and the link has already navigated.\n    el.addEventListener('click', onClickCapture, true);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('click', onClickCapture, true);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n      // Without this the timer can fire after unmount and write to a ref that\n      // nothing reads, and on a remount the widget starts life armed.\n      disarm();\n    };\n  }, [ref, enabled, disarm]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The effect removes the capture-phase `click` listener with the same capture flag it was added with — passing `true` on the way out is required, since a listener added with capture is a different registration from one added without — removes the `pointerdown` listener, and calls `disarm()`, which clears the pending timeout and resets the flag so a remount does not start armed. No rAF, no pointer capture, nothing else held.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing changes, and the omission is deliberate rather than an oversight: this hook cancels an event, it does not animate anything, and there is no duration, distance, or easing in it to reduce. Behaviour identical under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. Note that the keyboard path is unconditional for the same reason it matters here — the pass-through for `detail === 0` is an accessibility guarantee, not a motion setting, and it is not something a preference should be able to switch off.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Registering in the bubble phase. The card's own `onClick` runs first and the link has already navigated by the time the handler sees the event — the listener is correct and completely useless. The third argument to `addEventListener` is the whole fix.\n- Calling `stopPropagation()` without `preventDefault()`. Every JavaScript handler is silenced and the `<a href>` still navigates, because following a link is a default action rather than a listener. The failure is confusing precisely because the swallow visibly \"works\" on buttons and does nothing on links.\n- Leaving the flag set after the swallow. It eats the click after the drag as well, so the user drags, then clicks a card deliberately, and nothing happens. Consuming it inside the handler is one line and is the difference between a fix and a new bug.\n- Setting the flag with no expiry at all. A drag that ends over empty space never produces a click, so the arm just sits there — and swallows a genuine click a minute later, at which point nobody will connect the two events.\n- Swallowing keyboard activation. Enter and Space on a focused card dispatch a click with `detail === 0`, and if the flag happens to be armed the widget silently stops responding to the keyboard. It never shows up in testing because a carousel is tested by dragging it.\n- Arming on every gesture instead of only on ones that moved. Without a dead zone upstream reporting `moved`, a two-pixel wobble during a click arms the swallow and the click is cancelled — the exact behaviour that makes drag carousels feel unreliable.\n- Assuming the click lands on the element the pointer is over. Click targets the nearest common ancestor of the `mousedown` and `mouseup` targets, and while a pointer is captured the compatibility mouse events are retargeted to the capturing element. Scoping the listener to the drag surface is what makes it see the click at all.\n- Reaching for `stopImmediatePropagation()` by default. It also silences capture-phase listeners registered earlier on the same node, which can include analytics or a focus manager that had nothing to do with the drag. Escalate to it only when a library has attached its own capture listener to the same element.\n- Leaving the timer running at unmount. It fires against a ref nothing reads, and on remount the widget can start life armed and swallow the first click a user makes.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Designed to pair with `drag-deadzone-capture`: that hook decides whether the gesture moved, this one suppresses the consequence. They are separate because the classification is useful on its own, and because the swallow has edge cases — one click and no more, keyboard passes through — that have nothing to do with dragging.\n- A component that activates on `pointerup` rather than on `click` cannot be helped by this; there is no click to cancel. That is a good argument for activating on `click` in a draggable surface, since `click` is also what the keyboard produces.\n- `preventDefault` on the click does not undo focus. Focus was moved by `mousedown`, earlier in the sequence, so a swallowed drag can leave the card under the pointer focused. It is invisible in practice because focus from a pointer does not match `:focus-visible`.\n- A `dblclick` is dispatched independently of the clicks that preceded it, so a component listening for double-click needs the same treatment applied to `dblclick`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick a card: it opens, and the on-screen counter shows one activation and no swallow. Drag the row and release over a card: the row moves, the card does not open, and the swallow counter goes up by exactly one. Immediately click that same card: it opens — the swallow was consumed by the drag and did not carry over. Drag and release over the gap between cards, then click a card: it opens, because the arm expired instead of waiting for a click that never came. Tab to a card and press Enter, then Space: it opens both times, including immediately after a drag, and the swallow counter does not move. Put a real `<a href>` in the row and drag onto it: the page does not navigate.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "carousel": "# Build: Carousel\n\nA horizontally scrolling track with `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory`, not a transformed strip, so touch swipe, trackpad momentum and keyboard scrolling are the browser's own. JavaScript only answers what the DOM will not: which slide is showing and whether either end has been reached. Positions are read from each slide's bounding box rather than computed from a card width, so mixed widths, responsive widths and right-to-left layouts need no extra maths.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA gallery you slide through on native scroll-snap: swipe, trackpad and arrow keys for free, plus end-aware arrows and a dot indicator.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The track is a flex row with `overflow-x-auto`, `overscroll-x-contain`, `snap-x snap-mandatory` and the scrollbar hidden inline (`[scrollbar-width:none]` plus `[&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden`). `gap` is an inline style from the `gap` prop. `tabIndex={0}` makes the track focusable, so once it has focus the arrow keys scroll it natively, and a `focus-visible` ring shows where focus went.\n2. Each `CarouselItem` is a `role=\"group\"` with `aria-roledescription=\"slide\"` and `w-full shrink-0 snap-start`, so by default exactly one slide fills the track. The width lives on the item, which lets a consumer pass `w-[85%]` for a peek of the next slide. With `align=\"center\"` the track adds `[&>*]:snap-center` so every slide rests in the middle instead.\n3. `Carousel` walks its children with `Children.map` and clones each valid element with `aria-label` set to \"`n` of `count`\", where `count` is the number of valid elements. Callers never number their own slides. The outer wrapper is a `role=\"group\"` with `aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"` and takes the required `aria-label`.\n4. `measure()` reads the track. `inlineScrollOffset(scrollLeft, direction)` turns the physical `scrollLeft` (negative in RTL) into a distance from the inline start. `atStart` is that offset `<= 1` and `atEnd` is `>= scrollWidth - clientWidth - 1`. The active index is pinned to 0 at the start and to the last slide at the end. Between the ends it is the slide whose `scrollOffsetForItem(trackRect, itemRect, 0, direction, align)` is smallest in absolute value, which means the slide closest to its own snap position.\n5. One effect attaches a `ResizeObserver` to the track and a passive `scroll` listener, both calling `measure`. The observer fires once on `observe()`, and that call takes the first measurement, so the effect body never calls `setState` synchronously.\n6. `scrollToIndex(i)` clamps `i` to the slide range, computes the target `left` with `scrollOffsetForItem(trackRect, targetRect, track.scrollLeft, direction, align)` and calls `track.scrollTo({ left, behavior })`. `behavior` is `\"auto\"` when `matchMedia(\"(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\")` matches at click time and `\"smooth\"` otherwise. The prev and next arrows call it with `active - 1` and `active + 1`, and each dot calls it with its own index.\n7. The arrows are two small `IconButton`s (\"Previous slide\", \"Next slide\") in a right-aligned row above the track, disabled at the matching end. The dots are real `<button>`s below the track with `aria-current` on the active one. The active dot widens from `w-1.5` to `w-4` and switches to `bg-text-primary`, with `transition-all duration-200 ease-out`.\n8. The wrapper sets `dir` from `useDirection()` (React Aria's `useLocale().direction`), so inside an RTL `I18nProvider` the track scrolls from the right and the geometry helpers use the right edges.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `edge slack = 1px`\n  `scrollLeft` is fractional on hi-DPI displays, so an exact comparison leaves a fully scrolled track one sub-pixel short of the end and the Next arrow stays enabled. One pixel absorbs the rounding without disabling an arrow while a real slide is still hidden.\n- `gap = 16 (px)`\n  The default space between slides, applied as an inline `gap`. Snap positions come from each slide's measured box, so changing it never desynchronises the arrows or dots.\n- `dot sizes = 6px round, 16px × 6px active, 200ms ease-out`\n  The active dot is a short pill rather than a colour swap alone, so the position reads without relying on contrast. 200ms is long enough to see the dot grow and short enough to keep up with a fast swipe across several slides.\n- `arrow size = IconButton size=\"small\" (32 × 32)`\n  Keeps the control row compact above a card. The arrows sit in their own `justify-end` row so they never overlap slide content or cover a swipe target.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Carousel(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"children\" | \"onScroll\"> & { children: ReactNode; \"aria-label\": string; showArrows?: boolean; showDots?: boolean; align?: \"start\" | \"center\"; gap?: number; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction CarouselItem(props: HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement> & { children: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Required. Names the carousel group for assistive tech.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  `CarouselItem` elements, as direct children. Each one is labelled \"n of count\" automatically.\n- `showArrows: boolean` — default `true`\n  Previous and next icon buttons in a right-aligned row above the track. Each disables at its end.\n- `showDots: boolean` — default `true`\n  Clickable position dots below the track. Only rendered when there are at least two slides.\n- `align: \"start\" | \"center\"` — default `\"start\"`\n  Where a slide comes to rest when it snaps. Also drives which slide counts as active.\n- `gap: number` — default `16`\n  Space between slides in px, applied as an inline style.\n- `className (CarouselItem): string` — default `\"w-full\"`\n  Override the slide width, such as `w-[85%]` for a peek or `w-[220px]` for fixed cards.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Carousel({\n  children,\n  showArrows = true,\n  showDots = true,\n  align = \"start\",\n  gap = 16,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: CarouselProps) {\n  const trackRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const [active, setActive] = useState(0);\n  const [atStart, setAtStart] = useState(true);\n  const [atEnd, setAtEnd] = useState(false);\n\n  const count = Children.toArray(children).filter(isValidElement).length;\n\n  const items = useCallback(\n    () => Array.from(trackRef.current?.children ?? []) as HTMLElement[],\n    [],\n  );\n\n  const measure = useCallback(() => {\n    const track = trackRef.current;\n    if (!track) return;\n\n    // 1px of slack: scrollLeft is fractional on hi-DPI displays, so an exact\n    // comparison leaves the end arrow enabled on a fully scrolled track.\n    const offset = inlineScrollOffset(track.scrollLeft, direction);\n    const start = offset <= 1;\n    const end = offset >= track.scrollWidth - track.clientWidth - 1;\n    setAtStart(start);\n    setAtEnd(end);\n\n    const all = items();\n    if (all.length === 0) return;\n\n    // At the far end the last cards share the viewport, so the final card can\n    // never reach the start edge and \"nearest\" would settle on the one before\n    // it — leaving the last dot permanently unlit and making a click on it\n    // look broken. The ends are therefore pinned rather than measured.\n    if (end) {\n      setActive(all.length - 1);\n      return;\n    }\n    if (start) {\n      setActive(0);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let nearest = 0;\n    let shortest = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;\n    all.forEach((item, index) => {\n      const distance = Math.abs(scrollOffsetForItem(track.getBoundingClientRect(), item.getBoundingClientRect(), 0, direction, align));\n      if (distance < shortest) {\n        shortest = distance;\n        nearest = index;\n      }\n    });\n    setActive(nearest);\n  }, [items, direction, align]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const track = trackRef.current;\n    if (!track) return;\n\n    // ResizeObserver fires once on observe, which is what takes the first\n    // measurement — no synchronous setState in the effect body.\n    const observer = new ResizeObserver(measure);\n    observer.observe(track);\n    track.addEventListener(\"scroll\", measure, { passive: true });\n    return () => {\n      observer.disconnect();\n      track.removeEventListener(\"scroll\", measure);\n    };\n  }, [measure]);\n\n  const scrollToIndex = (index: number) => {\n    const track = trackRef.current;\n    const target = items()[Math.max(0, Math.min(index, count - 1))];\n    if (!track || !target) return;\n    // `matchMedia` rather than a hook: this runs on click, so reading the\n    // preference at that moment is both current and cheap.\n    const reduce = window.matchMedia(\"(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\").matches;\n    const left = scrollOffsetForItem(track.getBoundingClientRect(), target.getBoundingClientRect(), track.scrollLeft, direction, align);\n    track.scrollTo({ left, behavior: reduce ? \"auto\" : \"smooth\" });\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={ref}\n      role=\"group\"\n      dir={direction}\n      aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"\n      className={cx(\"flex w-full flex-col gap-4\", className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      {showArrows && count > 0 ? (\n        <div className=\"flex items-center justify-end gap-2\">\n          <IconButton\n            icon={RiArrowLeftSLine}\n            size=\"small\"\n            aria-label=\"Previous slide\"\n            disabled={atStart}\n            onClick={() => scrollToIndex(active - 1)}\n          />\n          <IconButton\n            icon={RiArrowRightSLine}\n            size=\"small\"\n            aria-label=\"Next slide\"\n            disabled={atEnd}\n            onClick={() => scrollToIndex(active + 1)}\n          />\n        </div>\n      ) : null}\n\n      {/* `tabIndex` makes the track focusable so it can be scrolled with the\n          arrow keys, which is the browser's own behaviour once it has focus.\n          The scrollbar is hidden inline rather than through a global class, so\n          the component stays self-contained. */}\n      <div\n        ref={trackRef}\n        tabIndex={0}\n        className={cx(\n          \"relative flex w-full overflow-x-auto overscroll-x-contain outline-none\",\n          \"snap-x snap-mandatory [scrollbar-width:none] [&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden\",\n          \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2\",\n          align === \"center\" && \"[&>*]:snap-center\",\n        )}\n        style={{ gap }}\n      >\n        {/* The position label is applied here so callers never have to\n            hand-number their own slides. */}\n        {Children.map(children, (child, index) =>\n          isValidElement<CarouselItemProps>(child)\n            ? cloneElement(child, { \"aria-label\": `${index + 1} of ${count}` })\n            : child,\n        )}\n      </div>\n\n      {showDots && count > 1 ? (\n        <div className=\"flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5\">\n          {Array.from({ length: count }, (_, index) => (\n            <button\n              key={index}\n              type=\"button\"\n              aria-label={`Go to slide ${index + 1}`}\n              aria-current={index === active}\n              onClick={() => scrollToIndex(index)}\n              className={cx(\n                \"h-1.5 cursor-pointer rounded-full transition-all duration-200 ease-out\",\n                index === active\n                  ? \"w-4 bg-text-primary\"\n                  : \"w-1.5 bg-background-tertiary-default hover:bg-border-button-active\",\n              )}\n            />\n          ))}\n        </div>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The single effect disconnects its `ResizeObserver` and removes the passive `scroll` listener from the track on unmount, or when `direction` or `align` change and `measure` is rebuilt. No timers or animation frames are used.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Arrow and dot clicks jump straight to the target slide (`behavior: \"auto\"`) instead of smooth-scrolling. The check reads `matchMedia` at click time, so a preference changed mid-session applies immediately. Swipe and trackpad scrolling are the user's own input and stay as they are.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Replacing the scroll track with a `translateX` strip. That throws away native swipe, momentum, trackpad and keyboard scrolling, and then all of them have to be rebuilt by hand. The design depends on the browser doing the scrolling.\n- Computing positions as `index * cardWidth`. It breaks for mixed widths, a responsive `w-[85%]` peek, a changed gap and RTL. Always measure bounding rects and go through `scrollOffsetForItem`.\n- Choosing the \"nearest\" slide at the far end. When the last slides share the viewport the final one can never reach the start edge, so the last dot would never light and clicking it would look broken. The ends must stay pinned.\n- Wrapping slides in fragments or extra elements. The track reads `children` as DOM slides and the labels come from `Children.map`, so a fragment of three slides counts as one and the labels and dots go out of step. Render `CarouselItem`s as direct children.\n- Rendering conditional slides as `{flag && <CarouselItem/>}`. `count` ignores the `false`, but `Children.map` still advances its index for it, so labels read \"2 of 2\" for the only slide. Filter the array before rendering.\n- Hard-coding `behavior: \"smooth\"`. Under reduced motion the jump must be instant. The component reads the media query at click time, so keep that check when adding new ways to move the track.\n\n## Notes\n\n- There is no controlled index API on purpose. To drive the track yourself, scroll the `.overflow-x-auto` element exactly as `scrollToIndex` does. The arrows and dots pick the change up from the `scroll` event.\n- `showArrows={false} showDots={false}` gives a bare swipe-only track, for example a logo strip.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith four full-width slides, the Previous arrow starts disabled and the first dot is the wide one. Clicking Next smooth-scrolls exactly one slide and the second dot widens. Swiping or trackpad-scrolling snaps to a slide and updates the dots. At the last slide Next disables and the last dot lights, even when slides are narrower than the track. Clicking any dot lands on that slide. With reduced motion the arrows jump instantly. In an RTL locale the track starts at the right edge and Next moves leftward.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "changes-diff-panel": "# Build: Changes Diff Panel\n\nThe review side of an agent that edits code: a header with the number of uncommitted changes and their total added and removed lines, then one row per changed file with its path, +/− counts and a New or Deleted tag. Each row opens to an inline unified diff with line numbers and green or red line backgrounds. Accept or reject a file from its row, or every pending file from the header; decided files show an Accepted or Rejected tag with an undo, and the header totals only count what is still pending.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nUncommitted-changes list with +/− counts, an inline diff per file and accept / reject per file or for all; totals update live.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. parsePatch reads each file as a unified diff body: an \"@@ -a,b +c,d @@\" header resets the old and new line counters, \"+\" lines take the next new number, \"-\" lines the next old number, context lines advance both, and \"\\ No newline\" markers are skipped. The same pass counts additions and removals, so counts never drift from the diff.\n2. Decisions are a map from path to \"accepted\" or \"rejected\"; a missing key means pending. They can be controlled (decisions + onDecisionsChange) or left to the panel (defaultDecisions). applyDecision writes one decision, or deletes it for undo, across a list of paths, so a single file and Accept all share one code path.\n3. The header derives everything from that map on each render: pending files, their summed +/− lines, and the accepted and rejected counts. When nothing is pending it switches to \"All changes reviewed\" and the bulk buttons disable.\n4. Rows are React Aria Disclosures inside a DisclosureGroup with allowsMultipleExpanded. The trigger button sits inside a heading and the accept / reject buttons are its siblings, so no button is nested in another.\n5. Expansion is held by the panel (defaultExpanded seeds it); with collapseOnDecide a decided file folds its diff, so the list shortens as you review.\n6. Below 28rem wide the panel hides the folder part of each path and the New / Deleted tag (the file icon still shows the status), so file names stay readable.\n7. Rejected files strike through their path and dim their diff instead of hiding it, so the reviewer can still read what was turned down and undo it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `diff max height = 18rem (max-h-72)`\n  Long files scroll inside their own box, so one big diff does not push every other file off the panel.\n- `line tones = notification success / error background tokens`\n  Soft lime and rose fills that keep code readable in both themes; the +/− signs and counts use the matching foreground tokens.\n- `rejected dim = opacity 0.6`\n  Clearly secondary while staying legible, unlike hiding the diff, which would make undo a blind action.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ChangesDiffPanel(props: { files: { path: string; patch: string; status?: \"added\" | \"modified\" | \"deleted\" }[]; decisions?: Record<string, \"accepted\" | \"rejected\">; defaultDecisions?: Record<string, \"accepted\" | \"rejected\">; onDecisionsChange?: (next, change: { paths: string[]; decision: \"accepted\" | \"rejected\" | null }) => void; defaultExpanded?: string[]; collapseOnDecide?: boolean; title?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `files: { path: string; patch: string; status?: \"added\" | \"modified\" | \"deleted\" }[]`\n  Changed files with a unified diff body each.\n- `decisions: Record<string, \"accepted\" | \"rejected\">`\n  Controlled decisions keyed by path; missing means pending.\n- `defaultDecisions: Record<string, \"accepted\" | \"rejected\">`\n  Initial decisions when uncontrolled.\n- `onDecisionsChange: (next, change: { paths, decision }) => void`\n  Called by every accept, reject and undo, single or bulk.\n- `defaultExpanded: string[]` — default `[]`\n  Paths whose diff starts open.\n- `collapseOnDecide: boolean` — default `true`\n  Fold a file once it is accepted or rejected.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Changes\"`\n  Accessible name of the panel.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the panel surface (height, width).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst HUNK_HEADER = /^@@ -(\\d+)(?:,\\d+)? \\+(\\d+)(?:,\\d+)? @@/;\n\n/** Turns a unified diff body into rows with old and new line numbers, and counts its additions and removals. */\nexport function parsePatch(patch: string): ParsedPatch {\n  const rows: DiffRow[] = [];\n  let oldNo = 1;\n  let newNo = 1;\n  let added = 0;\n  let removed = 0;\n  for (const raw of patch.replace(/\\n+$/, \"\").split(\"\\n\")) {\n    const hunk = HUNK_HEADER.exec(raw);\n    if (hunk) {\n      oldNo = Math.max(1, Number(hunk[1]));\n      newNo = Math.max(1, Number(hunk[2]));\n      rows.push({ kind: \"hunk\", text: raw });\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (raw.startsWith(\"\\\\\")) continue;\n    const sign = raw.charAt(0);\n    if (sign === \"+\") {\n      rows.push({ kind: \"add\", text: raw.slice(1), oldNo: null, newNo: newNo++ });\n      added += 1;\n    } else if (sign === \"-\") {\n      rows.push({ kind: \"del\", text: raw.slice(1), oldNo: oldNo++, newNo: null });\n      removed += 1;\n    } else {\n      rows.push({ kind: \"ctx\", text: sign === \" \" ? raw.slice(1) : raw, oldNo: oldNo++, newNo: newNo++ });\n    }\n  }\n  return { rows, added, removed };\n}\n\n/** Applies one decision (or null to undo) to several paths and returns a new map. */\nfunction applyDecision(prev: ChangeDecisions, paths: string[], decision: ChangeDecision | null): ChangeDecisions {\n  const next = { ...prev };\n  for (const path of paths) {\n    if (decision) next[path] = decision;\n    else delete next[path];\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers; all state is plain React state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only the chevron turn, row hover and the rejected dim fade animate, and all use motion-reduce:transition-none; diffs open and close instantly in every mode.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Paths are the keys for decisions and expansion; two entries with the same path will share one decision.\n- In controlled mode, update decisions from onDecisionsChange or the buttons appear dead.\n- Pass only the diff body (hunks and lines). File headers such as \"diff --git\" or \"+++ b/file\" would be read as added lines.\n\n## Notes\n\n- DiffCounts and parsePatch are exported for summaries elsewhere, for example a commit bar that reads \"3 files, +24 −7\".\n- The status tag is optional: status \"added\" shows New, \"deleted\" shows Deleted, anything else shows only the counts.\n- Line text is rendered as plain text in a monospace table; plug in your own highlighter if you need colours per token.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith five pending files the header reads \"5 uncommitted changes\" with the summed +/− lines; accepting one file drops it from the totals, shows Accepted and folds its diff; Reject all marks every remaining file Rejected and the header reads \"All changes reviewed\"; undo returns a file to pending and restores the totals; clicking a row toggles its inline diff with old or new line numbers.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "channel-tabs-bar-list": "# Build: Channel Tabs Bar List\n\nA grey card with the kit Tabs on top and an uppercase metric caption on the right. Each tab holds a ranked list where every row sits on a light blue bar sized against the leader, with an optional icon, the label and the row's share of the tab total. Five rows show by default; the last one fades and a chevron button reveals the rest.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA tabbed card of ranked horizontal bars: switch channels, campaigns or pages and each row shows its share of sessions.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Render the kit `Tabs`, `TabList` and one `TabPanel` per tab, selection controlled (`selectedKey`) or uncontrolled (`defaultSelectedKey`, else the first tab); React Aria gives the arrow-key roving focus and `aria-selected`.\n2. Rank each tab with `rankBars`: sort by value descending (label as tie-break), take `share = value / total` for the percent label and `width = value / max × 100` for the bar, so the leader always spans the row and the rest read as fractions of it.\n3. Draw each row as a `relative isolate` list item with an absolutely positioned bar behind the content (`-z-10`), its width set through a `--bar` custom property and `w-(--bar)`, so the class can also carry `starting:w-0` and the bar grows from zero whenever a panel mounts.\n4. Slice to `limit` rows while collapsed, fade the last visible row to 45% and show a small kit `IconButton` with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` pointing at the list; expanding rotates its chevron 180° and lists every row.\n5. Show the metric caption beside the tabs from `@sm` (24rem), sharing the tab bar's bottom border, and as its own right-aligned line above the list in narrower containers so three tabs still fit at 300px.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `row height = 36px (h-9), 4px gap`\n  Matches the 14px body line with room for the bar to read as a block rather than a stripe.\n- `limit = 5`\n  Five ranked rows cover most of the traffic; the long tail stays one click away.\n- `bar grow = 500ms ease-out from 0 via @starting-style`\n  Long enough to read the ranking settle when switching tabs, short enough not to delay reading values.\n- `teaser opacity = 45%`\n  Signals that more rows follow the fifth without hiding its value.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype BarListItem = { id: string; label: string; value: number; icon?: ComponentType }\ntype BarListTab = { id: string; label: string; items: BarListItem[] }\nfunction ChannelTabsBarList(props: ChannelTabsBarListProps): JSX.Element\nfunction rankBars(items: BarListItem[]): (BarListItem & { share: number; width: number })[]\n```\n\n- `tabs: BarListTab[]`\n  One tab per grouping, each with its rows in any order.\n- `selectedKey / defaultSelectedKey / onSelectionChange: string` — default `first tab id`\n  Selected tab, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `metricLabel: string` — default `\"Sessions\"`\n  Caption for the value column and part of each list's accessible name.\n- `display: \"share\" | \"value\"` — default `\"share\"`\n  Percent of the tab total or the raw number.\n- `limit: number` — default `5`\n  Rows shown before the expand button.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for numbers and percents.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Traffic sources\"`\n  Accessible name of the card and the tab list.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Sort largest first and size every bar against the leader, so the top row\n *  always spans the track and the rest read as fractions of it. Shares are\n *  of the tab total and drive the percent labels. */\nexport function rankBars(items: BarListItem[]): RankedRow[] {\n  const total = items.reduce((sum, it) => sum + Math.max(0, it.value), 0);\n  const max = items.reduce((m, it) => Math.max(m, it.value), 0);\n  return [...items]\n    .sort((a, b) => b.value - a.value || a.label.localeCompare(b.label))\n    .map((it) => ({\n      ...it,\n      share: total > 0 ? Math.max(0, it.value) / total : 0,\n      width: max > 0 ? (Math.max(0, it.value) / max) * 100 : 0,\n    }));\n}\n\nfunction BarRows({\n  rows,\n  limit,\n  display,\n  label,\n  number,\n  percent,\n}: {\n  rows: RankedRow[];\n  limit: number;\n  display: \"share\" | \"value\";\n  label: string;\n  number: Intl.NumberFormat;\n  percent: Intl.NumberFormat;\n}) {\n  const listId = useId();\n  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const hidden = Math.max(0, rows.length - limit);\n  const shown = expanded || hidden === 0 ? rows : rows.slice(0, limit);\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-2\">\n      <ol id={listId} aria-label={label} className=\"flex flex-col gap-1\">\n        {shown.map((row, i) => {\n          const Icon = row.icon;\n          // The last collapsed row fades out to say \"the list goes on\".\n          const teaser = !expanded && hidden > 0 && i === shown.length - 1;\n          return (\n            <li key={row.id} className={cx(\"relative isolate h-9\", teaser && \"opacity-45\")}>\n              <span\n                aria-hidden\n                className=\"absolute inset-y-0 start-0 -z-10 w-(--bar) rounded-lg bg-status-blue-background transition-[width] duration-500 ease-out starting:w-0 motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                style={{ \"--bar\": `${row.width.toFixed(2)}%` } as CSSProperties}\n              />\n              <span className=\"flex h-full items-center justify-between gap-3 px-2.5\">\n                <span className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-2\">\n                  {Icon ? <Icon className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" aria-hidden /> : null}\n                  <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{row.label}</span>\n                </span>\n                <span className=\"shrink-0 text-body-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">\n                  {display === \"share\" ? percent.format(row.share) : number.format(row.value)}\n                </span>\n              </span>\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ol>\n      {hidden > 0 ? (\n        <IconButton\n          icon={RiArrowDownSLine}\n          size=\"small\"\n          aria-label={expanded ? \"Show fewer\" : `Show ${hidden} more`}\n          aria-expanded={expanded}\n          aria-controls={listId}\n          onClick={() => setExpanded((v) => !v)}\n          className={cx(\n            \"self-center [&>svg]:transition-transform [&>svg]:duration-200 [&>svg]:ease-out motion-reduce:[&>svg]:transition-none\",\n            expanded && \"[&>svg]:rotate-180\",\n          )}\n        />\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component adds no timers or listeners. The kit TabList disconnects its underline ResizeObserver on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The bars grow over 500ms, the chevron rotates over 200ms and the kit tab underline slides; the bar and chevron carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` they appear at full width and flip instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Size bars against the largest value, not the total. Against the total, a 39% leader fills only 39% of the row and the list looks empty.\n- Set the width through a CSS variable and a class. An inline `width` style beats the `starting:w-0` class, so the bars would never animate in.\n- The bars are `aria-hidden`; the written percent carries the value, so each row reads once as \"Google Ads 39%\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `display=\"value\"` to show raw session counts instead of shares.\n- Inactive panels unmount (React Aria default), so the expanded state resets per tab and the bars regrow on every switch.\n- `rankBars` is exported for reuse in other ranked lists.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo opens on Channels with Google Ads 39%, Meta 25%, X Ads 14%, LinkedIn 8% and a faded Email 7%, each bar sized against Google Ads. Clicking the chevron shows TikTok 4% and Referral 3% and sets aria-expanded. Choosing Campaigns ranks \"Free trial push · US\" first with five rows and a \"Show 1 more\" button. At 360px the three tabs fit and the Sessions caption moves above the list. Light and dark both read correctly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "char-stagger-scale-up": "# Build: Char Stagger Then Scale-Up\n\nThe headline is on screen from the first frame — small and ghosted, reading as a label. Characters resolve one at a time from left to right, and just before the last one lands the whole word scales to full size with a `back.out` overshoot. It assembles itself, then it asserts itself. Supporting copy fades up a quarter of a second into the scale, so the page is never doing only one thing at a time.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCharacters resolve left to right, then the whole word scales up with an overshoot as the last one lands.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The text is split in JSX, not in the DOM: `text.split(\" \")` gives word boxes, `Array.from(word)` gives one `inline-block` span per character. React owns every node, so no library mutates the tree and no `revert()` is needed to restore it.\n2. The idle state — `opacity: dimOpacity` per character and `transform: scale(scaleFrom)` on the word — is written as inline JSX styles, not by GSAP. That makes the server-rendered markup and the no-JS render identical to the pre-animation frame, and it means a parent re-render cannot wipe out what GSAP wrote, because React only ever diffs props against props.\n3. A `gsap.timeline` first tweens `[data-char]` to `opacity: 1` with `stagger: 0.07` and `duration: 0.28` on `power1.out`.\n4. The word scale is appended at position `\">-0.05\"` — 50 ms *before* the character stagger ends. The overlap is what fuses two phases into one gesture; at `\">\"` they read as two separate animations queued back to back.\n5. Supporting copy (`[data-fade]`) is appended at `\"<0.25\"` — 250 ms after the *scale started*, not after it finished. Relative-to-previous-start positioning is what keeps the choreography overlapping rather than sequential.\n6. `transformOrigin: \"50% 50%\"` is set explicitly on the scale tween even though it is the default: a heading inside a flex column can inherit an origin from an earlier transform on an ancestor, and the word then grows out of one corner.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SCALE_FROM = 0.42`\n  Starting scale of the word. Around 0.4 the text reads as a caption; past ~0.65 the growth is not large enough to register as a change of role, and below ~0.3 the characters are illegible while they stagger, so the first half of the animation is wasted.\n- `DIM_OPACITY = 0.24`\n  Resting strength of an unresolved character, as opacity on `currentColor`. Zero would make the reveal a pop-in and lose the ghosted-label reading; above ~0.4 there is not enough contrast between resolved and unresolved for the sweep to be visible.\n- `CHAR_STAGGER = 0.07`\n  Seconds between adjacent characters. A ten-character word takes 0.7s to sweep. Below ~0.04 the characters resolve as a block; above ~0.12 a long headline outlasts the reader's patience.\n- `CHAR_DURATION = 0.28`\n  Seconds for one character. Deliberately four times the stagger, so several characters are always mid-resolve — that overlap is what makes it a sweep rather than a sequence of individual pops.\n- `SCALE_DURATION = 0.9`\n  Seconds for the scale-up. `back.out` spends most of its curve on the settle, so a shorter duration compresses the overshoot into something that reads as a bounce artefact.\n- `OVERSHOOT = 1.3`\n  The `back.out(n)` parameter. Around 1.3 the word passes a few percent beyond full size and returns. At 1.7 (GSAP's default) the overshoot on large type is tens of pixels and reads as a wobble.\n- `FADE_STAGGER / FADE_DURATION / FADE_Y = 0.12 / 0.7 / 18`\n  Supporting copy rises 18px over 0.7s, 0.12s apart. The travel is small on purpose — body copy that moves as far as the headline competes with it.\n- `VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.35`\n  Fraction visible before the timeline runs. A headline is tall; requiring more than about a third means it has to be nearly centred before anything happens, and requiring less starts it while it is still a sliver at the bottom edge.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CharStaggerScaleUp(props: { text: string; children?: ReactNode; scaleFrom?: number; dimOpacity?: number; charStagger?: number; charDuration?: number; scaleDuration?: number; overshoot?: number; delay?: number; startOnView?: boolean; className?: string; textClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `text: string`\n  The headline. Split per word and per character in JSX.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Supporting copy. Fades up after the scale has begun.\n- `scaleFrom: number` — default `0.42`\n  Starting scale of the whole word.\n- `dimOpacity: number` — default `0.24`\n  Starting opacity of each character.\n- `charStagger: number` — default `0.07`\n  Seconds between character reveals.\n- `charDuration: number` — default `0.28`\n  Seconds for one character to resolve.\n- `scaleDuration: number` — default `0.9`\n  Seconds for the scale-up.\n- `overshoot: number` — default `1.3`\n  The `back.out(n)` parameter — how far past 100% the word travels.\n- `delay: number` — default `0`\n  Seconds before the timeline starts.\n- `startOnView: boolean` — default `true`\n  Run when scrolled into view rather than on mount.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n- `textClassName: string`\n  Merged onto the headline span — this is where type size and tracking go.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst SCALE_FROM = 0.42;\nconst DIM_OPACITY = 0.24;\nconst CHAR_STAGGER = 0.07;\nconst CHAR_DURATION = 0.28;\nconst SCALE_DURATION = 0.9;\nconst OVERSHOOT = 1.3;\nconst FADE_DURATION = 0.7;\nconst FADE_STAGGER = 0.12;\nconst FADE_Y = 18;\nconst VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.35;\n\nexport function CharStaggerScaleUp({\n  text,\n  children,\n  scaleFrom = SCALE_FROM,\n  dimOpacity = DIM_OPACITY,\n  charStagger = CHAR_STAGGER,\n  charDuration = CHAR_DURATION,\n  scaleDuration = SCALE_DURATION,\n  overshoot = OVERSHOOT,\n  delay = 0,\n  startOnView = true,\n  className,\n  textClassName,\n}: CharStaggerScaleUpProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const word = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { inView } = useInView(root, { threshold: VIEW_THRESHOLD, once: true });\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const wordEl = word.current;\n    // Reduced motion needs no branch here: the idle state written into the\n    // JSX below is already the finished state, so doing nothing is correct.\n    if (!el || !wordEl || reduced) return;\n    if (startOnView && !inView) return;\n\n    const chars = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-char]'));\n    const fades = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-fade]'));\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({ delay });\n\n      tl.to(chars, {\n        opacity: 1,\n        duration: charDuration,\n        ease: 'power1.out',\n        stagger: charStagger,\n      }).to(\n        wordEl,\n        {\n          scale: 1,\n          duration: scaleDuration,\n          ease: `back.out(${overshoot})`,\n          // Set explicitly even though it is the default: a block-level\n          // heading inside a flex column can inherit an origin from an\n          // earlier transform, and the word then grows out of one corner.\n          transformOrigin: '50% 50%',\n        },\n        '>-0.05',\n      );\n\n      if (fades.length > 0) {\n        tl.to(\n          fades,\n          {\n            opacity: 1,\n            y: 0,\n            duration: FADE_DURATION,\n            ease: 'power3.out',\n            stagger: FADE_STAGGER,\n          },\n          '<0.25',\n        );\n      }\n    }, el);\n\n    // revert(), not kill(): it also strips the inline transform and opacity\n    // GSAP wrote, handing the nodes back in exactly the state React rendered.\n    return () => ctx.revert();\n  }, [\n    text,\n    reduced,\n    inView,\n    startOnView,\n    delay,\n    charDuration,\n    charStagger,\n    scaleDuration,\n    overshoot,\n  ]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The timeline is built inside a `gsap.context` scoped to the root, and teardown calls `ctx.revert()`. That kills the tweens and strips the inline `transform` and `opacity` GSAP wrote, handing the nodes back in exactly the state React rendered — which matters under React 19 StrictMode, where the effect runs twice on mount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The idle inline styles collapse to the finished state (`scale(1)`, `opacity: 1`, no translate) and the effect returns before building a timeline. The headline and its supporting copy are fully rendered at full size on first paint — nothing is hidden and nothing waits for a trigger.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Splitting text before the webfont has loaded. Character and word boxes are measured against the fallback face, so widths and line breaks are wrong and never re-flow once the real font arrives — see `font-safe-text-split` for the gating and re-split lifecycle this class of component needs.\n- Letting a splitting library mutate the DOM under React. React has no record of the injected spans, so the next parent render replaces the whole subtree and the animation targets nodes that are no longer in the document. Splitting in JSX avoids the problem entirely.\n- Writing the idle state with `gsap.set` in the effect. The first paint then shows the finished headline at full size before the effect runs, and it visibly snaps down before animating up.\n- Splitting straight to characters without word boxes. A line break can then land between two letters of the same word.\n- Hardcoding the dim colour as a hex value. `#3d3d3d` is invisible on a light background; opacity on `currentColor` composites correctly against any theme.\n- Queuing the scale at `\">\"` instead of `\">-0.05\"`. The two phases stop overlapping and the headline reads as \"characters appear… then a separate thing happens\".\n- Cleaning up with `kill()` rather than `revert()`. `kill()` stops the tweens but leaves GSAP's inline `transform` and `opacity` on the nodes, so a remount starts from the finished state.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No text-splitting dependency: the split is declarative JSX. GSAP `SplitText` is reserved for the two components whose subject *is* SplitText (`nested-splittext-restore`, `font-safe-text-split`); everything else here splits in React so consumers are not forced to adopt a DOM-mutating splitter for a per-character stagger.\n- Each word is one `inline-block whitespace-nowrap` box with a real text node for the space between words, so lines still wrap normally between words.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn entering the viewport the characters brighten one at a time from left to right, and the word begins growing before the last character has finished. The word overshoots full size once and settles. Supporting copy starts rising while the word is still growing, not after. Unmount mid-animation and the nodes carry no inline `transform` or `opacity`. With reduced motion enabled the finished headline is present on first paint.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "chat-message-bubble": "# Build: Chat Message Bubble\n\nOne row of an AI chat transcript. User messages sit on the right in a filled bubble with an initials avatar, and assistant messages sit on the left in an outlined bubble with a sparkle avatar. Each row shows its time and an action row that appears on hover or focus. Copy writes to the real clipboard; assistant rows also get regenerate and a like/dislike pair. Single-backtick spans render as inline code.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nUser and assistant chat rows with avatar, bubble, time and inline code. Hovering shows copy, regenerate and like/dislike actions.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The row is an `<article>` with the avatar and a bubble column. `from=\"user\"` adds `flex-row-reverse` and `items-end`, so the same markup mirrors to the right. The bubble column is capped at `min(85%, 36rem)`, and the corner nearest the avatar is cut to `rounded-*-md` so the bubble points at its sender.\n2. `renderMessageText` splits the text on blank lines into `<p>` elements, then splits each paragraph with the capturing regex ``/(`[^`\\n]+`)/``. Because the group is captured, every code span lands at an odd index and becomes a `<code>` in mono at 0.875em on the opposite surface colour. Anything else, including a lone backtick, stays plain text.\n3. The action row starts at `opacity-0` and is revealed by `group-hover/message`, `group-focus-within/message` and `data-[pinned]`, which is set while the copy result is showing. On `@media (hover: none)` it is always visible. It uses opacity rather than `hidden`, so the buttons stay in the tab order and tabbing onto one reveals the row.\n4. Copy calls `navigator.clipboard.writeText`. If that rejects (an insecure origin or a refused permission), it copies from a hidden, selected `<textarea>` with `document.execCommand(\"copy\")`, removes it and puts focus back. A success swaps the icon to a check in lime and the label to \"Copied\", and a failure shows a rose warning and \"Copy failed\". A visually hidden `role=\"status\"` announces the result, and a 1600ms timer resets it.\n5. Like and dislike are React Aria `ToggleButton`s that both read one `rating` value, so pressing one clears the other and pressing it again clears the rating. They work controlled (`feedback`) or uncontrolled (`defaultFeedback`), and the selected one swaps to the filled thumb icon. Regenerate is a plain React Aria `Button` that only renders on assistant rows when `onRegenerate` is passed.\n6. Every action is wrapped in the kit `TooltipTrigger` with a 300ms delay, and its tooltip text matches its `aria-label`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `COPY_RESET_MS = 1600ms`\n  Long enough to read \"Copied\" after moving the pointer away, short enough that a second copy a moment later starts from the normal icon.\n- `tooltip delay = 300ms`\n  Four small icon buttons sit side by side. Without a delay, moving the pointer along the row flashes a tooltip for each one.\n- `bubble max width = min(85%, 36rem)`\n  About 70 characters per line on a wide screen. On a phone, 85% leaves room for the avatar and keeps user and assistant sides apart.\n- `action button = 28px, 16px icon`\n  Fits under the bubble next to the time without making the row taller than the caption needs.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ChatMessageBubble(props: { from: \"user\" | \"assistant\"; text: string; time?: string; dateTime?: string; name?: string; avatar?: ReactNode; feedback?: \"like\" | \"dislike\" | null; defaultFeedback?: \"like\" | \"dislike\" | null; onFeedbackChange?: (value: \"like\" | \"dislike\" | null) => void; onRegenerate?: () => void; onCopy?: (text: string) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction renderMessageText(text: string, codeClassName?: string): ReactNode\n```\n\n- `from: \"user\" | \"assistant\"`\n  Which side the row sits on, which bubble style it uses and which actions it gets.\n- `text: string`\n  Message text. Single backticks make inline code; a blank line starts a paragraph. This is also what Copy writes.\n- `time: string`\n  Time shown under the bubble, already formatted.\n- `dateTime: string`\n  Machine-readable value for the `<time>` element.\n- `name: string` — default `\"You\" / \"Assistant\"`\n  Sender name for the initials avatar and the row's accessible label.\n- `avatar: ReactNode`\n  Replaces the default avatar.\n- `feedback: \"like\" | \"dislike\" | null`\n  Controlled rating for an assistant row.\n- `defaultFeedback: \"like\" | \"dislike\" | null` — default `null`\n  Starting rating when uncontrolled.\n- `onFeedbackChange: (value: \"like\" | \"dislike\" | null) => void`\n  Called with the new rating, or null when it is cleared.\n- `onRegenerate: () => void`\n  Shows the regenerate button on an assistant row.\n- `onCopy: (text: string) => void`\n  Called after the text reaches the clipboard.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the row.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nasync function writeClipboard(text: string): Promise<boolean> {\n  try {\n    await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);\n    return true;\n  } catch {\n    // No Clipboard API (insecure origin) or permission refused: copy from a\n    // hidden, selected textarea instead, then hand focus back.\n    const previous = document.activeElement instanceof HTMLElement ? document.activeElement : null;\n    const area = document.createElement(\"textarea\");\n    area.value = text;\n    area.setAttribute(\"readonly\", \"\");\n    area.style.position = \"fixed\";\n    area.style.opacity = \"0\";\n    document.body.appendChild(area);\n    area.select();\n    let ok = false;\n    try {\n      ok = document.execCommand(\"copy\");\n    } catch {\n      ok = false;\n    }\n    area.remove();\n    previous?.focus({ preventScroll: true });\n    return ok;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction useCopy(text: string, onCopy?: (text: string) => void) {\n  const [state, setState] = useState<CopyState>(\"idle\");\n  const timer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\n  const alive = useRef(true);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    alive.current = true;\n    return () => {\n      alive.current = false;\n      if (timer.current !== null) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  const copy = async () => {\n    const ok = await writeClipboard(text);\n    // The promise can settle after the message unmounts.\n    if (!alive.current) return;\n    if (ok) onCopy?.(text);\n    setState(ok ? \"copied\" : \"failed\");\n    if (timer.current !== null) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    timer.current = setTimeout(() => setState(\"idle\"), COPY_RESET_MS);\n  };\n\n  return [state, copy] as const;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The copy reset timer is cleared when copy runs again and on unmount. A mounted ref stops a late clipboard promise from setting state, and the fallback textarea is removed straight after copying.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The action row fade and the button colour and press-scale transitions use `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and the press scale is held at 100%. The row still appears on hover and focus, just without fading. Tooltips follow the kit's own reduced-motion rule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Hiding the actions with `hidden` or `display: none` until hover. That removes them from the tab order, so keyboard users can never reach them. Opacity plus `group-focus-within` keeps them focusable.\n- Giving like and dislike their own `isSelected` state. Both can end up pressed. Derive both from one value.\n- Splitting on backticks with a non-capturing regex. The code spans are dropped from the result. With a capturing group they come back at odd indices.\n- Setting state when the clipboard promise settles after the row has unmounted. The hook checks a mounted ref first and clears its timer on unmount.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `text` is a plain string, not Markdown. Only blank-line paragraphs and single-backtick code are handled; fenced blocks, links and lists are not.\n- In the dark theme the primary and tertiary backgrounds are the same grey, so the user bubble, the code chips in assistant bubbles and the user avatar step up to the quaternary background with `dark:`.\n- A user avatar uses the kit `Avatar` with initials from `name` (\"Ada Park\" becomes AP). Pass `avatar` to replace either avatar.\n- The demo is a four-message thread. Regenerate cycles each reply through canned alternatives and clears its rating, both replies use controlled `feedback`, and a status line at the bottom reports each copy, rating and regenerate.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover or Tab onto a message and its actions fade in under the bubble. Copy puts the exact text on the clipboard and shows a check and \"Copied\" for about 1.6s. Like and dislike are exclusive and pressing one again clears it. Regenerate appears only on assistant rows. Backtick spans show as inline code.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "chat-starter": "# Build: Chat Starter\n\nA full chat page: the kit dashboard sidebar on the left and a rounded chat card holding the first screen of a conversation. With no messages the card centres a greeting, a pill composer and a row of suggestion chips; a chip fills the composer instead of sending, so the visitor can edit first. Set `apiKeyMissing` and the card shows a setup checklist for adding a provider key, with a button that skips straight to the demo. Sending posts the message and answers with a scripted reply after a short thinking state, so the screen works with no backend.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nEmpty AI chat screen with a greeting, suggestion chips that fill the pill composer, and an \"Add an API key to start\" setup card.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. State is local: `draft` (the composer text), `messages` (an array of `{ id, role, text }` with ids from a counter ref, never `Math.random`), `thinking`, the chosen `model`, and `skipped`. The setup card renders while `apiKeyMissing && !skipped`; \"Skip, show me the demo\" sets `skipped` and calls `onSkip`.\n2. The composer zone is one element in both layouts. With no messages it gets `my-auto` inside a flex column, which centres the greeting, composer and chips vertically. Once a message exists, a scrolling list is inserted before it as a sibling, so the composer drops to the bottom without remounting and keeps focus.\n3. A suggestion chip calls `setDraft(prompt ?? label)`, focuses the textarea and raises a `caretToEnd` ref. The layout effect that sizes the field runs after the new value is in the DOM, so it is the place that parks the caret at the end and clears the flag.\n4. The textarea auto-grows: the layout effect sets `height: auto`, reads `scrollHeight` and writes it back capped at 6 lines × 20px + 16px of vertical padding. Past the cap the field scrolls.\n5. Enter sends and Shift+Enter inserts a newline. The keydown handler skips Enter while `nativeEvent.isComposing` is true, so confirming an IME candidate never sends a half-typed message. The send button is a real `type=\"submit\"` in a `<form>`, disabled while the trimmed draft is empty or a reply is pending.\n6. Send appends the user message, clears the draft and starts one timeout (`replyDelay`, 900ms). When it fires it appends the reply of the suggestion whose prompt matches the sent text exactly, else `fallbackReply`. The timer id lives in a ref and is cleared on reset, before a new send, and on unmount.\n7. The model chip is the kit `Dropdown`, controlled through `isOpen` so picking a row can close it. The selected row shows a check. The header \"Start a new chat\" button clears messages, draft and any pending reply.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `composer radius = 26px`\n  Half of the 52px single-line height (36px controls + 8px padding top and bottom), so a one-line composer is a true pill. When the field grows to several lines the same radius reads as a soft card instead of a stretched capsule.\n- `MAX_LINES = 6`\n  Enough to see a pasted paragraph without the composer covering the conversation on an 800px-tall screen. Past six lines the field scrolls inside itself.\n- `replyDelay = 900ms`\n  Long enough for the thinking indicator to register as a state, not a flicker. Much longer and the scripted demo feels broken.\n- `content column = max-w-[720px]`\n  Keeps line length near 90 characters at 14px, which stays readable on a wide screen while the card itself stretches.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ChatStarter(props: { apiKeyMissing?: boolean; onSkip?: () => void; greeting?: string; subtitle?: string; suggestions?: ChatStarterSuggestion[]; placeholder?: string; models?: string[]; onSubmit?: (text: string) => void; fallbackReply?: string; replyDelay?: number; navItems?: DashboardNavItem[] | null; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface ChatStarterSuggestion { label: string; prompt?: string; icon?: RemixiconComponentType; reply?: string }\n```\n\n- `apiKeyMissing: boolean` — default `false`\n  Show the \"Add an API key to start\" setup card instead of the greeting and composer, until the visitor skips it.\n- `onSkip: () => void`\n  Called when \"Skip, show me the demo\" is pressed. The card hides either way.\n- `greeting: string` — default `\"What can I help with?\"`\n  Heading of the empty state.\n- `subtitle: string` — default `\"Ask anything, or start from one of these.\"`\n  Line under the greeting. Pass an empty string to hide it.\n- `suggestions: ChatStarterSuggestion[]` — default `[]`\n  Chips under the composer. A click fills the composer with `prompt ?? label`; `reply` is the scripted answer for that exact prompt.\n- `placeholder: string` — default `\"Ask me anything\"`\n  Composer placeholder.\n- `models: string[]` — default `[\"Swift 2\", \"Swift 2 Mini\", \"Deep 1\"]`\n  Names listed in the model chip. The first is selected.\n- `onSubmit: (text: string) => void`\n  Called with the trimmed text on every send.\n- `fallbackReply: string`\n  Scripted answer for prompts that match no suggestion.\n- `replyDelay: number` — default `900`\n  Milliseconds of thinking state before the scripted answer lands.\n- `navItems: DashboardNavItem[] | null` — default `CHAT_STARTER_NAV`\n  Sidebar rows (Chat, Dashboard, Components and Blocks, Sign in, Sign up). `null` hides the sidebar.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst fillFromSuggestion = (s: ChatStarterSuggestion) => {\n  const text = s.prompt ?? s.label;\n  setDraft(text);\n  // Focus now; the layout effect parks the caret at the end once the value lands.\n  caretToEnd.current = true;\n  inputRef.current?.focus();\n};\n\nconst send = () => {\n  const text = draft.trim();\n  if (!text || thinking) return;\n  onSubmit?.(text);\n  const match = suggestions.find((s) => (s.prompt ?? s.label) === text);\n  setMessages((m) => [...m, { id: nextId.current++, role: \"user\", text }]);\n  setDraft(\"\");\n  setThinking(true);\n  if (timerRef.current) clearTimeout(timerRef.current);\n  timerRef.current = setTimeout(() => {\n    timerRef.current = null;\n    setThinking(false);\n    setMessages((m) => [\n      ...m,\n      { id: nextId.current++, role: \"assistant\", text: match?.reply ?? fallbackReply },\n    ]);\n  }, replyDelay);\n};\n\nconst onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLTextAreaElement>) => {\n  // Enter sends, Shift+Enter breaks the line; never send mid IME composition.\n  if (e.key === \"Enter\" && !e.shiftKey && !e.nativeEvent.isComposing) {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    send();\n  }\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** One `setTimeout` for the scripted reply, held in a ref and cleared on unmount, on \"Start a new chat\" and before every new send. The model dropdown and the sidebar clean up after themselves.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Composer, chip and button transitions carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, the send button no longer scales on press, and new replies appear without the fade-in because that animation is behind `motion-safe:`. The kit thinking indicator falls back to its static frame. The scripted delay stays, since it is a state, not motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sending on a suggestion click. The chip only fills the composer. Prompts usually need a word changed, and sending on click also makes it impossible to read the chips before committing.\n- Moving the caret with `setSelectionRange` straight after `setDraft`. The DOM still holds the old value at that point, so the caret lands at the old length. Do it in the layout effect after the value commits.\n- Sending on Enter during IME composition. Japanese, Chinese and Korean input confirm candidates with Enter, so check `nativeEvent.isComposing` first.\n- Rendering the composer twice, once centred and once docked. The swap remounts the textarea and drops focus and the caret on the first send. Keep one element and change the layout around it.\n- Leaving the reply timeout running on unmount or reset. A late `setMessages` writes an answer into a fresh chat.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The setup copy names Vercel and `AI_API_KEY` because that is the deploy path of the starter. Change the card copy to match your own host and variable name.\n- Pass `navItems={null}` to drop the sidebar and use the chat card alone inside another layout. The sidebar is also hidden below the `lg` breakpoint.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith `apiKeyMissing` the setup card shows; \"Skip, show me the demo\" reveals the greeting, composer and chips. Clicking a chip fills the composer with its prompt without sending. Enter sends, Shift+Enter adds a line, and the send button is disabled while the field is empty. A sent message appears on the right, \"Thinking\" shows, then a reply appears on the left.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "checkbox": "# Build: Checkbox\n\nReact Aria's `Checkbox` wrapped around a shared `CheckboxGlyph`: a 16px (or 14px) box with a 4px radius that fills with the accent gradient when checked, while a white tick draws itself on over 200ms. The indeterminate state swaps the tick for a flat dash, which is what a \"select all\" parent shows when only some of its children are on. The glyph lives in its own file so Checkbox Card renders the identical box.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact Aria checkbox in two sizes: a gradient box whose white tick draws itself on, plus an indeterminate dash for partial selections.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Checkbox` spreads every React Aria prop onto `AriaCheckbox`, which renders a `<label>` around the native input (hence `ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement>`). Its children are a render function: the state object (`isSelected`, `isIndeterminate`, `isHovered`, `isFocusVisible`, `isDisabled`) goes straight into `<CheckboxGlyph state={state} size={size} />`, and `children`, when not null, becomes a label span in the size's type style.\n2. The glyph derives `isMarked = isSelected || isIndeterminate`. Marked boxes paint `bg-linear-to-b from-accent-500 to-accent-600` plus `shadow-checkbox-selected` (the inner highlight), lightening to `from-accent-400 to-accent-500` on hover. Unmarked boxes are a 1px `border-border-checkbox-default` on `bg-background-primary-default` with `shadow-xs`, and the border darkens to `border-border-checkbox-hover` on hover. Hover only counts when the box is not disabled.\n3. The box transitions `background-color`, `border-color` and `box-shadow` over 150ms. The gradient itself is a `background-image`, which does not interpolate, so the fill switches at once and the tick carries the motion.\n4. Inside a 16×16 `viewBox`, indeterminate wins over selected: it renders the dash `M4.5 8H8H11.5`. A selected box renders the tick path with `pathLength={1}` and `animate-check-draw`. The theme gives that class `stroke-dasharray: 1; stroke-dashoffset: 1` and animates the offset to 0 over 200ms `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)` with `forwards`. Because `pathLength` is 1, the same numbers mean \"the whole stroke\" at either size.\n5. An unchecked box renders no path at all. So every check mounts a fresh path and replays the draw, and unchecking removes the tick immediately with no reverse animation.\n6. Sizes come from `checkboxSizes`: md is a `size-4` box, `text-body-medium` label and `gap-2`; sm is a `size-3.5` box, `text-body-2-medium` label and `gap-1.5`. Focus draws `ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-2` only while `isFocusVisible` is true, so it shows for keyboard focus and not for a mouse click. Disabled dims the glyph to `opacity-50` and sets `cursor-not-allowed` on the label. `className` accepts a string or React Aria's state function and is merged last through `cx`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `box size = md 16px (size-4), sm 14px (size-3.5), radius 4px (rounded-sm)`\n  Figma's two sizes. Each is paired with its own label style (14px Body 1 for md, 13px Body 2 for sm), so box and text centre on one line.\n- `tick draw = 200ms cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1), forwards`\n  A symmetric ease-in-out, so the stroke starts and lands softly like a pen mark. It is short enough to finish before the pointer has moved on. `forwards` holds the final frame, otherwise the tick would vanish when the animation ends.\n- `pathLength = 1`\n  Normalises the dash arithmetic. A dasharray and offset of 1 mean \"the whole path\" whatever the rendered size, so one keyframe serves both sizes.\n- `stroke = 2px white, round caps and joins`\n  The Figma glyph. Round caps keep a 2px stroke from looking clipped inside a 14px box.\n- `colour transition = 150ms ease on background-color, border-color, box-shadow`\n  The hover border change and the focus ring arrive quickly without looking abrupt.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Checkbox(props: Omit<AriaCheckboxProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction CheckboxGlyph(props: { state: CheckboxGlyphState; size?: CheckboxSize }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  16px box with a 14px label, or 14px box with a 13px label.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The label. Omit it for a bare box, and pass `aria-label` instead.\n- `isSelected / defaultSelected: boolean`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled checked state (React Aria).\n- `onChange: (isSelected: boolean) => void`\n  Called with the new checked state.\n- `isIndeterminate: boolean`\n  Shows the dash instead of the tick. Presentational; the parent clears it.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Dims the glyph to 50% and blocks interaction.\n- `className: string | ((state) => string)`\n  Merged onto the label element after the defaults.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Checkbox({ className, children, size = \"md\", ref, ...props }: CheckboxProps) {\n  const s = checkboxSizes[size];\n\n  return (\n    <AriaCheckbox\n      ref={ref}\n      {...props}\n      className={(state) =>\n        cx(\n          \"group inline-flex items-center select-none\",\n          s.gap,\n          state.isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed\" : \"cursor-pointer\",\n          typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {(state) => (\n        <>\n          <CheckboxGlyph state={state} size={size} />\n          {children !== undefined && children !== null && (\n            <span className={cx(s.label, \"text-text-primary\")}>{children}</span>\n          )}\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaCheckbox>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release. There are no effects, listeners or timers, and the tick animation belongs to the SVG path, which unmounts with the component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The theme's `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` rule sets `animation: none` and `stroke-dashoffset: 0` on `.animate-check-draw`, so the tick appears whole at once. The 150ms colour and border transition still runs. It changes colour only, nothing moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Rendering the tick path all the time and toggling its opacity. The draw is a CSS animation that runs when the path mounts, so it would play once on page load and never again. The glyph returns `null` for an unchecked box for this reason.\n- Expecting `isIndeterminate` to clear itself. It is presentational: React Aria keeps showing the dash until the parent stops passing it. A \"select all\" parent has to compute it from its children on every render (some but not all selected).\n- Wrapping `Checkbox` in another `<label>`, or putting a link or button in `children`. `AriaCheckbox` already renders the label, so a nested interactive element toggles the box as well as doing its own job.\n- Rendering a bare box (no `children`) without `aria-label`. The box then has no accessible name, and a screen reader announces only \"checkbox\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- `CheckboxGlyph` takes a plain state object (five booleans), so any component that can supply them can draw the same box. Checkbox Card is the in-kit example.\n- Disabled dims only the glyph. The label text keeps full colour, as the Figma states do; pass a `className` if a form needs the whole row dimmed.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking the label or pressing Space toggles the box. When it is checked, the white tick draws on from left to right over about 200ms on a blue gradient, and an indeterminate box shows a flat white dash instead. With reduced motion the tick appears at once. Tab focus shows a 2px ring offset from the box, and a mouse click does not.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "checkbox-card": "# Build: Checkbox Card\n\nA larger hit target for an on/off choice: a 10px-radius bordered card with a medium-weight title and an optional secondary description, and the same checkbox glyph the plain Checkbox uses, pinned to the trailing edge. The card is the React Aria `Checkbox` label, so clicking the text, the padding or the box all toggle it, and the tick draws itself on exactly as it does in the small control.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBordered option card with title and description on the left and the kit checkbox on the right; a click anywhere on it toggles.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `CheckboxCard` renders `AriaCheckbox` directly. React Aria makes that a `<label>` wrapping a visually hidden native `<input type=\"checkbox\">`, so the whole card is the click target, Space toggles it when focused, and it takes part in forms and `CheckboxGroup`s like any checkbox. Every Aria prop (`isSelected`, `defaultSelected`, `onChange`, `isDisabled`, `isIndeterminate`, `name`, `value`) passes through.\n2. The card class comes from the render-state function: `flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-2lg border border-border-button-default py-3 pe-5 ps-4 select-none`, with `transition-colors duration-150`. When hovered and not disabled the background is `bg-background-primary-hover`, otherwise `bg-background-primary-default`. Disabled adds `cursor-not-allowed opacity-50`.\n3. The children render function receives the same state object and lays out a text column (`flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5`) with the title in `text-body-medium text-text-primary` and, when `description` is not null or undefined, a second line in `text-body-regular text-text-secondary`. Both lines `truncate`, so long copy never pushes the box off the card.\n4. On the trailing side, `<CheckboxGlyph state={state} />` (wrapped in `shrink-0 py-1`) draws the 16px box: accent gradient and a white tick that draws on over 200ms when selected, a dash when indeterminate, and a focus ring only while `isFocusVisible`. Sharing the glyph is what keeps the card and the plain Checkbox identical.\n5. Physical padding is logical (`ps-4 pe-5`), so in RTL the box moves to the left edge and the extra 4px of padding stays on the box side.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `padding = ps-4 pe-5 py-3 (16 / 20 / 12px)`\n  Taken from the Figma checkbox item (node 3793:2966). The trailing side is 4px wider so the 16px box sits optically centred in its corner rather than tight against the border.\n- `radius = rounded-2lg (10px)`\n  The kit's card-level control radius, the same as the gray pill tab and other selectable surfaces, so a stack of option cards matches the inputs around it.\n- `hover transition = 150ms transition-colors`\n  Only the background changes on hover. 150ms is the kit-wide timing for surface colour changes.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CheckboxCard(props: Omit<AriaCheckboxProps, \"children\"> & { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Main label in body medium. Also the accessible name of the checkbox.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Optional secondary line in body regular, truncated to one line.\n- `isSelected / defaultSelected: boolean`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled checked state, from React Aria.\n- `onChange: (isSelected: boolean) => void`\n  Called when the card is toggled.\n- `isIndeterminate: boolean`\n  Shows the dash instead of the tick.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Dims the card to 50%, sets a not-allowed cursor and ignores input.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CheckboxCard({\n  className,\n  title,\n  description,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: CheckboxCardProps) {\n  return (\n    <AriaCheckbox\n      ref={ref}\n      {...props}\n      className={(state) =>\n        cx(\n          \"group flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-2lg border border-border-button-default py-3 pe-5 ps-4 select-none\",\n          \"transition-colors duration-150 ease\",\n          state.isHovered && !state.isDisabled\n            ? \"bg-background-primary-hover\"\n            : \"bg-background-primary-default\",\n          state.isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\" : \"cursor-pointer\",\n          typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {(state) => (\n        <>\n          <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n            <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</span>\n            {description !== undefined && description !== null && (\n              <span className=\"truncate text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{description}</span>\n            )}\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center py-1\">\n            <CheckboxGlyph state={state} />\n          </span>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaCheckbox>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The card holds no state, listeners or timers of its own; React Aria owns the checkbox state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The card itself only fades its background over 150ms. The tick draw animation comes from the shared checkbox glyph, which plays the theme's `animate-check-draw`. The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion` itself, so remove that animation globally if the tick should appear instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not put a link or a button inside `title` or `description`. The card is a `<label>`, so any click inside it toggles the checkbox, and an interactive child would both fire and toggle.\n- Do not wrap the card in your own `<label>` or attach an extra `onClick`. React Aria already makes the whole card the label of the hidden input.\n- Do not remove `truncate` from the text spans. Without it a long description wraps unpredictably and pushes the checkbox glyph down off the centre line.\n- Do not signal selection with the card border. In this kit the card keeps its neutral border and only the checkbox shows the state.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Put several cards in a React Aria `CheckboxGroup` with `value` props to get a multi-select option list with one shared value array.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThree stacked cards show a title, a description and a box on the right. Clicking anywhere on a card, including its text, checks it and the white tick draws on. Clicking again clears it. Hovering a card tints its background. Tab focuses each card in turn with a focus ring on the box, and Space toggles it. A disabled card is at half opacity and ignores clicks.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "chip": "# Build: Chip\n\nA single `<span>` label with a tinted background and a matching text colour, used for statuses (\"Completed\", \"Waiting\"), percentage deltas, prices and role tags. Three variants change only the vertical padding and type style, and ten colours map to semantic background/text pairs, so a chip follows light and dark mode without any `dark:` classes. The colour map is exported as `CHIP_COLORS` and reused by Tag Group.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nColoured status, delta and role chips in three emphasis levels and ten theme-aware colour pairs on a 6px radius.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Export `CHIP_COLORS`, a `Record<ChipColor, string>` of background + text pairs. The seven hues use `bg-status-<hue>-background text-status-<hue>-text`. `neutral`, `gray` and `soft` reuse surface tokens (`bg-background-tertiary-default` / `bg-background-secondary-default` with `text-text-secondary` or `text-text-primary`).\n2. Build the style map with `sortCx`: `base` is `inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md px-1.5 whitespace-nowrap`, and `variant` sets `py-0.5 text-body-medium` (bold), `py-1 text-body-medium` (subtle) or `py-1 text-caption-1-medium` (caption).\n3. Define the status tokens per theme. Light pairs the 200 step background with 800 text (purple: 100 with 600). Dark mixes the 950 step at 60% with `transparent` behind 500/400/300 text (purple: 900 at 50% behind 300), so chips glow softly on dark cards instead of sitting as bright blocks.\n4. Render one `<span>` whose classes are `cx(base, variant, color, className)`. `cx` is the kit's `tailwind-merge`, so a caller `px-2` or `rounded-full` replaces the default instead of fighting it.\n5. Add `transition-[padding,font-size] duration-200` to the base, so a chip that changes variant in place (for example when a table switches density) eases its size instead of jumping.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `horizontal padding / radius = px-1.5 (6px), rounded-md (6px)`\n  Shared by every variant so chips of different emphasis line up on their left edges in a column and keep the same corner.\n- `variant padding = bold py-0.5 (2px), subtle py-1 (4px), caption py-1 (4px)`\n  Bold is the dense status chip that fits a 32px table row (20px line + 4px). Subtle is the roomier price chip. Caption pairs 4px padding with the 12px type style for role tags.\n- `type = text-body-medium (14/20, 500) or text-caption-1-medium (12/16, 500)`\n  Medium weight keeps the coloured text readable on a tinted fill; the caption tier exists for secondary tags that must not compete with the row's main label.\n- `dark status fill = color-mix(in srgb, <hue>-950 60%, transparent)`\n  A translucent fill takes on the colour of whatever card it sits on, so the same chip works on primary and secondary dark surfaces.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype ChipColor = \"orange\" | \"lime\" | \"rose\" | \"yellow\" | \"cyan\" | \"blue\" | \"purple\" | \"neutral\" | \"gray\" | \"soft\"\nconst CHIP_COLORS: Record<ChipColor, string>\ninterface ChipProps extends HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement> { variant?: \"bold\" | \"subtle\" | \"caption\"; color?: ChipColor; ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement> }\nfunction Chip(props: ChipProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `variant: \"bold\" | \"subtle\" | \"caption\"` — default `\"bold\"`\n  Vertical padding and type style: 2px + 14px, 4px + 14px, or 4px + 12px.\n- `color: ChipColor` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  One of ten background/text pairs from `CHIP_COLORS`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged last through `tailwind-merge`.\n- `ref: Ref<HTMLSpanElement>`\n  Forwarded to the span (React 19 ref-as-prop).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Background and text pair per colour, shared with Tag Group's tags. */\nexport const CHIP_COLORS: Record<ChipColor, string> = {\n  orange: \"bg-status-orange-background text-status-orange-text\",\n  lime: \"bg-status-lime-background text-status-lime-text\",\n  rose: \"bg-status-rose-background text-status-rose-text\",\n  yellow: \"bg-status-yellow-background text-status-yellow-text\",\n  cyan: \"bg-status-cyan-background text-status-cyan-text\",\n  blue: \"bg-status-blue-background text-status-blue-text\",\n  purple: \"bg-status-purple-background text-status-purple-text\",\n  neutral: \"bg-background-tertiary-default text-text-secondary\",\n  gray: \"bg-background-secondary-default text-text-primary\",\n  soft: \"bg-background-secondary-default text-text-secondary\",\n};\n\nexport interface ChipProps extends HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement> {\n  variant?: ChipVariant;\n  color?: ChipColor;\n  ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement>;\n}\n\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  base: \"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md px-1.5 whitespace-nowrap transition-[padding,font-size] duration-200 ease\",\n  variant: {\n    bold: \"py-0.5 text-body-medium\",\n    subtle: \"py-1 text-body-medium\",\n    caption: \"py-1 text-caption-1-medium\",\n  },\n  color: CHIP_COLORS,\n});\n\nexport function Chip({\n  variant = \"bold\",\n  color = \"neutral\",\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: ChipProps) {\n  return (\n    <span\n      ref={ref}\n      className={cx(styles.base, styles.variant[variant], styles.color[color], className)}\n      {...props}\n    />\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is a 200ms padding/font-size transition when a chip changes variant in place. It is not disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion`, since it is a size change, not movement across the screen.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not hard-code palette classes such as `bg-lime-200 text-lime-800` on a chip. The semantic `status-*` tokens are what switch to the translucent dark-mode pair.\n- Do not remove `whitespace-nowrap`. A two-word status such as \"In review\" wraps inside a narrow table cell and doubles the row height.\n- Do not encode meaning in colour alone. A rose chip still needs its \"Failed\" or \"-3.2%\" text.\n- Do not add a new colour only to `ChipColor`. It has to exist in `CHIP_COLORS` and as a token in both themes, or the chip renders with no fill.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `CHIP_COLORS` is imported by `tag-group`, so its tags share the exact chip palette.\n- The default is `variant=\"bold\"` and `color=\"neutral\"`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTwelve chips across all three variants and the ten colours wrap into a ragged cloud. Bold chips are 24px tall, subtle 28px and caption 24px, all with 6px side padding and 6px corners. In light mode the hue chips show a pale fill with dark text; in dark mode the same chips show a translucent dark fill with lighter text. Text never wraps inside a chip.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "clip-path-variable-tween": "# Build: CSS Variable Clip-Path Tween\n\nA panel reveal that tweens a CSS custom property instead of a CSS property. The stage clips itself with `inset(var(--kx-clip))`, and because custom properties inherit, any descendant carrying `.kx-clip-follow` clips with the same value in its own box — one tween, several clip boxes, no possible drift. The media pushes from 1.4 to 1 over half again the clip duration and the lines slide up on a stagger; the exit is the same timeline reversed, which turns the positive stagger into a last-line-first peel and the scale-down back into a push.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne tweened custom property drives every clip-path on the panel, and reversing the timeline is the exit.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. GSAP's CSSPlugin special-cases any property whose name starts with `--`: it reads the start value with `getComputedStyle(target).getPropertyValue(prop)` and writes with `style.setProperty(prop, value)`. Because neither end is a plain number, the tween routes through the complex-string interpolator, which walks both strings and interpolates every number it finds pairwise — `100% 0% 0% 0%` to `0% 0% 0% 0%` is four independent number tweens in one string.\n2. The tweened value feeds `clip-path: inset(var(--kx-clip))` on the stage. `inset()` takes `top right bottom left`, so a top inset of 100% collapses the box to nothing and 0% leaves it whole: the reveal is the top edge travelling down.\n3. The reason to tween a variable rather than `clipPath` itself is inheritance. `.kx-clip-follow` on any descendant re-uses the same inherited value against its own border box, so a caption reveals in proportion with the panel without a second tween. Tweening `clipPath` on each element instead means N tweens that agree only until someone edits one.\n4. The initial `--kx-clip` is declared in the injected stylesheet, not in React's inline style. GSAP writes the tweened value inline, and a React re-render owning that same inline property would reset it mid-tween. It also has to be declared somewhere: an undeclared custom property computes to the empty string, and a tween with no readable start value snaps to its end.\n5. One paused timeline is built and then `play()`ed or `reverse()`d. Reversing is not a shortcut — it is the exit the source had: a reversed positive stagger runs last-element-first, and a reversed `scale: 1.4 -> 1` runs `1 -> 1.4`, so the panel leaves with its lines peeling off in reverse while the media is still pushing in.\n6. The timeline is built inside `gsap.context()` with `open` deliberately absent from its dependencies. Rebuilding per state change would call `context.revert()`, which strips the inline value GSAP wrote — the closing tween would then read the stylesheet default, fully shut, as its start and animate from nowhere to nowhere.\n7. Media and line timings are ratios of the clip duration (1.5x, 0.6x, offset 0.35x) rather than independent numbers, so retuning the reveal cannot desynchronise the three groups.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 1.2`\n  Seconds for the clip. The source ran 2 s, but that timeline was scrubbed against scroll where duration is only a proportion of the scroll range. Played back at real time, 2 s is a panel you wait for; below ~0.8 s the `power3.inOut` has no room for its slow start and the edge appears to jump.\n- `mediaScale = 1.4`\n  Start scale of the media. Big enough that the settle is legible for the full duration, small enough that a 1x asset is not visibly softened. Also the reason the media needs an `overflow: hidden` wrapper: at 1.4 it is 40% wider than its box.\n- `lineStagger = 0.125`\n  Seconds between lines. Exactly the source value. At 0 the block arrives as one slab; past ~0.2 s the last line is still climbing after the clip has finished and the reveal appears to have two endings.\n- `MEDIA_DURATION_RATIO = 1.5`\n  The push outlives the clip by half again. The clip is the event; the scale still resolving after it is what stops the panel feeling like it stopped dead on arrival.\n- `LINE_OFFSET_RATIO = 0.35`\n  Fraction of the clip before the lines start. Any earlier and they climb inside a box that is still mostly clipped, so the first thing the eye catches is text being cut in half.\n- `CLIP_SHUT = '100% 0% 0% 0%'`\n  Four percentages, matching the four in `CLIP_OPEN`. The complex-string interpolator pairs numbers positionally, so `inset(100%)` against `inset(0% 0% 0% 0%)` would interpolate one number and leave the other three as literal text.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ClipPathVariableTween(props: { open: boolean; children: ReactNode; duration?: number; mediaScale?: number; lineStagger?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `open: boolean`\n  `true` plays the reveal, `false` reverses it. Every change re-drives the same timeline.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content. `[data-clip-media]` is pushed from `mediaScale` to 1; `[data-clip-line]` slides up from 120%.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.2`\n  Seconds for the clip. Media and line timings are ratios of it.\n- `mediaScale: number` — default `1.4`\n  Scale the media starts at. 1 disables the push.\n- `lineStagger: number` — default `0.125`\n  Seconds between lines. Reversed on exit, so the last line leaves first.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the stage, which is the element that carries the clip.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** `inset(top right bottom left)`. Top at 100% collapses the box entirely. */\nconst CLIP_SHUT = '100% 0% 0% 0%';\nconst CLIP_OPEN = '0% 0% 0% 0%';\n\n/**\n * Media scale outlives the clip by half again. The clip is the event; the push\n * continuing past it is what stops the panel feeling like it stopped dead.\n */\nconst MEDIA_DURATION_RATIO = 1.5;\nconst LINE_DURATION_RATIO = 0.6;\n/** Lines start when the clip is a third open — before that they are hidden. */\nconst LINE_OFFSET_RATIO = 0.35;\n\nconst CLIP_CSS = `\n.kx-clip-stage {\n  /* Declared in CSS, never in React's inline style: GSAP writes the tweened\n     value to the inline style, and a React re-render would reset it mid-tween.\n     It also has to be declared SOMEWHERE — an undeclared custom property\n     computes to the empty string, and a tween with no start value snaps. */\n  --kx-clip: ${CLIP_SHUT};\n  clip-path: inset(var(--kx-clip));\n}\n/* Any descendant clipped by the same inherited variable. */\n.kx-clip-follow {\n  clip-path: inset(var(--kx-clip));\n}\n`;\n\nexport interface ClipPathVariableTweenProps {\n  /** `true` reveals, `false` clips away. Every change plays or reverses. */\n  open: boolean;\n  /**\n   * Content. Two opt-in hooks:\n   * `[data-clip-media]` is pushed from `mediaScale` to 1, and\n   * `[data-clip-line]` slides up from 120% with a stagger.\n   */\n  children: ReactNode;\n  /** Seconds for the clip itself. Media and lines are derived ratios of it. */\n  duration?: number;\n  /** Scale the media starts at. 1 disables the push. */\n  mediaScale?: number;\n  /** Seconds between lines. Reversed on exit, so the last line leaves first. */\n  lineStagger?: number;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function ClipPathVariableTween({\n  open,\n  children,\n  duration = 1.2,\n  mediaScale = 1.4,\n  lineStagger = 0.125,\n  className,\n}: ClipPathVariableTweenProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const timeline = useRef<ReturnType<typeof gsap.timeline> | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Built once and held paused. Rebuilding it per `open` change would be\n  // wrong twice over: `gsap.context().revert()` strips the inline value GSAP\n  // wrote, so a closing tween would read the CSS default — fully shut — as its\n  // start and animate from nowhere to nowhere.\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      if (!el) return;\n\n      const media = el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-clip-media]');\n      const lines = el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-clip-line]');\n\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });\n\n      tl.fromTo(\n        el,\n        { '--kx-clip': CLIP_SHUT },\n        { '--kx-clip': CLIP_OPEN, duration, ease: 'power3.inOut' },\n        0,\n      );\n\n      if (media.length > 0) {\n        tl.fromTo(\n          media,\n          { scale: mediaScale },\n          {\n            scale: 1,\n            duration: duration * MEDIA_DURATION_RATIO,\n            ease: 'power2.out',\n          },\n          0,\n        );\n      }\n\n      if (lines.length > 0) {\n        tl.fromTo(\n          lines,\n          { yPercent: 120 },\n          {\n            yPercent: 0,\n            duration: duration * LINE_DURATION_RATIO,\n            ease: 'power3.out',\n            stagger: lineStagger,\n          },\n          duration * LINE_OFFSET_RATIO,\n        );\n      }\n\n      timeline.current = tl;\n      return () => {\n        timeline.current = null;\n      };\n    },\n    root,\n    [duration, mediaScale, lineStagger],\n  );\n\n  // Same dependency list plus `open`, so this always runs after the effect\n  // above has rebuilt the timeline rather than driving a stale one.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const tl = timeline.current;\n    if (!tl) return;\n\n    if (reduced) {\n      // A jump, not a zero-duration tween: the timeline is seeked to the\n      // correct end state and stays paused there. Clip, scale and line offsets\n      // all land correctly because they are all on this one timeline.\n      tl.progress(open ? 1 : 0).pause();\n      return;\n    }\n\n    if (open) tl.play();\n    else tl.reverse();\n  }, [open, reduced, duration, mediaScale, lineStagger]);\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={root} className={cn('kx-clip-stage', className)}>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-clip-path-variable-tween\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {CLIP_CSS}\n      </style>\n      {children}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` runs everything inside `gsap.context()` and calls `revert()` on unmount, which kills the timeline and removes the inline `--kx-clip`, `transform` and `scale` GSAP wrote so React never inherits a stray style. The context callback also nulls the timeline ref, so the effect that plays it cannot touch a reverted timeline.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The timeline is seeked with `tl.progress(open ? 1 : 0).pause()` — a jump to the correct end state, not a zero-duration tween. Because the clip, the media scale and every line offset all live on that one timeline, one seek lands all of them correctly, which is the practical argument for putting them there in the first place.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing the custom property through React's inline `style` as well. React owns that attribute and GSAP writes to it; the first re-render during a tween resets the value and the clip snaps back.\n- Never declaring the custom property. An undeclared custom property computes to the empty string, so GSAP has nothing to parse as a start value and the tween jumps straight to its end on the first frame.\n- Mismatching the number count between the two clip strings. The interpolator pairs numbers positionally, so `inset(100%)` to `inset(0% 0% 0% 0%)` animates the first number and carries the rest through as text.\n- Rebuilding the timeline whenever the open state changes. `gsap.context().revert()` removes the inline value GSAP wrote, so the reverse tween reads the stylesheet default as its start and animates from the closed state to the closed state — the exit silently does nothing.\n- Tweening `clipPath` on each element instead of one shared variable. It works on the day you write it and drifts the first time one of the durations is tuned, because nothing structural keeps them equal.\n- Forgetting `overflow: hidden` on the media wrapper and on each line. A 1.4x scale overflows its box by 40%, and a line entering from `yPercent: 120` is visible below its own box the entire way up.\n- Assuming a clipped-away panel is inert. `clip-path` does clip hit-testing, but anything positioned outside the clip region — a fixed-position child, a focusable control — can still be reached by the keyboard, so the panel also needs its interactive state driven from the same flag.\n- Tweening `--kx-clip` with a scrubbed ScrollTrigger and expecting sub-pixel smoothness. Custom property writes invalidate style for the whole inheritance subtree, so a stage with hundreds of descendants pays for it on every frame. The technique earns its keep with a handful of followers, not hundreds.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The `open` flag is the API surface, so a faked route change, a real router, an `IntersectionObserver` or a click all drive it identically. Nothing here imports a router.\n- Registering the property with `@property { syntax: \"<percentage>+\" }` is not needed: GSAP interpolates the string itself and never asks the browser to. Register it only if you also want CSS transitions or keyframes to animate the same variable.\n- `inset()` is the only basic shape whose numbers interpolate this cleanly. `polygon()` works the same way as long as both sides have the same vertex count, which is the same constraint stated differently.\n- For a full-page version, put the stage on the layout wrapper and the follower class on the header rule and the footer — one tween, three clip boxes, and the whole chrome resolves together.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick an index row: the panel's top edge travels down over 1.2 s, the media resolves from a 1.4x push, the three lines climb into place 0.125 s apart, and the meta rule at the bottom clips itself in step without a tween of its own. Click Close: the panel clips back up, the last line leaves first, and the media pushes in again — the index underneath is revealed, not covered by a second animation. With reduced motion the panel is simply present or absent with the media at 1x and the lines in place.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "close-button": "# Build: Close Button\n\nThe small round \"X\" that dismisses announcements, toasts, modals and drawers. It ships its own glyph instead of taking an icon: each size draws its X as two round-capped lines in an SVG whose viewBox equals its pixel size, so `strokeWidth` is a literal CSS pixel value instead of a scaled approximation. The circle is a tertiary fill, and hover darkens only the glyph.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCircular dismiss button in four sizes whose X is redrawn per size, so the stroke is a true 1.6 to 2.5px at every scale.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Three lookup tables keyed by size drive the glyph: `GLYPH_SIZE` (the X's bounding square in px), `STROKE_WIDTH` (line weight in px) and `GLYPH_INSET` (how far the line ends sit in from the square's corners).\n2. The SVG is `width = height = glyph` with `viewBox=\"0 0 glyph glyph\"`, so one user unit is one CSS pixel. The two diagonals go from `(inset, inset)` to `(glyph − inset, glyph − inset)` and mirrored, with `strokeLinecap=\"round\"` so the caps add half a stroke beyond each end.\n3. Scaling one shared icon would scale its stroke with it: a 2px line drawn for 20px becomes about 3.2px at 32px. Redrawing per size keeps 2px at xs and sm and lets md deliberately step up to 2.5px so it holds its own in a modal header.\n4. The container is a `rounded-full` square (`size-4/5/6/8`) on `background-tertiary-default`. The glyph uses `currentColor`, so `hover:text-text-primary` darkens the X while the circle stays put, transitioning colour over 150ms.\n5. `aria-label` is required by the props type and the SVG is `aria-hidden`. `type` defaults to `\"button\"` so a close button inside a form dialog does not submit it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `GLYPH_SIZE = 2xs 6.8 / xs 10.8 / sm 12.6 / md 16.2 px`\n  xs, sm and md are 90% of the natural 12 / 14 / 18px glyphs for 20, 24 and 32px circles; at full size the X crowds the circle edge, and 10% smaller reads as centred with breathing room. 2xs takes its 6.8px straight from the compact Figma variant.\n- `STROKE_WIDTH = 2xs 1.6 / xs 2 / sm 2 / md 2.5 px`\n  Literal pixel weights from Figma. md is bolder on purpose: a 2px X inside a 32px circle looks spindly next to a modal title.\n- `GLYPH_INSET = 2xs 0.57 / others 2 px`\n  Room for the round caps to stay inside the glyph box. The 2xs value comes from Figma's 8px bars rotated 45° inside a 6.8px square, which leaves about 0.57px at each corner when projected onto the diagonal.\n- `container sizes = 16 / 20 / 24 / 32 px`\n  2xs for compact floating chips, xs for the announcement corner, sm for toasts, md for modal and drawer headers.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CloseButton(props: Omit<ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>, \"children\"> & { size?: \"2xs\" | \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\"; \"aria-label\": string; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `size: \"2xs\" | \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"xs\"`\n  16, 20, 24 or 32px circle, each with its own glyph size and stroke weight.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Required. Names what the button dismisses.\n- `type: \"button\" | \"submit\" | \"reset\"` — default `\"button\"`\n  Defaults to `button` so it never submits a surrounding form.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge`, for example to absolutely position it in a corner.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CloseButton({\n  size = \"xs\",\n  className,\n  type = \"button\",\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: CloseButtonProps) {\n  const glyph = GLYPH_SIZE[size];\n  const strokeWidth = STROKE_WIDTH[size];\n  const inset = GLYPH_INSET[size];\n\n  return (\n    <button\n      ref={ref}\n      type={type}\n      className={cx(styles.base, styles.container[size], className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <svg width={glyph} height={glyph} viewBox={`0 0 ${glyph} ${glyph}`} fill=\"none\" aria-hidden>\n        <path\n          d={`M${inset} ${inset}L${glyph - inset} ${glyph - inset}`}\n          stroke=\"currentColor\"\n          strokeWidth={strokeWidth}\n          strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n        />\n        <path\n          d={`M${glyph - inset} ${inset}L${inset} ${glyph - inset}`}\n          stroke=\"currentColor\"\n          strokeWidth={strokeWidth}\n          strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n        />\n      </svg>\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. Hover only darkens the glyph colour over 150ms.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Replacing the hand-drawn SVG with an icon font or a single scaled icon. The stroke weight then drifts with size and the md X looks either hairline or blobby.\n- Removing `aria-label`. The glyph is hidden from assistive tech, so the button announces as just \"button\" and nobody knows it closes something.\n- Rounding the computed coordinates, for example `Math.round(glyph − inset)`. The sub-pixel values are what keep both diagonals the same length and the X optically centred.\n- Placing it on a background that matches `background-tertiary-default`. The circle disappears and only the X remains, which reads as a smaller, unanchored target.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Label it after what it closes (\"Dismiss announcement\", \"Close dialog\") rather than a generic \"Close\" when several dismissible surfaces can be on screen at once.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFour circles render at 16, 20, 24 and 32px, each with a centred X whose lines look equally crisp, and the 32px X is visibly a touch bolder. Hovering darkens the X but not the circle. Tabbing shows an offset focus ring around the circle. Inside the announcement card, clicking the xs button hides the card and a restore button brings it back.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "code-block": "# Build: Code Block\n\nThe snippet frame from a docs page: a grey shell holding a header row (filename or role chip on the left, wrap and copy icon buttons on the right) above a white, inset code panel. TSX is coloured by a small tokenizer that ships with the component, so there is no highlighter package, grammar file or WASM to load. Line numbers sit in a sticky gutter, long lines scroll sideways or soft-wrap on demand, and the copy button swaps its glyph for a check for 1.6 seconds.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDocs code block with a filename chip, sticky line numbers, a dependency-free TSX highlighter, wrap toggle and one-click copy.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `tokenizeTsx` walks the source once with sticky (`/y`) regexes, trying whitespace, comments, strings, then mode-specific rules. A mode stack of `code`, `tag` and `text` entries is what separates `a < b` from `<Card>`: a `<` opens a tag only in expression position (start of input, after a keyword, or after punctuation other than `)` and `]`), and inside a tag every bare identifier is an attribute.\n2. Tags manage the stack: `/>` pops the tag, `>` pops it and pushes a `text` entry for an opening tag or pops the enclosing `text` entry for a closing one. In `text` mode everything up to the next `<` or `{` is plain JSX text, so an apostrophe in copy never starts a string. `{` inside a tag or text pushes a fresh `code` entry with its own brace counter, and the matching `}` pops it.\n3. Identifiers become `keyword` (from a fixed set), `number` (for `true`, `null` and other literals), `function` (when followed by `(` or a `<T>(` type argument) or `plain`. Adjacent tokens of the same kind merge, which keeps the span count low.\n4. `splitLines` re-cuts the token stream at every newline so a multi-line comment or template string keeps its colour on each row. Each row is a flex line: an `aria-hidden`, `select-none` gutter cell that is `sticky start-0` with the panel background, then the code cell, which is `whitespace-pre` or `whitespace-pre-wrap break-words` depending on the wrap state.\n5. The gutter width is `calc(<digits>ch + 2rem)`, computed from the line count, so numbers right-align without measuring. Without wrap the `<code>` element is `w-max min-w-full`, so long rows widen the scroll area while highlighted rows still span the panel.\n6. Copy goes through `writeClipboard`: `navigator.clipboard.writeText` first, then a hidden read-only textarea with `execCommand(\"copy\")`, and the previous focus is restored. `useCopyToClipboard` holds the `copied` flag and a reset timeout that restarts on each copy and is cleared on unmount.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `copied window = 1600 ms`\n  Long enough to see the check after the pointer leaves the button, and short enough that a second copy a moment later still gets fresh feedback.\n- `shell / panel radii = rounded-2xl outer, rounded-xl inner, p-1.5 gap`\n  The inner panel sits 6px inside the shell, so a 16px outer radius and a 12px inner radius stay concentric.\n- `code type = font-mono + text-body-regular (14 / 20 px)`\n  Rows have `min-h-5` (20px) to match the line height, so empty lines keep their height without a placeholder character.\n- `gutter width = calc(digits ch + 2rem)`\n  16px of padding on each side of the widest number. Using `ch` keeps 9 to 10 and 99 to 100 from shifting the code column by a pixel or two.\n- `icon swap = 200 ms opacity + scale 0.5 to 1`\n  The copy and check glyphs are stacked absolutely and cross-faded, so the button never changes size.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CodeBlock(props: { code: string; filename?: string; chipTone?: \"neutral\" | \"file\"; language?: \"tsx\" | \"plain\"; showLineNumbers?: boolean; highlightLines?: number[]; wrap?: boolean; defaultWrap?: boolean; onWrapChange?: (wrap: boolean) => void; hideWrapToggle?: boolean; hideCopy?: boolean; maxHeight?: number; actions?: ReactNode; onCopied?: (code: string) => void; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction tokenizeTsx(src: string): Token[]\nfunction CopyButton(props: { value: string | (() => string); label?: string; copiedLabel?: string; resetMs?: number; onCopied?: (text: string) => void } & ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `code: string`\n  Source to show and copy. Leading blank lines and trailing whitespace are trimmed.\n- `filename: string`\n  Header chip label: a filename or a role such as `usage`. Omit it for an empty chip slot.\n- `chipTone: \"neutral\" | \"file\"` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  White label chip, or the purple `docs-file-chip-*` chip with a code glyph.\n- `language: \"tsx\" | \"plain\"` — default `\"tsx\"`\n  Run the built-in TSX tokenizer or print the text as is.\n- `showLineNumbers: boolean` — default `true`\n  Sticky, unselectable line-number gutter.\n- `highlightLines: number[]`\n  1-based lines tinted with `background-secondary`, gutter included.\n- `wrap / defaultWrap / onWrapChange: boolean / boolean / (wrap: boolean) => void` — default `uncontrolled, false`\n  Soft-wrap state, controlled or uncontrolled. The header toggle is `aria-pressed`.\n- `hideWrapToggle / hideCopy: boolean` — default `false`\n  Drop either header action.\n- `maxHeight: number`\n  Pixel cap on the code panel. It scrolls past this.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Extra header controls placed before wrap and copy.\n- `onCopied: (code: string) => void`\n  Called after a successful copy.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Tokenize once per source, then cut into rows so each line owns its gutter\n// cell. The gutter is sticky, so numbers stay put while long lines scroll.\nconst lines = useMemo(\n  () => splitLines(language === \"tsx\" ? tokenizeTsx(source) : plainTokens(source)),\n  [source, language],\n);\nconst highlighted = useMemo(() => new Set(highlightLines ?? []), [highlightLines]);\nconst gutterCh = String(lines.length).length;\n\nconst rows = lines.map((tokens, index) => {\n  const lineNo = index + 1;\n  const isHighlighted = highlighted.has(lineNo);\n  return (\n    <span\n      key={lineNo}\n      data-line={lineNo}\n      data-highlighted={isHighlighted || undefined}\n      className={cx(\"flex min-h-5\", isHighlighted && \"bg-background-secondary-default\")}\n    >\n      {showLineNumbers && (\n        <span\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            \"sticky start-0 z-10 shrink-0 select-none ps-4 pe-4 text-end text-text-tertiary\",\n            isHighlighted ? \"bg-background-secondary-default\" : \"bg-docs-code-background\",\n          )}\n          style={{ width: `calc(${gutterCh}ch + 2rem)` }}\n        >\n          {lineNo}\n        </span>\n      )}\n      <span\n        className={cx(\n          \"min-w-0 flex-1 pe-5\",\n          !showLineNumbers && \"ps-5\",\n          wrap ? \"whitespace-pre-wrap break-words\" : \"whitespace-pre\",\n        )}\n      >\n        {tokens.map((token, t) => (\n          <span key={t} className={TOKEN_CLASS[token.kind]}>\n            {token.text}\n          </span>\n        ))}\n        {\"\\n\"}\n      </span>\n    </span>\n  );\n});\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The reset timeout in `useCopyToClipboard` is cleared on unmount and whenever a new copy restarts it. The fallback textarea is removed in the same tick it is created. There are no listeners or observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 200ms copy-to-check cross-fade and the 150ms hover colour change. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the glyph swap has `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so the check appears at once. Nothing moves on scroll or load.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Treating every `<` as a tag. Generics (`useState<Period>`) and comparisons would light up green. The expression-position test on the last significant token is what prevents it.\n- Rendering rows without the trailing `\\n` inside each code cell. Selecting several lines and pressing Cmd+C would join them into one line, because flex rows carry no newline of their own.\n- Letting line numbers be selectable or part of the copied text. The gutter is `select-none` and `aria-hidden`, and the copy button reads the trimmed `code` prop, never the DOM.\n- Building the token classes dynamically (`text-${kind}`). Tailwind only compiles literal class names, so the colour map is a literal object.\n- Calling `navigator.clipboard.writeText` with no fallback. It throws in insecure contexts and many embedded iframes, and the copy then silently does nothing.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `language=\"plain\"` skips the tokenizer for shell output, JSON or anything else.\n- `CopyButton`, `useCopyToClipboard` and `writeClipboard` are exported from `code-block-copy` for other docs pieces such as install commands and copy-page buttons.\n- The tokenizer handles the TSX found in docs snippets. It does not understand regex literals or nested template literal expressions, which render as strings or punctuation.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA TSX snippet renders with purple keywords, orange strings, grey comments and green JSX tag names, while `useState<Period>` and `a < b` stay uncoloured. Line numbers stay pinned while a long line scrolls sideways and are not included when the text is selected. Pressing the wrap toggle sets `aria-pressed=\"true\"` and long lines wrap inside the panel. Pressing copy puts the exact `code` string on the clipboard, swaps the icon to a check, announces \"Copied\" and reverts after about 1.6s.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "code-diff-viewer": "# Build: Code Diff Viewer\n\nGive it the file before and after a change and it shows the difference the way a code review does: removed lines on red, added lines on green, each with its old and new line number, grouped into hunks with \"@@ -a,b +c,d @@\" headers. Long runs of unchanged code fold into a \"Show N unchanged lines\" bar; open one, or every one from the toolbar, to read the whole file. A Unified / Split switch moves between one column and old and new side by side. Long lines wrap instead of scrolling sideways.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBefore / after line diff with old and new line numbers, hunk headers, a unified or split layout and folded unchanged lines.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. diffLines splits both texts into lines, trims the lines they share at the start and end, and fills a longest-common-subsequence table for the changed middle only. Walking the table gives unchanged, removed and added lines in order, each numbered against the old file, the new file or both. On a tie a removal goes first, so a replaced block reads \"- old\" then \"+ new\".\n2. findFolds looks at every run of unchanged lines and keeps `context` lines (3 by default) next to each change. What is left becomes a fold if it hides at least 3 lines; runs at the very top or bottom keep no context on their outer side.\n3. layoutDiff turns the closed folds into hunks: each visible stretch between closed folds gets a header with its old and new start and length, read from running line counts. A closed fold and the header of the hunk after it share one row; an opened fold keeps a slim \"Hide N lines\" row where it was, and the header of the surrounding hunk widens to include the lines it revealed.\n4. Fold ids are the index where their unchanged run starts, which does not depend on `context`. Opened ids are stored together with the diff they belong to, so passing new code starts folded again without an effect.\n5. The fold button keeps the same React key whether open or closed, so keyboard focus stays on it after you press it.\n6. Split view pairs the rows of a change: the n-th removal sits beside the n-th addition, and a side with nothing to pair shows an empty grey cell. Unchanged lines show on both sides with their own numbers.\n7. The table uses fixed layout with set gutter widths, so code cells wrap long lines (overflow-wrap anywhere) instead of widening the table. Below 28rem the gutters narrow, and below 32rem the folder part of the path hides.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `context = 3 lines`\n  The same amount git shows by default; enough to see which block a change sits in.\n- `MIN_HIDDEN = 3 lines`\n  Folding one or two lines behind a bar that takes a row itself saves nothing.\n- `gutter width = 2.75rem (2rem under 28rem)`\n  Room for four-digit line numbers at caption size; narrower on phones so code keeps most of the width.\n- `line tones = notification success / error background tokens`\n  The same soft lime and rose fills as the Changes Diff Panel, readable in both themes.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CodeDiffViewer(props: { oldCode: string; newCode: string; fileName?: string; view?: \"unified\" | \"split\"; defaultView?: \"unified\" | \"split\"; onViewChange?: (view: \"unified\" | \"split\") => void; context?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `oldCode: string`\n  The file before the change.\n- `newCode: string`\n  The file after the change.\n- `fileName: string`\n  Path shown in the toolbar and in the accessible name.\n- `view: \"unified\" | \"split\"`\n  Controlled layout.\n- `defaultView: \"unified\" | \"split\"` — default `\"unified\"`\n  Initial layout when uncontrolled.\n- `onViewChange: (view: \"unified\" | \"split\") => void`\n  Called when the Unified / Split switch changes.\n- `context: number` — default `3`\n  Unchanged lines kept beside each change before the rest folds.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the viewer surface (height, width).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function diffLines(oldCode: string, newCode: string): DiffLine[] {\n  const a = splitLines(oldCode);\n  const b = splitLines(newCode);\n  // Trim the shared head and tail first, so a typical edit only runs the table on the changed middle.\n  let pre = 0;\n  while (pre < a.length && pre < b.length && a[pre] === b[pre]) pre++;\n  let suf = 0;\n  while (suf < a.length - pre && suf < b.length - pre && a[a.length - 1 - suf] === b[b.length - 1 - suf]) suf++;\n  const n = a.length - pre - suf;\n  const m = b.length - pre - suf;\n  const w = m + 1;\n  // lcs[i * w + j] = longest common run of lines between a[pre + i..] and b[pre + j..]\n  const lcs = new Uint32Array((n + 1) * w);\n  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    for (let j = m - 1; j >= 0; j--) {\n      lcs[i * w + j] =\n        a[pre + i] === b[pre + j]\n          ? (lcs[(i + 1) * w + j + 1] ?? 0) + 1\n          : Math.max(lcs[(i + 1) * w + j] ?? 0, lcs[i * w + j + 1] ?? 0);\n    }\n  }\n  const out: DiffLine[] = [];\n  let oldNo = 1;\n  let newNo = 1;\n  const same = (text: string) => out.push({ kind: \"ctx\", text, oldNo: oldNo++, newNo: newNo++ });\n  for (let k = 0; k < pre; k++) same(a[k] ?? \"\");\n  let i = 0;\n  let j = 0;\n  while (i < n || j < m) {\n    const x = a[pre + i] ?? \"\";\n    const y = b[pre + j] ?? \"\";\n    if (i < n && j < m && x === y) {\n      same(x);\n      i++;\n      j++;\n    } else if (i < n && (j >= m || (lcs[(i + 1) * w + j] ?? 0) >= (lcs[i * w + j + 1] ?? 0))) {\n      // On a tie, removals go first so a replaced block reads \"- old\" then \"+ new\".\n      out.push({ kind: \"del\", text: x, oldNo: oldNo++ });\n      i++;\n    } else {\n      out.push({ kind: \"add\", text: y, newNo: newNo++ });\n      j++;\n    }\n  }\n  for (let k = a.length - suf; k < a.length; k++) same(a[k] ?? \"\");\n  return out;\n}\n\n/** Every run of unchanged lines, minus `context` lines next to a change, is a fold candidate. */\nfunction findFolds(lines: DiffLine[], context: number): Fold[] {\n  const folds: Fold[] = [];\n  let s = 0;\n  while (s < lines.length) {\n    if (lines[s]?.kind !== \"ctx\") {\n      s++;\n      continue;\n    }\n    let e = s;\n    while (e < lines.length && lines[e]?.kind === \"ctx\") e++;\n    const from = s === 0 ? 0 : s + context;\n    const to = e === lines.length ? e : e - context;\n    if (to - from >= MIN_HIDDEN) folds.push({ id: s, from, to });\n    s = e;\n  }\n  return folds;\n}\n\nexport function layoutDiff(lines: DiffLine[], context: number, openIds: ReadonlySet<number>) {\n  const folds = findFolds(lines, context);\n  const oldAt: number[] = [];\n  const newAt: number[] = [];\n  let o = 0;\n  let nw = 0;\n  for (const l of lines) {\n    oldAt.push(o);\n    newAt.push(nw);\n    if (l.kind !== \"add\") o++;\n    if (l.kind !== \"del\") nw++;\n  }\n  oldAt.push(o);\n  newAt.push(nw);\n  const span = (at: number[], from: number, to: number) => {\n    const start = at[from] ?? 0;\n    const len = (at[to] ?? 0) - start;\n    return `${len ? start + 1 : start},${len}`;\n  };\n  const header = (from: number, to: number) => `@@ -${span(oldAt, from, to)} +${span(newAt, from, to)} @@`;\n  const openAt = new Map(folds.filter((f) => openIds.has(f.id)).map((f) => [f.from, f]));\n\n  const rows: Row[] = [];\n  let cursor = 0;\n  let pending: Fold | null = null;\n  // A hunk is a visible stretch between closed folds; its header covers any opened folds inside it.\n  const emit = (from: number, to: number, before: Fold | null) => {\n    rows.push(\n      before\n        ? { t: \"fold\", fold: before, open: false, header: header(from, to) }\n        : { t: \"header\", from, header: header(from, to) },\n    );\n    for (let i = from; i < to; i++) {\n      const opened = openAt.get(i);\n      if (opened) rows.push({ t: \"fold\", fold: opened, open: true, header: null });\n      const line = lines[i];\n      if (line) rows.push({ t: \"line\", i, line });\n    }\n  };\n  for (const f of folds) {\n    if (openIds.has(f.id)) continue;\n    if (f.from > cursor) emit(cursor, f.from, pending);\n    pending = f;\n    cursor = f.to;\n  }\n  if (cursor < lines.length) emit(cursor, lines.length, pending);\n  else if (pending) rows.push({ t: \"fold\", fold: pending, open: false, header: null });\n  return { rows, foldIds: folds.map((f) => f.id) };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers. The diff and layout are memoised React values.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only the hover and selected colours of the layout switch and fold buttons transition, and they use motion-reduce:transition-none; folds open and close instantly in every mode.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- The line diff fills an old × new table for the changed middle, so it is meant for source files up to a few thousand lines. For whole generated files or huge diffs, compute the diff on a server or in a worker.\n- Lines are compared exactly: a change in indentation or line endings counts as a change. Normalise \"\\r\\n\" to \"\\n\" before passing text from Windows files.\n- Code is plain text in a monospace table; add your own highlighter per line if you need token colours.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Uses DiffCounts from Changes Diff Panel for the +/− totals in the toolbar, so both read the same.\n- diffLines and layoutDiff are exported for building other views, such as a commit summary or a minimap of changed lines.\n- An unchanged file still renders: the whole file sits behind one \"Show N unchanged lines\" bar and the totals read +0 −0.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo opens unified with three hunks and three \"Show N unchanged lines\" bars; the toolbar reads +7 −4. Pressing a bar reveals its lines and turns it into \"Hide N lines\" with focus still on it; \"Show all lines\" opens every fold and its label becomes \"Fold unchanged\". Split puts each removed line beside its replacement with old numbers on the left and new on the right. At 360px wide lines wrap and nothing scrolls sideways.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "color": "# Build: Color\n\nA docs palette for the semantic layer of the kit theme. The tokens are grouped into Background, Text, Border, Status and Chart. Each group is a bordered table, and each row has a live swatch, the custom property name, the utility that consumes it, and what the token points to in the light and the dark theme. The light and dark columns are read from the loaded stylesheets, not typed in, so they stay correct when the theme changes. They show both themes whichever one the page is in. Clicking a token copies `--color-…` to the clipboard.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSemantic colour token palette: grouped swatch tables with light and dark values read from the theme, click a token to copy its variable.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `COLOR_GROUPS` lists 74 tokens in five groups. Each token has its variable name and a display-only utility string (`bg-…`, `text-…`, `border-…`, `ring-…`, `fill-…`, `placeholder:text-…`).\n2. On mount, `readThemeValues` walks `document.styleSheets`, recursing into `@import` and `@layer`/`@supports` blocks and skipping `@media`. It keeps only style rules whose selector list contains exactly `:root` (light) or `.dark` (dark), and reads each wanted custom property with `rule.style.getPropertyValue`. Cross-origin sheets throw on `cssRules` and are skipped. A token that `.dark` never overrides takes its light value.\n3. `formatColorValue` turns the raw declarations into palette names: `var(--color-neutral-950)` becomes `neutral-950`, and `color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-neutral-800) 60%, transparent)` becomes `neutral-800 / 60%`. The light and dark chips are painted with the raw value itself, so they resolve through the primitives and show the other theme correctly.\n4. The main swatch in each row is painted with `var(<token>)`, so it follows whichever theme is active with no JS. Rows are a CSS grid with ARIA table roles. One template gives aligned columns: three columns on phones (the utility moves under the name and the light/dark labels become screen-reader text), and four from `lg`.\n5. One `useCopyToClipboard` instance in `ColorPalette` serves every row. `lastCopied` records which token was clicked, so only that row shows the lime check for 1.4s. A `role=\"status\"` span announces \"Copied --color-…\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `groups = background 14, text 10, border 13, status 16, chart 21`\n  Every semantic colour that components consume. Primitives (`neutral-*`, `accent-*`) stay out, because components must never use them directly.\n- `row grid = 1.4fr / 1fr / 9.5rem / 9.5rem from lg, 1fr / auto / auto (chips only) on phones`\n  Fixed value columns keep the light and dark chips aligned across rows and fit \"neutral-700 / 60%\". On phones the names get almost the whole width and wrap instead of truncating.\n- `swatch = 22px, rounded-md, 1px inset ring border-button-default`\n  The inset ring keeps white and transparent swatches visible on a white card without changing their size.\n- `copy reset = 1400 ms`\n  Long enough to see the check, short enough for copying several tokens in a row.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ColorPalette(props: { groups?: readonly string[]; query?: string; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; onCopied?: (name: string) => void; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction ColorTokenTable(props: { group: ColorGroup; values: ReadonlyMap<string, ThemeValue>; copiedName?: string | null; onCopy: (name: string) => void; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction readThemeValues(names: readonly string[]): Map<string, { light?: string; dark?: string }>\nfunction formatColorValue(raw: string): string\nconst COLOR_GROUPS: readonly ColorGroup[]\n```\n\n- `groups: readonly string[]` — default `all five`\n  Group ids to show: `background`, `text`, `border`, `status`, `chart`.\n- `query: string` — default `\"\"`\n  Case-insensitive filter on variable and utility names. Groups with no matches are hidden.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4` — default `3`\n  Heading level of each group title.\n- `onCopied: (name: string) => void`\n  Called with the variable name after a successful copy.\n- `className: string`\n  Extra classes on the stack of groups.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction collect(rules: CSSRuleList, names: ReadonlySet<string>, out: Map<string, ThemeValue>) {\n  for (const rule of Array.from(rules)) {\n    if (rule instanceof CSSImportRule) {\n      if (rule.styleSheet) safeCollect(rule.styleSheet, names, out);\n    } else if (rule instanceof CSSStyleRule) {\n      // Only the two theme scopes count; `.dark[data-accent]` and friends are refinements.\n      const selectors = rule.selectorText.split(\",\").map((s) => s.trim());\n      const scope = selectors.includes(\".dark\") ? \"dark\" : selectors.includes(\":root\") ? \"light\" : null;\n      if (!scope) continue;\n      for (const name of names) {\n        const value = rule.style.getPropertyValue(name).trim();\n        if (value) out.set(name, { ...out.get(name), [scope]: value });\n      }\n    } else if (!(rule instanceof CSSMediaRule) && \"cssRules\" in rule) {\n      // @layer / @supports blocks; @media is skipped so a colour-scheme query is never read as light.\n      collect((rule as CSSGroupingRule).cssRules, names, out);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction safeCollect(sheet: CSSStyleSheet, names: ReadonlySet<string>, out: Map<string, ThemeValue>) {\n  try {\n    collect(sheet.cssRules, names, out);\n  } catch {\n    // Cross-origin sheets throw on cssRules; they never hold the kit theme.\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * Reads what each token is declared as in the light (`:root`) and dark\n * (`.dark`) scopes straight from the loaded stylesheets, so the table shows\n * both themes no matter which one the page is in. Client only.\n */\nexport function readThemeValues(names: readonly string[]): Map<string, ThemeValue> {\n  const out = new Map<string, ThemeValue>();\n  const wanted = new Set(names);\n  for (const sheet of Array.from(document.styleSheets)) safeCollect(sheet, wanted, out);\n  for (const [name, value] of out) if (!value.dark && value.light) out.set(name, { ...value, dark: value.light });\n  return out;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The stylesheet read runs once in a mount effect and adds no listeners or observers. The copied-state timeout belongs to `useCopyToClipboard` and is cleared on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates apart from the hover tint and the copy icon fade. Both use `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reading values with `getComputedStyle(document.documentElement)`. That only returns the active theme, and only the resolved colour, so the light column shows dark values in dark mode and the primitive name is lost.\n- Treating a `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { … } }` block as light. That is why `@media` rules are skipped while walking.\n- Painting the dark chip with `var(--token)`. On a light page that resolves to the light value. The chip has to use the declared `.dark` value, which references primitives that do not change with the theme.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `readThemeValues` and `formatColorValue` are exported, so a design-review tool can diff token declarations between two themes.\n- Pass `groups={[\"status\"]}` to put a single table on a badge or chip docs page, and `query` to wire a search field.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nIn light mode the `--color-text-primary` row shows a near-black swatch, `text-text-primary`, Light `neutral-950` and Dark `neutral-50`. Switching the page to dark turns the swatch near-white while the Light and Dark columns stay the same. `--color-background-inner-default` shows Dark `neutral-800 / 60%`. Clicking a token puts exactly `--color-…` on the clipboard and shows a lime check on that row only. Filtering with \"chart\" leaves only the Chart group. At 360px each row shows the swatch, name, utility and two value chips with no horizontal scroll.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "color-picker": "# Build: Color Picker\n\nA compact colour editor on React Aria's colour components: a 3:2 saturation and brightness field, a hue rail, an optional opacity rail over a checkerboard, a swatch beside a hex field, and a row of presets. `ColorPicker` renders it inline; `ColorWell` is a 32px round swatch that opens the same editor in a popover. Thumbs are the kit slider's white embossed dot and grow slightly while dragged.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA saturation field, hue and opacity rails, a hex field and presets, inline or behind a round swatch that opens a popover.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Both exports wrap React Aria's `ColorPicker`, which owns the value and shares it through context, so every control inside binds to it with no props. `ColorPicker` renders the private `Editor` directly; `ColorWell` renders a `<button>` holding a `ColorSwatch` inside the kit `PopoverTrigger`, and the kit `Popover` (`w-[248px] rounded-3xl`, `p-1` dialog) holds the `Editor` at full width.\n2. `Editor` is a `w-[228px] max-w-full` column with `gap-2.5`. First a `ColorArea` with `colorSpace=\"hsb\"`, `xChannel=\"saturation\"`, `yChannel=\"brightness\"`, `aspect-[3/2]` and `rounded-2xl`; working in HSB means the hue survives dragging through grey. React Aria paints the gradient itself.\n3. Under it a `ColorSlider` on `channel=\"hue\"` with an `h-3` rounded `SliderTrack`, and with `showAlpha` a second slider on `channel=\"alpha\"` whose track sits on a CHECKERBOARD div: a `repeating-conic-gradient` in 8px tiles, with darker tiles under `dark:`. Every surface gets an inset 1px `rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06)` hairline span so pale colours do not melt into the page.\n4. All thumbs share one class: `size-4 rounded-full border border-white` with a two-part shadow (2px drop plus inset hairline), `data-[dragging]:scale-110` over 150ms, and a focus-visible ring. Rail thumbs add `top-1/2`.\n5. The hex row is a 32px `ColorSwatch` and a `ColorField` with an `Input` (`dir=\"ltr\"`, mono 13px uppercase, tertiary surface that turns primary with a focus ring). React Aria commits a colour field on blur only, so Enter is handled by blurring and immediately refocusing the input.\n6. `swatches` renders a `ColorSwatchPicker` as a 7-column grid; each item is a `ColorSwatchPickerItem` that scales to 110% on hover. The selected ring is always rendered and fades in through `group-data-[selected]/swatch:opacity-100`: a 2px white inset inside a 1px dark inset, which reads on any colour.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `editor width = w-[228px] inline; w-full inside the w-[248px] popover`\n  Seven presets at gap-1.5 come out at about 27px each, a comfortable hit target, and the 3:2 area is then 152px tall, enough vertical travel for brightness.\n- `thumb = size-4, scale-110 while dragging, 150ms`\n  Sixteen pixels is the kit slider's thumb, so colour rails and range sliders match. The grow confirms the grab without hiding the colour under the pointer.\n- `rail height = h-3 (12px)`\n  Thinner than the thumb, so the thumb visibly overhangs the rail and reads as draggable.\n- `checkerboard tile = 8px, #e6e6e6 / #ffffff (dark: #3a3a3a / #262626)`\n  Small enough to show partial opacity on a 12px rail; the dark pair keeps the rail from glaring in dark mode.\n- `hairline = inset 0 0 0 1px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06)`\n  Separates white and near-white from the page background without drawing a visible border on saturated colours.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ColorPicker(props: Omit<AriaColorPickerProps, \"children\"> & { showAlpha?: boolean; swatches?: string[]; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ColorWell(props: ColorPickerProps & { \"aria-label\"?: string; placement?: PopoverProps[\"placement\"]; wellClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value / defaultValue: string | Color`\n  Controlled or initial colour.\n- `onChange: (value: Color) => void`\n  Fires continuously while dragging and on hex commit.\n- `showAlpha: boolean` — default `false`\n  Adds the opacity rail over a checkerboard.\n- `swatches: string[]`\n  Preset CSS colours in a 7-column row under the editor.\n- `className: string` — default `w-[228px]`\n  Classes on the editor column, e.g. a width.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Color\"`\n  ColorWell only. Names the swatch button and its popover.\n- `placement: PopoverProps[\"placement\"]` — default `\"bottom start\"`\n  ColorWell only. Where the popover opens.\n- `wellClassName: string` — default `size-8`\n  ColorWell only. Classes on the swatch button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction Editor({ showAlpha = false, swatches, className }: EditorProps) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n\n  return (\n    <div className={cx(\"flex w-[228px] max-w-full flex-col gap-2.5\", className)}>\n      <AriaColorArea\n        colorSpace=\"hsb\"\n        xChannel=\"saturation\"\n        yChannel=\"brightness\"\n        className=\"relative aspect-[3/2] w-full shrink-0 cursor-crosshair rounded-2xl\"\n      >\n        <span aria-hidden className={cx(HAIRLINE, \"rounded-2xl\")} />\n        <AriaColorThumb className={THUMB} />\n      </AriaColorArea>\n\n      <AriaColorSlider colorSpace=\"hsb\" channel=\"hue\" className=\"w-full\">\n        <AriaSliderTrack className=\"relative h-3 w-full cursor-pointer rounded-full\">\n          <span aria-hidden className={cx(HAIRLINE, \"rounded-full\")} />\n          <AriaColorThumb className={cx(THUMB, \"top-1/2\")} />\n        </AriaSliderTrack>\n      </AriaColorSlider>\n\n      {showAlpha ? (\n        <AriaColorSlider channel=\"alpha\" className=\"w-full\">\n          <div className={cx(\"relative h-3 w-full rounded-full\", CHECKERBOARD)}>\n            <AriaSliderTrack className=\"relative h-3 w-full cursor-pointer rounded-full\">\n              <span aria-hidden className={cx(HAIRLINE, \"rounded-full\")} />\n              <AriaColorThumb className={cx(THUMB, \"top-1/2\")} />\n            </AriaSliderTrack>\n          </div>\n        </AriaColorSlider>\n      ) : null}\n\n      <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n        <AriaColorSwatch className=\"relative size-8 shrink-0 rounded-full shadow-[inset_0_0_0_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.06)]\" />\n        <AriaColorField aria-label={localize(\"Hex color\")} className=\"min-w-0 flex-1\">\n          <AriaInput\n            dir=\"ltr\"\n            spellCheck={false}\n            onKeyDown={(event) => {\n              // React Aria commits the field on blur only; Enter commits it\n              // too, and focus stays in the field.\n              if (event.key !== \"Enter\") return;\n              const input = event.currentTarget;\n              input.blur();\n              input.focus();\n            }}\n            className={cx(\n              \"h-8 w-full min-w-0 rounded-2lg bg-background-tertiary-default px-2.5 font-mono text-[13px] text-text-primary uppercase\",\n              \"outline-none ring-2 ring-transparent ring-inset transition-[background-color,box-shadow] duration-150 ease\",\n              \"focus:bg-background-primary-default focus:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n            )}\n          />\n        </AriaColorField>\n      </div>\n\n      {swatches?.length ? (\n        <AriaColorSwatchPicker aria-label={localize(\"Presets\")} className=\"grid grid-cols-7 gap-1.5 pt-0.5\">\n          {swatches.map((swatch) => (\n            <AriaColorSwatchPickerItem\n              key={swatch}\n              color={swatch}\n              className={cx(\n                \"group/swatch relative aspect-square w-full cursor-pointer rounded-full outline-none transition-[scale] duration-150 ease-out hover:scale-110\",\n                \"data-[focus-visible]:ring-2 data-[focus-visible]:ring-border-focus-ring data-[focus-visible]:ring-offset-2\",\n              )}\n            >\n              <AriaColorSwatch className=\"size-full rounded-full shadow-[inset_0_0_0_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.08)]\" />\n              {/* Selected: a white ring inside a dark hairline reads on any colour. */}\n              <span\n                aria-hidden\n                className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 rounded-full opacity-0 shadow-[inset_0_0_0_2px_#fff,inset_0_0_0_3px_rgb(0_0_0/0.25)] transition-opacity duration-150 group-data-[selected]/swatch:opacity-100\"\n              />\n            </AriaColorSwatchPickerItem>\n          ))}\n        </AriaColorSwatchPicker>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** React Aria releases its drag listeners when a drag ends or the control unmounts; the popover's overlay and focus management are torn down on close. No timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only 150ms scale on thumbs, swatches and the well plus opacity on the selection ring and the kit popover's entrance. The component does not branch on `prefers-reduced-motion`; the movements are a few pixels and colour updates are instant either way.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not keep the value in RGB or hex while dragging. Use `colorSpace=\"hsb\"`; in RGB, dragging to grey loses the hue and the area snaps to red.\n- Do not expect Enter to commit the hex field. React Aria commits on blur; the component blurs and refocuses on Enter, so keep that handler if you replace the input.\n- Do not mount the selected ring only when selected. It is always present and fades on opacity, which is what makes the selection change smooth.\n- Do not drop the hairline layers to \"clean up\" the markup. Without them white and pale presets vanish against a white card.\n- Do not render `ColorWell` without a meaningful `aria-label`. The button contains only a swatch, so \"Color\" is all a screen reader would announce.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `value` / `defaultValue` take a CSS colour string or a React Aria `Color` (`parseColor(\"#6366F1\")`); `onChange` hands back a `Color`, so call `toString(\"hex\")` or `toString(\"css\")` as needed.\n- Every control is keyboard reachable: arrows nudge the area and rails, Shift or Page keys take bigger steps, and the hex field accepts any CSS colour.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe inline picker shows a saturation field with a white thumb, a full-spectrum hue rail and a hex field reading the current colour. Dragging in the field updates the swatch and hex live; dragging the hue rail through any position keeps the saturation and brightness. Typing \"#22C55E\" in the hex field and pressing Enter applies it without leaving the field. Clicking a preset selects it with a white inner ring. A ColorWell opens the same editor in a popover from its round swatch; with `showAlpha`, the opacity rail sits on a checkerboard.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "combo-chart-card": "# Build: Combo Chart Card\n\nA grey dashboard card that plots monthly sessions as rounded Recharts bars against a left axis and the conversion rate as a line with dots against a right percent axis. The header shows total sessions with their change on the period before, the session-weighted conversion rate, a legend and a \"This year\" calendar pill that swaps the data set. Hovering a month darkens its bar, dims the others, pops a pulsing dot on the line, opens a tooltip with both values and switches the headline and the two tiles to that month.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dashboard card with bars on a left axis and a rate line on a right axis; hovering a month spotlights its bar and pops a dot on the line.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `ComposedChart` holds two `YAxis` components: `yAxisId=\"bars\"` on the left with compact ticks (\"3K\", \"6K\") and `yAxisId=\"line\"` with `orientation=\"right\"` and percent ticks. The `Bar` and the `Line` each name their axis, so sessions and percentages scale independently on one plot.\n2. The right axis domain is `[0, max => Math.max(3, Math.ceil(max * 1.2 / 3) * 3)]`: 20% headroom rounded up to a multiple of 3, so four ticks always land on whole percents (0%, 2%, 4%, 6% for the default data).\n3. `onMouseMove` stores `state.activeTooltipIndex` in `hovered` when `isTooltipActive` is true and the index is finite; `onMouseLeave` clears it. Each bar is a `Cell`: the hovered one is filled with the tone's `-active` colour, the others keep the base colour at `fillOpacity` 0.45 with a 200ms `transition-[fill-opacity]`, and all return to full opacity when the pointer leaves.\n4. The `Tooltip` has `cursor={false}` because the spotlighted bar already marks the month. Its content renders `ChartTooltipCard` from `activeIndex` (mapped from `null` to -1 first) with the bar value and the rate to one decimal.\n5. The line has 3px dots ringed in the card colour, and its `activeDot` is the shared `PulseDot`, which pings while the month is hovered.\n6. With nothing hovered the headline is the period total against `previousTotal` and the rate is the session-weighted average `Σ(bar × line) / Σ bar`. With a month hovered both switch to that month, the chip compares it with the month before (hidden for the first month), and the two tiles relabel from \"total\" / \"average\" to the month name.\n7. The period switch is the kit `Select` as a calendar pill (or a segmented control with `periodControl=\"segmented\"`). Choosing a period clears `hovered`, updates internal state only when `period` is uncontrolled, and calls `onPeriodChange`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `dimmed bars = fillOpacity 0.45, 200ms ease-out`\n  Low enough that the hovered bar clearly leads, high enough that the rest still read as data for comparison.\n- `bar shape = radius 6 on all corners, maxBarSize 28, barCategoryGap 22%`\n  Fully rounded pills match the preview; the size cap keeps bars slim on wide cards so the line stays readable over them.\n- `axis widths = left 44px, right 40px`\n  Left fits a compact label such as \"10.5K\" and right fits \"6%\" at 12px without clipping.\n- `line dots = r 3 with a 1.5px background-secondary stroke; active PulseDot r 4.5 with ping`\n  Small resting dots show where each monthly rate is measured; the bigger pulsing dot only appears on the hovered month.\n- `plot height = 240px`\n  The card grows with its header and tiles; a fixed plot box gives `ResponsiveContainer` a definite height.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ComboChartCard(props?: ComboChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype ComboChartCardProps = { bars?: ComboMeasure; line?: ComboMeasure; periods?: ComboPeriod[]; period?: string; defaultPeriod?: string; onPeriodChange?: (id: string) => void; periodControl?: \"segmented\" | \"select\"; className?: string }\ntype ComboMeasure = { label: string; tone: ChartTone }\ntype ComboPoint = { label: string; bar: number; line: number }\ntype ComboPeriod = { id: string; label: string; data: ComboPoint[]; previousTotal?: number }\n```\n\n- `bars: ComboMeasure` — default `{ label: \"Sessions\", tone: 2 }`\n  Name and colour of the bar measure on the left axis.\n- `line: ComboMeasure` — default `{ label: \"Conversion\", tone: 6 }`\n  Name and colour of the line measure, in percent, on the right axis.\n- `periods: ComboPeriod[]` — default `COMBO_PERIODS`\n  Data sets the pill switches between; `previousTotal` feeds the chip.\n- `period: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultPeriod: string` — default `first period id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onPeriodChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the chosen period id.\n- `periodControl: \"segmented\" | \"select\"` — default `\"select\"`\n  Calendar pill with a listbox, or a segmented switch.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ComboChartCard({\n  bars = { label: \"Sessions\", tone: 2 },\n  line = { label: \"Conversion\", tone: 6 },\n  periods = COMBO_PERIODS,\n  period,\n  defaultPeriod,\n  onPeriodChange,\n  periodControl = \"select\",\n  className,\n}: ComboChartCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [innerPeriod, setInnerPeriod] = useState(defaultPeriod ?? periods[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const periodId = period ?? innerPeriod;\n  const active = periods.find((p) => p.id === periodId) ?? periods[0];\n  const data = active?.data ?? [];\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState<number | null>(null);\n\n  const point = hovered !== null ? data[hovered] : undefined;\n  const total = data.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.bar, 0);\n  // Session-weighted, so a quiet month does not move the yearly rate as much as a busy one.\n  const average = total > 0 ? data.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.bar * p.line, 0) / total : 0;\n  const rate = point ? point.line : average;\n  const headline = point ? point.bar : total;\n  const delta = point\n    ? hovered !== null && hovered > 0\n      ? describeDelta(point.bar, data[hovered - 1]?.bar)\n      : null\n    : describeDelta(total, active?.previousTotal);\n\n  const selectPeriod = (id: string) => {\n    setHovered(null);\n    if (period === undefined) setInnerPeriod(id);\n    onPeriodChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  const barFill = (i: number) => (i === hovered ? toneActiveColor(bars.tone) : toneColor(bars.tone));\n\n  return (\n    <section aria-label={`${bars.label} and ${line.label} chart`} className={cx(CHART_CARD_FRAME, \"gap-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 px-2 pt-2 @md:flex-row @md:items-start @md:justify-between\">\n        <ChartHeadline\n          label={`${bars.label}${point ? ` · ${point.label}` : \"\"} · ${line.label} ${pct(rate)}`}\n          value={headline}\n          delta={delta}\n          swapKey={`${periodId}-${hovered ?? \"total\"}`}\n          reducedMotion={reduced}\n        />\n        <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-2 @md:items-end\">\n          {periods.length > 1 ? (\n            <ChartPeriodControl\n              options={periods}\n              value={periodId}\n              onChange={selectPeriod}\n              variant={periodControl}\n              label={`${bars.label} period`}\n              className=\"self-start @md:self-end\"\n            />\n          ) : null}\n          <ChartLegend\n            items={[\n              { key: \"bar\", label: bars.label, tone: bars.tone },\n              { key: \"line\", label: line.label, tone: line.tone },\n            ]}\n            className=\"@md:pe-1\"\n          />\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"h-[240px] w-full\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" initialDimension={{ width: 520, height: 240 }}>\n          <ComposedChart\n            data={data}\n            barCategoryGap=\"22%\"\n            margin={{ top: 8, right: 0, bottom: 0, left: 0 }}\n            onMouseMove={(state) => {\n              const next = Number(state?.activeTooltipIndex);\n              if (state?.isTooltipActive && Number.isFinite(next)) setHovered(next);\n            }}\n            onMouseLeave={() => setHovered(null)}\n          >\n            <CartesianGrid vertical={false} stroke={GRID_STROKE} strokeDasharray=\"4 4\" />\n            <YAxis\n              yAxisId=\"bars\"\n              width={44}\n              domain={[0, \"auto\"]}\n              tickCount={4}\n              tickFormatter={(v: number) => formatCompact(v)}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <YAxis\n              yAxisId=\"line\"\n              orientation=\"right\"\n              width={40}\n              domain={[0, (max: number) => Math.max(3, Math.ceil((max * 1.2) / 3) * 3)]}\n              tickCount={4}\n              tickFormatter={(v: number) => `${Math.round(v)}%`}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <XAxis\n              dataKey=\"label\"\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tickMargin={10}\n              interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"\n              minTickGap={8}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <Tooltip\n              cursor={false}\n              offset={16}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={160}\n              wrapperStyle={{ outline: \"none\", zIndex: 10 }}\n              content={({ active: isActive, activeIndex }) => {\n                const at = data[activeIndex == null ? -1 : Number(activeIndex)];\n                if (!isActive || !at) return null;\n                return (\n                  <ChartTooltipCard\n                    title={at.label}\n                    rows={[\n                      { key: \"bar\", label: bars.label, tone: bars.tone, value: formatNumber(at.bar) },\n                      { key: \"line\", label: line.label, tone: line.tone, value: pct(at.line) },\n                    ]}\n                  />\n                );\n              }}\n            />\n            <Bar\n              yAxisId=\"bars\"\n              dataKey=\"bar\"\n              name={bars.label}\n              radius={[6, 6, 6, 6]}\n              maxBarSize={28}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={450}\n            >\n              {data.map((p, i) => (\n                <Cell\n                  key={p.label}\n                  fill={barFill(i)}\n                  fillOpacity={hovered !== null && i !== hovered ? 0.45 : 1}\n                  className=\"transition-[fill-opacity] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                />\n              ))}\n            </Bar>\n            <Line\n              yAxisId=\"line\"\n              type=\"monotone\"\n              dataKey=\"line\"\n              name={line.label}\n              stroke={toneActiveColor(line.tone)}\n              strokeWidth={2.5}\n              dot={{ r: 3, fill: toneActiveColor(line.tone), stroke: \"var(--color-background-secondary-default)\", strokeWidth: 1.5 }}\n              activeDot={<PulseDot color={toneActiveColor(line.tone)} pulse={!reduced} />}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={450}\n            />\n          </ComposedChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n\n      <ChartStatTiles\n        columns={2}\n        tiles={[\n          {\n            key: \"bar\",\n            tone: bars.tone,\n            label: point ? `${bars.label} · ${point.label}` : `${bars.label} · total`,\n            value: formatNumber(headline),\n          },\n          {\n            key: \"line\",\n            tone: line.tone,\n            label: point ? `${line.label} · ${point.label}` : `${line.label} · average`,\n            value: pct(rate),\n          },\n        ]}\n      />\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame on target change and unmount, `useReducedMotion` unsubscribes its media-query listener, `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer, and the kit `Select` tears down its popover when closed. The card adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` turns off `isAnimationActive` on the Bar, Line and Tooltip, the headline shows its value without counting, the number fade and the bar opacity transition are disabled with `motion-reduce:` variants, and the active dot drops its ping.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Forgetting `yAxisId` on one of the series. Recharts then plots it against the wrong axis, and a 4% rate sits flat at the bottom of an axis that runs to 9,000.\n- Averaging the monthly rates directly. A plain mean gives 3.65% here; weighting by sessions gives 3.68% (shown as 3.7%), which is the rate actually observed across the year.\n- Leaving a tooltip cursor on as well as the bar spotlight. Two hover markers on the same month compete; the cursor is turned off.\n- Reading `activeIndex` with `Number()` directly. Recharts passes `null` when inactive and `Number(null)` is 0, which would pin the tooltip to January.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `COMBO_PERIODS` and the `ComboPoint`, `ComboPeriod` and `ComboMeasure` types are exported. This year totals 83,200 sessions against 76,050 (\"+9.4%\") at 3.7% conversion; last year totals 76,050 against 70,300 (\"+8.2%\") at 3.3%.\n- Frame, headline, period pill, legend, tooltip card, tiles and pulse dot come from `line-chart-card-parts.tsx`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Sessions · Conversion 3.7%\", \"83,200\" with a lime \"+9.4%\" chip, a \"This year\" pill, a legend, lime rounded bars on a 0 to 9K left axis and a blue dotted line on a 0% to 6% right axis, and tiles \"Sessions · total 83,200\" and \"Conversion · average 3.7%\". Hovering a month darkens its bar, fades the rest, pulses a dot on the line and shows a tooltip with both values. Picking \"Last year\" from the pill shows \"76,050\" and \"+8.2%\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "combobox": "# Build: Combobox\n\nReact Aria's `ComboBox` dressed in the kit's existing parts. The field is Input's `InputBase`, so hover, focus, invalid and disabled states match every other text field, and the menu is Select's surface, so the motion and row styling match every other list. Two details are its own: the popover anchors to the whole field and copies its measured width, and the list scrolls under a progressive top blur that ramps in over the first 24px of scroll.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nType to filter a list: Input's own field with a chevron toggle, and a field-width menu with sections, a check and a scroll fade.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. React Aria would anchor the popover to the bare `<input>`. The component instead passes `groupRef={fieldRef}` into `InputBase` and `triggerRef={fieldRef}` to the `AriaPopover`, so the menu opens under the full field, icon and chevron included.\n2. A `ResizeObserver` on that field writes `field.offsetWidth` into state, and the popover gets `style={{ width: fieldWidth }}`. The menu therefore stays exactly as wide as the field through container resizes and font loading.\n3. The toggle is an `AriaButton` in `InputBase`'s `trailingAddon`. React Aria finds it by context and wires it as the open button. Its `ChevronDownSmall` turns `rotate-180` while open over 200ms.\n4. `useScrollFade()` turns the listbox's `scrollTop` into a 0–1 `progress` over its distance, and `<ScrollFade progress>` overlays a masked backdrop blur plus a wash at the top of the list. `onOpenChange` calls `fade.reset()` on each open, because every opening starts the list at the top.\n5. `allowsEmptyCollection` keeps the popover open when the filter matches nothing, and the listbox's `renderEmptyState` shows `emptyMessage` (\"No results\" by default) instead of closing on the user mid-word.\n6. `ComboBoxItem` uses the shared `MENU_ITEM` row and only adds `MENU_ITEM_ACTIVE` for the focused row. Selection shows as a 16px `RiCheckLine` in the accent colour at the end of the row. `ComboBoxSection` adds a muted `Header` and 8px of top padding between groups.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover offset = 4px`\n  Matches Select, so a combobox and a select in the same form open their menus at the same distance.\n- `list height = max-h-[240px], overscroll-contain`\n  About six rows before the list scrolls. `overscroll-contain` stops a wheel at the end of the list from scrolling the page behind it.\n- `scroll-fade distance = 24px (SCROLL_FADE_DISTANCE)`\n  The fade reaches full strength after one row of scrolling. A longer ramp leaves the first rows sharp under the edge, and a shorter one makes the blur pop in.\n- `emptyMessage = \"No results\"`\n  Shown in tertiary text instead of closing the list, so a typo leaves the popover in place and the user can correct it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ComboBox<T extends object>(props: Omit<AriaComboBoxProps<T>, \"children\" | \"className\"> & { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; placeholder?: string; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; size?: InputSize; emptyMessage?: ReactNode; className?: string; popoverClassName?: string; children: ReactNode | ((item: T) => ReactNode); ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element; function ComboBoxItem(props: Omit<AriaListBoxItemProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode }): JSX.Element; function ComboBoxSection<T extends object>(props: Omit<AriaListBoxSectionProps<T>, \"className\" | \"children\"> & { title?: ReactNode; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Field label, wired as the accessible name. Without it, pass `aria-label`.\n- `placeholder: string`\n  Input placeholder.\n- `leadingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon at the start of the field, for example `RiSearchLine`.\n- `hint: ReactNode`\n  Help text under the field. Shown in the error colour while the field is invalid.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  Field height, 36px or 32px, as Input.\n- `emptyMessage: ReactNode` — default `\"No results\"`\n  Shown in the list when nothing matches.\n- `defaultItems / items: Iterable<T>`\n  Data for a function child. `defaultItems` lets React Aria filter; `items` means you filter.\n- `value / defaultValue / onChange: Key | null`\n  React Aria's selected key, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `inputValue / onInputChange / allowsCustomValue: string / boolean`\n  Control the typed text, or keep text that matches no row.\n- `popoverClassName: string`\n  Classes on the list popover.\n- `children: ReactNode | ((item: T) => ReactNode)`\n  `ComboBoxItem` rows, optionally grouped in `ComboBoxSection`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ComboBox<T extends object>({\n  label,\n  hint,\n  placeholder,\n  leadingIcon,\n  size,\n  emptyMessage = \"No results\",\n  className,\n  popoverClassName,\n  children,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: ComboBoxProps<T>) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const fieldRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const fade = useScrollFade();\n  const [fieldWidth, setFieldWidth] = useState<number>();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const field = fieldRef.current;\n    if (!field) return;\n    const observer = new ResizeObserver(() => setFieldWidth(field.offsetWidth));\n    observer.observe(field);\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\n  }, []);\n\n  return (\n    <AriaComboBox\n      ref={ref}\n      allowsEmptyCollection\n      {...props}\n      className={cx(\"group flex flex-col gap-1\", className)}\n      onOpenChange={(isOpen, trigger) => {\n        // Each opening starts the list at the top, so the fade starts hidden.\n        if (isOpen) fade.reset();\n        props.onOpenChange?.(isOpen, trigger);\n      }}\n    >\n      {({ isOpen, isRequired, isInvalid }) => (\n        <>\n          {label ? <Label isRequired={isRequired}>{label}</Label> : null}\n          <InputBase\n            groupRef={fieldRef}\n            size={size}\n            placeholder={placeholder}\n            leadingIcon={leadingIcon}\n            trailingAddon={\n              <AriaButton\n                className={({ isFocusVisible }) =>\n                  cx(\n                    \"-m-1 flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-md p-1 text-text-secondary outline-none\",\n                    isFocusVisible && \"ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring\",\n                  )\n                }\n              >\n                <ChevronDownSmall\n                  className={cx(\"size-4 transition-transform duration-200 ease\", isOpen && \"rotate-180\")}\n                />\n              </AriaButton>\n            }\n          />\n          {hint ? <HintText isInvalid={isInvalid}>{hint}</HintText> : null}\n          <AriaPopover\n            triggerRef={fieldRef}\n            dir={direction}\n            offset={4}\n            style={{ width: fieldWidth }}\n            className={cx(MENU_POPOVER_SURFACE, \"flex flex-col overflow-hidden p-2\", popoverClassName)}\n          >\n            <div className=\"relative flex min-h-0 flex-col\">\n              <AriaListBox\n                onScroll={fade.onScroll}\n                renderEmptyState={() => (\n                  <div className=\"px-2 py-2 text-body-medium text-text-tertiary\">{emptyMessage}</div>\n                )}\n                className={cx(MENU_ITEMS_CONTAINER, \"max-h-[240px] min-h-0 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain\")}\n              >\n                {children}\n              </AriaListBox>\n              <ScrollFade progress={fade.progress} />\n            </div>\n          </AriaPopover>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaComboBox>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The `ResizeObserver` on the field is disconnected in the effect's cleanup on unmount. React Aria removes the popover portal on close.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The menu fade, scale and blur and the 200ms chevron rotation are CSS transitions from the shared menu recipe. None of them moves content across the screen, so they stay under `prefers-reduced-motion`. The scroll fade follows scroll position directly and has no animation of its own.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Giving rows rich children with no `textValue`. Filtering and the input text after selection both use `textValue`, so an avatar row without it never matches anything typed.\n- Leaving out both `label` and `aria-label`. React Aria logs an accessibility warning and the input has no accessible name.\n- Expecting `className` to style the input. It goes on the root column. Width belongs there, and field styling comes from `InputBase` and `size`.\n- Passing data through `items` and expecting the list to filter as the user types. With `items` the consumer owns filtering. Use `defaultItems` to let React Aria filter by `textValue`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- With a function child, pass the data through `defaultItems` (uncontrolled filtering) or `items` (you filter). With static `ComboBoxItem` children, React Aria filters by `textValue` itself.\n- `menuTrigger=\"focus\"` opens the list on focus, which suits short lists such as timezones. The default `\"input\"` waits for typing.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTyping \"ma\" into Assignee narrows the list to Maya Chen and Mateo Ruiz, and the menu is exactly as wide as the field, icon to chevron. Typing \"zz\" keeps the menu open with \"No one by that name\". Choosing a row closes the menu, fills the input with the name and shows a check on that row the next time the menu opens. In Timezone the list opens on focus with three headed sections, and scrolling it blurs the rows that pass under the top edge. Escape closes either menu, and arrow keys move the highlight.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "command-palette": "# Build: Command Palette\n\nA searchable list of actions and destinations opened with ⌘K / Ctrl+K: jump to a page, run a command, find a record. It is React Aria's Autocomplete and Menu inside a modal dialog, so focus stays in the search field while the arrow keys move a highlight through the list, Enter runs the highlighted row, and focus goes back to where it was on close. The panel sits 12vh from the top over a 20% black scrim and fades in while rising 10px and sharpening from a 4px blur over 200ms.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\n⌘K palette of searchable commands with sections, icons and shortcuts, built-in or custom filtering, on React Aria Autocomplete and Menu.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `CommandPalette` keeps open state and the query, each controllable (`isOpen` / `onOpenChange`, `inputValue` / `onInputChange`). Its `setOpen` notifies the parent and, on every opening, empties the query (also through `onInputChange(\"\")`) and calls `fade.reset()`, so each opening starts with a blank field and the list at its top.\n2. The shortcut is a `keydown` listener on `window` that fires on `metaKey || ctrlKey` plus the `shortcut` letter (default \"k\", case-insensitive), calls `preventDefault` and toggles the palette. The effect has no dependency array, so it re-subscribes every render and always reads the current `isOpen`; `shortcut={false}` skips it.\n3. The overlay is an `AriaModalOverlay` (`isDismissable`, `dir` from `useDirection()`) laid out as `fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-100 h-(--visual-viewport-height) items-start justify-center px-4 pt-[12vh]`, so the panel stays above a phone's keyboard. A sibling `aria-hidden` div is the `bg-black/20` scrim, fading over 150ms via `group-data-[entering]/palette` and `group-data-[exiting]/palette`.\n4. `AriaModal` is `max-h-[min(560px,76vh)] w-full max-w-[560px]` with `transition-[opacity,translate,scale,filter] duration-200 ease-out`: it enters from `opacity-0` and, under `motion-safe:`, `translate-y-2.5 scale-[0.98] blur-[4px]`, and exits to `translate-y-2 scale-[0.98] blur-[3px]`. Inside, an `AriaDialog` (`rounded-3xl`, button border, `shadow-sidebar`, `overflow-clip`) labelled by `aria-label` holds the search row, the list and the footer.\n5. `AriaAutocomplete` wires an `autoFocus` `AriaSearchField` (search icon plus a bare `AriaInput`, native cancel button hidden) to an `AriaMenu`. Its filter is `filter ?? contains`, where `contains` comes from `useFilter({ sensitivity: \"base\" })` (ignores case and accents); `filter={null}` passes no filter, so the children show as given. The menu is the `overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain` scroller with a `ScrollFade` over its top, `renderEmptyState` shows `emptyState`, and `onAction` closes the palette first and then calls the consumer's `onAction(key)`.\n6. `CommandPaletteItem` is an `AriaMenuItem` whose `textValue` falls back to string children; it is a `rounded-xl px-3 py-2.5` row that takes `bg-background-secondary-default` while focused (100ms colour transition), with a 16px icon, the label, an optional muted `description` and an optional `Kbd` shortcut. `CommandPaletteSection` is an `AriaMenuSection` with an `AriaHeader` title (`text-body-2-medium text-text-tertiary`) and `pt-2` on every section after the first. The default footer reads \"Navigate with ↑ ↓\" and \"Select ↵\"; `footer={false}` hides it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `panel position = pt-[12vh], max-w-[560px], max-h-[min(560px,76vh)]`\n  High enough that the results list has room to grow downward, but not pinned to the top edge; the height cap keeps the footer on screen on short viewports.\n- `enter motion = 200ms ease-out, opacity 0, translate-y 10px, scale 0.98, blur 4px`\n  The docs-site search's motion: a short lift and sharpen reads as the panel arriving, without travelling far enough to delay typing.\n- `exit motion = translate-y 8px, scale 0.98, blur 3px`\n  Slightly smaller than the entrance so closing feels quicker than opening.\n- `scrim = bg-black/20, 150ms fade`\n  Dims the page enough to focus the palette while keeping the page legible behind it.\n- `shortcut = \"k\" with ⌘ or Ctrl`\n  The common convention for command palettes; `metaKey || ctrlKey` covers macOS and Windows/Linux with one listener.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CommandPalette(props: { isOpen?: boolean; defaultOpen?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void; shortcut?: string | false; \"aria-label\"?: string; placeholder?: string; inputValue?: string; onInputChange?: (value: string) => void; filter?: ((textValue: string, inputValue: string) => boolean) | null; onAction?: (key: Key) => void; emptyState?: ReactNode; footer?: ReactNode | false; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction CommandPaletteItem(props: Omit<AriaMenuItemProps, \"children\" | \"className\"> & { icon?: IconComponent; description?: ReactNode; shortcut?: string; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction CommandPaletteSection(props: { title?: ReactNode; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `isOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Controlled open state; `defaultOpen` for uncontrolled.\n- `shortcut: string | false` — default `\"k\"`\n  Letter that toggles the palette with ⌘ / Ctrl; `false` turns it off.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Command palette\"`\n  Accessible name of the dialog, field and menu.\n- `placeholder: string` — default `\"Type a command or search…\"`\n  Search field placeholder.\n- `inputValue / onInputChange: string / (value: string) => void`\n  Controlled query, e.g. for remote search.\n- `filter: ((textValue, inputValue) => boolean) | null` — default `locale-aware contains`\n  Row matcher; `null` shows the children unfiltered.\n- `onAction: (key: Key) => void`\n  Runs with the chosen item's id after the palette closes.\n- `emptyState: ReactNode` — default `\"No results\"`\n  Shown when nothing matches.\n- `footer: ReactNode | false` — default `navigate / select hints`\n  The hint row under the list; `false` hides it.\n- `CommandPaletteItem.icon / description / shortcut: IconComponent / ReactNode / string`\n  Leading icon, trailing muted text and a keyboard hint for the row.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CommandPalette({\n  isOpen: controlledOpen,\n  defaultOpen = false,\n  onOpenChange,\n  shortcut = \"k\",\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Command palette\",\n  placeholder = \"Type a command or search…\",\n  inputValue: controlledInput,\n  onInputChange,\n  filter,\n  onAction,\n  emptyState = \"No results\",\n  footer,\n  className,\n  children,\n}: CommandPaletteProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const label = localize(ariaLabel);\n  const { contains } = useFilter({ sensitivity: \"base\" });\n  const fade = useScrollFade();\n  const [uncontrolledOpen, setUncontrolledOpen] = useState(defaultOpen);\n  const [uncontrolledInput, setUncontrolledInput] = useState(\"\");\n  const isOpen = controlledOpen ?? uncontrolledOpen;\n  const inputValue = controlledInput ?? uncontrolledInput;\n\n  const setOpen = (next: boolean) => {\n    setUncontrolledOpen(next);\n    onOpenChange?.(next);\n    if (next) {\n      // Every opening starts fresh: an empty query and the list at its top.\n      setUncontrolledInput(\"\");\n      onInputChange?.(\"\");\n      fade.reset();\n    }\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (shortcut === false) return;\n    const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {\n      if (!(event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey) || event.key.toLowerCase() !== shortcut.toLowerCase()) return;\n      event.preventDefault();\n      setOpen(!isOpen);\n    };\n    window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <AriaModalOverlay\n      isOpen={isOpen}\n      onOpenChange={setOpen}\n      isDismissable\n      dir={direction}\n      className=\"group/palette fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-100 flex h-(--visual-viewport-height) items-start justify-center px-4 pt-[12vh]\"\n    >\n      <div\n        aria-hidden\n        className=\"absolute inset-0 bg-black/20 transition-opacity duration-150 ease-out group-data-[entering]/palette:opacity-0 group-data-[exiting]/palette:opacity-0\"\n      />\n      <AriaModal\n        className={cx(\n          \"relative flex max-h-[min(560px,76vh)] w-full max-w-[560px] flex-col outline-none\",\n          \"transition-[opacity,translate,scale,filter] duration-200 ease-out\",\n          \"data-[entering]:opacity-0 data-[exiting]:opacity-0\",\n          \"motion-safe:data-[entering]:translate-y-2.5 motion-safe:data-[entering]:scale-[0.98] motion-safe:data-[entering]:blur-[4px]\",\n          \"motion-safe:data-[exiting]:translate-y-2 motion-safe:data-[exiting]:scale-[0.98] motion-safe:data-[exiting]:blur-[3px]\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <AriaDialog\n          aria-label={label}\n          className={cx(\n            \"flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col overflow-clip rounded-3xl border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-sidebar outline-none\",\n            className,\n          )}\n        >\n          <AriaAutocomplete\n            inputValue={inputValue}\n            onInputChange={(value) => {\n              setUncontrolledInput(value);\n              onInputChange?.(value);\n            }}\n            filter={filter === null ? undefined : filter ?? contains}\n          >\n            <AriaSearchField aria-label={label} autoFocus className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2 border-b border-separator-border p-3\">\n              <RiSearchLine className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" aria-hidden />\n              <AriaInput\n                placeholder={localize(placeholder)}\n                className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 bg-transparent text-body-medium text-text-primary outline-none placeholder:text-text-tertiary [&::-webkit-search-cancel-button]:hidden\"\n              />\n            </AriaSearchField>\n            <div className=\"relative flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col\">\n              <AriaMenu\n                aria-label={label}\n                onScroll={fade.onScroll}\n                onAction={(key) => {\n                  setOpen(false);\n                  onAction?.(key);\n                }}\n                renderEmptyState={() => (\n                  <div className=\"flex min-h-32 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-1 px-4 text-center text-body-medium text-text-tertiary\">\n                    {localize(emptyState)}\n                  </div>\n                )}\n                className=\"flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain p-2 outline-none [scrollbar-width:thin]\"\n              >\n                {children}\n              </AriaMenu>\n              <ScrollFade progress={fade.progress} />\n            </div>\n          </AriaAutocomplete>\n          {footer === false ? null : (\n            <div className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center justify-between border-t border-separator-border px-3 py-2 text-body-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n              {localize(\n                footer ?? (\n                  <>\n                    <span>Navigate with ↑ ↓</span>\n                    <span className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n                      Select\n                      <Kbd aria-label=\"Enter\">\n                        <RiCornerDownLeftLine className=\"size-3\" aria-hidden />\n                      </Kbd>\n                    </span>\n                  </>\n                ),\n              )}\n            </div>\n          )}\n        </AriaDialog>\n      </AriaModal>\n    </AriaModalOverlay>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The `window` keydown listener is removed by its effect cleanup on every re-render and on unmount. React Aria restores focus to the previously focused element when the modal closes and unmounts it after the exit transition.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The lift, scale and blur are behind `motion-safe:`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the panel only fades over 200ms and the scrim over 150ms.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not move DOM focus into the list. Autocomplete keeps focus in the field and moves a virtual highlight, so typing keeps working while arrowing through results.\n- Do not give the shortcut effect an empty dependency array. The listener would capture the first `isOpen` and ⌘K could open the palette but never close it.\n- Do not pass non-string children without `textValue`. The filter matches on `textValue`, which only falls back to children when they are a plain string.\n- Do not pass `filter={null}` and forget to filter the children yourself. It turns off matching entirely, which is meant for custom ranking.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The palette's own copy (label, placeholder, empty state, footer hints) runs through `useTemplateCopy()`, so a TemplateCopyProvider translates it.\n- Escape follows React Aria's search field: with text in the field it clears the query first, and a second Escape closes the palette.\n- The overlay is z-100, the same layer as Modal and Drawer.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPressing the search bar, or ⌘K / Ctrl+K anywhere, dims the page and brings the palette in 12vh from the top with the field focused. Typing \"set\" leaves only Settings; arrow keys move the grey highlight; Enter closes the palette and the page reads \"Ran Settings\". With a query typed, Escape clears it, a second Escape closes the palette and focus returns to the search bar. Clicking the scrim also closes it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "composer-attachments": "# Build: Composer Attachments\n\nThe strip of file tiles that sits above a chat prompt. Each 56px tile shows a coloured icon for the file type, the name and the size, with a dismiss in the corner. Give a tile a `progress` value and an accent ring draws clockwise around its rounded edge from top centre while a 9px percentage counts in the corner; when it reaches 100 the percentage blurs out and the dismiss blurs in on the same spot. `ComposerAttachButton` opens the real file picker and hands back `File` objects; only names and sizes are read.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\n56px attachment tiles for a chat composer: type icon, name, size and remove, with a ring that traces the tile edge while uploading.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `kindOf(name, mime)` maps the extension through a lookup table (png → image, xlsx → spreadsheet, tsx → code, and so on), then falls back to the MIME family (`image/`, `video/`, `audio/`), then to \"file\". Each kind has one Remix icon and one status text token, so a PDF is always rose and a spreadsheet always lime in both themes.\n2. `formatBytes` divides by 1024 until the value is under 1024 and prints one decimal below 10 and a whole number above: 812 B, 4.2 KB, 37 MB.\n3. The upload ring is one SVG `<path>` laid over the tile at `-inset-px`, so the 2px stroke sits on the 1px border. The path is the tile outline itself: a 54px rect with an 11px radius, starting at (28, 1) top centre and running clockwise. `pathLength={100}` normalises its length, so `strokeDashoffset = 100 - progress` draws exactly that percentage of the edge, with a 200ms transition between ticks.\n4. The top-right corner holds two layers: the percentage and the remove button. While `progress < 100` the percentage is visible and the button is at opacity 0 with a 2px blur; landing swaps the two classes, so one blurs out as the other blurs in. Hovering or focusing the tile (`group-hover`, `group-focus-within`) swaps them early, which turns the button into \"Cancel <name>\" for a file still uploading.\n5. `ComposerAttachButton` renders a normal `<button>` that calls `.click()` on a hidden `<input type=\"file\" multiple>`. On change it copies `files` into an array, then clears `value`, so picking the same file twice still fires `change`. `attachmentsFromFiles` turns the `File` list into `{ id, name, size, kind, progress }` without reading file contents.\n6. The strip is a horizontally scrolling `<ul>`. New tiles pop in from 50% scale with the kit `cell-pop` keyframes over 240ms, behind `motion-safe:`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `tile = 56px square, 12px radius, 1px composer-panel-tile-border`\n  Large enough for an 18px icon over two 11px text lines with 6px padding, small enough that five tiles fit above a 360px-wide prompt. The border token is neutral-200 in light and 14% white in dark, so tiles separate from the composer surface in both themes.\n- `RING_PATH = M28 1H44A11 11 0 0 1 55 12 … Z (54px rect, r 11)`\n  Centring a 2px stroke 1px inside the 56px box puts it exactly over the border. The radius drops from 12 to 11 because the stroke centre is 1px in from the outer curve. Starting at x=28 makes the ring begin at top centre, not at a corner.\n- `pathLength = 100`\n  The real path length of a rounded rect is awkward to compute. Normalising it to 100 lets progress go straight into `strokeDashoffset` with no measuring and no `getTotalLength()` call.\n- `corner swap = 200ms opacity + blur(2px)`\n  Blur alongside opacity makes the percentage and the dismiss read as one element changing, not two elements fighting for the corner.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ComposerAttachments(props: { attachments: ComposerAttachment[]; onRemove?: (id: string) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\nfunction ComposerAttachmentTile(props: { attachment: ComposerAttachment; onRemove?: (id: string) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ComposerAttachButton(props: ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> & { onFiles: (files: File[]) => void; accept?: string; multiple?: boolean; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction kindOf(name: string, mime?: string): AttachmentKind\nfunction formatBytes(bytes: number): string\nfunction attachmentsFromFiles(files: File[], idPrefix: string, progress?: number): ComposerAttachment[]\ninterface ComposerAttachment { id: string; name: string; size: number; kind?: AttachmentKind; progress?: number }\n```\n\n- `attachments: ComposerAttachment[]`\n  Tiles in order. An empty array renders nothing.\n- `attachment.progress: number`\n  0-100 draws the ring and the corner percentage. Omit it, or pass 100, for a landed file.\n- `attachment.kind: AttachmentKind`\n  Icon and colour. Derived from the name with `kindOf` when omitted.\n- `onRemove: (id: string) => void`\n  Shows the corner dismiss. Called with the attachment id for remove and for cancel.\n- `onFiles: (files: File[]) => void`\n  `ComposerAttachButton`: receives the picked files. Never called with an empty array.\n- `accept: string`\n  `ComposerAttachButton`: forwarded to the file input, e.g. \"image/*,.pdf\".\n- `multiple: boolean` — default `true`\n  `ComposerAttachButton`: allow picking several files at once.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the strip, tile or button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/*\n * The ring traces the tile's own rounded edge, starting at top centre and\n * running clockwise. Tile is 56px with a 12px radius; the stroke is centred\n * 1px in, so the path is a 54px rect with an 11px radius. pathLength=100 turns\n * progress straight into a dash offset.\n */\nconst RING_PATH =\n  \"M28 1H44A11 11 0 0 1 55 12V44A11 11 0 0 1 44 55H12A11 11 0 0 1 1 44V12A11 11 0 0 1 12 1Z\";\n\nfunction UploadRing({ progress }: { progress: number }) {\n  return (\n    <svg viewBox=\"0 0 56 56\" aria-hidden className=\"pointer-events-none absolute -inset-px size-14 overflow-visible\">\n      <path\n        d={RING_PATH}\n        pathLength={100}\n        fill=\"none\"\n        stroke=\"currentColor\"\n        strokeWidth={2}\n        strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n        strokeDasharray=\"100 100\"\n        strokeDashoffset={100 - progress}\n        className=\"text-accent-500 transition-[stroke-dashoffset] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n      />\n    </svg>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface ComposerAttachmentTileProps {\n  attachment: ComposerAttachment;\n  /** Removes a landed file, or cancels one still uploading. */\n  onRemove?: (id: string) => void;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function ComposerAttachmentTile({ attachment, onRemove, className }: ComposerAttachmentTileProps) {\n  const { id, name, size, progress } = attachment;\n  const kind = attachment.kind ?? kindOf(name);\n  const { icon: Icon, tone, label } = KIND_STYLE[kind];\n  const pct = progress === undefined ? 100 : Math.max(0, Math.min(100, Math.round(progress)));\n  const uploading = pct < 100;\n  // The corner holds the percentage while uploading and the dismiss once landed.\n  // Hover or keyboard focus swaps them early, so an upload can be cancelled.\n  const fade = \"transition-[opacity,filter] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\";\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      title={`${name}, ${formatBytes(size)}`}\n      data-uploading={uploading || undefined}\n      className={cx(\n        \"group relative flex size-14 shrink-0 flex-col justify-between rounded-xl border border-composer-panel-tile-border bg-background-primary-default p-1.5\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <Icon className={cx(\"size-[18px] shrink-0\", tone)} aria-hidden />\n      <span className=\"sr-only\">{label}:</span>\n      <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col\">\n        <span className=\"truncate text-[9px]/[11px] font-medium text-text-primary\">{name}</span>\n        <span className=\"truncate text-[9px]/[11px] text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n          {formatBytes(size)}\n          {uploading && <span className=\"sr-only\">, uploading {pct}%</span>}\n        </span>\n      </span>\n\n      {uploading && <UploadRing progress={pct} />}\n\n      <span\n        aria-hidden\n        className={cx(\n          \"pointer-events-none absolute end-1 top-1 text-[9px]/[11px] font-semibold text-accent-500 tabular-nums\",\n          fade,\n          uploading\n            ? \"opacity-100 blur-none group-focus-within:opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-0\"\n            : \"opacity-0 blur-[2px]\",\n        )}\n      >\n        {pct}%\n      </span>\n\n      {onRemove && (\n        <button\n          type=\"button\"\n          aria-label={uploading ? `Cancel ${name}` : `Remove ${name}`}\n          onClick={() => onRemove(id)}\n          className={cx(\n            \"absolute end-1 top-1 grid size-[18px] place-items-center rounded-full bg-background-tertiary-default text-foreground-icon-secondary outline-none hover:bg-background-tertiary-hover hover:text-foreground-icon-primary focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n            fade,\n            uploading\n              ? \"opacity-0 blur-[2px] group-focus-within:opacity-100 group-focus-within:blur-none group-hover:opacity-100 group-hover:blur-none\"\n              : \"opacity-100 blur-none\",\n          )}\n        >\n          <RiCloseLine className=\"size-3\" aria-hidden />\n        </button>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component holds no timers or listeners; the file input is part of the tree. The demo's upload interval is keyed to the active file id and cleared when that file lands, is removed, or the demo unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The ring's dash transition, the corner blur swap and the hover colours all carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and the pop-in animation is behind `motion-safe:`, so tiles appear in place and states switch instantly. The demo also skips the simulated upload and lands files at once.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Forgetting to clear the input `value` after reading it. Without that, picking the same file a second time fires no `change` event and the click looks broken.\n- Hiding the dismiss completely while uploading. A visitor who attached the wrong file must be able to cancel, so hover and keyboard focus reveal it early.\n- Using a `<circle>` or a CSS conic gradient for the ring. The tile is a rounded square, and a ring that does not follow its edge reads as a separate spinner.\n- Reading file contents or creating object URLs just to show a name. The tile needs `name`, `size` and `type`, which are on the `File` object already. Object URLs also need revoking.\n- Generating tile ids from the file name alone. The same file picked twice would collide. Prefix ids with a per-pick counter, as `attachmentsFromFiles(files, prefix)` expects.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The component never uploads. A real uploader updates `progress` on each attachment; the demo simulates that with a 40ms interval that moves one queued file at a time, so tiles land in order.\n- Image files show the image icon, not a thumbnail. Pass your own tile for thumbnails if you create object URLs.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThree tiles show a coloured type icon, a truncated name and a size. \"Add sample files\" queues three more that upload one after another: an accent ring draws clockwise from top centre and the corner counts up, then the percentage blurs into a dismiss. Hovering an uploading tile shows \"Cancel\". The + button opens the browser file picker and picked files appear as tiles with their real names and sizes. Clicking a dismiss removes that tile.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "composer-footer": "# Build: Composer Footer\n\nThe row under a composer prompt. On the left are a paperclip that opens the file picker, icon toggles for tools such as web search, and a slot for a model picker. A keyboard hint fills the middle, and on the right one round button sends, then turns into stop while a reply is generating, with an accent arc spinning around it. The footer owns no text field; the host passes `canSend`, `generating` and the two callbacks.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBottom bar of a chat composer: attach, tool toggles, a model slot and a key hint, with a send button that becomes stop while replying.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `SendStopButton` is a single React Aria `Button`. While `generating` it is labelled \"Stop generating\" and calls `onStop`; otherwise it is labelled \"Send\", calls `onSend`, and is disabled until `canSend` is true. Keeping one element means focus and the pointer stay put when the job flips.\n2. The arrow and the stop square share one grid cell (`[grid-area:1/1]`). The one not in use goes to `scale-50 opacity-0 blur-[2px]` and the other to full size, over a 200ms transition, so the icons cross-fade instead of jumping.\n3. An SVG circle sits 4px outside the button. Its arc is `strokeDasharray=\"28 100\"` on `pathLength={100}`, and while generating the SVG fades in and gets `motion-safe:animate-spin`. A visually hidden `role=\"status\"` span says \"Generating a reply\" for screen readers.\n4. Tools are React Aria `ToggleButton`s, so each one exposes `aria-pressed`. The set of active ids is controlled through `activeTools` / `onToolsChange`, or kept in local state seeded from `defaultActiveTools`. Pressed tools take the `status-blue` background and text tokens.\n5. The paperclip is a React Aria `Button` that clicks a hidden `<input type=\"file\" multiple>`. Picked files go to `onAttach` as `File[]`, and the input is cleared so the same file can be picked again. Nothing is read or uploaded.\n6. The root is a container (`@container`). The hint paragraph carries `@max-[34rem]:hidden`, so in a narrow composer it disappears before the buttons have to shrink. The model slot is `min-w-0 shrink`, so a long model name truncates instead of pushing send off the edge.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `send button = 36px, arc ring 44px`\n  The ring sits 4px outside the button, which leaves a visible gap. The arc covers 28% of the circle, enough to read as spinning without looking like a progress bar.\n- `hint breakpoint = container width 34rem`\n  Below this, the hint would truncate into fragments like \"Enter to se…\". Hiding it entirely keeps the controls intact at phone widths.\n- `tooltip delay = 300ms`\n  The kit tooltip defaults to no delay. In a row of five icon buttons, that makes tooltips flash while the pointer is moving across to send.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ComposerFooter(props: { onAttach?: (files: File[]) => void; accept?: string; tools?: ComposerTool[]; activeTools?: string[]; defaultActiveTools?: string[]; onToolsChange?: (ids: string[]) => void; model?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; canSend?: boolean; generating?: boolean; onSend?: () => void; onStop?: () => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface ComposerTool { id: string; label: string; icon: RemixiconComponentType }\n```\n\n- `onAttach: (files: File[]) => void`\n  Shows the paperclip and receives picked files.\n- `accept: string`\n  Forwarded to the file input.\n- `tools: ComposerTool[]` — default `COMPOSER_TOOLS`\n  Icon toggles after the paperclip. Pass `[]` to hide them.\n- `activeTools: string[]`\n  Controlled ids of switched-on tools.\n- `defaultActiveTools: string[]` — default `[]`\n  Starting ids when uncontrolled.\n- `onToolsChange: (ids: string[]) => void`\n  Called with the new set after a toggle.\n- `model: ReactNode`\n  Slot after a divider, usually a ghost `ModelSelect`.\n- `hint: ReactNode`\n  Overrides the default hint (\"Enter to send…\" or \"Writing a reply…\").\n- `canSend: boolean` — default `false`\n  Enables send when not generating.\n- `generating: boolean` — default `false`\n  Turns send into stop and spins the ring.\n- `onSend: () => void`\n  Called when send is pressed.\n- `onStop: () => void`\n  Called when stop is pressed. Without it the stop button is disabled.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the footer row.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction SendStopButton({\n  generating,\n  canSend,\n  onSend,\n  onStop,\n}: Pick<ComposerFooterProps, \"generating\" | \"canSend\" | \"onSend\" | \"onStop\">) {\n  // One button, two jobs. While generating it is always pressable and stops;\n  // otherwise it sends and is disabled until there is something to send.\n  const isDisabled = generating ? !onStop : !canSend;\n  const swap = \"[grid-area:1/1] transition-[opacity,scale,filter] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\";\n  const shown = \"scale-100 opacity-100 blur-none\";\n  const hidden = \"scale-50 opacity-0 blur-[2px]\";\n\n  return (\n    <span className=\"relative grid shrink-0 place-items-center\">\n      <svg\n        aria-hidden\n        viewBox=\"0 0 44 44\"\n        className={cx(\n          \"pointer-events-none absolute -inset-1 size-11 text-accent-500 transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          generating ? \"opacity-100 motion-safe:animate-spin\" : \"opacity-0\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <circle cx=\"22\" cy=\"22\" r=\"20.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" strokeWidth=\"2\" strokeLinecap=\"round\" pathLength={100} strokeDasharray=\"28 100\" />\n      </svg>\n      <AriaButton\n        aria-label={generating ? \"Stop generating\" : \"Send\"}\n        isDisabled={isDisabled}\n        onPress={() => (generating ? onStop?.() : onSend?.())}\n        className={cx(\n          \"grid size-9 cursor-pointer place-items-center rounded-full bg-button-primary text-text-white shadow-xs outline-none\",\n          \"transition-[scale] duration-150 ease-out data-[pressed]:scale-95 motion-reduce:transition-none motion-reduce:data-[pressed]:scale-100\",\n          \"data-[focus-visible]:ring-2 data-[focus-visible]:ring-border-focus-ring data-[focus-visible]:ring-offset-2\",\n          \"disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:text-button-primary-disabled-foreground\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <RiArrowUpLine aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-[18px]\", swap, generating ? hidden : shown)} />\n        <RiStopFill aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-3.5\", swap, generating ? shown : hidden)} />\n      </AriaButton>\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The footer has no timers or listeners of its own; React Aria owns the press, toggle and tooltip handlers. The demo clears its streaming interval on completion, on stop and on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The ring keeps its arc but does not spin (`motion-safe:animate-spin`). The icon cross-fade, the ring fade and the press scale use `motion-reduce:transition-none` and `motion-reduce:data-[pressed]:scale-100`, so states switch instantly. Tooltips follow the kit's own reduced-motion rule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Rendering separate send and stop buttons. Swapping them unmounts the focused button, so keyboard focus drops to the page body mid-reply.\n- Disabling the button while generating. Stop must stay pressable, so the disabled check only applies when not generating.\n- Expecting the footer to send on Enter. It has no text field. The default hint describes the usual wiring, which the host does in its textarea's `onKeyDown`; pass `hint` if your keys differ.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The demo streams a canned reply one word every 55ms from an interval that is cleared on completion, on stop and on unmount. Escape in the prompt also stops it.\n- The model slot accepts any node. The demo passes a ghost `ModelSelect`, and attachments render above the prompt with `ComposerAttachments`. Below a 26rem container the demo hides the model name (`@max-[26rem]:[&_[data-model-name]]:sr-only`) so only the monogram shows, and the footer tightens its gap from 4px to 2px.\n- The tool list is sample data: Search the web, Run code and Think longer. Pass `tools` with your own `{ id, label, icon }` entries.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSend stays disabled until text is typed. Sending clears the prompt, the button turns into a stop square inside a spinning accent arc, and the reply streams in. Pressing stop, or Escape in the prompt, ends it and marks it Stopped. Letting it finish turns the button back into send. Tool toggles report pressed state, the paperclip opens the file picker, and the hint is hidden in narrow composers.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "composer-loader": "# Build: Composer Loader\n\nA wrapper that lights up a chat composer while a reply is on its way. It paints the pill surface itself, then runs a band of light around the rim above it: a crisp line, a tight glow and a wide bloom, all dash segments on one SVG rounded-rect stroke. The outer half of each stroke is clipped by the pill, so the glow only bleeds inward. Flipping `active` fades the whole effect in or out over 450ms.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nWraps a chat composer while the agent works: an iridescent band orbits the pill rim with a soft bloom, fading in and out.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `ResizeObserver` on the clipping layer keeps `box` (default 640×52) in sync with the rendered size, and the SVG uses that as its `viewBox` with `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"`, so stroke widths and blur radii stay in real pixels.\n2. Each layer is a `<rect>` with `rx = radius ?? h / 2` and `pathLength={100}`, so the dash is measured in percent of the perimeter and travels at constant speed around the end caps. `strokeDasharray` is `len (100 − len)` with `band = arc / 360 × 100`, and the theme's `bui-composer-loader-dash` keyframes move `stroke-dashoffset` from 0 to −100 once per `speed` seconds.\n3. Three layers share one horizontal `linearGradient` (c0, c1, the RGB average of c1 and c2, c2, c3): a wide bloom (`bloom × 2` wide, 14px blur, 0.9 × band long), a tight glow (`line × 3.2`, 6px blur, 0.95 × band) and the crisp line (`line`, 0.5px blur, full band).\n4. The softer layers are shorter, and their heads are pulled back behind the tip. The perimeter (`2(w + h) − 8r + 2πr`) converts pixels to path units, and each layer's phase becomes its `animation-delay` (at typical sizes a negative one, a head start) to line the heads up: the glow 10px behind the line, the bloom `bloom + 16`px behind.\n5. Blurs are SVG `feGaussianBlur` filters (regions widened to 200%) rather than CSS `filter`, because WebKit ignores CSS filters on individual SVG elements.\n6. With `taper > 0`, each layer is drawn as 14 centred copies, each shorter by `taper / 14` and at 1/14 of the opacity, so the two ends ramp up in steps that the blur (raised to `max(0.5, line × 0.8)` on the crisp line) melts together.\n7. `active` toggles the clipping layer's opacity between 1 and 0 with a 450ms ease transition. Children render in a `relative` layer above the light.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DEFAULT_COLORS = #5eead4, #46baec, #e633a4, #00faa7`\n  Teal, sky, magenta and mint spread left to right; the middle stop averages sky and magenta, so the band never washes out to white.\n- `speed / arc = 4.5s per lap / 120°`\n  A third of the rim lit at once, moving slowly enough to read as ambient rather than as a progress bar.\n- `line / bloom / bloomStrength / intensity = 2.5px / 16px / 0.3 / 0.7`\n  A thin bright edge with a soft inward wash, held under full opacity so it sits on the surface instead of glaring.\n- `blur radii = 14, 6, 0.5 (stdDeviation)`\n  One per layer: the wide bloom, the tight glow and a hint of softness on the crisp line.\n- `head pull-back = glow 10px, bloom `bloom + 16`px`\n  The sharp line leads and the soft light trails it, like a highlight sliding along glass.\n- `TAPER_STEPS = 14`\n  Enough copies that the stepped ramp at each end blurs into a gradient.\n- `fade = opacity 450ms ease`\n  Toggling on send and reply reads as the composer lighting up rather than snapping.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ComposerLoader(props: { children: ReactNode; active?: boolean; colors?: [string, string, string, string]; speed?: number; intensity?: number; bloom?: number; bloomStrength?: number; arc?: number; reverse?: boolean; radius?: number; line?: number; bloomOnly?: boolean; surface?: boolean; taper?: number; blend?: CSSProperties[\"mixBlendMode\"]; offset?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The composer. Rendered above the light, so keep it transparent while active.\n- `active: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the light; fades in and out over 450ms.\n- `colors: [string, string, string, string]` — default `teal, sky, magenta, mint`\n  Four `#rrggbb` colours spread across the pill from left to right.\n- `speed: number` — default `4.5`\n  Seconds per full lap.\n- `arc: number` — default `120`\n  Length of the band, in degrees of 360.\n- `intensity: number` — default `0.7`\n  Overall opacity of the light.\n- `line: number` — default `2.5`\n  Width of the crisp line in px; the tight glow is 3.2 times this.\n- `bloom / bloomStrength: number` — default `16 / 0.3`\n  How far the bloom bleeds inward in px, and its opacity.\n- `bloomOnly: boolean` — default `false`\n  Draw only the wide bloom, as a soft wash.\n- `taper: number` — default `0`\n  Fraction of the band that ramps at each end, 0 to 1.\n- `reverse: boolean` — default `false`\n  Travel counter-clockwise.\n- `radius: number`\n  Corner radius in px; omit for a full pill.\n- `surface: boolean` — default `true`\n  Paint the pill surface (primary background, xs shadow) behind the light.\n- `blend: CSSProperties[\"mixBlendMode\"]`\n  How the light composites over what is beneath it.\n- `offset: number` — default `0`\n  Moves the band forward by a fraction of the perimeter.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst band = (arc / 360) * 100;\nconst rx = radius ?? box.h / 2;\nconst cornerRadius = radius ?? 9999;\nconst direction = reverse ? \"reverse\" : \"normal\";\n\n// Path units per px, for pulling each soft layer's head behind the tip.\n// Rounded-rect perimeter: straight runs plus the four corner arcs (for the\n// default pill, rx = h/2 and this reduces to 2(w−h) + πh).\nconst cornerR = Math.min(rx, box.w / 2, box.h / 2);\nconst perimeter = Math.max(1, 2 * (box.w + box.h) - 8 * cornerR + 2 * Math.PI * cornerR);\nconst pxUnits = (px: number) => (px * 100) / perimeter;\n// A layer's delay is its phase: the larger the phase, the further BACK\n// along the lap the band sits (a negative delay is a head start). So\n// moving a band forward, `offset` included, means subtracting.\nconst dashPhase = (len: number, backPx: number) =>\n  (reverse ? pxUnits(backPx) : len - band + pxUnits(backPx)) - offset * 100;\n\n// Tapering stacks each layer as shorter, centred copies: the ramp at each\n// end is a staircase with one step per copy, so it takes many copies and\n// a little blur on the crisp line for the steps to melt into a gradient.\nconst TAPER_STEPS = 14;\nconst CRISP_BLUR = taper > 0 ? Math.max(0.5, line * 0.8) : 0.5;\nconst stroke = (width: number, blur: number, opacity: number, dashLen: number, backPx: number) => {\n  const copies = taper > 0 ? TAPER_STEPS : 1;\n  return Array.from({ length: copies }, (_, index) => {\n    // Copy 0 is the full band; each next one is shorter and moved forward\n    // by half the difference, so all of them share one midpoint and the\n    // ends thin out step by step.\n    const len = dashLen * (1 - (taper * index) / copies);\n    const phase = dashPhase(dashLen, backPx) - (dashLen - len) / 2;\n    return (\n      <rect\n        key={index}\n        x={0}\n        y={0}\n        width={box.w}\n        height={box.h}\n        rx={rx}\n        pathLength={100}\n        fill=\"none\"\n        stroke={`url(#${gradientId})`}\n        strokeWidth={width}\n        strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n        strokeDasharray={`${len} ${100 - len}`}\n        className=\"bui-composer-loader-rect\"\n        // SVG-native blur, not CSS filter: WebKit (iOS Safari and every iPhone\n        // browser) ignores CSS filters on individual SVG elements, which\n        // rendered the whole light razor-sharp on phones.\n        filter={blur > 0 ? `url(#${gradientId}-b${String(blur).replace(\".\", \"_\")})` : undefined}\n        style={{\n          opacity: opacity / copies,\n          animation: `bui-composer-loader-dash ${speed}s linear ${(phase * speed) / 100}s infinite ${direction}`,\n        }}\n      />\n    );\n  });\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The `ResizeObserver` is disconnected on unmount. The band is a CSS animation and stops with the element. Nothing else is scheduled.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The theme sets `animation: none !important` on every `.bui-composer-loader-rect` under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, which overrides the inline animation. The band then rests as a static glow at the start of the rect path, the top edge, near the top-left corner. The 450ms opacity fade on `active` still runs, since it is a state change rather than a loop.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- The wrapped composer must not paint its own background while `active`. Its children render above the light, so an opaque form covers the band completely. Switch it to `bg-transparent` while busy and let `surface` paint the pill.\n- `colors` must be four `#rrggbb` strings. The middle gradient stop is computed by parsing hex pairs, so CSS variables, `rgb()` or short hex give an invalid stop colour.\n- Pass `radius` when the composer is not a full pill. Without it the stroke uses `h / 2` and the clipping layer uses 9999px, which only match a pill shape.\n- Each layer is wrapped in an SVG blur and animates constantly, so the light costs a repaint every frame while it is visible. Set `active={false}` when nothing is pending, rather than leaving it on at low intensity.\n- The first paint uses a 640×52 box until the `ResizeObserver` reports, so a very different size may show a slightly mis-scaled band for one frame.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `offset` moves the band forward by a fraction of the lap, so two loaders on one pill can line up: a short beam with `offset` equal to the difference of the two arcs leads a wider glow tip to tip.\n- `blend` sets `mix-blend-mode` on the light layer, for example `screen` to read as light on a dark surface.\n- BoardUI's free `agent-composer` uses this exactly: `active={busy}` around a 52px form that turns transparent while the agent is busy.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith `active` on, a band covering about a third of the rim circles the composer pill once every 4.5s, turning smoothly around both rounded ends. The sharp gradient line leads, with a softer glow and a wide wash trailing slightly behind it, and the glow appears only inside the pill. Setting `active` to false fades it out over about 450ms; setting it back fades it in. Resizing the composer keeps the line at the same thickness and the same lap time. Under reduced motion the band holds still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "composer-panel": "# Build: Composer Panel\n\nThe taller composer for agent surfaces. A radius-24 card holds the prompt on top and the controls underneath: a round + that opens the file picker, a permission picker (Auto, Manual, Plan mode, Bypass all), a model menu, an optional mic and send. A 34px grey status tab can hang off the top edge with the branch, the project folder and a 16px context ring. Send is live only when there is text; Enter sends and Shift+Enter breaks the line.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTwo-row agent composer: a growing prompt over a toolbar with add, permission mode, model and send, plus a status tab with a context ring.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The prompt is a one-row `<textarea>`. A layout effect on every draft change sets `height: auto`, reads `scrollHeight`, and writes back `min(scrollHeight, 10 lines × 20px + 20px padding)`. Resetting to auto first lets the field shrink when lines are deleted; past ten lines it stops growing and scrolls.\n2. `onKeyDown` sends on Enter unless Shift is held or `nativeEvent.isComposing` is true, so an IME confirming a word never sends half a message. Shift+Enter falls through to the textarea's own newline. The form's submit handler calls the same `send()`.\n3. `send()` returns early unless the trimmed draft is non-empty and `disabled` is false. It calls `onSubmit({ text, attachments, model, permission })`, clears the draft and the attachment strip, and puts focus back in the field. The send button is `disabled` by the same check, and the kit `bg-button-primary` utility paints its own grey disabled gradient.\n4. The + is a `ComposerAttachButton` from Composer Attachments: a button that clicks a hidden `<input type=\"file\" multiple>`. Picked files become tiles through `attachmentsFromFiles`, each batch prefixed by a counter so the same file picked twice gets a new id. Tiles render in a `ComposerAttachments` strip above the prompt, and their dismiss removes them.\n5. The permission and model pickers are kit `Dropdown`s with controlled `isOpen`, opening upward (`top start` and `top end`). Each row is a `DropdownItem` with a label, a one-line description and a check on the current choice; choosing a row sets state and closes the menu. The permission trigger shows its surface only on hover, press or while open, so at rest it reads as a label.\n6. `ComposerStatusTab` is a separate element passed through the `status` slot, inset 28px (`mx-7`) with top corners rounded. Its ring is two SVG circles rotated -90°; the arc uses `pathLength={100}` and `strokeDasharray=\"<pct> 100\"`, so the percentage maps straight onto the dash. It is exposed as `role=\"meter\"` with `aria-valuenow`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MAX_LINES = 10`\n  Long enough for a pasted paragraph or a short stack trace without the toolbar leaving the screen; beyond that the field scrolls.\n- `LINE_HEIGHT / FIELD_PADDING_Y = 20px / 20px`\n  text-body-regular is 14/20, and the textarea carries pt-3.5 + pb-1.5. `scrollHeight` includes padding, so the cap must add it or the tenth line is clipped.\n- `status tab = 34px high, 28px inset, composer-panel-tab-background`\n  Inset more than the card's 24px radius, so the tab meets the flat part of the top edge and never the curve. The token is neutral-200 in light and half-opacity neutral-700 in dark, so it separates from the card in both themes.\n- `card radius = 24px (rounded-3xl)`\n  Close to concentric with the round 36px controls in the 8px-padded toolbar (18 + 8 = 26px), so the + and send sit evenly inside the curve. A smaller radius leaves a visible gap at the corners.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ComposerPanel(props: { placeholder?: string; status?: ReactNode; models?: ComposerModel[]; defaultModel?: string; defaultPermission?: ComposerPermission; onSubmit?: (message: ComposerPanelMessage) => void; disabled?: boolean; onVoice?: () => void; voiceActive?: boolean; accept?: string; inputRef?: RefObject<HTMLTextAreaElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ComposerStatusTab(props: { branch?: string; project?: string; context?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface ComposerPanelMessage { text: string; attachments: ComposerAttachment[]; model: ComposerModel; permission: ComposerPermission }\ntype ComposerPermission = \"auto\" | \"manual\" | \"plan\" | \"bypass\"\n```\n\n- `placeholder: string` — default `\"Hi, what do you need today?\"`\n  Prompt placeholder.\n- `status: ReactNode`\n  Slot above the card, usually a `ComposerStatusTab`. Omit for the bare card.\n- `models: ComposerModel[]` — default `COMPOSER_MODELS`\n  Rows of the model menu: `{ id, name, description? }`.\n- `defaultModel: string`\n  Model id to start on. Defaults to the first model.\n- `defaultPermission: ComposerPermission` — default `\"auto\"`\n  Starting permission mode.\n- `onSubmit: (message: ComposerPanelMessage) => void`\n  Receives the trimmed text, attachments, model and permission. The panel clears itself after.\n- `disabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Greys out send while a turn is in flight. Typing stays possible.\n- `onVoice: () => void`\n  Shows the mic button and is called when it is pressed.\n- `voiceActive: boolean` — default `false`\n  Pressed state of the mic button.\n- `accept: string`\n  Forwarded to the file input, e.g. \"image/*,.pdf\".\n- `inputRef: RefObject<HTMLTextAreaElement | null>`\n  Reaches the prompt field, e.g. to focus it.\n- `context: number`\n  `ComposerStatusTab`: context window used, 0-100, drawn as a 16px ring and a percentage.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Grow with the content a line at a time, then cap and scroll. Resetting to\n// \"auto\" first lets the field shrink again when lines are deleted.\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\n  const el = fieldRef.current;\n  if (!el) return;\n  el.style.height = \"auto\";\n  el.style.height = `${Math.min(el.scrollHeight, MAX_LINES * LINE_HEIGHT + FIELD_PADDING_Y)}px`;\n}, [draft, fieldRef]);\n\nconst send = () => {\n  if (!canSend || !model) return;\n  onSubmit?.({ text: draft.trim(), attachments, model, permission });\n  setDraft(\"\");\n  setAttachments([]);\n  fieldRef.current?.focus();\n};\n\n// Enter sends; Shift+Enter falls through to the textarea's own newline.\n// isComposing keeps an IME's confirming Enter from sending half a word.\nconst onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLTextAreaElement>) => {\n  if (e.key !== \"Enter\" || e.shiftKey || e.nativeEvent.isComposing) return;\n  e.preventDefault();\n  send();\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The panel holds no timers, listeners or observers. The textarea height is set inline in a layout effect and recomputed on each change; the dropdowns clean up through React Aria.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Hover colours, the chevron flip and the send press scale carry `motion-reduce:transition-none` (and `motion-reduce:active:scale-100`), and the attachment pop-in is behind `motion-safe:`, so states change instantly. The kit dropdown handles its own open animation.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Measuring `scrollHeight` without first setting `height: auto`. The field can then grow but never shrink back after text is deleted.\n- Sending on every Enter. During IME composition (Japanese, Chinese, Korean) Enter confirms the candidate; check `isComposing` or the half-typed word is sent.\n- Leaving the dropdowns uncontrolled. Kit `DropdownItem` is a plain button and does not close its popover; the panel closes each menu in `onSelect`.\n- Painting a background over the disabled send button. `bg-button-primary` already has a disabled gradient; overriding it breaks dark mode. Only the icon colour changes.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Attachments live inside the panel and are handed to `onSubmit`, then cleared. Nothing is uploaded; only names and sizes are read from the picked files.\n- The mic button appears only when `onVoice` is passed. The panel does no speech recognition; `voiceActive` only sets its pressed look.\n- The demo lists what was sent with the model and permission each message went out with, and moves the context ring up by 6% per turn.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA status tab with branch, folder and a context ring sits on top of a white card. Send stays grey until text is typed. Typing several lines grows the field; Shift+Enter adds a line and Enter sends. The demo lists the sent message with its model and permission mode and the ring moves up. The permission picker lists four modes with descriptions and a check, the model menu switches the label, and + opens the browser file picker.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "compound-group-hover": "# Build: Compound Group Hover Card\n\nA content card that answers a hover with a short sequence rather than a single state flip: it lifts, its icon tilts and overshoots its scale, a corner blob brightens, and the affordance arrow slides in — each on its own duration and delay. The trigger is `:hover, :has(:focus-visible)`, so a keyboard user tabbing to the link inside gets exactly the same four changes.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne hover drives four changes on three elements, each with its own duration and curve. Keyboard gets all four.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The card owns `translate` and `border-color` on a 260ms `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`. It is the fastest change in the set because it is the one that acknowledges the pointer — anything slower and the card feels like it is deciding whether to respond.\n2. The icon animates `rotate` over 320ms on the same ease and `scale` over 420ms on `cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1)`, both delayed 40ms. Two curves on one element is only possible because `rotate` and `scale` are independent properties; packed into `transform` they would share a single duration and a single easing, and the overshoot would drag the rotation with it.\n3. The corner blob is a radial gradient that is blurred once at paint time (`filter: blur(2rem)`) and thereafter only changes `opacity` 0.12 to 0.4 over 500ms and `scale` 1 to 1.15 over 700ms. It is the slowest element in the group, which is what makes it read as lighting rather than as a moving object.\n4. `z-index: -1` on the blob places it above the card background and below the card content with no extra wrapper, and `isolation: isolate` on the card stops that negative index from escaping into the page stacking context and sliding behind an ancestor background.\n5. The arrow translates 4px and goes from 0.4 to 1 opacity after an 80ms delay — last in the sequence, so the eye arrives at the call to action after the card has already committed to the hover.\n6. The whole card is clickable through a stretched pseudo-element on the anchor (`::after { position: absolute; inset: 0 }`), so there is exactly one focusable, screen-reader-announced target. The card responds to that anchor's focus through `:has(:focus-visible)`, which is what gives keyboard parity without a single line of JavaScript.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `lift = -6px`\n  Card travel on hover. Under 3px it is not distinguishable from a rendering shift; over about 10px the card visibly overlaps its neighbour in a tight grid and the shadow it does not have becomes conspicuous.\n- `durations = 260 / 320 / 420 / 500 / 700 ms`\n  Ascending by element weight: card, icon rotation, icon scale, blob opacity, blob scale. Equal durations collapse the sequence into a single snap; the spread is the entire effect. The 260ms floor is roughly the shortest a positional change can be and still be perceived as movement rather than a jump.\n- `delays = 40ms icon, 80ms arrow`\n  Small enough to feel like consequence rather than queueing. Past about 120ms the arrow reads as a second, separate event and a fast pointer pass leaves it half-animated.\n- `icon scale = 1.1 with cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1)`\n  The 1.56 control point overshoots to roughly 1.13 before settling. Overshoot on a small element reads as physical; on the card itself it would read as a bug, which is why only the icon gets it.\n- `blob opacity range = 0.12 to 0.40`\n  It must be visible at rest — a blob that appears only on hover reads as a new object arriving, not as a light coming up. 0.12 is enough to establish it; past about 0.5 the accent starts tinting the body copy.\n- `icon rotation = -6deg`\n  Anticlockwise, because the arrow moves right and the two would otherwise both push the same direction and read as a shear. Past about 10deg a glyph-based icon starts to look misaligned rather than tilted.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CompoundGroupHover(props: { title: string; description: string; eyebrow?: string; icon?: ReactNode; href?: string; accent?: string; action?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: string`\n  Card heading, rendered as an `h3`.\n- `description: string`\n  Body copy under the heading.\n- `eyebrow: string`\n  Small uppercase label above the title.\n- `icon: ReactNode`\n  Rendered inside the tilting badge. An inline SVG or a single character.\n- `href: string`\n  Renders the affordance link and stretches it over the whole card.\n- `accent: string` — default `'var(--color-accent, currentColor)'`\n  Drives the blob, the icon border and the link colour.\n- `action: string` — default `'Read more'`\n  Text of the affordance link.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card via `cn`. Use it for sizing inside a grid.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst HOVER_CARD_CSS = `\n.kx-hcard {\n  --kx-hcard-ease: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);\n  --kx-hcard-overshoot: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);\n\n  position: relative;\n  isolation: isolate;\n  overflow: hidden;\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  gap: 0.75rem;\n  border: 1px solid var(--color-line, currentColor);\n  border-radius: 12px;\n  background-color: var(--color-surface, transparent);\n\n  translate: 0 0;\n  transition:\n    translate 260ms var(--kx-hcard-ease),\n    border-color 260ms linear;\n}\n\n.kx-hcard:hover,\n.kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) {\n  translate: 0 var(--kx-hcard-lift, -6px);\n  border-color: var(--color-line-strong, currentColor);\n}\n\n/* Icon: two properties, two curves. The rotation settles cleanly; the scale\n   overshoots slightly and comes back, which is what reads as \"picked up\". */\n.kx-hcard-icon {\n  display: inline-flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n  width: 2.25rem;\n  height: 2.25rem;\n  border-radius: 9px;\n  border: 1px solid var(--color-line, currentColor);\n  color: var(--kx-hcard-accent);\n  rotate: 0deg;\n  scale: 1;\n  transition:\n    rotate 320ms var(--kx-hcard-ease) 40ms,\n    scale 420ms var(--kx-hcard-overshoot) 40ms,\n    border-color 260ms linear;\n}\n\n.kx-hcard:hover .kx-hcard-icon,\n.kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) .kx-hcard-icon {\n  rotate: -6deg;\n  scale: 1.1;\n  border-color: var(--kx-hcard-accent);\n}\n\n/* The blob is blurred ONCE, at paint. Only opacity and scale animate, so the\n   hover never re-runs a 2rem blur. Negative z-index puts it above the card\n   background and below the card content without a wrapper. */\n.kx-hcard-blob {\n  position: absolute;\n  right: -4rem;\n  bottom: -4rem;\n  width: 14rem;\n  height: 14rem;\n  border-radius: 9999px;\n  background: radial-gradient(circle, var(--kx-hcard-accent) 0%, transparent 70%);\n  filter: blur(2rem);\n  opacity: 0.12;\n  scale: 1;\n  pointer-events: none;\n  z-index: -1;\n  transition:\n    opacity 500ms var(--kx-hcard-ease),\n    scale 700ms var(--kx-hcard-ease);\n}\n\n.kx-hcard:hover .kx-hcard-blob,\n.kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) .kx-hcard-blob {\n  opacity: 0.4;\n  scale: 1.15;\n}\n\n.kx-hcard-arrow {\n  display: inline-block;\n  translate: 0 0;\n  opacity: 0.4;\n  transition:\n    translate 300ms var(--kx-hcard-ease) 80ms,\n    opacity 220ms linear 80ms;\n}\n\n.kx-hcard:hover .kx-hcard-arrow,\n.kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) .kx-hcard-arrow {\n  translate: 4px 0;\n  opacity: 1;\n}\n\n/* Stretched link: the anchor stays the only focusable, screen-reader-visible\n   target, and its pseudo-element makes the whole card the hit area. */\n.kx-hcard-link {\n  color: var(--kx-hcard-accent);\n}\n\n.kx-hcard-link::after {\n  content: \"\";\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;\n  border-radius: inherit;\n}\n\n/* No motion: keep every change that carries meaning — the border and icon\n   brighten, the blob comes up, the arrow reaches full opacity — and drop the\n   three that are purely positional. Hover still visibly does something. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-hcard:hover,\n  .kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) {\n    translate: 0 0;\n  }\n  .kx-hcard:hover .kx-hcard-icon,\n  .kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) .kx-hcard-icon {\n    rotate: 0deg;\n    scale: 1;\n  }\n  .kx-hcard:hover .kx-hcard-arrow,\n  .kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) .kx-hcard-arrow {\n    translate: 0 0;\n  }\n  .kx-hcard:hover .kx-hcard-blob,\n  .kx-hcard:has(:focus-visible) .kx-hcard-blob {\n    scale: 1;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport function CompoundGroupHover({\n  title,\n  description,\n  eyebrow,\n  icon,\n  href,\n  accent = 'var(--color-accent, currentColor)',\n  action = 'Read more',\n  className,\n}: CompoundGroupHoverProps) {\n  const style: StyleWithVars = { '--kx-hcard-accent': accent };\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-compound-group-hover\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {HOVER_CARD_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <article className={cn('kx-hcard p-6', className)} style={style}>\n        <span className=\"kx-hcard-blob\" aria-hidden=\"true\" />\n\n        {icon ? (\n          <span className=\"kx-hcard-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n            {icon}\n          </span>\n        ) : null}\n\n        {eyebrow ? <span className=\"meta\">{eyebrow}</span> : null}\n\n        <h3 className=\"text-base tracking-tight text-fg\">{title}</h3>\n        <p className=\"text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted\">{description}</p>\n\n        {href ? (\n          <a href={href} className=\"kx-hcard-link mt-auto pt-2 text-xs tracking-tight\">\n            {action}\n            <span className=\"kx-hcard-arrow ml-1.5\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n              &rarr;\n            </span>\n          </a>\n        ) : null}\n      </article>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. No listeners, no timers, no animation frames; the hover state lives entirely in the cascade. React removes the hoisted `<style>` when the last card unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block cancels the three positional changes — the card no longer lifts, the icon no longer tilts or scales, the arrow no longer slides — while keeping every non-positional one. The border still brightens, the blob still comes up from 0.12 to 0.4 opacity, and the arrow still reaches full opacity, so hover and keyboard focus remain visibly acknowledged.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Packing the icon lift into `transform: rotate(-6deg) scale(1.1)`. It works, but both halves are now one property with one duration — the moment you want the scale to overshoot and the rotation not to, you have to split them, and splitting `transform` mid-project means rewriting every rule that touches it.\n- Transitioning `filter: blur()` on the blob instead of `opacity`. Every frame re-runs a 2rem blur over a 14rem box; on a grid of six cards a single pointer sweep drops the page into the teens.\n- Making the card itself a `<div role=\"link\" tabindex=\"0\">` to get a focus state. It is not announced as a link, it does not middle-click, it does not open in a new tab, and it needs a keydown handler. The stretched `::after` on a real anchor gives all of that for one pseudo-element.\n- Using `:focus-within` instead of `:has(:focus-visible)`. `:focus-within` fires on mouse clicks too, so the card sticks in its hover state after every click and only releases when something else is focused.\n- Omitting `isolation: isolate` on the card. The blob's `z-index: -1` then resolves against whichever ancestor last created a stacking context and the blob disappears behind a section background — intermittently, depending on what else is on the page.\n- Leaving all four changes in place under reduced motion. Lift, tilt and slide are motion; the border, the blob and the arrow opacity are not. Disabling the whole hover state is over-correction and leaves the card with no feedback at all.\n- Forgetting `overflow: hidden` on the card. The blob is positioned 4rem outside the card on two sides and paints over the neighbouring card in a grid.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `:has()` needs Safari 15.4+, Chrome 105+ and Firefox 121+. Where it is unsupported the pointer hover still works exactly as written and the keyboard path degrades to the browser's own focus ring on the link.\n- Independent `translate` / `rotate` / `scale` need Chrome 104+, Safari 14.1+, Firefox 72+. In older engines the properties are dropped and the card still changes colour and opacity on hover.\n- The accent is one custom property, `--kx-hcard-accent`, read by the blob, the icon and the link. A grid of these differs only by that value.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover the card and four things change on four different timings — you can see the arrow arrive after the card has already lifted. Tab to the link with the keyboard and the identical four changes happen. Click the card anywhere, including on the description text, and the link activates. Turn reduced motion on: nothing moves, but the border, blob and arrow still respond.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "conic-property-border": "# Build: Conic @property Border\n\nA rounded box wearing an animated conic-gradient border. Two bright stops travel around the ring in opposite quadrants, so the ring reads as rotating rather than blinking. The whole thing is one element and one keyframe — the only moving part is a registered custom property holding an angle, which the browser interpolates for you.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA gradient ring that rotates around a rounded box. Two highlights chase each other; no JavaScript runs.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `@property --kx-conic-angle { syntax: \"<angle>\"; inherits: false; initial-value: 0deg }` registers the custom property so the browser knows its value is an angle. An unregistered custom property is an untyped token stream: a keyframe from `0deg` to `360deg` has no interpolation rule and the value flips at the 50% mark instead of sweeping.\n2. A single keyframe block, `@keyframes kx-conic-spin { to { --kx-conic-angle: 360deg } }`, drives that property from its `initial-value` to a full turn over `--kx-conic-duration` with `linear` timing. Linear is required: any ease makes the highlights visibly hesitate once per revolution.\n3. The element carries two backgrounds. `linear-gradient(fill, fill) padding-box` paints the interior flat; `conic-gradient(from var(--kx-conic-angle), …) border-box` paints under the border. With `border: 1.5px solid transparent`, the conic layer survives the clip only in the ring, which is how the gradient follows `border-radius` — `border-image` cannot round corners.\n4. The gradient has six stops with the glow colour at 55deg and 305deg and the rest colour everywhere else. Two highlights roughly opposite each other read as rotation; a single highlight reads as a light blinking on and off once per cycle.\n5. `inherits: false` on the registration stops descendants from picking up the animating value. With inheritance on, every element in the subtree re-resolves its computed style on every frame of the animation, which turns a one-element repaint into a whole-subtree style recalculation.\n6. Every colour is read through `var(--name, fallback)`, so the component renders correctly with no props and can be re-themed by setting `--conic-glow` or `--conic-fill` on any ancestor.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `glow stop positions = 55deg and 305deg`\n  Two highlights, not one, and not diametrically opposite. 250 degrees apart means one is entering the top edge as the other leaves the bottom, so there is always motion somewhere on the ring. At 180 degrees apart the pair reads as a symmetric pulse; with one stop it reads as a blinking dot.\n- `durationMs = 4000`\n  One revolution. Below ~2500 ms the ring reads as a loading spinner and demands attention; above ~7000 ms the highlight barely appears to move and the border looks like a static gradient someone got wrong.\n- `thickness = 1.5px`\n  Sub-pixel on a 1x display, which is what keeps it a hairline rather than a stripe. At 1px the highlight is too thin to read colour; at 3px or more the conic banding between stops becomes visible as facets.\n- `rest colour alpha = currentColor at 10%`\n  The dim majority of the ring. At 0% the border disappears between highlights and the box appears to lose its outline; above ~20% the contrast against the glow collapses and the rotation stops reading.\n- `reduced-motion parked angle = 132deg`\n  Puts one highlight past the top-left shoulder rather than exactly on an edge or a corner. At the `initial-value` of 0deg the highlight sits dead centre on the right edge, which looks like a rendering artefact rather than a decision.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ConicBorder(props: { children: ReactNode; durationMs?: number; thickness?: string; radius?: string; glow?: string; rest?: string; fill?: string; paused?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content rendered inside the ring. The element is `inline-flex`, centred.\n- `durationMs: number` — default `4000`\n  Milliseconds for one full revolution.\n- `thickness: string` — default `'1.5px'`\n  Ring thickness. Becomes the transparent border width.\n- `radius: string` — default `'9999px'`\n  Corner radius. The gradient follows it, unlike `border-image`.\n- `glow: string`\n  Colour of the two travelling highlights. Defaults to `currentColor`.\n- `rest: string`\n  Colour of the dim ring between highlights. Defaults to `currentColor` at 10%.\n- `fill: string`\n  Interior fill. Must be opaque or the gradient shows through the middle.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Sets `animation-play-state: paused`, freezing the ring at its current angle.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the ring element via `cn`. Use it for padding and typography.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/* Injected once per document. React 19 hoists `<style href precedence>` into\n   <head> and de-duplicates by `href`, so a hundred rings ship one rule set —\n   and `@property` registration is global anyway, so re-declaring it per\n   instance would be pure waste. */\nconst CONIC_BORDER_CSS = `\n@property --kx-conic-angle {\n  syntax: \"<angle>\";\n  inherits: false;\n  initial-value: 0deg;\n}\n\n@keyframes kx-conic-spin {\n  to { --kx-conic-angle: 360deg; }\n}\n\n.kx-conic {\n  position: relative;\n  display: inline-flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n  isolation: isolate;\n\n  border: var(--kx-conic-width, 1.5px) solid transparent;\n  border-radius: var(--kx-conic-radius, 9999px);\n\n  /* padding-box layer paints the interior, border-box layer paints under the\n     ring. Order matters: the first background listed is the topmost. */\n  background:\n    linear-gradient(var(--kx-conic-fill), var(--kx-conic-fill)) padding-box,\n    conic-gradient(\n      from var(--kx-conic-angle),\n      var(--kx-conic-rest)   0deg,\n      var(--kx-conic-glow)  55deg,\n      var(--kx-conic-rest) 130deg,\n      var(--kx-conic-rest) 230deg,\n      var(--kx-conic-glow) 305deg,\n      var(--kx-conic-rest) 360deg\n    ) border-box;\n\n  animation: kx-conic-spin var(--kx-conic-duration, 4s) linear infinite;\n}\n\n.kx-conic[data-paused='true'] {\n  animation-play-state: paused;\n}\n\n/* No motion: keep the ring, stop the revolution, and park a highlight\n   off-axis so the border still reads as decorated rather than broken. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-conic {\n    animation: none;\n    --kx-conic-angle: 132deg;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport function ConicBorder({\n  children,\n  durationMs = 4000,\n  thickness = '1.5px',\n  radius = '9999px',\n  glow,\n  rest,\n  fill,\n  paused = false,\n  className,\n}: ConicBorderProps) {\n  /* Every colour resolves through a `var(..., fallback)` chain, so the\n     component works with zero configuration and is themeable by setting one\n     property on any ancestor. `Canvas` is the system background keyword — a\n     last resort that is at least the right polarity in both colour schemes. */\n  const style: StyleWithVars = {\n    '--kx-conic-duration': `${durationMs}ms`,\n    '--kx-conic-width': thickness,\n    '--kx-conic-radius': radius,\n    '--kx-conic-glow': glow ?? 'var(--conic-glow, currentColor)',\n    '--kx-conic-rest':\n      rest ?? 'var(--conic-rest, color-mix(in oklab, currentColor 10%, transparent))',\n    '--kx-conic-fill': fill ?? 'var(--conic-fill, var(--color-bg, Canvas))',\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-conic-property-border\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {CONIC_BORDER_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <div\n        className={cn('kx-conic', className)}\n        style={style}\n        data-paused={paused ? 'true' : undefined}\n      >\n        {children}\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. There is no listener, no timer and no animation frame; unmounting the element removes the only thing that was running. The hoisted `<style>` is reference-counted by React and removed when the last instance unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block sets `animation: none` and pins `--kx-conic-angle` to 132deg. The gradient border stays — same colours, same thickness — it simply does not revolve. Nothing is removed from the layout and no size changes.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating the custom property without registering it with `@property`. The animation runs — `getComputedStyle` even reports it — but the gradient snaps from its start angle to its end angle at the halfway point, because an unregistered custom property interpolates as a discrete token stream. This is the single most common way this effect is reported \"broken\".\n- Writing `border: 1.5px solid` with no colour, or omitting the border entirely. With no border there is no border-box area outside the padding box, the conic layer is fully covered by the fill layer, and the ring is invisible. The border must exist and must be transparent.\n- Putting the fill layer second in the `background` list. Backgrounds paint first-listed on top; with the order reversed the flat fill is painted underneath and the conic gradient covers the entire box, not just the ring.\n- Leaving `inherits` at its default of `true`. Descendants then inherit an animating value, so the browser recomputes styles for the whole subtree every frame. On a card with 40 children this shows up as a steady 3-5 ms of style recalculation per frame in a performance profile.\n- Assuming the animation is compositor-driven and reaching for `will-change: transform`. It is not: animating a registered property repaints the element background on the main thread every frame. That is cheap on a button and expensive on a full-width hero — scale the element, not the hint.\n- Using a semi-transparent interior fill. The conic layer extends under the whole border box, so anything less than opaque lets the gradient bleed through the middle of the card.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `@property` needs Safari 16.4+, Chrome 85+, Firefox 128+. Where it is unsupported the `@property` rule is dropped, the keyframe cannot animate the custom property, and `from var(--kx-conic-angle)` falls back to the guaranteed-invalid initial value — the conic gradient still paints from 0deg. The degradation is a static two-highlight ring, which is an acceptable border on its own.\n- The CSS ships inside the component as a `<style href precedence>` element. React 19 hoists it into `<head>` and de-duplicates by `href`, so any number of instances contribute one rule set and one `@property` registration.\n- The class name is prefixed `kx-` rather than scoped per instance on purpose: `@property` registration is document-global, so per-instance scoping would buy nothing and cost a style element per mount.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTwo bright arcs travel continuously around the border and pass the corners without pausing or jumping — in particular, nothing snaps at the halfway point of the cycle. The interior stays fully opaque. Set `paused` and the arcs freeze where they are. Enable reduced motion at the OS level and the ring is still coloured but completely still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "container-animation-reveals": "# Build: One Reveal Engine, Two Axes\n\nLines rise out of a clipping box and fade up as their group enters. The interesting part is that the same builder serves a vertical page section and a panel riding a pinned horizontal strip — handed the strip's tween as `containerAnimation`, ScrollTrigger measures against that timeline's progress instead of scroll position. One set of numbers, two directions, nothing to keep in sync by hand.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe same staggered line reveal fires for vertical sections and for panels on a horizontal track.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The subtree is scanned for `[data-reveal-group]` elements; each becomes one ScrollTrigger. A subtree with no groups reveals as a single group rooted on the component itself.\n2. Inside a group, every `[data-reveal]` element is a target of one `gsap.fromTo` running `{ yPercent: 110, opacity: 0.15 }` to `{ yPercent: 0, opacity: 1 }` on `power3.out` with a 0.12 s stagger — so the group is one tween, not one tween per line, and the stagger is real rather than simulated with delays.\n3. When a `containerAnimation` is supplied it is spread into the trigger vars and the position keywords flip from `top 88% / top 45%` to `left 85% / left 45%`, because a trigger attached to a container animation measures along that container's axis.\n4. The `containerAnimation` prop is tri-state: `undefined` means vertical flow, an animation means horizontal, and `null` means \"a sibling owns the timeline and has not built it yet\" — in which case nothing is built at all this commit.\n5. `scrub: 0.4` ties the reveal to scroll position with a short catch-up, so reversing direction reverses the reveal instead of replaying it.\n6. Everything is created inside a `gsap.context` scoped to the root, so `ctx.revert()` kills the triggers and restores the inline `transform` and `opacity` the tween wrote.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `lift = 110`\n  Start offset as a percentage of the element's own height. 100 puts the line exactly at the bottom edge of its clipping box; 110 adds a sliver of overshoot so descenders are hidden through the whole travel rather than peeking under the mask on the first frame.\n- `START_OPACITY = 0.15`\n  Not 0. A line that fades from fully transparent reads as two separate events — a fade and a move. Starting at 0.15 makes the movement the thing you notice and the fade a supporting detail.\n- `stagger = 0.12`\n  Seconds between lines within a group. Below ~0.06 the lines read as one block; above ~0.2 the last line arrives after the eye has already moved on, which reads as lag rather than rhythm.\n- `scrub = 0.4`\n  Short catch-up so the reveal follows the scrollbar but survives a wheel step. At 0 the tween inherits every input spike; above ~1 the lines are still arriving after the section has left.\n- `start / end (horizontal) = 'left 85%' / 'left 45%'`\n  With `containerAnimation` the trigger is measured along the container's axis. Vertical keywords resolve to a position the element never occupies on that axis, so the tween simply never runs.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction AxisReveals(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; containerAnimation?: gsap.core.Animation | null; scrub?: number; lift?: number; stagger?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Markup containing `[data-reveal-group]` scopes and `[data-reveal]` targets.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container the triggers read. Omit for the document.\n- `containerAnimation: gsap.core.Animation | null`\n  Omit for vertical flow, pass a horizontal tween to measure against it, pass `null` to hold off until that tween exists.\n- `scrub: number` — default `0.4`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the reveal.\n- `lift: number` — default `110`\n  Start offset as a percentage of the element height.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.12`\n  Seconds between elements inside a group.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper. Use `contents` when the wrapper must not affect layout.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function AxisReveals({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  containerAnimation,\n  scrub = 0.4,\n  lift = 110,\n  stagger = 0.12,\n  className,\n}: AxisRevealsProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl || reduced) return;\n    // Explicit \"not ready\": a sibling owns the timeline and has not built it.\n    if (containerAnimation === null) return;\n\n    const scrollerEl = scroller?.current ?? undefined;\n    const horizontal = containerAnimation !== undefined;\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      const groups = Array.from(rootEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-reveal-group]'));\n      // A subtree with no explicit groups reveals as one group.\n      const scopes = groups.length > 0 ? groups : [rootEl];\n\n      for (const group of scopes) {\n        const targets = Array.from(group.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-reveal]'));\n        if (targets.length === 0) continue;\n\n        gsap.fromTo(\n          targets,\n          { yPercent: lift, opacity: START_OPACITY },\n          {\n            yPercent: 0,\n            opacity: 1,\n            ease: 'power3.out',\n            duration: 1,\n            stagger,\n            scrollTrigger: {\n              trigger: group,\n              scroller: scrollerEl,\n              // The line everyone gets wrong. With `containerAnimation` the\n              // trigger measures along the container's axis, so the position\n              // keywords must be horizontal. \"top 85%\" against a horizontal\n              // container resolves to a position the element never occupies,\n              // and the reveal silently never fires.\n              start: horizontal ? 'left 85%' : 'top 88%',\n              end: horizontal ? 'left 45%' : 'top 45%',\n              scrub,\n              // Spreading rather than passing `undefined`: ScrollTrigger\n              // branches on the key's presence, not on its value.\n              ...(containerAnimation ? { containerAnimation } : {}),\n            },\n          },\n        );\n      }\n    }, rootEl);\n\n    return () => ctx.revert();\n  }, [scroller, containerAnimation, scrub, lift, stagger, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `ctx.revert()` on unmount and on every dependency change: it kills each group's tween and ScrollTrigger and restores the inline `transform` and `opacity` GSAP wrote, so a re-mount measures clean markup rather than the last run's end state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No triggers are created and no `fromTo` runs, so every line renders at its final position and full opacity on first paint. The clipping boxes stay in the markup and do nothing, so the layout is byte-for-byte the finished state.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Keeping vertical keywords when passing `containerAnimation`. `start: \"top 85%\"` produces no error, no warning and no animation — the panels simply arrive already revealed, and the bug survives review because nothing is broken, only absent.\n- Passing `containerAnimation: undefined` explicitly. ScrollTrigger branches on whether the key exists, so an `undefined` value is not the same as an absent key; the code spreads the object conditionally for exactly this reason.\n- Treating a not-yet-built timeline as the vertical case. The strip's tween is created in a sibling effect and arrives one render later, so a naive `containerAnimation ?? undefined` builds a full set of vertical triggers on horizontal content and tears them down on the next commit — visible as a flash of pre-revealed panels.\n- Writing a second reveal system for the horizontal section. Two systems mean two staggers, two easings and two sets of position keywords, and they diverge the first time someone tunes one of them.\n- Building one tween per line instead of one tween per group. Stagger stops being a property of the group and becomes a pile of hand-computed delays, which no longer reverse correctly when the user scrolls back up.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Combine with `pinned-horizontal-track`: pass its `onTrack` payload's `animation` straight into `containerAnimation`.\n- ScrollTrigger forbids `pin` on a trigger that has a `containerAnimation`. Pin the strip's section, never the panels riding it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the vertical section: the three lines rise out of their masks in sequence and reverse cleanly when you scroll back up. Keep scrolling into the pinned strip: each panel's lines rise with the same rhythm as it crosses the container, not as it crosses the viewport. With reduced motion on, every line is already in place.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "context-menu": "# Build: Context Menu\n\nThe actions for whatever is under the pointer: a file, a row, a message, a spot on a canvas. It opens on right-click, on a 500ms touch long-press, and on Shift+F10 or the Menu key while the target has focus, with the menu's corner at the pointer. React Aria's Menu in a non-modal popover supplies arrows, typeahead, submenus, Escape and focus return; the component adds the pointer anchor and, like the browser's own menu, closes on any press or scroll outside it. The compact panel (14px radius, 32px rows, 20px icons) grows from the pointer with the menu family's 150ms fade, scale and blur.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRight-click, long-press and Shift+F10 menu anchored to the pointer, with icons, shortcuts, submenus and checkable rows.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `ContextMenuTrigger` wraps its children in a `display: contents` div (so it adds no box) with `[-webkit-touch-callout:none]`, and listens for `contextmenu`, `keydown`, `pointerdown`, `pointermove`, `pointerup` and `pointercancel`. Children can be a function of `{ isOpen }` so the target can highlight while its menu is open.\n2. Opening records the point and portals a 1px `pointer-events-none fixed` anchor div to `document.body` at `left: x, top: y`; `ContextMenu` positions an `AriaPopover` against it with `triggerRef={anchorRef}`, `placement=\"bottom start\"`, `offset={2}` and `dir` from `useDirection()`, so the panel's corner sits at the pointer towards the reading direction and flips or shifts to stay on screen. `openCount` is bumped on every opening and used as the popover's `key`, so each menu mounts fresh at its own point.\n3. Right-click calls `preventDefault` and opens at `clientX/clientY` with the menu itself focused (`autoFocus` true, as the browser does). Keyboard openings (Shift+F10, the `ContextMenu` key, or a `contextmenu` event at 0,0) use `openFrom(element)`: just inside the focused element's start edge, `min(24, width/2)` in and `min(height, 40)` down, with `autoFocus=\"first\"` so the first item is focused. A touch `pointerdown` starts a 500ms timer that is cancelled if the finger drifts more than 10px or lifts.\n4. The popover is `isNonModal`, so React Aria leaves it open on outside presses. While open, capture-phase `pointerdown` and `scroll` listeners on `document` close it unless the event target sits inside an element whose `data-context-menu` equals this trigger's `useId()` (the root panel and every submenu carry it). `contextmenu` events bubbling back through the portal are `preventDefault`ed so the browser menu never stacks on top; nested triggers: the innermost handles the event first and the outer sees `defaultPrevented`.\n5. Panels share `PANEL`: `z-110 min-w-52 max-w-[calc(100vw-32px)] rounded-[14px] border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-dropdown`, `origin-(--trigger-anchor-point)`, `transition-[opacity,scale,filter] duration-150 ease-out`, `opacity-0` on `data-[entering]` / `data-[exiting]` and, under `motion-safe:`, `scale-95 blur-[2px]`. `MenuList` is an `AriaMenu` scroller (`gap-1 p-1 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain`) with a `ScrollFade`.\n6. `ContextMenuItem` is an `AriaMenuItem` using `MENU_ITEM` plus `px-2 py-1.5 text-body-medium`, highlighted with `MENU_ITEM_ACTIVE` while focused or while its submenu is open; it renders a 20px icon, the label, a check when selected, a `<kbd>` shortcut and a chevron (mirrored in RTL) when it has a submenu. `ContextMenuSubmenu` is an `AriaSubmenuTrigger` whose popover uses `offset={9}` and `crossOffset={-5}` and forwards the root's `onAction` and `close` through `RootMenuContext`, so submenu item ids reach the root handler.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `LONG_PRESS_MS = 500`\n  How long a touch must rest before the menu opens: long enough not to fire on a tap or the start of a scroll, about what native long-press menus use.\n- `LONG_PRESS_SLOP = 10 (px)`\n  A resting finger still drifts a few pixels; more than 10px means the reader is scrolling or dragging, so the press is cancelled.\n- `keyboard anchor = min(24, width/2) in from the start edge, min(height, 40) down`\n  A keyboard opening has no pointer; this places the menu just inside the focused element so it visibly belongs to it, even on a small target.\n- `submenu offset = offset 9, crossOffset -5`\n  9px clears the parent's 4px padding and 1px border for a 4px gap; -5px lines the submenu's first row up with the row that opened it.\n- `panel = rounded-[14px], p-1, min-w-52 (208px), 150ms scale 0.95 / blur 2px`\n  The compact look of Multi-agent Chat's chat menu it was promoted from, with the shared menu motion so all anchored menus open alike.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ContextMenuTrigger(props: { children: ReactNode | ((state: { isOpen: boolean }) => ReactNode); isDisabled?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ContextMenu<T extends object>(props: Omit<AriaMenuProps<T>, \"className\" | \"autoFocus\" | \"onClose\"> & { \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ContextMenuItem(props: Omit<AriaMenuItemProps, \"children\" | \"className\"> & { icon?: IconComponent; shortcut?: string; variant?: \"default\" | \"danger\"; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction ContextMenuSubmenu(props: { label: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; textValue?: string; isDisabled?: boolean; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction ContextMenuSection(props: Omit<AriaMenuSectionProps<object>, \"children\" | \"className\"> & { title?: ReactNode; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction ContextMenuSeparator(props: { className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `ContextMenuTrigger.children: ReactNode | ((state: { isOpen: boolean }) => ReactNode)`\n  The target, then its ContextMenu. The function form lets the target highlight while open.\n- `ContextMenuTrigger.isDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Leave the browser's own menu in place.\n- `ContextMenuTrigger.onOpenChange: (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Called when the menu opens or closes.\n- `ContextMenu.aria-label: string` — default `\"Actions\"`\n  Names the menu for screen readers.\n- `ContextMenu.onAction: (key: Key) => void`\n  Runs with the chosen item's id, including items inside submenus.\n- `ContextMenuItem.icon: IconComponent`\n  Remix Icon component drawn at 20px before the label.\n- `ContextMenuItem.shortcut: string`\n  Keyboard hint at the end of the row, e.g. \"⌘C\".\n- `ContextMenuItem.variant: \"default\" | \"danger\"` — default `\"default\"`\n  Danger tints the row and its icon with the destructive text colour.\n- `ContextMenuSection.selectionMode: \"single\" | \"multiple\"`\n  Makes the section's rows checkable.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ContextMenuTrigger({ children, isDisabled = false, onOpenChange, className }: ContextMenuTriggerProps) {\n  const id = useId();\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const anchorRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const openRef = useRef(false);\n  const press = useRef<{ x: number; y: number; timer: number } | null>(null);\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\n  const [point, setPoint] = useState<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const [openCount, setOpenCount] = useState(0);\n  const [autoFocus, setAutoFocus] = useState<\"first\" | true>(true);\n\n  const setOpen = useCallback(\n    (next: boolean) => {\n      if (openRef.current === next) return;\n      openRef.current = next;\n      setIsOpen(next);\n      onOpenChange?.(next);\n    },\n    [onOpenChange],\n  );\n  const close = useCallback(() => setOpen(false), [setOpen]);\n\n  // A pointer opening focuses the menu itself, as the browser's menu does;\n  // a keyboard opening lands on the first item.\n  const openAt = (x: number, y: number, focus: \"first\" | true) => {\n    setPoint({ x, y });\n    setAutoFocus(focus);\n    setOpenCount((count) => count + 1);\n    setOpen(true);\n  };\n\n  /** Keyboard openings have no pointer: open just inside the focused element's start edge. */\n  const openFrom = (element: Element) => {\n    const rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const inset = Math.min(24, rect.width / 2);\n    openAt(direction === \"rtl\" ? rect.right - inset : rect.left + inset, rect.top + Math.min(rect.height, 40), \"first\");\n  };\n\n  const cancelPress = () => {\n    if (!press.current) return;\n    window.clearTimeout(press.current.timer);\n    press.current = null;\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => () => {\n    if (press.current) window.clearTimeout(press.current.timer);\n  }, []);\n\n  // React Aria leaves non-modal popovers open on outside presses, and the\n  // anchor is fixed, so close on a press or a scroll outside this menu tree.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!isOpen) return;\n    const onOutside = (event: Event) => {\n      if (menuOf(event.target) !== id) close();\n    };\n    document.addEventListener(\"pointerdown\", onOutside, true);\n    document.addEventListener(\"scroll\", onOutside, true);\n    return () => {\n      document.removeEventListener(\"pointerdown\", onOutside, true);\n      document.removeEventListener(\"scroll\", onOutside, true);\n    };\n  }, [isOpen, id, close]);\n\n  const onContextMenu = (event: MouseEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    // Events from the open menu bubble here through its portal; never show\n    // the browser's menu on top of it.\n    if (menuOf(event.target) === id) {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      return;\n    }\n    if (isDisabled || event.defaultPrevented || !event.currentTarget.contains(event.target as Node)) return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    if (openRef.current) return;\n    // Some keyboard-made events carry no position.\n    if (event.clientX === 0 && event.clientY === 0) openFrom(event.target as Element);\n    else openAt(event.clientX, event.clientY, true);\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (isDisabled || event.defaultPrevented || !event.currentTarget.contains(event.target as Node)) return;\n    if (event.key !== \"ContextMenu\" && !(event.shiftKey && event.key === \"F10\")) return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    openFrom(event.target as Element);\n  };\n\n  const onPointerDown = (event: ReactPointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (event.pointerType !== \"touch\" || isDisabled || !event.currentTarget.contains(event.target as Node)) return;\n    cancelPress();\n    const { clientX: x, clientY: y } = event;\n    press.current = {\n      x,\n      y,\n      timer: window.setTimeout(() => {\n        press.current = null;\n        if (!openRef.current) openAt(x, y, true);\n      }, LONG_PRESS_MS),\n    };\n  };\n\n  const onPointerMove = (event: ReactPointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (press.current && Math.hypot(event.clientX - press.current.x, event.clientY - press.current.y) > LONG_PRESS_SLOP) {\n      cancelPress();\n    }\n  };\n\n  const content = typeof children === \"function\" ? children({ isOpen }) : children;\n\n  return (\n    <TriggerContext.Provider value={{ id, isOpen, openCount, anchorRef, autoFocus, close }}>\n      <div\n        className={cx(\"contents [-webkit-touch-callout:none]\", className)}\n        data-open={isOpen || undefined}\n        onContextMenu={onContextMenu}\n        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n        onPointerDown={onPointerDown}\n        onPointerMove={onPointerMove}\n        onPointerUp={cancelPress}\n        onPointerCancel={cancelPress}\n      >\n        {content}\n      </div>\n      {point\n        ? createPortal(\n            <div\n              ref={anchorRef}\n              aria-hidden\n              className=\"pointer-events-none fixed size-px\"\n              style={{ left: point.x, top: point.y }}\n            />,\n            document.body,\n          )\n        : null}\n    </TriggerContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- menu */\n\n/** A menu's scrolling list, with the shared progressive top blur. */\nfunction MenuList<T extends object>({ children, ...props }: Omit<AriaMenuProps<T>, \"className\">) {\n  const fade = useScrollFade();\n  return (\n    <div className=\"relative flex min-h-0 flex-col\">\n      <AriaMenu\n        {...props}\n        onScroll={fade.onScroll}\n        className=\"flex min-h-0 flex-col gap-1 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain p-1 outline-none [scrollbar-width:thin]\"\n      >\n        {children}\n      </AriaMenu>\n      <ScrollFade progress={fade.progress} />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface ContextMenuProps<T extends object> extends Omit<AriaMenuProps<T>, \"className\" | \"autoFocus\" | \"onClose\"> {\n  /** Names the menu for screen readers. Default \"Actions\". */\n  \"aria-label\"?: string;\n  /** Classes on the panel, e.g. a width. */\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function ContextMenu<T extends object>({\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Actions\",\n  onAction,\n  className,\n  ...props\n}: ContextMenuProps<T>) {\n  const trigger = useContext(TriggerContext);\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  if (!trigger) throw new Error(\"ContextMenu must be inside a ContextMenuTrigger.\");\n  const { id, isOpen, openCount, anchorRef, autoFocus, close } = trigger;\n\n  return (\n    <RootMenuContext.Provider\n      value={{ id, onAction: onAction as ((key: Key, value: object) => void) | undefined, close }}\n    >\n      <AriaPopover\n        key={openCount}\n        triggerRef={anchorRef}\n        isOpen={isOpen}\n        onOpenChange={(open) => {\n          if (!open) close();\n        }}\n        isNonModal\n        placement=\"bottom start\"\n        offset={2}\n        dir={direction}\n        data-context-menu={id}\n        className={cx(PANEL, className)}\n      >\n        <MenuList {...props} aria-label={localize(ariaLabel)} autoFocus={autoFocus} onAction={onAction} onClose={close} />\n      </AriaPopover>\n    </RootMenuContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The document `pointerdown` and `scroll` capture listeners exist only while the menu is open and are removed by their effect cleanup. An unmount effect clears any pending long-press timer. The portalled anchor div unmounts with the trigger.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The panel's scale and blur are behind `motion-safe:`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the menu and its submenus only fade in and out over 150ms.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not anchor the popover to the target element. The menu belongs at the pointer; a 1px fixed anchor portalled to the body at the click point is what React Aria positions against.\n- Do not reuse one popover instance across openings. Without the `key={openCount}` remount, a second right-click elsewhere animates from the old position.\n- Do not rely on React Aria to close a non-modal popover on outside presses; it does not. Close on capture-phase `pointerdown` and `scroll` outside elements tagged with this menu's `data-context-menu` id, or submenus will count as \"outside\".\n- Do not let `contextmenu` from inside the open menu reach the browser. Events from the portal bubble through React to the trigger, so they must be `preventDefault`ed or the native menu appears on top.\n- Do not depend on the browser's `contextmenu` event for touch. Not every mobile browser fires it on a long-press, so the trigger runs its own 500ms timer on touch `pointerdown` and opens at the press point.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `isDisabled` on the trigger leaves the browser's own menu in place.\n- A `ContextMenuSection` with `selectionMode` makes its rows checkable; selected rows show a check in the accent colour.\n- The default `aria-label` is \"Actions\", passed through `useTemplateCopy()` so localized templates can translate it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nRight-clicking anywhere on the file card opens the menu with its top-left corner at the pointer (no browser menu), the card highlights, and pressing outside or scrolling closes it. Hovering \"Move to\" opens a submenu aligned with that row; choosing \"Archive\" closes everything and the status reads \"Moved to Archive\". Tabbing to the card and pressing Shift+F10 opens the menu just inside the card with the first item focused; arrows, typeahead and Escape work. On touch, holding still for half a second opens it, while dragging cancels.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "context-usage-ring": "# Build: Context Usage Ring\n\nA compact pill for a chat header or status bar that shows how much of the model's context window is in use. A 16px ring fills clockwise next to the percentage, and the pill moves from neutral to orange to rose as the window fills. Hovering or focusing it opens the kit tooltip with used and total tokens, a stacked bar, a per-source list and a \"Free\" row.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSmall ring and percentage for how full a chat context window is. Turns orange, then rose, near the limit; a tooltip breaks the tokens down.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `ratio = clamp(used / limit, 0, 1)` and `pct = round(ratio * 100)`. `contextLevel(ratio, warnAt, dangerAt)` returns `\"normal\"`, `\"warning\"` (from 0.75) or `\"danger\"` (from 0.9), and the level picks the pill and arc classes from `LEVEL_STYLE`.\n2. The ring is two SVG circles with `pathLength={100}`, rotated -90deg so it starts at 12 o'clock. The track uses the quaternary icon colour. The arc is `strokeDasharray=\"{pct} 100\"`, so the dash length is the percentage with no circumference maths. It is hidden at 0% because a round cap on a zero-length dash still draws a dot.\n3. Arc length and colour animate together with `transition-[stroke-dasharray,color] duration-300 ease-out`, and the pill background and text animate with `transition-colors`. A new token count slides the arc forward and crossing a threshold fades the colour.\n4. The pill is a React Aria `Button` wrapped in the kit `TooltipTrigger`, so the tooltip opens on hover and on keyboard focus. The button's accessible name is \"Context window N% used\", and it carries `data-level` for styling and tests.\n5. Inside the tooltip, each `breakdown` slice gets a width of `tokens / limit` in a flex bar on the track colour, so the empty tail of the bar is the free space. The list repeats the slices with a dot in the same tone and ends with a \"Free\" row of `limit - used`. Above the warning threshold a one-line note explains what happens next.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `warnAt / dangerAt = 0.75 / 0.9`\n  Orange at three quarters leaves room for a couple of large file reads before the window is full. Rose at 90% is roughly the point where a long reply or one more file forces older turns out.\n- `ring size = 16px, stroke 2px (2.5px above 16px)`\n  Matches the 16px icons in a status bar. Thicker strokes at that size close up the centre, and thinner ones vanish at 0-10%.\n- `tooltip width = 248px, capped at 100vw - 32px`\n  Wide enough for a label, a token count and the warning note on two lines. The cap keeps it inside a 360px screen.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ContextUsageRing(props: { used: number; limit: number; breakdown?: ContextSlice[]; warnAt?: number; dangerAt?: number; hideLabel?: boolean; size?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface ContextSlice { label: string; tokens: number; tone?: \"accent\" | \"blue\" | \"purple\" | \"orange\" | \"cyan\" | \"lime\" | \"rose\" | \"yellow\" }\nfunction contextLevel(ratio: number, warnAt?: number, dangerAt?: number): \"normal\" | \"warning\" | \"danger\"\nfunction formatTokens(n: number): string\n```\n\n- `used: number`\n  Tokens currently in the window.\n- `limit: number`\n  Window size in tokens.\n- `breakdown: ContextSlice[]`\n  What the used tokens are made of. Shown as a stacked bar and a list in the tooltip; zero slices are skipped.\n- `warnAt: number` — default `0.75`\n  Share of the window where the pill turns orange.\n- `dangerAt: number` — default `0.9`\n  Share of the window where the pill turns rose.\n- `hideLabel: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows the ring alone. The accessible name still includes the percentage.\n- `size: number` — default `16`\n  Ring diameter in px.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the pill button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction Ring({ pct, size, arcClass }: { pct: number; size: number; arcClass: string }) {\n  const stroke = size <= 16 ? 2 : 2.5;\n  const r = (size - stroke) / 2;\n  return (\n    <svg aria-hidden width={size} height={size} viewBox={`0 0 ${size} ${size}`} className=\"shrink-0 -rotate-90\">\n      <circle cx={size / 2} cy={size / 2} r={r} fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" strokeWidth={stroke} className=\"text-foreground-icon-quaternary\" />\n      <circle\n        cx={size / 2}\n        cy={size / 2}\n        r={r}\n        fill=\"none\"\n        stroke=\"currentColor\"\n        strokeWidth={stroke}\n        strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n        pathLength={100}\n        // A round cap pokes past a zero-length dash, so an empty window draws nothing.\n        strokeDasharray={pct > 0 ? `${pct} 100` : \"0 100\"}\n        opacity={pct > 0 ? 1 : 0}\n        className={cx(\n          \"transition-[stroke-dasharray,color] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          arcClass,\n        )}\n      />\n    </svg>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers. React Aria owns the hover, focus and tooltip state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The arc, its colour and the pill colours use `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so they jump straight to the new value. The tooltip follows the kit's own reduced-motion rule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `strokeDashoffset` with a computed circumference. `pathLength={100}` lets the dash array be the percentage directly and keeps working at any `size`.\n- Drawing the arc at 0% with a round cap. A zero-length dash still renders as a dot, so the arc is hidden until there is at least 1%.\n- Putting the tooltip on a plain `<div>`. The kit tooltip needs a focusable React Aria trigger, otherwise keyboard users never see the breakdown.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Slices without a `tone` take accent, purple, cyan, lime and yellow in turn. Orange and rose are left for the warning levels so a slice is never mistaken for the alert colour.\n- `formatTokens` prints 812, 4.2k, 114k and 1.2M, and is exported for labels next to the ring.\n- The demo is a session header with branch, project and the ring. Its buttons add tokens for a file, a tool run or a message, Compact keeps 20% of messages and drops tool output, and three cards show normal, warning and danger examples with `hideLabel`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 35% the pill is neutral with a third of the ring filled. Adding tokens grows the arc; at 75% the pill turns orange and at 90% rose, each with a note in the tooltip. Hover or Tab onto the pill to see used/limit, a stacked bar, one row per source and a Free row. Compacting brings it back to neutral.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "context-window-card": "# Build: Context Window Card\n\nA small usage tile that shows how much of a model's context window is taken and by what. Each category (messages, tools, memory, system prompt) is a coloured segment on a 4px track, and whatever is left of the track is the free space. A chevron next to the totals opens a legend with each category's tokens and share, and hovering a row or a segment dims the rest so the pair reads together.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn agent's context window as one thin segmented bar by category, with a disclosable legend of token counts and shares.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Sum the segment tokens, clamped to `capacity`, as `used`; `free = capacity − used`; the header prints `formatTokens(used) / formatTokens(capacity) (pct%)` with the percentage in the semibold primary style.\n2. Compute each segment's width as its share of the WHOLE window (`tokens / capacity × 100%`), walking a `remaining` budget so an over-full list never paints past the track. Draw them left to right in a `flex h-1 gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-chart-track` row, so the empty track on the right is the free space with no element of its own.\n3. Give the bar `role=\"meter\"` with `aria-valuemin`, `aria-valuemax=capacity`, `aria-valuenow=used` and an `aria-valuetext` like \"820k of 1M tokens used (82%)\". The segments themselves are decorative.\n4. The header totals are a `<button aria-expanded aria-controls>` that toggles the legend (controlled `expanded` or uncontrolled `defaultExpanded`). The legend collapses with a `grid-rows-[0fr] → [1fr]` transition plus opacity, and is `inert` while closed so its rows leave the tab and reading order.\n5. One `active` id is shared by the segments and the legend rows: `pointerenter` sets it, `pointerleave` clears it, and every other segment drops to 35% opacity (rows to 60%) while the active row gets the hover surface.\n6. Segment widths transition over 500ms ease-out, so adding or compacting tokens visibly grows or shrinks the bar. `children` render below an `<hr>` divider, which is how plan limits share the same tile.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `bar height = h-1 (4px) with gap-px between segments`\n  Matches the hairline bar on BoardUI's Agent limits tile. The 1px gap is what keeps seven adjacent colours readable as separate categories without outlines.\n- `width transition = 500ms ease-out`\n  Long enough to read a compaction as the bar draining, short enough that a fast run of turns does not lag behind the totals in the header.\n- `dim opacity = segments 35%, legend rows 60%`\n  The thin bar needs a stronger drop than the text rows for the highlighted segment to pop; rows keep 60% so their numbers stay legible.\n- `compact format = ≥1M → one decimal M (trailing .0 dropped), ≥1k → rounded k`\n  Gives \"820k / 1M\" exactly as on the tile; decimals in k would make the header jitter on every turn.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ContextWindowCard(props: { segments: ContextSegment[]; capacity: number; label?: ReactNode; freeLabel?: string; expanded?: boolean; defaultExpanded?: boolean; onExpandedChange?: (expanded: boolean) => void; formatTokens?: (tokens: number) => string; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype ContextSegment = { id: string; label: string; tokens: number; color?: \"blue\" | \"purple\" | \"pink\" | \"yellow\" | \"green\" | \"sky\" | \"teal\" | \"indigo\" | \"lime\" }\nfunction formatTokensCompact(tokens: number): string\n```\n\n- `segments: ContextSegment[]`\n  Category usage drawn left to right: id, label, tokens and a palette colour.\n- `capacity: number`\n  Window size in tokens. Segment widths are shares of this, the rest is free track.\n- `label: ReactNode` — default `\"Context window\"`\n  Header label; also the meter's accessible name when it is a string.\n- `freeLabel: string` — default `\"Free space\"`\n  Legend label for the unused remainder.\n- `expanded: boolean`\n  Controlled legend state.\n- `defaultExpanded: boolean` — default `false`\n  Uncontrolled legend state on mount.\n- `onExpandedChange: (expanded: boolean) => void`\n  Called when the header toggle is pressed.\n- `formatTokens: (tokens: number) => string` — default `formatTokensCompact`\n  Formats every count in the header and legend.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Rows rendered under a divider in the same tile (plan limits, actions).\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card surface.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ContextWindowCard({\n  segments,\n  capacity,\n  label = \"Context window\",\n  freeLabel = \"Free space\",\n  expanded,\n  defaultExpanded = false,\n  onExpandedChange,\n  formatTokens = formatTokensCompact,\n  children,\n  className,\n}: ContextWindowCardProps) {\n  const legendId = useId();\n  const [uncontrolledOpen, setUncontrolledOpen] = useState(defaultExpanded);\n  const open = expanded ?? uncontrolledOpen;\n  const [active, setActive] = useState<string | null>(null);\n\n  const setOpen = (next: boolean) => {\n    setUncontrolledOpen(next);\n    onExpandedChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  // Clamp so an over-full window never paints past the track.\n  const used = Math.min(\n    capacity,\n    segments.reduce((sum, s) => sum + Math.max(0, s.tokens), 0),\n  );\n  const free = Math.max(0, capacity - used);\n  const usedPct = Math.round(percentOf(used, capacity));\n\n  // Each segment's width is its share of the WHOLE window, so the empty\n  // track to the right is the free space with no extra element.\n  let remaining = capacity;\n  const widths = segments.map((s) => {\n    const tokens = Math.min(Math.max(0, s.tokens), remaining);\n    remaining -= tokens;\n    return percentOf(tokens, capacity);\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-3 rounded-[14px] bg-background-secondary-default p-3.5\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-3\">\n        <h3 className=\"text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">{label}</h3>\n        <button\n          type=\"button\"\n          aria-expanded={open}\n          aria-controls={legendId}\n          onClick={() => setOpen(!open)}\n          className=\"-me-1 inline-flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-1 rounded-md px-1 py-0.5 text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums outline-none transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-hover hover:text-text-primary focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\"\n        >\n          <span>\n            {formatTokens(used)} / {formatTokens(capacity)}{\" \"}\n            <span className=\"text-caption-1-semibold text-text-primary\">({usedPct}%)</span>\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"sr-only\">{open ? \"Hide breakdown\" : \"Show breakdown\"}</span>\n          <ChevronDownSmall\n            aria-hidden\n            className={cx(\n              \"size-3.5 shrink-0 transition-transform duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              open && \"rotate-180\",\n            )}\n          />\n        </button>\n      </div>\n\n      <div\n        role=\"meter\"\n        aria-label={typeof label === \"string\" ? label : \"Context window\"}\n        aria-valuemin={0}\n        aria-valuemax={capacity}\n        aria-valuenow={used}\n        aria-valuetext={`${formatTokens(used)} of ${formatTokens(capacity)} tokens used (${usedPct}%)`}\n        className=\"flex h-1 w-full gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-chart-track\"\n      >\n        {segments.map((s, i) => (\n          <span\n            key={s.id}\n            onPointerEnter={() => setActive(s.id)}\n            onPointerLeave={() => setActive(null)}\n            className={cx(\n              \"h-full shrink-0 first:rounded-s-full\",\n              \"transition-[width,opacity] duration-500 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              swatch[s.color ?? DEFAULT_SEGMENT_COLOR],\n              active !== null && active !== s.id && \"opacity-35\",\n            )}\n            style={{ width: `${widths[i] ?? 0}%` }}\n          />\n        ))}\n      </div>\n\n      <div\n        id={legendId}\n        className={cx(\n          \"grid transition-[grid-template-rows,opacity,margin] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          // Closed, the -mt-3 cancels the flex gap so the empty row adds no space.\n          open ? \"grid-rows-[1fr] opacity-100\" : \"-mt-3 grid-rows-[0fr] opacity-0\",\n        )}\n        inert={!open}\n      >\n        <ul className=\"min-h-0 overflow-hidden\">\n          {segments.map((s, i) => (\n            <li\n              key={s.id}\n              onPointerEnter={() => setActive(s.id)}\n              onPointerLeave={() => setActive(null)}\n              className={cx(\n                \"flex items-center gap-2 rounded-md px-1 py-1 text-caption-1-medium transition-[background-color,opacity] duration-150 ease-out\",\n                active === s.id && \"bg-background-secondary-hover\",\n                active !== null && active !== s.id && \"opacity-60\",\n              )}\n            >\n              <span aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-2 shrink-0 rounded-[3px]\", swatch[s.color ?? DEFAULT_SEGMENT_COLOR])} />\n              <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-text-primary\">{s.label}</span>\n              <span className=\"shrink-0 text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">{formatTokens(s.tokens)}</span>\n              <span className=\"w-10 shrink-0 text-end text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n                {(widths[i] ?? 0).toFixed(1)}%\n              </span>\n            </li>\n          ))}\n          <li className=\"flex items-center gap-2 px-1 py-1 text-caption-1-medium\">\n            <span aria-hidden className=\"size-2 shrink-0 rounded-[3px] bg-chart-track ring-1 ring-border-button-default ring-inset\" />\n            <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-text-secondary\">{freeLabel}</span>\n            <span className=\"shrink-0 text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">{formatTokens(free)}</span>\n            <span className=\"w-10 shrink-0 text-end text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n              {percentOf(free, capacity).toFixed(1)}%\n            </span>\n          </li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n\n      {children != null && children !== false ? (\n        <>\n          <hr className=\"border-border-table\" />\n          {children}\n        </>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: two pieces of React state, pointer handlers on rendered elements, and CSS transitions that end on their own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Segment width, legend open/close and chevron rotation transitions carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so the bar and legend jump straight to their new state. Hover dimming still applies because it carries meaning, not motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sizing segments as a share of `used` instead of `capacity`. The bar then always looks full and the free space disappears.\n- Rendering a separate \"free\" segment. The track already is the free space, and an extra element breaks the 1px gap rhythm at the end.\n- Hiding the collapsed legend with `height: 0` alone. Its rows stay focusable and announced; `inert` on the wrapper is what removes them.\n- Animating `flex-grow` instead of `width`. Grow values are relative to each other, so one segment changing reflows all the others in the same frame.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `formatTokensCompact` is exported so surrounding UI (a composer badge, a toast) can print counts the same way.\n- Colours come from the kit chart palette (`bg-chart-1…9`) plus `bg-accent-500` for the leading blue, so both themes are handled by the token sheet.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe header reads \"820k / 1M (82%)\" for the demo data and the bar fills 82% of its track in seven colours with 1px gaps. Pressing the header toggle flips aria-expanded and shows or hides the legend; when hidden, Tab skips its rows. Hovering \"System tools\" in the legend dims every other segment. \"Add a turn\" grows the blue segment and the totals; \"Compact\" shrinks it back with an eased width change.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "contributions-card": "# Build: Contributions Card\n\nA grey dashboard card with a yearly total, a change chip and four stat tiles above a contributions calendar: one rounded cell per day, seven weekday rows by up to 53 week columns, coloured in five intensity steps of an accent family. Weekly, Monthly and Yearly tabs regroup the same counts into days, weeks of each month or whole months across years, at the same grid height. Hovering a cell or moving to it with the arrow keys rings it and shows a tooltip with the count and the date. Everything is a CSS grid of spans, with no chart library and no Date objects.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA GitHub-style contributions card: a week by weekday calendar in five intensity steps, stat tiles, month labels and nine accent palettes.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Counts are plain arrays, one number per day of the year. Calendar positions come from integer arithmetic: Sakamoto's formula gives the weekday of Jan 1, a month-length table gives each day its month and date, and a day at index i sits in row `(offset + i) % 7` and column `floor((offset + i) / 7)`. Without `Date` there is no time zone, so the server and the client always agree.\n2. The view picks a matrix of cells. Weekly is 7 rows by `ceil((offset + days) / 7)` weeks, with `null` placeholders before Jan 1 and after Dec 31. Monthly sums days 1–7, 8–14, 15–21, 22–28 and 29–31 of each month into 5 rows by 12 columns. Yearly sums each month into one row per year.\n3. A cell's tier is 0 for no activity, else `ceil((count / peak) ** 0.7 * 5)` clamped to 1–5, where peak is the busiest cell in the current view. The 0.7 power lifts the many quiet days off tier 1 so a single spike does not wash the year out. The cell carries it as `data-tier`, and the theme's `.contributions-grid[data-accent]` rules map tiers 0–5 to the neutral chart colour and the 200, 400, 500, 600 and 700 shades of the accent (950 to 500 in dark mode). Swapping `accent` recolours the whole grid with one attribute.\n4. A ResizeObserver measures the grid. The weekly view keeps the most recent weeks that fit at 8px per cell plus a 3px gap, so a phone shows about half a year and a 640px grid shows all 53 weeks. Rows are as tall as the weekly cells are wide, clamped to 8–14px, and every view shares that seven-row height so switching tabs never resizes the card.\n5. Month labels sit in an extra grid row with `grid-column-start` set to the week the month begins in. In the weekly view a label is dropped when the next month starts less than 3 columns later.\n6. Cells pop in with `.animate-cell-pop`, delayed by `min(k * 9 + r * 12, 520)` ms so the sweep runs left to right. The grid is keyed by the view, so each tab switch replays it.\n7. The grid is one tab stop (`role=\"group\"`). Arrow keys move the active cell and skip placeholders, Home and End jump to the first and last day, and Escape, blur or leaving with the pointer clears it. The active cell gets a 2px outline and `z-10`, and holds a tooltip with the count and a label such as \"Wed, Jan 1, 2025\". A polite live line under the grid repeats it for screen readers.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MIN_CELL / GAP = 8px / 3px`\n  The smallest cell that still reads as a square with a visible gap. At this pitch a typical 640px card fits all 53 weeks.\n- `tiers = 0 + 5 steps, ceil((count / peak)^0.7 × 5)`\n  Empty days stay neutral grey, and any activity at all lands in at least tier 1, so a single contribution is never invisible.\n- `ROW_HEIGHT = 8–14px`\n  Weekly rows track the cell width so cells stay square; the clamp stops a wide card from growing a tall calendar.\n- `label spacing = ≥ 3 weeks`\n  A month that starts within two columns of the next would print its label on top of the neighbour, as happens when a year starts on a Saturday.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ContributionsCard(props?: ContributionsCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype ContributionYear = { year: number; counts: number[] }\ntype ContributionAccent = \"emerald\" | \"green\" | \"teal\" | \"cyan\" | \"blue\" | \"indigo\" | \"violet\" | \"rose\" | \"amber\"\ntype ContributionView = \"weekly\" | \"monthly\" | \"yearly\"\nconst CONTRIBUTION_YEARS: ContributionYear[]\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Contributions this year\"`\n  Header label.\n- `years: ContributionYear[]` — default `CONTRIBUTION_YEARS`\n  Daily counts per year, oldest first. The last year is the one on show; the Yearly view lists them all.\n- `accent: ContributionAccent` — default `\"violet\"`\n  Accent family for tiers 1–5.\n- `change: number` — default `change against the previous year`\n  Percent for the chip.\n- `stats: ContributionStat[] | false` — default `derived tiles`\n  Tiles under the header, or false to hide them.\n- `view: ContributionView`\n  Controlled granularity.\n- `defaultView: ContributionView` — default `\"weekly\"`\n  Uncontrolled starting granularity.\n- `onViewChange: (view: ContributionView) => void`\n  Called when a tab is picked.\n- `unit: [string, string]` — default `[\"contribution\", \"contributions\"]`\n  Singular and plural noun for the tooltip.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst summary = useMemo(() => {\n  const total = current.counts.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\n  const previous = years[years.length - 2];\n  const prevTotal = previous ? previous.counts.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) : 0;\n  let best = 0;\n  let bestAt = 0;\n  let streak = 0;\n  let longest = 0;\n  current.counts.forEach((n, i) => {\n    if (n > best) [best, bestAt] = [n, i];\n    streak = n > 0 ? streak + 1 : 0;\n    longest = Math.max(longest, streak);\n  });\n  const { m, d } = monthDay(current.year, bestAt);\n  const derived: ContributionStat[] = [\n    { label: \"Active days\", value: String(current.counts.filter((n) => n > 0).length) },\n    { label: `Best day · ${MONTHS[m]} ${d}`, value: String(best) },\n    { label: \"Longest streak\", value: `${longest} days` },\n    { label: \"Daily average\", value: numberFormat.format(total / Math.max(1, current.counts.length)) },\n  ];\n  return { total, change: prevTotal > 0 ? Math.round(((total - prevTotal) / prevTotal) * 1000) / 10 : undefined, derived };\n}, [current, years]);\n\nconst matrix = useMemo(\n  () => (view === \"weekly\" ? weeklyMatrix(current) : view === \"monthly\" ? monthlyMatrix(current) : yearlyMatrix(years)),\n  [view, current, years],\n);\n\n// Weekly view keeps the most recent weeks that fit at MIN_CELL; the other views always fit.\nconst allCols = matrix.rows[0]?.length ?? 0;\nconst weeks = Math.ceil((weekday(current.year, 1, 1) + (isLeap(current.year) ? 366 : 365)) / 7);\nconst weekCols = width > 0 ? Math.max(1, Math.min(weeks, Math.floor((width + GAP) / (MIN_CELL + GAP)))) : weeks;\nconst cols = view === \"weekly\" ? weekCols : allCols;\nconst first = allCols - cols;\n// Every view shares the height of seven square weekly rows, so switching never resizes the card.\nconst weekCell = width > 0 ? (width - (weekCols - 1) * GAP) / weekCols : 11;\nconst weekRow = Math.max(ROW_HEIGHT[0], Math.min(ROW_HEIGHT[1], weekCell));\nconst gridHeight = 7 * weekRow + 6 * GAP;\nconst rowHeight = (gridHeight - (matrix.rows.length - 1) * GAP) / matrix.rows.length;\n\nconst peak = Math.max(1, ...matrix.rows.flat().map((cell) => cell?.count ?? 0));\nconst tierOf = (count: number) => (count <= 0 ? 0 : Math.min(5, Math.max(1, Math.ceil((count / peak) ** 0.7 * 5))));\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useResizeObserver` disconnects its observer on unmount. Everything else is CSS and React event props, so nothing else needs tearing down.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the cells appear at once (`motion-reduce:animate-none` on `.animate-cell-pop`, which the theme also disables), and the tab colour change is instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building the calendar with `new Date(year, month, day)`. Local time zones and DST shift days across the server and the client and cause hydration mismatches; integer weekday arithmetic is exact everywhere.\n- Scaling tiers by the year's peak in every view. A month total is about thirty times a day, so each view needs its own peak or the Monthly view goes solid dark.\n- Leaving the hovered cell at the default stacking order. The pop animation gives every cell its own stacking context, so later cells paint over the tooltip unless the active cell gets `z-10`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `CONTRIBUTION_YEARS` (2023–2025, seeded with `createRng`) and the `ContributionYear`, `ContributionAccent`, `ContributionView` and `ContributionStat` types are exported.\n- Pass `stats` to replace the derived tiles (Active days, Best day, Longest streak, Daily average), or `stats={false}` to hide them.\n- The accent families come from the theme: emerald, green, teal, cyan, blue, indigo, violet, rose and amber.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe default card reads \"Contributions this year\", \"529\" and a lime \"+12.6%\" chip, four stat tiles (191 active days, best day 10 on Jun 26, a 14-day streak, 1.4 a day), an \"Activity\" row with Weekly, Monthly and Yearly tabs, and a violet 7-row calendar with Jan–Dec labels and a Less → More legend. Hovering a cell rings it and shows a count and a date such as \"Wed, Jan 1, 2025\". Monthly shows 5 × 12 week cells and Yearly shows three year rows. Picking another swatch in the demo recolours the grid and the legend.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "converging-flow-steps": "# Build: Converging Flow Steps\n\nA process diagram that assembles itself as you scroll. The two outer cards converge toward the middle from opposite ends while the connectors between them pop in at 35% through the timeline — after the cards are already moving. That ordering is the entire effect: connectors that arrive first read as \"boxes with arrows\", connectors that arrive during the movement read as \"these things connect\".\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA process diagram that closes from both ends while the connectors pop in a third of the way through.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One `gsap.timeline` on a `scrollTrigger` with `start: \"top 92%\"`, `end: \"center 52%\"` and `scrub: 1.2` — deliberately the slowest scrub in the set, because a diagram should settle rather than track the wheel.\n2. The first and last cards get `fromTo` tweens at position 0 with `±14` on the layout axis, so they close toward the centre from opposite ends. Cards in between have nowhere to converge from and only fade; giving them a direction too makes the row look shuffled rather than closing.\n3. The connectors get `fromTo({ autoAlpha: 0, scale: 0.6 }, { autoAlpha: 1, scale: 1, stagger: 0.2 })` placed at absolute timeline position `0.35`. The card tweens run 0 → 1, so 0.35 is a third of the way into their travel.\n4. The convergence axis is chosen from the measured container width, not the viewport: under `stackBelow` px the row becomes a column and the tweens switch from `xPercent` to `yPercent`. A stacked column with horizontal convergence has cards sliding past each other sideways.\n5. The single-step case is handled explicitly — `first === last` would otherwise mean two `fromTo` tweens fighting over one element, one pulling it from -14 and one from +14.\n6. `ease: \"none\"` throughout. Under a scrub the ease applies to timeline time, so easing here desynchronises the two converging cards from each other.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `scrub = 1.2`\n  Over a second of catch-up — very slow. Right for a diagram, which you want to see settle; wrong for a hero, which would feel unresponsive. Below ~0.6 the diagram snaps together as fast as you scroll and the assembly reads as a jump.\n- `connectorAt = 0.35`\n  Absolute timeline position, in seconds, where the connectors start. The cards run 0 → 1, so this is a third of the way into their travel. At 0 the arrows are present before there is anything to connect; past ~0.6 they arrive after everything has stopped and read as an afterthought.\n- `converge = 14`\n  Percent of the card's own size. Small on purpose: this is a settling gesture, not an entrance. Past ~25% the outer cards start visibly outside the tinted wrapper and the diagram looks broken at the start of the scrub.\n- `CONNECTOR_SCALE = 0.6`\n  Starting scale for the pop. Below ~0.4 the arrow is a dot for the first half of its tween; above ~0.8 there is no pop left to see.\n- `connectorStagger = 0.2`\n  Seconds between connectors. Long enough that a three-step flow reads left to right rather than all at once, which is what tells the reader the diagram has a direction.\n- `stackBelow = 420`\n  Container width in px below which three cards in a row are too narrow to hold a title and a line of body. Measured from the element, not the window, because this component is usually inside a column that is narrow while the window is wide.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ConvergingFlowSteps(props: { steps: { title: string; body?: string; kicker?: string }[]; converge?: number; scrub?: number; connectorAt?: number; connectorStagger?: number; stackBelow?: number; start?: string; end?: string; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `steps: { title: string; body?: string; kicker?: string }[]`\n  Ordered steps. Connectors are inserted between consecutive pairs.\n- `converge: number` — default `14`\n  Outer-card travel as a percentage of its own size, along the layout axis.\n- `scrub: number` — default `1.2`\n  Seconds of catch-up behind the scroll position.\n- `connectorAt: number` — default `0.35`\n  Timeline position, in seconds, at which the connectors begin.\n- `connectorStagger: number` — default `0.2`\n  Seconds between connectors.\n- `stackBelow: number` — default `420`\n  Container width in px below which the row becomes a column and the axis flips.\n- `start: string` — default `'top 92%'`\n  ScrollTrigger start.\n- `end: string` — default `'center 52%'`\n  ScrollTrigger end.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst CONNECTOR_SCALE = 0.6;\n\nexport function ConvergingFlowSteps({\n  steps,\n  converge = 14,\n  scrub = 1.2,\n  connectorAt = 0.35,\n  connectorStagger = 0.2,\n  stackBelow = 420,\n  start = 'top 92%',\n  end = 'center 52%',\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: ConvergingFlowStepsProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { width } = useResizeObserver(root);\n\n  // The convergence axis has to follow the layout axis, or a stacked column\n  // gets cards sliding in sideways past each other. Measured from the element\n  // rather than the viewport: this component is often inside a panel that is\n  // narrow while the window is wide.\n  const stacked = width > 0 && width < stackBelow;\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const container = root.current;\n      if (!container || reduced) return;\n\n      const cards = Array.from(container.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-flow-card]'));\n      const connectors = Array.from(\n        container.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-flow-connector]'),\n      );\n      const first = cards[0];\n      const last = cards[cards.length - 1];\n      if (!first || !last) return;\n\n      const axis = stacked ? 'yPercent' : 'xPercent';\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({\n        scrollTrigger: {\n          trigger: container,\n          scroller: scrollerRef?.current ?? undefined,\n          start,\n          end,\n          scrub,\n          invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n        },\n      });\n\n      // Outer cards travel toward the centre from opposite ends. When there is\n      // only one card, `first === last` and the two tweens would fight over\n      // the same target, so it takes the middle treatment instead.\n      if (first === last) {\n        tl.fromTo(first, { autoAlpha: 0 }, { autoAlpha: 1, ease: 'none', duration: 1 }, 0);\n      } else {\n        tl.fromTo(\n          first,\n          { autoAlpha: 0, [axis]: -converge },\n          { autoAlpha: 1, [axis]: 0, ease: 'none', duration: 1 },\n          0,\n        );\n        tl.fromTo(\n          last,\n          { autoAlpha: 0, [axis]: converge },\n          { autoAlpha: 1, [axis]: 0, ease: 'none', duration: 1 },\n          0,\n        );\n      }\n\n      // Everything between the ends has nowhere to converge from, so it only\n      // fades. Giving it a direction too would make the row look like it was\n      // being shuffled rather than closing.\n      const middle = cards.slice(1, -1);\n      if (middle.length > 0) {\n        tl.fromTo(middle, { autoAlpha: 0 }, { autoAlpha: 1, ease: 'none', duration: 1 }, 0);\n      }\n\n      if (connectors.length > 0) {\n        tl.fromTo(\n          connectors,\n          { autoAlpha: 0, scale: CONNECTOR_SCALE },\n          {\n            autoAlpha: 1,\n            scale: 1,\n            ease: 'none',\n            duration: 1 - connectorAt,\n            stagger: connectorStagger,\n          },\n          // Absolute timeline position in seconds. The cards occupy 0 -> 1, so\n          // 0.35 puts the first connector a third of the way into their\n          // travel: they are already moving when the link appears.\n          connectorAt,\n        );\n      }\n    },\n    root,\n    [converge, scrub, connectorAt, connectorStagger, start, end, stacked, reduced, steps.length],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` reverts the `gsap.context` on unmount and whenever the layout axis or any timing prop changes. That kills the ScrollTrigger and strips the inline transform and opacity from every card and connector, which matters here because the axis flip rebuilds the whole timeline on resize and a stale trigger would keep writing `xPercent` to elements now laid out in a column.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No timeline is built, so the diagram renders assembled: cards at their layout positions, connectors at full opacity and scale. The layout still responds to container width, because that is layout rather than motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating the connectors at position 0 alongside the cards. Everything arriving together reads as a set of boxes that happen to have arrows between them; the whole point is that the link appears while the boxes are still closing.\n- Giving the middle cards a convergence direction too. With every card sliding, the row reads as being reshuffled rather than closing, and the two outer cards stop being the subject.\n- Not handling the one-step case. `cards[0]` and `cards[cards.length - 1]` are the same element, so it receives two `fromTo` tweens with opposite start values and ends up wherever the second one decided.\n- Choosing the layout axis from `window.innerWidth`. The component is usually inside a content column; a wide window with a narrow column stacks the cards visually while the tweens still converge horizontally, so they cross each other on the way in.\n- Using `opacity` rather than `autoAlpha` on the connectors. An invisible SVG still takes hit-testing area between two cards, which quietly swallows clicks aimed at the card edges.\n- Assuming `stagger` inside a scrubbed timeline behaves like duration. Each connector's start is offset in timeline seconds, so a large stagger pushes the timeline total past the card tweens and the cards finish before the scroll range ends — which is intended here, but is a surprise if you assumed the total was 1.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The wrapper tint is derived from the accent token rather than a second hard-coded colour, so re-theming the accent moves the panel with it.\n- Connectors are inline SVG paths, so they inherit `currentColor` and need no asset.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the diagram into view: the leftmost and rightmost cards slide toward the middle, and only once they are moving do the arrows between them scale up one after the other. Stop scrolling mid-way and the diagram keeps settling for more than a second. Narrow the container below 420px and the same sequence plays vertically with the arrows rotated. With reduced motion on, the diagram is complete before it scrolls into view.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "converging-scrub-words": "# Build: Converging Headline Words\n\nA stacked headline assembles out of the margins as you approach it. Odd lines enter from the right, even lines from the left, and both land on zero at the same scrub position, so they cross the frame in opposite directions and lock into a sentence. Because the tween is scrubbed, scrolling back up disassembles it — the headline is a function of scroll position, not a playback that has happened or not happened.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nHeadline words slide in from opposite margins and lock into a sentence, pinned to scroll position.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each word is a block-level span with `data-word`. A single `gsap.timeline` with a `scrollTrigger` holds one `fromTo` per word, all inserted at position `0`, so every word shares the timeline's full length and therefore the full scroll range.\n2. The travel is `xPercent`, not `x`: percent of *the element's own width*, so a short word and a long word both start entirely outside the frame and neither needs a hand-tuned pixel value.\n3. Direction alternates on index parity — `i % 2 === 0 ? -travel : travel` — and the text alignment alternates with it, so each word enters from the side it is aligned to and travels the shortest distance to its resting place.\n4. `ease: \"none\"` on every tween. Under `scrub` the scroll position *is* the easing curve; layering `power2.out` on top means the words accelerate away from the scrollbar and then wait for it, which is precisely the sensation people describe as the scroll feeling sticky.\n5. `fromTo`, not `to`. A bare `to` reads its start value from the DOM, and the DOM already says `xPercent: 0` because the rendered markup is the assembled headline — that is what the no-JS and reduced-motion renders show.\n6. `gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger)` is called inside the effect rather than at module scope, because `registerPlugin` touches `window` and this module is still evaluated during server rendering.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TRAVEL = 120`\n  Percent of each word's own width. At exactly 100 the word starts flush against the frame edge and is already peeking in on the first pixel of scroll; the extra 20% buys a moment of empty margin before it arrives.\n- `SCRUB = 1`\n  One second of catch-up between the scrollbar and the words. `scrub: true` welds them exactly, which on a trackpad exposes every jitter in the input; past ~1.5s the headline is still assembling well after the reader has stopped scrolling.\n- `START = 'top bottom'`\n  The scrub begins the instant the headline's top edge enters the viewport. Starting later means the words are still outside the frame when the headline is already half on screen, which reads as missing content rather than as an entrance.\n- `END = 'top 25%'`\n  Assembly completes when the headline top reaches the upper quarter of the viewport. Ending at centre leaves the headline unresolved while it is the dominant thing on screen; ending earlier compresses the whole effect into the bottom sliver of the viewport.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ConvergingScrubWords(props: { words: string[]; travel?: number; scrub?: number; start?: string; end?: string; scroller?: Element | null; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `words: string[]`\n  One per line. Even indices enter from the left, odd from the right.\n- `travel: number` — default `120`\n  Percent of each word's own width that it starts offset by.\n- `scrub: number` — default `1`\n  Seconds of catch-up between the scrollbar and the words.\n- `start: string` — default `'top bottom'`\n  ScrollTrigger `start`.\n- `end: string` — default `'top 25%'`\n  ScrollTrigger `end`.\n- `scroller: Element | null` — default `null`\n  Scroll container, when the headline is not in the document scroller.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn` — type size and tracking go here.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TRAVEL = 120;\nconst SCRUB = 1;\nconst START = 'top bottom';\nconst END = 'top 25%';\n\nexport function ConvergingScrubWords({\n  words,\n  travel = TRAVEL,\n  scrub = SCRUB,\n  start = START,\n  end = END,\n  scroller = null,\n  className,\n}: ConvergingScrubWordsProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  /* The words by content, not by array identity. A caller writes\n     `words={['Between', 'Signals']}` inline, which is a new array on every\n     parent render — and this component's parent re-renders often, because the\n     preview host's `useInView` sets state on every IntersectionObserver entry\n     and there are five thresholds. With the array itself in the dep list below,\n     a single scroll past the card tore the timeline and its ScrollTrigger down\n     and rebuilt them up to five times, each rebuild forcing a layout-reading\n     `refresh()` and visibly resetting the scrub mid-gesture. The effect never\n     reads `words` anyway — it works off the rendered `[data-word]` nodes — so\n     content is the only thing that should re-run it. */\n  // Joined on an escaped NUL rather than a space: ['a b'] and ['a', 'b']\n  // render a different number of nodes and must not share a key.\n  const wordsKey = words.join('\\u0000');\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    // Reduced motion: the JSX below already renders the assembled headline at\n    // its final position, so declining to attach the trigger is the whole\n    // implementation. Nothing is hidden and nothing is left off-screen.\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n\n    // Registered here rather than at module scope: registerPlugin touches\n    // window, and this file is still evaluated during server rendering.\n    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);\n\n    const wordEls = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-word]'));\n    if (wordEls.length === 0) return;\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({\n        scrollTrigger: {\n          trigger: el,\n          scroller: scroller ?? undefined,\n          start,\n          end,\n          scrub,\n        },\n      });\n\n      wordEls.forEach((w, i) => {\n        // fromTo, not to. A bare `to` would read the start value off the DOM,\n        // and the DOM already says 0 because the inline style below is the\n        // final position for the no-JS and reduced-motion renders.\n        tl.fromTo(\n          w,\n          { xPercent: i % 2 === 0 ? -travel : travel },\n          { xPercent: 0, ease: 'none' },\n          0,\n        );\n      });\n    }, el);\n\n    return () => ctx.revert();\n    // `wordsKey` stands in for `words`, which the effect never reads.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [wordsKey, travel, scrub, start, end, scroller, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `ctx.revert()` on the `gsap.context` scoped to the root. That kills the timeline, disposes the ScrollTrigger instance (so it stops measuring a detached element and stops contributing to `ScrollTrigger.refresh()`), and strips the inline transforms GSAP wrote.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No ScrollTrigger is attached and no tween is created. The markup React renders is already the assembled headline at `xPercent: 0`, so the finished sentence is on screen from first paint — nothing is off-screen waiting for a scroll that will not drive it.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using an ease other than `none` under `scrub`. The words lead or lag the scrollbar and the page feels like it is fighting the input — the single most common complaint about scrubbed animation.\n- Using `to` instead of `fromTo`. GSAP samples the current DOM value as the start, which is the resting position, so nothing moves — or worse, it moves only after a resize invalidates the cached start.\n- Animating `x` in pixels. A word that is 400px wide and one that is 120px wide need different offsets to clear the frame; `xPercent` is self-scaling and survives a font-size change.\n- Setting `overflow: hidden` on the container. It stops the horizontal scrollbar, but CSS forces `overflow-y` to `auto` when one axis is `hidden`, which silently breaks any sticky ancestor and clips descenders. `overflow-x: clip` does the same job without that side effect.\n- Omitting the `scroller` option when the headline lives inside a scrolling panel rather than the document. ScrollTrigger measures against the window, so the trigger fires at the wrong time or never.\n- Registering ScrollTrigger at module scope. `registerPlugin` reaches for `window` and throws during server rendering.\n- Cleaning up with `kill()` on the timeline only. The ScrollTrigger instance survives and keeps measuring a detached element; `ctx.revert()` disposes both and strips the inline transforms.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No text-splitting library: the words arrive as an array prop and are rendered as spans by React. A splitter would add a dependency and a DOM-mutation lifecycle for something the caller already has as data.\n- If you feed this component text you split elsewhere, gate the split on `document.fonts.ready` — measuring before the webfont lands gives fallback-font line breaks that never re-flow. See `font-safe-text-split`.\n- The `scroller` prop exists so the component can be demoed and used inside an `overflow-y-auto` panel rather than only in the document scroller.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll down slowly: the words travel inward from alternating sides and are exactly aligned when the headline top reaches the upper quarter of the viewport. Scroll back up and they separate again, tracking the scrollbar. No horizontal scrollbar appears on the document at any point. With reduced motion enabled the assembled headline is present on first paint and does not move.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "copy-page-button": "# Build: Copy Page Button\n\nThe \"Copy Page\" control that sits beside a docs page title. One bordered 36px shell is split by a 1px rule into a copy button and a chevron trigger. The copy half writes the page Markdown to the clipboard and swaps its icon and label to a lime check and \"Copied\" for a moment. The chevron opens the kit Dropdown with \"Copy as Markdown\", \"View as Markdown\" and any extra actions you pass.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDocs split button: the main half copies the page as Markdown and flips to Copied, the chevron opens Copy and View as Markdown.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `markdown` is a string or a function, sync or async, resolved at click time by `resolveMarkdown`. A page can then build or fetch its Markdown only when someone asks for it. A `busy` ref drops repeat clicks while an async source is still pending.\n2. The resolved text goes through `useCopyToClipboard` from `code-block-copy`. It tries the async Clipboard API, falls back to a hidden textarea with `execCommand(\"copy\")`, and sets `copied` for `resetMs` (1600ms). The copy icon scales to 50% and fades out while the check scales in, and the label switches to `copiedLabel`.\n3. The chevron half is a `DropdownTrigger` inside a `Dropdown` whose open state is held here, controlled or uncontrolled. The chevron can then rotate 180 degrees while the menu is open. The `DropdownPopover` opens at `bottom end` with a 6px offset and 288px width, so its right edge lines up with the button.\n4. Each menu row closes the menu first and then runs its action. \"Copy as Markdown\" uses the same `copyPage` path, so the main half shows the copied state even though the click happened in the menu. \"View as Markdown\" becomes a real `<a target=\"_blank\">` when `markdownHref` is set. Otherwise it calls `onViewMarkdown(text)`, or, as the fallback, opens a `text/plain` Blob URL in a new tab and revokes it after 60s.\n5. A visually hidden `role=\"status\"` span announces \"Page copied as Markdown\" while `copied` is true, because the visible label change alone is not read out.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `shell = h-9, rounded-2lg (10px), 1px border-button-default, shadow-xs`\n  This matches the kit secondary Button at medium size, so the split button lines up with other toolbar buttons.\n- `chevron half = w-9 (36px square)`\n  It gives the trigger a square hit area the same height as the shell, like an icon-only medium button.\n- `resetMs = 1600`\n  Long enough to read \"Copied\", short enough that a second copy still gives fresh feedback.\n- `menu = placement bottom end, offset 6, w-[288px]`\n  The button sits at the right of the title row, so the panel aligns to its end edge and stays inside the content column.\n- `blob revoke = 60_000 ms`\n  The new tab needs the object URL alive while it loads. Revoking at once would show a blank tab in some browsers.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CopyPageButton(props: { markdown: string | (() => string | Promise<string>); markdownHref?: string; onViewMarkdown?: (markdown: string) => void; actions?: readonly { id: string; label: string; description?: string; icon?: ComponentType; onAction: () => void }[]; label?: string; copiedLabel?: string; resetMs?: number; onCopied?: (markdown: string) => void; isMenuOpen?: boolean; onMenuOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `markdown: string | (() => string | Promise<string>)`\n  The page as Markdown, or a function resolved at click time.\n- `markdownHref: string`\n  URL of the raw Markdown. \"View as Markdown\" becomes a new-tab link.\n- `onViewMarkdown: (markdown: string) => void`\n  Custom \"View as Markdown\" handler, used when no `markdownHref` is set.\n- `actions: readonly CopyPageAction[]` — default `[]`\n  Extra menu rows under the built-in two.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Copy Page\"`\n  Main button label.\n- `copiedLabel: string` — default `\"Copied\"`\n  Label while the copied state lasts.\n- `resetMs: number` — default `1600`\n  Duration of the copied state in ms.\n- `onCopied: (markdown: string) => void`\n  Called after a successful copy.\n- `isMenuOpen / onMenuOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void` — default `uncontrolled`\n  Controlled menu state.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst copyPage = async () => {\n  // Ignore double clicks while an async source is still resolving.\n  if (busy.current) return;\n  busy.current = true;\n  try {\n    const text = await resolveMarkdown(markdown);\n    if (await copy(text)) onCopied?.(text);\n  } finally {\n    busy.current = false;\n  }\n};\n\nconst viewMarkdown = async () => {\n  setOpen(false);\n  if (markdownHref) return; // the anchor navigates on its own\n  const text = await resolveMarkdown(markdown);\n  if (onViewMarkdown) onViewMarkdown(text);\n  else openMarkdownBlob(text);\n};\n\nconst runFromMenu = (action: () => void | Promise<void>) => () => {\n  setOpen(false);\n  void action();\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The copied-state timeout belongs to `useCopyToClipboard` and is cleared on unmount and before each new copy. The Blob URL fallback revokes its object URL after 60 seconds. Outside-press listeners belong to the kit Dropdown and go away when it closes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The icon cross-fade, the chevron rotation and the hover colours all carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`. The kit Dropdown panel skips its fade, scale and blur. Under reduced motion the state still changes, only without animation.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Awaiting a slow network fetch before `clipboard.writeText`. Safari drops the user activation after a long await and rejects the write. Keep async sources fast, or pass a string.\n- Leaving the menu open after \"Copy as Markdown\". The copied feedback is on the main button, so the menu has to close first or the reader never sees it.\n- Rendering \"View as Markdown\" as a button that calls `window.open` with a server URL. A plain anchor keeps middle-click and \"open in new tab\" working, which is why `markdownHref` renders an `<a>`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `actions` appends rows such as \"Open in ChatGPT\" or \"Copy page link\". Each row closes the menu before its `onAction` runs.\n- The copy logic is shared with the Code Block copy button through `useCopyToClipboard` in `code-block-copy.tsx`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking \"Copy Page\" puts the exact Markdown on the clipboard, and the button reads \"Copied\" with a lime check for about 1.6s before going back. Clicking the chevron opens a menu below the button, aligned to its right edge, with \"Copy as Markdown\" and \"View as Markdown\", and the chevron points up. Choosing \"Copy as Markdown\" closes the menu and shows the same copied state. Choosing \"View as Markdown\" closes the menu and calls `onViewMarkdown` with the text, or opens `markdownHref` in a new tab. Escape closes the menu.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cpu-mirror-picking": "# Build: Raycaster-Free Hover Picking\n\nA ring of plates spins entirely on the compositor, so the main thread has no idea where anything is. Instead of measuring the DOM, the component recomputes the same transform in JavaScript — rotate, tilt, perspective divide — and hit-tests the resulting screen points against the pointer. A crosshair marks the computed position so you can see the mirror agreeing with the pixels.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRun the GPU's transform again on the CPU and hit-test the result. No raycaster, no rect reads.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The spin is one `Element.animate` keyframe pair with `iterations: Infinity`. It runs off the main thread, which means it keeps time even while JS is busy, and means the main thread must reconstruct the angle rather than remember it: `animation.currentTime` divided by the period gives the exact phase the compositor is drawing.\n2. The mirror is four lines that shadow the CSS one for one. Two nested `rotateY` (ring spin, plate angle) collapse into a single angle sum, so a plate centre sits at `(r·sin a, 0, r·cos a)`. The stage `rotateX` turns that depth into vertical offset. The perspective divide `d / (d − z)` reproduces what the browser does with the `perspective` property.\n3. Picking runs once per frame, not per `pointermove`. The geometry moves under a stationary cursor, so the correct answer changes on frames where no input arrived at all. An event-driven hit test would leave the hover state stuck on whatever plate happened to be there when the pointer last moved.\n4. Plates with `z <= 0` are rejected before the distance compare. They are on the far side of the ring, drawn behind the near plates, and picking them would let a click pass through the front of the object.\n5. The winner is the nearest projected point within a squared pick radius. Squared, because a comparison against `r²` gives the same ordering as a comparison against `r` without the square root.\n6. This is the only correct approach when a vertex shader, a CSS transform, or GPU skinning moves the geometry after it leaves JavaScript. The CPU-side geometry never changed, so `Raycaster` intersects the undeformed mesh and reports hits on a shape that is not on screen. Mirroring the vertex transform on the CPU is the fix — the same maths, applied to one point instead of every vertex.\n7. Everything inside the perspective root is `pointer-events: none`. There is nothing for the browser to hit-test; the container takes the pointer and the mirror decides what was under it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PERSPECTIVE = 900`\n  Shared by the CSS `perspective` property and the JS divide. If the two ever disagree the crosshair drifts away from the plate it is meant to mark — which makes this the single most important value to keep in one place.\n- `TILT = 16`\n  Degrees of stage lean. Enough that the ring reads as a ring rather than a row; past ~30° the far plates hide behind the near ones and picking gets ambiguous even with the depth rejection.\n- `PICK_RADIUS = 44`\n  The pointer must land within 44 px of a projected centre. Matches the 44 px minimum touch target, and is wide enough to forgive the plate scaling down as it recedes.\n- `PICK_RADIUS_SQ = PICK_RADIUS * PICK_RADIUS`\n  Compared against squared distance so the loop never calls `Math.sqrt`. Fourteen square roots a frame is not a real cost, but the squared compare is free and is the habit worth having in a loop that might hold a thousand points.\n- `RADIUS_RATIO = 0.36`\n  Ring radius as a fraction of container width. Keeps the ring inside the box at its widest and leaves room for the pick-radius halo at the edges.\n- `SPIN_PERIOD = 26`\n  Seconds per revolution. Slow enough that a plate stays under the cursor long enough to click, fast enough that the per-frame re-pick is visibly necessary.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CpuMirrorPicking(props: { count?: number; period?: number; showMirror?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `14`\n  Plates around the ring.\n- `period: number` — default `26`\n  Seconds per revolution. The mirror divides the animation clock by this.\n- `showMirror: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draw the computed crosshair and the pick-radius halo.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the perspective root via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Must match the CSS `perspective` exactly — the mirror divides by this. */\nconst PERSPECTIVE = 900;\n/** Stage lean, degrees. Matches the CSS `rotateX`. */\nconst TILT = 16;\nconst SIN_TILT = Math.sin((TILT * Math.PI) / 180);\nconst COS_TILT = Math.cos((TILT * Math.PI) / 180);\n/** Pointer-to-plate distance that counts as a hit, in CSS pixels. */\nconst PICK_RADIUS = 44;\nconst PICK_RADIUS_SQ = PICK_RADIUS * PICK_RADIUS;\nconst RADIUS_RATIO = 0.36;\nconst MIN_RADIUS = 64;\nconst MAX_RADIUS = 190;\nconst SPIN_PERIOD = 26;\n\ninterface Projected {\n  x: number;\n  y: number;\n  z: number;\n  scale: number;\n}\n\n/**\n * The mirror. Every line here has a counterpart in the element's CSS:\n *\n *   ul   transform: rotateY(spin)              <- from the running animation\n *   li   transform: rotateY(theta) translateZ(radius)\n *   root perspective: PERSPECTIVE\n *   stage transform: rotateX(TILT)\n *\n * A plate's centre starts at the ring origin, so `rotateY(a) translateZ(r)`\n * puts it at (r·sin a, 0, r·cos a) with a = theta + spin. Composing the two\n * rotateY into one addition is the whole trick — the rest is the standard\n * perspective divide.\n */\nfunction project(theta: number, spin: number, radius: number, cx: number, cy: number, out: Projected): void {\n  const a = theta + spin;\n  const x = Math.sin(a) * radius;\n  const depth = Math.cos(a) * radius;\n  // rotateX(t): y' = y·cos t − z·sin t, z' = y·sin t + z·cos t. y is 0 here,\n  // so the tilt turns depth into vertical offset and shortens the ring.\n  const y = -depth * SIN_TILT;\n  const z = depth * COS_TILT;\n  // CSS perspective: everything at depth z scales by d / (d − z).\n  const scale = PERSPECTIVE / (PERSPECTIVE - z);\n  out.x = cx + x * scale;\n  out.y = cy + y * scale;\n  out.z = z;\n  out.scale = scale;\n}\n\n/**\n * `Animation.currentTime` is typed `CSSNumberish | null`: a plain number in\n * every shipping engine, but the spec permits a CSSNumericValue. Narrow it\n * rather than cast, and treat a null clock (animation not yet started) as 0.\n */\nfunction readMs(anim: Animation | null): number {\n  if (!anim) return 0;\n  const t = anim.currentTime;\n  if (t === null) return 0;\n  return typeof t === 'number' ? t : t.to('ms').value;\n}\n\nexport function CpuMirrorPicking({\n  count = 14,\n  period = SPIN_PERIOD,\n  showMirror = true,\n  className,\n}: CpuMirrorPickingProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const ring = useRef<HTMLUListElement>(null);\n  const plates = useRef<(HTMLLIElement | null)[]>([]);\n  const probe = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const halo = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n  const anim = useRef<Animation | null>(null);\n  /** Pointer in the root's own coordinate space. Null when it has left. */\n  const pointer = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const hovered = useRef(-1);\n  const scratch = useRef<Projected>({ x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, scale: 1 });\n\n  const [hover, setHover] = useState(-1);\n  const [picked, setPicked] = useState(-1);\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const size = useResizeObserver(root);\n  const radius = clamp(size.width * RADIUS_RATIO, MIN_RADIUS, MAX_RADIUS);\n\n  // The compositor owns the spin. One keyframe pair, then the main thread is\n  // out of the loop entirely — which is exactly why it has to reconstruct the\n  // angle instead of remembering it.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = ring.current;\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n    const a = el.animate(\n      [{ transform: 'rotateY(0deg)' }, { transform: 'rotateY(360deg)' }],\n      { duration: period * 1000, iterations: Infinity, easing: 'linear' },\n    );\n    anim.current = a;\n    return () => {\n      a.cancel();\n      anim.current = null;\n    };\n  }, [period, reduced]);\n\n  const readPointer = useCallback((e: ReactPointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    // One rect read per pointer event, for the container only. Reading a rect\n    // per plate per event is the version that costs fourteen forced layouts.\n    const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n    pointer.current = { x: e.clientX - rect.left, y: e.clientY - rect.top };\n  }, []);\n\n  const clearPointer = useCallback(() => {\n    pointer.current = null;\n  }, []);\n\n  useRaf((_dt) => {\n    if (size.width === 0) return;\n\n    // Picking runs per frame, not per pointermove. The ring moves under a\n    // stationary cursor, so the hit answer changes when nothing was pointed.\n    const spin = reduced ? 0 : (readMs(anim.current) / (period * 1000)) * TAU;\n    const cx = size.width / 2;\n    const cy = size.height / 2;\n    const p = pointer.current;\n    const out = scratch.current;\n\n    let best = -1;\n    let bestDist = PICK_RADIUS_SQ;\n    let bestX = 0;\n    let bestY = 0;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      project((i / count) * TAU, spin, radius, cx, cy, out);\n\n      // The projection pass already knows the depth, so the fade rides along\n      // rather than needing a second traversal.\n      const el = plates.current[i];\n      if (el) {\n        const depth = out.z / (radius * COS_TILT);\n        el.style.opacity = (0.18 + 0.82 * (depth * 0.5 + 0.5)).toFixed(3);\n      }\n\n      // z <= 0 is the far half of the ring: drawn, but behind the near plates\n      // and never the intended target of a click.\n      if (!p || out.z <= 0) continue;\n      const dx = p.x - out.x;\n      const dy = p.y - out.y;\n      const dist = dx * dx + dy * dy;\n      if (dist < bestDist) {\n        bestDist = dist;\n        best = i;\n        bestX = out.x;\n        bestY = out.y;\n      }\n    }\n\n    const marker = probe.current;\n    if (marker) {\n      marker.style.opacity = best >= 0 ? '1' : '0';\n      if (best >= 0) {\n        marker.style.transform = `translate3d(${bestX.toFixed(1)}px, ${bestY.toFixed(1)}px, 0) translate(-50%, -50%)`;\n      }\n    }\n    const cursor = halo.current;\n    if (cursor) {\n      cursor.style.opacity = p ? '1' : '0';\n      if (p) {\n        cursor.style.transform = `translate3d(${p.x.toFixed(1)}px, ${p.y.toFixed(1)}px, 0) translate(-50%, -50%)`;\n      }\n    }\n\n    if (best !== hovered.current) {\n      hovered.current = best;\n      setHover(best);\n    }\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The animation is cancelled on unmount or period change, which releases the compositor animation and nulls the ref. The frame loop unsubscribes with the hook; the shared RAF parks itself once no subscriber is left. No listeners are attached outside React.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The spin animation is never created, so the ring holds still at phase zero. Picking is unaffected — the mirror simply reads a spin of 0 — which is the point: the technique is about where things are, not about them moving.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- `getBoundingClientRect()` per plate per pointermove. That is N forced layouts per event, and the rects are wrong anyway: a compositor-driven transform does not update layout boxes, so every rect reports the plate's unanimated position.\n- Hit-testing only on `pointermove`. The ring rotates under a still cursor, so hover has to be re-evaluated every frame or it freezes on a stale plate.\n- Letting the CSS and the mirror drift apart. A tweak to `perspective`, the tilt, or the transform order in one place and not the other produces a crosshair that is subtly, maddeningly offset. Keep both reading the same constants.\n- Putting `overflow: hidden` on a `transform-style: preserve-3d` element. Overflow is a grouping property: it silently forces the subtree back to flat and the ring collapses into a line.\n- Trusting `Raycaster` against a shader-deformed mesh. The vertex shader runs on the GPU; the `BufferGeometry` in memory is the rest pose. The ray intersects the rest pose and hits nothing you can see.\n- Reading `animation.currentTime` as a number without narrowing. It is typed `CSSNumberish | null` — null before the animation starts, and permitted by spec to be a `CSSNumericValue`.\n- Forgetting the far-half rejection. Without it the ring picks through itself, and a click near the centre selects a plate on the back.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The depth fade rides along on the projection pass that was going to run anyway — one extra style write per plate, no second traversal and no extra maths.\n- The same mirror generalises: for a three.js scene, project the object's world position with `vector.project(camera)` and compare in NDC; for a shader-deformed mesh, re-run the deformation for that one vertex on the CPU. The principle is identical — hit-test the transform, not the source geometry.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHold the pointer still over a plate: the crosshair tracks the plate as it rotates away and hover releases at the pick radius, with no pointer events involved. Move to the middle of the ring and nothing on the far side lights up. Click and the plate under the crosshair — not the one under a stale rect — is the one that selects. With reduced motion the ring is static and picking still works.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "css-var-stagger-reveal": "# Build: CSS Variable Stagger Reveal\n\nA reveal system where JavaScript writes one number per element and then gets out of the way. The stagger delay is `calc(var(--kx-reveal-index) * var(--kx-reveal-step))`, so duration, travel, easing and the delay ramp all live in CSS where they can be retuned — or overridden from an unrelated stylesheet — without touching the component. The observer measures against the nearest scrollable ancestor, so the same component works inside a card, a modal, or the document.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nGroups reveal as they enter their scroll container; the stagger delay is a CSS calc over an index the JS writes once.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. On mount the component collects `[data-reveal-group]` descendants, falling back to itself as a single group, and writes `--kx-reveal-index` as an inline custom property on each `[data-reveal]` inside each group. Index restarts at zero per group, so a second group does not inherit a delay whose beginning the reader never saw.\n2. One shipped rule computes the per-element delay: `--kx-reveal-delay: calc(var(--kx-reveal-index, 0) * var(--kx-reveal-step, 90ms))`, and both the opacity and transform transitions use it as their `transition-delay`. This is the entire stagger — there is no timeline object and no rAF loop anywhere in the component.\n3. The start state (`opacity: 0`, `translate3d(0, var(--kx-reveal-distance), 0)`) is a CSS rule rather than an inline style, so it is already in effect before hydration and there is no frame of un-hidden content.\n4. An IntersectionObserver with `threshold: 0.15` and `rootMargin: \"0px 0px -8% 0px\"` sets `data-kx-revealed` on the group. The negative bottom margin shrinks the observation box up from the bottom edge, so an element must be genuinely inside the container rather than merely touching its edge — without it, a reveal fires while the element is still one pixel of its own height into view.\n5. The observer `root` is resolved in three steps: an explicit `scroller` ref if given, otherwise a walk up the ancestor chain for the first element whose computed `overflow-x` or `overflow-y` is `auto`, `scroll` or `overlay`, otherwise `null`, which is the value that means the viewport. Nothing in the component references `window`, so it behaves identically in a 320 px preview card and on a full page.\n6. Revealed groups are unobserved immediately unless `repeat` is set, so a long page is not still servicing a hundred settled groups. `repeat` instead deletes the attribute on exit, and CSS runs the same transition backwards.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `stepMs = 90`\n  Delay per index. Below ~50 ms the elements read as arriving together and the stagger is wasted; above ~140 ms a group of six takes over three quarters of a second to finish and the last line is still moving after the eye has reached it.\n- `durationMs = 700`\n  Per-element transition duration, roughly 8x the step. Keeping duration well above step is what makes the group overlap into one motion rather than resolve as six separate events.\n- `distance = 28px`\n  Travel. Small enough that the text never leaves its own line box — a reveal that starts 100 px down reads as an element flying in from elsewhere, which fights the impression that the content was always there.\n- `ease = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  An ease-out-quint. Nearly all of the travel happens in the first third, so the element appears to arrive and settle rather than to decelerate the whole way. A symmetric ease-in-out on a reveal always reads as slow.\n- `threshold = 0.15`\n  Fraction of the group that must be visible. At 0 the reveal fires on a single pixel of intersection, which for a tall group means the stagger completes before the content is on screen. Past ~0.4 a group taller than the container can never satisfy it and never reveals at all.\n- `rootMargin = 0px 0px -8% 0px`\n  Shrinks the observation box 8% up from the bottom, so the element is genuinely inside the container before it fires. Positive values here are the usual mistake: they fire the reveal below the fold and the user scrolls onto content that has already finished animating.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CssVarStaggerReveal(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; stepMs?: number; durationMs?: number; distance?: string; ease?: string; threshold?: number; rootMargin?: string; repeat?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Mark groups with `data-reveal-group` and targets with `data-reveal`. A scope with no groups is treated as one group.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container for the observer. Omit and the nearest scrollable ancestor is found by walking computed overflow.\n- `stepMs: number` — default `90`\n  Milliseconds of delay per index step. Written as `--kx-reveal-step`.\n- `durationMs: number` — default `700`\n  Transition duration. Written as `--kx-reveal-duration`.\n- `distance: string` — default `'28px'`\n  Starting vertical offset, any CSS length. Written as `--kx-reveal-distance`.\n- `ease: string` — default `'cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)'`\n  Timing function. Written as `--kx-reveal-ease`.\n- `threshold: number` — default `0.15`\n  Fraction of a group that must be visible before it reveals.\n- `rootMargin: string` — default `'0px 0px -8% 0px'`\n  Observation box inset. The negative bottom means \"genuinely in view\".\n- `repeat: boolean` — default `false`\n  Re-hide groups that leave the container. Off by default, which also lets settled groups be unobserved.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the scope element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Nearest ancestor that actually scrolls. `overflow: auto` on an element that\n * has not overflowed yet still counts — it is the container the reveal will\n * live inside once content arrives, and testing `scrollHeight > clientHeight`\n * on mount would pick the document instead and fire everything at once.\n *\n * Returns `null` for \"the viewport\", which is the IntersectionObserver\n * `root` value that means the document.\n */\nfunction nearestScrollParent(node: Element | null): Element | null {\n  let el = node?.parentElement ?? null;\n  while (el) {\n    const { overflowY, overflowX } = getComputedStyle(el);\n    const scrollable = /(auto|scroll|overlay)/.test(overflowY + overflowX);\n    if (scrollable) return el;\n    el = el.parentElement;\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nexport function CssVarStaggerReveal({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  stepMs = 90,\n  durationMs = 700,\n  distance = '28px',\n  ease = 'cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)',\n  threshold = 0.15,\n  rootMargin = '0px 0px -8% 0px',\n  repeat = false,\n  className,\n}: CssVarStaggerRevealProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl) return;\n\n    const groups = Array.from(rootEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(GROUP_SELECTOR));\n    // A subtree with no declared groups is one group: the scope itself.\n    const scopes: HTMLElement[] = groups.length > 0 ? groups : [rootEl];\n\n    // Index is per group, not per document. Two sibling groups both start\n    // their stagger at zero, so the second one does not inherit a delay the\n    // reader never sees the beginning of.\n    for (const group of scopes) {\n      const targets = group.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(REVEAL_SELECTOR);\n      targets.forEach((target, i) => {\n        target.style.setProperty('--kx-reveal-index', String(i));\n      });\n    }\n\n    const reveal = (group: HTMLElement) => {\n      group.dataset.kxRevealed = 'true';\n    };\n\n    // No observer available: reveal rather than leave the content at\n    // opacity 0 forever. A missing API must degrade to visible content.\n    if (typeof IntersectionObserver === 'undefined') {\n      for (const group of scopes) reveal(group);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // `undefined` from the prop means \"find it\"; `null` from the walk means\n    // \"the document\", which is what IntersectionObserver's root expects.\n    const container = scroller ? scroller.current : nearestScrollParent(rootEl);\n\n    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(\n      (entries) => {\n        for (const entry of entries) {\n          const target = entry.target;\n          if (!(target instanceof HTMLElement)) continue;\n          if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n            reveal(target);\n            // One-shot is the default: stop watching immediately so a long\n            // page is not still servicing a hundred settled groups.\n            if (!repeat) observer.unobserve(target);\n          } else if (repeat) {\n            delete target.dataset.kxRevealed;\n          }\n        }\n      },\n      { root: container ?? null, rootMargin, threshold },\n    );\n\n    for (const group of scopes) observer.observe(group);\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\n  }, [scroller, threshold, rootMargin, repeat]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `observer.disconnect()` in the effect teardown, which releases the observer's references to every group. The inline `--kx-reveal-index` properties and the `data-kx-revealed` attributes live on nodes React owns and are discarded with them. There is no rAF, no listener and no timer to cancel — the hoisted `<style>` is reference-counted and removed by React when the last instance unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block in the shipped rule set resolves every target to its END state — `opacity: 1`, `transform: none`, `transition: none` — so the content is simply present, with no travel and no delay ramp to sit through. The observer still runs and still sets `data-kx-revealed`, so anything else keyed off that attribute keeps working; it just has nothing to transition.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing the delay as an inline `transition-delay` per element. It works, and it moves the choreography into JavaScript where a designer cannot reach it and a stylesheet cannot override it. The point of the index-as-custom-property indirection is that JS contributes a number and CSS owns every unit.\n- Numbering targets across the whole scope instead of per group. The second group starts at index 6 and sits at a 540 ms delay, so the reader watches nothing happen for half a second after it enters view.\n- Assuming the scroll container is the window. Inside any `overflow-y: auto` panel, an observer with `root: null` compares against the viewport: everything in the panel is \"in view\" from the start, so the entire page reveals at once on mount and never staggers again.\n- Testing `scrollHeight > clientHeight` when hunting for the scroll parent. A panel that has not overflowed yet is still the container the reveal will live inside once content arrives; rejecting it picks the document and fires everything at once.\n- Putting the hidden start state in an inline style set from an effect. The server-rendered markup then paints visible, hides on hydration, and animates — a flash of correct content followed by a reveal, which is worse than no reveal.\n- Shipping a hidden-by-default rule with no escape hatch for a document that never runs scripts. `@media (scripting: none)` restores opacity and transform; without it the page is simply blank for anyone who has JS off.\n- Leaving the observer connected. It keeps a strong reference to every observed group, so an unmounted section is still measured on every scroll of the container that outlived it.\n- Adding only `[data-kx-revealed] [data-reveal]` as the revealed selector. When the scope declares no groups, the marker lands on the scope element itself, which is not a descendant of itself — so the reveal silently never applies.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Every knob is a custom property, which means a consumer can retune the whole system from any ancestor: `--kx-reveal-step`, `--kx-reveal-duration`, `--kx-reveal-distance`, `--kx-reveal-ease`. The props are conveniences that write those same properties onto the scope element.\n- This is the CSS-owned sibling of the GSAP reveal systems in the registry. Choose this one when the choreography is a fade-and-rise and the value is that a designer can retune it; choose a GSAP builder when the reveal needs scrubbing, a per-element timeline, or to be measured against a horizontal container animation.\n- The observer watches groups, not individual elements. A group that is taller than its container reveals when 15% of it is visible — if a section is very tall, wrap the parts you want revealed separately in their own `data-reveal-group`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the container: each group's children rise and fade in one after another, about 90 ms apart, and the group does not start until it is genuinely inside the box rather than touching its bottom edge. Inspect a revealed child and its computed `transition-delay` equals index x 90 ms. Set `--kx-reveal-step: 200ms` on any ancestor in dev tools and the stagger widens immediately with no re-render. With reduced motion on, everything is already visible and in place. Unmount the scroller and no observer callbacks fire.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cta-section": "# Build: CTA Section\n\nThe last section of a landing page. A fanned stack of product cards (the Stacked Card Fan, with its lower edge masked into the page and a \"See all\" pill) sits above a large two-line headline, a short paragraph and a row of actions, all centred on a full-width surface. The first time a quarter of the section scrolls into view, the visual, headline, copy and actions rise, unblur and fade in one after another. The cards stay live: hovering spreads them, clicking cycles them and the pill lays them all out.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nClosing call-to-action: a faded stack of product cards, a large headline, copy and buttons that rise in one after another on scroll.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Reveal flag: the kit `useInView` hook (shared IntersectionObserver, `once`, threshold 0.25) sets `data-shown` on the section. Each part carries the same transition class and is hidden by `group-data-[shown=false]/cta:` variants (16px lower, `opacity-0`, `blur-sm`). Flipping the one flag starts every transition.\n2. Stagger: parts are numbered in render order, skipping any that are not passed, and each gets an inline `transition-delay` of `order × 90ms`. A text-only CTA therefore starts with the headline and has no gap where the visual would be.\n3. Visual: `cards` are passed to `StackedCardFan` with `fade` (a `mask-b-from-55%` gradient mask) at 320×280px inside a 680px column. `fanProps` overrides any of its props, such as the pill label or the controlled front card.\n4. Fit: the section is a size container (`@container/cta`). Below `@2xl` the headline steps down from display-2 to display-4 and the copy from 16px to 14px, so it reads the same in a narrow preview as on a phone. `text-balance` keeps the headline lines even and `max-w-[18ch]` breaks it in two.\n5. Semantics: a `section` labelled by its `h2` through `useId`, so it appears as a named region; the action row takes any buttons or links.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `stagger = 90ms per part`\n  Close enough that the four parts read as one movement, far enough apart that the order shows.\n- `entrance = 700ms, 16px rise, blur-sm, cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  A soft settle, matching the card fan transition, without a bounce on a closing section.\n- `trigger = 25% visible, once`\n  Starts when the section is clearly arriving, and does not replay when it is scrolled past again.\n- `visual = 320 × 280px cards in a 680px column`\n  Large enough that the front card reads, narrow enough that the fan stays above the headline width.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CtaSection(props: { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; actions?: ReactNode; cards?: StackedCardFanItem[]; fanProps?: Omit<StackedCardFanProps, \"items\">; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Headline; rendered as the `h2` that labels the section.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Supporting copy under the headline.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Buttons or links, in a centred wrapping row.\n- `cards: StackedCardFanItem[]`\n  Cards for the faded stack above the headline; omit for text only.\n- `fanProps: Omit<StackedCardFanProps, \"items\">`\n  Overrides for the card stack (pill label, size, controlled state).\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Entrance: the visual, headline, copy and actions rise 16px, unblur and fade\n * in one after another the first time a quarter of the section is on screen.\n * Each part waits `order * STAGGER_MS` before starting, so one shared flag on\n * the root (`data-shown`) drives the whole sequence with CSS transitions only.\n * Hidden styles are keyed to `data-shown=\"false\"`, so a reduced-motion visitor,\n * for whom the flag starts true, never sees a hidden frame.\n */\nconst STAGGER_MS = 90;\n\nconst REVEAL =\n  \"transition-[opacity,translate,filter] duration-700 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)] motion-reduce:transition-none \" +\n  \"group-data-[shown=false]/cta:translate-y-4 group-data-[shown=false]/cta:opacity-0 group-data-[shown=false]/cta:blur-sm\";\n\nfunction revealDelay(order: number): CSSProperties {\n  return { transitionDelay: `${order * STAGGER_MS}ms` };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The shared IntersectionObserver stops watching the section after the first reveal (`once`) and on unmount. The entrance is CSS transitions with nothing to cancel.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The reveal flag starts true and the transitions are off (`motion-reduce:transition-none`), so everything is visible at once. The card fan changes state instantly under the same preference.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Hiding parts with an `opacity-0` class that a reveal removes: reduced-motion visitors and anything rendered before the observer fires get a blank section. Key the hidden state to the flag being false and start it true under reduced motion.\n- Animating `transform` while the cards inside also animate theirs: the entrance uses the standalone `translate` property and filter, so it composes with the fan transforms without overriding them.\n- Using `md:` breakpoints for the headline size: in a scaled preview or a narrow column they follow the window. Use container queries on the section.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Imports Stacked Card Fan and is shown with its `AGENT_DASHBOARD_CARDS` faces; any `StackedCardFanItem[]` works.\n- Omit `cards` for a text-only CTA; the stagger starts at the headline.\n- The demo actions are kit `ButtonLink`s with hash links.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScrolling the section in shows the card stack, then the headline, copy and two buttons rising and unblurring in that order. The stack fades into the page at the bottom with the See all five pill over the fade; hovering spreads it and the pill lays the cards out. At 360px the headline is smaller and wraps, the buttons wrap onto two lines and nothing overflows. Light and dark both read.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cursor-clip-path-mask": "# Build: Cursor Clip-Path Mask\n\nAn upper layer is clipped to a circle that trails the pointer, so a disc of alternate content moves across a base layer. GSAP never tweens `clip-path`; it tweens a plain `{ x, y, r }` object and one `onUpdate` writes both the clip string and the ring outline from the same numbers on the same frame.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA disc of a second layer follows the pointer; hovering the trigger grows it until it covers the panel.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The animated value is a proxy object, not a DOM property. `gsap.to(proxy, { x, y, onUpdate: write })` gives normal tween semantics — easing, overwrite, duration — over three plain numbers, and `write()` turns them into a `clip-path` string plus a transform for the ring.\n2. That indirection is the whole point. Tweening `clipPath` directly means GSAP parses and re-serialises a string each frame, and the ring outline drawn on top must be animated by a second, independent tween. Two interpolations of the same idea drift under fast movement, and a visible sliver of base layer appears between the ring and the edge of the disc.\n3. It also lets position and radius be separate tweens with different durations and eases — 0.4s linear for the follow, 0.55s `power3.inOut` for the expansion — while still producing one coherent write. That is not expressible as a single `clipPath` tween.\n4. The radius is written in px, not `%`. A percentage radius in `circle()` resolves against `sqrt(w^2 + h^2) / sqrt(2)`, not against the width, so the same percentage is a different physical size at every aspect ratio and the px-sized ring stops matching the disc it is supposed to outline.\n5. Expansion targets `Math.hypot(clientWidth, clientHeight)` — one full diagonal, which is enough to cover the panel from any point inside it, including a corner.\n6. The ring is a fixed-diameter element scaled by `r / radius` rather than an element whose width and height are rewritten, so each frame is a composited transform instead of a layout pass.\n7. Hover detection uses `pointerover`/`pointerout` on the container, which bubble, so one listener pair covers every trigger in the subtree regardless of how React re-renders it. `pointerenter` does not bubble and would need a listener per trigger plus subscription bookkeeping.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `radius = 130`\n  Resting disc radius in px. Small enough that the base layer still dominates and the disc reads as an instrument, large enough to show a readable fragment of the layer underneath. Below ~60 you are looking through a keyhole and cannot tell what the reveal layer is.\n- `FOLLOW_DURATION = 0.4`\n  Seconds of catch-up on position. Long enough that the disc reads as being dragged rather than teleported, short enough that it is still under the pointer when you stop to read what is inside it.\n- `ease: 'none' on the follow = linear`\n  Deliberately linear, unlike most cursor follows. The end value is remutated on every pointer event, so an eased tween re-eases from a fresh start each time and the disc reads as surging and stalling. Linear catch-up gives one steady drag speed no matter how the pointer moves.\n- `EXPAND_DURATION = 0.55`\n  Seconds for the disc to grow to a full diagonal and back. The distance travelled is large, so a follow-length 0.4s makes it feel like a cut; past ~0.8s the reveal has not finished before an impatient pointer has left the trigger.\n- `expand ease 'power3.inOut' = power3.inOut`\n  In-out for the expansion because it is a discrete state change with a beginning and an end, unlike the follow. The slow start keeps the growth from looking explosive; the slow finish stops the edge whipping off-panel.\n- `ring opacity ramp = clamp(0, 1, 2 - scale)`\n  The ring is scaled, so its 1px border thickens with it. Fading it out by the time the disc has doubled hides that artefact before it becomes visible, and it also gets the outline out of the way of a full-panel reveal.\n- `off-panel park = -radius * 3`\n  Where the disc sits before the first move and after leaving. Three radii guarantees no part of it clips back into view at any aspect ratio, and the reveal layer is invisible until the pointer earns it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CursorClipPathMask(props: { base: ReactNode; reveal: ReactNode; radius?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `base: ReactNode`\n  Always-visible bottom layer, and the layer that keeps all pointer events. Put `[data-mask-trigger]` elements here.\n- `reveal: ReactNode`\n  Top layer, visible only inside the disc. Clipped, `aria-hidden` and non-interactive.\n- `radius: number` — default `130`\n  Resting disc radius in CSS px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst FOLLOW_DURATION = 0.4;\nconst EXPAND_DURATION = 0.55;\n\nexport function CursorClipPathMask({\n  base,\n  reveal,\n  radius = 130,\n  className,\n}: CursorClipPathMaskProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const overlay = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const ring = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const layer = overlay.current;\n    const outline = ring.current;\n    if (!el || !layer || !outline) return;\n\n    const fine = window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches;\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;\n\n    /* One object, three numbers, tweened by GSAP like any DOM property. The\n       clip string is derived from it — never tweened itself. */\n    const proxy = { x: 0, y: 0, r: radius };\n\n    const write = () => {\n      /* Radius in px, not `%`. A percentage radius in `circle()` resolves\n         against sqrt(w^2 + h^2) / sqrt(2), NOT against the width — so a\n         percentage hole is a different physical size on every panel aspect\n         ratio, and the px-sized ring drawn on top of it stops matching. */\n      layer.style.clipPath = `circle(${proxy.r}px at ${proxy.x}px ${proxy.y}px)`;\n\n      /* Same frame, same numbers. The ring is a fixed-diameter element scaled\n         to the current radius, so this is one composited transform rather than\n         a width/height write that would relayout every frame. */\n      const scale = proxy.r / radius;\n      outline.style.transform = `translate3d(${proxy.x - radius}px, ${proxy.y - radius}px, 0) scale(${scale})`;\n      // The border thickens with the scale, so the ring is faded out well\n      // before the expansion makes that obvious.\n      outline.style.opacity = String(gsap.utils.clamp(0, 1, 2 - scale));\n    };\n\n    if (!fine || reduced) {\n      /* No pointer to follow, or motion the user declined. Park a static hole\n         left-of-centre so both layers are legible at once and the component\n         reads as a composed still rather than a blank panel. */\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      proxy.x = rect.width * 0.34;\n      proxy.y = rect.height * 0.5;\n      write();\n      return () => {\n        layer.style.clipPath = '';\n        outline.style.transform = '';\n        outline.style.opacity = '';\n      };\n    }\n\n    // Start off-panel so nothing is revealed before the first move.\n    proxy.x = -radius * 3;\n    proxy.y = -radius * 3;\n    write();\n\n    let entered = false;\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const x = e.clientX - rect.left;\n      const y = e.clientY - rect.top;\n\n      if (!entered) {\n        entered = true;\n        // Teleport on entry, otherwise the hole slides in from the corner\n        // over 400ms and reveals a diagonal streak of the layer beneath.\n        gsap.set(proxy, { x, y });\n        write();\n        return;\n      }\n\n      /* `ease: 'none'` is deliberate. An eased follow re-eases from a new\n         start value on every event, which under continuous movement reads as\n         the hole surging and stalling. Linear catch-up at 0.4s gives a steady\n         drag. `overwrite: 'auto'` claims x and y and leaves the radius tween\n         from the trigger running underneath. */\n      gsap.to(proxy, {\n        x,\n        y,\n        duration: FOLLOW_DURATION,\n        ease: 'none',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n        onUpdate: write,\n      });\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      entered = false;\n      gsap.to(proxy, {\n        x: -radius * 3,\n        y: -radius * 3,\n        duration: 0.35,\n        ease: 'power2.in',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n        onUpdate: write,\n      });\n    };\n\n    /* Enough radius to cover the panel from any point inside it: the far\n       corner is at most one diagonal away. */\n    const full = () => Math.hypot(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight);\n\n    const onEnterTrigger = (e: Event) => {\n      const target = e.target;\n      if (!(target instanceof Element) || !target.closest('[data-mask-trigger]')) return;\n      gsap.to(proxy, {\n        r: full(),\n        duration: EXPAND_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power3.inOut',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n        onUpdate: write,\n      });\n    };\n\n    const onLeaveTrigger = (e: Event) => {\n      const target = e.target;\n      if (!(target instanceof Element) || !target.closest('[data-mask-trigger]')) return;\n      gsap.to(proxy, {\n        r: radius,\n        duration: EXPAND_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power3.inOut',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n        onUpdate: write,\n      });\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n    /* `pointerover`/`pointerout` bubble, so one pair of listeners covers every\n       trigger in the subtree no matter how React re-renders it. `pointerenter`\n       does not bubble and would need a listener per trigger. */\n    el.addEventListener('pointerover', onEnterTrigger);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerout', onLeaveTrigger);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerover', onEnterTrigger);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerout', onLeaveTrigger);\n      gsap.killTweensOf(proxy);\n      layer.style.clipPath = '';\n      outline.style.transform = '';\n      outline.style.opacity = '';\n    };\n  }, [radius]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes all four container listeners, calls `gsap.killTweensOf(proxy)` so no tween can write to a detached node after React releases it, then clears the inline `clipPath`, `transform` and `opacity` that `write()` set. The proxy is a local object and is garbage-collected with the closure.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No listeners attach. The disc is written once, parked at 34% across and vertically centred, and never moves. Both layers stay readable and the composition still communicates what the component does; it simply does not animate.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Tweening `clipPath` as a string. GSAP re-parses it each frame and the ring must then be animated separately; the two interpolations diverge under fast movement and a crescent of the base layer shows between the outline and the disc edge.\n- A percentage radius. `circle(20% at ...)` resolves against `sqrt(w^2 + h^2) / sqrt(2)`, so the disc is a different size on a wide panel than a tall one and no px-based outline can be made to match it.\n- Forgetting `pointer-events: none` on the clipped layer. `clip-path` clips hit testing, not just paint, so the overlay becomes a region that swallows events inside the disc and passes them through everywhere else — hover targets then work or do not work depending on where the disc happens to be.\n- Putting the trigger inside the reveal layer. It is clipped away, so it is only hoverable when it is already inside the disc, which is exactly when you no longer need to hover it. Triggers belong in the base layer.\n- Easing the position follow. Because the tween restarts from a new end value on every event, an ease-out curve reads as the disc lunging and hesitating rather than being dragged.\n- Animating the ring by writing `width` and `height`. That is a layout invalidation on every frame of the expansion, and it shows as jank on the exact interaction that is meant to feel expensive.\n- Not teleporting on entry. Without the `gsap.set` on the first move, the disc eases in from its parked corner over 0.4s and drags a diagonal streak of the reveal layer across the panel every time the pointer enters.\n- `overwrite: true` on either tween. It kills the other one, so the disc either freezes mid-expansion or stops following. `overwrite: \"auto\"` claims only the properties that tween actually animates, which is why position and radius can coexist on one proxy.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch there is no pointer to follow: `pointermove` only fires mid-drag, so the disc would sit under the finger hiding it, and it would be stranded wherever the finger lifted. The touch path parks a static disc at 34% across and vertically centred, so both layers are legible at once and the panel reads as a composed still rather than blank.\n- Reduced motion takes the same static path — the entire effect is continuous movement, so there is nothing to slow down honestly.\n- The reveal layer is `aria-hidden`: it is a second rendering of content the base layer already presents, and exposing both would double every heading to a screen reader.\n- Scoped to its own container rather than the viewport. Position is measured against the container rect, so the component composes anywhere and does not assume it owns the page.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove across the panel and a disc of the second layer drags along behind the pointer at a steady speed, with a thin ring exactly on its edge — no gap, no crescent of the base layer showing between them, even when you flick the pointer. Hover the trigger and the disc grows smoothly past the corners until the reveal layer fills the panel, with the ring fading out as it goes; leave and it shrinks back to the pointer. Resize the panel to a very wide or very tall aspect ratio and the disc stays circular and stays the same physical size. On touch the disc is parked left-of-centre and static.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cursor-reactive-shader-glow": "# Build: Cursor-Reactive Shader Glow\n\nA hero backdrop: a slowly drifting domain-warped noise field with a soft light that follows the pointer through it. The pointer never reaches a uniform directly — the listener records a target and the render loop damps toward it, so the light trails the hand at the same rate on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display. The composed colour is then scaled down until its Rec.709 luminance fits under `ceiling`, which is what makes the glow safe to put white headline copy on top of.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA warped-noise atmosphere with a light that trails the cursor, held under a hard luminance ceiling so copy stays readable.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `PlaneGeometry(2, 2)` under an `OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1)` is a full-screen quad with no projection maths, so the camera never needs touching on resize and `vUv` is exactly 0..1 across the canvas.\n2. The `pointermove` handler converts the event into 0..1 UV space against the canvas rect (flipping y, since UV is up and client y is down) and stores it as a target. The loop then runs `damp(current, target, smoothing, dt)` per axis, where `smoothing` is the fraction of the gap still remaining after one second. The naive `x += (t - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120Hz as at 60Hz, so the same hand movement would produce a visibly tighter follow on better hardware.\n3. The noise is a domain warp: two decorrelated 2-octave fbm samples become a `vec2` offset fed into a third sample. The second channel is shifted by `vec2(2.3, 1.1)` — without that shift both channels return the same number and the warp degenerates into a plain diagonal translation of the field.\n4. The pointer is added to all three fbm samples, weighted 0.20, -0.20 and 0.12. The sign flip between the two warp channels makes them shear against each other around the cursor. Three weak influences read as an atmosphere that responds; one strong influence reads as a field glued to the cursor, which is a different and much worse effect.\n5. The light itself is two falling `smoothstep(radius, 0.0, d)` ramps — a tight core and a wide halo — mixed 0.65/0.35. The halo is squared, which pushes the last few percent of its falloff below the 8-bit quantisation floor and removes the termination ring the eye otherwise finds at the edge of a single smoothstep on a dark background.\n6. Finally `dot(col, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722))` measures the output luminance and the whole vector is multiplied by `min(1.0, ceiling / lum)`. Scaling rather than clamping per channel is deliberate: clamping crushes the brightest channel first and the glow core visibly shifts hue as it brightens.\n7. `energy` is measured from the follower's own displacement divided by `dt`, giving UV units per second. Measuring the follower rather than the raw pointer means the signal is already smooth, needs no second filter, and cannot be spiked by one stray event.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `smoothing = 0.0016`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second, so the light closes ~99.8% of the distance per second — roughly a 120ms tail. Raise it to 0.05 and the light lags far enough behind the cursor to feel broken rather than smooth; drop it to 1e-8 and it is welded to the pointer, at which point the damping is doing nothing and a raw uniform write would look identical.\n- `ceiling = 0.175`\n  Maximum Rec.709 luminance of the output. Against white text this leaves roughly 12:1 contrast, comfortably past the 4.5:1 floor. Past ~0.35 white headline copy over the glow core drops under 4.5:1 and the backdrop starts eating the content it exists to sit behind.\n- `coreRadius / haloRadius = 0.22 / 0.85`\n  Aspect-corrected UV units. Nearly 4x apart, which is what makes the pair read as one light with a soft surround rather than as two concentric discs. Bring them within ~2x and the halo's edge becomes visible as a ring at the core's boundary.\n- `scale = 0.7`\n  Noise frequency. Below 1.0 the features are LARGER than the canvas, which is what makes the field read as atmosphere. At 4.0 the same shader is visible turbulence and fights the copy for attention.\n- `warp = 0.9`\n  How far the two warp channels push the third sample, in noise-space units — just under one full cell. Near 0 the domain warp vanishes and the result is plain fbm with no flow. Past ~2 the warp folds the field back over itself and produces hard creases where the offset reverses.\n- `ENERGY_FULL_SCALE = 1.8`\n  UV units per second at which `uEnergy` saturates. A deliberate sweep across a hero is roughly 0.6/s, so normal movement lands mid-range and the brightening is legible. Set it to 0.3 and the reading pins at 1 on any movement at all, which makes the parameter meaningless.\n- `ENERGY_SMOOTHING / PRESENCE_SMOOTHING = 0.002 / 0.02`\n  Energy must track the hand or the brightening arrives after the gesture has finished; presence is a fade in and out and 400ms of it reads as a light coming up. Give presence the fast value and the glow flicks on and off at the canvas boundary.\n- `STILL_TIME = 8.2`\n  Time slice for the single reduced-motion frame. Not 0, because at t=0 the drift offset is zero on both axes and the field renders its least interesting slice.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CursorReactiveShaderGlow(props: { children?: ReactNode; ceiling?: number; coreRadius?: number; haloRadius?: number; scale?: number; warp?: number; fieldGain?: number; glowGain?: number; smoothing?: number; base?: readonly [number, number, number]; tint?: readonly [number, number, number]; glowTint?: readonly [number, number, number]; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Overlay content. Pointer-transparent so the canvas keeps receiving moves.\n- `ceiling: number` — default `0.175`\n  Rec.709 luminance the output may never exceed.\n- `coreRadius: number` — default `0.22`\n  Radius of the tight glow core, in aspect-corrected UV units.\n- `haloRadius: number` — default `0.85`\n  Radius of the wide halo. Squared in the shader, so it has no visible edge.\n- `scale: number` — default `0.7`\n  Noise frequency. Below 1 the features are larger than the canvas.\n- `warp: number` — default `0.9`\n  How far the two warp channels displace the third noise sample.\n- `fieldGain: number` — default `0.08`\n  Peak brightness the noise field adds over `base`.\n- `glowGain: number` — default `0.1`\n  Peak brightness the cursor light adds over `base`.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0016`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second.\n- `base: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.03, 0.031, 0.038]`\n  Deepest colour, linear 0..1 RGB.\n- `tint: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.62, 0.7, 0.95]`\n  Colour the noise field lifts toward.\n- `glowTint: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[1, 0.94, 0.86]`\n  Colour of the cursor light.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Device pixel ratio cap. The field is low-frequency, so 2 is already generous.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * UV units per second the follower must cover for `uEnergy` to read 1. A slow\n * deliberate sweep across a hero is roughly 0.6/s; above ~2.5 the reading\n * saturates on ordinary movement and the brightness stops meaning anything.\n */\nconst ENERGY_FULL_SCALE = 1.8;\n/** Fast: energy must track the hand, or the brightening arrives after the move. */\nconst ENERGY_SMOOTHING = 0.002;\n/** Slow: presence is a fade in and out, and 400ms of it reads as a light. */\nconst PRESENCE_SMOOTHING = 0.02;\n/** Fixed time slice for the reduced-motion still. Not 0 — that is a flat frame. */\nconst STILL_TIME = 8.2;\n/** Where the light sits in the reduced-motion still. Off-centre reads composed. */\nconst STILL_POINTER: readonly [number, number] = [0.34, 0.6];\nconst STILL_ENERGY = 0.35;\n\nconst BASE: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.03, 0.031, 0.038];\nconst TINT: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.62, 0.7, 0.95];\nconst GLOW_TINT: readonly [number, number, number] = [1, 0.94, 0.86];\n\ninterface GlowUniforms {\n  /** three types `uniforms` as an open record; the index signature matches it. */\n  [name: string]: THREE.IUniform;\n  uResolution: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Vector2>;\n  uTime: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uPointer: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Vector2>;\n  uPresence: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uEnergy: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uScale: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uWarp: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uCoreRadius: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uHaloRadius: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uFieldGain: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uGlowGain: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uCeiling: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uBase: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Color>;\n  uTint: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Color>;\n  uGlowTint: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Color>;\n}\n\ninterface Stage {\n  renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n  scene: THREE.Scene;\n  camera: THREE.OrthographicCamera;\n  geometry: THREE.PlaneGeometry;\n  material: THREE.ShaderMaterial;\n  uniforms: GlowUniforms;\n}\n\nexport function CursorReactiveShaderGlow({\n  children,\n  ceiling = 0.175,\n  coreRadius = 0.22,\n  haloRadius = 0.85,\n  scale = 0.7,\n  warp = 0.9,\n  fieldGain = 0.08,\n  glowGain = 0.1,\n  smoothing = 0.0016,\n  base = BASE,\n  tint = TINT,\n  glowTint = GLOW_TINT,\n  maxDpr = 2,\n  className,\n}: CursorReactiveShaderGlowProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const mount = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  /* Raw pointer target in 0..1 with y up. `null` means no real pointer has been\n     seen, which is distinct from a legitimate pointer in the bottom-left\n     corner — the glow must not exist before the first move. */\n  const target = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const inside = useRef(false);\n  /** The damped follower. Seeded at the centre so the first move eases outward. */\n  const pos = useRef({ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 });\n  const presence = useRef(0);\n  const energy = useRef(0);\n\n  /* Live config, read by the loop. Changing a number must not rebuild the\n     renderer: Chromium kills the OLDEST context past ~16 per process, so a\n     rebuild on every prop change blanks an unrelated canvas on the page. */\n  const live = useRef({\n    ceiling,\n    coreRadius,\n    haloRadius,\n    scale,\n    warp,\n    fieldGain,\n    glowGain,\n    smoothing,\n  });\n  live.current = {\n    ceiling,\n    coreRadius,\n    haloRadius,\n    scale,\n    warp,\n    fieldGain,\n    glowGain,\n    smoothing,\n  };\n\n  const ready = supported && width > 1 && height > 1;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const slot = mount.current;\n    if (!slot || !ready) return;\n\n    /* The canvas is created here, not owned by React. `reactStrictMode` is on,\n       so this effect runs twice on mount; the first cleanup calls\n       `forceContextLoss()`, and asking a React-owned <canvas> for a context\n       afterwards hands back the DEAD one rather than a fresh context, leaving\n       the preview permanently blank. A canvas created and removed per effect\n       run has no such history. */\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n        antialias: false,\n        alpha: false,\n        powerPreference: 'low-power',\n      });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n    renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    const canvas = renderer.domElement;\n    canvas.style.display = 'block';\n    canvas.style.width = '100%';\n    canvas.style.height = '100%';\n    /* `touch-action: none` is deliberately NOT set. This is a decorative\n       backdrop; swallowing a touch scroll to feed a glow nobody can see under\n       their own finger is a straight downgrade. */\n    slot.appendChild(canvas);\n\n    const uniforms: GlowUniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(width, height) },\n      uTime: { value: 0 },\n      uPointer: { value: new THREE.Vector2(pos.current.x, pos.current.y) },\n      uPresence: { value: 0 },\n      uEnergy: { value: 0 },\n      uScale: { value: scale },\n      uWarp: { value: warp },\n      uCoreRadius: { value: coreRadius },\n      uHaloRadius: { value: haloRadius },\n      uFieldGain: { value: fieldGain },\n      uGlowGain: { value: glowGain },\n      uCeiling: { value: ceiling },\n      uBase: { value: new THREE.Color(base[0], base[1], base[2]) },\n      uTint: { value: new THREE.Color(tint[0], tint[1], tint[2]) },\n      uGlowTint: { value: new THREE.Color(glowTint[0], glowTint[1], glowTint[2]) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: cursorReactiveShaderGlowVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: cursorReactiveShaderGlowFragmentShader,\n      uniforms,\n    });\n\n    // A 2x2 plane under an identity ortho camera is a full-screen quad with no\n    // projection maths and no camera to keep in sync on resize.\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material));\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1);\n\n    /* Listener on the CANVAS, not the window: no document-level handler\n       survives to drive a disposed renderer, and the rect it measures is the\n       rect it draws into. Passive, because nothing here ever calls\n       preventDefault and a non-passive move listener on a scrollable page\n       forces the compositor to wait for JS on every event. */\n    const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (rect.width === 0 || rect.height === 0) return;\n      target.current = {\n        x: (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width,\n        // Flipped: UV y is up, client y is down.\n        y: 1 - (event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height,\n      };\n      inside.current = true;\n    };\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      // Presence only. Leaving the position alone dims the light where it\n      // stands; tweening it away drags a visible streak off the edge.\n      inside.current = false;\n    };\n\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave, { passive: true });\n\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, geometry, material, uniforms };\n    renderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      // `dispose()` frees GPU objects but leaves the context alive until GC\n      // decides otherwise, which may be never. `disposeRenderer` is the one\n      // that calls `forceContextLoss()` and actually hands it back.\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n    // Gains are pushed through `live` from the loop and the colours through the\n    // effect below, so none of them may appear here — a rebuild per prop change\n    // leaks contexts.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, maxDpr]);\n\n  /* Colours, written onto the live uniforms. The build effect above reads them\n     exactly once, when it constructs the `THREE.Color` objects, and it is\n     deliberately not keyed on them — so without this every colour prop was\n     frozen at first render. A theme flip or any parent state driving the\n     palette left the canvas on its original colours while every other prop\n     kept responding, which reads as a broken theme switch rather than an\n     unsupported one. Depended on component-wise: the props are tuples, and a\n     caller writing one inline would otherwise re-run this on every render. */\n  const [baseR, baseG, baseB] = base;\n  const [tintR, tintG, tintB] = tint;\n  const [glowR, glowG, glowB] = glowTint;\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s) return;\n    s.uniforms.uBase.value.setRGB(baseR, baseG, baseB);\n    s.uniforms.uTint.value.setRGB(tintR, tintG, tintB);\n    s.uniforms.uGlowTint.value.setRGB(glowR, glowG, glowB);\n    // Render once: the loop is not running under reduced motion or while the\n    // component is off screen, and a recolour that waits for the next pointer\n    // move is indistinguishable from one that never arrived.\n    s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n  }, [baseR, baseG, baseB, tintR, tintG, tintB, glowR, glowG, glowB]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect, so a reflow never touches the renderer's lifetime.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    s.renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n    s.renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    // CSS pixels, not the drawing buffer: the shader only takes the ratio.\n    s.uniforms.uResolution.value.set(width, height);\n    // Render once, or a resize while paused stretches the previous framebuffer\n    // until the pointer moves again.\n    s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n  }, [width, height, maxDpr]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt, elapsed) => {\n      const s = stage.current;\n      if (!s) return;\n      const cfg = live.current;\n\n      const previousX = pos.current.x;\n      const previousY = pos.current.y;\n\n      const aim = target.current;\n      if (aim) {\n        pos.current.x = damp(pos.current.x, aim.x, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n        pos.current.y = damp(pos.current.y, aim.y, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n      }\n\n      // Speed of the follower, in UV units per second. Dividing by dt is what\n      // makes it a velocity rather than a per-frame delta that reads twice as\n      // large at 60Hz as at 120Hz for the same physical hand movement.\n      const travelled = Math.hypot(pos.current.x - previousX, pos.current.y - previousY);\n      const speed = travelled / Math.max(dt, 0.0001);\n      energy.current = damp(\n        energy.current,\n        clamp(speed / ENERGY_FULL_SCALE, 0, 1),\n        ENERGY_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n      presence.current = damp(\n        presence.current,\n        inside.current && aim !== null ? 1 : 0,\n        PRESENCE_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n\n      const u = s.uniforms;\n      u.uTime.value = elapsed;\n      u.uPointer.value.set(pos.current.x, pos.current.y);\n      u.uPresence.value = presence.current;\n      u.uEnergy.value = energy.current;\n      u.uScale.value = cfg.scale;\n      u.uWarp.value = cfg.warp;\n      u.uCoreRadius.value = cfg.coreRadius;\n      u.uHaloRadius.value = cfg.haloRadius;\n      u.uFieldGain.value = cfg.fieldGain;\n      u.uGlowGain.value = cfg.glowGain;\n      u.uCeiling.value = cfg.ceiling;\n\n      s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced,\n  );\n\n  /* Reduced motion gets a composed picture, not an empty box: one frame at a\n     fixed time slice with the light parked off-centre. Nothing ever moves, and\n     the pointer listener stays attached but its target is never consumed. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || !reduced || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    const [stillX = 0.34, stillY = 0.6] = STILL_POINTER;\n    s.uniforms.uTime.value = STILL_TIME;\n    s.uniforms.uPointer.value.set(stillX, stillY);\n    s.uniforms.uPresence.value = 1;\n    s.uniforms.uEnergy.value = STILL_ENERGY;\n    s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n  }, [reduced, width, height, ready]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the `pointermove` and `pointerleave` listeners from the canvas, nulls the stage ref so an in-flight frame cannot touch a disposed renderer, then calls `disposeRenderer` (which stops the animation loop, disposes the renderer and calls `forceContextLoss()` — `dispose()` alone leaves the context alive until GC decides otherwise, which may be never), disposes the geometry and the `ShaderMaterial`, and removes the canvas from the DOM. Because the canvas is created inside the effect, the StrictMode second run starts from a fresh element and a fresh context.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The render loop never starts. One frame is drawn at a fixed time slice with the light parked at (0.34, 0.6), presence at 1 and energy at 0.35, so the panel is a composed still of the effect at rest rather than a flat rectangle. The pointer listener stays attached but its target is never read, so moving the cursor changes nothing.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing the pointer straight into the uniform from the event handler. The shader then gets one raw sample per event: a 120Hz trackpad delivers a staircase, and a dropped frame delivers a jump. The visible symptom is a light that judders during fast movement and snaps after a stall.\n- Damping with a per-frame constant (`x += (t - x) * 0.1`). It is not frame-rate independent — the light trails noticeably further behind on a 60Hz laptop than on a 120Hz one, from identical code.\n- Aspect-correcting the noise sample space but not the pointer. The glow becomes an ellipse 1.78x too wide on a 16:9 canvas, and the error scales with the container so it looks correct in a square preview and wrong in the hero it was written for.\n- Deriving `energy` from raw pointer events. Event deltas depend on the sample rate, so the same hand movement measures differently per device, and one outlier event flashes the whole field.\n- Letting the pointer target slide back to the centre on leave. That drags a visible streak of light across the canvas to the middle. Only `presence` is faded, so the light dims where it stands.\n- Putting the pointer listener on `window` instead of the canvas. A document-level handler outlives the renderer it was written for, and the rect it measures is not the rect being drawn into — so after a scroll the glow sits at an offset from the cursor.\n- Clamping the output per channel instead of scaling the vector. The brightest channel saturates first, so the glow core shifts hue as it gets brighter — a warm light turns pink at its centre.\n- Rebuilding the renderer when a colour or gain prop changes. Chromium kills the oldest WebGL context past roughly 16 per renderer process, so a page with several of these blanks an unrelated canvas. Numbers go through a ref the loop reads; only size and support rebuild.\n- Owning the `<canvas>` in React while calling `forceContextLoss()` on cleanup. Under `reactStrictMode` the effect runs twice, and the second `getContext` on that same element hands back the dead context — the preview is permanently blank in development and fine in production, which is the worst possible failure mode. The canvas is created inside the effect and removed with it.\n- Making the content layer pointer-opaque. It covers the canvas, so `pointermove` stops firing exactly over the headline — the one place the glow is wanted. The overlay is `pointer-events-none`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The two warp channels sample the same fbm function, so the shader is three fbm evaluations of two octaves each: twelve gradient-noise samples per fragment. Two octaves is enough because the result is immediately crushed by `smoothstep(0.25, 0.85)` and the finer detail is discarded.\n- The hash is sin-free. `fract(sin(dot(p, k)) * 43758.5453)` quantises once the angle passes a few thousand radians and grows visible repeating bands — and it fails on mobile GPUs long before it fails on the desktop it was written on.\n- Gradient noise rather than value noise: the lattice value is a dot product with a random gradient and is therefore zero at every lattice point, which is what stops the cell corners reading as a faint dot grid in the flat areas.\n- Resize lives in its own effect and re-renders one frame, so a reflow while the loop is paused does not leave the previous framebuffer stretched across the new size.\n- `touch-action` is deliberately left alone. Swallowing a touch scroll to drive a glow hidden under the user's own finger is a straight downgrade.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove the pointer across the panel and a soft light follows it with a short, even tail; the same tail length on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display. Move fast and the light brightens; stop and it settles to a dimmer ember without going out. Leave the panel and the light fades where it stands instead of sliding to the centre. The noise field keeps drifting slowly whether or not the pointer moves. White text over the glow core stays readable at any `glowGain`. Resize the container and the glow stays circular, not elliptical. In development, with StrictMode double-mounting, the canvas renders — it is not blank. With reduced motion the panel is a single composed still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cursor-repulsion-burst": "# Build: Cursor Repulsion Burst\n\nUp to 1400 particles in one instanced draw call, each circling its own home on its own phase. The pointer opens a hole in the field with a linear falloff inside its radius, and a press fires a shock wave whose front tints the particles it is passing through. The pointer is damped on the CPU before it reaches a uniform, and bursts run in a pool of three slots so overlapping presses produce overlapping waves rather than one wave restarting.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn orbiting particle field the pointer pushes out of the way, and a press detonates into a shock wave with a heat band.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: ogl@^1.0.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Three displacements are stacked in field space in the vertex shader before anything becomes a position: an idle orbit, cursor repulsion, then up to three shock waves. Each stage reads the position the previous one produced, so a particle already pushed aside by the cursor is the particle the wave throws.\n2. All distances are measured in aspect-corrected space — x multiplied by `uResolution.x / uResolution.y` — and the resulting displacement is divided back out before it is added to the position. Without the correction the repulsion hole is an ellipse on any container that is not square, and the shock ring reaches the side edges before the top.\n3. Repulsion is `max(radius - distance, 0) * repel`: linear inside the radius and exactly zero outside it, so the field beyond the pointer is untouched and the whole idea costs one `length()` per particle. A `smoothstep` would be softer but would also mean every particle on screen is permanently displaced a little, which reads as the field sagging toward the cursor.\n4. The direction is `v / max(length(v), floor)` rather than `normalize(v)`. A true normalize is undefined at the origin and unstable just outside it, so the particle sitting under the cursor picks a new direction every frame from floating-point noise and buzzes. Dividing by a floor makes the vector shorter than unit length near the centre, so the push fades in and the direction stays put.\n5. A burst slot is `vec4(originX, originY, progress, active)`. The displacement is `(0.25 + seed * 0.75) * push * easeOutCubic(t) * settle(t) * reach(d)`, where `settle` walks the whole thing back to zero by t = 1 so the slot can be freed without the field snapping home, and `reach` limits the throw to the neighbourhood of the origin so the burst is not a camera jolt.\n6. The heat band is `exp(-b * b)` where `b = (distance - burstRadius * ease) / band`. The shock radius expands on the same cubic ease as the displacement, so only the particles the front is passing through right now take the tint. A flat tint over the whole burst just recolours the field; a Gaussian band travelling through it reads as heat.\n7. Only `active` and `progress` change per frame per slot, so a burst costs three vec4 uniforms and a three-iteration loop — no per-particle CPU state and no buffer upload.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `smoothing = 0.0008`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second: a tight ~90ms tail, because a repulsion hole that lags is read as broken rather than as smooth — the hole has a visible edge, unlike a glow. Raise it past ~0.01 and the field is still opening where the cursor was half a second ago.\n- `cursorRadius = 0.22`\n  Field units, where 1 is the container HEIGHT. Roughly a fifth of the panel, so the hole is obviously deliberate but the field still dominates. Past ~0.45 the pointer clears most of the visible particles and there is nothing left to displace.\n- `repel = 0.85`\n  Displacement at the centre of the field, as a fraction of the radius. Below ~0.4 the hole never actually opens — particles crowd instead of clearing. Above 1.0 the inner particles are pushed past the rim and pile into a bright ring, which is the same artefact as a hard-edged mask.\n- `BURST_SLOTS = 3`\n  Concurrent waves. One slot means a second press restarts the wave from zero, which visibly sucks the first one back into its origin. Three covers a fast double or triple click, and each slot is a vec4 and a loop iteration, so the ceiling is set by taste rather than by cost.\n- `burstDuration = 1.1`\n  Seconds of life. Under ~0.6 the wave is gone before the eye finds it; over ~2 the field spends most of its time recovering and a second press lands in a field that is still deformed.\n- `burstRadius / BURST_BAND = 0.55 / 0.085`\n  How far the front travels, and how thick it is. The band is roughly 15% of the travel, which is a front rather than a wash — take it past ~0.25 and the Gaussian covers the whole burst at once, which is indistinguishable from tinting every particle.\n- `burstPush = 0.17`\n  Peak displacement in field units, multiplied by a per-particle 0.25-1.0. The spread is what gives the wave a ragged front; a uniform magnitude produces a clean expanding circle, which reads as a UI ripple rather than as a detonation.\n- `MAX_DPR = 1.5`\n  Additive fill is the entire cost here and it scales with the square of the ratio. These are soft round dots with no fine detail, so a third sample per axis resolves nothing a viewer can see and costs 78% more fragments.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CursorRepulsionBurst(props: { density?: number; maxCount?: number; sizeRange?: readonly [number, number]; cursorRadius?: number; repel?: number; burstPush?: number; burstRadius?: number; burstDuration?: number; smoothing?: number; breathe?: number; opacity?: number; colorA?: string; colorB?: string; heatColor?: string; background?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `density: number` — default `1100`\n  Particles per megapixel of container area.\n- `maxCount: number` — default `1400`\n  Population ceiling, and the size the buffers are allocated at.\n- `sizeRange: readonly [number, number]` — default `[1.6, 5.2]`\n  Particle size in CSS pixels, biased toward the small end.\n- `cursorRadius: number` — default `0.22`\n  Repulsion radius in field units, where 1 is the container height.\n- `repel: number` — default `0.85`\n  Displacement at the centre of the repulsion field, as a fraction of its radius.\n- `burstPush: number` — default `0.17`\n  Peak burst displacement in field units, before the per-particle spread.\n- `burstRadius: number` — default `0.55`\n  How far a shock front travels before it dies.\n- `burstDuration: number` — default `1.1`\n  Life of one burst in seconds.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0008`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second.\n- `breathe: number` — default `0.22`\n  Depth of the orbit-radius breathing, 0 to 0.5.\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.9`\n  Overall alpha multiplier.\n- `colorA: string` — default `'#8fb6ff'`\n  First tint. Any value ogl's Color accepts.\n- `colorB: string` — default `'#e6ecf7'`\n  Second tint; each particle mixes between the two.\n- `heatColor: string` — default `'#ff8a3c'`\n  Colour of the shock front.\n- `background: string` — default `'#06070b'`\n  Opaque clear colour the additive field is composited onto.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'cursor-repulsion-burst'`\n  Seeds the scatter, sizes, phases and burst magnitudes.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Floor on the population. Below this the field reads as a few stray specks. */\nconst MIN_COUNT = 220;\n/** Slots in the burst pool. Three overlapping waves is the most anyone produces. */\nconst BURST_SLOTS = 3;\n/** Width of the Gaussian heat band, in field units. */\nconst BURST_BAND = 0.085;\n/** Fraction of the presence gap left after a second. ~200ms in and out. */\nconst PRESENCE_SMOOTHING = 0.007;\n/** Size distribution exponent. Above 1 crowds the population into small sizes. */\nconst SIZE_BIAS = 1.8;\n/**\n * Fill cost scales with dpr squared and these are soft round dots with no fine\n * detail, so a third sample per axis resolves nothing a viewer can see.\n */\nconst MAX_DPR = 1.5;\n/** Field-space bounds. Wider than 0..1 so orbits never bare the edges. */\nconst HOME_MIN = -0.06;\nconst HOME_MAX = 1.06;\n\n/** The reduced-motion still: a frozen frame with the pointer and one wave in it. */\nconst STILL_ELAPSED = 6.5;\nconst STILL_CURSOR: readonly [number, number] = [0.34, 0.56];\nconst STILL_BURST: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.66, 0.42, 0.42];\n\nexport function CursorRepulsionBurst({\n  density = 1100,\n  maxCount = 1400,\n  sizeRange = [1.6, 5.2],\n  cursorRadius = 0.22,\n  repel = 0.85,\n  burstPush = 0.17,\n  burstRadius = 0.55,\n  burstDuration = 1.1,\n  smoothing = 0.0008,\n  breathe = 0.22,\n  opacity = 0.9,\n  colorA = '#8fb6ff',\n  colorB = '#e6ecf7',\n  heatColor = '#ff8a3c',\n  background = '#06070b',\n  seed = 'cursor-repulsion-burst',\n  className,\n}: CursorRepulsionBurstProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1;\n\n  const [minSize = 1.6, maxSize = 5.2] = sizeRange;\n\n  /* Raw pointer target in field space, or null until a real move has happened —\n     which is not the same as a pointer resting at the bottom-left corner. */\n  const target = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const inside = useRef(false);\n  /** The damped follower, seeded at the centre so the first move eases outward. */\n  const follow = useRef({ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 });\n  const presence = useRef(0);\n\n  const slots = useRef<BurstSlot[]>(\n    Array.from({ length: BURST_SLOTS }, () => ({ x: 0.5, y: 0.5, t: 0, active: false })),\n  );\n  const nextSlot = useRef(0);\n\n  /* Live numbers the loop reads. Changing one must not rebuild the renderer:\n     Chromium drops the OLDEST WebGL context past roughly 16 per process, so a\n     rebuild per prop change can blank an unrelated canvas elsewhere on the page. */\n  const live = useRef({\n    cursorRadius,\n    repel,\n    burstPush,\n    burstRadius,\n    burstDuration,\n    smoothing,\n    breathe,\n    opacity,\n  });\n  live.current = {\n    cursorRadius,\n    repel,\n    burstPush,\n    burstRadius,\n    burstDuration,\n    smoothing,\n    breathe,\n    opacity,\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    /* ogl creates its own canvas, and it is appended and removed per effect\n       run. Reusing one React-owned canvas breaks under StrictMode: the first\n       cleanup force-loses the context, and `getContext` on that same element\n       afterwards returns the dead one rather than a fresh context — the panel\n       is then permanently blank in development and fine in production. */\n    const renderer = new Renderer({\n      dpr: pixelRatio(MAX_DPR),\n      // Opaque: the pass is additive, and additive onto a transparent canvas\n      // would have to brighten the page behind it, which the GPU cannot see.\n      alpha: false,\n      // Nothing occludes anything, so a depth buffer is memory plus a clear.\n      depth: false,\n      antialias: false,\n      // Fill-bound, not shader-bound. The discrete GPU buys nothing here.\n      powerPreference: 'low-power',\n    });\n\n    const gl = renderer.gl;\n    const canvas = gl.canvas;\n\n    const bg = new Color(background);\n    gl.clearColor(bg.r, bg.g, bg.b, 1);\n\n    const quad = new Float32Array([-0.5, -0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5]);\n    const index = new Uint16Array([0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 3]);\n\n    const total = Math.max(MIN_COUNT, Math.round(maxCount));\n    const homes = new Float32Array(total * 2);\n    const params = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n    const seeds = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const lo = Math.max(0.5, Math.min(minSize, maxSize));\n    const hi = Math.max(lo + 0.25, Math.max(minSize, maxSize));\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i += 1) {\n      homes[i * 2] = randRange(rng, HOME_MIN, HOME_MAX);\n      homes[i * 2 + 1] = randRange(rng, HOME_MIN, HOME_MAX);\n\n      // Biased small: a linear distribution puts a quarter of the population in\n      // the largest quarter of the range, and the field reads as gravel.\n      const sizeT = Math.pow(rng(), SIZE_BIAS);\n      params[i * 4] = lo + (hi - lo) * sizeT;\n      params[i * 4 + 1] = randRange(rng, 0.004, 0.03);\n      // Signed rate with a floor on the magnitude: a particle orbiting at\n      // 0.001 rad/s is a particle that is simply not moving.\n      params[i * 4 + 2] = (rng() < 0.5 ? -1 : 1) * randRange(rng, 0.12, 0.46);\n      // Bigger particles are dimmer, so total light stays roughly constant.\n      params[i * 4 + 3] = 0.34 + 0.5 * (1 - sizeT);\n\n      seeds[i * 4] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      // Burst magnitude. Per-particle, so the wave has a ragged front rather\n      // than a clean expanding circle — a clean circle reads as a UI ripple.\n      seeds[i * 4 + 1] = rng();\n      seeds[i * 4 + 2] = randRange(rng, 0.35, 1);\n      seeds[i * 4 + 3] = rng();\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new Geometry(gl, {\n      index: { data: index },\n      position: { size: 2, data: quad },\n      aHome: { size: 2, data: homes, instanced: 1 },\n      aParams: { size: 4, data: params, instanced: 1 },\n      aSeed: { size: 4, data: seeds, instanced: 1 },\n    });\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: [1, 1] },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.dpr },\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uCursor: { value: [0.5, 0.5] },\n      uCursorPresence: { value: 0 },\n      uCursorRadius: { value: cursorRadius },\n      uRepel: { value: repel },\n      uBreathe: { value: clamp(breathe, 0, 0.5) },\n      uBursts: { value: new Array<number>(BURST_SLOTS * 4).fill(0) },\n      uBurstRadius: { value: burstRadius },\n      uBurstPush: { value: burstPush },\n      uBurstBand: { value: BURST_BAND },\n      uColorA: { value: new Color(colorA) },\n      uColorB: { value: new Color(colorB) },\n      uHeatColor: { value: new Color(heatColor) },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n    };\n\n    const program = new Program(gl, {\n      vertex: CURSOR_REPULSION_BURST_VERT,\n      fragment: CURSOR_REPULSION_BURST_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      // ogl culls BACK faces by default and the quad's winding is not\n      // guaranteed to face the way the driver expects. A culled particle is\n      // not dim — it is absent.\n      cullFace: false,\n    });\n    // Additive, overriding the source-over that `transparent: true` selected.\n    // Order independent, so a thousand overlapping instances need no sort,\n    // which is what makes one draw call possible at all.\n    program.setBlendFunc(gl.ONE, gl.ONE);\n\n    const mesh = new Mesh(gl, { geometry, program });\n\n    el.appendChild(canvas);\n    canvas.style.display = 'block';\n\n    /* Listeners on the CANVAS, not the window. A document-level handler\n       outlives the renderer it was written for, and the rect it measures is not\n       the rect being drawn into — after a scroll the hole sits at an offset\n       from the cursor. Passive, because nothing here calls preventDefault and a\n       non-passive move handler makes the compositor wait for JS on every event. */\n    const toField = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (rect.width === 0 || rect.height === 0) return null;\n      return {\n        x: (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width,\n        // Flipped: field y is up, client y is down.\n        y: 1 - (event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height,\n      };\n    };\n\n    const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const point = toField(event);\n      if (!point) return;\n      target.current = point;\n      inside.current = true;\n    };\n\n    const onDown = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const point = toField(event);\n      if (!point) return;\n      // A press is also a position: on touch there is no hover, so without this\n      // the wave would fire from wherever the pointer last happened to be.\n      target.current = point;\n      inside.current = true;\n\n      const slot = slots.current[nextSlot.current % BURST_SLOTS];\n      if (!slot) return;\n      nextSlot.current = (nextSlot.current + 1) % BURST_SLOTS;\n      slot.x = point.x;\n      slot.y = point.y;\n      slot.t = 0;\n      slot.active = true;\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      // Position is left where it is. Tweening it back to the centre drags a\n      // visible furrow across the field on the way out.\n      inside.current = false;\n    };\n\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', onDown, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave, { passive: true });\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h);\n      // `setSize` writes literal px into the canvas's inline style, which wins\n      // over any class. Re-asserting percentages keeps it fluid between\n      // resize notifications.\n      canvas.style.width = '100%';\n      canvas.style.height = '100%';\n\n      uniforms.uResolution.value = [gl.drawingBufferWidth, gl.drawingBufferHeight];\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.dpr;\n\n      // Population from area, not a fixed number: the same density reads right\n      // in a 320px card and a 2000px hero. The buffers already hold `total`\n      // particles in scattered order, so a prefix of them is still spatially\n      // even — no rebuild, no reallocation, no new GPU memory.\n      const megapixels = (w * h) / 1_000_000;\n      const count = clamp(Math.round(megapixels * density), MIN_COUNT, total);\n      geometry.setInstancedCount(count);\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      renderer.render({ scene: mesh });\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { renderer, geometry, program, mesh, uniforms, resize, draw };\n    draw(0);\n\n    // A context we are never told about losing leaves a permanently blank panel\n    // and no fallback beneath it, so treat the loss as a machine that never had\n    // WebGL. `preventDefault` is what buys the chance of a restore at all.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      setUnsupported(true);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onDown);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave);\n      // ogl has no `dispose()`: `remove()` on the geometry deletes its VAOs and\n      // every buffer, `remove()` on the program deletes the shaders and the\n      // program, and only then is the context worth handing back. Dropping the\n      // context is the part that matters — the browser's per-process budget is\n      // about sixteen, and a lost one takes an unrelated canvas with it.\n      geometry.remove();\n      program.remove();\n      loseContext(gl);\n      canvas.width = 0;\n      canvas.height = 0;\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, [ready, density, maxCount, minSize, maxSize, seed]);\n\n  // Colours are pushed into the live uniforms rather than being effect deps, so\n  // recolouring never costs a context.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s) return;\n    s.uniforms.uColorA.value.set(colorA);\n    s.uniforms.uColorB.value.set(colorB);\n    s.uniforms.uHeatColor.value.set(heatColor);\n    const bg = new Color(background);\n    s.renderer.gl.clearColor(bg.r, bg.g, bg.b, 1);\n  }, [colorA, colorB, heatColor, background]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect: a reflow costs a `setSize` and an instanced-count\n  // change, never a buffer rebuild.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt, elapsed) => {\n      const s = stage.current;\n      if (!s) return;\n      const cfg = live.current;\n\n      const aim = target.current;\n      if (aim) {\n        follow.current.x = damp(follow.current.x, aim.x, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n        follow.current.y = damp(follow.current.y, aim.y, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n      }\n      presence.current = damp(\n        presence.current,\n        inside.current && aim !== null ? 1 : 0,\n        PRESENCE_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n\n      // Bursts advance on wall-clock seconds, not on frames, so a wave lasts\n      // `burstDuration` on a 30Hz phone and on a 144Hz monitor alike.\n      const flat = s.uniforms.uBursts.value;\n      const life = Math.max(cfg.burstDuration, 0.05);\n      let i = 0;\n      for (const slot of slots.current) {\n        if (slot.active) {\n          slot.t += dt / life;\n          if (slot.t >= 1) {\n            slot.t = 1;\n            slot.active = false;\n          }\n        }\n        flat[i * 4] = slot.x;\n        flat[i * 4 + 1] = slot.y;\n        flat[i * 4 + 2] = slot.t;\n        flat[i * 4 + 3] = slot.active ? 1 : 0;\n        i += 1;\n      }\n\n      const u = s.uniforms;\n      // Mutated in place. Assigning a fresh tuple allocates two arrays a frame\n      // and defeats ogl's redundant-uniform cache, which compares by length\n      // first and then element by element against the array it kept.\n      u.uCursor.value[0] = follow.current.x;\n      u.uCursor.value[1] = follow.current.y;\n      u.uCursorPresence.value = presence.current;\n      u.uCursorRadius.value = cfg.cursorRadius;\n      u.uRepel.value = cfg.repel;\n      u.uBurstPush.value = cfg.burstPush;\n      u.uBurstRadius.value = cfg.burstRadius;\n      u.uBreathe.value = clamp(cfg.breathe, 0, 0.5);\n      u.uOpacity.value = cfg.opacity;\n\n      s.draw(elapsed);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n\n  /* Reduced motion is a composed still, not an empty box: one frame with the\n     pointer parked off-centre and a single wave frozen part-way out, so the\n     panel shows what the effect produces. The listeners stay attached and their\n     target is never consumed, so moving or clicking changes nothing. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || !reduced || !ready) return;\n    const [cx = 0.34, cy = 0.56] = STILL_CURSOR;\n    const [bx = 0.66, by = 0.42, bt = 0.42] = STILL_BURST;\n    s.uniforms.uCursor.value[0] = cx;\n    s.uniforms.uCursor.value[1] = cy;\n    s.uniforms.uCursorPresence.value = 1;\n    const flat = s.uniforms.uBursts.value;\n    flat.fill(0);\n    flat[0] = bx;\n    flat[1] = by;\n    flat[2] = bt;\n    flat[3] = 1;\n    s.draw(STILL_ELAPSED);\n  }, [reduced, ready, width, height]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the four pointer listeners from the canvas, nulls the stage ref so an in-flight frame cannot touch a dead renderer, then `geometry.remove()` (deletes the VAOs and every buffer), `program.remove()` (deletes the shaders and the program), `loseContext(gl)`, zeroes the canvas dimensions and removes it from the DOM. ogl has no `dispose()`, and dropping the context is the part that matters — the per-process budget is about sixteen, and exceeding it kills the oldest context, which belongs to somebody else.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The render loop never starts. One frame is drawn with the pointer parked at (0.34, 0.56) at full presence and a single wave frozen 42% of the way out, so the panel is a composed still of the effect rather than a static dot field. The listeners stay attached but their target is never consumed, so moving or pressing changes nothing on screen.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing pointer events straight into `uCursor`. The hole lands exactly where the last event was: a high-rate trackpad delivers a staircase, and after a stalled frame it is a jump that shoves the whole field sideways in one step. The listener records a target and the loop damps toward it.\n- Damping with a per-frame constant (`x += (t - x) * 0.1`) instead of `damp(..., dt)`. The same code produces a noticeably tighter follow at 120Hz than at 60Hz, so the effect is tuned for whatever machine it was written on.\n- Using `normalize()` for the repulsion direction. It is undefined at the origin, so the particle under the cursor jitters between random directions every frame — a visible buzzing speck exactly where the eye is.\n- Aspect-correcting the distances but not dividing the displacement back out. Particles are then pushed further horizontally than vertically, and the hole slowly shears the field sideways as the pointer moves.\n- Restarting one burst slot on every press. The in-flight wave is yanked back to its origin before the new one starts, which looks like a rendering fault rather than a second click. Three round-robin slots let waves overlap.\n- Advancing burst progress per frame instead of by `dt / duration`. The wave is then twice as fast on a 120Hz display and half speed on a throttled tab.\n- Deactivating a slot while its displacement is still non-zero. Every particle in it snaps home on one frame. The magnitude is multiplied by a settle term that reaches zero exactly when the slot expires.\n- Handing ogl a Float32Array for `uniform vec4 uBursts[3]`. ogl resolves the active uniform name `uBursts[0]`, checks `Array.isArray(value)` to decide it is an array uniform, and a Float32Array fails that test — the uniform is silently skipped with a console warning and every burst is invisible. It must be a plain array.\n- Leaving ogl's default BACK-face culling on. The quad winding is not guaranteed to face the way the driver expects, and a culled particle is not dim, it is absent — so the field renders empty on some machines and fine on others.\n- Owning the canvas in React while calling `loseContext()` on cleanup. Under `reactStrictMode` the effect runs twice and the second `getContext` on that element hands back the dead context: blank in development, fine in production.\n- Rebuilding the renderer when a colour changes. Chromium drops the oldest WebGL context past roughly sixteen per process, so recolouring one field can blank a different canvas on the same page. Colours are written into live uniforms.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `pointerdown` sets the pointer target as well as firing the wave. On touch there is no hover, so without it the burst would originate from wherever the pointer last happened to be — usually the previous tap.\n- Blending is additive with an opaque clear colour, so a thousand overlapping instances need no depth sort and the whole field is one draw call. The fragment output is premultiplied because the destination is only ever added to.\n- The population is derived from container area rather than fixed, and selected with `setInstancedCount` out of buffers allocated once at `maxCount`. Resizing changes the count without touching a byte of GPU memory.\n- The scatter runs on `createRng(seed)`, never `Math.random()`, so the field is identical on every load and a visual regression is a real regression.\n- Particles inside the repulsion radius also brighten, which stops the hole reading as a bald patch — the field gets denser and hotter at the rim instead.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe field drifts on its own with no pointer on the page. Move the pointer in and a clear hole opens around it, tracking with a short even lag that is the same on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display, with the rim slightly brighter than the rest of the field. The particle directly under the cursor does not jitter. Press and a wave throws the nearby particles outward with a ragged front, a warm band travelling through them, and they drift back without snapping. Press three times quickly and you get three overlapping waves, not one wave restarting. Leave the panel and the hole closes where it stands. Resize to a wide container and the hole is still round. With reduced motion the panel is a single composed still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cylinder-dot-score": "# Build: Dot-Cloud Score Cylinder\n\nA score wrapped around a cylinder and drawn entirely in dots: measure rings, vertical bar dividers, and note clusters that light as a playhead sweeps them. Two cylinders are stacked and turned to different yaws so they never present the same face at once, and both live in one buffer. Nothing uses a depth buffer — every dot is flattened to z = 0 before projection, and solidity comes from alpha and point size falling off with the model-space z.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTwo counter-rotating cylinders of dots — rings, bar lines, notes — drawn from one buffer with no depth buffer.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One `Float32Array` pair is filled in six consecutive passes — both cylinders' measure rings, then both cylinders' dividers, then both cylinders' notes. Buffer order is the draw order, so notes end up on top of everything without a sort and without a second object.\n2. Identity travels in `aPointData = vec4(instanceIndex, isNote, pointScale, groupIndex)`, so the two cylinders share a geometry and the vertex shader picks the transform: instance 1 is mirrored with `p.z *= -1.0` and yawed -166°, instance 0 is yawed -90°.\n3. Both are then yawed by `uSpin` (degrees, advancing linearly at `spinSpeed` per second) and tilted 8° on X, and instance 0 is pushed up by 0.3 *after* the rotation so the stack separation is a pure screen-space offset that does not tip with the spin.\n4. Depth is faked from the transformed z: `depthT = clamp((z + 0.6) / 1.2, 0, 1)` drives alpha from 0.25 to 1.0 and point size from 0.65 to 1.0, so the far wall of the cylinder recedes without a depth test.\n5. The playhead is `mod(floor(uElapsed / uNoteInterval), uGroupCount)`, computed in the vertex shader from the clock that is already there. A note group whose index matches gets 2x point size and full alpha, so highlighting a beat costs no React render and no uniform write.\n6. Angles within each ring are sorted before being written into the buffer, and `gl_PointSize` gets the same clamp-plus-area-compensated-alpha treatment as every other point field here.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `BASE_SCALE = 1.5`\n  Applied in the shader, not baked into the buffer, so re-framing the piece never re-uploads a vertex.\n- `TILT = 8.0 degrees`\n  X tilt. Below ~4° the cylinder collapses to a straight band and stops reading as a cylinder; past ~15° you look down the tube and the far wall crowds the near one.\n- `INSTANCE_B_YAW = -166.0 degrees`\n  Not -180. At exactly half a turn the two cylinders present mirror-image faces and the pair looks like one object reflected; 166° keeps them legibly distinct through the whole rotation.\n- `STACK_OFFSET = 0.3`\n  Vertical separation, applied after `rotateX` so it is screen-space. Applied before, the offset would tilt with the cylinder and the two would drift into each other as they turn.\n- `DEPTH_HALF_RANGE = 0.6`\n  z is normalised over ±0.6 while the cloud actually spans ±0.75, so the nearest and furthest dots saturate. Normalising over the exact range leaves the middle band looking flat.\n- `DEPTH_MIN_SIZE / DEPTH_MAX_SIZE = 0.65 / 1.0`\n  Far dots at 65% of near dots. Alpha alone is not enough — without the size falloff the far wall reads as a grey copy of the near one rather than as distance.\n- `NOTE_ACTIVE_SCALE = 2.0`\n  Point size multiplier for the lit note group. Area goes as the square, so 2x is a 4x brighter mark — visible at a glance without any colour change being required.\n- `spinSpeed = 12 degrees/second`\n  One revolution every 30s. The original ran at 1°/s, which is right for an ambient full-page object and far too slow to read in a preview card. Linear either way: easing it would make it look like it is arriving somewhere.\n- `MEASURE_SCALE / NOTE_SCALE = 0.7 / 1.4`\n  Notes are exactly double the measure dots so they survive the depth size falloff on the far wall, where a measure dot is already down to 0.46.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CylinderDotScore(props: { ringCount?: number; ringDots?: number; dividerCount?: number; dividerDots?: number; noteGroups?: number; noteDots?: number; spinSpeed?: number; noteInterval?: number; dotScale?: number; viewScale?: number; opacity?: number; color?: string; noteColor?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `ringCount: number` — default `10`\n  Measure rings per cylinder, split evenly above and below the equator gap.\n- `ringDots: number` — default `260`\n  Dots in each measure ring.\n- `dividerCount: number` — default `8`\n  Vertical bar dividers per cylinder.\n- `dividerDots: number` — default `34`\n  Dots making up each divider.\n- `noteGroups: number` — default `6`\n  Note clusters per cylinder; the playhead cycles through them.\n- `noteDots: number` — default `26`\n  Dots per note cluster.\n- `spinSpeed: number` — default `12`\n  Degrees of yaw per second. Linear.\n- `noteInterval: number` — default `1.2`\n  Seconds each note group stays lit.\n- `dotScale: number` — default `1`\n  Global dot radius multiplier in CSS pixels.\n- `viewScale: number` — default `0.62`\n  Model-to-pixel scale as a fraction of the container short side.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied to every dot.\n- `color: string` — default `'#7d838c'`\n  Measure and divider dot colour.\n- `noteColor: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Note dot colour; lit notes go fully to this.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'cylinder-dot-score'`\n  Seeds ring angles and note scatter for a stable, hydration-safe layout.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CylinderDotScore({\n  ringCount = 10,\n  ringDots = 260,\n  dividerCount = 8,\n  dividerDots = 34,\n  noteGroups = 6,\n  noteDots = 26,\n  spinSpeed = 12,\n  noteInterval = 1.2,\n  dotScale = 1,\n  viewScale = 0.62,\n  opacity = 1,\n  color = '#7d838c',\n  noteColor = '#ffffff',\n  seed = 'cylinder-dot-score',\n  className,\n}: CylinderDotScoreProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0.1, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n\n    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n       Buffer layout IS the draw order. Points are blended in the order they\n       appear, with depth testing off, so the six passes below are written\n       consecutively into one pair of arrays: both cylinders' measures, then\n       both cylinders' dividers, then both cylinders' notes. Notes last means\n       notes on top, everywhere, without a sort and without a second object.\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\n    const perCylinder = ringCount * ringDots + dividerCount * dividerDots + noteGroups * noteDots;\n    const total = perCylinder * 2;\n\n    const positions = new Float32Array(total * 3);\n    const pointData = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n    let cursor = 0;\n\n    const write = (\n      x: number,\n      y: number,\n      z: number,\n      instance: number,\n      isNote: number,\n      scale: number,\n      group: number,\n    ) => {\n      positions[cursor * 3] = x;\n      positions[cursor * 3 + 1] = y;\n      positions[cursor * 3 + 2] = z;\n      pointData[cursor * 4] = instance;\n      pointData[cursor * 4 + 1] = isNote;\n      pointData[cursor * 4 + 2] = scale;\n      pointData[cursor * 4 + 3] = group;\n      cursor += 1;\n    };\n\n    /** y of measure ring `i`, mirrored around a gap at the equator. */\n    const ringY = (i: number) => {\n      const half = Math.floor(ringCount / 2);\n      const step = i < half ? half - i : i - half + 1;\n      return (i < half ? -1 : 1) * (GAP + step * RING_PITCH);\n    };\n\n    for (let instance = 0; instance < 2; instance += 1) {\n      for (let ring = 0; ring < ringCount; ring += 1) {\n        const y = ringY(ring);\n        // Sorted angles, not raw ones. Points blend in buffer order, so an\n        // unsorted ring interleaves near and far dots and the ring stipples\n        // instead of reading as one continuous line of engraving.\n        const angles = new Float32Array(ringDots);\n        for (let i = 0; i < ringDots; i += 1) angles[i] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n        angles.sort();\n        for (let i = 0; i < ringDots; i += 1) {\n          const a = angles[i] ?? 0;\n          write(RADIUS * Math.cos(a), y, RADIUS * Math.sin(a), instance, 0, MEASURE_SCALE, 0);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    const bandTop = GAP + Math.ceil(ringCount / 2) * RING_PITCH;\n    for (let instance = 0; instance < 2; instance += 1) {\n      for (let d = 0; d < dividerCount; d += 1) {\n        const a = (d / dividerCount) * Math.PI * 2 + instance * 0.19;\n        for (let i = 0; i < dividerDots; i += 1) {\n          const t = dividerDots === 1 ? 0.5 : i / (dividerDots - 1);\n          const y = -bandTop + t * bandTop * 2;\n          write(RADIUS * Math.cos(a), y, RADIUS * Math.sin(a), instance, 0, MEASURE_SCALE, 0);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    for (let instance = 0; instance < 2; instance += 1) {\n      for (let g = 0; g < noteGroups; g += 1) {\n        const centre = (g / noteGroups) * Math.PI * 2 + instance * 0.4;\n        for (let i = 0; i < noteDots; i += 1) {\n          const a = centre + (rng() - 0.5) * 0.22;\n          const y = (rng() - 0.5) * 2 * bandTop;\n          write(RADIUS * Math.cos(a), y, RADIUS * Math.sin(a), instance, 1, NOTE_SCALE, g);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aPointData', new THREE.BufferAttribute(pointData, 4));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uSpin: { value: 0 },\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uViewScale: { value: 1 },\n      uViewportYOffset: { value: 0 },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uDotScale: { value: dotScale },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uNoteInterval: { value: noteInterval },\n      uGroupCount: { value: noteGroups },\n      uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(color) },\n      uNoteColor: { value: new THREE.Color(noteColor) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: CYLINDER_DOT_SCORE_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: CYLINDER_DOT_SCORE_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      // Both off. With depth testing on, the first dot rasterised at a given\n      // pixel would win and the far wall of the cylinder would punch holes in\n      // the near one, because every dot is flattened to the same z.\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.left = -w / 2;\n      camera.right = w / 2;\n      camera.top = h / 2;\n      camera.bottom = -h / 2;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n      const scale = Math.min(w, h) * viewScale;\n      uniforms.uViewScale.value = scale;\n      // The stack spans 0 .. STACK_OFFSET in y; pulling the whole thing down\n      // by half of that puts the pair's centre on the container's centre.\n      uniforms.uViewportYOffset.value = -STACK_OFFSET * 0.5 * scale;\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      uniforms.uSpin.value = elapsed * spinSpeed;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    ringCount,\n    ringDots,\n    dividerCount,\n    dividerDots,\n    noteGroups,\n    noteDots,\n    spinSpeed,\n    noteInterval,\n    dotScale,\n    viewScale,\n    opacity,\n    color,\n    noteColor,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed), ready && !reduced && !unsupported);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref so a queued frame cannot touch a dead renderer, disposes the geometry through `disposeObject(scene)`, disposes the material, calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed backing store, and removes the canvas. `useRaf` unsubscribes on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No frame loop is opened. One frame is drawn at `uElapsed = 0.4`, which shows both cylinders at a legible yaw with the first note group lit, so the structure and the highlight convention are both visible. Neither the rotation nor the playhead advances.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving `depthTest` on. Every dot is projected with z = 0, so the first fragment written at a pixel wins and the far wall of the cylinder punches holes in the near one. The whole design depends on buffer order, not on the depth buffer.\n- Sorting note dots into the buffer before measure dots, or drawing them as a second object added earlier. With depth testing off, \"on top\" means \"later in the buffer\" — get it wrong and the notes are half-eaten by the rings that surround them.\n- Applying the stack offset before `rotateX`. It then rotates with the model, and the two cylinders slowly drift into each other over the course of a revolution instead of staying vertically separated.\n- Writing raw random angles into a ring. Blending happens in buffer order, so unsorted angles interleave near and far dots and the ring visibly stipples rather than reading as a continuous engraved line.\n- Driving the playhead from React state and an interval. It re-renders the tree once per beat, and the highlight lands one frame after the beat because the uniform write happens in a commit rather than in the frame loop — deriving it from `uElapsed` in the shader avoids both.\n- Rebuilding the buffer when the container resizes. Model space is size-independent; only `uViewScale` and `uViewportYOffset` change, so a reflow costs two uniform writes rather than a dropped GL context.\n- Setting `frustumCulled` at its default. Positions are unit-scale around the origin and the real placement happens in the shader, so three's bounding sphere does not describe what is on screen.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The two cylinders are \"instances\" only in the conceptual sense — this is one non-instanced `Points` with an index in an attribute, which is cheaper than instancing for a case where every vertex differs anyway.\n- Total dot count is `2 * (ringCount * ringDots + dividerCount * dividerDots + noteGroups * noteDots)`, about 6100 at the defaults, in one draw call.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTwo stacked cylinders of dots turn at a constant rate, showing different faces to the viewer. Dots on the far side are visibly smaller and dimmer than those on the near side, and no dot ever pops in front of one it should be behind. A cluster of larger note dots lights every 1.2 seconds and cycles around the cylinder. Resize the container and the piece rescales without a flicker. With reduced motion it holds a single legible frame.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "cylinder-gallery": "# Build: Cylinder Gallery Drum\n\nA flat PlaneGeometry per item, all sharing one compiled program, each bent around a common cylinder by its own uniform. The camera never moves and the CPU never touches a vertex: scrolling changes one float, `position`, and every plane recomputes its angle, its height and its focus weight from that single scalar. Because the ring offset is the shortest signed distance on a circular index, the list is genuinely endless in both directions with no clones and no rebuilding.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nNineteen planes wrapped around a drum in the vertex shader, spun by wheel or scroll, focus falling off from centre.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1, gsap@^3.15.0, lenis@^1.3.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The vertex shader treats a vertex's own x as an arc offset: `angle = uSpiralAngle + position.x`, then `pos.x = uRadius * sin(angle)` and `pos.z = uRadius * cos(angle)`, so one world unit of plane width wraps exactly one radian of drum. `pos.y = sin(position.y)` pulls the top and bottom edges in, which turns a rolled sheet into a barrel.\n2. Per frame and per plane, `wrappedOffset(i, position, count)` returns the shortest signed distance from that plane to the focused index — the value that makes index 0 sit one step before index 18 rather than eighteen steps after it. That offset drives `uSpiralAngle = (offset / count) * 2π * turns`, the vertical stack `y = -offset * spacing`, and the focus term.\n3. Focus is `gaussian(offset)` = `exp(-offset²)`: 1.0 at the centre, 0.368 one step out, 0.018 two, 0.0001 three. The fragment shader mixes toward luminance and dims by that weight, so attention falls off continuously instead of one plane being flagged \"selected\".\n4. A single `uIntro` scalar lerps between two complete layouts inside the shader — `mix(rowPos, ringPos, uIntro)` — where `rowPos` is a flat facing row and `ringPos` is the drum. Every intermediate value is a valid frame, and the CPU lerps the mesh x/y positions with the same scalar so the two layouts stay consistent.\n5. Signed scroll velocity feeds `uDeform`, which adds `sin(position.y * 3.0 + 100.0) * uDeform * 0.07` to z and `sin(position.x * 3.0) * uDeform * 0.04` to y. It samples `position` — the original flat coordinates — not the already-bent `pos`, so the ripple is locked to the picture and reads as the image flexing rather than the drum wobbling.\n6. Pointer proximity is measured inside the vertex shader by projecting each vertex once as a probe (`ndc = probe.xy / probe.w`), taking `exp(-mouseDist² * 5.0)`, lifting z by half of it and passing it to the fragment shader, which pushes the centred UV outward by the same weight so the bump also magnifies — a lens, not a bump.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PLANE_WIDTH / PLANE_HEIGHT = 1 / 1.95`\n  Width 1 because the bend uses the vertex's raw x as radians — at width 1 each plane spans exactly 1 rad of the drum, which is the whole reason no trigonometry is needed on the CPU. Height must stay under 2π/3 or `sin(position.y)` leaves its monotonic range and the barrel folds its own edges back through the surface.\n- `CAMERA_FOV / CAMERA_Z = 45 / 5`\n  The camera never animates, so these only set how much drum is in shot. Below ~35° the ring flattens into a row and the depth cue vanishes; above ~60° the near plane balloons and the barrel curve reads as a keystone error.\n- `OVERSCAN = 1.12`\n  The cover-fit UV is divided by this, zooming the crop in 12%. At exactly 1.0 the wave and the bulge push vertices past the sampled region and the texture's own clamped border smears along the edge. Beyond ~1.25 the crop eats the subject.\n- `segments = 64`\n  The bend is evaluated per vertex, so subdivision *is* the curve. Below ~24 the drum visibly becomes a folded card and the mouse bulge turns into a triangular tent; past ~128 you are paying vertex cost for a curve the pixel grid cannot resolve.\n- `POSITION_SMOOTHING = 0.0006`\n  Fraction of remaining distance left after one second. Larger (slower) and the drum lags visibly behind the wheel; much smaller and the velocity spike that drives `uDeform` never has time to build, so the wave disappears.\n- `SNAP_DELAY_MS = 170`\n  Idle time before the drum eases to a whole index. Under ~120 ms it fires between the discrete events of a single trackpad flick and fights the gesture; over ~250 ms the gallery feels like it forgot to settle.\n- `MAX_DEFORM / DEFORM_PER_INDEX = 1.5 / 0.42`\n  Deform is clamped to ±1.5 because the shader multiplies it by 0.07 world units — past that the planes intersect each other and the far side of the drum pokes through the near side. 0.42 sets how many index-steps per second count as full deflection.\n- `RADIUS_VELOCITY_GAIN = 0.5`\n  The drum widens up to 50% at full speed, which is what sells inertia. Above ~0.8 the nearest plane crosses the camera's near plane during a fast flick and clips away.\n- `gaussian falloff = 1 (exp(-offset²))`\n  A falloff of 1 keeps the immediate neighbours legible at 0.37 while the third plane out is effectively gone. Raising it dims the neighbours to black and the ring loses its context; lowering it flattens every plane to the same weight.\n- `renderOrder scale = -worldZ * 1000`\n  Reconstructed drum depth, scaled up so it survives rounding to an integer sort key. At a smaller scale adjacent planes collapse into the same bucket and flicker between frames.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CylinderGallery(props: { items: CylinderGalleryItem[]; drive?: \"pointer\" | \"page\"; radius?: number; turns?: number; spacing?: number; segments?: number; pixelsPerIndex?: number; opacity?: number; paint?: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, item: CylinderGalleryItem, index: number, size: { width: number; height: number }) => void; onSelect?: (item: CylinderGalleryItem, index: number) => void; onFocusChange?: (item: CylinderGalleryItem, index: number) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: CylinderGalleryItem[]`\n  `{ id, title, meta?, hue? }` per plane. One mesh and one generated texture each.\n- `drive: \"pointer\" | \"page\"` — default `\"pointer\"`\n  Wheel and drag inside the component, or document scroll via a shared infinite Lenis.\n- `radius: number` — default `2`\n  Drum radius in world units, before the velocity widening.\n- `turns: number` — default `3`\n  Full revolutions the ring spans across the whole list.\n- `spacing: number` — default `0.8`\n  World units of vertical travel per index step.\n- `segments: number` — default `64`\n  Plane subdivisions per axis. This is the resolution of the bend.\n- `pixelsPerIndex: number` — default `240`\n  Document scroll pixels per index step, in `page` mode only.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Global alpha multiplier applied before the focus falloff.\n- `paint: (ctx, item, index, size) => void`\n  Override the generated plate. Receives a 2D context for a 512x1024 canvas.\n- `onSelect: (item, index) => void`\n  Tap on the drum. A drag never counts as a tap.\n- `onFocusChange: (item, index) => void`\n  Fires when a different index takes the centre.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function CylinderGallery({\n  items,\n  drive = 'pointer',\n  radius = 2,\n  turns = 3,\n  spacing = 0.8,\n  segments = 64,\n  pixelsPerIndex = 240,\n  opacity = 1,\n  paint,\n  onSelect,\n  onFocusChange,\n  className,\n}: CylinderGalleryProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.0015 });\n\n  // `hasWebGL` touches `document`, so it cannot run during render without\n  // disagreeing with the server. Start optimistic and correct after mount:\n  // both renders then agree and nothing hydrates twice.\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const [focused, setFocused] = useState(0);\n\n  const position = useRef(0);\n  const target = useRef(0);\n  const velocity = useRef(0);\n  const intro = useRef(0);\n  const strength = useRef(0);\n  const hovered = useRef(false);\n  const focusedRef = useRef(0);\n  const snapTimer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\n  const snapTween = useRef<Tween | null>(null);\n\n  // Everything the frame loop reads but must not resubscribe on. Written on\n  // every render, read once per frame; the loop itself never re-creates.\n  const live = useRef({ radius, turns, spacing, opacity, drive, pixelsPerIndex, items, onSelect, onFocusChange });\n  live.current = { radius, turns, spacing, opacity, drive, pixelsPerIndex, items, onSelect, onFocusChange };\n\n  // Rebuilding the scene is expensive, so it is keyed on what actually changes\n  // the meshes — identity, label and hue — not on the array's reference, which\n  // is new on every render for any caller who writes the list inline.\n  const itemsKey = items.map((item) => `${item.id}:${item.title}:${item.hue ?? ''}`).join('|');\n\n  const lenis = useLenis({ infinite: true, enabled: drive === 'page' });\n\n  const killSnap = useCallback(() => {\n    if (snapTimer.current !== null) {\n      clearTimeout(snapTimer.current);\n      snapTimer.current = null;\n    }\n    snapTween.current?.kill();\n    snapTween.current = null;\n  }, []);\n\n  const scheduleSnap = useCallback(() => {\n    if (snapTimer.current !== null) clearTimeout(snapTimer.current);\n    snapTimer.current = setTimeout(() => {\n      snapTimer.current = null;\n      // Snap the TARGET, not the position: the frame loop keeps damping\n      // toward it, so the settle stays one continuous motion instead of a\n      // second animation fighting the first.\n      snapTween.current = gsap.to(target, {\n        current: Math.round(target.current),\n        duration: SNAP_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power3.out',\n      });\n    }, SNAP_DELAY_MS);\n  }, []);\n\n  /* ---- scene construction ------------------------------------------------ */\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready || items.length === 0) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    // Capped, not raw. A 3x phone with an uncapped ratio pays 9x the fill rate\n    // for a difference nobody can see.\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(\n      CAMERA_FOV,\n      el.clientWidth / Math.max(el.clientHeight, 1),\n      0.1,\n      100,\n    );\n    camera.position.z = CAMERA_Z;\n\n    // One geometry for every plane. The bend is per-vertex, so the mesh has to\n    // be dense enough that the curve reads as a curve: below ~24 segments the\n    // drum visibly becomes a folded card.\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(PLANE_WIDTH, PLANE_HEIGHT, segments, segments);\n    const planeAspect = PLANE_WIDTH / PLANE_HEIGHT;\n    const paintItem = paint ?? paintPlate;\n    const maxAnisotropy = Math.min(4, renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy());\n\n    const planes: Plane[] = items.map((item, index) => {\n      const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n      canvas.width = TEXTURE_WIDTH;\n      canvas.height = TEXTURE_HEIGHT;\n      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n      if (ctx) paintItem(ctx, item, index, { width: TEXTURE_WIDTH, height: TEXTURE_HEIGHT });\n\n      const texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas);\n      // sRGB in, sRGB out. Skip this and the plates render noticeably darker\n      // than the CSS colours sitting next to them.\n      texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n      texture.anisotropy = maxAnisotropy;\n      texture.needsUpdate = true;\n\n      const uniforms: PlaneUniforms = {\n        uTexture: { value: texture },\n        uTextureAspect: { value: TEXTURE_WIDTH / TEXTURE_HEIGHT },\n        uPlaneAspect: { value: planeAspect },\n        uOverscan: { value: OVERSCAN },\n        uSpiralAngle: { value: 0 },\n        uRadius: { value: radius },\n        uIntro: { value: 0 },\n        uFocus: { value: 1 },\n        uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n        uDeform: { value: 0 },\n        uMouseNdc: { value: new THREE.Vector2(0, 0) },\n        uMouseStrength: { value: 0 },\n      };\n\n      // A material per mesh, because every plane needs its own spiralAngle,\n      // radius and focus. three keys its program cache on the shader source,\n      // so all of these share one compiled program regardless.\n      const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n        vertexShader: cylinderGalleryVertexShader,\n        fragmentShader: cylinderGalleryFragmentShader,\n        uniforms,\n        transparent: true,\n        side: THREE.DoubleSide,\n        // Painted strictly back to front via renderOrder below, so neither\n        // depth write nor depth test has anything to contribute — and both\n        // would be wrong anyway, since the depth they compare is the depth of\n        // a mesh the CPU thinks is still flat at the origin.\n        depthWrite: false,\n        depthTest: false,\n      });\n\n      const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n      // The CPU-side geometry is a flat plane at mesh.position; the shape you\n      // can actually see is somewhere else entirely. Frustum culling compares\n      // against that stale bounding sphere and blanks planes that are plainly\n      // on screen.\n      mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n      scene.add(mesh);\n      return { mesh, uniforms, texture };\n    });\n\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, geometry, planes };\n    renderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      for (const plane of planes) {\n        scene.remove(plane.mesh);\n        // disposeObject walks a material's own properties looking for\n        // textures. These live inside `uniforms`, two levels down, so it will\n        // never find them — they have to be released by hand.\n        plane.texture.dispose();\n      }\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      // dispose() frees GPU objects and leaves the context alive. Only\n      // forceContextLoss, which disposeRenderer calls, hands it back.\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // `items` is read through the key; `radius`/`opacity` only seed uniforms\n    // the frame loop overwrites immediately.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, itemsKey, segments, paint]);\n\n  /* ---- size ------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    current.renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    // `false` — the canvas is sized by CSS at 100%/100%; letting three write\n    // inline pixel dimensions makes it fight the flex parent on every resize.\n    current.renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    current.camera.aspect = width / height;\n    current.camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  /* ---- intro morph ------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!ready) return;\n    if (reduced) {\n      intro.current = 1;\n      return;\n    }\n    intro.current = 0;\n    const tween = gsap.to(intro, {\n      current: 1,\n      duration: INTRO_DURATION,\n      delay: INTRO_DELAY,\n      ease: 'power3.inOut',\n    });\n    return () => {\n      tween.kill();\n    };\n  }, [ready, reduced]);\n\n  /* ---- input ------------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || drive !== 'pointer') return;\n\n    const onWheel = (event: WheelEvent) => {\n      // Non-passive on purpose: without preventDefault the drum spins AND the\n      // page scrolls under it, which reads as two competing gestures.\n      event.preventDefault();\n      killSnap();\n      target.current += event.deltaY * WHEEL_INDEX_PER_PIXEL;\n      scheduleSnap();\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('wheel', onWheel, { passive: false });\n    return () => el.removeEventListener('wheel', onWheel);\n  }, [drive, killSnap, scheduleSnap]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    const enter = () => {\n      hovered.current = true;\n    };\n    const leave = () => {\n      hovered.current = false;\n    };\n    el.addEventListener('pointerenter', enter);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', leave);\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerenter', enter);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', leave);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  useDrag(host, {\n    enabled: drive === 'pointer',\n    onDragStart: killSnap,\n    onDrag: (state) => {\n      target.current -= state.deltaY * DRAG_INDEX_PER_PIXEL;\n    },\n    onDragEnd: (_state, moved) => {\n      if (!moved) {\n        const { items: list, onSelect: select } = live.current;\n        const index = wrapIndex(Math.round(position.current), list.length);\n        const item = list[index];\n        if (item) select?.(item, index);\n      }\n      scheduleSnap();\n    },\n  });\n\n  useEffect(() => killSnap, [killSnap]);\n\n  /* ---- the frame --------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n      const {\n        radius: baseRadius,\n        turns: baseTurns,\n        spacing: baseSpacing,\n        opacity: alpha,\n        drive: mode,\n        pixelsPerIndex: perIndex,\n        items: list,\n        onFocusChange: notify,\n      } = live.current;\n\n      const count = current.planes.length;\n      if (count === 0) return;\n\n      if (mode === 'page') {\n        const instance = lenis.current;\n        if (instance) target.current = instance.scroll / Math.max(perIndex, 1);\n      }\n\n      const previous = position.current;\n      position.current = damp(position.current, target.current, POSITION_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      // Index steps per second, smoothed by the same damp that produced the\n      // position, so the wave can never lead the geometry it is bending.\n      const instant = dt > 0 ? (position.current - previous) / dt : 0;\n      velocity.current = damp(velocity.current, instant, 0.002, dt);\n      const normalized = clamp(velocity.current / VELOCITY_REFERENCE, -1, 1);\n\n      strength.current = damp(\n        strength.current,\n        hovered.current && !reduced ? 1 : 0,\n        STRENGTH_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n\n      const s = intro.current;\n      const speed = Math.abs(normalized);\n      const liveRadius = baseRadius * (1 + RADIUS_VELOCITY_GAIN * speed);\n      const liveSpacing = baseSpacing * (1 + SPACING_VELOCITY_GAIN * speed);\n      const angularScale = 1 + ANGULAR_VELOCITY_GAIN * speed;\n      const deform = reduced\n        ? 0\n        : clamp(velocity.current * DEFORM_PER_INDEX, -MAX_DEFORM, MAX_DEFORM);\n\n      // The flat-row layout spans the frustum's visible WIDTH at z = 0. Sizing\n      // it from window.innerWidth, or from the visible height, leaves the row\n      // short on wide viewports and clipped on narrow ones.\n      const visibleWidth =\n        2 * Math.tan((CAMERA_FOV * Math.PI) / 360) * CAMERA_Z * current.camera.aspect * 0.86;\n      const step = visibleWidth / Math.max(count - 1, 1);\n      const rowWidth = (count - 1) * step;\n\n      const mx = pointer.current.active ? pointer.current.ndcX : 0;\n      const my = pointer.current.active ? pointer.current.ndcY : 0;\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n        const plane = current.planes[i];\n        if (!plane) continue;\n\n        // The one call that makes a finite array behave like an infinite ring:\n        // item 0 is one step BEFORE item 18, not eighteen steps after it.\n        const offset = wrappedOffset(i, position.current, count);\n        const angle = (offset / count) * Math.PI * 2 * baseTurns * angularScale;\n        const y = -offset * liveSpacing;\n\n        plane.mesh.position.x = (i * step - rowWidth / 2) * (1 - s);\n        plane.mesh.position.y = y * s;\n        plane.mesh.scale.setScalar(lerp(0.62, 1, s));\n\n        const u = plane.uniforms;\n        u.uSpiralAngle.value = angle;\n        u.uRadius.value = liveRadius;\n        u.uIntro.value = s;\n        u.uDeform.value = deform;\n        u.uOpacity.value = alpha;\n        u.uMouseStrength.value = strength.current;\n        u.uMouseNdc.value.set(mx, my);\n        // exp(-d²): 1.000 at the centre, 0.368 one step out, 0.018 two, 0.0001\n        // three. Continuous, branchless, and the reason the drum reads as\n        // analogue rather than as a list with a selected row.\n        u.uFocus.value = lerp(1, gaussian(offset), s);\n\n        // Transparent meshes are sorted by three using the CPU position, which\n        // for this drum is (0, y, 0) for every plane. Reconstructing the depth\n        // the shader will actually produce and painting back to front is the\n        // only way the far side of the ring stays behind the near side.\n        const worldZ = liveRadius * Math.cos(angle) * s;\n        plane.mesh.renderOrder = Math.round(-worldZ * 1000);\n      }\n\n      const nextFocus = wrapIndex(Math.round(position.current), count);\n      if (nextFocus !== focusedRef.current) {\n        focusedRef.current = nextFocus;\n        setFocused(nextFocus);\n        const item = list[nextFocus];\n        if (item) notify?.(item, nextFocus);\n      }\n\n      current.renderer.render(current.scene, current.camera);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the wheel and pointerenter/pointerleave listeners, kills the intro tween and any pending snap timeout plus its tween, removes every mesh from the scene, disposes each `CanvasTexture` by hand (they sit inside `material.uniforms`, where `disposeObject` cannot reach), runs `disposeObject` for the geometries and materials, disposes the shared geometry, then `disposeRenderer` — `setAnimationLoop(null)`, `dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()` — and removes the canvas from the DOM. The frame loop reads a stage ref that is nulled first, so a queued frame after teardown is a no-op rather than a null context throw.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The intro morph is skipped — `uIntro` is set to 1 on mount, so the drum is simply already assembled. The velocity wave is pinned to 0 and the pointer bulge never engages, so the surface stays flat. Navigation still works and still eases: wheel, drag and the snap-to-index tween are unchanged, because the user asked for less motion, not for a gallery that cannot move.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Scaling `uRadius` from 0 to animate the intro. At radius 0 every vertex of every plane lands on the origin, so the \"flat\" state is nineteen invisible slivers stacked in one spot. Lerp between two position vectors — `mix(rowPos, ringPos, uIntro)` — and the flat state is a real layout you can hold.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` at its default. The CPU-side geometry is still a flat plane sitting at `mesh.position`; the visible shape is somewhere else entirely. three culls against that stale bounding sphere and planes that are plainly on screen blink out at the edges.\n- Trusting three's transparency sort. It orders transparent meshes by their object position, which for this drum is `(0, y, 0)` for every single plane, so the far side of the ring paints over the near side at random. Reconstruct the depth the shader will produce (`radius * cos(angle) * intro`) and set `renderOrder` from it each frame.\n- Expecting `disposeObject` to free the plate textures. It scans a material's own properties for anything with `isTexture`; these textures live two levels down inside `material.uniforms`, so it never sees them and nineteen 512x1024 canvases stay resident on the GPU after unmount. They are disposed explicitly from a tracked list.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` and stopping there. That releases GPU objects but keeps the WebGL context alive; Chromium caps a renderer process at roughly 16 and silently kills the oldest, so a docs page that mounts several of these blanks the ones above it. `disposeRenderer` calls `forceContextLoss`.\n- Attaching the wheel listener passively. Without `preventDefault` the drum spins and the host page scrolls at the same time, which reads as two gestures fighting; the listener is registered with `{ passive: false }`.\n- Sampling the deformation wave from the already-bent `pos` instead of the original `position`. The ripple then travels with the drum rotation rather than staying pinned to the image, and the effect looks like a rendering fault instead of the picture flexing.\n- Sizing the flat-row layout from `window.innerWidth`. The row has to span the frustum's visible width at z = 0, which is `2 * tan(fov/2) * cameraZ * aspect` — the container width in pixels has no fixed relationship to that, so the row overshoots on wide viewports and clips on narrow ones.\n- Omitting `#include <colorspace_fragment>` from a `ShaderMaterial`. three only injects output conversion into its own materials, so raw linear values ship into an sRGB framebuffer and the plates render visibly washed out next to the CSS around them.\n- Rebuilding the scene when the `items` array identity changes. Any caller who writes the list inline hands over a new array every render, which would tear down nineteen textures and a GL context on every keystroke. The setup effect keys on a string built from id, title and hue.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Textures are drawn at mount to a 512x1024 canvas per item — a hue ramp, hairline bands and a numeral. The bands are not decoration: a smooth gradient hides every deformation the shader applies, so the plate needs structure for the wave and the bulge to bend.\n- All nineteen materials are separate `ShaderMaterial` instances because each needs its own uniforms, but three keys its program cache on the shader source, so they share a single compiled program.\n- `drive: \"page\"` opts into a shared refcounted Lenis with `infinite: true`, which is the technique in its original form — the gallery *is* the page. The default `drive: \"pointer\"` keeps every gesture inside the component's own box.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn mount the planes start as a flat row facing you and curl into a drum over about 1.6 s. Wheel or drag spins it endlessly in both directions with no seam and no jump at the ends; stop moving and it eases to centre one plane. The centred plane is full colour and the neighbours fade toward grey within two steps. Flick hard and the images ripple, the drum widens, and the ripple runs opposite ways depending on direction. Move the pointer over a plane and a soft lens follows it, magnifying rather than just denting. Unmount from React DevTools and the canvas leaves the DOM with no context-lost warning; mount and unmount twenty times and no context is dropped. With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the drum is already assembled on the first frame and neither the ripple nor the lens ever appears.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dashboard-page-header": "# Build: Dashboard Page Header\n\nThe strip that opens every dashboard page: a small breadcrumb whose team and person crumbs carry a 16px initials circle, a 20px title, and a toolbar on the right. The toolbar has three fixed slots in order (date range, notification bell, actions), so pages line up the same way, and it wraps under the title on narrow screens. A companion PeriodStepper gives the \"< Jun 29 - Jul 5 >\" control that walks the window by its own length.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDashboard page header: breadcrumb with initials crumbs, title, week stepper, unread bell and trailing action buttons.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, react-aria@^3.53.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Breadcrumbs are plain data ({ label, href, onPress, initials, tone, icon }); each maps to the kit BreadcrumbItem, and the last one is rendered with current (aria-current=\"page\", no link or click handler).\n2. A crumb with initials renders an aria-hidden 16px rounded-full span tinted with status-blue / status-lime / status-purple tokens or the tertiary background for neutral; otherwise its icon goes through BreadcrumbItem's icon prop.\n3. The title row is flex-wrap justify-between: the heading (h1 by default, headingLevel 1-3) truncates, and the toolbar is its own flex-wrap gap-2 group so it drops onto a second line instead of squeezing the title.\n4. The bell slot is the notifications prop when given, else a built-in PageHeaderBell whenever notificationCount is defined: an IconButton whose aria-label carries the unread count, plus an aria-hidden red badge that scales from 50% and fades out at zero.\n5. PeriodStepper shifts the range by an explicit step when given; otherwise a whole-calendar-month range moves by its month count (the end rebuilt with endOfMonth from the new start), and any other range moves by its own length in days.\n6. The stepper label comes from useDateFormatter({ day: numeric, month: short, timeZone: UTC }).formatRange, shown in an aria-live <output> so each step is announced; previous/next disable when the shifted range would cross minValue / maxValue.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `crumb initials = 16px circle, 9px semibold`\n  Sits on the 12px breadcrumb line at the same size as the kit's 16px crumb icons.\n- `badge position = top-1 start-[18px], h-3.5 min-w-3.5`\n  Puts the count over the bell glyph's top-right shoulder inside the 36px IconButton without covering the bell.\n- `badge max = 9`\n  A 14px badge holds two characters; larger counts read as 9+.\n- `stepper height = h-9 (medium) / h-8 (small)`\n  Matches the 36px Button and IconButton beside it so the toolbar shares one baseline.\n- `badge transition = 200ms ease-out on scale and opacity`\n  Quick enough to confirm \"marked read\" without delaying the next click; removed under motion-reduce.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function DashboardPageHeader({ title, description, breadcrumbs, breadcrumbLabel, dateRange, notificationCount, onNotificationsPress, notificationsLabel, notifications, actions, headingLevel, className, ...props }: DashboardPageHeaderProps): JSX.Element\nexport function PageHeaderBell({ count, max, label, onPress, className }: PageHeaderBellProps): JSX.Element\nexport function PeriodStepper({ value, defaultValue, onChange, step, minValue, maxValue, formatOptions, size, 'aria-label', previousLabel, nextLabel, className }: PeriodStepperProps): JSX.Element\nexport function shiftPeriod(range: PeriodRange, direction: 1 | -1, step?: DateDuration): PeriodRange\nexport function usePeriodLabel(range: PeriodRange, formatOptions?: Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions): string\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Page title, rendered as a truncating 20px heading.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Optional secondary line under the title.\n- `breadcrumbs: readonly PageHeaderCrumb[]` — default `[]`\n  Ancestors first; the last crumb is the current page. Each crumb: label, href, onPress, initials, tone, icon.\n- `breadcrumbLabel: string` — default `'Breadcrumb'`\n  Accessible name of the breadcrumb nav.\n- `dateRange: ReactNode`\n  First toolbar slot, typically a PeriodStepper.\n- `notificationCount: number`\n  Unread count for the built-in bell; leaving it undefined (and notifications unset) hides the bell.\n- `onNotificationsPress: () => void`\n  Called when the built-in bell is clicked.\n- `notificationsLabel: string` — default `'Notifications'`\n  Base accessible label of the bell; \", N unread\" is appended when the count is above zero.\n- `notifications: ReactNode`\n  Replaces the built-in bell in the second toolbar slot.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Trailing buttons; put the primary action last.\n- `headingLevel: 1 | 2 | 3` — default `1`\n  Heading element used for the title.\n- `PeriodStepper.value / defaultValue: PeriodRange`\n  Controlled or initial { start, end } CalendarDate range.\n- `PeriodStepper.onChange: (range: PeriodRange) => void`\n  Called with the shifted range after each previous / next press.\n- `PeriodStepper.step: DateDuration`\n  Fixed shift per press; defaults to the range length, or its month count for whole months.\n- `PeriodStepper.minValue / maxValue: CalendarDate`\n  Bounds; the matching button disables once a step would cross them.\n- `PeriodStepper.size: 'medium' | 'small'` — default `'medium'`\n  36px or 32px tall.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DashboardPageHeader({\n  title,\n  description,\n  breadcrumbs = [],\n  breadcrumbLabel = 'Breadcrumb',\n  dateRange,\n  notificationCount,\n  onNotificationsPress,\n  notificationsLabel,\n  notifications,\n  actions,\n  headingLevel = 1,\n  className,\n  ...props\n}: DashboardPageHeaderProps) {\n  const Heading = (['h1', 'h2', 'h3'] as const)[headingLevel - 1] ?? 'h1';\n  const bell =\n    notifications ??\n    (notificationCount !== undefined ? (\n      <PageHeaderBell count={notificationCount} label={notificationsLabel} onPress={onNotificationsPress} />\n    ) : null);\n  const hasToolbar = Boolean(dateRange || bell || actions);\n  const lastIndex = breadcrumbs.length - 1;\n\n  return (\n    <header {...props} className={cx('flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-4', className)}>\n      {breadcrumbs.length > 0 ? (\n        <Breadcrumb aria-label={breadcrumbLabel} className=\"-mx-1\">\n          {breadcrumbs.map((crumb, index) => (\n            <BreadcrumbItem\n              key={`${index}-${crumb.label}`}\n              icon={crumb.initials ? undefined : crumb.icon}\n              href={index === lastIndex ? undefined : crumb.href}\n              onClick={index === lastIndex ? undefined : crumb.onPress}\n              current={index === lastIndex}\n            >\n              {crumb.initials ? <CrumbInitials initials={crumb.initials} tone={crumb.tone} /> : null}\n              {crumb.label}\n            </BreadcrumbItem>\n          ))}\n        </Breadcrumb>\n      ) : null}\n\n      <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-x-6 gap-y-3\">\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          <Heading className=\"truncate text-title-2-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</Heading>\n          {description ? <p className=\"text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{description}</p> : null}\n        </div>\n        {hasToolbar ? (\n          <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\">\n            {dateRange}\n            {bell}\n            {actions}\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n    </header>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release: no timers, listeners or observers; all state is React state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The badge appears and disappears without its scale/opacity transition, and the stepper and crumb hover colours change instantly (motion-reduce:transition-none). Nothing else moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not shift a whole-month range by adding months to its end: Jan 31 + 1 month clamps to Feb 28 and stays clamped (Mar 28). Rebuild the end from the new start.\n- Do not format CalendarDate ranges in the local time zone: toDate(local) plus a local formatter can render the previous day for users west of UTC. Format with timeZone UTC from toDate(\"UTC\").\n- Do not put the unread count only in the badge: it is aria-hidden, so the IconButton's aria-label must carry it.\n- Do not give the last crumb a link; it is the current page and gets aria-current=\"page\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass notifications={<YourBellPopover />} to replace the built-in bell, e.g. with a bell that opens a notification list.\n- PeriodStepper is controlled (value + onChange) or uncontrolled (defaultValue); it throws when given neither.\n- Without dateRange, the bell and actions the toolbar is not rendered, leaving a plain breadcrumb and title.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPressing \"Next week\" changes the stepper label from Jun 29 - Jul 5 to Jul 6 - 12, and pressing the bell hides its badge and drops the count from its accessible name.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "data-grid": "# Build: Data Grid\n\nA spreadsheet-style grid for thousands of rows: only the rows in view are mounted, a frozen first column and a row-number gutter stay put while the body scrolls, and a keyboard-driven cell cursor selects ranges, edits cells and pastes blocks from a spreadsheet. Every edit, paste and row delete goes back through `onDataChange` as a new array and lands on a 100-step undo stack. Around the body sit a toolbar (search, filters, column visibility, density, export) that swaps to a bulk-action bar when rows are checked, a summary row and a status bar with the selection address, count, sum and average.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA virtualized spreadsheet with range selection, validated in-cell edits, block paste, undo and redo, filters, summaries and CSV export.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, motion@^12.43.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Virtualization: the body is a fixed-height scroller (`height`, default 396) holding one spacer `rows.length * rowHeight` tall. Only rows from `floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - 4` to `ceil((scrollTop + height) / rowHeight) + 4` render, each absolutely positioned at `top: r * rowHeight`. Rows are 44px, or 36px in Compact density, and every row shares one CSS grid template: a 48px gutter plus each column's width.\n2. The header and summary rows sit outside the scroller with `overflow: hidden`; the body's `onScroll` copies its `scrollLeft` into both so the columns stay aligned. A `ResizeObserver` measures the body, and the first column is frozen (`sticky` at `inset-inline-start: 48px`) only while the body is at least gutter + first column + 96px wide, so on a phone it scrolls away instead of covering everything.\n3. Range selection stores an anchor and a focus as `{ rowId, columnKey }`, not indices. `bounds` maps them through the current row and column index maps, so sorting, filtering or hiding columns keeps the selection on the same cells, and if an end cell drops out no range is drawn. Click selects, Shift+click and mouse drag (`pointerenter` with the button held) extend, Ctrl/Cmd+A selects everything, and `selectCell` scrolls the cursor into view on both axes, accounting for the frozen column and RTL.\n4. The grid is one focusable `role=\"grid\"` element with `aria-activedescendant` pointing at the focused cell id (only while that row is mounted). Arrows move (mirrored in RTL), Page Up/Down jump by a viewport of rows, Home/End go to the row ends (with Ctrl/Cmd, to the first or last row), Enter or F2 opens the editor, Escape clears the range, Ctrl/Cmd+Z undoes and Shift+Ctrl/Cmd+Z or Ctrl/Cmd+Y redoes. The cursor outline is a `motion.span` with a shared `layoutId`, so it springs from cell to cell.\n5. Editing: double-click, Enter or F2 swaps the cell for an input (select columns show their select whenever the cell is active). On commit the text goes through `parseGridValue` (numbers, min, max, allowed options) and the column's `validate`; a failure keeps the editor open and shows \"Header: message\" in a `role=\"alert\"` bar. Enter commits and moves down, Shift+Enter up, Tab and Shift+Tab to the next or previous cell in reading order, and Escape cancels.\n6. Clipboard and history: `onCopy` writes the range as TSV; `onPaste` parses TSV (quotes, CRLF), validates every cell first and cancels the whole paste on the first bad value, so a paste is all or nothing and one undo step. `commitEdits` applies `{ rowId, key, before, after }` edits with `applyGridEdits` and pushes them onto the undo stack (last 100 kept); deleting checked rows records each row with its index so undo splices them back. Before undoing, the grid checks the current data still holds the values it expects and clears the history instead if something outside changed them.\n7. Toolbar and footer: the default toolbar shows the row count, Undo, Redo, Filter (a column switcher, a value search and one exact-match value per column), Columns, Row density, Export and Search. Checking rows cross-fades it (180ms, `AnimatePresence`, the leaving one `inert`) to a bar with Delete, Copy and Export for the checked rows. Summary cells show Sum, Avg or Count over the filtered rows; the status bar shows the range address (\"B3:D7\"), the count of filled cells and, for numbers, their sum and average.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `row height = 44px Comfortable, 36px Compact`\n  Fixed row heights are what make virtualization pure arithmetic: the scroll offset alone tells the grid which rows to mount and where.\n- `OVERSCAN = 4 rows above and below the viewport`\n  A small buffer so a fast wheel flick does not show blank rows before React renders the next window.\n- `GUTTER = 48px row-number and checkbox column`\n  Holds the row number and the row checkbox, which fades in over the number on hover or focus and stays visible once checked.\n- `column width = default 160px, resizable 96 to 480px, 16px per arrow key`\n  The clamp keeps a column from vanishing or eating the viewport; double-clicking the resize handle restores the column's own width.\n- `cursor spring = stiffness 480, damping 38, mass 0.7`\n  Stiff and well damped, so the outline arrives under key repeat without overshooting into the next cell.\n- `undo depth = 100 transactions`\n  Bounds memory for long sessions; each paste or bulk delete is one transaction however many cells it touched.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DataGrid<T extends object>(props: DataGridProps<T>): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Names the grid; also labels the search field.\n- `data: readonly T[]`\n  The rows. Treat as immutable; changes come back through `onDataChange`.\n- `columns: readonly DataGridColumn<T>[]`\n  Key, header, width, type (text, number or select), options, min, max, align, editable, filterable, summary (sum, average or count), format, formatSummary and validate.\n- `getRowId: (row: T) => string`\n  Stable id per row; selection, edits and undo are keyed by it.\n- `onDataChange: (data: T[]) => void`\n  Receives the next array after an edit, paste, delete, undo or redo. Omit for read-only.\n- `height: number` — default `396`\n  Height of the scrolling body in pixels.\n- `exportFileName: string` — default `\"data-grid.csv\"`\n  File name for CSV export.\n- `emptyState: ReactNode` — default `\"No matching rows. Try another search or clear your filters.\"`\n  Shown when search or filters leave no rows.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the outer frame.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst rows = useMemo(() => {\n  const search = query.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();\n  const filtered = data.filter((row) =>\n    (!search || columns.some((column) => String(row[column.key] ?? \"\").toLocaleLowerCase().includes(search))) &&\n    columns.every((column) => filters[column.key] === undefined || String(row[column.key] ?? \"\") === filters[column.key]),\n  );\n  if (!sort) return filtered;\n  const key = sort.key as keyof T;\n  return filtered.sort((a, b) => {\n    const left = a[key], right = b[key];\n    const result = typeof left === \"number\" && typeof right === \"number\" ? left - right : String(left ?? \"\").localeCompare(String(right ?? \"\"), undefined, { numeric: true });\n    return sort.descending ? -result : result;\n  });\n}, [data, columns, filters, query, sort]);\nconst rowIndices = useMemo(() => new Map(rows.map((row, index) => [getRowId(row), index])), [rows, getRowId]);\nconst columnIndices = useMemo(() => new Map(visibleColumns.map((column, index) => [column.key as string, index])), [visibleColumns]);\nconst bounds: Bounds | null = useMemo(() => {\n  if (!range) return null;\n  const a = rowIndices.get(range.anchor.rowId), b = rowIndices.get(range.focus.rowId);\n  const c = columnIndices.get(range.anchor.columnKey), d = columnIndices.get(range.focus.columnKey);\n  return a === undefined || b === undefined || c === undefined || d === undefined ? null : {\n    top: Math.min(a, b), bottom: Math.max(a, b), start: Math.min(c, d), end: Math.max(c, d),\n  };\n}, [range, rowIndices, columnIndices]);\nconst columnWidths = visibleColumns.map((column) => widths[column.key] ?? column.width ?? 160);\nconst freezeFirstColumn = viewportWidth === 0 || viewportWidth >= GUTTER + (columnWidths[0] ?? 0) + 96;\nconst totalWidth = GUTTER + columnWidths.reduce((sum, width) => sum + width, 0);\nconst rowStyle: CSSProperties = { gridTemplateColumns: `${GUTTER}px ${columnWidths.map((width) => `${width}px`).join(\" \")}`, width: totalWidth, minWidth: \"100%\" };\nconst firstRow = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - OVERSCAN);\nconst lastRow = Math.min(rows.length, Math.ceil((scrollTop + height) / rowHeight) + OVERSCAN);\nconst filterCount = Object.keys(filters).length;\nconst visibleSelected = rows.reduce((count, row) => count + Number(selectedRows.has(getRowId(row))), 0);\nconst filterColumns = columns.filter((column) => column.filterable || column.type === \"select\");\nconst filterOptions = useMemo(() => Object.fromEntries(columns.filter((column) => column.filterable || column.type === \"select\").map((column) => [column.key, column.options ?? Array.from(new Set(data.map((row) => String(row[column.key] ?? \"\")))).sort()])), [columns, data]);\nconst summaries = useMemo(() => Object.fromEntries(visibleColumns.map((column) => {\n  if (!column.summary) return [column.key, null];\n  const values = rows.map((row) => row[column.key]).filter((value): value is T[Extract<keyof T, string>] & number => typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value));\n  const sum = values.reduce((total, value) => total + value, 0);\n  return [column.key, column.summary === \"count\" ? rows.length : column.summary === \"average\" ? (values.length ? sum / values.length : null) : sum];\n})), [rows, visibleColumns]);\nconst selectionStats = useMemo(() => {\n  if (!bounds) return { count: 0, sum: null as number | null, average: null as number | null };\n  let count = 0, sum = 0, numbers = 0;\n  for (let r = bounds.top; r <= bounds.bottom; r++) for (let c = bounds.start; c <= bounds.end; c++) {\n    const value = rows[r]![visibleColumns[c]!.key];\n    if (value !== null && value !== undefined && value !== \"\") count++;\n    if (typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value)) { sum += value; numbers++; }\n  }\n  return { count, sum: numbers ? sum : null, average: numbers ? sum / numbers : null };\n}, [bounds, rows, visibleColumns]);\nconst focusRow = range ? rowIndices.get(range.focus.rowId) : undefined;\nconst focusColumn = range ? columnIndices.get(range.focus.columnKey) : undefined;\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!freezeFirstColumn && focusColumn === 0 && bodyRef.current) bodyRef.current.scrollLeft = 0;\n}, [freezeFirstColumn, focusColumn]);\nconst cellId = (r: number, c: number) => `${id}-cell-${r}-${c}`;\nconst activeId = focusRow !== undefined && focusColumn !== undefined && focusRow >= firstRow && focusRow < lastRow ? cellId(focusRow, focusColumn) : undefined;\nconst selectionAddress = bounds ? `${columnLetter(bounds.start)}${bounds.top + 1}${bounds.top !== bounds.bottom || bounds.start !== bounds.end ? `:${columnLetter(bounds.end)}${bounds.bottom + 1}` : \"\"}` : \"—\";\n\nfunction resetScroll() {\n  if (bodyRef.current) bodyRef.current.scrollTop = 0;\n  setScrollTop(0);\n  setEditing(null);\n  setErrorMessage(\"\");\n}\n\nfunction selectCell(r: number, c: number, extend = false) {\n  if (!rows.length || !visibleColumns.length) return;\n  r = Math.max(0, Math.min(rows.length - 1, r));\n  c = Math.max(0, Math.min(visibleColumns.length - 1, c));\n  const cell = { rowId: getRowId(rows[r]!), columnKey: visibleColumns[c]!.key };\n  setRange((previous) => ({ anchor: extend && previous && bounds ? previous.anchor : cell, focus: cell }));\n  setErrorMessage(\"\");\n  gridRef.current?.focus({ preventScroll: true });\n  const body = bodyRef.current;\n  if (body) {\n    const top = r * rowHeight;\n    if (top < body.scrollTop) body.scrollTop = top;\n    else if (top + rowHeight > body.scrollTop + body.clientHeight) body.scrollTop = top + rowHeight - body.clientHeight;\n    if (c > 0 || !freezeFirstColumn) {\n      const start = GUTTER + columnWidths.slice(0, c).reduce((sum, width) => sum + width, 0);\n      const offset = Math.abs(body.scrollLeft);\n      const frozenWidth = GUTTER + (freezeFirstColumn ? (columnWidths[0] ?? 0) : 0);\n      if (start < offset + frozenWidth) body.scrollLeft = (direction === \"rtl\" ? -1 : 1) * Math.max(0, start - frozenWidth);\n      else if (start + columnWidths[c]! > offset + body.clientWidth) body.scrollLeft = (direction === \"rtl\" ? -1 : 1) * (start + columnWidths[c]! - body.clientWidth);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction commitEdits(edits: GridEdit<T>[]) {\n  if (!onDataChange || !edits.length) return;\n  onDataChange(applyGridEdits(data, getRowId, edits));\n  setHistory((previous) => ({ undo: [...previous.undo.slice(-99), { type: \"edit\", edits }], redo: [] }));\n  setMessage(`${integerFormat.format(edits.length)} ${edits.length === 1 ? \"cell updated\" : \"cells updated\"}.`);\n  setErrorMessage(\"\");\n}\n\nfunction travelHistory(undo: boolean) {\n  const source = undo ? history.undo : history.redo;\n  const transaction = source.at(-1);\n  if (!transaction || !onDataChange) return;\n  // External updates must not be overwritten by an old local history entry.\n  const current = new Map(data.map((row) => [getRowId(row), row]));\n  const stale = transaction.type === \"edit\"\n    ? transaction.edits.some((edit) => !current.has(edit.rowId) || !Object.is(current.get(edit.rowId)![edit.key], undo ? edit.after : edit.before))\n    : transaction.rows.some(({ row }) => undo ? current.has(getRowId(row)) : current.get(getRowId(row)) !== row);\n  if (stale) {\n    setHistory({ undo: [], redo: [] });\n    setMessage(\"The data changed outside this grid. Change history has been cleared.\");\n    return;\n  }\n  if (transaction.type === \"edit\") onDataChange(applyGridEdits(data, getRowId, transaction.edits, undo));\n  else if (undo) {\n    const next = [...data];\n    for (const { row, index } of transaction.rows) next.splice(Math.min(index, next.length), 0, row);\n    onDataChange(next);\n  } else {\n    const removed = new Set(transaction.rows.map(({ row }) => getRowId(row)));\n    onDataChange(data.filter((row) => !removed.has(getRowId(row))));\n  }\n  setHistory((previous) => undo ? { undo: previous.undo.slice(0, -1), redo: [...previous.redo, transaction] } : { undo: [...previous.undo, transaction], redo: previous.redo.slice(0, -1) });\n  setEditing(null);\n  setMessage(undo ? \"Change undone.\" : \"Change redone.\");\n  setErrorMessage(\"\");\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The `ResizeObserver` on the body disconnects on unmount. Column-resize listeners live on the handle and are removed on `lostpointercapture`. The CSV export's object URL is revoked one second after the download starts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion` (via motion's `useReducedMotion`) the toolbar cross-fade and the cursor spring get zero duration, so the bars swap and the outline jumps instantly. The 150ms cell background fade is dropped with `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not track the selection by row index. The range stores row ids and column keys, which is why a sort or filter keeps it on the same cells; index-based ranges jump to different data.\n- Do not mutate `data` in place. The filtered rows, index maps, filter options and summaries are memoized on the `data` reference, so an in-place change leaves them stale; replace the array, as the one `onDataChange` hands back does.\n- Do not create `columns` or `getRowId` inline on every render. They are memo dependencies, so each new identity rebuilds the filtered and sorted rows and the index maps.\n- Do not swap the grid's number formatting back to `toLocaleString()`. Footer summaries without `formatSummary`, the selection Count/Sum/Avg and the row counts all go through module-level `Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\")` formatters, so a de-DE browser shows the same \"100,716\" as the server render instead of \"100.716\". To show another locale, pass `formatSummary` with your own formatter.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Omit `onDataChange` for a read-only grid: editing, paste, undo, redo and row delete are disabled and the grid reports `aria-readonly`.\n- Search matches any column's text, case-insensitively; filters match exact values. Filter options come from `options` or from the distinct values in `data`.\n- Export writes the visible columns of the checked rows (or of every filtered row when none are checked) as UTF-8 CSV with a BOM. `serializeGridRows` prefixes cells that start with =, +, - or @ with an apostrophe so spreadsheets do not run them as formulas.\n- Copy selected rows uses `navigator.clipboard.writeText`, so it needs a secure context and clipboard permission; a refusal shows an error instead of failing silently.\n- Composes kit items: Button, CloseButton, Checkbox, Dropdown, Input, Select, SegmentedControl and Tooltip.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe grid shows its row count, a header with a frozen first column and a body that scrolls smoothly with only a window of rows in the DOM. Arrow keys move the outlined cursor, Shift+arrows extend a range and the status bar shows its address, count, sum and average. Double-clicking a number cell and entering text shows \"Seats: Enter a valid number.\" and keeps the editor open; a valid value updates the cell and the column summary, and Ctrl/Cmd+Z restores it. Pasting a 2x2 block from a spreadsheet fills four cells as one undo step. Checking rows swaps the toolbar to the bulk bar; Delete removes them and Undo brings them back in place. Filter by a value, hide a column, switch to Compact and export a CSV.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "data-table": "# Build: Data Table\n\nThe dashboard customers table from BoardUI: a toolbar with a result count, price, product and region selects and a search field, then a sortable, selectable, paginated table of 48 seeded customers with avatar initials, a purchase-status select, a status chip, a date, a price and row actions. A @tanstack/react-table instance owns the sort, selection and page state, and the kit's React Aria `Table` only renders what TanStack hands it. A Normal / Compact switch under the table changes the density of every cell at once.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA customers table where TanStack owns sorting, selection and pages, filters narrow the rows, and React Aria renders the grid.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, @tanstack/react-table@^8.21.3, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Rows come from a module-level array: 48 customers drawn from a seeded PRNG (seed 42), so server and client generate the same list. The first eight use fixed names; the rest combine random first and last names. Each row carries a purchase state, one of four statuses, a product, a region, a price from $20 to $3,999 and a 2026 date, plus `updatedTs` for sorting.\n2. Filtering happens before TanStack sees the data: a `useMemo` keeps the customers that match the price bucket, product, region and a case-insensitive name search. Every filter `onSelectionChange` and every keystroke in search calls `table.setPageIndex(0)`, so narrowing the list never strands you on a page that no longer exists.\n3. `useReactTable` gets the filtered data, column defs, `getRowId: row => row.id` and controlled `sorting`, `rowSelection` and `pagination` state, with the core, sorted and pagination row models. Name, Last updated (sorted on `updatedTs`) and Price are sortable; Purchase, Status and Actions set `enableSorting: false`. Two rows start selected.\n4. The kit `Table` is React Aria's with `selectionMode=\"none\"`, so React Aria never keeps a second selection. Selection is TanStack's: each name cell holds a `Checkbox` with `slot={null}` wired to `row.toggleSelected`, the header holds a select-all-on-this-page checkbox that shows indeterminate for a partial page, and selected rows get the secondary background through an inline style.\n5. Sortable headers are plain buttons calling `column.getToggleSortingHandler()`; the `SortChevron` turns 180 degrees for ascending over 150ms and darkens when active. Rows render `flexRender` cells into fixed-width columns inside a `min-w-[1000px]` table whose container scrolls horizontally on narrow screens. An empty filter result shows \"No customers match your filters.\"\n6. Row actions are Delete and Edit `IconButton`s wrapped in `Focusable` so a React Aria `TooltipTrigger` can attach (200ms delay), and a More trigger styled like an IconButton that opens a four-item dropdown. The pagination footer appears only when there is more than one page. The density switch sets the table `size` to `md` or `sm`, which also swaps avatar, select and chip sizes, and the `.bui-table` styles animate cell padding and font size over 200ms.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `page size = 8 rows (prop `pageSize`)`\n  Eight Normal-density rows plus the toolbar and footer fit a laptop viewport; BoardUI's landing collage passes 5 to match the Figma crop.\n- `seeded data = mulberry32-style PRNG, seed 42, 48 rows`\n  Enough rows for six pages and meaningful filtering, and deterministic so server-rendered markup matches the client.\n- `column widths = name 240, purchase 172, status 168, updated 160, price 140, actions 132 (px); table min-w-[1000px]`\n  Fixed widths stop columns from jumping when sorting or paging changes the longest value; the minimum width keeps the action buttons from wrapping and lets the container scroll instead.\n- `cell density = Normal px-3 py-2.5 body; Compact px-2.5 py-1.5 body-2; 200ms transition`\n  The density switch animates padding and font size together, so the rows compress smoothly instead of snapping.\n- `tooltip delay = 200ms`\n  Long enough that sweeping across a row of icon buttons does not flash tooltips, short enough to label an icon on purpose.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DataTableExample(props?: { showSizeToggle?: boolean; pageSize?: number }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `pageSize: number` — default `8`\n  Rows per page.\n- `showSizeToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Shows the Normal / Compact density switch below the table.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst data = useMemo(() => {\n  const bucket = PRICE_BUCKETS.find((b) => b.id === priceFilter) ?? PRICE_BUCKETS[0]!;\n  const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();\n  return CUSTOMERS.filter(\n    (c) =>\n      bucket.test(c.price) &&\n      (productFilter === \"all\" || c.product === productFilter) &&\n      (regionFilter === \"all\" || c.region === regionFilter) &&\n      (q === \"\" || c.name.toLowerCase().includes(q)),\n  );\n}, [priceFilter, productFilter, regionFilter, query]);\n\nconst columns = useMemo<ColumnDef<Customer>[]>(\n  () => [\n    {\n      accessorKey: \"name\",\n      header: \"Customer name\",\n      cell: ({ row }) => {\n        const c = row.original;\n        return localize((\n          <div className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-2\">\n            <Checkbox\n              slot={null}\n              aria-label={`Select ${c.name}`}\n              isSelected={row.getIsSelected()}\n              onChange={(v) => row.toggleSelected(!!v)}\n            />\n            {c.avatar ? (\n              <Avatar size={avatarSize} src={c.avatar} alt=\"\" />\n            ) : (\n              <Avatar size={avatarSize} color={c.initialsColor} initials={initialsOf(c.name)} />\n            )}\n            <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{c.name}</span>\n          </div>\n        ));\n      },\n    },\n    {\n      id: \"purchase\",\n      enableSorting: false,\n      header: \"Purchase\",\n      cell: ({ row }) => localize((\n        <PurchaseSelect value={row.original.purchase} name={row.original.name} size={size} />\n      )),\n    },\n    {\n      id: \"status\",\n      enableSorting: false,\n      header: \"Status\",\n      cell: ({ row }) => localize((\n        <Chip variant={size === \"sm\" ? \"caption\" : \"bold\"} color={row.original.status.color}>\n          {row.original.status.label}\n        </Chip>\n      )),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorKey: \"updatedTs\",\n      header: \"Last updated\",\n      cell: ({ row }) => localize((\n        <span className=\"whitespace-nowrap text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{row.original.updated}</span>\n      )),\n    },\n    {\n      accessorKey: \"price\",\n      header: \"Price\",\n      cell: ({ row }) => localize((\n        <Chip variant={size === \"sm\" ? \"caption\" : \"subtle\"} color=\"gray\">\n          {formatPrice(row.original.price)}\n        </Chip>\n      )),\n    },\n    {\n      id: \"actions\",\n      enableSorting: false,\n      header: \"Actions\",\n      cell: ({ row }) => localize((\n        <div className=\"flex items-center justify-end gap-2.5\">\n          <RowActionButton icon={RiDeleteBin6Line} label=\"Delete\" />\n          <RowActionButton icon={RiEditLine} label=\"Edit\" />\n          <RowMoreMenu name={row.original.name} />\n        </div>\n      )),\n    },\n  ],\n  [avatarSize, size, localize],\n);\n\nconst table = useReactTable({\n  data,\n  columns,\n  getRowId: (row) => row.id,\n  state: { sorting, rowSelection, pagination },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onRowSelectionChange: setRowSelection,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n  getCoreRowModel: getCoreRowModel(),\n  getSortedRowModel: getSortedRowModel(),\n  getPaginationRowModel: getPaginationRowModel(),\n  enableRowSelection: true,\n});\n\nconst resetPage = () => table.setPageIndex(0);\nconst headers = table.getHeaderGroups()[0]?.headers ?? [];\nconst rows = table.getRowModel().rows;\nconst totalPages = table.getPageCount();\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or global listeners of its own. The TanStack instance lives in component state, and React Aria tears down tooltip, select and dropdown overlays when they close or unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The block does not branch on `prefers-reduced-motion`. Its motion is the 150ms sort-chevron turn, 150ms row and button colour fades, the 200ms density change and the kit popovers' short entrances; none of it carries information the static state lacks.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not let React Aria and TanStack both own selection. The table uses `selectionMode=\"none\"` and the checkboxes use `slot={null}`; otherwise React Aria claims the checkboxes for its own selection model and the two states drift apart.\n- Do not drop `getRowId`. With TanStack's default index ids, selection belongs to positions, so filtering or sorting moves the checkmarks onto different customers.\n- Do not change filters without resetting `pageIndex`. Filtering to fewer rows while on page 5 leaves the table showing an empty page.\n- Do not put the real `IconButton` inside `DropdownTrigger`. Both render buttons, so the More trigger restyles the trigger itself to avoid nesting a button in a button.\n- Do not generate the sample data with `Math.random()` at render time. The seeded PRNG at module level is what keeps hydration stable.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The purchase select in each row is uncontrolled (`defaultSelectedKey`) and does not write back to the data, so a change resets when the row remounts, for example after paging. Lift it into state if it must persist.\n- `formatPrice` prints prices of 1,000 or more with a dot separator (\"$1.250\"), as in the Figma frame; swap in `Intl.NumberFormat` for your locale.\n- All strings go through `useTemplateCopy`, so a `TemplateCopyProvider` can translate the toolbar, headers and empty state.\n- Composes kit items: Table, Select, Checkbox, Avatar, Chip, StatusDot, IconButton, Tooltip, Dropdown, InputBase, Pagination and SegmentedControl.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe toolbar reads \"Total Results 48 customers\" with All prices, All products and All regions selects and a Search field; the table shows 8 rows with two selected and a pagination footer. Clicking \"Customer name\" sorts A to Z and turns the chevron, clicking again sorts Z to A. Choosing \"Over $1,000\" or typing a name narrows the count, returns to page 1 and keeps the selection on the same customers. The header checkbox selects or clears the current page and shows indeterminate when only some rows are selected. Hovering Edit shows its tooltip; the More button opens View profile, Duplicate row, Download invoice and Archive customer. Compact tightens every row smoothly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dataset-parallax": "# Build: Dataset-Driven Parallax\n\nDepth is declared in the markup: any descendant carrying `data-parallax=\"0.3\"` drifts at that rate. One effect walks the container, parses the attribute and builds a scrubbed tween per layer, so adding a new plane to a hero is an attribute rather than a code change. Travel is expressed as a percentage of the element's own height, which keeps the effect proportional at every breakpoint.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLayers drift at a rate written on the element itself, over the widest possible scroll window.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The container is walked once for `[data-parallax]` descendants. The depth is read with a double fallback — `parseFloat(el.getAttribute(attr) ?? \"\") || fallback` — so a missing value, an empty value and a non-numeric value all land on the same default.\n2. Each layer gets `gsap.to(layer, { yPercent: -(100 * depth), ease: \"none\" })`. `yPercent` is a percentage of the element's own height, so a 2000px hero and a 400px mobile hero travel proportionally with no media query.\n3. `ease: \"none\"` is required, not stylistic. Any easing on a scrubbed tween means the layer accelerates and decelerates independently of the wheel, so two layers at different depths drift out of sync mid-scroll and snap back at the ends.\n4. The trigger window is `start: \"top bottom\"` to `end: \"bottom top\"` — the full travel range, from the instant the element enters the scroller to the instant it leaves. That is the widest window available, which spreads the same travel across the most scroll distance.\n5. `invalidateOnRefresh: true` makes ScrollTrigger recompute start and end on resize; percentage-based travel is derived from element height and a stale measurement leaves every layer offset.\n6. Under reduced motion the effect returns before creating anything, so the layers render at their authored positions.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DEFAULT_DEPTH = 0.15`\n  Fallback drift: 15% of the element's height across the whole window. Below ~0.05 the layer looks static next to its neighbours; above ~0.4 it visibly separates from the content it belongs to and reads as a rendering fault.\n- `start / end = 'top bottom' / 'bottom top'`\n  The maximum-length window. Narrowing it (say `top 80%` to `bottom 20%`) forces the same travel through less scroll, which is the difference between drift and the page appearing to come apart.\n- `yPercent multiplier = -100`\n  Negative so layers move against the scroll — the direction that reads as distance. A positive multiplier moves them with the scroll, which reads as the layer being dragged.\n- `scrub = true`\n  Locked to scroll position with no lag. Parallax is a spatial relationship: a numeric scrub adds lag, and lagging layers cross each other during a fast flick.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DatasetParallax(props: { children: ReactNode; attribute?: string; fallback?: number; scrub?: boolean | number; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Section content. Any descendant with the depth attribute becomes a layer.\n- `attribute: string` — default `'data-parallax'`\n  Attribute carrying the depth. Its value is parsed as a float.\n- `fallback: number` — default `0.15`\n  Depth used when the attribute is missing, empty or not a number.\n- `scrub: boolean | number` — default `true`\n  ScrollTrigger scrub. A number adds that many seconds of lag.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst DEFAULT_DEPTH = 0.15;\n\nexport function DatasetParallax({\n  children,\n  attribute = 'data-parallax',\n  fallback = DEFAULT_DEPTH,\n  scrub = true,\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: DatasetParallaxProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      if (!el || reduced) return;\n\n      const scroller = scrollerRef?.current ?? undefined;\n\n      for (const layer of Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(`[${attribute}]`))) {\n        // `?? ''` handles a bare `data-parallax` with no value, and the finite\n        // check handles `parseFloat` returning NaN on anything non-numeric. A\n        // NaN reaching `yPercent` does not throw — it silently pins the layer\n        // at its untransformed position, which looks like the effect simply\n        // not working on that one element.\n        //\n        // Tested with `Number.isFinite`, not `||`. `data-parallax=\"0\"` is the\n        // documented way to hold a layer still against the scroll, and zero is\n        // falsy: `|| fallback` read that as \"no value given\" and moved the one\n        // layer the author had explicitly asked not to move.\n        const raw = layer.getAttribute(attribute) ?? '';\n        const parsed = parseFloat(raw);\n        const depth = Number.isFinite(parsed) ? parsed : fallback;\n\n        gsap.to(layer, {\n          yPercent: -(100 * depth),\n          ease: 'none',\n          scrollTrigger: {\n            trigger: layer,\n            scroller,\n            // The widest possible window: begins the instant the layer's top\n            // touches the bottom of the scroller and ends when its bottom\n            // leaves the top. Any narrower range concentrates the same travel\n            // into less scroll, which is what makes cheap parallax feel like\n            // the page is sliding apart.\n            start: 'top bottom',\n            end: 'bottom top',\n            scrub,\n            // Percent-based travel is recomputed from element height; without\n            // this a resize leaves every layer measured against the old one.\n            invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n          },\n        });\n      }\n    },\n    root,\n    [attribute, fallback, scrub, reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` wraps the whole walk in a `gsap.context`; `ctx.revert()` on unmount kills every tween and its ScrollTrigger and strips the inline `transform` GSAP wrote. Without the revert the triggers survive the unmount and keep scrubbing detached nodes on the next page.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The effect returns before creating any tween, so every layer stays exactly where the stylesheet put it. The layout is authored to be correct at rest, so nothing is missing — the planes simply do not separate.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `y` in pixels instead of `yPercent`. It looks correct at the breakpoint you tuned and wrong at every other one — on a short viewport a fixed 300px offset can push the layer entirely out of its own container.\n- A single `|| 0.15` fallback. `el.dataset.parallax || \"0.15\"` covers the missing attribute, but `parseFloat(\"fast\")` returns `NaN` and `NaN` reaching `yPercent` does not throw — the layer just silently stops moving, which is a bug you find by eye weeks later.\n- Adding an ease to a scrubbed tween. The layer then accelerates on its own schedule rather than the wheel's, so layers at different depths drift out of relative order and snap at each end of the range.\n- Omitting `invalidateOnRefresh`. Rotating a phone or opening a sidebar changes element height; the tween keeps using the height measured on mount and every layer sits at the wrong offset until reload.\n- Leaving the scroller as the document when the content lives in an `overflow-y-auto` container. Every start and end is then measured against the window, so nothing moves at the right time — or at all.\n- Building one giant timeline for all layers. Independent triggers per layer are correct here: a layer must respond to its own position in the scroller, not to a shared progress value.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The attribute name is configurable so the component can coexist with an existing convention rather than forcing `data-parallax` onto a codebase that already has one.\n- Depths above 1 are legal and useful for a single foreground element that should outrun everything, but two such layers next to each other stop reading as depth.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the section: layers with a larger `data-parallax` value visibly outrun the ones with smaller values, and every layer is back in its authored position when it leaves the scroller. Change an attribute value in devtools and reload — the rate changes with no code edit. Resize the window mid-scroll and the layers stay aligned with their content.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "date-calendar": "# Build: Date Calendar\n\nA month laid out on the page instead of in a popover: `DateCalendar` picks one day, `DateRangeCalendar` picks a span across one or two months. Both are thin wrappers that hand React Aria's `Calendar` and `RangeCalendar` the Date Picker's shared `MonthPanel`, so the card, the 32px day cells and the accent range band are identical to the popover pickers. Selection fades in on opacity rather than popping.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn inline month calendar for one date or a range, with a continuous range band, a today dot and struck-through unavailable days.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `DateCalendar` renders `<Calendar>` from react-aria-components with one `MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev showNext}`. `DateRangeCalendar` renders `<RangeCalendar visibleDuration={{ months: visibleMonths }}>` and either one panel with both chevrons or two panels in a `flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-2` row, the first carrying only the previous chevron and the second (`offset={1}`) only the next, so the pair pages as one unit and stacks when the row is narrow.\n2. `MonthPanel` reads `RangeCalendarStateContext` and `CalendarStateContext`; exactly one is non-null, which tells it whether it sits in a range or a single calendar. The title is `visibleRange.start.add({ months: offset })` formatted with `Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale, { month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\" })`. The prev/next buttons are RAC `Button`s with `slot=\"previous\"` / `slot=\"next\"`, so the calendar wires their paging itself.\n3. The grid is a `CalendarGrid` table with `border-separate border-spacing-3` (12px), pulled out by `-m-3` so the outer spacing does not pad the card. At `max-[380px]` the column spacing drops to 8px (`border-spacing-x-2`, `-mx-2`), so the card is 302px and fits a 360px phone. Weekday headers are cut to two letters when the locale's short weekday is Latin script, and fall back to the `narrow` style otherwise.\n4. Each `DayCell` always renders two absolutely positioned layers: a range band (`bg-date-range-background`) and an edge pill (`bg-date-range-edge-background`). Selection only flips their `opacity` between 0 and 1 with `transition-[opacity,border-radius] duration-100`, so dragging a range across ~40 cells is a CSS transition, not mounts and unmounts.\n5. The band bridges the 12px gaps: a selected middle cell extends 6px (`-start-1.5` / `-end-1.5`) toward each selected neighbour, the start and end cells begin at `start-1/2` / `end-1/2` so the band runs under half the pill, and the first and last column of a week never extend and get `rounded-s-lg` / `rounded-e-lg` instead.\n6. A plain `Calendar` never sets `isSelectionStart` / `isSelectionEnd`, so the cell computes `isSingleDay` and `isEdge` from `isSelected` when not in a range; otherwise the one picked day would render as a mid-range band. Days outside the month render as an empty `size-8` box, unavailable days (`isDateUnavailable`) get `line-through text-text-tertiary`, and `showToday` adds a 4px dot at `bottom-1`, accent on a plain day and `bg-text-primary` on a selected one.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `panel = w-[326px], p-[15px], rounded-2xl, shadow-xs; w-[302px] at max-[380px]`\n  Seven 32px cells with six 12px gaps is 296px; 15px of padding on each side gives the 326px card from Figma. The bare variant is exactly that 296px. A 360px phone leaves about 312px, so up to 380px the column gap is 8px: 272px of grid, a 302px card.\n- `day cell / gap = 32px (size-8) / border-spacing 12px (8px × 12px at max-[380px])`\n  The Figma grid. The gap is real table spacing, which is why the range band needs the 6px bridge to read as one continuous strip.\n- `band bridge = 6px (-start-1.5 / -end-1.5); 4px at max-[380px]`\n  Half of the 12px gap from each side meets in the middle. More would overlap the neighbouring band and double the colour where the two layers cross.\n- `selection fade = 100ms ease-out on opacity and border-radius`\n  Fast enough that a range follows the pointer, slow enough that the band visibly grows rather than flickering. Radius rides the same transition so a deselected edge eases square in sync with the fade.\n- `today dot = size-1 (4px) at bottom-1`\n  Sits under the numeral inside the 32px cell without touching it, and stays visible on top of the selected pill by switching to `bg-text-primary`.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DateCalendar<T extends DateValue>(props: Omit<CalendarProps<T>, \"className\" | \"children\" | \"visibleDuration\"> & { showToday?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction DateRangeCalendar<T extends DateValue>(props: Omit<RangeCalendarProps<T>, \"className\" | \"children\" | \"visibleDuration\"> & { visibleMonths?: 1 | 2; showToday?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value / defaultValue: T | null (DateCalendar) · RangeValue<T> | null (DateRangeCalendar)`\n  Controlled or initial selection as `@internationalized/date` values.\n- `onChange: (value) => void`\n  Fires with the picked date, or with `{ start, end }` once a range is complete.\n- `visibleMonths: 1 | 2` — default `2`\n  DateRangeCalendar only. Two months side by side that wrap to a stack when narrow, or one.\n- `showToday: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draws a 4px dot under today's date.\n- `isDateUnavailable: (date: DateValue) => boolean`\n  Days that can be focused but not picked; rendered struck through.\n- `minValue / maxValue: DateValue`\n  Bounds; days outside are disabled and faded.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the `inline-flex` root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DateCalendar<T extends DateValue>({ showToday = true, className, ...props }: DateCalendarProps<T>) {\n  return (\n    <AriaCalendar {...props} className={cx(\"inline-flex outline-none\", className)}>\n      <MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev showNext showToday={showToday} />\n    </AriaCalendar>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface DateRangeCalendarProps<T extends DateValue>\n  extends Omit<AriaRangeCalendarProps<T>, \"className\" | \"children\" | \"visibleDuration\"> {\n  /** One month, or two side by side (default). They stack when the space is narrow. */\n  visibleMonths?: 1 | 2;\n  /** Mark today with a dot. Default true. */\n  showToday?: boolean;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function DateRangeCalendar<T extends DateValue>({\n  visibleMonths = 2,\n  showToday = true,\n  className,\n  ...props\n}: DateRangeCalendarProps<T>) {\n  return (\n    <AriaRangeCalendar\n      {...props}\n      visibleDuration={{ months: visibleMonths }}\n      className={cx(\"inline-flex outline-none\", className)}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-2\">\n        {visibleMonths === 2 ? (\n          <>\n            <MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev showToday={showToday} />\n            <MonthPanel offset={1} showNext showToday={showToday} />\n          </>\n        ) : (\n          <MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev showNext showToday={showToday} />\n        )}\n      </div>\n    </AriaRangeCalendar>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release. The calendar holds React Aria state only; there are no timers, observers or global listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 100ms opacity and radius fade on selection plus 150ms colour on the chevrons. Neither moves anything, so nothing changes under `prefers-reduced-motion`; months swap instantly with no slide.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not mount the range band and edge pill only when selected. They exist on every cell and change `opacity`; conditional rendering removes the fade and makes range drags janky.\n- Do not derive the single-day pill from `isSelectionStart && isSelectionEnd` in a plain `Calendar`. Those flags are range-only and stay false, so the picked day falls through to the mid-range band. Branch on whether a `RangeCalendarStateContext` exists.\n- Do not replace `border-spacing` with `gap` utilities. `CalendarGrid` is a `<table>`; only `border-separate` plus `border-spacing` spaces its cells.\n- Do not render the calendar without a `value` or `defaultValue` in server-rendered pages. React Aria then focuses today, and today can differ between server and client.\n- Do not omit `aria-label` (or a visible label wired with `aria-labelledby`). The grid is a labelled region, and React Aria warns without one.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Values are `@internationalized/date` objects (`parseDate(\"2026-03-18\")` gives a `CalendarDate`). `minValue` and `maxValue` fade the days outside them; `isDateUnavailable` keeps a day focusable but unpickable and strikes it through.\n- The same `MonthPanel` and `DayCell` power `DatePicker` and `DateRangePicker`, so a fix to the cell lands in all four components.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA month card renders with two-letter weekday headers and 32px days spaced 12px apart. Clicking a day in `DateCalendar` fades in a rounded accent pill and fires `onChange`. In `DateRangeCalendar`, clicking a start and then an end day draws one continuous band between two pills that breaks with rounded ends at week boundaries. Arrow keys move focus by day and Page Up/Down by month; the chevrons page the month. Days passed to `isDateUnavailable` are struck through and cannot be picked.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "date-picker": "# Build: Date Picker\n\nA single-date picker: a calendar-icon trigger shows the committed date, and clicking it opens a popover holding one month, a typed date chip and Cancel / Apply buttons. Picking a day only changes a draft; `onChange` fires on Apply. The popover scales in from 95% with a 2px blur, and the date chip drops in with a slight overshoot the first time a day is chosen.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA trigger button that opens one month in a popover, with an editable DD/MM/YYYY chip and Cancel / Apply so nothing commits early.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The trigger is a plain `<button>` styled with the shared `triggerButtonClassName` (white surface, `rounded-2lg`, `shadow-xs`, 150ms colour transition). Its label is the committed value through `Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale, { month: \"short\", day: \"numeric\", year: \"numeric\" })`, or \"Select date\" when empty, next to a 20px `RiCalendarLine`.\n2. The surface is a standalone React Aria `Popover` with `triggerRef`, `isOpen`, `offset={4}`, `placement=\"bottom end\"` and `isNonModal`, wrapping a `Dialog`. Non-modal avoids the scroll lock and focus trap, but it also turns off outside-click dismissal, so `useDismissOnOutsidePress` closes it on any `pointerdown` outside both the trigger and the popover.\n3. Value is split in two. `committedValue` is the controlled `value` or internal state from `defaultValue`. `pendingValue` is what the `Calendar` edits. Apply calls `commit(pendingValue)`, which sets internal state when uncontrolled, fires `onChange` and closes; Cancel resets the draft to the committed value and closes.\n4. An effect keyed on `isOpen` resets the draft to the committed value and increments `openKey` on every open, and `Calendar` is keyed on `openKey`. React Aria's Calendar keeps its own visible month while mounted (the Popover stays mounted through its exit animation), so without the remount a value changed from outside would reopen on the old month.\n5. Inside the Calendar sits the shared `MonthPanel` (326px card, 32px cells, 12px spacing). The footer's `AnimatePresence` mounts a `motion.div` with the editable `DateChipInput` once a draft exists: `initial { opacity: 0, y: -12 }` to `{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }` over 0.25s with the overshoot curve `[0.34, 1.2, 0.64, 1]`. The chip keeps its own text while typing and commits on blur or Enter; text that does not parse as `DD/MM/YYYY` reverts.\n6. Passing `triggerRef` switches to external mode: DatePicker hides its own button, anchors the popover to the given element and takes `isOpen` / `onOpenChange` from the caller.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover offset / placement = 4px, bottom end`\n  Four pixels clears the trigger's shadow without looking detached. `end` right-aligns the 342px popover to the trigger, so a trigger at the right of a toolbar or card header opens inward.\n- `popover enter / exit = 150ms ease-out, opacity 0, scale-95, blur-[2px], origin-top`\n  From the shared `popoverClassName`. The scale grows down from the trigger edge; the blur hides the text snapping in on the first frame.\n- `date chip entrance = y -12 → 0, 0.25s, ease [0.34, 1.2, 0.64, 1]`\n  The control point above 1 overshoots slightly, so the chip settles rather than sliding to a dead stop. Twelve pixels reads as dropping out of the calendar above it.\n- `z-index = z-110`\n  Above the kit's modal layer (z-100), so a date picker inside a dialog opens over it.\n- `chip width = w-[104px]`\n  Fits \"DD/MM/YYYY\" at the body-medium size with 8px padding, so the field does not resize as digits are typed.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DatePicker(props: { value?: CalendarDate | null; defaultValue?: CalendarDate | null; onChange?: (value: CalendarDate | null) => void; isDisabled?: boolean; className?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; triggerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; isOpen?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: CalendarDate | null`\n  Controlled committed date. Leave undefined for uncontrolled use.\n- `defaultValue: CalendarDate | null` — default `null`\n  Initial committed date when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (value: CalendarDate | null) => void`\n  Fires on Apply with the drafted date, never while browsing the month.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Disables the built-in trigger button.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Date\"`\n  Labels the dialog and the calendar grid.\n- `triggerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Anchor the popover to your own element; DatePicker's button is then not rendered.\n- `isOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Open state, used only together with `triggerRef`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the trigger button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DatePicker({\n  value,\n  defaultValue = null,\n  onChange,\n  isDisabled,\n  className,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Date\",\n  triggerRef: externalTriggerRef,\n  isOpen: controlledIsOpen,\n  onOpenChange: controlledOnOpenChange,\n}: DatePickerProps) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const { locale } = useLocale();\n  const ownTriggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const triggerRef = externalTriggerRef ?? ownTriggerRef;\n  const isExternal = externalTriggerRef !== undefined;\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n\n  const [internalOpen, setInternalOpen] = useState(false);\n  const isOpen = isExternal ? (controlledIsOpen ?? false) : internalOpen;\n  const setIsOpen = isExternal ? (controlledOnOpenChange ?? (() => {})) : setInternalOpen;\n\n  const isControlled = value !== undefined;\n  const [internalValue, setInternalValue] = useState<CalendarDate | null>(defaultValue);\n  const committedValue = isControlled ? (value ?? null) : internalValue;\n\n  const [pendingValue, setPendingValue] = useState<CalendarDate | null>(committedValue);\n  // React Aria's `Calendar` keeps its own internal `visibleRange` state for\n  // as long as it stays mounted (Popover keeps its content mounted between\n  // opens for the exit animation), so it never re-derives the visible month\n  // from a later `value` change on its own. Bumping this on every open and\n  // keying `Calendar` on it forces a fresh mount showing the right month —\n  // needed for the external-trigger case, where `value` can jump (e.g. the\n  // calendar template's month switcher) between one open and the next.\n  const [openKey, setOpenKey] = useState(0);\n\n  // Re-sync the calendar's displayed month/selection to the current\n  // committed value every time the popover opens — not just when DatePicker's\n  // own trigger toggles it, but also when `isOpen` is flipped true by an\n  // external trigger (`triggerRef`), which never runs `openChange` below.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (isOpen) {\n      setPendingValue(committedValue);\n      setOpenKey((k) => k + 1);\n    }\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps -- resync on the open transition only, not every committedValue change\n  }, [isOpen]);\n\n  const commit = (next: CalendarDate | null) => {\n    if (!isControlled) setInternalValue(next);\n    onChange?.(next);\n    setIsOpen(false);\n  };\n\n  const openChange = (open: boolean) => {\n    setIsOpen(open);\n  };\n\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setIsOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\n\n  return localize((\n    <>\n      {!isExternal && (\n        <button\n          ref={ownTriggerRef}\n          type=\"button\"\n          disabled={isDisabled}\n          onClick={() => openChange(!isOpen)}\n          className={cx(triggerButtonClassName, className)}\n        >\n          <RiCalendarLine className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-primary\" aria-hidden />\n          <span className=\"flex items-center justify-center whitespace-nowrap px-1 text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n            {committedValue ? formatTriggerDate(committedValue, locale) : \"Select date\"}\n          </span>\n        </button>\n      )}\n      <Popover\n        ref={popoverRef}\n        triggerRef={triggerRef}\n        isOpen={isOpen}\n        onOpenChange={openChange}\n        offset={4}\n        placement=\"bottom end\"\n        isNonModal\n        className={popoverClassName}\n      >\n        <Dialog aria-label={ariaLabel} className=\"outline-none\">\n          <Calendar key={openKey} aria-label={ariaLabel} value={pendingValue} onChange={setPendingValue}>\n            <div className=\"flex flex-col pt-2 pe-2 pb-3 ps-2\">\n              <MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev showNext />\n              <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between pt-3 pe-4 ps-4\">\n                <div>\n                  <AnimatePresence>\n                    {pendingValue && (\n                      <motion.div\n                        key=\"date-summary\"\n                        initial={{ opacity: 0, y: -12 }}\n                        animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}\n                        exit={{ opacity: 0, y: -12 }}\n                        transition={{ duration: 0.25, ease: [0.34, 1.2, 0.64, 1] }}\n                      >\n                        <DateChipInput date={pendingValue} label=\"Date\" onCommit={setPendingValue} />\n                      </motion.div>\n                    )}\n                  </AnimatePresence>\n                </div>\n                <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2.5\">\n                  <Button\n                    variant=\"secondary\"\n                    onClick={() => {\n                      setPendingValue(committedValue);\n                      setIsOpen(false);\n                    }}\n                  >\n                    Cancel\n                  </Button>\n                  <Button onClick={() => commit(pendingValue)} disabled={!pendingValue}>\n                    Apply\n                  </Button>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </Calendar>\n        </Dialog>\n      </Popover>\n    </>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The outside-press hook removes its document `pointerdown` listener when the popover closes or the component unmounts. Motion's `AnimatePresence` finishes or cancels the chip animation on unmount. No timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not branch on `prefers-reduced-motion`: the popover's 150ms fade, scale and blur and the chip's 12px drop still play. Both are short. Wrap the app in Motion's `<MotionConfig reducedMotion=\"user\">` to drop the chip's transform; the day-cell selection only fades opacity.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not fire `onChange` from the Calendar's own `onChange`. The calendar edits a draft; only Apply commits, and Cancel must leave the committed value untouched.\n- Do not remove the `openKey` remount. The popover content stays mounted between opens, so the Calendar keeps showing the month it last displayed even after `value` moved to another month.\n- Do not set `isNonModal` without restoring outside dismissal. React Aria couples the two, and the popover would then only close through Cancel, Apply or Escape.\n- Do not pass a JS `Date`. `value`, `defaultValue` and `onChange` use `CalendarDate` from `@internationalized/date` (`parseDate(\"2026-03-18\")`).\n- Do not render it with an initial `isOpen` on a server-rendered page. Open it from the trigger.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Strings are run through `useTemplateCopy`, so a `TemplateCopyProvider` higher up can translate \"Select date\", \"Cancel\" and \"Apply\" without forking the component.\n- The month grid, day cell, chip input and popover chrome come from the shared date-picker module, the same code that renders `DateRangePicker` and `DateCalendar`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe trigger shows the current date (e.g. \"Mar 18, 2026\"). Clicking it opens a month card below, right-aligned, fading and scaling in. Clicking a day moves the pill and drops a DD/MM/YYYY chip into the footer, but the trigger label does not change. Typing a valid date in the chip and pressing Enter moves the selection; invalid text reverts on blur. Apply updates the trigger and fires `onChange` once; Cancel or a click outside closes it with the old date intact. Reopening shows the month of the committed date.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "date-range-picker": "# Build: Date Range Picker\n\nA dashboard-style range picker. The trigger shows the committed span (\"Mar 2, 2026 - Mar 15, 2026\"); the popover pairs a column of presets (Today through All time) with two side-by-side months whose selection reads as one continuous band. The footer drops in editable start and end chips and a \"14 days selected\" pill, and nothing is committed until Apply.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTwo adjacent months in a popover with a quick-select sidebar, typed start and end chips, a day count and Cancel / Apply.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A controlled React Aria `DialogTrigger` wraps an `AriaButton` trigger and a `Popover` (`offset={4}`, `placement=\"bottom end\"`, `isNonModal`). Its `onOpenChange` first asks `useTriggerToggle` whether to allow the change, then on open resets the draft to the committed range. `useDismissOnOutsidePress` restores the outside-click close that `isNonModal` turns off.\n2. State is two ranges: `committedValue` (controlled `value`, or internal state seeded from `defaultValue`) and `pendingValue`, which the `RangeCalendar`, the presets and the chips all edit. Apply calls `commit(pendingValue)` (internal state when uncontrolled, then `onChange`) and the Dialog render prop's `close()`; Cancel resets the draft and closes.\n3. `RangeCalendar` gets `visibleDuration={{ months: 2 }}` and renders two shared `MonthPanel`s in a `flex gap-2` row: `offset={0}` with only the previous chevron, `offset={1}` with only the next one. The inner chevron slots render as invisible 16px spacers, because two adjacent months share one navigation state.\n4. The 118px `QuickSelect` column lists eight presets built in a `useMemo` from `today(getLocalTimeZone())`: Today, Yesterday, Last week (the 7 days before today), This month, Last month, This year, Last year and All time (10 years back). A preset whose start and end match the draft with `isSameDay` gets `bg-background-tertiary-default`.\n5. The footer's `AnimatePresence` mounts the summary once a draft exists, sliding from `y: -12` over 0.25s on `[0.34, 1.2, 0.64, 1]`. Two `DateChipInput`s edit start and end as DD/MM/YYYY; a typed start after the end moves the end to it, and a typed end before the start moves the start. The pill counts days inclusively: `Math.round((end - start) / 86_400_000) + 1` using local-time `Date`s.\n6. Day cells come from the shared `DayCell`: a band layer and an edge-pill layer that only change opacity (100ms), with the band reaching 6px into the 12px cell gaps so a range across both months reads as one strip broken only at week edges.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `ms per day + round = 86_400_000, Math.round(...) + 1`\n  Local-time dates across a DST change are 23 or 25 hours apart; rounding absorbs that. The +1 makes the count inclusive, so a one-day range says \"1 day selected\", not 0.\n- `quick-select width = w-[118px], gap-1.5`\n  Fits the longest preset label (\"Last month\") at body-medium with 8px padding, so the months beside it never shift as the active preset changes.\n- `popover offset / placement = 4px, bottom end`\n  The popover is wider than most triggers (sidebar plus two 326px months), so aligning its end edge to the trigger keeps it opening inward from a right-hand toolbar slot.\n- `summary entrance = y -12 → 0, 0.25s, ease [0.34, 1.2, 0.64, 1]`\n  A small overshoot so the chips settle in under the months. The same values as the single Date Picker, so the two read as one family.\n- `All time preset = today minus 10 years`\n  A bounded stand-in for \"everything\". An unbounded start would leave the calendar no month to show.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ninterface DateRangeValue { start: CalendarDate; end: CalendarDate }\nfunction DateRangePicker(props: { value?: DateRangeValue | null; defaultValue?: DateRangeValue | null; onChange?: (value: DateRangeValue | null) => void; isDisabled?: boolean; className?: string; popoverClassName?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; placeholder?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: DateRangeValue | null`\n  Controlled committed range of `CalendarDate`s.\n- `defaultValue: DateRangeValue | null` — default `null`\n  Initial range when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (value: DateRangeValue | null) => void`\n  Fires once on Apply.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Disables the trigger.\n- `placeholder: string` — default `\"Select date range\"`\n  Trigger text when no range is committed.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Date range\"`\n  Labels the dialog and the range calendar.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the trigger button.\n- `popoverClassName: string`\n  Extra classes for the portalled popover surface.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DateRangePicker({\n  value,\n  defaultValue = null,\n  onChange,\n  isDisabled,\n  className,\n  popoverClassName: calendarClassName,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Date range\",\n  placeholder = \"Select date range\",\n}: DateRangePickerProps) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const { locale } = useLocale();\n  const isControlled = value !== undefined;\n  const [internalValue, setInternalValue] = useState<DateRangeValue | null>(defaultValue);\n  const committedValue = isControlled ? (value ?? null) : internalValue;\n\n  const [pendingValue, setPendingValue] = useState<DateRangeValue | null>(committedValue);\n\n  const commit = (next: DateRangeValue | null) => {\n    if (!isControlled) setInternalValue(next);\n    onChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\n  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setIsOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\n  // Pressing the trigger while open closes the popover instead of reopening\n  const allowOpenChange = useTriggerToggle(isOpen, triggerRef);\n\n  return localize((\n    <DialogTrigger\n      isOpen={isOpen}\n      onOpenChange={(open) => {\n        if (!allowOpenChange(open)) return;\n        if (open) setPendingValue(committedValue);\n        setIsOpen(open);\n      }}\n    >\n      <AriaButton ref={triggerRef} isDisabled={isDisabled} className={cx(triggerButtonClassName, className)}>\n        <RiCalendarLine className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-primary\" aria-hidden />\n        <span className=\"flex items-center justify-center whitespace-nowrap px-1 text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n          {committedValue ? `${formatTriggerDate(committedValue.start, locale)} - ${formatTriggerDate(committedValue.end, locale)}` : placeholder}\n        </span>\n      </AriaButton>\n      <Popover ref={popoverRef} offset={4} placement=\"bottom end\" isNonModal className={cx(popoverClassName, calendarClassName)}>\n        <Dialog aria-label={ariaLabel} className=\"outline-none\">\n          {({ close }) => (\n            <RangeCalendar\n              aria-label={ariaLabel}\n              visibleDuration={{ months: 2 }}\n              value={pendingValue}\n              onChange={setPendingValue}\n            >\n              <div className=\"flex gap-3\">\n                <div className=\"pt-4 ps-4\">\n                  <QuickSelect\n                    value={pendingValue}\n                    onSelect={(range) => setPendingValue(range)}\n                  />\n                </div>\n                <div className=\"flex flex-col pt-2 pe-2 pb-3\">\n                  <div className=\"flex gap-2\">\n                    <MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev />\n                    <MonthPanel offset={1} showNext />\n                  </div>\n                  <Footer\n                    value={pendingValue}\n                    onChange={setPendingValue}\n                    onCancel={() => {\n                      setPendingValue(committedValue);\n                      close();\n                    }}\n                    onApply={() => {\n                      commit(pendingValue);\n                      close();\n                    }}\n                  />\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </RangeCalendar>\n          )}\n        </Dialog>\n      </Popover>\n    </DialogTrigger>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The outside-press and trigger-toggle hooks add document `pointerdown` listeners only while open and remove them on close and unmount. `AnimatePresence` handles the footer's exit. No timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing branches on `prefers-reduced-motion`. The popover's 150ms fade, scale and 2px blur and the footer's 12px drop still play; day selection only fades opacity. Wrap the app in Motion's `<MotionConfig reducedMotion=\"user\">` to drop the footer's transform.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not remove `useTriggerToggle`. With `isNonModal`, pressing the trigger while open makes React Aria fire `onOpenChange(false)` and then `onOpenChange(true)` in the same press, so the popover would never close from its trigger.\n- Do not wire the two inner chevrons. The months are always adjacent, so there is one paging state; four buttons would give two that do the same thing as the outer pair.\n- Do not count days as `end.day - start.day` or with a floor. That breaks across months and across DST changes; subtract real dates, round, and add one.\n- Do not hoist the presets to module scope. They are built from `today()` when the popover renders, so a module-level list would freeze \"today\" at import time and differ between server and client.\n- Do not commit on every calendar click. The draft only becomes the value on Apply, and Cancel must restore the trigger's range.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `popoverClassName` adds classes to the portalled surface (for a locale-specific font, for example), since the popover renders outside the trigger's tree.\n- Strings run through `useTemplateCopy`, so preset labels, \"days selected\", Cancel and Apply can be translated by a `TemplateCopyProvider`.\n- The popover is wide (sidebar plus two months, about 810px). On a phone-width viewport the popover is capped at `calc(100vw - 2rem)` and scrolls sideways inside, so Apply stays reachable; for narrow layouts, `DateRangeCalendar` with `visibleMonths={1}` fits without scrolling.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe trigger reads \"Mar 2, 2026 - Mar 15, 2026\" for that committed range. Clicking it opens a popover with eight presets on the left and two consecutive months; clicking a start and then an end day draws a single band (crossing into the second month if needed) and the footer shows both dates as DD/MM/YYYY chips and \"N days selected\" counted inclusively. Clicking \"This month\" selects today's month and highlights the preset. Pressing the trigger again closes the popover. Apply updates the trigger and fires `onChange` once; Cancel or an outside click leaves it unchanged.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "date-range-stepper": "# Build: Date Range Stepper\n\nThe small period switcher from BoardUI's dashboard card headers: a 32px white pill with a left chevron, a centred \"Jun 29 – Jul 5\" (or \"July\") label and a right chevron. It steps whole weeks or months of `CalendarDate`s from @internationalized/date, controlled or uncontrolled, and greys out an arrow once the next step would leave `minValue` / `maxValue`. The pure helpers that compute and format a period are exported for the card that shows the data.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA bordered pill with prev/next chevrons that steps a week or month period, with min/max bounds and a label that slides in.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The current period is derived on every render from an anchor date (`value`, or internal state seeded from `defaultValue`): `getStepperRange` returns `startOfWeek`/`endOfWeek` (with `weekStartsOn`, Monday by default) or `startOfMonth`/`endOfMonth`. `shiftStepperRange` moves the period start by `{ weeks: ±1 }` or `{ months: ±1 }` and re-derives the range, giving the previous and next periods.\n2. Bounds: Previous is allowed while `prev.end >= minValue`, Next while `next.start <= maxValue` (`CalendarDate.compare`). A blocked arrow keeps focus and gets `aria-disabled` (styled with `aria-disabled:` variants) instead of `disabled`, so stepping to the edge never drops keyboard focus to the body; the click handler ignores it.\n3. A step updates internal state only when uncontrolled and always calls `onChange(start, range)`. The group handles ArrowLeft/PageUp and ArrowRight/PageDown as steps and Home as a jump back to the first period shown.\n4. The label is formatted with `DateFormatter` (en-US by default) on `date.toDate(\"UTC\")` with `timeZone: \"UTC\"`: week as two \"MMM d\" parts joined with a plain \" – \", month as the long month name. With `yearDisplay=\"auto\"` the year is added only when the period leaves the year of the first period shown. A visually hidden `aria-live=\"polite\"` span announces the full range with year; the visible label is aria-hidden.\n5. The slide direction is derived during render by comparing the new period start (ISO string) with the last one shown, so it also follows a controlled value. The label span is keyed by unit and start, so it remounts; Tailwind `starting:translate-x-2 starting:opacity-0` (or `-translate-x-2` going back) with a 200ms opacity/translate transition slides the new label in from the side it came from. The first render has direction 0 and no starting style, so nothing animates on load.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pill height = 32px (h-8) with 28px (size-7) arrow buttons`\n  Matches the header row of the medical and dashboard cards; the arrows fit inside the 1px border.\n- `label min width = 6.75rem week, 5.5rem month`\n  Keeps the pill from jumping between \"Jun 29 – Jul 5\" and \"Jul 6 – Jul 12\", and gives \"July\" the roomy centred look of the reference.\n- `slide = 8px (translate-x-2), 200ms ease-out`\n  Enough to show the direction of the step without the label leaving its overflow-hidden slot.\n- `weekStartsOn = \"mon\"`\n  The reference periods run Monday 29 Jun to Sunday 5 Jul.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function DateRangeStepper({ value, defaultValue, onChange, unit = \"week\", minValue, maxValue, weekStartsOn = \"mon\", locale = \"en-US\", yearDisplay = \"auto\", formatLabel, label = \"Date range\", isDisabled = false, className }: DateRangeStepperProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: CalendarDate`\n  Controlled: any day inside the shown period.\n- `defaultValue: CalendarDate`\n  Uncontrolled: any day inside the first period (one of value/defaultValue is required).\n- `onChange: (start: CalendarDate, range: DateRangeStepperRange) => void`\n  Called with the first day of the new period and the whole period.\n- `unit: \"week\" | \"month\"` — default `\"week\"`\n  Length of one step.\n- `minValue: CalendarDate`\n  Earliest day a period may reach.\n- `maxValue: CalendarDate`\n  Latest day a period may reach.\n- `weekStartsOn: DayOfWeek` — default `\"mon\"`\n  First day of a week period.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale of the label and announcement.\n- `yearDisplay: \"auto\" | \"always\" | \"never\"` — default `\"auto\"`\n  Auto adds the year once the period leaves the first period's year.\n- `formatLabel: (range: DateRangeStepperRange, unit: DateRangeStepperUnit) => string`\n  Replaces the visible label.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Date range\"`\n  Accessible name of the group.\n- `isDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Blocks both arrows and dims the pill.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the pill.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DateRangeStepper(props: DateRangeStepperProps) {\n  const {\n    value,\n    defaultValue,\n    onChange,\n    unit = \"week\",\n    minValue,\n    maxValue,\n    weekStartsOn = \"mon\",\n    locale = \"en-US\",\n    yearDisplay = \"auto\",\n    formatLabel,\n    label = \"Date range\",\n    isDisabled = false,\n    className,\n  } = props;\n\n  // The union type guarantees one of the two is set.\n  const seed = (value ?? defaultValue) as CalendarDate;\n  const [internal, setInternal] = useState<CalendarDate>(seed);\n  // The first period shown: Home returns to it and \"auto\" years compare to it.\n  const [home] = useState<CalendarDate>(seed);\n\n  const anchor = value ?? internal;\n  const range = getStepperRange(anchor, unit, locale, weekStartsOn);\n  const prev = shiftStepperRange(anchor, unit, -1, locale, weekStartsOn);\n  const next = shiftStepperRange(anchor, unit, 1, locale, weekStartsOn);\n  const canPrev = !isDisabled && (!minValue || prev.end.compare(minValue) >= 0);\n  const canNext = !isDisabled && (!maxValue || next.start.compare(maxValue) <= 0);\n\n  // Slide direction is derived from the change of period start, so it also\n  // follows a controlled value set from outside. ISO date strings sort in\n  // date order, so a string compare is enough.\n  const startKey = range.start.toString();\n  const [shownKey, setShownKey] = useState(startKey);\n  const [direction, setDirection] = useState<-1 | 0 | 1>(0);\n  if (shownKey !== startKey) {\n    setShownKey(startKey);\n    setDirection(startKey > shownKey ? 1 : -1);\n  }\n\n  const go = (target: DateRangeStepperRange) => {\n    if (!isControlled(value)) setInternal(target.start);\n    onChange?.(target.start, target);\n  };\n  const step = (offset: -1 | 1) => {\n    if (offset < 0 ? !canPrev : !canNext) return;\n    go(offset < 0 ? prev : next);\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (event.key === \"ArrowLeft\" || event.key === \"PageUp\") step(-1);\n    else if (event.key === \"ArrowRight\" || event.key === \"PageDown\") step(1);\n    else if (event.key === \"Home\" && !isDisabled) {\n      const target = getStepperRange(home, unit, locale, weekStartsOn);\n      if (target.start.compare(range.start) !== 0) go(target);\n    } else return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n  };\n\n  const showYear =\n    yearDisplay === \"always\" ||\n    (yearDisplay === \"auto\" && (range.start.year !== home.year || range.end.year !== home.year));\n  const text = formatLabel ? formatLabel(range, unit) : formatStepperRange(range, unit, locale, showYear);\n  const noun = unit === \"month\" ? \"month\" : \"week\";\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      role=\"group\"\n      aria-label={label}\n      aria-disabled={isDisabled || undefined}\n      onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n      data-unit={unit}\n      className={cx(\n        \"inline-flex h-8 shrink-0 items-center rounded-lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default px-0.5 shadow-2xs\",\n        isDisabled && \"opacity-60\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        className={arrowClass}\n        aria-label={`Previous ${noun}`}\n        aria-disabled={!canPrev || undefined}\n        onClick={() => step(-1)}\n      >\n        <RiArrowLeftSLine className=\"size-4\" aria-hidden />\n      </button>\n      <span\n        aria-hidden\n        className={cx(\n          \"flex justify-center overflow-hidden px-1\",\n          unit === \"month\" ? \"min-w-[5.5rem]\" : \"min-w-[6.75rem]\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <span\n          key={`${unit}:${startKey}`}\n          data-stepper-label=\"\"\n          className={cx(\n            \"inline-block text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums\",\n            \"transition-[opacity,translate] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            direction === 1 && \"starting:translate-x-2 starting:opacity-0\",\n            direction === -1 && \"starting:-translate-x-2 starting:opacity-0\",\n          )}\n        >\n          {text}\n        </span>\n      </span>\n      <span className=\"sr-only\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        {announceRange(range, unit, locale)}\n      </span>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        className={arrowClass}\n        aria-label={`Next ${noun}`}\n        aria-disabled={!canNext || undefined}\n        onClick={() => step(1)}\n      >\n        <RiArrowRightSLine className=\"size-4\" aria-hidden />\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers: the slide is a CSS @starting-style transition on a remounted span.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The label slide uses motion-reduce:transition-none, so the new label appears in place without sliding or fading.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not use `Intl.DateTimeFormat.formatRange` for the label: its spacing around the dash differs between ICU builds (Node vs browser) and causes a hydration mismatch. Format start and end separately and join them.\n- Do not format a `CalendarDate` in the viewer's timezone: convert with `toDate(\"UTC\")` and format with `timeZone: \"UTC\"`, or the label can show the previous day west of UTC.\n- Do not set `disabled` on an arrow that reached a bound while it has focus: the focus falls to the body. Use `aria-disabled` and ignore the click.\n- Do not default the value to today during render: server and browser can disagree on the date. The props type requires `value` or `defaultValue`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Exports `getStepperRange`, `shiftStepperRange` and `formatStepperRange` so a card can compute the same period for its data.\n- Chevrons are Remix `RiArrowLeftSLine` / `RiArrowRightSLine` at 16px.\n- The demo pairs a week stepper bounded to Jun 1 – Jul 12, 2026 over a seeded steps bar chart with a month stepper over an activity calendar.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nStarting at Jun 29 2026 the week pill reads \"Jun 29 – Jul 5\"; Next shows \"Jul 6 – Jul 12\" and calls onChange with 2026-07-06; with maxValue Jul 12 the Next arrow then has aria-disabled=\"true\" and clicking it changes nothing. A month pill at July reads \"July\", and stepping back past January reads \"December 2025\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "delayed-unmount-loader": "# Build: Delayed-Unmount Loader\n\nAn inverted full-cover loading panel that fades out on a plain CSS transition. The mechanism is the timing of its own removal: when `loading` goes false the element sets `data-exit` and starts a 600ms fade, and a matching 600ms timeout is what finally unmounts it. Nothing is animated in JavaScript — one boolean, one timeout, one transition.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA loading overlay that stays mounted 600ms past the signal so its fade can actually play before it leaves.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Internal `mounted` state starts true so the overlay is present in the very first paint, before any effect has run. Deriving visibility from the `loading` prop alone is what makes the exit impossible to animate.\n2. When `loading` flips false, `data-exit=\"true\"` lands on the element and the CSS rule `.k-dul[data-exit=\"true\"] { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none }` takes over the transition declared on `.k-dul`.\n3. In the same effect a `setTimeout(() => setMounted(false), exitMs)` is armed. The timeout and the transition read the same `exitMs`, passed to CSS as the custom property `--k-dul-exit`, so they cannot drift apart.\n4. If `loading` goes true again — a second route, a refetch — the effect clears the pending timeout and re-arms the overlay. Because the exit is a CSS transition rather than a keyframe, an interruption mid-fade reverses from wherever the opacity had got to instead of jumping to 1.\n5. The panel is drawn with the foreground colour as its background and the background colour as its text, so the reveal reads as a tonal inversion rather than a curtain, and a single pulsing dot between two brackets is the only moving part.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `exitMs = 600`\n  Drives both the CSS transition and the unmount timeout. Under ~350ms a full-bleed tonal flip reads as a flicker; over ~800ms the page is legible behind a veil that is still technically covering it. Whatever value you pick, the timeout must be >= the transition or the node disappears mid-fade.\n- `pulse period = 1s`\n  One cycle per second is close to a resting heart rate and reads as \"alive, waiting\". Faster reads as urgency and makes a slow load feel worse than it is.\n- `pulse scale range = 0.86 -> 1`\n  Small enough that the dot never appears to change its typographic size between the brackets; a larger range makes the bracket pair look mis-set.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DelayedUnmountLoader(props: { loading: boolean; exitMs?: number; label?: string; hint?: string; position?: 'fixed' | 'absolute'; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `loading: boolean`\n  While true the overlay is opaque. False starts the reveal.\n- `exitMs: number` — default `600`\n  Fade length and unmount delay. One number drives both.\n- `label: string` — default `'Loading experience'`\n  Left-hand text.\n- `hint: string` — default `'it won’t be long'`\n  Right-hand text.\n- `position: 'fixed' | 'absolute'` — default `'fixed'`\n  Viewport cover, or cover of the nearest positioned ancestor.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the overlay.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DelayedUnmountLoader({\n  loading,\n  exitMs = 600,\n  label = 'Loading experience',\n  hint = 'it won’t be long',\n  position = 'fixed',\n  className,\n}: DelayedUnmountLoaderProps) {\n  // Starts true so the overlay is in the very first paint. Deriving it from\n  // `loading` alone is what makes the exit un-animatable.\n  const [mounted, setMounted] = useState(true);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (loading) {\n      // A second load (route change, refetch) re-arms the overlay.\n      setMounted(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    const t = setTimeout(() => setMounted(false), exitMs);\n    return () => clearTimeout(t);\n  }, [loading, exitMs]);\n\n  if (!mounted && !loading) return null;\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      // `data-exit` is the only thing that changes on the element. The\n      // transition is declarative, so an interrupted reveal (loading goes\n      // true again mid-fade) reverses from wherever it got to instead of\n      // jumping.\n      data-exit={!loading}\n      aria-hidden={!loading}\n      className={cn(\n        'k-dul z-50 flex items-center justify-center',\n        // Inverted: the loader is the page's negative, so the reveal reads as\n        // a tonal flip rather than a curtain being pulled aside.\n        'bg-fg text-bg',\n        position === 'fixed' ? 'fixed inset-0' : 'absolute inset-0',\n        className,\n      )}\n      style={{ ['--k-dul-exit' as string]: `${exitMs}ms` }}\n    >\n      <style>{CSS}</style>\n      <div className=\"flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-2 px-4\">\n        <p className=\"w-1/3 font-mono text-[0.6875rem] uppercase leading-none tracking-[0.08em]\">\n          {label}\n        </p>\n        <p className=\"flex w-1/3 justify-center font-mono text-2xl leading-none tracking-[0.1em]\">\n          <span aria-hidden>[</span>\n          <span className=\"k-dul-dot inline-block px-[0.15em]\" aria-hidden>\n            &middot;\n          </span>\n          <span aria-hidden>]</span>\n        </p>\n        <p className=\"w-1/3 text-right font-mono text-[0.6875rem] uppercase leading-none tracking-[0.08em]\">\n          {hint}\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The single timeout is cleared in the effect's cleanup, so a route change or an unmount during the fade cannot call `setMounted` on a component React has already released.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The dot stops pulsing and sits at a fixed opacity, and the fade collapses to 1ms so the overlay simply disappears. The unmount delay is unchanged, and the exiting overlay is `pointer-events: none` throughout, so nothing is blocked while it waits.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Rendering `{loading && <Loader />}`. React removes the node on the same commit that flips the flag, so the transition has no element to run on and the loader disappears on a cut. The component owns its own removal.\n- Using a keyframe animation with `forwards` for the exit. Interrupt it by starting a second load and it snaps to the start; a transition on a class/attribute is interruptible in both directions for free.\n- Letting the timeout and the transition duration live in different files. They drift, and either the loader is cut off mid-fade or a fully transparent overlay sits on top of the page eating clicks. One `exitMs` feeds both, and the CSS reads it from a custom property.\n- Forgetting `pointer-events: none` in the exit state. For the whole fade the page is visible but not clickable, which reads as the site having frozen just as it finished loading.\n- Not clearing the timeout when `loading` goes true again. The stale timer unmounts the re-armed overlay mid-load and the page flashes through underneath.\n- Leaving the pulse running under `prefers-reduced-motion`. An indefinitely looping animation is exactly what that preference exists to stop; the media query drops it to a static dot.\n\n## Notes\n\n- App Router wiring: render this in `app/layout.tsx` above `{children}` and drive `loading` from your own readiness state — `document.fonts.ready`, a data promise, or a route-change flag from `usePathname()`. For a first-load preloader that must never hang, use `safe-preloader` instead: this component trusts whoever owns the boolean.\n- `position=\"absolute\"` covers the nearest positioned ancestor instead of the viewport, which is what makes it embeddable in a card or a preview frame.\n- The delayed-unmount pattern generalises: any exit animation on a conditionally rendered node needs the node to outlive the condition by at least the animation duration.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nLoad the page and the inverted panel is in the first paint with its dot pulsing. When loading ends the panel fades over 600ms and the node is gone from the DOM immediately afterwards — not before. Trigger a second load mid-fade and the panel returns from its current opacity rather than snapping to full.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "depth-pulse-travelling-wave": "# Build: Depth Fade And Travelling Pulse\n\nA lattice of dots bent around a shallow arc and lit by a pulse that travels across it. Depth is faked entirely from model-space z, which drives alpha and point size at the same time, so the arc reads as curved with no depth buffer and no perspective camera. The pulse is a cosine whose phase varies per column, and its crest also pushes dots toward the viewer, so the two techniques compound: the wave arrives as a moving band of brighter, larger dots.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dot lattice on an arc: z drives alpha and point size together, and a per-column phase sweeps the pulse across the field.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Columns are placed by angle across `arcSpan`, then z is renormalised so the centre column lands at exactly `+arcDepth` and both edges at `-arcDepth`, independent of the span. Without that renormalisation the usable z range shrinks with the span and the depth cue silently weakens whenever the arc is flattened.\n2. The depth cue is `depthT = clamp((z + 0.55) / 1.1, 0, 1)`, which drives alpha from 0.25 to 1.0 *and* point size from 0.65 to 1.0. Both together are the point: alpha alone reads as a grey copy of the near dots, size alone reads as a resolution change, and only the pair reads as distance.\n3. The pulse is `raw = 0.65 + 0.35 * cos(2π * (uElapsed / pulseCycle - phase))`, with `phase = columnT * waveTurns + rowT * 0.12` baked into an attribute. Subtracting the phase inside the cosine is what makes the crest move *along* the lattice rather than the whole field breathing in unison.\n4. The crest, renormalised to 0..1, is added to z as `p.z += crest * uWaveDepth`, so the wave feeds the depth cue. A dot at the crest is pushed nearer, and the depth fade then brightens and enlarges it — one displacement drives three visible changes.\n5. The pulse ramps in over the first second (`clamp(uElapsed / 1.0, 0, 1)`), so the first frame does not land on an arbitrary point of the cosine and flash. Yaw is `sin(elapsed * yawSpeed * 2π) * yawAmplitude` degrees, applied in the shader after the wave so the lattice rocks through its own depth range.\n6. `gl_PointSize` is clamped to a 2px floor and alpha is compensated by `unclamped² / clamped²`, so the field keeps the same apparent density at DPR 1 and DPR 2 instead of getting heavier on retina.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PULSE_BASE / PULSE_SWING = 0.65 / 0.35`\n  The pulse rides between 0.30 and 1.00 of nominal size and alpha. A trough at 0 would make dots vanish entirely and the lattice would look like it is dropping frames rather than breathing.\n- `DEFAULT_CYCLE / pulseCycle = 3.0 seconds`\n  Under ~2s the crest outruns the eye following it across the lattice; over ~5s it stops reading as a wave and becomes an idle shimmer.\n- `INTRO_SECONDS = 1.0`\n  Ramps the pulse amplitude from 0. Without it the first rendered frame samples the cosine at an arbitrary phase and the whole field flashes on mount.\n- `DEPTH_HALF_RANGE = 0.55`\n  The arc spans ±0.34 and the wave adds up to +0.18, so normalising over ±0.55 leaves headroom at both ends — no dot sits at exactly 0 or 1 and the falloff never visibly clips.\n- `DEPTH_MIN_SIZE / DEPTH_MAX_SIZE = 0.65 / 1.0`\n  Far dots at 65% of near ones. Paired with the 0.25→1.0 alpha ramp; either cue on its own is ambiguous, and this ratio is the smallest that still reads as depth at preview-card sizes.\n- `waveTurns = 1.4`\n  Wavelengths visible across the lattice at any instant. At 1 the crest and trough sit at opposite edges and it reads as a tilt; past ~2.5 the bands are too narrow to track and the field just shimmers.\n- `ROW_PHASE_LEAN = 0.12`\n  Small row contribution to the phase, so the crest leans instead of arriving as a perfectly vertical bar. Bigger than ~0.3 and the wave starts reading as diagonal stripes.\n- `yawAmplitude / yawSpeed = 16 degrees / 0.25 Hz`\n  The lattice rocks rather than spins. A full rotation would carry columns through the arc's entire depth range and destroy the near/far reading the crest depends on.\n- `crest tint threshold = smoothstep(0.65, 1.0, crest)`\n  Only the top third of the crest picks up `crestColor`. Tinting the whole cosine spreads the accent across the field and the wave stops having a location.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DepthPulseTravellingWave(props: { columns?: number; rows?: number; arcSpan?: number; arcDepth?: number; fieldHeight?: number; pulseCycle?: number; waveTurns?: number; waveDepth?: number; yawAmplitude?: number; yawSpeed?: number; dotScale?: number; viewScale?: number; opacity?: number; color?: string; crestColor?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `columns: number` — default `110`\n  Columns across the arc.\n- `rows: number` — default `44`\n  Rows down each column.\n- `arcSpan: number` — default `110`\n  Arc the lattice is bent around, in degrees.\n- `arcDepth: number` — default `0.34`\n  Half-depth of the arc in model units — how far the edges recede.\n- `fieldHeight: number` — default `1.1`\n  Lattice height in model units.\n- `pulseCycle: number` — default `3`\n  Seconds per pulse.\n- `waveTurns: number` — default `1.4`\n  Wavelengths visible across the lattice at any instant.\n- `waveDepth: number` — default `0.18`\n  How far a crest pushes a dot toward the viewer, in model units.\n- `yawAmplitude: number` — default `16`\n  Peak yaw of the whole lattice, in degrees.\n- `yawSpeed: number` — default `0.25`\n  Yaw oscillations per second.\n- `dotScale: number` — default `1`\n  Dot radius multiplier in CSS pixels.\n- `viewScale: number` — default `0.46`\n  Model-to-pixel scale as a fraction of the container short side.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied to every dot.\n- `color: string` — default `'#6f757e'`\n  Base dot colour.\n- `crestColor: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Colour mixed in at the top of the crest.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'depth-pulse-travelling-wave'`\n  Seeds the sub-cell jitter for a stable, hydration-safe lattice.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DepthPulseTravellingWave({\n  columns = 110,\n  rows = 44,\n  arcSpan = 110,\n  arcDepth = 0.34,\n  fieldHeight = 1.1,\n  pulseCycle = 3,\n  waveTurns = 1.4,\n  waveDepth = 0.18,\n  yawAmplitude = 16,\n  yawSpeed = 0.25,\n  dotScale = 1,\n  viewScale = 0.46,\n  opacity = 1,\n  color = '#6f757e',\n  crestColor = '#ffffff',\n  seed = 'depth-pulse-travelling-wave',\n  className,\n}: DepthPulseTravellingWaveProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0.1, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n\n    const total = columns * rows;\n    const positions = new Float32Array(total * 3);\n    const wave = new Float32Array(total * 2);\n\n    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n       The arc. Columns are placed by angle, then z is renormalised so the\n       centre column sits at +arcDepth and the two edges at -arcDepth\n       exactly. Without the renormalisation the usable z range depends on\n       arcSpan, and the depth fade would get weaker every time the arc was\n       flattened — the cue would silently stop working at shallow spans.\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\n    const halfSpan = (arcSpan * Math.PI) / 360;\n    const cosEdge = Math.cos(halfSpan);\n    // Guard the degenerate case: a 0 degree span has no depth to normalise.\n    const depthSpan = Math.max(1e-4, 1 - cosEdge);\n\n    let cursor = 0;\n    for (let c = 0; c < columns; c += 1) {\n      const colT = columns === 1 ? 0.5 : c / (columns - 1);\n      const angle = (colT - 0.5) * 2 * halfSpan;\n      const x = Math.sin(angle);\n      const z = ((Math.cos(angle) - cosEdge) / depthSpan) * 2 * arcDepth - arcDepth;\n\n      for (let r = 0; r < rows; r += 1) {\n        const rowT = rows === 1 ? 0.5 : r / (rows - 1);\n        // Sub-cell jitter, seeded. A perfect lattice moirés against the pixel\n        // grid and shows banding as the field yaws.\n        const jx = (rng() - 0.5) * (1 / columns) * 0.9;\n        const jy = (rng() - 0.5) * (1 / rows) * 0.9;\n\n        positions[cursor * 3] = x + jx;\n        positions[cursor * 3 + 1] = (rowT - 0.5 + jy) * fieldHeight;\n        positions[cursor * 3 + 2] = z;\n\n        // Phase is what makes it travel. Constant phase across the lattice\n        // would give a field that breathes in unison.\n        wave[cursor * 2] = colT * waveTurns + rowT * ROW_PHASE_LEAN;\n        wave[cursor * 2 + 1] = rng() - 0.5;\n        cursor += 1;\n      }\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aWave', new THREE.BufferAttribute(wave, 2));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uViewScale: { value: 1 },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uDotScale: { value: dotScale },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uPulseCycle: { value: pulseCycle },\n      uWaveDepth: { value: waveDepth },\n      uYaw: { value: 0 },\n      uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(color) },\n      uCrestColor: { value: new THREE.Color(crestColor) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: DEPTH_PULSE_WAVE_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: DEPTH_PULSE_WAVE_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      // Every dot is projected at z = 0; the depth cue is alpha and size, not\n      // the depth buffer. Leaving these on would let near dots reject far ones\n      // that were rasterised first and punch holes through the lattice.\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    // Positions are unit-scale around the origin and the real placement happens\n    // in the shader, so three's bounding sphere does not describe what is on\n    // screen and culling against it would blank the field at some sizes.\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.left = -w / 2;\n      camera.right = w / 2;\n      camera.top = h / 2;\n      camera.bottom = -h / 2;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n      uniforms.uViewScale.value = Math.min(w, h) * viewScale;\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      // Yaw oscillates rather than spins. A full rotation would carry columns\n      // through the arc's own depth range and the travelling crest would stop\n      // being readable as a wave.\n      uniforms.uYaw.value = Math.sin(elapsed * yawSpeed * Math.PI * 2) * yawAmplitude;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    columns,\n    rows,\n    arcSpan,\n    arcDepth,\n    fieldHeight,\n    pulseCycle,\n    waveTurns,\n    waveDepth,\n    yawAmplitude,\n    yawSpeed,\n    dotScale,\n    viewScale,\n    opacity,\n    color,\n    crestColor,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed), ready && !reduced && !unsupported);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref so a queued frame cannot touch a dead renderer, disposes the geometry through `disposeObject(scene)`, disposes the material, calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed backing store, and removes the canvas. `useRaf` unsubscribes on unmount, so a StrictMode double-invoke leaves no orphaned context.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No frame loop is opened. A single frame is drawn at `uElapsed = 1.9` — past the one-second amplitude ramp, and a third of the way through the default cycle, so the crest sits inside the lattice rather than off its edge. Both the depth falloff and the wave band are visible in that still; neither advances.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Giving every dot the same phase. The field then pulses in unison, which reads as a loading state rather than as something moving through the lattice — the per-column phase offset *is* the effect.\n- Fading alpha with depth but leaving point size constant. The far edge of the arc becomes a grey copy of the near edge instead of receding, because size is the cue the eye actually uses for distance at these scales.\n- Leaving `depthTest` on. Every dot is projected at z = 0, so whichever dot rasterises first wins the pixel and the lattice develops holes wherever it folds back on itself.\n- Starting the pulse at full amplitude. The first frame samples the cosine at whatever phase `elapsed` happens to be, and the field flashes on mount — hence the one-second amplitude ramp.\n- Placing columns by angle without renormalising z. The depth range then scales with `cos(arcSpan/2)`, so flattening the arc quietly kills the depth fade and the component looks broken at shallow spans while working at wide ones.\n- A perfectly regular lattice. It moirés against the pixel grid and shows banding as the field yaws; the seeded sub-cell jitter costs nothing and removes it.\n- Rebuilding the buffer on resize. Model space is size-independent — only `uViewScale` and `uPixelRatio` change, so a container reflow costs two uniform writes rather than a dropped GL context.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Dot count is `columns * rows`, 4840 at the defaults, in one draw call with one attribute beyond position.\n- The wave is purely a function of `uElapsed` and a baked per-dot phase, so there is no simulation state and no drift — pausing and resuming lands exactly where the clock says it should.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA curved lattice of dots is visibly dimmer and smaller toward its left and right edges than at its centre. A band of brighter, larger dots sweeps repeatedly across the field, roughly once every three seconds, rather than the whole field brightening at once. The lattice rocks gently side to side. Nothing flashes on mount. Resize the container and the field rescales without a flicker or a dropped context. With reduced motion it holds a single frame showing both the depth falloff and the crest.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "device-preview-frame": "# Build: Device Preview Frame\n\nA rounded app-window frame (three chrome dots, a lock-and-address pill, a refresh glyph) around a screen that shows real content laid out at a device width: 1280px for desktop, 768 for tablet, 375 for mobile, scaled down to fit the space. A segmented Desktop / Tablet / Mobile toggle above it narrows or widens the frame, and the content reflows continuously while it does, like dragging a window edge. Because the screen is a size container named `frame`, children respond with `@…/frame:` container queries.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBrowser-window frame that lays any content out at a device width and scales it in; a Desktop/Tablet/Mobile toggle resizes it live.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One virtual width `p` drives the whole fit. With `available` the measured row width, the scale is `s = min(1, (available − 16) / p)`; the frame is set to `p · s + 16` px wide (16 = frame border, padding and screen border), the content box to `p` px wide and `height / s` px tall, with `scale: s` from its top-left corner. Content therefore always fills the screen exactly, and a narrow device is shown at up to 1:1 instead of shrinking with the desktop.\n2. Device switch: a rAF loop tweens `p` from the old device width to the new one over 560ms with `easeOutQuart` and writes the four styles straight to the DOM each frame, with no React re-render. Frame width and content width derive from the same number, so they cannot drift apart mid-animation the way two independent CSS transitions would when the scale also changes.\n3. `available` comes from a ResizeObserver content box on the row that holds the frame: layout pixels, unaffected by transforms, so the fit is correct inside CSS-scaled previews. Resizing the page re-applies the fit at the current `p` (mid-tween included).\n4. Toggle: the kit `SegmentedControl` (react-aria ToggleButtonGroup with a sliding thumb). It works controlled (`device` plus `onDeviceChange`) or uncontrolled (`defaultDevice`). The root carries `data-device` for styling and tests.\n5. The screen scrolls vertically inside the frame (`overflow-y-auto`, `overscroll-contain`), so tall pages can be explored without scrolling the host page.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `device widths = 1280 / 768 / 375 px`\n  Common laptop, portrait tablet and phone CSS widths; each hits a different container-query step in typical layouts.\n- `tween = 560ms easeOutQuart`\n  Most of the reflow happens in the first third, so the result is readable quickly while the frame settles softly.\n- `chrome = 16px horizontal`\n  Border + 6px padding + screen border; subtracted before computing the scale so content never overflows the screen.\n- `max scale = 1`\n  Content is never enlarged; a phone width in a wide container is shown at true size.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DevicePreviewFrame(props: { children: ReactNode; device?: \"desktop\" | \"tablet\" | \"mobile\"; defaultDevice?: PreviewDevice; onDeviceChange?: (device: PreviewDevice) => void; widths?: Partial<Record<PreviewDevice, number>>; height?: number; url?: string; showToggle?: boolean; toolbar?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content laid out at the device width; may use `@…/frame:` container queries.\n- `device: \"desktop\" | \"tablet\" | \"mobile\"`\n  Controlled device.\n- `defaultDevice: PreviewDevice` — default `\"desktop\"`\n  Initial device when uncontrolled.\n- `onDeviceChange: (device) => void`\n  Called when the toggle changes.\n- `widths: Partial<Record<PreviewDevice, number>>`\n  Override device CSS widths.\n- `height: number` — default `420`\n  Visible screen height in px, constant across devices.\n- `url: string` — default `\"app.example.com\"`\n  Text in the address pill.\n- `showToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the device toggle.\n- `toolbar: ReactNode`\n  Extra controls beside the toggle.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * One number drives everything: `p`, the virtual width the content is laid out\n * at. For a given p the screen scale is s = min(1, (available - chrome) / p),\n * the frame is p * s + chrome wide and the content box is p wide, scaled by s,\n * and height / s tall so it always fills the screen. Switching device tweens p\n * from the old width to the new one in a rAF loop that writes those styles\n * straight to the DOM, so the page inside reflows continuously like a window\n * being resized and frame and content can never drift apart mid-animation.\n * `available` comes from a ResizeObserver content box: layout pixels, which\n * stay correct inside CSS-scaled previews where rects would not.\n */\nfunction useDeviceFit(targetWidth: number, height: number, reduced: boolean) {\n  const measureRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const frameRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const contentRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const fit = useRef({ p: targetWidth, available: 0, raf: 0, height });\n  fit.current.height = height;\n\n  const apply = () => {\n    const { p, available, height: h } = fit.current;\n    const frame = frameRef.current;\n    const content = contentRef.current;\n    if (!frame || !content || available <= CHROME_X) return;\n    const s = Math.min(1, (available - CHROME_X) / p);\n    frame.style.width = `${p * s + CHROME_X}px`;\n    content.style.width = `${p}px`;\n    content.style.height = `${h / s}px`;\n    content.style.scale = String(s);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = measureRef.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    const ro = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {\n      const box = entry?.contentBoxSize?.[0];\n      fit.current.available = box ? box.inlineSize : el.clientWidth;\n      apply();\n    });\n    ro.observe(el);\n    return () => ro.disconnect();\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const state = fit.current;\n    cancelAnimationFrame(state.raf);\n    const from = state.p;\n    if (reduced || from === targetWidth || state.available === 0) {\n      state.p = targetWidth;\n      apply();\n      return;\n    }\n    const start = performance.now();\n    const step = (now: number) => {\n      const t = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (now - start) / DURATION));\n      state.p = from + (targetWidth - from) * easeOutQuart(t);\n      apply();\n      if (t < 1) state.raf = requestAnimationFrame(step);\n    };\n    state.raf = requestAnimationFrame(step);\n    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(state.raf);\n  }, [targetWidth, reduced, height]);\n\n  return { measureRef, frameRef, contentRef };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The ResizeObserver disconnects on unmount, and any running animation frame is cancelled when the device changes again or the frame unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The device switch applies the new width at once (no tween); the kit SegmentedControl drops its thumb slide under the same preference.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating the frame width and the content scale as two CSS transitions. When both the virtual width and the scale change, their product is not linear in time and the content overflows or leaves a gap mid-animation. Derive both from one tweened width.\n- Fitting with `getBoundingClientRect`: inside a scaled parent it returns scaled sizes and the computed scale compounds. Measure layout boxes.\n- Using `md:`/`lg:` media queries inside the previewed content. They follow the real window, not the frame; use `@…/frame:` container queries.\n- Using `transform: scale()` on the content while it also has its own transforms; the standalone `scale` property composes cleanly.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The demo previews a small dashboard: sidebar and two-column body at desktop, sidebar gone at tablet, single column at mobile.\n- Pass `showToggle={false}` with a controlled `device` to drive it from elsewhere, e.g. the tabs of a template showcase.\n- `DEVICE_WIDTHS` is exported, and `widths` overrides any of them.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px the frame fills the row and shows the dashboard with a sidebar and a two-column body. Choosing Tablet narrows the frame over about half a second while the content reflows: the sidebar disappears and the body stacks. Mobile narrows it further to a single column at true 375px width. Desktop restores it. At 360px the frame still fits, just smaller. Light and dark both read.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dither-trail-image-reveal": "# Build: Dither Trail Image Reveal\n\nThe image rests dimmed and near-monochrome; dragging across it restores it through a trail of 5px blocks, each block either lit or not according to an 8x8 Bayer threshold. Twenty-four pointer samples live about a second and ride in two flat uniform arrays, so the whole trail is one fullscreen quad with no geometry and no render targets. Samples are spawned on distance travelled by a damped follower rather than per event, and each carries the velocity it was born with, so the reveal smears the way the hand moved.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dimmed image restored through a trail of Bayer-thresholded blocks that smear in the direction the pointer moved.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The trail is state, not geometry. Twenty-four samples sit in a ring buffer as `vec4(u, v, life, speed)` plus a parallel `vec2` of velocity, uploaded as two flat uniform arrays every frame. The fragment shader loops all twenty-four and takes `max()` of their contributions, so a full trail costs one quad and twenty-four `length()` calls per fragment — no instanced sprites, no ping-pong feedback buffer, nothing to resize.\n2. A sample is released when the FOLLOWER has travelled `spawnDistance`, or after `spawnInterval` with no movement. Spawning per pointer event instead clumps on a slow drag — events fire on sub-pixel movement — and leaves gaps on a flick, because the ring is short and a fast sweep can consume all of it between two frames. The time floor is what keeps a stationary hover from decaying to nothing under the cursor.\n3. The Bayer threshold is sampled at the CENTRE of the 5px block the fragment sits in, not at the fragment itself. Per-fragment sampling lets the disc edge cut through a block, which puts a soft antialiased boundary on an effect whose entire argument is that it has none. Per-block, a block is wholly lit or wholly absent and the reveal has a dither vocabulary.\n4. The 8x8 matrix is built recursively — `bayer4(a) = bayer2(a * 0.5) * 0.25 + bayer2(a)`, and again for 8 — because GLSL ES 1.00 has no integer bitwise operators and the usual derivation is bit-twiddled. Three multiplies and a `fract` produce the same 64-value ordering with no lookup texture and no GLSL3 upgrade.\n5. Each sample contributes its stored velocity to a flow vector, weighted by how much it contributed to the mask, and the sum is clamped. The image is then sampled at `uv - flow`, with the flow SNAPPED to the same 5px grid as the dither: a smooth sub-pixel offset is a directional blur laid on top of the picture, while a snapped one moves whole blocks and belongs to the same rendering as the dots.\n6. Colour is quantised twice at two different frequencies — the reveal mask against a per-block threshold, the tone against a per-pixel one — so the shape of the trail and the banding of the image do not moire against each other.\n7. `object-fit: cover` is reproduced in uv space from the intrinsic aspect of the decoded texture, so the shader crops exactly the way the equivalent CSS would and a resize is two uniform writes rather than a re-layout.\n8. The pointer state updates every frame while the DRAW is throttled to 30fps. The trail geometry is smooth because the samples are, and 30fps is invisible through 5px blocks; halving the draw rate halves the fill, which is the only real cost here.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TRAIL_POINTS = 24`\n  Ring length, and a compile-time `#define` because GLSL ES 1.00 needs a constant loop bound. At the default spacing this is roughly a second of trail. Past ~48 the shader is doing more work than the image is worth, and the far end of the trail is below the first dither level anyway.\n- `MAX_BUFFER_WIDTH = 1100`\n  Absolute cap on drawing-buffer width in device pixels, applied on top of the dpr cap. The output is 5px blocks quantised to four levels, so a wider buffer resolves nothing: a 2560px hero at dpr 2 would shade 26 million fragments per draw for a picture with no detail above the block size.\n- `fps = 30`\n  Draw ceiling. This is an overlay, not the subject, and the block size hides the difference between 30 and 120. The accumulator subtracts the budget rather than zeroing, so the long-run rate is exact instead of quietly losing the remainder each frame.\n- `smoothing = 0.0002`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second — an ~80ms half-life. Tighter than a glow needs, because the trail has a visible head: lag past ~150ms and the reveal is drawing where the pointer was, which reads as dropped frames.\n- `spawnDistance / spawnInterval = 0.012 / 0.042`\n  Roughly 1.2% of the container per sample, or one every 42ms if the pointer is still. Below ~0.006 a slow drag exhausts the ring in a few centimetres; above ~0.03 a fast sweep is visibly a string of beads.\n- `trail radius = 0.055 -> 0.115`\n  A sample GROWS as it ages, so the trail widens and softens behind the head instead of fading as a constant-width stripe. Equal radii read as a paint stroke; the taper is what makes it a wake.\n- `FLOW_MAX / FLOW_CLAMP = 0.04 / 0.055`\n  Per-sample and total displacement ceilings in uv units. Uncapped, a flick tears the image into unrecognisable stripes; the total clamp also stops overlapping samples from summing into a displacement several times either individual one.\n- `levels = 4`\n  Quantisation steps per channel inside the reveal. Two is a duotone stencil that loses the photograph; past ~8 the dither is too fine to read as one and the effect looks like a slightly noisy image.\n- `Bayer epsilon = 0.001`\n  The matrix contains a zero cell, so `step(threshold, soft)` lights that cell in every block on the canvas — a permanent sparse grid of stray pixels everywhere the trail has never been. Biasing the threshold is what keeps the untouched area untouched.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DitherTrailImageReveal(props: { src: string; alt?: string; levels?: number; blockSize?: number; trailLife?: number; spawnDistance?: number; spawnInterval?: number; flowGain?: number; smoothing?: number; restBrightness?: number; restSaturation?: number; fps?: number; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `src: string`\n  Required. Image URL — http(s), blob: or data:. Never a bundled asset.\n- `alt: string` — default `''`\n  Accessible name for the panel. Empty marks it decorative.\n- `levels: number` — default `4`\n  Quantisation steps per channel inside the reveal.\n- `blockSize: number` — default `5`\n  Dither block size in CSS pixels.\n- `trailLife: number` — default `1.05`\n  Life of one trail sample in seconds.\n- `spawnDistance: number` — default `0.012`\n  Follower travel in uv units that releases the next sample.\n- `spawnInterval: number` — default `0.042`\n  Seconds after which a sample is released even with no movement.\n- `flowGain: number` — default `0.03`\n  uv offset stored per uv/second of pointer velocity.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0002`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second.\n- `restBrightness: number` — default `0.5`\n  Brightness of the untouched image, 0 to 1.\n- `restSaturation: number` — default `0.16`\n  Saturation of the untouched image, 0 to 1.\n- `fps: number` — default `30`\n  Draw rate ceiling. Pointer state still updates every frame.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `1.5`\n  Device pixel ratio cap, before the 1100px absolute buffer cap.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Samples in the ring. Must match TRAIL_POINTS in the fragment shader. */\nconst TRAIL_POINTS = 24;\n/**\n * Hard cap on drawing-buffer width in device pixels. An overlay of 5px blocks\n * carries no detail a wider buffer could resolve, and fill is the whole cost:\n * a 2560px hero at dpr 2 would shade 26 million fragments per draw for a\n * picture that is quantised to four levels.\n */\nconst MAX_BUFFER_WIDTH = 1100;\n/** uv units per second at which the speed term saturates. */\nconst SPEED_FULL_SCALE = 1.6;\n/** Fraction of the velocity gap left after a second. Fast: speed must track. */\nconst VELOCITY_SMOOTHING = 0.001;\n/** Ceiling on a single sample's stored flow, in uv units. */\nconst FLOW_MAX = 0.04;\n/** Ceiling on the SUM of all samples' flow, applied in the shader. */\nconst FLOW_CLAMP = 0.055;\n\n/** The reduced-motion still: a frozen swipe with a plausible velocity. */\nconst STILL_POINTS = 9;\nconst STILL_FLOW: readonly [number, number] = [0.02, 0.008];\n\ninterface RevealUniforms {\n  /** three types `uniforms` as an open record; the index signature matches it. */\n  [name: string]: THREE.IUniform;\n  uResolution: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Vector2>;\n  uImage: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Texture | null>;\n  uHasImage: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uUvScale: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Vector2>;\n  uUvOffset: THREE.IUniform<THREE.Vector2>;\n  /** Flat vec4 array: three's uniform setters take a typed array unchanged. */\n  uTrail: THREE.IUniform<Float32Array>;\n  uFlow: THREE.IUniform<Float32Array>;\n  uBlock: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uLevels: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uFlowClamp: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uRestBrightness: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n  uRestSaturation: THREE.IUniform<number>;\n}\n\ninterface Stage {\n  renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n  scene: THREE.Scene;\n  camera: THREE.OrthographicCamera;\n  geometry: THREE.PlaneGeometry;\n  material: THREE.ShaderMaterial;\n  uniforms: RevealUniforms;\n  placeholder: THREE.DataTexture;\n  render: () => void;\n}\n\ninterface TrailSample {\n  x: number;\n  y: number;\n  /** Elapsed seconds at spawn. Negative means the slot has never been used. */\n  born: number;\n  flowX: number;\n  flowY: number;\n  speed: number;\n}\n\n/**\n * A 1x1 opaque texture, bound before anything has loaded. Sampling an unbound\n * sampler2D is undefined behaviour: some drivers return black, some warn on\n * every draw, and one of them will be the reviewer's.\n */\nfunction createPlaceholder(): THREE.DataTexture {\n  const texture = new THREE.DataTexture(new Uint8Array([16, 17, 21, 255]), 1, 1);\n  texture.needsUpdate = true;\n  return texture;\n}\n\nexport function DitherTrailImageReveal({\n  src,\n  alt = '',\n  levels = 4,\n  blockSize = 5,\n  trailLife = 1.05,\n  spawnDistance = 0.012,\n  spawnInterval = 0.042,\n  flowGain = 0.03,\n  smoothing = 0.0002,\n  restBrightness = 0.5,\n  restSaturation = 0.16,\n  fps = 30,\n  maxDpr = 1.5,\n  className,\n}: DitherTrailImageRevealProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const mount = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  /* A trail needs a pointer that exists while no button is held. On touch\n     `pointermove` only fires mid-drag, so the reveal would appear under the\n     finger that is covering it and then be stranded on lift. */\n  const finePointer = useMediaQuery('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)');\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n  const [failed, setFailed] = useState(false);\n\n  /** The decoded image, kept across renderer rebuilds so a resize never refetches. */\n  const image = useRef<THREE.Texture | null>(null);\n  /** Intrinsic aspect of that image; 0 until one has decoded. */\n  const imageAspect = useRef(0);\n\n  const target = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const inside = useRef(false);\n  const follow = useRef({ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 });\n  const velocity = useRef({ x: 0, y: 0 });\n  const lastSpawn = useRef({ x: 0.5, y: 0.5, at: -1 });\n  const writeIndex = useRef(0);\n  const budget = useRef(0);\n\n  const trail = useRef<TrailSample[]>(\n    Array.from({ length: TRAIL_POINTS }, () => ({\n      x: 0,\n      y: 0,\n      born: -1,\n      flowX: 0,\n      flowY: 0,\n      speed: 0,\n    })),\n  );\n\n  const live = useRef({\n    levels,\n    blockSize,\n    trailLife,\n    spawnDistance,\n    spawnInterval,\n    flowGain,\n    smoothing,\n    restBrightness,\n    restSaturation,\n    fps,\n  });\n  live.current = {\n    levels,\n    blockSize,\n    trailLife,\n    spawnDistance,\n    spawnInterval,\n    flowGain,\n    smoothing,\n    restBrightness,\n    restSaturation,\n    fps,\n  };\n\n  const ready = supported && width > 1 && height > 1;\n\n  /* The measured size, readable from an async callback. The image loader below\n     runs on `[src, ready]`, so its success handler closes over whichever size\n     was current when the fetch STARTED — and a decode routinely lands a few\n     hundred milliseconds later, after the observer has reported the real box.\n     Fitting against the stale numbers left the picture visibly mis-cropped\n     until something else happened to resize the element. */\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  /** object-fit: cover, replicated in uv space so the shader matches the CSS. */\n  const applyCover = (w: number, h: number) => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || w < 1 || h < 1) return;\n    const ia = imageAspect.current;\n    if (ia <= 0) {\n      s.uniforms.uUvScale.value.set(1, 1);\n      s.uniforms.uUvOffset.value.set(0, 0);\n      return;\n    }\n    const canvasAspect = w / h;\n    if (ia > canvasAspect) {\n      // Image is wider than the box: crop the sides.\n      const scale = canvasAspect / ia;\n      s.uniforms.uUvScale.value.set(scale, 1);\n      s.uniforms.uUvOffset.value.set((1 - scale) * 0.5, 0);\n    } else {\n      const scale = ia / canvasAspect;\n      s.uniforms.uUvScale.value.set(1, scale);\n      s.uniforms.uUvOffset.value.set(0, (1 - scale) * 0.5);\n    }\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const slot = mount.current;\n    if (!slot || !ready) return;\n\n    /* The canvas is created here rather than owned by React. `reactStrictMode`\n       double-invokes this effect; the first cleanup calls `forceContextLoss()`,\n       and `getContext` on that same element afterwards returns the DEAD context\n       instead of a fresh one — blank in development, fine in production, which\n       is the worst way for a bug to behave. */\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n        antialias: false,\n        alpha: false,\n        powerPreference: 'low-power',\n      });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    // Two caps, and the lower wins: a ratio cap for sane machines and an\n    // absolute width cap so a 4K hero cannot ask for a 7680px buffer.\n    const ratio = Math.min(pixelRatio(maxDpr), MAX_BUFFER_WIDTH / Math.max(width, 1));\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.max(ratio, 0.5));\n    renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    const canvas = renderer.domElement;\n    canvas.style.display = 'block';\n    canvas.style.width = '100%';\n    canvas.style.height = '100%';\n    slot.appendChild(canvas);\n\n    const placeholder = createPlaceholder();\n\n    const uniforms: RevealUniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(width, height) },\n      uImage: { value: image.current ?? placeholder },\n      uHasImage: { value: image.current ? 1 : 0 },\n      uUvScale: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1, 1) },\n      uUvOffset: { value: new THREE.Vector2(0, 0) },\n      uTrail: { value: new Float32Array(TRAIL_POINTS * 4) },\n      uFlow: { value: new Float32Array(TRAIL_POINTS * 2) },\n      uBlock: { value: blockSize },\n      uLevels: { value: levels },\n      uFlowClamp: { value: FLOW_CLAMP },\n      uRestBrightness: { value: restBrightness },\n      uRestSaturation: { value: restSaturation },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: ditherTrailImageRevealVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: ditherTrailImageRevealFragmentShader,\n      uniforms,\n    });\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material));\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1);\n    const render = () => renderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n    /* Listeners on the CANVAS, not the window: a document-level handler\n       outlives the renderer it drives, and the rect it measures is not the rect\n       being drawn into. Passive, because nothing calls preventDefault here and\n       a non-passive move handler stalls the compositor on every event. */\n    const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (rect.width === 0 || rect.height === 0) return;\n      target.current = {\n        x: (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width,\n        // Flipped: uv y is up, client y is down.\n        y: 1 - (event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height,\n      };\n      if (!inside.current) {\n        // Entering: teleport the follower rather than damping to it, or the\n        // trail draws a stripe from wherever the pointer was last seen.\n        follow.current.x = target.current.x;\n        follow.current.y = target.current.y;\n        lastSpawn.current.x = target.current.x;\n        lastSpawn.current.y = target.current.y;\n        velocity.current.x = 0;\n        velocity.current.y = 0;\n      }\n      inside.current = true;\n    };\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      inside.current = false;\n      // Samples already in the ring keep ageing out on their own, so the trail\n      // finishes its life instead of vanishing the instant the pointer leaves.\n    };\n\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave, { passive: true });\n\n    stage.current = {\n      renderer,\n      scene,\n      camera,\n      geometry,\n      material,\n      uniforms,\n      placeholder,\n      render,\n    };\n    applyCover(width, height);\n    render();\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave);\n      // `dispose()` frees GPU objects but leaves the context alive until GC\n      // decides otherwise. `disposeRenderer` is the one that force-loses it,\n      // and the per-process budget is about sixteen contexts.\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      placeholder.dispose();\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n    // Everything else is pushed through `live` or the uniforms from the loop.\n    // Rebuilding on a prop change would churn WebGL contexts.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, maxDpr]);\n\n  /* Loading is its own effect and its own lifetime. It never throws: a 404\n     leaves `uHasImage` at 0 and the shader keeps drawing its stand-in. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!ready || !src) return;\n    let cancelled = false;\n    setFailed(false);\n\n    const loader = new THREE.TextureLoader();\n    loader.load(\n      src,\n      (texture) => {\n        if (cancelled) {\n          // The component unmounted, or `src` changed, mid-decode. Without this\n          // the texture is uploaded to a dead context and then leaked.\n          texture.dispose();\n          return;\n        }\n        texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n        // No mipmaps: the quad is screen-filling, the image is never minified\n        // past 1:1, and mipmapping a non-power-of-two texture silently fails.\n        texture.generateMipmaps = false;\n        texture.minFilter = THREE.LinearFilter;\n        texture.magFilter = THREE.LinearFilter;\n        texture.wrapS = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n        texture.wrapT = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n\n        image.current?.dispose();\n        image.current = texture;\n\n        const source = texture.image as { width?: number; height?: number } | null;\n        const w = source?.width ?? 0;\n        const h = source?.height ?? 0;\n        imageAspect.current = w > 0 && h > 0 ? w / h : 0;\n\n        const s = stage.current;\n        if (!s) return;\n        s.uniforms.uImage.value = texture;\n        s.uniforms.uHasImage.value = 1;\n        // From the ref, not the closure: see the note on `size` above.\n        applyCover(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n        s.render();\n      },\n      undefined,\n      () => {\n        if (cancelled) return;\n        // A missing image is a content problem, not a crash. The stand-in field\n        // keeps the panel legible and the trail keeps working on top of it.\n        setFailed(true);\n        imageAspect.current = 0;\n        const s = stage.current;\n        if (!s) return;\n        s.uniforms.uHasImage.value = 0;\n        s.render();\n      },\n    );\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n    };\n    // `width`/`height` are read for the cover fit but must not retrigger a\n    // fetch; the resize effect re-applies the fit on its own.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [src, ready]);\n\n  // The decoded texture outlives the renderer, so it is released on unmount\n  // rather than in the loader effect, which reruns whenever `src` changes.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    return () => {\n      image.current?.dispose();\n      image.current = null;\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    const ratio = Math.min(pixelRatio(maxDpr), MAX_BUFFER_WIDTH / width);\n    s.renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.max(ratio, 0.5));\n    s.renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    s.uniforms.uResolution.value.set(width, height);\n    applyCover(width, height);\n    // Redraw once, or a resize while the loop is paused leaves the previous\n    // framebuffer stretched across the new size.\n    s.render();\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [width, height, maxDpr]);\n\n  const writeTrailUniforms = (elapsed: number, life: number) => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s) return;\n    const points = s.uniforms.uTrail.value;\n    const flows = s.uniforms.uFlow.value;\n    let i = 0;\n    for (const sample of trail.current) {\n      const age = sample.born < 0 ? Infinity : elapsed - sample.born;\n      const remaining = clamp(1 - age / life, 0, 1);\n      points[i * 4] = sample.x;\n      points[i * 4 + 1] = sample.y;\n      points[i * 4 + 2] = remaining;\n      points[i * 4 + 3] = sample.speed;\n      flows[i * 2] = sample.flowX;\n      flows[i * 2 + 1] = sample.flowY;\n      i += 1;\n    }\n  };\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt, elapsed) => {\n      const s = stage.current;\n      if (!s) return;\n      const cfg = live.current;\n\n      const previousX = follow.current.x;\n      const previousY = follow.current.y;\n\n      const aim = target.current;\n      if (aim) {\n        follow.current.x = damp(follow.current.x, aim.x, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n        follow.current.y = damp(follow.current.y, aim.y, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n      }\n\n      // Velocity of the FOLLOWER, in uv units per second. Dividing by dt is\n      // what makes it a velocity rather than a per-frame delta that measures\n      // twice as large at 60Hz as at 120Hz for the same hand movement.\n      const step = Math.max(dt, 0.0001);\n      velocity.current.x = damp(\n        velocity.current.x,\n        (follow.current.x - previousX) / step,\n        VELOCITY_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n      velocity.current.y = damp(\n        velocity.current.y,\n        (follow.current.y - previousY) / step,\n        VELOCITY_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n\n      /* Spawn on DISTANCE travelled, or on a time floor. Distance alone gives\n         an evenly spaced trail but lets the reveal decay to nothing under a\n         pointer that has stopped; the time floor keeps a stationary hover\n         alive. Spawning per event instead would clump on a slow drag and smear\n         on a flick, because events fire on sub-pixel movement. */\n      if (inside.current && aim) {\n        const moved = Math.hypot(\n          follow.current.x - lastSpawn.current.x,\n          follow.current.y - lastSpawn.current.y,\n        );\n        const waited = elapsed - lastSpawn.current.at;\n        if (moved >= cfg.spawnDistance || waited >= cfg.spawnInterval) {\n          const slot = trail.current[writeIndex.current % TRAIL_POINTS];\n          if (slot) {\n            writeIndex.current = (writeIndex.current + 1) % TRAIL_POINTS;\n            slot.x = follow.current.x;\n            slot.y = follow.current.y;\n            slot.born = elapsed;\n            slot.flowX = clamp(velocity.current.x * cfg.flowGain, -FLOW_MAX, FLOW_MAX);\n            slot.flowY = clamp(velocity.current.y * cfg.flowGain, -FLOW_MAX, FLOW_MAX);\n            slot.speed = clamp(\n              Math.hypot(velocity.current.x, velocity.current.y) / SPEED_FULL_SCALE,\n              0,\n              1,\n            );\n          }\n          lastSpawn.current.x = follow.current.x;\n          lastSpawn.current.y = follow.current.y;\n          lastSpawn.current.at = elapsed;\n        }\n      }\n\n      const life = Math.max(cfg.trailLife, 0.05);\n      writeTrailUniforms(elapsed, life);\n\n      const u = s.uniforms;\n      u.uBlock.value = Math.max(cfg.blockSize, 1);\n      u.uLevels.value = Math.max(cfg.levels, 2);\n      u.uRestBrightness.value = cfg.restBrightness;\n      u.uRestSaturation.value = cfg.restSaturation;\n\n      /* Draw-rate throttle. The state above updates every frame — a trail\n         sampled at 30Hz has visible corners — but the DRAW is the expensive\n         half and 30fps is indistinguishable through 5px blocks. Subtracting\n         the budget rather than zeroing the accumulator keeps the long-run rate\n         exact instead of quietly losing the remainder every frame. */\n      const frameBudget = 1 / clamp(cfg.fps, 10, 120);\n      budget.current += dt;\n      if (budget.current < frameBudget) return;\n      budget.current = Math.min(budget.current - frameBudget, frameBudget);\n\n      s.render();\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && finePointer,\n  );\n\n  /* Reduced motion, and coarse pointers, get a composed still: a short frozen\n     swipe baked into the ring so the panel shows what the effect produces. The\n     listeners stay attached and their target is never consumed. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || !ready) return;\n    if (!reduced && finePointer) return;\n\n    const [flowX = 0.02, flowY = 0.008] = STILL_FLOW;\n    let i = 0;\n    for (const sample of trail.current) {\n      const t = i / (STILL_POINTS - 1);\n      const inSwipe = i < STILL_POINTS;\n      sample.x = 0.3 + t * 0.42;\n      sample.y = 0.4 + Math.sin(t * 2.2) * 0.12;\n      sample.flowX = flowX;\n      sample.flowY = flowY;\n      sample.speed = inSwipe ? 0.45 : 0;\n      // `born` is unused by the still: lives are written straight below.\n      sample.born = -1;\n      i += 1;\n    }\n\n    const points = s.uniforms.uTrail.value;\n    const flows = s.uniforms.uFlow.value;\n    let j = 0;\n    for (const sample of trail.current) {\n      const remaining = j < STILL_POINTS ? 1 - j / STILL_POINTS : 0;\n      points[j * 4] = sample.x;\n      points[j * 4 + 1] = sample.y;\n      points[j * 4 + 2] = remaining;\n      points[j * 4 + 3] = sample.speed;\n      flows[j * 2] = sample.flowX;\n      flows[j * 2 + 1] = sample.flowY;\n      j += 1;\n    }\n    s.render();\n  }, [reduced, finePointer, ready, width, height]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the three pointer listeners from the canvas, nulls the stage ref so an in-flight frame cannot touch a dead renderer, then `disposeRenderer(renderer)` — which force-loses the context, not just `dispose()`, since Chromium keeps roughly sixteen per process and drops the oldest — followed by the geometry, the material and the 1x1 placeholder texture, and finally removes the canvas from the DOM. The decoded image texture is disposed in a separate unmount effect, and an in-flight decode is cancelled by a flag so its callback disposes the result instead of binding it.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The loop never starts. A short frozen swipe — nine samples with descending life and a plausible velocity — is baked into the ring and one frame is drawn, so the panel shows what the effect produces rather than a flat dimmed image. Coarse pointers get the same still, for the same reason. The listeners stay attached but their target is never consumed, so moving the pointer changes nothing.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Pushing pointer events straight into the trail. Event positions are not a path: they arrive at whatever rate the device chooses, cluster on slow movement and stop dead when the hand pauses. The handler records a target only; the loop damps a follower toward it and samples the follower.\n- Damping with a per-frame constant (`x += (t - x) * 0.1`) instead of `damp(..., dt)`. The trail is visibly tighter at 120Hz than at 60Hz, so the feel is tuned to whichever machine it was authored on.\n- Measuring velocity as a per-frame delta rather than dividing by `dt`. The same hand movement then reads as half the speed at 120Hz, and the smear strength — and with it the whole character of the effect — depends on the display.\n- Sampling the Bayer threshold per fragment instead of per block. The reveal edge becomes smooth, which is precisely the thing a dither is supposed to replace, and the block grid stops being legible.\n- Comparing against the Bayer matrix without an epsilon. Its zero cell passes `step()` everywhere, so one pixel in every 5x5 block is lit across the entire canvas, including areas the pointer has never visited.\n- Dividing the 8x8 matrix by 63 instead of 64. A threshold of exactly 1.0 means the brightest level can never be selected, and the whitest part of the image clips one step down.\n- Assuming `src` is decoded. A `TextureLoader` result arrives asynchronously and may never arrive at all. Leaving the sampler unbound until then is undefined behaviour — black on some drivers, a console warning per draw on others. A 1x1 placeholder is bound from the first frame and a `uHasImage` float selects a procedural stand-in.\n- Letting a 404 throw or blank the panel. A component that cannot survive a missing image cannot be dropped into a CMS-backed page. The error callback leaves `uHasImage` at 0 and the trail keeps working over the stand-in field.\n- Not cancelling an in-flight decode. If `src` changes or the component unmounts mid-load, the callback fires against a dead renderer and the texture is uploaded and then leaked. A `cancelled` flag disposes it instead.\n- Rendering at full device pixel ratio. Fill is the entire cost of a fullscreen quad and it scales with the square of the ratio, for an image whose smallest feature is deliberately five pixels wide.\n- Owning the canvas in React while calling `forceContextLoss()` on cleanup. Under `reactStrictMode` the effect runs twice and the second `getContext` on that same element returns the DEAD context: blank in development, fine in production, which is the worst way for a bug to behave.\n- Shipping the demo image as a committed asset. The image is a prop; the demo paints its own canvas and passes a data URL, so the registry carries no binary.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `src` is required and is never bundled. Anything a browser can fetch works: an http(s) URL, a `blob:` from an upload, or a `data:` URL.\n- Coarse pointers get the composed still rather than the live effect. `pointermove` only fires mid-drag on touch, so the reveal would appear under the finger covering it and then be stranded on lift.\n- Without WebGL the component renders the image plainly with `object-fit: cover`. The picture is the content; the dither is decoration on top of it.\n- The decoded texture is held across renderer rebuilds, so a resize or a dpr change re-uploads it rather than refetching. It is disposed in its own unmount effect, separate from the loader effect that reruns on every `src` change.\n- Leaving the panel does not clear the trail. Samples keep ageing out on their own, so the reveal finishes its life instead of vanishing on `pointerleave`.\n- The root carries `role=\"img\"` and the `alt` text; an empty `alt` marks the whole panel decorative with `aria-hidden` instead.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt rest the image is visible but dimmed and nearly grey. Drag across it and full colour appears in a trail of hard-edged square blocks that widens and fades behind the pointer, with the blocks smeared along the direction of travel — more on a flick, less on a slow drag. The untouched area contains no stray lit pixels anywhere. Stop moving and the reveal stays alive under the cursor instead of dying. Leave the panel and the trail finishes ageing out where it stands. The follow lag is identical on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display. Point at a `src` that 404s: the panel shows a banded stand-in field, the trail still works, and the console carries no exception. Resize wide and the image stays cover-cropped with no stretching, and the trail discs stay round.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "divider": "# Build: Divider\n\nOne primitive for every horizontal break in the kit. With no children it is a bare `role=\"separator\"` rule; with children (a label such as \"or\", a date heading, a small button) it becomes a flex row whose lines grow around the content. Three variants change the treatment: a single hairline, a pair of framing hairlines, or a soft filled strip on the secondary surface.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nHorizontal divider in single-hairline, double-line and filled-strip treatments, with optional start, centre or end content.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Decide the branch with `hasContent = children !== undefined && children !== null`, so `0` or an empty string still count as content and only a missing child yields a bare rule.\n2. Empty branch: render one `<div role=\"separator\" aria-orientation=\"horizontal\">` with `w-full` plus the variant's empty class: `h-px bg-separator-border` (single), `h-2 border-y border-separator-border` (double) or `h-2 rounded-full bg-background-secondary-default` (fill).\n3. Content branch: render a `<div>` with `w-full`, the variant's row class and the `align` justify class. Single is `flex items-center gap-3`; double is `flex items-center border-y border-separator-border py-2.5`; fill is `flex items-center rounded-2lg bg-background-secondary-default px-4 py-2.5`.\n4. Only the single variant draws inline lines. Compute `showLeadingLine = variant === \"single\" && align !== \"start\"` and `showTrailingLine = variant === \"single\" && align !== \"end\"`, and render each as `<span aria-hidden className=\"h-px min-w-0 flex-1 bg-separator-border\">`, so the content sits at the start, centre or end and the remaining line stays flexible.\n5. Wrap the children in `<div className=\"shrink-0 text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">` merged with `contentClassName`, so a label never shrinks and inherits the kit's secondary body style. The root gets `data-variant` and `data-align` for styling hooks.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `gap / line = gap-3 (12px), h-px flex-1`\n  12px of air on both sides of the label keeps \"or\" from touching the rule. `min-w-0` on the lines lets them shrink to nothing in a narrow column rather than pushing the label out.\n- `double / fill padding = py-2.5 (10px); fill adds px-4 and rounded-2lg (10px)`\n  A 20px body line plus 10px top and bottom gives a 40px band, the height of a list section heading in the dashboards.\n- `empty heights = single 1px, double / fill 8px (h-2)`\n  The double rule needs height between its two borders to read as two lines; the fill strip at 8px reads as a soft spacer band.\n- `separator colour = light neutral-200, dark neutral-800`\n  `--color-separator-border` is the kit's quietest line colour, so dividers never compete with card borders.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype DividerVariant = \"single\" | \"double\" | \"fill\"\ntype DividerAlign = \"start\" | \"center\" | \"end\"\ninterface DividerProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"children\"> { variant?: DividerVariant; align?: DividerAlign; children?: ReactNode; contentClassName?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }\nfunction Divider(props: DividerProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `variant: \"single\" | \"double\" | \"fill\"` — default `\"single\"`\n  One hairline, two framing hairlines, or a filled secondary-surface strip.\n- `align: \"start\" | \"center\" | \"end\"` — default `\"center\"`\n  Where the content sits. On the single variant it also removes the line on that side.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Optional label or control. Without it the divider is a bare `role=\"separator\"` rule.\n- `contentClassName: string`\n  Extra classes for the content wrapper.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root through `tailwind-merge`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  root: \"w-full\",\n  line: \"h-px min-w-0 flex-1 bg-separator-border\",\n  content: \"shrink-0 text-body-medium text-text-secondary\",\n  align: {\n    start: \"justify-start\",\n    center: \"justify-center\",\n    end: \"justify-end\",\n  },\n  withContent: {\n    single: \"flex items-center gap-3\",\n    double: \"flex items-center border-y border-separator-border py-2.5\",\n    fill: \"flex items-center rounded-2lg bg-background-secondary-default px-4 py-2.5\",\n  },\n  empty: {\n    single: \"h-px bg-separator-border\",\n    double: \"h-2 border-y border-separator-border\",\n    fill: \"h-2 rounded-full bg-background-secondary-default\",\n  },\n});\n\n/**\n * Horizontal content divider with three BoardUI surface treatments.\n *\n * `single` places content within one continuous hairline, `double` frames it\n * with a line above and below, and `fill` uses the secondary surface color.\n * Children stay completely composable, so labels, buttons, and small controls\n * can share the same primitive.\n */\nexport function Divider({\n  variant = \"single\",\n  align = \"center\",\n  children,\n  contentClassName,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: DividerProps) {\n  const hasContent = children !== undefined && children !== null;\n\n  if (!hasContent) {\n    return (\n      <div\n        ref={ref}\n        role=\"separator\"\n        aria-orientation=\"horizontal\"\n        data-variant={variant}\n        className={cx(styles.root, styles.empty[variant], className)}\n        {...props}\n      />\n    );\n  }\n\n  const showLeadingLine = variant === \"single\" && align !== \"start\";\n  const showTrailingLine = variant === \"single\" && align !== \"end\";\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={ref}\n      data-variant={variant}\n      data-align={align}\n      className={cx(\n        styles.root,\n        styles.withContent[variant],\n        styles.align[align],\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      {showLeadingLine ? <span aria-hidden className={styles.line} /> : null}\n      <div className={cx(styles.content, contentClassName)}>{children}</div>\n      {showTrailingLine ? <span aria-hidden className={styles.line} /> : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates, so the divider renders identically under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not expect the content form to be announced as a separator. Only the empty form carries `role=\"separator\"`; with children the row is plain layout and its label is read as text.\n- Do not put long, wrapping text in a divider. The content wrapper is `shrink-0`, so a long label pushes the lines to zero and can overflow a narrow column.\n- Do not pass `children={null}` expecting a labelled divider; `null` and `undefined` both select the bare-rule branch.\n- Do not hard-code `bg-neutral-200` on a custom line. Use `bg-separator-border` so the hairline flips to neutral-800 in dark mode.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Children are fully composable: a label, a date, or a compact control such as a \"Show more\" button all work.\n- Defaults are `variant=\"single\"` and `align=\"center\"`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`<Divider />` renders a full-width 1px rule with `role=\"separator\"`. `<Divider>or</Divider>` renders \"or\" centred with a hairline growing on each side and 12px between label and line. `align=\"start\"` removes the leading line so the label sits flush left. The double variant frames its label between a top and bottom border with 10px padding, the fill variant sits on a rounded secondary-surface strip, and all lines switch to the dark separator colour in dark mode.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "docs-callout": "# Build: Docs Callout\n\nThe aside a docs page drops between paragraphs: a softly tinted, bordered box with a coloured icon chip, a bold title and body copy that styles inline `code` and links. Info is blue, tip lime, warning yellow and danger rose, all from the kit status tokens so both themes stay legible. A dismissible callout shows a close button and folds its row shut before it unmounts.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDocs callout in four variants (note, tip, warning, danger) with a tinted icon chip, title, rich body and an optional collapsing dismiss.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each variant maps to one status token pair: the surface is `bg-status-*-background/45` with a `border-status-*-text/20` hairline, and the 24px icon chip uses the full-strength background with the status text colour. The title defaults to the variant label; a custom title keeps the label for screen readers as an `sr-only` \"Warning: \" prefix.\n2. The box is `role=\"note\"` labelled by its title, which is the ARIA role for a parenthetical aside; it is never `alert`, because docs callouts are static content.\n3. The body gets descendant styles through arbitrary variants (`[&_code]:`, `[&_a]:`, `[&_p+p]:`), so MDX or plain JSX children pick up code chips, underlined links and paragraph spacing without extra components.\n4. Dismiss runs a three-phase state: pressing close calls `onDismiss` immediately (so the caller can move focus), then the wrapper goes to \"leaving\": `inert`, `grid-rows-[0fr]`, `opacity-0` and `-translate-y-1` over 200ms. A 200ms timeout in an effect flips it to \"gone\" and the component returns null. With reduced motion it skips straight to \"gone\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `surface tint = status background at 45%, border at 20% of status text`\n  Reads as a coloured note in both themes without competing with the page heading.\n- `exit = 200ms ease-out, grid rows 1fr to 0fr`\n  Short enough to feel like a direct response; collapsing the row lets the content below slide up instead of jumping.\n- `icon chip = 24px, rounded-lg, 16px glyph`\n  Matches the close button size so the first line aligns on both edges.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DocsCallout(props: { variant?: \"info\" | \"tip\" | \"warning\" | \"danger\"; title?: ReactNode; children?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent | null; dismissible?: boolean; onDismiss?: () => void; dismissLabel?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `variant: \"info\" | \"tip\" | \"warning\" | \"danger\"` — default `\"info\"`\n  Colour, icon and default title.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Note\" / \"Tip\" / \"Warning\" / \"Danger\"`\n  Heading; a custom one keeps the variant name as an sr-only prefix.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Body copy; inline `code`, links and paragraphs are styled.\n- `icon: IconComponent | null`\n  Replaces the variant icon; `null` hides the chip.\n- `dismissible: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows a close button that collapses and unmounts the callout.\n- `onDismiss: () => void`\n  Called on press, before the exit animation; move focus here.\n- `dismissLabel: string` — default `\"Dismiss <variant>\"`\n  Accessible name of the close button.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer wrapper.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Dismiss: \"shown\" -> \"leaving\" (row collapses 1fr -> 0fr while fading, content inert)\n// -> \"gone\" (unmounted). Reduced motion skips straight to \"gone\". onDismiss runs on\n// press so the caller can move focus before the close button disappears.\nconst [phase, setPhase] = useState<\"shown\" | \"leaving\" | \"gone\">(\"shown\");\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (phase !== \"leaving\") return;\n  const t = window.setTimeout(() => setPhase(\"gone\"), LEAVE_MS);\n  return () => window.clearTimeout(t);\n}, [phase]);\n\nconst dismiss = () => {\n  if (phase !== \"shown\") return;\n  onDismiss?.();\n  setPhase(reduced ? \"gone\" : \"leaving\");\n};\n\nconst leaving = phase === \"leaving\";\nconst wrapperClass = cx(\n  \"grid transition-[grid-template-rows,opacity,translate] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n  leaving ? \"-translate-y-1 grid-rows-[0fr] opacity-0\" : \"grid-rows-[1fr] opacity-100\",\n  className,\n);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The only timer is the 200ms leaving timeout, set in an effect keyed on the phase and cleared in its cleanup, so unmounting mid-exit leaves nothing running.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion` the component skips the leaving phase and unmounts at once (`useReducedMotion`), and the wrapper transition carries `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `role=\"alert\"` for danger callouts. Screen readers interrupt to announce static docs text on page load; `role=\"note\"` is the right semantics.\n- Unmounting on press with no focus plan. The focused close button vanishes and focus falls to `<body>`; use `onDismiss` to move focus to a nearby control.\n- Animating `height: auto` with JS measurements. Transitioning `grid-template-rows` from 1fr to 0fr collapses to zero with pure CSS.\n- Relying on colour alone for the variant. The title (or the sr-only prefix) names it in words.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The callout keeps its own dismissed state. To bring it back, remount it (change its `key`), as the demo's Restore button does.\n- `className` lands on the outer wrapper, so grid placement and margins work; pass `icon={null}` to drop the chip.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo shows Note, Tip, \"Captions are for labels\" (warning) and Danger callouts in a two-column grid that stacks to one column on phones. Each is a `role=\"note\"` named by its title. Pressing \"Dismiss tip\" moves focus to Restore, the tip folds away within about 200ms and Restore reads \"Restore (1)\"; dismissing the warning makes it \"Restore (2)\". Pressing Restore brings both back and the button returns to \"Restore\", aria-disabled.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "docs-pager": "# Build: Docs Pager\n\nTwo bordered cards that close a documentation page, pointing to the page before and the page after. The previous card sits left with a back arrow, the next card sits right with its text end-aligned, and each shows the page title over a short tertiary description. On hover the card fills and its arrow nudges toward where it leads.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPrevious / next cards for the foot of a docs page: arrow and title on top, a one-line summary below, mirrored for RTL.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The nav is a one-column grid that becomes two columns from `sm`. The next card always takes `sm:col-start-2`, so on the first page (no previous) it still sits on the right instead of sliding into the empty left slot.\n2. Each card is an `<a rel=\"prev|next\">` when the link has an `href`, otherwise a `<button>` that only calls `onNavigate(direction, link)`, which suits client routers and demos. The direction is read out through a visible label (`showLabels`) or an `sr-only` \"Previous:\" / \"Next:\" prefix, so the title and description stay part of the accessible name.\n3. The arrow uses `directionalIconClass` (`rtl:rotate-180`) and the hover nudge flips sign under `rtl:`, so in a right-to-left document the previous card points right and moves right. Because Tailwind v4 writes `translate` and `rotate` as separate properties, the nudge and the mirror compose without overriding each other.\n4. In button mode the pager re-renders in place. When the clicked card disappears (the first or last page was reached), an effect keyed on the two titles sees focus on `<body>` and moves it to the card that remains, so keyboard users are not dropped to the top of the document.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card = rounded-xl, 1px border, px-4 py-3.5, shadow-xs`\n  Matches the reference pager: a quiet bordered card that reads as part of the page rather than a primary button.\n- `arrow nudge = 2px (translate-x-0.5), 200ms ease-out`\n  Enough to hint at direction on hover without moving the title text.\n- `type = title body-medium primary, description caption-1 tertiary`\n  Same hierarchy as the reference: page name first, a muted one-liner under it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DocsPager(props: { prev?: DocsPagerLink | null; next?: DocsPagerLink | null; onNavigate?: (direction: \"prev\" | \"next\", link: DocsPagerLink) => void; showLabels?: boolean; prevLabel?: string; nextLabel?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\ntype DocsPagerLink = { title: string; description?: ReactNode; href?: string }\n```\n\n- `prev / next: DocsPagerLink | null`\n  `{ title, description?, href? }` for each neighbour; omit one at the ends of a section.\n- `onNavigate: (direction: \"prev\" | \"next\", link: DocsPagerLink) => void`\n  Called on click; the only effect when the link has no `href`.\n- `showLabels: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows \"Previous\" / \"Next\" above the titles instead of keeping them screen-reader only.\n- `prevLabel / nextLabel: string` — default `\"Previous\" / \"Next\"`\n  Direction words, visible or `sr-only`.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Previous and next page\"`\n  Name of the wrapping `<nav>`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the grid.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// In button mode the pager re-renders in place with new neighbours. When the\n// card that was clicked disappears (first or last page reached), focus would\n// fall to <body>; hand it to the card that is still there instead.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const side = pendingFocus.current;\n  if (!side) return;\n  pendingFocus.current = null;\n  const active = document.activeElement;\n  if (active && active !== document.body && active.isConnected) return;\n  const other: DocsPagerDirection = side === \"prev\" ? \"next\" : \"prev\";\n  (refs.current[side] ?? refs.current[other])?.focus();\n}, [prev?.title, next?.title]);\n\nconst card = (direction: DocsPagerDirection, link: DocsPagerLink) => {\n  const isPrev = direction === \"prev\";\n  const Arrow = isPrev ? RiArrowLeftLine : RiArrowRightLine;\n  const label = isPrev ? prevLabel : nextLabel;\n  const className = cx(\n    \"group flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-1 rounded-xl border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default px-4 py-3.5 shadow-xs outline-none\",\n    \"transition-[background-color,border-color] duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n    \"hover:border-border-button-hover hover:bg-background-secondary-hover\",\n    \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-background-primary-default\",\n    isPrev ? \"items-start text-start\" : \"items-end text-end sm:col-start-2\",\n  );\n  const content = (\n    <>\n      {showLabels ? (\n        <span className=\"text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{label}</span>\n      ) : (\n        <span className=\"sr-only\">{label}: </span>\n      )}\n      <span className={cx(\"flex max-w-full items-center gap-1.5 text-body-medium text-text-primary\", !isPrev && \"flex-row-reverse\")}>\n        <Arrow\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            \"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary transition-transform duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            \"group-hover:text-foreground-icon-primary\",\n            isPrev\n              ? \"group-hover:-translate-x-0.5 rtl:group-hover:translate-x-0.5\"\n              : \"group-hover:translate-x-0.5 rtl:group-hover:-translate-x-0.5\",\n            directionalIconClass(Arrow),\n          )}\n        />\n        <span className=\"truncate\">{link.title}</span>\n      </span>\n      {link.description ? (\n        <span className=\"max-w-full truncate text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{link.description}</span>\n      ) : null}\n    </>\n  );\n  const setRef = (node: HTMLElement | null) => {\n    refs.current[direction] = node;\n  };\n  const handle = () => {\n    pendingFocus.current = direction;\n    onNavigate?.(direction, link);\n  };\n  return link.href ? (\n    <a key={direction} ref={setRef} href={link.href} rel={direction} onClick={handle} className={className}>\n      {content}\n    </a>\n  ) : (\n    <button key={direction} ref={setRef} type=\"button\" onClick={handle} className={cx(className, \"cursor-pointer\")}>\n      {content}\n    </button>\n  );\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release: there are no timers, listeners or observers. The focus hand-off is a one-shot effect that reads `document.activeElement`.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The hover arrow nudge and the card colour transitions carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`; the arrow still shifts 2px but without animating.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Letting the next card fall into the first grid column when there is no previous page. Readers expect \"next\" on the right; pin it with `col-start-2`.\n- Putting `aria-label=\"Next page\"` on the card. It replaces the title and description for screen readers; prefix an `sr-only` label instead.\n- Mirroring the arrow with a `rotate-180` class that also sets `transform`, then adding a hover translate on the same property. One wins and the other is lost; keep them on separate properties.\n- Re-rendering a pager whose clicked card unmounts without moving focus. Focus falls back to `<body>` and the next Tab starts from the top of the page.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `href` from your router's page list; `rel=\"prev\"` / `rel=\"next\"` also helps crawlers and reader modes.\n- The component renders nothing when both links are missing, so it can sit in a layout unconditionally.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo opens on Line Chart Card with \"Heatmap Chart\" on the left and \"Most Active Days\" on the right. Clicking Next twice reaches Sankey Flow (6 of 6), the right card disappears and focus moves to the remaining Previous card. Clicking Previous walks back; on Area Chart Card (1 of 6) only the Next card shows and it stays on the right.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "docs-sidebar-nav": "# Build: Docs Sidebar Nav\n\nThe left rail of a documentation site: a rounded floating panel with a brand slot, a pill filter, and grouped page links drawn on a thin tree rail. Group headers fold their lists with a height transition, the current page sits on a grey fill, and New/Pro pills flag pages. Typing in the filter narrows every group at once, opens all groups and highlights the matched letters.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nFloating docs sidebar with collapsible groups, tree rails, New/Pro badges, an active page and a ⌘K filter that highlights matches.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Data is an array of groups, each with items `{ id, label, icon?, badge?, href?, disabled?, keywords? }`. Active page, collapsed group ids and filter text are each controlled-or-uncontrolled through a small `useControllable` helper, so the nav works stand-alone or wired to a router.\n2. `filterDocsNav(groups, query)` lower-cases the query; a group whose own label matches is kept whole, otherwise only items whose label or `keywords` contain the query survive, and empty groups drop out. While a query is active every surviving group is forced open and its header renders as a plain label instead of a toggle, so a match can never hide inside a folded group.\n3. Each group panel is a CSS grid that transitions `grid-template-rows` between `0fr` and `1fr` (with opacity) over 200ms. The inner list has `min-h-0 overflow-hidden`, so the fold animates to the real content height without measuring. A folded panel gets `inert`, which removes its links from the tab order and from the accessibility tree.\n4. Every row is a `<button>` (or an `<a>` when `href` is given) with `aria-current=\"page\"` on the active one. The tree rail is two absolutely positioned spans per row painted after the button: a 8px \"corner\" with `border-s border-b rounded-es-[6px]` from the row top to its middle, and, for all but the last row, a straight line from the middle to the next row. Because they come after the row they stay visible over the active fill.\n5. Keyboard: ArrowUp/ArrowDown/Home/End on the nav move focus across the filter, group headers and rows (anything with `data-docs-nav-focus` that is not inside an `inert` panel or disabled). Escape in the filter clears it. With `hotkey`, a window `keydown` listener focuses and selects the filter on ⌘K / Ctrl+K and is removed on unmount.\n6. On mount, if the active row is outside the list viewport, the list scroller's `scrollTop` is adjusted to centre it (rect maths on the scroller, never `scrollIntoView`, so the page itself never jumps). The list fades out over its last 32px with a mask and pads 32px at the bottom so the last row can scroll clear of the fade.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `rail inset = start-[13px], 8px corner, rounded-es-[6px]`\n  Puts the rail under the group label's first letter column and leaves room for the 16px icon at 33px, matching the reference spacing where icons start 33px from the list edge and plain labels 32px.\n- `row height / gap = h-8 with gap-0.5 (34px pitch)`\n  The reference rows sit 34px apart with a 32px tall active fill; the 2px gap keeps neighbouring hover fills from touching.\n- `fold duration = 200ms ease-out on grid-template-rows + opacity`\n  Long enough to read as a fold, short enough that toggling several groups in a row never queues up visible motion.\n- `bottom fade = mask-b-from-[calc(100%-32px)] with pb-8`\n  The fade signals more content below; the matching 32px of padding lets the last row scroll fully out of the faded band.\n- `panel width = w-[260px], rounded-3xl, shadow-sidebar`\n  Same floating panel recipe as the kit dashboard sidebar so the two read as one system.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DocsSidebarNav(props: { groups: DocsNavGroup[]; value?: string | null; defaultValue?: string | null; onValueChange?: (id: string, item: DocsNavItem) => void; collapsedGroups?: string[]; defaultCollapsedGroups?: string[]; onCollapsedGroupsChange?: (ids: string[]) => void; query?: string; defaultQuery?: string; onQueryChange?: (query: string) => void; showFilter?: boolean; filterPlaceholder?: string; filterLabel?: string; hotkey?: boolean; hotkeyLabel?: string; header?: ReactNode; footer?: ReactNode; emptyMessage?: (query: string) => ReactNode; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction filterDocsNav(groups: DocsNavGroup[], query: string): DocsNavGroup[]\n```\n\n- `groups: DocsNavGroup[]`\n  Sections of `{ id, label, items }`; each item is `{ id, label, icon?, badge?: \"new\" | \"pro\", href?, disabled?, keywords? }`.\n- `value / defaultValue: string | null` — default `null`\n  Id of the active page. Gets the grey fill and `aria-current=\"page\"`.\n- `onValueChange: (id: string, item: DocsNavItem) => void`\n  Called when a row is activated.\n- `collapsedGroups / defaultCollapsedGroups: string[]` — default `[]`\n  Ids of folded groups.\n- `onCollapsedGroupsChange: (ids: string[]) => void`\n  Called with the next folded ids when a header is toggled.\n- `query / defaultQuery: string` — default `\"\"`\n  Filter text. Non-empty text filters rows, opens all groups and highlights matches.\n- `onQueryChange: (query: string) => void`\n  Called as the filter changes or is cleared.\n- `showFilter: boolean` — default `true`\n  Renders the pill filter input.\n- `hotkey: boolean` — default `false`\n  Focuses the filter on ⌘K / Ctrl+K and shows `hotkeyLabel` in the pill.\n- `header / footer: ReactNode`\n  Brand row above the filter and a pinned row below the list.\n- `emptyMessage: (query: string) => ReactNode` — default `No pages match “…”`\n  Shown when the filter leaves nothing.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the panel; set the height here so the list can scroll.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Keeps groups whose label matches whole; otherwise keeps only matching items. Empty groups drop out. */\nexport function filterDocsNav(groups: DocsNavGroup[], rawQuery: string): DocsNavGroup[] {\n  const query = rawQuery.trim().toLowerCase();\n  if (!query) return groups;\n  const out: DocsNavGroup[] = [];\n  for (const group of groups) {\n    if (group.label.toLowerCase().includes(query)) {\n      out.push(group);\n      continue;\n    }\n    const items = group.items.filter(\n      (item) =>\n        item.label.toLowerCase().includes(query) ||\n        (item.keywords ?? []).some((word) => word.toLowerCase().includes(query)),\n    );\n    if (items.length > 0) out.push({ ...group, items });\n  }\n  return out;\n}\n\ninterface GroupSectionProps {\n  group: DocsNavGroup;\n  open: boolean;\n  filtering: boolean;\n  query: string;\n  activeId: string | null;\n  onToggle: (id: string) => void;\n  onSelect: (item: DocsNavItem) => void;\n}\n\nfunction GroupSection({ group, open, filtering, query, activeId, onToggle, onSelect }: GroupSectionProps) {\n  const panelId = useId();\n  const headerClass = \"flex h-7 w-full items-center justify-between rounded-lg pr-2.5 pl-2.5 text-caption-1-medium tracking-[0.04em] text-text-tertiary uppercase\";\n\n  return (\n    <li className=\"flex flex-col\">\n      {filtering ? (\n        <div className={headerClass}>{group.label}</div>\n      ) : (\n        <button\n          type=\"button\"\n          data-docs-nav-focus=\"\"\n          aria-expanded={open}\n          aria-controls={panelId}\n          onClick={() => onToggle(group.id)}\n          className={cx(\n            headerClass,\n            \"cursor-pointer outline-none hover:text-text-secondary focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-inset\",\n          )}\n        >\n          <span>{group.label}</span>\n          <RiArrowDownSLine\n            aria-hidden\n            className={cx(\n              \"size-4 transition-transform duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              !open && \"-rotate-90 rtl:rotate-90\",\n            )}\n          />\n        </button>\n      )}\n\n      {/* 0fr -> 1fr animates to the content's real height without measuring it. */}\n      <div\n        id={panelId}\n        inert={!open}\n        className={cx(\n          \"grid transition-[grid-template-rows,opacity] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          open ? \"grid-rows-[1fr] opacity-100\" : \"grid-rows-[0fr] opacity-0\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <ul className=\"flex min-h-0 flex-col gap-0.5 overflow-hidden pt-1\">\n          {group.items.map((item, index) => {\n            const last = index === group.items.length - 1;\n            const active = item.id === activeId;\n            const Icon = item.icon;\n            const badge = item.badge ? badgeStyles[item.badge] : null;\n            const rowClass = cx(\n              \"flex h-8 w-full items-center gap-2 rounded-lg pr-2.5 text-start text-body-medium outline-none\",\n              \"transition-colors duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-inset\",\n              Icon ? \"ps-[33px]\" : \"ps-8\",\n              item.disabled\n                ? \"cursor-not-allowed text-text-disabled\"\n                : active\n                  ? \"bg-background-tertiary-default text-text-primary\"\n                  : \"cursor-pointer text-text-secondary hover:bg-background-secondary-hover hover:text-text-primary\",\n            );\n            const content = (\n              <>\n                {Icon ? (\n                  <Icon\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className={cx(\"size-4 shrink-0\", active ? \"text-foreground-icon-primary\" : \"text-foreground-icon-secondary\")}\n                  />\n                ) : null}\n                <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate\">\n                  <Highlight text={item.label} query={query} />\n                </span>\n                {badge ? (\n                  <span\n                    className={cx(\n                      \"rounded-[4px] px-1.5 py-px text-caption-2-semibold tracking-[0.02em] uppercase\",\n                      badge.className,\n                    )}\n                  >\n                    {badge.label}\n                  </span>\n                ) : null}\n              </>\n            );\n\n            return (\n              <li key={item.id} className=\"relative\">\n                {item.href && !item.disabled ? (\n                  <a\n                    href={item.href}\n                    data-docs-nav-focus=\"\"\n                    aria-current={active ? \"page\" : undefined}\n                    onClick={() => onSelect(item)}\n                    className={rowClass}\n                  >\n                    {content}\n                  </a>\n                ) : (\n                  <button\n                    type=\"button\"\n                    data-docs-nav-focus=\"\"\n                    disabled={item.disabled}\n                    aria-current={active ? \"page\" : undefined}\n                    onClick={() => onSelect(item)}\n                    className={rowClass}\n                  >\n                    {content}\n                  </button>\n                )}\n                {/* Tree rail: drawn after the row so it stays visible over the active fill. */}\n                <span\n                  aria-hidden\n                  className=\"pointer-events-none absolute start-[13px] top-0 h-1/2 w-2 rounded-es-[6px] border-b border-s border-border-checkbox-default\"\n                />\n                {last ? null : (\n                  <span\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className=\"pointer-events-none absolute start-[13px] top-1/2 -bottom-0.5 border-s border-border-checkbox-default\"\n                  />\n                )}\n              </li>\n            );\n          })}\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </li>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The optional ⌘K `keydown` listener is attached to `window` in an effect and removed on unmount or when `hotkey` turns off. The mount-time scroll correction is a one-shot read with nothing to release.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The group fold, chevron rotation and row colour transitions all carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so groups open and close instantly and the chevron flips without turning. Nothing else moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating `height` from 0 to `auto` instead of the `0fr`/`1fr` grid trick. `auto` does not interpolate, so the fold snaps; measuring heights in JS breaks when badges or wrapping labels change the content.\n- Hiding a collapsed panel with only `overflow-hidden`. Its links stay focusable and Tab lands inside an invisible list. The panel must be `inert` while closed.\n- Keeping collapsed groups collapsed while filtering. A match inside a folded group looks like \"no results\"; the filter forces every surviving group open.\n- Calling `scrollIntoView` to reveal the active row. It scrolls every scrollable ancestor, including the page, so a docs page would jump on load.\n- Drawing the rail before the row button. The active row's fill then paints over it and the tree looks broken at the current page.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `filterDocsNav` is exported so a command palette can reuse the same matching rules as the sidebar.\n- The hotkey is opt-in because a host app may already own ⌘K for its own command palette.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTypography is active with a grey fill and `aria-current=\"page\"`. Clicking \"Base\" folds that group (its header gets `aria-expanded=\"false\"`) and clicking again unfolds it. Typing \"car\" into the filter leaves Carousel and Line Chart Card with \"car\" highlighted, opening the folded Charts group; Escape clears it and the full list returns. Pressing ⌘K focuses the filter. Clicking Badge moves the active fill to Badge and the side panel heading reads Badge. The disabled Sankey Flow row cannot be activated.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "domain-warped-fbm": "# Build: Domain-Warped FBM Fog\n\nA full-screen fragment shader on a single triangle. Two octaves of value-noise fbm are sampled twice to build a 2D offset, and that offset is fed back into a third sample — the result flows instead of cross-fading. The pointer enters the warp three times with different weights and a sign flip, so the fog leans toward the cursor without following it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSlow, low-contrast fog that flows rather than fades, with a faint light that trails the cursor.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `fbm(p)` sums two octaves of 3D value noise with amplitudes 0.6 and 0.3 and lacunarity 1.93. The third dimension is time, so the field evolves continuously instead of scrolling.\n2. The domain warp is `q = vec2(fbm(sp + m*0.20, t), fbm(sp + vec2(2.3,1.1) - m*0.20, t))`, then `n = fbm(sp + q*0.4 + m*0.12, t*0.8) * 0.5 + 0.5`. The `vec2(2.3, 1.1)` offset decorrelates the second channel from the first; without it both return the same number and the warp collapses into a diagonal translation.\n3. Both the sample point and the pointer are multiplied by `aspect` on x before use, so the noise stays isotropic and the cursor glow stays circular on a wide screen.\n4. `shade = smoothstep(0.25, 0.85, n)` crushes the noise into a soft two-tone field, and the final colour is `base + tint * (gain * shade + lift)` where `lift = (pointerY - 0.5) * 0.03` tilts the whole frame slightly as the cursor moves up and down.\n5. The cursor light is `smoothstep(0.85, 0.0, d)` — arguments deliberately reversed to produce a falling ramp, 1 at the cursor and 0 at distance 0.85, without the extra `1.0 -`.\n6. A vignette `smoothstep(1.25, 0.5, distance(uv, 0.5))` multiplies the result rather than subtracting from it, so the cursor glow dims with the corners instead of punching through them.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `scale = 0.7`\n  Multiplier on the sample coordinate. Below 1.0 the noise features are LARGER than the screen, so you get a handful of slow masses. At 3.0 the same shader is television static.\n- `warp = 0.4`\n  How far `q` displaces the final sample. Under ~0.15 the warp is invisible and you are looking at plain fbm; over ~0.9 the field folds back on itself and produces hard creases that read as compression artefacts.\n- `octaves = 2`\n  Textbook fbm uses 4-6. Two is enough because the output goes straight through `smoothstep(0.25, 0.85, n)`, which discards the fine detail anyway. fbm is essentially the whole cost of this shader, so halving the octaves halves the frame time.\n- `lacunarity = 1.93`\n  Frequency step between octaves. Exactly 2.0 makes the two lattices coincide on every other cell boundary and stamps a faint axis-aligned cross into the field; an off-integer step decorrelates them.\n- `amplitude schedule = 0.6 then 0.3`\n  Starting below 1.0 keeps the two-octave sum inside roughly -0.9..0.9, so `n * 0.5 + 0.5` lands in 0..1 without clipping and the smoothstep window sits where the data actually is.\n- `time scale = 0.03`\n  The field advances 0.0005 units per frame at 60 Hz. It has to read as weather, not animation — at 0.3 it becomes a lava lamp and starts competing with the content on top of it.\n- `gain = 0.22`\n  Peak brightness above the base. The reference site sits at 0.08, which keeps the entire image inside the bottom 17% of the value range so white type sits on it perfectly. 0.22 is the standalone default; drop it toward 0.08 the moment you put text on top.\n- `pointer smoothing = 0.02`\n  Fraction of the gap left after one second. The pointer displaces a noise domain, so raw values make the whole field jump between pointer events rather than drift.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DomainWarpedFbm(props: { scale?: number; warp?: number; gain?: number; speed?: number; base?: readonly [number, number, number]; tint?: readonly [number, number, number]; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `scale: number` — default `0.7`\n  Noise frequency. Below 1.0 the features are larger than the screen.\n- `warp: number` — default `0.4`\n  How far the first two fbm samples displace the third.\n- `gain: number` — default `0.22`\n  Peak brightness added above the base colour.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier on the drift, which is already very slow.\n- `base: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.03, 0.032, 0.042]`\n  Base colour, linear 0..1 RGB, straight into the shader.\n- `tint: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.86, 0.9, 1]`\n  Colour of the lit fog and the cursor glow.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Upper bound on device pixel ratio.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TRIANGLE = new Float32Array([-1, -1, 3, -1, -1, 3]);\nconst SCALE = 0.7;\nconst WARP = 0.4;\nconst GAIN = 0.22;\nconst BASE: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.03, 0.032, 0.042];\nconst TINT: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.86, 0.9, 1];\n\ninterface Gpu {\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext;\n  program: WebGLProgram;\n  vao: WebGLVertexArrayObject;\n  buffer: WebGLBuffer;\n  locations: Map<string, WebGLUniformLocation | null>;\n}\n\n/* A local 60-line bootstrap rather than three.js or ogl. This component draws\n   three vertices; a framework would be 100x its own weight in dependency. */\nfunction build(gl: WebGL2RenderingContext, vertSrc: string, fragSrc: string): Gpu | null {\n  const compile = (type: number, src: string): WebGLShader | null => {\n    const shader = gl.createShader(type);\n    if (!shader) return null;\n    gl.shaderSource(shader, src);\n    gl.compileShader(shader);\n    if (gl.getShaderParameter(shader, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) return shader;\n    console.error('[domain-warped-fbm]', gl.getShaderInfoLog(shader));\n    gl.deleteShader(shader);\n    return null;\n  };\n\n  const vert = compile(gl.VERTEX_SHADER, vertSrc);\n  const frag = compile(gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER, fragSrc);\n  const program = gl.createProgram();\n  const vao = gl.createVertexArray();\n  const buffer = gl.createBuffer();\n  if (!vert || !frag || !program || !vao || !buffer) return null;\n\n  gl.attachShader(program, vert);\n  gl.attachShader(program, frag);\n  gl.linkProgram(program);\n  gl.deleteShader(vert);\n  gl.deleteShader(frag);\n  if (!gl.getProgramParameter(program, gl.LINK_STATUS)) {\n    console.error('[domain-warped-fbm]', gl.getProgramInfoLog(program));\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.bindVertexArray(vao);\n  gl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, buffer);\n  gl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, TRIANGLE, gl.STATIC_DRAW);\n  const attrib = gl.getAttribLocation(program, 'a_pos');\n  if (attrib >= 0) {\n    gl.enableVertexAttribArray(attrib);\n    gl.vertexAttribPointer(attrib, 2, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);\n  }\n  gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n\n  return { gl, program, vao, buffer, locations: new Map() };\n}\n\nfunction loc(gpu: Gpu, name: string): WebGLUniformLocation | null {\n  const cached = gpu.locations.get(name);\n  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;\n  const found = gpu.gl.getUniformLocation(gpu.program, name);\n  gpu.locations.set(name, found);\n  return found;\n}\n\nexport function DomainWarpedFbm({\n  scale = SCALE,\n  warp = WARP,\n  gain = GAIN,\n  speed = 1,\n  base = BASE,\n  tint = TINT,\n  maxDpr = 2,\n  className,\n}: DomainWarpedFbmProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n  const gpuRef = useRef<Gpu | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  // Heavy smoothing: the pointer drives a warp offset, and an unsmoothed\n  // pointer makes the whole field snap between frames rather than drift.\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.02 });\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n\n  const paramsRef = useRef({ scale, warp, gain, speed, base, tint });\n  paramsRef.current = { scale, warp, gain, speed, base, tint };\n\n  const drawRef = useRef<(elapsed: number) => void>(() => {});\n  drawRef.current = (elapsed: number) => {\n    const gpu = gpuRef.current;\n    if (!gpu) return;\n    const { gl } = gpu;\n    const params = paramsRef.current;\n    const p = pointer.current;\n\n    gl.useProgram(gpu.program);\n    gl.uniform2f(\n      loc(gpu, 'u_resolution'),\n      gl.drawingBufferWidth,\n      gl.drawingBufferHeight,\n    );\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_time'), elapsed * params.speed);\n    gl.uniform2f(loc(gpu, 'u_pointer'), p.nx, 1 - p.ny);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_scale'), params.scale);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_warp'), params.warp);\n    gl.uniform1f(loc(gpu, 'u_gain'), params.gain);\n    gl.uniform3f(loc(gpu, 'u_base'), params.base[0], params.base[1], params.base[2]);\n    gl.uniform3f(loc(gpu, 'u_tint'), params.tint[0], params.tint[1], params.tint[2]);\n\n    gl.bindVertexArray(gpu.vao);\n    gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLES, 0, 3);\n    gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    /* The canvas is created here, not rendered by React, because the cleanup\n       below hands the context back through `WEBGL_lose_context` — and\n       `getContext` on an element that has been through that returns the SAME\n       dead context, not a new one. Keeping React's element across the cycle\n       therefore made the loss permanent. This is not a dev-only StrictMode\n       concern: the preview host hides components with `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">`,\n       which unmounts effects while keeping the DOM, so scrolling a preview out\n       of view and back is exactly that cycle. */\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.className = 'block h-full w-full';\n\n    const gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', {\n      antialias: false,\n      alpha: false,\n      depth: false,\n      stencil: false,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n    });\n    if (!gl) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const gpu = build(gl, DOMAIN_WARPED_FBM_VERT, DOMAIN_WARPED_FBM_FRAG);\n    if (!gpu) {\n      loseContext(gl);\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    el.append(canvas);\n    canvasRef.current = canvas;\n    gpuRef.current = gpu;\n    setSupported(true);\n\n    // Ignore the loss and the browser keeps handing us a dead context: the\n    // canvas is then blank permanently, with no fallback underneath it. The\n    // `preventDefault` is the part that leaves a restore on the table.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      // Detached as well: the fallback renders as a sibling in normal flow,\n      // so a canvas left in place would cover it.\n      canvas.remove();\n      setSupported(false);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      gpuRef.current = null;\n      canvasRef.current = null;\n      gl.deleteVertexArray(gpu.vao);\n      gl.deleteBuffer(gpu.buffer);\n      gl.deleteProgram(gpu.program);\n      loseContext(gl);\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const canvas = canvasRef.current;\n    const gpu = gpuRef.current;\n    if (!canvas || !gpu || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n\n    const dpr = pixelRatio(maxDpr);\n    const w = Math.max(1, Math.round(width * dpr));\n    const h = Math.max(1, Math.round(height * dpr));\n    if (canvas.width !== w || canvas.height !== h) {\n      canvas.width = w;\n      canvas.height = h;\n    }\n    gpu.gl.viewport(0, 0, w, h);\n    // One frame here covers both the first paint and the reduced-motion path.\n    drawRef.current(reduced ? 12 : 0);\n  }, [width, height, maxDpr, supported, reduced]);\n\n  const running = supported && visible && !reduced && width > 0 && height > 0;\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => drawRef.current(elapsed), running);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Deletes the VAO, the vertex buffer and the program, then `loseContext(gl)`. The rAF subscription and the pointer listener are removed by their hooks.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF loop never subscribes. One frame is drawn at a fixed `u_time` of 12 seconds — far enough into the sequence that the field is fully developed rather than at its symmetric t=0 state — and redrawn on resize. The cursor light does not track.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Aspect-correcting the sample space but not the pointer. The noise looks right and the cursor glow becomes an ellipse that stretches further the wider the window gets — the two must live in the same coordinate system.\n- Using the same offset for both warp channels. `fbm(sp)` and `fbm(sp)` are the same number, so `q` becomes a scalar along the diagonal and the \"warp\" is a plain translation; you get sliding blobs with none of the folding.\n- Feeding `sin()` a large argument inside the hash. `fract(sin(dot(p, ...)))` degrades badly once `p` is in the thousands: the noise develops repeating bands, and it fails on mobile GPUs at scales where the desktop still looks fine. Fold the input with `fract()` first.\n- Running 5 octaves because that is what the tutorial did. The output is immediately crushed by a smoothstep, so octaves 3-5 cost real milliseconds and change nothing you can see.\n- Letting the shader run in a hidden tab. `useVisibility` gates the loop; without it a backgrounded page keeps a GPU-bound fragment shader resident and the laptop fan tells the user about it.\n- Relying on `dispose()`-style cleanup. Raw GL has none — the VAO, buffer and program are deleted explicitly and `loseContext(gl)` hands the context back, without which mounting this component twenty times kills the oldest contexts on the page.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Value noise rather than simplex: after `smoothstep(0.25, 0.85, n)` the two are indistinguishable, and value noise is eight hash calls and seven mixes with no permutation polynomial.\n- Total brightness at the defaults runs roughly 0.03 to 0.30. Set `gain={0.08}` for the original hero behaviour, where the whole field stays under 0.18 and white type needs no scrim.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWatch for ten seconds: the masses should visibly fold and stretch, not just brighten and dim in place. Move the cursor and a faint circular light follows it a beat late while the fog around it shears. Resize from 320px to full width and the noise features stay the same shape rather than stretching horizontally.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dotted-notepad-surface": "# Build: Dotted Notepad Surface\n\nA panel textured with a dot grid drawn by a single tiled radial gradient, masked so the pattern dissolves toward the edges instead of ending on a rectangle. An optional turned-down corner is one hard-stopped linear gradient. Nothing is an image, nothing animates, and the whole surface costs one background layer.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA tiled dot grid that dissolves at the panel edges, with a hard-stopped folded corner.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One `radial-gradient(circle, dot 1.1px, transparent 1.3px)` is tiled by `background-size: 26px 26px`, so `background-size` alone controls the pitch of the grid and the gradient controls the dot. Changing pitch never requires touching the gradient.\n2. The 0.2px between the coloured stop and the transparent stop is the antialiasing ramp. With both stops at the same offset the dot edge quantises to the device pixel grid and the dots render as squares at fractional zoom; wider than about 0.4px and they visibly blur.\n3. `background-position: center` rather than `0 0` keeps the lattice symmetric about the panel as it resizes. Pinned to the top-left, a resize crops the pattern on one side only and the grid appears to slide.\n4. `mask-image` takes two axis gradients — one vertical, one horizontal — each opaque in the middle and transparent for `--kx-pad-fade` at both ends, combined with `mask-composite: intersect`. Intersecting two linear masks fades all four edges while keeping the interior perfectly flat; a single radial mask would instead darken the corners more than the edges and make a rectangular panel read as a lens.\n5. `background-attachment` decides what the tile origin is anchored to, and only matters once the dotted element is itself a scroll container. `scroll` anchors to the element border box so the grid holds still while content slides over it; `local` anchors to the scrolled content so the grid travels with the text and the panel reads as one continuous sheet.\n6. The fold is a single absolutely-positioned square carrying `linear-gradient(225deg, void 0 43%, crease 43% 48%, under 48% 100%)`. Hard stops (a stop pair at the same offset) mean no interpolation, so the boundary is a crease and not a shadow. The 225deg gradient line points down-left, which puts the void band at the top-right corner where the paper is turned back.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pitch = 26px`\n  Tile size, therefore dot spacing. Below ~18px the grid starts to read as a flat tone rather than a texture and interferes with body text; above ~34px the dots read as individual objects on the page and compete with content.\n- `dotSize = 1.1px`\n  Dot radius. At exactly 1px the dot lands on a whole device pixel and hardens; the fractional value is what keeps it soft on 1x displays. Above ~1.6px the surface reads as polka dots rather than paper grain.\n- `antialias gap = 0.2px`\n  Distance from the colour stop to the transparent stop. This is the only softening the dot gets. At 0 the edge quantises and the dots go square at fractional zoom levels; at 0.5px and above the dot loses its centre and the grid looks smudged.\n- `fade = 10%`\n  How far the edge mask reaches in. Below ~6% the dissolve is too abrupt to read as intentional and looks like a clipping bug; above ~18% the usable flat area of a small panel disappears and content near the edge visibly ghosts.\n- `fold stop positions = 43% / 48%`\n  The crease is a 5% sliver. Thinner than ~3% and it drops below a pixel on a small fold and disappears; wider than ~8% and it stops reading as a lit edge and becomes a grey stripe. The 43% start places the crease off-centre so the flap looks lifted rather than symmetrically folded.\n- `shadow = 0 34px 70px -34px`\n  Negative spread equal to the Y offset. It shrinks the shadow box by exactly the amount the offset moved it, so the blur only escapes below the panel. Without the negative spread a 70px blur haloes all four sides and the sheet looks lit from nowhere.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DottedNotepadSurface(props: { children?: ReactNode; pitch?: number; dotSize?: number; fade?: string; scrollable?: boolean; attachment?: 'scroll' | 'local' | 'fixed'; fold?: boolean; radius?: string; className?: string; contentClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Panel content. Rendered above the dot layer.\n- `pitch: number` — default `26`\n  Grid pitch in pixels. Becomes `background-size`.\n- `dotSize: number` — default `1.1`\n  Dot radius in pixels. The transparent stop lands 0.2px further out.\n- `fade: string` — default `'10%'`\n  How far the edge mask reaches in from each side.\n- `scrollable: boolean` — default `false`\n  Makes the dotted layer the scroll container, which is what gives `attachment` meaning.\n- `attachment: 'scroll' | 'local' | 'fixed'` — default `'scroll'`\n  Tile origin. `scroll` holds the grid still under scrolling content; `local` scrolls the grid with it.\n- `fold: boolean` — default `false`\n  Draws the turned-down top-right corner.\n- `radius: string` — default `'20px'`\n  Panel corner radius. The fold inherits it on its own corner.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the panel. Use for sizing and border.\n- `contentClassName: string`\n  Merged onto the content wrapper. Put padding here so text clears the mask.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst NOTEPAD_CSS = `\n.kx-notepad {\n  position: relative;\n  isolation: isolate;\n  border-radius: var(--kx-pad-radius, 20px);\n  overflow: hidden;\n\n  /* Negative spread equal to the Y offset pulls the blur back inside the\n     footprint, so the shadow only escapes below the panel. Without it a\n     70px blur haloes all four sides and the sheet looks lit from nowhere. */\n  box-shadow: 0 34px 70px -34px var(--kx-pad-shadow, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.55));\n}\n\n.kx-notepad-sheet {\n  height: 100%;\n  background-color: var(--kx-pad-bg, var(--color-bg-subtle, Canvas));\n\n  /* One dot per tile. The 0.2px between the colour stop and the transparent\n     stop IS the antialiasing — with the stops coincident the dot edge snaps\n     to the device pixel grid and the dots go square at odd zoom levels. */\n  background-image: radial-gradient(\n    circle,\n    var(--kx-pad-dot, color-mix(in oklab, currentColor 12%, transparent))\n      var(--kx-pad-dot-size, 1.1px),\n    transparent calc(var(--kx-pad-dot-size, 1.1px) + 0.2px)\n  );\n  background-size: var(--kx-pad-pitch, 26px) var(--kx-pad-pitch, 26px);\n\n  /* Centred, not 0 0: the pattern then stays symmetric about the panel as it\n     resizes instead of being pinned to the top-left and cropped on one side. */\n  background-position: center;\n  background-repeat: repeat;\n  background-attachment: var(--kx-pad-attachment, scroll);\n\n  /* Two axis gradients intersected. Mask reads alpha only, so the colour\n     keyword here is arbitrary — 'black' means \"keep\", 'transparent' means\n     \"drop\". A single radial mask would round the corners of the fade and\n     make a rectangular panel look like a lens. */\n  mask-image:\n    linear-gradient(\n      to bottom,\n      transparent 0,\n      black var(--kx-pad-fade, 10%),\n      black calc(100% - var(--kx-pad-fade, 10%)),\n      transparent 100%\n    ),\n    linear-gradient(\n      to right,\n      transparent 0,\n      black var(--kx-pad-fade, 10%),\n      black calc(100% - var(--kx-pad-fade, 10%)),\n      transparent 100%\n    );\n  mask-composite: intersect;\n}\n\n.kx-notepad-sheet[data-scrollable='true'] {\n  overflow-y: auto;\n  overscroll-behavior: contain;\n}\n\n/* The turned corner: one hard-stopped gradient, no blur, no extra element.\n   Band one is the void the fold reveals and must match whatever sits behind\n   the panel; band two is a 5% sliver of highlight along the crease; band\n   three is the underside of the flap. Hard stops ('0 43%', '43% 48%') are\n   what make it read as a crease rather than a soft shadow. */\n.kx-notepad-fold {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0 0 auto auto;\n  width: var(--kx-pad-fold, 52px);\n  height: var(--kx-pad-fold, 52px);\n  border-top-right-radius: var(--kx-pad-radius, 20px);\n  pointer-events: none;\n  background: linear-gradient(\n    225deg,\n    var(--kx-pad-void, var(--color-bg, Canvas)) 0 43%,\n    var(--kx-pad-crease, color-mix(in oklab, currentColor 20%, transparent)) 43% 48%,\n    var(--kx-pad-under, var(--color-surface, Canvas)) 48% 100%\n  );\n}\n\n.kx-notepad-body {\n  position: relative;\n  z-index: 1;\n}\n\n/* Nothing here animates, so reduced motion has nothing to switch off. The\n   surface is identical in both states by construction. */\n`;\n\nexport function DottedNotepadSurface({\n  children,\n  pitch = 26,\n  dotSize = 1.1,\n  fade = '10%',\n  scrollable = false,\n  attachment = 'scroll',\n  fold = false,\n  radius = '20px',\n  className,\n  contentClassName,\n}: DottedNotepadSurfaceProps) {\n  const style: StyleWithVars = {\n    '--kx-pad-pitch': `${pitch}px`,\n    '--kx-pad-dot-size': `${dotSize}px`,\n    '--kx-pad-fade': fade,\n    '--kx-pad-radius': radius,\n    '--kx-pad-attachment': attachment,\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-dotted-notepad-surface\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {NOTEPAD_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <div className={cn('kx-notepad', className)} style={style}>\n        <div className=\"kx-notepad-sheet\" data-scrollable={scrollable ? 'true' : undefined}>\n          <div className={cn('kx-notepad-body', contentClassName)}>{children}</div>\n        </div>\n        {fold ? <span className=\"kx-notepad-fold\" aria-hidden=\"true\" /> : null}\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. No listeners, no timers, no animation frames. The hoisted `<style>` is reference-counted by React and removed when the last instance unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing in this component moves in either state — there is no animation, transition or timer to disable. The surface renders identically with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` set, including the fold and the edge fade.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Putting the mask on the panel and expecting only the dots to fade. A mask applies to everything the element paints, children included, so text within `--kx-pad-fade` of an edge ghosts out. Content padding must exceed the fade inset — that is why the component has a separate content wrapper for padding rather than padding the panel.\n- Reaching for `background-attachment: fixed` to get a parallax grid. The tile origin becomes the viewport, but any ancestor with a `transform`, `filter`, `backdrop-filter` or `will-change` becomes the containing block instead, so the effect silently changes meaning as soon as someone animates a parent. It also repaints the element across its full area on every scroll frame, and iOS Safari has never implemented it usefully.\n- Setting `background-attachment: local` on a panel that does not scroll and expecting a difference. `local` and `scroll` are identical until the element itself has overflow — the value is inert, so the \"fix\" appears to do nothing and gets removed.\n- Coincident gradient stops on the dot (`radial-gradient(circle, c 1.1px, transparent 1.1px)`). It looks correct in a screenshot at 100% zoom and turns into a field of tiny squares on a 1.25x or 1.5x display.\n- Using the `background` shorthand after setting `background-size`. The shorthand resets `background-size` to `auto`, the tile collapses to the gradient default, and the grid becomes one enormous dot.\n- Assuming the fold reads as a hole without matching `--kx-pad-void` to what is actually behind the panel. The first band of the fold gradient is a flat colour pretending to be the page; against a different background it reads as a grey triangle stuck on the corner.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `mask-composite: intersect` needs Safari 15.4+, Chrome 120+ and Firefox 53+. Where it is unsupported only the first mask layer applies, so the grid fades top and bottom but not left and right — a degradation, not a break.\n- The dot layer is a real background rather than a pseudo-element so that `background-attachment` has a scroll container to refer to. A decorative absolutely-positioned overlay could not express the `local` behaviour at all.\n- The CSS ships inside the component as a `<style href precedence>` element; React 19 hoists it into `<head>` and de-duplicates by `href`, so any number of panels contribute one rule set.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe dot grid is crisp at 100% and at 150% browser zoom — individual dots stay round, not square. All four edges dissolve rather than ending on a line, and the interior is perfectly even. With `scrollable` and `attachment=\"local\"`, scrolling the panel moves the dots with the text; with `attachment=\"scroll\"` the dots stay put while the text slides over them. With `fold`, the corner reads as turned paper with a single sharp crease and no blur.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "drag-deadzone-capture": "# Build: Drag with a Dead Zone and Pointer Capture\n\nA deliberate click moves the pointer one to three pixels; a tap on a phone moves five or more. A drag handler with no dead zone calls all of them drags, so the track lurches on every click and the code that suppresses the post-drag click starts suppressing clicks the user meant. This hook captures the pointer on `pointerdown` — so a fast throw that leaves the element keeps delivering moves — and only commits to a drag once the pointer is further than the threshold from the press point. Every one of the four ways a gesture can end funnels through a single exit, because the path you forget is the one that leaves the track welded to a pointer that is no longer there.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCaptures the pointer on press but only commits to a drag past a distance threshold, so a shaky click stays a click.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `pointerdown` records the press point and calls `setPointerCapture(e.pointerId)` immediately, before any distance test. Capture first, commit second: at the moment the threshold is crossed the pointer may already be outside the element, and `setPointerCapture` on a pointer that has since ended throws `NotFoundError`. Inside its own `pointerdown` the pointer is guaranteed active, so it cannot throw there.\n2. `pointermove` computes `distance = Math.hypot(clientX - startX, clientY - startY)` and returns early while it is under the threshold — 8 px for mouse and pen, 16 px for touch, read from `pointerType` on the live pointer rather than from a media query. Until it commits, no callback fires and nothing moves.\n3. At the moment of commitment the delta origin is re-based to the current point (`lastX = clientX`). A consumer that integrates `deltaX` therefore starts from zero at the commit point instead of receiving the entire dead zone as the first frame's delta, which would snap the track 8 px the instant it engaged. `dx` still measures from the true press point, so the distance maths stays honest.\n4. With `axis` set to `x` or `y` the threshold test becomes directional: if the cross axis clears the dead zone first *and* dominates the main axis, the gesture is abandoned as cancelled so the browser can scroll. Combined with `touch-action: pan-y`, a horizontal dragger and vertical page scrolling coexist on a phone.\n5. One `finish(clientX, clientY, cancelled)` handles `pointerup`, `pointercancel`, `lostpointercapture` and the returned `cancel()`. It releases the capture if still held, nulls `pointerId`, and calls `onDragEnd(state, { moved, cancelled })`. The `pointerId === null` guard at the top makes the second arrival for one gesture a no-op — `lostpointercapture` always follows a release, so without it every drag would end twice.\n6. The effect sets `touch-action` (from `axis`) and `user-select: none` on the element and restores the previous inline values on cleanup, and it cancels HTML5 `dragstart` so a press on an image does not turn into a native file drag.\n7. `moved` is the whole output that matters downstream: false means the gesture was a tap and the consumer should treat it as a click; true means a click that arrives next should be swallowed.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `THRESHOLD = 8`\n  Dead-zone radius in pixels for mouse and pen. A deliberate trackpad click slips one to three pixels, and a click while the hand is moving toward the target slips more, so below about 6 the dead zone stops catching real clicks. Above about 14 the beginning of a genuine drag feels glued down — the pointer has visibly moved and the track has not.\n- `TOUCH_THRESHOLD = 16`\n  Dead-zone radius for touch. A finger pad rolls as it presses and the reported point moves with it, typically 5-10 px on a tap; 8 px would classify a large share of taps as drags. Chrome's own touch-slop for scroll arbitration is in the same range, so this roughly matches what the platform already considers a tap.\n- `touch-action (axis 'x') = 'pan-y'`\n  Not `none`. `none` gives the element every gesture, including the vertical swipe that was meant to scroll the page, and a horizontal carousel that eats vertical swipes makes a phone page feel broken. `pan-y` hands vertical panning back to the browser, which then fires `pointercancel` when it claims the gesture — which is exactly why `pointercancel` has to be handled.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useDragDeadzone(ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>, options?: { threshold?: number; touchThreshold?: number; axis?: 'both' | 'x' | 'y'; primaryOnly?: boolean; manageStyles?: boolean; enabled?: boolean; onPress?: (state: DragDeadzoneState) => void; onDragStart?: (state: DragDeadzoneState) => void; onDrag?: (state: DragDeadzoneState) => void; onDragEnd?: (state: DragDeadzoneState, end: { moved: boolean; cancelled: boolean }) => void }): { dragging: RefObject<boolean>; pressed: RefObject<boolean>; distance: RefObject<number>; cancel: () => void }\n```\n\n- `threshold: number` — default `8`\n  Dead-zone radius in pixels for mouse and pen input.\n- `touchThreshold: number` — default `16`\n  Dead-zone radius for touch, which needs roughly double.\n- `axis: 'both' | 'x' | 'y'` — default `'both'`\n  A single axis adds a directional lock and narrows `touch-action` to the other one.\n- `primaryOnly: boolean` — default `true`\n  Ignore secondary touches and any mouse button but the left one.\n- `manageStyles: boolean` — default `true`\n  Set `touch-action` and `user-select` on the element, restoring inline values on cleanup.\n- `enabled: boolean` — default `true`\n  Detach every listener when false. Any live capture is released as it detaches.\n- `onPress: (state: DragDeadzoneState) => void`\n  An accepted `pointerdown`, before any commitment. Kill in-flight settle tweens here.\n- `onDragStart: (state: DragDeadzoneState) => void`\n  The dead zone was cleared. At most once per gesture.\n- `onDrag: (state: DragDeadzoneState) => void`\n  Every move after commitment. `deltaX/deltaY` are re-based to the commit point.\n- `onDragEnd: (state: DragDeadzoneState, end: DragEnd) => void`\n  The single exit. `moved: false` means treat the gesture as a click.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Dead-zone radius for a mouse, in pixels. Below ~6 a normal click's own\n * tremor clears it and every click becomes a two-pixel drag; above ~14 the\n * start of a deliberate drag feels glued down.\n */\nconst THRESHOLD = 8;\n/** Dead-zone radius for touch. A finger rolls further than a mouse slips. */\nconst TOUCH_THRESHOLD = 16;\n\n/**\n * Pointer drag that captures immediately and commits on distance.\n *\n * Deliberately does not swallow the trailing `click` — that is a separate\n * concern with its own edge cases (see `capture-phase-click-swallow`). What\n * this reports is the fact the swallow needs: whether the gesture moved.\n */\nexport function useDragDeadzone(\n  ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>,\n  options: UseDragDeadzoneOptions = {},\n): DragDeadzoneHandle {\n  const {\n    threshold = THRESHOLD,\n    touchThreshold = TOUCH_THRESHOLD,\n    axis = 'both',\n    primaryOnly = true,\n    manageStyles = true,\n    enabled = true,\n  } = options;\n\n  const dragging = useRef(false);\n  const pressed = useRef(false);\n  const distance = useRef(0);\n  const abort = useRef<() => void>(() => {});\n\n  const config = useRef({ threshold, touchThreshold, axis, primaryOnly, options });\n  config.current = { threshold, touchThreshold, axis, primaryOnly, options };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = ref.current;\n    if (!el || !enabled) return;\n\n    /** Non-null exactly while a gesture is live. The guard on every handler. */\n    let pointerId: number | null = null;\n    let pointerType = '';\n    let startX = 0;\n    let startY = 0;\n    /** Origin for `deltaX/deltaY`. Re-based at commitment. */\n    let lastX = 0;\n    let lastY = 0;\n    /** Previous sample, for the path-length integral. */\n    let prevX = 0;\n    let prevY = 0;\n    let travel = 0;\n    let committed = false;\n\n    const previousTouchAction = el.style.touchAction;\n    const previousUserSelect = el.style.userSelect;\n    if (manageStyles) {\n      // Without this, the first touch on a drag surface is arbitrated by the\n      // browser: it starts a pan, fires `pointercancel`, and the drag never\n      // happens. `pan-y` on a horizontal dragger keeps vertical page\n      // scrolling working, which `none` would break.\n      el.style.touchAction = axis === 'x' ? 'pan-y' : axis === 'y' ? 'pan-x' : 'none';\n      el.style.userSelect = 'none';\n    }\n\n    const stateAt = (clientX: number, clientY: number): DragDeadzoneState => {\n      const dx = clientX - startX;\n      const dy = clientY - startY;\n      return {\n        dx,\n        dy,\n        deltaX: clientX - lastX,\n        deltaY: clientY - lastY,\n        clientX,\n        clientY,\n        distance: Math.hypot(dx, dy),\n        travel,\n        pointerId: pointerId ?? -1,\n        pointerType,\n      };\n    };\n\n    /**\n     * The single exit. `pointerup`, `pointercancel`, `lostpointercapture` and\n     * `cancel()` all arrive here, and the `pointerId` guard makes the second\n     * arrival for one gesture a no-op — `lostpointercapture` always follows a\n     * release, so without the guard every drag would end twice.\n     */\n    const finish = (clientX: number, clientY: number, cancelled: boolean) => {\n      if (pointerId === null) return;\n      const state = stateAt(clientX, clientY);\n      const moved = committed;\n\n      if (el.hasPointerCapture(pointerId)) el.releasePointerCapture(pointerId);\n      pointerId = null;\n      committed = false;\n      travel = 0;\n      dragging.current = false;\n      pressed.current = false;\n      distance.current = 0;\n\n      config.current.options.onDragEnd?.(state, { moved, cancelled });\n    };\n\n    const onPointerDown = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const cfg = config.current;\n      // A second finger is not a new gesture. Overwriting `pointerId` here\n      // orphans the first pointer's capture, which is then never released.\n      if (pointerId !== null) return;\n      if (cfg.primaryOnly && !e.isPrimary) return;\n      // Right and middle button, and the second click of a double-click drag.\n      if (e.pointerType === 'mouse' && e.button !== 0) return;\n\n      pointerId = e.pointerId;\n      pointerType = e.pointerType;\n      startX = lastX = prevX = e.clientX;\n      startY = lastY = prevY = e.clientY;\n      travel = 0;\n      committed = false;\n      pressed.current = true;\n      distance.current = 0;\n\n      // Inside its own `pointerdown` the pointer is guaranteed active, so this\n      // cannot throw. Deferring it to the commit is where it can: by then the\n      // pointer may have ended, and `setPointerCapture` throws NotFoundError.\n      el.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);\n      cfg.options.onPress?.(stateAt(e.clientX, e.clientY));\n    };\n\n    const onPointerMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n      const cfg = config.current;\n\n      travel += Math.hypot(e.clientX - prevX, e.clientY - prevY);\n      prevX = e.clientX;\n      prevY = e.clientY;\n\n      const dx = e.clientX - startX;\n      const dy = e.clientY - startY;\n      distance.current = Math.hypot(dx, dy);\n\n      if (!committed) {\n        const limit = pointerType === 'touch' ? cfg.touchThreshold : cfg.threshold;\n\n        if (cfg.axis === 'both') {\n          if (distance.current < limit) return;\n        } else {\n          const main = cfg.axis === 'x' ? dx : dy;\n          const cross = cfg.axis === 'x' ? dy : dx;\n          // Whichever axis clears the dead zone first owns the gesture. A\n          // horizontal dragger that skips this eats every vertical flick that\n          // happens to start on it, and the page will not scroll.\n          if (Math.abs(cross) >= limit && Math.abs(cross) > Math.abs(main)) {\n            finish(e.clientX, e.clientY, true);\n            return;\n          }\n          if (Math.abs(main) < limit) return;\n        }\n\n        committed = true;\n        dragging.current = true;\n        // Re-base the delta origin at the commit point. Without this the first\n        // `onDrag` carries the whole dead zone as its delta, and a consumer\n        // integrating deltas sees the track snap 8 px the instant it commits.\n        lastX = e.clientX;\n        lastY = e.clientY;\n        cfg.options.onDragStart?.(stateAt(e.clientX, e.clientY));\n        return;\n      }\n\n      cfg.options.onDrag?.(stateAt(e.clientX, e.clientY));\n      lastX = e.clientX;\n      lastY = e.clientY;\n    };\n\n    const onPointerUp = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n      finish(e.clientX, e.clientY, false);\n    };\n\n    /** Browser took the gesture: its own pan, a long-press menu, a stylus lift. */\n    const onPointerCancel = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n      finish(e.clientX, e.clientY, true);\n    };\n\n    /**\n     * Capture ended without a release — an OS window switch, a disconnected\n     * device, another element calling `setPointerCapture`. Omitting this is\n     * how a carousel ends up stuck to a pointer that left the building:\n     * `dragging` stays true and no further event will ever clear it.\n     */\n    const onLostCapture = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n      finish(e.clientX, e.clientY, true);\n    };\n\n    /** HTML's own drag-and-drop. Left alone it hijacks any press on an image. */\n    const onNativeDragStart = (e: Event) => e.preventDefault();\n\n    abort.current = () => finish(lastX, lastY, true);\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerup', onPointerUp);\n    el.addEventListener('pointercancel', onPointerCancel);\n    el.addEventListener('lostpointercapture', onLostCapture);\n    el.addEventListener('dragstart', onNativeDragStart);\n\n    return () => {\n      // Release first, while `pointerId` still says which pointer. An element\n      // that unmounts holding a capture leaves the browser routing pointer\n      // events at a detached node, and the pointer stays captured until the\n      // next `pointerdown` anywhere in the document.\n      if (pointerId !== null && el.hasPointerCapture(pointerId)) {\n        el.releasePointerCapture(pointerId);\n      }\n      pointerId = null;\n      committed = false;\n      dragging.current = false;\n      pressed.current = false;\n      distance.current = 0;\n      abort.current = () => {};\n\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerup', onPointerUp);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onPointerCancel);\n      el.removeEventListener('lostpointercapture', onLostCapture);\n      el.removeEventListener('dragstart', onNativeDragStart);\n\n      if (manageStyles) {\n        el.style.touchAction = previousTouchAction;\n        el.style.userSelect = previousUserSelect;\n      }\n    };\n  }, [ref, enabled, manageStyles, axis]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The cleanup releases the pointer capture first, while `pointerId` still records which pointer holds it, then nulls the gesture state, removes all six listeners (`pointerdown`, `pointermove`, `pointerup`, `pointercancel`, `lostpointercapture`, `dragstart`), neuters the `cancel()` closure, and restores the element's previous inline `touch-action` and `user-select`. There is no timer and no rAF. Under React 19 StrictMode the first effect has fully released its capture before the second attaches.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing changes, and that is the correct behaviour rather than an omission. Every position this hook reports is one the user is currently holding under their own finger — direct manipulation, one to one, with no animation of its own to shorten or remove, which is why the reduced-motion guidance explicitly exempts it. Reduced motion belongs to whatever happens after release: the settle, the snap, the momentum. Note that the dead zone is deliberately not widened either; a tremor threshold and a motion preference are different user needs and conflating them would degrade dragging for people who never asked for it.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- No dead zone at all. Every click becomes a two-pixel drag, so the track twitches on each press, and any click suppression built on \"did it drag\" now eats clicks the user intended. This is the failure that makes drag carousels feel untrustworthy.\n- Capturing the pointer at the moment of commitment rather than on press. Two bugs at once: the moves that happened during the dead zone are lost, and if the pointer has already ended, `setPointerCapture` throws `NotFoundError` and the drag silently never starts.\n- No pointer capture. Throw the track quickly and the pointer leaves the element; `pointermove` stops arriving, the track freezes mid-throw with the button still down, and the eventual `pointerup` lands somewhere else entirely, so the gesture never ends.\n- Handling only `pointerup`. `pointercancel` fires whenever the browser claims the gesture for scrolling or a long-press menu — routine on touch — and `lostpointercapture` fires when the capture is dropped without a release. Miss either and `dragging` stays true forever: the next `pointermove` from an unrelated hover keeps driving the track.\n- Not guarding against the second end event. `lostpointercapture` always follows a release, so a naive implementation runs its end logic twice per drag — two snap tweens, two velocity readings, and a click-swallow flag set and then cleared.\n- Ending the drag without re-basing the delta origin at commitment. The first `onDrag` then reports the full dead-zone offset, and the track jumps by the threshold the moment it engages — small, but it is exactly the wrong 8 px because it lands on the frame the user is watching for feedback.\n- Overwriting `pointerId` when a second finger lands. The first pointer's capture is then never released, so the element holds a capture for a pointer that has ended and the next gesture on it behaves unpredictably.\n- Forgetting `touch-action`. On touch the browser arbitrates the gesture, decides it is a pan, and fires `pointercancel` before the dead zone is even cleared. The drag works perfectly on a desktop and does nothing at all on a phone, which is a hard bug to find without a device.\n- Setting `touch-action: none` on a horizontal dragger. The element swallows vertical swipes too, so the page cannot be scrolled by dragging anywhere over the carousel — on a phone that is often most of the screen.\n- Unmounting while the capture is held. The browser keeps routing pointer events to a detached node, and the pointer stays captured until the next `pointerdown` anywhere in the document, so the rest of the page appears to ignore the mouse.\n- Using cumulative path length instead of straight-line distance for the dead zone. A hand tremor that wobbles back and forth over the same three pixels accumulates past any threshold without the pointer ever leaving the press point, so the click is classified as a drag — the tremor case the dead zone existed to catch.\n- Calling `preventDefault()` on `pointerdown` to stop text selection. It also prevents focus, so the element can no longer be reached or activated from the keyboard. Setting `user-select: none` costs nothing and breaks nothing.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pairs with `capture-phase-click-swallow`: this hook decides whether the gesture moved, that one suppresses the resulting click. They are separate because the swallow has its own edge cases — one click and no more, and keyboard activation must pass through — and because plenty of consumers want the classification without wanting to intercept clicks at all.\n- Both `distance` and `travel` are reported. `distance` is what the dead zone tests; `travel` is the path integral, and their ratio is a cheap measure of how straight the gesture was, which is useful if you want to distinguish a flick from a scribble.\n- The returned `cancel()` exists so a host can abort a gesture on Escape, on a route change, or when a modal opens. It runs the same single exit, so the capture is released and `onDragEnd` reports `cancelled: true`.\n- Momentum, snapping and rubber-banding are deliberately absent. This is the input layer; what the position does after release belongs to whatever integrates the deltas.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPress and release without moving: the row does not shift by even a pixel and the readout says the gesture was a tap. Press, wobble a few pixels inside the dead-zone ring, release: still a tap, and the ring never fills. Push past the ring and the row starts following the pointer from where it committed, with no initial jump. Drag fast off the side of the panel and keep going — the row keeps tracking, because the capture holds. Release outside the panel and the gesture ends there. On a phone, swipe horizontally and the row moves; swipe vertically and the page scrolls while the readout reports a cancelled gesture. Right-click on the row: nothing starts. Drag with a second finger already down: the first gesture keeps control. Unmount mid-drag and the rest of the page still responds to the mouse.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "drag-marquee": "# Build: Drag Marquee\n\nOne unwrapped accumulator carries the idle drift, the drag and the throw; the wrap happens once, in the follow tween's `modifiers`, as `mod(x, period) - period`. Because that is a true modulo it is total — a delta of four periods in one frame lands correctly — and because it is the identity for `x` already inside `[-period, 0)` it never shows a seam. Pressing scatters the cards on a single paused timeline that is played forwards and reversed, and releasing hands the row over to a plugin-free inertial throw.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn endless row that drifts, takes a drag and a throw, and scatters its cards while your hand is down.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The row is `N` copies of the children inside a `w-max` flex track. `period` is the measured width of one copy — `getBoundingClientRect().width`, not `offsetWidth`, because the latter rounds and half a pixel of error is half a pixel of gap that only ever shows at the seam. The copy count is `clamp(ceil(hostWidth / period) + 1, 2, 8)`, which is exactly enough that a translation bounded by one period can never expose the end of the track.\n2. Everything that moves the row adds to one number that is never corrected: the drift subtracts `speed * dt`, each `pointermove` adds `dx`, the release adds the throw distance. It walks off to -50000 and nothing cares, because the only consumer of it applies a modulo.\n3. That modulo lives in `gsap.quickTo`'s `modifiers`, which post-processes the value on its way to the target. It cannot feed back: `resetTo` advances the tween's start from its own `s + ratio * c` rather than re-reading the target, so the wrapped value is output-only. Wrapping in `onUpdate` instead makes the next retarget interpolate from a value the tween does not believe in, and the row lurches a whole period.\n4. For `x` in `[-period, 0)`, `mod(x, period) === x + period`, so `mod(x, period) - period === x`. The wrap is therefore the identity across the entire range the row normally occupies and only bites when the accumulator leaves it — the two sides of the boundary paint identical pixels, so there is nothing to snap.\n5. The gap is a trailing `margin-right` on each tile *inside* a copy rather than `gap` on the track. A track-level gap sits between copies too, so the measured copy width would no longer equal the wrap period and the seam would gain one gap of drift every lap.\n6. Release velocity is measured over a rolling ~70 ms window of `(accumulator, time)`, then projected with the `power3.out` identity: `p(t) = 1 - (1 - t)^3` has `p'(0) = 3`, so a tween of duration `D` covering `delta` departs at `3 * delta / D`. Retargeting by `v * D / 3` continues the gesture at exactly the speed the hand left, with the core library and no `InertiaPlugin`.\n7. The press scatter is one paused timeline built once and stored in a ref, `play()` on `pointerdown` and `reverse()` on the release path. Per-tile rotation and offset come from a seeded generator indexed by position *within* a copy, so every copy of a tile scatters identically and the duplication stays invisible while the cards move.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `copies = clamp(ceil(hostWidth / period) + 1, 2, 8)`\n  The `+ 1` is the copy that covers the wrap: with the translation bounded to one period, the window can start one full period into the track and still needs `hostWidth` of content after it. Drop it and the tail of the row shows background for part of every lap. The floor of 2 is the minimum that can hide a seam at all; the ceiling of 8 stops a one-word tile in a wide hero from producing hundreds of nodes.\n- `POWER3_OUT_SLOPE = 3`\n  The initial derivative of `power3.out`, and the divisor in `delta = v * D / 3`. Exact, not tuned: change the follow tween's ease and this changes with it — `power2` needs 2, `power4` needs 4. Leave it at 3 against a different ease and every throw is off by a constant factor.\n- `smoothing = 0.5`\n  Seconds the row takes to catch up with the pointer, and the `D` in the throw projection. Under ~0.2 s the row is welded to the finger and the throw is over before it registers; over ~0.8 s the lag between hand and content is large enough to read as dropped frames rather than as weight.\n- `speed = 60`\n  Idle drift in px/s. Below ~20 the row looks frozen at a glance and the affordance is lost; above ~120 the text is unreadable at rest, which defeats the point of putting words in it. At 0 the ticker is never registered at all, so a parked marquee costs nothing per frame.\n- `VELOCITY_WINDOW = 0.07`\n  Seconds of gesture history the release speed is averaged over. Shorter is effectively the last delta, which is noisy at 120 Hz and gives visibly different throws for identical gestures; much longer averages away a deliberate slow-down before release, so the row flies off when the user meant to place it.\n- `MAX_VELOCITY = 4000`\n  px/s the measured speed is clamped to. A coalesced pointer batch, a tab switch mid-drag or a synthetic event can report tens of thousands; without the clamp one bad sample throws the row several periods, and the wrap makes the result look random rather than fast.\n- `MAX_THROW = 1200`\n  Pixels one throw may carry. Roughly a screen and a half — far enough that a hard flick feels unbounded, close enough that the content the user was looking at is still findable when it stops.\n- `DRAG_THRESHOLD = 5`\n  Pixels of travel before a press becomes a drag. Below ~3 the tremor in a normal tap counts as a drag and every tap on a card is swallowed; above ~10 a small deliberate nudge does nothing at all.\n- `scatter / spin = 6 (percent) / 10 (deg)`\n  Half-ranges for the per-tile offset and rotation on press. The offset is a percentage of each tile's own size, so mixed tile widths scatter proportionally instead of the small ones flying off. Past ~15% and ~25° the tiles overlap their neighbours and the row stops reading as a row; below ~3% and ~5° the press produces no visible acknowledgement.\n- `ease: back.inOut(3) = 3`\n  The overshoot of the scatter, applied at both ends because the same timeline is played and reversed. It is what makes the cards spring apart and spring back rather than easing politely; at 1 the overshoot is invisible, past ~5 the return visibly overshoots the resting layout and the row wobbles after release.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DragMarquee(props: { children: ReactNode; speed?: number; gap?: number; smoothing?: number; scatter?: number; spin?: number; pressScale?: number; scatterDuration?: number; seed?: number | string; className?: string; tileClassName?: string; 'aria-label'?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One tile per child. The whole set is duplicated as many times as the width needs.\n- `speed: number` — default `60`\n  Idle drift in px/s. Positive drifts left; 0 parks the row and skips the ticker entirely.\n- `gap: number` — default `24`\n  Pixels between tiles, applied inside a copy so the spacing also spans the seam.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.5`\n  Seconds the row takes to catch up with the pointer. Doubles as the throw duration.\n- `scatter: number` — default `6`\n  Scatter distance on press, as a percentage of each tile's own size.\n- `spin: number` — default `10`\n  Half-range of the per-tile rotation on press, in degrees.\n- `pressScale: number` — default `0.95`\n  Tile scale while the pointer is down.\n- `scatterDuration: number` — default `0.5`\n  Seconds for the scatter out, and the same again back.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'drag-marquee'`\n  Seeds the scatter. The same seed gives the same arrangement on server and client.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the drag surface.\n- `tileClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every tile. Size the items here.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Marquee'`\n  Label for the focusable row, which also takes left and right arrow keys.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** The only place the track's transform is written. */\nconst paint = useCallback(() => {\n  const track = trackRef.current;\n  if (!track) return;\n  track.style.transform = `translate3d(${proxyRef.current.x.toFixed(2)}px, 0px, 0px)`;\n}, []);\n\n/**\n * Move the accumulator to `value`. With motion allowed this retargets the\n * follow tween — `quickTo` reuses one tween and one `PropTween` rather than\n * allocating a tween per frame — and the wrap happens in its modifier. With\n * reduced motion there is no tween, so the wrap is applied here instead.\n */\nconst push = useCallback(\n  (value: number) => {\n    rawRef.current = value;\n    const xTo = xToRef.current;\n    if (xTo) {\n      xTo(value);\n      return;\n    }\n    const period = periodRef.current;\n    proxyRef.current.x = period < 1 ? 0 : mod(value, period) - period;\n    paint();\n  },\n  [paint],\n);\n\n/**\n * One follow tween for the life of the component. The wrap lives in\n * `modifiers`, which post-processes the value on its way to the target and\n * never feeds back: `resetTo` advances the tween's start from its own\n * `s + ratio * c`, not by re-reading `proxy.x`. Put the wrap anywhere else\n * — in `onUpdate`, say — and the next retarget interpolates from a value the\n * tween does not believe in, and the row lurches a full period.\n */\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const proxy = proxyRef.current;\n  if (reduced) {\n    xToRef.current = null;\n    return;\n  }\n\n  const xTo = gsap.quickTo(proxy, 'x', {\n    duration: smoothing,\n    ease: 'power3',\n    modifiers: {\n      // Reads the ref, so a resize changes the period without rebuilding\n      // the tween — rebuilding mid-throw would restart the ease from rest.\n      x: (value: number) => {\n        const period = periodRef.current;\n        return period < 1 ? 0 : mod(value, period) - period;\n      },\n    },\n    onUpdate: paint,\n  });\n  xToRef.current = xTo;\n\n  return () => {\n    xToRef.current = null;\n    gsap.killTweensOf(proxy);\n  };\n}, [reduced, smoothing, paint]);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const host = hostRef.current;\n  if (!host) return;\n\n  let pointerId: number | null = null;\n  let lastX = 0;\n  let pressX = 0;\n  let pressY = 0;\n  let moved = false;\n  let swallowClick = false;\n  let swallowTimer: number | null = null;\n\n  // The gate expires. A throw released over a gap in the row, or one the\n  // browser cancels, produces no click at all — and a gate left standing\n  // eats the visitor's next real click, however long afterwards it lands.\n  const disarmSwallow = () => {\n    swallowClick = false;\n    if (swallowTimer !== null) {\n      window.clearTimeout(swallowTimer);\n      swallowTimer = null;\n    }\n  };\n  /** Rolling window: the accumulator and clock at the start of the window. */\n  let sampleRaw = 0;\n  let sampleTime = 0;\n\n  const now = () => performance.now() / 1000;\n\n  const onPointerDown = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n    // One pointer owns the row. A second finger arriving mid-throw would\n    // otherwise reset the origin and the row would jump.\n    if (pointerId !== null || !e.isPrimary) return;\n    if (e.pointerType === 'mouse' && e.button !== 0) return;\n\n    // A new press means the last gesture is finished and whatever click it\n    // was owed never arrived.\n    disarmSwallow();\n    pointerId = e.pointerId;\n    lastX = e.clientX;\n    pressX = e.clientX;\n    pressY = e.clientY;\n    moved = false;\n    sampleRaw = rawRef.current;\n    sampleTime = now();\n    pressedRef.current = true;\n\n    // Capture on press, not once the threshold is crossed: by then the\n    // pointer may already have left the element and `pointermove` would have\n    // stopped arriving.\n    host.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);\n    // Scatter on press, like a physical stack of cards being pinned — the\n    // press, not the drag, is what the eye reads as \"I have hold of this\".\n    scatterRef.current?.play();\n  };\n\n  const onPointerMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n    if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n\n    const dx = e.clientX - lastX;\n    lastX = e.clientX;\n\n    if (!moved) {\n      if (Math.hypot(e.clientX - pressX, e.clientY - pressY) < DRAG_THRESHOLD) return;\n      moved = true;\n    }\n\n    push(rawRef.current + dx);\n\n    // Retire the velocity window once it is full. Measuring over a window\n    // rather than off the last delta is what stops \"drag, pause, lift\" from\n    // throwing the row: with no movement there are no events, the window\n    // never advances, and the measured speed decays toward zero on its own.\n    const t = now();\n    if (t - sampleTime > VELOCITY_WINDOW) {\n      sampleRaw = rawRef.current;\n      sampleTime = t;\n    }\n  };\n\n  /**\n   * Idempotent. Called from `pointerup`, `pointercancel` and\n   * `lostpointercapture`, all three of which can be the last event a gesture\n   * produces, and at least one of which always arrives — including when the\n   * pointer is released outside the window or the OS takes it away for a\n   * gesture of its own. Guarding on `pointerId` makes the extra calls no-ops\n   * instead of a double throw, and it is what stops a drag that ended\n   * off-screen from leaving the row stuck mid-throw with the cards still\n   * scattered.\n   */\n  const finish = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n    if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n    const id = pointerId;\n    pointerId = null;\n    pressedRef.current = false;\n\n    const elapsed = now() - sampleTime;\n    const velocity =\n      moved && elapsed > 0\n        ? clamp((rawRef.current - sampleRaw) / elapsed, -MAX_VELOCITY, MAX_VELOCITY)\n        : 0;\n\n    disarmSwallow();\n    if (moved) {\n      swallowClick = true;\n      swallowTimer = window.setTimeout(disarmSwallow, SWALLOW_TIMEOUT_MS);\n    }\n    moved = false;\n\n    if (host.hasPointerCapture(id)) host.releasePointerCapture(id);\n    scatterRef.current?.reverse();\n\n    if (velocity !== 0) {\n      // v·D/3 is the distance a `power3.out` of duration D covers when it\n      // departs at v. Retargeting by exactly that hands the row over from\n      // the finger without a change of speed.\n      push(\n        rawRef.current +\n          clamp((velocity * smoothing) / POWER3_OUT_SLOPE, -MAX_THROW, MAX_THROW),\n      );\n    }\n  };\n\n  // Capture phase, ahead of any child's own handler. A drag ends with a\n  // `click` on whatever happens to be under the pointer, so without this the\n  // gesture that scrolls the row also activates a card.\n  const onClick = (e: MouseEvent) => {\n    if (!swallowClick) return;\n\n    // Enter or Space on a focused card dispatches a click with `detail === 0`\n    // and no pointer sequence behind it. That is not the tail of a throw, and\n    // swallowing it is what makes the row keyboard-inoperable.\n    if (e.detail === 0) return;\n\n    disarmSwallow();\n    e.preventDefault();\n    e.stopPropagation();\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {\n    const direction = e.key === 'ArrowRight' ? 1 : e.key === 'ArrowLeft' ? -1 : 0;\n    if (direction === 0) return;\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const step = perCopy > 0 ? periodRef.current / perCopy : periodRef.current;\n    push(rawRef.current + direction * step);\n  };\n\n  host.addEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n  host.addEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n  host.addEventListener('pointerup', finish);\n  host.addEventListener('pointercancel', finish);\n  host.addEventListener('lostpointercapture', finish);\n  host.addEventListener('click', onClick, true);\n  host.addEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);\n\n  return () => {\n    host.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n    host.removeEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n    host.removeEventListener('pointerup', finish);\n    host.removeEventListener('pointercancel', finish);\n    host.removeEventListener('lostpointercapture', finish);\n    host.removeEventListener('click', onClick, true);\n    host.removeEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);\n    // Or the timer outlives the effect and a remount starts life armed.\n    disarmSwallow();\n    if (pointerId !== null && host.hasPointerCapture(pointerId)) {\n      host.releasePointerCapture(pointerId);\n    }\n    pointerId = null;\n    pressedRef.current = false;\n  };\n}, [push, smoothing, perCopy]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes `pointerdown`, `pointermove`, `pointerup`, `pointercancel`, `lostpointercapture`, the capture-phase `click` and `keydown`; releases pointer capture if a gesture is still live at unmount; removes the drift callback from `gsap.ticker`; kills the tweens on the proxy object so an in-flight throw cannot call `onUpdate` against a detached track; and reverts the `gsap.context` holding the scatter timeline, which also restores the tiles' inline transforms. Both shared resize observers and the shared intersection observer unregister.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Dragging is unchanged — it is the user's own motion, and tracking it is the component. Everything the component would have animated by itself is dropped: the idle drift is never registered on the ticker, the follow tween is never created (so the row is written straight to the wrapped position, one-to-one with the pointer, and a release does not throw), and the scatter timeline is not built, so pressing moves nothing but the row. Arrow keys still step one tile, instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Correcting the offset with `if (x < -period) x += period` instead of a modulo. It only ever undoes one period, so a fast flick, a coalesced pointer batch, or a tab that was backgrounded for a second leaves the row several periods out and it jumps to the wrong tile. `mod` is total: any magnitude, one step, right answer.\n- Applying the wrap in `onUpdate` rather than in `modifiers`. The tween then interpolates from a start value it computed internally toward a target the wrap has already moved, so the next retarget travels a whole extra period. `modifiers` is post-processing on the way out and never feeds back into the tween's own bookkeeping.\n- Putting the gap on the track with `gap` or `space-x`. It lands between copies as well as between tiles, so the measured copy width no longer equals the wrap period; the seam gains one gap per lap and slowly slides out of register. The gap belongs inside the copy, as a trailing margin on every tile.\n- Measuring the period with `offsetWidth`. It rounds to whole pixels, and a sub-pixel error in the period is a permanent sub-pixel misalignment at the seam that shows as a hairline flicker once per lap.\n- Hard-coding two copies. Two is enough only while one copy is at least as wide as the container; a short tile set in a wide hero runs out of content mid-lap and the row ends in empty space. The count has to come from the measured width.\n- Randomising the scatter per DOM node. The duplicates are the whole trick, and the instant the cards move with different rotations the eye reads them as different content — the seam becomes obvious. Index the seed by position within a copy, so copy 3 tile 5 matches copy 1 tile 5.\n- Rebuilding the scatter timeline per press, or putting the pressed flag in the `useGsap` deps. `revert()` strips the inline transforms GSAP wrote, so the reverse starts from the untouched stylesheet values and animates nowhere — the cards stay scattered. One paused timeline, `play()` and `reverse()` from the pointer handlers.\n- Leaving the idle drift running during a drag. The row creeps out from under the finger, which is precisely what one-to-one tracking exists to prevent; over a slow careful drag the divergence is several tiles.\n- Calling `setPointerCapture` only after the drag threshold is crossed. A fast gesture has already left the element by then, `pointermove` stops arriving, and the row sticks at the offset it had when the pointer crossed the boundary.\n- Handling only `pointerup`. Release outside the window, or let the OS claim the gesture, and no `pointerup` arrives: the row stays captured, the drift never resumes, and the cards stay scattered mid-throw. `pointercancel` and `lostpointercapture` route to the same idempotent finish, guarded on the stored `pointerId` so the duplicate is a no-op rather than a second throw.\n- Letting the click through after a drag. The gesture ends with a `click` on whichever card is under the pointer, so dragging the row also opens a project. It is swallowed in the capture phase, once, and only when the gesture actually moved.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The follow tween is retargeted, not recreated: `gsap.quickTo` keeps one tween and one `PropTween` alive and calls `resetTo` on it, so a marquee dragged for ten seconds allocates nothing per frame.\n- The drift is registered on `gsap.ticker` rather than a second rAF loop, so the target and the tween that chases it advance in the same tick. On separate loops the row is one frame stale every other frame, which reads as a faint shimmer at speed.\n- Because the drift keeps retargeting the same tween after release, the throw decays exponentially with a time constant of `smoothing / 3` rather than tracing a literal `power3` curve. The departure speed is still exactly the hand's, which is the part the eye judges.\n- Only the first copy is exposed to assistive technology; the rest carry `aria-hidden`, so a screen reader reads the list once rather than four times.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe row drifts on its own. Grab it and it follows your hand exactly, with no creep in the drift direction, and the cards tilt and shrink apart the instant you press — then settle back when you let go. Flick and release: it coasts on at the speed you left, slows, and rejoins the drift with no visible seam or jump. Drag several screens' worth in one fast swipe and the tiles stay in order — nothing teleports. Watch one tile go off the left edge and it reappears on the right at the same spacing as every other gap. Release the pointer outside the browser window and the row keeps drifting instead of freezing scattered. Release over a card and no click reaches it; tap without moving and it does. Resize the container narrower and wider — the seam never appears. Scroll it out of view and the drift stops until it is back.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "drag-throw-scroll": "# Build: Drag-Throw Scroll\n\nA third input modality bolted onto a scroll position that already has two. Wheel, pointer drag and arrow keys all write to one place, so they cannot disagree — press stops whatever was coasting exactly where it looks, the drag maps 1:1 from the press point, and release converts the last 100 ms of hand movement into a decaying throw. Pointed at the document it drives the shared Lenis instance; pointed at a box it drives `scrollTop` through the same physics.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nGrab the page and pull it; let go mid-move and it coasts on, decaying, as far as the flick earned.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: lenis@^1.3.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. On `pointerdown` the surface calls `setPointerCapture` and records `startY` plus `startScroll = lenis.scroll` — the animated position, not `targetScroll`, so grabbing a coasting page stops the content where the eye sees it rather than where the throw was headed.\n2. Every `pointermove` recomputes the scroll target absolutely: `clamp(startScroll - (clientY - startY) * gain, 0, limit)`. Gain is `multiplier` for mouse and pen and hard-wired to 1 for touch, because a finger is physically on the content and any other value slides the content out from under it.\n3. Positions are pushed into a 5-entry ring of `{ y, timeStamp }`. On release, velocity is `(newest.y - oldest.y) / (newest.t - oldest.t) * 1000` px/s using only samples inside a 100 ms window, so the number describes the hand rather than the event rate.\n4. The throw target is `target + (-velocity * gain) * throwSeconds`, clamped to the scroll limit. Below `MIN_THROW_SPEED` nothing is issued at all, so a careful release parks instead of drifting.\n5. The decay is one curve expressed twice: `lenis.scrollTo(y, { lerp: 0.05 })` for the page, and `damp(scrollTop, y, perFrameToSmoothing(0.05), dt)` on the shared rAF for a box scroller, which stops writing once it is within 0.5 px of target.\n6. `ArrowUp` / `ArrowDown` on the focused surface step by `keyStep` px through `scrollTo(y, { duration: 0.2 })`, so keyboard, wheel and hand all mutate the same single position.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `multiplier = 2`\n  Scroll px per mouse px. At 1 a long page needs a grab per viewport, because the cursor runs out of window; above ~3 the content outruns the hand and the gesture stops reading as direct manipulation. Touch ignores this entirely and uses 1.\n- `throwSeconds = 0.65`\n  Coast distance is velocity × this, so it is the time constant of the flick. Under ~0.3 a hard flick barely outruns the release and the throw is invisible; over ~1 a flick crosses the whole document and lands somewhere the user did not aim.\n- `THROW_LERP = 0.05`\n  Per-frame fraction of the remaining gap, i.e. a long slow settle — the point of a throw is that it decays visibly. At 0.2 it arrives in a few frames and reads as a jump; `perFrameToSmoothing` converts the same number for the `damp` path so the two sinks decay identically.\n- `SAMPLE_WINDOW_MS = 100`\n  Only samples newer than this count toward the release velocity. Without the window, a drag that travels fast and then stops dead for a second still throws, because the fast samples are still in the buffer — the page launches from a hand that was holding still.\n- `SAMPLE_COUNT = 5`\n  Ring length. One sample is pure jitter, and more than about five spans long enough that the window filter is doing all the work anyway; five covers roughly 80 ms at 60 Hz, which is one flick.\n- `MIN_THROW_SPEED = 120 px/s`\n  Below this no throw is issued. At 0.65 s of coast, 120 px/s is a 78 px slide — around the point where a deliberate stop stops feeling deliberate. Lower and the content creeps after every release.\n- `SETTLE_EPSILON = 0.5 px`\n  An exponential never actually arrives. Without a floor the box sink writes a sub-pixel `scrollTop` every frame forever, so the rAF subscription never idles and the scroll event never stops firing.\n- `KEY_STEP_SECONDS = 0.2`\n  Arrow-key travel time. Instant is disorienting because nothing connects the two positions; beyond ~0.35 s holding the key queues steps faster than they complete and the page falls behind the keyboard.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DragThrowScroll(props: { children?: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; multiplier?: number; throwSeconds?: number; keyStep?: number; enabled?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The draggable content. The surface is `position: relative` and nothing else.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Box to scroll. Omit to drive the document through the shared Lenis instance.\n- `multiplier: number` — default `2`\n  Scroll px per pointer px for mouse and pen. Touch is always 1.\n- `throwSeconds: number` — default `0.65`\n  Time constant of the release throw; distance is velocity × this.\n- `keyStep: number` — default `100`\n  Arrow-key step in px while the surface has focus.\n- `enabled: boolean` — default `true`\n  False removes every listener and restores native scrolling.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the drag surface.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Lenis's per-frame lerp for the throw, and the same curve expressed for\n * `damp` so a box scroller decays identically to the page. One number, two\n * sinks — tuning it in one place cannot desync them.\n */\nconst THROW_LERP = 0.05;\nconst THROW_SMOOTHING = perFrameToSmoothing(THROW_LERP);\n\nconst SAMPLE_COUNT = 5;\nconst SAMPLE_WINDOW_MS = 100;\nconst MIN_THROW_SPEED = 120;\nconst SETTLE_EPSILON = 0.5;\nconst KEY_STEP_SECONDS = 0.2;\n\n/** Presses on real controls belong to those controls, not to the drag. */\nconst NO_DRAG = 'a,button,input,select,textarea,label,[data-no-drag]';\n\nfunction scrollBoxOf(scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>): Element | null {\n  if (scroller) return scroller.current;\n  return typeof document === 'undefined' ? null : document.scrollingElement;\n}\n\nexport function DragThrowScroll({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  multiplier = 2,\n  throwSeconds = 0.65,\n  keyStep = 100,\n  enabled = true,\n  className,\n}: DragThrowScrollProps) {\n  const surface = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  /* Lenis owns the document and nothing else. A component pointed at its own\n     box must not build one — and under reduced motion nobody wants smoothing\n     at all, so the page sink degrades to direct `scrollTop` writes. The hook\n     is refcounted: several of these on one page share the single instance\n     rather than stacking `wheel` handlers that each call `preventDefault`. */\n  const viaLenis = enabled && !reduced && scroller === undefined;\n  const lenis = useLenis({ enabled: viaLenis });\n\n  /** Coast target for the box sink. `null` means nothing is in flight. */\n  const glide = useRef<number | null>(null);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const to = glide.current;\n      const box = scrollBoxOf(scroller);\n      if (to === null || !box) return;\n      const next = damp(box.scrollTop, to, THROW_SMOOTHING, dt);\n      // Snap and stop. An exponential never arrives, so without a floor this\n      // writes a sub-pixel delta every frame forever and the loop never idles.\n      if (Math.abs(to - next) < SETTLE_EPSILON) {\n        glide.current = null;\n        box.scrollTop = to;\n        return;\n      }\n      box.scrollTop = next;\n    },\n    // Only the box sink needs a loop: Lenis animates its own scrollTo, and\n    // reduced motion has nothing to animate.\n    enabled && !reduced && scroller !== undefined,\n  );\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = surface.current;\n    if (!el || !enabled) return;\n\n    let pointerId: number | null = null;\n    let startY = 0;\n    let startScroll = 0;\n    let target = 0;\n    let gain = 1;\n    const samples: Sample[] = [];\n\n    /* -- the sink: Lenis when it exists, the raw box otherwise -------------- */\n\n    const read = (): number => {\n      const l = lenis.current;\n      // `scroll`, not `targetScroll`. This is read on press, and grabbing a\n      // coasting page has to stop it where the content LOOKS, not where it was\n      // headed — otherwise the first pixel of the drag jumps forward by\n      // whatever the throw had left to run.\n      if (l) return l.scroll;\n      return scrollBoxOf(scroller)?.scrollTop ?? 0;\n    };\n\n    const limitOf = (): number => {\n      const l = lenis.current;\n      if (l) return l.limit;\n      const box = scrollBoxOf(scroller);\n      return box ? Math.max(0, box.scrollHeight - box.clientHeight) : 0;\n    };\n\n    /** Direct manipulation: the content is under a finger, so no smoothing. */\n    const jump = (y: number) => {\n      glide.current = null;\n      const l = lenis.current;\n      if (l) {\n        l.scrollTo(y, { immediate: true });\n        return;\n      }\n      const box = scrollBoxOf(scroller);\n      if (box) box.scrollTop = y;\n    };\n\n    /** Release: an exponential decay toward the thrown target. */\n    const coast = (y: number) => {\n      const l = lenis.current;\n      if (l) {\n        l.scrollTo(y, { lerp: THROW_LERP });\n        return;\n      }\n      glide.current = y;\n    };\n\n    const step = (y: number) => {\n      const l = lenis.current;\n      if (l) {\n        l.scrollTo(y, { duration: KEY_STEP_SECONDS });\n        return;\n      }\n      glide.current = null;\n      scrollBoxOf(scroller)?.scrollTo({ top: y, behavior: reduced ? 'auto' : 'smooth' });\n    };\n\n    /* -- velocity ---------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    /**\n     * Pointer velocity in px/s, measured from the oldest sample still inside\n     * the window to the newest. Time-based rather than per-event, so the\n     * number describes the hand rather than the display.\n     */\n    const velocityAt = (now: number): number => {\n      let oldest: Sample | undefined;\n      for (const sample of samples) {\n        if (now - sample.t <= SAMPLE_WINDOW_MS) {\n          oldest = sample;\n          break;\n        }\n      }\n      const newest = samples[samples.length - 1];\n      if (!oldest || !newest) return 0;\n      const span = newest.t - oldest.t;\n      if (span <= 0) return 0;\n      return ((newest.y - oldest.y) / span) * 1000;\n    };\n\n    /* -- pointer lifecycle ------------------------------------------------- */\n\n    const onPointerDown = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (pointerId !== null || !e.isPrimary) return;\n      if (e.pointerType === 'mouse' && e.button !== 0) return;\n      if (e.target instanceof Element && e.target.closest(NO_DRAG)) return;\n\n      pointerId = e.pointerId;\n      // A finger is physically on the content: any gain but 1 slides the\n      // content out from under it. A mouse is an indirect device with a\n      // window-height of travel available, so it gets the multiplier.\n      gain = e.pointerType === 'touch' ? 1 : multiplier;\n      startY = e.clientY;\n      startScroll = read();\n      target = startScroll;\n      jump(startScroll);\n      samples.length = 0;\n      samples.push({ y: e.clientY, t: e.timeStamp });\n      // Captured on press, not on some later threshold: by the time a\n      // threshold would trip, the pointer may already be off the element.\n      el.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);\n      el.dataset.dragging = 'true';\n    };\n\n    const onPointerMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (e.pointerId !== pointerId) return;\n      // Absolute, from the press point. Accumulating per-event deltas onto the\n      // live scroll position loses whatever the sink has not applied yet, and\n      // the content drifts behind the hand over a long drag.\n      target = clamp(startScroll - (e.clientY - startY) * gain, 0, limitOf());\n      jump(target);\n      samples.push({ y: e.clientY, t: e.timeStamp });\n      if (samples.length > SAMPLE_COUNT) samples.shift();\n    };\n\n    const release = (e: PointerEvent, thrown: boolean) => {\n      if (e.pointerId !== pointerId) return;\n      pointerId = null;\n      el.dataset.dragging = 'false';\n      if (el.hasPointerCapture(e.pointerId)) el.releasePointerCapture(e.pointerId);\n\n      if (!thrown || reduced) {\n        samples.length = 0;\n        return;\n      }\n      samples.push({ y: e.clientY, t: e.timeStamp });\n      const velocity = velocityAt(e.timeStamp);\n      samples.length = 0;\n      const scrollVelocity = -velocity * gain;\n      if (Math.abs(scrollVelocity) < MIN_THROW_SPEED) return;\n      coast(clamp(target + scrollVelocity * throwSeconds, 0, limitOf()));\n    };\n\n    const onPointerUp = (e: PointerEvent) => release(e, true);\n    // Both of these are aborts, so neither throws. `pointercancel` is the OS\n    // taking the gesture; `lostpointercapture` is the catch-all — capture\n    // revoked with no `pointerup` at all, which is the one path that would\n    // otherwise leave the surface stuck believing a finger is still down. It\n    // also fires immediately after a normal release, where the `pointerId`\n    // guard above has already made it a no-op.\n    const onPointerCancel = (e: PointerEvent) => release(e, false);\n    const onLostCapture = (e: PointerEvent) => release(e, false);\n\n    // Native HTML5 drag on any image or link inside would hijack the gesture\n    // and hand back a ghost image instead of a scroll.\n    const onDragStart = (e: Event) => e.preventDefault();\n\n    const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {\n      const dir = e.key === 'ArrowDown' ? 1 : e.key === 'ArrowUp' ? -1 : 0;\n      if (dir === 0) return;\n      e.preventDefault();\n      step(clamp(read() + dir * keyStep, 0, limitOf()));\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerup', onPointerUp);\n    el.addEventListener('pointercancel', onPointerCancel);\n    el.addEventListener('lostpointercapture', onLostCapture);\n    el.addEventListener('dragstart', onDragStart);\n    el.addEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerup', onPointerUp);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onPointerCancel);\n      el.removeEventListener('lostpointercapture', onLostCapture);\n      el.removeEventListener('dragstart', onDragStart);\n      el.removeEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);\n      if (pointerId !== null && el.hasPointerCapture(pointerId)) {\n        el.releasePointerCapture(pointerId);\n      }\n      el.dataset.dragging = 'false';\n      glide.current = null;\n    };\n  }, [scroller, lenis, multiplier, throwSeconds, keyStep, enabled, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** All seven listeners are removed, any pointer still captured is released, the coast target is cleared so the shared rAF idles, and `data-dragging` is reset. The Lenis instance is refcounted by `useLenis`: the last consumer to unmount destroys it, earlier ones only detach.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Dragging still works and is still 1:1 — pulling content with a finger is direct manipulation, not motion someone asked to be spared. What is removed is everything that keeps moving on its own: no Lenis instance is built, so the page scrolls natively, release stops exactly where the hand stopped, and arrow keys jump rather than travel.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Constructing a second Lenis instance instead of sharing one. Both bind `wheel`, both call `preventDefault`, and the page scrolls at exactly double speed — which looks like a badly tuned multiplier and gets \"fixed\" by halving the multiplier instead.\n- Measuring velocity as the mean of per-event deltas. That is px per event, not px per second: the identical physical flick throws half as far on a 120 Hz display, because each event covers half the distance. Divide by real elapsed time.\n- Not expiring old samples. Drag fast, hold still for a second, release — the buffer still holds the fast samples and the page launches from a stationary hand.\n- Accumulating `scrollTo(current + delta)` per move. `current` is the animated position, which lags the target, so every event throws away the part the sink has not applied yet and the content drifts behind the hand over a long drag. Track the press point and compute the target absolutely.\n- Omitting `setPointerCapture`. Drag past the edge of the surface and `pointermove` stops arriving, so the content freezes mid-gesture and then teleports when the pointer comes back.\n- Treating `pointerup` as the only terminator. Capture can be revoked with no `pointerup` — the OS claims the gesture, the element is detached, another finger lands — and the surface is left believing a finger is still down, ignoring every later press. `pointercancel` and `lostpointercapture` both have to end the drag.\n- Leaving `touch-action` at its default. The compositor claims the touch gesture, `pointermove` stops, and the drag dies in a `pointercancel` on the first frame while the page scrolls natively underneath.\n- Forgetting that a host stylesheet with `scroll-behavior: smooth` fights a direct `scrollTop` write on the box sink, which shows up as a rubbery lag under the finger. Set `scroll-behavior: auto` on the scroll container.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The surface takes `tabIndex={0}` and binds `keydown` on itself rather than on `window`, so arrow keys still belong to a search field elsewhere on the page.\n- Presses that land on `a, button, input, select, textarea, label` or anything marked `[data-no-drag]` are left alone, so a link inside the draggable area is still a link.\n- `dragstart` is prevented on the surface: native HTML5 drag on an inner image or link would otherwise hand back a ghost image instead of a scroll.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPress and pull upward: the content follows the pointer and stops the moment the pointer does. Flick and release: it coasts on and decays to a stop. Grab it mid-coast and it stops dead under your hand with no forward jump. Start a drag, run the pointer off the edge of the window and release there — the content stops instead of sticking, and the next press still works. Focus the surface and hold ArrowDown for stepped travel. With reduced motion on, release stops instantly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "drag-to-pin-progress": "# Build: Drag Mapped To Pin Progress\n\nA drag surface laid over a pinned horizontal section. Instead of a tuned drag multiplier it reads the pin's live geometry — the scroll span it occupies and the pixels the strip travels — and converts one into the other, so a pixel of thumb is a pixel of track regardless of panel widths or zoom. Release throws it, and the throw decays in wall-clock time rather than per frame.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDrag a pinned horizontal strip and it moves exactly one pixel per pixel, then throws and settles.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The conversion is `ratio = (trigger.end - trigger.start) / getDistance()`: scroll pixels the pin occupies, divided by horizontal pixels the strip travels. Both are read on every use because ScrollTrigger recomputes `end` on each refresh.\n2. During a drag the scroll position is set absolutely — `scrollTop = pressScrollTop - dx * ratio` — using the offset from the press point, never an accumulation of per-move deltas.\n3. Velocity is sampled as `(clientX - lastX) / dt` in seconds and clamped to ±4000 px/s, so a single stuttering event pair cannot record an implausible throw.\n4. On release the velocity decays with `damp(v, 0, friction, dt)` and the scroll advances by `-v * ratio * dt` each frame, so a 144 Hz display and a 60 Hz display travel the same distance in the same wall-clock time.\n5. The loop self-parks: below 20 px/s the velocity is zeroed and the callback returns immediately on subsequent frames, so an idle component costs one comparison per frame.\n6. `trigger.progress` is rounded to three decimals and written to a `--pin-progress` custom property on the surface only when it changes, so a progress rail can be pure CSS with no per-frame React render.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `friction = 0.002`\n  Fraction of throw velocity remaining after one second. 0.002 lands a hard flick in roughly half a second. Near 0.1 the throw stops dead and the release feels like hitting a wall; above ~0.05 it coasts long enough that the user reaches for the scrollbar to stop it.\n- `THROW_FLOOR = 20`\n  Pixels per second below which the throw is cut and the loop parks. Exponential decay never reaches zero, so without a floor the component writes a sub-pixel scroll change forever and never idles.\n- `MAX_THROW = 4000`\n  Velocity clamp in px/s. Two pointer events 2 ms apart produce an enormous instantaneous velocity; unclamped, a single jittery sample launches the track across its entire range.\n- `release timeout = 80 ms`\n  Hold still for longer than this before letting go and the release is not a throw. Without it, pausing mid-drag and releasing fires the stale velocity from the last movement.\n- `progress quantisation = 3 decimals`\n  Below this the custom property is rewritten on nearly every frame with a change no one can see; above it the progress rail visibly steps.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DragToPinProgress(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; trigger: ScrollTrigger | null; getDistance?: () => number; friction?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The pinned section and anything overlaying it.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container whose `scrollTop` the drag writes.\n- `trigger: ScrollTrigger | null`\n  The pinned trigger being scrubbed. Dragging is disabled while null.\n- `getDistance: () => number`\n  Live horizontal travel of the strip in px. Omit for a 1:1 scroll mapping.\n- `friction: number` — default `0.002`\n  Fraction of throw velocity left after one second.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the drag surface.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DragToPinProgress({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  trigger,\n  getDistance,\n  friction = 0.002,\n  className,\n}: DragToPinProgressProps) {\n  const surface = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const startScroll = useRef(0);\n  const throwVelocity = useRef(0);\n  const lastMoveX = useRef(0);\n  const lastMoveTime = useRef(0);\n  const lastProgress = useRef(-1);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Scroll pixels per pixel of horizontal track travel. Read live, because\n  // `end` is recomputed on every ScrollTrigger refresh and the panel widths\n  // change with the container.\n  const ratio = () => {\n    if (!trigger) return 1;\n    const span = trigger.end - trigger.start;\n    const travel = getDistance?.() ?? span;\n    return travel > 0 ? span / travel : 1;\n  };\n\n  const applyScroll = (value: number) => {\n    const el = scroller.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    const max = el.scrollHeight - el.clientHeight;\n    el.scrollTop = clamp(value, 0, max);\n  };\n\n  useDrag(surface, {\n    enabled: trigger !== null,\n    onDragStart: (state) => {\n      throwVelocity.current = 0;\n      startScroll.current = scroller.current?.scrollTop ?? 0;\n      lastMoveX.current = state.clientX;\n      lastMoveTime.current = performance.now();\n    },\n    onDrag: (state) => {\n      // Absolute, from the press point — not an accumulation of deltas.\n      // Accumulating drifts, because every clamp at the ends silently eats\n      // part of a delta and the track never returns to where the hand is.\n      applyScroll(startScroll.current - state.dx * ratio());\n\n      const now = performance.now();\n      const dt = (now - lastMoveTime.current) / 1000;\n      if (dt > 0) {\n        const instant = (state.clientX - lastMoveX.current) / dt;\n        throwVelocity.current = clamp(instant, -MAX_THROW, MAX_THROW);\n        lastMoveX.current = state.clientX;\n        lastMoveTime.current = now;\n      }\n    },\n    onDragEnd: (_state, moved) => {\n      // A tap is not a throw. Without this, releasing after a pause launches\n      // the track at whatever velocity the last move happened to record.\n      if (!moved || reduced || performance.now() - lastMoveTime.current > 80) {\n        throwVelocity.current = 0;\n      }\n    },\n  });\n\n  useRaf((dt) => {\n    const el = surface.current;\n\n    // Publish the pin's own progress as a CSS variable rather than state:\n    // a progress rail must not cost a React render per frame.\n    if (el && trigger) {\n      const p = Math.round(trigger.progress * 1000) / 1000;\n      if (p !== lastProgress.current) {\n        lastProgress.current = p;\n        el.style.setProperty('--pin-progress', String(p));\n      }\n    }\n\n    if (throwVelocity.current === 0) return;\n\n    // Exponential decay in wall-clock time, so the throw travels the same\n    // distance on a 60 Hz panel as on a 144 Hz one.\n    throwVelocity.current = damp(throwVelocity.current, 0, friction, dt);\n    if (Math.abs(throwVelocity.current) < THROW_FLOOR) {\n      throwVelocity.current = 0;\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const current = scroller.current?.scrollTop ?? 0;\n    applyScroll(current - throwVelocity.current * ratio() * dt);\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useDrag` removes its pointerdown/move/up/cancel and capture-phase click listeners and releases any outstanding pointer capture; `useRaf` unsubscribes from the shared loop. The `--pin-progress` property dies with the element, and no timers or tweens are created that could outlive it.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Dragging still works and still maps one pixel to one pixel — it is an input method, not decoration. The inertial throw is suppressed, so the strip stops the instant the pointer is released instead of coasting.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Inventing a drag multiplier. It is correct for exactly one panel count and one container width; change either and the strip either outruns the hand or lags behind it, and the number gets re-tuned by eye forever.\n- Accumulating per-move deltas instead of using the offset from the press point. Every clamp at the ends of the range silently swallows part of a delta, so the track drifts away from the hand and never comes back.\n- Caching `trigger.end - trigger.start`. ScrollTrigger recomputes it on every refresh — a font swap or a resize — and a cached span makes the drag mapping wrong for the rest of the session.\n- Decaying the throw with a per-frame constant (`v *= 0.95`). The throw travels less than half as far on a 120 Hz display, so the component feels different on different hardware.\n- Setting `touch-action: none` on the drag surface. It kills native vertical scrolling over the whole pinned section, so on a phone the page appears frozen; `pan-y` keeps vertical scroll and hands over only the horizontal gesture.\n- Not swallowing the click after a drag. Releasing over a panel fires that panel's click handler, so throwing the track opens whatever happened to be under the finger.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Designed to sit around `pinned-horizontal-track`, whose `onTrack` payload supplies both `trigger` and `getDistance`.\n- Because the drag writes `scrollTop`, ScrollTrigger updates through its normal scroll listener — no manual `ScrollTrigger.update()` call, and no second source of truth for position.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPress and drag left: the strip follows the pointer exactly, and a panel edge under your thumb stays under your thumb. Flick and release: it coasts and settles smoothly. Drag to either end and it stops flush with no rubber band. The progress rail matches the strip at all times, including while throwing. With reduced motion on, release stops it dead.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "drawer": "# Build: Drawer\n\nModal's sibling on the same React Aria `ModalOverlay`: a floating panel that slides in from the start, end, top or bottom edge over a black/40 backdrop. Focus is trapped and returned to the trigger, the page stops scrolling, and Escape or a backdrop press closes it. The header, body, footer and trigger are Modal's parts re-exported under Drawer names, so a drawer and a dialog share one content vocabulary.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPanel that slides in from any edge over a dimmed page, for filters, details and bottom sheets, sharing Modal's header, body and actions.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Drawer` renders `AriaModalOverlay` as a `group/drawer` (`fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-100 flex h-(--visual-viewport-height) p-2 sm:p-3`) with `dir` from `useDirection()` and `isDismissable` defaulting to true. The placement map adds the overlay alignment: `justify-end` for end, `justify-start` for start, `items-end` for bottom, `items-start` for top.\n2. Inside, an `aria-hidden` backdrop div (`absolute inset-0 bg-black/40`) fades over 300ms ease-out via `group-data-[entering]/drawer:opacity-0` and `group-data-[exiting]/drawer:opacity-0`. The `AriaModal` panel is `h-full` for side drawers with a width from the size map (sm 320, md 400, lg 600px), or `max-h-[85%]` and full width for top and bottom drawers.\n3. The slide is a CSS `translate` transition keyed on React Aria's `data-entering` / `data-exiting`: 300ms on `cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)` (Modal's timing), transitioning `translate, filter, opacity`. Each edge starts `calc(100%+24px)` past its edge so no sliver of the 12px-inset panel or its shadow shows before it moves; `motion-safe:` also adds a 4px blur on entry and exit. React Aria holds the panel mounted until the exit transition ends, so closing slides it back out.\n4. Start and end follow the reading direction: the classes are written per direction (`motion-safe:ltr:data-[entering]:translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)]` vs `motion-safe:rtl:data-[entering]:-translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)]`), so an end drawer opens from the right in LTR and from the left in RTL. Every class is a literal so Tailwind can see it.\n5. The surface is Modal's `ModalDialog` with `flex-1` added, so the panel uses the same `rounded-3xl border bg-background-full` card and `aria-describedby` wiring; `ModalBody` takes the remaining height (`flex-1 min-h-0`) and scrolls on its own while header and footer stay pinned.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `off-screen offset = calc(100% + 24px)`\n  The panel floats 12px inside the viewport (8px on phones) and casts a shadow; starting 24px past its own size guarantees nothing of it is visible before the slide begins.\n- `motion = 300ms, cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1), blur 4px`\n  Same duration and curve as Modal so dialogs and drawers feel like one family; the iOS sheet curve arrives fast and settles.\n- `side widths = sm 320 / md 400 / lg 600 px`\n  320 holds a filter list on the narrowest phones; 400 is the default detail panel; 600 fits a two-column form. Top and bottom drawers ignore `size` and span the screen.\n- `top / bottom max height = max-h-[85%]`\n  Leaves a strip of the dimmed page visible, which tells the reader a tap there dismisses the sheet.\n- `backdrop = bg-black/40`\n  Lighter than Modal's black/70 because a drawer leaves most of the page visible as context rather than replacing it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Drawer(props: Omit<ModalOverlayProps, \"children\" | \"className\" | \"style\"> & { placement?: \"start\" | \"end\" | \"top\" | \"bottom\"; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string; overlayClassName?: string; children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode) }): JSX.Element\nexport { ModalBody as DrawerBody, ModalFooter as DrawerFooter, ModalHeader as DrawerHeader, ModalTrigger as DrawerTrigger }\n```\n\n- `placement: \"start\" | \"end\" | \"top\" | \"bottom\"` — default `\"end\"`\n  The edge the panel slides in from. Start and end follow the reading direction.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Width of a start or end drawer: 320, 400 or 600px. Ignored for top and bottom.\n- `isDismissable: boolean` — default `true`\n  Whether a press on the backdrop closes the drawer.\n- `isOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Controlled open state when used without a DrawerTrigger.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Accessible name when the content has no DrawerHeader title.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the panel surface.\n- `overlayClassName: string`\n  Classes on the full-screen overlay.\n- `children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode)`\n  Drawer content; the function form receives `close`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  overlay: \"group/drawer fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-100 flex h-(--visual-viewport-height) p-2 sm:p-3\",\n  backdrop: [\n    \"absolute inset-0 bg-black/40\",\n    \"transition-opacity duration-300 ease-out\",\n    \"group-data-[entering]/drawer:opacity-0 group-data-[exiting]/drawer:opacity-0\",\n  ].join(\" \"),\n  modal: [\n    \"relative flex w-full flex-col outline-none\",\n    \"transform-gpu transition-[translate,filter,opacity] duration-300 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)]\",\n    \"motion-safe:data-[entering]:blur-[4px] motion-safe:data-[exiting]:blur-[4px]\",\n    \"motion-reduce:data-[entering]:opacity-0 motion-reduce:data-[exiting]:opacity-0\",\n  ].join(\" \"),\n  dialog: \"flex-1\",\n  // Each panel starts 24px past its edge (the 12px inset plus its shadow),\n  // so no sliver of it shows before it moves. Literal per edge and direction\n  // so Tailwind can see every class.\n  placement: {\n    end: {\n      overlay: \"justify-end\",\n      modal: [\n        \"h-full\",\n        \"motion-safe:ltr:data-[entering]:translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)] motion-safe:ltr:data-[exiting]:translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)]\",\n        \"motion-safe:rtl:data-[entering]:-translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)] motion-safe:rtl:data-[exiting]:-translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)]\",\n      ].join(\" \"),\n    },\n    start: {\n      overlay: \"justify-start\",\n      modal: [\n        \"h-full\",\n        \"motion-safe:ltr:data-[entering]:-translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)] motion-safe:ltr:data-[exiting]:-translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)]\",\n        \"motion-safe:rtl:data-[entering]:translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)] motion-safe:rtl:data-[exiting]:translate-x-[calc(100%+24px)]\",\n      ].join(\" \"),\n    },\n    bottom: {\n      overlay: \"items-end\",\n      modal: \"max-h-[85%] motion-safe:data-[entering]:translate-y-[calc(100%+24px)] motion-safe:data-[exiting]:translate-y-[calc(100%+24px)]\",\n    },\n    top: {\n      overlay: \"items-start\",\n      modal: \"max-h-[85%] motion-safe:data-[entering]:-translate-y-[calc(100%+24px)] motion-safe:data-[exiting]:-translate-y-[calc(100%+24px)]\",\n    },\n  },\n  // Side drawers only; top and bottom drawers span the screen.\n  size: {\n    sm: \"max-w-[320px]\",\n    md: \"max-w-[400px]\",\n    lg: \"max-w-[600px]\",\n  },\n});\n\nexport interface DrawerProps extends Omit<ModalOverlayProps, \"children\" | \"className\" | \"style\"> {\n  /** The edge the panel slides in from. `start` and `end` follow the reading direction. */\n  placement?: DrawerPlacement;\n  /** Width of a start or end drawer: 320, 400 (default), or 600px. */\n  size?: DrawerSize;\n  /** Accessible name when the content has no DrawerHeader title. */\n  \"aria-label\"?: string;\n  /** Classes on the panel surface. */\n  className?: string;\n  /** Classes on the full-screen overlay. */\n  overlayClassName?: string;\n  children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode);\n}\n\nexport function Drawer({\n  placement = \"end\",\n  size = \"md\",\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  overlayClassName,\n  isDismissable = true,\n  children,\n  ...props\n}: DrawerProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const edge = styles.placement[placement];\n  const side = placement === \"start\" || placement === \"end\";\n\n  return (\n    <AriaModalOverlay\n      {...props}\n      dir={direction}\n      isDismissable={isDismissable}\n      className={cx(styles.overlay, edge.overlay, overlayClassName)}\n    >\n      <div aria-hidden className={styles.backdrop} />\n      <AriaModal className={cx(styles.modal, edge.modal, side && styles.size[size])}>\n        <ModalDialog aria-label={ariaLabel} className={cx(styles.dialog, className)}>\n          {children}\n        </ModalDialog>\n      </AriaModal>\n    </AriaModalOverlay>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** React Aria releases the focus trap, restores focus to the trigger and removes the page scroll lock when the overlay unmounts after the exit transition. The component adds no listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the translate and blur are not applied; the panel instead fades in and out (`motion-reduce:data-[entering]:opacity-0`) over the same 300ms, and the backdrop still fades.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not build the translate classes with string interpolation (`translate-x-[${offset}]`). Tailwind only generates literal class names it can find in the source, so every edge and direction is spelled out.\n- Do not use `left` / `right` for start and end. The per-direction `ltr:` / `rtl:` variants are what make an end drawer open from the left in RTL.\n- Do not animate the slide with `transform` and the blur with a separate keyframe. React Aria waits for the transition on the exiting element before unmounting, so keep `translate`, `filter` and `opacity` in the one transition list.\n- Do not apply `size` to top or bottom drawers; they are full width by design, which is why the size class is only added when `placement` is start or end.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `DrawerTrigger`, `DrawerHeader`, `DrawerBody` and `DrawerFooter` are `ModalTrigger`, `ModalHeader`, `ModalBody` and `ModalFooter` re-exported, so `useModalClose()` from the modal file also closes a drawer.\n- Under reduced motion the slide and blur (both `motion-safe:`) are dropped and `motion-reduce:` adds an opacity fade instead.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPressing \"Filters\" dims the page and slides a 320px panel in from the right edge (from the left with `dir=\"rtl\"`), sharpening from a slight blur; its body scrolls on its own while the header and footer stay put. Escape or a click on the dimmed area slides it back out and returns focus to the button. \"Bottom sheet\" rises from the bottom edge, spans the width and stops at 85% of the viewport height.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "drifting-smoke-layers": "# Build: Drifting Smoke Layers\n\nAn absolutely-positioned atmosphere layer for the back of a hero or a card. Three oversized elements, each painting two soft radial gradients, blurred and drifting on periods that never line up. It looks like a rendered volumetric pass and costs three composited transforms.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSlow volumetric fog built from blurred radial gradients on three coprime cycles. No texture, no canvas.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each layer is `position: absolute; inset: -25%`, making it 150% of the container in both axes with the excess hanging off every side. The layer can then translate by ±10% of its own box and scale to 1.55 without ever bringing an edge into the container — an exactly-fitting layer shows a hard corner the moment it moves.\n2. A layer paints two `radial-gradient(W% H% at X% Y%, tint, transparent E%)` ellipses at different centres and different alphas. Two overlapping ellipses per layer, six across the stack, produce density variation that a single gradient cannot: the visible boundary between clouds is where two soft fields disagree, not where a gradient stops.\n3. `filter: blur(40px | 55px | 70px)` erases the elliptical shape entirely and leaves only the density field. The three different radii are what separate the layers in apparent depth — identical blur reads as three copies of one cloud sliding over each other.\n4. One `@keyframes kx-smoke-drift` block serves all layers. Its two poses read `translate(var(--kx-smoke-x0), var(--kx-smoke-y0)) scale(var(--kx-smoke-s0)) rotate(0deg)` and the matching `…-x1/-y1/-s1/-r`; custom properties resolve per element, so each layer walks a different path through the same cycle without a keyframe block of its own.\n5. The periods are 16s, 22s and 29s. The combined pattern only repeats at lcm(16, 22, 29) = 5104 seconds, roughly 85 minutes, so no viewer will ever see it loop. Equal periods repeat every cycle and the fog reads as a looping video.\n6. Only `transform` and `opacity` are animated. The blurred result of each layer is rasterised once and composited thereafter, so a 70px blur is paid for at paint time, not sixty times a second.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `inset = -25%`\n  Makes each layer 150% of the container. Worst case is scale 1.55 plus a 10% self-relative translate plus an 11deg rotation; 25% of overflow on each side covers all three with room left. At -10% the corner of the largest layer swings into view during the rotation.\n- `periods = 16s / 22s / 29s`\n  Chosen so lcm(16, 22, 29) = 5104s. Any pair sharing a factor collapses the combined period — 16/24/32 repeats every 96 seconds and is clearly a loop. 29 is prime, which is what pushes the total past an hour.\n- `blur radii = 40px / 55px / 70px`\n  Different radii are the depth cue. Below ~25px the elliptical shape of the gradient survives the blur and the fog reads as coloured blobs; above ~90px the layer flattens into an even wash with no internal structure and the drift stops being visible.\n- `layer opacities = 0.55 / 0.40 / 0.30`\n  Descending with distance. The three layers composite multiplicatively against the background, so equal opacities stack to an opaque haze; the falloff keeps total coverage under about 0.75 and preserves whatever sits behind.\n- `scale range = 1.25 to 1.55`\n  The breathing. Under about 1.15 the scale change is imperceptible next to the translation; over about 1.8 the browser re-rasterises the blurred layer at the new scale, which shows as a visible sharpness pop mid-cycle.\n- `gradient edge stops = 60% to 70%`\n  Where each ellipse reaches full transparency, relative to its own size. Below ~50% the falloff is steep enough that the blur cannot hide the ellipse; at 100% the gradient never fully clears and the layer becomes a flat tint with a bright spot.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DriftingSmokeLayers(props: { layers?: 1 | 2 | 3; tint?: string; intensity?: number; speed?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `layers: 1 | 2 | 3` — default `3`\n  How many layers to render. Fewer layers, less depth and less fill-rate.\n- `tint: string` — default `'var(--smoke-tint, currentColor)'`\n  Any CSS colour. Mixed down per blob with `color-mix`.\n- `intensity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplies every layer opacity. Below 1 for fog behind text.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplies every period. 2 is half speed; the periods stay coprime under any multiplier.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`. Use it to change the inset or add a mask.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/* Hand-tuned rather than seeded-random: these are the poses, not samples from\n   a distribution, and a PRNG here would only make them harder to adjust. */\nconst LAYER_SPECS: readonly LayerSpec[] = [\n  {\n    durationS: 16,\n    blurPx: 40,\n    opacity: 0.55,\n    fromX: '-8%',\n    fromY: '-6%',\n    fromScale: 1.25,\n    toX: '10%',\n    toY: '8%',\n    toScale: 1.45,\n    rotateDeg: 8,\n    blobs: [\n      { w: 40, h: 50, x: 70, y: 30, alpha: 55, edge: 60 },\n      { w: 45, h: 55, x: 30, y: 65, alpha: 40, edge: 62 },\n    ],\n  },\n  {\n    durationS: 22,\n    blurPx: 55,\n    opacity: 0.4,\n    fromX: '6%',\n    fromY: '4%',\n    fromScale: 1.3,\n    toX: '-9%',\n    toY: '-7%',\n    toScale: 1.5,\n    rotateDeg: -11,\n    blobs: [\n      { w: 55, h: 45, x: 25, y: 35, alpha: 45, edge: 64 },\n      { w: 50, h: 60, x: 75, y: 70, alpha: 32, edge: 66 },\n    ],\n  },\n  {\n    durationS: 29,\n    blurPx: 70,\n    opacity: 0.3,\n    fromX: '-4%',\n    fromY: '7%',\n    fromScale: 1.35,\n    toX: '8%',\n    toY: '-9%',\n    toScale: 1.55,\n    rotateDeg: 6,\n    blobs: [\n      { w: 60, h: 60, x: 50, y: 20, alpha: 38, edge: 68 },\n      { w: 48, h: 52, x: 20, y: 80, alpha: 28, edge: 70 },\n    ],\n  },\n];\n\nconst SMOKE_CSS = `\n/* One keyframe block for every layer. The transform endpoints are read from\n   custom properties, which are resolved per element, so each layer follows a\n   different path through the same two-pose cycle. */\n@keyframes kx-smoke-drift {\n  0%, 100% {\n    transform:\n      translate(var(--kx-smoke-x0), var(--kx-smoke-y0))\n      scale(var(--kx-smoke-s0))\n      rotate(0deg);\n  }\n  50% {\n    transform:\n      translate(var(--kx-smoke-x1), var(--kx-smoke-y1))\n      scale(var(--kx-smoke-s1))\n      rotate(var(--kx-smoke-r));\n  }\n}\n\n.kx-smoke {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;\n  overflow: hidden;\n  pointer-events: none;\n  /* Cuts the layers out of the page's paint and layout tree entirely. Without\n     it the browser considers the oversized blurred children when computing\n     the ancestor's ink overflow on every frame. */\n  contain: paint;\n}\n\n.kx-smoke-layer {\n  position: absolute;\n  /* 150% of the container in both axes. A layer that exactly fits would show\n     a hard edge the instant it translates. */\n  inset: -25%;\n  background: var(--kx-smoke-bg);\n  filter: blur(var(--kx-smoke-blur));\n  opacity: var(--kx-smoke-opacity);\n  animation: kx-smoke-drift var(--kx-smoke-dur) ease-in-out infinite;\n  will-change: transform;\n}\n\n/* Reduced motion parks every layer on its 50% pose rather than its 0% pose.\n   The 50% pose is the fuller, more composed frame; 0% is where the layers are\n   most nearly stacked, which reads as a single flat wash. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-smoke-layer {\n    animation: none;\n    will-change: auto;\n    transform:\n      translate(var(--kx-smoke-x1), var(--kx-smoke-y1))\n      scale(var(--kx-smoke-s1))\n      rotate(var(--kx-smoke-r));\n  }\n}\n`;\n\n/** Two offset ellipses per layer. Percentages keep it resolution-independent. */\nfunction backgroundFor(spec: LayerSpec, tint: string): string {\n  return spec.blobs\n    .map(\n      (b) =>\n        `radial-gradient(${b.w}% ${b.h}% at ${b.x}% ${b.y}%, ` +\n        `color-mix(in oklab, ${tint} ${b.alpha}%, transparent), transparent ${b.edge}%)`,\n    )\n    .join(', ');\n}\n\nexport function DriftingSmokeLayers({\n  layers = 3,\n  tint = 'var(--smoke-tint, currentColor)',\n  intensity = 1,\n  speed = 1,\n  className,\n}: DriftingSmokeLayersProps) {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-drifting-smoke-layers\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {SMOKE_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <div className={cn('kx-smoke', className)} aria-hidden=\"true\">\n        {LAYER_SPECS.slice(0, layers).map((spec) => {\n          const style: StyleWithVars = {\n            '--kx-smoke-bg': backgroundFor(spec, tint),\n            '--kx-smoke-blur': `${spec.blurPx}px`,\n            '--kx-smoke-opacity': spec.opacity * intensity,\n            '--kx-smoke-dur': `${spec.durationS * speed}s`,\n            '--kx-smoke-x0': spec.fromX,\n            '--kx-smoke-y0': spec.fromY,\n            '--kx-smoke-s0': spec.fromScale,\n            '--kx-smoke-x1': spec.toX,\n            '--kx-smoke-y1': spec.toY,\n            '--kx-smoke-s1': spec.toScale,\n            '--kx-smoke-r': `${spec.rotateDeg}deg`,\n          };\n          return <span key={spec.durationS} className=\"kx-smoke-layer\" style={style} />;\n        })}\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. No listeners, no timers, no animation frames, no observers. Unmounting removes the elements and the CSS animations with them; React removes the hoisted `<style>` when the last instance unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block sets `animation: none` and applies each layer's 50% pose as a static transform, so the fog is still there, still layered, still at full density — it simply stops drifting. `will-change` is reset to `auto` in the same block so the three large blurred layers are not held in compositor memory for an animation that is not running.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating `filter: blur()` itself, or animating the gradient stops. Both force the layer to be re-blurred every frame — a 70px blur over a 150%-size layer is tens of milliseconds of GPU work per frame and the whole page drops to single-digit FPS. Animate the transform of an already-blurred layer instead.\n- Giving the layers the same duration with different delays. A delay does not decorrelate anything: after the first cycle every layer is at the same phase offset forever, and the stack visibly repeats on the shared period. The periods themselves have to be coprime.\n- Sizing the layers to the container. At `inset: 0` the blur samples transparent pixels beyond the element box, so the fog fades out at exactly the container edge and the effect reads as a rectangle of smoke rather than smoke seen through a window.\n- Omitting `overflow: hidden` on the wrapper. The layers are 150% of the container by design and will paint over neighbouring content, including through a parent with its own background.\n- Leaving off `pointer-events: none`. The layer covers the whole container, so every button underneath stops being clickable and the cause is invisible in the DOM inspector unless you notice the transparent sibling on top.\n- Setting `will-change: transform` and leaving it set under reduced motion. `will-change` promotes the element to its own compositor layer permanently; with the animation switched off that is three large blurred textures held in GPU memory for nothing. The reduced-motion block resets it to `auto`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The component is `position: absolute; inset: 0`, so it expects a positioned ancestor and contributes nothing to layout. Put it as the first child of the section it should sit behind and give that section `position: relative`.\n- `contain: paint` on the wrapper keeps the oversized blurred children out of the ancestor ink-overflow calculation, which otherwise runs on every frame of the drift.\n- The tint reads `var(--smoke-tint, currentColor)` and is mixed with `color-mix(in oklab, …)`, so the fog inherits the text colour by default and can be re-themed by setting one property on any ancestor. `color-mix` needs Safari 16.2+, Chrome 111+, Firefox 113+.\n- Fill-rate is the real cost: three full-container layers at 150% size, blurred. On a low-end phone drop to `layers={2}` — the depth reads almost as well and it removes the widest blur.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWatch for a full minute: the density pattern never obviously repeats, and no layer edge or gradient ellipse becomes visible at any point in the cycle. Content behind the smoke stays clickable. In a performance profile the frames show composite work only — no repaints of the blurred layers. With reduced motion on, the fog is still present and completely still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dropdown": "# Build: Dropdown\n\nA free-form menu surface rather than a value picker: grouped rows of actions, headers, avatars, footers of buttons, whatever the panel needs. It is built from React Aria's `DialogTrigger`, `Popover` and `Dialog` and shares the kit's menu recipe with Select, so the 266px white panel opens with the same 150ms fade, scale 0.95 and 2px blur, grown from the corner nearest the trigger. Open state can be controlled so the trigger can react to it (a turning chevron) and rows can close the panel.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nComposable popover menu (trigger, panel, groups, rows, dividers) built on React Aria: the recipe behind the sidebar team and account menus.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Dropdown` holds the open state, bridging controlled (`isOpen` / `onOpenChange`) and uncontrolled use, and shares a `triggerRef` and a `popoverRef` through context. It renders `AriaDialogTrigger` with `onOpenChange={(o) => allowOpenChange(o) && setOpen(o)}`.\n2. The panel is `isNonModal`: React Aria's modal scroll lock puts `overflow: hidden` on `<html>`, which collapses the page scroll position and yanks sticky layout whenever a menu opens. Non-modal loses outside-press dismissal, so `useDismissOnOutsidePress` restores it with a capture-phase `pointerdown` listener that closes the menu when a press lands outside both refs, and `useTriggerToggle` swallows the reopen when the open trigger itself is pressed, so the trigger toggles.\n3. `DropdownTrigger` is a React Aria `Button` carrying `triggerRef`, with only `cursor-pointer outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring` of its own; the consumer styles the rest through `className`.\n4. `DropdownPopover` renders `AriaPopover` (`placement` \"bottom start\", `offset` 4) with `MENU_POPOVER_WIDTH` (`w-[266px]`) and `MENU_POPOVER_SURFACE`: `z-110 max-w-[calc(100vw-32px)] overflow-y-auto rounded-2xl border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-2.5 shadow-dropdown`, a `transition duration-150 ease-out`, and `data-[entering]` / `data-[exiting]` set to `opacity-0 scale-95 blur-[2px]`; `DropdownPopover` adds `motion-reduce:transition-none` after them. The transform origin follows `data-[placement]` and the reading direction (`origin-top-left`, `rtl:origin-top-right`, and so on). Inside, an `AriaDialog` labelled by the required `aria-label` is a `flex flex-col gap-1` column.\n5. Rows: `DropdownGroup` stacks an optional `ps-2 text-body-medium text-text-secondary` label over a `gap-1` column and only adds `pt-1` when there is a label. `DropdownItem` is a native `<button type=\"button\">` with `aria-pressed={selected}`, `MENU_ITEM` (`flex w-full items-center gap-2 rounded-2lg p-2 text-start text-text-primary transition-colors`) and either the active background or hover / focus-visible backgrounds (`bg-dropdown-item-hover-background`). `DropdownDivider` bleeds through the panel padding with `-mx-2.5 my-1.5 h-px bg-border-button-default`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `panel width = w-[266px], max-w-[calc(100vw-32px)]`\n  The width of the dashboard sidebar's team and account menus, shared with Select through `MENU_POPOVER_WIDTH`; the cap keeps a 16px gutter on phones.\n- `motion = 150ms ease-out, scale 0.95, blur 2px`\n  The menu family's appear animation, identical in Select and Popover so every anchored surface opens alike.\n- `row rhythm = gap-1 (4px) between rows, p-2 per row`\n  The same rhythm as the Select listbox. The divider's `my-1.5` plus the dialog's `gap-1` on both sides gives the original 10px around a divider.\n- `offset = 4px, placement \"bottom start\"`\n  Close enough that the panel reads as belonging to its trigger; start alignment keeps the menu's left edge (right in RTL) under the trigger's.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Dropdown(props: { isOpen?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction DropdownTrigger(props: ComponentProps<typeof AriaButton>): JSX.Element\nfunction DropdownPopover(props: Pick<ComponentProps<typeof AriaPopover>, \"placement\" | \"offset\" | \"crossOffset\"> & { \"aria-label\": string; className?: string; dialogClassName?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction DropdownGroup(props: { label?: string; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction DropdownItem(props: { selected?: boolean; onSelect?: () => void; className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction DropdownDivider(props: { className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `isOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Optional controlled state, for a trigger that reacts to it or rows that close the panel.\n- `DropdownPopover.aria-label: string`\n  Required accessible name for the menu dialog.\n- `DropdownPopover.placement: Placement` — default `\"bottom start\"`\n  Preferred side and alignment; flips on collision.\n- `DropdownPopover.offset: number` — default `4`\n  Gap between trigger and panel in pixels.\n- `DropdownPopover.className: string`\n  Extra panel classes, e.g. a width override for the default 266px.\n- `DropdownGroup.label: string`\n  Muted body-medium heading above the rows.\n- `DropdownItem.selected: boolean`\n  Keeps the hover pill on the row and sets `aria-pressed`.\n- `DropdownItem.onSelect: () => void`\n  Click handler for the row.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Dropdown({ isOpen: controlledOpen, onOpenChange, children }: DropdownProps) {\n  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const [uncontrolledOpen, setUncontrolledOpen] = useState(false);\n\n  const isOpen = controlledOpen ?? uncontrolledOpen;\n  const setOpen = (next: boolean) => {\n    setUncontrolledOpen(next);\n    onOpenChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\n  // Pressing the trigger while open closes the menu instead of reopening\n  const allowOpenChange = useTriggerToggle(isOpen, triggerRef);\n\n  return (\n    <DropdownContext.Provider value={{ triggerRef, popoverRef }}>\n      <AriaDialogTrigger isOpen={isOpen} onOpenChange={(o) => allowOpenChange(o) && setOpen(o)}>\n        {children}\n      </AriaDialogTrigger>\n    </DropdownContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\n/** The element that opens the menu. Style it entirely via className. */\nexport function DropdownTrigger({ className, ...props }: ComponentProps<typeof AriaButton>) {\n  const context = useContext(DropdownContext);\n  return (\n    <AriaButton\n      ref={context?.triggerRef}\n      {...props}\n      className={cx(\n        \"cursor-pointer outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n        className as string,\n      )}\n    />\n  );\n}\n\n/* ------------------------------------------------------------------- panel */\n\nexport interface DropdownPopoverProps\n  extends Pick<ComponentProps<typeof AriaPopover>, \"placement\" | \"offset\" | \"crossOffset\"> {\n  \"aria-label\": string;\n  /** Extra classes on the panel — e.g. a width override (default w-[266px]). */\n  className?: string;\n  /** Classes on the inner dialog (the flex column), e.g. gap between groups. */\n  dialogClassName?: string;\n  children: ReactNode;\n}\n\nexport function DropdownPopover({\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  placement = \"bottom start\",\n  offset = 4,\n  crossOffset,\n  className,\n  dialogClassName,\n  children,\n}: DropdownPopoverProps) {\n  const context = useContext(DropdownContext);\n  return (\n    <AriaPopover\n      ref={context?.popoverRef}\n      isNonModal\n      placement={placement}\n      offset={offset}\n      crossOffset={crossOffset}\n      className={cx(\n        MENU_POPOVER_WIDTH,\n        MENU_POPOVER_SURFACE,\n        // No fade, scale or blur under reduced motion: the panel opens and\n        // closes at once, and react-aria unmounts it without waiting.\n        \"motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {/* gap-1 keeps bare DropdownItems 4px apart, the same rhythm as the\n          Select listbox; DropdownDivider's margins are sized to absorb it. */}\n      <AriaDialog aria-label={ariaLabel} className={cx(\"flex flex-col gap-1 outline-none\", dialogClassName)}>\n        {children}\n      </AriaDialog>\n    </AriaPopover>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The capture-phase `pointerdown` listeners on `document` exist only while the menu is open and are removed by their effect cleanups on close or unmount. React Aria returns focus to the trigger.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `motion-reduce:transition-none` on the panel removes its 150ms scale, blur and fade: it opens and closes at once, and react-aria unmounts it without waiting. Row hover changes are colour only.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not make the popover modal to get outside-press dismissal back. The modal scroll lock sets `overflow: hidden` on `<html>` and shunts sticky layout every time a menu opens.\n- Do not nest a `<button>` inside `DropdownItem`. The row is itself a native button; put footer buttons outside the rows, as the demo does.\n- Do not add `pt-1` to a label-less group. Its first row would float 4px lower than the panel padding implies, visible as extra space above the first hover pill.\n- Do not omit `aria-label` on `DropdownPopover`; it is required because the dialog has no heading to name it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Unlike Select, Dropdown does not put a value into the trigger; it is the composable shell behind the sidebar team / account menus and the AI chat template's add, model and folder menus.\n- The `MENU_*` class constants live in `registry/lib/menu-styles`, shared with Select and Context Menu so the menu surfaces stay identical.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking \"Team access\" grows a 266px white panel from its top-left corner below the trigger and turns the chevron; the page keeps scrolling normally. The current owner's row keeps the grey pill, hovering another row shows the pill, and clicking it changes the owner and closes the panel. Clicking the trigger again, clicking outside, or pressing Escape closes it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dual-canvas-z-split": "# Build: Dual-Canvas Depth Split\n\nTwo stacked canvases render the same instanced particle system with opposite `uZSign`, and the page content is a DOM sibling between them. Particles with negative depth pass behind the text and positive ones pass in front. Because size, parallax rate, brightness and softness are one continuous ramp across the depth axis, the plane where the split happens cannot be seen — the effect is that the copy is embedded in the field, and it stays live, selectable text throughout.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne particle field rendered twice with the depth sign flipped, so the text sits inside the field rather than on top of it.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: ogl@^1.0.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `aDepth` is the camera axis, not a distance: -1 is far behind the content plane, +1 is close to the viewer, 0 is the content plane itself. The SIGN chooses which canvas draws a particle; the magnitude drives everything else, which is why the two layers look like one field.\n2. Both layers share the same typed arrays, so the two contexts hold identical particles rather than two independent fields. Only the attribute descriptor objects are per-layer, because ogl writes the buffer handle and element count into the object it is given.\n3. A particle on the wrong side of the plane is rejected in the VERTEX shader by writing `gl_Position = vec4(2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 1.0)` — outside the frustum on every axis, so all four vertices are clipped before rasterisation and it costs no fragments. The vertex cost doubles across the two layers; the fragment cost, which is the only cost that mattered, does not.\n4. Near particles are bigger (`mix(minSize, maxSize, depth01)`), drift further per second (`0.35 + 0.65 * depth01`), are brighter, and have a softer edge — a defocus that stops the front layer reading as dirt on the screen. Four cues from one number.\n5. The back canvas is opaque and blends additively (`ONE, ONE`), which is order independent so nothing needs sorting. The front canvas sits over live text and must be transparent, which rules additive out — there is nothing on the GPU to add to — so it blends premultiplied source-over (`ONE, ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA`). One fragment shader emits premultiplied colour and serves both.\n6. The population is derived from container area — `clamp(round(megapixels * density), 250, maxCount)` — and applied with `setInstancedCount` on both layers. Buffers are allocated once at `maxCount`, so a resize never reallocates.\n7. Drift is a pure function of `elapsed` and the per-particle seed, with `fract` wrapping the field vertically and `uFadeBand` fading alpha to zero at both ends so the wrap seam is never visible. No per-particle state, so a still frame is drawable from one number.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `split = 0.38`\n  Fraction of the population placed in front of the content. Under a half on purpose: dust in front of body copy is a legibility cost, and at 0.5 the text reads as fogged rather than embedded.\n- `FRONT_OPACITY = 0.55`\n  Extra alpha scale on the front layer only. Depth already brightens near particles, and the front layer is entirely near particles, so without this the layer that overlaps the text is the brightest thing on screen.\n- `DEPTH_FLOOR = 0.05`\n  Dead zone around the content plane. A particle at exactly 0 is rejected by both layers — `aDepth * uZSign <= 0.0` is false for neither sign — so it would silently never render.\n- `sizeRange = [3, 34]`\n  Disc diameter at the far and near ends of the ramp. The span is what sells the depth; at 8-to-14 the field is flat, and above ~40 the near particles are large enough that their own softness becomes the dominant texture of the page.\n- `parallax factor = 0.35 + 0.65 * depth01`\n  Near particles travel about three times as far per second as far ones. A larger ratio makes the far layer look frozen; a smaller one and the field moves as a single sheet, which removes the only depth cue that survives in a static screenshot.\n- `MAX_DPR = 1.5`\n  Two layers of soft additive discs is a fill-rate problem and fill scales with the square of the pixel ratio. Dust has no detail a third sample per axis would resolve, so a 3x phone renders at 1.5x and pays a quarter of the fragments.\n- `FADE_BAND = 0.14`\n  Fraction of the field height the wrap fade occupies at each end. Larger than a single-layer field needs, because the near particles are up to 34px across and their radius alone is a few percent of a short container.\n- `size jitter = 0.7 .. 1.3`\n  Multiplier on top of the depth ramp. Without it the depth ordering is perfectly readable from size alone, which reads as a diagram of a depth field rather than a depth field.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DualCanvasZSplit(props: { children?: ReactNode; density?: number; maxCount?: number; split?: number; sizeRange?: readonly [number, number]; speed?: number; wander?: number; opacity?: number; colorA?: string; colorB?: string; background?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content rendered between the two particle layers. Ordinary DOM, fully interactive.\n- `density: number` — default `850`\n  Particles per megapixel of container area. The count is derived from this, not fixed.\n- `maxCount: number` — default `1600`\n  Population ceiling, and the size the shared buffers are allocated at.\n- `split: number` — default `0.38`\n  Fraction of the population placed in front of the content.\n- `sizeRange: readonly [number, number]` — default `[3, 34]`\n  Disc diameter in CSS pixels at the far and near ends of the depth ramp.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier on the whole drift, including the upward march.\n- `wander: number` — default `0.05`\n  Sinusoidal wander amplitude in field units, where 1 is the container.\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.6`\n  Overall alpha multiplier. The front layer takes 0.55 of it.\n- `colorA: string` — default `'#8ea6c4'`\n  First tint. Any value ogl's `Color` accepts.\n- `colorB: string` — default `'#e6d9c4'`\n  Second tint; each particle mixes between the two by a seeded amount.\n- `background: string` — default `'#06080c'`\n  Opaque clear colour of the back canvas. The front canvas is transparent.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'dual-canvas-z-split'`\n  Seeds the scatter. Same seed, same field on every reload and on the server.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction createLayer(\n  host: HTMLElement,\n  data: FieldData,\n  opts: {\n    zSign: number;\n    opaque: boolean;\n    background: Color;\n    colorA: Color;\n    colorB: Color;\n    speed: number;\n    wander: number;\n    opacity: number;\n    minSize: number;\n    maxSize: number;\n  },\n): Layer {\n  const renderer = new Renderer({\n    dpr: pixelRatio(MAX_DPR),\n    // The back layer owns its pixels, so it is opaque and can blend\n    // additively. The front layer sits over live DOM text and must be\n    // transparent, which rules additive out — there is nothing on the GPU to\n    // add to. It blends premultiplied source-over instead.\n    alpha: !opts.opaque,\n    premultipliedAlpha: !opts.opaque,\n    // Nothing occludes anything in either layer; the depth ORDER is expressed\n    // by which canvas a particle lands on, not by a z-buffer.\n    depth: false,\n    antialias: false,\n    powerPreference: 'low-power',\n  });\n\n  const gl = renderer.gl;\n  const canvas = gl.canvas;\n\n  if (opts.opaque) gl.clearColor(opts.background.r, opts.background.g, opts.background.b, 1);\n  else gl.clearColor(0, 0, 0, 0);\n\n  // Each layer needs its own attribute descriptors — ogl writes the buffer\n  // handle and the element count into the object it is handed — but the\n  // underlying typed arrays are shared, so the two contexts hold identical\n  // particles rather than two independent fields.\n  const geometry = new Geometry(gl, {\n    index: { data: data.index },\n    position: { size: 2, data: data.quad },\n    aOrigin: { size: 2, data: data.origins, instanced: 1 },\n    aDepth: { size: 1, data: data.depths, instanced: 1 },\n    aSeed: { size: 4, data: data.seeds, instanced: 1 },\n  });\n\n  const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n    uResolution: { value: [1, 1] },\n    uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.dpr },\n    uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n    uZSign: { value: opts.zSign },\n    uDrift: { value: opts.speed },\n    uRise: { value: RISE },\n    uFlowScale: { value: FLOW_SCALE },\n    uWander: { value: opts.wander },\n    uFadeBand: { value: FADE_BAND },\n    uMinSize: { value: opts.minSize },\n    uMaxSize: { value: opts.maxSize },\n    uColorA: { value: opts.colorA },\n    uColorB: { value: opts.colorB },\n    uOpacity: { value: opts.opacity },\n  };\n\n  const program = new Program(gl, {\n    vertex: DUAL_CANVAS_Z_SPLIT_VERT,\n    fragment: DUAL_CANVAS_Z_SPLIT_FRAG,\n    uniforms,\n    transparent: true,\n    depthTest: false,\n    depthWrite: false,\n    // ogl defaults `cullFace` to BACK, and a culled particle is absent with no\n    // error anywhere. Nothing here has a back face worth culling.\n    cullFace: false,\n  });\n  if (opts.opaque) program.setBlendFunc(gl.ONE, gl.ONE);\n  else program.setBlendFunc(gl.ONE, gl.ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA);\n\n  const mesh = new Mesh(gl, { geometry, program });\n\n  host.appendChild(canvas);\n  canvas.style.display = 'block';\n\n  return { renderer, geometry, program, mesh, uniforms, canvas };\n}\n\nexport function DualCanvasZSplit({\n  children,\n  density = 850,\n  maxCount = 1600,\n  split = 0.38,\n  sizeRange = [3, 34],\n  speed = 1,\n  wander = 0.05,\n  opacity = 0.6,\n  colorA = '#8ea6c4',\n  colorB = '#e6d9c4',\n  background = '#06080c',\n  seed = 'dual-canvas-z-split',\n  className,\n}: DualCanvasZSplitProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const backHost = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const frontHost = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1;\n\n  const [minSize = 3, maxSize = 34] = sizeRange;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const back = backHost.current;\n    const front = frontHost.current;\n    if (!back || !front || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const total = Math.max(MIN_COUNT, Math.round(maxCount));\n    const data: FieldData = {\n      quad: new Float32Array([-0.5, -0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5]),\n      index: new Uint16Array([0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 3]),\n      origins: new Float32Array(total * 2),\n      depths: new Float32Array(total),\n      seeds: new Float32Array(total * 4),\n    };\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const frontShare = clamp(split, 0, 1);\n    const lo = Math.max(1, Math.min(minSize, maxSize));\n    const hi = Math.max(lo + 0.5, Math.max(minSize, maxSize));\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i += 1) {\n      data.origins[i * 2] = randRange(rng, X_MIN, X_MAX);\n      data.origins[i * 2 + 1] = rng();\n\n      // The sign picks the canvas, the magnitude drives size, parallax,\n      // brightness and softness. A particle exactly at 0 would be on the\n      // content plane and belongs to neither layer, so the range excludes it.\n      const inFront = rng() < frontShare;\n      data.depths[i] = inFront\n        ? randRange(rng, DEPTH_FLOOR, 1)\n        : -randRange(rng, DEPTH_FLOOR, 1);\n\n      data.seeds[i * 4] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      data.seeds[i * 4 + 1] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      data.seeds[i * 4 + 2] = rng();\n      // Size jitter on top of the depth ramp. Without it the depth ordering is\n      // perfectly legible from size alone, which reads as a diagram.\n      data.seeds[i * 4 + 3] = randRange(rng, 0.7, 1.3);\n    }\n\n    const colA = new Color(colorA);\n    const colB = new Color(colorB);\n    const bg = new Color(background);\n    const shared = {\n      background: bg,\n      colorA: colA,\n      colorB: colB,\n      speed,\n      wander,\n      minSize: lo,\n      maxSize: hi,\n    };\n\n    const layers = [\n      createLayer(back, data, { ...shared, zSign: -1, opaque: true, opacity }),\n      createLayer(front, data, {\n        ...shared,\n        zSign: 1,\n        opaque: false,\n        opacity: opacity * FRONT_OPACITY,\n      }),\n    ];\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      // Population from area, not a fixed number, so one density reads right in\n      // a 320px card and a 2000px hero. The buffers already hold `total`\n      // particles in scattered order, so a prefix is still an even field —\n      // nothing is reallocated.\n      const megapixels = (w * h) / 1_000_000;\n      const count = clamp(Math.round(megapixels * density), MIN_COUNT, total);\n\n      for (const layer of layers) {\n        layer.renderer.setSize(w, h);\n        // `setSize` writes literal px into the canvas's inline style, which\n        // beats any class. Re-asserting percentages keeps it fluid.\n        layer.canvas.style.width = '100%';\n        layer.canvas.style.height = '100%';\n        layer.uniforms.uResolution.value = [\n          layer.renderer.gl.drawingBufferWidth,\n          layer.renderer.gl.drawingBufferHeight,\n        ];\n        layer.uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = layer.renderer.dpr;\n        layer.geometry.setInstancedCount(count);\n      }\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      for (const layer of layers) {\n        layer.uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n        layer.renderer.render({ scene: layer.mesh });\n      }\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { layers, resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    // Two contexts means two ways to be dropped, and a layer nobody hears lose\n    // its context is a blank canvas for the rest of the page's life, with no\n    // fallback. Either loss retires both layers; `preventDefault` is required\n    // or the browser will never offer a restore.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      setUnsupported(true);\n    };\n    for (const layer of layers) layer.canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      for (const layer of layers) {\n        layer.canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n        // ogl has no `dispose()`: `remove()` deletes the VAOs and every buffer,\n        // then the program and its shaders, and only `WEBGL_lose_context` gives\n        // the context itself back. This component holds two of them.\n        layer.geometry.remove();\n        layer.program.remove();\n        loseContext(layer.renderer.gl);\n        layer.canvas.width = 0;\n        layer.canvas.height = 0;\n        layer.canvas.remove();\n      }\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    density,\n    maxCount,\n    split,\n    minSize,\n    maxSize,\n    speed,\n    wander,\n    opacity,\n    colorA,\n    colorB,\n    background,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect: a reflow costs two `setSize` calls and an\n  // instanced-count change, never a rebuild of the shared buffers.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed),\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Per layer: `geometry.remove()` deletes the VAOs and every buffer, `program.remove()` deletes the shaders and the program, `loseContext(gl)` hands the context back through `WEBGL_lose_context` — ogl has no `dispose()` and nothing else releases a context — then the canvas backing store is zeroed and the node removed. Both layers are torn down in the same cleanup, so the component never holds one context after the other has gone.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF subscription is never made and both layers are drawn once at `elapsed` 0. That is a complete frame, not a collapsed one: the scatter, depths, sizes, tints and wrap fade are all per-particle seeds that already vary at t=0, so the still shows the same depth split, in front of and behind the same text, with only the drift absent. Resizing redraws the same still in both layers.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Trying to do this with one canvas. A canvas is a single stacking-context leaf, so DOM content cannot be interleaved with parts of it. Two contexts is not an inefficiency here, it is the requirement — and it is two of the roughly sixteen a Chromium renderer process allows, which is exactly why both are released on unmount.\n- Making the front canvas additive. With `alpha: true` the destination is zero, so an additive particle over text is a flat colour patch: the layer stops looking like light and starts looking like a sticker.\n- Forgetting `pointer-events: none` on the front layer. It covers the content by construction, so every link and button underneath it stops responding, and nothing about the failure looks graphical.\n- Letting a particle sit at depth exactly 0. `aDepth * uZSign <= 0.0` rejects it in BOTH layers, so it is uploaded, transformed and never seen — a silent leak of the population.\n- Rejecting with `discard` in the fragment shader instead of parking the vertex. `discard` still rasterises the quad and runs the shader per fragment; a clip-space position outside the frustum throws the primitive away after four vertex invocations.\n- Writing `gl_Position = vec4(2.0)` for the rejection. w is then 2, the perspective divide brings it back to exactly 1.0 on every axis, and the vertex survives clipping at the frustum boundary — a row of specks in the corner.\n- Giving both layers the same attribute descriptor objects. ogl mutates them with a buffer handle belonging to the first context, and the second layer then renders from a buffer its context has never heard of.\n- Assuming `renderer.setSize` leaves the CSS alone. ogl writes literal px into the canvas inline style, which beats any class, so the percentages must be re-asserted after every call in both layers.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The content is a DOM sibling, not a texture. It stays selectable, focusable and available to a screen reader while particles pass on both sides of it — which is the entire reason to accept two contexts instead of drawing the text into the field.\n- ogl rather than three: two contexts, two instanced meshes, no lights, no cameras and no scene graph. `renderer.render` is called without a camera, so ogl skips frustum culling and the matrix uniforms — the shader writes clip space itself.\n- `depth: false` on both contexts. The depth ORDER is expressed by which canvas a particle lands on, so a z-buffer would be memory plus a clear per frame for no result.\n- Sizes are authored in CSS pixels and scaled by the pixel ratio in the shader, so the field is identical across a dpr change and nothing rebuilds when one happens.\n- GLSL ES 1.00 with no derivative functions — the anti-aliasing floor is `1.0 / sizePx`, exact for a quad of that pixel size — so the same source compiles under a WebGL1 fallback context.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nParticles visibly pass both behind and in front of the text, and the text is still selectable with the mouse while they do. There is no horizontal line anywhere in the container where the field changes character. Near particles are larger, softer and move further per second than far ones. Links inside the content still respond to hover and click. Resize narrow and wide: discs stay circular and the population changes without a stutter. Two contexts per instance, both released on unmount — mount and unmount six instances and none of the earlier ones go black.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "dual-direction-scrub-entrance": "# Build: Dual-Direction Scrub Entrance\n\nTwo elements arrive on the same scrubbed trigger from different directions — the copy rises 60px, the panel slides in from the side by 55% of its own width. Because both tweens sit at position 0 of one timeline, their progress is identical at every scroll position, so the pair reads as one composed arrival. The 0.6s scrub lets them keep moving briefly after the wheel stops, which is the difference between weighted and welded.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCopy rises while a panel slides in from the side, both on one scrub with 600ms of lag.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A single `gsap.timeline` is created with `scrollTrigger: { trigger: root, start: \"top 82%\", end: \"top 38%\", scrub: 0.6 }`. One trigger drives both elements, so there is exactly one progress value in the system.\n2. The primary element gets `fromTo({ autoAlpha: 0, y: 60 }, { autoAlpha: 1, y: 0 })` at position 0. `autoAlpha` rather than `opacity`: it also flips `visibility`, so the invisible element is not a click target on the way in.\n3. The secondary gets `fromTo({ autoAlpha: 0, xPercent: ±55 }, { autoAlpha: 1, xPercent: 0 })`, also at position 0. `xPercent` is a share of its own width, so the entrance distance scales with the layout instead of being tuned per breakpoint.\n4. Both tweens use `ease: \"none\"`. Under a scrub the ease is applied to timeline time, so easing here means the two elements travel at different rates through the same scroll range and stop looking like one gesture.\n5. The secondary is wrapped in an `overflow-hidden` cell. At `xPercent: 55` it begins more than half its width outside the grid column, which without the clip is a horizontal scrollbar on the whole document.\n6. `scrub: 0.6` gives ScrollTrigger 0.6s to catch the timeline up to the scroll position, so the elements decelerate into place after the wheel stops rather than freezing with it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `scrub = 0.6`\n  Seconds of catch-up. `true` (zero lag) welds the animation to the wheel and reads as dragging a slider. Past about 1.5s the section is still arriving long after you have stopped, which reads as lag rather than weight. 0.6 is the point where it feels like mass.\n- `start / end = 'top 82%' / 'top 38%'`\n  The section begins arriving just as it clears the bottom edge and is fully composed while still in the lower half of the screen. Ending at `top 38%` means the entrance completes before the content reaches the reading position — an animation that is still resolving under the eye is unreadable.\n- `rise = 60`\n  Pixels of vertical travel for the copy. Enough to register as arriving; small enough that the text is legible for most of the transit, which matters because the user is reading during it.\n- `slide = 55`\n  Percent of the secondary's own width. Above ~60% it starts entirely outside its column and the first half of the scrub shows an empty cell; below ~30% it reads as a nudge rather than an entrance.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction DualDirectionScrubEntrance(props: { primary: ReactNode; secondary: ReactNode; from?: \"left\" | \"right\"; rise?: number; slide?: number; scrub?: number; start?: string; end?: string; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `primary: ReactNode`\n  Rises from below. The reading anchor — usually the copy.\n- `secondary: ReactNode`\n  Slides in horizontally. Usually the card, figure or panel.\n- `from: \"left\" | \"right\"` — default `'right'`\n  Which edge the secondary enters from. Also flips the grid order.\n- `rise: number` — default `60`\n  Pixels the primary rises through.\n- `slide: number` — default `55`\n  Secondary travel as a percentage of its own width.\n- `scrub: number` — default `0.6`\n  Seconds of catch-up behind the scroll position.\n- `start: string` — default `'top 82%'`\n  ScrollTrigger start.\n- `end: string` — default `'top 38%'`\n  ScrollTrigger end.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the grid container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function DualDirectionScrubEntrance({\n  primary,\n  secondary,\n  from = 'right',\n  rise = 60,\n  slide = 55,\n  scrub = 0.6,\n  start = 'top 82%',\n  end = 'top 38%',\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: DualDirectionScrubEntranceProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const primaryRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const secondaryRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const container = root.current;\n      const a = primaryRef.current;\n      const b = secondaryRef.current;\n      if (!container || !a || !b || reduced) return;\n\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({\n        scrollTrigger: {\n          trigger: container,\n          scroller: scrollerRef?.current ?? undefined,\n          start,\n          end,\n          // The number, not `true`. `true` welds the timeline to the wheel and\n          // the section stops moving in the same millisecond the wheel does.\n          scrub,\n          invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n        },\n      });\n\n      // `fromTo`, deliberately, and not `from`. A `from` tween on a scrubbed\n      // trigger renders its start state immediately at build time and then\n      // treats the element's *current* values as the destination — so a second\n      // tween, a resize, or React re-applying a style mid-scroll silently\n      // rewrites where the animation is heading. Stating both ends makes the\n      // timeline idempotent.\n      tl.fromTo(\n        a,\n        { autoAlpha: 0, y: rise },\n        { autoAlpha: 1, y: 0, ease: 'none', duration: 1 },\n        0,\n      );\n\n      tl.fromTo(\n        b,\n        { autoAlpha: 0, xPercent: from === 'right' ? slide : -slide },\n        { autoAlpha: 1, xPercent: 0, ease: 'none', duration: 1 },\n        // Position 0: both tweens share one progress value. Offsetting them\n        // would make the pair read as a queue instead of a single arrival.\n        0,\n      );\n    },\n    root,\n    [from, rise, slide, scrub, start, end, reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` builds the timeline inside a `gsap.context` and reverts it on unmount or any prop change. The revert kills the ScrollTrigger and restores the inline `transform`, `opacity` and `visibility` the tweens wrote — without it an unmount mid-scrub leaves the panel permanently half-slid and invisible if React reuses the node.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No timeline and no trigger are created, so both elements render in their final layout position at full opacity on first paint. Nothing is hidden waiting for a scroll that the user may never make.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- `gsap.from()` on a scrubbed trigger. It renders the start state immediately at build time and treats whatever the element's values happen to be as the destination — so any later style change silently rewrites the target and the element ends up somewhere other than where the layout says. `fromTo` states both ends and is idempotent under a scrub.\n- Two separate ScrollTriggers, one per element. Their start positions are computed from their own bounding boxes, so on a tall screen the copy is already finished before the panel has begun, and the composition falls apart at exactly the viewport heights you did not test.\n- Easing a scrubbed tween. The ease is applied to timeline time, so the two elements move at different rates through the same scroll window and the pair stops reading as one gesture. Use `ease: \"none\"` and let the scrub supply the smoothing.\n- No `overflow-hidden` around the sliding element. At `xPercent: 55` it sits outside its column at the start of the scrub, which extends the document width and adds a horizontal scrollbar that appears and disappears as you scroll.\n- Using `opacity` instead of `autoAlpha`. The invisible element still receives pointer events, so a link inside a panel that has not arrived yet is clickable and focusable.\n- Omitting `invalidateOnRefresh`. `xPercent` and the trigger boundaries are both derived from measurements taken at build time; after a resize the panel travels the wrong distance and starts at the wrong scroll position.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `from=\"left\"` also reverses the grid order, so the panel enters from the side it visually occupies rather than crossing the copy on its way in.\n- The `will-change-transform` hints sit on the two moving wrappers rather than on their contents, so the promoted layer is created once and the children are not each given their own.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the section up from the bottom: the copy rises and the panel comes in from the right at exactly the same rate, both finishing together before the section reaches the middle of the screen. Stop the wheel mid-entrance and they keep moving for about half a second before settling. Scroll back down and the entrance reverses. No horizontal scrollbar appears at any point. With reduced motion on, the section is composed and static from the start.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "earnings-chart-card": "# Build: Earnings Chart Card\n\nA grey dashboard card with a Weekly / Monthly / Yearly switch and twelve rounded Recharts bars, each standing in a full-height grey track. The newest bar is the bucket still running, so it is drawn in the darker active tone and its value is the \"Earned so far\" headline, counted up in dollars with a chip comparing it to the bar before. Hovering another bar moves the highlight there, fades in an outline ring around its track, opens a small tooltip and switches the headline and chip to that bar.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn \"Earned so far\" card of rounded bars in grey tracks; the current or hovered bar darkens, gets an outline ring and drives the headline.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The tracks are the `Bar` `background`, given as a render function. Recharts calls it once per category with the full-height column (`x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `index`); it draws a `--color-chart-track` rect with an 8px corner radius and, around it, a second rect 3px larger on every side with no fill and a 1.5px stroke in the tone's `-active` colour.\n2. The ring is always mounted. Its `opacity` is 1 only for `index === hovered`, and a 150ms `transition-opacity` fades it in and out, so moving across bars reads as the ring sliding between columns.\n3. `onMouseMove` stores `state.activeTooltipIndex` in `hovered` when `isTooltipActive` is true and the index is finite; `onMouseLeave` clears it. The focused bar is `hovered ?? data.length - 1`. Each bar is a `Cell` whose fill is the `-active` colour for the focused bar and the base `chart-N` colour otherwise, with a 200ms `transition-[fill]`.\n4. The headline renders the focused bar through `ChartHeadline` with a \"$\" formatter, so it counts from the old value to the new one in 320ms. Its chip is `describeDelta(focused, previous)`; the first bar has nothing before it, so the chip is hidden. The label reads \"Earned so far\" at rest and \"Earned · W3\" while hovering.\n5. A period can carry fixed Y ticks. The weekly set uses `[0, 3000, 5000, 10000]` (\"$0 $3K $5K $10K\") as in the preview; the others leave `ticks` out and get four automatic ticks from `tickCount`. All ticks go through `formatCompact` with the \"$\" prefix.\n6. The `Tooltip` has `cursor={false}` because the ring already marks the column. Its content maps `activeIndex` from `null` to -1 first, then shows the bar's value and its change against the bar before.\n7. Choosing a period clears `hovered`, updates internal state only when `period` is uncontrolled, and calls `onPeriodChange`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `bar shape = radius 8, maxBarSize 32, barCategoryGap 30%`\n  Slim pill bars inside slightly wider-looking tracks match the preview; the size cap keeps six yearly bars from turning into blocks.\n- `hover ring = 3px outside the track, radius 11, 1.5px stroke, 150ms opacity fade`\n  The gap separates the ring from the grey track so it reads as an outline, and the chart margin is 3px wider on the top and right so the ring is never clipped.\n- `weekly ticks = [0, 3000, 5000, 10000]`\n  Uneven on purpose, as in the preview: the $3K and $5K rules help read the many smaller weeks while $10K marks the peaks.\n- `plot height = 248px`\n  A fixed plot box gives `ResponsiveContainer` a definite height while the card grows with its header.\n- `motion = bars 450ms, tooltip 160ms, bar fill 200ms, ring 150ms, count-up 320ms`\n  Fast enough to follow a pointer sweeping across twelve bars, and all of it is off under reduced motion.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction EarningsChartCard(props?: EarningsChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype EarningsChartCardProps = { title?: string; periods?: EarningsPeriod[]; period?: string; defaultPeriod?: string; onPeriodChange?: (id: string) => void; tone?: ChartTone; valuePrefix?: string; periodControl?: \"segmented\" | \"select\"; className?: string }\ntype EarningsPeriod = { id: string; label: string; data: { label: string; value: number }[]; ticks?: number[] }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Earned so far\"`\n  Header label while nothing is hovered.\n- `periods: EarningsPeriod[]` — default `EARNINGS_PERIODS`\n  Data sets for the switch, oldest bar first; the last bar is the current one. `ticks` fixes the Y ticks.\n- `period: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultPeriod: string` — default `first period id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onPeriodChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the chosen period id.\n- `tone: ChartTone` — default `2`\n  Bar hue; the current or hovered bar and the ring use its `-active` step.\n- `valuePrefix: string` — default `\"$\"`\n  Prefix for the headline, axis ticks and tooltip.\n- `periodControl: \"segmented\" | \"select\"` — default `\"segmented\"`\n  Segmented switch, or a calendar pill that opens a listbox.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function EarningsChartCard({\n  title = \"Earned so far\",\n  periods = EARNINGS_PERIODS,\n  period,\n  defaultPeriod,\n  onPeriodChange,\n  tone = 2,\n  valuePrefix = \"$\",\n  periodControl = \"segmented\",\n  className,\n}: EarningsChartCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [innerPeriod, setInnerPeriod] = useState(defaultPeriod ?? periods[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const periodId = period ?? innerPeriod;\n  const active = periods.find((p) => p.id === periodId) ?? periods[0];\n  const data = active?.data ?? [];\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState<number | null>(null);\n\n  // The highlighted bar is the hovered one, else the newest (the bucket still running).\n  const focus = hovered ?? data.length - 1;\n  const point = data[focus];\n  const before = focus > 0 ? data[focus - 1] : undefined;\n  const delta = point && before ? describeDelta(point.value, before.value) : null;\n  const money = (value: number) => `${valuePrefix}${formatNumber(value)}`;\n\n  const selectPeriod = (id: string) => {\n    setHovered(null);\n    if (period === undefined) setInnerPeriod(id);\n    onPeriodChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  // Grey full-height track behind each bar, plus an outline ring that fades in on the hovered one.\n  const renderTrack = ({ x, y, width, height, index }: TrackShape) => {\n    if (x === undefined || y === undefined || width === undefined || height === undefined) return <g />;\n    return (\n      <g pointerEvents=\"none\">\n        <rect x={x} y={y} width={width} height={height} rx={BAR_RADIUS} fill=\"var(--color-chart-track)\" />\n        <rect\n          x={x - RING_OFFSET}\n          y={y - RING_OFFSET}\n          width={width + RING_OFFSET * 2}\n          height={height + RING_OFFSET * 2}\n          rx={BAR_RADIUS + RING_OFFSET}\n          fill=\"none\"\n          stroke={toneActiveColor(tone)}\n          strokeWidth={1.5}\n          opacity={index === hovered ? 1 : 0}\n          className=\"transition-opacity duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n        />\n      </g>\n    );\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section aria-label={`${title} chart`} className={cx(CHART_CARD_FRAME, \"gap-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 px-2 pt-2 @md:flex-row @md:items-start @md:justify-between\">\n        <ChartHeadline\n          label={hovered !== null && point ? `Earned · ${point.label}` : title}\n          value={point?.value ?? 0}\n          format={money}\n          delta={delta}\n          swapKey={`${periodId}-${hovered ?? \"current\"}`}\n          reducedMotion={reduced}\n        />\n        {periods.length > 1 ? (\n          <ChartPeriodControl\n            options={periods}\n            value={periodId}\n            onChange={selectPeriod}\n            variant={periodControl}\n            label={`${title} period`}\n            className=\"self-start\"\n          />\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"h-[248px] w-full pe-2 pb-1\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" initialDimension={{ width: 520, height: 248 }}>\n          <BarChart\n            data={data}\n            barCategoryGap=\"30%\"\n            margin={{ top: RING_OFFSET + 4, right: RING_OFFSET, bottom: 0, left: 0 }}\n            onMouseMove={(state) => {\n              const next = Number(state?.activeTooltipIndex);\n              if (state?.isTooltipActive && Number.isFinite(next)) setHovered(next);\n            }}\n            onMouseLeave={() => setHovered(null)}\n          >\n            <CartesianGrid vertical={false} stroke={GRID_STROKE} strokeDasharray=\"4 4\" />\n            <YAxis\n              width={48}\n              domain={[0, \"auto\"]}\n              ticks={active?.ticks}\n              tickCount={4}\n              tickFormatter={(v: number) => formatCompact(v, valuePrefix)}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <XAxis\n              dataKey=\"label\"\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tickMargin={10}\n              interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"\n              minTickGap={6}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <Tooltip\n              cursor={false}\n              offset={16}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={160}\n              wrapperStyle={{ outline: \"none\", zIndex: 10 }}\n              content={({ active: isActive, activeIndex }) => {\n                const i = activeIndex == null ? -1 : Number(activeIndex);\n                const at = data[i];\n                if (!isActive || !at) return null;\n                const prev = data[i - 1];\n                return (\n                  <ChartTooltipCard\n                    title={at.label}\n                    rows={[\n                      { key: \"earned\", label: \"Earned\", tone, value: money(at.value) },\n                      ...(prev\n                        ? [{ key: \"change\", label: `vs ${prev.label}`, value: describeDelta(at.value, prev.value).label }]\n                        : []),\n                    ]}\n                  />\n                );\n              }}\n            />\n            <Bar\n              dataKey=\"value\"\n              name=\"Earned\"\n              radius={BAR_RADIUS}\n              maxBarSize={32}\n              background={renderTrack}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={450}\n            >\n              {data.map((p, i) => (\n                <Cell\n                  key={p.label}\n                  fill={i === focus ? toneActiveColor(tone) : toneColor(tone)}\n                  className=\"transition-[fill] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                />\n              ))}\n            </Bar>\n          </BarChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame on target change and unmount, `useReducedMotion` unsubscribes its media-query listener, and `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer. The card adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` turns off `isAnimationActive` on the Bar and the Tooltip, the headline shows its value without counting, and the number fade, bar fill transition and ring fade are disabled with `motion-reduce:` variants.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `Bar background={{ fill }}` for the tracks. The object form cannot vary per bar, so there is nowhere to draw the hover ring; the render function gets `index` and can.\n- Leaving the chart margin at 0. The ring sits 3px outside the track, and the track runs to the top of the plot, so the ring's top and the last column's right edge would be cut off.\n- Reading `activeIndex` with `Number()` directly. Recharts passes `null` when inactive and `Number(null)` is 0, which would pin the tooltip to the first bar.\n- Comparing the first bar with `undefined`. There is no bar before it, so the chip is hidden instead of showing \"New\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- `EARNINGS_PERIODS` and the `EarningsPoint` and `EarningsPeriod` types are exported. Weekly ends on $7,462 against $6,500 (\"+14.8%\"), monthly on $32,450 against $29,100 (\"+11.5%\") and yearly on $362,400 against $318,900 (\"+13.6%\").\n- BoardUI's preview shows month labels under the Weekly tab; here each tab has its own buckets (W1 to W12, Jan to Dec, 2019 to 2024) so the labels match the switch.\n- The frame, headline, period switch and tooltip card come from `line-chart-card-parts.tsx`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Earned so far\", \"$7,462\" and a lime \"+14.8%\" chip with Weekly selected, Y ticks $0, $3K, $5K and $10K, and twelve rounded lime bars in grey tracks with the last one darker. Hovering a bar darkens it, rings its track, shows a tooltip and switches the headline to \"Earned · W<n>\" and that value. Choosing Monthly shows \"$32,450\" and \"+11.5%\"; Yearly shows \"$362,400\" and \"+13.6%\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "eased-number-counter": "# Build: Eased Number Counter\n\nA number tweens from one value to another on `power2.inOut` rather than linearly, so it reads as loading rather than as a progress meter — slow to commit, quick through the middle, easing onto the final figure. The thing being tweened is a plain numeric object; the string on screen is formatted from it on every update and written with `textContent`, so React never re-renders while the count runs. A transparent sizer reserves the width of the wider endpoint, so nothing after the counter moves as digits are added.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA statistic that counts up on an eased curve and settles on its exact final figure without shifting the layout.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A local object `{ v: from }` is the tween target. `gsap.to(counter, { v: to, duration, ease })` interpolates the number; the DOM is not the tween target at all.\n2. `onUpdate` calls a `write()` closure that does `el.textContent = prefix + format.format(counter.v) + suffix`. Writing `textContent` rather than React children means the tween never touches the reconciler.\n3. The `Intl.NumberFormat` is built once in a `useMemo` keyed on `[locale, decimals, grouping]` — three primitives, so the memo actually holds. `minimumFractionDigits === maximumFractionDigits` also performs the rounding, so no `Math.round` or `toFixed` is needed in the tick.\n4. `onComplete` assigns `counter.v = to` and writes once more. Tween interpolation lands on values like 99.999999997, and a headline number stopping one unit short of itself is a real bug report.\n5. Before the tween starts, `write()` runs once with `v = from`, so the live node is never empty on the first frame and the element does not pop into existence when the tween begins.\n6. The width reservation compares `format.format(to).length` against `format.format(from).length` and renders the longer of the two as a `text-transparent` cell stacked under the live node in an `inline-grid`, both at `col-start-1 row-start-1`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DURATION = 1.6`\n  Seconds. Under ~1s the eye cannot track the digits and the effect is just a flicker before the answer; past ~2.5s the reader has finished the sentence around it and is waiting on a number that has stopped being interesting.\n- `EASE = 'power2.inOut'`\n  The in-out pair is what separates a counter from a progress bar. `none` reads as a machine metering out a value; `power2.out` starts at full speed and looks like it began before you looked. The slow start is the part that reads as \"committing\".\n- `VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.6`\n  60% of the element visible before counting. A stat that starts at 1% visibility has finished before it clears the fold, so the reader arrives at a static number and never sees the effect.\n- `decimals = 0`\n  Also the rounding rule, via matched `minimumFractionDigits`/`maximumFractionDigits`. Leaving them unmatched lets `Intl` vary the digit count between frames, so the string length oscillates and the number jitters horizontally.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction EasedNumberCounter(props: { to: number; from?: number; duration?: number; ease?: string; delay?: number; locale?: string; decimals?: number; grouping?: boolean; prefix?: string; suffix?: string; startOnView?: boolean; className?: string; onComplete?: () => void }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `to: number`\n  The value to land on.\n- `from: number` — default `0`\n  The value to start from.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.6`\n  Seconds of counting.\n- `ease: string` — default `'power2.inOut'`\n  Any GSAP ease string. `none` makes it read as a meter.\n- `delay: number` — default `0`\n  Seconds to wait after the trigger before counting.\n- `locale: string`\n  BCP 47 tag. Omitted uses the runtime default locale.\n- `decimals: number` — default `0`\n  Fixed fraction digits. Also the rounding.\n- `grouping: boolean` — default `true`\n  Thousands separators.\n- `prefix: string` — default `''`\n  Literal text before the number, e.g. a currency sign.\n- `suffix: string` — default `''`\n  Literal text after the number, e.g. a percent sign.\n- `startOnView: boolean` — default `true`\n  Count when scrolled into view rather than on mount.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer span via `cn`.\n- `onComplete: () => void`\n  Fired once the final figure is written.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst DURATION = 1.6;\nconst EASE = 'power2.inOut';\nconst VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.6;\n\nexport function EasedNumberCounter({\n  to,\n  from = 0,\n  duration = DURATION,\n  ease = EASE,\n  delay = 0,\n  locale,\n  decimals = 0,\n  grouping = true,\n  prefix = '',\n  suffix = '',\n  startOnView = true,\n  className,\n  onComplete,\n}: EasedNumberCounterProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const live = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { inView } = useInView(root, { threshold: VIEW_THRESHOLD, once: true });\n\n  // Held in a ref so an inline arrow from the caller does not restart the\n  // count on every parent render.\n  const complete = useRef(onComplete);\n  complete.current = onComplete;\n\n  // One formatter for the whole tween, keyed only on the primitives that\n  // change it. An options *object* prop would be a new reference every render\n  // and would rebuild the formatter — and restart the tween — continuously.\n  const format = useMemo(\n    () =>\n      new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {\n        minimumFractionDigits: decimals,\n        maximumFractionDigits: decimals,\n        useGrouping: grouping,\n      }),\n    [locale, decimals, grouping],\n  );\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = live.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n\n    const counter = { v: from };\n    const write = () => {\n      el.textContent = `${prefix}${format.format(counter.v)}${suffix}`;\n    };\n\n    // Reduced motion is the final figure, immediately — not a blank slot and\n    // not a 0.01s tween that still flickers through every intermediate value.\n    if (reduced) {\n      counter.v = to;\n      write();\n      complete.current?.();\n      return;\n    }\n\n    write();\n    if (startOnView && !inView) return;\n\n    const tween = gsap.to(counter, {\n      v: to,\n      duration,\n      ease,\n      delay,\n      overwrite: true,\n      onUpdate: write,\n      onComplete: () => {\n        // Snap to the exact target. Floating-point tweens land on\n        // 99.99999999 often enough that a counter visibly stopping one short\n        // of its own headline number is a real bug report.\n        counter.v = to;\n        write();\n        complete.current?.();\n      },\n    });\n\n    return () => {\n      tween.kill();\n    };\n  }, [\n    from,\n    to,\n    duration,\n    ease,\n    delay,\n    prefix,\n    suffix,\n    format,\n    reduced,\n    inView,\n    startOnView,\n  ]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The effect returns `tween.kill()`. Because the tween target is a local object rather than a DOM node, nothing is left written to the element beyond the final string, and there are no inline styles to clear.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Writes the final formatted figure immediately and fires `onComplete`, with no tween created at all. It is never left blank and never runs a shortened tween that still flickers through intermediate values.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Constructing `new Intl.NumberFormat()` inside `onUpdate`. It costs tens of microseconds and it is being built 60-120 times a second per counter; a page of eight stats spends milliseconds a frame building formatters it throws away.\n- Tweening the string, or holding the value in `useState`. A `setState` per frame re-renders the subtree at display refresh rate, and worse, any unrelated parent render snaps the number back to the last committed value mid-count.\n- Rendering the live value as React children. React will diff and overwrite whatever the tween wrote, so the count freezes on the first parent re-render.\n- Not snapping to the exact target in `onComplete`. Floating-point interpolation lands near, not on, the target — the counter finishes at 99,999 instead of 100,000.\n- Letting the box size itself from the current text. Every added digit widens the element and shoves the following copy sideways; the count ends up looking like a layout bug.\n- Using `visibility: hidden` for the sizer. That removes it from the accessibility tree as well, and the transparent copy is the only readable text on the element — the live node is `aria-hidden` because it is mid-count garbage.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The `onComplete` callback is held in a ref that is reassigned on every render, so passing an inline arrow from the caller does not restart the count on every parent render.\n- A number formatting *options object* prop was deliberately not exposed: an object literal is a new reference every render, which would rebuild the formatter, invalidate the effect, and restart the tween continuously.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the stat into view: the number counts from its start value, decelerates, and stops on exactly the stated figure — check the last digit. Nothing to the right of the number moves while it counts. Enable reduced motion, reload, and the final figure is present on first paint. Force a parent re-render mid-count and the number keeps counting rather than snapping backwards.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "event-detail-popover": "# Build: Event Detail Popover\n\nWraps any trigger, usually a calendar event chip, in the kit Popover and shows the event behind it: a colour key and title, the long date, the time range, location, a meeting code with a Join button, and the guest list with RSVP labels. Edit and Delete sit as neutral icon buttons in the header. Every action closes the popover before handing the event to the caller, so a delete that removes the chip is safe. `EventDetails` is exported on its own for side panels.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAnchored calendar event card: colour key, date and time, location, meeting code with Join, guest RSVPs, edit and delete.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `EventDetailPopover` keeps its own open state (or takes `isOpen` / `onOpenChange`) and renders `PopoverTrigger` with the child as trigger and a 340px `Popover` placed \"bottom start\". React Aria positions and flips it, Escape closes it and focus returns to the chip.\n2. The card header is a 12px rounded square in the event colour (the `dot` class from `calendarEventColorStyles`), a React Aria `Heading slot=\"title\"` that names the dialog, and the long date from `formatEventDate`, which formats a fixed UTC noon instant so server and client print the same day.\n3. A tinted `dl` lists time (\"11:30 – 11:45\", or \"All day\"), location and the meeting code; each label is an icon plus an `sr-only` term. The Join button sits at the end of the meeting row.\n4. Guests render as initials avatars with the name and an RSVP label (Organizer, Going, Maybe, Declined, Awaiting); declined names are struck through. Past `maxAttendees` the rest collapse into \"+N more\", and the header counts guests and how many are going.\n5. Join, Edit and Delete are wrapped by `act()`: it sets the popover closed first and then calls the handler, so removing the event in `onDelete` unmounts a closed trigger instead of yanking an open overlay.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover width = 340px`\n  Fits the longest row (meeting code plus Join) on one line; the kit Popover caps it at 100vw − 32px on phones.\n- `placement = \"bottom start\"`\n  Opens under the chip aligned to its start edge, like the template; React Aria flips it when the chip is near the bottom.\n- `maxAttendees = 4`\n  Keeps the card under ~420px tall so it fits beside a month cell without scrolling.\n- `colour key = 12px, radius 4px`\n  Matches the chip tint so the card reads as the same event at a glance.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction formatEventDate(date: { year: number; month: number; day: number }, locale?: string, options?: Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions): string\nfunction EventDetails(props: { event: CalendarEvent; onJoin?: (e: CalendarEvent) => void; onEdit?: (e: CalendarEvent) => void; onDelete?: (e: CalendarEvent) => void; joinLabel?: string; locale?: string; hourCycle?: 12 | 24; maxAttendees?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction EventDetailPopover(props: EventDetailsProps & { children: ReactNode; isOpen?: boolean; defaultOpen?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (open: boolean) => void; placement?: Placement; closeOnAction?: boolean }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `event: CalendarEvent`\n  The event to show (title, colour, start/end, location, meetingCode, attendees, description).\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The trigger; must forward its ref and spread props.\n- `isOpen / defaultOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / boolean / (open) => void` — default `uncontrolled, closed`\n  Open state.\n- `onJoin: (event) => void`\n  Shows the Join button on the meeting row.\n- `onEdit: (event) => void`\n  Shows the edit icon button.\n- `onDelete: (event) => void`\n  Shows the delete icon button; always closes first.\n- `placement: Placement` — default `\"bottom start\"`\n  Preferred side; flips when needed.\n- `closeOnAction: boolean` — default `true`\n  Close after Join or Edit.\n- `maxAttendees: number` — default `4`\n  Guests listed before \"+N more\".\n- `hourCycle: 12 | 24` — default `24`\n  Time format.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for dates and times.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function EventDetails({\n  event,\n  onJoin,\n  onEdit,\n  onDelete,\n  joinLabel = \"Join\",\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  hourCycle = 24,\n  maxAttendees = 4,\n  className,\n}: EventDetailsProps) {\n  const c = calendarEventColorStyles[event.color];\n  const dateLabel = formatEventDate(event.start, locale);\n  const sameDay =\n    event.start.year === event.end.year && event.start.month === event.end.month && event.start.day === event.end.day;\n  const timeLabel = event.allDay\n    ? \"All day\"\n    : `${formatEventTime(event.start, { locale, hourCycle })} – ${formatEventTime(event.end, { locale, hourCycle })}${\n        sameDay ? \"\" : ` (${formatEventDate(event.end, locale, { month: \"short\", day: \"numeric\" })})`\n      }`;\n  const attendees = event.attendees ?? [];\n  const going = attendees.filter((a) => a.status === \"accepted\" || a.organizer).length;\n  const shown = attendees.slice(0, maxAttendees);\n  const hidden = attendees.length - shown.length;\n\n  return (\n    <div className={cx(\"flex flex-col gap-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex items-start gap-2.5 ps-1\">\n        <span aria-hidden className={cx(\"mt-1.5 size-3 shrink-0 rounded-[4px]\", c.dot)} />\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          <Heading slot=\"title\" className=\"text-headline-semibold break-words text-text-primary\">\n            {event.title}\n          </Heading>\n          <p className=\"text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\">{dateLabel}</p>\n        </div>\n        {onEdit || onDelete ? (\n          <div className=\"-me-1 -mt-1 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-0.5\">\n            {onEdit ? (\n              <Button\n                variant=\"secondary\"\n                size=\"small\"\n                iconOnly\n                leadingIcon={RiPencilLine}\n                aria-label={`Edit ${event.title}`}\n                onClick={() => onEdit(event)}\n              />\n            ) : null}\n            {onDelete ? (\n              <Button\n                variant=\"secondary\"\n                size=\"small\"\n                iconOnly\n                leadingIcon={RiDeleteBinLine}\n                aria-label={`Delete ${event.title}`}\n                className=\"hover:text-text-destructive\"\n                onClick={() => onDelete(event)}\n              />\n            ) : null}\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <dl className=\"flex flex-col gap-2.5 rounded-[14px] bg-background-secondary-default p-3\">\n        <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2.5\">\n          <dt className=\"shrink-0\">\n            <RiTimeLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" />\n            <span className=\"sr-only\">Time</span>\n          </dt>\n          <dd className=\"min-w-0 text-body-2-medium tabular-nums text-text-primary\">{timeLabel}</dd>\n        </div>\n        {event.location ? (\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2.5\">\n            <dt className=\"shrink-0\">\n              <RiMapPin2Line aria-hidden className=\"size-4 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" />\n              <span className=\"sr-only\">Location</span>\n            </dt>\n            <dd className=\"min-w-0 truncate text-body-2-regular text-text-primary\">{event.location}</dd>\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n        {event.meetingCode ? (\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2.5\">\n            <dt className=\"shrink-0\">\n              <RiVideoOnLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" />\n              <span className=\"sr-only\">Meeting code</span>\n            </dt>\n            <dd className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center justify-between gap-2\">\n              <span className=\"truncate font-mono text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">{event.meetingCode}</span>\n              {onJoin ? (\n                <Button size=\"xs\" onClick={() => onJoin(event)} aria-label={`${joinLabel} ${event.title}`}>\n                  {joinLabel}\n                </Button>\n              ) : null}\n            </dd>\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n      </dl>\n\n      {attendees.length > 0 ? (\n        <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-2 px-1\">\n          <p className=\"flex items-center gap-2 text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">\n            <RiGroupLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" />\n            {attendees.length} {attendees.length === 1 ? \"guest\" : \"guests\"}\n            <span aria-hidden className=\"text-text-tertiary\">·</span>\n            <span>{going} going</span>\n          </p>\n          <ul className=\"flex flex-col gap-1.5\">\n            {shown.map((person, i) => (\n              <li key={person.email ?? person.name} className=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n                <Avatar size=\"sm\" initials={person.initials} color={AVATAR_TINTS[i % AVATAR_TINTS.length]} aria-hidden />\n                <span\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-body-2-regular text-text-primary\",\n                    person.status === \"declined\" && \"text-text-tertiary line-through\",\n                  )}\n                >\n                  {person.name}\n                </span>\n                <span className=\"shrink-0 text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n                  {person.organizer ? \"Organizer\" : RSVP_LABEL[person.status ?? \"pending\"]}\n                </span>\n              </li>\n            ))}\n          </ul>\n          {hidden > 0 ? <p className=\"ps-8 text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">+{hidden} more</p> : null}\n        </div>\n      ) : null}\n\n      {event.description ? (\n        <p className=\"border-t border-border-table px-1 pt-3 text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\">{event.description}</p>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface EventDetailPopoverProps extends Omit<EventDetailsProps, \"className\"> {\n  /** The trigger, usually a CalendarEventChip. It must forward its ref and spread props. */\n  children: ReactNode;\n  isOpen?: boolean;\n  defaultOpen?: boolean;\n  onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void;\n  /** Default \"bottom start\"; flips when there is no room. */\n  placement?: PopoverProps[\"placement\"];\n  /** Close after Join / Edit. Delete always closes. Default true. */\n  closeOnAction?: boolean;\n  /** Classes on the popover surface (default width 340px). */\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function EventDetailPopover({\n  children,\n  event,\n  isOpen: controlledOpen,\n  defaultOpen = false,\n  onOpenChange,\n  placement = \"bottom start\",\n  closeOnAction = true,\n  onJoin,\n  onEdit,\n  onDelete,\n  className,\n  ...details\n}: EventDetailPopoverProps) {\n  const [uncontrolledOpen, setUncontrolledOpen] = useState(defaultOpen);\n  const isOpen = controlledOpen ?? uncontrolledOpen;\n  const setOpen = (next: boolean) => {\n    setUncontrolledOpen(next);\n    onOpenChange?.(next);\n  };\n  // Each action closes the popover first, then hands the event to the\n  // caller, so a delete that unmounts the trigger never races the overlay.\n  const act = (handler: ((e: CalendarEvent) => void) | undefined, close: boolean) =>\n    handler\n      ? (e: CalendarEvent) => {\n          if (close) setOpen(false);\n          handler(e);\n        }\n      : undefined;\n\n  return (\n    <PopoverTrigger isOpen={isOpen} onOpenChange={setOpen}>\n      {children}\n      <Popover placement={placement} className={cx(\"w-[340px]\", className)} dialogClassName=\"p-2\">\n        <EventDetails\n          event={event}\n          onJoin={act(onJoin, closeOnAction)}\n          onEdit={act(onEdit, closeOnAction)}\n          onDelete={act(onDelete, true)}\n          {...details}\n        />\n      </Popover>\n    </PopoverTrigger>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No effects of its own. The kit PopoverTrigger owns the outside-press listener and removes it on close and unmount; date formatters are cached in a module-level map.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The kit Popover fades in 150ms and adds a 0.95 scale and 2px blur only under `motion-safe`, so reduced motion keeps just the fade.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling the delete handler before closing the popover. The trigger unmounts while React Aria still tracks the open overlay; close first, then remove.\n- Passing a trigger that does not forward its ref. PopoverTrigger wraps the child in React Aria `Pressable`, which needs the DOM node to anchor and to restore focus.\n- Formatting the date with `toLocaleDateString` on a local Date. The server and browser zones can disagree about the day; format a UTC instant built from the calendar fields.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Control `isOpen` from the parent when the trigger should show a selected state while open, as the demo does with the chip `selected` prop.\n- Pass `closeOnAction={false}` to keep the card open after Join or Edit.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking the \"Stand-up\" chip opens a dialog named \"Stand-up\" under it with \"Thursday, August 20\", \"11:30 – 11:45\", \"Studio 2, 4th floor\", the code \"brd-xqtm-wav\" with a Join button, and \"6 guests · 3 going\" with four guests and \"+2 more\". Escape closes it and focus returns to the chip. Join and Edit close it and report below the card. Delete closes it, removes the chip, lowers the event count and shows Undo, which puts the event back in time order.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "exit-warp-navigation": "# Build: Exit Warp Before Navigate\n\nChoosing a destination does not navigate. It plays an exit: the DOM chrome lifts and fades, the ring of cards is shoved thirty slots forward while its vertical spacing explodes and its radius collapses, the planes dissolve in index order, and only after one settle frame does the router move. The ease is expo.in, so almost nothing happens for the first half-second and then everything happens at once.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA ring of cards tears itself apart and rushes past the camera, and only then does the route change.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1, gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Four scalars describe the ring — `position`, `spacing`, `hSpacing`, `radius` — and one `layout()` reads them: `angle = wrappedOffset(i, position, count) * (2*PI/count) * hSpacing`, then `mesh.position.set(sin(angle) * radius, offset * spacing * 0.12, cos(angle) * radius - radius)` and `mesh.rotation.y = angle`. Idle and exit both drive the same function, so no second code path can disagree about where a card belongs.\n2. Idle rotation is `position += dt * 0.16` inside `setAnimationLoop`, with `dt` in seconds clamped to 50 ms — a per-frame constant would spin twice as fast on a 120 Hz display.\n3. On select, a timeline fades the chrome (`opacity: 0, y: -20, 0.5s, power2.out, stagger 0.05`) and a `tl.call` pinned at an absolute 0.4s starts the warp, so the ring begins moving 0.1s before the text has finished leaving.\n4. The warp is one `gsap.to` on the scalar object: `position + 30`, `spacing 0.8 -> 10`, `hSpacing 1 -> 3`, `radius 2.3 -> 0.6`, 1.4s, `expo.in`, with `onUpdate` calling `layout()`. Radius shrinking while spacing explodes is what makes the planes leave in different directions instead of receding as a group.\n5. Each material tweens `opacity: 0` over 1s with `delay: 0.03 * i`, so the ring dissolves in index order rather than as a single fade.\n6. The tween's `onComplete` sets a 50 ms timeout, and only that timeout calls `onNavigate`. Textures are canvas-drawn gradients with the item label baked in, so the component ships without a single image.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 1.4`\n  Seconds of warp. With expo.in the perceived length is roughly half the real one; below ~1.1s the accelerating half is too brief to register and it reads as a cut, above ~1.8s the destination feels withheld.\n- `shove = 30`\n  Ring slots travelled during the exit. For a six-item ring that is five full revolutions — enough that individual cards stop being trackable and the ring reads as a blur rather than as a carousel scrubbing.\n- `WARP_CUE = 0.4`\n  Absolute seconds at which the scene warp starts, against a 0.5s chrome fade. The 0.1s overlap fuses two moves into one gesture; at 0.5 they read as sequential steps, and at 0 the ring pulls the eye off the text before it has gone.\n- `FADE_STAGGER = 0.03`\n  Seconds between per-card dissolves. 30 ms x 6 cards is 180 ms of sweep — visible as a direction, short enough that the last card still fades inside the warp.\n- `SETTLE_MS = 50`\n  GSAP fires onComplete on the same frame the tween ends, before that frame is presented. Navigating there shows one frame of the un-warped ring. One extra tick guarantees the final layout has been painted.\n- `WARP_SPACING / WARP_RADIUS = 10 / 0.6`\n  Vertical gap goes 0.8 -> 10 while radius goes 2.3 -> 0.6. Spreading without shrinking looks like a zoom out; shrinking without spreading looks like a dolly. Both together are the only combination that reads as the ring tearing apart around the camera.\n- `IDLE_SPEED = 0.16`\n  Ring slots per second at rest. One card takes about six seconds to reach the front — slow enough to read as ambience, fast enough that the ring is never mistaken for a static image.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ExitWarpNavigation(props: { items: { id: string; label: string }[]; onNavigate: (id: string) => void; children?: ReactNode; duration?: number; shove?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: { id: string; label: string }[]`\n  Destinations. One card and one nav button each; the label is drawn into the card texture.\n- `onNavigate: (id: string) => void`\n  Called after the warp and its settle frame. Do the router push here.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Chrome over the canvas. Descendants marked `data-warp-chrome` fade and lift first.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.4`\n  Warp length in seconds.\n- `shove: number` — default `30`\n  Ring slots the drum travels during the warp.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the positioned wrapper.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const el = host.current;\n  if (!el) return;\n\n  // Checked in an effect, never during render: `hasWebGL()` needs `document`\n  // and returning different markup on the server than on the client is a\n  // hydration mismatch, not a graceful degradation.\n  if (!hasWebGL() || reduced) {\n    setFallback(true);\n    return;\n  }\n  setFallback(false);\n\n  let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n  try {\n    renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n  } catch {\n    // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n    // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n    // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n    // page with it.\n    setFallback(true);\n    return;\n  }\n  renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(2));\n  renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n  el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n  renderer.domElement.style.cssText = 'display:block;width:100%;height:100%';\n\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(45, 1, 0.1, 60);\n\n  const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, CARD_H / CARD_W);\n  const meshes: THREE.Mesh<THREE.PlaneGeometry, THREE.MeshBasicMaterial>[] = [];\n  const textures: THREE.CanvasTexture[] = [];\n\n  items.forEach((item, i) => {\n    const texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(drawCard(item.label, i, items.length));\n    texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    textures.push(texture);\n    const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: texture, transparent: true });\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    scene.add(mesh);\n    meshes.push(mesh);\n  });\n\n  const state: Warp = {\n    position: 0,\n    spacing: REST_SPACING,\n    hSpacing: 1,\n    radius: REST_RADIUS,\n  };\n\n  /* One layout function, driven by four scalars. The idle loop and the exit\n     tween both call it; there is no second code path that could disagree\n     about where a card belongs. */\n  const layout = () => {\n    const count = meshes.length;\n    const step = count > 0 ? (Math.PI * 2) / count : 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      const mesh = meshes[i];\n      if (!mesh) continue;\n      const offset = wrappedOffset(i, state.position, count);\n      const angle = offset * step * state.hSpacing;\n      mesh.position.set(\n        Math.sin(angle) * state.radius,\n        offset * state.spacing * Y_PER_SLOT,\n        Math.cos(angle) * state.radius - state.radius,\n      );\n      mesh.rotation.y = angle;\n    }\n  };\n\n  const resize = () => {\n    const w = el.clientWidth;\n    const h = el.clientHeight;\n    if (w === 0 || h === 0) return;\n    renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n    camera.aspect = w / h;\n    // Pull the camera back on narrow boxes so the ring never grows wider\n    // than the frame; a fixed z crops the front card on a phone.\n    camera.position.z = 3.2 * Math.max(1, 1.35 / camera.aspect);\n    camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n  };\n\n  const observer = new ResizeObserver(resize);\n  observer.observe(el);\n  resize();\n  layout();\n\n  let last = 0;\n  renderer.setAnimationLoop((time) => {\n    const dt = last === 0 ? 0 : Math.min((time - last) / 1000, 0.05);\n    last = time;\n    const b = bundle.current;\n    if (b && !b.exiting) {\n      // Seconds, not frames: a per-frame increment idles twice as fast on a\n      // 120 Hz display as it does on 60 Hz.\n      state.position += dt * IDLE_SPEED;\n      layout();\n    }\n    renderer.render(scene, camera);\n  });\n\n  bundle.current = { renderer, scene, camera, geometry, meshes, textures, state, exiting: false };\n\n  return () => {\n    observer.disconnect();\n    // A rebuild — the caller changed the item ids — must not leave the latch\n    // set, or every button on the new ring is dead for the life of the page.\n    exitTl.current?.kill();\n    exitTl.current = null;\n    exiting.current = false;\n    gsap.killTweensOf(state);\n    for (const mesh of meshes) gsap.killTweensOf(mesh.material);\n    // disposeRenderer stops the animation loop first: a queued frame that\n    // renders into a context we just released throws out of cleanup.\n    disposeRenderer(renderer);\n    for (const mesh of meshes) mesh.material.dispose();\n    for (const texture of textures) texture.dispose();\n    geometry.dispose();\n    renderer.domElement.remove();\n    bundle.current = null;\n  };\n  // `ids` stands in for `items` so a caller passing a fresh array literal\n  // every render does not rebuild the whole scene each time.\n  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n}, [ids, reduced]);\n\nconst start = useCallback(\n  (id: string) => {\n    const b = bundle.current;\n    const chromeEl = chrome.current;\n\n    // Re-entrancy guard. Without it a double click starts two warps that\n    // tween the same scalars in opposite directions and the ring jitters —\n    // and on the no-GL branch below, two crossfades each ending in their own\n    // `onNavigate`.\n    if (exiting.current) return;\n    exiting.current = true;\n\n    const chromeTargets = chromeEl\n      ? Array.from(chromeEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-warp-chrome]'))\n      : [];\n\n    if (!b) {\n      // No GL: the static crossfade. Same shape, no warp.\n      const target = chromeTargets.length > 0 ? chromeTargets : chromeEl;\n      if (target) {\n        // On the same ref as the GL path's timeline, so one kill on unmount\n        // covers both branches — and this is the branch the build effect\n        // returns early from, so it has no cleanup of its own.\n        exitTl.current = gsap.timeline().to(target, {\n          opacity: 0,\n          duration: reduced ? 0.15 : 0.3,\n          ease: 'power2.out',\n          onComplete: () => navigate.current(id),\n        });\n      } else {\n        navigate.current(id);\n      }\n      return;\n    }\n\n    b.exiting = true;\n    const state = b.state;\n\n    const tl = gsap.timeline();\n    exitTl.current = tl;\n    if (chromeTargets.length > 0) {\n      tl.to(chromeTargets, {\n        opacity: 0,\n        y: -20,\n        duration: CHROME_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power2.out',\n        stagger: CHROME_STAGGER,\n      });\n    }\n\n    // Absolute position: the warp starts 0.1 s before the chrome finishes\n    // leaving, so the two overlap into one gesture instead of reading as\n    // \"text goes, then something else happens\".\n    tl.call(\n      () => {\n        gsap.to(state, {\n          position: state.position + shove,\n          spacing: WARP_SPACING,\n          hSpacing: WARP_HSPACING,\n          radius: WARP_RADIUS,\n          duration,\n          ease: 'expo.in',\n          onComplete: () => {\n            // One more frame before the route changes. GSAP's onComplete\n            // fires on the same frame the tween ends, so navigating here\n            // shows a single frame of the un-warped ring.\n            const t = setTimeout(() => navigate.current(id), SETTLE_MS);\n            timers.current.push(t);\n          },\n        });\n        for (let i = 0; i < b.meshes.length; i += 1) {\n          const mesh = b.meshes[i];\n          if (!mesh) continue;\n          gsap.to(mesh.material, {\n            opacity: 0,\n            duration: 1,\n            ease: 'power2.in',\n            delay: FADE_STAGGER * i,\n          });\n        }\n      },\n      undefined,\n      WARP_CUE,\n    );\n  },\n  [duration, shove, reduced],\n);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Disconnects the ResizeObserver, kills the tweens on the scalar object and on every material, clears the pending navigation timeout, calls `disposeRenderer` (which stops the animation loop, disposes, forces context loss and zeroes the canvas), then disposes every material, texture and the shared geometry, and removes the canvas element.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No WebGL context is created. The same cards render as a static CSS-gradient grid, and choosing one fades the chrome over 0.15s before navigating — the transition still covers the swap, it just does not move.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `router.push` in the click handler and animating afterwards. React unmounts the canvas as the route resolves, so the warp plays for two frames and then disappears; the navigation must be the last step, inside the tween's completion.\n- No re-entrancy guard. A double click starts two warps tweening the same four scalars from different origins — the ring jitters and the second navigation fires into an already-unmounting tree.\n- Navigating in `onComplete` instead of one tick later. The final frame has not been presented, so a single frame of the intact ring flashes just before the route changes.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` and stopping there. The GL context stays alive; Chromium kills the oldest context once about sixteen exist, so an unrelated canvas elsewhere on the page goes black. `disposeRenderer` also calls `forceContextLoss()` and zeroes the backing store.\n- Leaving the exit tweens running through unmount. They keep writing to disposed materials and to a scalar object nobody reads; the material writes throw once the context is gone. Cleanup kills the tweens on the state object and every material before disposing.\n- Deciding whether WebGL exists during render. `hasWebGL()` needs `document`, so it returns false on the server and true on the client, and the two renders disagree — the check runs in an effect and flips to the static fallback afterwards.\n- Advancing the idle rotation by a per-frame constant. The ring idles at double speed on a 120 Hz laptop, which is exactly the sort of bug that never reproduces on the developer's machine.\n\n## Notes\n\n- App Router wiring: `onNavigate={(id) => router.push(`/work/${id}`)}`. Call `router.prefetch` on hover (see `hover-prefetch-wipe`) so the 1.4s warp is spent downloading the destination rather than waiting after it.\n- Reduced motion and no-WebGL share one path: a static grid of the same cards, a 0.3s fade of the chrome, then navigate. No context is ever created in that branch.\n- The ring is index-driven, not raycast-driven. Picking is done with real DOM buttons over the canvas, which keeps the nav keyboard-reachable and screen-reader-legible for free.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe ring idles at the same speed on a 60 Hz and a 120 Hz display. Choosing a destination fades the caption, then the ring barely moves for about half a second before tearing outward and past the camera; the new route appears only once the frame is empty, with no flash of the intact ring. Unmount mid-warp and the console stays clean.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "faq-section": "# Build: FAQ Section\n\nA `<section>` that pairs a heading block (eyebrow, title, copy, an optional aside) with the kit `Accordion` in its `contained` variant: white question cards on a grey tray, each with a plus that turns into a close. Below 64rem of container width it stacks and centres like the landing page. From 64rem the heading block becomes a sticky left column and the questions fill the right. The question headers also answer to the arrow, Home and End keys.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLanding FAQ: heading block plus the kit accordion tray, two-column at wide container widths, with arrow-key navigation.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is `@container w-full`, and every responsive switch is a container variant (`@xl:`, `@5xl:`), not a media query. The section therefore lays out by the width it is given: a scaled preview, a docs column or a full page all get the right arrangement.\n2. In the `split` layout the inner wrapper is `grid grid-cols-1`, which becomes `@5xl:grid-cols-[minmax(0,5fr)_minmax(0,7fr)]` with a 64px gap inside a 1120px max width. The heading block switches from `items-center text-center` to `@5xl:items-start @5xl:text-start @5xl:sticky @5xl:top-10 @5xl:self-start`, so it stays in view while a long answer list scrolls past. `self-start` is what lets sticky work inside a grid cell. `stacked` keeps the single centred 760px column at every width.\n3. Questions map to `AccordionItem`s inside `<Accordion variant=\"contained\" indicator=\"plus\">`. The expansion props (`expandedKeys`, `defaultExpandedKeys`, `onExpandedChange`, `allowsMultipleExpanded`) pass straight through to React Aria's DisclosureGroup, so the section can be controlled or uncontrolled. Each question heading is one level below the section title (`headingLevel + 1`), and the section is `aria-labelledby` the title.\n4. Every answer body is wrapped in `[data-faq-answer]`. An `onKeyDown` on the list wrapper collects `button[aria-expanded]` elements that are not disabled and not inside an answer. ArrowDown and ArrowUp move focus with wrap-around, Home and End jump to the ends, and `preventDefault` stops the page from scrolling. Keys pressed inside an answer are ignored, so a nested control keeps its own keyboard behaviour.\n5. The title runs from `text-display-4-medium` (32px) up to `@xl:text-display-2-medium` (48px), with letter-spacing tightened to -0.035em at the large size. The description is capped at 440px so it wraps to two balanced lines under the title.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `split breakpoint = @5xl (64rem of container width)`\n  Below about 1024px a 5:7 split leaves the question column under 560px, and the longest questions start wrapping to three lines. Above it both columns read comfortably.\n- `column ratio = minmax(0,5fr) minmax(0,7fr), gap 64px, max 1120px`\n  The heading column only needs to fit a two-line display title and its copy. The extra width goes to the questions. `minmax(0, …)` stops a long unbroken answer from pushing the grid wider than its container.\n- `title scale = 32px → 48px at @xl, tracking -0.02em → -0.035em`\n  The two-line display title from the landing page needs tight tracking at 48px to read as one block. At 32px the same tracking crowds the letters, so it eases off.\n- `answer measure = max-w-[60ch]`\n  In the wide right column an unbounded answer runs over 100 characters per line. 60ch keeps answers readable without changing the card width.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FaqSection(props: { items: { id: string; question: ReactNode; answer: ReactNode; isDisabled?: boolean }[]; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; eyebrow?: ReactNode; layout?: \"split\" | \"stacked\"; variant?: \"divided\" | \"contained\" | \"separated\"; expandedKeys?: Iterable<Key>; defaultExpandedKeys?: Iterable<Key>; onExpandedChange?: (keys: Set<Key>) => void; allowsMultipleExpanded?: boolean; aside?: ReactNode; footer?: ReactNode; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5; id?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: FaqEntry[]`\n  Questions in order: `{ id, question, answer, isDisabled? }`. `id` is the key that `expandedKeys` holds.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Frequently asked questions\"`\n  Section title, rendered as `h{headingLevel}` and used as the section's accessible name.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Supporting copy under the title, capped at 440px.\n- `eyebrow: ReactNode`\n  Small uppercase label above the title.\n- `layout: \"split\" | \"stacked\"` — default `\"split\"`\n  `split` turns two-column from 64rem of container width; `stacked` stays a centred column.\n- `variant: \"divided\" | \"contained\" | \"separated\"` — default `\"contained\"`\n  Accordion look, forwarded to the kit Accordion.\n- `expandedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Controlled open question ids.\n- `defaultExpandedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Initially open question ids when uncontrolled.\n- `onExpandedChange: (keys: Set<Key>) => void`\n  Called with the new set of open ids.\n- `allowsMultipleExpanded: boolean` — default `false`\n  Allow several answers open at once. When false, opening one closes the other.\n- `aside: ReactNode`\n  Content under the heading block (left column when split).\n- `footer: ReactNode`\n  Content under the question list.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5` — default `2`\n  Title level. Questions use the next level down.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Question headers of this list only: answers may hold their own disclosure buttons. */\nfunction questionButtons(list: HTMLElement) {\n  return Array.from(list.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>(\"button[aria-expanded]\")).filter(\n    (button) => !button.disabled && button.closest(\"[data-faq-answer]\") === null,\n  );\n}\n\nfunction moveFocus(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) {\n  const target = event.target as HTMLElement;\n  if (target.closest(\"[data-faq-answer]\")) return;\n  const buttons = questionButtons(event.currentTarget);\n  const index = buttons.indexOf(target as HTMLButtonElement);\n  if (index === -1 || buttons.length === 0) return;\n\n  let next: number;\n  switch (event.key) {\n    case \"ArrowDown\":\n      next = (index + 1) % buttons.length;\n      break;\n    case \"ArrowUp\":\n      next = (index - 1 + buttons.length) % buttons.length;\n      break;\n    case \"Home\":\n      next = 0;\n      break;\n    case \"End\":\n      next = buttons.length - 1;\n      break;\n    default:\n      return;\n  }\n  event.preventDefault();\n  buttons[next]?.focus();\n}\n\nexport function FaqSection({\n  items,\n  title = \"Frequently asked questions\",\n  description,\n  eyebrow,\n  layout = \"split\",\n  variant = \"contained\",\n  expandedKeys,\n  defaultExpandedKeys,\n  onExpandedChange,\n  allowsMultipleExpanded = false,\n  aside,\n  footer,\n  headingLevel = 2,\n  id,\n  className,\n}: FaqSectionProps) {\n  const autoId = useId();\n  const titleId = `${id ?? autoId}-title`;\n  const TitleTag = TITLE_TAGS[headingLevel];\n  const questionLevel = (headingLevel + 1) as 3 | 4 | 5 | 6;\n\n  return (\n    <section id={id} aria-labelledby={titleId} className={cx(\"@container w-full\", className)}>\n      <div className={styles.grid[layout]}>\n        <div className={styles.head[layout]}>\n          {eyebrow ? <p className={styles.eyebrow}>{eyebrow}</p> : null}\n          <TitleTag id={titleId} className={styles.title}>\n            {title}\n          </TitleTag>\n          {description ? <p className={styles.description}>{description}</p> : null}\n          {aside ? <div className=\"mt-2 w-full max-w-[440px] text-start\">{aside}</div> : null}\n        </div>\n\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-4\">\n          <div data-faq-list=\"\" onKeyDown={moveFocus}>\n            <Accordion\n              variant={variant}\n              indicator=\"plus\"\n              expandedKeys={expandedKeys}\n              defaultExpandedKeys={defaultExpandedKeys}\n              onExpandedChange={onExpandedChange}\n              allowsMultipleExpanded={allowsMultipleExpanded}\n            >\n              {items.map((item) => (\n                <AccordionItem\n                  key={item.id}\n                  id={item.id}\n                  title={item.question}\n                  headingLevel={questionLevel}\n                  isDisabled={item.isDisabled}\n                >\n                  <div data-faq-answer=\"\" className=\"max-w-[60ch] text-pretty\">\n                    {item.answer}\n                  </div>\n                </AccordionItem>\n              ))}\n            </Accordion>\n          </div>\n          {footer}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The section adds one React `onKeyDown` handler and no effects, timers or observers. React Aria owns the disclosure state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the accordion's own: the panel height, the content fade-and-blur and the plus rotation. All of them carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so answers open and close instantly. Sticky positioning is not animation and stays.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using viewport breakpoints (`lg:`) for the split. Inside a scaled preview, a dashboard column or a modal the viewport says \"wide\" while the section is narrow, and the two columns crush the questions. The layout must read the container.\n- Dropping `self-start` from the sticky heading. Grid items stretch to the row height by default, so a sticky element as tall as the row has nowhere to stick and scrolls away with the page.\n- Finding headers with a broad `button` selector. An answer that holds its own disclosure or button would join the arrow-key cycle. Filter by `aria-expanded` and exclude anything inside `[data-faq-answer]`.\n- Keeping the question headings at the same level as the section title. Screen-reader heading lists then show the questions as siblings of the section instead of its children. Use `headingLevel + 1`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `layout=\"stacked\"` for the exact centred column of the landing page at every width. The `aside` slot sits under the heading in either layout, which suits a contact card or an expand-all control.\n- Collapsed answers use React Aria's `hidden=\"until-found\"`, so the browser's find-in-page still searches them and opens the matching row.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nIn a container at least 1024px wide the title and copy sit in a left column and the nine questions run down the right column. Below that width everything centres in one column. Clicking a question opens its answer and turns its plus into a close. With `allowsMultipleExpanded` false, opening a second question closes the first. With a header focused, ArrowDown and ArrowUp move focus to the next and previous header (wrapping), Home and End jump to the ends, and Enter toggles the focused one. Expanding all from controlled state shows every answer.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "feature-card-grid": "# Build: Feature Card Grid\n\nThe \"what is in the box\" section of a component-kit landing page: a centred header (eyebrow, title, copy, CTAs) over a responsive grid of rounded cards. Each card shows a cropped, fading illustration on top and a title with a two-line description below. The illustration can be a render function that receives `{ active }`, so a picture assembled from real kit components (ProgressTrack, Switch, Checkbox, Chip, Avatar, Kbd) can flip a switch, fill a bar or complete a step while the card is hovered or focused.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLanding grid of feature cards, each topped by a small illustration built from kit parts that plays a state change on hover or focus.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Active state: `FeatureCard` keeps `hovered` (pointerenter/leave) and `focused` (focus, and blur only when focus leaves the card, checked with `relatedTarget`) and ORs them into `active`. It writes `data-active` on the card and calls the illustration with `{ active }` when it is a function, so the artwork changes props on real components and their own transitions animate the change.\n2. Artwork frame: the top area is a fixed 196px, `overflow-hidden`, with `mask-b-from-75%` so artwork that runs past the bottom fades out instead of being cut, and wider artwork is simply cropped at the card edge. While active the artwork lifts 4px on a 500ms ease-out curve.\n3. The artwork is `aria-hidden` and `inert`: it is made of interactive kit components (checkboxes, switches, buttons) shown read-only, and inert keeps them out of the tab order and the accessibility tree, so the card is one stop with one name.\n4. Card element: with `href` the whole card is an `<a>`, otherwise an `<article>`; both get the hover border and shadow lift and a focus-visible ring offset from the page background.\n5. Grid: the `<section>` is an `@container/features`; columns step 1 → 2 (@xl) → 3 (@4xl) → 4 (@6xl) with the gap opening from 16 to 24px at four columns. `span={2}` widens a card from two columns up, for bento layouts.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `artwork height = 196px`\n  About two thirds of a 300px-wide card, leaving room for a title and two lines of copy at a near-square card.\n- `fade = mask-b-from-75%`\n  Artwork can be taller than the frame and trail off; the last quarter fades into the card.\n- `lift = -4px, 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)`\n  Enough to read as a response, small enough that cropped artwork never reveals its top edge.\n- `columns = 1 / 2 @xl / 3 @4xl / 4 @6xl`\n  Keeps each card between roughly 260 and 340px wide at every container size.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FeatureCardGrid(props: { eyebrow?: ReactNode; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; actions?: ReactNode; children: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction FeatureCard(props: { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; illustration?: ReactNode | ((state: { active: boolean }) => ReactNode); href?: string; onClick?: MouseEventHandler; span?: 1 | 2; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Card heading (rendered as h3).\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Up to two lines of supporting copy.\n- `illustration: ReactNode | ({ active }) => ReactNode`\n  Artwork for the top frame; the function form reacts to hover and focus.\n- `href: string`\n  Makes the card a link.\n- `span: 1 | 2` — default `1`\n  Columns spanned from the two-column breakpoint up.\n- `eyebrow / title / description / actions: ReactNode`\n  FeatureCardGrid header slots; all optional.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * One tile of the grid. Hover and focus-within raise an `active` flag that is\n * handed to the illustration, so artwork built from kit parts can play a\n * small state change (a bar fills, a switch flips, a step completes) instead\n * of the whole card merely brightening. The artwork is `inert` and hidden from\n * assistive tech: it is a picture of a component, not a working one.\n */\nexport function FeatureCard({ title, description, illustration, href, onClick, span = 1, className }: FeatureCardProps) {\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(false);\n  const [focused, setFocused] = useState(false);\n  const active = hovered || focused;\n  const art = typeof illustration === \"function\" ? illustration({ active }) : illustration;\n\n  const onBlur = (event: FocusEvent<HTMLElement>) => {\n    if (!event.currentTarget.contains(event.relatedTarget as Node | null)) setFocused(false);\n  };\n\n  const body = (\n    <>\n      <div\n        aria-hidden=\"true\"\n        inert\n        className=\"relative h-[196px] overflow-hidden mask-b-from-75% mask-b-to-100%\"\n      >\n        <div\n          className={cx(\n            \"h-full w-full transition-transform duration-500 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)] motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            active && \"-translate-y-1 motion-reduce:translate-y-0\",\n          )}\n        >\n          {art}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-1 px-5 pt-3 pb-5\">\n        <h3 className=\"text-headline-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</h3>\n        {description ? (\n          <p className=\"line-clamp-2 text-body-regular text-text-tertiary\">{description}</p>\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n\n  const shared = {\n    \"data-active\": active ? \"true\" : \"false\",\n    onPointerEnter: () => setHovered(true),\n    onPointerLeave: () => setHovered(false),\n    onFocus: () => setFocused(true),\n    onBlur,\n    onClick,\n    className: cx(\n      \"group/feature relative flex flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-3xl border bg-background-primary-default text-start\",\n      \"border-border-button-default shadow-xs transition-[border-color,box-shadow] duration-200 ease-out\",\n      \"hover:border-border-button-hover hover:shadow-md\",\n      \"outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-background-full\",\n      span === 2 && \"@xl/features:col-span-2\",\n      className,\n    ),\n  };\n\n  return href ? (\n    <a href={href} {...shared}>\n      {body}\n    </a>\n  ) : (\n    <article {...shared}>{body}</article>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No listeners, timers or effects; hover and focus state are plain React handlers on the card.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The artwork lift and its transition are removed with `motion-reduce:` variants. State changes still happen on hover, they just snap; kit components drop their own transitions under the same preference.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving the illustration interactive. Kit checkboxes and switches inside a card link nest focusable controls in a link and add a dozen tab stops; mark the frame `inert` and `aria-hidden`.\n- Clearing focus state on every `blur`. Focus moving between elements inside the card fires blur; check `relatedTarget` against the card first.\n- Using `md:`/`lg:` for the column count. The grid often sits in a narrower column than the window; container queries keep cards a sane width.\n- Animating the illustration with its own timers. Reacting to `active` keeps every card still until a visitor points at it, which also costs nothing off screen.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The demo builds eight illustrations entirely from kit components and tokens: ProgressTrack bars that fill further, a Switch that flips, Checkboxes that select, a sidebar selection that moves.\n- Pass a plain node as `illustration` for static artwork.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px the section shows the install line, title, copy and two CTAs above four columns of cards. Hovering \"Agent limits\" raises the card and its context bar moves from 82% to 91%; hovering \"Notification settings\" flips the Mentions switch on; leaving restores both. Tab skips everything inside the artwork. In a narrow container the grid drops to two and then one column.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "file-upload": "# Build: File Upload\n\nA 164px dashed drop zone that accepts a click, Enter or Space, or a dropped file. A bad extension or an oversized file swaps the prompt for a red message for 2.6 seconds; a good one fades the dashes out, draws an accent line clockwise around the rounded border from top centre as a percentage badge rises over the edge, and shows the file icon, name and size. The upload is simulated, so `onUploadComplete` is where the real storage call goes.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA drag-and-drop zone that validates type and size, traces progress around its own border, then reports the finished file.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The zone is a `div role=\"button\"` (tabbable only when idle) holding a visually hidden `<input type=\"file\">` whose `accept` is built from `allowedExtensions`. Click and Enter / Space call `input.click()`; `onDragOver` prevents the default and sets `dragOver`, `onDrop` takes `dataTransfer.files[0]`. The input clears its `value` after reading so choosing the same file again still fires `change`.\n2. `startUpload` checks the lower-cased extension against `allowedExtensions` and the size against `maxBytes`, and on failure sets a rejection string that a 2600ms timeout clears. On success it stores the file, sets phase `uploading` and starts a self-scheduling `tick`: first after 250ms, then every 90ms, adding the next of `PROGRESS_STEPS` (3, 6, 4, 7, 2, 5 percent, cycled) until 100. At 100 the phase becomes `complete`, and 1600ms later it calls `onUploadComplete(file)` and returns to `idle`. Every timeout id goes into one ref that unmount clears.\n3. A ResizeObserver keeps the measured box size in state. An SVG with `viewBox` equal to that size draws a 2px grey rounded rect and, over it, a path from `ringPath(width, height, 1, 15)`: start at top centre, run clockwise with 15px arcs, close. The path has `pathLength={100}`, so `strokeDasharray` is simply `${progress} 200` (102 once at 100, so the round caps overlap and close the loop) with a 200ms linear transition on `stroke-dasharray`.\n4. The idle layer (icon disc, prompt, allowed types) and the busy layer (file icon, name, uploading / success line) are both always rendered on top of each other. `useStaggerState` turns a boolean into `shown`, `hiding` (200ms) or `hidden`, and the `.t-stagger` classes in the theme sheet animate each `.t-stagger-line` from 12px down, blurred 3px and transparent to rest over 500ms on `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`, with lines 2 and 3 delayed 40ms and 80ms. Hiding is a plain 200ms fade with no movement.\n5. The \"Uploading 1.7 MB...\" and \"Uploaded successfully!\" lines share one 18px slot and cross-fade with the same three-state helper. The percentage badge sits at `-top-[9.5px]` centred on the border and enters by sliding 10px down while unblurring (opacity and blur 160ms, translate 300ms). The dashed border and the SVG ring cross-fade on `busy` over 300ms, and the background shifts from secondary to primary.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `defaults = maxBytes 8 * 1024 * 1024; extensions pdf, jpg, jpeg, png, xlsx`\n  From the Figma copy (\"PDF, JPG, PNG, XLSX (max 8 MB)\"). JPEG is accepted but hidden from the label so the copy does not list JPG twice.\n- `simulated progress = 250ms lead-in, 90ms ticks, +3 +6 +4 +7 +2 +5 percent cycled, 1600ms success hold`\n  Averages a little under two seconds per file with uneven steps, so it reads like a network upload instead of a linear timer, and the success state stays up long enough to read. The steps are a fixed cycle rather than `Math.random()`, so every run (and every test) sees the same percentages.\n- `ring geometry = inset 1, radius 15, strokeWidth 2, pathLength 100`\n  The zone is `rounded-2xl` (16px); a 2px stroke centred 1px inside it needs a 15px radius to sit exactly on the border. `pathLength` lets progress map straight onto the dash length whatever the measured size.\n- `closing dash = 102 at 100%`\n  At exactly 100 the round caps leave a hairline gap at the top centre; overshooting by 2 closes the loop.\n- `stagger = 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), 12px, blur 3px, 40ms per line; hide 200ms`\n  The theme's shared text-reveal. A long ease-out reveal and a fast, motionless hide keep the swap from looking like content sliding back down.\n- `rejection = 2600ms`\n  Long enough to read a sentence-length message, short enough that the prompt comes back before the user tries again.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FileUpload(props: { onUploadComplete?: (file: File) => void; allowedExtensions?: readonly string[]; maxBytes?: number; renderFileIcon?: (file: File) => ReactNode; incomingFile?: File | null; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction formatFileSize(bytes: number): string\n```\n\n- `onUploadComplete: (file: File) => void`\n  Called after the progress ring fills and the success line has shown for 1.6s.\n- `allowedExtensions: readonly string[]` — default `[\"pdf\", \"jpg\", \"jpeg\", \"png\", \"xlsx\"]`\n  Extensions without dots; drives validation, `accept` and the hint line.\n- `maxBytes: number` — default `8 * 1024 * 1024`\n  Larger files are rejected with \"That file is larger than 8 MB\".\n- `renderFileIcon: (file: File) => ReactNode`\n  Custom icon for the file being uploaded.\n- `incomingFile: File | null`\n  Starts an upload of this file as if it were dropped (same validation; ignored while busy). Each new File object starts once.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the 164px zone, e.g. a width.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function FileUpload({\n  onUploadComplete,\n  allowedExtensions = DEFAULT_EXTENSIONS,\n  maxBytes = DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES,\n  renderFileIcon,\n  incomingFile,\n  className,\n}: FileUploadProps) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const [phase, setPhase] = useState<UploadPhase>(\"idle\");\n  const [progress, setProgress] = useState(0);\n  const [file, setFile] = useState<File | null>(null);\n  const [rejection, setRejection] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [dragOver, setDragOver] = useState(false);\n\n  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\n  const boxRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [box, setBox] = useState({ width: 533, height: 164 });\n  const timers = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>[]>([]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const element = boxRef.current;\n    if (!element) return;\n    const observer = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {\n      if (!entry) return;\n      const { width, height } = entry.contentRect;\n      if (width > 0) setBox({ width, height });\n    });\n    observer.observe(element);\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const pending = timers.current;\n    return () => pending.forEach(clearTimeout);\n  }, []);\n\n  const startUpload = (nextFile: File) => {\n    const extension = extensionFor(nextFile.name);\n    if (!allowedExtensions.map((value) => value.toLowerCase()).includes(extension)) {\n      setRejection(`Only ${allowedExtensions.map((value) => value.toUpperCase()).join(\", \")} files are supported`);\n      timers.current.push(setTimeout(() => setRejection(null), 2600));\n      return;\n    }\n    if (nextFile.size > maxBytes) {\n      setRejection(`That file is larger than ${formatFileSize(maxBytes)}`);\n      timers.current.push(setTimeout(() => setRejection(null), 2600));\n      return;\n    }\n\n    setFile(nextFile);\n    setProgress(0);\n    setPhase(\"uploading\");\n\n    let value = 0;\n    let step = 0;\n    const tick = () => {\n      value = Math.min(100, value + PROGRESS_STEPS[step++ % PROGRESS_STEPS.length]!);\n      setProgress(Math.round(value));\n      if (value < 100) {\n        timers.current.push(setTimeout(tick, 90));\n        return;\n      }\n\n      setPhase(\"complete\");\n      timers.current.push(\n        setTimeout(() => {\n          onUploadComplete?.(nextFile);\n          setPhase(\"idle\");\n          setFile(null);\n        }, 1600),\n      );\n    };\n    timers.current.push(setTimeout(tick, 250));\n  };\n\n  const busy = phase !== \"idle\";\n\n  // A new `incomingFile` behaves like a drop: ignored while an upload runs.\n  // Keyed on the File object itself, so a re-render never restarts it.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (incomingFile && !busy) startUpload(incomingFile);\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [incomingFile]);\n\n  const progressPath = ringPath(box.width, box.height, 1, 15);\n  const idleReveal = useStaggerState(!busy);\n  const busyReveal = useStaggerState(busy);\n  const uploadingLine = useStaggerState(phase === \"uploading\");\n  const completeLine = useStaggerState(phase === \"complete\");\n  const allowedLabel = allowedExtensions\n    .filter((extension) => extension.toLowerCase() !== \"jpeg\")\n    .map((extension) => extension.toUpperCase())\n    .join(\", \");\n\n  return localize(\n    <div\n      ref={boxRef}\n      role=\"button\"\n      tabIndex={busy ? -1 : 0}\n      aria-label=\"Upload a file\"\n      onClick={() => !busy && inputRef.current?.click()}\n      onKeyDown={(event) => {\n        if (!busy && (event.key === \"Enter\" || event.key === \" \")) {\n          event.preventDefault();\n          inputRef.current?.click();\n        }\n      }}\n      onDragOver={(event) => {\n        event.preventDefault();\n        if (!busy) setDragOver(true);\n      }}\n      onDragLeave={() => setDragOver(false)}\n      onDrop={(event) => {\n        event.preventDefault();\n        setDragOver(false);\n        const droppedFile = event.dataTransfer.files?.[0];\n        if (droppedFile && !busy) startUpload(droppedFile);\n      }}\n      className={cx(\n        \"group relative h-[164px] w-full shrink-0 rounded-2xl outline-none\",\n        \"transition-colors duration-300 ease-out\",\n        busy ? \"bg-background-primary-default\" : \"cursor-pointer bg-background-secondary-default\",\n        \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <input\n        ref={inputRef}\n        type=\"file\"\n        aria-label=\"Choose a file to upload\"\n        accept={allowedExtensions.map((extension) => `.${extension}`).join(\",\")}\n        className=\"sr-only\"\n        tabIndex={-1}\n        onChange={(event) => {\n          const selectedFile = event.target.files?.[0];\n          event.target.value = \"\";\n          if (selectedFile) startUpload(selectedFile);\n        }}\n      />\n\n      <div\n        aria-hidden\n        className={cx(\n          \"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 rounded-2xl border-2 border-dashed\",\n          dragOver\n            ? \"border-border-button-active\"\n            : \"border-border-checkbox-default group-hover:border-border-button-active\",\n          \"transition-[opacity,border-color] duration-300 ease-out\",\n          busy ? \"opacity-0\" : \"opacity-100\",\n        )}\n      />\n\n      <svg\n        aria-hidden\n        viewBox={`0 0 ${box.width} ${box.height}`}\n        preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"\n        className={cx(\n          \"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 size-full transition-opacity duration-300 ease-out\",\n          busy ? \"opacity-100\" : \"opacity-0\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <rect\n          x={1}\n          y={1}\n          width={box.width - 2}\n          height={box.height - 2}\n          rx={15}\n          fill=\"none\"\n          stroke=\"var(--color-border-button-default)\"\n          strokeWidth={2}\n        />\n        <path\n          d={progressPath}\n          fill=\"none\"\n          stroke=\"var(--color-accent-400)\"\n          strokeWidth={2}\n          strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n          pathLength={100}\n          strokeDasharray={`${busy ? (progress >= 100 ? 102 : progress) : 0} 200`}\n          className=\"transition-[stroke-dasharray] duration-200 ease-linear\"\n        />\n      </svg>\n\n      <div\n        aria-hidden\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute -top-[9.5px] left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 rounded-md bg-accent-400 px-1.5 py-0.5\",\n          \"text-caption-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-white tabular-nums\",\n          \"[transition:opacity_160ms_ease-out,filter_160ms_ease-out,translate_300ms_ease-out]\",\n          busy ? \"translate-y-0 opacity-100 blur-none\" : \"-translate-y-2.5 opacity-0 blur-[2px]\",\n        )}\n      >\n        {progress}%\n      </div>\n\n      <div\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute inset-0 flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-3.5\",\n          staggerContainer(idleReveal),\n          busy && \"pointer-events-none\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <span className=\"t-stagger-line t-stagger-line--1 flex size-10 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-file-upload-icon-background p-2.5\">\n          <RiUploadCloud2Line\n            className=\"size-6 shrink-0 text-file-upload-icon-foreground transition-colors duration-150 ease group-hover:text-file-upload-icon-foreground-hover\"\n            aria-hidden\n          />\n        </span>\n        <div className=\"flex flex-col items-center gap-2 text-center\">\n          <p\n            className={cx(\n              \"t-stagger-line t-stagger-line--2 text-body-medium\",\n              rejection ? \"text-text-error-primary\" : \"text-text-secondary\",\n            )}\n          >\n            {rejection ?? (\n              <>\n                Drag and drop to upload or <span className=\"text-accent-500\">select</span>\n              </>\n            )}\n          </p>\n          <p className=\"t-stagger-line t-stagger-line--3 text-body-2-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n            {allowedLabel} (max {formatFileSize(maxBytes)})\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute inset-0 flex flex-col items-center justify-center\",\n          staggerContainer(busyReveal),\n          !busy && \"pointer-events-none\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <span className=\"t-stagger-line t-stagger-line--1 flex size-10 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-2\">\n          {file && (renderFileIcon ? renderFileIcon(file) : <DefaultFileIcon file={file} />)}\n        </span>\n        <p className=\"t-stagger-line t-stagger-line--2 mt-3.5 max-w-[90%] truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n          {file?.name}\n        </p>\n        <div className=\"t-stagger-line t-stagger-line--3 relative mt-1 h-[18px] w-full\">\n          <p\n            className={cx(\n              \"absolute inset-x-0 text-center text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\",\n              staggerLine(uploadingLine),\n            )}\n          >\n            Uploading {file ? formatFileSize(file.size) : \"\"}\n            ...\n          </p>\n          <p\n            className={cx(\n              \"absolute inset-x-0 text-center text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\",\n              staggerLine(completeLine),\n            )}\n          >\n            Uploaded successfully!\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The ResizeObserver disconnects on unmount, every upload and rejection timeout is cleared from the shared timer ref, and the 200ms hide timer in `useStaggerState` clears itself.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The theme sheet sets `transition: none` on `.t-stagger-line` under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, so the text layers swap instantly with no rise or blur. The progress ring still fills (it carries the progress information) and the border, badge and background still cross-fade.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not mount and unmount the idle and busy layers. Both stay rendered and swap through the stagger classes; conditional rendering kills the exit fade and the blur-in.\n- Do not leave the input's `value` set after reading it. The browser then fires no `change` when the same file is chosen again.\n- Do not hard-code the ring path to the default 533 by 164 box. The path is rebuilt from the ResizeObserver size so the corners stay 15px at any width; a fixed path with `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` would stretch the arcs.\n- Do not schedule ticks without tracking them. Every `setTimeout` id is pushed to a ref and cleared on unmount, otherwise a navigation mid-upload sets state on an unmounted component and still fires `onUploadComplete`.\n- Do not feed the zone a file with `new DataTransfer()` and a synthetic `change` event. The constructor is missing in older browsers, and assigning `input.files` bypasses the zone's busy check. Pass the File to `incomingFile` instead; a new File object starts one upload.\n- Do not treat the progress as real. It is a timer; wire the actual upload in `onUploadComplete` or replace `tick` with your request's progress events.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `incomingFile` starts an upload as if that file had been dropped: same validation, ignored while busy, keyed on the File object so re-renders never restart it.\n- `renderFileIcon(file)` replaces the default icon in the busy state (spreadsheet for xlsx, image for jpg/jpeg/png, document otherwise).\n- `formatFileSize` is exported: KB below 1 MB (minimum 1 KB), one decimal under 10 MB, whole MB above.\n- Strings go through `useTemplateCopy`, so the prompt and status lines can be translated by a `TemplateCopyProvider`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAn idle dashed zone shows the upload icon, \"Drag and drop to upload or select\" and \"PDF, JPG, PNG, XLSX (max 8 MB)\". Dropping a .zip shows \"Only PDF, JPG, JPEG, PNG, XLSX files are supported\" in red, which clears after about 2.6s. Dropping a 1.7 MB PDF fades the dashes out, the icon, file name and \"Uploading 1.7 MB...\" rise in, an accent line runs clockwise from top centre around the border with a rising percentage badge, and at 100% the line closes and the status changes to \"Uploaded successfully!\". About 1.6s later `onUploadComplete` fires once and the zone returns to idle.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "finance-dashboard": "# Build: Finance Dashboard\n\nThe BoardUI finance dashboard as one working page. The kit sidebar switches views in local state, and the header carries breadcrumbs, a live notification bell, a Filters menu that shows and hides each widget and an Add transaction dialog. Under it sit four KPI cards, a cash-flow Sankey, spending rings, a portfolio scatter, a daily-spending heatmap and a transactions table that searches, filters, sorts and pages.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full finance dashboard page: in-block sidebar views, KPI cards, cash flow, spending, portfolio, daily spend and a live transactions table.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The frame comes from `home-dashboard-shell.tsx`: `DashboardFrame` is the `h-full w-full` sidebar and scrolling main column, and it turns nav clicks into view keys. `DashboardHeader` draws the title, the crumbs and the bell. `\"finance\"` renders the dashboard, and any other key renders `DashboardViewPlaceholder` with a \"Back to Finance\" button.\n2. Filters is the shared `DashboardWidgetMenu` over `useWidgetToggles`, with six widget ids. The lower row counts its visible cards and sets one, two or three columns at `lg`, so hiding a card widens the others. When every widget is hidden, `DashboardEmptyGrid` offers \"Show all widgets\".\n3. Add transaction opens a controlled `Modal`. Merchant and a positive amount are required, and submit stays disabled until both are valid. Direction signs the amount, and picking \"Money in\" switches the category to Income. Submitting prepends a completed row dated `today`, closes the dialog and fires a success toast.\n4. The KPI cards are derived in a `useMemo` from fixed bases plus the rows added on this page. Money in raises Income and Total balance, money out raises Expenses and lowers the balance, and an Investing row also raises Invested. Each card's sparkline ends on its current value.\n5. Every chart card is a kit item fed with fixed ranges. Cash flow is built from a sources-by-spending matrix (This month sums to $18,420). Spending uses the `labels` ring variant, so each ring names itself, innermost first from Subscriptions to Housing. The portfolio plots risk score against return with bubble size as the holding, and the daily heatmap is a deterministic weekday by week grid.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `today = 2026-11-05`\n  Date stamped on added rows, fixed so server and client renders agree.\n- `KPI bases = 84,230 / 18,420 / 9,140 / 32,400`\n  Total balance, Income, Expenses and Invested before any row is added; the sample rows are already counted in them.\n- `transactions pageSize = 8`\n  Twenty sample rows give three pages, so paging is visible and works.\n- `cash flow chartHeight = 320`\n  Fits ten nodes on the right without crowding while the row of three cards stays above the fold at 1280x800.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FinanceDashboard(props: { view?: string; defaultView?: string; onViewChange?: (key: string) => void; transactions?: readonly Transaction[]; today?: string; defaultWidgets?: readonly FinanceWidget[]; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `view: string`\n  Nav key on screen. Controlled; pair with onViewChange.\n- `defaultView: string` — default `\"finance\"`\n  Initial nav key when uncontrolled. \"finance\" is the dashboard; any other key shows the placeholder view.\n- `onViewChange: (key: string) => void`\n  Called with the new key when a nav row, a crumb or a back button is used.\n- `transactions: readonly Transaction[]` — default `FINANCE_TRANSACTIONS`\n  Initial rows of the transactions table (twenty samples); added rows stay in local state.\n- `today: string` — default `\"2026-11-05\"`\n  ISO date stamped on rows added through the dialog.\n- `defaultWidgets: readonly FinanceWidget[]` — default `all six`\n  Widgets shown on first render: kpis, cashflow, spending, portfolio, daily, transactions.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the frame root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function FinanceDashboard({\n  view,\n  defaultView = 'finance',\n  onViewChange,\n  transactions: initialTransactions = FINANCE_TRANSACTIONS,\n  today = '2026-11-05',\n  defaultWidgets = ALL_WIDGETS,\n  className,\n}: FinanceDashboardProps) {\n  const [innerView, setInnerView] = useState(defaultView);\n  const current = view ?? innerView;\n  const go = useCallback(\n    (key: string) => {\n      if (view === undefined) setInnerView(key);\n      onViewChange?.(key);\n    },\n    [view, onViewChange],\n  );\n\n  const widgets = useWidgetToggles(ALL_WIDGETS, defaultWidgets);\n  const shown = widgets.isShown;\n  const [rows, setRows] = useState<Transaction[]>(() => [...initialTransactions]);\n  const [added, setAdded] = useState<Transaction[]>([]);\n  const [formOpen, setFormOpen] = useState(false);\n  const nextId = useRef(1);\n\n\n  // Only transactions added on this page move the cards; the sample rows are already in the bases.\n  const kpis = useMemo<FinanceKpi[]>(() => {\n    const inflow = added.reduce((sum, t) => sum + Math.max(0, t.amount), 0);\n    const outflow = added.reduce((sum, t) => sum + Math.max(0, -t.amount), 0);\n    const invested = added.reduce((sum, t) => sum + (t.category === 'investing' ? -t.amount : 0), 0);\n    const balance = BASE.balance + inflow - outflow;\n    return [\n      { id: 'balance', label: 'Total balance', icon: WalletIcon, value: balance, change: 0.032, trend: [...TRENDS.balance, balance] },\n      { id: 'income', label: 'Income', icon: IncomeIcon, value: BASE.income + inflow, change: 0.061, trend: [...TRENDS.income, BASE.income + inflow] },\n      {\n        id: 'expenses',\n        label: 'Expenses',\n        icon: CardIcon,\n        value: BASE.expenses + outflow,\n        change: 0.024,\n        goodDirection: 'down',\n        trend: [...TRENDS.expenses, BASE.expenses + outflow],\n      },\n      { id: 'invested', label: 'Invested', icon: InvestIcon, value: BASE.invested + invested, change: 0.084, trend: [...TRENDS.invested, BASE.invested + invested] },\n    ];\n  }, [added]);\n\n  const addTransaction = (draft: Omit<Transaction, 'id' | 'date' | 'status'>) => {\n    const tx: Transaction = { ...draft, id: `tx-new-${nextId.current++}`, date: today, status: 'completed' };\n    setRows((prev) => [tx, ...prev]);\n    setAdded((prev) => [...prev, tx]);\n    setFormOpen(false);\n    toast('Transaction added', { status: 'success', description: `${tx.name}: ${tx.amount > 0 ? '+' : '-'}${formatMoney(Math.abs(tx.amount))}` });\n  };\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `CashFlowCard` schedules one animation frame and one zero-delay timeout to mount the Sankey, and its effect cancels both on unmount. The page adds no listeners; the id counter is a ref and everything else is React state. The composed charts, overlays and the Toaster clean up their own timers and frames.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The page adds no motion of its own. The composed chart cards follow `useReducedMotion` and draw at their final state, and the dropdown, dialog and toasts fall back to instant open and close.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Passing Remix icons straight into `FinanceKpi.icon`. Their type includes a class component whose `defaultProps` does not match the row's icon type, so the page wraps each one in a small function component.\n- Recomputing the KPIs from every table row. The bases already include the sample rows, so only rows added through the dialog move the cards.\n- Sankey links index into one node list. The sources come first, so each spending target index is offset by the number of sources.\n- Mounting the Sankey in the same commit as the whole page. Its sweep reads the frame timestamp, which can be earlier than the start time taken after a long commit, giving a negative clip width. `CashFlowCard` therefore mounts the chart a frame later in its own small subtree, holding the card's height with a spacer meanwhile.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Every card is an existing kit item (`FinanceKpiRow`, `SankeyChartCard`, `RadialChartCard`, `ScatterChartCard`, `HeatmapChartCard`, `TransactionsTable`, `Modal`, `Select`, `Input`). The page owns the wiring, the sample data and the transaction form.\n- Search, status filter, sort and paging are built into `TransactionsTable`, so the page only owns the rows.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280x800 the sidebar shows Finance selected and the main column shows the Finance title, four KPI cards, Cash flow, a row of Spending by category, Portfolio and Daily spending, and the transactions table. Clicking Home in the sidebar shows the Home placeholder, and \"Back to Finance\" returns. Unticking Portfolio in Filters removes that card and the button reads 5/6. Adding a $250 money-out transaction puts it at the top of the table, raises Expenses from $9,140 to $9,390 and shows a toast.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "finance-kpi-row": "# Build: Finance KPI Row\n\nA responsive row of grey KPI cards. Each card has a white icon tile, a label, the value in a 24px medium figure, a percent delta chip and a 96×36 sparkline with a soft area fill and an end dot. The chip and line colour follow whether the move is good news, so rising expenses read rose while rising income reads lime. With `selectable`, the cards become pressed-state buttons that pick one metric for a chart.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBalance, income, expense and savings cards with a delta chip coloured by good or bad news and a tiny area sparkline.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Wrap the list in a `@container` and lay it out as a grid: one column, two from `@sm` (24rem) and four from `@3xl` (48rem), so the row wraps by its own width rather than the viewport. Below `@sm` each card collapses to one compact line (icon, figures, sparkline) by turning the top line into `display: contents` and reordering with `order`, so four cards fit a phone screen.\n2. Classify each card with `toneOf(change, goodDirection)`: zero is flat, otherwise good when the sign of the change matches the good direction (`\"up\"` by default, `\"down\"` for expenses). Good maps to a lime chip and a chart-7 line, bad to rose and chart-3, flat to neutral.\n3. Project the trend with `sparkPoints` into a 96×36 box with 3 units of padding: x steps evenly across the points, y is inverted against the series min and max, and a flat series sits on the middle line.\n4. Draw a `M…L…` line path with a 1.75 stroke, close a copy down to the baseline for the area and fill it with a per-instance `useId` linear gradient from 28% to 0% of `currentColor`, then mark the last point with a 3-unit dot outlined in the card colour.\n5. Format values with `Intl.NumberFormat(locale, { style: \"currency\", maximumFractionDigits: 0 })` unless the item passes its own `format`, and deltas with a percent format using `signDisplay: \"exceptZero\"`; the chip also carries a screen-reader-only comparison label.\n6. When `selectable`, render each card as a `<button aria-pressed>` and resolve the selection controlled (`selectedId`) or from local state seeded by `defaultSelectedId` or the first item; the selected card lifts to a white surface with a hairline ring.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `sparkline box = 96×36 with 3px padding`\n  Fits the empty top-right corner beside the 32px icon tile without crowding the value line.\n- `area gradient = 28% → 0% currentColor`\n  Enough fill to read the shape at a glance, faint enough that the line stays the mark.\n- `grid breakpoints = 1 col, 2 from @sm (24rem), 4 from @3xl (48rem)`\n  A $84,230 figure plus its chip needs about 180px; below that the cards stack.\n- `value type = text-title-1-medium (24px)`\n  Matches the headline figures of the other dashboard cards.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype FinanceKpi = { id: string; label: string; value: number; change: number; trend?: number[]; icon?: ComponentType; goodDirection?: \"up\" | \"down\"; format?: (v: number) => string }\nfunction FinanceKpiRow(props: FinanceKpiRowProps): JSX.Element\nfunction sparkPoints(values: number[], w?: number, h?: number, pad?: number): [number, number][]\n```\n\n- `items: FinanceKpi[]`\n  Cards in display order.\n- `selectable: boolean` — default `false`\n  Turns the cards into aria-pressed buttons that pick one metric.\n- `selectedId / defaultSelectedId / onSelectionChange: string` — default `first item id`\n  Selected card, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `currency: string` — default `\"USD\"`\n  ISO 4217 code for the default value format.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for values and percents.\n- `comparisonLabel: string` — default `\"vs last month\"`\n  Screen-reader text appended to each delta chip.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Key metrics\"`\n  Accessible name of the list.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Map values onto the sparkline box: x spreads evenly, y is inverted so a\n *  larger value sits higher. A flat series draws along the middle. */\nexport function sparkPoints(values: number[], w = SPARK_W, h = SPARK_H, pad = SPARK_PAD): [number, number][] {\n  if (values.length < 2) return [];\n  const min = Math.min(...values);\n  const max = Math.max(...values);\n  const span = max - min;\n  const step = (w - pad * 2) / (values.length - 1);\n  return values.map((v, i) => [\n    pad + i * step,\n    span === 0 ? h / 2 : pad + (h - pad * 2) * (1 - (v - min) / span),\n  ]);\n}\n\nfunction Sparkline({\n  values,\n  tone,\n  label,\n  className,\n}: {\n  values: number[];\n  tone: \"good\" | \"bad\" | \"flat\";\n  label: string;\n  className?: string;\n}) {\n  const gradientId = useId();\n  const pts = sparkPoints(values);\n  const last = pts[pts.length - 1];\n  const first = pts[0];\n  if (!last || !first) return null;\n  const line = pts.map(([x, y], i) => `${i ? \"L\" : \"M\"}${x.toFixed(2)} ${y.toFixed(2)}`).join(\" \");\n  const area = `${line} L${last[0].toFixed(2)} ${SPARK_H} L${first[0].toFixed(2)} ${SPARK_H} Z`;\n  return (\n    <svg\n      role=\"img\"\n      aria-label={label}\n      viewBox={`0 0 ${SPARK_W} ${SPARK_H}`}\n      className={cx(\n        \"h-9 w-24 shrink-0 overflow-visible\",\n        className,\n        tone === \"good\" ? \"text-chart-7\" : tone === \"bad\" ? \"text-chart-3\" : \"text-chart-neutral\",\n      )}\n    >\n      <defs>\n        <linearGradient id={gradientId} x1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y1=\"0\" y2=\"1\">\n          <stop offset=\"0\" stopColor=\"currentColor\" stopOpacity=\"0.28\" />\n          <stop offset=\"1\" stopColor=\"currentColor\" stopOpacity=\"0\" />\n        </linearGradient>\n      </defs>\n      <path d={area} fill={`url(#${gradientId})`} />\n      <path d={line} fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" strokeWidth={1.75} strokeLinecap=\"round\" strokeLinejoin=\"round\" />\n      <circle cx={last[0]} cy={last[1]} r={3} fill=\"currentColor\" className=\"stroke-background-secondary-default\" strokeWidth={1.5} />\n    </svg>\n  );\n}\n\n/** Good news is lime and bad news rose, whatever the sign: a rising expense\n *  line is bad, a falling one good. */\nfunction toneOf(change: number, goodDirection: \"up\" | \"down\"): \"good\" | \"bad\" | \"flat\" {\n  if (change === 0) return \"flat\";\n  return (change > 0) === (goodDirection === \"up\") ? \"good\" : \"bad\";\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component has no timers, listeners or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 200ms surface change when a card is selected; it carries `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the selection switches instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Colour the delta by good or bad news, not by sign. A +2.4% expense increase is bad and must not read lime.\n- Give each sparkline gradient its own id (`useId`). A shared id makes every card paint with the first card's colour.\n- A series with fewer than two points has no line; the sparkline is skipped rather than drawn as a dot.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `format` per item for non-currency metrics such as a savings rate.\n- Without `selectable` the cards are plain `<div>`s with no focus stops.\n- `sparkPoints` is exported for reuse in other tiny charts.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFour cards show Total balance $84,230 +3.2%, Income $18,420 +6.1%, Expenses $9,140 +2.4% and Savings $32,400 +8.4%. The Expenses chip and sparkline are rose, the others lime. Each sparkline ends in a dot at its last value. Clicking Income sets aria-pressed on it, lifts it to a white surface and the demo caption reads \"Charting Income\". The row shows four cards across at 1100px, two at medium widths and, at 360px, one compact single-line card per row so all four plus the caption stay on screen. Light and dark both read correctly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "floating-stickers": "# Build: Floating Stickers\n\nPlayful stickers for a landing section, like the characters that peek over a footer line. Each sticker sits at a fractional position in its field and bobs on a slow float whose duration, phase, lift and sway come from a seed, so every load looks the same. Grab one with a pointer and it scales up, gains a deeper shadow and rises to the top of the stack. It follows the pointer through pointer capture and stays clamped to the field. Focused stickers move with the arrow keys.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDie-cut SVG characters that bob on seeded floats. Drag one and it lifts above the rest, clamped to the frame. Arrow keys nudge it.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is an `@container` field. Its `children` render in a `z-0` layer, and a `pointer-events-none` overlay above them holds the stickers. Each sticker is absolutely placed at `left: x%`, `top: y%` and centred with `translate(-50%, -50%)`. Its width is `min(size px, size/6.4 cqw)`, so it shrinks with a field narrower than 640px.\n2. Every sticker has four layers. The outer layer carries the drag offset and the z-index. The lift layer scales to 1.1 and swaps `drop-shadow-md` for `drop-shadow-xl` while held. The float layer runs a Web Animations API loop, and the art layer holds the resting tilt. Because the float is WAAPI on `transform`, it runs on the compositor and never re-renders React.\n3. Float parameters come from `createRng(`${seed}:${id}`)`: a duration of 4.2 to 7.4s, a negative start delay so the stickers start out of phase, 5 to 11px of lift, ±3px of drift and 1.5 to 4.5° of sway. The loop alternates with ease-in-out, and is paused on grab and resumed on drop.\n4. On pointerdown the sticker calls `setPointerCapture`, records the start pointer and offset, and computes bounds once: the field rect minus the sticker's resting rect. A sticker placed partly outside the field keeps its current offset as a valid edge, so it never jumps. Pointermove clamps the offset into those bounds. Pointerup, pointercancel and lostpointercapture all end the drag through one handler.\n5. Stacking is an order array in the parent, with the last id on top. Grabbing or key-nudging a sticker moves its id to the end, and each sticker's z-index is its rank + 1. The top sticker carries `data-top`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `float = 4200 to 7400ms, alternate, ease-in-out, lift 5 to 11px, sway 1.5 to 4.5°`\n  At this pace the motion reads as idle breathing rather than a call to action, and differing durations keep the stickers from syncing up.\n- `grab lift = scale 1.1, drop-shadow-md → drop-shadow-xl, 200ms ease-out`\n  A sticker peeled off a page gets bigger and casts a softer, larger shadow. The scale is on its own layer, so it never fights the drag offset or the float.\n- `key step = 10px, Shift 40px`\n  Small enough to place precisely, and Shift crosses the field in a few presses.\n- `responsive size = min(size px, size / 6.4 cqw)`\n  Full size from a 640px field. At a 328px phone field an 88px sticker drops to about 45px and stays clear of the headline.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FloatingStickers(props: { stickers: StickerItem[]; seed?: string; draggable?: boolean; onGrab?: (id: string) => void; onMove?: (id: string, offset: { x: number; y: number }) => void; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `stickers: StickerItem[]`\n  `{ id, label, kind?, art?, x, y, size?, rotate? }`. x and y are 0 to 1 fractions of the field, `kind` is blob, star, cloud, heart or chart, and `art` is custom artwork.\n- `seed: string` — default `\"kinetic\"`\n  Seeds every float. Same seed, same motion.\n- `draggable: boolean` — default `true`\n  Enables pointer drag and arrow-key nudging. When false, the stickers are decorative and aria-hidden.\n- `onGrab: (id) => void`\n  Fires on pointer grab and key nudge.\n- `onMove: (id, offset) => void`\n  Fires on drop (if moved) and on each key nudge, with the offset in px from the resting place.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Section content beneath the stickers.\n- `className: string`\n  Field classes. Give it a height.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction Sticker({ item, seed, draggable, reduced, z, top, fieldRef, raise, onMove }: StickerProps) {\n  const [offset, setOffset] = useState<StickerOffset>({ x: 0, y: 0 });\n  const [dragging, setDragging] = useState(false);\n  const floatRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const anim = useRef<Animation | null>(null);\n  const drag = useRef<{ pointer: number; sx: number; sy: number; ox: number; oy: number; x: number; y: number; b: Bounds } | null>(null);\n  const size = item.size ?? 88;\n\n  // The float runs on the compositor through WAAPI, so a bobbing sticker\n  // never re-renders React. Reduced motion skips it: the sticker rests still.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = floatRef.current;\n    if (!el || reduced || typeof el.animate !== \"function\") return;\n    const p = floatParams(seed, item.id);\n    const a = el.animate(\n      [\n        { transform: `translate3d(0, 0, 0) rotate(${-p.sway}deg)` },\n        { transform: `translate3d(${p.drift}px, ${-p.lift}px, 0) rotate(${p.sway}deg)` },\n      ],\n      { duration: p.duration, delay: p.delay, iterations: Infinity, direction: \"alternate\", easing: \"ease-in-out\" },\n    );\n    anim.current = a;\n    return () => {\n      a.cancel();\n      anim.current = null;\n    };\n  }, [reduced, seed, item.id]);\n\n  /** Allowed offsets that keep the sticker box inside the field. A sticker placed half outside may stay there. */\n  const bounds = (el: HTMLElement): Bounds | null => {\n    const field = fieldRef.current;\n    if (!field) return null;\n    const f = field.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const r = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n    return {\n      minX: Math.min(f.left - (r.left - offset.x), offset.x),\n      maxX: Math.max(f.right - (r.right - offset.x), offset.x),\n      minY: Math.min(f.top - (r.top - offset.y), offset.y),\n      maxY: Math.max(f.bottom - (r.bottom - offset.y), offset.y),\n    };\n  };\n\n  const onPointerDown = (event: PointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (!draggable || event.button !== 0 || drag.current) return;\n    const b = bounds(event.currentTarget);\n    if (!b) return;\n    event.currentTarget.setPointerCapture(event.pointerId);\n    drag.current = { pointer: event.pointerId, sx: event.clientX, sy: event.clientY, ox: offset.x, oy: offset.y, x: offset.x, y: offset.y, b };\n    anim.current?.pause();\n    setDragging(true);\n    raise(item.id);\n  };\n\n  const onPointerMove = (event: PointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    const d = drag.current;\n    if (!d || d.pointer !== event.pointerId) return;\n    d.x = clamp(d.ox + event.clientX - d.sx, d.b.minX, d.b.maxX);\n    d.y = clamp(d.oy + event.clientY - d.sy, d.b.minY, d.b.maxY);\n    setOffset({ x: d.x, y: d.y });\n  };\n\n  // Up, cancel and lost capture all land here; the first one wins.\n  const end = (event: PointerEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    const d = drag.current;\n    if (!d || d.pointer !== event.pointerId) return;\n    drag.current = null;\n    anim.current?.play();\n    setDragging(false);\n    if (d.x !== d.ox || d.y !== d.oy) onMove?.(item.id, { x: Math.round(d.x), y: Math.round(d.y) });\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    const step = KEY_STEPS[event.key];\n    if (!draggable || !step || drag.current) return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    const b = bounds(event.currentTarget);\n    if (!b) return;\n    const by = event.shiftKey ? 40 : 10;\n    const next = { x: clamp(offset.x + step[0] * by, b.minX, b.maxX), y: clamp(offset.y + step[1] * by, b.minY, b.maxY) };\n    raise(item.id);\n    if (next.x === offset.x && next.y === offset.y) return;\n    setOffset(next);\n    onMove?.(item.id, { x: Math.round(next.x), y: Math.round(next.y) });\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      data-sticker={item.id}\n      data-top={top ? \"\" : undefined}\n      data-dragging={dragging ? \"\" : undefined}\n      role=\"img\"\n      aria-label={item.label}\n      aria-roledescription={draggable ? \"draggable sticker\" : undefined}\n      aria-keyshortcuts={draggable ? \"ArrowUp ArrowDown ArrowLeft ArrowRight\" : undefined}\n      tabIndex={draggable ? 0 : undefined}\n      onPointerDown={onPointerDown}\n      onPointerMove={onPointerMove}\n      onPointerUp={end}\n      onPointerCancel={end}\n      onLostPointerCapture={end}\n      onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n      className={cx(\n        \"absolute aspect-square rounded-[28%] outline-none\",\n        draggable &&\n          \"pointer-events-auto cursor-grab touch-none select-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-background-primary-default\",\n        dragging && \"cursor-grabbing\",\n      )}\n      style={{\n        left: `${item.x * 100}%`,\n        top: `${item.y * 100}%`,\n        width: `min(${size}px, ${(size / 6.4).toFixed(2)}cqw)`,\n        zIndex: z,\n        transform: `translate(-50%, -50%) translate3d(${offset.x}px, ${offset.y}px, 0)`,\n      }}\n    >\n      <div\n        className={cx(\n          \"size-full transition-[transform,filter] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          dragging ? \"scale-110 drop-shadow-xl\" : \"drop-shadow-md\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <div ref={floatRef} className=\"size-full\">\n          <div className=\"size-full\" style={{ transform: `rotate(${item.rotate ?? 0}deg)` }}>\n            {item.art ?? <StickerArtwork kind={item.kind ?? \"blob\"} className=\"size-full\" />}\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Each float animation is cancelled in its effect cleanup, which runs on unmount, on a reduced-motion change or on a new seed. Pointer listeners are React props on the sticker, and pointer capture is released by the browser on pointerup. Lost capture ends the drag through the same handler, so no drag state outlives the gesture.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion, `useReducedMotion` skips the WAAPI float, so every sticker rests still at its tilt. The lift scale and shadow apply instantly (`motion-reduce:transition-none`). Dragging and key nudging still work, because they follow the user's own input.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Putting the float and the drag on the same `transform`. Each would overwrite the other every frame, so the sticker snaps back mid-drag. Keep separate nested layers.\n- Skipping `touch-action: none`. On touch screens the browser starts scrolling and fires pointercancel after a few pixels, so the drag dies. The draggable stickers carry `touch-none`.\n- Seeding with `Math.random()`. The server and client would render different phases, so hydration fails and screenshots never match. Use the seeded rng.\n- Clamping against bounds computed on every move. Reading layout in each pointermove is a forced reflow per frame. Bounds are computed once on pointerdown.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `draggable={false}` for pure decoration. Stickers then ignore the pointer and are hidden from assistive tech.\n- To reset positions, remount with a new `key` (the demo's Reset button does this). Offsets are internal state relative to each resting place.\n- Give the field an explicit height. The overlay is `absolute inset-0`, so it covers whatever box the children produce.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFive stickers (blob, star, chart, cloud, heart) bob out of sync around the \"Make it yours\" headline. Dragging the star moves it with the pointer, scales it up, puts it above the other stickers and keeps it inside the field, even when the pointer leaves it. The status line reads \"Sleepy star moved +x, +y px\". A focused sticker moves 10px per arrow key. Reset puts every sticker back. Under reduced motion nothing bobs. At 360px the stickers shrink and stay clear of the copy.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "font-safe-text-split": "# Build: Font-Safe Text Splitting\n\nSplitting text into lines is a measurement, and measuring before the webfont arrives measures the fallback face. The line boxes are then wrapped in the wrong places and never re-flow when the real font swaps in — with a line mask, the visible symptom is words sliced in half by an invisible edge, and it only reproduces on a cold cache. This component owns the whole lifecycle: gate the first split on `document.fonts.ready`, re-split when a late font finishes loading, and re-split on width change behind a debounce.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLine splitting gated on document.fonts.ready, re-split on late fonts and on width change, debounced.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The first split is deferred behind `document.fonts.ready`, so the browser has finished laying out with the real faces before any line box is measured.\n2. A `loadingdone` listener on `document.fonts` re-applies the split. Fonts requested after first paint — a lazily mounted section, a second weight, an icon face — trigger another layout pass that moves the line breaks under wrappers that were already fixed.\n3. A `ResizeObserver` on the container re-applies the split behind a `debounceMs` timer. Line breaks are a function of container width and nothing else recomputes them; without this, rotating a phone leaves the wrappers describing a layout that no longer exists.\n4. The resize guard compares `Math.round(entry.contentRect.width)` against the last value and returns when they match. On iOS the URL bar collapsing changes viewport *height* and fires resize; re-splitting there destroys and rebuilds the DOM mid-scroll for no benefit, on the device least able to spare the frames.\n5. `apply()` always tears down first — `ctx.revert()` then `split.revert()` — so a re-split never nests one set of line wrappers inside the previous set. Nesting is what turns three re-splits into a paragraph wrapped nine deep.\n6. A `revealed` flag is set by the ScrollTrigger `onEnter`. After the entrance has played, a re-split writes `gsap.set(lines, { yPercent: 0, opacity: 1 })` instead of `gsap.from(...)`, so rotating the device does not replay the entrance from off-screen.\n7. `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` runs after every split, because wrapping lines changes this element's height and every trigger positioned below it is now measuring against stale coordinates.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DEBOUNCE_MS = 200`\n  Milliseconds of quiet after the last resize before re-splitting. A split is a full teardown and rebuild of the subtree; running it per resize event during a window drag rebuilds it dozens of times a second. Below ~120ms a drag still triggers repeatedly; past ~350ms the text is visibly broken while the user waits.\n- `Y = 110`\n  Percent of a line's own height it starts below the mask. Past 100 rather than at it, because the mask clips to the line box and descenders otherwise peek below the mask edge on the first frame.\n- `DURATION = 0.9`\n  Seconds per line. `power3.out` needs this much room for its deceleration to read; below ~0.5s the curve flattens and lines snap into place.\n- `STAGGER = 0.09`\n  Seconds between lines. Roughly a tenth of the duration, so several lines are always rising together and the block reads as one mass rather than as a queue.\n- `START = 'top 88%'`\n  The reveal fires as the block's top edge clears the bottom eighth of the viewport — after it is unambiguously on screen, but before the reader has arrived at it.\n- `width comparison = Math.round(contentRect.width)`\n  Sub-pixel `contentRect` values differ by fractions on a scrollbar appearing or a zoom level change, so an exact float comparison never matches and the debounce fires forever. Rounding to whole pixels is the smallest unit a line break can actually respond to.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FontSafeTextSplit(props: { children: ReactNode; type?: string; mask?: boolean; y?: number; duration?: number; stagger?: number; start?: string; scroller?: Element | null; debounceMs?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Text or markup to split into lines.\n- `type: string` — default `'lines'`\n  SplitText `type`. Lines are what the mask and the re-split are for.\n- `mask: boolean` — default `true`\n  Wrap each line in an overflow-hidden box.\n- `y: number` — default `110`\n  Percent of a line's height it starts below the mask.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.9`\n  Seconds per line.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.09`\n  Seconds between lines.\n- `start: string` — default `'top 88%'`\n  ScrollTrigger `start` for the reveal.\n- `scroller: Element | null` — default `null`\n  Scroll container, when the text is not in the document scroller.\n- `debounceMs: number` — default `200`\n  Milliseconds of quiet after the last resize before re-splitting.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst Y = 110;\nconst DURATION = 0.9;\nconst STAGGER = 0.09;\nconst START = 'top 88%';\nconst DEBOUNCE_MS = 200;\n\n/**\n * A stable string identity for `children`: the text that will be measured and\n * split, plus the element tags inside it.\n *\n * React builds a fresh node object on every parent render for anything that is\n * not a bare string, so naming `children` in a dependency array actually says\n * \"tear the whole lifecycle down and rebuild it whenever the parent renders,\n * for any reason at all\". That costs more than a wasted split here. `revealed`\n * is a local of the effect, so a rebuild resets it to false; the `once: true`\n * ScrollTrigger is then recreated on an element already past its start and\n * fires `onEnter` straight away. A paragraph the reader is half way through\n * drops back below the mask and plays its entrance a second time, and the\n * tween that was running is discarded with the context that owned it. A\n * sibling toggling a menu is enough to trigger it.\n *\n * Dropping the dependency outright is not the fix either — the line wrappers\n * would go on describing text that is no longer there. So the node is\n * flattened to the only things a line measurement depends on, and that string\n * is the dependency. Same content, same string, no re-split.\n */\nfunction childrenSignature(node: ReactNode): string {\n  if (node === null || node === undefined || typeof node === 'boolean') return '';\n  if (typeof node === 'string' || typeof node === 'number' || typeof node === 'bigint') {\n    return String(node);\n  }\n  if (Array.isArray(node)) {\n    // A separator that cannot occur in text, so ['ab', 'c'] and ['a', 'bc'] do\n    // not collapse onto the same key.\n    return (node as ReactNode[]).map(childrenSignature).join('\\u0001');\n  }\n  if (isValidElement<{ children?: ReactNode }>(node)) {\n    // The tag belongs in the signature. SplitText walks the markup it is given,\n    // and an <em>, a <strong> or a <br> moves the line boxes even when the text\n    // reads exactly the same.\n    const { type } = node;\n    const tag =\n      typeof type === 'string'\n        ? type\n        : (type as { displayName?: string; name?: string }).displayName ||\n          (type as { name?: string }).name ||\n          'component';\n    return `<${tag}>${childrenSignature(node.props.children)}`;\n  }\n  // Iterables, portals and promises have no cheap textual identity. A constant\n  // under-reports a change, which is the safe direction: the alternative is a\n  // fresh value every render, i.e. exactly the bug this function exists to\n  // remove.\n  return '';\n}\n\nexport function FontSafeTextSplit({\n  children,\n  type = 'lines',\n  mask = true,\n  y = Y,\n  duration = DURATION,\n  stagger = STAGGER,\n  start = START,\n  scroller = null,\n  debounceMs = DEBOUNCE_MS,\n  className,\n}: FontSafeTextSplitProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Compared by value, not by identity. The node itself still goes into the\n  // tree below — only the effect's notion of \"the content changed\" is derived\n  // from this string. See `childrenSignature` for what listing `children` here\n  // costs.\n  const contentKey = childrenSignature(children);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    // Reduced motion never splits. The markup React rendered is the finished\n    // state, so the entire font/resize lifecycle below is unnecessary work.\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n\n    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger, SplitText);\n\n    let cancelled = false;\n    let split: SplitText | null = null;\n    let ctx: gsap.Context | null = null;\n    let timer = 0;\n    // Once the lines have been revealed, a re-split must render them at their\n    // final position. Replaying the entrance because the user rotated their\n    // phone is the classic symptom of a re-split that forgot it had run.\n    let revealed = false;\n    // Last measured *width*. Height changes are ignored on purpose.\n    let lastWidth = Math.round(el.getBoundingClientRect().width);\n\n    const teardown = () => {\n      ctx?.revert();\n      ctx = null;\n      split?.revert();\n      split = null;\n    };\n\n    const apply = () => {\n      if (cancelled) return;\n      teardown();\n\n      const s = new SplitText(el, {\n        type,\n        mask: mask ? 'lines' : undefined,\n        linesClass: 'kinetic-fs-line',\n        // SplitText restores the original text for assistive technology.\n        // Without it a screen reader announces one fragment per line box.\n        aria: 'auto',\n      });\n      split = s;\n\n      ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n        if (revealed) {\n          // Already seen. Land the new line boxes in their final state.\n          gsap.set(s.lines, { yPercent: 0, opacity: 1 });\n          return;\n        }\n        gsap.from(s.lines, {\n          yPercent: y,\n          duration,\n          stagger,\n          ease: 'power3.out',\n          scrollTrigger: {\n            trigger: el,\n            scroller: scroller ?? undefined,\n            start,\n            once: true,\n            onEnter: () => {\n              revealed = true;\n            },\n          },\n        });\n      }, el);\n\n      // Wrapping lines changes this element's height, so every trigger below\n      // it on the page is now measuring against stale coordinates.\n      ScrollTrigger.refresh();\n    };\n\n    // 1. The first split waits for the fonts the document already knows about.\n    void document.fonts.ready.then(apply);\n\n    // 2. A font requested later — a lazy section, a second weight, an icon\n    //    face — triggers another layout pass that moves the line breaks.\n    const onFontsDone = () => apply();\n    document.fonts.addEventListener('loadingdone', onFontsDone);\n\n    // 3. Line breaks are a function of container width. Nothing else\n    //    recomputes them, so a resize without a re-split leaves the wrappers\n    //    describing a layout that no longer exists.\n    const ro = new ResizeObserver((entries) => {\n      const entry = entries[0];\n      if (!entry) return;\n      const width = Math.round(entry.contentRect.width);\n      // Width-only. iOS fires resize when the URL bar collapses, which changes\n      // height only; re-splitting there rebuilds the DOM mid-scroll for\n      // nothing and drops frames on the device least able to spare them.\n      if (width === lastWidth) return;\n      lastWidth = width;\n      window.clearTimeout(timer);\n      timer = window.setTimeout(apply, debounceMs);\n    });\n    ro.observe(el);\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n      window.clearTimeout(timer);\n      ro.disconnect();\n      document.fonts.removeEventListener('loadingdone', onFontsDone);\n      teardown();\n    };\n  }, [\n    contentKey,\n    type,\n    mask,\n    y,\n    duration,\n    stagger,\n    start,\n    scroller,\n    debounceMs,\n    reduced,\n  ]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Sets a `cancelled` flag that closes the async `document.fonts.ready` gap, clears the pending debounce timeout, disconnects the `ResizeObserver`, removes the `loadingdone` listener, then reverts the `gsap.context` and the split in that order. Nothing can re-split or tween after unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The effect returns before registering anything: no split, no font listener, no ResizeObserver. The text React rendered is the finished state and is fully visible on first paint — which also means the entire measurement lifecycle costs nothing for users who asked for less motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Splitting synchronously on mount. On a cold cache the fallback face is measured, the line wrappers are fixed at the wrong break points, and when the real font swaps in the masked lines clip words in half. It works perfectly on every reload after the first, which is why it ships.\n- Waiting only on `document.fonts.ready` and stopping there. It resolves once for the fonts the document knew about at that moment; a font requested later re-flows the text under wrappers that are already fixed.\n- Re-splitting on every resize event. Each split is a full DOM teardown and rebuild, so dragging a window edge rebuilds the subtree dozens of times a second and the page locks up.\n- Re-splitting on any resize rather than on width change. iOS fires resize on every URL-bar collapse, so scrolling on a phone continuously destroys and rebuilds the paragraph.\n- Comparing raw `contentRect.width` floats. They wobble by fractions of a pixel and the debounce re-arms forever.\n- Re-splitting without reverting the previous split first. The new line wrappers nest inside the old ones, and after a few resizes the paragraph is wrapped several layers deep with stale masks at every level.\n- Replaying the entrance on re-split. The text drops back below its mask and rises again because the user rotated their phone, which reads as a rendering glitch.\n- Skipping `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` after the split. The element's height changed, so every trigger below it on the page fires at the wrong scroll position — usually the bug reported as \"the section below animates too early\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- This is one of the two components that deliberately use GSAP `SplitText`, because the technique *is* SplitText's lifecycle. `SplitText` ships inside the main `gsap` package and is free to use; it is imported from `gsap/SplitText` rather than vendored, so this is a use grant with no redistribution. The other text components in this set split declaratively in JSX so consumers are not forced into GSAP for effects that do not need it.\n- Every other split-based component in the registry inherits this problem. If you split text anywhere — by hand, with `split-type`, or with SplitText — the `document.fonts.ready` gate and the width-only debounced re-split apply just as much.\n- GSAP 3.13+ offers `autoSplit` and `onRevert`, which cover part of this. The explicit lifecycle here is kept because it also handles the late-`loadingdone` case and gives you the `revealed` flag, and because it is legible: you can see exactly which of the three triggers fired.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHard-reload with the cache disabled and throttled network: the lines break correctly and no word is clipped by a mask edge, on the first load as well as subsequent ones. Drag the window narrower and the lines re-break about 200ms after you stop, once, without replaying the entrance. Scroll on an iOS device and the paragraph never rebuilds as the URL bar collapses. Resize repeatedly and inspect the DOM: line wrappers are exactly one level deep, never nested. A section below the text still triggers at the correct scroll position after a re-split.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "fov-derived-row-step": "# Build: FOV-Derived Row Spacing\n\nA perspective row that never guesses its spacing. Given a vertical field of view and a camera distance, it computes the visible width on the row plane, divides it across the gaps between item centres, and converts to pixels once at the end. The outermost item centres land on the frame edges by construction, at any aspect ratio, with no breakpoint table and no tuned magic number. It is the flat pre-intro layout of a three.js drum, done in the DOM with the identical arithmetic.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA row whose item spacing is solved from a field of view and a camera distance, so it spans the frame exactly at any size.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A pinhole camera at distance d from a plane sees `visibleHeight = 2 · d · tan(fov / 2)` on that plane, and `visibleWidth = visibleHeight · aspect`. Both quantities are in world units; nothing pixel-sized is involved yet.\n2. The step is `visibleWidth · spanRatio / (n − 1)` and item centres are `x_i = i · step − rowWidth / 2` where `rowWidth = (n − 1) · step`. The subtraction centres the row on the origin without a second measurement, because the outermost centres are at ±rowWidth/2 by definition.\n3. World units become pixels through a single scalar, `pxPerUnit = heightPx / visibleHeight`, applied at the last multiplication. Every intermediate value stays resolution-independent, which is why a resize needs no re-derivation of anything but this one number.\n4. CSS `perspective` is set to `cameraZ · pxPerUnit`, which reduces to `heightPx / (2 · tan(fov / 2))` — the cameraZ cancels. That cancellation is the proof that the world-unit scale and the CSS projection describe the same camera: if it did not cancel, the two coordinate systems would disagree and depth would foreshorten by the wrong amount.\n5. Items are placed with `translate3d(x · pxPerUnit, 0, z · pxPerUnit)` and the browser performs the perspective divide. An element at world z projects with scale `cameraZ / (cameraZ − z)`, which is exactly what a `PerspectiveCamera` does to a plane at the same depth.\n6. The optional bow pushes outer items away on a parabola, `z = −bow · t²` with `t = x / (visibleWidth / 2)`. Depth would shrink their projected x, so the unprojected x is pre-multiplied by `(cameraZ − z) / cameraZ` — the exact inverse of the divide — and the row still spans the frame with the bow at any strength.\n7. The four camera scalars are damped rather than assigned, so changing the field of view reads as a dolly. The layout is a pure function of them, so an interruption mid-spread cannot desynchronise anything: there is no accumulated position to corrupt.\n8. The frame loop snaps inside an epsilon, writes one final layout, and then returns immediately. Exponential damping never actually arrives, so without that gate the component would rewrite n transforms every frame forever for a row that has visibly stopped moving.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `visibleHeight = 2 · cameraZ · tan(fov / 2)`\n  The exact height of the frustum cross-section at the row plane. Half the FOV subtends a right triangle of opposite `visibleHeight / 2` over adjacent `cameraZ`, so `tan(fov / 2) = (visibleHeight / 2) / cameraZ`. The factor of 2 and the half-angle are the two places this is most often written wrong; using `tan(fov)` overstates the span by roughly 3x at 70 degrees.\n- `step = visibleWidth · spanRatio / (n − 1)`\n  n − 1, not n. The step is the distance between adjacent item CENTRES, and n centres have n − 1 gaps between them. Dividing by n leaves the row one step short, so the last item never reaches the frame edge — the discrepancy is 1/n, which is invisible at twenty items and glaring at four.\n- `x_i = i · step − rowWidth / 2`\n  Centres the row on the origin arithmetically. With `rowWidth = (n − 1) · step`, item 0 sits at −rowWidth/2 and item n−1 at +rowWidth/2, so the row is symmetric for both odd and even counts without a special case and without measuring the assembled row afterwards.\n- `perspective = heightPx / (2 · tan(fov / 2))`\n  The CSS perspective distance in pixels that reproduces the same vertical FOV over a box of height H. Derived by asking at what distance the visible height equals H: `H = 2 · P · tan(fov / 2)`. It is `cameraZ · pxPerUnit` with the cameraZ cancelled, which is why the row can be moved nearer or further without touching this value.\n- `depthScale = (cameraZ − z) / cameraZ`\n  Perspective divides a point's projected x by `(cameraZ − z) / cameraZ`, so pre-multiplying the unprojected x by the same factor leaves the projection exactly where the flat row put it. Omitting it makes any bow pull the outer items inward, which is the bug usually patched by nudging spanRatio above 1 until it happens to look right at one screen size.\n- `fov = 70`\n  Degrees, vertical. Wide enough that the bow foreshortens visibly rather than reading as a flat scale-down. Above ~90 the near items grow fast enough to clip the frame; below ~40 the perspective is so weak that the depth compensation has nothing to compensate for and the row may as well be 2D.\n- `cameraZ = 5`\n  World units. It sets nothing visible on its own — it only fixes the unit scale, since a larger distance widens the visible area by the same ratio it shrinks everything on screen. 5 keeps the bow and the item sizes in a range where single decimal digits are meaningful, matching the drum this arithmetic came from.\n- `bow = 0.6`\n  World units of depth at the frame edge, about 12% of cameraZ. Enough that the outer items read as receding; past ~0.3 · cameraZ they shrink far enough that the row looks like a fisheye rather than a row, and the compensated x starts pushing content beyond the overflow clip.\n- `smoothing = 0.0015`\n  Fraction of the remaining gap left after one second, so the row is ~99.9% of the way to a new field of view within that second. Frame-rate independent by construction, so a 120 Hz display and a throttled tab converge on the same curve rather than the same per-frame ratio.\n- `EPSILON = 0.0005`\n  Summed absolute gap across the four scalars below which the layout snaps and the loop stops writing. Small enough to be sub-pixel at any plausible pxPerUnit, large enough that exponential damping crosses it in under a second instead of asymptoting forever.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FovDerivedRow(props: { children: ReactNode; fov?: number; cameraZ?: number; spanRatio?: number; bow?: number; smoothing?: number; className?: string; cellClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One positioned cell per child, laid out on the derived step.\n- `fov: number` — default `70`\n  Vertical field of view in degrees. Drives both the spacing and the CSS perspective.\n- `cameraZ: number` — default `5`\n  Camera distance to the row plane in world units. Fixes the unit scale.\n- `spanRatio: number` — default `1`\n  Fraction of the visible width the row spans. 1 puts the outer centres on the frame edges.\n- `bow: number` — default `0.6`\n  World units the outermost items recede. Their x is compensated so the span is preserved.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0015`\n  Fraction of the gap remaining after one second while the row re-spreads.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the stage element.\n- `cellClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every positioned cell. Size the items here.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function FovDerivedRow({\n  children,\n  fov = FOV,\n  cameraZ = CAMERA_Z,\n  spanRatio = SPAN_RATIO,\n  bow = BOW,\n  smoothing = SMOOTHING,\n  className,\n  cellClassName,\n}: FovDerivedRowProps) {\n  const stage = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const cells = useRef<Array<HTMLDivElement | null>>([]);\n\n  const size = useResizeObserver(stage);\n  const { inView } = useInView(stage, { threshold: 0 });\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const items = Children.toArray(children);\n  const count = items.length;\n\n  /* The three scalars the layout is a pure function of. They are damped rather\n     than assigned so that changing the FOV reads as a dolly, not as a jump —\n     and because the layout is a pure function of them, interrupting mid-spread\n     cannot desynchronise anything. */\n  const live = useRef({ fov, cameraZ, spanRatio, bow });\n  const current = useRef({ fov, cameraZ, spanRatio, bow });\n  const settled = useRef(false);\n  live.current = { fov, cameraZ, spanRatio, bow };\n\n  /**\n   * Lay the row out for one set of camera scalars. Pure: same inputs, same\n   * transforms, no accumulated state.\n   */\n  const apply = (params: { fov: number; cameraZ: number; spanRatio: number; bow: number }) => {\n    const el = stage.current;\n    if (!el || count === 0) return;\n\n    const widthPx = el.clientWidth;\n    const heightPx = el.clientHeight;\n    if (widthPx < 1 || heightPx < 1) return;\n\n    // Half-angle first: every formula below wants tan(fov/2), never tan(fov).\n    const tanHalfFov = Math.tan((params.fov * Math.PI) / 360);\n    const visibleHeight = 2 * params.cameraZ * tanHalfFov;\n    const visibleWidth = visibleHeight * (widthPx / heightPx);\n\n    // World units to CSS pixels. The whole layout is computed in world units\n    // and converted exactly once, here.\n    const pxPerUnit = heightPx / visibleHeight;\n    // P = H / (2 tan(fov/2)). Independent of cameraZ, which is the sanity\n    // check that the world-unit scale and the projection agree.\n    const perspectivePx = params.cameraZ * pxPerUnit;\n\n    // n − 1, not n: the step is the distance between adjacent CENTRES, and a\n    // row of n centres has n − 1 gaps. Dividing by n leaves the row short by\n    // one step and the last item never reaches the frame edge.\n    const gaps = Math.max(count - 1, 1);\n    const step = count > 1 ? (visibleWidth * params.spanRatio) / gaps : 0;\n    const rowWidth = (count - 1) * step;\n    const halfSpan = Math.max(visibleWidth / 2, 1e-6);\n\n    el.style.perspective = `${perspectivePx}px`;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      const cell = cells.current[i];\n      if (!cell) continue;\n\n      const flatX = i * step - rowWidth / 2;\n\n      // Parabolic bow: 0 at the centre, `bow` units back at the frame edge.\n      const t = flatX / halfSpan;\n      const worldZ = -params.bow * t * t;\n\n      // Depth compensation. Moving a point from z = 0 to z shrinks its\n      // projected x by cameraZ / (cameraZ − z), so scaling the unprojected x\n      // by the inverse leaves the projection exactly where the flat row put\n      // it. Without this line, any bow pulls the outer items inward and the\n      // row stops spanning the frame — the bug that gets \"fixed\" by nudging\n      // spanRatio past 1 until it looks right at one screen size.\n      const depthScale = (params.cameraZ - worldZ) / params.cameraZ;\n      const worldX = flatX * depthScale;\n\n      cell.style.transform =\n        `translate(-50%, -50%) translate3d(${worldX * pxPerUnit}px, 0px, ${worldZ * pxPerUnit}px)`;\n    }\n  };\n\n  // Resize is not an animation: re-derive and write immediately, or the row\n  // lags a visible beat behind a window drag.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    apply(current.current);\n    // `apply` closes over refs only; re-running on every render would fight\n    // the frame loop for the same transforms.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [size.width, size.height, count]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!reduced) return;\n    // Reduced motion is the resolved layout, not a fast spread: adopt the\n    // target scalars outright and lay out once.\n    current.current = { ...live.current };\n    settled.current = false;\n    apply(current.current);\n  }, [reduced, fov, cameraZ, spanRatio, bow]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const target = live.current;\n      const now = current.current;\n\n      const next = {\n        fov: damp(now.fov, target.fov, smoothing, dt),\n        cameraZ: damp(now.cameraZ, target.cameraZ, smoothing, dt),\n        spanRatio: damp(now.spanRatio, target.spanRatio, smoothing, dt),\n        bow: damp(now.bow, target.bow, smoothing, dt),\n      };\n\n      // Exponential damping never actually arrives, so it would rewrite n\n      // transforms forever. Snap inside epsilon, lay out one last time, and\n      // then skip the writes entirely until a prop moves again.\n      const gap =\n        Math.abs(next.fov - target.fov) +\n        Math.abs(next.cameraZ - target.cameraZ) +\n        Math.abs(next.spanRatio - target.spanRatio) +\n        Math.abs(next.bow - target.bow);\n\n      if (gap < EPSILON) {\n        if (settled.current) return;\n        current.current = { ...target };\n        settled.current = true;\n        apply(current.current);\n        return;\n      }\n\n      settled.current = false;\n      current.current = next;\n      apply(next);\n    },\n    inView && !reduced,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes from the shared loop on unmount and cancels the frame when it is the last subscriber; `useResizeObserver` and `useInView` remove their targets from their shared observers. No listeners, timers or tweens are created directly. Inline transforms are written only to elements this component owns and rendered, so they are removed with them.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The reduced branch adopts the target scalars outright and lays the row out once, so the resolved geometry is on screen rather than a fast spread toward it. The frame loop is not subscribed at all, so no work is scheduled for a user who asked for none, and a later prop change still re-derives the layout immediately through the same code path. Because the layout is a pure function of the four scalars, the reduced render is pixel-identical to the settled animated one.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Spacing a perspective row by `containerWidth / n`. It is wrong twice: it uses n instead of n − 1, and it ignores that the row plane is not the screen plane. It looks correct at one viewport because the error was absorbed into a hand-tuned padding, and it breaks on the first aspect-ratio change.\n- Using `tan(fov)` instead of `tan(fov / 2)`. At 70 degrees that overstates the visible extent by about 3x, so the row spreads far past the frame. It survives review because the row still looks deliberate — just wildly too wide — and the fix gets applied as a 0.35 multiplier somewhere else.\n- Applying degrees to `Math.tan` directly. `Math.tan(70)` is a perfectly finite number, so nothing throws and nothing warns; the layout is simply nonsense. `(fov * Math.PI) / 360` converts and halves in one step, which is also why that constant is 360 and not 180.\n- Bowing items in z without compensating x. Depth shrinks projected x, so the outer items drift inward and the row silently stops spanning the frame. The formula is one multiplication and its absence is normally treated as a spacing problem rather than a projection one.\n- Setting `perspective` from a fixed pixel value while deriving the spacing from an FOV. The two then describe different cameras: the arithmetic places items for one frustum and the browser projects them through another, so depth foreshortens by the wrong amount and no amount of spacing tuning fixes it.\n- Deriving the layout from `window.innerWidth`. The component is correct on load and wrong the moment it sits in a resizable panel or a grid cell that reflows. Its own `clientWidth` and `clientHeight` are the only inputs that stay true, which is what `useResizeObserver` supplies.\n- Letting the damping loop run forever. Exponential approach never reaches its target, so a settled row keeps writing n transforms per frame and the profiler shows a permanent baseline cost for a component that has not moved in a minute. Snap inside an epsilon and return.\n- Recomputing on resize only inside the frame loop. A resize is not an animation; deferring it by a frame makes the row visibly lag a window drag by one tick, which reads as jank rather than as smoothing.\n- Omitting `transform-style: preserve-3d`, or setting the perspective on an ancestor with its own transform. Either flattens the subtree and every `translateZ` collapses to no effect, leaving a row that is correctly spaced but stubbornly two-dimensional.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The spacing arithmetic is identical to the flat pre-intro row of a three.js drum gallery, which is the point: the formula belongs to the projection, not to the renderer. Porting it means replacing one conversion line, and this component is the DOM half of the pair that `layout-lerp-scalar` interpolates away from.\n- With `bow: 0` and `spanRatio: 1` the component has a hands-on correctness test: the outermost item centres sit exactly on the left and right edges of the box, at every viewport, with no tuning. If they do not, one of the formulas above has been mistyped.\n- `spanRatio` is the only aesthetic knob here — everything else is determined. Values below 1 inset the row from the frame; above 1 it deliberately overflows the clip, which is useful when the outer items are meant to be cut off.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSet `bow` to 0 and the leftmost and rightmost item centres sit exactly on the edges of the box — resize the panel from narrow to wide and they stay there, with no jump and no breakpoint. Restore the bow and the outer items recede and shrink while their horizontal positions do not move. Raise the field of view and the row spreads on a smooth dolly rather than snapping. Item counts of 1 and 2 both render without a divide-by-zero or a NaN transform. With reduced motion on, the row is at its final geometry on first paint and the profiler shows no animation frames for it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "fractional-render-scale": "# Build: Fractional Render Scale\n\nA drifting fog field with a latent grid that develops under the cursor, rendered into a canvas whose backing store is deliberately 0.6x the size of the element it fills. The browser upscales it, and the upscale is invisible because the content was authored for it: no detail finer than a dozen pixels, and the one thing with an edge — the grid — has its line width specified in backing-store pixels rather than CSS pixels. It is a background you can afford, and a demonstration of the cheapest large performance win in WebGL.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA field rendered into a backing store 0.6x its box — 36% of the fragments — with a grid measured in texels so the upscale never shows.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The scale is applied through the pixel ratio: `renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr) * scale)` followed by `setSize(w, h, false)`. three then sizes the backing store to `floor(w * dpr * scale)` while the canvas keeps `width: 100%` from CSS, so one number controls the ratio and there is no second sizing path to keep in sync.\n2. The achieved ratio is measured rather than assumed. `setSize` floors, so `getDrawingBufferSize().x / cssWidth` can differ from `dpr * scale` by up to a pixel; that measured value becomes `uScale`, and every length the shader treats as a CSS pixel is `gl_FragCoord.xy / uScale`.\n3. The fog is three sines multiplied and added, then pushed through `smoothstep(-0.15, 1.15, fog)`. It is not trying to be fbm — it is trying to contain no feature smaller than roughly a dozen pixels, which is the precondition for a bilinear upscale being undetectable.\n4. The grid is drawn from `abs(fract(cell - 0.5) - 0.5) * pxPerCell`, which is the distance to the nearest line expressed in backing-store pixels, and a `smoothstep(width - 0.5, width + 0.5, d)` ramp across exactly one texel. A line specified this way is `1 / scale` CSS pixels wide after the upscale: softer as the scale drops, never thinner, so it cannot fall through the sampling grid.\n5. No `fwidth`. The grid is axis-aligned in screen space, so the derivative of the cell coordinate is a constant the shader already holds — `uScale * uGridSize` backing pixels per cell. Computing it directly is exact, is one multiply, and needs no derivatives extension.\n6. The geometry is a single triangle with vertices at (-1,-1), (3,-1) and (-1,3). It covers the viewport with three vertices and no diagonal, so the GPU never shades the seam pixels twice as it does where two triangles of a fullscreen quad meet.\n7. The pointer is smoothed by `useSmoothPointer` with `perFrameToSmoothing(0.05)`, which reproduces the source's `x += (target - x) * 0.05` at 60 Hz while staying frame-rate independent, and its `nx`/`ny` are flipped to GL's y-up before reaching the uniform.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `renderScale = 0.6`\n  Linear, so the saving is quadratic: 0.36 of the fragments, a 64% cut, about 2.8x less fragment work. A 2560x1440 window drops from 3.7 M fragments a frame to 1.3 M. Below roughly 0.5 the grid lines start to look conspicuously soft against crisp DOM text next to them; above 0.8 there is little left to win.\n- `MIN_SCALE = 0.25`\n  Floor on the prop. At 0.25 a one-texel line is already four CSS pixels wide — a third of a 44 px cell — and the grid stops reading as a grid and starts reading as a plaid.\n- `lineWidth = 1 backing pixel`\n  The unit is the whole point. One texel is the finest line the buffer can actually hold; asking for one CSS pixel at 0.6 scale asks for 0.6 of a texel, which the rasteriser answers with a dashed line that crawls as the box resizes.\n- `gridSize = 44 CSS pixels`\n  Authored in CSS pixels so the grid holds its apparent size when the render scale changes — the entire visual difference between 0.6 and 1.0 should be a slight softening, never a different layout.\n- `majorEvery = 5`\n  A 220 px major cell. Without a second scale the grid is a flat screen door; with one it acquires depth. Its line is 1.6x the minor width, which survives the upscale as a clear hierarchy where a colour difference alone would not.\n- `POINTER_ALPHA = 0.05 per frame at 60 Hz`\n  The source chose it to be slower than the eye expects, which reads as viscosity: about 330 ms to close 63% of the gap. Passed through `perFrameToSmoothing` so a 120 Hz display gets the same 330 ms rather than half of it.\n- `glow exponent = exp(-d * d * 2.2)`\n  A Gaussian on distance normalised by the radius, so `glowRadius` is where the glow has fallen to about 11%. A smoothstep here would put a visible circular edge on a field that has no other edges — the one place the upscale would be caught.\n- `grid reveal = 0.18 + 0.82 * glow`\n  The grid is mostly latent and the cursor develops it. At full strength everywhere it is a background; appearing under the pointer, it is a surface reacting to being touched. The 0.18 floor keeps the structure legible before the pointer arrives.\n- `POINTER_EPSILON = 0.0002`\n  Under reduced motion the image is a pure function of the pointer, so frames where it has not moved are skipped entirely. The threshold is well under one pixel of an ordinary box, so no real movement is ever swallowed.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FractionalRenderScale(props: { renderScale?: number; maxDpr?: number; gridSize?: number; majorEvery?: number; lineWidth?: number; glowRadius?: number; baseColor?: string; fogColor?: string; lineColor?: string; glowColor?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `renderScale: number` — default `0.6`\n  Backing store size relative to the display pixel ratio. Clamped to 0.25..1.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Cap on the device pixel ratio before the render scale is applied.\n- `gridSize: number` — default `44`\n  Grid spacing in CSS pixels. Floored at 4.\n- `majorEvery: number` — default `5`\n  Every nth line is drawn as a major one, 1.6x the width.\n- `lineWidth: number` — default `1`\n  Minor line width in backing-store pixels — not CSS pixels. Floored at 0.5.\n- `glowRadius: number` — default `240`\n  Cursor glow radius in CSS pixels, at which the glow has fallen to about 11%.\n- `baseColor: string` — default `'#080b12'`\n  Darkest colour of the field.\n- `fogColor: string` — default `'#1d3350'`\n  Colour the fog lifts toward.\n- `lineColor: string` — default `'#8fb6df'`\n  Grid line colour.\n- `glowColor: string` — default `'#3f7fd0'`\n  Additive colour under the pointer.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function FractionalRenderScale({\n  renderScale = 0.6,\n  maxDpr = 2,\n  gridSize = 44,\n  majorEvery = 5,\n  lineWidth = 1,\n  glowRadius = 240,\n  baseColor = '#080b12',\n  fogColor = '#1d3350',\n  lineColor = '#8fb6df',\n  glowColor = '#3f7fd0',\n  className,\n}: FractionalRenderScaleProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: POINTER_SMOOTHING });\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  /* Read inside resize and draw. Keeping them out of the effect's dependency\n     list means dragging a render-scale slider re-sizes the backing store\n     rather than tearing down the GL context on every step. */\n  const live = useRef({ renderScale, maxDpr, gridSize, majorEvery, lineWidth, glowRadius });\n  live.current = { renderScale, maxDpr, gridSize, majorEvery, lineWidth, glowRadius };\n\n  const lastPointer = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n        // Nothing in the field has a geometric edge, so MSAA has nothing to\n        // sample — and at a fractional scale it would be antialiasing the wrong\n        // grid anyway.\n        antialias: false,\n        // Opaque: the shader covers every pixel, and an alpha channel would make\n        // the compositor blend the canvas over the page for no reason.\n        alpha: false,\n        depth: false,\n        stencil: false,\n        // The entire point of the component. On a laptop with switchable\n        // graphics this keeps a decorative background off the discrete GPU.\n        powerPreference: 'low-power',\n      });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 1);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    // CSS size is 1x and fixed to the box; the backing store is set separately\n    // by setSize(..., false). The gap between the two IS the render scale.\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.Camera();\n\n    // One oversized triangle, not a quad. It covers the viewport with three\n    // vertices and no diagonal seam for the GPU to shade twice.\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute(\n      'position',\n      new THREE.BufferAttribute(new Float32Array([-1, -1, 0, 3, -1, 0, -1, 3, 0]), 3),\n    );\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1, 1) },\n      uScale: { value: 1 },\n      uTime: { value: 0 },\n      uPointer: { value: new THREE.Vector2(0.5, 0.5) },\n      uGridSize: { value: gridSize },\n      uMajorEvery: { value: majorEvery },\n      uLineWidth: { value: lineWidth },\n      uGlowRadius: { value: glowRadius },\n      uBaseColor: { value: new THREE.Color(baseColor) },\n      uFogColor: { value: new THREE.Color(fogColor) },\n      uLineColor: { value: new THREE.Color(lineColor) },\n      uGlowColor: { value: new THREE.Color(glowColor) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: FRACTIONAL_SCALE_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: FRACTIONAL_SCALE_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    // The triangle's vertices sit outside a unit bounding sphere, and there is\n    // no camera transform to cull against in the first place.\n    mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(mesh);\n\n    const buffer = new THREE.Vector2();\n\n    stage.current = {\n      resize(w, h) {\n        const state = live.current;\n        const scale = clamp(state.renderScale, MIN_SCALE, 1);\n        // Scale folded into the pixel ratio: three then multiplies it through\n        // setSize for the backing store while the CSS size stays whatever the\n        // stylesheet says. One number, no second sizing path to keep in sync.\n        renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(state.maxDpr) * scale);\n        renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n\n        renderer.getDrawingBufferSize(buffer);\n        const bw = Math.max(1, Math.round(buffer.x));\n        const bh = Math.max(1, Math.round(buffer.y));\n        uniforms.uResolution.value.set(bw, bh);\n        // Measured, not assumed. setSize floors, so the achieved ratio differs\n        // from dpr * scale by up to a pixel — and the grid is authored in CSS\n        // pixels, which would then drift against the layout.\n        uniforms.uScale.value = w > 0 ? bw / w : 1;\n\n        uniforms.uGridSize.value = Math.max(4, state.gridSize);\n        uniforms.uMajorEvery.value = Math.max(1, Math.round(state.majorEvery));\n        uniforms.uLineWidth.value = Math.max(0.5, state.lineWidth);\n        uniforms.uGlowRadius.value = Math.max(1, state.glowRadius);\n      },\n\n      draw(time, pointerX, pointerY) {\n        uniforms.uTime.value = time;\n        uniforms.uPointer.value.set(pointerX, pointerY);\n        renderer.render(scene, camera);\n      },\n    };\n\n    stage.current.resize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight);\n    stage.current.draw(0, 0.5, 0.5);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, baseColor, fogColor, lineColor, glowColor]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    current.resize(width, height);\n    // Resizing a canvas clears its drawing buffer. When time is frozen the\n    // loop skips every frame in which the pointer has not moved, so the\n    // redraw has to happen here or the field stays blank until it does. When\n    // time is running the next frame covers it a few milliseconds later.\n    if (reduced) {\n      const state = pointer.current;\n      current.draw(0, state.nx, 1 - state.ny);\n      lastPointer.current = { x: state.nx, y: 1 - state.ny };\n    }\n  }, [\n    width,\n    height,\n    reduced,\n    renderScale,\n    maxDpr,\n    gridSize,\n    majorEvery,\n    lineWidth,\n    glowRadius,\n    pointer,\n  ]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n\n      const state = pointer.current;\n      const px = state.nx;\n      // GL's y axis points up; the pointer's does not.\n      const py = 1 - state.ny;\n\n      // Reduced motion freezes time at 0 rather than stopping the render, so\n      // the fog, the vignette and the cursor glow all survive — only the drift\n      // is gone. With time constant the image is a pure function of the\n      // pointer, so a frame where the pointer has not moved is skippable.\n      if (reduced) {\n        const last = lastPointer.current;\n        if (\n          last &&\n          Math.abs(px - last.x) < POINTER_EPSILON &&\n          Math.abs(py - last.y) < POINTER_EPSILON\n        ) {\n          return;\n        }\n      }\n\n      lastPointer.current = { x: px, y: py };\n      current.draw(reduced ? 0 : elapsed, px, py);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref, disposes the triangle geometry and the ShaderMaterial, then calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed drawing buffer, and removes the canvas from the DOM. The canvas is three's own, created inside the effect and appended per effect run, so a StrictMode remount builds a new context instead of reattaching one that was just force-lost. The pointer listener belongs to `useSmoothPointer` and is removed with it; sizing, visibility and the frame loop are all shared subscriptions that release themselves.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component reads `useReducedMotion` itself and freezes `uTime` at 0 rather than stopping the render. The fog, the vignette, the grid and the cursor glow are all still drawn — none of them depends on time — so what is lost is exactly the drift and nothing else. With time constant the image is a pure function of the pointer, so the loop skips any frame in which the pointer has not moved by more than 0.0002, and a still page costs nothing beyond the empty rAF callback.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Setting only `canvas.width` and forgetting the CSS size, or the reverse. Either mistake produces a canvas that is simply smaller or simply blurrier than intended; the technique is the deliberate gap between the two sizes, and both have to be set on purpose.\n- Putting `image-rendering: pixelated` on the canvas — sometimes inherited from a global rule aimed at sprite art. It replaces the bilinear upscale with nearest-neighbour and the entire effect turns into visible blocks.\n- Specifying line widths, glow radii or grid spacing in device pixels via raw `gl_FragCoord`. Everything is tuned on one machine and then renders at 0.6 of that size at 0.6 scale, and a third of it again on a 3x phone. Divide by the measured scale and author in CSS pixels.\n- Rendering content with fine detail at a fractional scale. Text, hairlines, high-frequency noise and hard-edged shapes all alias the moment the buffer shrinks. This technique is for soft fields, and content and scale have to be chosen together.\n- Listening to `window.resize` instead of observing the element. A sidebar opening, a font loading and reflowing, or a flex sibling changing all resize the box with no window event — and `ResizeObserver` also fires once on observe, which handles the initial size with no separate call.\n- Forgetting that resizing the canvas clears its drawing buffer. Any component that skips frames — as this one does when time is frozen — goes blank after a resize unless the resize path redraws explicitly.\n- Reading `dpr * scale` back as the achieved ratio. `setSize` floors the backing store, so on most box widths the real ratio is slightly lower; using the nominal value makes the grid drift by a pixel against the layout as the window changes.\n- Suppressing the render entirely under reduced motion. Time frozen at 0 still gives the fog, the vignette and the cursor glow, because none of them depend on it — a far better degradation than a flat colour, and the accessibility requirement is about motion, not about the image.\n- Assuming `antialias: true` helps. There is no geometric edge to sample: the only visible edges come from the fragment shader, MSAA does not see them, and the request costs a multisampled buffer of the same size as everything just saved.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The renderer is created with `powerPreference: \"low-power\"`. On a laptop with switchable graphics this keeps a decorative background off the discrete GPU, which is worth more battery than the render scale itself.\n- `renderScale` and `maxDpr` are read from a ref inside the resize path, so changing them re-sizes the backing store without rebuilding the GL context — a render-scale slider can be dragged live.\n- A useful way to see the technique: set `renderScale` to 1 and then to 0.6 and watch the grid rather than the fog. The lines soften very slightly and nothing else about the image changes, while the fragment count falls by two thirds.\n- The colours are individual string props rather than an array, so a caller passing literals does not hand the effect a new identity on every render.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA soft blue fog drifting behind a faint grid, with the grid brightening into a pool under the cursor that trails a third of a second behind it. Open DevTools and the canvas element reports a backing store of 0.6 of its CSS size — the technique is verifiable, not merely claimed — while the image shows no blockiness and the grid lines stay continuous rather than dashed. Resize the window slowly: the grid keeps its 44 px spacing against the layout and never crawls. With reduced motion the fog stops drifting and everything else, cursor glow included, still works.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "fullscreen-triangle-pass": "# Build: Fullscreen Triangle Pass\n\nThe smallest possible host for a full-screen fragment shader: a raw WebGL2 context, one buffer, one triangle whose vertices sit at (-1,-1), (3,-1) and (-1,3). The clipped triangle covers the viewport exactly, so there is no diagonal seam and no doubly-shaded band where two triangles would meet. Pass any GLSL ES 3.00 fragment source and it runs against `v_uv`, `u_resolution`, `u_time` and `u_pointer`.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne 3-vertex triangle covers the viewport and runs your fragment shader. No three, no ogl, no seam.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A single `Float32Array([-1,-1, 3,-1, -1,3])` is uploaded once as `STATIC_DRAW` and bound into a VAO. The vertex shader is `gl_Position = vec4(a_pos, 0.0, 1.0)` — the positions are already in clip space, so there is no model, view or projection matrix anywhere in the component.\n2. `v_uv = a_pos * 0.5 + 0.5` gives uv 0..1 at the visible corners and uv 2.0 at the two overshooting vertices; the rasteriser clips the triangle to the viewport and interpolates the varying linearly, so the surviving fragments carry exactly the uv a two-triangle quad would have produced.\n3. The context is requested with `antialias: false, depth: false, stencil: false, alpha: false` — a fullscreen pass has no polygon interiors to multisample and no depth to test, and every one of those buffers costs width x height x DPR bytes plus a resolve.\n4. Uniform locations are resolved lazily and memoised in a `Map` keyed by name, because `getUniformLocation` is a synchronous driver round-trip and calling it per frame per uniform is the single most common cause of a raw-GL component being slower than a three.js one.\n5. `u_pointer` is fed from `useSmoothPointer` with the y axis flipped (`1 - ny`), so the shader receives the same handedness as `gl_FragCoord`.\n6. Resize writes `canvas.width/height` at `pixelRatio(maxDpr)` and calls `gl.viewport`, then draws one frame immediately — which is also the only draw that happens under reduced motion.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TRIANGLE = [-1,-1, 3,-1, -1,3]`\n  The minimum overshoot that still guarantees full coverage. With 1.0 instead of 3.0 you get a half-screen triangle; with 5.0 the uv range widens to 0..3 and any shader that assumes uv is 0..1 at the vertices misinterprets its own derivatives.\n- `MAX_DPR = 2`\n  Fragment count is quadratic in DPR. A fullscreen shader at DPR 3 costs 2.25x what it costs at 2 for no perceptible gain, and it is the usual reason a hero shader stutters on a phone but not on the laptop it was built on.\n- `pointer smoothing = 0.002`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap remaining after one second. Raw pointer coordinates arrive in 8-16 ms bursts and pushing them straight into a uniform makes the light jitter one pixel per event.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FullscreenTrianglePass(props: { fragment?: string; uniforms?: Record<string, number | readonly number[]>; speed?: number; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `fragment: string` — default `FULLSCREEN_TRIANGLE_FRAG`\n  GLSL ES 3.00 fragment source. Must open with `#version 300 es` and declare an `out vec4`. Changing it relinks the program.\n- `uniforms: Record<string, number | readonly number[]>`\n  Extra uniforms pushed every frame. Numbers become `uniform1f`; arrays of length 2/3/4 become `uniform2f`/`3f`/`4f`.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied to `u_time`.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Upper bound on device pixel ratio.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/* Two vertices outside the viewport, one at the origin corner. Anything larger\n   than 3.0 also works; 3.0 is the minimum that guarantees full coverage. */\nconst TRIANGLE = new Float32Array([-1, -1, 3, -1, -1, 3]);\nconst MAX_DPR = 2;\n\ninterface Pass {\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext;\n  program: WebGLProgram;\n  vao: WebGLVertexArrayObject;\n  buffer: WebGLBuffer;\n  locations: Map<string, WebGLUniformLocation | null>;\n}\n\nfunction compileShader(\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext,\n  type: number,\n  source: string,\n): WebGLShader | null {\n  const shader = gl.createShader(type);\n  if (!shader) return null;\n  gl.shaderSource(shader, source);\n  gl.compileShader(shader);\n  if (!gl.getShaderParameter(shader, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) {\n    // Log rather than throw: a shader that fails to compile should degrade to\n    // the static fallback, not take the whole React tree down with it.\n    console.error('[fullscreen-triangle-pass]', gl.getShaderInfoLog(shader));\n    gl.deleteShader(shader);\n    return null;\n  }\n  return shader;\n}\n\nfunction createPass(gl: WebGL2RenderingContext, fragment: string): Pass | null {\n  const vert = compileShader(gl, gl.VERTEX_SHADER, FULLSCREEN_TRIANGLE_VERT);\n  const frag = compileShader(gl, gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER, fragment);\n  const program = gl.createProgram();\n  if (!vert || !frag || !program) {\n    if (vert) gl.deleteShader(vert);\n    if (frag) gl.deleteShader(frag);\n    if (program) gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.attachShader(program, vert);\n  gl.attachShader(program, frag);\n  gl.linkProgram(program);\n  // Safe to delete immediately after linking — the program holds a reference.\n  gl.deleteShader(vert);\n  gl.deleteShader(frag);\n\n  if (!gl.getProgramParameter(program, gl.LINK_STATUS)) {\n    console.error('[fullscreen-triangle-pass]', gl.getProgramInfoLog(program));\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  const vao = gl.createVertexArray();\n  const buffer = gl.createBuffer();\n  if (!vao || !buffer) {\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.bindVertexArray(vao);\n  gl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, buffer);\n  gl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, TRIANGLE, gl.STATIC_DRAW);\n  const attrib = gl.getAttribLocation(program, 'a_pos');\n  if (attrib >= 0) {\n    gl.enableVertexAttribArray(attrib);\n    gl.vertexAttribPointer(attrib, 2, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);\n  }\n  gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n\n  return { gl, program, vao, buffer, locations: new Map() };\n}\n\n/** Uniform locations are stable for the life of a program — look up once. */\nfunction uniformLocation(pass: Pass, name: string): WebGLUniformLocation | null {\n  const cached = pass.locations.get(name);\n  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;\n  const found = pass.gl.getUniformLocation(pass.program, name);\n  pass.locations.set(name, found);\n  return found;\n}\n\nfunction setUniform(\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext,\n  location: WebGLUniformLocation | null,\n  value: UniformValue,\n): void {\n  // Null means the uniform was optimised out because the shader never reads\n  // it. Silently skipping is correct; calling uniform*f with null is a no-op\n  // that some drivers log as an error every frame.\n  if (location === null) return;\n  if (typeof value === 'number') {\n    gl.uniform1f(location, value);\n    return;\n  }\n  const a = value[0] ?? 0;\n  const b = value[1] ?? 0;\n  const c = value[2] ?? 0;\n  const d = value[3] ?? 0;\n  if (value.length === 2) gl.uniform2f(location, a, b);\n  else if (value.length === 3) gl.uniform3f(location, a, b, c);\n  else if (value.length >= 4) gl.uniform4f(location, a, b, c, d);\n  else gl.uniform1f(location, a);\n}\n\nfunction disposePass(pass: Pass): void {\n  const { gl } = pass;\n  gl.deleteVertexArray(pass.vao);\n  gl.deleteBuffer(pass.buffer);\n  gl.deleteProgram(pass.program);\n  // dispose alone leaves the context alive; only the extension hands it back.\n  loseContext(gl);\n}\n\nexport function FullscreenTrianglePass({\n  fragment = FULLSCREEN_TRIANGLE_FRAG,\n  uniforms,\n  speed = 1,\n  maxDpr = MAX_DPR,\n  className,\n}: FullscreenTrianglePassProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n  const passRef = useRef<Pass | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.002 });\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n\n  // Held in refs so a changed uniform object never re-creates the program.\n  const uniformsRef = useRef(uniforms);\n  uniformsRef.current = uniforms;\n  const speedRef = useRef(speed);\n  speedRef.current = speed;\n\n  const drawRef = useRef<(elapsed: number) => void>(() => {});\n  drawRef.current = (elapsed: number) => {\n    const pass = passRef.current;\n    if (!pass) return;\n    const { gl } = pass;\n\n    gl.useProgram(pass.program);\n    setUniform(gl, uniformLocation(pass, 'u_resolution'), [\n      gl.drawingBufferWidth,\n      gl.drawingBufferHeight,\n    ]);\n    setUniform(gl, uniformLocation(pass, 'u_time'), elapsed * speedRef.current);\n    const p = pointer.current;\n    // Flip y: DOM coordinates grow downward, gl_FragCoord grows upward.\n    setUniform(gl, uniformLocation(pass, 'u_pointer'), [p.nx, 1 - p.ny]);\n\n    const extra = uniformsRef.current;\n    if (extra) {\n      for (const name of Object.keys(extra)) {\n        const value = extra[name];\n        if (value !== undefined) setUniform(gl, uniformLocation(pass, name), value);\n      }\n    }\n\n    gl.bindVertexArray(pass.vao);\n    gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLES, 0, 3);\n    gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // The canvas is built here rather than rendered by React. Cleanup releases\n    // the context through `WEBGL_lose_context`, and a canvas that has been\n    // through that never yields another one — `getContext` hands back the same\n    // lost object. So the element must not be one React keeps and re-gives to\n    // the next effect run: a changed `fragment`, or StrictMode's double invoke,\n    // did exactly that and every shader compiled against a dead context, which\n    // left the component permanently on its fallback.\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.className = 'block h-full w-full';\n\n    // `antialias: false` — a fullscreen pass has no polygon edges to smooth,\n    // so MSAA buys nothing and costs a multisample resolve every frame.\n    const gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', {\n      antialias: false,\n      alpha: false,\n      depth: false,\n      stencil: false,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n    });\n    if (!gl) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const pass = createPass(gl, fragment);\n    if (!pass) {\n      loseContext(gl);\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    el.append(canvas);\n    canvasRef.current = canvas;\n    passRef.current = pass;\n    setSupported(true);\n\n    // A dropped context with nobody listening is a pass that never draws again:\n    // a blank rectangle, no error, no fallback. Better to concede to the\n    // fallback. `preventDefault` is required or the loss is final by spec.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      // Detached as well as conceded: the fallback renders as a sibling in\n      // normal flow, and a canvas left in place sits on top of it.\n      canvas.remove();\n      setSupported(false);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      passRef.current = null;\n      canvasRef.current = null;\n      disposePass(pass);\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, [fragment]);\n\n  // Resize and the static frame share one effect: whenever the backing store\n  // changes we must redraw, and under reduced motion that redraw is the only\n  // one that ever happens.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const canvas = canvasRef.current;\n    const pass = passRef.current;\n    if (!canvas || !pass || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n\n    const dpr = pixelRatio(maxDpr);\n    const w = Math.max(1, Math.round(width * dpr));\n    const h = Math.max(1, Math.round(height * dpr));\n    if (canvas.width !== w || canvas.height !== h) {\n      canvas.width = w;\n      canvas.height = h;\n    }\n    pass.gl.viewport(0, 0, w, h);\n    drawRef.current(0);\n  }, [width, height, maxDpr, supported, fragment]);\n\n  const running = supported && visible && !reduced && width > 0 && height > 0;\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => drawRef.current(elapsed), running);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Deletes the VAO, the vertex buffer and the program, then calls `loseContext(gl)` to hand the WebGL context back to the browser. The rAF subscription unsubscribes with the hook. The effect is keyed on `fragment`, so a source change tears the old program down before building the new one rather than leaking it.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF subscription never starts. The pass still draws exactly one frame on mount and one on every resize, so you get a fully composed still image rather than a blank canvas — `u_time` is pinned to 0 and the pointer light sits wherever it was seeded.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Putting a newline, a comment, or a leading space before `#version 300 es`. The directive must be the first token in the source or the compile fails with \"version directive must occur before anything else\", and because the shader source lives in a template literal the offending newline is invisible in the diff.\n- Drawing a quad out of two triangles. GPUs shade in 2x2 fragment quads, so every quad the shared diagonal crosses is rasterised by both triangles and those fragments are shaded twice — a band of pure waste down the diagonal. It also breaks `fwidth`/`dFdx` across the seam, which shows up as a visible hairline in any analytic-AA shader.\n- Calling `gl.getUniformLocation` inside the render loop. It is a driver round-trip, not a hash lookup; the locations are constant for the life of the program, so resolve them once and cache them.\n- Passing a uniform that the shader never reads. `getUniformLocation` returns `null` for anything the compiler optimised out, and `gl.uniform1f(null, x)` is a silent no-op that some drivers log to the console 60 times a second. Skip on null.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` or just dropping the canvas and assuming the context is released. Only `WEBGL_lose_context.loseContext()` hands it back, and Chromium kills the OLDEST live context when a page exceeds roughly 16 — so a leaked context here makes an unrelated component elsewhere on the page go black.\n- Throwing on a shader compile error. A syntax error in a user-supplied fragment then unmounts the entire React subtree; logging the info log and falling back to the static poster keeps the page alive and puts the actual error in the console.\n\n## Notes\n\n- WebGL2 only. GLSL ES 3.00 gives `in`/`out`, integer bit operators and core derivatives without an extension dance; WebGL2 is available in every browser that has shipped since 2021 except pre-15.4 Safari, which the `hasWebGL` guard catches.\n- Everything else in the WebGL category that does not use three.js is built on this exact pass — it is the shared floor, not a demo.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe grid fills the frame edge to edge with no lighter or darker band along either diagonal, and the fine grid brightens under the cursor. Resize the container and the grid stays square with hairlines exactly one pixel wide at any DPR. Unmount and `WEBGL_lose_context` fires — mount and unmount twenty times and the twenty-first still renders instead of going black.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "funnel-chart-card": "# Build: Funnel Chart Card\n\nA dashboard card that draws a conversion funnel left to right. Every stage is a column whose band is sized to its share of the top stage and eases into the next stage's height, with a white share pill in the middle and a stat tile underneath. Hovering, focusing or tapping a stage dims the others, darkens it one tone and swaps the count-up headline for that stage's count with its step conversion from the stage before. A period select swaps the data and the bands re-grow from their old shape.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA horizontal conversion funnel: tapered SVG bands per stage with share pills, stat tiles and a headline that follows the hovered stage.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each stage is a `<button>` in a flex row (`gap-0.5`, so the card surface shows as a 2px seam between columns). Inside it an absolutely positioned SVG uses `viewBox=\"0 0 100 {chartHeight}\"` with `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"`: vertical units are pixels, horizontal units are 0-100 of the column, so the band stretches with the card while its heights stay exact.\n2. Shares (`value / top`) go through `useTweenedValues`, a rAF tween with ease-out cubic over 520ms that starts from zeros on mount and from the current shares on every period change. Heights are `max(2, share * (chartHeight - 24))`, centred on `chartHeight / 2`.\n3. `bandPath(h0, h1)` draws one column from its own height to the next stage's. Curved: flat to x=50, then two cubic Béziers with both control points at x=75 (horizontal tangents at both ends), so the edge is an S-curve. Sharp: a plain trapezoid. The last column uses its own height for both ends, so it runs flat.\n4. Curved columns paint two halo copies of the band first, grown by 12px and 6px per side at 0.16 and 0.32 opacity, then the core. Sharp columns instead paint a 36px track rect at 0.18 opacity, so stages near 0% still have a visible lane.\n5. The active stage is `hovered ?? pinned`. Pointer enter and focus set `hovered`; pointer leave and blur clear it; click toggles `pinned` (`aria-pressed`). Inactive columns and tiles fade to opacity 0.35 / 0.45 over 200ms, and the active core switches from `--color-chart-N` to `--color-chart-N-active`.\n6. The header shows the title, `headline ?? top` and a lime/rose delta chip. With a stage active it shows the stage label, its value and a neutral chip reading \"56% of Link opened\" (step conversion) or \"Top of funnel\". The number counts through `useCountUp` (320ms) inside a `<p>` keyed by the active index, so the fade replays per swap.\n7. Under the chart, `StatTiles` lays out one white tile per stage with a 2.5 swatch, label and value. Tiles take `flex-basis: calc((100% - gaps) / columns)` with a 120px minimum and `grow`, so a 360px card wraps to two per row. Hovering a tile sets the same active stage.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `EDGE / halo layers = 12px; layers +12px @ 0.16 and +6px @ 0.32`\n  Leaves 136px of core in the default 160px chart, and the two soft layers give the curved shape its layered edge without a stroke.\n- `FLAT = 50 (of 100 column units)`\n  The band holds its height for the first half of its column, so the share pill always sits on a flat stretch, then eases into the next stage across the second half.\n- `SHARP_TRACK = 36px at 0.18 opacity`\n  Sharp trapezoids of 1% stages collapse to a line; the track keeps a lane to hover and read.\n- `MIN_CORE = 2px`\n  A stage with a tiny share still draws a visible line instead of vanishing.\n- `tween = 520ms ease-out cubic, from 0 on mount`\n  Long enough to read as growth, short enough that switching periods feels immediate. Shares are tweened rather than heights so `chartHeight` changes never animate.\n- `dim opacity = columns 0.35, tiles 0.45, 200ms`\n  Pushes the other stages back without hiding them, so the active stage still reads in context.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction FunnelChartCard(props: { title?: string; ranges?: FunnelRange[]; stages?: FunnelStage[]; delta?: number; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; shape?: \"curved\" | \"sharp\"; mono?: boolean; chartHeight?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype FunnelStage = { label: string; value: number; color?: ChartColor }\ntype FunnelRange = { id: string; label: string; stages: FunnelStage[]; delta?: number; headline?: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Sign-up funnel\"`\n  Header label while no stage is active; also the card's accessible name.\n- `ranges: FunnelRange[]` — default `SIGNUP_FUNNEL_RANGES`\n  Periods with their own stages and delta. Two or more show the period select.\n- `stages: FunnelStage[]`\n  A single funnel, top stage first. Overrides `ranges` and hides the select.\n- `delta: number`\n  Headline chip percentage when `stages` is used.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the period select changes.\n- `shape: \"curved\" | \"sharp\"` — default `\"curved\"`\n  Eased taper with layered halo, or plain trapezoids over a faint track.\n- `mono: boolean` — default `false`\n  Paints every stage in the secondary text colour (primary when active) and hides tile swatches.\n- `chartHeight: number` — default `160`\n  Chart height in px including the 12px halo above and below.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function FunnelChartCard({\n  title = \"Sign-up funnel\",\n  ranges = SIGNUP_FUNNEL_RANGES,\n  stages: stagesProp,\n  delta: deltaProp,\n  range: rangeProp,\n  defaultRange,\n  onRangeChange,\n  shape = \"curved\",\n  mono = false,\n  chartHeight = 160,\n  className,\n}: FunnelChartCardProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [innerRange, setInnerRange] = useState(defaultRange ?? ranges[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const rangeId = rangeProp ?? innerRange;\n  const activeRange = ranges.find((r) => r.id === rangeId) ?? ranges[0];\n  const stages = stagesProp ?? activeRange?.stages ?? [];\n  const delta = stagesProp ? deltaProp : activeRange?.delta;\n\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState<number | null>(null);\n  const [pinned, setPinned] = useState<number | null>(null);\n  const active = hovered ?? (pinned !== null && pinned < stages.length ? pinned : null);\n\n  const top = stages[0]?.value ?? 0;\n  const coreMax = chartHeight - EDGE * 2;\n  const mid = chartHeight / 2;\n  // Shares are tweened, not heights, so a new period re-grows from the old shape.\n  const shares = useTweenedValues(\n    stages.map((s) => (top > 0 ? s.value / top : 0)),\n    { disabled: reduced },\n  );\n  const heights = shares.map((share) => Math.max(MIN_CORE, share * coreMax));\n\n  const activeStage = active !== null ? stages[active] : undefined;\n  const prevStage = active !== null && active > 0 ? stages[active - 1] : undefined;\n  const headline = activeStage ? activeStage.value : (!stagesProp && activeRange?.headline) || top;\n\n  const selectRange = (id: string) => {\n    if (rangeProp === undefined) setInnerRange(id);\n    setPinned(null);\n    onRangeChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <ChartCardFrame className={className} aria-label={title}>\n      <ChartCardHeader\n        label={activeStage ? activeStage.label : title}\n        value={headline}\n        valueKey={active ?? \"total\"}\n        delta={activeStage ? undefined : delta}\n        note={\n          activeStage\n            ? prevStage\n              ? `${pct(activeStage.value, prevStage.value)}% of ${prevStage.label}`\n              : \"Top of funnel\"\n            : undefined\n        }\n        action={\n          !stagesProp && ranges.length > 1 ? (\n            <PeriodSelect options={ranges} selectedId={activeRange?.id ?? \"\"} onChange={selectRange} />\n          ) : undefined\n        }\n      />\n\n      <div\n        role=\"group\"\n        aria-label={`${title} stages`}\n        className=\"flex gap-0.5 px-2\"\n        style={{ height: chartHeight }}\n        onPointerLeave={() => setHovered(null)}\n      >\n        {stages.map((stage, index) => {\n          const color = CHART_COLORS[chartColorAt(index, stage.color)];\n          const isActive = active === index;\n          const fill = mono ? (isActive ? \"var(--color-text-primary)\" : MONO_FILL) : isActive ? color.active : color.fill;\n          const h0 = heights[index] ?? MIN_CORE;\n          const h1 = heights[index + 1] ?? h0;\n          const share = pct(stage.value, top);\n          const prev = index > 0 ? stages[index - 1] : undefined;\n          return (\n            <button\n              key={`${stage.label}-${index}`}\n              type=\"button\"\n              aria-pressed={pinned === index}\n              aria-label={`${stage.label}: ${formatInt(stage.value)}, ${share}% of ${stages[0]?.label ?? \"top\"}${\n                prev ? `, ${pct(stage.value, prev.value)}% of ${prev.label}` : \"\"\n              }`}\n              onPointerEnter={() => setHovered(index)}\n              onFocus={() => setHovered(index)}\n              onBlur={() => setHovered(null)}\n              onClick={() => setPinned((p) => (p === index ? null : index))}\n              className={cx(\n                \"relative min-w-0 flex-1 cursor-pointer rounded-md outline-none\",\n                \"transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n                active !== null && !isActive && \"opacity-35\",\n              )}\n            >\n              <svg\n                aria-hidden\n                viewBox={`0 0 100 ${chartHeight}`}\n                preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"\n                className=\"absolute inset-0 size-full overflow-visible\"\n              >\n                {shape === \"sharp\" ? (\n                  <rect\n                    x={0}\n                    y={mid - SHARP_TRACK / 2}\n                    width={100}\n                    height={SHARP_TRACK}\n                    style={{ fill: mono ? MONO_FILL : color.fill, opacity: 0.18 }}\n                  />\n                ) : (\n                  LAYERS.map((layer) => (\n                    <path\n                      key={layer.grow}\n                      d={bandPath(h0 + layer.grow * 2, h1 + layer.grow * 2, mid, shape)}\n                      style={{ fill: mono ? MONO_FILL : color.fill, opacity: layer.opacity }}\n                    />\n                  ))\n                )}\n                <path\n                  d={bandPath(h0, h1, mid, shape)}\n                  className=\"transition-[fill] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                  style={{ fill }}\n                />\n              </svg>\n              <span\n                aria-hidden\n                className=\"absolute top-1/2 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full bg-background-primary-default px-1.5 py-px text-caption-1-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums shadow-xs\"\n              >\n                {share}%\n              </span>\n            </button>\n          );\n        })}\n      </div>\n\n      <StatTiles\n        columns={Math.max(1, stages.length)}\n        activeIndex={active}\n        onActiveChange={setHovered}\n        items={stages.map((stage, index) => ({\n          id: `${stage.label}-${index}`,\n          label: stage.label,\n          value: formatInt(stage.value),\n          color: mono ? null : chartColorAt(index, stage.color),\n        }))}\n      />\n    </ChartCardFrame>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The tween and `useCountUp` cancel their pending animation frame on every new target and on unmount. Hover state is plain React state; the Select's outside-press listener is removed by the Select itself when it closes or unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useTweenedValues` is disabled, so the bands render at their final shares on mount and jump on a period change. The headline count runs over 1ms (a single frame), the number fade and the opacity/fill transitions are removed with `motion-reduce:` variants.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `preserveAspectRatio` other than `\"none\"`. The viewBox is 100 units wide but the column is not, so a uniform scale letterboxes the band and the columns stop meeting at their edges.\n- Putting the share pill inside the SVG. With a non-uniform scale the text would stretch. The pill is an HTML span centred over the column.\n- Tweening the path strings or the pixel heights. Interpolating shares keeps the shape correct at every frame and lets a new period start from wherever the previous tween had got to.\n- Expecting Recharts' `<Funnel>`: it stacks vertical trapezoids and cannot draw the eased horizontal taper.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `SIGNUP_FUNNEL_RANGES`, `FunnelStage`, `FunnelRange` and `FunnelShape` are exported. Pass `stages` (and `delta`) for a single static funnel without the period select.\n- The shell companion (`ChartCardFrame`, `ChartCardHeader`, `PeriodSelect`, `StatTiles`, `CHART_COLORS`, `useTweenedValues`) is shared by the other BoardUI chart cards in this batch.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Sign-up funnel\", \"197\" and a lime \"+5.2%\" chip, four columns (lime, blue, purple, pink) with pills 100%, 56%, 39%, 19%, and tiles Link opened 197, Started 110, Completed 77, Converted 38. Hovering Started dims the other columns and tiles, and the header reads \"Started\", \"110\" and \"56% of Link opened\". Choosing \"Last 30 days\" re-grows the bands and the headline counts to 842. Clicking a stage sets its aria-pressed to true; clicking again clears it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "github-star-button": "# Build: GitHub Star Button\n\nA compact pill button in the style of a repository star control, without any logo: a star glyph, a \"Star\" label and a count in an inset pill. Pressing it toggles starred: the outline star swaps to a filled amber one with a small springy pop, the label reads \"Starred\" and the count rolls up by one; pressing again rolls it back down.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA star toggle with a glyph, a Star label and a count pill. Starring pops the star and rolls the en-US formatted count by one.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, motion@^12.43.0, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. React Aria `ToggleButton` owns the state semantics: it renders a native button with `aria-pressed`, handles pointer, keyboard and touch presses, and exposes `data-selected`, `data-hovered`, `data-pressed` and `data-focus-visible` for styling.\n2. The shown total is `count + starred - defaultStarred`, so `count` is the number as loaded (including the viewer when they had already starred) and toggling moves it by exactly one in either direction.\n3. The count is keyed by its value inside `AnimatePresence mode=\"popLayout\"`. Each toggle stores a direction (+1 up, -1 down) and passes it as `custom`, so the old number exits upward while the new one enters from below on a star, and the reverse on an unstar. `popLayout` takes the exiting number out of flow so the two never stack.\n4. The star glyph is a `motion.span` keyed by the starred state: starring remounts it from `scale 0.4, rotate -40deg` with a bouncy spring, unstarring from `scale 0.8` with no bounce.\n5. All numbers use one `Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\")`, so 999 to 1,000 gets its comma and the server and client strings always agree.\n6. The accessible name is fixed text plus the current total (\"Star kinetic, 12,481 stars\"); the visible label and pill are `aria-hidden`, so the Star / Starred swap does not rename the control and `aria-pressed` carries the state.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `count roll = spring, 0.35s, bounce 0.2`\n  Quick enough to feel like a counter tick, with a hint of overshoot so the new digit lands.\n- `star pop = spring, 0.45s, bounce 0.55 from scale 0.4 / -40deg`\n  Starring is the rewarding action and gets the expressive motion; unstarring settles without bounce.\n- `press scale = 0.97`\n  Tactile press feedback on `data-pressed` without shifting surrounding layout.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction GithubStarButton(props: { count: number; defaultStarred?: boolean; onStarredChange?: (starred: boolean) => void; label?: string; starredLabel?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number`\n  Star total as loaded, including the viewer when `defaultStarred` is true.\n- `defaultStarred: boolean` — default `false`\n  Initial starred state.\n- `onStarredChange: (starred: boolean) => void`\n  Fires on every toggle.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Star\"`\n  Visible label when not starred.\n- `starredLabel: string` — default `\"Starred\"`\n  Visible label when starred.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Accessible name; defaults to `label`. \", N stars\" is appended.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function GithubStarButton({\n  count,\n  defaultStarred = false,\n  onStarredChange,\n  label = \"Star\",\n  starredLabel = \"Starred\",\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n}: GithubStarButtonProps) {\n  const reduce = useReducedMotion();\n  const [starred, setStarred] = useState(defaultStarred);\n  // +1 for \"count went up\", -1 for \"went down\"; drives which way the digits roll.\n  const [dir, setDir] = useState(1);\n  const total = count + Number(starred) - Number(defaultStarred);\n  const formatted = numberFormat.format(total);\n\n  const toggle = (next: boolean) => {\n    setDir(next ? 1 : -1);\n    setStarred(next);\n    onStarredChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const Icon = starred ? RiStarFill : RiStarLine;\n\n  return (\n    <ToggleButton\n      isSelected={starred}\n      onChange={toggle}\n      aria-label={`${ariaLabel ?? label}, ${formatted} stars`}\n      className={cx(\n        \"group inline-flex h-9 items-center gap-2 rounded-xl bg-background-primary-default pr-1.5 pl-3 text-body-2-medium text-text-primary shadow-xs ring-1 ring-separator-border outline-none select-none\",\n        \"cursor-pointer transition-[background-color,box-shadow,scale] duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n        \"data-hovered:bg-background-secondary-hover data-pressed:scale-[0.97] data-focus-visible:ring-2 data-focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <span className=\"relative grid size-4 place-items-center\">\n        {reduce ? (\n          <Icon aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-4\", starred ? \"text-status-yellow-text\" : \"text-foreground-icon-secondary\")} />\n        ) : (\n          <motion.span\n            key={String(starred)}\n            initial={{ scale: starred ? 0.4 : 0.8, rotate: starred ? -40 : 0 }}\n            animate={{ scale: 1, rotate: 0 }}\n            transition={{ type: \"spring\", duration: 0.45, bounce: starred ? 0.55 : 0 }}\n            className=\"grid place-items-center\"\n          >\n            <Icon aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-4\", starred ? \"text-status-yellow-text\" : \"text-foreground-icon-secondary\")} />\n          </motion.span>\n        )}\n      </span>\n      <span aria-hidden>{starred ? starredLabel : label}</span>\n      <span\n        aria-hidden\n        data-count\n        className=\"relative inline-flex h-6 items-center overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-background-secondary-default px-2 text-caption-1-semibold text-text-secondary tabular-nums ring-1 ring-separator-border group-data-selected:text-text-primary\"\n      >\n        {reduce ? (\n          formatted\n        ) : (\n          <AnimatePresence mode=\"popLayout\" initial={false} custom={dir}>\n            <motion.span\n              key={total}\n              custom={dir}\n              variants={roll}\n              initial=\"enter\"\n              animate=\"center\"\n              exit=\"exit\"\n              transition={ROLL}\n              className=\"inline-block\"\n            >\n              {formatted}\n            </motion.span>\n          </AnimatePresence>\n        )}\n      </span>\n    </ToggleButton>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners. Motion stops any running spring when its element unmounts, and AnimatePresence removes the exiting number when its exit finishes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` (via `useReducedMotion`) the star and count render as plain elements without motion wrappers, so the glyph and number swap instantly; the press scale and colour transitions are removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Formatting with `toLocaleString()` and no locale. A German visitor sees \"12.480\" on the client after \"12,480\" from the server, which is a hydration mismatch; the locale is fixed to en-US.\n- Computing the roll direction from the new value in the exit animation. The exiting element keeps its own props, so without `AnimatePresence custom` it exits using the previous toggle's direction.\n- Changing the accessible name between \"Star\" and \"Starred\". A toggle button should keep its name and report state through `aria-pressed`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Call your API in `onStarredChange`; the button updates optimistically and does not roll back on its own.\n- For a real repo, link the surrounding card to GitHub and keep this control for in-app starring only.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe first button reads \"Star\" with an outline star and 12,480. Clicking it sets aria-pressed=\"true\", fills the star amber with a pop, changes the label to \"Starred\" and rolls the pill to 12,481 from below. Clicking again rolls back to 12,480 from above. The second button starts starred at 1,000 and unstarring rolls it to 999. Space and Enter toggle it from the keyboard with a visible focus ring. With reduced motion every change is instant.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "github-stars-cta": "# Build: GitHub Stars CTA\n\nA two-column landing block. The left side has a pill eyebrow, a large heading (\"The free tier lives on GitHub\" by default), a paragraph, a checklist and an optional secondary link. The right side is a repo card: an accent gradient band with a faded dot grid, a repository glyph tile overlapping the band, the owner / name in mono with a Public pill, the description, a stats panel with a large star count and a Star button, and a row of forks, license and language.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOpen-source free tier section: copy and checklist beside a repo card with a counting star total, star button and repo facts.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The section is a size container. Below `@4xl` the copy and card stack; from `@4xl` they sit in a `minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,420px)` grid, so the layout follows the space it is given rather than the window.\n2. The star total counts up once: `useCountUp` resets the shown value to 0 in a layout effect (before first paint), then an `IntersectionObserver` at 40% visibility starts a `requestAnimationFrame` loop that eases from 0 to `stars` over `COUNT_MS` with an ease-out cubic, `1 - (1 - t)^3`. The observer disconnects on first sight, so scrolling back does not replay it.\n3. Only the small `StarCount` component re-renders per frame. Its visible digits are `aria-hidden`; a visually hidden sibling always holds the final \"12,480 stars\" text, so screen readers never hear the intermediate numbers.\n4. All numbers go through one `Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\")`, so server and client format the same way and hydration matches whatever the visitor locale is.\n5. The band art is CSS only: a `bg-linear-to-br` accent gradient into the card surface, and a dot grid drawn with a `radial-gradient(currentColor ...)` background tinted `accent-500/35` and faded with `mask-l-from-10% mask-l-to-90%`.\n6. Repo facts are a `dl` with icon `dt`s carrying sr-only labels (Forks, License, Language); missing fields are left out.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `COUNT_MS = 1200`\n  Long enough to read as counting, short enough to finish before the eye moves to the button.\n- `IntersectionObserver threshold = 0.4`\n  The count starts once the stats panel is clearly on screen, not while it is a sliver at the fold.\n- `card column = minmax(0, 420px)`\n  A repo card wider than this turns into a long, sparse strip; the copy column takes the rest.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction GithubStarsCta(props: { repo: GithubRepo; eyebrow?: ReactNode; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; points?: ReactNode[]; secondaryAction?: { label: string; href: string }; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface GithubRepo { owner: string; name: string; href: string; description?: string; stars: number; forks?: number; license?: string; language?: string }\n```\n\n- `repo: GithubRepo`\n  Owner, name, link, star total and optional description, forks, license and language.\n- `eyebrow: ReactNode` — default `\"Open source\"`\n  Pill above the heading; pass null to hide it.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"The free tier lives on GitHub\"`\n  Section heading.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Paragraph under the heading; a default about the public MIT repo is provided.\n- `points: ReactNode[]`\n  Checklist items under the paragraph.\n- `secondaryAction: { label: string; href: string }`\n  Secondary link button under the checklist.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Counts from 0 to `target` the first time `ref` is 40% visible. Reduced motion shows the target at once. */\nfunction useCountUp(target: number, ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>) {\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(target);\n\n  // Layout effect: the reset to 0 lands before the first paint, so the final number never flashes first.\n  useLayoutEffect(() => {\n    const node = ref.current;\n    const reduce = window.matchMedia(\"(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\").matches;\n    if (!node || reduce || typeof IntersectionObserver === \"undefined\") {\n      setValue(target);\n      return;\n    }\n    setValue(0);\n    let frame = 0;\n    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(\n      (entries) => {\n        if (!entries.some((entry) => entry.isIntersecting)) return;\n        observer.disconnect();\n        const start = performance.now();\n        const tick = (now: number) => {\n          const t = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (now - start) / COUNT_MS));\n          setValue(Math.round(target * (1 - (1 - t) ** 3)));\n          if (t < 1) frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n        };\n        frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n      },\n      { threshold: 0.4 },\n    );\n    observer.observe(node);\n    return () => {\n      observer.disconnect();\n      cancelAnimationFrame(frame);\n    };\n  }, [target, ref]);\n\n  return value;\n}\n\nfunction StarCount({ stars }: { stars: number }) {\n  const ref = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const value = useCountUp(stars, ref);\n  return (\n    <>\n      <span ref={ref} aria-hidden className=\"tabular-nums\">\n        {numberFormat.format(value)}\n      </span>\n      <span className=\"sr-only\">{numberFormat.format(stars)} stars</span>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The layout effect disconnects the IntersectionObserver and cancels any pending animation frame on unmount or when `stars` changes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the star count shows the final number straight away and no animation frame is requested. Nothing else on the block moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Starting the count in `useEffect` from the server-rendered final value. The real number paints for one frame and then jumps to 0; resetting in a layout effect avoids the flash.\n- Formatting with the visitor locale (`toLocaleString()`). Server and browser can disagree (\"12,480\" vs \"12.480\") and React reports a hydration mismatch; the formatter is pinned to en-US.\n- Announcing every frame of the count. The animated digits are hidden from assistive tech and the final total is exposed separately.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass a real star count from your build step or an API route; the component does no fetching.\n- Use the GitHub URL as `repo.href`; the Star button is a plain link, so add `target` handling in your app if you want a new tab.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe block shows the heading, paragraph and three checks on the left and the repo card on the right at 1280px, stacked at narrow widths. When the card comes into view the star total counts from 0 to 12,480 in about a second and stops there. Forks read \"642 forks\", license \"MIT license\". The Star button and the secondary link are links with visible focus rings. With reduced motion the total is 12,480 from the start. Light and dark both keep the gradient band and dot grid visible.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "gpu-bezier-burst": "# Build: GPU Cubic-Bezier Burst\n\nConcentric rings of dots turning slowly. Fire a sector — by clicking it, or by waiting for the automatic cycle — and its particles burst outward and fade, each one following its own cubic-bezier curve. The curve is the same four numbers CSS takes, but solved per vertex per frame by Newton's method, which is what lets two hundred particles carry two hundred different easings while the CPU only advances a clock.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRings of dots that burst on click, every particle solving its own cubic-bezier easing in the vertex shader.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A cubic bezier is parametric: for a parameter `t` it gives a point `(x(t), y(t))`. Easing needs `y` at a given *x*, and a cubic has no closed-form inverse, so the shader runs Newton's method — 8 iterations of `t -= (x(t) - target) / x'(t)`, seeded with `t = x` because the curve is monotonic in x — then evaluates `y(t)`.\n2. The four control numbers ride in an `aBezier` attribute rather than a uniform. That is the whole reason to solve on the GPU: a uniform would give every particle in the burst one shared easing, and per-particle easing computed on the CPU would mean a buffer upload every frame.\n3. Firing writes the current burst-clock time into a contiguous slice of the `aStart` attribute — one sector, one range, one `needsUpdate`. Each particle then computes `age = uBurstTime - aStart - delay` and animates only while `0 <= age <= uBurstDuration`; a start time of 0 means never fired, which is why the clock starts at 1.\n4. Resting dots and burst particles live in the same buffer and the same draw call, distinguished by an `isBurst` flag. Burst particles sit invisible on the outer ring until fired, rather than the resting dots being displaced — displacing them would tear a hole in the ring and then pop it shut when the burst ended.\n5. There are two clocks. `uRotation` accumulates from delta time only when motion is allowed; `uBurstTime` always accumulates. Under reduced motion the ring freezes at 0.35 rad and the automatic cycle stops, but a click still fires a full burst, and the frame loop is subscribed only for as long as one is in flight.\n6. Flight direction is `angle + uRotation`, evaluated live rather than baked, so a burst stays radial to the ring while the ring keeps turning underneath it. Alpha fades linearly against the eased flight, and point size shrinks to 45%, so the particle dissipates instead of blinking out at full size.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `NEWTON_ITERATIONS = 8`\n  Newton converges quadratically here, so 4 iterations already land inside a pixel. 8 is cheap insurance for control points near 0 or 1, where the derivative shrinks and convergence slows.\n- `MIN_DERIVATIVE = 0.000001`\n  Newton divides by the derivative. A curve with a flat segment divides by ~0, `t` becomes NaN, and the NaN propagates into `gl_Position` — the vertex silently disappears rather than erroring.\n- `base curve = (0, 0.9, 0.5, 1)`\n  A hard ease-out: almost all the distance is covered in the first third. Each particle jitters around it, with x-coordinates clamped to 0..1 because the solver needs monotonicity in x — the same constraint CSS puts on `cubic-bezier()`.\n- `MAX_LAUNCH_DELAY = 0.09s`\n  Particles all leaving on one frame read as a single expanding ring. Staggering launches over ~90ms reads as a spray, and staying well under `burstDuration` keeps the last launch ahead of the first landing.\n- `burstDuration = 0.45s`\n  Longer than the 0.25s a click ping wants, because at preview-card size the eye needs the extra frames to actually see the easing differ between particles. Shorten it toward 0.25 when the burst is real button feedback.\n- `BURST_END_SIZE = 0.45`\n  Particles shrink to 45% over the flight. Constant-size particles read as confetti; shrinking ones read as one thing dissipating.\n- `STATIC_ROTATION = 0.35 rad`\n  Angle the ring is frozen at under reduced motion. Off-axis, so the field does not look like it snapped to a grid the moment motion was disabled.\n- `RING_DOT_SCALE / BURST_DOT_SCALE = 0.7 / 1.15`\n  Burst particles are visibly larger than the resting field, so the burst reads as the event rather than as the ring shedding pixels.\n- `flight fan = +/- 0.175 rad`\n  Jitter added to each particle's radial direction. Pure radial launches leave on rails and the burst looks like a line drawing; more than ~0.3 rad and the sectors start to overlap.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction GpuBezierBurst(props: { ringCount?: number; ringDots?: number; innerRadius?: number; groups?: number; burstDots?: number; spinSpeed?: number; chordInterval?: number; burstDuration?: number; burstRadius?: number; dotScale?: number; viewScale?: number; opacity?: number; color?: string; burstColor?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `ringCount: number` — default `4`\n  Concentric rings in the resting field.\n- `ringDots: number` — default `190`\n  Dots on the outermost ring; inner rings scale down to keep density even.\n- `innerRadius: number` — default `0.55`\n  Radius of the innermost ring as a fraction of the outermost.\n- `groups: number` — default `6`\n  Sectors that can be fired independently.\n- `burstDots: number` — default `34`\n  Burst particles per sector.\n- `spinSpeed: number` — default `0.18`\n  Ambient ring rotation in radians per second.\n- `chordInterval: number` — default `1.5`\n  Seconds between automatic firings.\n- `burstDuration: number` — default `0.45`\n  Seconds a single particle's flight lasts.\n- `burstRadius: number` — default `26`\n  How far a particle travels, in CSS pixels.\n- `dotScale: number` — default `1`\n  Dot radius multiplier in CSS pixels.\n- `viewScale: number` — default `0.4`\n  Model-to-pixel scale as a fraction of the container short side.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied to every dot.\n- `color: string` — default `'#6f757e'`\n  Resting dot colour.\n- `burstColor: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Colour mixed in at the hot start of a flight.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'gpu-bezier-burst'`\n  Seeds every curve, delay and fan angle for a stable, hydration-safe field.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function GpuBezierBurst({\n  ringCount = 4,\n  ringDots = 190,\n  innerRadius = 0.55,\n  groups = 6,\n  burstDots = 34,\n  spinSpeed = 0.18,\n  chordInterval = 1.5,\n  burstDuration = 0.45,\n  burstRadius = 26,\n  dotScale = 1,\n  viewScale = 0.4,\n  opacity = 1,\n  color = '#6f757e',\n  burstColor = '#ffffff',\n  seed = 'gpu-bezier-burst',\n  className,\n}: GpuBezierBurstProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  // Gates the frame loop under reduced motion: no ambient animation, but a\n  // burst in flight still needs frames until it lands.\n  const [burstLive, setBurstLive] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Read inside the frame loop rather than closed over, so toggling the OS\n  // setting does not tear down and rebuild the GL context.\n  const reducedRef = useRef(reduced);\n  reducedRef.current = reduced;\n\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0.1, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n\n    const positions: number[] = [];\n    const burstData: number[] = [];\n    const bezier: number[] = [];\n\n    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n       Resting rings. Dot count scales with radius so the inner rings are\n       not visibly denser than the outer ones — constant dots-per-ring is\n       the classic tell of a generated field.\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\n    for (let r = 0; r < ringCount; r += 1) {\n      const t = ringCount === 1 ? 1 : r / (ringCount - 1);\n      const radius = innerRadius + (1 - innerRadius) * t;\n      const count = Math.max(8, Math.round(ringDots * radius));\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n        const angle = (i / count) * Math.PI * 2 + rng() * 0.01;\n        positions.push(Math.cos(angle) * radius, Math.sin(angle) * radius, 0);\n        // isBurst 0: these never move, so flight angle, delay and curve are unused.\n        burstData.push(0, angle, RING_DOT_SCALE, 0);\n        bezier.push(0, 0, 1, 1);\n      }\n    }\n\n    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------\n       Burst particles, grouped into contiguous sectors so firing one is a\n       single contiguous write into the start-time buffer. They sit on the\n       outer ring invisible until fired: displacing the resting dots\n       instead would leave a hole in the ring, then pop them back.\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\n    const groupRanges: { start: number; end: number }[] = [];\n    const restingDots = positions.length / 3;\n    const sector = (Math.PI * 2) / groups;\n\n    for (let g = 0; g < groups; g += 1) {\n      const start = positions.length / 3;\n      const centre = g * sector;\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < burstDots; i += 1) {\n        const angle = centre + (rng() - 0.5) * sector * 0.9;\n        const radius = 0.94 + rng() * 0.06;\n        positions.push(Math.cos(angle) * radius, Math.sin(angle) * radius, 0);\n        burstData.push(\n          1,\n          // Flight direction fans slightly off pure radial, so the burst\n          // spreads instead of leaving on rails.\n          angle + (rng() - 0.5) * 0.35,\n          BURST_DOT_SCALE,\n          rng() * MAX_LAUNCH_DELAY,\n        );\n\n        /* Each particle gets its own curve, jittered around an ease-out\n           (0, 0.9, 0.5, 1). x-coordinates are clamped to 0..1 because the\n           solver needs the curve monotonic in x — exactly the constraint\n           CSS puts on cubic-bezier(). y is free to overshoot. */\n        bezier.push(\n          Math.min(1, Math.max(0, rng() * 0.25)),\n          0.9 + (rng() - 0.5) * 0.3,\n          Math.min(1, Math.max(0, 0.5 + (rng() - 0.5) * 0.4)),\n          0.95 + rng() * 0.1,\n        );\n      }\n      groupRanges.push({ start, end: positions.length / 3 });\n    }\n\n    const total = positions.length / 3;\n    const starts = new Float32Array(total);\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(Float32Array.from(positions), 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aBurstData', new THREE.BufferAttribute(Float32Array.from(burstData), 4));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aBezier', new THREE.BufferAttribute(Float32Array.from(bezier), 4));\n\n    const startAttribute = new THREE.BufferAttribute(starts, 1);\n    // The only buffer that changes at runtime, and only on a firing.\n    startAttribute.setUsage(THREE.DynamicDrawUsage);\n    geometry.setAttribute('aStart', startAttribute);\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uBurstTime: { value: 0 },\n      uRotation: { value: STATIC_ROTATION },\n      uViewScale: { value: 1 },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uDotScale: { value: dotScale },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uBurstDuration: { value: burstDuration },\n      uBurstRadius: { value: burstRadius },\n      uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(color) },\n      uBurstColor: { value: new THREE.Color(burstColor) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: GPU_BEZIER_BURST_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: GPU_BEZIER_BURST_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    // Vertices are unit-scale and the real placement happens in the shader, so\n    // three's bounding sphere does not describe what is on screen.\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    /* -- the two clocks ------------------------------------------------ */\n    let rotation = STATIC_ROTATION; // ambient; frozen under reduced motion\n    let burstTime = 1; // feedback; always runs. Starts at 1 so a start\n    //                    time of 0 can mean 'never fired'.\n    let nextFire = chordInterval;\n    let liveUntil = 0;\n    let cursor = 0;\n\n    const fire = (group: number) => {\n      const range = groupRanges[group % groupRanges.length];\n      if (!range) return;\n      for (let i = range.start; i < range.end; i += 1) starts[i] = burstTime;\n      startAttribute.needsUpdate = true;\n      liveUntil = burstTime + burstDuration + MAX_LAUNCH_DELAY + 0.05;\n      setBurstLive(true);\n    };\n\n    const render = () => renderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n    const step = (dt: number) => {\n      burstTime += dt;\n\n      if (!reducedRef.current) {\n        rotation += dt * spinSpeed;\n        if (burstTime >= nextFire) {\n          fire(cursor);\n          cursor += 1;\n          nextFire = burstTime + chordInterval;\n        }\n      } else if (burstTime > liveUntil) {\n        // Nothing in flight and no ambient motion to draw: stand the loop\n        // down. Idempotent, so this is safe to call every frame.\n        setBurstLive(false);\n      }\n\n      uniforms.uRotation.value = rotation;\n      uniforms.uBurstTime.value = burstTime;\n      render();\n    };\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.left = -w / 2;\n      camera.right = w / 2;\n      camera.top = h / 2;\n      camera.bottom = -h / 2;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n      uniforms.uViewScale.value = Math.min(w, h) * viewScale;\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n      render();\n    };\n\n    /* -- pointer: user-initiated, so it fires under reduced motion too -- */\n    const onPointerDown = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const x = event.clientX - rect.left - rect.width / 2;\n      // Screen y grows downward, model y grows upward.\n      const y = rect.height / 2 - (event.clientY - rect.top);\n      // Undo the ambient rotation so the sector under the cursor is the one\n      // that fires, not the one that was there before the ring turned.\n      const angle = Math.atan2(y, x) - rotation;\n      const normalised = ((angle % (Math.PI * 2)) + Math.PI * 2) % (Math.PI * 2);\n      fire(Math.round(normalised / sector) % groups);\n    };\n    el.addEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, step };\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    ringCount,\n    ringDots,\n    innerRadius,\n    groups,\n    burstDots,\n    spinSpeed,\n    chordInterval,\n    burstDuration,\n    burstRadius,\n    dotScale,\n    viewScale,\n    opacity,\n    color,\n    burstColor,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  useRaf((dt) => stage.current?.step(dt), ready && !unsupported && (!reduced || burstLive));\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the pointerdown listener, nulls the stage ref so a queued frame cannot touch a dead renderer, disposes the geometry via `disposeObject(scene)`, disposes the material, calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed backing store, and removes the canvas. `useRaf` unsubscribes on unmount, so a StrictMode double-invoke leaves no orphaned context.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The ambient rotation freezes at 0.35 rad and the automatic firing cycle stops, so nothing moves unprompted. A pointer press still fires a full burst, because that is feedback for something the user did rather than decoration. The frame loop is subscribed only while a burst is actually in flight and unsubscribes as soon as the last particle lands, so the idle cost is zero.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Freezing the burst along with the ambient rotation under reduced motion. The component then stops answering clicks, which is a broken interface, not an accessible one — feedback for a user action is not decoration, hence the two separate clocks.\n- Leaving the frame loop running under reduced motion to keep the burst alive. It burns a GPU-backed rAF forever for a static image; subscribe only while a burst is in flight and stand down when it lands.\n- Passing the control points as a uniform. It compiles, it animates, and every particle in the burst moves identically — the effect collapses into one expanding ring and the GPU solve buys nothing.\n- Omitting the derivative guard in the Newton loop. Certain control points produce a near-zero derivative, `t` goes NaN, and the affected vertices vanish with no error anywhere — the symptom is a burst that is missing particles at some curves and not others.\n- Displacing the resting ring dots instead of using dedicated burst particles. The ring develops a gap for the duration of the burst and snaps shut at the end.\n- Baking the flight direction at fire time. The ring keeps rotating during the 0.45s flight, so the particles visibly drift off-radial and appear to curve away from their launch point.\n- Sizing burst radius in model units. It then scales with the container, and the burst that felt right on a large canvas barely leaves the ring on a small preview card — pixels are the right unit for an effect this small.\n\n## Notes\n\n- One draw call and one buffer for the resting field and every burst particle. The only runtime upload is the start-time attribute, written once per firing rather than once per frame.\n- Clicking maps the pointer angle back through the current rotation, so the sector under the cursor is the one that fires.\n- The automatic cycle exists so the component reads as alive in a preview grid; drop `chordInterval` handling if the burst is wired to real user actions.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nConcentric rings of dots turn slowly. Roughly every 1.5 seconds one sector throws a spray of brighter particles outward that fade as they travel, and the particles visibly do not move in lockstep — some shoot ahead and settle, others trail. Clicking a sector fires it immediately. The ring never develops a gap where a burst came from. With reduced motion the ring is still and nothing fires on its own, but a click still produces a full burst.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "gsap-cursor-follower": "# Build: GSAP Cursor Follower\n\nA container hides its native cursor and draws its own: a small disc that lags the pointer by a settling tween, and swells into a labelled puck whenever it passes over an element marked `data-cursor-target`. The lag comes from GSAP rather than from a damped rAF loop, which is why the disc comes to rest when the pointer stops instead of asymptoting toward it forever.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA puck that replaces the arrow inside a panel, trailing 0.4s behind and swelling to a label over targets.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. On mount the puck is centred on its own coordinates with `gsap.set(circle, { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50 })` and the positioning wrapper is parked at `x: -999, y: -999`. From then on the follow tween writes raw x/y and the centring composes into the same matrix, so there is no per-move offset arithmetic and exactly one composited transform per node.\n2. Every `pointermove` issues `gsap.to(wrapper, { x: e.clientX - rect.left, y: e.clientY - rect.top, duration, ease: \"power2.out\", overwrite: \"auto\" })`. `overwrite: \"auto\"` retires the previous tween so the ~120 events a second do not accumulate into ~120 concurrent tweens writing the same transform.\n3. This is deliberately GSAP smoothing rather than `useSmoothPointer`. A tween re-eases from its current velocity to a *rest* at the target, so the puck decelerates into stillness the instant the pointer stops; an exponential `damp` never actually arrives. Reach for the damped hook when you want a value that is permanently chasing, and for this when you want it to settle.\n4. `pointerenter` teleports first (`duration: 0`) and only then fades in, so re-entering the panel does not drag a visible streak across it from wherever the puck was last parked.\n5. The hover state is delegated: one handler runs `e.target.closest(\"[data-cursor-target]\")` per move and compares the result with the previous state, tweening `scale` on the disc and `autoAlpha`/`scale` on the label only on the transition. Scale — not width/height — keeps the resize on the compositor with no layout pass.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PARK = -999`\n  Initial x/y. The element exists from first paint but must be *nowhere*; at 0,0 it flashes in the top-left corner for the frames between mount and the first pointer event.\n- `duration = 0.4`\n  Seconds of catch-up. Below ~0.2 the puck is welded to the pointer and the trailing read disappears; past ~0.7 it feels detached from the hand and users start chasing it.\n- `size = 16`\n  Resting diameter. Small enough to point precisely with, large enough to see against a busy panel. Applied as `scale: size / targetSize` so only the transform changes.\n- `targetSize = 112`\n  Diameter over a target. It has to hold a short label at 14px with real breathing room — under ~90 the text touches the circle edge and the puck reads as a broken badge.\n- `RESIZE_DURATION = 0.45`\n  The swell. With `expo.out` this is almost all its motion in the first 120ms, which is what makes the state change feel like a snap rather than an inflation.\n- `FADE_DURATION = 0.3`\n  Enter/leave opacity. Fast enough that the puck is present before the user has moved a meaningful distance into the panel.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction CursorFollower(props: { children: ReactNode; label?: string; duration?: number; size?: number; targetSize?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Panel content. Anything inside carrying `data-cursor-target` triggers the labelled state.\n- `label: string` — default `'View'`\n  Text revealed inside the puck over a target.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.4`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the follow tween.\n- `size: number` — default `16`\n  Resting diameter in px.\n- `targetSize: number` — default `112`\n  Diameter over a target, in px. Also the disc's laid-out size.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Far outside any container, so the puck is nowhere until a pointer exists. */\nconst PARK = -999;\nconst FADE_DURATION = 0.3;\nconst RESIZE_DURATION = 0.45;\n\nexport function CursorFollower({\n  children,\n  label = 'View',\n  duration = 0.4,\n  size = 16,\n  targetSize = 112,\n  className,\n}: CursorFollowerProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const wrap = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const disc = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const text = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const point = wrap.current;\n    const circle = disc.current;\n    const caption = text.current;\n    if (!el || !point || !circle || !caption) return;\n\n    // A coarse pointer has no hover state, so the puck could only ever exist\n    // between pointerdown and pointerup — and `cursor: none` on a touch device\n    // hides nothing while still suppressing the text-selection caret.\n    if (!window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) return;\n\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;\n    const follow = reduced ? 0 : duration;\n    const fade = reduced ? 0 : FADE_DURATION;\n    const resize = reduced ? 0 : RESIZE_DURATION;\n\n    // Centre both layers on their own coordinates once. After this the follow\n    // tween writes raw x/y and the centring composes into the same matrix —\n    // no offset arithmetic per move, and one composited transform per node.\n    gsap.set(point, { x: PARK, y: PARK, autoAlpha: 0 });\n    gsap.set(circle, { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, scale: size / targetSize });\n    gsap.set(caption, { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, autoAlpha: 0, scale: 0.5 });\n\n    // The puck *replaces* the arrow rather than accompanying it. Set from JS,\n    // not from the class list, so a touch device never loses its cursor.\n    const previousCursor = el.style.cursor;\n    el.style.cursor = 'none';\n\n    let overTarget = false;\n\n    const place = (e: PointerEvent, instant: boolean) => {\n      // Live rect: the container can scroll, resize, or be inside a\n      // transformed ancestor. A rect cached on mount puts the puck at a\n      // constant offset from the real pointer as soon as anything moves.\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      gsap.to(point, {\n        x: e.clientX - rect.left,\n        y: e.clientY - rect.top,\n        duration: instant ? 0 : follow,\n        ease: 'power2.out',\n        // Each move REPLACES the last tween instead of queueing behind it.\n        // Without it, ~120 events a second means ~120 live 0.4s tweens all\n        // writing the same transform, and the puck visibly stutters.\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n      });\n    };\n\n    const onEnter = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      // Teleport on entry, then fade in. Tweening from wherever the puck was\n      // parked draws a visible streak diagonally across the panel every time\n      // the pointer re-enters.\n      place(e, true);\n      gsap.to(point, { autoAlpha: 1, duration: fade, ease: 'power2.out', overwrite: 'auto' });\n    };\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      place(e, false);\n\n      const hit =\n        e.target instanceof Element ? e.target.closest('[data-cursor-target]') : null;\n      const next = hit !== null;\n      // Only tween on the transition. Re-firing the same resize tween on every\n      // move restarts its ease and the puck never reaches the target size.\n      if (next === overTarget) return;\n      overTarget = next;\n\n      gsap.to(circle, {\n        scale: next ? 1 : size / targetSize,\n        duration: resize,\n        ease: 'expo.out',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n      });\n      gsap.to(caption, {\n        autoAlpha: next ? 1 : 0,\n        scale: next ? 1 : 0.5,\n        duration: resize,\n        ease: 'expo.out',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n      });\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      gsap.to(point, { autoAlpha: 0, duration: fade, ease: 'power2.out', overwrite: 'auto' });\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointerenter', onEnter);\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerenter', onEnter);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      el.style.cursor = previousCursor;\n      // Kill before clearing, or an in-flight tween writes the transform\n      // straight back onto a node React has already recycled.\n      gsap.killTweensOf([point, circle, caption]);\n      gsap.set([point, circle, caption], { clearProps: 'all' });\n    };\n  }, [duration, size, targetSize]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the three container listeners, restores the container's previous inline `cursor`, calls `gsap.killTweensOf` on the wrapper, disc and label, then `gsap.set(..., { clearProps: \"all\" })`. Kill precedes clear so no in-flight tween can write the transform back after React has recycled the node.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The puck still appears and still swells over targets — the affordance is preserved — but every duration collapses to 0, so it is welded to the pointer with no trailing lag and the size change is a hard cut. The native cursor stays hidden, because a container with no cursor at all would be worse than one that moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `gsap.to` per pointermove without `overwrite: \"auto\"`. Sixty to a hundred and twenty 0.4s tweens end up live at once, all writing the same transform, and the puck stutters instead of gliding — the single most common way this effect is got wrong.\n- Fading in without teleporting first. On every re-entry the puck tweens from its parked coordinates, drawing a diagonal streak across the whole panel before it reaches the pointer.\n- Re-issuing the resize tween on every move instead of only on the hover transition. Each call restarts the `expo.out` ease from the beginning, so the puck creeps toward the target size and never gets there while the pointer keeps moving.\n- Transitioning `width` and `height` for the swell. That is a layout write per frame on an element the compositor could otherwise leave alone; `scale` on a fixed-size disc is the same picture for free.\n- Caching the container rect on mount. The panel scrolls, resizes, and may sit inside a transformed ancestor — a stale rect offsets the puck from the real pointer by a constant amount that never corrects itself.\n- Putting `cursor: none` in the class list. It then applies on touch devices too, where it hides nothing but does suppress the text-selection caret; setting it from the effect means it only ever exists where the puck does.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch nothing attaches: no listeners, no `cursor: none`, and the puck stays at `autoAlpha: 0` parked off-container, so a tap does not flash a disc at the touch point.\n- Mark any element inside with `data-cursor-target` to trigger the labelled state — including elements nested arbitrarily deep, since the check is a `closest()` walk.\n- The root sets `isolate`, so the puck stacks above the panel content without needing a page-level z-index.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove into the panel: the arrow disappears and a small disc arrives at the pointer without sweeping in from an edge, then trails about 0.4s behind as you move. Pass over a marked target and it swells into a labelled puck; leave and it shrinks back. Stop moving and it comes fully to rest rather than creeping. On a touch device the native cursor and all default behaviour are untouched.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "heatmap-chart-card": "# Build: Heatmap Chart Card\n\nA grey dashboard card that lays out any two-axis data, such as weekday by hour or region by month, as a CSS grid of rounded cells. Each cell's colour is the accent mixed into the chart track by its share of the maximum, so the ramp follows the theme in light and dark. Hovering a cell, or moving to it with the arrow keys, rings it, darkens its row and column labels, floats a small tooltip and swaps the count-up headline to that cell with its gap to the average. A period pill switches between datasets and the cells pop in again on every switch.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA two-axis heatmap card on a plain CSS grid: cells mix the accent into the chart track, and hovering one rings it and swaps the headline.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Resolve the data: `ranges` wins, then `rows`; with neither, the card runs on `HEATMAP_RANGES`, three built-in weekday by two-hour datasets. Those come from a working-hours bell plus an evening bump, a weekday weight and a seeded `createRng` wobble, scaled so the cells sum to an exact total (1,892 for the last 7 days). The rounding residue goes onto the busiest cell.\n2. One `useMemo` derives the total, the mean cell and the ceiling, which is `max` (prop or range) or else the largest value. A cell at value v paints `color-mix(in oklab, <accent> s%, var(--color-chart-track))` with `s = 10 + 90 * min(1, v / ceiling)`. The legend draws the same mix at 0.15, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 and 1.\n3. The grid is one CSS grid with `grid-template-columns: max-content repeat(n, minmax(0, 1fr))` and a 4px gap: a label column, then one track per column, and a last row of column labels. Row height is `floor((168 - (rows - 1) * 4) / rows)` clamped to 18–40px, which gives 20px cells for 7 rows and 39px cells for 4. The section is a size container, and when it is under 24rem with more than 8 columns, every other column label is hidden so the labels never touch.\n4. Each cell has `.animate-cell-pop` (fade and scale from 0.5) with a delay of `c * 24 + r * 16` ms, so the grid fills in a diagonal sweep. The grid is keyed by the active range id, so a period switch remounts it and the sweep plays again.\n5. Hovering a cell sets `active`. The cell gets a 2px `outline` in `--color-text-primary`, offset 1px and lifted with `z-10`, and its row and column labels turn from tertiary to primary text. A tooltip inside the cell shows the cell name and value, anchored left on the first two columns, right on the last two and centred elsewhere.\n6. The header swaps `title` for the cell name (\"Tue · 14:00\" for the built-in data) and the total for the cell value through `useCountUp`. The value sits in a `<p>` keyed by the cell so `.animate-number-fade` replays. The chip changes from the period change to the cell's percent gap to the mean, with a \"vs avg\" suffix.\n7. The grid is one tab stop (`role=\"group\"`, `aria-roledescription=\"heatmap\"`). Arrow keys move the active cell in two dimensions, Home and End jump to the row ends, and Escape, blur or leaving the grid with the pointer clears it. The header is an `aria-live=\"polite\"` region, so the new cell is announced.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MIX_FLOOR = 10%`\n  A zero cell keeps a trace of the accent, so it still reads as part of the grid rather than as a hole in the card.\n- `PLOT_HEIGHT / row clamp = 168px shared, rows 18–40px`\n  Cards with 4 or 7 rows end up about the same height, so a dashboard row of heatmaps lines up without fixing the row count.\n- `cell delay = c × 24ms + r × 16ms`\n  The sweep runs left to right first, the way the eye reads time, and the whole 7 × 12 grid lands in about 700ms including the 380ms pop.\n- `LEGEND_STEPS = 0.15, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1`\n  Five evenly readable swatches. The first step is above the floor so the \"Less\" swatch is still visibly tinted.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction HeatmapChartCard(props?: HeatmapChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype HeatmapRow = { label: string; values: number[] }\ntype HeatmapRange = { id: string; label: string; rows: HeatmapRow[]; columns?: string[]; max?: number; change?: number; headline?: number }\nconst HEATMAP_RANGES: HeatmapRange[]\nconst HEATMAP_HOURS: string[]\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Active users\"`\n  Header label while no cell is active.\n- `rows: HeatmapRow[]`\n  One entry per row; `values` line up with `columns`. Ignored when `ranges` is passed.\n- `columns: string[]` — default `HEATMAP_HOURS for the built-in data, else \"1\"…\"n\"`\n  Column labels under the grid.\n- `color: string` — default `\"var(--color-chart-6)\"`\n  Ramp accent; any CSS colour.\n- `max: number` — default `largest value`\n  Value of a fully saturated cell.\n- `change: number`\n  Percent change for the chip when `ranges` is not used.\n- `headline: number` — default `sum of all cells`\n  Headline figure when `ranges` is not used.\n- `periodLabel: string` — default `\"This week\"`\n  Text of the static pill when no ranges are in play. An empty string hides it.\n- `ranges: HeatmapRange[]` — default `HEATMAP_RANGES when no rows are passed`\n  Periods for the dropdown pill; each brings its own rows.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled active range id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range`\n  Uncontrolled starting range id.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the id picked from the period menu.\n- `format: (value: number) => string` — default `rounded en-US number`\n  Formats the headline and tooltip values.\n- `cellLabel: (row: string, column: string) => string` — default `\"Row · Column\"`\n  Names a cell in the header and tooltip.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst model = useMemo(() => {\n  const all = rows.flatMap((r) => r.values.slice(0, columns.length));\n  const total = all.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);\n  const largest = Math.max(0, ...all);\n  const ceiling = maxProp ?? activeRange?.max ?? largest;\n  return { total, mean: all.length ? total / all.length : 0, ceiling: ceiling > 0 ? ceiling : 1, largest };\n}, [rows, columns.length, maxProp, activeRange]);\n\n// Each cell mixes the accent into the chart track, so the ramp follows the theme.\nconst mix = (t: number) => {\n  const share = MIX_FLOOR + (100 - MIX_FLOOR) * Math.min(1, Math.max(0, t));\n  return `color-mix(in oklab, ${color} ${Math.round(share)}%, var(--color-chart-track))`;\n};\nconst rowHeight = Math.max(\n  ROW_HEIGHT[0],\n  Math.min(ROW_HEIGHT[1], Math.floor((PLOT_HEIGHT - (rows.length - 1) * GAP) / Math.max(1, rows.length))),\n);\n\nconst activeValue = active ? rows[active.r]?.values[active.c] : undefined;\nconst isActive = active !== null && activeValue !== undefined;\nconst headlineValue = isActive ? activeValue : (activeRange?.headline ?? headlineProp ?? model.total);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame when the target changes and on unmount. The dropdown removes its outside-press listener when it closes. Everything else is CSS and React event props, so nothing else needs tearing down.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the cells appear at once (`motion-reduce:animate-none` on `.animate-cell-pop`, and no delays), the headline shows its value with no count-up and no fade, and the label colour and chevron changes are instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building the ramp from opacity. A translucent accent over the grey card looks right in light mode but turns muddy in dark mode. Mixing into `--color-chart-track` gives an opaque colour that follows each theme.\n- Leaving the hovered cell at the default stacking order. Every cell has a transform from the pop animation and so its own stacking context, and later cells would paint over an earlier cell's tooltip. The active cell gets `z-10`.\n- Normalising each range by its own largest value when comparing periods. Pin `max` (prop or per range) when the colours have to mean the same thing across ranges.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `HEATMAP_RANGES` and `HEATMAP_HOURS` are exported, as are the `HeatmapRow` and `HeatmapRange` types. A range can carry its own `columns`, `max`, `change` and `headline`.\n- Pass `cellLabel` to name cells differently in the header and tooltip, for example `(row, col) => `${col} ${row}``.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe default card reads \"Active users\", \"1,892\" and a lime \"+5.2%\" chip with a \"Last 7 days\" pill. Below is a 7 × 12 blue grid, Mon–Sun by 00–22, darkest around midday on weekdays and lighter at night and on weekends, with a Less → More legend. Hovering a cell rings it, darkens its row and column labels, shows a tooltip and changes the header to \"Tue · 14:00\" style text with that cell's value and a \"vs avg\" chip. Picking \"Last 30 days\" pops the cells in again and shows \"8,214\". The emerald region card reads \"963\" and switches to H2 columns Jul–Dec.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "height-first-media-stage": "# Build: Height-First Media Stage\n\nAlmost every media stage on the web is sized width-first: a full-width box with an aspect ratio and whatever height falls out of it. On a short laptop that makes a 3:2 hero taller than the viewport, so the piece built to be seen at a glance has to be scrolled. This inverts the constraint — the height is authored, the width is derived from it through `aspect-ratio`, and `max-width: 100%` is the only escape hatch. The stage always fits vertically, and on a narrow screen it gives up size rather than overflowing. Layers cross-fade on opacity alone; the only JavaScript is the index.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA media stage sized from its height, with the width derived from the ratio, cross-fading layers and a caption that replays per index.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The figure gets exactly three sizing declarations: `height` (authored, e.g. `min(80dvh, 100%)`), `aspect-ratio` (which derives the width from that height), and `max-width: 100%`. No width is ever set — setting one would over-constrain the box and `aspect-ratio` would be ignored.\n2. When the derived width would exceed the container, `max-width: 100%` clamps it and the ratio then pulls the *height* down to match. That single line is what turns \"always fits vertically\" into \"always fits\", with no media query and no measurement.\n3. The default height is `min(80dvh, 100%)`: `dvh` so a collapsing mobile URL bar cannot make the stage overflow, and the `100%` term so the same component also behaves inside a bounded parent instead of insisting on a fraction of the viewport.\n4. Every layer stays mounted, absolutely positioned in the same stacking context, and only `opacity` changes — from 0 to 1 on the incoming layer and back on the outgoing one, with `pointer-events: none` on the hidden ones so an invisible layer cannot swallow clicks meant for the visible one.\n5. The cross-fade transition is `linear`, not eased. Two layers whose opacities are each eased sum to less than 1 halfway through, and the stage visibly dips darker mid-transition; linear keeps the sum at exactly 1 throughout.\n6. The caption wrapper carries `key={current}`, so changing index unmounts it and mounts a fresh node. A CSS entrance animation that has never run on a node plays from 0%, which is how the caption re-enters on every change without any JavaScript replay logic.\n7. The keyframes live in a scoped `<style href=\"...\" precedence=\"medium\">`. React 19 hoists it into the head and deduplicates by `href`, so ten mounted stages inject one copy and nothing has to be added to a global sheet.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `height = 'min(80dvh, 100%)'`\n  80% of the *dynamic* viewport height leaves room for a header and the top of the next section, so the stage reads as part of a page rather than as a takeover. `dvh` rather than `vh` because on iOS Safari `vh` is the URL-bar-collapsed height, so `80vh` overflows the actually visible area at rest. The `min(..., 100%)` term keeps it correct inside a bounded parent.\n- `aspectRatio = '3 / 2'`\n  Wider than the 80dvh height on any landscape display, so the width clamp is the binding constraint on desktop and the height clamp binds on a phone — both paths are exercised by the default. A ratio taller than the height would make the stage narrower than its own caption row.\n- `duration = 500`\n  Milliseconds of cross-fade. Under ~250ms the two layers read as a cut and the point of stacking them is lost; past ~800ms both layers are legible at once for long enough to look like a rendering fault.\n- `caption entrance = 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), translateY(14px)`\n  Matched to the cross-fade duration so caption and image resolve together. 14px is roughly one line of the caption type — enough travel to read as arrival, small enough that the text is never legible in the wrong place.\n- `cross-fade easing = linear`\n  Not a stylistic choice. Any easing applied to both layers makes their opacities sum to less than 1 in the middle of the transition, and the stage flashes darker halfway through every change.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction HeightFirstMediaStage(props: { children: ReactNode; height?: string; aspectRatio?: string; duration?: number; caption?: (index: number) => ReactNode; index?: number; defaultIndex?: number; onIndexChange?: (index: number) => void; className?: string; 'aria-label'?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One media layer per child. All layers stay mounted and stacked.\n- `height: string` — default `'min(80dvh, 100%)'`\n  Authored height as a CSS length. The width is derived from it — never set a width here.\n- `aspectRatio: string` — default `'3 / 2'`\n  Ratio the derived width follows, as a CSS `aspect-ratio` value.\n- `duration: number` — default `500`\n  Cross-fade duration in milliseconds.\n- `caption: (index: number) => ReactNode`\n  Rendered into a node re-keyed on every index change, so its entrance keyframe replays.\n- `index: number`\n  Controlled index. Omit for uncontrolled behaviour.\n- `defaultIndex: number` — default `0`\n  Starting index when uncontrolled.\n- `onIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Called with the wrapped index after either control is used.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the centring wrapper via `cn`.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Media stage'`\n  Accessible name for the figure group.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Scoped stylesheet. React 19 hoists and deduplicates it by `href`, so the\n * keyframe exists once no matter how many stages are mounted and nothing has\n * to be added to a global sheet.\n *\n * The class exists only so a `key` change can restart it: React unmounts the\n * old caption node and mounts a new one, the animation has never run on that\n * node, so it plays from 0%. Toggling a class on a persistent node would not\n * work — the animation would have to be removed for a frame first.\n */\nconst STAGE_CSS = `\n@keyframes k-hfms-in {\n  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(14px); }\n  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }\n}\n\n.k-hfms-in {\n  animation: k-hfms-in 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) both;\n}\n\n/* The caption's content is the information; its arrival is decoration. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .k-hfms-in { animation: none; }\n}\n`;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release. There are no listeners, no rAF, no timers and no observers: the cross-fade and the caption entrance are both CSS on the elements themselves, so they die with the nodes. React 19 reference-counts the hoisted `<style>` and removes it when the last stage unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The layer transition is set to `none`, so an index change is an instant cut between two fully opaque layers rather than a zero-length fade. The caption entrance is disabled by a `prefers-reduced-motion` block inside the scoped stylesheet, so the caption appears immediately at its final position and full opacity — the information still arrives, only its arrival is dropped. Sizing behaviour is identical either way.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sizing width-first — `w-full` plus `aspect-ratio` — which is the common shape. On a 13\" laptop the derived height exceeds the viewport, so the hero has to be scrolled to be seen and the layout silently breaks on exactly the machines designers use.\n- Setting a width as well as the height. The box is then over-constrained, `aspect-ratio` is discarded, and the stage quietly stops being the ratio you asked for while still looking plausible in your own window.\n- Using `vh` instead of `dvh`. On mobile Safari `100vh` is the tall viewport with the browser chrome hidden, so a stage sized against it overflows the visible area until the user scrolls — which is the exact failure this layout exists to prevent.\n- Easing the opacity transition. Both layers dim toward the middle, their sum drops below 1, and the stage visibly darkens on every change. It looks like a dropped frame and is very hard to attribute to the easing curve.\n- Mounting only the current layer. Each change then pays a fresh decode and paint, so the first frame of the incoming layer is empty and the transition starts from blank instead of from the outgoing image.\n- Replaying the caption entrance by toggling a class on a persistent node. The animation is already applied, so re-adding the class does nothing — you would have to remove it, force a reflow, then re-add. Changing `key` remounts the node instead, and the animation runs because on that node it never has.\n- Wrapping the index with `%`. In JavaScript `-1 % 5` is `-1`, so pressing Previous on the first item indexes past the start and the stage goes blank. `wrapIndex` uses a true modulo.\n- Leaving hidden layers hittable. At `opacity: 0` a layer still receives pointer events and sits on top of the visible one, so links and buttons in the current layer stop responding.\n- Putting the keyframes in a plain inline `<style>` with no `href`. Every mounted stage then injects its own copy of the same rule into the document.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Uncontrolled by default via `defaultIndex`, controlled when `index` is passed — in controlled mode the internal state is never written, so the parent stays the single source of truth.\n- The figure is marked `role=\"group\"` with `aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"`, and non-current layers are `aria-hidden`, so a screen reader is offered one media layer at a time rather than all of them stacked.\n- The only content this component draws itself is the counter and the controls; the layers are children, so it composes with a canvas, a video, a CSS gradient, or a Lottie without knowing which.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nShorten the viewport to about 600px tall and the stage shrinks with it — no vertical scrollbar appears and no part of the media is cut off. Narrow the window until the derived width would exceed the container and the stage gives up height instead of clipping sideways. Press Next and the layers cross-fade with no dark dip halfway through, and the caption rises 14px into place on every change including the wrap from the last item back to the first. Press Previous on the first item and it lands on the last, not on a blank stage. Enable reduced motion and the change becomes an instant cut with the caption already in place.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "hinged-container-intro": "# Build: Hinged Container Intro\n\nA page-enter that tips its container up from below the fold like a lid rather than sliding it in. The panel starts at 120% of its own height, rotated -45deg in X about its bottom edge, and lands over 1.4s on expo.out; captions inside it stagger in starting 0.6s before it arrives. A cue fires at 0.8s so an expensive scene can start while the panel is still in flight.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe page body swings up from below the fold, hinged on its own bottom edge, with captions following it in.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A wrapper carries `perspective: 1200px` and the panel inside it gets `transformOrigin: \"center bottom\"` plus `transformPerspective`, so the rotation pivots on the edge the panel is about to rest on and the top edge sweeps a much longer arc than the bottom.\n2. The panel tween is `fromTo(el, { yPercent: 120, rotateX: -45, opacity: 0 }, { yPercent: 0, rotateX: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 1.4, ease: \"expo.out\" })`. `yPercent` rather than a viewport unit means the same component works inside a 320px card and a full-bleed layout without re-tuning.\n3. Elements matching `detailSelector` are collected with `querySelectorAll` on the panel and appended at `\"-=0.6\"`, so their 0.9s stagger overlaps the tail of the flight and the two moves read as one gesture.\n4. A `tl.call(cue, undefined, 0.8)` sits at an absolute timeline position, so the cue stays at 0.8s even if either tween is retimed. That is where a WebGL scene, a video `play()`, or a data fetch is kicked off — mid-flight, while the eye is on the moving panel.\n5. The whole timeline is built inside `useGsap`, keyed on `routeKey`; a key change reverts the previous context (restoring the inline styles GSAP wrote) and builds a fresh timeline from frame zero.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 1.4`\n  Panel flight in seconds. Under ~1.1s the expo.out tail is too short to read as deceleration and the panel appears to snap; over ~1.8s the visitor is waiting on chrome before they can read anything.\n- `tilt = -45`\n  Degrees of X rotation at the start. At -20deg the perspective foreshortening is barely visible so it looks like a plain slide; past about -70deg the panel is nearly edge-on and its content is an unreadable sliver for most of the flight.\n- `travel = 120`\n  Percent of the panel's own height it starts below rest. 100 would put its top edge exactly at the bottom of its box, but the rotation lifts the top edge upward, so 100 lets a corner peek in at frame zero. The extra 20% covers the rotated bounding box.\n- `perspective = 1200`\n  Pixels. Around 600 the lens is wide enough that the panel visibly skews toward one corner; above ~2000 the projection is near-orthographic and the hinge stops reading as depth.\n- `DETAIL_OVERLAP = 0.6`\n  Seconds the caption stagger starts before the panel lands. At 0 the two moves read as two separate events with a dead beat between them; beyond ~0.9 the captions are moving while the panel is still steeply rotated and the text shears.\n- `DETAIL_STAGGER = 0.1`\n  Seconds between captions. Fast enough that three to five items finish inside the panel tween, slow enough that each is individually perceptible.\n- `sceneCue = 0.8`\n  Seconds into the timeline before the heavy cue fires. Late enough that the panel has visually committed to arriving (so a first-frame stall is hidden by motion), early enough that a 1.6s scene intro finishes about 1s after the panel lands rather than long after.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction HingedContainerIntro(props: { children: ReactNode; routeKey?: string; duration?: number; tilt?: number; travel?: number; perspective?: number; detailSelector?: string; onSceneCue?: () => void; sceneCue?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Page body. Rendered inside the hinging panel.\n- `routeKey: string`\n  Change to replay. Pass `usePathname()` in an App Router layout.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.4`\n  Panel flight time in seconds.\n- `tilt: number` — default `-45`\n  Starting X rotation in degrees.\n- `travel: number` — default `120`\n  Starting offset as a percentage of the panel's own height.\n- `perspective: number` — default `1200`\n  Perspective on the wrapper, in pixels.\n- `detailSelector: string` — default `'[data-hinge-detail]'`\n  Descendants that stagger in behind the panel.\n- `onSceneCue: () => void`\n  Called `sceneCue` seconds in. Start expensive work here.\n- `sceneCue: number` — default `0.8`\n  Absolute timeline position of the cue, in seconds.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the perspective wrapper.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function HingedContainerIntro({\n  children,\n  routeKey,\n  duration = 1.4,\n  tilt = -45,\n  travel = 120,\n  perspective = 1200,\n  detailSelector = '[data-hinge-detail]',\n  onSceneCue,\n  sceneCue = 0.8,\n  className,\n}: HingedContainerIntroProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const panel = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Held in a ref so a caller passing an inline arrow does not have to\n  // memoise it to avoid replaying the whole intro on every parent render.\n  const cue = useRef(onSceneCue);\n  cue.current = onSceneCue;\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = panel.current;\n      if (!el || reduced) return;\n\n      const details = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(detailSelector));\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({ defaults: { ease: 'expo.out' } });\n\n      // transformOrigin bottom + negative rotateX is the whole trick: the\n      // panel pivots on the edge it is about to sit on, so the top edge\n      // travels much further than the bottom and the move reads as a hinge\n      // instead of a translation with a tilt bolted on.\n      tl.fromTo(\n        el,\n        {\n          yPercent: travel,\n          rotateX: tilt,\n          opacity: 0,\n          transformOrigin: 'center bottom',\n          transformPerspective: perspective,\n        },\n        { yPercent: 0, rotateX: 0, opacity: 1, duration },\n      );\n\n      // Guarded: GSAP logs \"target not found\" for an empty array, and a\n      // component that warns on every mount trains people to ignore the\n      // console.\n      if (details.length > 0) {\n        tl.fromTo(\n          details,\n          { opacity: 0, y: 30 },\n          { opacity: 1, y: 0, duration: DETAIL_DURATION, stagger: DETAIL_STAGGER },\n          `-=${DETAIL_OVERLAP}`,\n        );\n      }\n\n      // A number position is absolute on the timeline, so the cue stays at\n      // 0.8 s no matter how the two tweens above are retimed.\n      tl.call(\n        () => {\n          cue.current?.();\n        },\n        undefined,\n        sceneCue,\n      );\n    },\n    root,\n    [routeKey, reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` reverts the `gsap.context` on unmount and on every `routeKey` change, which kills the timeline and restores the inline styles GSAP wrote to the panel and captions. The cue callback lives in a ref that React discards with the component, so a call queued at 0.8s cannot reach a stale closure.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No timeline is built. A `prefers-reduced-motion` rule in the component's own stylesheet cancels the `opacity: 0` start state, so the panel and its captions render in their final position immediately, and `onSceneCue` fires on mount so anything it gates still loads.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Setting the start state only inside the effect. `useEffect` can run after the browser has painted, so the finished page flashes for one frame and then jumps below the fold. The start state is a CSS rule on `[data-hinged-panel]`; the reduced-motion branch of that same rule is what keeps the content from depending on JS at all.\n- Rotating without `transformOrigin: \"center bottom\"`. About the centre the panel appears to tumble through its own resting position, and the bottom edge dips below the fold on the way in.\n- Putting `perspective` on the panel instead of an ancestor. Then any transformed child gets its own vanishing point and the parallax between panel and captions is wrong at the edges.\n- Positioning the cue with a relative `\"-=0.6\"` string. Retiming the panel tween silently moves the cue with it; a numeric position pins it to 0.8s absolute.\n- Firing the cue only in the animated branch. Reduced-motion users then never get the scene, the video or the fetch it was gating — the cue is content delivery, not decoration.\n- Passing an inline arrow as `onSceneCue` and putting it in the effect deps. Every parent render rebuilds the timeline and the intro restarts mid-flight; the callback is held in a ref instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- App Router wiring: render this inside `app/layout.tsx` around `{children}` and pass `routeKey={usePathname()}`. The component itself never imports a router, so the same file works in Pages Router, Remix, or a modal keyed on a record id.\n- The cue is not a substitute for a load event. If the thing it starts can fail, give it its own timeout — see `safe-preloader` for the ceiling pattern.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn mount the panel rises from below with its top edge visibly further away than its bottom, settles without overshoot, and the captions land about a third of a second later. Switching route replays it from frame zero with no flash of the previous content. With reduced motion on, the panel is simply there, and the cue still fires.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "home-dashboard": "# Build: Home Dashboard\n\nThe BoardUI home dashboard as one working page. The kit sidebar switches views in local state, the header carries breadcrumbs, a live notification bell, a Filters menu that shows and hides each widget and a Create ticket dialog. Under it sit Recent hires, Earned so far, Revenue, Contributions, four stat cards and a customers table that filters, sorts, selects, edits, duplicates and deletes rows.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full home dashboard page: in-block sidebar views, hires, earnings, revenue, contributions, key numbers and a working customers table.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `DashboardFrame` (in `home-dashboard-shell.tsx`) is a `h-full w-full` flex row: the sidebar and a `main` column that scrolls on its own. Every nav row gets `href=\"#view-<key>\"`, and one `onClickCapture` on the sidebar wrapper finds the closest link, checks it is inside that wrapper in the DOM (so portalled menus are ignored), calls `preventDefault()` and reports the key. The `href=\"#\"` Support row maps to a `support` view. Enter on a link fires the same click, so the keyboard path is covered.\n2. The page holds the view key controlled or uncontrolled (`view` / `defaultView` / `onViewChange`). `\"home\"` renders the card grid; any other key renders `DashboardViewPlaceholder` with that row's icon and label and a \"Back to Home\" button. The title and the last breadcrumb follow the view, and the Board team and Mertcan crumbs jump to Home and Profile.\n3. Filters is a `Dropdown` whose `DropdownItem`s are `aria-pressed` toggles over a `Set` of widget ids. A hidden widget is skipped, and when one half of a two-column row is hidden the other takes the full width. The trigger shows `shown/total` while anything is hidden, and \"Show all\" restores the full set.\n4. Create ticket opens a controlled `Modal` with a Subject input and a Priority select. Submit stays disabled until the subject has text. Submitting closes the dialog, adds one to the Support tickets stat (708 to 709) and fires a success toast through the `Toaster` mounted inside the frame.\n5. Recent hires is controlled by team: Board team shows all eight sample hires with a total of 56, and any other team filters the cards and counts the visible hires. The earnings and revenue cards get Weekly, Monthly and Yearly series whose last two points give the headline and its change (7,462 vs 6,500 = +14.8%, 18,240 vs 16,673 = +9.4%).\n6. `HomeCustomersTable` keeps rows controlled or uncontrolled. Price, product and region selects and the search field filter the rows and reset to page 1. Columns sort through the table's `sortDescriptor`, and the header checkbox selects the page, with an indeterminate state. A bulk bar replaces the results count while rows are selected. Each row has an inline Purchase select and Edit, Duplicate and Delete actions; Edit opens a dialog that validates the price.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `today = 2026-11-05`\n  Fixed anchor for the hires' relative start dates, so server and client renders agree.\n- `customers pageSize = 8`\n  Twenty-two sample rows give three pages, so pagination is visible and works.\n- `Recent hires pageSize = 4`\n  Four hire cards fill the left half of the first row at 1280px; Previous and Next page through the rest.\n- `support tickets base = 708`\n  The stat's starting value; each created ticket adds one.\n- `first row split = minmax(0,0.82fr) / minmax(0,1fr)`\n  Gives the earnings bars a little more room than the hire cards, as in the reference layout.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction HomeDashboard(props: { view?: string; defaultView?: string; onViewChange?: (key: string) => void; userName?: string; today?: CalendarDate; hires?: Hire[]; customers?: readonly Customer[]; defaultWidgets?: readonly HomeWidget[]; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `view: string`\n  Nav key on screen. Controlled; pair with onViewChange.\n- `defaultView: string` — default `\"home\"`\n  Initial nav key when uncontrolled. \"home\" is the dashboard; any other key shows the placeholder view.\n- `onViewChange: (key: string) => void`\n  Called with the new key when a nav row, a crumb or a back button is used.\n- `userName: string` — default `\"Mertcan\"`\n  First name in the \"Welcome\" title.\n- `today: CalendarDate` — default `parseDate(\"2026-11-05\")`\n  Anchor for the hires' relative start dates.\n- `hires: Hire[]` — default `HOME_HIRES`\n  Cards in Recent hires (eight sample hires across three teams).\n- `customers: readonly Customer[]` — default `HOME_CUSTOMERS`\n  Initial rows of the customers table; edits stay in local state.\n- `defaultWidgets: readonly HomeWidget[]` — default `all six`\n  Widgets shown on first render: hires, earnings, revenue, contributions, stats, customers.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the frame root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function HomeDashboard({\n  view,\n  defaultView = 'home',\n  onViewChange,\n  userName = 'Mertcan',\n  today = HOME_TODAY,\n  hires = HOME_HIRES,\n  customers,\n  defaultWidgets = ALL_WIDGETS,\n  className,\n}: HomeDashboardProps) {\n  const [innerView, setInnerView] = useState(defaultView);\n  const current = view ?? innerView;\n  const go = useCallback(\n    (key: string) => {\n      if (view === undefined) setInnerView(key);\n      onViewChange?.(key);\n    },\n    [view, onViewChange],\n  );\n\n  const widgets = useWidgetToggles(ALL_WIDGETS, defaultWidgets);\n  const [ticketOpen, setTicketOpen] = useState(false);\n  const [ticketsCreated, setTicketsCreated] = useState(0);\n  const [team, setTeam] = useState<string | null>(null);\n\n  const shown = widgets.isShown;\n\n  const stats: Stat[] = [\n    { icon: RiGroupLine, label: 'Customers', value: '14,592', delta: '+5.3%', deltaColor: 'lime' },\n    { icon: RiBox3Line, label: 'Unit sold', value: '385', delta: '-2.1%', deltaColor: 'rose' },\n    { icon: RiShoppingBasketLine, label: 'Orders', value: '1,394', delta: '0.00%', deltaColor: 'neutral' },\n    {\n      icon: RiChatSmile2Line,\n      label: 'Support tickets',\n      value: (SUPPORT_TICKETS + ticketsCreated).toLocaleString('en-US'),\n      delta: '+12.8%',\n      deltaColor: 'lime',\n    },\n  ];\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners in the page; the nav handler is a React capture prop and the drawer is plain state. The composed charts, overlays and the Toaster clean up their own timers and frames.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The page adds no motion of its own. The composed charts and cards follow `useReducedMotion` and draw at their final state, and the dropdown, dialogs and toasts fall back to instant open and close.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Letting the sidebar links navigate. Without the capture handler a click on a nav row writes a hash into the host page's URL.\n- Using React's tree instead of DOM containment to decide whether a click came from the sidebar. Portalled menus bubble through React ancestors, so `event.currentTarget.contains(link)` is what keeps them out.\n- Swapping the edit dialog's content out as it closes. The table keeps the edited customer in state and toggles a separate `open` flag, so the exit animation still has content to draw.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Every card is an existing kit item (`DashboardSidebar`, `DashboardPageHeader`, `NotificationBellBadge`, `RecentHiresCard`, `EarningsChartCard`, `LineChartCard`, `ContributionsCard`, `StatCards`, `Table`, `RowActions`, `Pagination`). The page owns the wiring, the ticket form and the customers table.\n- The finance and marketing dashboard templates import `DashboardFrame`, `DashboardHeader`, `DashboardViewPlaceholder`, `DashboardWidgetMenu`, `useWidgetToggles` and `DashboardEmptyGrid` from `home-dashboard-shell.tsx`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280x800 the sidebar shows Home selected and the main column shows \"Welcome Mertcan\", Recent hires next to Earned so far, Revenue next to Contributions, four stat cards and the customers table. Clicking Finance in the sidebar shows the Finance placeholder, and \"Back to Home\" returns. Unticking Revenue in Filters removes that card and the button reads 5/6. Creating a ticket with a subject raises Support tickets from 708 to 709 and shows a toast. Searching the table narrows the rows, Edit renames a row in place, and confirming Delete removes the row with a toast.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "hover-list-image-trail": "# Build: Hover List Image Trail\n\nEach row entered adds a plate to a column beside the list, and the column follows the pointer vertically with `quickTo`. Every plate is a masked-reveal pair — a clipping wrapper sliding up while the canvas inside slides down — and the stack is bounded by culling the oldest node rather than by pooling.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nHovering a row stacks a plate into a column that follows the pointer; the oldest is culled past twenty.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The spawn is two elements on one tween, moving in opposite directions. The wrapper animates `yPercent` from 100 to 0 while the canvas inside it animates from -100 to 0, on the same duration and the same ease. Because the wrapper clips, the two motions cancel and the plate is uncovered in place instead of flying in — the difference between a card arriving and a card being developed.\n2. The cancellation only holds if both tweens share duration and ease exactly. Any mismatch and the plate visibly slides inside its own frame, which reads as a parallax bug rather than a reveal.\n3. A node is created per hover and the stack is bounded by a cull, which is the deliberate opposite of `pooled-image-trail`. That is affordable here because hover entry is bounded by how fast a human can cross rows — roughly ten a second at worst — where a pointermove trail is bounded by the display refresh rate. The choice between pooling and culling follows from the event arrival rate, not from taste.\n4. Every spawn drops the oldest node once the stack holds twenty, so the DOM has a hard ceiling at all times rather than a ceiling per interaction. Tweens are killed before removal so GSAP cannot write to a node the DOM has already released.\n5. The column is centred on the pointer once with `gsap.set(stack, { yPercent: -50 })`, so the follow tween writes a raw `y` and never subtracts a half-height it would have to re-measure. A single `quickTo` at 0.5s with `power4` gives a long, heavy tail: `power4` spends most of its time near the destination, so the column snaps toward the pointer and then settles.\n6. Plates are painted once into offscreen canvases at mount and blitted with a single `drawImage` on spawn, so no gradient, sheen or text work lands on the frame where a tween starts — the one frame that cannot afford it. There are no image assets and nothing allocated through `createObjectURL`, so nothing needs revoking.\n7. `pointerover` bubbles, so one listener covers every row however React re-renders the list, but it also fires again for each descendant crossed inside the same row. The spawn is therefore gated on `relatedTarget` being outside the row, which turns \"the event fired\" into \"the pointer actually changed rows\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MAX_PLATES = 20`\n  Hard ceiling on live plates. Enough that a quick scan down a list leaves a visible stack with depth; low enough that twenty composited layers of 168x116 stay trivial. Remove this and an idle user running the pointer up and down the list allocates for as long as the page is open.\n- `REVEAL_DURATION = 0.6`\n  Seconds for the masked pair. Long for a hover response, deliberately: the reveal is the content of the interaction, not feedback about it. Below ~0.35 the opposite-direction pair is too fast to read as a reveal and just looks like a fade.\n- `ease: 'expo.inOut' = expo.inOut`\n  The most extreme standard curve, and the reason the reveal reads as mechanical rather than soft. Nearly all the travel happens in the middle third, so the plate is still at the start of its move, then has arrived, with very little visible in between.\n- `followDuration = 0.5`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the column. Longer than a cursor follow because the column is a heavy object carrying several plates; at 0.2 it is welded to the pointer and the stack stops feeling like it has mass.\n- `follow ease 'power4' = power4 (in-out)`\n  A very late curve, so the column covers the gap early and creeps the last few pixels. quickTo remutates the end value on every event, so an in-out curve absorbs direction changes instead of restarting an outward ramp each time.\n- `PLATE_SCALE = 2`\n  Backing-store multiplier for the offscreen plates. Fixed rather than `devicePixelRatio` so the drawn output is byte-identical on every machine and a screenshot diff of this component is meaningful.\n- `hue step = 137.508 degrees`\n  The golden angle. Consecutive plates are always far apart in hue and the sequence never repeats a near-duplicate, so a fast scan down the list never stacks two plates that read as the same colour.\n- `monotonic zIndex = order += 1, never reset`\n  A later plate must always sit above an earlier one. Reusing or resetting the counter lets two live plates tie, and tied z-indices resolve by DOM order, which changes as the cull removes nodes — the observable symptom is a plate flickering behind one that arrived before it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction HoverListImageTrail(props: { rows: TrailRow[]; tileWidth?: number; tileHeight?: number; followDuration?: number; seed?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `rows: TrailRow[]`\n  List rows. `{ id, label, meta? }`; one plate is painted per row at mount.\n- `tileWidth: number` — default `168`\n  Plate width in CSS px.\n- `tileHeight: number` — default `116`\n  Plate height in CSS px.\n- `followDuration: number` — default `0.5`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the column follow.\n- `seed: string` — default `'hover-list-image-trail'`\n  Seeds the plate palette. Same seed, same colours, every render.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst PLATE_SCALE = 2;\n/** Hard ceiling on live plates. Every spawn past this drops the oldest. */\nconst MAX_PLATES = 20;\nconst REVEAL_DURATION = 0.6;\n\n/**\n * Plates are painted once into offscreen canvases and blitted on spawn. No\n * image assets, and — more importantly — no gradient, text or shadow work on\n * the frame where a tween starts, which is exactly the frame that cannot\n * afford it.\n */\nfunction paintPlate(\n  ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D,\n  w: number,\n  h: number,\n  index: number,\n  seed: string,\n) {\n  // Seeded, never `Math.random()`: the plate colours must be identical on the\n  // server and the client, and identical across reloads so a visual diff of\n  // this component means something.\n  const rng = createRng(`${seed}-${index}`);\n  // Golden-angle hue walk: consecutive plates are always far apart in hue, so\n  // a fast scan down the list never stacks two similar colours.\n  const hue = (index * 137.508 + rng() * 18) % 360;\n\n  const bg = ctx.createLinearGradient(0, 0, w, h);\n  bg.addColorStop(0, `hsl(${hue} 46% 26%)`);\n  bg.addColorStop(1, `hsl(${(hue + 44) % 360} 52% 13%)`);\n  ctx.fillStyle = bg;\n  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n\n  // A soft off-centre sheen so the plate has a light direction rather than\n  // reading as a flat swatch.\n  const sheen = ctx.createRadialGradient(w * 0.3, h * 0.22, 0, w * 0.3, h * 0.22, h * 1.15);\n  sheen.addColorStop(0, 'rgba(255,255,255,0.22)');\n  sheen.addColorStop(1, 'rgba(255,255,255,0)');\n  ctx.fillStyle = sheen;\n  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, w, h);\n\n  ctx.strokeStyle = 'rgba(255,255,255,0.14)';\n  ctx.lineWidth = 1;\n  ctx.strokeRect(0.5, 0.5, w - 1, h - 1);\n\n  ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,255,255,0.82)';\n  ctx.font = `500 ${Math.round(h * 0.19)}px ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace`;\n  ctx.textBaseline = 'alphabetic';\n  ctx.fillText(String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0'), w * 0.07, h * 0.88);\n}\n\nexport function HoverListImageTrail({\n  rows,\n  tileWidth = 168,\n  tileHeight = 116,\n  followDuration = 0.5,\n  seed = 'hover-list-image-trail',\n  className,\n}: HoverListImageTrailProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const column = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const plates = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement[]>([]);\n\n  // Paint one plate per row, once. Re-running only when the geometry or the\n  // row count actually changes — not on every render.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const bw = tileWidth * PLATE_SCALE;\n    const bh = tileHeight * PLATE_SCALE;\n    const made: HTMLCanvasElement[] = [];\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < rows.length; i += 1) {\n      const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n      canvas.width = bw;\n      canvas.height = bh;\n      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n      if (!ctx) continue;\n      ctx.scale(PLATE_SCALE, PLATE_SCALE);\n      paintPlate(ctx, tileWidth, tileHeight, i, seed);\n      made.push(canvas);\n    }\n\n    plates.current = made;\n    return () => {\n      // Plain canvases: nothing was allocated through `createObjectURL`, so\n      // there is nothing to revoke. Dropping the references is the disposal.\n      plates.current = [];\n    };\n  }, [rows.length, tileWidth, tileHeight, seed]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const stack = column.current;\n    if (!el || !stack) return;\n\n    // A trail that follows a cursor has no meaning without a cursor. On touch\n    // `pointerenter` fires on tap, so every tap would stack a plate under the\n    // finger and leave it there.\n    if (!window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) return;\n    if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;\n\n    /* Centring the column on the pointer once, so the follow tween writes a\n       raw `y` and never has to subtract half a height it does not know. */\n    gsap.set(stack, { yPercent: -50 });\n    const yTo = gsap.quickTo(stack, 'y', { duration: followDuration, ease: 'power4' });\n\n    let seeded = false;\n    let order = 0;\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const y = e.clientY - rect.top;\n      if (seeded) {\n        yTo(y);\n      } else {\n        seeded = true;\n        // Second argument is quickTo's START value, so the column teleports on\n        // entry instead of sweeping down the list from wherever it was left.\n        yTo(y, y);\n      }\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      seeded = false;\n    };\n\n    const spawn = (index: number) => {\n      const plate = plates.current[index];\n      if (!plate) return;\n\n      /* The wrapper is the mask. `overflow: hidden` plus a fixed size is what\n         makes the opposite-direction pair read as a reveal instead of two\n         elements sliding past each other. */\n      const wrap = document.createElement('div');\n      wrap.style.position = 'absolute';\n      wrap.style.left = '0';\n      wrap.style.top = '0';\n      wrap.style.width = `${tileWidth}px`;\n      wrap.style.height = `${tileHeight}px`;\n      wrap.style.overflow = 'hidden';\n      wrap.style.borderRadius = '4px';\n      wrap.style.willChange = 'transform';\n      // Monotonic, never reset: a later plate is always above an earlier one,\n      // with no possibility of two plates tying and flickering.\n      order += 1;\n      wrap.style.zIndex = String(order);\n\n      const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n      canvas.width = plate.width;\n      canvas.height = plate.height;\n      canvas.style.width = `${tileWidth}px`;\n      canvas.style.height = `${tileHeight}px`;\n      canvas.style.display = 'block';\n      canvas.style.willChange = 'transform';\n      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n      if (!ctx) return;\n      // One blit. All the expensive drawing happened at mount.\n      ctx.drawImage(plate, 0, 0);\n\n      wrap.appendChild(canvas);\n      stack.appendChild(wrap);\n\n      /* The masked-reveal pair: wrapper up from +100%, canvas down from -100%,\n         on the same duration and ease so they cancel exactly. Any mismatch in\n         either and the plate visibly slides inside its own frame. */\n      gsap.fromTo(\n        wrap,\n        { yPercent: 100 },\n        { yPercent: 0, duration: REVEAL_DURATION, ease: 'expo.inOut', overwrite: 'auto' },\n      );\n      gsap.fromTo(\n        canvas,\n        { yPercent: -100 },\n        { yPercent: 0, duration: REVEAL_DURATION, ease: 'expo.inOut', overwrite: 'auto' },\n      );\n\n      /* The cull. Not a nicety — without it the stack grows for as long as the\n         page is open, and a user idly running the pointer up and down the list\n         builds thousands of composited layers. Killing tweens before removal\n         stops GSAP writing to a node React and the DOM have both let go of. */\n      while (stack.children.length > MAX_PLATES) {\n        const oldest = stack.firstElementChild;\n        if (!oldest) break;\n        gsap.killTweensOf(oldest);\n        gsap.killTweensOf(oldest.firstElementChild);\n        oldest.remove();\n      }\n    };\n\n    const onOver = (e: Event) => {\n      const target = e.target;\n      if (!(target instanceof Element)) return;\n      const row = target.closest('[data-trail-row]');\n      if (!row) return;\n      /* `pointerover` bubbles and fires again for every descendant crossed\n         inside the same row, so the spawn is gated on actually changing rows\n         rather than on the event firing. `relatedTarget` still inside the row\n         means the pointer never left it. */\n      const from = (e as PointerEvent).relatedTarget;\n      if (from instanceof Node && row.contains(from)) return;\n      const index = Number(row.getAttribute('data-trail-row'));\n      if (!Number.isInteger(index)) return;\n      spawn(index);\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerover', onOver);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerover', onOver);\n      gsap.killTweensOf(stack);\n      // Every live plate is a node this effect created and React does not know\n      // about, so this effect has to remove them itself.\n      while (stack.firstElementChild) {\n        const child = stack.firstElementChild;\n        gsap.killTweensOf(child);\n        gsap.killTweensOf(child.firstElementChild);\n        child.remove();\n      }\n      gsap.set(stack, { clearProps: 'all' });\n    };\n  }, [followDuration, tileWidth, tileHeight]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes all three container listeners, kills tweens on the column and on every live plate and its canvas, then removes each plate node — these were created imperatively, so React will not clean them up — and clears the column transform. The offscreen plate canvases are released by nulling the ref array; they are plain canvases, never object URLs, so there is nothing to revoke.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No listeners attach and no plates are ever created. What remains is the list itself — real links, real hover colour — which loses nothing functional. There is no meaningful static version of a trail, so the component removes itself rather than pretending.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- No cull. The stack grows for the lifetime of the page; a user idly running the pointer across the list builds thousands of composited layers and the tab degrades with no single frame that looks like the culprit.\n- Culling by index or on a timer instead of on spawn. The ceiling then applies per interaction rather than at all times, and a burst of hovers still spikes the node count.\n- Removing a node without `killTweensOf`. GSAP keeps writing transforms to a detached element, which is wasted work per frame forever and, in dev, a confusing set of live tweens for nodes that no longer exist.\n- Mismatched duration or ease between the wrapper and the canvas. The cancellation breaks and the plate slides inside its own frame — it looks like a parallax effect nobody asked for, and reads as a bug.\n- Putting `overflow: hidden` on the column instead of on each wrapper. Every plate is then clipped by one shared box, so plates offset from the column are cut off and the reveal only works for the newest one.\n- Spawning on `pointerover` without checking `relatedTarget`. The event refires for every descendant crossed inside the same row, so a single hover over a row containing a number, a label and a date spawns three plates.\n- Using `pointerenter` per row instead. It does not bubble, so it needs a listener on every row plus re-subscription whenever React re-renders the list — the bookkeeping is the bug surface.\n- Letting the column receive pointer events. Plates then intercept the very moves that drive the follow, and the column stalls whenever it catches up with the pointer.\n- `Math.random()` for the plate colours. The server and client disagree, React reports a hydration mismatch, and every reload produces a different set of plates so no visual regression is ever detectable.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch nothing mounts: `pointerenter` fires on tap, so every tap would stack a plate directly under the finger and leave it there with no way to dismiss it, and the vertical follow has nothing to follow.\n- Reduced motion disables the component entirely rather than shortening it. The plates and the column exist only as motion — there is no static state that still communicates anything — so the honest reduction is the plain list, which is fully functional on its own.\n- The column is `aria-hidden`. The rows are real links and remain the accessible content; the plates are decoration with no text equivalent.\n- Compare with `pooled-image-trail`, which solves the same visual problem for a pointermove-rate event by reusing a fixed ring of nodes. Neither approach is correct in general; the arrival rate of the event decides.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove down the list and each row entered drops a plate into the column, uncovered from the bottom rather than sliding in — watch the numeral in the corner and it should not move relative to the plate edge. The column follows your vertical position with a long settle. Hover a row, move within it across the number and the date, and exactly one plate appears. Run the pointer up and down the list for ten seconds and inspect the column in DevTools: it holds at most twenty children. On touch, or with reduced motion, the list is plain and no plates appear.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "hover-prefetch-wipe": "# Build: Prefetch-On-Hover Wipe\n\nTwo halves of one idea: spend the gap between \"the visitor has decided\" and \"the visitor has clicked\" making the next page free. `pointerenter` on any link inside the container prefetches it — 200 to 800 ms of warm-up nobody paid for — and the click covers the screen with a wipe, navigating only once the wipe has genuinely finished covering. Both halves are delegated from a single container, and the same predicate that decides what to intercept decides what to prefetch, so a download link or a cross-origin link is left entirely alone by both.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nWarms the next page on hover, wipes the screen on click, and navigates once the wipe has actually covered.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One container holds both delegated listeners. The click listener bubbles normally; the `pointerenter` listener cannot, because `pointerenter` does not bubble at all — so it is registered with `{ capture: true }`. The capture phase runs root-to-target for every event whether it bubbles or not, which makes it the only way to delegate the pointer-enter family.\n2. `resolveLink` walks up from the event target with `closest('a[href]')` and returns null for anything the browser must handle itself: a `target` other than `_self`, a `download` attribute, `rel=\"external\"`, a different origin, or a hash pointing at the current document. It gates both halves — a link you must not intercept is a link you must not prefetch either, and a cross-origin prefetch leaks the visitor's intent to a third party.\n3. The prefetch is `onPrefetch(href)` if a callback was supplied, otherwise an injected `<link rel=\"prefetch\" as=\"document\">`. `as=\"document\"` matters: without it the browser cannot pick a cache partition or a priority and will report the hint as unused even when the navigation follows immediately. A module-scope `Set` of already-hinted hrefs means a nav that remounts on every route change does not re-hint its whole link set each time.\n4. The click calls `preventDefault()`, stores the resolved href in a ref, and flips `data-leaving` on the overlay, which runs a `transform: scaleX(0) -> scaleX(1)` transition from the anchored edge. Navigation waits for `transitionend`, filtered on both `event.target === overlay` and `event.propertyName === \"transform\"`, because `transitionend` fires once per property and also for descendants.\n5. `transitionend` never fires for an element that is not being rendered — a `display: none` ancestor, a tab backgrounded mid-wipe — so a `duration + 120 ms` timer races it and a `fired` flag makes whichever wins the only one that navigates. A navigation that silently never happens is a dead link, which is strictly worse than an early one.\n6. A `pageshow` listener clears the wipe. Back-navigation out of the browser's back/forward cache restores the DOM exactly as it was left, including the wipe mid-cover, with no script running to undo it — and `pageshow` is the only event dispatched on a bfcache restore. `load` is not, and neither are React's mount effects. `data-instant` suppresses the transition for exactly one frame so the restore snaps rather than playing a reveal of a page the visitor already saw.\n7. The base rule carries an ease-out curve and the `[data-leaving=\"true\"]` rule overrides the timing function with an ease-in, so covering accelerates away and retracting decelerates into place. One declaration, and it is the difference between a page transition and a garage door.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 0.3`\n  Seconds to cover. This is dead time the visitor pays for on every click, so it is priced against the prefetch: roughly 300 ms is what a warm document needs to parse and paint, which is why the wipe and the fetch finish together. Past ~0.5 s the wipe is the slowest part of the navigation it was added to hide.\n- `SAFETY_MS = 120`\n  Milliseconds past the duration before navigation is forced. Long enough to lose the race with a real `transitionend` under load — a frame or two of jank costs more than 16 ms — short enough that a wipe which genuinely never rendered does not strand the visitor.\n- `cover ease = cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1)`\n  Ease-in, no ease-out. The wipe must be at its fastest as it reaches full coverage: the last sliver of uncovered page is the part that reads as sluggishness, and an ease-out spends its longest stretch there.\n- `retract ease = cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1)`\n  Ease-out, the mirror. Arriving should settle rather than snap. Reusing the cover curve for the retract makes the wipe linger and then flick away, because a reversed transition traverses the same curve in the same direction.\n- `zIndex = 190`\n  Above a custom cursor (150) and a scroll progress bar (120), both of which must be hidden while the screen is covered, and below a preloader (200), which owns first load outright.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction HoverPrefetchWipe(props: { children: ReactNode; onNavigate: (href: string) => void; onPrefetch?: (href: string) => void; pageKey?: string; duration?: number; color?: string; direction?: \"left\" | \"right\" | \"up\" | \"down\"; zIndex?: number; position?: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Anything containing anchors. Every `<a href>` inside is handled by delegation.\n- `onNavigate: (href: string) => void`\n  Runs once the wipe has fully covered. Receives `pathname + search + hash`, which is what a router expects.\n- `onPrefetch: (href: string) => void`\n  The framework-specific half. Omitted, a `<link rel=\"prefetch\" as=\"document\">` is injected instead.\n- `pageKey: string`\n  Identity of the current page; a change retracts the wipe. Leave undefined for a full document navigation so the cover holds.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.3`\n  Seconds to cover, and to retract.\n- `color: string` — default `'#fff'`\n  Any CSS colour. Must be opaque or the swap shows through.\n- `direction: \"left\" | \"right\" | \"up\" | \"down\"` — default `\"left\"`\n  Edge the wipe grows from.\n- `zIndex: number` — default `190`\n  Above a custom cursor and a progress bar, below a preloader.\n- `position: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"` — default `\"fixed\"`\n  `absolute` to scope the wipe to a bounded box; `fixed` for a real page transition.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Hrefs already hinted to the browser. Module scope, so it is shared across\n * instances and survives remounts: a nav that remounts on every route change\n * would otherwise re-hint its entire link set on every navigation.\n */\nconst prefetched = new Set<string>();\n\n/**\n * Milliseconds past the wipe duration before the navigation is forced.\n * `transitionend` is the accurate signal but it never fires if the overlay is\n * not being rendered — a `display: none` ancestor, a tab backgrounded mid-wipe\n * — and a navigation that silently never happens is a dead link.\n */\nconst SAFETY_MS = 120;\n\nconst WIPE_CSS = `\n.kinetic-wipe {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;\n  background: var(--kx-wipe-color, #fff);\n  transform: var(--kx-wipe-from, scaleX(0));\n  transform-origin: var(--kx-wipe-origin, left center);\n  /* Base curve is the RETRACT: ease-out, so arriving decelerates. */\n  transition: transform var(--kx-wipe-duration, 0.3s)\n    cubic-bezier(0, 0, 0.2, 1);\n  will-change: transform;\n  /* Transparent and idle: must not eat the click it is waiting for. */\n  pointer-events: none;\n}\n.kinetic-wipe[data-fixed='true'] {\n  position: fixed;\n}\n.kinetic-wipe[data-leaving='true'] {\n  transform: var(--kx-wipe-to, scaleX(1));\n  /* Cover curve: ease-in, so leaving accelerates away. Asymmetry is the\n     difference between a page transition and a garage door. */\n  transition-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1);\n  /* Covering: now it SHOULD eat clicks. The outgoing page is still live\n     underneath and a second click on a nav item would queue a second\n     navigation behind an opaque surface. */\n  pointer-events: auto;\n}\n/* Set for exactly one frame after a bfcache restore, so the overlay snaps back\n   to rest instead of playing a reveal of a page the visitor already saw. */\n.kinetic-wipe[data-instant='true'] {\n  transition: none;\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  /* Removed entirely rather than zero-duration: a zero-duration wipe is still\n     one painted frame of full-bleed colour, which is a flash. The click\n     handler navigates synchronously in this mode, so nothing is lost. */\n  .kinetic-wipe { display: none; }\n}\n`;\n\n/** Per-direction geometry: the two transforms and the anchored edge. */\nconst GEOMETRY = {\n  left: { from: 'scaleX(0)', to: 'scaleX(1)', origin: 'left center' },\n  right: { from: 'scaleX(0)', to: 'scaleX(1)', origin: 'right center' },\n  up: { from: 'scaleY(0)', to: 'scaleY(1)', origin: 'bottom center' },\n  down: { from: 'scaleY(0)', to: 'scaleY(1)', origin: 'top center' },\n} as const;\n\nexport type WipeDirection = keyof typeof GEOMETRY;\n\nexport interface HoverPrefetchWipeProps {\n  /** Anything containing anchors. Every `<a href>` inside is handled. */\n  children: ReactNode;\n  /**\n   * Runs once the wipe has fully covered. Receives the app-relative href —\n   * `pathname + search + hash` — which is what a router expects.\n   */\n  onNavigate: (href: string) => void;\n  /**\n   * The framework-specific half. Next.js: `(href) => router.prefetch(href)`.\n   * Omitted, a `<link rel=\"prefetch\" as=\"document\">` is injected instead,\n   * which is the right hint for a plain multi-page site.\n   */\n  onPrefetch?: (href: string) => void;\n  /**\n   * Identity of the current page — `usePathname()` in Next.js. A change\n   * retracts the wipe. Left undefined the wipe stays up after `onNavigate`,\n   * which is correct for a full document navigation: the browser keeps\n   * painting the old page until the new one is ready, so lowering the curtain\n   * early re-reveals the page you just left.\n   */\n  pageKey?: string;\n  /** Seconds the wipe takes to cover, and to retract. */\n  duration?: number;\n  /** Any CSS colour. Must be opaque or the swap shows through. */\n  color?: string;\n  /** Edge the wipe grows from. */\n  direction?: WipeDirection;\n  zIndex?: number;\n  /** `absolute` to demo inside a bounded box; `fixed` for a real page. */\n  position?: 'fixed' | 'absolute';\n  className?: string;\n}\n\n/**\n * The anchor a delegated event belongs to, or null if the browser should be\n * left alone. Every clause is a link that would break if intercepted — and the\n * same predicate gates both halves, because a link you must not intercept is a\n * link you must not prefetch either.\n */\nfunction resolveLink(event: Event, root: HTMLElement): HTMLAnchorElement | null {\n  const target = event.target;\n  if (!(target instanceof Element)) return null;\n\n  const anchor = target.closest('a[href]');\n  if (!(anchor instanceof HTMLAnchorElement)) return null;\n  if (!root.contains(anchor)) return null;\n\n  // Opens elsewhere, downloads, or is explicitly marked as leaving the site.\n  if (anchor.target !== '' && anchor.target !== '_self') return null;\n  if (anchor.hasAttribute('download')) return null;\n  if (anchor.rel.split(/\\s+/).includes('external')) return null;\n\n  // Cross-origin: prefetching leaks the visitor's intent to a third party and\n  // intercepting breaks the navigation outright.\n  if (anchor.origin !== window.location.origin) return null;\n\n  // Same document. Nothing to fetch, and covering the screen in order to\n  // scroll down the page is absurd.\n  //\n  // The hash is deliberately not part of this test, and that is the whole\n  // point. Requiring one meant a plain `<a href=\"/work\">` clicked while\n  // already on `/work` — the nav item for the page you are on, the most\n  // common accidental click there is — was treated as a real navigation:\n  // `preventDefault()` suppressed the browser, the wipe covered the screen,\n  // and `onNavigate` was handed the route already showing. `pageKey` then\n  // never changed, so the retract effect below early-returned on\n  // `seenKey.current === pageKey` and nothing else in the component ever\n  // clears `leaving`. The overlay takes `pointer-events: auto` while it\n  // covers, so the site stayed opaque and unclickable until a hard reload.\n  if (\n    anchor.pathname === window.location.pathname &&\n    anchor.search === window.location.search\n  ) {\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  return anchor;\n}\n\nfunction hrefOf(anchor: HTMLAnchorElement): string {\n  // Resolved and app-relative. `getAttribute('href')` hands a router raw\n  // authored text like '../work', which it cannot resolve on its own.\n  return `${anchor.pathname}${anchor.search}${anchor.hash}`;\n}\n\nexport function HoverPrefetchWipe({\n  children,\n  onNavigate,\n  onPrefetch,\n  pageKey,\n  duration = 0.3,\n  color = '#fff',\n  direction = 'left',\n  zIndex = 190,\n  position = 'fixed',\n  className,\n}: HoverPrefetchWipeProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const wipe = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const pending = useRef<string | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [leaving, setLeaving] = useState(false);\n  const [instant, setInstant] = useState(false);\n\n  // Held in refs so a new handler identity on the parent's render does not\n  // re-bind the delegated listeners.\n  const navigate = useRef(onNavigate);\n  navigate.current = onNavigate;\n  const prefetch = useRef(onPrefetch);\n  prefetch.current = onPrefetch;\n\n  /* -- halves one and two, both delegated from the same container ------- */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n\n    const onEnter = (event: Event) => {\n      const anchor = resolveLink(event, el);\n      if (!anchor) return;\n\n      const href = hrefOf(anchor);\n      if (prefetched.has(href)) return;\n      prefetched.add(href);\n\n      const custom = prefetch.current;\n      if (custom) {\n        custom(href);\n        return;\n      }\n\n      const link = document.createElement('link');\n      link.rel = 'prefetch';\n      // Without `as`, the browser cannot choose a cache partition or a\n      // priority, and logs the hint as unused even when the navigation\n      // follows immediately.\n      link.as = 'document';\n      link.href = href;\n      document.head.appendChild(link);\n    };\n\n    const onClick = (event: MouseEvent) => {\n      // Someone upstream already handled it, or the visitor asked for a new\n      // tab, a new window, or a download. Middle and right clicks are not\n      // navigations and must reach the browser untouched.\n      if (event.defaultPrevented || event.button !== 0) return;\n      if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.shiftKey || event.altKey) return;\n\n      const anchor = resolveLink(event, el);\n      if (!anchor) return;\n\n      event.preventDefault();\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        navigate.current(hrefOf(anchor));\n        return;\n      }\n\n      // A wipe already covering owns the navigation. Without this a second\n      // click retargets `pending` mid-cover and the visitor lands on a page\n      // they never aimed at.\n      if (pending.current !== null) return;\n\n      pending.current = hrefOf(anchor);\n      setLeaving(true);\n    };\n\n    // `pointerenter` does not bubble, so delegation only works in the capture\n    // phase — which runs root-to-target for every event, bubbling or not.\n    el.addEventListener('pointerenter', onEnter, { capture: true, passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('click', onClick);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerenter', onEnter, { capture: true });\n      el.removeEventListener('click', onClick);\n    };\n  }, [reduced]);\n\n  /* -- navigate only once the wipe has actually finished covering ------- */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!leaving) return;\n    const overlay = wipe.current;\n    const href = pending.current;\n    if (!overlay || href === null) return;\n\n    let fired = false;\n    const commit = () => {\n      if (fired) return;\n      fired = true;\n      navigate.current(href);\n    };\n\n    // `transitionend` fires once per animated property and also for\n    // descendants, so both filters are required: without the target check a\n    // transition on anything inside the overlay would trigger navigation.\n    const onEnd = (event: TransitionEvent) => {\n      if (event.target !== overlay || event.propertyName !== 'transform') return;\n      commit();\n    };\n\n    overlay.addEventListener('transitionend', onEnd);\n    const safety = window.setTimeout(commit, duration * 1000 + SAFETY_MS);\n\n    return () => {\n      overlay.removeEventListener('transitionend', onEnd);\n      clearTimeout(safety);\n    };\n  }, [leaving, duration]);\n\n  /* -- retract when the destination is on screen ----------------------- */\n  const seenKey = useRef(pageKey);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (pageKey === undefined || seenKey.current === pageKey) return;\n    seenKey.current = pageKey;\n    // Driven by the same render that swapped the content in, so there is no\n    // window in which the retracting wipe exposes the page just left.\n    pending.current = null;\n    setLeaving(false);\n  }, [pageKey]);\n\n  /* -- half three: survive the back/forward cache ---------------------- */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const onShow = () => {\n      pending.current = null;\n      setInstant(true);\n      setLeaving(false);\n    };\n    window.addEventListener('pageshow', onShow);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener('pageshow', onShow);\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!instant) return;\n    // One frame is enough for the resting transform to be committed with the\n    // transition suppressed. Re-enable any earlier and style has not been\n    // recalculated yet, so the snap becomes a fade after all.\n    const frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => setInstant(false));\n    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(frame);\n  }, [instant]);\n\n  const geometry = GEOMETRY[direction];\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={root} className={cn(position === 'absolute' && 'relative', className)}>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-hover-prefetch-wipe\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {WIPE_CSS}\n      </style>\n\n      {children}\n\n      <div\n        ref={wipe}\n        aria-hidden\n        data-leaving={leaving ? 'true' : 'false'}\n        data-instant={instant ? 'true' : 'false'}\n        data-fixed={position === 'fixed' ? 'true' : 'false'}\n        className=\"kinetic-wipe\"\n        style={\n          {\n            zIndex,\n            '--kx-wipe-duration': `${duration}s`,\n            '--kx-wipe-color': color,\n            '--kx-wipe-from': geometry.from,\n            '--kx-wipe-to': geometry.to,\n            '--kx-wipe-origin': geometry.origin,\n          } as CSSProperties\n        }\n      />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Both delegated listeners are removed with the same `{ capture: true }` flag they were added with, or the removal silently fails to match. The `transitionend` listener and the safety timer are torn down together whenever the leaving state changes, the rAF that re-enables the transition after a bfcache restore is cancelled, and the `pageshow` listener is removed on unmount. Injected `<link rel=\"prefetch\">` tags are deliberately left in the head: removing a hint mid-flight cancels the fetch it exists to start.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The overlay gets `display: none` and the click handler calls `onNavigate` synchronously, so navigation is a hard cut with no coloured frame in either direction. Not a zero-duration wipe — that is still one painted frame of full-bleed colour, which is a flash. The prefetch half is untouched, because a warm cache is not motion and is precisely the thing a visitor on a slow connection wants kept.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Delegating `pointerenter` with a normal bubbling listener. It does not bubble, so the handler fires only when the pointer enters the container itself — once — and every link in the nav is silently never prefetched. `{ capture: true }` is not an optimisation here, it is the mechanism.\n- Prefetching everything on mount. A nav of twelve links becomes twelve speculative document fetches competing with the page the visitor is actually reading. Hover is the signal that makes it worth the bandwidth.\n- Prefetching cross-origin hrefs. Beyond the wasted request, it tells a third party which of its pages this visitor was considering before they chose. `resolveLink` rejects them for both halves.\n- Navigating on a timer instead of `transitionend`. Under load the timer fires while the page is still visible through a half-drawn wipe, which is the exact artefact the wipe exists to hide. Under a throttled background tab it fires long after.\n- Trusting `transitionend` alone. It never fires for an unrendered element, so a wipe inside a collapsed ancestor turns every link into a no-op. The safety timer is not belt-and-braces, it is the only thing standing between a CSS edge case and a dead nav.\n- Filtering `transitionend` on neither target nor property name. It fires once per animated property and bubbles from descendants, so a hover transition on a child inside the overlay triggers the navigation early.\n- Forgetting `pageshow`. On a bfcache restore the visitor lands on a full-bleed sheet of colour with nothing running to clear it. This is the single most common way a wipe transition ships broken, because it never reproduces on a hard reload.\n- Leaving the overlay `pointer-events: none` while it covers. The outgoing page is still live underneath, so a second click on a nav item queues a second navigation behind an opaque surface. It flips to `auto` only in the leaving state, because at rest it must not eat the click it is waiting for.\n- Retracting the wipe immediately after a full document navigation. The browser keeps painting the old page until the new one is ready, so lowering the curtain early re-reveals the page just left. That is why the retract is driven by `pageKey` and why leaving it undefined holds the cover.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Next.js App Router: `onPrefetch={(href) => router.prefetch(href)}`, `onNavigate={(href) => router.push(href)}`, `pageKey={usePathname()}`. Nothing in this file imports `next/link` or `next/navigation` — the prefetch half is the only framework-specific part of the technique, so it is a callback, and `router.prefetch` already dedupes and no-ops in dev.\n- React Router: `onPrefetch` can stay omitted and let the injected `<link rel=\"prefetch\">` warm the loader's data URL, or call your own route-module preloader. `pageKey={useLocation().pathname}`.\n- Plain multi-page site: omit `onPrefetch` entirely and set `onNavigate={(href) => window.location.assign(href)}`. Leave `pageKey` undefined so the wipe stays up while the browser fetches, and let `pageshow` clear it on Back.\n- Wrap the whole layout, not each nav: one container, one overlay, one pair of listeners, and every link on the page is covered including ones rendered later. `position=\"fixed\"` for that; `absolute` only when the transition is scoped to a panel.\n- The colour must be opaque. A translucent wipe shows the outgoing page through the swap, which is the one artefact the whole sequence exists to prevent.\n- Pairs with `page-reveal-overlay` if you want the inbound half animated too — this component covers and hands off, that one replays a reveal on arrival.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover a nav item and its dot lights — the href has been hinted, once, and hovering again does not re-hint. Click and the panel wipes over from the left in 0.4 s, the content swaps while covered, and the wipe retracts to the same edge with the opposite ease. Cmd-click, middle-click and a `download` link all behave like ordinary links with no wipe. On a real site, navigate away and press Back: the page appears with no leftover overlay and no reveal animation. With reduced motion the content swaps instantly and no coloured surface is ever painted.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "icon-button": "# Build: Icon Button\n\nA square `<button>` that draws one icon component at a fixed size inside a secondary-button surface: white fill, 1px border and `shadow-xs`. `aria-label` is a required prop in the type, so a nameless icon button does not compile. `IconLinkButton` renders the same box as an `<a>` for actions that navigate, such as opening a profile or an external dashboard.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSquare bordered icon-only button in 36 and 32px with a fixed 10px radius, plus an anchor twin for navigational actions.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The icon is a component prop (`icon={RiSearchLine}`), not an element. The button renders it with `aria-hidden` at 20px (medium) or 16px (small), so the glyph size always matches the box and the button's accessible name comes only from `aria-label`.\n2. The box is `size-9` (36px) or `size-8` (32px) with `rounded-2lg` at both sizes. Unlike `Button`, whose small tier drops to an 8px radius, the icon button keeps 10px so a row of mixed sizes still reads as one family.\n3. Hover, press and disabled reuse the secondary Button tokens: the fill steps through `background-primary-hover` and `-active`, and the border steps through `border-button-hover` and `-active`. Only `background-color`, `border-color`, `box-shadow` and `color` transition (150ms), so nothing animates layout.\n4. Disabled drops the shadow, fades to 60% opacity and swaps the icon colour to `icon-button-disabled-foreground`. `directionalIconClass` adds `rtl:rotate-180` to arrow and sidebar glyphs, so a \"Next\" arrow points the reading direction in right-to-left layouts.\n5. `type` defaults to `\"button\"` so an icon button inside a form (a clear or add-row control) never submits it. `IconLinkButton` shares the exact style object and takes anchor attributes instead.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `box sizes = 36 × 36 / 32 × 32 px`\n  Match the kit's medium and small Button heights, so an icon button sits flush beside a labelled button or a 36px input.\n- `icon sizes = 20px / 16px`\n  Leaves 8px of padding on each side at both sizes (36 − 20 = 16, 32 − 16 = 16), which is the spacing in the Figma table-row and notification buttons.\n- `radius = 10px (rounded-2lg) at both sizes`\n  A 32px square with an 8px radius looks visibly rounder than its 36px neighbour. Holding the radius constant keeps toolbars of mixed sizes consistent.\n- `transition = 150ms ease on colour, border and shadow`\n  Fast enough that sweeping across a row of table actions does not leave half-lit buttons behind.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction IconButton(props: Omit<ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>, \"children\"> & { icon: IconComponent; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; \"aria-label\": string; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction IconLinkButton(props: Omit<AnchorHTMLAttributes<HTMLAnchorElement>, \"children\"> & { icon: IconComponent; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; \"aria-label\": string; ref?: Ref<HTMLAnchorElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `icon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Required. Icon component rendered at 20px (medium) or 16px (small) with `aria-hidden`.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Required. The only accessible name, since there is no visible label.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px or 32px square. The radius stays 10px at both.\n- `type: \"button\" | \"submit\" | \"reset\"` — default `\"button\"`\n  On `IconButton` only. Defaults to `button` so it never submits a surrounding form.\n- `href: string`\n  On `IconLinkButton`: the destination. All other anchor attributes pass through.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function IconButton({\n  icon: Icon,\n  size = \"medium\",\n  className,\n  type = \"button\",\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: IconButtonProps) {\n  return (\n    <button\n      ref={ref}\n      type={type}\n      className={cx(styles.base, styles.size[size], className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <Icon className={cx(styles.icon[size], directionalIconClass(Icon))} aria-hidden />\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. Both components are stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. Hover and press change only colours, border and shadow over 150ms, and the button never scales or shifts.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Passing an element (`icon={<RiAddLine />}`). The prop takes the component so the button can size it. Anything else fails the type check or renders at Remix Icon's default 24px and overflows the 36px box.\n- Reusing one `aria-label` for different actions in a row, such as three \"Action\" buttons. Each label should name its own action (\"Edit row\", \"Delete row\"), because screen-reader users hear only the label.\n- Using `IconButton` with an `onClick` that navigates. Use `IconLinkButton` with `href` so middle-click, open-in-new-tab and the status-bar URL preview all work.\n- Overriding the radius per size from outside. The 10px radius at both sizes is intentional, so a `className=\"rounded-lg\"` on small buttons breaks the visual rhythm of a mixed toolbar.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This is the bordered secondary surface only. For a borderless icon inside a toolbar or a primary-filled square, use `Button` with `iconOnly` and the matching variant.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAdd, Search and Delete render as three 36px squares with 20px icons centred and identical 10px corners. The small row is 32px with 16px icons and the same corner radius. Hovering lightens the fill and darkens the border without any movement. Tabbing shows a 2px offset focus ring. The disabled button is faded, has no shadow and shows a not-allowed cursor. A screen reader announces each button by its label, never as just \"button\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "idle-gated-page-states": "# Build: Idle-Gated Page States\n\nA page entrance is the one animation guaranteed to run at the worst possible moment: hydration, font swap, image decode and the first layout pass all land in the same few hundred milliseconds. Start a stagger there and it does not look slow, it looks broken — three elements arrive, the thread blocks, the rest snap in. This gates the entrance on two conditions at once, a floor delay resolved on a frame boundary and at least one report of an idle main thread, and starts only when the slower of the two finishes. Enter and leave are one paused timeline played forward and reversed.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA page entrance that will not start until a floor delay has elapsed and the main thread has reported itself idle.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The machine is `preload -> gating -> entering -> settled`, with `leave(commit)` sending it to `leaving` and the exit's completion sending it back to `gating` for the next page. The current phase is written to `data-page-state` on the root so CSS can read it without a second source of truth.\n2. `whenIdleAfter(delay, timeout, done)` is the gate. It schedules two independent waits and calls `done` only when both flags are set: a `setTimeout(delay)` whose callback schedules a `requestAnimationFrame`, and either `requestIdleCallback(cb, { timeout })` or — where that is missing — `setTimeout(cb, timeout)`. It returns a canceller that clears the timer, cancels the frame, and releases whichever of the two idle mechanisms was actually used.\n3. The floor delay resolves inside the rAF rather than in the timer itself. A timer callback can fire in the middle of a long task; the frame callback after it cannot, so the first tween is handed a fresh frame budget instead of the tail end of somebody else's work.\n4. `requestIdleCallback`'s `timeout` option is the safety net — the callback is guaranteed to run within that many milliseconds whether the thread ever goes idle or not — and the `setTimeout` fallback uses the same number as its delay, so both code paths have identical worst-case behaviour.\n5. The first entrance uses `preloadDelay` (0.8 s) and every subsequent one uses `routeDelay` (0.15 s), switched by a `firstEnter` ref. Only the first competes with hydration; keeping 0.8 s for route changes would put nearly a second of nothing between every click and its page.\n6. The choreography is ONE paused GSAP timeline: `fromTo([data-page-enter], { opacity: 0, y: 24 }, { opacity: 1, y: 0, stagger: 0.08 })`. `state === \"entering\"` calls `play()`; `state === \"leaving\"` calls `timeScale(enterDuration / leaveDuration).reverse()`. The timeline's `onComplete` and `onReverseComplete` report back into the machine, so no phase is advanced by a timer that could disagree with what is on screen.\n7. The pre-JS hidden state is a stylesheet rule keyed on `[data-page-state=\"preload\"]` and `[data-page-state=\"gating\"]`, not a `gsap.set` in an effect. Effects run after the first paint, so a JS-only hidden state paints the page fully composed and then hides it. The build then applies `gsap.set(targets, from)` immediately, in the same React commit that swapped the page in, which covers every frame after that.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PRELOAD_DELAY = 0.8`\n  Seconds of floor delay before the FIRST entrance — the one competing with hydration, font loading and the initial layout pass. Below ~0.5 s the stagger still lands inside the hydration burst on a mid-range phone; above ~1 s a fast device shows a finished, blank-looking page and waits.\n- `ROUTE_DELAY = 0.15`\n  Seconds of floor delay for every entrance after the first. Hydration has already happened; the only competing work is the destination subtree's own mount. Long enough that the mount lands inside it, short enough that the click still feels answered.\n- `IDLE_TIMEOUT = 1200`\n  Milliseconds after which the idle wait gives up, passed both as `requestIdleCallback`'s `timeout` and as the fallback timer's delay. A saturated thread — a WebGL hero, a heavy hydration, a third-party long task — may never report idle, and an entrance that never starts is a blank page. Longer than a typical hydration burst, comfortably shorter than the ~2 s at which a visitor decides the site is broken.\n- `ENTER_DURATION = 0.5`\n  Seconds for the entrance, matching the source's `enterDuration`. With a 0.08 s stagger over four elements the last one finishes at 0.74 s, which is the upper limit for a page that should feel like it is already there.\n- `LEAVE_DURATION = 0.5`\n  Seconds for the exit, matching the source's `leaveDuration`. Applied as `timeScale(enterDuration / leaveDuration)` on the reversed timeline rather than as a second set of tweens, so the exit cannot drift from the entrance. Drop it to 0.25 for an exit that feels decisive against a gradual arrival.\n- `ENTER_STAGGER = 0.08`\n  Seconds between elements. Below ~0.05 s the group reads as one block and the stagger is wasted; above ~0.12 s the last element arrives after the eye has already moved on and the page feels like it is still loading.\n- `ENTER_DISTANCE = 24`\n  Pixels each element rises through. Small enough that no element crosses another's resting position — which would read as the layout reflowing rather than arriving — and large enough to be legible at the 0.5 s duration.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction IdleGatedPageStates(props: { controller: IdlePageStates; pageKey: string; children: ReactNode; enterDuration?: number; leaveDuration?: number; stagger?: number; distance?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction useIdlePageStates(options?: { preloadDelay?: number; routeDelay?: number; idleTimeout?: number; onGateOpen?: () => void }): { state: PageState; ready: () => void; leave: (commit: () => void) => void; entered: () => void; left: () => void }\nfunction whenIdleAfter(delaySeconds: number, timeout: number, done: () => void): () => void\ntype PageState = \"preload\" | \"gating\" | \"entering\" | \"settled\" | \"leaving\"\n```\n\n- `controller: IdlePageStates`\n  From `useIdlePageStates()`. Supplies the phase and receives the timeline's completions.\n- `pageKey: string`\n  Identity of the current page. A change rebuilds the timeline against the new `[data-page-enter]` elements. `usePathname()` in Next.js.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The page. Descendants marked `data-page-enter` are staggered in and, reversed, out.\n- `enterDuration: number` — default `0.5`\n  Seconds for the entrance.\n- `leaveDuration: number` — default `0.5`\n  Seconds for the exit, applied as a `timeScale` on the reversed timeline.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.08`\n  Seconds between staggered elements. Reversed on exit, so the last one leaves first.\n- `distance: number` — default `24`\n  Pixels each element rises through.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Seconds of floor delay before the FIRST entrance. The one enter that\n * competes with hydration, font loading and the initial layout pass, so it is\n * the one that needs real headroom. Below ~0.5 s the stagger still lands\n * inside the hydration burst on a mid-range phone; above ~1 s a fast device\n * shows a finished, blank-looking page and waits.\n */\nexport const PRELOAD_DELAY = 0.8;\n\n/**\n * Seconds of floor delay before every SUBSEQUENT entrance. Hydration has\n * already happened by then; the only competing work is the destination\n * subtree's own mount, which is a fraction of it. Keeping the first-load value\n * here would put nearly a second of nothing between every click and its page.\n */\nexport const ROUTE_DELAY = 0.15;\n\n/**\n * Milliseconds after which the idle wait gives up. A thread that is genuinely\n * saturated — a WebGL hero, a heavy hydration, a long task from a third-party\n * script — may never report idle, and an entrance that never starts is a blank\n * page. 1200 ms is longer than a typical hydration burst and comfortably\n * shorter than the ~2 s at which a visitor decides the site is broken. It is\n * passed as `requestIdleCallback`'s own `timeout` option, and it is also the\n * fallback timer's delay, so both paths have identical worst-case behaviour.\n */\nexport const IDLE_TIMEOUT = 1200;\n\n/** Seconds for the entrance. */\nexport const ENTER_DURATION = 0.5;\n/** Seconds for the exit. */\nexport const LEAVE_DURATION = 0.5;\n\n/** Seconds between staggered elements. */\nconst ENTER_STAGGER = 0.08;\n/** Pixels each element rises through. */\nconst ENTER_DISTANCE = 24;\nconst ENTER_EASE = 'power2.out';\n\nconst PAGE_CSS = `\n/* The pre-JS hidden state. It cannot come from a 'gsap.set' in an effect —\n   effects run after the first paint, so the page would flash fully composed\n   and then hide itself. Once GSAP is involved it writes 'opacity' to the\n   inline style, which outranks this rule in both directions. */\n.kx-idle-page[data-page-state='preload'] [data-page-enter],\n.kx-idle-page[data-page-state='gating'] [data-page-enter] {\n  opacity: 0;\n}\n/* Reduced motion never runs the gate, so nothing would ever remove the rule\n   above before hydration. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-idle-page[data-page-state='preload'] [data-page-enter],\n  .kx-idle-page[data-page-state='gating'] [data-page-enter] {\n    opacity: 1;\n  }\n}\n/* Media Queries 5. Where it is unsupported the declaration is simply dropped,\n   so this is a bonus rather than the fix — but where it is supported, a\n   scripting-disabled visitor gets the page instead of an empty box. */\n@media (scripting: none) {\n  .kx-idle-page[data-page-state='preload'] [data-page-enter],\n  .kx-idle-page[data-page-state='gating'] [data-page-enter] {\n    opacity: 1;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\n/**\n * Call `done` once BOTH a floor delay has elapsed and the main thread has gone\n * idle. Returns the canceller — call it from effect cleanup.\n *\n * `requestIdleCallback` is used where it exists and a plain `setTimeout` where\n * it does not: Safari only shipped it in 17.4, so a fallback is not\n * hypothetical, and the two are cancelled with different functions. Exactly one\n * of them is ever scheduled, and the canceller releases whichever it was.\n */\nexport function whenIdleAfter(\n  delaySeconds: number,\n  timeout: number,\n  done: () => void,\n): () => void {\n  let cancelled = false;\n  let idleReady = false;\n  let delayReady = false;\n\n  const join = () => {\n    if (cancelled || !idleReady || !delayReady) return;\n    done();\n  };\n\n  // The floor delay resolves inside a rAF rather than directly in the timer.\n  // A timer callback can fire in the middle of a long task; the frame callback\n  // after it cannot, so the first tween is guaranteed a fresh frame budget\n  // instead of the tail end of someone else's.\n  let frame = 0;\n  const delayTimer = setTimeout(() => {\n    frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {\n      frame = 0;\n      delayReady = true;\n      join();\n    });\n  }, delaySeconds * 1000);\n\n  let idleHandle = 0;\n  let idleTimer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;\n\n  const onIdle = () => {\n    idleHandle = 0;\n    idleTimer = undefined;\n    idleReady = true;\n    join();\n  };\n\n  if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && 'requestIdleCallback' in window) {\n    // The `timeout` option is the guarantee: the callback runs within this many\n    // milliseconds whether the thread ever goes idle or not.\n    idleHandle = window.requestIdleCallback(onIdle, { timeout });\n  } else {\n    idleTimer = setTimeout(onIdle, timeout);\n  }\n\n  return () => {\n    cancelled = true;\n    clearTimeout(delayTimer);\n    if (frame !== 0) cancelAnimationFrame(frame);\n    // Whichever branch ran is the one that gets released. Cancelling an idle\n    // handle with `clearTimeout` is a silent no-op, which is how a gate leaks\n    // into the next page and starts an entrance on a tree that no longer\n    // exists.\n    if (idleHandle !== 0) window.cancelIdleCallback(idleHandle);\n    if (idleTimer !== undefined) clearTimeout(idleTimer);\n  };\n}\n\nexport type PageState =\n  | 'preload'\n  | 'gating'\n  | 'entering'\n  | 'settled'\n  | 'leaving';\n\nexport interface IdlePageStatesOptions {\n  /** Seconds of floor delay before the first entrance. */\n  preloadDelay?: number;\n  /** Seconds of floor delay before every entrance after the first. */\n  routeDelay?: number;\n  /** Milliseconds after which the idle wait gives up. */\n  idleTimeout?: number;\n  /**\n   * Fired on the frame the gate opens, before the entrance starts. The place\n   * for `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` — measure while nothing is moving, or every\n   * trigger is offset by the distance the entrance travels.\n   */\n  onGateOpen?: () => void;\n}\n\nexport interface IdlePageStates {\n  /** Also written to `data-page-state` on the root, so CSS can read it. */\n  state: PageState;\n  /** Preload finished — fonts resolved, critical images decoded. */\n  ready: () => void;\n  /** Run the exit, then `commit` — your navigation. */\n  leave: (commit: () => void) => void;\n  /** Reported by the component when the entrance timeline completes. */\n  entered: () => void;\n  /** Reported by the component when the exit timeline reaches zero. */\n  left: () => void;\n}\n\nexport function useIdlePageStates(\n  options: IdlePageStatesOptions = {},\n): IdlePageStates {\n  const {\n    preloadDelay = PRELOAD_DELAY,\n    routeDelay = ROUTE_DELAY,\n    idleTimeout = IDLE_TIMEOUT,\n    onGateOpen,\n  } = options;\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [state, setState] = useState<PageState>('preload');\n\n  const commit = useRef<(() => void) | null>(null);\n  const firstEnter = useRef(true);\n  const gateOpen = useRef(onGateOpen);\n  gateOpen.current = onGateOpen;\n\n  // The gate. Scheduled only while gating, and cancelled by returning the\n  // canceller — so a route change or an unmount mid-wait releases the idle\n  // handle instead of firing an entrance into a tree that has gone.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (state !== 'gating') return;\n\n    // Reduced motion skips the gate entirely rather than waiting it out. The\n    // gate exists to protect an animation; with no animation to protect, all it\n    // would do is hold a blank page in front of someone who asked for less\n    // motion, not more waiting.\n    if (reduced) {\n      firstEnter.current = false;\n      setState('settled');\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const delay = firstEnter.current ? preloadDelay : routeDelay;\n    return whenIdleAfter(delay, idleTimeout, () => {\n      firstEnter.current = false;\n      gateOpen.current?.();\n      setState('entering');\n    });\n  }, [state, reduced, preloadDelay, routeDelay, idleTimeout]);\n\n  const ready = useCallback(() => {\n    setState((s) => (s === 'preload' ? 'gating' : s));\n  }, []);\n\n  const entered = useCallback(() => {\n    setState((s) => (s === 'entering' ? 'settled' : s));\n  }, []);\n\n  const leave = useCallback(\n    (next: () => void) => {\n      if (reduced) {\n        next();\n        return;\n      }\n      // A second click during an exit would queue a second commit and land the\n      // visitor one page past where they aimed.\n      if (commit.current !== null) return;\n\n      // Only these three phases have an entrance on screen to reverse. Clicking\n      // during 'preload' left the state unchanged — the updater below returned\n      // `s` — but the commit had already been latched, and the only thing that\n      // clears it is `left()`, which runs off a reverse that was never started.\n      // One early click therefore killed every link on the page for the rest of\n      // the session. Nothing to reverse means go straight there, as in the\n      // reduced-motion branch above.\n      if (state !== 'entering' && state !== 'settled' && state !== 'gating') {\n        next();\n        return;\n      }\n\n      commit.current = next;\n      setState('leaving');\n    },\n    [reduced, state],\n  );\n\n  const left = useCallback(() => {\n    const next = commit.current;\n    commit.current = null;\n    next?.();\n    // Straight back to gating, not to preload: the preloader is a first-load\n    // state and showing it again on every route change is the classic version\n    // of this mistake.\n    setState('gating');\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      commit.current = null;\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n\n  return useMemo(\n    () => ({ state, ready, leave, entered, left }),\n    [state, ready, leave, entered, left],\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface IdleGatedPageStatesProps {\n  /** From `useIdlePageStates()`. */\n  controller: IdlePageStates;\n  /**\n   * Identity of the current page. A change rebuilds the timeline against the\n   * new page's `[data-page-enter]` elements. `usePathname()` in Next.js.\n   */\n  pageKey: string;\n  /** The page. Descendants marked `data-page-enter` are staggered in and out. */\n  children: ReactNode;\n  /** Seconds for the entrance. */\n  enterDuration?: number;\n  /** Seconds for the exit. Applied as a `timeScale` on the reversed timeline. */\n  leaveDuration?: number;\n  /** Seconds between staggered elements. */\n  stagger?: number;\n  /** Pixels each element rises through. */\n  distance?: number;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function IdleGatedPageStates({\n  controller,\n  pageKey,\n  children,\n  enterDuration = ENTER_DURATION,\n  leaveDuration = LEAVE_DURATION,\n  stagger = ENTER_STAGGER,\n  distance = ENTER_DISTANCE,\n  className,\n}: IdleGatedPageStatesProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const timeline = useRef<ReturnType<typeof gsap.timeline> | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const { state, entered, left } = controller;\n\n  // Held in refs so the timeline's completion callbacks are wired once, at\n  // build time, and still call the current functions.\n  const onEntered = useRef(entered);\n  onEntered.current = entered;\n  const onLeft = useRef(left);\n  onLeft.current = left;\n\n  // Built once per page and held paused. `state` is deliberately NOT a\n  // dependency: `useGsap` reverts on every dependency change, and `revert()`\n  // strips the inline values GSAP wrote — so a rebuild keyed on the phase would\n  // hand the reverse tween the stylesheet default as its start value and the\n  // exit would animate from nowhere to nowhere.\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      if (!el) return;\n\n      const targets = Array.from(\n        el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-page-enter]'),\n      );\n\n      const from = { opacity: 0, y: distance };\n\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({\n        paused: true,\n        onComplete: () => onEntered.current(),\n        onReverseComplete: () => onLeft.current(),\n      });\n\n      if (targets.length === 0) {\n        // A page that marked nothing still has to run the state machine. This\n        // used to bail before the timeline existed, which left the phase effect\n        // with nothing to play: `entered()` never fired, the page sat in\n        // 'entering' forever, and the first click latched a commit that no\n        // reverse would ever release. An empty tween of the same length carries\n        // the callbacks instead.\n        tl.to({}, { duration: enterDuration });\n      } else {\n        // Applied immediately, in the same commit React swapped the page in, so\n        // the destination is never painted composed and then hidden. The CSS\n        // rule covers the frames before this file has run at all; this covers\n        // every frame after.\n        gsap.set(targets, from);\n\n        tl.fromTo(\n          targets,\n          from,\n          {\n            opacity: 1,\n            y: 0,\n            duration: enterDuration,\n            ease: ENTER_EASE,\n            stagger,\n          },\n          0,\n        );\n      }\n\n      timeline.current = tl;\n      return () => {\n        timeline.current = null;\n      };\n    },\n    root,\n    [pageKey, enterDuration, stagger, distance],\n  );\n\n  // The phase driver. Separate from the build for the reason above, and it\n  // carries `pageKey` so it runs after — never before — a rebuild.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const tl = timeline.current;\n    if (!tl) return;\n\n    if (reduced) {\n      // A seek, not a zero-duration tween: the timeline is parked on its end\n      // frame, so opacity, offset and stagger all land correctly because they\n      // are all on this one timeline.\n      tl.progress(1).pause();\n      return;\n    }\n\n    if (state === 'entering') {\n      tl.timeScale(1).play();\n    } else if (state === 'leaving') {\n      // One timeline, two speeds. `timeScale` is a ratio, so a 0.25 s exit off\n      // a 0.5 s entrance is `0.5 / 0.25 = 2`, and the reversed stagger takes\n      // the last element out first.\n      tl.timeScale(enterDuration / Math.max(leaveDuration, 0.01)).reverse();\n    }\n  }, [state, reduced, pageKey, enterDuration, leaveDuration]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The gate's canceller is the effect's cleanup: it clears the floor-delay timer, cancels the pending `requestAnimationFrame`, and releases whichever idle mechanism was scheduled — `cancelIdleCallback` for the handle, `clearTimeout` for the fallback — with a `cancelled` flag so a callback already in the queue cannot fire after teardown. `useGsap` reverts the `gsap.context`, killing the timeline and stripping every inline style it wrote. The pending commit is nulled on unmount so a leave in flight cannot navigate a tree that has gone.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The gate is skipped entirely — holding a blank page would be more waiting, not less motion — so `gating` advances straight to `settled` with no idle callback and no timer scheduled, and therefore nothing to cancel. The timeline is never played: the phase effect parks it with `tl.progress(1).pause()`, so opacity, offset and stagger all land on their end frame together because they are all on the one timeline. `leave()` calls the commit synchronously, and a stylesheet rule inside `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` keeps `[data-page-enter]` visible for the frames before hydration.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Starting the entrance on mount. Mount is the single busiest moment in the page's life. The stagger runs against hydration and the first layout pass, so the first two or three elements animate and the rest jump — and it only reproduces on the devices you do not test on.\n- Waiting for idle ALONE. On a fast machine `requestIdleCallback` fires almost immediately, before the hydration burst has even begun, so the gate opens straight into the work it was supposed to avoid. The floor delay is what makes the idle check meaningful.\n- Waiting for a delay ALONE. On a slow machine the timer expires while the thread is still saturated and you are back to a stuttering stagger. Neither condition works without the other.\n- Calling `requestIdleCallback` without the `timeout` option. On a page with a long-running rAF loop the callback may never fire, and the entrance never starts — a permanently blank page, on exactly the devices that can least afford it.\n- Cancelling the wrong handle. `requestIdleCallback` returns a handle for `cancelIdleCallback`, and a `setTimeout` returns one for `clearTimeout`; passing either to the other is a silent no-op. Exactly one is ever scheduled and the canceller releases the one that was, or the gate outlives the page and fires an entrance into a tree that no longer exists.\n- Assuming `requestIdleCallback` exists. Safari only shipped it in 17.4, so `window.requestIdleCallback(...)` throws on a browser that is still in real use — and it throws inside an effect, which takes the whole page down rather than degrading.\n- Putting the phase in the `useGsap` dependency list. Every dependency change reverts the context, and `revert()` strips the inline values GSAP wrote — so the reverse tween reads the stylesheet default as its start value and the exit animates from nowhere to nowhere. The timeline is built once per page and driven from a second effect.\n- Hiding `[data-page-enter]` from JavaScript only. An effect runs after the first paint, so the destination is painted fully composed and then hides itself — a flash of the finished page immediately before it animates in. The hidden state belongs in a stylesheet rule keyed on the phase.\n- Returning to `preload` after a route change. The preloader is a first-load state; showing it again on every click is the classic version of this mistake. The machine goes back to `gating`, which re-runs the gate without re-running the preloader.\n- Gating with reduced motion on. There is no animation left to protect, so all the gate does is hold a blank page in front of someone who asked for less motion, not more waiting. That path skips straight to `settled`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- In Next.js App Router: mount once in `app/layout.tsx` with `pageKey={usePathname()}`, call `ready()` from `document.fonts.ready`, and replace nav `<Link>` elements with buttons calling `leave(() => router.push(href))`. Nothing here imports `next/navigation` — the commit is a callback, so the same machine works under React Router or a plain `useState` page switch.\n- `onGateOpen` fires on the frame the gate opens, before the entrance starts. That is where `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` belongs: measure while nothing is moving, or every trigger is offset by the distance the entrance travels. The source did the same thing, refreshing after its preload completed and before its enter.\n- Neither `requestIdleCallback` nor `requestAnimationFrame` runs in a backgrounded tab, so the gate does not open until the tab is foregrounded. That is the correct behaviour — an entrance nobody can see is wasted work — but it is also why a headless screenshot taken against a background tab captures the pre-entrance state. Foreground the tab, or drive the timeline to `progress(1)` for capture.\n- The preloader should stay up for the whole of `gating`, not just `preload`. The wait is dead time only if you show the visitor an empty page during it; behind a preloader it is indistinguishable from loading.\n- The `@media (scripting: none)` rule reveals the content for visitors with JavaScript disabled. It is Media Queries 5 and is simply dropped where unsupported, so treat it as a bonus rather than as the fix.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nLoad the preview: the preloader holds, then the content staggers up and in as one group with no element jumping ahead of the tween. Throttle the CPU to 6x in DevTools and reload — the entrance still runs as a smooth stagger rather than arriving in two jerks, because it starts later rather than starting on time and stuttering. Click a nav item: the current content staggers out last-element-first, the page swaps, and the next one arrives after a much shorter gate than the first load used. Click two nav items in quick succession and you land on the first one you clicked. With reduced motion the content is present immediately with no gate, no stagger, and no preloader held past the font load.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "image-gallery-masonry": "# Build: Image Gallery Masonry\n\nThe gallery wall of an image-generation app. Pictures of different aspect ratios stack in CSS columns so they interlock without measuring, and hovering or focusing one raises a caption scrim and two round actions: a favourite heart and a download button. Clicking a picture opens it in the kit Modal with its caption, a counter, previous / next buttons and arrow-key paging. The twelve sample pictures are inline SVG illustrations, so nothing loads from the network.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA masonry wall of generated images with hover captions, a favourite toggle, a safe download action and a modal lightbox with paging.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The wall is a `<ul>` with `columns-2 sm:columns-3` (overridable through `columnsClassName`). Each `<li>` is `break-inside-avoid` with a bottom margin, and its open button carries the item's CSS `aspect-ratio`, so the browser balances the columns from the ratios alone with no JavaScript layout.\n2. The tile is a `group/tile`. The caption scrim (`bg-linear-to-t from-black/65`) and the action row start at opacity 0 and turn visible on `group-hover/tile` and `group-focus-within/tile`, so keyboard users get the same reveal as pointer users. A `[@media(hover:none)]` variant keeps them shown on touch screens, and a favourited tile keeps its heart visible.\n3. The open button, the favourite and the download are siblings inside the `<li>`, never nested, so each is its own tab stop with its own label. Favourite is a toggle button with `aria-pressed` and a filled rose heart when on. Download uses `aria-disabled` rather than `disabled`, so it stays focusable and its label says why it is not ready, and its click handler does nothing unless `onDownload` exists and the item is not `downloadable: false`.\n4. Favourites can be controlled (`favourites` + `onFavouritesChange`) or left to an internal `Set`. The toggle always builds a new `Set`, so a parent can filter on it, as the demo's Favourites view does.\n5. The lightbox is the kit `Modal` at size `lg`, opened by an `isOpen` flag and showing the item whose id is stored separately. Tracking an id rather than an index keeps the right picture when the list is filtered underneath, and keeping the id after closing lets the content stay in place while the Modal animates out. Paging wraps around both ends, and a `keydown` listener for ArrowLeft / ArrowRight is attached only while the lightbox is open.\n6. Each paged picture is keyed by its id inside an `animate-number-fade` wrapper, so it cross-fades in over 220ms. On hover the tile art scales to 1.04 over 500ms inside the clipped tile.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `columns and gaps = columns-2, 3 from sm (demo: 4 from md); 12px gap and 12px bottom margin`\n  Two columns keep tiles near 160px wide on a phone; three or four suit the panel widths of an app sidebar or a full page. Matching gap and margin keeps the vertical and horizontal gutters equal.\n- `caption scrim = black 65% → transparent, 32px top padding, rises 8px over 200ms`\n  Dark enough for white caption text on any picture, short enough to cover only the bottom of the tile. The 8px rise reads as the caption arriving, not the image moving.\n- `tile actions = 32px round buttons, 90% surface with backdrop blur, active scale 0.9`\n  Large enough to tap, light enough not to hide the picture, and the press scale gives feedback on the toggle before the heart fills.\n- `lightbox frame = Modal lg (640px), picture height min(52vh, 400px) at its own ratio`\n  Leaves room for the header and the paging row on a laptop screen while keeping tall portraits whole.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ImageGalleryMasonry(props: ImageGalleryMasonryProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ImageGalleryMasonryProps { items?: GalleryItem[]; favourites?: ReadonlySet<string>; defaultFavourites?: Iterable<string>; onFavouritesChange?: (next: Set<string>) => void; onDownload?: (item: GalleryItem) => void; columnsClassName?: string; className?: string }\ninterface GalleryItem { id: string; title: string; style: string; ratio: string; kind: GalleryArtKind; palette: readonly [string, string, string, string]; halftone?: boolean; downloadable?: boolean }\n```\n\n- `items: GalleryItem[]` — default `GALLERY_ITEMS`\n  Pictures in display order; each sets its own aspect ratio.\n- `favourites: ReadonlySet<string>`\n  Controlled favourite ids.\n- `defaultFavourites: Iterable<string>`\n  Initial favourite ids when uncontrolled.\n- `onFavouritesChange: (next: Set<string>) => void`\n  Called with a new set after every toggle.\n- `onDownload: (item: GalleryItem) => void`\n  Download handler. Without it, or for an item with `downloadable: false`, the button is aria-disabled.\n- `columnsClassName: string` — default `\"columns-2 sm:columns-3\"`\n  Tailwind column-count classes for the wall.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the list.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ImageGalleryMasonry({\n  items = GALLERY_ITEMS,\n  favourites: favouritesProp,\n  defaultFavourites,\n  onFavouritesChange,\n  onDownload,\n  columnsClassName = \"columns-2 sm:columns-3\",\n  className,\n}: ImageGalleryMasonryProps) {\n  const [ownFavourites, setOwnFavourites] = useState<Set<string>>(() => new Set(defaultFavourites));\n  const favourites = favouritesProp ?? ownFavourites;\n  // The lightbox tracks an id, not an index, so filtering or reordering the\n  // items while it is open never shows the wrong picture. The id outlives\n  // `isOpen` so the picture stays put while the Modal animates out.\n  const [openId, setOpenId] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\n  const openIndex = openId === null ? -1 : items.findIndex((item) => item.id === openId);\n  const current = openIndex >= 0 ? items[openIndex] : undefined;\n  const open = isOpen && current !== undefined;\n\n  const toggleFavourite = (id: string) => {\n    const next = new Set(favourites);\n    if (next.has(id)) next.delete(id);\n    else next.add(id);\n    if (favouritesProp === undefined) setOwnFavourites(next);\n    onFavouritesChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const step = (delta: number) => {\n    if (openIndex < 0 || items.length === 0) return;\n    const next = items[(openIndex + delta + items.length) % items.length];\n    if (next) setOpenId(next.id);\n  };\n\n  // Arrow keys page the lightbox while it is open; the listener lives only\n  // as long as the lightbox does (re-bound each render so `step` is fresh).\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!open) return;\n    const onKey = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {\n      if (event.key === \"ArrowRight\") step(1);\n      else if (event.key === \"ArrowLeft\") step(-1);\n    };\n    window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", onKey);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener(\"keydown\", onKey);\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <ul className={cx(\"gap-3 [&>li]:mb-3\", columnsClassName, className)}>\n        {items.map((item) => {\n          const fav = favourites.has(item.id);\n          return (\n            <li key={item.id} className=\"group/tile relative break-inside-avoid overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-background-tertiary-default\">\n              <button\n                type=\"button\"\n                onClick={() => {\n                  setOpenId(item.id);\n                  setIsOpen(true);\n                }}\n                aria-label={`Open ${captionOf(item)}`}\n                className=\"block w-full cursor-zoom-in outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-inset\"\n                style={{ aspectRatio: item.ratio }}\n              >\n                <GalleryArt\n                  item={item}\n                  className=\"transition-transform duration-500 ease-out group-hover/tile:scale-[1.04] motion-reduce:transition-none motion-reduce:group-hover/tile:scale-100\"\n                />\n              </button>\n              {/* Caption: a bottom scrim that rises on hover or keyboard focus.\n                  Touch screens without hover keep it shown. */}\n              <div\n                aria-hidden\n                className={cx(\n                  \"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 bg-linear-to-t from-black/65 to-transparent px-3 pt-8 pb-2.5\",\n                  \"translate-y-2 opacity-0 transition-[opacity,translate] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:translate-y-0 motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                  \"group-hover/tile:translate-y-0 group-hover/tile:opacity-100 group-focus-within/tile:translate-y-0 group-focus-within/tile:opacity-100\",\n                  \"[@media(hover:none)]:translate-y-0 [@media(hover:none)]:opacity-100\",\n                )}\n              >\n                <p className=\"truncate text-caption-1-medium text-text-white\">{item.title}</p>\n                <p className=\"truncate text-caption-2-regular text-text-white/75\">{item.style}</p>\n              </div>\n              <div\n                className={cx(\n                  \"absolute top-2 right-2 flex gap-1.5 transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                  \"opacity-0 group-hover/tile:opacity-100 group-focus-within/tile:opacity-100 [@media(hover:none)]:opacity-100\",\n                  // A favourite keeps its heart on show.\n                  fav && \"opacity-100\",\n                )}\n              >\n                <FavouriteButton item={item} on={fav} onToggle={() => toggleFavourite(item.id)} />\n                <DownloadButton item={item} onDownload={onDownload} />\n              </div>\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n\n      <Modal\n        isOpen={open}\n        onOpenChange={setIsOpen}\n        size=\"lg\"\n      >\n        {current ? (\n          <>\n            <ModalHeader title={current.title} description={current.style} />\n            <ModalBody className=\"flex flex-col gap-4\">\n              <div className=\"flex h-[min(52vh,400px)] items-center justify-center\">\n                <div\n                  key={current.id}\n                  className=\"h-full max-w-full animate-number-fade overflow-hidden rounded-2xl motion-reduce:animate-none\"\n                  style={{ aspectRatio: current.ratio }}\n                >\n                  <GalleryArt item={current} />\n                </div>\n              </div>\n              <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-3 pb-1\">\n                <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n                  <IconButton icon={RiArrowLeftSLine} size=\"small\" aria-label=\"Previous image\" onClick={() => step(-1)} />\n                  <span className=\"min-w-12 text-center text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n                    {openIndex + 1} / {items.length}\n                  </span>\n                  <IconButton icon={RiArrowRightSLine} size=\"small\" aria-label=\"Next image\" onClick={() => step(1)} />\n                </div>\n                <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n                  <FavouriteButton\n                    item={current}\n                    on={favourites.has(current.id)}\n                    onToggle={() => toggleFavourite(current.id)}\n                    className=\"border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-none\"\n                  />\n                  <DownloadButton\n                    item={current}\n                    onDownload={onDownload}\n                    className=\"border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-none\"\n                  />\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </ModalBody>\n          </>\n        ) : null}\n      </Modal>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The only listener is the lightbox's window `keydown`, added in an effect while the lightbox is open and removed on close, on every re-render and on unmount. The Modal's overlay, focus trap and scroll lock are handled by React Aria.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the tile art does not zoom on hover, the caption appears without rising or fading, the actions appear without a fade, and the lightbox picture swaps without a cross-fade. The kit Modal keeps its opacity fade and drops its scale and blur.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Nesting the favourite and download buttons inside the open button. Interactive content inside a button is invalid, screen readers merge the labels and the clicks bubble into opening the lightbox. They are siblings positioned over the tile.\n- Using `disabled` for an unavailable download. It drops out of the tab order and a hover title never shows, so nobody learns why. `aria-disabled` keeps it focusable and the label explains it.\n- Clearing the open item when the Modal closes. The content would vanish before the exit animation ends. Only `isOpen` flips; the id stays until another picture is opened.\n- Expecting row-first reading order. CSS columns fill top to bottom, so tab order runs down the first column, then the next. Use a grid if strict row order matters more than the interlocking layout.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `GALLERY_ITEMS`, `GalleryArt` and the `GalleryItem` type are re-exported from the main file, so a prompt composer or a loader can show the same pictures.\n- Pass your own items with real images by replacing `GalleryArt` in a fork; the tile only needs `ratio` and a child that fills the box.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTwelve flat illustrations of different heights interlock in columns. Hovering one zooms it slightly, raises its title and style over a dark scrim and shows a heart and a download button. The heart toggles (aria-pressed, rose fill) and the Favourites count updates; the Favourites view shows only hearted pictures. Download on a ready picture updates the status line; on \"Underwater highway\" it does nothing. Clicking a picture opens a 640px lightbox with the title, style, \"n / 12\", previous and next buttons; the arrow keys page it, wrapping at the ends, and Escape closes it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "image-generating-loader": "# Build: Image Generating Loader\n\nThe placeholder an image model leaves in a chat thread while it paints. A 17 by 17 field of dots forms a ring that ripples outward from the centre, a frosted pill in the middle shows the percentage and the current stage, and the footer pairs a shimmering \"Generating image\" label with a seconds countdown. Past 40% the finished picture develops through a halftone mask whose dots swell until they merge, so the image grows in rather than fading. It runs on its own timer or takes a controlled `progress`.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA chat tile for an image still being generated: a rippling halftone ring, a % and stage pill, a seconds countdown and a halftone reveal.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The dot field is computed once at module load from fixed constants. Each grid cell gets a normalised distance from the centre, a Gaussian ring weight `exp(-((d - 0.7) / 0.22)²)` and, outside the ring, a floor of 0.18. Cells under 0.08 or beyond d 1.04 are skipped, so the middle stays empty. The result is rendered by a memoised `DotField` SVG with `fill=\"currentColor\"`, so it follows `text-text-primary` in both themes.\n2. One `@keyframes kx-igl-ripple` rule is injected through a React 19 `<style href precedence>`, which hoists it to `<head>` once per document. Each circle carries its own `--kx-igl-a` (its ring alpha) and an animation delay of `d * 1.1s - 1.8s`. The keyframes read the variable per element, so every dot pulses from 20% to 100% of its own alpha and the negative, outward-growing delay turns the field into an expanding wave.\n3. Without a `progress` prop, a requestAnimationFrame loop adds each frame's delta to an `elapsed` ref, capped at `duration`, and stores progress rounded to 0.1%. Pausing cancels the loop and leaves the ref alone, so resuming continues where it stopped. `animation-play-state: paused` freezes the ripple at the same time.\n4. The result layer sits over the dots with a two-layer CSS mask. The first layer is a 6px repeating radial dot whose radius grows from 0 to 4.5px as `t` goes from 0 at 40% to 1 at 100%. The second is a centred disc that ends at `t² * 100%` with a 22% feather. Their union lets the image show first as a fine halftone and then as a solid disc spreading from the centre. At 100% the mask is removed and the dot field fades out over 500ms.\n5. The stage name is `stages[floor(progress / 100 * stages.length)]`, so four stages each cover a quarter of the run. The countdown is `ceil((100 - progress) / 100 * duration / 1000)` in its own chip, keyed by its value so `.animate-number-fade` replays on each tick, with an sr-only \"N seconds remaining\" beside it.\n6. The square is `role=\"progressbar\"` with `aria-valuenow` and an `aria-valuetext` of \"38%, Sketching\". A polite live region announces `doneLabel` once, and `onComplete` fires from an effect keyed on the done flag through a ref, so a new callback every render does not refire it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `grid = 17 × 17 dots, 10-unit pitch, r 1.7`\n  Fine enough to read as a halftone at 176px, coarse enough that each dot is still a distinct mark. An odd count puts a cell exactly on the centre so the ring is symmetric.\n- `ring = peak at 0.7 of the half-width, spread 0.22, outer floor 0.18`\n  Leaves room in the middle for the percentage pill and keeps a faint halo out to the corners of the circle, the way a printed halftone fades.\n- `ripple = 1.8s cycle, centre-to-edge lag 1.1s, opacity 20%→100%, scale 0.5→1.2`\n  Slow enough to feel like work in progress and not an alert. The lag is shorter than the cycle, so a new wave starts before the last one reaches the rim.\n- `reveal = starts at 40%, 6px halftone cell, dot radius up to 0.75 of the cell, core disc t² with a 22% feather`\n  The first 40% stays abstract, as a real model's early steps are noise. At 0.75 of the cell neighbouring dots overlap, so the halftone closes into a solid picture just before the disc covers it.\n- `tile = 200px wide, rounded-2xl, p-3, square media with rounded-xl`\n  The size of a single image card in a chat thread. It leaves room for the footer label and the countdown on one line.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ImageGeneratingLoader(props: ImageGeneratingLoaderProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ImageGeneratingLoaderProps { progress?: number; duration?: number; paused?: boolean; remainingSeconds?: number; result?: ReactNode; label?: string; doneLabel?: string; stages?: readonly string[]; onComplete?: () => void; className?: string }\n```\n\n- `progress: number`\n  Controlled progress from 0 to 100. Leave it out to run on the internal timer.\n- `duration: number` — default `6000`\n  Length of the internal run in ms. Ignored when `progress` is set.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Freezes the internal timer and the ripple.\n- `remainingSeconds: number`\n  Seconds shown in the countdown chip. Derived from `duration` when uncontrolled.\n- `result: ReactNode`\n  The finished image. It develops through the halftone mask from 40% and is fully shown at 100%.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Generating image\"`\n  Footer label while working, and the progress bar's accessible name.\n- `doneLabel: string` — default `\"Image ready\"`\n  Footer label and live announcement at 100%.\n- `stages: readonly string[]` — default `[\"Composing\", \"Sketching\", \"Painting\", \"Refining\"]`\n  Stage names under the percentage, spread evenly across the run.\n- `onComplete: () => void`\n  Called once when progress reaches 100.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the tile, for example to change its width.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ImageGeneratingLoader({\n  progress: progressProp,\n  duration = 6000,\n  paused = false,\n  remainingSeconds,\n  result,\n  label = \"Generating image\",\n  doneLabel = \"Image ready\",\n  stages = DEFAULT_STAGES,\n  onComplete,\n  className,\n}: ImageGeneratingLoaderProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const controlled = progressProp !== undefined;\n  const [autoProgress, setAutoProgress] = useState(0);\n  const elapsed = useRef(0);\n\n  // Uncontrolled run: accumulate elapsed time frame by frame, so pausing and\n  // resuming continues from where it stopped instead of jumping ahead.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (controlled || paused) return;\n    let frame = 0;\n    let last = performance.now();\n    const tick = (now: number) => {\n      elapsed.current = Math.min(duration, elapsed.current + (now - last));\n      last = now;\n      setAutoProgress(Math.round((elapsed.current / duration) * 1000) / 10);\n      if (elapsed.current < duration) frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    };\n    frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(frame);\n  }, [controlled, paused, duration]);\n\n  const progress = clamp(controlled ? progressProp : autoProgress, 0, 100);\n  const done = progress >= 100;\n  const percent = Math.floor(progress);\n\n  const onCompleteRef = useRef(onComplete);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    onCompleteRef.current = onComplete;\n  });\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (done) onCompleteRef.current?.();\n  }, [done]);\n\n  const stage = stages.length\n    ? stages[Math.min(stages.length - 1, Math.floor((progress / 100) * stages.length))]\n    : undefined;\n  const secondsLeft =\n    remainingSeconds ?? (controlled ? undefined : Math.ceil((((100 - progress) / 100) * duration) / 1000));\n\n  // Halftone + feathered-disc reveal. `t` runs 0→1 from REVEAL_START to 100%.\n  const t = clamp((progress - REVEAL_START) / (100 - REVEAL_START), 0, 1);\n  const dotRadius = round3(t * MASK_CELL * 0.75);\n  const core = round3(t * t * 100);\n  const revealStyle: CSSProperties | undefined =\n    done || reduced\n      ? undefined\n      : {\n          maskImage: `radial-gradient(circle, black ${dotRadius}px, transparent ${round3(dotRadius + 0.75)}px), radial-gradient(circle closest-side, black ${core}%, transparent ${round3(core + FEATHER)}%)`,\n          maskSize: `${MASK_CELL}px ${MASK_CELL}px, 100% 100%`,\n          maskRepeat: \"repeat, no-repeat\",\n          maskPosition: \"center, center\",\n        };\n  // Reduced motion: no developing halftone, the image simply appears at 100%.\n  const resultVisible = done || (!reduced && t > 0);\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-[200px] flex-col gap-2 rounded-2xl bg-background-primary-default p-3 shadow-2xs\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div\n        role=\"progressbar\"\n        aria-label={label}\n        aria-valuemin={0}\n        aria-valuemax={100}\n        aria-valuenow={percent}\n        aria-valuetext={done ? doneLabel : `${percent}%${stage ? `, ${stage}` : \"\"}`}\n        className=\"relative aspect-square w-full overflow-hidden rounded-xl\"\n      >\n        <style href=\"kinetic-image-generating-loader\" precedence=\"medium\">\n          {RIPPLE_CSS}\n        </style>\n        <div\n          className={cx(\n            \"absolute inset-0 transition-opacity duration-500 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            done && result ? \"opacity-0\" : \"opacity-100\",\n          )}\n        >\n          <DotField animated={!reduced && !done} paused={paused} />\n        </div>\n\n        {result ? (\n          <div\n            aria-hidden={!done}\n            className={cx(\"absolute inset-0 [&>*]:size-full\", resultVisible ? \"opacity-100\" : \"opacity-0\")}\n            style={revealStyle}\n          >\n            {result}\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n\n        {!done ? (\n          <div className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center\" aria-hidden>\n            <div className=\"flex flex-col items-center rounded-lg bg-background-primary-default/80 px-2 py-1 backdrop-blur-sm\">\n              <span className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums\">{percent}%</span>\n              {stage ? <span className=\"text-caption-2-medium text-text-tertiary\">{stage}</span> : null}\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-2 px-1 pb-0.5\">\n        <span\n          className={cx(\n            \"truncate text-body-regular\",\n            done ? \"text-text-primary\" : paused ? \"text-text-secondary\" : \"agent-progress-loading-text\",\n          )}\n        >\n          {done ? doneLabel : label}\n        </span>\n        {done ? (\n          <RiCheckLine className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-status-lime-text\" aria-hidden />\n        ) : secondsLeft !== undefined ? (\n          <span className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-0.5 text-body-regular text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n            <span\n              key={secondsLeft}\n              className=\"animate-number-fade rounded bg-background-tertiary-default px-1 text-text-secondary dark:bg-background-quaternary-default motion-reduce:animate-none\"\n              aria-hidden\n            >\n              {secondsLeft}\n            </span>\n            <span aria-hidden>s</span>\n            <span className=\"sr-only\">\n              {secondsLeft} {secondsLeft === 1 ? \"second\" : \"seconds\"} remaining\n            </span>\n          </span>\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n      <span className=\"sr-only\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        {done ? doneLabel : \"\"}\n      </span>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The timer effect cancels its pending animation frame on pause, on unmount and when `duration` or controlled mode changes. The ripple is pure CSS, and the hoisted `<style>` is shared and removed by React when no loader is left. There are no listeners or observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the dots drop their animation and sit at a fixed 70% of their ring alpha, the halftone reveal is skipped (the result stays hidden until 100%, then appears at once), the dot field does not fade out, and the countdown chip does not fade between values. The percentage, stage and countdown still update, since they carry information.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating the dots with React state. Two hundred circles re-rendering every frame would cost far more than the ripple is worth. The field is memoised and only its `animated` and `paused` flags ever change; the motion lives in CSS.\n- Using a positive animation delay for the outward wave. Every dot would sit still until its delay passed and the field would start blank. A negative delay `d * lag - period` starts each dot mid-cycle, so the wave is already running on the first frame.\n- Writing `mask-image` without `mask-size` and `mask-repeat` per layer. The dot layer has to repeat at 6px and the disc layer must not repeat, so both lists need two comma-separated values in the same order as the images.\n- Setting the `animation` shorthand and `animationPlayState` on the same inline style. React warns on every re-render that toggles one of them, once per dot. The circles use the animation longhands only.\n- Rounding progress to whole percent inside the timer. The mask would then step visibly. The loop keeps one decimal and only the label floors it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass any node as `result`; direct children are stretched to fill the square, so an `<svg>` or `<img>` with `object-cover` works as is.\n- Restart a run by changing the component's `key`, as the demo does. Changing `duration` mid-run rescales the remaining time without resetting it.\n- With `progress` controlled, pass `remainingSeconds` yourself if you want the countdown chip; there is no duration to derive it from.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA 200px white tile on a grey thread shows a ring of dark dots rippling outward around a frosted \"0% Composing\" pill, with \"Generating image\" shimmering in the footer and a \"7 s\" countdown. The percentage climbs through Sketching, Painting and Refining. From about 40% a blue poster shows through as fine dots that thicken and merge from the centre. At 100% the dots fade, the footer reads \"Image ready\" with a lime check, and the demo status reads \"Done\". Pause freezes the count and the ripple; Restart starts again from 0%.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "image-prompt-composer": "# Build: Image Prompt Composer\n\nThe prompt box at the foot of an image-generation chat. A white card on a grey tray stacks a row of style chips, a prompt that grows with its text, an aspect-ratio select with a proportional glyph, an image-count stepper and a Generate button; the tray's footer reads back the output size or what is missing. Submitting hands a typed request to `onGenerate`, and while `isGenerating` is set the whole form locks, Generate becomes Stop and the footer shimmers.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe request form of an image model: style chips, an auto-growing prompt, ratio select, count stepper and a Generate that turns into Stop.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The style chips are a React Aria `ToggleButtonGroup` in `multiple` mode, so they share one tab stop, move with the arrow keys and expose `aria-pressed`. Each chip shows a two-tone swatch, swapped for a check while selected, and the selected chips take the status-blue tokens. The request lists styles in option order, not click order.\n2. The prompt is the kit `Textarea` with `autoResize` from 2 to 6 rows. Its own field shell is made transparent and borderless, and the card shows focus instead through `has-[textarea:focus]:ring-border-button-hover`, so the whole card reads as one input. A `keydown` handler on the field submits on ⌘ / Ctrl + Enter through `form.requestSubmit()`, so the same `onSubmit` runs as for a click; a plain Enter stays a new line.\n3. The ratio select is the kit `Select` at `sm` size. Every option draws a glyph whose box is the ratio scaled to 14px on its long side, and the trigger shows only the glyph and the ratio id. The count is the kit `NumberField` with steppers, clamped to `1…maxImages`, and a cleared field falls back to 1.\n4. Generate is a submit button that uses `aria-disabled` while the prompt is blank, so it stays focusable and the footer explains why (\"Write a prompt to generate\"). The submit handler trims the prompt and returns early unless the form can generate.\n5. With `isGenerating`, the chips, select and stepper get `isDisabled`, the prompt becomes read-only, the form sets `aria-busy`, and the Generate button becomes a secondary Stop button. It stays the same submit button, so focus does not jump, and `onSubmit` calls `onStop` instead of `onGenerate` while a run is on. The footer is a polite live region whose text switches to \"Generating N images · ratio\" with the kit's `agent-progress-loading-text` shimmer.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `tray and card = tray radius 26px with 6px padding, card radius 20px, 12px padding and 12px gaps`\n  The 6px tray reads as a frame around the card, and the inner radius is the outer one minus the padding, so the two curves stay concentric.\n- `prompt rows = auto-resize from 2 to 6 lines, 600 characters`\n  Two lines invite a sentence rather than a keyword. Six lines is where the toolbar would leave a laptop viewport, so the field scrolls from there.\n- `aspect ratios = 1:1 1024², 4:5 896×1120, 3:2 1216×832, 16:9 1344×768, 9:16 768×1344`\n  Common output sizes of current diffusion models, each about one megapixel and divisible by 64, so every ratio costs roughly the same to render.\n- `image count = 1 to 4 (maxImages)`\n  Four is a full row of thumbnails under the message. More belongs to a batch tool, not a chat.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ImagePromptComposer(props: ImagePromptComposerProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ImagePromptComposerProps { defaultPrompt?: string; placeholder?: string; aspectRatios?: readonly AspectRatioOption[]; defaultAspectRatio?: string; styleOptions?: readonly StyleOption[]; defaultStyles?: Iterable<string>; defaultCount?: number; maxImages?: number; maxLength?: number; isGenerating?: boolean; onGenerate?: (request: ImagePromptRequest) => void; onStop?: () => void; className?: string }\ninterface ImagePromptRequest { prompt: string; aspectRatio: AspectRatioOption; count: number; styles: string[] }\ninterface AspectRatioOption { id: string; label: string; width: number; height: number }\ninterface StyleOption { id: string; label: string; swatch: readonly [string, string] }\n```\n\n- `defaultPrompt: string` — default `\"\"`\n  Initial prompt text.\n- `placeholder: string` — default `\"Describe the image you want…\"`\n  Prompt placeholder; also the field's hint.\n- `aspectRatios: readonly AspectRatioOption[]` — default `ASPECT_RATIOS`\n  Ratio options with their output size.\n- `defaultAspectRatio: string`\n  Id of the starting ratio; the first option when omitted.\n- `styleOptions: readonly StyleOption[]` — default `IMAGE_STYLES`\n  Style chips with a two-colour swatch each.\n- `defaultStyles: Iterable<string>`\n  Style ids selected at first.\n- `defaultCount: number` — default `1`\n  Starting image count.\n- `maxImages: number` — default `4`\n  Upper bound of the count stepper.\n- `maxLength: number` — default `600`\n  Character limit on the prompt.\n- `isGenerating: boolean` — default `false`\n  Locks the form, swaps Generate for Stop and shimmers the footer.\n- `onGenerate: (request: ImagePromptRequest) => void`\n  Called on submit with the trimmed prompt, ratio, count and styles.\n- `onStop: () => void`\n  Called when the button is pressed during a run (it reads Stop then).\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the tray.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ImagePromptComposer({\n  defaultPrompt = \"\",\n  placeholder = \"Describe the image you want…\",\n  aspectRatios = ASPECT_RATIOS,\n  defaultAspectRatio,\n  styleOptions = IMAGE_STYLES,\n  defaultStyles,\n  defaultCount = 1,\n  maxImages = 4,\n  maxLength = 600,\n  isGenerating = false,\n  onGenerate,\n  onStop,\n  className,\n}: ImagePromptComposerProps) {\n  const formRef = useRef<HTMLFormElement>(null);\n  const [prompt, setPrompt] = useState(defaultPrompt);\n  const [ratioId, setRatioId] = useState<Key | null>(defaultAspectRatio ?? aspectRatios[0]?.id ?? null);\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(defaultCount);\n  const [styles, setStyles] = useState<Set<Key>>(() => new Set(defaultStyles));\n\n  const ratio = aspectRatios.find((option) => option.id === ratioId) ?? aspectRatios[0];\n  const trimmed = prompt.trim();\n  const canGenerate = trimmed.length > 0 && ratio !== undefined && !isGenerating;\n  const images = `${count} ${count === 1 ? \"image\" : \"images\"}`;\n\n  const submit = (event: FormEvent) => {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    if (isGenerating) {\n      onStop?.();\n      return;\n    }\n    if (!canGenerate) return;\n    onGenerate?.({\n      prompt: trimmed,\n      aspectRatio: ratio,\n      count,\n      // Keep the option order, not the click order.\n      styles: styleOptions.filter((option) => styles.has(option.id)).map((option) => option.id),\n    });\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <form\n      ref={formRef}\n      onSubmit={submit}\n      aria-busy={isGenerating || undefined}\n      className={cx(\"flex w-full flex-col rounded-[26px] bg-background-secondary-default p-1.5\", className)}\n    >\n      <div\n        className={cx(\n          \"flex flex-col gap-3 rounded-[20px] bg-background-primary-default p-3 shadow-xs ring-1 ring-border-ai-profile-card\",\n          \"transition-shadow duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none has-[textarea:focus]:ring-border-button-hover\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <ToggleButtonGroup\n          aria-label=\"Style\"\n          selectionMode=\"multiple\"\n          selectedKeys={styles}\n          onSelectionChange={setStyles}\n          isDisabled={isGenerating}\n          className=\"flex flex-wrap gap-1.5\"\n        >\n          {styleOptions.map((option) => (\n            <ToggleButton key={option.id} id={option.id} className={chip}>\n              {({ isSelected }) => (\n                <>\n                  {isSelected ? <RiCheckLine aria-hidden className=\"size-3 shrink-0\" /> : <Swatch colors={option.swatch} />}\n                  {option.label}\n                </>\n              )}\n            </ToggleButton>\n          ))}\n        </ToggleButtonGroup>\n\n        <Textarea\n          aria-label=\"Prompt\"\n          placeholder={placeholder}\n          value={prompt}\n          onChange={setPrompt}\n          isReadOnly={isGenerating}\n          maxLength={maxLength}\n          rows={2}\n          maxRows={6}\n          autoResize\n          onKeyDown={(event) => {\n            // ⌘ / Ctrl + Enter sends; a plain Enter is a new line.\n            if (event.key === \"Enter\" && (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey)) {\n              event.preventDefault();\n              if (!isGenerating) formRef.current?.requestSubmit();\n            } else {\n              event.continuePropagation();\n            }\n          }}\n          fieldClassName=\"bg-transparent p-0 ring-transparent\"\n          inputClassName=\"px-0.5 text-body-regular\"\n        />\n\n        <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\">\n          <Select\n            aria-label=\"Aspect ratio\"\n            size=\"sm\"\n            value={ratioId}\n            onChange={(key) => {\n              if (key !== null) setRatioId(key);\n            }}\n            isDisabled={isGenerating}\n            renderValue={\n              ratio ? (\n                <>\n                  <RatioGlyph ratio={ratio} />\n                  {ratio.id}\n                </>\n              ) : null\n            }\n            triggerClassName=\"h-8 min-w-[84px] tabular-nums\"\n          >\n            {aspectRatios.map((option) => (\n              <SelectItem key={option.id} id={option.id} textValue={`${option.id} ${option.label}`}>\n                <RatioGlyph ratio={option} />\n                <span className=\"tabular-nums\">{option.id}</span>\n                <span className=\"ms-auto text-text-tertiary\">{option.label}</span>\n              </SelectItem>\n            ))}\n          </Select>\n\n          <NumberField\n            aria-label=\"Number of images\"\n            size=\"small\"\n            leadingIcon={RiImage2Line}\n            minValue={1}\n            maxValue={maxImages}\n            value={count}\n            onChange={(next) => setCount(Number.isNaN(next) ? 1 : next)}\n            isDisabled={isGenerating}\n            className=\"w-[108px]\"\n          />\n\n          {/* One submit button that turns into Stop, so focus stays on it. Two\n              buttons swapped in place would share a DOM node: a click on Stop\n              re-renders it as a submit button before the browser runs the\n              click's default action, which then submits the form again. */}\n          <Button\n            type=\"submit\"\n            variant={isGenerating ? \"secondary\" : \"primary\"}\n            size=\"small\"\n            leadingIcon={isGenerating ? RiStopFill : RiSparkling2Line}\n            // aria-disabled keeps it focusable; the footer says what is missing.\n            aria-disabled={(!isGenerating && !canGenerate) || undefined}\n            aria-keyshortcuts={isGenerating ? undefined : \"Meta+Enter Control+Enter\"}\n            className=\"ms-auto\"\n          >\n            {isGenerating ? \"Stop\" : \"Generate\"}\n          </Button>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-3 px-3 pt-2 pb-1.5 text-caption-1-medium\">\n        <p aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"min-w-0 truncate\" data-testid=\"composer-footer\">\n          {isGenerating ? (\n            <span className=\"agent-progress-loading-text\">\n              Generating {images} · {ratio?.id}\n            </span>\n          ) : trimmed ? (\n            <span className=\"text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">\n              {images} · {ratio ? `${ratio.width} × ${ratio.height}` : \"no size\"}\n            </span>\n          ) : (\n            <span className=\"text-text-tertiary\">Write a prompt to generate</span>\n          )}\n        </p>\n        <p className=\"hidden shrink-0 items-center gap-1 text-text-tertiary sm:flex\">\n          <Kbd aria-hidden>⌘</Kbd>\n          <Kbd aria-hidden>↵</Kbd>\n          <span aria-hidden>to generate</span>\n          <span className=\"sr-only\">Press Command or Control and Enter to generate</span>\n        </p>\n      </div>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component adds no listeners, timers or observers of its own: the shortcut is a React `onKeyDown` on the field, and the Select's outside-press listener and the Textarea's resize measuring belong to those kit parts. The demo's fake run is one `setTimeout`, cleared when the run ends, is stopped or the demo unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the chips and the card change colour and ring without a transition, and the kit Textarea, Select and NumberField follow their own reduced-motion rules. The footer shimmer is the kit's `agent-progress-loading-text`, which stops moving and keeps its text readable.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Submitting on a plain Enter. A prompt is often several lines, so Enter must stay a newline; only ⌘ / Ctrl + Enter submits, and the button advertises it through `aria-keyshortcuts`.\n- Calling `onGenerate` straight from the shortcut. It would skip the guard in `onSubmit` and send blank prompts. `requestSubmit()` routes the shortcut through the same handler as the button.\n- Putting the focus ring on the textarea shell inside the card. It draws a box within a box. The shell is made transparent and the card reacts to `has-[textarea:focus]` instead.\n- Swapping a submit Generate and a `type=\"button\"` Stop in place. React reuses the DOM node, so a click on Stop re-renders it as a submit button before the browser runs the click's default action, and the form submits again: the run restarts instead of stopping. One submit button that changes its label and `onSubmit` branch avoids it.\n- Letting a cleared NumberField pass `NaN` into the request. The change handler maps it back to 1.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `ASPECT_RATIOS` and `IMAGE_STYLES` are exported defaults; pass `aspectRatios` and `styleOptions` to use your model's sizes and presets.\n- The form keeps its own prompt, ratio, count and styles. It does not clear the prompt after a run, so a user can tweak and regenerate.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA grey tray holds a white card with six style chips (Illustration selected), the prompt \"A hoopoe on an olive branch, warm paper texture\", a 4:5 ratio select and a stepper at 2. The footer reads \"2 images · 896 × 1120\". Clearing the prompt makes Generate look disabled and the footer reads \"Write a prompt to generate\"; clicking it does nothing. Generate (or ⌘/Ctrl + Enter) shows the prompt as a bubble with two shimmering frames, locks the form, swaps in Stop and shimmers \"Generating 2 images · 4:5\"; after about 2.4s two pictures fade in and the status reads \"2 ready\". Stop during a run leaves dashed \"Stopped\" frames.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "important-alerts-card": "# Build: Important Alerts Card\n\nThe alerts card from BoardUI's medical profile dashboard. A header shows \"Important alerts\" with a large weekly count (12 this week) and a static week pill (\"29 Jun - 5 Jul\"). Below it, a fixed-height feed lists the six most recent alerts: a coloured circle with a white Remix icon, a title, a description and a date tag pinned to the row's top-right corner. The feed runs flush to the card's bottom edge and clips there, so the third row is cut off as in the Figma frame, and a card-coloured gradient fades in over the top of the feed once it has been scrolled.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA health dashboard card: weekly alert count, a week pill, and a scrolling feed of tinted-icon alerts that fades under its header.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The `section` is a fixed `h-[330px]` flex column with `overflow-hidden rounded-[20px]` and 10px padding on the top and sides only, so the feed meets the bottom edge. Its 1px border is the same colour as its background: invisible, but content clips inside it so rows stop 1px short of the rounded corner.\n2. The header is a flex row: label in `text-body-medium text-text-secondary`, the count `12` in `text-title-1-medium tabular-nums` with \"this week\" baseline-aligned beside it, and `WeekRangePill` on the end.\n3. The feed is `flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain` with `gap-2.5` and `pb-2.5`, so it scrolls inside the card without chaining to the page and the last row has room at the bottom.\n4. Each alert row is `rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default p-2.5`: a `size-8` circle in the alert's colour with an 18px white icon, the title (`text-body-medium`, no wrap) and description (`text-body-2-regular`), plus an absolutely positioned date tag at `top-2.5 end-2.5`.\n5. An `onScroll` handler sets `scrolled` to `scrollTop > 0`. An `aria-hidden` 40px `bg-linear-to-b from-background-secondary-default to-transparent` overlay at the top of the feed transitions to `opacity-100` while scrolled, so rows dissolve under the header instead of being cut by it.\n6. `WeekRangePill` renders a 32px tall, min-151px pill with a 1px button border and `shadow-xs`. Without `onPrev`/`onNext` both chevrons are `aria-hidden` spans and the label is plain text.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card height = 330px, rounded-[20px], px-2.5 pt-2.5 (no bottom padding)`\n  Matches the Figma frame, where the feed is deliberately cut by the card's bottom edge to show it scrolls.\n- `ALERTS_THIS_WEEK_COUNT = 12`\n  The week's total, higher than the six rows in the feed, which only lists the most recent alerts.\n- `top fade = h-10 (40px) gradient, opacity 200ms ease-out`\n  Shown only once `scrollTop > 0`, so the first row is never dimmed at rest.\n- `icon tints = rose-600, amber-400, emerald-500, blue-400, purple-400, teal-400`\n  One hue per alert type (heart rate, Medical ID, lab results, medication, sleep, blood oxygen) so a row can be recognised at a glance.\n- `week pill = h-8, min-w-[151px], rounded-2lg, border-border-button-default, shadow-xs`\n  The same 151×32 chrome as the dashboard's other range pills; min-width rather than width so a longer label grows the pill.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ImportantAlertsCard(props?: { className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction WeekRangePill(props: { label: string; onPrev?: () => void; onNext?: () => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `className: string`\n  ImportantAlertsCard: merged onto the card section, e.g. to set its width.\n- `label: string`\n  WeekRangePill: the range text, e.g. \"29 Jun - 5 Jul\".\n- `onPrev / onNext: () => void`\n  WeekRangePill: makes the chevrons buttons and turns on the rolling label.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ImportantAlertsCard({ className }: { className?: string } = {}) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const [scrolled, setScrolled] = useState(false);\n\n  return localize((\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        // The 1px border matches the card's own bg — invisible as a line,\n        // but content clips at the padding box (inside the border), so the\n        // white alert rows stop 1px shy of the rounded edge instead of\n        // touching it when scrolled to the bottom.\n        \"flex h-[330px] w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-4 overflow-hidden rounded-[20px] border border-background-secondary-default bg-background-secondary-default px-2.5 pt-2.5\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex w-full items-start justify-between gap-2 px-1.5 pt-1.5\">\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          <p className=\"text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">Important alerts</p>\n          <div className=\"flex items-baseline gap-1\">\n            <p className=\"text-title-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums\">\n              {ALERTS_THIS_WEEK_COUNT}\n            </p>\n            <span className=\"text-caption-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-secondary\">this week</span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n        <WeekRangePill label=\"29 Jun - 5 Jul\" />\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"relative min-h-0 w-full flex-1\">\n        <div\n          className=\"flex h-full w-full flex-col gap-2.5 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain pb-2.5\"\n          onScroll={(e) => setScrolled(e.currentTarget.scrollTop > 0)}\n        >\n          {ALERTS.map((alert, index) => (\n            <div\n              key={`${alert.title}-${index}`}\n              className=\"relative flex w-full shrink-0 flex-col gap-2 rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default p-2.5\"\n            >\n              <span className={cx(\"flex size-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full\", alert.iconBg)}>\n                <alert.icon className=\"size-[18px] text-white\" aria-hidden />\n              </span>\n              <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-0.5\">\n                <p className=\"text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary\">{alert.title}</p>\n                <p className=\"text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\">{alert.description}</p>\n              </div>\n              <span className=\"absolute top-2.5 end-2.5 rounded-md bg-background-secondary-default px-1.5 py-px text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-secondary\">\n                {alert.date}\n              </span>\n            </div>\n          ))}\n        </div>\n        {/* Top fade — card-colored gradient that eases in once the feed is\n            scrolled, so rows dissolve under the header instead of cutting. */}\n        <div\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            \"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-10 bg-linear-to-b from-background-secondary-default to-transparent\",\n            \"transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out\",\n            scrolled ? \"opacity-100\" : \"opacity-0\",\n          )}\n        />\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The scroll handler is a React prop and the only state is a boolean.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion in the card is the 200ms opacity fade of the top gradient while scrolling. It does not move anything, so there is no reduced-motion branch; scrolling itself is native.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- The alerts, the count and the week label are constants inside the file. To use real data, lift `ALERTS` and the count into props; the component takes only `className` today.\n- Do not give the card a fixed height and remove `min-h-0` from the feed wrapper. Without it the flex child grows to its content and the feed stops scrolling.\n- Do not add bottom padding to the card. The flush bottom edge is what tells people the feed scrolls.\n- If you change the card background, change the fade's `from-` colour and the border colour with it, or the fade shows as a band and the border as a line.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The date tag is absolutely positioned, so a very long title can run under it; titles are kept short and `whitespace-nowrap`.\n- `WeekRangePill` also has an interactive mode: pass `onPrev`/`onNext` and the chevrons become real buttons and the label rolls like a counter (220ms `label-out` / `label-in` keyframes). This card uses the static mode.\n- All copy passes through `useTemplateCopy`, which translates display strings when a `TemplateCopyProvider` is present and is a no-op otherwise.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card renders 330px tall with \"Important alerts\", a large 12 and \"this week\", and a \"29 Jun - 5 Jul\" pill with chevrons on the right. The feed starts with High Heart rate and is cut off mid-row by the card's bottom edge. Scrolling the feed fades a gradient in under the header within 200ms and fades it out again when scrolled back to the top; the page does not scroll when the feed reaches its end. Each row has a coloured circle with a white icon and a date tag in its top-right corner.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "input": "# Build: Input\n\nThe kit's text field in three layers: `TextField` (React Aria field plus a size context), `InputBase` (the filled shell with icons or addons around the `<input>`) and `Input`, which composes a label, the shell and a hint or error line. The shell has no border; hover and focus draw a 2px inset ring and an invalid field tints red, all over 150ms. React Aria wires the label, description and error message to the input.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact Aria text field on a filled shell with an inset ring for hover and focus, label with required mark, hint or error line, and icons.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `TextField` renders `AriaTextField` as `group flex h-max w-full flex-col items-start gap-1` with `data-input-size`, and provides `TextFieldContext` with `size`, `fieldClassName` and `inputClassName`, so an `InputBase` (or the kit Textarea) further down picks them up without prop drilling. Children may be a render function that receives `isRequired`, `isInvalid`, `isDisabled` and `isReadOnly`.\n2. `InputBase` renders `AriaGroup`, whose className function calls `inputFieldClassName(state, size, hasAddon)`. The base shell is `rounded-2lg bg-background-tertiary-default` with `ring-2 ring-inset ring-transparent` and a `transition-[background-color,box-shadow,color]` over `var(--input-transition-ms)` (150ms in the theme). Hover, when not focused, disabled or invalid, sets `ring-border-button-hover`; focus within sets `ring-border-button-active`; disabled sets `bg-input-disabled-background`; invalid sets `bg-background-tertiary-error text-foreground-icon-error`.\n3. Inside the shell, a leading `leadingAddon` (if given) or `leadingIcon` (a component rendered at `size-5`, `aria-hidden`) sits before `AriaInput`, then `trailingAddon` or `trailingIcon`. Icons have no colour of their own, so they inherit the shell's text colour and turn red with it when invalid.\n4. The `<input>` is transparent and borderless in `text-body-regular text-text-primary`. Its placeholder is `text-text-tertiary`, darkens to `text-text-primary` on focus and turns `text-text-error-placeholder` when `aria-invalid`. `useLocale` decides `pl-1 text-left` or, in RTL, `pr-1 text-right`.\n5. `Input` composes the three: it renders `Label` (when `label` is given) with the field's `isRequired`, then `InputBase`, then React Aria's `FieldError` styled by `hintTextClassName(true)` when the field is invalid and `errorMessage` is set, otherwise `HintText` with `hint`. With no `label` and no `aria-label`, a string `placeholder` becomes the `aria-label`. For `type` email, url or tel it sets `dir=\"ltr\"` on the input unless `inputDir` says otherwise.\n6. `Label` wraps React Aria `Label` in `text-body-medium text-text-primary` with a 2px gap, adds an `aria-hidden` red `*` when `isRequired`, and a 16px `RiInformationFill` when `tooltip` is set. `HintText` wraps React Aria `Text` with `slot=\"description\"` (or `slot=\"errorMessage\"` when `isInvalid`) in `pt-px text-caption-1-medium`, `text-text-secondary` or `text-text-error-primary`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `--input-transition-ms = 150ms`\n  One theme variable for every field shell (Input, Textarea and others read it), so ring and fill changes run at the same speed across a form.\n- `ring = ring-2 ring-inset, transparent at rest`\n  An inset box shadow instead of a border, so the hover and focus outlines appear without the field growing or the text shifting by a pixel.\n- `sizes = medium p-2 (36px with a 20px line) · small h-8 px-1.5 py-2 (32px)`\n  Figma node 3665:1849. With a `leadingAddon` the start padding drops to 4px so an addon such as a country picker sits close to the edge.\n- `icon = size-5 (20px)`\n  The same height as the 20px body line, so icons and text centre on one axis inside the 36px shell.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Input(props: Omit<TextFieldProps, \"children\"> & { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; errorMessage?: FieldErrorProps[\"children\"]; tooltip?: boolean | string; placeholder?: string; inputDir?: \"ltr\" | \"rtl\" | \"auto\"; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; trailingIcon?: IconComponent; leadingAddon?: ReactNode; fieldClassName?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLInputElement>; groupRef?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction TextField(props: AriaTextFieldProps & { size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; fieldClassName?: string; inputClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction InputBase(props: Omit<AriaInputProps, \"size\" | \"className\"> & { size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; className?: string; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; trailingIcon?: IconComponent; leadingAddon?: ReactNode; trailingAddon?: ReactNode; fieldClassName?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLInputElement>; groupRef?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Visible label above the field, wired to the input by React Aria.\n- `hint: ReactNode`\n  Caption below the field, wired as the description.\n- `errorMessage: ReactNode | (validation) => ReactNode`\n  Replaces the hint while the field is invalid.\n- `value / defaultValue / onChange: string / (value: string) => void`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled value, from React Aria.\n- `isRequired: boolean`\n  Adds the red asterisk to the label and `aria-required`.\n- `isInvalid: boolean`\n  Red-tinted shell, red placeholder and the error line.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Grey shell and text, not-allowed cursor.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px or 32px shell.\n- `leadingIcon / trailingIcon: ComponentType`\n  20px icon components on either side of the text.\n- `tooltip: boolean | string`\n  Shows an info glyph after the label.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * The field shell's classes for a state. InputBase uses it, and so do fields\n * whose editable part isn't an <input> (Time Field's segments).\n */\nexport function inputFieldClassName(\n  { isFocusWithin, isHovered, isDisabled, isInvalid }: InputFieldState,\n  size: InputSize = \"medium\",\n  hasAddon = false,\n) {\n  return cx(\n    inputStyles.field,\n    hasAddon ? inputStyles.fieldWithAddonSize[size] : inputStyles.fieldSize[size],\n    // Hover: idle, no focus, no disabled, no invalid\n    isHovered && !isFocusWithin && !isDisabled && !isInvalid && \"ring-border-button-hover\",\n    // Focus wins over hover\n    isFocusWithin && !isDisabled && !isInvalid && \"ring-border-button-active\",\n    // Disabled\n    isDisabled && \"bg-input-disabled-background text-input-disabled-foreground\",\n    // Invalid\n    isInvalid && \"bg-background-tertiary-error text-foreground-icon-error\",\n  );\n}\n\nexport function InputBase({\n  size: sizeProp,\n  leadingIcon: Leading,\n  trailingIcon: Trailing,\n  leadingAddon,\n  trailingAddon,\n  fieldClassName,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  groupRef,\n  ...inputProps\n}: InputBaseProps) {\n  const { direction } = useLocale();\n  const ctx = useContext(TextFieldContext);\n  const size: InputSize = sizeProp ?? ctx.size ?? \"medium\";\n  const hasAddon = leadingAddon !== undefined && leadingAddon !== null;\n\n  return (\n    <AriaGroup\n      ref={groupRef}\n      className={(state) => cx(inputFieldClassName(state, size, hasAddon), ctx.fieldClassName, fieldClassName)}\n    >\n      <div className={inputStyles.content}>\n        <div className={inputStyles.leftSection}>\n          {hasAddon ? (\n            leadingAddon\n          ) : Leading ? (\n            <Leading className={inputStyles.icon} aria-hidden />\n          ) : null}\n          <AriaInput\n            ref={ref}\n            {...inputProps}\n            // Align to the field's start even when email/URL values use LTR.\n            className={cx(inputStyles.input, direction === \"rtl\" ? \"pr-1 text-right\" : \"pl-1 text-left\", ctx.inputClassName, className)}\n          />\n        </div>\n        {trailingAddon ?? (Trailing ? (\n          <Trailing className={inputStyles.icon} aria-hidden />\n        ) : null)}\n      </div>\n    </AriaGroup>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The components hold no state, listeners or timers of their own; React Aria owns the field state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. The only motion is the 150ms colour and ring transition on the shell, which a global rule that removes transitions turns into an instant change.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not add a `border` to show focus. The shell uses an inset ring that takes no layout space; a border would shift the content by a pixel on focus.\n- Do not pass an element to `leadingIcon` (`<RiMailLine />`). It expects the component (`RiMailLine`) and renders it with the shell's classes.\n- Do not set `isInvalid` without an `errorMessage` or `hint` if users need to know why. Only the shell tints red; the error text comes from those props.\n- Do not leave a field with no `label`, no `aria-label` and no string `placeholder`. Then nothing names it and React Aria warns.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `inputFieldClassName` is exported for fields whose editable part is not an `<input>` (date and time segments), so they share the exact shell states.\n- `Label`'s `isInvalid` prop is accepted but unused; it is reserved for invalid styling later.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA labelled field shows a grey filled shell with no visible border. Hovering draws a light inset ring; clicking in darkens the ring and the placeholder, with no shift in layout. A required field shows a red asterisk after its label. Typing an invalid email in a field marked invalid tints the shell and its icon red and swaps the hint for the error message. A disabled field is lighter and ignores input. Screen readers announce the label, the hint and, when invalid, the error.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "input-otp": "# Build: Input OTP\n\nA verification-code field that looks like six boxes but holds one digit string. Every box is a real `<input>` with `autoComplete=\"one-time-code\"`, and all edits go through one `writeFrom` function, so typing, pasting and OS autofill all write digits from the current box onward and move focus to the next empty one. It has no dependency beyond React and works controlled or uncontrolled.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne-time-code field: a monospace box per digit that advances on type, steps back on Backspace, spreads a paste and accepts OS autofill.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The value is a single string of digits. `code` is the controlled `value` (or internal state seeded from `defaultValue`) with every non-digit removed (`/\\D/g`) and cut to `length`. Box `i` shows `code[i]`, or an empty string.\n2. `writeFrom(index, digits)` strips non-digits, pads `code` to `length` with spaces, overwrites positions from `index` onward, and passes the result to `commit`, then calls `focusBox(index + digits.length)`. `focusBox` clamps the index to the last box, focuses it and selects its content, so the next keystroke replaces rather than appends.\n3. `commit` strips non-digits again, sets internal state when uncontrolled, calls `onChange`, and calls `onComplete` whenever the result has `length` digits. Each box's `onChange` calls `writeFrom(index, event.target.value)`. `maxLength` is `length`, not 1, so when iOS or Chrome autofill drops the whole code into one box, all of it is distributed instead of five digits being lost.\n4. `onKeyDown` handles Backspace itself (`preventDefault`): on a filled box it clears that position and keeps focus; on an empty box it clears the previous position and moves focus back. ArrowLeft and ArrowRight move focus without editing. `onPaste` prevents the default and sends the clipboard text to `writeFrom` from the box with the caret. `onFocus` selects the box content.\n5. The wrapper is `role=\"group\"` with the `aria-label` (default \"One-time code\") and `dir=\"ltr\"`, so digits run left to right even in an RTL page. Each input has `aria-label=\"Digit i of n\"`, `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"numeric\"`, and `aria-invalid` when `isInvalid`. `groupEvery` adds `ms-3` before every nth box to split the code, for example into 3 + 3.\n6. Each box is `size-12 rounded-2lg` with `font-mono text-title-3-medium tabular-nums`, a 1px `border-border-button-default` and `shadow-xs`. Hover darkens the border; `focus-visible` draws the focus border and a 2px ring; `isInvalid` sets a red border and tint; `isDisabled` greys it out. These changes transition over 150ms.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `length = 6 (default)`\n  The common length for email and SMS codes, and the default the `aria-label`s count against (\"Digit 1 of 6\").\n- `maxLength = length (not 1)`\n  OS autofill writes the whole code into the focused box. With `maxLength={1}` the browser would keep only the first digit.\n- `box = size-12 (48px), rounded-2lg, font-mono text-title-3-medium tabular-nums`\n  Monospace digits keep a row of fixed boxes optically even; in a proportional face a 1 is visibly narrower than an 8.\n- `group gap = gap-2 between boxes, extra ms-3 at each groupEvery boundary`\n  An 8px gap keeps the boxes separate; the extra 12px reads as a break, so 000 000 is easier to copy from a phone.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction InputOtp(props: { length?: number; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (value: string) => void; onComplete?: (value: string) => void; isDisabled?: boolean; isInvalid?: boolean; groupEvery?: number; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `length: number` — default `6`\n  Number of digit boxes.\n- `value / defaultValue: string`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled digits. Non-digits are dropped and the string is cut to `length`.\n- `onChange: (value: string) => void`\n  Called with the new digit string on every edit.\n- `onComplete: (value: string) => void`\n  Called after any edit that leaves every box filled, so editing a digit of a full code calls it again.\n- `groupEvery: number`\n  Adds a wider gap before every nth box, for example 3.\n- `isInvalid: boolean` — default `false`\n  Red border and tint, `aria-invalid` on every box.\n- `isDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Disables every box.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"One-time code\"`\n  Name of the group.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst commit = (next: string) => {\n  const clean = next.replace(DIGITS_ONLY, \"\").slice(0, length);\n  if (!controlled) setInternal(clean);\n  onChange?.(clean);\n  if (clean.length === length) onComplete?.(clean);\n};\n\nconst focusBox = (index: number) => {\n  const target = inputsRef.current[Math.max(0, Math.min(index, length - 1))];\n  target?.focus();\n  target?.select();\n};\n\n/** Writes `digits` starting at `index`, which covers typing and autofill. */\nconst writeFrom = (index: number, digits: string) => {\n  const clean = digits.replace(DIGITS_ONLY, \"\");\n  if (clean === \"\") return;\n\n  const chars = code.padEnd(length, \" \").split(\"\");\n  for (let offset = 0; offset < clean.length && index + offset < length; offset += 1) {\n    chars[index + offset] = clean.charAt(offset);\n  }\n  commit(chars.join(\"\").trimEnd());\n  focusBox(index + clean.length);\n};\n\nconst onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>, index: number) => {\n  if (event.key === \"Backspace\") {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    if (code[index]) {\n      // Clear in place; the caret only steps back on an already-empty box,\n      // which is what makes holding Backspace feel right.\n      const chars = code.padEnd(length, \" \").split(\"\");\n      chars[index] = \" \";\n      commit(chars.join(\"\").trimEnd());\n      return;\n    }\n    const chars = code.padEnd(length, \" \").split(\"\");\n    chars[Math.max(0, index - 1)] = \" \";\n    commit(chars.join(\"\").trimEnd());\n    focusBox(index - 1);\n    return;\n  }\n\n  if (event.key === \"ArrowLeft\") {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    focusBox(index - 1);\n  } else if (event.key === \"ArrowRight\") {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    focusBox(index + 1);\n  }\n};\n\nconst onPaste = (event: ClipboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>, index: number) => {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  writeFrom(index, event.clipboardData.getData(\"text\"));\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component keeps a ref array of inputs and at most one piece of state; it sets no timers or listeners outside React's own handlers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. Its only motion is the 150ms border, background and shadow transition on each box, which a global rule that removes transitions makes instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not use `type=\"number\"` for the boxes. It adds spinners, accepts `e` and `-`, and reports an empty value for input it considers malformed. Use `type=\"text\"` with `inputMode=\"numeric\"`.\n- Do not set `maxLength={1}`. Autofill hands the full code to one box and the browser would drop the rest.\n- Do not put `aria-invalid` on the `role=\"group\"` wrapper. The group role does not support it; each input carries its own.\n- Do not remove `dir=\"ltr\"` from the wrapper. In an RTL page the code would otherwise read right to left.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The value is stored as a compact digit string, so clearing a box in the middle of a full code shifts the digits after it one place left.\n- Arrow keys are not mirrored; the wrapper is always left to right.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSix empty boxes sit in a row. Typing 7 fills the first box and moves focus to the second. Pasting \"731428\" into the first box fills all six and calls `onComplete`. Backspace on a filled box clears it and keeps focus; Backspace on an empty box clears the previous one and moves back. Arrow keys move between boxes without changing anything. Letters are ignored. With `groupEvery={3}` a wider gap separates 3 + 3. `isInvalid` turns every box red.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "install-command-tabs": "# Build: Install Command Tabs\n\nThe install box from a docs page. A grey shell holds a row of package-manager tabs and a copy button above a white command line. The runner (`npx`, `pnpm dlx`, `bun add` and so on) is set in the stronger purple and the arguments in the lighter command accent. Commands are derived from one description (a binary to run, or packages to add), so the four spellings never drift apart. A controlled `value` keeps every block on the page on the manager the reader picked. `InstallSteps` and `InstallStep` lay blocks out as numbered steps joined by the `docs-install-rail` line.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\npnpm, npm, yarn and bun tabs that spell the same install or run command four ways, with copy and numbered docs steps on a rail.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `getInstallCommand(manager, source)` builds each spelling. An explicit `commands[manager]` wins. Otherwise `run` is prefixed with the manager's runner (`npx`, `pnpm dlx`, `yarn dlx`, `bunx --bun`), or `install` packages follow its add verb (`npm install`, `pnpm add`, `yarn add`, `bun add`), with `-D` or `--dev` when `dev` is set.\n2. React Aria `Tabs` provide one `Tab` and one `TabPanel` per manager, with roving focus, arrow keys and `aria-controls`. Selection is controlled through `value` and `onValueChange` or uncontrolled through `defaultValue`. A `value` that is not in `managers` falls back to the first one rather than leaving nothing selected.\n3. Each panel splits its command with `splitRunner`, which matches the longest known runner first so that `pnpm dlx` beats `pnpm`. The runner renders in `status-purple-text` and the rest in `docs-command-accent`, inside a horizontally scrollable `whitespace-pre` line.\n4. The header `CopyButton` takes a function value, so it copies the command of the tab selected at click time, and its accessible name follows the tab (\"Copy yarn command\"). It shows the shared copy-to-check feedback from `code-block-copy`.\n5. `InstallStep` is an `<li>` with a 28px zero-padded number badge and a 1px `bg-docs-install-rail` line that fills the remaining height down to the next badge. The `group-last/step:hidden` modifier removes the rail on the last step without a prop.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `tab pill = h-7, px-2.5, rounded-lg, white + shadow-2xs when selected`\n  The selected pill sits 6px inside the rounded-2xl shell, like the file chip in the code block, so the two docs pieces line up.\n- `runner order = longest runner first`\n  `pnpm dlx` and `pnpm add` both start with `pnpm`, so matching the shortest first would colour only `pnpm`.\n- `step badge = size-7, rounded-full, mono caption-1, zero-padded`\n  Two-digit labels (`01`, `02`) keep the badge text the same width from step 1 to step 12.\n- `rail = w-px, docs-install-rail, mt-2 below the badge`\n  The token is the button border in light mode and a 60% neutral-800 in dark mode, so the rail stays visible without competing with the code panels.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype PackageManager = \"pnpm\" | \"npm\" | \"yarn\" | \"bun\";\nfunction InstallCommandTabs(props: { run?: string; install?: string | readonly string[]; dev?: boolean; commands?: Partial<Record<PackageManager, string>>; managers?: readonly PackageManager[]; value?: PackageManager; defaultValue?: PackageManager; onValueChange?: (manager: PackageManager) => void; \"aria-label\"?: string; onCopied?: (command: string) => void; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction getInstallCommand(manager: PackageManager, source: { run?: string; install?: string | readonly string[]; dev?: boolean; commands?: Partial<Record<PackageManager, string>> }): string\nfunction InstallSteps(props: { children: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction InstallStep(props: { step: number; title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; children?: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `run: string`\n  Arguments for a one-off binary run, e.g. `boardui@latest add line-chart-card`.\n- `install: string | readonly string[]`\n  Packages to add to the project.\n- `dev: boolean` — default `false`\n  With `install`, add as dev dependencies.\n- `commands: Partial<Record<PackageManager, string>>`\n  Exact command per manager. Overrides `run` and `install`.\n- `managers: readonly PackageManager[]` — default `[\"pnpm\", \"npm\", \"yarn\", \"bun\"]`\n  Tabs to show, in order.\n- `value / defaultValue / onValueChange: PackageManager` — default `uncontrolled, first manager`\n  Selected manager. Share it to sync blocks.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Package manager\"`\n  Accessible name of the tab list.\n- `onCopied: (command: string) => void`\n  Called with the copied command.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Every command is derived, never stored: changing `run` or `install`\n// updates all four spellings at once, and the copy button reads the one\n// that matches the selected tab at click time.\nconst source: InstallCommandSource = { run, install, dev, commands };\nconst lines = managers.map((manager) => {\n  const command = getInstallCommand(manager, source);\n  const [runner, rest] = splitRunner(command);\n  return { manager, command, runner, rest };\n});\nconst current = lines.find((line) => line.manager === active)?.command ?? \"\";\n\nconst select = (key: PackageManager) => {\n  setUncontrolled(key);\n  onValueChange?.(key);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** There are no effects, timers or listeners in this file. The copy reset timeout lives in `useCopyToClipboard` and is cleared on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing travels. The tab pill fades its colour and shadow over 150ms, and the copy glyph cross-fades over 200ms. Both have `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so they switch at once.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Storing the copied text in state when the tab changes. A stale closure would copy the npm command after the reader switched to bun. The copy button reads the selected command at click time.\n- Assuming every runner is one word. `pnpm dlx`, `yarn dlx` and `bunx --bun` are two tokens, and colouring by first word would split them wrongly.\n- Giving each block its own state on a multi-step page. The reader would have to pick their manager once per block. Lift the value and pass `value` and `onValueChange` to every block.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `getInstallCommand` and `PACKAGE_MANAGERS` are exported for building other docs pieces, such as an llms.txt entry or a shell snippet.\n- Use `commands` for anything the builder does not cover, such as `npm create` or a global install.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith `run=\"boardui@latest add line-chart-card\"`, the pnpm tab shows `pnpm dlx boardui@latest add line-chart-card`, npm shows `npx …`, yarn `yarn dlx …` and bun `bunx --bun …`, with the runner in a darker purple than the arguments. Clicking yarn, or arrowing to it, switches the command and renames the copy button to \"Copy yarn command\", which copies exactly the visible text. When two blocks share `value`, choosing bun in one switches the other. Step badges read 01 and 02, and a rail joins them but not past the last step.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "instanced-quad-particles": "# Build: Instanced Quad Particles\n\nFour vertices and six indices are shared by every particle; three instanced attributes carry position, size, spin, alpha, tint and shape. One `drawElementsInstanced` puts the whole field on screen. Quads instead of `gl_PointSize` because point sprites are capped at 255px on many drivers, cannot be rotated, and are culled whole the instant their centre leaves the viewport — a large sprite disappears while half of it is still visible.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nUp to 1800 drifting particles in one draw call — quads rather than point sprites, so each one has its own size, spin and shape.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: ogl@^1.0.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The geometry is one unit quad in `position` (-0.5..0.5) plus an index buffer, and three attributes marked `instanced: 1`: `aOrigin` (field-space rest position), `aParams` (size in CSS px, signed spin rate, base alpha, tint mix) and `aSeed` (two flow phases, spin phase, shape mix). Ten floats per particle, 72 KB at the 1800 ceiling.\n2. Buffers are allocated once at `maxCount` and never reallocated. The visible population comes from `geometry.setInstancedCount(count)` where `count` is derived from container area — `clamp(round(megapixels * density), 250, maxCount)` — so the same density reads correctly in a 320px card and a 2000px hero. Because the scatter is random, any prefix of the buffer is still a spatially even field.\n3. The vertex shader rotates the quad corner in PIXEL space and only then divides by the drawing-buffer resolution to reach clip space. Rotating in clip space would shear the quad by the container aspect ratio, and a square particle would become a lozenge that changes shape as it spins.\n4. Drift is a pure function of `elapsed` and the per-particle seed: the field marches upward with `fract(y + t * RISE)`, then two out-of-phase sinusoids sampled at the particle's own position add wander. No per-particle state is stored, so a resize, a tab switch or a reduced-motion still can all be drawn from one number.\n5. `fract` teleports a particle from the top edge to the bottom, which is a visible pop. `uFadeBand` smoothsteps alpha to zero within 12% of each end, so the population always arrives and leaves at zero opacity.\n6. One rounded-box SDF in the fragment shader covers both shapes. At `shape` 0 the half-extents and the corner radius are both 0.5, which reduces exactly to `length(uv) - 0.5` — a true circle. At 1 it is a narrow shard, and the shard is the only reason per-particle rotation is visible at all.\n7. Blending is additive (`ONE, ONE`) onto an opaque clear colour, which is order independent — a thousand overlapping instances need no depth sort, which is what makes one draw call possible. The canvas is opaque rather than transparent because additive blending onto transparency would have to brighten the page background, and the GPU cannot see it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `density = 900`\n  Particles per megapixel. Tuned so a typical card holds 250-400 and a full-width hero approaches the ceiling. Doubling it does not double the perceived density — it doubles the fill rate and flattens the depth cue that comes from having gaps.\n- `maxCount = 1800`\n  Ceiling and allocation size. Not a performance limit on the vertex side — 1800 quads is 7200 vertices, nothing — but the fill cost of 1800 soft additive quads averaging 12px is roughly one full screen of blended pixels per frame.\n- `MAX_DPR = 1.5`\n  Additive fill is the entire cost of this component and it scales with the square of the pixel ratio. Dust has no fine detail that a third sample per axis resolves, so 3x phones render at 1.5x and the frame budget drops by a factor of four against an uncapped ratio.\n- `SIZE_BIAS = 2.2`\n  Exponent on the uniform random used for size. At 1 a quarter of the population lands in the largest quarter of the range, and a hundred 28px blobs is fog rather than dust. At 2.2 the large sizes are rare enough to read as individuals.\n- `RISE = 0.045`\n  Field heights per second at `speed` 1 — about 22 seconds to cross. Slow enough that the eye reads drift rather than travel; above ~0.15 the field looks like falling snow played in reverse.\n- `FADE_BAND = 0.12`\n  Fraction of the field height the wrap fade occupies at each end. Below ~0.06 the pop at the seam becomes visible for the largest particles, whose radius alone is a couple of percent of the height. Above 0.2 a quarter of the field is permanently dim.\n- `FLOW_SCALE = 1.4`\n  Cycles of wander across the field. Under 1 the whole population slides sideways as a sheet; well above 2 adjacent particles move in opposite directions and the motion reads as noise rather than air.\n- `X_MIN / X_MAX = -0.08 / 1.08`\n  Field-space x range. Seeding wider than the container means horizontal wander can never bare a strip at the left or right edge. The off-screen quads are clipped by the rasteriser and cost nothing — which is exactly what a point sprite could not do.\n- `alpha per particle = 1 - 0.78 * sizeT`\n  Large particles are dimmed so total emitted light is roughly independent of size. Without it the few big instances dominate every additive overlap and the field looks like it has hot spots.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction InstancedQuadParticles(props: { density?: number; maxCount?: number; sizeRange?: readonly [number, number]; softness?: number; spin?: number; speed?: number; wander?: number; opacity?: number; colorA?: string; colorB?: string; background?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `density: number` — default `900`\n  Particles per megapixel of container area. The count is derived from this, not fixed.\n- `maxCount: number` — default `1800`\n  Population ceiling, and the size the buffers are allocated at.\n- `sizeRange: readonly [number, number]` — default `[5, 28]`\n  Particle size in CSS pixels. The distribution is biased toward the small end.\n- `softness: number` — default `0.62`\n  Edge width, 0..1. 0 is a hard silhouette, 1 is a pure gradient. Floored at one device pixel.\n- `spin: number` — default `0.5`\n  Peak spin in radians per second; each particle takes a signed fraction of it.\n- `speed: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier on the whole drift, including the upward march.\n- `wander: number` — default `0.06`\n  Sinusoidal wander amplitude in field units, where 1 is the container.\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.55`\n  Overall alpha multiplier applied in the fragment shader.\n- `colorA: string` — default `'#9fb4cc'`\n  First tint. Any value ogl's `Color` accepts.\n- `colorB: string` — default `'#e8ddc9'`\n  Second tint; each particle mixes between the two by a seeded amount.\n- `background: string` — default `'#07090d'`\n  Opaque clear colour the additive field is composited onto.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'instanced-quad-particles'`\n  Seeds the scatter. Same seed, same field on every reload and on the server.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function InstancedQuadParticles({\n  density = 900,\n  maxCount = 1800,\n  sizeRange = [5, 28],\n  softness = 0.62,\n  spin = 0.5,\n  speed = 1,\n  wander = 0.06,\n  opacity = 0.55,\n  colorA = '#9fb4cc',\n  colorB = '#e8ddc9',\n  background = '#07090d',\n  seed = 'instanced-quad-particles',\n  className,\n}: InstancedQuadParticlesProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1;\n\n  const [minSize = 5, maxSize = 28] = sizeRange;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // ogl creates its own canvas, and it is appended and removed per effect\n    // run. Reusing one React-owned canvas would break under StrictMode: the\n    // cleanup force-loses the context, and `getContext` on that same element\n    // afterwards hands back the dead one rather than a fresh context.\n    const renderer = new Renderer({\n      dpr: pixelRatio(MAX_DPR),\n      // Opaque, so the field owns every pixel and reads the same on a light or\n      // a dark page. Additive blending onto a transparent canvas would have to\n      // brighten whatever is behind it, which is the page background, which\n      // the GPU cannot see.\n      alpha: false,\n      // Nothing is sorted and nothing occludes anything, so a depth buffer is\n      // memory and a clear per frame for no result.\n      depth: false,\n      antialias: false,\n      // The field is fill-bound, not shader-bound. Asking for the discrete GPU\n      // on a laptop costs battery and buys nothing.\n      powerPreference: 'low-power',\n    });\n\n    const gl = renderer.gl;\n    const canvas = gl.canvas;\n\n    const bg = new Color(background);\n    gl.clearColor(bg.r, bg.g, bg.b, 1);\n\n    // Four vertices, six indices, shared by every instance.\n    const quad = new Float32Array([-0.5, -0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5]);\n    const index = new Uint16Array([0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 3]);\n\n    const total = Math.max(MIN_COUNT, Math.round(maxCount));\n    const origins = new Float32Array(total * 2);\n    const params = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n    const seeds = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const lo = Math.max(1, Math.min(minSize, maxSize));\n    const hi = Math.max(lo + 0.5, Math.max(minSize, maxSize));\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i += 1) {\n      origins[i * 2] = randRange(rng, X_MIN, X_MAX);\n      origins[i * 2 + 1] = rng();\n\n      // Biased toward small: a linear distribution puts a quarter of the\n      // population in the largest quarter of the size range, and a hundred\n      // 28px blobs is fog rather than dust.\n      const sizeT = Math.pow(rng(), SIZE_BIAS);\n      params[i * 4] = lo + (hi - lo) * sizeT;\n      params[i * 4 + 1] = randRange(rng, -1, 1) * spin;\n      // Big particles are dimmer. Alpha per particle rather than per pixel\n      // keeps the total light roughly constant as the size scales.\n      params[i * 4 + 2] = 1 - 0.78 * sizeT;\n      params[i * 4 + 3] = rng();\n\n      seeds[i * 4] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      seeds[i * 4 + 1] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      seeds[i * 4 + 2] = rng();\n      // A third are discs. The rest are shards, and the shards are the only\n      // reason per-particle rotation is visible at all.\n      seeds[i * 4 + 3] = rng() < 0.34 ? 0 : randRange(rng, 0.55, 1);\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new Geometry(gl, {\n      index: { data: index },\n      position: { size: 2, data: quad },\n      aOrigin: { size: 2, data: origins, instanced: 1 },\n      aParams: { size: 4, data: params, instanced: 1 },\n      aSeed: { size: 4, data: seeds, instanced: 1 },\n    });\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: [1, 1] },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.dpr },\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uDrift: { value: speed },\n      uRise: { value: RISE },\n      uFlowScale: { value: FLOW_SCALE },\n      uWander: { value: wander },\n      uFadeBand: { value: FADE_BAND },\n      uColorA: { value: new Color(colorA) },\n      uColorB: { value: new Color(colorB) },\n      uSoftness: { value: clamp(softness, 0, 1) },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n    };\n\n    const program = new Program(gl, {\n      vertex: INSTANCED_QUAD_PARTICLES_VERT,\n      fragment: INSTANCED_QUAD_PARTICLES_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      // ogl defaults `cullFace` to BACK. The quad's index winding is not\n      // guaranteed to face the way the driver expects once it is rotated in\n      // the vertex shader, and a culled particle is simply absent.\n      cullFace: false,\n    });\n    // Additive, overriding the source-over that `transparent: true` selected.\n    // Order independent, so a thousand overlapping instances need no sort —\n    // which is what makes one draw call possible in the first place.\n    program.setBlendFunc(gl.ONE, gl.ONE);\n\n    const mesh = new Mesh(gl, { geometry, program });\n\n    el.appendChild(canvas);\n    canvas.style.display = 'block';\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h);\n      // `setSize` writes literal px into the canvas's inline style, which wins\n      // over any class. Re-asserting percentages keeps the element fluid\n      // between resize notifications.\n      canvas.style.width = '100%';\n      canvas.style.height = '100%';\n\n      uniforms.uResolution.value = [gl.drawingBufferWidth, gl.drawingBufferHeight];\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.dpr;\n\n      // Population from area, not from a fixed number: the same density looks\n      // right in a 320px card and in a 2000px hero. The buffers already hold\n      // `total` particles in scattered order, so a prefix of them is still a\n      // spatially even field — no rebuild, no reallocation.\n      const megapixels = (w * h) / 1_000_000;\n      const count = clamp(Math.round(megapixels * density), MIN_COUNT, total);\n      geometry.setInstancedCount(count);\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      renderer.render({ scene: mesh });\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { renderer, geometry, program, mesh, uniforms, resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    // An unheard context loss is a field that goes blank and never returns —\n    // no error, no fallback — so it is routed into the same no-WebGL path. The\n    // `preventDefault` is mandatory: without it a restore can never be offered.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      setUnsupported(true);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      // ogl has no `dispose()`: `remove()` on the geometry deletes its VAOs and\n      // every buffer, `remove()` on the program deletes the shaders and the\n      // program, and only then is the context worth handing back.\n      geometry.remove();\n      program.remove();\n      loseContext(gl);\n      canvas.width = 0;\n      canvas.height = 0;\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    density,\n    maxCount,\n    minSize,\n    maxSize,\n    softness,\n    spin,\n    speed,\n    wander,\n    opacity,\n    colorA,\n    colorB,\n    background,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect: a reflow costs a `setSize` and an instanced-count\n  // change, never a buffer rebuild.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed),\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `geometry.remove()` deletes every VAO and every buffer, `program.remove()` deletes the shaders and the program, then `loseContext(gl)` hands the context back through `WEBGL_lose_context` — ogl has no `dispose()` and nothing else releases a context. The canvas backing store is zeroed and the node removed, so the drawing buffer goes with it rather than waiting for GC.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF subscription is never made and the field is drawn once at `elapsed` 0. That is a complete, correct frame rather than a collapsed one: the scatter, sizes, tints, shapes and spin phases are all per-particle seeds that already vary at t=0, and the wrap fade applies exactly as it does in motion. Resizing redraws the same still.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reaching for `gl_PointSize`. It is capped — 255px on a great many drivers, and the cap is silent — it cannot be rotated, and the sprite is culled by its centre point, so anything large blinks out while half of it is still on screen.\n- Leaving ogl's `cullFace` at its default. `Program` defaults it to `gl.BACK`; once the quad corners are rotated in the vertex shader the winding the driver sees is not the winding you authored, and culled particles are simply absent with no error anywhere.\n- Rotating the corner offset after converting to clip space. The container aspect ratio shears it, so a particle is square only when the container is, and it visibly changes shape through a spin.\n- Rebuilding the buffers on resize to change the particle count. `setInstancedCount` selects a prefix of what is already uploaded; reallocating 72 KB of GPU memory on every ResizeObserver notification is a stall for no visual difference.\n- Wrapping with `fract` and no fade. The seam is a hard teleport, and at 1800 particles something is crossing it constantly, so the field flickers along one edge.\n- Compositing an additive field onto a transparent canvas. Additive needs to know what it is adding to; with `alpha: true` the destination is zero, the result is the particle colour flat, and the field looks pasted on rather than glowing.\n- Reusing one React-owned canvas across effect runs. Cleanup force-loses the context, and `getContext` on that same element afterwards returns the dead one — under StrictMode's mount / unmount / mount that means every instance is permanently black. ogl creates its own canvas, which is appended and removed per run.\n- Assuming `renderer.setSize` leaves the CSS alone. ogl writes literal px into the canvas's inline `style`, which beats any class; the percentages have to be re-asserted after every call or the element stops being fluid.\n\n## Notes\n\n- ogl rather than three: the whole component is one instanced mesh with no lights, no camera and no scene graph, and ogl's `Geometry` exposes instanced attributes directly with `instanced: 1` instead of through an `InstancedBufferGeometry` wrapper.\n- No camera is passed to `renderer.render`, which is deliberate. Without one ogl skips frustum culling and the matrix uniforms entirely — the shader writes clip space itself, so a projection matrix would be an identity multiply per vertex.\n- `depth: false` on the context. Nothing occludes anything under additive blending, so a depth buffer would be memory plus a clear per frame for no result.\n- Sizes are authored in CSS pixels and multiplied by the pixel ratio in the shader, so the field looks identical across a dpr change and nothing needs rebuilding when one happens.\n- GLSL ES 1.00 with no derivative functions: the edge anti-aliasing floor is `1.0 / sizePx`, which is exact for a quad of that pixel size, so the same source compiles under a WebGL1 fallback context where `fwidth` needs an extension.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe field drifts upward and sideways with no visible seam at the top or bottom edge, and no particle ever appears or vanishes abruptly. Individual particles spin, and the shards are visibly rotating rather than shimmering. Resize the container narrow and wide: particles stay square at every aspect ratio, and the population changes without a stutter. One draw call per frame in a frame capture, whatever the count. Mount and unmount a dozen instances and none of the earlier ones go black.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "inverse-scroll-scale": "# Build: Inverse Scroll Scale\n\nThe inverse of the usual scroll-scale. Instead of growing as it rises, the media starts oversized and resolves down to exactly 1.0 over the first 60% of the scroller, so it settles into its frame rather than bursting out of it — and it is at rest for the whole time it is the thing you are actually looking at. No animation library: one rAF callback, gated by an IntersectionObserver.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nMedia enters oversized at 1.25x and settles to exactly 1.0 as it rises through the first 60% of the scroller.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each frame the component reads the frame element's `getBoundingClientRect().top`, converts it to a position inside the scroller by subtracting the scroller's own rect top, and computes `t = clamp((height - top) / (span * height), 0, 1)`.\n2. `t` is 0 while the frame's top edge sits on the bottom of the scroller and reaches 1 once it has climbed `span` of the scroller's height — 60% by default, so it is finished long before the element is centred.\n3. Scale is a straight linear ramp: `from - (from - 1) * t`. It ends on exactly 1.0 by construction, which matters because a scale below 1 on an element sized to its frame would let the frame show through at the edges.\n4. Optional smoothing runs the result through `damp(current, wanted, smoothing, dt)`, which raises the retention factor to the power of elapsed time and so converges at the same rate on 60Hz and 120Hz displays. The naive `x += (target - x) * 0.1` would run twice as fast on the faster display.\n5. The frame is rounded to four decimals and compared against what was last written; on the many frames where nothing changed the style write is skipped entirely, so an idle instance costs one rect read.\n6. An IntersectionObserver with a 20% rootMargin flips an `active` ref, and the rAF callback returns immediately when it is false. The observer is hand-rolled rather than pooled because it needs the demo's scroller as its `root`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `from = 1.25`\n  Starting scale. Must be at least 1 — anything less shows the frame through the edges of the media, because the media is sized to exactly fill the frame. Past ~1.4 the visible crop at the start is severe enough that the composition changes rather than just the size.\n- `span = 0.6`\n  Fraction of scroller height over which the scale resolves, measured up from the bottom edge. The whole argument for the effect: at 1.0 the media is still moving while it is centred and being read, which is exactly when it should be still. At 0.6 it lands well before that.\n- `smoothing = 0`\n  Off by default. The mapping from scroll position to scale is already continuous, so smoothing adds lag with nothing to hide. It exists for the case where the scroll source is itself steppy (a trackpad-free mouse wheel, or a snapped container).\n- `rootMargin = '20% 0px 20% 0px'`\n  Starts the rAF work a fifth of a scroller height before the frame is visible, so the first painted frame is already at the correct scale. Without the margin the element pops from its authored scale to its computed one on the frame it enters.\n- `toFixed precision = 4`\n  Below one device pixel of difference on a 4000px-wide element, so the rounding is invisible; its purpose is to make the \"unchanged\" comparison actually hit, which it never would on raw floats.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction InverseScrollScale(props: { children: ReactNode; from?: number; span?: number; smoothing?: number; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The media. Sized to fill the frame; this is what gets scaled.\n- `from: number` — default `1.25`\n  Starting scale. Must be >= 1.\n- `span: number` — default `0.6`\n  Fraction of scroller height over which the scale resolves.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0`\n  Fraction of the remaining gap left after one second. 0 disables smoothing.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the frame via `cn`. Set the frame size here.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function InverseScrollScale({\n  children,\n  from = 1.25,\n  span = 0.6,\n  smoothing = 0,\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: InverseScrollScaleProps) {\n  const frame = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const media = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const active = useRef(false);\n  const scale = useRef(from);\n  const written = useRef(-1);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Gate the rAF work on visibility. Hand-rolled rather than `useInView`\n  // because the observer needs a custom `root` — the demo's own scroller —\n  // and a pooled observer keyed only by threshold would hand back one rooted\n  // at the document.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = frame.current;\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n\n    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(\n      (entries) => {\n        for (const entry of entries) active.current = entry.isIntersecting;\n      },\n      { root: scrollerRef?.current ?? null, rootMargin: '20% 0px 20% 0px' },\n    );\n    observer.observe(el);\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\n  }, [reduced, scrollerRef]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const el = frame.current;\n      const target = media.current;\n      if (!el || !target || !active.current) return;\n\n      // One read pass, then one write pass. Reading the rect after writing a\n      // transform in the same frame forces a synchronous layout.\n      const scroller = scrollerRef?.current ?? null;\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const height = scroller ? scroller.clientHeight : window.innerHeight;\n      const top = scroller ? rect.top - scroller.getBoundingClientRect().top : rect.top;\n\n      // t is 0 while the frame's top edge is at the bottom of the scroller and\n      // reaches 1 once it has risen `span` of the scroller's height.\n      const t = clamp((height - top) / (span * height), 0, 1);\n      const wanted = from - (from - 1) * t;\n\n      scale.current =\n        smoothing > 0 ? damp(scale.current, wanted, smoothing, dt) : wanted;\n\n      // Four decimals is below one device pixel on a 4000px element, and the\n      // equality check skips the style write on the many frames where nothing\n      // changed — an idle instance then costs a rect read and nothing else.\n      const next = Number(scale.current.toFixed(4));\n      if (next === written.current) return;\n      written.current = next;\n      target.style.transform = `scale(${next})`;\n    },\n    !reduced,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes on unmount and drops the shared loop entirely when it was the last subscriber; the IntersectionObserver is disconnected in its own effect cleanup. Without the disconnect the observer holds a reference to a removed element and keeps firing on every scroll of a container the component no longer participates in.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The media renders at scale 1 — its resolved state, filling the frame — and neither the IntersectionObserver nor the rAF subscription is created, so there is no per-frame work at all.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `window.innerHeight` when the element scrolls inside a container. Inside a 300px-tall panel on a 1000px-tall window, `t` never exceeds about 0.3, so the media stays visibly oversized for the whole scroll and never resolves.\n- Scaling from below 1 up to 1 instead. The media is sized to its frame, so any scale under 1 leaves a gap at the edges and the background shows through as a hairline border that grows and shrinks.\n- Reading `getBoundingClientRect()` after writing the transform in the same callback. That is a forced synchronous layout every frame, and it appears in a profile as a long purple bar attached to the rAF, not as anything obviously related to this component.\n- Running the rAF callback for every instance on the page. Ten of these in a long article means ten rect reads per frame for elements that are nowhere near the viewport; the IntersectionObserver gate is what keeps the cost proportional to what is on screen.\n- A per-frame lerp constant instead of `damp` when smoothing is enabled. On a 120Hz display the media reaches its target in half the scroll distance, so the effect is materially different on a MacBook Pro than on an external 60Hz monitor.\n- Omitting `overflow: hidden` on the frame. At 1.25 the media is 25% larger than its box and overlaps whatever is next to it, which is most visible as text from the following section being covered as you scroll.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No animation library. The whole component is one rAF subscription and one IntersectionObserver.\n- The transform is written imperatively rather than through React state, so no scroll frame causes a render.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the frame up from the bottom of the scroller: the media is visibly cropped and oversized as it appears, and reaches its natural size a little past halfway up — after which it does not move at all while you read it. Scroll back down and it re-expands. No hairline of background is ever visible inside the frame. With reduced motion on, the media is at natural size at all scroll positions.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "io-reveal-lite": "# Build: IntersectionObserver Reveal\n\nA wrapper that fades and lifts its child into place the first time it enters the scroll container. The transition is one inline string built from four numbers, so nothing is injected into the document and nothing has to load. Every instance that asks for the same root and the same observation box shares one IntersectionObserver, which is what makes it safe to put two hundred of these on a page.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne element, one fade-and-rise on entry, no stylesheet and no library — with a single observer shared across every instance.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The element renders at `opacity: 0` and `translate3d(0, 28px, 0)` with a transition string of `opacity 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1) {delay}s, transform 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1) {delay}s`. State flips both properties at once, so the browser interpolates on the compositor and the main thread does nothing after the flip.\n2. The observer is pooled in a two-level module map: root element first, then a `rootMargin|threshold` string. `null` is a legal Map key, so \"the viewport\" gets its own bucket without a sentinel. Each bucket holds a `Map<Element, handler>` and the observer callback dispatches by target — one callback for every reveal in a container.\n3. The pool is refcounted. Teardown deletes the handler and unobserves, then disconnects and drops the bucket only when the last target has left. Disconnecting per instance would tear the shared observer out from under every other reveal in the same container.\n4. The root is resolved as: an explicit `scroller` ref, else the first ancestor whose computed `overflow-x` or `overflow-y` is `auto`, `scroll` or `overlay`, else `null` for the viewport. `window` is never referenced, so the component behaves the same inside a 320 px preview card as on a full page.\n5. `threshold: 0.15` with `rootMargin: \"0px 0px -8% 0px\"` shrinks the observation box 8% up from the bottom, so an element must be genuinely inside the container before it fires rather than merely touching its edge.\n6. Staggering is the caller's job and costs one number: give siblings `delay={0}`, `0.08`, `0.16`. There is no group concept and no shared timeline, which is precisely what keeps the component this small.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 0.7`\n  Seconds. Long enough that a 28 px rise reads as a settle rather than a snap; past ~1s a fast scroller has already read the text while it is still moving.\n- `distance = 28`\n  Pixels of travel. Roughly one and a half line heights — the element stays inside its own block, so the reveal reads as the content arriving rather than as a card flying in from off-screen. Above ~60 px the motion starts to compete with the scroll itself.\n- `ease = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  Ease-out-quint. Most of the travel is spent in the first third, which is what makes a reveal feel like arrival. A symmetric curve on an entrance always reads as sluggish because the slow part happens while the element is still visibly out of place.\n- `threshold = 0.15`\n  15% of the element must be visible. At 0 a single intersecting pixel fires the reveal, so a tall element finishes animating before it is on screen. Above ~0.4 an element taller than the container can never satisfy it and never reveals at all.\n- `rootMargin = 0px 0px -8% 0px`\n  The negative bottom is the whole point: it pulls the trigger line 8% up from the container's bottom edge so the element is inside the box, not touching it. A positive bottom margin fires below the fold and the user scrolls onto content that has already finished.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction IoReveal(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; delay?: number; duration?: number; distance?: number; ease?: string; threshold?: number; rootMargin?: string; repeat?: boolean; as?: ElementType; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The revealed content.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container for the observer. Omit and the nearest scrollable ancestor is found by walking computed overflow.\n- `delay: number` — default `0`\n  Seconds of delay. Staggering siblings is done by passing 0, 0.08, 0.16.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.7`\n  Seconds of travel and fade.\n- `distance: number` — default `28`\n  Starting vertical offset in pixels. Positive rises into place.\n- `ease: string` — default `'cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)'`\n  Any CSS timing function.\n- `threshold: number` — default `0.15`\n  Fraction of the element that must be visible before it fires.\n- `rootMargin: string` — default `'0px 0px -8% 0px'`\n  Observation box inset. Part of the shared-observer pool key.\n- `repeat: boolean` — default `false`\n  Re-hide when the element leaves. Off by default, which also lets settled targets be unobserved.\n- `as: ElementType` — default `'div'`\n  Element to render, so the wrapper can be inline or a list item.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the rendered element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n   The shared observer pool.\n\n   Keyed by root element first, then by the option string. `null` is a legal\n   key in a Map, which is what lets \"the viewport\" share a bucket with itself\n   without a sentinel value. Each bucket refcounts its targets and the\n   observer is disconnected when the last one leaves, so a route change does\n   not leave observers watching detached nodes.\n   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */\ntype Handler = (entry: IntersectionObserverEntry) => void;\n\ninterface Bucket {\n  observer: IntersectionObserver;\n  handlers: Map<Element, Handler>;\n}\n\nconst pools = new Map<Element | null, Map<string, Bucket>>();\n\nfunction bucketFor(root: Element | null, rootMargin: string, threshold: number): Bucket {\n  let byOptions = pools.get(root);\n  if (!byOptions) {\n    byOptions = new Map<string, Bucket>();\n    pools.set(root, byOptions);\n  }\n  const key = `${rootMargin}|${threshold}`;\n  const existing = byOptions.get(key);\n  if (existing) return existing;\n\n  const handlers = new Map<Element, Handler>();\n  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(\n    (entries) => {\n      for (const entry of entries) handlers.get(entry.target)?.(entry);\n    },\n    { root, rootMargin, threshold },\n  );\n  const bucket: Bucket = { observer, handlers };\n  byOptions.set(key, bucket);\n  return bucket;\n}\n\nfunction release(root: Element | null, rootMargin: string, threshold: number): void {\n  const byOptions = pools.get(root);\n  if (!byOptions) return;\n  const key = `${rootMargin}|${threshold}`;\n  const bucket = byOptions.get(key);\n  if (!bucket || bucket.handlers.size > 0) return;\n  bucket.observer.disconnect();\n  byOptions.delete(key);\n  if (byOptions.size === 0) pools.delete(root);\n}\n\n/**\n * Nearest ancestor that actually scrolls. An `overflow: auto` element that has\n * not overflowed yet still counts: it is the container this reveal will live\n * inside once content arrives. Returning `null` means the viewport, which is\n * the value IntersectionObserver's `root` already uses for that.\n */\nfunction nearestScrollParent(node: Element | null): Element | null {\n  let el = node?.parentElement ?? null;\n  while (el) {\n    const { overflowY, overflowX } = getComputedStyle(el);\n    if (/(auto|scroll|overlay)/.test(overflowY + overflowX)) return el;\n    el = el.parentElement;\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n\nexport function IoReveal({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  delay = 0,\n  duration = DURATION,\n  distance = DISTANCE,\n  ease = EASE,\n  threshold = THRESHOLD,\n  rootMargin = ROOT_MARGIN,\n  repeat = false,\n  as,\n  className,\n}: IoRevealProps) {\n  const ref = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [revealed, setRevealed] = useState(false);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = ref.current;\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n    if (typeof IntersectionObserver === 'undefined') {\n      // A missing API degrades to visible content, never to a blank page.\n      setRevealed(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const root = scroller ? scroller.current : nearestScrollParent(el);\n    const bucket = bucketFor(root, rootMargin, threshold);\n\n    const handle: Handler = (entry) => {\n      if (entry.isIntersecting) {\n        setRevealed(true);\n        if (!repeat) {\n          bucket.handlers.delete(el);\n          bucket.observer.unobserve(el);\n        }\n      } else if (repeat) {\n        setRevealed(false);\n      }\n    };\n\n    bucket.handlers.set(el, handle);\n    bucket.observer.observe(el);\n\n    return () => {\n      bucket.handlers.delete(el);\n      bucket.observer.unobserve(el);\n      // Refcounted: the observer only dies with its last target. Disconnecting\n      // per instance would tear the shared observer out from under every other\n      // reveal in the same container.\n      release(root, rootMargin, threshold);\n    };\n  }, [scroller, rootMargin, threshold, repeat, reduced]);\n\n  /* Reduced motion is a different render, not a faster one: no transition\n     property is emitted at all, and the element is at its resolved end state\n     from the first paint. */\n  const style: CSSProperties = reduced\n    ? {}\n    : {\n        opacity: revealed ? 1 : 0,\n        transform: revealed ? 'translate3d(0, 0, 0)' : `translate3d(0, ${distance}px, 0)`,\n        // One string, two properties, one shared delay. Written inline on\n        // purpose: this variant ships no stylesheet, so there is nothing for a\n        // consumer's CSS to load and nothing to hoist.\n        transition: `opacity ${duration}s ${ease} ${delay}s, transform ${duration}s ${ease} ${delay}s`,\n        willChange: revealed ? undefined : 'opacity, transform',\n      };\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The effect deletes its handler from the shared bucket, unobserves its own element, then calls `release`, which disconnects the observer and drops the bucket only when no handlers remain. No listeners, no rAF, no timers. The pool itself holds no reference to an element once its handler is removed, so nothing keeps a detached node alive.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The reduced branch renders a different element, not a faster one: the style object is empty, so no `opacity`, no `transform` and no `transition` are emitted and the content is at its resolved final state on the first paint. No observer is created either, which also means no work is scheduled for a user who asked for none. `useReducedMotion` reads through `useSyncExternalStore`, so the value lands in the hydration commit and the animated variant is never painted.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Keying the shared observer on `rootMargin` and `threshold` but not on the root. The first instance to mount then decides which container everyone measures against, and every later instance inside a different scroll panel reports \"in view\" from the start — so an entire panel reveals on mount and never animates again. The root has to be part of the key.\n- Calling `observer.disconnect()` in a single instance's cleanup. With a shared observer that stops every other reveal in the same container dead, and the symptom appears only after a route change unmounts one card. Delete the handler, unobserve the one target, and disconnect only when the handler map is empty.\n- Constructing an observer per element. It works and it is invisible at ten elements; at two hundred the browser services two hundred separate intersection computations on every scroll of the container and the frame budget goes to bookkeeping.\n- One observer for the whole page with `root: null` when the content is inside an `overflow-y: auto` panel. Everything in the panel intersects the viewport from the start, so every reveal fires at mount.\n- Animating `top` or `margin-top` instead of `transform`. Both are layout properties: the reveal then triggers layout on every frame for every element currently animating, and a page with a dozen simultaneous reveals visibly stutters.\n- Leaving `will-change: opacity, transform` on after the reveal. Every settled element keeps its own compositor layer, and a long page accumulates hundreds of them until scrolling gets slower the further down you go. It is set only while the element is still hidden.\n- Treating reduced motion as `duration: 0`. That still emits a transition and still runs the state flip through the compositor. Here the reduced branch emits no `transition`, no `opacity` and no `transform` at all, so the element is simply in its final position from the first paint.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This variant ships zero CSS, which is the trade it makes: the hidden start state is an inline style on the first render, so a document that never executes scripts renders the content hidden. `css-var-stagger-reveal` is the sibling that pays for a hoisted stylesheet and gets a `@media (scripting: none)` escape hatch in return. Choose this one for a component library where injecting global CSS is unwelcome; choose that one for a site where a designer will retune the choreography and no-JS has to look right.\n- Compared to a GSAP reveal system, this cannot scrub, cannot be measured against a `containerAnimation`, and has no timeline you can seek. It is deliberately the floor of the feature set: entry, once, with a delay.\n- `as` lets the wrapper be a `span` inside a paragraph or an `li` inside a list, so the reveal does not force an invalid nesting or an extra block-level box into the layout.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the container and each element rises 28 px and fades in as it comes properly into view, not as it touches the bottom edge. Give three siblings delays of 0, 0.08 and 0.16 and they arrive in sequence. Mount fifty of them and the browser reports one IntersectionObserver, not fifty. Unmount one card and the rest keep revealing. With reduced motion on, every element is already in place and inspecting it shows no inline `transition` at all.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "kanban-card": "# Build: Kanban Card\n\nThe ticket card from the project board: a \"DS-38 › Agent handoff\" breadcrumb with overlapping assignee avatars on the right, a row with the priority pill and folder-tagged project chips, the title in medium weight, an optional two-line description and a footer with the \"Since 14 Sep\" line, comment and attachment counts and a tinted due chip. The whole card opens through its title button, whose hit area is stretched over the card, and it carries `source` and `overlay` looks so a board can drag it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nProject board ticket: key and area, assignee stack, priority and project tags, title, description, counts and due date.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The card is an `<article>` labelled by its heading (`aria-labelledby` → the `useId` title id). Layout is one flex column with `p-3` and fixed gaps: breadcrumb row (`min-h-5`), `mt-2` tag row, `mt-2` title, `mt-1` description clamped with `line-clamp-2`, `mt-1.5` footer.\n2. When `onPress` is set, the title renders as a `<button data-kanban-press data-kanban-focus>` whose `::after` is `absolute inset-0 rounded-xl`, so a click anywhere on the card lands on the button. The avatar stack and the `actions` slot are `relative z-10`, which lifts them over that overlay and keeps them separately hoverable and focusable without nesting interactive elements. Focus shows as a 2px focus-ring on the overlay, so the whole card outlines.\n3. The breadcrumb right side is an `AvatarStack` at size `xs` with `max` 2 by default (the stack only collapses when two or more would hide, so three people show as three discs). The tag row is a `PriorityBadge` with `showIcon={false}` (the text-only pill the board uses) followed by bordered chips with a 14px `RiFolderLine`.\n4. Footer stats are text plus a 14px icon; each number carries an `sr-only` noun (\"4 comments\", \"Overdue, was due 12 Sep\") so the icons can stay `aria-hidden`. `dueState` picks no tint, `bg-status-orange-background` (soon) or `bg-status-rose-background` (overdue).\n5. `dragState` swaps the visual role during a board drag: `source` drops to 40% opacity in place, `overlay` tilts 2° and scales to 1.02 with `shadow-xl`, removes the press button, actions and tooltips, and sets `aria-hidden` so the ghost copy never doubles up in the accessibility tree.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card padding / radius = p-3 (12px), rounded-xl (12px)`\n  Measured off the board: text starts 12px in from a 12px-radius white card that sits 6px inside the 20px-radius column.\n- `row gaps = mt-2 tags, mt-2 title, mt-1 description, mt-1.5 footer`\n  Reproduces the 146px card height of the reference with a two-line title.\n- `maxAssignees = 2`\n  Cards are ~260px wide; two 20px discs plus \"+N\" leave room for a long area name before it truncates.\n- `overlay transform = rotate-2 scale-[1.02] shadow-xl`\n  Enough lift to read as \"picked up\" without the ghost covering the drop slot beneath it.\n- `source opacity = 0.4`\n  The origin stays visible as a placeholder so the user can see where a cancelled drag will return.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction KanbanCard(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>, \"title\" | \"children\"> & { code: string; area?: string; title: string; description?: string; priority?: Priority; tags?: readonly string[]; assignees?: readonly AvatarStackPerson[]; maxAssignees?: number; comments?: number; attachments?: number; due?: string; dueState?: \"upcoming\" | \"soon\" | \"overdue\"; meta?: ReactNode; onPress?: () => void; isSelected?: boolean; dragState?: \"idle\" | \"source\" | \"overlay\"; actions?: ReactNode; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5; ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `code: string`\n  Ticket key shown first in the breadcrumb, e.g. \"DS-38\".\n- `area: string`\n  Epic or area after the chevron; truncates.\n- `title: string`\n  Ticket title; also the accessible name of the card and its press button.\n- `description: string`\n  Muted text under the title, clamped to two lines.\n- `priority: \"urgent\" | \"high\" | \"medium\" | \"low\"`\n  Shows a text-only PriorityBadge.\n- `tags: readonly string[]` — default `[]`\n  Project chips with a folder glyph.\n- `assignees: readonly AvatarStackPerson[]` — default `[]`\n  Avatar stack on the breadcrumb row, with name tooltips.\n- `maxAssignees: number` — default `2`\n  Discs before the rest collapse into \"+N\".\n- `comments / attachments: number`\n  Footer counts with chat and paperclip glyphs.\n- `due: string`\n  Pre-formatted due label for the calendar chip.\n- `dueState: \"upcoming\" | \"soon\" | \"overdue\"` — default `\"upcoming\"`\n  Tints the due chip (none, orange, rose) and words its sr-only prefix.\n- `meta: ReactNode`\n  Age line at the start of the footer, e.g. \"Since 14 Sep\".\n- `onPress: () => void`\n  Makes the card pressable through a stretched title button.\n- `isSelected: boolean` — default `false`\n  Focus-ring outline and `aria-current` for the open ticket.\n- `dragState: \"idle\" | \"source\" | \"overlay\"` — default `\"idle\"`\n  Dimmed origin or lifted, aria-hidden ghost during a drag.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Trailing footer controls, layered above the press overlay.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4 | 5` — default `3`\n  Heading element used for the title.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function KanbanCard({\n  code,\n  area,\n  title,\n  description,\n  priority,\n  tags = [],\n  assignees = [],\n  maxAssignees = 2,\n  comments,\n  attachments,\n  due,\n  dueState = \"upcoming\",\n  meta,\n  onPress,\n  isSelected = false,\n  dragState = \"idle\",\n  actions,\n  headingLevel = 3,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: KanbanCardProps) {\n  const titleId = useId();\n  const Heading = `h${headingLevel}` as const;\n  const hasStats = comments !== undefined || attachments !== undefined || due !== undefined;\n  // The ghost copy under the pointer is decoration: keep it out of the tab order and the a11y tree.\n  const isOverlay = dragState === \"overlay\";\n\n  return (\n    <article\n      ref={ref}\n      aria-labelledby={isOverlay ? undefined : titleId}\n      aria-hidden={isOverlay || undefined}\n      data-selected={isSelected || undefined}\n      data-drag-state={dragState}\n      className={cx(\n        styles.card,\n        styles.drag[dragState],\n        onPress && dragState === \"idle\" && \"hover:shadow-sm\",\n        isSelected && styles.selected,\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex min-h-5 items-center gap-2\">\n        <p className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-center gap-0.5 text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">\n          <span className=\"shrink-0\">{code}</span>\n          {area && (\n            <>\n              <RiArrowRightSLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-tertiary rtl:rotate-180\" />\n              <span className=\"truncate\">{area}</span>\n            </>\n          )}\n        </p>\n        {assignees.length > 0 && (\n          <AvatarStack\n            people={assignees}\n            max={maxAssignees}\n            size=\"xs\"\n            tooltips={!isOverlay}\n            className=\"relative z-10\"\n          />\n        )}\n      </div>\n\n      {(priority || tags.length > 0) && (\n        <div className=\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1.5\">\n          {priority && <PriorityBadge priority={priority} showIcon={false} />}\n          {tags.map((tag) => (\n            <span key={tag} className={styles.tag}>\n              <RiFolderLine aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-tertiary\" />\n              <span className=\"truncate\">{tag}</span>\n            </span>\n          ))}\n        </div>\n      )}\n\n      <Heading id={titleId} className=\"mt-2 text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n        {onPress && !isOverlay ? (\n          <button\n            type=\"button\"\n            data-kanban-press\n            data-kanban-focus\n            aria-current={isSelected ? \"true\" : undefined}\n            onClick={onPress}\n            className={styles.press}\n          >\n            {title}\n          </button>\n        ) : (\n          title\n        )}\n      </Heading>\n\n      {description && <p className=\"mt-1 line-clamp-2 text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\">{description}</p>}\n\n      {(meta || hasStats || actions) && (\n        <div className=\"mt-1.5 flex min-h-6 flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1\">\n          {meta && <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{meta}</span>}\n          {hasStats && (\n            <span className={cx(\"flex items-center gap-2.5\", !meta && \"flex-1\")}>\n              {comments !== undefined && (\n                <span className={styles.stat}>\n                  <RiChat3Line aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5 text-foreground-icon-tertiary\" />\n                  {comments}\n                  <span className=\"sr-only\">{comments === 1 ? \" comment\" : \" comments\"}</span>\n                </span>\n              )}\n              {attachments !== undefined && (\n                <span className={styles.stat}>\n                  <RiAttachment2 aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5 text-foreground-icon-tertiary\" />\n                  {attachments}\n                  <span className=\"sr-only\">{attachments === 1 ? \" attachment\" : \" attachments\"}</span>\n                </span>\n              )}\n              {due !== undefined && (\n                <span className={cx(styles.stat, styles.due[dueState])}>\n                  <RiCalendarLine aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5\" />\n                  <span className=\"sr-only\">{dueState === \"overdue\" ? \"Overdue, was due \" : \"Due \"}</span>\n                  {due}\n                </span>\n              )}\n            </span>\n          )}\n          {actions && !isOverlay && <span className=\"relative z-10 ms-auto flex items-center\">{actions}</span>}\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </article>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: the card has no effects, timers or listeners. Avatar tooltips are React Aria overlays that unmount with the card.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only the hover shadow, opacity and ghost transform transition; all of it is dropped with `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and the overlay keeps its shadow but loses the tilt and scale (`motion-reduce:rotate-0 motion-reduce:scale-100`).\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Wrapping the whole card in a `<button>`: the avatar tooltips and any menu in `actions` become nested interactive content. Use the stretched title button and keep extra controls `relative z-10`.\n- Forgetting `aria-hidden` on the drag ghost. Two cards with the same name in the tree confuse screen readers and the flow tests that query by role.\n- Colouring the due date only. The `sr-only` \"Overdue, was due\" prefix carries the meaning for people who cannot see the tint.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The card owns no state; selection, drag state and actions come from the board around it. Kanban Column passes `dragState` through its item render prop.\n- Photos work through `AvatarStackPerson.src`; the demo uses initials so it stays offline.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo shows three cards: DS-38 with two avatars, a blue \"Low\" pill and a \"vibl\" folder chip; FE-79 with a description, \"4\" comments, \"2\" attachments and an orange \"18 Sep\" due chip; DS-31 with an \"Urgent\" pill, two avatars plus \"+2\" and a rose overdue chip. FE-79 starts outlined. Clicking DS-31 anywhere moves the outline to it and the caption reads \"Open: DS-31\"; clicking it again clears the selection. Hovering an avatar shows the person's name. Tab reaches each card's title button and Enter selects it. Dark mode keeps white text on neutral-800 cards on a neutral-900 panel.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "kanban-column": "# Build: Kanban Column\n\nThe project-board column: a 20px-radius grey panel with \"Backlog 3/8\" in the header, a ghost \"…\" menu and \"+\" button, and a stack of ticket cards. `KanbanBoard` wraps the columns in a horizontal scroller and owns one move model with three inputs: pointer drag with a ghost card and an opening drop slot, Alt + arrow keys on a focused card, and a per-card \"Move\" menu. Every route ends in a single `onMove({ itemId, from, to, index })`, which `moveItem` applies to a column map.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBoard column with name, WIP count and add button; cards move by pointer drag, Alt + arrow keys or a per-card move menu.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A press on a `KanbanItem` row (mouse or pen, primary button, not on a nested control) records the pointer and attaches window `pointermove` / `pointerup` / `pointercancel` / `keydown` / `blur` listeners. Nothing renders until the pointer has travelled 4px, so a plain click still opens the card.\n2. Once the drag starts, the row renders its card as `dragState: \"source\"` (dimmed in place) and portals a `dragState: \"overlay\"` copy into a fixed, `pointer-events-none`, `inert` ghost. The ghost is moved with `translate3d` written straight to its style from the pointer events, so following the pointer never re-renders React.\n3. Hit testing runs `document.elementFromPoint` (the ghost is transparent to it), takes the closest `[data-kanban-column]` and counts the items whose vertical midline is above the pointer. The dragged card still counts, so its own spot and the one after it are no-ops and draw no slot. The column splices a dashed `<li>` the height of the card into its children at that index. Items below the slot shift down by its height, which gives built-in hysteresis: the pointer must cross back over the unshifted midline before the slot moves up again.\n4. Release re-runs the hit test at the `pointerup` point, so a single move then release with no frame in between (how automated drags behave) still lands. A drop below the origin in the same column subtracts one from the index. Escape, `pointercancel` or window blur end the session without a move, and the click that follows a drag is swallowed in the capture phase.\n5. A `requestAnimationFrame` loop scrolls the board sideways while the pointer sits within 56px of its left or right edge, at up to 18px per frame scaled by depth. It re-tests the hit after each scroll step.\n6. Keyboard and menu moves read the live column order and counts from the DOM (`data-kanban-column`, `data-kanban-title`), commit the move, then stash `itemId + column` in a ref. The row that mounts there pulls focus onto its `[data-kanban-focus]` element, and a `role=\"status\"` region announces \"FE-79 … moved to Review, position 2 of 2.\"\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DRAG_THRESHOLD = 4px`\n  Below this a press stays a click; above it, a drag. Small enough that a quick flick still picks the card up.\n- `EDGE / MAX_SCROLL_STEP = 56px / 18px per frame`\n  A band about two cursor widths deep; speed grows with depth so a nudge creeps and a push races.\n- `column width = w-68 (272px), p-1.5, gap-1.5`\n  Matches the board: 260px cards sit 6px inside a 20px-radius panel with 6px between cards.\n- `drop slot = dragged card height, dashed border-button-hover, tertiary 60%`\n  Same height as the card so the column does not jump on drop; dashed so it reads as an empty place, not a card.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction KanbanBoard(props: HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement> & { onMove: (move: KanbanMove) => void; instructions?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element; function KanbanColumn(props: { id: string; title: string; count?: number; limit?: number; onAdd?: () => void; addLabel?: string; menu?: ReactNode; empty?: ReactNode; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; children?: ReactNode }): JSX.Element; function KanbanItem(props: { id: string; label: string; children: ReactNode | ((state: { dragState: \"idle\" | \"source\" | \"overlay\" }) => ReactNode) }): JSX.Element; function KanbanMoveMenu(props: { className?: string }): JSX.Element | null; function moveItem<T extends { id: string }>(columns: Record<string, readonly T[]>, move: KanbanMove): Record<string, T[]>; interface KanbanMove { itemId: string; from: string; to: string; index: number }\n```\n\n- `onMove (KanbanBoard): (move: KanbanMove) => void`\n  Fires once per finished move from drag, keyboard or menu. `index` is the position in `to` after the card left `from`.\n- `instructions (KanbanBoard): string`\n  Screen-reader help linked to the board with aria-describedby.\n- `id (KanbanColumn): string`\n  Column key used in `KanbanMove.from` / `to`.\n- `title (KanbanColumn): string`\n  Header name; also used in announcements and the move menu.\n- `count / limit (KanbanColumn): number`\n  Header shows \"count/limit\"; count defaults to the item count and turns red past the limit.\n- `onAdd / addLabel (KanbanColumn): () => void / string`\n  Shows the \"+\" button and names it (default \"Add card to {title}\").\n- `menu (KanbanColumn): ReactNode`\n  Extra header controls before \"+\"; style triggers with `kanbanIconButtonClass`.\n- `empty (KanbanColumn): ReactNode`\n  Replaces the dashed \"No cards\" placeholder.\n- `id / label (KanbanItem): string`\n  Card key, and the short name used in announcements and the menu.\n- `children (KanbanItem): ReactNode | ({ dragState }) => ReactNode`\n  The card; the function form receives \"idle\", \"source\" or \"overlay\".\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function KanbanBoard({\n  onMove,\n  instructions = \"Drag a card to another column, or focus a card and press Alt with an arrow key: left and right change column, up and down change position. Each card also has a move menu.\",\n  className,\n  children,\n  ref,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Board\",\n  ...props\n}: KanbanBoardProps) {\n  const boardRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);\n  const ghostRef = useRef<HTMLElement | null>(null);\n  const session = useRef<Session | null>(null);\n  const onMoveRef = useRef(onMove);\n  const pendingFocus = useRef<string | null>(null);\n  const suppressClick = useRef(false);\n  const [drag, setDrag] = useState<DragView | null>(null);\n  const [lastMoved, setLastMoved] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [announcement, setAnnouncement] = useState(\"\");\n  const hintId = useId();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    onMoveRef.current = onMove;\n  });\n  // Unmounting mid-drag must not leave window listeners or a frame loop behind.\n  useEffect(() => () => session.current?.detach(), []);\n\n  const setRefs = useCallback(\n    (element: HTMLDivElement | null) => {\n      boardRef.current = element;\n      if (typeof ref === \"function\") ref(element);\n      else if (ref) ref.current = element;\n    },\n    [ref],\n  );\n\n  const locate = (itemId: string): Location | null => {\n    const columns = Array.from(boardRef.current?.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(\"[data-kanban-column]\") ?? []).map((el) => ({\n      id: el.dataset.kanbanColumn ?? \"\",\n      title: el.dataset.kanbanTitle ?? \"\",\n      items: Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(\"[data-kanban-item]\")),\n    }));\n    for (const [column, entry] of columns.entries()) {\n      const index = entry.items.findIndex((el) => el.dataset.kanbanItem === itemId);\n      if (index >= 0) return { columns: columns.map(({ id, title, items }) => ({ id, title, count: items.length })), column, index };\n    }\n    return null;\n  };\n\n  const commit = (move: KanbanMove, label: string, focus: boolean) => {\n    const dest = locate(move.itemId)?.columns.find((column) => column.id === move.to);\n    onMoveRef.current(move);\n    if (focus) pendingFocus.current = move.itemId + FOCUS_KEY + move.to;\n    setLastMoved(move.itemId);\n    if (dest) {\n      const total = dest.count + (move.from === move.to ? 0 : 1);\n      setAnnouncement(`${label} moved to ${dest.title}, position ${move.index + 1} of ${total}.`);\n    }\n  };\n\n  const step = (itemId: string, label: string, direction: Direction) => {\n    const location = locate(itemId);\n    const here = location?.columns[location.column];\n    if (!location || !here) return;\n    let to = here;\n    let index = location.index;\n    if (direction === \"up\") index -= 1;\n    else if (direction === \"down\") index += 1;\n    else {\n      const target = location.columns[location.column + (direction === \"next\" ? 1 : -1)];\n      if (!target) return;\n      to = target;\n      index = Math.min(location.index, target.count);\n    }\n    if (to === here && (index < 0 || index >= here.count)) return;\n    commit({ itemId, from: here.id, to: to.id, index }, label, true);\n  };\n\n  /** Column under the point and the insertion index from item midlines (the dragged card still counts). */\n  const hitTest = (x: number, y: number) => {\n    const column = document.elementFromPoint(x, y)?.closest<HTMLElement>(\"[data-kanban-column]\");\n    if (!column || !boardRef.current?.contains(column)) return null;\n    let index = 0;\n    for (const item of column.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(\"[data-kanban-item]\")) {\n      const rect = item.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (y < rect.top + rect.height / 2) break;\n      index += 1;\n    }\n    return { column: column.dataset.kanbanColumn ?? \"\", index };\n  };\n\n  const placeGhost = () => {\n    const s = session.current;\n    if (s && ghostRef.current) ghostRef.current.style.transform = `translate3d(${s.x - s.offsetX}px, ${s.y - s.offsetY}px, 0)`;\n  };\n\n  const track = () => {\n    const s = session.current;\n    if (!s?.started) return;\n    const hit = hitTest(s.x, s.y);\n    const isNoop = hit !== null && hit.column === s.from && (hit.index === s.fromIndex || hit.index === s.fromIndex + 1);\n    const slot = hit && !isNoop ? hit : null;\n    const key = slot ? `${slot.column}:${slot.index}` : \"\";\n    if (key === s.slotKey) return;\n    s.slotKey = key;\n    setDrag((view) => view && { ...view, slot });\n  };\n\n  const autoScroll = () => {\n    const s = session.current;\n    const board = boardRef.current;\n    if (!s?.started || !board) return;\n    const rect = board.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const depth = s.x < rect.left + EDGE ? s.x - (rect.left + EDGE) : s.x > rect.right - EDGE ? s.x - (rect.right - EDGE) : 0;\n    if (depth !== 0) {\n      const before = board.scrollLeft;\n      board.scrollLeft += Math.sign(depth) * Math.max(1, Math.min(MAX_SCROLL_STEP, Math.round(Math.abs(depth) / 3)));\n      if (board.scrollLeft !== before) track();\n    }\n    s.frame = requestAnimationFrame(autoScroll);\n  };\n\n  const press = (event: ReactPointerEvent<HTMLElement>, itemId: string, label: string) => {\n    suppressClick.current = false;\n    const row = event.currentTarget;\n    const target = event.target as Element;\n    if (event.button !== 0 || !event.isPrimary || event.pointerType === \"touch\" || !row.contains(target)) return;\n    const blocker = target.closest(NO_DRAG);\n    if (blocker && row.contains(blocker)) return;\n    const location = locate(itemId);\n    const from = location?.columns[location.column];\n    if (!location || !from) return;\n    session.current?.detach();\n\n    const rect = row.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const end = () => {\n      const s = session.current;\n      if (!s) return;\n      s.detach();\n      session.current = null;\n      setDrag(null);\n    };\n    const onPointerMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const s = session.current;\n      if (!s || e.pointerId !== s.pointerId) return;\n      s.x = e.clientX;\n      s.y = e.clientY;\n      if (!s.started) {\n        if (Math.hypot(s.x - s.startX, s.y - s.startY) < DRAG_THRESHOLD) return;\n        s.started = true;\n        setDrag({ itemId: s.itemId, width: s.width, height: s.height, slot: null });\n        s.frame = requestAnimationFrame(autoScroll);\n      }\n      placeGhost();\n      track();\n    };\n    const onPointerUp = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const s = session.current;\n      if (!s || e.pointerId !== s.pointerId) return;\n      end();\n      if (!s.started) return;\n      suppressClick.current = true;\n      // Re-test at the release point so a single move + release (no frame in between) still lands.\n      const hit = hitTest(e.clientX, e.clientY);\n      if (!hit) {\n        setAnnouncement(`${s.label} returned to ${from.title}.`);\n        return;\n      }\n      const index = hit.column === s.from && hit.index > s.fromIndex ? hit.index - 1 : hit.index;\n      if (hit.column === s.from && index === s.fromIndex) return;\n      commit({ itemId: s.itemId, from: s.from, to: hit.column, index }, s.label, false);\n    };\n    const cancel = () => {\n      const s = session.current;\n      end();\n      if (!s?.started) return;\n      suppressClick.current = true;\n      setAnnouncement(`Move cancelled. ${s.label} stays in ${from.title}.`);\n    };\n    const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {\n      if (e.key !== \"Escape\" || !session.current?.started) return;\n      e.preventDefault();\n      e.stopPropagation();\n      cancel();\n    };\n    const onPointerCancel = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      if (e.pointerId === session.current?.pointerId) cancel();\n    };\n\n    window.addEventListener(\"pointermove\", onPointerMove);\n    window.addEventListener(\"pointerup\", onPointerUp);\n    window.addEventListener(\"pointercancel\", onPointerCancel);\n    window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown, true);\n    window.addEventListener(\"blur\", cancel);\n    const next: Session = {\n      pointerId: event.pointerId,\n      itemId,\n      label,\n      from: from.id,\n      fromIndex: location.index,\n      startX: event.clientX,\n      startY: event.clientY,\n      x: event.clientX,\n      y: event.clientY,\n      offsetX: event.clientX - rect.left,\n      offsetY: event.clientY - rect.top,\n      width: rect.width,\n      height: rect.height,\n      started: false,\n      slotKey: \"\",\n      frame: 0,\n      detach: () => {\n        cancelAnimationFrame(next.frame);\n        window.removeEventListener(\"pointermove\", onPointerMove);\n        window.removeEventListener(\"pointerup\", onPointerUp);\n        window.removeEventListener(\"pointercancel\", onPointerCancel);\n        window.removeEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown, true);\n        window.removeEventListener(\"blur\", cancel);\n      },\n    };\n    session.current = next;\n  };\n\n  // The ghost mounts a render after the drag starts; put it under the pointer before it paints.\n  const ghost = useCallback((element: HTMLElement | null) => {\n    ghostRef.current = element;\n    const s = session.current;\n    if (s && element) element.style.transform = `translate3d(${s.x - s.offsetX}px, ${s.y - s.offsetY}px, 0)`;\n  }, []);\n\n  return (\n    <BoardContext.Provider\n      value={{ drag, lastMoved, pendingFocus, suppressClick, ghost, press, step, locate, commit }}\n    >\n      <div\n        ref={setRefs}\n        role=\"region\"\n        aria-label={ariaLabel}\n        aria-describedby={hintId}\n        data-dragging={drag ? \"\" : undefined}\n        className={cx(\"flex w-full items-start gap-2 overflow-x-auto overscroll-x-contain\", drag && \"cursor-grabbing\", className)}\n        {...props}\n      >\n        {children}\n        <p id={hintId} hidden>\n          {instructions}\n        </p>\n        <div role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"sr-only\">\n          {announcement}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </BoardContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Window listeners and the edge-scroll `requestAnimationFrame` belong to the drag session. They are removed on release, Escape, pointercancel and window blur, and by the board's unmount effect if it unmounts mid-drag. The ghost is a portal owned by the dragged row, so it unmounts with the session.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only decoration is animated: the moved card's 200ms fade and scale-in (`starting:`) and the menu trigger's fade are dropped with `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and Kanban Card drops the ghost's tilt and scale. The ghost still follows the pointer and the board still edge-scrolls, because both are direct manipulation.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Starting the drag on `pointerdown`. Every click then flashes a ghost; wait for movement past a threshold.\n- Hit testing against the ghost. Without `pointer-events-none` the ghost is always the element under the pointer and no column is found.\n- Committing from the last rendered slot instead of re-testing on release. An automated drag sends one move and a release in the same frame, before the slot state has rendered.\n- Alt + Left / Right is browser Back / Forward on Windows and Linux. The handler calls `preventDefault`, and the move menu stays available as the route that never collides with a shortcut.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Touch pointers are left to scroll the board, since dragging would hijack the pan. On touch screens the move menu trigger is always visible (`pointer-coarse:opacity-100`), and that menu is how cards move.\n- Give `KanbanBoard` a height (the demo uses `h-125`) and every column caps at it with `max-h-full`; the card list then scrolls on its own (`min-h-0 overflow-y-auto`), so a long Backlog never pushes the headers off screen. Without a height the columns grow with their cards.\n- Items must be direct children of `KanbanColumn` (an array from `.map` is fine) because the column splices the drop slot into `Children.toArray(children)`.\n- The demo uses Kanban Card for the cards, passes `dragState` through, and puts the move menu in the card's `actions` slot.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFour columns (Backlog 3/8, To do 2/5, In progress 2/4, Review 1/3) fit at desktop width and scroll sideways at 360px. Dragging DS-31 onto Review shows a dashed slot, and on release Review reads 2/3, the log says \"Moved DS-31 to Review\", and Escape mid-drag cancels. Focusing FE-79 and pressing Alt+ArrowRight moves it to Review with focus kept on the card. Its \"…\" Move menu lists the other columns, and choosing \"Backlog\" moves it there; Backlog now holds four cards and scrolls inside its column while every header stays in view. \"+\" prepends a new ticket. A plain click on a card still toggles its outline.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "kbd": "# Build: Kbd\n\nA one-element component: a native `<kbd>` styled as a small neutral pill with 12px semibold type. It is the shortcut hint the kit puts on the sidebar's Quick Search row (`⌘L`) and on command-palette rows, and it takes any `<kbd>` attribute through, so the caller decides the label and the accessible text.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA fully rounded keyboard-shortcut pill on a semantic <kbd>, 20px tall, for search triggers, menus and command rows.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Render a native `<kbd>` element, not a `<span>`, so assistive tech and copy-paste both know the text is a key. All `HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>` spread onto it and React 19's `ref` arrives as an ordinary prop.\n2. Shape it with `inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full px-1 py-0.5`: 4px side padding, 2px top and bottom, and a full radius so a one-glyph key reads as a lozenge and a chord such as `⌘K` stretches into a pill.\n3. Set type with the kit's `text-caption-1-semibold` (12px, 16px line height, weight 600) plus `font-sans`, `tracking-normal` and `whitespace-nowrap`, so the pill is 20px tall and a chord never wraps between its keys.\n4. Colour it with two semantic tokens, `bg-kbd-background` and `text-kbd-foreground`. Light maps them to neutral-300 / neutral-500, dark to neutral-700 / neutral-400, so the hint stays quiet on either theme without a `dark:` class.\n5. Merge the caller's `className` last through `cx` (the kit's `tailwind-merge` instance), so a consumer `px-1.5` replaces `px-1` instead of both landing in the class list.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `padding = px-1 py-0.5 (4px / 2px)`\n  From the Figma quick-search shortcut (node 3731:2955). With the 16px line height this gives a 20px pill, short enough to sit inside a 36px search row or a 32px menu item without stretching it.\n- `type style = text-caption-1-semibold: 12px / 16px, weight 600`\n  Semibold keeps glyphs such as ⌘ and ⇧ legible at 12px on a low-contrast fill; regular weight blurs into the background.\n- `colours = light neutral-300 on neutral-500 text; dark neutral-700 on neutral-400 text`\n  A hint must sit below the label it annotates. Both pairs are deliberately low contrast against the surface so the shortcut reads as secondary information.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ninterface KbdProps extends HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement> { ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }\nfunction Kbd(props: KbdProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The key or chord to show, e.g. `⌘K` or `Esc`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge`, so conflicting utilities replace the defaults.\n- `ref: Ref<HTMLElement>`\n  Forwarded to the `<kbd>` element (React 19 ref-as-prop).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Kbd({ className, ref, ...props }: KbdProps) {\n  return (\n    <kbd\n      ref={ref}\n      className={cx(\n        \"inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-kbd-background px-1 py-0.5 font-sans text-caption-1-semibold tracking-normal whitespace-nowrap text-kbd-foreground\",\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    />\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates. The pill is static markup, so it renders identically under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not swap `<kbd>` for a `<span>`. The element is the semantics: screen readers and reader modes treat it as keyboard input.\n- Do not hard-code neutral colours on the pill. Use `bg-kbd-background` / `text-kbd-foreground`, or the dark theme gets a light chip on a dark row.\n- Do not drop `whitespace-nowrap`. In a narrow sidebar a chord like `⌘ ⇧ P` would wrap one key per line.\n- Do not rely on a bare symbol for meaning when it matters. `⌘` is announced inconsistently, so add `aria-label` (as the command palette does with \"Enter\") where the key name must be spoken.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Used inside the kit by `dashboard-sidebar` (the `⌘L` Quick Search hint) and `command-palette` (the Enter key and per-row shortcuts).\n- One `Kbd` per chord is the kit's convention (`<Kbd>⌘L</Kbd>`). Render several pills side by side only for sequences such as G then I.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA `<Kbd>⌘K</Kbd>` renders as a `<kbd>` element 20px tall with 4px side padding, a full radius and 12px semibold text, using neutral-300 fill and neutral-500 text in light mode and neutral-700 / neutral-400 in dark mode. A long chord stays on one line, and a `className` such as `px-2` replaces the default horizontal padding.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "keyframe-param-object": "# Build: Single Param Object Timeline\n\nA scroll-driven scene written as data instead of as tweens. One mutable object holds every animated quantity, GSAP tweens the object rather than the DOM, and a single onUpdate writes one transform string and two opacities. The story is then an array of rows — section id, point through the section, params to move to — each anchored to where its section actually sits after layout.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nOne plain object holds every animated value; the scroll story is a table of keyframes anchored to real sections.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every animated quantity lives in one object: `{ scale, spin, driftX, driftY, glow, dissolve }`. No element is a tween target; GSAP tweens the plain object and one `onUpdate` renders it, so the number of tweens is the number of story beats rather than the number of moving parts.\n2. Each keyframe is anchored to a real section: `anchor = clamp(0, 1, (el.offsetTop + el.offsetHeight * t - view / 2) / total)`, where `total = container.offsetHeight - view` is the scroll distance the trigger covers.\n3. That expression reads as \"when the point `t` through this section reaches the middle of the viewport\". `t = 0.5` is the section's own middle, so the beat fires when the reader is looking at the copy it belongs to, at any column width.\n4. Rows are sorted by anchor and each is given `duration = nextAnchor - thisAnchor`, so the scene is continuously in motion between beats instead of arriving early and sitting still until the next anchor.\n5. `ease: \"none\"` on every row. Under a scrub the ease warps timeline time, not screen time, so an eased row reaches its params before or after the section it is anchored to — which defeats the anchoring.\n6. A `useResizeObserver` on the container re-runs the whole build when its height changes, because every anchor is a function of `offsetTop` and `offsetTop` moves whenever a line wraps.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `scrub = 0.8`\n  Just under a second of catch-up. Enough that the scene continues to resolve after the wheel stops, which is what makes it read as a film rather than a slider; `true` welds it to the wheel and every scroll jitter shows up in the mark.\n- `at = 0.5`\n  Default point through a section at which its keyframe lands. 0.5 puts the beat on the section's midpoint crossing the middle of the scroller. Values near 0 fire while the section is still entering, which reads as the scene reacting to copy the reader has not got to yet.\n- `REST.glow = 0.35`\n  The scene's idle state, not zero. The stage has to be worth looking at before the first keyframe and after the last one; starting at 0 means the reader's first impression of the component is an empty box.\n- `total floor = 1`\n  Guards `container.offsetHeight - view` against 0 or negative, which happens for one frame during layout and on a short container. Without it every anchor divides by zero, becomes Infinity, clamps to 1, and the whole story collapses onto the last frame.\n- `span floor = 0.001`\n  Two keyframes anchored to the same point would otherwise get duration 0. GSAP accepts it, but the row then applies as an instant jump, so a carefully anchored beat becomes a snap.\n- `section gap = 45vh`\n  Spacing between story sections, in scroller heights. Below about 30vh two anchors land close enough that their spans overlap heavily and the beats blur together; well above it the scene is static for long stretches.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction KeyframeParamObject(props: { sections: { id: string; label: string; title: string; body?: string; at?: number; to: Partial<StageParams> }[]; scrub?: number; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `sections: KeyframeSection[]`\n  The story. Each row names a section, an optional point through it, and the params to move to.\n- `sections[].at: number` — default `0.5`\n  Point through the section, 0-1, at which its keyframe lands.\n- `sections[].to: Partial<StageParams>`\n  Params this beat moves to. Anything omitted holds its current value.\n- `scrub: number` — default `0.8`\n  Seconds of catch-up behind the scroll position.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function KeyframeParamObject({\n  sections,\n  scrub = 0.8,\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: KeyframeParamObjectProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const mark = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const halo = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Rebuild the anchor table whenever the column reflows: every anchor is a\n  // function of offsetTop, and offsetTop changes when a line wraps.\n  const { height } = useResizeObserver(root);\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const container = root.current;\n      const markEl = mark.current;\n      const haloEl = halo.current;\n      if (!container || !markEl || !haloEl) return;\n\n      // The one mutable object. Cloned per build so a rebuild starts clean\n      // rather than inheriting wherever the last scrub left it.\n      const params: StageParams = { ...REST };\n\n      const render = () => {\n        markEl.style.transform =\n          `translate3d(${params.driftX}%, ${params.driftY}%, 0) ` +\n          `rotate(${params.spin * 360}deg) scale(${params.scale})`;\n        markEl.style.opacity = String(Math.max(0, 1 - params.dissolve));\n        haloEl.style.opacity = String(params.glow);\n        haloEl.style.transform = `scale(${1 + params.glow * 0.6})`;\n      };\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        // Land on the last keyframe: the end of the story, statically.\n        for (const section of sections) Object.assign(params, section.to);\n        render();\n        return;\n      }\n\n      const scroller = scrollerRef?.current ?? null;\n      const view = scroller ? scroller.clientHeight : window.innerHeight;\n      // Scroll distance the trigger actually covers. Guarded against 0, which\n      // would make every anchor Infinity and stack the whole story on frame 1.\n      const total = Math.max(1, container.offsetHeight - view);\n\n      const anchor = (el: HTMLElement, t: number) =>\n        gsap.utils.clamp(0, 1, (el.offsetTop + el.offsetHeight * t - view / 2) / total);\n\n      const rows = sections\n        .map((section) => {\n          const el = container.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-keyframe=\"${section.id}\"]`);\n          return el ? { at: anchor(el, section.at ?? 0.5), to: section.to } : null;\n        })\n        .filter((row): row is { at: number; to: Partial<StageParams> } => row !== null)\n        // Authoring order is document order, but a custom `at` can reorder\n        // them; the timeline needs them sorted to compute each row's span.\n        .sort((a, b) => a.at - b.at);\n\n      const timeline = gsap.timeline({\n        scrollTrigger: {\n          trigger: container,\n          scroller: scroller ?? undefined,\n          start: 'top top',\n          end: 'bottom bottom',\n          scrub,\n          invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n        },\n        onUpdate: render,\n      });\n\n      rows.forEach((row, i) => {\n        const next = rows[i + 1];\n        // Each row travels until the next one begins, so the scene is always\n        // in motion between beats and never sits finished waiting for the\n        // next anchor.\n        const span = Math.max(0.001, (next ? next.at : 1) - row.at);\n        // `ease: 'none'` — under a scrub the ease warps timeline time, so an\n        // eased row arrives at its params before or after its own section.\n        timeline.to(params, { ...row.to, duration: span, ease: 'none' }, row.at);\n      });\n\n      render();\n    },\n    root,\n    [sections, scrub, scrollerRef, reduced, height],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` reverts the `gsap.context`, which kills the timeline and its ScrollTrigger and drops the reference to the params object. This runs on every reflow as well as on unmount: a surviving trigger would keep scrubbing a timeline built against stale `offsetTop` values while a new one runs alongside it, and the two would write conflicting transforms to the same element on the same frame.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No timeline and no ScrollTrigger are created. Every keyframe is folded into the params object in order and rendered once, so the stage shows the end of the story and the copy is fully readable and correctly laid out.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Hand-tuning keyframe positions as raw timeline fractions. A single line wrap moves every section below it, so the beat that was on the right paragraph at 1440px is a paragraph early at 1280px. Anchoring to `offsetTop` is the fix and is the reason for the whole `anchor` expression.\n- Not rebuilding when the container reflows. The anchors are computed once from measurements taken at build time; a font swap, an image load or an orientation change moves the sections and the timeline keeps using the old numbers.\n- Tweening the DOM directly instead of the object. With six properties across three elements you get a tween per property per beat, they fight over the same transform string, and the last one to write wins non-deterministically.\n- Easing the rows under a scrub. The ease is applied to timeline time, so the params reach their target at a different scroll position than the anchor says — the beat visibly detaches from its heading.\n- Reusing one module-level params object across builds. On rebuild the tweens start from wherever the previous scrub left the object rather than from the resting state, so the scene is subtly different after every resize.\n- Letting `total` be zero. It happens on the first layout pass and on any container shorter than the viewport; every anchor becomes Infinity, clamps to 1, and the entire story plays in the last pixel of scroll.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The stage is `position: sticky`, not fixed, so it is bounded by the component rather than by the page and works inside any scroll container.\n- Pass `sections` as a stable reference (module scope or memoised) — it is a dependency of the build effect.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll through the column: the mark scales, spins, drifts and dims, and each change lines up with the heading that names it reaching the middle of the panel. Resize the panel so the copy rewraps and scroll again — the beats still land on their headings. Stop mid-scroll and the scene keeps resolving for around a second. With reduced motion on, the stage shows the final state and nothing moves.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "knockout-zoom-pin": "# Build: Knockout Zoom Pin\n\nA sticky section holds a single word while a manufactured scroll range plays a zoom out: the word starts several screens wide, showing a gradient through its letters, and settles into a normal headline position. No library — the progress is read from the sticky section's own rect, smoothed exponentially in wall-clock time, and written as one composited transform per changed frame.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA word the size of the screen shrinks into a headline as you scroll, with the scene showing through its letters.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The scroll range is manufactured from the scroller's own height: the section is sized `hostHeight * (1 + viewports)` in pixels and the sticky child is sized `hostHeight`, so the leftover is exactly the pin duration. Pixels, not `vh`, because inside a scrollable box viewport units describe the wrong element.\n2. Progress is `(hostTop - sectionTop) / (total - hostHeight)`, clamped. It comes from `getBoundingClientRect()` on the frame it is used, so it stays correct through resizes, font swaps and layout shifts with nothing to invalidate.\n3. The raw progress is smoothed with `damp(smoothed, target, smoothing, dt)` — `x + (t - x) * (1 - s^dt)` — so the settle takes the same wall-clock time at 60 and 144 Hz. The per-frame form (`x += (t - x) * 0.1`) converges more than twice as fast on a high-refresh display.\n4. Scale and position use different curves on purpose. Scale is `cover - (cover - 1) * easeOutCubic(L)`, front-loading the shrink; the recentre is `easeInCubic(L)`, which keeps the word centred until it is small enough for its position to mean anything.\n5. The transform is built as a string, rounded to 4 decimals of scale and 0.1 px of translate, and compared with the previous frame's string. Unchanged frames skip the DOM write entirely, which at both ends of the range is most of them.\n6. The letters are a window rather than a fill: `background-clip: text` with a transparent colour, so the gradient behind them scales with the word and the section reads as cut out of the page.\n7. The word is sized in `cqw` against a `container-type: inline-size` pin box, so the component is identical in a 320 px preview card and a full-bleed page without a single breakpoint.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `cover = 7`\n  Scale at progress 0 — enough that only the counters of one or two letters are on screen, so the opening frame reads as texture and the reveal of the word is the payoff. Below ~4 the word is legible immediately and there is nothing to reveal.\n- `viewports = 1.6`\n  Scroll travel as a multiple of the scroller height. Under ~1 the zoom is over before it registers; past ~2.5 the pin outstays its welcome and users start scrolling hard to escape it.\n- `smoothing = 0.0067`\n  Fraction of the gap remaining after one second — `e^-5`, i.e. the familiar `1 - exp(-5 * dt)` filter. It removes trackpad and wheel-step judder without the word feeling detached from the hand. At 0.05 the lag is visible as rubberiness.\n- `rest = -0.3`\n  Final vertical position as a fraction of the pinned box: three tenths above centre, which is where a display headline sits when body copy follows it.\n- `veil window = progress 0.45 → 0.95`\n  When the content behind fades up. Earlier and it competes with a word still too large to read past; later and it arrives after the motion has stopped, which reads as a second, unrelated event.\n- `write quantisation = 4 dp scale, 0.1 px translate`\n  Below a tenth of a device pixel nothing changes on screen, so the comparison lets idle frames cost one string build and one comparison instead of a style write and a composite.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction KnockoutZoomPin(props: { children: ReactNode; behind?: ReactNode; cover?: number; viewports?: number; smoothing?: number; rest?: number; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The word. Kept on one line — it is a display element.\n- `behind: ReactNode`\n  Content faded up behind the word as it settles.\n- `cover: number` — default `7`\n  Scale at progress 0.\n- `viewports: number` — default `1.6`\n  Scroll travel as a multiple of the scroller height.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0067`\n  Fraction of the gap left after one second.\n- `rest: number` — default `-0.3`\n  Final vertical position as a fraction of the pinned box height.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container. Omit to auto-detect the nearest scrollable ancestor.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function KnockoutZoomPin({\n  children,\n  behind,\n  cover = 7,\n  viewports = 1.6,\n  smoothing = 0.0067,\n  rest = -0.3,\n  scroller,\n  className,\n}: KnockoutZoomPinProps) {\n  const section = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const pin = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const word = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const veil = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n  const smoothed = useRef(0);\n  const lastTransform = useRef('');\n  const lastVeil = useRef('');\n  const lastHeights = useRef(0);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useRaf((dt) => {\n    const sectionEl = section.current;\n    const pinEl = pin.current;\n    const wordEl = word.current;\n    if (!sectionEl || !pinEl || !wordEl) return;\n\n    const host = scroller?.current ?? nearestScroller(sectionEl);\n    const hostIsRoot = host === document.documentElement;\n    const hostTop = hostIsRoot ? 0 : host.getBoundingClientRect().top;\n    const hostHeight = hostIsRoot ? window.innerHeight : host.clientHeight;\n    if (hostHeight === 0) return;\n\n    // The pin range is manufactured from the scroller's own height, not from\n    // `vh`: inside a box, viewport units describe the wrong thing entirely.\n    const total = Math.round(hostHeight * (reduced ? 1 : 1 + viewports));\n    if (total !== lastHeights.current) {\n      lastHeights.current = total;\n      sectionEl.style.height = `${total}px`;\n      pinEl.style.height = `${hostHeight}px`;\n    }\n\n    const rect = sectionEl.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const travel = total - hostHeight;\n    const target = travel > 0 ? clamp((hostTop - rect.top) / travel, 0, 1) : 1;\n\n    // `damp(x, t, s, dt)` is `x + (t - x) * (1 - s^dt)`; with s = e^-5 that is\n    // exactly the `1 - exp(-5 * dt)` constant the technique is named for.\n    smoothed.current = reduced ? target : damp(smoothed.current, target, smoothing, dt);\n    const L = smoothed.current;\n\n    // Ease-out on the scale: most of the shrink happens early, so the last\n    // third of the scroll is a slow settle rather than a continued rush.\n    const scale = cover - (cover - 1) * easeOutCubic(L);\n    // Ease-IN on the recentre: while the word is huge its position is\n    // meaningless, so it must not start travelling until it is nearly small.\n    const y = lerp(0, rest * hostHeight, easeInCubic(L));\n\n    const transform = `translate3d(0, ${y.toFixed(1)}px, 0) scale(${scale.toFixed(4)})`;\n    if (transform !== lastTransform.current) {\n      lastTransform.current = transform;\n      wordEl.style.transform = transform;\n    }\n\n    const veilEl = veil.current;\n    if (veilEl) {\n      const opacity = easeInCubic(clamp((L - 0.45) / 0.5, 0, 1)).toFixed(3);\n      if (opacity !== lastVeil.current) {\n        lastVeil.current = opacity;\n        veilEl.style.opacity = opacity;\n      }\n    }\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes from the shared loop on unmount; the only state is refs. The inline `height` values written to the section and the pin die with the elements, and no observers, listeners or timers are registered.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The manufactured range collapses to one screen and the smoothing is bypassed, so progress tracks scroll exactly. The word passes through at its resting scale with the caption already present — the section becomes an ordinary headline block instead of leaving a screen and a half of empty scrolling behind.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sizing the range in `vh` inside a scrollable container. The section is then sized against the window while progress is measured against the box, so the zoom finishes early, late, or never — and it looks correct only when the container happens to be full-height.\n- Smoothing with a per-frame constant. The zoom lags noticeably further behind the scroll on a 60 Hz display than on a 120 Hz one, and every attempt to tune it makes the other refresh rate worse.\n- Using the same easing for scale and for position. Move the word early and it slides around the screen while still too large to locate, which reads as a bug rather than as choreography.\n- Animating `font-size` or `width` instead of `transform: scale`. Every frame relayouts and re-rasterises glyphs at a new size — the single most reliable way to turn this effect into a slideshow on a laptop.\n- Writing the transform unconditionally each frame. At the top and bottom of the range the value is identical for dozens of frames, and each write still costs a style invalidation and a composite.\n- Caching the section rect. A font swap or an image landing above the section moves it, and a cached top makes the zoom finish at the wrong scroll position for the rest of the session.\n- Leaving the manufactured range in place under reduced motion. The user gets a screen and a half of scrolling where nothing happens at all; the height must collapse along with the animation.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The scroll container is auto-detected by walking ancestors for a scrollable `overflow-y`, so the component works unchanged on a normal page. Pass `scroller` when the container is ambiguous or created later.\n- The knockout is `background-clip: text`, not an SVG mask: no extra element, no viewBox to keep in sync, and the text stays selectable and readable to assistive technology.\n- A single word works best. Two lines re-wrap as the scale changes and the knockout stops reading as one shape.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe word is unreadably large at the top of the section and lands as a normal headline exactly as the section releases. Scrolling backwards reverses it at the same rate. Stopping mid-zoom settles within about a quarter second with no drift. Resizing the window mid-zoom keeps the word at the same progress. With reduced motion on, the section is one screen tall and nothing scales.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "language-select": "# Build: Language Select\n\nThe small \"English\" pill from a site footer, built on the kit `Select`, so it keeps the kit popover motion, outside-press dismissal, typeahead and arrow-key navigation. Each option shows a decorative region flag, the language name in that language and the name in the interface language. It works controlled or uncontrolled and ships a 16-language default list, including two right-to-left scripts.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA footer language picker on the kit Select: translate glyph or flag in the trigger, flag emoji, native names with lang and dir per option.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The selected code lives in `value` when the parent controls it, otherwise in local state seeded from `defaultValue` (or the first language). It is always passed to the kit `Select` as `value`, so the listbox and the trigger read one source. `onChange` ignores a null key and a re-pick of the current language, then calls `onChange(code, language)`.\n2. Each `SelectItem` gets `id={code}` and `textValue={nativeName}`. Typeahead matches what the user sees first, and the flag emoji does not get in the way of matching.\n3. The native name is a `<span lang={code} dir={dir}>`, so screen readers switch voice for \"Deutsch\" or \"日本語\", and Arabic and Hebrew render right-to-left inside an otherwise left-to-right menu. The flag is `aria-hidden`.\n4. `flagEmoji(region)` maps the two letters of an ISO 3166 region to the regional-indicator code points `0x1F1E6 + (letter - 65)`. Fonts draw the pair as a single flag glyph.\n5. The trigger uses the kit Select `renderValue`. It shows `RiTranslate2` (the default) or the selected flag, then the native name. A trailing `RiCheckLine` marks the current row and is only made `invisible` on the others, so every row keeps the same width.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `regional indicator A = 0x1F1E6`\n  Unicode puts the 26 regional-indicator symbols in A–Z order from U+1F1E6. Two of them in sequence are rendered as the flag of that region.\n- `popover width = w-[248px]`\n  This is narrower than the shared 266px menu but still fits the longest default row, \"🇨🇳 简体中文 Chinese (Simplified)\", with the check mark. Longer names truncate.\n- `list height = max-h-[240px] (kit Select)`\n  About six rows are visible. The 16 defaults scroll, and the popover still fits above a footer trigger at the bottom of a viewport.\n- `size = sm by default (px-[7px] py-1, Body 2 Medium, 16px glyph)`\n  This matches the compact footer pill. Use `size=\"md\"` and a `label` when the select sits in a settings form.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LanguageSelect(props: { languages?: readonly Language[]; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (code: string, language: Language) => void; trigger?: \"icon\" | \"flag\"; showName?: boolean; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; label?: ReactNode; \"aria-label\"?: string; isDisabled?: boolean; className?: string; triggerClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `languages: readonly Language[]` — default `LANGUAGES`\n  Options in display order: `{ code, nativeName, name, region, dir? }`.\n- `value: string`\n  Selected language code (controlled).\n- `defaultValue: string` — default `first language`\n  Initial code when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (code: string, language: Language) => void`\n  Called when a different language is picked.\n- `trigger: \"icon\" | \"flag\"` — default `\"icon\"`\n  What precedes the native name in the trigger.\n- `showName: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the interface-language name after the native name in each option.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"sm\"`\n  Kit Select size.\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Visible label above the trigger. It replaces `aria-label` as the name.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Language\"`\n  Accessible name when there is no visible label.\n- `isDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Disables the trigger.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function LanguageSelect({\n  languages = LANGUAGES,\n  value,\n  defaultValue,\n  onChange,\n  trigger = \"icon\",\n  showName = true,\n  size = \"sm\",\n  label,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Language\",\n  isDisabled,\n  className,\n  triggerClassName,\n}: LanguageSelectProps) {\n  const [inner, setInner] = useState(() => defaultValue ?? languages[0]?.code ?? \"\");\n  const current = value ?? inner;\n  const selected = languages.find((l) => l.code === current) ?? languages[0];\n\n  const change = (key: Key | null) => {\n    if (key === null) return;\n    const next = languages.find((l) => l.code === String(key));\n    if (!next || next.code === current) return;\n    if (value === undefined) setInner(next.code);\n    onChange?.(next.code, next);\n  };\n\n  const iconSize = size === \"sm\" ? \"size-4\" : \"size-[18px]\";\n\n  return (\n    <Select\n      label={label}\n      aria-label={label ? undefined : ariaLabel}\n      size={size}\n      value={selected?.code ?? null}\n      onChange={change}\n      isDisabled={isDisabled}\n      className={cx(\"w-fit\", className)}\n      triggerClassName={triggerClassName}\n      popoverClassName=\"w-[248px]\"\n      renderValue={\n        selected ? (\n          <>\n            {trigger === \"flag\" ? (\n              <span aria-hidden className=\"shrink-0 text-[15px] leading-none\">\n                {flagEmoji(selected.region)}\n              </span>\n            ) : (\n              <RiTranslate2 aria-hidden className={cx(iconSize, \"shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary\")} />\n            )}\n            <span lang={selected.code} dir={selected.dir ?? \"ltr\"} className=\"truncate\">\n              {selected.nativeName}\n            </span>\n          </>\n        ) : null\n      }\n    >\n      {languages.map((language) => {\n        const isCurrent = language.code === selected?.code;\n        return (\n          <SelectItem\n            key={language.code}\n            id={language.code}\n            textValue={language.nativeName}\n            className=\"gap-2.5\"\n          >\n            <span aria-hidden className=\"w-5 shrink-0 text-center text-[17px] leading-none\">\n              {flagEmoji(language.region)}\n            </span>\n            <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 items-baseline gap-1.5\">\n              <span lang={language.code} dir={language.dir ?? \"ltr\"} className=\"truncate\">\n                {language.nativeName}\n              </span>\n              {showName && language.name !== language.nativeName ? (\n                <span className=\"truncate text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{language.name}</span>\n              ) : null}\n            </span>\n            <RiCheckLine\n              aria-hidden\n              className={cx(\"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-primary\", !isCurrent && \"invisible\")}\n            />\n          </SelectItem>\n        );\n      })}\n    </Select>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** There is nothing of its own to clean up. The kit Select owns its outside-press listener and removes it when the popover closes or the component unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the kit Select popover fade and scale, which is a short CSS transition. Nothing in this component animates on its own.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using the flag as the language label. A flag names a country: English is not only 🇺🇸, and Arabic is not only 🇸🇦. The trigger defaults to the translate glyph, and every flag is `aria-hidden` decoration next to a real name.\n- Windows does not draw flag emoji, so it shows the two letters (\"DE\") instead. That is acceptable here only because the flag is never the sole label.\n- Leaving out `lang` on native names. A screen reader then reads \"日本語\" or \"Deutsch\" with the interface voice and mispronounces it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass your own `languages` to limit the list to the locales you ship. Each needs `code`, `nativeName`, `name` and `region`, and optionally `dir`.\n- `trigger=\"flag\"` with `showName={false}` gives the most compact footer variant, the second select in the demo.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe trigger reads \"English\" after a translate glyph. Opening it lists 16 languages, each with a flag, a native name and a grey English name, and a check mark on the current one. Typing \"d\" moves focus to Deutsch and Enter selects it. The trigger then reads \"Deutsch\", and `onChange` receives \"de\". In the list, العربية and עברית render right-to-left. Escape or a click outside closes the list without changing the value.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "layout-lerp-scalar": "# Build: Layout Lerp Scalar\n\nOne function places every card from seven scalars — radius, spacing, rowSpread, turns, depth, focus, scale — and each named layout is only a set of values for them. Morphing is therefore `gsap.to(params, preset)`: a tween over an object literal, with no per-item choreography anywhere. Crucially it interpolates the *inputs*, `f(lerp(a, b, s))`, not the outputs, so every intermediate frame is a valid member of the same family instead of a set of cards sliding along straight lines through each other.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRow, stack, drum and fan are one placement function of seven numbers. Switching layout is a tween over an object.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1, gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Placement is `offset = wrappedOffset(i, position, n)`, then `angle = (offset / n) * 2PI * turns` and `p = (radius * sin(angle) + offset * rowSpread * step, -offset * spacing, radius * (cos(angle) - 1) - depth * |offset|)`, with `rotation.y = angle`. Setting `radius` and `turns` to 0 leaves a straight line; adding `spacing` makes it a column; adding `turns` and `radius` wraps that column into a drum. There is no branch anywhere in it.\n2. Because the parameters are what get tweened, the shape passes through the family rather than across it. `f` is nonlinear in `radius` and `turns`, so `f(lerp(a, b, s))` and `lerp(f(a), f(b), s)` are different animations: the first keeps the cards on a well-formed helix at every `s`, the second sends each card down a chord that cuts through the middle of the drum and through its neighbours.\n3. `cos(angle) - 1` rather than `cos(angle)` puts the focused card at z = 0 for any radius. Growing the drum then pushes the far side away instead of pulling the near card into the camera, which is what makes `radius` safe to tween through large values.\n4. The `depth` term gives the flat layouts a strictly monotonic z in `|offset|`. That is not a look — it is what lets three's painter sort order the transparent cards correctly. With every card at z = 0 the sort has nothing to rank and the overlap order in a stack is whatever the traversal happened to produce.\n5. The row step is derived from the camera: `visibleHeight = 2 * cameraZ * tan(fov / 2)`, `visibleWidth = visibleHeight * aspect`, and `step = visibleWidth * fill / (n - 1)`. Card size comes from the same `visibleHeight`, so the whole layout is defined in fractions of the frustum and holds its proportions at any viewport.\n6. Focus is `1 - focus * (1 - exp(-offset^2))` — 1.000 at the centre, 0.368 one step out, 0.018 two — driving both the card scale and a single `uFocus` uniform that desaturates and dims in the fragment shader. `focus` is itself one of the tweened parameters, so a layout can decide how much of a selection it is.\n7. The vertex shader is the default transform and nothing else. Everything that moves is a CPU write to `position`, `rotation` and `scale`, which means the bounding sphere three culls against and the view depth it sorts by both describe where the card actually is. A drum that bends its planes in the vertex shader has to disable culling and reconstruct `renderOrder` by hand; this one gets both for free.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PRESETS.drum = { radius: 2.55, spacing: 0.74, turns: 2, focus: 1, scale: 1 }`\n  Two turns across twelve cards puts adjacent cards 60° apart, so the ring reads as a ring rather than as a coil. Below about 1.5 turns the far side is barely behind the near side and the drum flattens into a wheel; above 3 the cards on the back face are edge-on for most of a lap and the set looks half empty.\n- `PRESETS.row.scale = 0.42`\n  Matched to the derived row step so twelve cards just touch at the reference aspect. Larger and the row overlaps into a fan; smaller and it becomes a sparse dotted line. It is a scalar for exactly this reason — the row layout gets to pick its own card size.\n- `depth = 0.35 (column) / 0.1 (row) / 0 (drum, fan)`\n  World units of recession per index step. The drum and fan get their z from the radius, so they need none. The flat layouts need a non-zero value or their transparent cards have identical view depth and the sort order is arbitrary — the symptom is a stack whose overlap flickers as the ring drifts.\n- `CARD_HEIGHT_RATIO = 0.4`\n  Card height as a fraction of the frustum height at z = 0. Above ~0.55 the drum's vertical spacing has to grow to match or the cards intersect; below ~0.25 the whole formation floats in the middle of the frame with nothing at the edges.\n- `CAMERA_FOV / CAMERA_Z = 42 / 7.2`\n  A matched pair: they set the visible height, and everything else is a fraction of it. A wider FOV exaggerates the perspective divide so the far side of the drum shrinks dramatically — dramatic, but it also makes the row visibly bow. Change one and the layout is unchanged only if the other keeps `z * tan(fov / 2)` the same.\n- `ROW_FILL = 0.92`\n  Fraction of the visible width the flat row spans. At 1.0 the outermost cards are cut exactly by the frustum edge and their antialiased rims flicker as they cross it; much below 0.85 the row no longer reads as spanning the frame.\n- `SCALE_FLOOR = 0.72`\n  The scale an entirely out-of-focus card keeps. At 1.0 the focus falloff is only a dimming and the arrangement loses its centre; below ~0.5 the outer cards shrink so fast the drum looks like it is receding into a tunnel.\n- `duration / MORPH_EASE = 1.25 / power3.inOut`\n  Long enough to read the shape changing rather than snapping, short enough not to feel like a loading state. `inOut` because the morph has no impulse behind it — an `out` ease implies the shape was thrown, and the sudden start looks like a dropped frame.\n- `drift = 0.16`\n  Index steps per second of idle travel. About six seconds per card: slow enough to read a caption, fast enough that the wrap is visible within a few seconds of arriving. At 0 the ticker still runs but nothing moves, which is the right default for a layout driven by scroll instead.\n- `CARD_CORNER = 0.055`\n  Corner radius in units of card height, evaluated as an SDF in the fragment shader. Because the mask is drawn rather than sampled it stays crisp at any scale, and one number gives the same physical radius on both axes since the SDF works in height units and multiplies x by the aspect.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LayoutLerpScalar(props: { count?: number; layout?: 'row' | 'column' | 'drum' | 'fan'; params?: Partial<LayoutParams>; duration?: number; drift?: number; hueStart?: number; hueStep?: number; opacity?: number; onFocusChange?: (index: number) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `12`\n  How many cards. They wrap, so this is also the ring's period.\n- `layout: 'row' | 'column' | 'drum' | 'fan'` — default `'drum'`\n  Which preset the parameter vector is tweened toward.\n- `params: Partial<LayoutParams>`\n  Overrides individual scalars on top of the preset — radius, spacing, rowSpread, turns, depth, focus, scale. Compared by value.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.25`\n  Seconds of the morph between presets.\n- `drift: number` — default `0.16`\n  Index steps per second of idle travel. 0 parks the ring.\n- `hueStart: number` — default `232`\n  First card's hue, 0-360.\n- `hueStep: number` — default `17`\n  Degrees of hue between adjacent cards.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Alpha multiplier applied before the focus falloff.\n- `onFocusChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Fires when a different card takes the focus position.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper. The canvas fills it absolutely.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/* ---- the morph: one tween over an object literal ------------------------ */\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const target: LayoutParams = { ...PRESETS[layout], ...params };\n  const current = live.current;\n\n  if (reduced) {\n    Object.assign(current, target);\n    return;\n  }\n\n  // Every parameter eases together, so the shape passes through a continuous\n  // family of shapes rather than each card taking its own path. `overwrite`\n  // matters: switch layout twice quickly and the second tween must take the\n  // parameters from wherever the first one had got to, not fight it.\n  const tween = gsap.to(current, { ...target, duration, ease: MORPH_EASE, overwrite: true });\n  return () => {\n    tween.kill();\n  };\n  // `params` is compared by value through `overrideKey`; the object literal\n  // most callers pass is a new reference on every render.\n  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n}, [layout, overrideKey, duration, reduced]);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const current = live.current;\n  return () => {\n    gsap.killTweensOf(current);\n  };\n}, []);\n\n/* ---- the frame ---------------------------------------------------------- */\n\nuseRaf(\n  (dt) => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current) return;\n    const n = current.cards.length;\n    if (n === 0) return;\n\n    const p = live.current;\n    const speed = reduced ? 0 : drift;\n    // Wrapped rather than left to grow: at 0.16 steps/s an unwrapped\n    // accumulator is fine for days, but the modulo costs nothing and keeps\n    // float precision constant no matter how long the tab stays open.\n    position.current = mod(position.current + dt * speed, n);\n    const pos = position.current;\n\n    // The one place the camera and the layout meet. `visibleHeight` is the\n    // frustum's height at z = 0 — `2 * z * tan(fov/2)` — and the width is\n    // that times the aspect. Deriving the row step from it is why the row\n    // spans the frame at any viewport instead of being a guessed constant.\n    const visibleHeight = 2 * Math.tan((CAMERA_FOV * DEG_TO_RAD) / 2) * CAMERA_Z;\n    const visibleWidth = visibleHeight * current.camera.aspect;\n    const step = (visibleWidth * ROW_FILL) / Math.max(n - 1, 1);\n    const cardHeight = visibleHeight * CARD_HEIGHT_RATIO;\n    const cardWidth = cardHeight * CARD_ASPECT;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      const card = current.cards[i];\n      if (!card) continue;\n\n      // The call that makes a finite array behave like an endless ring:\n      // card 0 is one step BEFORE card 11, not eleven steps after it.\n      const offset = wrappedOffset(i, pos, n);\n      const angle = (offset / n) * TWO_PI * p.turns;\n\n      // The placement function. Seven scalars in, one transform out, and\n      // every layout this component has is somewhere in here.\n      card.mesh.position.set(\n        p.radius * Math.sin(angle) + offset * p.rowSpread * step,\n        -offset * p.spacing,\n        // cos(angle) - 1 keeps the focused card at z = 0 whatever the\n        // radius, so growing the drum pushes the ring back rather than\n        // pulling the front card into the camera. The depth term is what\n        // gives the flat layouts a strictly monotonic z, which is what lets\n        // three sort them back-to-front without any renderOrder fixing.\n        p.radius * (Math.cos(angle) - 1) - p.depth * Math.abs(offset),\n      );\n      card.mesh.rotation.y = angle;\n\n      // exp(-d^2): 1.000 at the focus, 0.368 one step out, 0.018 two,\n      // 0.0001 three. Continuous and branchless, and `focus` scales how much\n      // of it applies — at 0 every card is lit equally, which is what makes\n      // the flat row read as an index rather than as a selection.\n      const f = 1 - p.focus * (1 - gaussian(offset));\n      const s = p.scale * (SCALE_FLOOR + (1 - SCALE_FLOOR) * f);\n      card.mesh.scale.set(cardWidth * s, cardHeight * s, 1);\n      card.uniforms.uFocus.value = f;\n      card.uniforms.uOpacity.value = opacity;\n    }\n\n    const nextFocus = wrapIndex(Math.round(pos), n);\n    if (nextFocus !== focusedRef.current) {\n      focusedRef.current = nextFocus;\n      notify.current?.(nextFocus);\n    }\n\n    current.renderer.render(current.scene, current.camera);\n  },\n  ready && inView && visible,\n);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Kills the parameter tween (and any tween of the same object) so GSAP cannot write into a vector nothing reads; removes every mesh from the scene, then `disposeObject` for the materials, `geometry.dispose()`, `prototype.dispose()`, and `disposeRenderer` — which stops the animation loop, disposes, calls `forceContextLoss()` and zeroes the backing store — before removing the canvas from the DOM. The shared rAF unsubscribes, and the shared resize, intersection and visibility observers all unregister. No textures are allocated, so there are none to leak.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The idle drift is set to zero, so the ring holds still and the same card stays in focus. Layout changes still happen — refusing them would leave the component unable to answer its own prop — but the parameters are assigned rather than tweened, so the new arrangement is simply there on the next frame with nothing travelling across the screen. The frame loop keeps running only because it is what draws; with no drift and no tween it writes the same transforms every frame.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Cross-fading two layouts instead of blending their parameters. `lerp(f(a), f(b), s)` moves every card along the straight line between its two homes, so the drum passes through its own axis and the cards slide through one another. Interpolate the inputs and the shape is always a real member of the family.\n- Reaching for `if (layout === \"drum\")` in the frame loop. Branching per layout means each new arrangement is new code and no pair of them can be shown halfway. Every parameter here is a number precisely so it can be tweened, and the presets are data.\n- Multiplying a whole layout to zero and calling that a morph. Scaling `radius` to 0 does not produce a flat row — it produces every card stacked on the axis. A second term (`rowSpread * step`) has to take over as the first one leaves, which is why the row and the ring are separate parameters rather than one.\n- Letting the transparent cards share a z. three ranks transparent meshes by view depth; with a flat layout at z = 0 the ranking is arbitrary and the overlap in a stack flips as the ring drifts. The `depth` parameter exists to keep the ordering strict.\n- Copying the bend-in-the-vertex-shader approach and keeping frustum culling on. That combination blanks planes that are plainly on screen, because the bounding sphere describes the flat geometry at the CPU position. This component avoids the whole class of problem by keeping the layout on the CPU — but the two approaches cannot be mixed carelessly.\n- Guessing the row step in world units. Hard-code it and the row overflows a narrow viewport and floats in the middle of a wide one. It has to come from the frustum: `2 * cameraZ * tan(fov / 2) * aspect`, divided across the set.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` and stopping there. It frees GPU objects but leaves the WebGL context alive, and Chromium kills the oldest context once about sixteen exist — so an unrelated canvas elsewhere on the page goes black. `disposeRenderer` calls `forceContextLoss()`, which is the call that actually hands it back.\n- Building the colours with `new THREE.Color(...)` from a hex string and expecting them to match the CSS beside them. Colours have to enter the linear working space (`setHSL(..., THREE.SRGBColorSpace)`) and the fragment shader has to end with `#include <colorspace_fragment>`, because three injects that chunk only into its own materials, never into a `ShaderMaterial`.\n- Passing `params` as an inline object literal and putting it straight in the effect deps. It is a new reference every render, so the morph tween is killed and rebuilt on each one and the parameters never arrive. The override is compared by value through a serialised key.\n- Rendering every frame regardless. The loop is gated on the element being in view and the tab being visible; a scrolled-past WebGL canvas that keeps drawing is one of the easier ways to halve a page's battery life for nothing.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The four presets are ordinary data — add a fifth by adding seven numbers, with no new code and no new branch. Any two of them can also be blended by hand at a fixed `s` by passing `params`.\n- Each card owns a cloned `ShaderMaterial` so it can hold its own colours, but three keys its program cache on the shader source, so all twelve still share one compiled program and the clone costs a uniform bag.\n- Nothing is fetched and no image ships: the card face — rounded rectangle, gradient, hairlines, rim — is a signed distance field in the fragment shader, and the shading of a card turning edge-on is one dot product against the transformed normal.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe cards turn slowly and endlessly — watch one leave the bottom of the drum and come back at the top, at the same spacing. Switch layout: the whole formation unwinds into a row, or collapses into a stack, in one continuous motion, and at every instant it looks like a formation rather than cards travelling to new addresses. Nothing passes through the centre of the drum on the way. Switch layout twice quickly and the second morph starts from wherever the first had reached, with no jump. In the stack, the overlap order is stable while it drifts — nothing flickers in front of its neighbour. Resize the window from narrow to wide: the row still spans the frame and the cards keep their proportions. Scroll the canvas out of view and the GPU goes quiet.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "legend-stat-tiles": "# Build: Legend Stat Tiles\n\nA grey dashboard card: a title, the headline total with an optional delta chip, a thin bar split into one coloured segment per series, and a grid of white legend tiles. Each tile carries a chart-token dot, its label, its value and its share of the total. Clicking a tile takes that series out of the total, and the bar, headline and percents rebalance; hovering or focusing a tile spotlights its segment.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA headline total over a segmented proportion bar and legend tiles with dots, values and percents that toggle series in and out.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Resolve the hidden ids through a small controllable-state helper: `hidden` wins when defined, otherwise local state seeded by `defaultHidden`; `onHiddenChange` fires either way.\n2. Mark each segment shown when it is not hidden and its value is above zero, and sum only shown values into the headline total.\n3. Render the bar as a flex row with `flex-grow: value` and `flex-basis: 0` on every segment (0 for hidden ones). The widths are then the exact shares of the shown total, with no percentage arithmetic, and `transition-[flex-grow]` animates a rebalance over 300ms.\n4. Print percents with largest-remainder rounding (`wholePercents`): floor every share, then hand the leftover points to the largest remainders, so the tiles always add up to exactly 100%.\n5. Render each tile as a toggle `<button aria-pressed>`. Hiding the last shown series is refused (the tile gets `aria-disabled`), so the bar never empties. A hidden tile fades to 60% and its dot turns into an outlined ring.\n6. Track the hovered or focused id in state. When that series is shown, dim every other bar segment to 35% and every other tile to 60%; a hidden series spotlights nothing, so the bar never fades as a whole.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `colour order = chart-6, chart-2, chart-3, chart-5, chart-1, chart-8, chart-4, chart-7, chart-9`\n  Neighbouring defaults alternate cool and warm hues, so adjacent bar segments stay distinct even when no colours are passed.\n- `bar = h-2, 2px gaps, rounded-full track`\n  Thin enough to read as a legend summary rather than a chart; the 1px padding on each side of a segment makes the 2px seams without a gap property that would leave space for hidden segments.\n- `tile grid = 2 columns, 4 from @xl (container 36rem)`\n  A container query, so the tiles reflow by the card width, not the viewport.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype LegendSegment = { id: string; label: string; value: number; color?: \"chart-1\" | … | \"chart-9\" }\nfunction LegendStatTiles(props: LegendStatTilesProps): JSX.Element\nfunction wholePercents(values: number[]): number[]\n```\n\n- `segments: LegendSegment[]`\n  Series in display order.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Total\"`\n  Header label and the card's accessible name.\n- `change: number`\n  Headline delta as a fraction (0.148 → +14.8%). Lime when positive, rose when negative.\n- `hidden / defaultHidden / onHiddenChange: string[]` — default `[]`\n  Ids left out of the total, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `toggleable: boolean` — default `true`\n  Tiles toggle their series when clicked.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string`\n  Formats the total and tile values. Defaults to Intl.NumberFormat(locale).\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for the default number and percent formats.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Only shown segments count: the headline total is their sum, and the bar\n// gives each one `flex-grow: value` on a zero basis, so the widths are the\n// exact shares of that total. The printed percents use largest-remainder\n// rounding so they also add up to 100.\nconst rows = useMemo(() => {\n  const shown = segments.map((s) => !hidden.includes(s.id) && s.value > 0);\n  const percents = wholePercents(segments.map((s, i) => (shown[i] ? s.value : 0)));\n  return segments.map((s, i) => ({\n    ...s,\n    color: s.color ?? ORDER[i % ORDER.length] ?? \"chart-1\",\n    shown: shown[i] ?? false,\n    percent: percents[i] ?? 0,\n  }));\n}, [segments, hidden]);\nconst total = rows.reduce((sum, r) => sum + (r.shown ? r.value : 0), 0);\nconst shownCount = rows.filter((r) => r.shown).length;\n// Spotlight only a series that is in the bar; a hidden one would dim them all.\nconst spotlight = rows.find((r) => r.id === active && r.shown)?.id ?? null;\n\nconst toggle = (id: string) => {\n  const isHidden = hidden.includes(id);\n  // Keep at least one segment in the total, so the bar never empties.\n  if (!isHidden && shownCount <= 1) return;\n  setHidden(isHidden ? hidden.filter((h) => h !== id) : [...hidden, id]);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component has no timers, listeners outside React or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The bar rebalance and the dimming transitions carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the bar and tiles jump straight to their new state.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not compute the bar widths as rounded percents. Rounded widths can sum to 99% or 101% and leave a sliver or overflow; flex-grow on a zero basis is always exact.\n- Give every segment a stable `id`. The hidden list and the hover spotlight key off it.\n- Values at or below zero are treated as hidden. Negative contributions do not fit a proportion bar.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Set `toggleable={false}` for a read-only legend; tiles then render as plain `<div>`s.\n- Pass `formatValue` for currency or compact numbers. It formats both the headline and each tile value.\n- The bar is a single `role=\"img\"` element whose label lists every shown series and its percent.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe headline equals the sum of shown values and the bar segments are proportional to them. The tile percents add up to 100. Clicking a tile hides its series: its percent reads \"Off\", its dot becomes a ring, its bar segment collapses and the total drops. Clicking it again restores it. The last shown tile cannot be hidden. Hovering a tile dims the other segments. Tiles sit two per row on narrow cards and four per row on wide ones. Light and dark both read correctly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "lenis-gsap-ticker": "# Build: Lenis On The GSAP Ticker\n\nLenis and ScrollTrigger each work alone and tear together, because each runs its own clock: Lenis advances the scroll position on its rAF, ScrollTrigger samples it on GSAP's, and a pinned section ends up a frame behind the page it is pinned to. Mounted once near the root, this hands Lenis to `gsap.ticker`, updates ScrollTrigger on Lenis's scroll event rather than per frame, and disables lag smoothing so the two agree about how much time passed.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSmooth scroll and ScrollTrigger on one clock, with the lag-smoothing line everyone omits.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0, lenis@^1.3.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Lenis is driven by `gsap.ticker.add((t) => lenis.raf(t * 1000))` — seconds to milliseconds — and its own `autoRaf` is switched off. One clock advances the scroll position and the tweens, in that order, every frame.\n2. `gsap.ticker.lagSmoothing(0)` is the line that makes it correct rather than merely working. GSAP normally clamps a large `deltaTime` after a stall so tweens do not jump; but that clamped value is now what Lenis integrates, so an under-reported frame leaves Lenis's scroll position permanently offset from ScrollTrigger's idea of it. Nothing resyncs them afterwards.\n3. ScrollTrigger is updated from `lenis.on(\"scroll\")`, not from the ticker. It then samples on the same tick the position actually changed, instead of one frame after — the difference is visible as a shimmering seam at the top edge of a pin.\n4. `paused` calls `lenis.stop()` and `lenis.start()` rather than tearing the instance down. This is the preloader gate: construct on mount so everything is measured and ready, but refuse wheel input until the intro clears — which is why you cannot scroll during a well-built intro sequence.\n5. Coming out of `paused`, and again on `load` and on `document.fonts.ready`, `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` re-measures. Images and fonts change document height after hydration, and every trigger below the fold was computed against the old one.\n6. The instance itself is refcounted at module scope in `use-lenis`, so mounting the provider twice — a layout and a page, say — reuses one Lenis instead of creating two that both `preventDefault` on wheel and scroll the page at double speed.\n7. Under `prefers-reduced-motion` no instance is constructed at all. The page scrolls natively and ScrollTrigger reads the native position, which is the path it is already correct on.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 1.05`\n  Seconds for a wheel step to settle. Roughly the feel of Lenis's `lerp: 0.09`, which is the value most award-site builds land on: slow enough to read as smooth, fast enough that a trackpad flick does not feel like it is dragging the page through syrup. Past ~1.4 the page feels detached from the hand.\n- `easing = 1.001 - 2^(-10t)`\n  Lenis's own default — an exponential decay that is ~99.9 percent settled at t=1, so the tail never visibly crawls. The `1.001` guarantees it reaches exactly 1 rather than approaching it forever.\n- `lagSmoothing = 0`\n  Disabled, not tuned. Any non-zero threshold means GSAP will eventually report a deltaTime that did not happen, and Lenis will integrate it into a scroll position that does not match the document.\n- `ScrollTrigger.update trigger = lenis scroll event`\n  One update per position change, on the tick it changed. Calling it from the ticker instead costs an update on frames where nothing moved and delivers a stale position on frames where it did.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LenisGsapTicker(props: { children?: ReactNode; duration?: number; easing?: (t: number) => number; paused?: boolean; enabled?: boolean }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Rendered as-is; the provider adds no DOM of its own.\n- `duration: number` — default `1.05`\n  Seconds for a wheel step to settle.\n- `easing: (t: number) => number`\n  Decay curve. Must be a stable reference — define it at module scope.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Holds input via `lenis.stop()` without destroying the instance. Releasing it also refreshes ScrollTrigger.\n- `enabled: boolean` — default `true`\n  False restores native scrolling and constructs nothing.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function LenisGsapTicker({\n  children,\n  duration = 1.05,\n  easing = DEFAULT_EASING,\n  paused = false,\n  enabled = true,\n}: LenisGsapTickerProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Smooth scrolling is the motion. Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the\n  // instance is never built at all, so the page scrolls natively and\n  // ScrollTrigger reads the native position — which it already does correctly.\n  const active = enabled && !reduced;\n\n  const lenis = useLenis({\n    duration,\n    easing,\n    smoothWheel: true,\n    enabled: active,\n    // One clock. `useLenis` turns off Lenis's `autoRaf` when a ticker is\n    // present and calls `lagSmoothing(0)` on it.\n    ticker: gsap.ticker,\n    // Sampled on scroll rather than every frame: ScrollTrigger then updates on\n    // the same tick the position changed, instead of one frame later.\n    onScroll: () => ScrollTrigger.update(),\n  });\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const instance = lenis.current;\n    if (!instance) return;\n    if (paused) {\n      instance.stop();\n      return;\n    }\n    instance.start();\n    // Whatever was covering the page while paused was almost certainly holding\n    // layout at a different height.\n    ScrollTrigger.refresh();\n  }, [lenis, paused]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!active) return;\n    let cancelled = false;\n    const refresh = () => {\n      if (!cancelled) ScrollTrigger.refresh();\n    };\n\n    // Images and fonts change document height after hydration. Without a\n    // refresh here, every trigger below the fold is measured against a height\n    // that no longer exists, and they all fire early by the difference.\n    if (document.readyState === 'complete') refresh();\n    else window.addEventListener('load', refresh, { once: true });\n    void document.fonts?.ready.then(refresh);\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n      window.removeEventListener('load', refresh);\n    };\n  }, [active]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `use-lenis` removes its scroll listener, removes the update function from `gsap.ticker`, and destroys the instance only when the last consumer unmounts. The provider removes its own `load` listener and guards the `document.fonts.ready` continuation with a cancelled flag, so a refresh cannot land after unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No Lenis instance is constructed when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` matches, so scrolling is entirely native and the ticker is left alone. ScrollTrigger keeps working against the native position. The query is live: toggling the OS setting takes effect on the next render without a reload.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving lag smoothing at its default. Everything looks right until the first stall — a heavy image decode, a devtools pause — after which pinned sections sit permanently a few pixels off and no interaction resyncs them.\n- Letting Lenis keep `autoRaf` while also adding it to the ticker. It then advances twice per frame: scrolling runs at double speed and the smoothing constant no longer means anything.\n- Mounting the provider in two places. Two instances both listen on `wheel`, both call `preventDefault`, and the page moves twice as far as the gesture — the refcount in `use-lenis` exists specifically to make the second mount a no-op.\n- Calling `ScrollTrigger.update` from the ticker instead of from the scroll event. Triggers lag the smoothed position by exactly one frame, which is most visible as a seam that shivers at the top of a pinned section.\n- Skipping `ScrollTrigger.refresh()` after `load` and after fonts settle. Every trigger below the fold is measured against a document height that no longer exists, so they all fire early by the difference.\n- Passing an inline arrow as `easing`. It is a new identity every render, so the effect that owns the instance tears Lenis down and rebuilds it on each one — visible as scroll position snapping to the top mid-gesture.\n- Smooth-scrolling a subtree by mounting a second window-level Lenis. Lenis attaches to the window unless given an explicit wrapper; a nested instance fights the outer one for the same wheel events.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The preview does not mount an enabled instance. `useLenis` attaches to the window, and a window-level smooth scroll inside a documentation page would take over the page hosting the preview rather than the preview. The demo therefore shows the provider mounted with `enabled={false}` beside a local reproduction: the right-hand column is offset by the same exponential filter Lenis applies, the left is the raw native position, and the bar at the bottom is the instantaneous gap between them. The ticker wiring itself is exactly as shipped.\n- The demo derives its filter constant with `perFrameToSmoothing(0.09)` from `lib/damp`, which converts Lenis's per-frame `lerp` into the per-second constant `damp` wants. That conversion is the same one that makes any ported per-frame easing frame-rate independent.\n- Mount above everything that registers a ScrollTrigger. Triggers created before the ticker wiring exists still work, but they are measured against a document that has not yet been refreshed.\n- Smooth scroll is a preference, not an improvement. Ship the `enabled` escape hatch and honour reduced motion — a real fraction of users find hijacked scrolling nauseating.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScrolling is smoothed and a pinned section stays welded to the page with no shivering seam at its top edge. Pause during a stall — hold a devtools breakpoint for a second — and on release the pin is still aligned. Mounting the provider twice does not double scroll speed. With reduced motion on, scrolling is native and ScrollTrigger still fires at the right positions.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "line-chart-card": "# Build: Line Chart Card\n\nA 376px grey dashboard card that draws revenue and costs as two Recharts lines over a dashed horizontal grid, with a soft gradient under the headline series. The header shows the latest revenue with its change against the bucket before, a segmented Weekly / Monthly / Yearly switch that swaps the whole data set, and legend dots. Hovering a point draws a dashed cursor, a pinging dot on each line and a tooltip card with every series value, and the headline counts to that point.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dashboard card with smooth multi-series lines on a dashed grid, a Weekly/Monthly/Yearly switch and a hover tooltip listing every series.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The chart is a `ComposedChart` inside a `ResponsiveContainer` that fills a `min-h-0 flex-1` box, so the card height is fixed and the plot takes what the header leaves. `CartesianGrid vertical={false}` with `strokeDasharray=\"4 4\"` in `--color-chart-track` gives the dashed horizontal rules.\n2. Each series is a 2.5px `Line` in its tone's `-active` colour; a series with `area: true` also gets a stroke-less `Area` behind its line, filled by a vertical `linearGradient` from `--color-chart-N` at 0.4 opacity to 0. Gradient ids are `useId()` plus the series key, so two cards never share a definition. Series paint in reverse order so the first (headline) series is on top.\n3. `shape=\"curved\"` maps to `type=\"monotone\"` and `shape=\"sharp\"` to `type=\"linear\"` on every Area and Line.\n4. `onMouseMove` stores `state.activeTooltipIndex` in `hovered` when `isTooltipActive` is true and the index is finite; `onMouseLeave` clears it. The headline reads `data[hovered]` or, with nothing hovered, the last point, and compares it with the point before through `describeDelta` (\"New\" with no baseline, \"0%\" when the one-decimal change rounds to zero, otherwise a lime or rose percentage).\n5. The `Tooltip` keeps a dashed `--color-chart-cursor` cursor and renders `ChartTooltipCard` from its `activeIndex` (guarded against `null`, since `Number(null)` is 0): the point label, then one row per series with its dot and formatted value.\n6. Every Line's `activeDot` is `PulseDot`: a 4.5px dot ringed 2px in the card colour, over a 6px circle at 0.35 opacity running `animate-ping` with `transform-box: fill-box` so it scales about its own centre.\n7. The period switch is `ChartPeriodControl` (the kit `SegmentedControl`, or the kit `Select` as a calendar pill with `periodControl=\"select\"`). Choosing a period clears `hovered`, updates internal state only when `period` is not controlled, and calls `onPeriodChange`.\n8. `ChartHeadline` counts with `useCountUp` (320ms) and replays `.animate-number-fade` by keying the figure on period and hovered index.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card height = 376px`\n  Fits the two-row header (figure on the left, switch and legend on the right) plus a plot of about 280px at the 576px preview width, and still leaves a 200px plot when the header stacks on a phone.\n- `area gradient = chart-N at stopOpacity 0.4 → 0, top to bottom`\n  Enough tint to read the headline series as volume while the second line stays visible through it; fading to 0 keeps the axis quiet.\n- `grid and cursor dash = strokeDasharray \"4 4\", grid in chart-track, cursor in chart-cursor`\n  Same dash for both so the hover cursor reads as part of the grid system, while the darker cursor token keeps it visible over the tinted area.\n- `pulse dot = core r 4.5 with 2px background-secondary stroke, ping r 6 at 0.35 opacity`\n  The card-coloured ring separates the dot from its line in both themes; the ping makes the hovered point findable without a heavier marker.\n- `axes = YAxis width 48, domain [0, \"auto\"], tickCount 4, ticks 12px tertiary; XAxis preserveStartEnd, minTickGap 12`\n  48px fits \"$700K\" at 12px. Starting at 0 keeps line height honest. preserveStartEnd plus the gap drops middle labels on narrow cards instead of overlapping them.\n- `motion = lines 450ms, tooltip 160ms, count-up 320ms, number-fade 220ms`\n  Fast enough to follow a pointer sweeping along twelve points; all of it is skipped under reduced motion.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LineChartCard(props?: LineChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype LineChartCardProps = { title?: string; series?: LineSeries[]; periods?: LinePeriod[]; period?: string; defaultPeriod?: string; onPeriodChange?: (id: string) => void; shape?: \"curved\" | \"sharp\"; periodControl?: \"segmented\" | \"select\"; valuePrefix?: string; className?: string }\ntype LineSeries = { key: string; label: string; tone: ChartTone; area?: boolean }\ntype LinePeriod = { id: string; label: string; data: LinePoint[] }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Revenue\"`\n  Header label; while hovering it becomes \"Revenue · <point label>\".\n- `series: LineSeries[]` — default `LINE_SERIES`\n  Series in legend order. The first drives the headline and is drawn on top; `area: true` adds the gradient fill.\n- `periods: LinePeriod[]` — default `LINE_PERIODS`\n  Data sets the switch moves between. The switch hides when there is only one.\n- `period: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultPeriod: string` — default `first period id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onPeriodChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the chosen period id.\n- `shape: \"curved\" | \"sharp\"` — default `\"curved\"`\n  Monotone splines or straight segments between points.\n- `periodControl: \"segmented\" | \"select\"` — default `\"segmented\"`\n  Segmented switch, or a calendar pill that opens a listbox.\n- `valuePrefix: string` — default `\"$\"`\n  Prepended to the headline, tooltip values and Y-axis ticks.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function LineChartCard({\n  title = \"Revenue\",\n  series = LINE_SERIES,\n  periods = LINE_PERIODS,\n  period,\n  defaultPeriod,\n  onPeriodChange,\n  shape = \"curved\",\n  periodControl = \"segmented\",\n  valuePrefix = \"$\",\n  className,\n}: LineChartCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const gradientBase = useId();\n  const [innerPeriod, setInnerPeriod] = useState(defaultPeriod ?? periods[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const periodId = period ?? innerPeriod;\n  const active = periods.find((p) => p.id === periodId) ?? periods[0];\n  const data = active?.data ?? [];\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState<number | null>(null);\n\n  const primary = series[0];\n  const index = hovered !== null && hovered < data.length ? hovered : data.length - 1;\n  const point = data[index];\n  const headline = primary ? valueAt(point, primary.key) : 0;\n  const before = index > 0 && primary ? valueAt(data[index - 1], primary.key) : undefined;\n  const money = (n: number) => `${valuePrefix}${formatNumber(n)}`;\n  const curve = shape === \"sharp\" ? \"linear\" : \"monotone\";\n\n  const selectPeriod = (id: string) => {\n    setHovered(null);\n    if (period === undefined) setInnerPeriod(id);\n    onPeriodChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section aria-label={`${title} chart`} className={cx(CHART_CARD_FRAME, \"h-[376px]\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 px-2 pt-2 @md:flex-row @md:items-start @md:justify-between\">\n        <ChartHeadline\n          label={hovered !== null && point ? `${title} · ${point.label}` : title}\n          value={headline}\n          format={money}\n          delta={describeDelta(headline, before)}\n          swapKey={`${periodId}-${hovered ?? \"latest\"}`}\n          reducedMotion={reduced}\n        />\n        <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-2 @md:items-end\">\n          {periods.length > 1 ? (\n            <ChartPeriodControl\n              options={periods}\n              value={periodId}\n              onChange={selectPeriod}\n              variant={periodControl}\n              label={`${title} period`}\n              className=\"self-start @md:self-end\"\n            />\n          ) : null}\n          {series.length > 1 ? <ChartLegend items={series} className=\"@md:pe-1\" /> : null}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"min-h-0 w-full flex-1 pt-3 pe-2\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" initialDimension={{ width: 520, height: 240 }}>\n          <ComposedChart\n            data={data}\n            margin={{ top: 8, right: 8, bottom: 0, left: 0 }}\n            onMouseMove={(state) => {\n              const next = Number(state?.activeTooltipIndex);\n              if (state?.isTooltipActive && Number.isFinite(next)) setHovered(next);\n            }}\n            onMouseLeave={() => setHovered(null)}\n          >\n            <defs>\n              {series.map((s) =>\n                s.area ? (\n                  <linearGradient key={s.key} id={`${gradientBase}-${s.key}`} x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\">\n                    <stop offset=\"0%\" stopColor={toneColor(s.tone)} stopOpacity={0.4} />\n                    <stop offset=\"100%\" stopColor={toneColor(s.tone)} stopOpacity={0} />\n                  </linearGradient>\n                ) : null,\n              )}\n            </defs>\n            <CartesianGrid vertical={false} stroke={GRID_STROKE} strokeDasharray=\"4 4\" />\n            <YAxis\n              width={48}\n              domain={[0, \"auto\"]}\n              tickCount={4}\n              tickFormatter={(v: number) => formatCompact(v, valuePrefix)}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <XAxis\n              dataKey=\"label\"\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tickMargin={10}\n              interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"\n              minTickGap={12}\n              tick={AXIS_TICK}\n            />\n            <Tooltip\n              cursor={CHART_CURSOR}\n              offset={16}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={160}\n              wrapperStyle={{ outline: \"none\", zIndex: 10 }}\n              content={({ active: isActive, activeIndex }) => {\n                const at = data[activeIndex == null ? -1 : Number(activeIndex)];\n                if (!isActive || !at) return null;\n                return (\n                  <ChartTooltipCard\n                    title={at.label}\n                    rows={series.map((s) => ({ key: s.key, label: s.label, tone: s.tone, value: money(valueAt(at, s.key)) }))}\n                  />\n                );\n              }}\n            />\n            {/* Paint in reverse so the first (headline) series sits on top. */}\n            {[...series].reverse().map((s) => (\n              <Fragment key={s.key}>\n                {s.area ? (\n                  <Area\n                    type={curve}\n                    dataKey={s.key}\n                    name={s.label}\n                    stroke=\"none\"\n                    fill={`url(#${gradientBase}-${s.key})`}\n                    activeDot={false}\n                    isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n                    animationDuration={450}\n                  />\n                ) : null}\n                <Line\n                  type={curve}\n                  dataKey={s.key}\n                  name={s.label}\n                  stroke={toneActiveColor(s.tone)}\n                  strokeWidth={2.5}\n                  dot={false}\n                  activeDot={<PulseDot color={toneActiveColor(s.tone)} pulse={!reduced} />}\n                  isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n                  animationDuration={450}\n                />\n              </Fragment>\n            ))}\n          </ComposedChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame when the target changes and on unmount, `useReducedMotion` unsubscribes its media-query listener, and `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer. The card adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` turns off `isAnimationActive` on every Line, Area and the Tooltip, the headline shows its target instead of counting, the number fade gets `motion-reduce:animate-none`, and the active dots render without the ping circle.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reading `activeIndex` with `Number()` without a null check. Recharts passes `null` when nothing is active, `Number(null)` is 0, and the tooltip would show the first point. The content function maps `null` to -1 first.\n- Putting the `Area` after its `Line`. Series paint in source order and the gradient would cover the lower half of the stroke; each series renders Area then Line.\n- Using `@md:` on the card itself. The card is the `@container`, and container variants only match its descendants, so the header reflows with `@md:` but the card height is a single fixed value.\n- Dropping the `<Tooltip>` because the headline already follows the pointer. Without it Recharts never reports `activeTooltipIndex`, so neither the headline nor the active dots move.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `LINE_SERIES`, `LINE_PERIODS` and the `LineSeries`, `LinePoint` and `LinePeriod` types are exported. The defaults end on \"$18,240\" \"+9.4%\" (Weekly), \"$71,450\" \"+8.1%\" (Monthly) and \"$731,000\" \"+13.9%\" (Yearly).\n- `line-chart-card-parts.tsx` holds the frame, headline, period control, tooltip card, legend, stat tiles and pulse dot that the other BoardUI-kit chart cards in this batch reuse.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Revenue\", \"$18,240\" and a lime \"+9.4%\" chip, a segmented Weekly / Monthly / Yearly switch with Weekly selected, and Revenue / Costs legend dots. A lime line over a fading lime area sits above a blue line on dashed horizontal rules. Hovering a week draws a dashed cursor, a pinging dot on both lines and a tooltip with both values, and the headline counts to that week. Choosing Monthly swaps the data and shows \"$71,450\" \"+8.1%\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "link-button": "# Build: Link Button\n\nAn inline text action sized on the same scale as `Button` but without a fill, border or shadow: just the label and optional icons. Passing `href` renders an `<a>`; leaving it out renders a `<button type=\"button\">`, so the same visual works for \"Learn more\" copy links and for handler-driven actions like \"Resend code\". The props type is a discriminated union, so anchor-only and button-only attributes are checked against the element actually rendered.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nContainer-less text action that renders an anchor with href or a button without, with an underline as its only hover cue.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `LinkButtonProps` is `LinkButtonAnchorProps | LinkButtonButtonProps`, discriminated on `href` (a string versus `undefined`). At runtime the component checks `props.href !== undefined` and destructures the matching attribute set, so `ref` is typed to `HTMLAnchorElement` or `HTMLButtonElement` correctly.\n2. Hover never changes colour. `hover:underline` with `underline-offset-3` is the whole hover state; only `:active` darkens (accent 600 to 800 for primary, secondary to primary text for secondary). A link that changes colour and gains an underline at once reads as two states flickering.\n3. Disabled is handled per element. A `<button>` gets the native `disabled` attribute. An `<a>` cannot be disabled natively, so the component drops its `href` (an anchor without `href` is not focusable and not clickable) and sets `aria-disabled`. Both paths style to `text-text-tertiary` with no underline.\n4. Sizes only change type and icon size, never padding: medium and small are Body 1 Medium with 20 or 18px icons, xs is Caption 1 Semibold with 14px icons. `rounded-sm` exists only so the 2px offset focus ring has soft corners around the text.\n5. Icons are component props drawn at the tier's size with `aria-hidden`; arrows get `rtl:rotate-180` via `directionalIconClass`, so a trailing \"→\" points the reading direction in RTL.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `underline offset = 3px (underline-offset-3)`\n  Clears descenders on the 14px body style so \"g\" and \"y\" do not collide with the line, while keeping the underline visually attached to the word.\n- `icon sizes = 20 / 18 / 14 px (medium / small / xs)`\n  Same steps as `Button`, so a link button and a button with the same label and size have matching glyphs.\n- `transition = colors 150ms ease`\n  Only the press colour animates. The underline toggles instantly, which is how text links behave everywhere else.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype LinkButtonProps = LinkButtonAnchorProps | LinkButtonButtonProps\nfunction LinkButton(props: LinkButtonProps): JSX.Element\n// shared: { variant?: \"primary\" | \"secondary\"; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\" | \"xs\"; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; trailingIcon?: IconComponent; children?: ReactNode; className?: string; disabled?: boolean }\n// anchor: Omit<AnchorHTMLAttributes<HTMLAnchorElement>, \"children\" | \"className\"> & { href: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLAnchorElement> }\n// button: Omit<ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>, \"children\" | \"className\"> & { href?: undefined; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }\nconst linkButtonStyles: { base: string; size: Record<Size, string>; icon: Record<Size, string>; variant: Record<Variant, string> }\n```\n\n- `href: string`\n  When set, renders an `<a>`. When omitted, renders a `<button type=\"button\">`.\n- `variant: \"primary\" | \"secondary\"` — default `\"primary\"`\n  Accent text (primary) or secondary text colour (secondary).\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\" | \"xs\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  Type style and icon size: 14px Body Medium with 20 or 18px icons, or 12px Caption Semibold with 14px icons.\n- `leadingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon component drawn before the label.\n- `trailingIcon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  Icon component drawn after the label, typically an arrow.\n- `disabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Native `disabled` on the button form. On the anchor form, removes `href` and sets `aria-disabled`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged through `tailwind-merge`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function LinkButton({\n  variant = \"primary\",\n  size = \"medium\",\n  leadingIcon: Leading,\n  trailingIcon: Trailing,\n  children,\n  className,\n  disabled = false,\n  ...props\n}: LinkButtonProps) {\n  const classes = cx(styles.base, styles.size[size], styles.variant[variant], className);\n  const content = (\n    <>\n      {Leading ? <Leading className={cx(styles.icon[size], directionalIconClass(Leading))} aria-hidden /> : null}\n      {children !== undefined && children !== null && <span>{children}</span>}\n      {Trailing ? <Trailing className={cx(styles.icon[size], directionalIconClass(Trailing))} aria-hidden /> : null}\n    </>\n  );\n\n  if (props.href !== undefined) {\n    const { ref, href, ...anchorProps } = props as LinkButtonAnchorProps;\n    return (\n      <a\n        ref={ref}\n        href={disabled ? undefined : href}\n        aria-disabled={disabled || undefined}\n        className={classes}\n        {...anchorProps}\n      >\n        {content}\n      </a>\n    );\n  }\n\n  const { ref, type = \"button\", ...buttonProps } = props as LinkButtonButtonProps;\n  return (\n    <button ref={ref} type={type} disabled={disabled} className={classes} {...buttonProps}>\n      {content}\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. The only transition is a 150ms colour change on press.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Passing `disabled` and expecting an anchor to keep its `href`. The component removes it on purpose; if the link must stay discoverable, render it enabled and explain the restriction nearby.\n- Using the button form (no `href`) for navigation. Middle-click, \"open in new tab\" and the hover URL preview only work on a real anchor.\n- Adding `hover:text-…` from outside. The design relies on the underline as the only hover cue, and a colour change on top makes inline links in paragraphs look like they are flashing.\n- Wrapping it in another `<a>` or `<button>`. It already renders an interactive element, and nested interactive content is invalid HTML.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `linkButtonStyles` is exported so a router link component can share the exact classes, for example `<Link className={cx(linkButtonStyles.base, linkButtonStyles.variant.primary)}>`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPrimary links render in the accent colour and secondary ones in secondary text, with no box around them. Hovering underlines the label without changing its colour, and pressing darkens it. The trailing arrow sits 4px after the label and, in RTL, points left. The disabled link is grey, shows no underline on hover, cannot be clicked and is skipped by Tab. Tabbing to an enabled one shows a ring with slightly rounded corners around the text.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "looping-scroll-cue": "# Build: Looping Scroll Cue\n\nA short line that falls repeatedly through a clipping tube, the way a hero says \"keep going\" without saying it. It is pure CSS — one keyframe pair on one transform — so it costs nothing after mount and a dozen of them still emit a single rule. The character is entirely in the easing: the line hangs, dives, and hangs again, and the pause between dives is what reads as an instruction.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe hairline that dives down a hero. One keyframe pair, a hard ease, and a seamless loop.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `span` is the tube: `position: relative`, `overflow: hidden`, `block-size: var(--kx-cue-length)`, `inline-size: var(--kx-cue-thickness)`. Everything visible happens inside its clip.\n2. A child span is the line: absolutely positioned at `top: 0`, `block-size: var(--kx-cue-run)` — 40% of the tube — with `background-color: currentColor`, so the cue takes the colour of whatever text it sits beside.\n3. One keyframe pair moves it from `translateY(-100%)` to `translateY(250%)`. Percentages on `translateY` resolve against the element's own height, not the tube's, so with the line at 40% the two ends are exactly flush with the outside of the clip: 40% x 1 above the top, 40% x 2.5 = 100% below it.\n4. The line's resting transform is set to `translateY(-100%)`, identical to the keyframe start, so the element is already outside the clip before the animation attaches and there is no first-frame flash.\n5. The timing function is `cubic-bezier(.76, 0, .24, 1)`, applied over the whole iteration rather than per keyframe — a hard ease-in-out that spends most of the cycle nearly stationary at the two extremes, both of which are off-screen.\n6. The stylesheet is a module-level template literal emitted through React 19's `<style href precedence>`, so it is hoisted to the document head once no matter how many cues render.\n7. Reduced motion is answered twice: a `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block so the server-rendered first paint is already correct, and `data-static` from `useReducedMotion` so a setting changed mid-session takes effect. Both resolve to `animation: none; top: 50%; transform: translateY(-50%)`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `travel = -100% to 250%`\n  Relative to the line, not the tube. 350% of its own height means it is entirely outside the clip at both ends, so the wrap from the last frame back to the first happens while nothing is visible. Animating 0% to 100% instead makes the line vanish at the bottom edge and reappear at the top in the same frame, which is the visible seam every hand-rolled version has.\n- `--kx-cue-run = 40%`\n  The line's height as a fraction of the tube, and the number that makes 250% exactly the far edge. At 100% the line fills the tube and the cue reads as a wipe rather than a falling object; below about 20% it is too short to register as travelling at this speed.\n- `timing function = cubic-bezier(.76, 0, .24, 1)`\n  A hard ease-in-out with almost no linear section. `linear` reads as a loading bar and `ease-in-out` is too soft to punctuate. The steep middle is what compresses the visible part of the cycle into a dive, leaving a deliberate pause between repetitions.\n- `--kx-cue-period = 1.8s`\n  One dive plus the pause. Under about 1.2s the cue nags; past roughly 2.5s a visitor can land, look, and leave without ever seeing it move, which defeats the point of having it.\n- `--kx-cue-length = 3.5rem`\n  Long enough that the dive is legible as travel rather than as a blink, short enough to sit under a line of body copy without becoming a design element in its own right.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LoopingScrollCue(props: { length?: string; thickness?: string; run?: string; period?: number; delay?: number; paused?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `length: string` — default `'3.5rem'`\n  Height of the tube. Any CSS length.\n- `thickness: string` — default `'1px'`\n  Width of the line.\n- `run: string` — default `'40%'`\n  Height of the travelling segment as a percentage of the tube.\n- `period: number` — default `1.8`\n  Seconds for one dive plus its pause.\n- `delay: number` — default `0`\n  Seconds before the first dive. Use it to stagger a row.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Park the line in the middle of the tube instead of running it.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the tube. Set `text-*` to colour the line.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst CUE_CSS = `\n@keyframes kx-scroll-cue-travel {\n  from { transform: translateY(-100%); }\n  to   { transform: translateY(250%); }\n}\n.kx-scroll-cue {\n  position: relative;\n  display: block;\n  overflow: hidden;\n  flex: none;\n  inline-size: var(--kx-cue-thickness, 1px);\n  block-size: var(--kx-cue-length, 3.5rem);\n  color: currentColor;\n}\n.kx-scroll-cue-line {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset-inline: 0;\n  top: 0;\n  block-size: var(--kx-cue-run, 40%);\n  background-color: currentColor;\n  /* Matches the keyframe start, so the line is already outside the clip\n     before the first frame and never flashes at rest. */\n  transform: translateY(-100%);\n  animation: var(--kx-cue-period, 1.8s) cubic-bezier(.76, 0, .24, 1) infinite\n    kx-scroll-cue-travel;\n  animation-delay: var(--kx-cue-delay, 0s);\n}\n/* Parked, not frozen. 'animation-play-state: paused' stops on whatever frame\n   it reached, and for roughly half the cycle that frame is off-screen — the\n   cue would simply vanish. */\n.kx-scroll-cue[data-static='true'] .kx-scroll-cue-line {\n  animation: none;\n  top: 50%;\n  transform: translateY(-50%);\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-scroll-cue .kx-scroll-cue-line {\n    animation: none;\n    top: 50%;\n    transform: translateY(-50%);\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport interface LoopingScrollCueProps {\n  /** Height of the tube. Any CSS length. */\n  length?: string;\n  /** Width of the line. Any CSS length. */\n  thickness?: string;\n  /** Height of the travelling segment, as a percentage of the tube. */\n  run?: string;\n  /** Seconds for one full dive. */\n  period?: number;\n  /** Seconds before the first dive. Stagger a row of cues with this. */\n  delay?: number;\n  /** Park the line in the middle instead of running it. */\n  paused?: boolean;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function LoopingScrollCue({\n  length = '3.5rem',\n  thickness = '1px',\n  run = '40%',\n  period = 1.8,\n  delay = 0,\n  paused = false,\n  className,\n}: LoopingScrollCueProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {/* Hoisted once by React 19 regardless of how many cues are on the page. */}\n      <style href=\"kinetic-looping-scroll-cue\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {CUE_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <span\n        aria-hidden\n        data-static={reduced || paused ? 'true' : 'false'}\n        className={cn('kx-scroll-cue', className)}\n        style={\n          {\n            '--kx-cue-length': length,\n            '--kx-cue-thickness': thickness,\n            '--kx-cue-run': run,\n            '--kx-cue-period': `${period}s`,\n            '--kx-cue-delay': `${delay}s`,\n          } as CSSProperties\n        }\n      >\n        <span className=\"kx-scroll-cue-line\" />\n      </span>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release. No timers, no rAF, no listeners, no observers — the only side effect is the hoisted stylesheet, which React reference-counts and removes when the last cue unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The line stops travelling and parks in the middle of the tube — visible, centred, still a cue. This is enforced both by a `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block in the hoisted stylesheet, which is correct from the first server-rendered paint, and by `data-static` driven from `useReducedMotion`, which catches the setting being changed while the page is open.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating `top` or `margin-top` instead of `transform`. Both are laid out on the main thread every frame; the transform runs on the compositor and is the difference between a cue that stays smooth during a heavy hero animation and one that stutters exactly when the page is busiest.\n- Animating 0% to 100%. The line disappears at the bottom edge and is already back at the top on the next frame — the loop has a visible seam, and it is the single most common way this effect is got wrong.\n- Assuming the percentages are relative to the tube. `translateY` percentages resolve against the transformed element, so changing the line's height silently changes where the travel ends, and a taller line stops exiting the clip.\n- Using `animation-play-state: paused` for a paused state. The line stops on whatever frame it reached, and for roughly half the cycle that frame is outside the clip, so \"paused\" means \"gone\" about half the time it is used.\n- Honouring reduced motion by rendering the animation's end state. The end state here is off-screen, so the cue would correctly render nothing. Park it in the middle instead: the point of a scroll cue is that it is visible.\n- Relying on the `useReducedMotion` hook alone. It reads `false` during server render, so the first client paint animates for a frame before the hook corrects it — the media query in the stylesheet is what covers that window.\n- Leaving the line's resting transform at its default. Without `transform: translateY(-100%)` matching the keyframe start, the line is briefly visible at the top of the tube between paint and the animation's first frame.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Cost is CSS. Nothing runs after mount, there is nothing to clean up, and a row of cues is one rule plus one custom-property block each.\n- Stagger a row with `delay` — the same period at three different offsets reads as a single wave without any coordination.\n- The cue is `aria-hidden`. It is a hint, not content, and a screen reader announcing a decorative line is noise.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe line falls through the tube, disappears, and pauses before falling again — and at no point is it visible in two places or seen jumping back to the top. Watch a full minute: the loop point cannot be identified. Turn on reduced motion and the line is a static hairline in the centre of the tube, not an empty box.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "lottie-ratio-poster": "# Build: Lottie Ratio Poster\n\nA slot for a Lottie that costs nothing until the slot is nearly on screen. The box is reserved at a provisional ratio and filled with a poster drawn in CSS; an IntersectionObserver on the already-sized box starts the fetch; the resolved JSON reports its own composition size in its `w` and `h` fields, the box takes that exact ratio, the player mounts underneath and the poster crossfades away. The readout prices the provisional guess in pixels of layout shift. No Lottie runtime is imported — loading and playback go through a render prop, so the packaged part is the reservation, not the player.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReserves the box, holds a drawn poster, fetches the JSON only in view, then corrects the ratio from the animation's own w and h.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The reserved box is a single `aspect-ratio` declaration on a full-width element, applied on first paint from the provisional ratio. That is what makes everything after it possible: a box with height cannot shift when it fills, and a box with height gives the IntersectionObserver something real to test.\n2. The intersection test runs on that reserved box, with `once: true` and a 240px root margin, so the fetch starts roughly one flick of the wrist before the box arrives. Observing an unreserved, zero-height element instead is the classic failure — a collapsed box intersects the viewport from almost anywhere on the page, so the lazy load fires immediately and the laziness was decorative.\n3. A Lottie JSON carries its composition size at the root: `w` and `h`, alongside `v`, `fr`, `ip`, `op` and `layers`. `w / h` is therefore the exact box before the player has been constructed, let alone measured, so the correction happens on the frame the file lands rather than after a layout pass.\n4. The provisional ratio and the real ratio are both kept, and the difference is reported as `Math.abs(width / ratio - width / actual)` — the number of pixels everything below the box moved, at the current width. It is the same quantity that feeds Cumulative Layout Shift, in the units the user actually experienced.\n5. The in-flight promise is stored in a ref rather than guarded by a boolean. StrictMode double-invokes the Effect: with a `started` flag the first call is abandoned by its own cleanup and the second is blocked by the flag, so the load silently never completes. Reusing the promise means one request and one subscription per Effect run.\n6. The poster is dissolved two animation frames after `ready`, not on the same tick — one frame for React to commit the player and one for the browser to paint it. It is then unmounted on a timer rather than on `transitionend`, because a transition on a subtree that has been hidden never fires the event and the poster would sit over the player permanently.\n7. A zero or missing dimension is treated as a failed load rather than divided by. `w / 0` is Infinity, which collapses `aspect-ratio` and takes the reserved box with it — the exact failure the component exists to prevent, arriving through the component itself.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DEFAULT_RATIO = 16 / 9`\n  The provisional ratio, and named as a guess rather than a default so nobody inherits it by accident. Take the real number out of the design file: against the 3:2 composition in the demo, 16:9 costs 47 pixels of shift at 380px wide.\n- `ROOT_MARGIN = '240px'`\n  About a third of a laptop viewport, or roughly 400ms of ordinary scrolling — enough for a one-megabyte JSON on a decent connection to land before the box arrives. Larger and you are prefetching things the user will never reach; smaller and the poster is still up when the box is centred.\n- `FADE_MS = 320`\n  Poster crossfade. Long enough to read as a dissolve rather than a cut, short enough that the poster is not still visibly over the first second of the animation.\n- `FADE_SLACK = 60`\n  Extra milliseconds before the poster is unmounted. The transition is driven by a class change, so the removal timer and the transition clock do not share an origin; without the slack the poster can be pulled at opacity 0.04.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LottieRatioPoster<T extends LottieRatioSource>(props: { load: () => Promise<T>; children: (state: { source: T; reduced: boolean }) => ReactNode; ratio?: number; rootMargin?: string; fade?: number; label?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `load: () => Promise<T>`\n  Fetches the animation JSON. Called at most once, and only once the reserved box is within `rootMargin`.\n- `children: (state: { source: T; reduced: boolean }) => ReactNode`\n  Render prop for the player. Mounted absolutely inside the reserved box.\n- `ratio: number` — default `16 / 9`\n  Provisional width / height, used until the JSON reports its own.\n- `rootMargin: string` — default `'240px'`\n  How early to start fetching, as an IntersectionObserver root margin.\n- `fade: number` — default `320`\n  Poster crossfade in milliseconds.\n- `label: string` — default `'animation'`\n  Short text on the poster.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!inView) return;\n  let alive = true;\n\n  request.current ??= loadRef.current();\n  setPhase((prev) => (prev === 'waiting' ? 'loading' : prev));\n\n  request.current.then(\n    (data) => {\n      if (alive) apply(data);\n    },\n    () => {\n      if (alive) setPhase('failed');\n    },\n  );\n\n  return () => {\n    alive = false;\n  };\n}, [inView, apply]);\n\n// The real ratio comes from the JSON's own composition size, so the box is\n// exact from the frame the file lands rather than from the frame the player\n// finishes measuring itself.\nconst actual = source ? source.w / source.h : null;\nconst applied = actual ?? ratio;\n\n// What the guess cost, in the units the user experiences it in. Everything\n// below this box moves by exactly this many pixels at the current width.\nconst shift =\n  actual !== null && width > 0 ? Math.abs(width / ratio - width / actual) : 0;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The intersection subscription is removed by `useInView`, which also unobserves after the first hit because it is used with `once`. Both timers are cleared and both `requestAnimationFrame` handles cancelled on teardown. The load promise cannot be aborted, so it is neutered instead: an `alive` flag in the Effect closure means a late resolution calls no setter after teardown. The promise itself is deliberately retained in a ref across Effect runs, which is what makes the request happen once rather than once per run.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The poster loses its shimmer and the crossfade collapses to a zero-duration swap, so the poster is replaced rather than dissolved. The fetch is unaffected — reduced motion is a request about movement, not about content, and withholding the animation entirely would leave a permanent placeholder where the user expected something. The render prop is handed `reduced: true` so the player itself can park on its last frame, which is the conventional still for a Lottie.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Lazy-loading against an unreserved box. A zero-height element intersects the viewport from nearly anywhere in the document, so the observer fires on mount and the fetch was never deferred. Reserve first, observe second — in that order, in the same element.\n- Waiting for the player to report the size. `lottie-web` measures its own SVG after construction, which is after the layout has already happened at the wrong ratio. The JSON's `w` and `h` are available the instant the fetch resolves, before anything is instantiated.\n- Guarding a one-shot fetch with a boolean ref. StrictMode runs the Effect, tears it down and runs it again; the first call is discarded by its own cleanup and the second is refused by the flag, so the promise never lands and the poster stays up in development only. Hold the promise itself in the ref.\n- Dissolving the poster in the same commit that mounts the player. The player has not painted, so the crossfade runs over an empty box and reads as a flash. Two `requestAnimationFrame` calls put it after the paint.\n- Unmounting the poster on `transitionend`. If the subtree is hidden mid-transition — a route change, a tab switch, an `Activity` boundary — no event is delivered and the poster is stranded on top of the player. Use a timer.\n- Dividing by a dimension you did not check. A malformed or truncated JSON with `h: 0` produces an Infinite ratio, `aspect-ratio` gives up, and the reserved box collapses to nothing.\n- Shipping the Lottie runtime for a decoration. `lottie-web` is around 60KB gzipped before any animation data; if the thing being animated is one badge in a footer, the runtime costs more than the effect is worth, and this component is where you notice.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No Lottie runtime is imported and no `.json` is committed. `load` is any function returning the parsed animation, and `children` is where the player goes, so the same slot works for `lottie-web`, `@lottiefiles/dotlottie-web`, a video, or a canvas.\n- Type the resolved JSON yourself and pass it as the generic: `LottieRatioPoster<MyLottie>` gives the render prop the full object rather than only `w` and `h`.\n- For reduced motion the render prop receives `reduced: true`. The conventional response with `lottie-web` is to construct it with `autoplay: false` and call `anim.goToAndStop(anim.getDuration(true) - 1, true)` — `getDuration(true)` returns frames, so the last frame is one less than that.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn load the box already occupies its full reserved height, with a poster in it and a readout reading `reserved 1.78 · waiting for viewport`. Scroll it into view and the readout goes to `fetching json` roughly a scroll-flick before the box arrives. When the JSON lands the box snaps to 1.50, the player fades up under the dissolving poster, and the readout names the shift in pixels. Set the provisional ratio to 1.5 and reload: same sequence, and the readout says `no shift`. With reduced motion the poster does not shimmer and the swap is instant.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "magnetic-button": "# Build: Magnetic Pointer Button\n\nThe element translates toward the pointer by a fraction of its offset from centre, and the label translates further still, so the text appears to lead the box it sits in. Release is an elastic overshoot rather than a linear return, which is the difference between \"springy\" and \"sliding back\".\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA button that leans toward the cursor and springs back, with the label leading the box.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A window-level pointermove handler measures the pointer against the button's live bounding rect, not a cached one — the element is being translated by this same effect, so a rect captured on mount goes stale immediately.\n2. Inside a hit area padded past the border, the offset from centre is scaled by `strength` and pushed into four `gsap.quickTo` setters: x and y for the button, x and y for the label at `labelStrength - 1` so it moves relative to its already-moving parent.\n3. Outside that area, a single `gsap.to` with `elastic.out(1, 0.4)` and `overwrite: \"auto\"` returns both nodes to zero, cancelling any in-flight follow tween.\n4. Cleanup kills tweens before clearing transforms, so nothing writes to the node after React releases it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `strength = 0.35`\n  Fraction of pointer offset the button travels. Below ~0.2 the effect is invisible; above ~0.6 the button outruns the cursor and reads as a rendering fault rather than an affordance.\n- `labelStrength = 1.6`\n  The label moves 0.6x the button offset *on top of* the button's own movement. That parallax between text and border is what makes the surface feel elastic instead of rigid.\n- `padding = 24`\n  Pixels of grace beyond the border. Without it the effect engages exactly at the edge, and the button visibly jumps as it snaps into the field.\n- `FOLLOW_DURATION = 0.35`\n  quickTo duration. Short enough to feel attached to the cursor, long enough to smooth the 8-16 ms gaps between pointer events.\n- `RETURN_DURATION = 0.6`\n  Elastic return. Shorter than this and the overshoot has no room to read as a spring.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MagneticButton(props: { children: ReactNode; strength?: number; labelStrength?: number; padding?: number; className?: string; onClick?: () => void; disabled?: boolean }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Button label. Wrapped in the span that receives the parallax offset.\n- `strength: number` — default `0.35`\n  Fraction of the pointer offset the button travels.\n- `labelStrength: number` — default `1.6`\n  Label travel relative to the button. 1 disables parallax.\n- `padding: number` — default `24`\n  Pixels beyond the border that still count as near.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the button element via `cn`.\n- `onClick: () => void`\n  Standard click handler.\n- `disabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Disables the button and skips the effect entirely.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst RETURN_DURATION = 0.6;\nconst FOLLOW_DURATION = 0.35;\n\nexport function MagneticButton({\n  children,\n  strength = 0.35,\n  labelStrength = 1.6,\n  padding = 24,\n  className,\n  onClick,\n  disabled = false,\n}: MagneticButtonProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const label = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const text = label.current;\n    if (!el || !text || disabled) return;\n\n    // A coarse pointer has no hover state: the effect would only ever fire on\n    // touch-down, which reads as a glitch rather than an affordance.\n    const fine = window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)');\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');\n    if (!fine.matches || reduced.matches) return;\n\n    // quickTo builds one reusable tween per property. The naive version calls\n    // gsap.to() on every pointermove, which allocates a tween per event (~120\n    // a second) and makes each one fight the last for the same property.\n    const xTo = gsap.quickTo(el, 'x', { duration: FOLLOW_DURATION, ease: 'power3.out' });\n    const yTo = gsap.quickTo(el, 'y', { duration: FOLLOW_DURATION, ease: 'power3.out' });\n    const labelXTo = gsap.quickTo(text, 'x', { duration: FOLLOW_DURATION, ease: 'power3.out' });\n    const labelYTo = gsap.quickTo(text, 'y', { duration: FOLLOW_DURATION, ease: 'power3.out' });\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      // Read the rect per move, not once on mount: the element is being\n      // translated by this very effect, and any scroll or reflow invalidates\n      // a cached rect. Stale centres make the button drift away permanently.\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const cx = rect.left + rect.width / 2;\n      const cy = rect.top + rect.height / 2;\n\n      const withinX = Math.abs(e.clientX - cx) < rect.width / 2 + padding;\n      const withinY = Math.abs(e.clientY - cy) < rect.height / 2 + padding;\n\n      if (withinX && withinY) {\n        const dx = (e.clientX - cx) * strength;\n        const dy = (e.clientY - cy) * strength;\n        xTo(dx);\n        yTo(dy);\n        labelXTo(dx * (labelStrength - 1));\n        labelYTo(dy * (labelStrength - 1));\n        return;\n      }\n\n      // Release. Overshoot on the way home is what sells the elastic.\n      gsap.to([el, text], {\n        x: 0,\n        y: 0,\n        duration: RETURN_DURATION,\n        ease: 'elastic.out(1, 0.4)',\n        overwrite: 'auto',\n      });\n    };\n\n    // Listen on the window, not the element. On the element, `pointerleave`\n    // fires the moment the button moves out from under the cursor — it\n    // chases itself away and stutters at the edge of the hit area.\n    window.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n\n    return () => {\n      window.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      // Kill before clearing: an in-flight tween would otherwise write a\n      // transform back onto a node React has already handed to something else.\n      gsap.killTweensOf([el, text]);\n      gsap.set([el, text], { clearProps: 'transform' });\n    };\n  }, [strength, labelStrength, padding, disabled]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the window listener, calls `gsap.killTweensOf` on both nodes, then clears the transform with `gsap.set(..., { clearProps: \"transform\" })`. Kill precedes clear so no in-flight tween can restore it.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The effect never attaches when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` matches. The button keeps its colour transition on hover, so it still responds — it just does not move.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Listening on the element instead of the window. `pointerleave` fires the instant the button translates out from under the cursor, so it chases itself away and stutters along the edge of its own hit area.\n- Calling `gsap.to()` per pointermove. That allocates a fresh tween ~120 times a second and each one fights the previous for the same property. `quickTo` reuses one tween per property.\n- Caching the bounding rect on mount. The element moves and the page scrolls; a stale centre makes the button drift further away with every pass.\n- Skipping `overwrite: \"auto\"` on the return tween — the follow tween keeps running underneath and the button never actually reaches zero.\n- Leaving the transform behind on unmount. Clear it, but kill tweens first, or an in-flight tween writes it straight back.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated behind `(pointer: fine)`. On touch there is no hover, so the effect would only fire on press, which reads as a glitch.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nApproach from any side and the button leans toward the cursor with the label leading it. Leave the padded area and it springs back past zero once before settling. Unmount mid-approach and no transform remains on the node. On a touch device it does not move at all.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "manufactured-scroll-range": "# Build: Manufactured Scroll Range\n\nSome content has no height. A ring of projects, a canvas scene, a stack of captions — one screen of markup that wants to be a page of interaction. The fix is to manufacture the range: a container sized `count x viewports` screens with a sticky viewport inside it, and `scrolled / scrollable` as the single input. One screen of scroll then means exactly one item, with no per-item measurement to keep in sync.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn empty box N screens tall turns a one-screen scene into a scrollable sequence, with progress as a CSS variable and the index as state.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The container height is written in the scroller's own pixels — `hostHeight * (1 + count * viewports)` — and the sticky child is written at exactly `hostHeight`, so the difference between them is the scroll range and nothing else. Pixels rather than `vh` because inside a scrollable box, viewport units measure a different element entirely.\n2. Progress is `(hostTop - containerTop) / (total - hostHeight)`, read from live rects on the frame it is used. Nothing is cached, so a font swap, an image landing above, or a resize corrects itself on the next frame with no invalidation step.\n3. The two outputs are separated by cost. Continuous progress goes out as `--range-progress` and `--range-position` custom properties and to an optional `onFrame` callback — sixty times a second, never through React. The discrete index goes through `useState`, and only on the frame it changes.\n4. Progress is quantised to four decimals before writing. Below that nothing downstream can render differently, so a stationary scroller performs zero style writes rather than 60 per second.\n5. The index uses `floor`, not `round`, so item `i` owns `[i/count, (i+1)/count)` — equal slots, and the last item owns the end of the range instead of a half-slot.\n6. A hysteresis band of 0.04 of a slot guards the index commit. A trackpad resting exactly on a boundary otherwise flips the index every frame, and each flip is a full React re-render of the viewport contents.\n7. The render prop receives `{ index, count }`, so the child tree re-renders on item change only — the per-frame value is available to the same tree as an inherited custom property.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `viewports = 1`\n  Screens of scroll per item. One is the value that makes the range legible: a full flick moves exactly one item, which is the mapping users infer within two gestures. Below ~0.6 items blur past; above ~2 the sequence feels padded.\n- `HYSTERESIS = 0.04`\n  Fraction of a slot the position must travel past a boundary before the index commits. Sized above trackpad jitter and sub-pixel rect noise, and far below anything a user could intend as a move.\n- `progress quantisation = 4 decimal places`\n  One ten-thousandth of the range is below the resolution of any downstream transform or colour mix, so comparing the formatted string lets an idle scroller skip the DOM write entirely.\n- `range height = hostHeight * (1 + count * viewports)`\n  The extra `1` is the sticky viewport's own screen. Without it the last item is only reachable at the exact bottom of the scroller and can never be centred.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ManufacturedScrollRange(props: { count: number; viewports?: number; children: (state: { index: number; count: number }) => ReactNode; onFrame?: (state: { progress: number; position: number; index: number }) => void; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number`\n  How many items the manufactured range represents.\n- `viewports: number` — default `1`\n  Screens of scroll per item.\n- `children: (state: { index: number; count: number }) => ReactNode`\n  Rendered inside the sticky viewport; called again only when the index changes.\n- `onFrame: (state: ScrollRangeState) => void`\n  Per-frame progress, for work that must not go through React.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container. Omit to auto-detect the nearest scrollable ancestor.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer container.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ManufacturedScrollRange({\n  count,\n  viewports = 1,\n  children,\n  onFrame,\n  scroller,\n  className,\n}: ManufacturedScrollRangeProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const viewport = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n  const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);\n  const indexRef = useRef(0);\n  const lastHeight = useRef(0);\n  const lastProgress = useRef('');\n\n  useRaf((_dt) => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    const viewportEl = viewport.current;\n    if (!rootEl || !viewportEl || count <= 0) return;\n\n    const host = scroller?.current ?? nearestScroller(rootEl);\n    const hostIsRoot = host === document.documentElement;\n    const hostTop = hostIsRoot ? 0 : host.getBoundingClientRect().top;\n    const hostHeight = hostIsRoot ? window.innerHeight : host.clientHeight;\n    if (hostHeight === 0) return;\n\n    // The range is measured in the scroller's own pixels, not `vh`. Inside a\n    // scrollable box, viewport units describe a completely different element.\n    const total = Math.round(hostHeight * (1 + count * viewports));\n    if (total !== lastHeight.current) {\n      lastHeight.current = total;\n      rootEl.style.height = `${total}px`;\n      viewportEl.style.height = `${hostHeight}px`;\n    }\n\n    const travel = total - hostHeight;\n    const progress = travel > 0 ? clamp((hostTop - rootEl.getBoundingClientRect().top) / travel, 0, 1) : 0;\n    const position = progress * count;\n\n    // Continuous value: out as a custom property, never as state. Quantised\n    // so an idle scroller stops writing to the DOM entirely.\n    const text = progress.toFixed(4);\n    if (text !== lastProgress.current) {\n      lastProgress.current = text;\n      rootEl.style.setProperty('--range-progress', text);\n      rootEl.style.setProperty('--range-position', position.toFixed(3));\n    }\n\n    // Discrete value: equal slots, `floor` not `round`, so item i owns\n    // [i/count, (i+1)/count) and the last item owns the end of the range.\n    const candidate = clamp(Math.floor(position), 0, count - 1);\n    if (candidate !== indexRef.current) {\n      // Distance past the boundary being crossed — going forward that is the\n      // candidate's own edge, going back it is the current item's edge.\n      const boundary = candidate > indexRef.current ? candidate : indexRef.current;\n      if (Math.abs(position - boundary) > HYSTERESIS) {\n        indexRef.current = candidate;\n        setIndex(candidate);\n      }\n    }\n\n    onFrame?.({ progress, position, index: indexRef.current });\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes from the shared loop on unmount and no other listener, observer or timer is registered. The inline height and the custom properties are written on nodes React owns and disappear with them.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The range is layout, not animation: it is how the sequence is navigated, so collapsing it would remove access to the content rather than remove motion. The component itself animates nothing — it publishes numbers. Consumers should read the discrete `index` under reduced motion and drive cuts, not `--range-progress` and continuous parallax.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sizing the spacer in `vh` while the component lives inside a scrollable box. The height comes from the window and the progress comes from the box, so the sequence finishes early or never finishes — and it looks correct only when the box happens to be full-height.\n- Pushing continuous progress through `setState`. That is a React render per frame for a value that only ever feeds a transform; the tree re-reconciles 60 times a second to move one bar.\n- Committing the index with no hysteresis. Resting on a boundary makes the index oscillate, which reads as flicker and, if items fetch anything, as a request storm.\n- Using `round` for the index. Items 0 and count-1 get half-width slots, so the first and last feel twice as fast as the middle for no reason the user can see.\n- Putting the spacer as a sibling *before* the sticky element. Sticky positioning is bounded by the parent, so the viewport unsticks as soon as the spacer scrolls past and the sequence stops halfway.\n- Deriving progress from `scroller.scrollTop / scrollHeight`. That is the whole document's progress, not the section's, so the mapping breaks the moment anything is added above or below.\n- Forgetting `overflow: hidden` on the sticky viewport. Content sized to the viewport bleeds over neighbouring sections during the range and paints outside its own box.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The scroll container is auto-detected by walking ancestors for a scrollable `overflow-y`, so the component works unchanged on a normal page; pass `scroller` when the container is created later or ambiguous.\n- Custom properties inherit, so anything inside the render prop can read `--range-progress` without prop drilling and without a re-render.\n- For a ring rather than a list, replace the `clamp` on the index with `((i % count) + count) % count` and let the position wrap.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOne screen of scroll advances exactly one item. Resting the pointer on a slot boundary does not flip the index back and forth. The progress bar moves continuously while the item title changes in steps. Resizing the scroller keeps the current item on screen. React DevTools shows a render only when the item changes, not per frame.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "marketing-dashboard": "# Build: Marketing Dashboard\n\nThe BoardUI marketing dashboard as one working page. The kit sidebar switches views in local state, and the header carries breadcrumbs, a live notification bell, a Filters menu that shows and hides each widget and a New campaign dialog. Under it sit four stat cards, an acquisition funnel, spend by channel, traffic sources, ad spend with click-through rate, visitors by source and a campaigns table that filters, sorts and pages.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full marketing dashboard page: in-block sidebar views, key metrics, funnel, channel spend, traffic, ad spend, visitors and campaigns.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The frame comes from `home-dashboard-shell.tsx`: `DashboardFrame` is the `h-full w-full` sidebar and scrolling main column, and it turns nav clicks into view keys. `DashboardHeader` draws the title, the crumbs and the bell. `\"marketing\"` renders the dashboard, and any other key renders `DashboardViewPlaceholder` with a \"Back to Marketing\" button.\n2. Filters is the shared `DashboardWidgetMenu` over `useWidgetToggles`, with seven widget ids. The funnel, spend and traffic row and the ad spend and visitors row each count their visible cards and set one, two or three columns at `lg`, so hiding a card widens the rest. When every widget is hidden, `DashboardEmptyGrid` offers \"Show all widgets\".\n3. New campaign opens a controlled `Modal`. A name and a budget above zero are required, and submit stays disabled until both are valid. Submitting prepends a draft campaign with nothing spent, stamped `today`, so it sorts first under the table's default updated-newest order. It also closes the dialog and fires a success toast.\n4. The stat cards are derived in a `useMemo` over the campaign rows. Ad spend, Impressions and Conversions are fixed figures for last month, and Draft campaigns counts the rows whose status is draft, with the row total as its caption.\n5. Every chart card is a kit item fed with fixed ranges. The funnel has three ranges of Visits, Sign-ups, Trials and Customers. Spend by channel is a segmented gauge over four channels formatted as dollars. Traffic sources tabs between channels, campaigns and landing pages. Ad spend pairs monthly bars with a CTR line on its own axis, and Visitors stacks organic, paid and social traffic.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `today = 2026-11-05`\n  Date stamped on new campaigns, fixed so server and client renders agree and new rows sort above the newest sample (2026-11-04).\n- `campaigns pageSize = 6`\n  Fourteen sample campaigns give three pages, so paging is visible and works.\n- `sample drafts = 3 of 14`\n  The Draft campaigns card starts at 3 with \"14 total\" and moves to 4 and \"15 total\" after one new campaign.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MarketingDashboard(props: { view?: string; defaultView?: string; onViewChange?: (key: string) => void; campaigns?: readonly Campaign[]; today?: string; defaultWidgets?: readonly MarketingWidget[]; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `view: string`\n  Nav key on screen. Controlled; pair with onViewChange.\n- `defaultView: string` — default `\"marketing\"`\n  Initial nav key when uncontrolled. \"marketing\" is the dashboard; any other key shows the placeholder view.\n- `onViewChange: (key: string) => void`\n  Called with the new key when a nav row, a crumb or a back button is used.\n- `campaigns: readonly Campaign[]` — default `MARKETING_CAMPAIGNS`\n  Initial rows of the campaigns table (fourteen samples); new campaigns stay in local state.\n- `today: string` — default `\"2026-11-05\"`\n  ISO date stamped on campaigns created through the dialog.\n- `defaultWidgets: readonly MarketingWidget[]` — default `all seven`\n  Widgets shown on first render: kpis, funnel, spend, traffic, adspend, visitors, campaigns.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the frame root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function MarketingDashboard({\n  view,\n  defaultView = 'marketing',\n  onViewChange,\n  campaigns: initialCampaigns = MARKETING_CAMPAIGNS,\n  today = '2026-11-05',\n  defaultWidgets = ALL_WIDGETS,\n  className,\n}: MarketingDashboardProps) {\n  const [innerView, setInnerView] = useState(defaultView);\n  const current = view ?? innerView;\n  const go = useCallback(\n    (key: string) => {\n      if (view === undefined) setInnerView(key);\n      onViewChange?.(key);\n    },\n    [view, onViewChange],\n  );\n\n  const widgets = useWidgetToggles(ALL_WIDGETS, defaultWidgets);\n  const shown = widgets.isShown;\n  const [campaigns, setCampaigns] = useState<Campaign[]>(() => [...initialCampaigns]);\n  const [formOpen, setFormOpen] = useState(false);\n  const nextId = useRef(1);\n\n  // Ad spend, impressions and conversions are last month's figures; the drafts count follows the table.\n  const stats = useMemo<Stat[]>(() => {\n    const drafts = campaigns.filter((c) => c.status === 'draft').length;\n    return [\n      { icon: RiMoneyDollarCircleLine, label: 'Ad spend', value: '$24,380', delta: '+8.4%', deltaColor: 'lime' },\n      { icon: RiEyeLine, label: 'Impressions', value: '1.94M', delta: '+12.6%', deltaColor: 'lime' },\n      { icon: RiUserFollowLine, label: 'Conversions', value: '1,286', delta: '+5.2%', deltaColor: 'lime' },\n      { icon: RiMegaphoneLine, label: 'Draft campaigns', value: String(drafts), delta: `${campaigns.length} total`, deltaColor: 'neutral' },\n    ];\n  }, [campaigns]);\n\n  const addCampaign = (draft: Pick<Campaign, 'name' | 'channel' | 'objective' | 'budget'>) => {\n    const campaign: Campaign = {\n      ...draft,\n      id: `cmp-new-${nextId.current++}`,\n      status: 'draft',\n      spent: 0,\n      impressions: 0,\n      clicks: 0,\n      updated: today,\n    };\n    setCampaigns((prev) => [campaign, ...prev]);\n    setFormOpen(false);\n    toast('Campaign created', { status: 'success', description: `${campaign.name} is saved as a draft.` });\n  };\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners in the page; the id counter is a ref and everything else is React state. The composed charts, overlays and the Toaster clean up their own timers and frames.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The page adds no motion of its own. The composed chart cards follow `useReducedMotion` and draw at their final state, and the dropdown, dialog and toasts fall back to instant open and close.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Passing Remix icons straight into `BarListItem.icon`. Their type includes a class component whose props do not match the bar list's narrow icon type, so the page wraps each one in a small function component.\n- Giving a new campaign an older date. `CampaignsTable` sorts by updated date, newest first, by default, so a row stamped before the newest sample would land on a later page.\n- Opening Filters and then scrolling. The menu is a react-aria popover, which closes on scroll, so its toggles should be used before moving down the page.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Every card is an existing kit item (`StatCards`, `FunnelChartCard`, `SpeedometerChartCard`, `ChannelTabsBarList`, `ComboChartCard`, `AreaChartCard`, `CampaignsTable`, `Modal`, `Select`, `Input`). The page owns the wiring, the sample data and the campaign form.\n- The status filter, channel filter, sort and paging are built into `CampaignsTable`, so the page only owns the rows.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280x800 the sidebar shows Marketing selected and the main column shows the Marketing title, four stat cards, a row of Acquisition funnel, Spend by channel and Traffic sources, then Ad spend and Visitors, then the campaigns table. Clicking Home in the sidebar shows the Home placeholder, and \"Back to Marketing\" returns. Unticking Visitors in Filters removes that card and the button reads 6/7. Creating a campaign puts it at the top of the table as a draft, moves Draft campaigns from 3 to 4 and shows a toast.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "matchmedia-layout-teardown": "# Build: matchMedia Layout Teardown\n\nA fanned card layout on desktop and a plain column on mobile, with the entire desktop branch registered inside `gsap.matchMedia()`. GSAP records every `set`, tween and returned cleanup made inside the callback and reverts them the moment the query stops matching, so crossing the breakpoint leaves no inline transforms, no live listeners and no half-applied layout behind.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTwo layouts behind one gsap.matchMedia block. Cross the breakpoint and the desktop branch reverts itself.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `gsap.matchMedia().add({ isDesktop, isMobile, reduced }, self => …)` uses the object form: several queries are evaluated at once and arrive as booleans on `self.conditions`, so one callback branches on width and on motion preference together instead of needing an `add` per combination. GSAP re-runs the callback when any of the queries flips, which means toggling the OS \"reduce motion\" setting reconfigures the layout live, without a reload.\n2. The desktop branch positions the cards entirely through `gsap.set` — `position: absolute`, `left`, `top`, `rotation`, `zIndex` — rather than through breakpoint utility classes. That is deliberate: the breakpoint then exists in exactly one place, the query string, and the base markup can stay a plain flex column that doubles as the mobile layout.\n3. Positions are percentages of the container (`left: 50 + offset * spread`%, `top: 50 + |offset| * arc`%) with `xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50` for centring. Because percentages resolve against the container box, the fan rescales on resize with no measuring pass, no `ResizeObserver` and no resize listener inside the branch.\n4. Layout and interaction are kept on separate transform channels. `left`/`top` carry the fan, the entrance tween owns `y` and `autoAlpha`, and the pointer parallax writes only `x` via `gsap.quickTo`. Two animations sharing one property means one of them silently loses, usually the one that started first.\n5. The callback returns a cleanup function that removes the pointer listeners. GSAP invokes it when the branch stops matching, not only on unmount, so a desktop-only listener cannot survive into the mobile layout.\n6. On teardown the effect calls `mm.revert()`, which restores the inline styles GSAP wrote — including the `position: absolute` — so the authored column comes back unmodified. `mm.kill()` would stop the tweens but leave the fan frozen exactly where it was.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `breakpoint = 768`\n  Where the fan takes over. The mobile query is deliberately `max-width: breakpoint - 1` rather than `max-width: breakpoint`: overlapping queries both match at exactly 768px and the two branches register at once, which is how a card ends up absolutely positioned inside a column.\n- `spread = 15`\n  Percent of container width per card step. At this value a five-card fan spans about 60% of the box with visible overlap. Past ~22% the outer cards leave the container; under ~8% they stack almost exactly and the fan reads as a printing error.\n- `arc = 4`\n  Percent of container height each step drops. A small positive arc puts the centre card highest, which is what makes the row read as a hand of cards rather than a straight strip. Zero is flat; past ~10% the outer cards fall out of the box bottom.\n- `tilt = 5`\n  Degrees per step, applied about a `50% 90%` origin so the cards rotate around their lower edge like a fan pivot. Rotating about the centre swings the tops and bottoms in opposite directions and the arc stops reading.\n- `parallax = 26`\n  Maximum pixels the outermost card travels with the pointer, scaled by its distance from centre. Enough to give the fan depth; past ~50px the cards visibly separate from their own shadows and the layout looks unstable.\n- `ENTER_DURATION / ENTER_STAGGER = 0.7 / 0.06`\n  A five-card fan finishes in 0.7 + 4 x 0.06 = 0.94 s. Longer staggers make the last card arrive after the user has already started reading; shorter ones collapse the sequence into a single flash.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MatchMediaLayoutTeardown(props: { children: ReactNode; breakpoint?: number; spread?: number; arc?: number; tilt?: number; parallax?: number; className?: string; itemClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One card per child. Each is wrapped in the element GSAP positions.\n- `breakpoint: number` — default `768`\n  Pixel width at which the fan layout takes over.\n- `spread: number` — default `15`\n  Horizontal step per card, as a percentage of container width.\n- `arc: number` — default `4`\n  Vertical drop per step from centre, as a percentage of container height.\n- `tilt: number` — default `5`\n  Degrees of rotation per step from centre.\n- `parallax: number` — default `26`\n  Maximum pointer parallax in pixels for the outermost card.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container.\n- `itemClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every card wrapper. Give the cards a width here — the fan does not measure them.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const root = rootRef.current;\n  if (!root || count === 0) return;\n\n  const mm = gsap.matchMedia();\n\n  mm.add(\n    {\n      // Object form: every query is evaluated, and the results arrive as\n      // booleans on `self.conditions`. One callback can therefore branch on\n      // several axes (width AND motion preference) instead of needing a\n      // separate `add` per combination, and GSAP re-runs it whenever ANY of\n      // them flips — including the motion query, so a user toggling\n      // \"reduce motion\" at runtime gets the static layout without a reload.\n      isDesktop: `(min-width: ${breakpoint}px)`,\n      isMobile: `(max-width: ${breakpoint - 1}px)`,\n      reduced: '(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)',\n    },\n    (self) => {\n      // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` makes every lookup `boolean | undefined`,\n      // and `conditions` itself is optional. Default rather than assert.\n      const conditions = self.conditions ?? {};\n      const isDesktop = conditions.isDesktop ?? false;\n      const reduced = conditions.reduced ?? false;\n\n      // Mobile registers nothing at all. The base markup already IS the\n      // mobile layout, so there is no work to do and, more importantly,\n      // nothing for the revert to have to undo when we come back.\n      if (!isDesktop) return;\n\n      const cards = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-mm-card]'));\n      const mid = (cards.length - 1) / 2;\n\n      cards.forEach((card, i) => {\n        const offset = i - mid;\n        const away = Math.abs(offset);\n        gsap.set(card, {\n          position: 'absolute',\n          // Percentages of the CONTAINER, so the fan rescales with the box\n          // and no resize listener is needed inside this branch.\n          left: `${50 + offset * spread}%`,\n          top: `${50 + away * arc}%`,\n          xPercent: -50,\n          yPercent: -50,\n          rotation: offset * tilt,\n          zIndex: 100 - Math.round(away * 10),\n          transformOrigin: '50% 90%',\n        });\n      });\n\n      if (reduced) return;\n\n      gsap.from(cards, {\n        y: ENTER_Y,\n        autoAlpha: 0,\n        duration: ENTER_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power3.out',\n        stagger: ENTER_STAGGER,\n      });\n\n      // Parallax lives on `x`; the entrance owns `y` and `autoAlpha`. Two\n      // animations on one property means one of them silently loses.\n      const setters = cards.map((card) =>\n        gsap.quickTo(card, 'x', { duration: PARALLAX_DURATION, ease: 'power3' }),\n      );\n\n      const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n        const rect = root.getBoundingClientRect();\n        const norm = (e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width - 0.5;\n        setters.forEach((setter, i) => {\n          const depth = mid === 0 ? 0 : (i - mid) / mid;\n          setter(norm * parallax * depth);\n        });\n      };\n      const onLeave = () => setters.forEach((setter) => setter(0));\n\n      root.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      root.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n      // A cleanup returned from the callback runs when this branch stops\n      // matching, not only on unmount. Without it, the desktop listener\n      // survives into the mobile layout and keeps writing transforms to\n      // elements that are back in the column.\n      return () => {\n        root.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n        root.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  // `revert()` restores the inline styles GSAP wrote — including the\n  // `position: absolute` — so the base column comes back exactly as\n  // authored. `kill()` would stop the tweens but leave the fan frozen in\n  // place.\n  return () => {\n    mm.revert();\n  };\n}, [count, breakpoint, spread, arc, tilt, parallax]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `mm.revert()` on unmount reverts every recorded `set` and tween back to the pre-branch inline styles and runs each branch's returned cleanup, which removes the pointermove and pointerleave listeners. Nothing else is registered, so there is nothing else to release.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` is one of the queries in the same `add` block. When it matches, the desktop branch still composes the full fan with `gsap.set` and then returns early — no entrance tween, no pointer listener. The layout is identical; only the movement is gone. Because it is a matchMedia condition rather than a mount-time read, changing the OS setting reconfigures a mounted component immediately.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Registering animations once at mount and relying on CSS to hide them below the breakpoint. Resize past a breakpoint and a desktop-only ScrollTrigger keeps pinning on mobile, leaving a section stuck: the pin spacer is still in the document, the section will not scroll past, and nothing in the mobile stylesheet can undo it because the offending styles are inline. `matchMedia` reverts them instead.\n- Using `mm.kill()` instead of `mm.revert()`. Kill stops the tweens but leaves every inline style GSAP wrote, so the fan freezes in place and the column never comes back.\n- Overlapping queries: `(min-width: 768px)` paired with `(max-width: 768px)`. Both match at exactly 768px, both branches register, and the mobile teardown fights the desktop setup for one very confusing breakpoint width.\n- Adding an event listener inside the callback without returning a cleanup. The listener outlives the branch, so a desktop pointer handler keeps writing transforms to cards that are back in the mobile column.\n- Putting the responsive layout in Tailwind breakpoint classes AND the animation in matchMedia. The breakpoint now exists in two places and they drift; the classic symptom is a layout that switches at 768px while its animation switches at 1024px.\n- Measuring the container inside the branch and hard-coding pixel offsets. The branch does not re-run on resize — only on a query change — so the fan is correct at the width it was registered at and wrong at every other width until you cross the breakpoint twice.\n- Treating `self.conditions` as always defined. It is optional, and with `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` each lookup is `boolean | undefined`; a non-null assertion here compiles and then reads `undefined` as truthy-adjacent in the one edge case that matters.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No ScrollTrigger is used here, but the failure mode described in the first pitfall is the reason this pattern exists — a pinned desktop section that survives into a mobile layout is the single most common GSAP responsiveness bug, and `matchMedia` is the sanctioned fix.\n- The same block also handles reduced motion: the `reduced` condition composes the final fan with `gsap.set` and returns before registering the entrance tween or the parallax listener, so the layout is identical and only the motion is absent.\n- `gsap.context()` (via the `use-gsap` hook) is the right tool when you need scoped selectors and cleanup but no breakpoints. `matchMedia` is `context` plus queries — reach for it the moment a layout differs across breakpoints, not just its timing.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAbove 768px the cards fan out from the centre with a stagger, tilt outward, and drift with the pointer. Drag the window narrower past 768px and they return to a plain vertical column with no leftover transforms and no inline `position: absolute` on any card. Widen again and the fan re-registers and re-plays its entrance. Resize within the desktop range and the fan rescales with the box. Enable reduce-motion while it is open and the fan stays but stops moving.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "mcp-install-card": "# Build: MCP Install Card\n\nA card that tells people how to connect an MCP server to their editor. A white panel holds a plug tile, title and copy, then underline tabs, one per client. Each tab shows a file bar (the config path, or \"Terminal\" for a command), a copy button and the snippet with light syntax colouring, plus an optional note. A link to the full guide sits on the grey shell below.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nInstall card with client tabs (Cursor, Claude Code, VS Code), a highlighted config snippet per client and a copy button with feedback.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Client switching is the kit `Tabs` (React Aria): `defaultSelectedKey` picks the first client unless `defaultClient` is set, and `onSelectionChange` clears any \"Copied\" state so it never shows on a tab that was not copied.\n2. Copy calls `navigator.clipboard.writeText(client.code)`. Success shows a check and \"Copied\" and calls `onCopy(client.id)`; a missing API or a rejected write (insecure context, denied permission) shows a warning icon and \"Failed\". Either state resets after `RESET_MS` through one timeout that is replaced on every copy.\n3. Copy state is `{ id, ok } | null`, compared against the panel client id, so only the copied panel changes. A visually hidden `role=\"status\"` span in each panel announces \"Copied to clipboard\" or the failure text.\n4. `tokenize()` is a small regex highlighter. For JSON it walks strings and structural characters: a string followed by a colon is a key (accent), any other string is a value (success green), and braces, brackets and commas are muted. For shell it prefixes a non-selectable \"$ \" and colours the program name.\n5. The snippet sits in a `pre` with horizontal overflow, so long commands scroll inside the card instead of widening it. The card is `max-w-xl` and full width below that, and the tab list scrolls horizontally if the labels do not fit.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `RESET_MS = 2000`\n  Long enough to notice the check after looking back from the editor, short enough that a second copy reads as a fresh action.\n- `code size = 12.5px / 20px`\n  Keeps a typical `mcp.json` line under the card width at 360px while staying readable.\n- `copy button min-width = 84px`\n  \"Copy\", \"Copied\" and \"Failed\" swap without the file bar reflowing.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction McpInstallCard(props: { clients: McpClient[]; defaultClient?: string; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; guide?: { label: string; href: string }; onCopy?: (clientId: string) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface McpClient { id: string; label: string; icon?: IconComponent; file?: string; language: \"json\" | \"shell\"; code: string; note?: ReactNode }\n```\n\n- `clients: McpClient[]`\n  One tab per client with its snippet and language.\n- `defaultClient: string`\n  Initially selected client id; defaults to the first.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Install with your agent through MCP\"`\n  Card heading.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Copy under the heading.\n- `guide: { label: string; href: string }`\n  Link shown on the shell under the panel.\n- `onCopy: (clientId: string) => void`\n  Fires after a successful clipboard write.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\ntype CopyState = { id: string; ok: boolean } | null;\n\nexport function McpInstallCard({\n  clients,\n  defaultClient,\n  title = \"Install with your agent through MCP\",\n  description,\n  guide,\n  onCopy,\n  className,\n}: McpInstallCardProps) {\n  const [copy, setCopy] = useState<CopyState>(null);\n  const timer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\n\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      if (timer.current) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n\n  const flash = (next: CopyState) => {\n    if (timer.current) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    setCopy(next);\n    timer.current = setTimeout(() => setCopy(null), RESET_MS);\n  };\n\n  const copyCode = async (client: McpClient) => {\n    try {\n      if (!navigator.clipboard?.writeText) throw new Error(\"Clipboard API unavailable\");\n      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(client.code);\n      flash({ id: client.id, ok: true });\n      onCopy?.(client.id);\n    } catch {\n      flash({ id: client.id, ok: false });\n    }\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <article\n      className={cx(\n        \"w-full max-w-xl rounded-3xl bg-background-secondary-default p-1 ring-1 ring-separator-border\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"rounded-[20px] bg-background-primary-default px-5 pt-5 pb-1 shadow-xs ring-1 ring-separator-border\">\n        <div className=\"flex items-start gap-3.5\">\n          <span className=\"grid size-10 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-xl bg-background-secondary-default ring-1 ring-separator-border\">\n            <RiPlugLine aria-hidden className=\"size-5 text-foreground-icon-primary\" />\n          </span>\n          <div className=\"min-w-0\">\n            <h3 className=\"text-headline-semibold text-balance text-text-primary\">{title}</h3>\n            {description && <p className=\"mt-1 text-body-2-regular text-pretty text-text-secondary\">{description}</p>}\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <Tabs\n          className=\"mt-4\"\n          defaultSelectedKey={defaultClient ?? clients[0]?.id}\n          onSelectionChange={() => {\n            if (timer.current) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n            setCopy(null);\n          }}\n        >\n          <TabList aria-label=\"MCP client\" className=\"overflow-x-auto\">\n            {clients.map((client) => (\n              <Tab key={client.id} id={client.id} icon={client.icon}>\n                {client.label}\n              </Tab>\n            ))}\n          </TabList>\n\n          {clients.map((client) => {\n            const state = copy?.id === client.id ? copy : null;\n            return (\n              <TabPanel key={client.id} id={client.id} className=\"py-4 outline-none\">\n                <div className=\"overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default ring-1 ring-separator-border\">\n                  <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-separator-border py-1.5 pr-1.5 pl-3\">\n                    <RiFileCodeLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-tertiary\" />\n                    <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate font-mono text-caption-1-regular text-text-secondary\">\n                      {client.file ?? \"Terminal\"}\n                    </span>\n                    <Button\n                      size=\"xs\"\n                      variant=\"secondary\"\n                      leadingIcon={state ? (state.ok ? RiCheckLine : RiErrorWarningLine) : RiFileCopyLine}\n                      onClick={() => void copyCode(client)}\n                      aria-label={`Copy ${client.label} ${client.language === \"shell\" ? \"command\" : \"config\"}`}\n                      className=\"min-w-[84px]\"\n                    >\n                      {state ? (state.ok ? \"Copied\" : \"Failed\") : \"Copy\"}\n                    </Button>\n                  </div>\n                  <pre className=\"overflow-x-auto p-4 font-mono text-[12.5px] leading-5 text-text-primary\">\n                    <code>\n                      {tokenize(client.code, client.language).map((token, i) => (\n                        <span key={i} className={token.cls}>\n                          {token.text}\n                        </span>\n                      ))}\n                    </code>\n                  </pre>\n                </div>\n                {client.note && <p className=\"mt-2.5 px-1 text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{client.note}</p>}\n                <span role=\"status\" className=\"sr-only\">\n                  {state ? (state.ok ? \"Copied to clipboard\" : \"Copy failed, select the text and copy it manually\") : \"\"}\n                </span>\n              </TabPanel>\n            );\n          })}\n        </Tabs>\n      </div>\n\n      {guide && (\n        <a\n          href={guide.href}\n          className=\"group flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 rounded-b-[20px] px-4 py-3 text-body-medium text-accent-600 outline-none hover:text-accent-700 focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring dark:text-accent-400 dark:hover:text-accent-300\"\n        >\n          {guide.label}\n          <RiArrowRightLine\n            aria-hidden\n            className=\"size-4 transition-transform duration-150 ease-out group-hover:translate-x-0.5 motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n          />\n        </a>\n      )}\n    </article>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The copy-reset timeout is cleared on unmount, on every new copy and when the tab changes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the guide arrow nudging 2px on hover and the kit tab underline sliding; the arrow uses `motion-reduce:transition-none`. Copy feedback is a static icon and label swap.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Assuming `navigator.clipboard` exists. It is undefined outside secure contexts and can reject when permission is denied; the write is wrapped so the button reports failure instead of throwing.\n- Leaving a reset timer running after unmount or a new copy. The timeout is kept in a ref, cleared before each new one and on unmount.\n- Highlighting with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. The tokenizer returns plain spans, so snippet text is never parsed as HTML.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Add more clients (Codex, Windsurf, Zed) by appending to `clients`; each can be JSON or a shell command.\n- The selected tab is uncontrolled. Remount with a different `defaultClient` (for example from the visitor OS) to preselect another client.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows Cursor selected with `.cursor/mcp.json` and a coloured JSON snippet. Clicking \"Claude Code\" shows \"Terminal\" and the `claude mcp add` command; clicking \"VS Code\" shows `.vscode/mcp.json`. Clicking Copy writes the exact snippet to the clipboard, swaps the button to a check and \"Copied\" for two seconds and announces it. With the clipboard unavailable the button reads \"Failed\" instead. At 360px the card fits and long lines scroll inside the code area.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "medical-profile": "# Build: Medical Profile\n\nThe Medical Profile template as one working page. A floating sidebar switches views in local state, the header carries a live notification bell, a Filters popover and a File a report dialog, and a three-column grid holds the patient card, steps and sleep charts, the most active days calendar wired to the activity rings, and the alerts feed, with the patients table underneath.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full patient dashboard page: profile, steps, sleep, a day picker driving activity rings, alerts and a filterable patients table.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is a `h-full w-full` flex row: `DashboardSidebar` (260px floating panel, theme toggle hidden) and a `main` column that scrolls on its own. The sidebar rows are `href=\"#\"` links, so a single `onClickCapture` on the root finds the closest `a[href=\"#\"]`, matches its `aria-label` against `DASHBOARD_NAV` and calls `setView(key)` after `preventDefault()`, keeping the host URL untouched.\n2. The view key decides the body. `\"medical\"` renders the dashboard; any other key renders `WorkspacePlaceholder` with that row's icon and label plus a \"Back to Medical Report\" button. The breadcrumb crumbs are `onPress` buttons that jump to the Home and Profile views, and the last crumb and the title follow the current view.\n3. The dashboard is a `grid-cols-3 gap-4` grid. `MostActiveDaysCard` is controlled (`selected={day}`, `onSelectedChange={setDay}`); `sampleDayActivity(day)` is memoised and its `rings` feed `ActivityRingsCard`, whose title becomes `Activity for <formatLongDate(day)>`. Picking a day in the calendar redraws the rings for that day.\n4. Filters is a `PopoverTrigger` with one `Checkbox` per section id held in a `Set`. Unchecking removes the id and the grid simply skips that card; the button shows the hidden count and \"Show all\" restores the full set.\n5. File a report is a controlled `ModalTrigger` around a form (report type `Select`, `Textarea` with a 280 character cap). Submit is disabled until the details are non-empty; submitting prepends a success item to the controlled notification list, so the bell badge goes up by one, and fires `toast.success`.\n6. The patients table gets the page's own `patients` state. Its row actions toast a short confirmation; Archive removes the row and the toast offers Undo, which splices the patient back at its old index.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `sidebar width = 260px (from DashboardSidebar)`\n  Leaves about 990px for the main column inside the 1280px block viewport, enough for three 320px cards with 16px gaps.\n- `grid = grid-cols-3, gap-4 (16px)`\n  BoardUI's two rows of three cards; the cards share a row height so the chart cards line up with the patient card.\n- `today = 2026-07-10`\n  Fixed date so the calendar, the ring title and every server render agree; later days render as empty tracks.\n- `patients pageSize = 8`\n  Twelve sample rows give two pages, so pagination is visible and works.\n- `report maxLength = 280`\n  Keeps the note short enough to read as a single notification line in the bell.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MedicalProfile(props: { patients?: Patient[]; defaultNotifications?: BellNotification[]; defaultView?: string; today?: CalendarDate; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `patients: Patient[]` — default `MEDICAL_PATIENTS`\n  Rows of the patients table (12 sample patients by default). Archive and Undo edit a local copy.\n- `defaultNotifications: BellNotification[]` — default `MEDICAL_NOTIFICATIONS`\n  Initial bell contents; unread items drive the badge count.\n- `defaultView: string` — default `\"medical\"`\n  Sidebar key shown first. \"medical\" is the dashboard, any other key shows the placeholder view.\n- `today: CalendarDate` — default `parseDate(\"2026-07-10\")`\n  Last day with activity data and the day picked at first.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The sidebar's rows are `href=\"#\"` links. One capture listener turns a\n// click on any of them into a view switch, so the host URL never changes.\nconst onNavClickCapture = (event: MouseEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n  if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;\n  const link = event.target.closest('a[href=\"#\"]');\n  if (!link) return;\n  event.preventDefault();\n  const item = DASHBOARD_NAV.find((entry) => entry.label === link.getAttribute(\"aria-label\"));\n  if (item) setView(item.key);\n};\n\nconst fileReport = (report: FiledReport) => {\n  const kind = REPORT_KINDS.find((k) => k.id === report.kind)?.label ?? \"Report\";\n  setNotifications((list) => [\n    { id: `report-${list.length + 1}`, title: `${kind} filed`, description: report.details, time: \"Just now\", icon: RiFileList3Line, tone: \"success\" },\n    ...list,\n  ]);\n  toast.success(\"Report filed\", { description: `${kind} sent to Dr. Clarkson.` });\n};\n\nconst onPatientAction = (action: PatientAction, patient: Patient) => {\n  if (action !== \"archive\") {\n    toast(ACTION_COPY[action](patient.name));\n    return;\n  }\n  const index = patients.findIndex((p) => p.id === patient.id);\n  setPatients((list) => list.filter((p) => p.id !== patient.id));\n  toast(`${patient.name} archived`, {\n    actions: [\n      {\n        label: \"Undo\",\n        onClick: () =>\n          setPatients((list) => {\n            if (list.some((p) => p.id === patient.id)) return list;\n            const next = [...list];\n            next.splice(Math.max(index, 0), 0, patient);\n            return next;\n          }),\n      },\n    ],\n  });\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners in the page itself; the nav handler is a React capture prop. The patient card revokes its object URL on change and unmount. The composed cards and the Toaster clean up their own frames and timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The page adds no motion of its own. The composed charts (steps bars, sleep ring, activity rings) check `useReducedMotion` and draw at their final size, and overlays fall back to instant open and close.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Letting the sidebar links navigate. They are `href=\"#\"`; without the capture handler a click scrolls the host page and writes a hash into its URL.\n- Using viewport breakpoints (`lg:`) inside the page. Blocks render in a scaled 1280px virtual viewport, so media queries see the real window and would collapse the layout on a phone even though the page is drawn at desktop width.\n- Deriving the rings from the calendar's internal state. Make the calendar controlled and read `day` from the page, or the ring card cannot know which day is picked.\n- Keeping object URLs from the photo picker alive. The patient card revokes the previous URL whenever the photo changes and on unmount.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Every card is an existing kit item (`StepsCard`, `SleepScoreCard`, `MostActiveDaysCard`, `ActivityRingsCard`, `ImportantAlertsCard`, `PatientsTable`, `NotificationBellBadge`, `DashboardPageHeader`). The page only owns the wiring and the patient card, filter popover and report dialog in `medical-profile-parts.tsx`.\n- A `Toaster` is mounted inside the page so the action toasts work wherever the block is dropped.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280x800 the sidebar sits on the left with Medical Report selected, and the main column shows three cards per row and the patients table below. Clicking day 3 in Most active days changes the ring card title to \"Activity for July 3, 2026\" and the rings redraw. Unchecking Steps in Filters removes the Steps card and the button shows 1 hidden. Filing a report with some text closes the dialog, shows a toast and raises the bell count from 5 to 6. Clicking Calendar in the sidebar shows the placeholder, and \"Back to Medical Report\" returns to the dashboard.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "meeting-scheduler": "# Build: Meeting Scheduler\n\nA compact \"Schedule a meeting\" button that opens a three-column booking panel: who you are meeting and what about on the left, a single-month calendar in the middle, and a timezone label, 12h / 24h switch and scrolling half-hour slots on the right. Choices are a draft until \"Send meeting\", which stays disabled until both a day and a time are picked, then commits and closes. The trigger is a plain button driving a controlled React Aria `Popover`, so the wide panel floats above the page and never pushes layout.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA Cal.com-style booking popover: host and meeting summary, a month calendar and a half-hour slot list, committed by \"Send meeting\".\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The trigger is a native `<button>` with the shared date-picker trigger styling and a chevron that rotates 180 degrees over 200ms while open. It toggles `isOpen` and, on opening, copies the committed value into `pendingValue`, so a draft abandoned last time never resurfaces.\n2. The panel is a React Aria `Popover` wired with `triggerRef`, `isOpen` / `onOpenChange`, `placement=\"bottom end\"`, `offset={8}` and `isNonModal`, so the page behind stays interactive. Non-modal popovers lose React Aria's outside-click dismissal, so `useDismissOnOutsidePress` adds a document `pointerdown` listener that closes it unless the press lands in the trigger or the panel.\n3. Inside a `Dialog`, a React Aria `Calendar` bound to `pendingValue.date` wraps the whole 429px-tall row, so the shared `MonthPanel` (`offset={0}` with both arrows) renders the grid and day cells. Picking a day keeps any time already chosen; picking a slot keeps the day (falling back to today).\n4. The left column (207px) shows the host avatar initial, name and email, the meeting title and description, then duration, language and conferencing rows pinned to the bottom. Under the calendar a chip shows the pending date and time (\"Select a time\" until one is picked) beside the \"Send meeting\" button.\n5. The right column (170px) holds the timezone label, a weekday + day chip, a 12h / 24h switch (24h by default) and the slots: 09:00 to 18:30 every 30 minutes, generated once and shown as \"2:30 PM\" in 12h mode. The list scrolls inside a mask that fades the top 16px, and the chosen slot gets the tertiary background.\n6. \"Send meeting\" calls `commit(pendingValue)`: uncontrolled, it stores the value internally; either way it calls `onChange({ date, time })` with `time` as 24-hour \"HH:mm\", and closes the popover.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `panel columns = 207px summary, 326px MonthPanel, 170px slots, gap-4, h-[429px]`\n  From the Figma \"Meeting\" frame (node 3881:7618). A fixed height lets the slot column scroll while the summary and calendar stay put.\n- `slots = 09:00 to 18:30, every 30 minutes (20 slots)`\n  Replaces Figma's static mock (which repeated 14:00) with a generated working-day range.\n- `popover = placement bottom end, offset 8, isNonModal`\n  Right-aligned to the trigger so it can sit at the end of a toolbar; non-modal so the page stays scrollable and clickable while the panel is open.\n- `slot mask = linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, black 16px, black 100%)`\n  Slots scrolled under the 24h toggle fade out instead of being cut off at a hard edge.\n- `defaultOpen threshold = (min-width: 640px), applied after mount`\n  If the ~735px panel is in the first paint, mobile Safari sizes its layout viewport to it and the page stays zoomed out after it closes.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MeetingScheduler(props: { host?: { name: string; email: string; avatarInitial: string }; meeting?: { title: string; description?: string; durationMinutes: number; language?: string; conferencing?: string }; timezone?: string; value?: MeetingSchedulerValue | null; defaultValue?: MeetingSchedulerValue | null; onChange?: (value: MeetingSchedulerValue | null) => void; defaultOpen?: boolean; className?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; triggerLabel?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value / defaultValue: { date: CalendarDate; time: string | null } | null`\n  Controlled or initial booking. Without either, the draft starts on today with no time.\n- `onChange: (value: MeetingSchedulerValue | null) => void`\n  Fires once, when \"Send meeting\" is pressed.\n- `host: MeetingSchedulerHost` — default `Jordan Lee`\n  Name, email and avatar initial in the summary column.\n- `meeting: MeetingSchedulerDetails` — default `30 min intro meeting`\n  Title, description, duration, language and conferencing rows.\n- `timezone: string` — default `\"Amsterdam\"`\n  Label in the slot column header.\n- `triggerLabel: string` — default `\"Schedule a meeting\"`\n  Text on the trigger button.\n- `defaultOpen: boolean` — default `false`\n  Opens after mount on viewports 640px and wider.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Schedule meeting\"`\n  Names the dialog and the calendar grid.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function MeetingScheduler({\n  host = DEFAULT_HOST,\n  meeting = DEFAULT_MEETING,\n  timezone = \"Amsterdam\",\n  value,\n  defaultValue = null,\n  onChange,\n  defaultOpen = false,\n  className,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Schedule meeting\",\n  triggerLabel = \"Schedule a meeting\",\n}: MeetingSchedulerProps) {\n  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const timeSlots = useTimeSlots();\n  const isControlled = value !== undefined;\n  const [internalValue, setInternalValue] = useState<MeetingSchedulerValue | null>(\n    defaultValue ?? { date: today(getLocalTimeZone()), time: null },\n  );\n  const committedValue = isControlled ? value : internalValue;\n\n  const [pendingValue, setPendingValue] = useState<MeetingSchedulerValue | null>(committedValue);\n  const [use24h, setUse24h] = useState(true);\n  // Never honor `defaultOpen` on the initial render: the ~700px-wide panel\n  // being in the very first paint makes mobile Safari compute its layout\n  // viewport from that width, permanently zooming the whole page out even\n  // after the panel closes. Open it post-mount instead, and only on\n  // viewports wide enough to show the panel without overflowing.\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!defaultOpen) return;\n    if (window.matchMedia(\"(min-width: 640px)\").matches) {\n      setIsOpen(true);\n    }\n  }, [defaultOpen]);\n\n  const commit = (next: MeetingSchedulerValue | null) => {\n    if (!isControlled) setInternalValue(next);\n    onChange?.(next);\n    setIsOpen(false);\n  };\n\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setIsOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\n\n  const chip = pendingValue ? formatChipDateTime(pendingValue) : null;\n  const dayChip = pendingValue\n    ? {\n        weekday: new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, { weekday: \"short\" }).format(pendingValue.date.toDate(getLocalTimeZone())).slice(0, 2),\n        day: pendingValue.date.day,\n      }\n    : null;\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <button\n        ref={triggerRef}\n        type=\"button\"\n        onClick={() => {\n          if (!isOpen) setPendingValue(committedValue);\n          setIsOpen((open) => !open);\n        }}\n        className={cx(triggerButtonClassName, className)}\n      >\n        <RiCalendarLine className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-primary\" aria-hidden />\n        <span className=\"flex items-center justify-center whitespace-nowrap px-1 text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n          {triggerLabel}\n        </span>\n        <ChevronDownSmall\n          className={cx(\"size-4 shrink-0 text-text-secondary transition-transform duration-200 ease\", isOpen && \"rotate-180\")}\n        />\n      </button>\n      <Popover\n        ref={popoverRef}\n        triggerRef={triggerRef}\n        isOpen={isOpen}\n        onOpenChange={setIsOpen}\n        placement=\"bottom end\"\n        offset={8}\n        isNonModal\n        className={popoverClassName}\n      >\n        <Dialog aria-label={ariaLabel} className=\"outline-none\">\n          <Calendar\n            aria-label={ariaLabel}\n            value={pendingValue?.date ?? null}\n            onChange={(date) => setPendingValue((prev) => ({ date, time: prev?.time ?? null }))}\n          >\n            <div className=\"flex h-[429px] gap-4\">\n              {/* Host + meeting summary */}\n              <div className=\"flex w-[207px] shrink-0 flex-col gap-4 py-4 ps-4\">\n                <div className=\"flex flex-col items-start gap-2\">\n                  <Avatar size=\"md\" color=\"blue\" initials={host.avatarInitial} />\n                  <div className=\"flex flex-col text-body-medium\">\n                    <span className=\"text-text-primary\">{host.name}</span>\n                    <span className=\"text-text-secondary\">{host.email}</span>\n                  </div>\n                </div>\n                <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-1\">\n                  <p className=\"text-title-3-medium text-text-primary\">{meeting.title}</p>\n                  {meeting.description && (\n                    <p className=\"text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary\">{meeting.description}</p>\n                  )}\n                </div>\n                <div className=\"mt-auto flex flex-col gap-3\">\n                  <InfoRow icon={<GlyphIcon icon={RiGlobalLine} />}>{meeting.durationMinutes} minutes</InfoRow>\n                  {meeting.language && <InfoRow icon={<GlyphIcon icon={RiFlagLine} />}>{meeting.language}</InfoRow>}\n                  {meeting.conferencing && <InfoRow icon={<GoogleMeetIcon />}>{meeting.conferencing}</InfoRow>}\n                </div>\n              </div>\n\n              {/* Calendar + footer */}\n              <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 pt-2 pb-3\">\n                <MonthPanel offset={0} showPrev showNext />\n                <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n                  <span className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-2lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default px-2 py-2 text-body-medium text-text-primary shadow-xs\">\n                    {chip && (\n                      <>\n                        <span>{chip.date}</span>\n                        <span className=\"text-text-secondary\">{chip.time}</span>\n                      </>\n                    )}\n                  </span>\n                  <Button onClick={() => commit(pendingValue)} disabled={!pendingValue?.time}>\n                    Send meeting\n                  </Button>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n\n              {/* Timezone + hour format + time slots */}\n              <div className=\"flex h-full w-[170px] shrink-0 flex-col gap-3 overflow-hidden pt-4 pe-4\">\n                <button\n                  type=\"button\"\n                  className=\"flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between rounded-2lg outline-none\"\n                >\n                  <span className=\"flex items-center gap-[5px] text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n                    <RiGlobalLine className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-primary\" aria-hidden />\n                    {timezone}\n                  </span>\n                  <ChevronDownSmall className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-text-secondary\" />\n                </button>\n\n                <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between\">\n                  {dayChip && (\n                    <span className=\"inline-flex items-center gap-[3px] rounded-2lg bg-background-tertiary-default px-2 py-1 text-body-medium\">\n                      <span className=\"text-text-primary\">{dayChip.weekday}</span>\n                      <span className=\"text-text-secondary\">{dayChip.day}</span>\n                    </span>\n                  )}\n                  <HourFormatToggle use24h={use24h} onChange={setUse24h} />\n                </div>\n\n                <div\n                  className=\"flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-2 overflow-y-auto pb-3\"\n                  style={{\n                    maskImage: \"linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, black 16px, black 100%)\",\n                    WebkitMaskImage: \"linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent, black 16px, black 100%)\",\n                  }}\n                >\n                  {timeSlots.map((slot) => (\n                    <button\n                      key={slot}\n                      type=\"button\"\n                      onClick={() =>\n                        setPendingValue((prev) => ({ date: prev?.date ?? today(getLocalTimeZone()), time: slot }))\n                      }\n                      className={cx(\n                        \"w-full shrink-0 cursor-pointer rounded-2lg px-2 py-1.5 text-center text-body-medium text-text-primary transition-colors duration-150 ease\",\n                        pendingValue?.time === slot\n                          ? \"bg-background-tertiary-default\"\n                          : \"hover:bg-background-secondary-hover\",\n                      )}\n                    >\n                      {formatTime(slot, use24h)}\n                    </button>\n                  ))}\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </Calendar>\n        </Dialog>\n      </Popover>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The outside-press hook removes its document listener when the panel closes or the component unmounts, and React Aria tears down the popover's positioning and focus handling on close. No timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not branch on `prefers-reduced-motion`. The motion is the shared popover entrance (150ms opacity, scale and blur), the 200ms chevron turn and 150ms colour fades on slots and the hour toggle, all brief and none needed to understand the state.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not open the panel during the first render. `defaultOpen` is applied in an effect and only on viewports of 640px or wider; rendering a ~735px overlay in the first paint zooms mobile Safari out permanently.\n- Do not commit on every click. Date and slot edits go to `pendingValue`; only \"Send meeting\" calls `onChange`, so closing the panel discards the draft.\n- Do not leave `isNonModal` without the outside-press hook. React Aria skips light dismiss for non-modal popovers, so clicking the page would leave the panel stuck open.\n- Do not rely on the uncontrolled default in server-rendered pages. With no `value` or `defaultValue` it seeds today's date, which can differ between server and client around midnight; pass a value.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The value is `{ date: CalendarDate; time: string | null }`; `time` is always 24-hour \"HH:mm\" regardless of the 12h / 24h display.\n- The timezone row is a label with a chevron, not a working selector; wire your own picker there if you need one.\n- The trigger keeps `triggerLabel` (\"Schedule a meeting\") after booking; show the booked time elsewhere, as the demo does.\n- BoardUI ships a Google Meet bitmap for conferencing; this port uses a Remix video icon.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA \"Schedule a meeting\" button with a calendar icon and chevron. Clicking it opens a panel below, right-aligned, with the host card and meeting rows, a month calendar and a slot column from 09:00 to 18:30; \"Send meeting\" is disabled. Picking a day updates the chip and the weekday badge, picking a slot highlights it and fills the chip, and \"Send meeting\" becomes enabled. Toggling 12h shows \"9:00 AM\" style labels. Pressing \"Send meeting\" closes the panel and calls `onChange` with the date and the 24-hour time; clicking outside instead closes it without calling `onChange`.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "meter": "# Build: Meter\n\nA labelled rail that shows a quantity within a known range: storage used, seats taken, API quota. It is React Aria's `Meter`, so it is announced as `role=\"meter\"` (a level, not a task heading towards done), and it is drawn on Progress's `ProgressTrack`, sharing the same rail and eased translate fill. A `tone` recolours the fill from semantic tokens so a meter can turn amber or red as it nears its limit.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA level within a known range (storage, seats, quotas) with accent, success, warning and error tones, on React Aria Meter.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Map `tone` to a text-colour class in a `sortCx` map: `accent` → `text-accent-500`, `success` → `text-notification-success-foreground`, `warning` → `text-chart-8-active`, `error` → `text-notification-error-foreground`. The fill paints in `currentColor`, so a text class is all it takes.\n2. Render `AriaMeter` with every React Aria prop passed through (`value`, `minValue`, `maxValue`, `valueLabel`, `formatOptions`) and `flex w-full flex-col gap-1.5` on the root.\n3. Use the render props `{ percentage, valueText }`. When there is a label or the value is shown, render a `flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3` row: the kit `Label` (truncated) and `valueText` in `text-body-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums`. `showValueLabel` defaults to `label != null`.\n4. Render `<ProgressTrack percentage={percentage} size={size} className={tones[tone]} />`: a `bg-border-button-default` rail 4 / 6 / 8px tall whose full-width fill is shifted by `translate` and eases over 500ms on `cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)` when the value changes.\n5. Render the optional `description` underneath in `text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `tone tokens = accent-500; success lime-600 (dark lime-300); warning yellow-500 (chart-8-active); error rose-600 (dark rose-300)`\n  Semantic tokens rather than palette classes, so the fill keeps its contrast on dark cards, where success and error lighten to the 300 step.\n- `track = h-1 / h-1.5 / h-2 (4 / 6 / 8px), default md`\n  Shared with ProgressBar so a meter and a progress bar stacked on one card are identical rails.\n- `fill easing = 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) on translate`\n  Inherited from ProgressTrack: a value change eases in on the compositor instead of animating width.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype MeterTone = \"accent\" | \"success\" | \"warning\" | \"error\"\ninterface MeterProps extends Omit<AriaMeterProps, \"children\" | \"className\"> { label?: ReactNode; showValueLabel?: boolean; description?: ReactNode; tone?: MeterTone; size?: ProgressSize; className?: string }\nfunction Meter(props: MeterProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: number` — default `0`\n  Current level (React Aria).\n- `minValue / maxValue: number` — default `0 / 100`\n  The range the value sits in (React Aria).\n- `valueLabel: ReactNode`\n  Custom value text instead of the formatted percentage (React Aria).\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Label above the rail; also turns the value label on by default.\n- `showValueLabel: boolean`\n  Show the value at the end of the label row. Defaults to `label != null`.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Helper text under the rail.\n- `tone: \"accent\" | \"success\" | \"warning\" | \"error\"` — default `\"accent\"`\n  Colour of the fill.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  4, 6 or 8px rail.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst tones = sortCx({\n  accent: \"text-accent-500\",\n  success: \"text-notification-success-foreground\",\n  warning: \"text-chart-8-active\",\n  error: \"text-notification-error-foreground\",\n});\n\nexport interface MeterProps extends Omit<AriaMeterProps, \"children\" | \"className\"> {\n  label?: ReactNode;\n  /** Show the formatted value at the end of the label row. Default: shown with a label. */\n  showValueLabel?: boolean;\n  /** Helper text under the bar. */\n  description?: ReactNode;\n  /** Colour of the fill. Default `accent`. */\n  tone?: MeterTone;\n  /** Track thickness: 4, 6 (default), or 8px. */\n  size?: ProgressSize;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function Meter({\n  label,\n  showValueLabel,\n  description,\n  tone = \"accent\",\n  size = \"md\",\n  className,\n  ...props\n}: MeterProps) {\n  const showValue = showValueLabel ?? label != null;\n  return (\n    <AriaMeter {...props} className={cx(\"flex w-full flex-col gap-1.5\", className)}>\n      {({ percentage, valueText }) => (\n        <>\n          {label != null || showValue ? (\n            <div className=\"flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3\">\n              {label != null ? <Label className=\"min-w-0 truncate\">{label}</Label> : <span />}\n              {showValue ? (\n                <span className=\"shrink-0 text-body-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">{valueText}</span>\n              ) : null}\n            </div>\n          ) : null}\n          <ProgressTrack percentage={percentage} size={size} className={tones[tone]} />\n          {description ? <span className=\"text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">{description}</span> : null}\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaMeter>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless apart from React Aria's render props; there are no listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The fill's 500ms translate transition is removed by ProgressTrack's `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so a new value appears immediately.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not use a Meter for a task in progress (an upload, an import). That is a ProgressBar; Meter is announced as a level and has no indeterminate state.\n- Do not pass a raw count without `maxValue`. React Aria assumes 0..100, so `value={19}` for 19 of 20 seats renders as 19% full.\n- Do not encode the status only in the tone colour. Put the number or a description next to it (\"1 seat left\").\n- Do not recolour the fill with `bg-*` classes. The fill is `bg-current`; set a text colour (or `tone`) instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `valueLabel` (React Aria) replaces the formatted percentage with custom text, e.g. \"7.2 of 10 GB\".\n- Tone thresholds are the caller's decision; the component never switches tone by itself.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`<Meter label=\"Seats\" value={19} maxValue={20} tone=\"warning\" />` renders \"Seats\" with \"95%\" on the right and a 6px rail filled 95% in amber, exposed as `role=\"meter\"` with aria-valuenow 19. `valueLabel=\"7.2 of 10 GB\"` replaces the percentage text. Changing the value eases the fill to its new length over half a second, and the error tone fill is rose in light mode and a lighter rose in dark mode.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "mixed-scrub-depth-layers": "# Build: Mixed Scrub Depth Layers\n\nElements marked with a depth index get two things from that index: how far they travel, and how much they lag. The travel is ordinary parallax; the lag — a different `scrub` value per depth — is what keeps the planes visibly apart while the eye is moving and lets them converge at rest. Works on a vertical page and, given a horizontal strip's tween, along that strip instead.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLayers separate while you scroll and converge when you stop, because each depth has its own scrub.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each `[data-depth]` element is read once: `depth = clamp(parseInt(dataset.depth), 0, ladder.length - 1)`, so bad or missing markup lands on the nearest plane rather than throwing.\n2. Distance comes from the normalised depth: `amount = (depth / (ladder.length - 1)) * travel`, tweened `fromTo(-amount → +amount)` in `yPercent` so it is relative to the element's own height and survives any layout.\n3. Lag comes from the ladder: `scrub = ladder[depth]`, giving 0 / 0.25 / 0.5 / 1 seconds of catch-up across four planes. Depth 0 uses `scrub: true` rather than `scrub: 0`, because ScrollTrigger reads 0 as falsy and would drop scrubbing entirely.\n4. The trigger window is the element's full crossing — `top bottom` to `bottom top` — so nothing is ever caught mid-tween on load and every layer is at a defined offset for any scroll position.\n5. Passing a `containerAnimation` swaps the position keywords to `left right` / `right left`, so the same ladder drives layers riding a pinned horizontal strip.\n6. The ladder array is serialised to a string for the effect dependency list, so a caller passing a fresh literal every render does not rebuild every trigger every render.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DEFAULT_LADDER = [0, 0.25, 0.5, 1]`\n  Seconds of catch-up per plane. Four values is the most the eye separates reliably; a fifth reads as the same distance as the fourth. Doubling at each step keeps the ratio between adjacent planes constant, which is what makes them read as evenly spaced.\n- `travel = 26`\n  yPercent at the deepest plane, so a tile moves just over a quarter of its own height across the whole crossing. Past ~40 the tiles visibly leave their grid cells and the layout looks broken; below ~10 the parallax is invisible once the lag is doing the work.\n- `scrub for depth 0 = true`\n  ScrollTrigger treats `scrub: 0` as falsy and turns scrubbing off, which makes the nearest plane a toggle animation instead of a locked one. `true` is the documented spelling of \"no catch-up\".\n- `trigger window = 'top bottom' → 'bottom top'`\n  The element's entire crossing of the scroller. A shorter window leaves the layer frozen at a partial offset above and below it, which shows up as a jump the first time it enters.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MixedScrubLayers(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; containerAnimation?: gsap.core.Animation | null; ladder?: number[]; travel?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Markup containing `[data-depth]` elements; 0 is nearest.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container the triggers read. Omit for the document.\n- `containerAnimation: gsap.core.Animation | null`\n  A horizontal strip tween to measure along. `null` defers until it exists.\n- `ladder: number[]` — default `[0, 0.25, 0.5, 1]`\n  Seconds of catch-up per depth index. Length defines how many planes exist.\n- `travel: number` — default `26`\n  yPercent travel at the deepest plane.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function MixedScrubLayers({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  containerAnimation,\n  ladder = DEFAULT_LADDER,\n  travel = 26,\n  className,\n}: MixedScrubLayersProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Serialised so a fresh array literal from the caller does not rebuild\n  // every trigger on every render.\n  const ladderKey = ladder.join(',');\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl || reduced) return;\n    if (containerAnimation === null) return;\n\n    const steps = ladderKey.split(',').map(Number);\n    const last = Math.max(1, steps.length - 1);\n    const scrollerEl = scroller?.current ?? undefined;\n    const horizontal = containerAnimation !== undefined;\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      for (const el of Array.from(rootEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-depth]'))) {\n        const raw = Number.parseInt(el.dataset.depth ?? '0', 10);\n        const depth = clamp(Number.isFinite(raw) ? raw : 0, 0, last);\n        // Normalised depth drives distance; the ladder drives lag. Distance\n        // alone collapses the moment the scroll stops — the lag is what keeps\n        // the planes apart while the eye is still moving.\n        const amount = (depth / last) * travel;\n        const scrub = steps[depth] ?? 0;\n\n        gsap.fromTo(\n          el,\n          { yPercent: -amount },\n          {\n            yPercent: amount,\n            ease: 'none',\n            scrollTrigger: {\n              trigger: el,\n              scroller: scrollerEl,\n              // Full-crossing window: the layer starts moving as it enters\n              // and stops as it leaves, so no layer is ever mid-jump on load.\n              start: horizontal ? 'left right' : 'top bottom',\n              end: horizontal ? 'right left' : 'bottom top',\n              scrub: scrub === 0 ? true : scrub,\n              ...(containerAnimation ? { containerAnimation } : {}),\n            },\n          },\n        );\n      }\n    }, rootEl);\n\n    return () => ctx.revert();\n  }, [scroller, containerAnimation, ladderKey, travel, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `ctx.revert()` on unmount and on any dependency change kills each layer's tween and ScrollTrigger and strips the inline `transform` GSAP wrote, so a remount measures the authored layout rather than the previous run's offsets.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No triggers are created, so every layer sits at its natural document position with no transform at all. The grid is the same grid — it simply does not separate, which is the point of the setting.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building parallax from distance alone. Every layer lands on its final offset on the same frame, so the depth cue vanishes the instant scrolling stops and the composition flattens.\n- Writing `scrub: 0` for the locked plane. It is falsy, so ScrollTrigger disables scrubbing and the layer plays as a one-shot toggle animation — the one plane that was supposed to be rock solid becomes the one that pops.\n- Using `y` in pixels instead of `yPercent`. The offsets stop being proportional to the tiles, so a layout that reads well at desktop width has layers sliding out of their cells on a phone.\n- Recreating triggers on every render because the ladder is a fresh array literal in the caller's JSX. The triggers are torn down and rebuilt mid-scroll and the layers snap back to their start offsets.\n- Indexing the ladder without a bound. `data-depth=\"7\"` on a four-value ladder yields `undefined`, which GSAP coerces into a scrub of `NaN` and the layer stops responding to scroll entirely.\n- Keeping vertical position keywords when a `containerAnimation` is supplied. The trigger measures along the container's axis, so vertical keywords resolve to a position the element never reaches and the layer never moves.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Depth 0 is deliberately motionless. Something in the frame has to be still, or the whole composition reads as drift rather than depth.\n- Combine with `pinned-horizontal-track` by feeding its `onTrack` payload's `animation` into `containerAnimation`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll through the grid at speed: the tiles visibly separate into distinct planes, the depth-0 tile stays locked to the grid, and the deepest tiles trail furthest behind. Stop scrolling and, within about a second, everything settles into an aligned composition. Scroll backwards and the separation reverses rather than replaying.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "modal": "# Build: Modal\n\nA centred dialog built on React Aria's `ModalOverlay`, `Modal` and `Dialog`: focus is trapped and returned to the trigger, the page behind stops scrolling, and Escape or a backdrop press dismisses it. The panel fades in from scale 0.85 with a 4px blur on the iOS sheet curve and plays the same transition in reverse before it unmounts. `ModalHeader`, `ModalBody` and `ModalFooter` lay out the content, and `AlertDialog` is the destructive-confirm preset whose async `onConfirm` keeps it open and busy until the promise settles.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDialog over a dimmed page with a header, scrolling body and actions, plus an alert dialog for destructive confirms.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `ModalTrigger` splits its children with `Children.toArray`: the first child is the trigger and is wrapped in React Aria's `Pressable`, so any kit button that forwards its ref and spreads props opens the dialog; the rest (the `Modal`) are passed through to `DialogTrigger`. Without a trigger, `Modal` is controlled from outside with `isOpen` / `onOpenChange`.\n2. `Modal` renders `ModalOverlay` (`fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-100`, height `h-(--visual-viewport-height)`, centred with `p-4 sm:p-6`) with `isDismissable` defaulting to true and `dir` from `useDirection()`. Inside it sits an `aria-hidden` backdrop div (`absolute inset-0 bg-black/70`) and the `AriaModal` panel, whose width comes from the `size` map: sm 400, md 480, lg 640, xl 800px.\n3. Motion is pure CSS keyed on React Aria's `data-entering` / `data-exiting` attributes. The overlay is a `group/modal`, so the backdrop fades with `group-data-[entering]/modal:opacity-0` over `duration-300 ease-out`. The panel transitions `opacity, scale, filter` over 300ms on `cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)` from `opacity-0` plus, under `motion-safe:` only, `scale-[0.85]` and `blur-[4px]`. React Aria waits for the exit transition before unmounting, so closing replays the entrance in reverse.\n4. `ModalDialog` (shared with Drawer) renders the `AriaDialog` surface: `rounded-3xl border border-separator-border bg-background-full shadow-xs`, `py-6`, `gap-4`, and `overflow-clip` rather than `overflow-hidden`. It owns a `useId()` description id and a `described` flag in context; `ModalHeader` sets that flag in a `useLayoutEffect` when it has a description, so `aria-describedby` points at the description only while one is rendered.\n5. `ModalHeader` renders an optional 40px icon circle (tinted with the notification-error tokens when `tone=\"danger\"`), the title as a React Aria `Heading slot=\"title\"` so it names the dialog, the description, and a `CloseButton` that calls `useModalClose()` (which reads `OverlayTriggerStateContext`). `ModalBody` is the only scroller (`overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain`) and shows a `ScrollFade` once scrolled; `ModalFooter` stacks actions `flex-col-reverse` on phones and becomes a trailing row from `sm`.\n6. `AlertDialog` renders `Modal` with `role=\"alertdialog\"`, `size=\"sm\"` and `isDismissable` false by default. Its confirm handler calls `onConfirm`; if that returns a thenable it sets `pending`, disables Cancel, turns the confirm button `aria-busy` with a spinner and `pendingLabel`, blocks Escape (`isKeyboardDismissDisabled`) and backdrop dismissal, closes on resolve and stays open on reject. Cancel carries `autoFocus`, so focus lands on the safe action.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `panel widths = sm 400 / md 480 / lg 640 / xl 800 px`\n  `md` is the default for forms; `AlertDialog` defaults to `sm` because a confirm holds one sentence and two buttons, and a wider panel stretches the line length past comfortable reading.\n- `entrance / exit = 300ms, cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1), scale 0.85, blur 4px`\n  The iOS sheet curve front-loads the motion so the panel arrives fast and settles; the blur hides the scaled-up rasterisation of the first frames. The same values run in reverse on exit.\n- `backdrop = bg-black/70, 300ms ease-out opacity`\n  Dark enough to separate the panel from a busy page in both themes, and timed with the panel so the two arrive together.\n- `icon circle = size-10 (40px) with a size-5 (20px) glyph, mb-4`\n  Matches the Settings Modal header so dialogs across the kit share one header rhythm.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ModalTrigger(props: Omit<DialogTriggerProps, \"children\"> & { children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction Modal(props: Omit<ModalOverlayProps, \"children\" | \"className\" | \"style\"> & { size?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\" | \"xl\"; role?: \"dialog\" | \"alertdialog\"; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string; overlayClassName?: string; children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode) }): JSX.Element\nfunction ModalHeader(props: { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; tone?: \"default\" | \"danger\"; showCloseButton?: boolean; closeLabel?: string; className?: string; children?: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction ModalBody(props: { className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction ModalFooter(props: { className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction AlertDialog(props: AlertDialogProps): JSX.Element\nfunction useModalClose(): () => void\n```\n\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\" | \"xl\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Panel max width: 400, 480, 640 or 800px. AlertDialog defaults to \"sm\".\n- `isDismissable: boolean` — default `true (Modal) / false (AlertDialog)`\n  Whether a press on the backdrop closes the dialog.\n- `isOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Controlled open state when the Modal is used without a ModalTrigger.\n- `children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode)`\n  Dialog content; the function form receives `close`.\n- `overlayClassName: string`\n  Classes on the full-screen overlay, e.g. to pin the panel to the top.\n- `ModalHeader.description: ReactNode`\n  Supporting copy under the title, wired to the dialog's `aria-describedby`.\n- `ModalHeader.tone: \"default\" | \"danger\"` — default `\"default\"`\n  Danger tints the 40px icon circle with the error tokens.\n- `AlertDialog.onConfirm: () => void | Promise<unknown>`\n  Return a promise to keep the dialog open and busy until it settles; it closes on resolve and stays open on reject.\n- `AlertDialog.variant: \"danger\" | \"default\"` — default `\"danger\"`\n  Danger pairs the warning icon tint with a danger confirm button.\n- `AlertDialog.isConfirmDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Disables the confirm action, e.g. until a confirmation phrase is typed.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Modal({\n  size = \"md\",\n  role,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  overlayClassName,\n  isDismissable = true,\n  children,\n  ...props\n}: ModalProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n\n  return (\n    <AriaModalOverlay {...props} dir={direction} isDismissable={isDismissable} className={cx(styles.overlay, overlayClassName)}>\n      <div aria-hidden className={styles.backdrop} />\n      <AriaModal className={cx(styles.modal, styles.size[size])}>\n        <ModalDialog role={role} aria-label={ariaLabel} className={className}>\n          {children}\n        </ModalDialog>\n      </AriaModal>\n    </AriaModalOverlay>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface ModalDialogProps {\n  role?: \"dialog\" | \"alertdialog\";\n  \"aria-label\"?: string;\n  /** Classes merged onto the panel surface. */\n  className?: string;\n  children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode);\n}\n\n/**\n * The panel surface and dialog element shared by Modal and Drawer: the\n * ModalHeader title names it and the description is wired to\n * aria-describedby. Render it inside a React Aria Modal.\n */\nexport function ModalDialog({ role, \"aria-label\": ariaLabel, className, children }: ModalDialogProps) {\n  const descriptionId = useId();\n  const [described, setDescribed] = useState(false);\n\n  return (\n    <AriaDialog\n      role={role}\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      aria-describedby={described ? descriptionId : undefined}\n      className={cx(styles.dialog, className)}\n    >\n      {(renderProps) => (\n        <ModalContext.Provider value={{ descriptionId, setDescribed }}>\n          {typeof children === \"function\" ? children(renderProps) : children}\n        </ModalContext.Provider>\n      )}\n    </AriaDialog>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** React Aria releases the focus trap, restores focus to the trigger, and removes the scroll lock when the overlay unmounts after its exit transition. The ModalHeader layout effect resets the `described` flag on unmount. No timers or listeners of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The panel's scale and blur are behind `motion-safe:` and are dropped under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`; the backdrop and panel still fade over 300ms, so opening and closing remain perceptible without movement.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not use `overflow-hidden` on the dialog surface. A hidden box is still programmatically scrollable, so focusing a field clipped by the body scroller can scroll the whole panel; `overflow-clip` cannot be scrolled.\n- Do not let the panel or the header scroll. Only `ModalBody` scrolls (`min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto`), so the title and the actions stay put on a short viewport.\n- Do not animate the panel with JS or a keyframe that ignores `data-exiting`. React Aria only delays unmount for a CSS transition or animation on the exiting element, which is what makes the close replay the entrance.\n- Do not hard-code `aria-describedby`. It is set only while `ModalHeader` renders a description, otherwise it would point at an id that does not exist.\n- Do not make an `AlertDialog` dismissable by default or put the initial focus on the destructive button. An alert asks for an answer, and Enter on a focused Delete is an accident waiting to happen.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `useModalClose()` is exported so deep children (a form submit handler, a list row) can close the surrounding Modal, Drawer or AlertDialog without threading `close` down.\n- Reduced motion keeps the opacity fade and only drops the scale and blur, because those are behind `motion-safe:`.\n- The surface uses `bg-background-full`: page white in light mode and, in dark mode, a ground darker than the inputs and menus inside, so they keep an edge.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPressing \"Invite teammates\" fades in a dimmed backdrop and a panel that grows from slightly smaller and blurred to sharp; Tab cycles only inside the panel, Escape or a click on the backdrop closes it with the reverse animation, and focus returns to the trigger. In the delete AlertDialog, focus starts on Cancel, a backdrop click does nothing, and Delete shows a spinner with \"Deleting…\" while Cancel is disabled, then closes when the promise resolves.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "model-select": "# Build: Model Select\n\nA model picker for chat composers, built on the kit Select so it keeps React Aria listbox behaviour: arrow keys, typeahead, Escape and screen-reader selection state. Each row shows a tinted two-letter monogram, the model name with optional New and Pro badges, a muted description and a check on the selected model. The trigger shows a small monogram and the name; the ghost variant sits flat in a toolbar, and the outline variant is the regular bordered field.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nModel picker on the kit Select. Rows show a tinted monogram, name, description, New/Pro badges and a check on the current model.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The kit `Select` is driven through `selectedKey` and `onSelectionChange`. When `value` is passed the component is controlled; otherwise it keeps its own pick in state, seeded from `defaultValue` or the first enabled model. `onChange` receives both the id and the full model object.\n2. Rows are kit `SelectItem`s (React Aria `ListBoxItem`) with `textValue` set to the model name, so typing \"de\" jumps to Deep 1 even though the row also holds badges and a description.\n3. Inside each row the name and badges sit in a React Aria `Text slot=\"label\"` and the description in `Text slot=\"description\"`. The option is labelled by the name and described by the line under it, so the description is announced as a hint, not as part of the name.\n4. The monogram is the `monogram` field or, when it is missing, the first character of the first two words of the name (\"Deep 1\" becomes \"D1\"). Its square takes a `status-*-background` / `status-*-text` token pair from `tone`, which the kit theme defines for light and dark.\n5. The check is always rendered and switches between `opacity-0` and `opacity-100`, so rows never shift width when the selection moves. React Aria already sets `aria-selected` on the option, so the icon is `aria-hidden`.\n6. `renderValue` puts a 20px monogram and the name in the trigger. `compact` makes the name `sr-only`, leaving only the monogram on screen while the trigger keeps its accessible text.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover size = 320px wide, list up to 336px tall`\n  Wide enough for a name with both badges and a description of about 34 characters on one line; the kit menu surface caps it at `100vw - 32px` on phones. The kit listbox stops at 240px, which would hide the fifth two-line row, so the popover lifts that cap through a `[&_[role=listbox]]` variant and longer lists scroll past 336px.\n- `monogram = 32px rows / 20px trigger, two characters`\n  Two characters keep model families apart (\"S2\" and \"SM\") without becoming a logo. 32px matches the height of the two-line text block, so rows stay centred.\n- `GHOST_TRIGGER = transparent border, background and shadow; secondary surface on hover or press`\n  Inside a composer toolbar a bordered field reads as a form input. The ghost trigger looks like a label until it is pointed at.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ModelSelect(props: { models?: ModelOption[]; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (id: string, model: ModelOption) => void; variant?: \"ghost\" | \"outline\"; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; label?: ReactNode; \"aria-label\"?: string; isDisabled?: boolean; compact?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface ModelOption { id: string; name: string; description?: string; monogram?: string; tone?: ModelTone; badges?: (\"new\" | \"pro\")[]; isDisabled?: boolean }\n```\n\n- `models: ModelOption[]` — default `MODEL_OPTIONS`\n  Rows of the menu, in order.\n- `value: string`\n  Selected model id. Passing it makes the select controlled.\n- `defaultValue: string`\n  Starting model when uncontrolled. Defaults to the first enabled model.\n- `onChange: (id: string, model: ModelOption) => void`\n  Called with the new id and its model when a row is chosen.\n- `variant: \"ghost\" | \"outline\"` — default `\"ghost\"`\n  Flat trigger for toolbars, or the kit's bordered field.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Trigger and row density, passed to the kit Select. `sm` also shrinks the row monograms.\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Visible label above the trigger. Without it the select is named by `aria-label`.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Model\"`\n  Accessible name when there is no visible label.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Disables the whole select.\n- `compact: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows only the monogram in the trigger; the name stays for screen readers.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the select wrapper.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ModelSelect({\n  models = MODEL_OPTIONS,\n  value,\n  defaultValue,\n  onChange,\n  variant = \"ghost\",\n  size = \"md\",\n  label,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Model\",\n  isDisabled,\n  compact = false,\n  className,\n}: ModelSelectProps) {\n  const fallback = models.find((m) => !m.isDisabled)?.id ?? models[0]?.id ?? null;\n  const [internal, setInternal] = useState<string | null>(defaultValue ?? fallback);\n  // Controlled when `value` is passed; otherwise the select keeps its own pick.\n  const selectedId = value ?? internal;\n  const selected = models.find((m) => m.id === selectedId);\n\n  return (\n    <Select<ModelOption>\n      label={label}\n      aria-label={label ? undefined : ariaLabel}\n      size={size}\n      isDisabled={isDisabled}\n      selectedKey={selectedId}\n      disabledKeys={models.filter((m) => m.isDisabled).map((m) => m.id)}\n      onSelectionChange={(key) => {\n        if (key == null) return;\n        const id = String(key);\n        const model = models.find((m) => m.id === id);\n        if (!model) return;\n        if (value === undefined) setInternal(id);\n        onChange?.(id, model);\n      }}\n      className={cx(\"w-fit\", className)}\n      triggerClassName={variant === \"ghost\" ? GHOST_TRIGGER : undefined}\n      // The kit listbox caps at 240px; five two-line rows need about 300px.\n      popoverClassName=\"w-[320px] [&_[role=listbox]]:max-h-[336px]\"\n      renderValue={\n        selected ? (\n          <>\n            <ModelMonogram model={selected} small />\n            <span data-model-name className={cx(\"truncate\", compact && \"sr-only\")}>{selected.name}</span>\n          </>\n        ) : (\n          <span className=\"text-text-placeholder\">Choose a model</span>\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {models.map((model) => {\n        const isSelected = model.id === selectedId;\n        return (\n          <SelectItem key={model.id} id={model.id} textValue={model.name} className=\"gap-2.5\">\n            <ModelMonogram model={model} small={size === \"sm\"} />\n            <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n              <Text slot=\"label\" className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5\">\n                <span className=\"truncate\">{model.name}</span>\n                <ModelBadges badges={model.badges} />\n              </Text>\n              {model.description && (\n                <Text slot=\"description\" className=\"truncate text-body-2-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n                  {model.description}\n                </Text>\n              )}\n            </span>\n            <RiCheckLine\n              aria-hidden\n              className={cx(\n                \"size-4 shrink-0 text-accent-500 transition-opacity duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                isSelected ? \"opacity-100\" : \"opacity-0\",\n              )}\n            />\n          </SelectItem>\n        );\n      })}\n    </Select>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component adds no timers or listeners of its own. Open state, outside-press dismissal and focus restore belong to the kit Select and React Aria.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The check fades with `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so it switches instantly. The popover open animation and chevron turn come from the kit Select.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving out `textValue` on rows with rich content. React Aria then cannot build typeahead text or the trigger's accessible value from the row.\n- Putting the description inside the label. Screen readers then read \"Deep 1 Slower, reasons for longer\" as the model's name; the `description` slot keeps it as a hint.\n- Mounting the check only on the selected row. The text column then reflows as the check appears; toggling opacity keeps every row the same width.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The model list, names and descriptions are sample data. Pass your own `models`; badges are any mix of `\"new\"` and `\"pro\"`.\n- Disabled models go to the kit Select as `disabledKeys`, and the uncontrolled default skips them.\n- The kit Select places its popover below the trigger and flips it above when there is no room, which is what happens at the foot of a chat.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe trigger shows a tinted monogram and the model name. Opening it lists five models with descriptions; Atlas 3 and Lumen 1 carry New, Deep 1 and Lumen 1 carry Pro. The current model has a check. Choosing a row closes the menu, moves the check and updates the trigger and the demo sentence. Arrow keys and typeahead work, and Escape closes the menu.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "models-picker": "# Build: Models Picker\n\nThe model list from an agent's settings. Each row shows a small tinted provider monogram, the model name and a control on the right. In `single` mode the rows form one radio group and the chosen row sits on a soft grey fill, the way an active-model menu reads. In `multiple` mode every row is a switch that adds the model to the composer, and the header keeps an \"N of M enabled\" count. A search field filters by name and provider, and `children` render under a divider for things like an effort slider.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA card of models with provider monograms: pick one with radios, or enable several with switches, a search filter and a live count.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Split the props into a discriminated union on `selectionMode`: `single` takes `value: string` and `onChange(id)`, `multiple` takes `value: string[]` and `onChange(ids)`. Both are controlled-or-uncontrolled: local state seeds from `defaultValue` and `value` wins when given.\n2. Single mode renders a react-aria `RadioGroup` whose `Radio`s ARE the rows (monogram, name, kit `RadioDot`), so one Tab stop enters the list, arrow keys move and select, and the selected row gets `bg-background-secondary-default`.\n3. Multiple mode renders each row as a react-aria `Switch` with the kit `SwitchTrack` on the right, inside a `role=\"group\"` named by the label. Toggling rebuilds the value by filtering `models` in their own order, so the array is stable no matter which order rows were flipped.\n4. The search query is trimmed, lowercased and matched against `name + provider + description`; the filtered list replaces the rows, and an empty result prints \"No models match …\" instead. The count in the header always counts the whole list, not the filtered rows.\n5. The monogram is a 28px rounded square with one or two letters (`monogram` or the provider's first letter) on a status tint, `aria-hidden`; the provider name is added as visually hidden text so the radio or switch is announced as \"Fable 5, Anthropic\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `monogram = size-7, rounded 8px, caption-1 semibold`\n  Big enough for two letters at a glance, small enough that the model name stays the first thing read.\n- `row padding = px-2.5 py-2, rounded-2lg, 2px gap`\n  The selected fill needs air around it inside the 6px card padding, and a 2px gap keeps adjacent hover fills from merging.\n- `searchable default = more than 5 models`\n  A short list is faster to scan than to search; past a screenful, a filter pays for its height.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ModelsPicker(props: ({ selectionMode?: \"single\"; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (id: string) => void } | { selectionMode: \"multiple\"; value?: string[]; defaultValue?: string[]; onChange?: (ids: string[]) => void }) & { models: ModelOption[]; label?: ReactNode; searchable?: boolean; searchPlaceholder?: string; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype ModelOption = { id: string; name: string; provider: string; monogram?: string; tone?: ModelMonogramTone; description?: string; isDisabled?: boolean }\n```\n\n- `models: ModelOption[]`\n  Rows in display order: id, name, provider, optional monogram letters, tint, caption and disabled flag.\n- `selectionMode: \"single\" | \"multiple\"` — default `\"single\"`\n  Radio rows for one active model, or switch rows for an enabled set.\n- `value: string | string[]`\n  Controlled selection: an id in single mode, an id array in multiple mode.\n- `defaultValue: string | string[]`\n  Initial selection when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (id: string) => void | (ids: string[]) => void`\n  Called with the new id or the new id array, in the models' order.\n- `label: ReactNode` — default `\"Models\"`\n  Header title; a string also names the radio group or switch group.\n- `searchable: boolean` — default `models.length > 5`\n  Show the search field above the rows.\n- `searchPlaceholder: string` — default `\"Search models\"`\n  Placeholder of the search field.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Footer content under a divider, e.g. an effort control.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ModelsPicker(props: ModelsPickerProps) {\n  const { models, label = \"Models\", searchPlaceholder = \"Search models\", children, className } = props;\n  const multiple = props.selectionMode === \"multiple\";\n  const searchable = props.searchable ?? models.length > 5;\n\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState(\"\");\n  const [single, setSingle] = useState<string | undefined>(\n    props.selectionMode === \"multiple\" ? undefined : props.defaultValue,\n  );\n  const [many, setMany] = useState<string[]>(\n    props.selectionMode === \"multiple\" ? (props.defaultValue ?? []) : [],\n  );\n\n  const selectedOne = props.selectionMode === \"multiple\" ? undefined : (props.value ?? single);\n  const selectedMany = props.selectionMode === \"multiple\" ? (props.value ?? many) : [];\n\n  const pick = (id: string) => {\n    if (props.selectionMode === \"multiple\") return;\n    setSingle(id);\n    props.onChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  const toggle = (id: string, on: boolean) => {\n    if (props.selectionMode !== \"multiple\") return;\n    // Keep the models' own order so the value is stable however it was toggled.\n    const next = models.filter((m) => (m.id === id ? on : selectedMany.includes(m.id))).map((m) => m.id);\n    setMany(next);\n    props.onChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();\n  const shown = q\n    ? models.filter((m) => `${m.name} ${m.provider} ${m.description ?? \"\"}`.toLowerCase().includes(q))\n    : models;\n\n  const groupLabel = typeof label === \"string\" ? label : \"Models\";\n  const count = multiple\n    ? `${models.filter((m) => selectedMany.includes(m.id)).length} of ${models.length} enabled`\n    : `${models.length} available`;\n\n  const empty = (\n    <p className=\"px-2.5 py-6 text-center text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary\">\n      No models match “{query.trim()}”\n    </p>\n  );\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        // Dark: primary and tertiary are both neutral-800, so the card drops to\n        // secondary or the off switch tracks and the search field disappear.\n        \"flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-1 rounded-2xl border border-border-table bg-background-primary-default p-1.5 shadow-xs dark:bg-background-secondary-default\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <header className=\"flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 px-2.5 pt-1.5 pb-1\">\n        <h3 className=\"text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">{label}</h3>\n        <span className=\"text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n          {count}\n        </span>\n      </header>\n\n      {searchable ? (\n        <SearchField\n          aria-label={`Search ${groupLabel.toLowerCase()}`}\n          placeholder={searchPlaceholder}\n          size=\"small\"\n          value={query}\n          onChange={setQuery}\n          className=\"px-1 pb-1\"\n        />\n      ) : null}\n\n      {multiple ? (\n        <div role=\"group\" aria-label={groupLabel} className=\"flex flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          {shown.length === 0\n            ? empty\n            : shown.map((m) => (\n                <AriaSwitch\n                  key={m.id}\n                  isSelected={selectedMany.includes(m.id)}\n                  isDisabled={m.isDisabled}\n                  onChange={(on) => toggle(m.id, on)}\n                  className={({ isHovered, isDisabled }) =>\n                    cx(\n                      row,\n                      isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\" : \"cursor-pointer\",\n                      isHovered && \"bg-background-primary-hover\",\n                    )\n                  }\n                >\n                  {(state) => (\n                    <>\n                      <ModelMonogram model={m} />\n                      <ModelText model={m} />\n                      <SwitchTrack state={state} size=\"sm\" />\n                    </>\n                  )}\n                </AriaSwitch>\n              ))}\n        </div>\n      ) : (\n        <AriaRadioGroup\n          aria-label={groupLabel}\n          value={selectedOne ?? null}\n          onChange={pick}\n          className=\"flex flex-col gap-0.5\"\n        >\n          {shown.length === 0\n            ? empty\n            : shown.map((m) => (\n                <AriaRadio\n                  key={m.id}\n                  value={m.id}\n                  isDisabled={m.isDisabled}\n                  className={({ isSelected, isHovered, isDisabled }) =>\n                    cx(\n                      row,\n                      isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\" : \"cursor-pointer\",\n                      isSelected\n                        ? \"bg-background-secondary-default dark:bg-background-primary-default\"\n                        : isHovered && \"bg-background-primary-hover\",\n                    )\n                  }\n                >\n                  {({ isSelected, isFocusVisible }) => (\n                    <>\n                      <ModelMonogram model={m} />\n                      <ModelText model={m} />\n                      <RadioDot selected={isSelected} focusVisible={isFocusVisible} />\n                    </>\n                  )}\n                </AriaRadio>\n              ))}\n        </AriaRadioGroup>\n      )}\n\n      {children != null && children !== false ? (\n        <>\n          <hr className=\"mx-1 mt-1 border-border-table\" />\n          <div className=\"px-2.5 pt-1.5 pb-2\">{children}</div>\n        </>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: state is the query and the uncontrolled selection, with no timers, listeners or observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 150ms row hover and selected fill, plus the kit switch thumb; rows carry `motion-reduce:transition-none` and the switch track follows its own reduced-motion rule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Wrapping a radio input in a clickable div. The row must BE the radio (or switch) so the whole row is the hit target and the accessible name covers the model, not just the dot.\n- Counting the filtered rows. \"2 of 8 enabled\" must not drop to \"1 of 3\" while a search is typed.\n- Drawing provider logos. The glyph is letters on a tint, which avoids trademark artwork and still tells providers apart.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `searchable` explicitly to force the field on a short list or hide it on a long one.\n- The demo puts the kit `Slider` (0–3, no tooltip) under the active-model list as an effort control with Faster and Smarter labels.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nLeft card: \"Fable 5\" is selected; clicking \"Sonnet 5\" moves the grey fill and the radio, and ArrowDown from it wraps to \"Composer 2.5\". Pressing ArrowRight on the Effort slider changes \"Medium\" to \"High\". Right card reads \"4 of 8 enabled\"; switching on Gemini makes it \"5 of 8\"; typing \"anthropic\" leaves 3 rows with the count unchanged, and \"zzz\" shows \"No models match\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "month-stepper": "# Build: Month Stepper\n\nThe calendar header's month switcher: a 36px bordered pill with a chevron button at each end and the formatted month centred between them. The label is a focusable spinbutton, so the whole control is usable from the keyboard without tabbing between the buttons. Each step slides the new label in from the side it came from.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA bordered month label between prev/next chevrons, with spinbutton keys: arrows step a month, Page Up/Down a year, Home/End the bounds.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. State is a `CalendarDate` from `@internationalized/date`, normalised with `startOfMonth`. `value` makes it controlled; otherwise `defaultValue` (or the current month) seeds internal state. Every change is emitted as the first day of the month.\n2. Bounds and comparisons use `monthIndex(date) = year * 12 + month - 1`, a monotonic integer. `step(n)` clamps `current + n` to `[min, max]`, does nothing when the clamped target equals the current month, and otherwise calls `month.add({ months: target - current })`. The chevrons are disabled at the bounds.\n3. The label is a `role=\"spinbutton\"` span with `aria-valuenow` set to the month index and `aria-valuetext` set to the formatted label. Its key handler maps ArrowUp and the reading-direction arrow to +1, ArrowDown and the opposite arrow to -1, PageUp/PageDown to ±12, and Home/End to the min/max when they are set. ArrowLeft/ArrowRight swap under RTL.\n4. The label text comes from a `DateFormatter(locale, { month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\", ...formatOptions, timeZone: \"UTC\" })` applied to `month.toDate(\"UTC\")`, so server and client format the same instant and hydration matches.\n5. An effect compares the new month index with the previous one and runs `element.animate()` on the inner label: opacity 0 → 1 and translateX(±8px) → 0 over 180ms, with the sign from the step direction (mirrored in RTL). The effect returns `animation.cancel()`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `height = 36px medium / 32px small`\n  Matches the kit's icon buttons and inputs, so the stepper lines up with the notification and inbox buttons beside it in the calendar header.\n- `button box = 34px / 30px inside a 1px border`\n  The chevron hit area fills the pill's inner height exactly, so the hover tint touches the border with a 9px (or 7px) radius that follows the outer 10px (or 8px) corner.\n- `default width = w-80 (320px)`\n  The calendar header gives the stepper a fixed width so the chevrons do not jump as the month name changes length (May vs September).\n- `label slide = 8px, 180ms, cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1)`\n  Enough travel to show which way the month moved without the text leaving the pill. Short enough that holding an arrow key stays readable.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MonthStepper(props: { value?: CalendarDate; defaultValue?: CalendarDate; onChange?: (month: CalendarDate) => void; minValue?: CalendarDate; maxValue?: CalendarDate; locale?: string; formatOptions?: Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; \"aria-label\"?: string; previousLabel?: string; nextLabel?: string; isDisabled?: boolean; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction monthIndex(date: CalendarDate): number\n```\n\n- `value: CalendarDate`\n  Controlled month. Any day in the month works.\n- `defaultValue: CalendarDate` — default `current month`\n  Uncontrolled starting month.\n- `onChange: (month: CalendarDate) => void`\n  Called with the first day of the newly shown month.\n- `minValue / maxValue: CalendarDate`\n  Inclusive month bounds. The chevrons disable and Home/End jump to them.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for the month label.\n- `formatOptions: Intl.DateTimeFormatOptions` — default `{ month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\" }`\n  Overrides for the label format, e.g. `{ month: \"short\" }`.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px or 32px tall.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Month\"`\n  Name of the group and the spinbutton.\n- `previousLabel / nextLabel: string` — default `\"Previous month\" / \"Next month\"`\n  Accessible names of the chevron buttons.\n- `isDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Disables both buttons and removes the label from the tab order.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function MonthStepper({\n  value,\n  defaultValue,\n  onChange,\n  minValue,\n  maxValue,\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  formatOptions,\n  size = \"medium\",\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Month\",\n  previousLabel = \"Previous month\",\n  nextLabel = \"Next month\",\n  isDisabled = false,\n  className,\n  ref,\n}: MonthStepperProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const reducedMotion = useReducedMotion();\n  const [uncontrolled, setUncontrolled] = useState<CalendarDate>(() =>\n    startOfMonth(defaultValue ?? today(getLocalTimeZone())),\n  );\n  const month = startOfMonth(value ?? uncontrolled);\n  const current = monthIndex(month);\n  const min = minValue ? monthIndex(minValue) : -Infinity;\n  const max = maxValue ? monthIndex(maxValue) : Infinity;\n\n  const formatter = useMemo(\n    () => new DateFormatter(locale, { month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\", ...formatOptions, timeZone: \"UTC\" }),\n    [locale, formatOptions],\n  );\n  const label = formatter.format(month.toDate(\"UTC\"));\n\n  const step = (months: number) => {\n    const target = Math.min(max, Math.max(min, current + months));\n    if (isDisabled || target === current) return;\n    const next = month.add({ months: target - current });\n    setUncontrolled(next);\n    onChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  // Slide the label in from the side the user stepped towards. The Web\n  // Animations API needs no keyframes in the stylesheet and cancels cleanly.\n  const labelRef = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const previous = useRef(current);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const delta = current - previous.current;\n    previous.current = current;\n    const el = labelRef.current;\n    if (!el || delta === 0 || reducedMotion || typeof el.animate !== \"function\") return;\n    const from = (delta > 0 ? 8 : -8) * (direction === \"rtl\" ? -1 : 1);\n    const animation = el.animate(\n      [\n        { opacity: 0, transform: `translateX(${from}px)` },\n        { opacity: 1, transform: \"translateX(0)\" },\n      ],\n      { duration: 180, easing: \"cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1)\" },\n    );\n    return () => animation.cancel();\n  }, [current, reducedMotion, direction]);\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLSpanElement>) => {\n    const forward = direction === \"rtl\" ? \"ArrowLeft\" : \"ArrowRight\";\n    const back = direction === \"rtl\" ? \"ArrowRight\" : \"ArrowLeft\";\n    let delta: number | null = null;\n    if (event.key === \"ArrowUp\" || event.key === forward) delta = 1;\n    else if (event.key === \"ArrowDown\" || event.key === back) delta = -1;\n    else if (event.key === \"PageUp\") delta = 12;\n    else if (event.key === \"PageDown\") delta = -12;\n    else if (event.key === \"Home\" && Number.isFinite(min)) delta = min - current;\n    else if (event.key === \"End\" && Number.isFinite(max)) delta = max - current;\n    if (delta === null) return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    step(delta);\n  };\n\n  const s = sizes[size];\n  const buttonClass = cx(\n    \"inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center text-foreground-icon-primary\",\n    \"transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-primary-hover active:bg-background-primary-active\",\n    \"outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n    \"disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:text-foreground-disabled disabled:hover:bg-transparent\",\n    s.button,\n  );\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={ref}\n      role=\"group\"\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      data-disabled={isDisabled || undefined}\n      className={cx(\n        \"inline-flex w-80 max-w-full items-center justify-between gap-1 border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs\",\n        \"data-[disabled]:opacity-60\",\n        s.root,\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        aria-label={previousLabel}\n        disabled={isDisabled || current <= min}\n        onClick={() => step(-1)}\n        className={buttonClass}\n      >\n        <RiArrowLeftSLine aria-hidden className={cx(s.icon, \"rtl:rotate-180\")} />\n      </button>\n      <span\n        role=\"spinbutton\"\n        tabIndex={isDisabled ? -1 : 0}\n        aria-label={ariaLabel}\n        aria-valuenow={current}\n        aria-valuetext={label}\n        aria-valuemin={Number.isFinite(min) ? min : undefined}\n        aria-valuemax={Number.isFinite(max) ? max : undefined}\n        aria-disabled={isDisabled || undefined}\n        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n        className={cx(\n          \"min-w-0 flex-1 cursor-default select-none overflow-hidden rounded-md text-center text-text-primary outline-none\",\n          \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n          s.label,\n        )}\n      >\n        <span ref={labelRef} className=\"block truncate tabular-nums\" aria-hidden>\n          {label}\n        </span>\n      </span>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        aria-label={nextLabel}\n        disabled={isDisabled || current >= max}\n        onClick={() => step(1)}\n        className={buttonClass}\n      >\n        <RiArrowRightSLine aria-hidden className={cx(s.icon, \"rtl:rotate-180\")} />\n      </button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The slide effect returns `animation.cancel()`, so a fast second step or an unmount stops the running animation. There are no listeners, timers or observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The label slide is skipped entirely: `useReducedMotion()` gates the `element.animate()` call, so the new month replaces the old one instantly. Button hover tints still change colour.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Comparing months with `CalendarDate.compare`. Day-of-month differences make 2026-08-15 \"after\" 2026-08-01 even though they are the same month. Compare `monthIndex` values instead.\n- Formatting with the runtime's local time zone. `toDate(getLocalTimeZone())` can land on the previous day in UTC on the server, and the label hydrates as July. Format a UTC date with `timeZone: \"UTC\"`.\n- Stepping with `.set({ month: month + 1 })`. It does not roll the year over; `.add({ months })` does.\n- Animating on every render. The effect must compare with the previous month index, or a parent re-render replays the slide.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Without `value` or `defaultValue` the stepper starts on the current month in the viewer's time zone. Pass a fixed date when the markup is server rendered.\n- The chevron buttons stay in the tab order for pointer-first users of assistive tech, and the spinbutton covers keyboard stepping.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe stepper shows \"August 2026\". Clicking the right chevron shows \"September 2026\" and the label slides in from the right; the left chevron goes back. Focusing the label and pressing ArrowRight steps forward, PageUp jumps to the same month next year. A stepper bounded to January–December 2026 disables its next chevron on December and ignores further ArrowUp presses; Home jumps to January.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "month-view-grid": "# Build: Month View Grid\n\nThe month page of the calendar template: a grey frame, weekday headers and six rows of rounded day cells. Days before and after the month are muted but keep their events in full colour. Each day lists its events as compact kit chips at the bottom of the cell; past `maxVisible` the rest collapse into a \"+N more\" button on the date line, which selects the day and calls `onShowMore`. The selected day gets an accent pill and today an accent number. It is an ARIA grid with a single tab stop: arrows, Home / End and Page Up / Page Down move focus and turn the month when needed. Below 560px of its own width the cells swap chips for colour dots. `renderEvent` wraps chips, for example in an EventDetailPopover, and `eventsOnDay` feeds an agenda panel.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSix-week month calendar: muted outside days, event chips per day with \"+N more\", a selected-day pill and full arrow-key navigation.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The grid always shows 42 days starting at `startOfWeek(startOfMonth(month), locale, firstDayOfWeek)`, so every month has the same six rows and the layout never jumps. `month`, `selectedDate` and the focused day are each controlled or uncontrolled independently.\n2. One memoised pass sorts the events (all-day first, then start, longer first) and buckets them by every visible day they touch; an event ending exactly at midnight stops the day before, so all-day events stored midnight to midnight cover the right days. Each cell shows the first `maxVisible` and counts the rest for \"+N more\", which sits on the number line so it never costs a chip row.\n3. Roving focus: exactly one gridcell has `tabIndex=0`, the focused day if it is on screen, else the selected day, else today, else the 1st. The chips and \"+N more\" inside that cell are tabbable; all others are `-1`, so Tab enters the grid once and leaves after the active day.\n4. A single `onKeyDown` on the grid acts only when the target is a gridcell (keys on chips, buttons and portaled popovers are left alone). It reads the day from `data-date`, moves ±1 day (mirrored in RTL), ±7 days, to `startOfWeek` / `endOfWeek`, or ±1 month (Shift: ±1 year), calls `onMonthChange` when the target leaves the month, and sets a flag so an effect focuses `[data-date=…]` after the new month renders.\n5. Cells carry `--cell-min-h = 34 + 24 × maxVisible` px and use it only at container widths of 560px and up (`@min-[560px]:min-h-(--cell-min-h)`); narrower, they drop to 48px with up to three colour dots and the header shows narrow weekday letters.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `weeks = 6 (42 days)`\n  Months span four to six weeks; always drawing six keeps the grid height fixed when paging.\n- `maxVisible = 3 (demo 2)`\n  Three 22px chips fill a ~110px cell; the demo uses two so six rows fit in 560px.\n- `cell min height = 34 + 24 × maxVisible px`\n  12px padding, the 20px number line, a 4px gap and 22px chips with 2px gaps.\n- `compact breakpoint = 560px container width`\n  Below ~80px per column a chip cannot show a title, so cells switch to colour dots.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MonthViewGrid(props: { events: CalendarEvent[]; month?: CalendarDate; defaultMonth?: CalendarDate; onMonthChange?: (month: CalendarDate) => void; selectedDate?: CalendarDate | null; defaultSelectedDate?: CalendarDate | null; onSelectedDateChange?: (date: CalendarDate) => void; today?: CalendarDate; firstDayOfWeek?: DayOfWeek; selectedEventId?: string | null; maxVisible?: number; onShowMore?: (date: CalendarDate, events: CalendarEvent[]) => void; onEventClick?: (event: CalendarEvent) => void; renderEvent?: (event: CalendarEvent, chip: ReactElement, day: CalendarDate) => ReactNode; locale?: string; hourCycle?: 12 | 24; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction eventsOnDay(events: CalendarEvent[], day: CalendarDate): CalendarEvent[]\n```\n\n- `events: CalendarEvent[]`\n  Timed and all-day events; multi-day events appear on each day.\n- `month / defaultMonth / onMonthChange: CalendarDate`\n  Any day in the month to show; called with the 1st when keyboard navigation leaves the month.\n- `selectedDate / defaultSelectedDate / onSelectedDateChange: CalendarDate | null` — default `null`\n  The day with the accent pill; set by click, Enter, Space or \"+N more\".\n- `today: CalendarDate`\n  Accent number and initial tab stop. Never read from the clock inside.\n- `firstDayOfWeek: 'sun' | 'mon' | … | 'sat'` — default `locale`\n  First column.\n- `maxVisible: number` — default `3`\n  Chips per day before \"+N more\".\n- `onShowMore: (date, events) => void`\n  \"+N more\" was pressed; the day is selected too.\n- `selectedEventId: string | null`\n  Rings the matching chip.\n- `onEventClick: (event) => void`\n  Called when a chip is pressed.\n- `renderEvent: (event, chip, day) => ReactNode`\n  Wrap each chip, for example in an EventDetailPopover; key open state on the day too, since multi-day events render once per day.\n- `hourCycle: 12 | 24` — default `24`\n  Chip times.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Weekday names, first day of week, labels.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function MonthViewGrid({\n  events,\n  month: controlledMonth,\n  defaultMonth,\n  onMonthChange,\n  selectedDate: controlledSelected,\n  defaultSelectedDate = null,\n  onSelectedDateChange,\n  today,\n  firstDayOfWeek,\n  selectedEventId,\n  maxVisible = 3,\n  onShowMore,\n  onEventClick,\n  renderEvent,\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  hourCycle = 24,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  ref,\n}: MonthViewGridProps) {\n  const [ownMonth, setOwnMonth] = useState<CalendarDate | undefined>(defaultMonth);\n  const [ownSelected, setOwnSelected] = useState<CalendarDate | null>(defaultSelectedDate);\n  const [focused, setFocused] = useState<CalendarDate | null>(null);\n  const gridRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const moveFocus = useRef(false);\n\n  const selected = controlledSelected !== undefined ? controlledSelected : ownSelected;\n  const first = events[0];\n  const month = startOfMonth(\n    controlledMonth ?? ownMonth ?? selected ?? today ?? (first ? toCalendarDate(first.start) : parseDate(\"2026-01-01\")),\n  );\n  const gridStart = startOfWeek(month, locale, firstDayOfWeek);\n  // Always six weeks, so the grid keeps its height from month to month.\n  const days = useMemo(() => Array.from({ length: 42 }, (_, i) => gridStart.add({ days: i })), [gridStart.toString()]); // eslint-disable-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  const gridEnd = days[41] ?? gridStart;\n\n  // Bucket events by the visible days they touch (multi-day events repeat on each day).\n  const byDay = useMemo(() => {\n    const map = new Map<string, CalendarEvent[]>();\n    for (const event of [...events].sort(byStart)) {\n      let day = toCalendarDate(event.start);\n      if (day.compare(gridStart) < 0) day = gridStart;\n      const last = lastDayOf(event);\n      for (; day.compare(last) <= 0 && day.compare(gridEnd) <= 0; day = day.add({ days: 1 })) {\n        const key = day.toString();\n        const list = map.get(key);\n        if (list) list.push(event);\n        else map.set(key, [event]);\n      }\n    }\n    return map;\n  }, [events, gridStart.toString()]); // eslint-disable-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n\n  // One tab stop: the focused day if it is on screen, else the selected day\n  // in this month, else today in this month, else the 1st.\n  const inGrid = (d: CalendarDate | null | undefined): d is CalendarDate =>\n    !!d && d.compare(gridStart) >= 0 && d.compare(gridEnd) <= 0;\n  const tabStop = inGrid(focused)\n    ? focused\n    : selected && isSameMonth(selected, month)\n      ? selected\n      : today && isSameMonth(today, month)\n        ? today\n        : month;\n\n  // After a keyboard move (possibly to another month) focus the new cell\n  // once it has rendered.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!moveFocus.current || !focused) return;\n    moveFocus.current = false;\n    gridRef.current?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-date=\"${focused.toString()}\"]`)?.focus();\n  }, [focused, month]);\n\n  const changeMonth = (next: CalendarDate) => {\n    const start = startOfMonth(next);\n    if (start.compare(month) === 0) return;\n    setOwnMonth(start);\n    onMonthChange?.(start);\n  };\n  const select = (date: CalendarDate) => {\n    setOwnSelected(date);\n    onSelectedDateChange?.(date);\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    const cell = e.target as HTMLElement;\n    const iso = cell.dataset.date;\n    // Keys pressed on a chip or \"+N more\" belong to that button.\n    if (!iso || cell.getAttribute(\"role\") !== \"gridcell\") return;\n    const from = parseDate(iso);\n    if (e.key === \"Enter\" || e.key === \" \") {\n      e.preventDefault();\n      select(from);\n      return;\n    }\n    if (!NAV_KEYS.has(e.key)) return;\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const rtl = getComputedStyle(cell).direction === \"rtl\";\n    const step = { ArrowLeft: rtl ? 1 : -1, ArrowRight: rtl ? -1 : 1, ArrowUp: -7, ArrowDown: 7 } as Record<string, number>;\n    let to: CalendarDate;\n    if (e.key in step) to = from.add({ days: step[e.key] ?? 0 });\n    else if (e.key === \"Home\") to = startOfWeek(from, locale, firstDayOfWeek);\n    else if (e.key === \"End\") to = endOfWeek(from, locale, firstDayOfWeek);\n    else to = from.add(e.shiftKey ? { years: e.key === \"PageUp\" ? -1 : 1 } : { months: e.key === \"PageUp\" ? -1 : 1 });\n    moveFocus.current = true;\n    setFocused(to);\n    if (!isSameMonth(to, month)) changeMonth(to);\n  };\n\n  const weekdays = days.slice(0, 7);\n  const rows = Array.from({ length: 6 }, (_, r) => days.slice(r * 7, r * 7 + 7));\n  // 12px padding + 20px number line + 4px gap + 22px chips with 2px gaps.\n  const cellStyle = { \"--cell-min-h\": `${34 + maxVisible * 24}px` } as CSSProperties;\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={ref}\n      className={cx(\"@container w-full rounded-[20px] bg-background-secondary-default p-1.5\", className)}\n    >\n      <div\n        ref={gridRef}\n        role=\"grid\"\n        aria-label={ariaLabel ?? formatDay(month, locale, { month: \"long\", year: \"numeric\" })}\n        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n        className=\"flex flex-col gap-1\"\n      >\n        <div role=\"row\" className=\"grid grid-cols-7 gap-1 @min-[560px]:gap-x-1.5\">\n          {weekdays.map((day) => (\n            <div\n              key={day.toString()}\n              role=\"columnheader\"\n              aria-label={formatDay(day, locale, { weekday: \"long\" })}\n              className=\"py-1 text-center text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\"\n            >\n              <span className=\"@min-[560px]:hidden\">{formatDay(day, locale, { weekday: \"narrow\" })}</span>\n              <span className=\"hidden @min-[560px]:inline\">{formatDay(day, locale, { weekday: \"short\" })}</span>\n            </div>\n          ))}\n        </div>\n\n        {rows.map((week) => (\n          <div key={week[0]?.toString()} role=\"row\" className=\"grid grid-cols-7 gap-1 @min-[560px]:gap-x-1.5\">\n            {week.map((day) => {\n              const key = day.toString();\n              const list = byDay.get(key) ?? [];\n              const outside = !isSameMonth(day, month);\n              const isSelected = !!selected && isSameDay(day, selected);\n              const isToday = !!today && isSameDay(day, today);\n              const isStop = isSameDay(day, tabStop);\n              // \"+N more\" shares the number's line, so it never costs a chip row.\n              const shown = list.slice(0, maxVisible);\n              const hidden = list.length - shown.length;\n              const name = `${formatDay(day, locale, { weekday: \"long\", month: \"long\", day: \"numeric\" })}${\n                isToday ? \", today\" : \"\"\n              }${list.length ? `, ${list.length} ${list.length === 1 ? \"event\" : \"events\"}` : \"\"}`;\n\n              return (\n                <div\n                  key={key}\n                  role=\"gridcell\"\n                  data-date={key}\n                  data-outside-month={outside || undefined}\n                  data-today={isToday || undefined}\n                  aria-selected={isSelected}\n                  aria-label={name}\n                  tabIndex={isStop ? 0 : -1}\n                  style={cellStyle}\n                  onClick={() => {\n                    setFocused(day);\n                    select(day);\n                  }}\n                  onFocus={(e) => {\n                    if (e.target === e.currentTarget) setFocused(day);\n                  }}\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"flex min-h-10 min-w-0 cursor-default flex-col gap-1 rounded-lg p-1 outline-none @min-[560px]:min-h-(--cell-min-h) @min-[560px]:rounded-xl @min-[560px]:p-1.5\",\n                    \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n                    outside\n                      ? \"bg-background-tertiary-default dark:bg-background-inner-default\"\n                      : \"bg-background-primary-default\",\n                  )}\n                >\n                  <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-1\">\n                    <span\n                      aria-hidden\n                      className={cx(\n                        \"flex h-5 min-w-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full px-1.5 tabular-nums\",\n                        isToday ? \"text-body-2-semibold\" : \"text-body-2-medium\",\n                        isSelected\n                          ? \"bg-accent-500 text-text-white\"\n                          : isToday\n                            ? \"text-accent-500\"\n                            : outside\n                              ? \"text-text-secondary\"\n                              : \"text-text-primary\",\n                      )}\n                    >\n                      {day.day}\n                    </span>\n                    {hidden > 0 ? (\n                      <button\n                        type=\"button\"\n                        tabIndex={isStop ? 0 : -1}\n                        aria-label={`${hidden} more on ${formatDay(day, locale, { month: \"long\", day: \"numeric\" })}`}\n                        onClick={(e) => {\n                          e.stopPropagation();\n                          setFocused(day);\n                          select(day);\n                          onShowMore?.(day, list);\n                        }}\n                        className=\"hidden h-5 truncate rounded-md px-1 text-caption-2-semibold text-text-secondary outline-none transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-default hover:text-text-primary focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring motion-reduce:transition-none @min-[560px]:block\"\n                      >\n                        +{hidden} more\n                      </button>\n                    ) : null}\n                  </div>\n\n                  {list.length > 0 ? (\n                    <>\n                      <ul className=\"mt-auto hidden min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5 @min-[560px]:flex\">\n                        {shown.map((event) => {\n                          const chip = (\n                            <CalendarEventChip\n                              title={event.title}\n                              color={event.color}\n                              start={event.start}\n                              end={event.end}\n                              allDay={event.allDay}\n                              locale={locale}\n                              hourCycle={hourCycle}\n                              selected={selectedEventId === event.id}\n                              tabIndex={isStop ? 0 : -1}\n                              onClick={onEventClick ? () => onEventClick(event) : undefined}\n                            />\n                          );\n                          return (\n                            <li key={event.id} className=\"flex min-w-0\">\n                              {renderEvent ? renderEvent(event, chip, day) : chip}\n                            </li>\n                          );\n                        })}\n                      </ul>\n                      <div aria-hidden className=\"mt-auto flex justify-center gap-0.5 pb-0.5 @min-[560px]:hidden\">\n                        {list.slice(0, 3).map((event) => (\n                          <span key={event.id} className={cx(\"size-1.5 rounded-full\", calendarEventColorStyles[event.color].dot)} />\n                        ))}\n                      </div>\n                    </>\n                  ) : null}\n                </div>\n              );\n            })}\n          </div>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** One effect focuses the new cell after a keyboard move and returns nothing; there are no listeners, timers or observers outside React handlers. Formatters are cached in a module-level map.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves: paging swaps the cells instantly and focus jumps without scrolling animation. The only transitions are 150ms colour changes on \"+N more\" (dropped under `motion-reduce`) and the kit chip hover brightness and selection ring, which change no position or size.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Importing `compareDate` from @internationalized/date. It is not exported from the package entry; use `a.compare(b)` on the dates instead.\n- Handling arrow keys for every keydown in the grid. React bubbles events from portaled popovers through their React parent, so a popover opened from a chip would move the grid focus; check that the target is a gridcell.\n- Making every cell and chip tabbable. 42 cells and their chips turn Tab into a trap; keep one tab stop and move it with the arrows.\n- Calling `.focus()` right after changing the month. The new cell is not in the DOM yet; set the focused day and focus it in an effect after the render.\n- Formatting weekday names from a local `Date`. Server and browser zones can disagree about the day; the grid formats a fixed UTC noon built from the calendar fields.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Clicking a muted outside day selects it without turning the month; arrowing onto it does turn the month, as a focused cell must stay in the visible month to keep the tab stop predictable.\n- `eventsOnDay(events, day)` is exported and uses the same multi-day and midnight rules, for agenda or side panels.\n- Pass `selectedEventId` to ring the chip whose popover is open.\n- Multi-day events render a chip on every day they touch. When wrapping chips in popovers, key the open state on the event and the `day` argument, or one click opens a popover per day.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAugust 2026 opens with Jul 26 to Sep 5 in six rows, July and September days muted with their Coffee, Payday and Payday chips still coloured. Thursday the 20th shows a selected pill, two chips and \"+4 more\"; the side panel lists its six events. Clicking a cell moves the pill and the panel. With a cell focused, ArrowRight / ArrowDown move by a day and a week, Page Down turns to September and focuses the same date, and Page Up returns. Holidays repeats on the 11th to 13th. Clicking a chip opens its details popover; Delete removes it, updates \"+N more\" and offers Undo. At 360px the cells show colour dots.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "morphsvg-letter-scramble": "# Build: Letter Scramble\n\nEvery character flips through a handful of wrong glyphs and then lands on the right one, each character starting a tenth of a second after the one before it, so the resolution sweeps left to right across the word. Each successive flip is held slightly longer than the last, which is what makes it read as settling rather than as a spinner that was switched off. Character widths are frozen to their final glyph before the first substitution, so the word does not breathe while it scrambles.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA word assembles out of noise — each character flips through wrong glyphs, decelerating onto the right one.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The word is split in JSX to one `inline-block` span per character, each carrying `data-final` with its resolved glyph. React owns every node; nothing is injected into the DOM.\n2. A single `gsap.timeline` schedules `tl.call()` at absolute times. For character `i` the start time is `START_DELAY + i * stagger`; for flip `f` the time advances by `baseFlip + f * flipGrowth`, so hold durations grow 0.08, 0.105, 0.13, 0.155 … That arithmetic progression is the deceleration.\n3. Each `call` assigns `c.textContent = randomGlyph`. After the last flip a final `call` writes `c.dataset.final`, and a `fromTo(c, { opacity: DIM }, { opacity: 1, duration: settle, ease: \"power3.out\" })` runs at the same time — the fade is what separates \"resolved\" from \"one more wrong glyph\".\n4. Glyphs come from a seeded PRNG (`createRng(seed ?? text + \":\" + run)` and `randInt`), never `Math.random`. The glyph sequence decides what is painted on the first client frame, and a server/client disagreement about that is a hydration mismatch.\n5. Before the timeline is built, every character box is pinned with `c.style.width = getBoundingClientRect().width + \"px\"`, measured from the *final* glyph that is currently in the DOM. Proportional type gives `i` and `W` wildly different advance widths, so an unpinned word visibly breathes and every element after it on the line shudders in sympathy. A final `tl.call` at position `\">\"` clears the widths so the word reflows normally afterwards.\n6. The whole build is deferred behind `document.fonts.ready`, because measuring before the webfont lands pins every box to a fallback-font width.\n7. The real word is rendered once in an `sr-only` span and the animated characters are `aria-hidden`, since mid-flight they are literal garbage.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `GLYPHS = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789#%&@$*'`\n  The substitution pool. Uppercase and figures keep the visual weight even; adding lowercase mixes x-height and cap-height so the word appears to jitter vertically as well as horizontally. The punctuation adds texture without introducing very narrow glyphs.\n- `FLIPS = 7`\n  Wrong glyphs per character before resolving. Below ~4 there is no sense of searching; past ~10 the tail characters of a long word are still churning after the reader has looked away.\n- `STAGGER = 0.1`\n  Seconds between adjacent characters starting. Roughly the flip interval, so the sweep front is a couple of characters wide. Much smaller and the whole word scrambles as a block, losing the left-to-right read.\n- `BASE_FLIP = 0.08`\n  Seconds the first wrong glyph is held. About five frames at 60Hz — fast enough to be a blur, slow enough that the substitution is not skipped entirely on a slow frame.\n- `FLIP_GROWTH = 0.025`\n  Seconds added to each successive hold. This is the deceleration and it is the whole character of the effect: at 0 the flipping is constant-interval and reads as a slot machine that stopped, rather than as a search converging.\n- `SETTLE = 0.45`\n  Seconds of the opacity fade landing with the final glyph. Long enough to be a distinct beat after 0.08s flips; a shorter fade is indistinguishable from another substitution.\n- `DIM = 0.35`\n  Opacity the settle fade starts from. Fading from 0 makes the character disappear for a frame between the last wrong glyph and the right one, which reads as a dropped frame.\n- `START_DELAY = 0.25`\n  Seconds before the first character begins. Gives the page a moment to settle, so the scramble is not competing with layout for the reader's attention.\n- `VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.4`\n  Fraction visible before the timeline runs, so a headline low on the page has actually arrived before it scrambles.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction LetterScramble(props: { text: string; glyphs?: string; flips?: number; stagger?: number; baseFlip?: number; flipGrowth?: number; settle?: number; replayOnHover?: boolean; startOnView?: boolean; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `text: string`\n  The resolved word. Rendered as-is for SSR, reduced motion, and assistive technology.\n- `glyphs: string` — default `'A-Z0-9#%&@$*'`\n  Substitution pool. Keep to glyphs of similar visual weight.\n- `flips: number` — default `7`\n  Wrong glyphs shown per character before it resolves.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.1`\n  Seconds between adjacent characters starting.\n- `baseFlip: number` — default `0.08`\n  Seconds the first wrong glyph is held.\n- `flipGrowth: number` — default `0.025`\n  Seconds added to each successive hold. This is the deceleration.\n- `settle: number` — default `0.45`\n  Seconds of the fade that lands with the final glyph.\n- `replayOnHover: boolean` — default `true`\n  Re-run on pointer enter, on fine pointers only.\n- `startOnView: boolean` — default `true`\n  Run when scrolled into view rather than on mount.\n- `seed: number | string`\n  Any string or number. The same seed gives the same sequence on every render.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer span via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst GLYPHS = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789#%&@$*';\nconst FLIPS = 7;\nconst STAGGER = 0.1;\nconst BASE_FLIP = 0.08;\nconst FLIP_GROWTH = 0.025;\nconst SETTLE = 0.45;\nconst START_DELAY = 0.25;\nconst DIM = 0.35;\nconst VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.4;\n\nexport function LetterScramble({\n  text,\n  glyphs = GLYPHS,\n  flips = FLIPS,\n  stagger = STAGGER,\n  baseFlip = BASE_FLIP,\n  flipGrowth = FLIP_GROWTH,\n  settle = SETTLE,\n  replayOnHover = true,\n  startOnView = true,\n  seed,\n  className,\n}: LetterScrambleProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { inView } = useInView(root, { threshold: VIEW_THRESHOLD, once: true });\n  const [run, setRun] = useState(0);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n    if (startOnView && !inView) return;\n\n    const chars = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-char]'));\n    if (chars.length === 0 || glyphs.length === 0) return;\n\n    // Seeded, never Math.random: the glyph sequence decides what is painted\n    // on the first client frame, and a server/client disagreement about that\n    // is a hydration mismatch.\n    const rng = createRng(seed ?? `${text}:${run}`);\n\n    let cancelled = false;\n    let tl: gsap.core.Timeline | null = null;\n\n    const restore = () => {\n      for (const c of chars) {\n        c.textContent = c.dataset.final ?? '';\n        c.style.width = '';\n        c.style.opacity = '';\n      }\n    };\n\n    const build = () => {\n      if (cancelled) return;\n\n      // Measured after fonts are ready, and measured from the *final* glyph,\n      // which is what is in the DOM right now. Measuring a substituted glyph\n      // would lock in the wrong box.\n      for (const c of chars) {\n        c.style.width = `${c.getBoundingClientRect().width}px`;\n      }\n\n      tl = gsap.timeline();\n\n      chars.forEach((c, i) => {\n        const final = c.dataset.final ?? '';\n        let t = START_DELAY + i * stagger;\n\n        for (let f = 0; f < flips; f += 1) {\n          const g = glyphs.charAt(randInt(rng, 0, glyphs.length));\n          tl?.call(\n            () => {\n              c.textContent = g;\n            },\n            undefined,\n            t,\n          );\n          t += baseFlip + f * flipGrowth;\n        }\n\n        tl?.call(\n          () => {\n            c.textContent = final;\n          },\n          undefined,\n          t,\n        );\n        // The fade is what separates \"resolved\" from \"one more wrong glyph\".\n        tl?.fromTo(\n          c,\n          { opacity: DIM },\n          { opacity: 1, duration: settle, ease: 'power3.out' },\n          t,\n        );\n      });\n\n      // Release the frozen widths once nothing is substituting any more, so\n      // the word reflows normally if the viewport or the font changes later.\n      tl?.call(\n        () => {\n          for (const c of chars) c.style.width = '';\n        },\n        undefined,\n        '>',\n      );\n    };\n\n    // Splitting or measuring before the webfont lands measures the fallback,\n    // and every character box is then frozen to the wrong width.\n    void document.fonts.ready.then(build);\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n      tl?.kill();\n      restore();\n    };\n  }, [\n    text,\n    glyphs,\n    flips,\n    stagger,\n    baseFlip,\n    flipGrowth,\n    settle,\n    seed,\n    startOnView,\n    inView,\n    reduced,\n    run,\n  ]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** A `cancelled` flag guards the async `document.fonts.ready` boundary so a build cannot start after unmount. Teardown kills the timeline and then restores every character: `textContent` back to `data-final`, and `style.width` and `style.opacity` cleared. Without that restore, unmounting mid-scramble would leave the word frozen as garbage inside pinned boxes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The effect returns before building anything, so the word stays exactly as React rendered it — the resolved text, at full opacity, on first paint. Hover replay is also disabled. Nothing is scrambled, hidden, or left mid-substitution.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `Math.random()` for the glyph sequence. The server and the client pick different glyphs, React reports a hydration mismatch, and the first frame flickers as the tree is patched.\n- Not freezing the character widths. Every substitution changes the advance width, so the word pulses and everything after it on the line jitters for the whole animation.\n- Measuring those widths before `document.fonts.ready`. Each box is then pinned to a fallback-font width, and when the real font arrives the glyphs are clipped or swimming inside boxes that are visibly the wrong size — the same failure mode described in `font-safe-text-split`.\n- Measuring the width of a substituted glyph rather than the final one. The box is locked to the wrong character forever.\n- Never releasing the frozen widths. The word cannot reflow on a viewport change or a late font swap, so it breaks out of its container at narrow widths.\n- Constant-interval flipping. Without the growing hold the effect reads as a slot machine that was switched off rather than as a search that converged.\n- Leaving the scrambling characters in the accessibility tree. A screen reader announces the random glyphs as they change; the real word must be exposed separately and the animated spans hidden.\n- Re-running the effect from a `Math.random`-seeded value or an inline callback prop. The dependency array must contain only stable values or the scramble restarts on every parent render.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The original technique used GSAP MorphSVGPlugin to morph hand-drawn letter paths into random variants. MorphSVG is a paid GSAP Club plugin, so this is the dependency-free equivalent: character substitution from a glyph set on a per-character timeline, preserving the two things that actually make the original read the way it does — the 0.1s stagger and the `0.08 + n * 0.025` deceleration.\n- No text-splitting library either. The split is declarative JSX; `SplitText` is reserved for the components whose subject is SplitText itself.\n- Hover replay is gated on `(pointer: fine)` and on reduced motion being off — on touch there is no hover, so it would only fire on press and read as a glitch.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn entering the viewport each character flips through several wrong glyphs and lands on the right one, left to right. Time the flips: the last is visibly slower than the first. The word occupies exactly the same width throughout, and nothing on the same line moves. Reload repeatedly with a fixed `seed` and the identical glyph sequence appears every time, with no hydration warning in the console. Unmount mid-scramble and remount, and the word is correct. With reduced motion enabled the resolved word is present on first paint.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "most-active-days-card": "# Build: Most Active Days Card\n\nA grey dashboard card with the visible month's step total, a month pill with previous and next arrows, and a fixed-height white panel that scrolls through all twelve months of the year. Every day is a numbered cell with a 26px stack of three concentric progress rings; days after \"today\" show faded empty tracks and cannot be picked. Scrolling updates the pill and the total to the month in view, the arrows scroll smoothly to the neighbouring month, and picking a day (click or arrow keys) reports it so a paired Activity Rings Card can show that day.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA scrolling year calendar where each day is a mini activity ring, with a month pill that follows the scroll and selectable days.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One memoised pass builds the year: for each month, `getDaysInMonth` gives the day count, each `CalendarDate` gets its data from `getActivity` (default `sampleDayActivity`, seeded by `createRng(date.toString())` so every date is stable across renders and server/client), and each month sums `steps` for days on or before `today` into its total.\n2. Each month is a section with a sticky 44px heading (\"Jul\") over a 7-column grid of day buttons that simply runs 1–7, 8–14 and so on, like the reference, rather than aligning to weekdays. A cell is 42×74px: the number above `MiniActivityRings`, an SVG where each ring is a tinted track (ring colour mixed 22% into `--color-chart-track`) plus a `pathLength={100}` arc with `stroke-dasharray: pct 100`, rotated -90° to start at twelve o'clock.\n3. Scroll-spy: the scroller's `onScroll` schedules one `requestAnimationFrame`, which picks the last month section whose `offsetTop` is at or above `scrollTop + 35%` of the viewport (or December once the scroll is at the end). That month names the pill and its total; both are keyed spans, so `.animate-number-fade` replays as the label walks along.\n4. The arrows call `scrollTo({ top: section.offsetTop })` on the scroller (smooth, or instant under reduced motion) and let the spy update the label as the scroll passes. On mount, and again whenever `height` changes, an isomorphic layout effect sets `scrollTop` to the focused day's month (the selection, else `today`) and reveals that day before paint.\n5. Selection is controlled (`selected` + `onSelectedChange`) or uncontrolled (`defaultSelected`, else `today`). Day buttons use a roving tabindex: arrow keys move ±1 or ±7 days, Home and End go to the month's first and last day, Page Up and Page Down move a month, clamped to January 1 and `today`; the focused button is revealed inside the scroller (never the page) below the sticky heading. Enter or Space picks it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `RING_SIZE / RING_STROKE / RING_GAP = 26px / 2.75px / 1px`\n  Three rings fit in a day cell with a visible hole in the middle and still read as separate bands at a glance.\n- `HEADING_H = 44px`\n  Height of the sticky month heading; a revealed day is kept below it so keyboard focus never hides under \"Jul\".\n- `SPY_LINE = 0.35`\n  The pill switches once the next month's heading is a third of the way up the panel, so the label changes while that month is clearly arriving rather than at the very top.\n- `height = 232px`\n  Shows two full weeks of cells and the start of a third, which hints that the panel scrolls.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MostActiveDaysCard(props?: MostActiveDaysCardProps): JSX.Element\nfunction MiniActivityRings(props: { rings: ActivityRing[]; empty?: boolean; size?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction sampleDayActivity(date: CalendarDate): DayActivity\ntype DayActivity = { steps: number; rings: ActivityRing[] }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Most active days\"`\n  Header label.\n- `totalLabel: string` — default `\"total steps\"`\n  Unit after the month total.\n- `year: number` — default `today.year`\n  Year on show; all twelve months scroll in one column.\n- `today: CalendarDate` — default `parseDate(\"2026-07-10\")`\n  Later days are empty and disabled, and totals stop here.\n- `getActivity: (date: CalendarDate) => DayActivity` — default `sampleDayActivity`\n  Steps and ring values per day.\n- `selected: CalendarDate | null`\n  Controlled selected day.\n- `defaultSelected: CalendarDate | null` — default `today`\n  Uncontrolled initial selection.\n- `onSelectedChange: (date: CalendarDate) => void`\n  Called when a day is picked by click, Enter or Space.\n- `height: number` — default `232`\n  Height of the scrolling calendar in px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Scroll the calendar so `el` sits fully below the sticky month heading.\nconst reveal = (el: HTMLElement, behavior: ScrollBehavior) => {\n  const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n  if (!scroller) return;\n  const top = el.offsetTop - HEADING_H;\n  const bottom = el.offsetTop + el.offsetHeight + 8;\n  if (top < scroller.scrollTop) scroller.scrollTo({ top, behavior });\n  else if (bottom > scroller.scrollTop + scroller.clientHeight)\n    scroller.scrollTo({ top: bottom - scroller.clientHeight, behavior });\n};\n\n// Scroll-spy: the month whose top has passed SPY_LINE of the viewport names the pill.\nconst spy = () => {\n  const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n  if (!scroller) return;\n  const atEnd = scroller.scrollTop + scroller.clientHeight >= scroller.scrollHeight - 1;\n  const line = scroller.scrollTop + scroller.clientHeight * SPY_LINE;\n  let month = 1;\n  monthRefs.current.forEach((section, i) => {\n    if (section && section.offsetTop <= line) month = i + 1;\n  });\n  setVisibleMonth(atEnd ? 12 : month);\n};\n\nconst onScroll = () => {\n  cancelAnimationFrame(spyFrame.current);\n  spyFrame.current = requestAnimationFrame(spy);\n};\n\nconst goToMonth = (month: number) => {\n  const section = monthRefs.current[month - 1];\n  const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n  if (!section || !scroller) return;\n  scroller.scrollTo({ top: section.offsetTop, behavior: reduced ? \"auto\" : \"smooth\" });\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The pending scroll-spy animation frame is cancelled on every new scroll event and on unmount. Scrolling, clicks and keys use React event props, so there are no listeners to remove.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, the month arrows and keyboard reveal scroll instantly instead of smoothly, the pill label and total no longer fade in (`motion-reduce:animate-none`), and the cell hover and selection border changes are instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `scrollIntoView` to reveal the focused day. In a preview frame or a scrolled page it also scrolls every ancestor; setting `scrollTop` on the panel only moves the calendar.\n- Setting the pill label straight from the arrow click. The spy would then flip it back for a few frames while the smooth scroll is still short of the line; letting the spy own the label keeps it in step with what is on screen.\n- Passing a fresh `CalendarDate` for `today` each render rebuilds the whole year. The memo is keyed on `today.toString()` so equal dates reuse it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Exports `MostActiveDaysCard`, `MiniActivityRings`, `sampleDayActivity`, `formatLongDate` and the `DayActivity` type (`{ steps, rings: ActivityRing[] }`). The rings use the `ActivityRing` shape from the Activity Rings Card, so a day's rings can be passed straight to it.\n- The demo pairs the two cards: the selected day drives the Activity Rings Card's title (\"Activity for July 10, 2026\") and rings, which glide to the new values. On screens under 640px the demo stacks the cards and passes a 168px calendar `height` and a 120px ring `size` so both fit.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe default card reads \"Most active days\", \"39,919 total steps\" and a pill showing \"July\", with the panel opened on July: days 1–10 have three-colour mini rings, 11–31 are faded empty tracks and disabled, and the 10th has a grey outline. Clicking the next arrow scrolls smoothly to August and the pill then reads \"August\" with a total of 0; scrolling back up changes it to \"July\" again. Clicking July 3 outlines it and fires `onSelectedChange` (in the demo the Activity card title becomes \"Activity for July 3, 2026\" and its rings move); with July 3 focused, Arrow Right moves focus to July 4 and Arrow Down to July 10, but not past it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "multi-agent-chat": "# Build: Multi Agent Chat\n\nA full multi-agent chat for a 1280 × 800 frame. The left card lists conversations with stacked agent avatars, a marketplace button, support, settings and a workspace switcher. The middle pane is the open conversation: send one message and every agent in it answers in turn, each thinking for a moment before its reply streams in. The right panel edits any agent with the full agent creator, creates new ones, browses the workspace file tree with who last touched each file, and shows the progress of the current round of replies.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA group chat with a team of agents who reply in turn, plus a workspace switcher and a panel to edit agents and browse files.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. All state lives in `MultiAgentChat`: agents, conversations, workspaces, the open conversation, the single active run, the last run per conversation, the side panel tab and the open modal. The conversation view and side panel are controlled components in their own files.\n2. A run is `{ queue, index, phase, full, shown, paused }`. One effect keyed on the run arms one timeout: thinking waits on the agent's model (500, 900 or 1600ms, scaled by the composer model), streaming appends a chunk every 24ms scaled by the agent's speed, and a finished reply holds 380ms, is committed to the conversation and hands the turn to the next agent. Cleanup clears that timeout, so Stop, Pause, deleting the chat or unmounting never leaves a callback.\n3. The run lives in the page, not the conversation, so it keeps going while another conversation is open. That conversation's list row says who is replying and collects unread replies, and other composers stay disabled until the round ends.\n4. Each reply is written by `replyFor` from the agent's role label, the prompt and a counter, so the same agent answers differently on each turn. Plan mode in the composer makes every agent say it changed nothing.\n5. The participant row above the composer toggles agents in and out of the open conversation; the next round only queues the agents still in it. Clicking an agent avatar in the thread, or a file owner in the Files tab, opens that agent in the editor.\n6. Edits in the agent editor update the agent everywhere at once, because messages store only the agent id. The editor snapshots the agent on open and remounts after Save so its Reset returns to the saved version.\n7. The Activity tab drives the run through `AgentDetailPanel`: Pause holds the current timer off, Resume re-arms it, Stop keeps the partial reply, and Run again replays the last prompt with the same agents.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `THINK_MS = swift 500ms, balanced 900ms, deep 1600ms`\n  Deeper models visibly think longer, so a mixed team answers at different rhythms instead of in lockstep.\n- `MODEL_PACE = Swift 2 Mini 0.5, Swift 2 1, Deep 1 1.6`\n  The composer's model choice scales every agent's thinking so it has a felt effect.\n- `stream tick = 3 characters every 24ms times the agent speed`\n  About 125 characters a second at speed 1; a three-agent round finishes in under ten seconds.\n- `HAND_OFF_MS = 380ms`\n  A short beat on each finished reply makes the change of speaker readable.\n- `side panel width = 356px`\n  Fits the agent creator's pickers while leaving about 600px for the conversation next to the 272px list.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MultiAgentChat(props: MultiAgentChatProps): JSX.Element\ninterface MultiAgentChatProps { agents?: MacAgent[]; conversations?: MacConversation[]; workspaces?: Workspace[]; defaultWorkspaceId?: string; defaultConversationId?: string; className?: string }\n```\n\n- `agents: MacAgent[]` — default `MAC_AGENTS`\n  The team: each agent is an AgentDraft with an id.\n- `conversations: MacConversation[]` — default `MAC_CONVERSATIONS`\n  Seed conversations; each names its workspace, its agents and its messages.\n- `workspaces: Workspace[]` — default `MAC_WORKSPACES`\n  Workspaces offered by the switcher.\n- `defaultWorkspaceId: string` — default `\"board\"`\n  Workspace selected on first render.\n- `defaultConversationId: string` — default `\"group\"`\n  Conversation open on first render.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root, which is h-full w-full.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// One timeout at a time drives the run. Thinking waits on the agent's model,\n// streaming appends a chunk per tick, and a finished reply is committed to\n// the conversation before the next agent in the queue starts thinking.\n// Every change to the run re-arms the effect, so cleanup always clears the\n// only pending timer: Stop, Pause, deleting the chat or unmounting is safe.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!run || run.paused) return;\n  const agent = agents.find((a) => a.id === run.queue[run.index]);\n  const advance = () => {\n    const index = run.index + 1;\n    if (index < run.queue.length) {\n      record(run, index, \"running\");\n      setRun({ ...run, index, phase: \"thinking\", full: \"\", shown: 0 });\n    } else {\n      record(run, run.queue.length, \"done\");\n      setRun(null);\n    }\n  };\n  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;\n  if (!agent) {\n    timer = setTimeout(advance, 0);\n  } else if (run.phase === \"thinking\") {\n    timer = setTimeout(() => {\n      const full = replyFor(agent, run.prompt, run.id + run.index, run.plan);\n      setRun({ ...run, phase: \"streaming\", full, shown: reduced ? full.length : 0 });\n    }, (THINK_MS[agent.model] ?? 900) * run.pace);\n  } else if (run.shown < run.full.length) {\n    const step = Math.max(1, Math.round(CHUNK * Number(agent.speed || 1)));\n    timer = setTimeout(() => setRun({ ...run, shown: Math.min(run.full.length, run.shown + step) }), TICK_MS);\n  } else {\n    timer = setTimeout(() => {\n      commit(run, run.full);\n      advance();\n    }, HAND_OFF_MS);\n  }\n  return () => clearTimeout(timer);\n  // record and commit only read state through setters and refs.\n  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n}, [run, agents, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The single run timeout is cleared on every run change, on pause and on unmount. The share and support copied-state timers clear on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under reduced motion each reply appears whole once its agent has thought, avatars stop floating and blinking, the thinking label drops its shimmer, and hover transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none. Turn-taking and its timing stay so the order of speakers is still readable.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Running replies inside the conversation component. Switching conversations would unmount it and drop the round; the page owns the run and passes the live reply only to the conversation it belongs to.\n- Storing agent names or colours on messages. Renaming an agent would leave old messages stale; messages keep the agent id and look the agent up at render.\n- Passing the live agent as the creator's default. Its Reset would revert to whatever was typed last; snapshot on open and remount on Save.\n- Letting a deleted or cleared conversation keep its run. The timer would commit a reply into a conversation that no longer has it; both actions end the run first.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Replies are canned and local: nothing is sent anywhere. The share link, support address and workspace data are fictional.\n- Adding a bot from the marketplace also adds it to the open conversation and opens it in the editor.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280 × 800 a conversation list, a conversation and a side panel fill the frame. Typing a message and pressing Enter adds a bubble, then each agent in the conversation thinks and replies in turn. Toggling an agent in the participant row removes it from the next round. The Files tab shows the workspace tree and who last edited each file; the Activity tab pauses, stops and replays the round. Renaming an agent in the editor renames it in the thread. The workspace switcher swaps the conversation list, and the marketplace adds a bot to the team.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "multipass-offscreen-composite": "# Build: Multipass Offscreen Composite\n\nA hero field built the way a compositor builds one: a background ramp and three additive gradient fills are accumulated into an offscreen target, a bright-pass and blur run into a second target at half size, and a final pass adds them together with a vignette and a dither. The canvas is written exactly once, by the last pass. It is a small piece of art in its own right and a complete, disposable template for any offscreen chain.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nFour passes into two render targets — ramp, additive fills, half-size bloom — and one composite that writes the canvas once.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Passes 1 and 2 share one target. `renderer.autoClear = false` is set at construction, the scene target is cleared explicitly once with `renderer.clear(true, false, false)`, and the ramp and the fills are then rendered back to back. The absence of a clear between those two renders is the entire composite.\n2. The ramp is a clip-space quad. Each fragment projects onto the ramp axis with `dot(uv - 0.5, dir) / (abs(dir.x) + abs(dir.y)) + 0.5`; the divisor is the largest that projection can reach inside the unit square, so the ramp reaches its end colour exactly at the far corner for any angle rather than saturating early.\n3. The fills are three `PlaneGeometry(1, 1)` meshes in one rotated group, each drawn with a Gaussian falloff `exp(-d * d * uSoftness)` and `AdditiveBlending`. They travel around ellipses of different radii at speeds of 0.05, -0.08 and 0.11 revolutions per second, so the arrangement takes about 200 seconds to return to its start.\n4. Both targets are `HalfFloatType`. Three additive fills overlap well past 1.0, and on an unsigned-byte target that overlap clips flat before the bright-pass runs — the bloom would then be told the image is uniformly hot wherever any two fills cross.\n5. Pass 3 renders into a target at `bloomScale` (0.5) of the scene target. The bright-pass is a soft knee, `smoothstep(uThreshold, uThreshold + 0.25, luma)`, and the blur is a 3x3 tent with weights 4 / 2 / 1 normalised by 16. Its offsets are measured in the half-size target's texels, so the blur reaches twice as far across the image for the same nine taps.\n6. Pass 4 reads both targets, adds `bloom * uBloomStrength`, multiplies by a vignette measured in a square space so it stays circular in a wide box, and adds triangular-PDF dither — the difference of two hashes, about 1/255 — to break the 8-bit banding a smooth ramp otherwise shows as hard contour lines.\n7. Resizing calls `setSize` on both targets and rewrites `uResolution` and the bloom `uTexel`. Nothing is reallocated, and because no pass reads its own previous contents there is no clear-and-settle: the next frame redraws all four passes from scratch.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `renderer.autoClear = false`\n  The one line that makes multipass possible in three. Left at its default, every `render()` clears its target first, so pass 2 wipes pass 1 and the layering silently collapses into whatever was drawn last — with no error and no warning.\n- `bloomScale = 0.5`\n  Quarter the pixels for the blur pass, and the tent's texel-sized offsets cover twice the distance. Below about 0.35 the half-resolution grid becomes visible as a soft blockiness in the upsample; at 1.0 the pass costs four times as much for a tighter, less useful glow.\n- `BLOOM_RADIUS = 1.4`\n  Tap spacing in bloom-target texels, so 2.8 scene pixels. Above roughly 2 the nine taps no longer overlap and the tent starts sampling a sparse grid, which shows up as a faint cross-shaped ringing around bright cores.\n- `bloomThreshold = 0.34`\n  Luma where the bright-pass starts. The ramp peaks near 0.18, so this sits above the background and below the fills: the glow comes from the lights, not from the whole frame. Drop it under the ramp and the bloom becomes a flat wash that just raises exposure.\n- `knee width = 0.25`\n  The smoothstep span above the threshold. With a hard step, a pixel pops into the bright set the instant it crosses the cut, so the bloom boundary crawls as the fills drift — the most common giveaway of a hand-rolled bloom.\n- `DISC_SOFTNESS = 3.1`\n  The k in `exp(-d*d*k)`, which puts the fill at 4.5% of peak at the edge of its own quad. Much lower and the Gaussian is still bright where the geometry stops, giving a visible square edge; much higher and the fill shrinks to a hard dot inside an oversized quad.\n- `DITHER = 0.004`\n  One 8-bit code. The ramp crosses only a few hundred codes over a thousand pixels, and without a sub-LSB perturbation that gradient bands into visible contour lines on any display. Larger values are perceptible as noise rather than as smoothness.\n- `REVEAL_START / REVEAL_SPAN = 0.7 / 0.2`\n  The source ties the fills to scroll with exactly this ramp: nothing until 70% of the progress, full by 90%, held after. The dead zone is deliberate — the ramp establishes the space first, and the light arrives as a separate event.\n- `DISC_SQUASH / PLANE_TILT = 0.62 / -0.19 rad`\n  The fills are round in their own plane; squashing them and rotating their shared group is what makes three moving blobs read as one ellipse seen at an angle instead of three unrelated circles.\n- `STATIC_ELAPSED = 6.2 s`\n  The frame drawn when motion is suppressed. At t = 0 all three fills sit at their starting phases; six seconds in, the composition is spread out and reads as a designed still rather than as a default state.\n- `maxDpr = 2`\n  Four full-screen passes are already 2.5 screens of fragments per frame (1 + 1 + 0.25 + 1). At a 3x backing store that is over twenty million fragment invocations per frame on a phone, for no visible gain over 2x.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction MultipassOffscreenComposite(props: { reveal?: number; bloomStrength?: number; bloomThreshold?: number; bloomScale?: number; vignette?: number; drift?: number; rampAngle?: number; rampColors?: readonly [string, string, string]; fillColors?: readonly [string, string, string]; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `reveal: number` — default `1`\n  Progress 0..1. The fills fade in between 0.7 and 0.9 and hold; the ramp is always drawn.\n- `bloomStrength: number` — default `0.85`\n  How much of the blurred bright-pass is added back in the composite.\n- `bloomThreshold: number` — default `0.34`\n  Luminance where the bright-pass begins to contribute, with a 0.25 knee above it.\n- `bloomScale: number` — default `0.5`\n  Bloom target size relative to the scene target. Changing it rebuilds both targets.\n- `vignette: number` — default `0.38`\n  Corner darkening in the composite, 0..1.\n- `drift: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier on the fills' orbital speeds. 0 freezes them without stopping the passes.\n- `rampAngle: number` — default `141`\n  Ramp direction in degrees, counter-clockwise from +x.\n- `rampColors: readonly [string, string, string]` — default `['#0a0f1a', '#16283c', '#070a12']`\n  Start, bend and end stops of the background ramp.\n- `fillColors: readonly [string, string, string]` — default `['#3f7ae0', '#2fd0b4', '#a866f0']`\n  Colours of the three additive fills, largest first.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Backing-store cap. Both targets follow the drawing buffer.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function MultipassOffscreenComposite({\n  reveal = 1,\n  bloomStrength = 0.85,\n  bloomThreshold = 0.34,\n  bloomScale = 0.5,\n  vignette = 0.38,\n  drift = 1,\n  rampAngle = 141,\n  rampColors = RAMP_COLORS,\n  fillColors = FILL_COLORS,\n  maxDpr = 2,\n  className,\n}: MultipassOffscreenCompositeProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  /* Everything that only feeds a uniform lives here and is read inside the\n     draw loop. None of it belongs in the effect's dependency list: rebuilding\n     two render targets because a vignette changed by 0.01 would drop frames. */\n  const live = useRef({ reveal, bloomStrength, bloomThreshold, vignette, drift });\n  live.current = { reveal, bloomStrength, bloomThreshold, vignette, drift };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  /* Joined, not spread. Array props are compared by identity, so an inline\n     literal would be a new value every render and the effect would thrash. */\n  const colorKey = `${rampColors.join('|')}/${fillColors.join('|')}`;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n        antialias: false,\n        // Opaque. Pass 4 covers every pixel with alpha 1, so an alpha channel\n        // would only cost bandwidth and force the compositor to blend.\n        alpha: false,\n        depth: false,\n        stencil: false,\n      });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 1);\n    // The single most important line in the file. See the header.\n    renderer.autoClear = false;\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const buffer = new THREE.Vector2();\n    renderer.getDrawingBufferSize(buffer);\n    const bufferW = Math.max(2, Math.round(buffer.x));\n    const bufferH = Math.max(2, Math.round(buffer.y));\n    const bloomW = Math.max(2, Math.round(bufferW * bloomScale));\n    const bloomH = Math.max(2, Math.round(bufferH * bloomScale));\n\n    const sceneTarget = makeTarget(bufferW, bufferH);\n    const bloomTarget = makeTarget(bloomW, bloomH);\n\n    // Two cameras. The flat one is for the clip-space quads, whose vertex\n    // shader ignores both matrices; the ortho one gives the fills a stable\n    // -aspect..aspect x -1..1 space to move in.\n    const flatCamera = new THREE.Camera();\n    const contentCamera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 10);\n    contentCamera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const flatGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n    const discGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, 1);\n\n    const [rampA = '#000000', rampB = '#000000', rampC = '#000000'] = rampColors;\n    const rampUniforms: RampUniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(bufferW, bufferH) },\n      uAngle: { value: (rampAngle * Math.PI) / 180 },\n      uColorA: { value: new THREE.Color(rampA) },\n      uColorB: { value: new THREE.Color(rampB) },\n      uColorC: { value: new THREE.Color(rampC) },\n    };\n    const rampMaterial = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: MULTIPASS_FLAT_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: MULTIPASS_RAMP_FRAG,\n      uniforms: rampUniforms,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n    const rampScene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const rampQuad = new THREE.Mesh(flatGeometry, rampMaterial);\n    rampQuad.frustumCulled = false;\n    rampScene.add(rampQuad);\n\n    const fillScene = new THREE.Scene();\n    // One group, rotated once: the fills share a plane, and tilting the group\n    // is what makes their paths read as one ellipse seen at an angle rather\n    // than three unrelated circles.\n    const fillGroup = new THREE.Group();\n    fillGroup.rotation.z = PLANE_TILT;\n    fillScene.add(fillGroup);\n\n    const discMaterials: THREE.ShaderMaterial[] = [];\n    const discMeshes: THREE.Mesh[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < DISCS.length; i++) {\n      const spec = DISCS[i];\n      if (!spec) continue;\n      const uniforms: DiscUniforms = {\n        uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(fillColors[i] ?? '#ffffff') },\n        uIntensity: { value: 0 },\n        uSoftness: { value: DISC_SOFTNESS },\n      };\n      const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n        vertexShader: MULTIPASS_DISC_VERT,\n        fragmentShader: MULTIPASS_DISC_FRAG,\n        uniforms,\n        transparent: true,\n        blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,\n        depthTest: false,\n        depthWrite: false,\n      });\n      const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(discGeometry, material);\n      mesh.scale.set(spec.size, spec.size * DISC_SQUASH, 1);\n      mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n      fillGroup.add(mesh);\n      discMaterials.push(material);\n      discMeshes.push(mesh);\n    }\n\n    const bloomUniforms: BloomUniforms = {\n      uSource: { value: sceneTarget.texture },\n      uTexel: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1 / bloomW, 1 / bloomH) },\n      uThreshold: { value: bloomThreshold },\n      uRadius: { value: BLOOM_RADIUS },\n    };\n    const bloomMaterial = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: MULTIPASS_FLAT_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: MULTIPASS_BLOOM_FRAG,\n      uniforms: bloomUniforms,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n    const bloomScene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const bloomQuad = new THREE.Mesh(flatGeometry, bloomMaterial);\n    bloomQuad.frustumCulled = false;\n    bloomScene.add(bloomQuad);\n\n    const compositeUniforms: CompositeUniforms = {\n      uScene: { value: sceneTarget.texture },\n      uBloom: { value: bloomTarget.texture },\n      uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(bufferW, bufferH) },\n      uBloomStrength: { value: bloomStrength },\n      uVignette: { value: vignette },\n      uDither: { value: DITHER },\n    };\n    const compositeMaterial = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: MULTIPASS_FLAT_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: MULTIPASS_COMPOSITE_FRAG,\n      uniforms: compositeUniforms,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n    const compositeScene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const compositeQuad = new THREE.Mesh(flatGeometry, compositeMaterial);\n    compositeQuad.frustumCulled = false;\n    compositeScene.add(compositeQuad);\n\n    stage.current = {\n      resize(w, h) {\n        renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n        renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n        renderer.getDrawingBufferSize(buffer);\n        const bw = Math.max(2, Math.round(buffer.x));\n        const bh = Math.max(2, Math.round(buffer.y));\n\n        const aspect = w / h;\n        contentCamera.left = -aspect;\n        contentCamera.right = aspect;\n        contentCamera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n\n        // setSize, never new. Allocating a pair of half-float targets is a\n        // synchronous driver call; doing it from a ResizeObserver during a\n        // window drag is how a smooth resize turns into a slideshow — and the\n        // old targets would leak unless every one of them were disposed.\n        sceneTarget.setSize(bw, bh);\n        rampUniforms.uResolution.value.set(bw, bh);\n        compositeUniforms.uResolution.value.set(bw, bh);\n\n        const sw = Math.max(2, Math.round(bw * bloomScale));\n        const sh = Math.max(2, Math.round(bh * bloomScale));\n        bloomTarget.setSize(sw, sh);\n        // Texel size is per target, and this one is not the canvas. Leaving it\n        // at the old size makes the blur radius drift with the window.\n        bloomUniforms.uTexel.value.set(1 / sw, 1 / sh);\n      },\n\n      draw(elapsed) {\n        const state = live.current;\n\n        // The source ties the fills to scroll with exactly this ramp.\n        const fade = clamp((state.reveal - REVEAL_START) / REVEAL_SPAN, 0, 1);\n\n        for (let i = 0; i < discMeshes.length; i++) {\n          const mesh = discMeshes[i];\n          const spec = DISCS[i];\n          const material = discMaterials[i];\n          if (!mesh || !spec || !material) continue;\n          const angle = spec.phase + elapsed * spec.speed * state.drift * Math.PI * 2;\n          mesh.position.set(Math.cos(angle) * spec.radius, Math.sin(angle) * spec.radius, 0);\n          const intensity = material.uniforms.uIntensity;\n          if (intensity) intensity.value = spec.intensity * fade;\n        }\n\n        // Passes 1 and 2 — one clear, two renders. The absence of a clear\n        // between them IS the composite.\n        renderer.setRenderTarget(sceneTarget);\n        renderer.clear(true, false, false);\n        renderer.render(rampScene, flatCamera);\n        renderer.render(fillScene, contentCamera);\n\n        // Pass 3 — bright-pass and blur at bloomScale. Nothing accumulates, so\n        // this target never needs to survive a frame.\n        bloomUniforms.uThreshold.value = state.bloomThreshold;\n        renderer.setRenderTarget(bloomTarget);\n        renderer.clear(true, false, false);\n        renderer.render(bloomScene, flatCamera);\n\n        // Pass 4 — the only write to the canvas.\n        compositeUniforms.uBloomStrength.value = state.bloomStrength;\n        compositeUniforms.uVignette.value = state.vignette;\n        renderer.setRenderTarget(null);\n        renderer.clear(true, false, false);\n        renderer.render(compositeScene, flatCamera);\n      },\n    };\n\n    stage.current.resize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight);\n    stage.current.draw(0);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      // Explicit, not disposeObject over the scenes: flatGeometry is shared by\n      // three of them, and a traversal would dispose it three times.\n      flatGeometry.dispose();\n      discGeometry.dispose();\n      rampMaterial.dispose();\n      bloomMaterial.dispose();\n      compositeMaterial.dispose();\n      for (const material of discMaterials) material.dispose();\n      // Both targets. Nothing walks a render target for you — each holds a\n      // half-float texture and a framebuffer object.\n      sceneTarget.dispose();\n      bloomTarget.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, colorKey, rampAngle, bloomScale, maxDpr]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    current.resize(width, height);\n    // No pass reads its own previous contents, so a resized target needs no\n    // clear-and-settle — but a still frame does need redrawing, since resizing\n    // a target discards what it held.\n    if (reduced) current.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => {\n      stage.current?.draw(elapsed);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref, disposes both `PlaneGeometry` instances, all six materials, and — separately, because nothing traverses them — both render targets. Then `disposeRenderer` performs `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed drawing buffer, and the canvas is removed from the DOM. The canvas is created inside the effect by three and appended per effect run, so a StrictMode remount builds a fresh context rather than reattaching the one that was just force-lost. `useRaf` releases its own subscription; the component adds no listeners of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component reads `useReducedMotion` itself and never subscribes to rAF when it is set. All four passes still run, once, at a fixed elapsed of 6.2 seconds, and again after any resize — so the artwork is complete, correctly exposed and fully bloomed, it simply does not drift. A hero background is composition first and motion second; deleting it under reduced motion would leave a hole in the page, and freezing it at t = 0 would show an arrangement nobody designed.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving `autoClear` at its default and wondering why only the last pass appears. Nothing errors; the ramp is simply gone, and the usual next move — reordering the renders — makes it worse.\n- Setting `autoClear = false` and then forgetting to clear at all. The first target accumulates frame over frame, so the fills smear into permanent trails that look almost intentional for about two seconds.\n- Recreating render targets in the resize handler. Allocating a pair of half-float framebuffers is a synchronous driver call; doing it on every ResizeObserver callback turns a window drag into a slideshow, and every previous pair leaks unless it is explicitly disposed.\n- Disposing the renderer but not the targets. Render targets are not part of any scene graph, so nothing traverses them — each surviving one holds a full-resolution half-float texture plus a framebuffer object until the tab is closed.\n- Using an 8-bit target for anything that feeds a bright-pass. Additive overlap clips at 1.0, so the bloom sees a plateau instead of a peak and the glow spreads uniformly across the crossing region.\n- Premultiplying alpha in a shader that uses three's `AdditiveBlending`. That blend is already `(SrcAlpha, One)`, so multiplying in the shader squares the alpha and the fills all but vanish — then get \"fixed\" by tripling the intensity, which clips the cores.\n- Reusing one `uTexel` or `uResolution` uniform across passes that render into differently sized targets. Everything looks right at one window size and the blur radius drifts as the window changes.\n- Reading a colour buffer while it is still bound as the render target. It is undefined behaviour in WebGL and shows as tearing, black, or a correct image depending on the driver — which is why the bright-pass needs an offscreen target and cannot run on the canvas.\n- Letting three convert colour space in the middle of the chain. Its output conversion is injected into built-in materials only, so a chain that mixes `ShaderMaterial` and `MeshBasicMaterial` converts at some passes and not others; this component uses `ShaderMaterial` everywhere and converts nowhere.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The renderer is created with `alpha: false`. Pass 4 covers every pixel at full alpha, so an alpha channel would only cost bandwidth and force the compositor to blend the canvas over the page.\n- `reveal` is deliberately a plain number rather than an internal scroll listener. Drive it from whatever already tracks progress — `useInView`'s ratio, a scroll library, or a constant — and the component stays a renderer.\n- The array props are compared through a joined string key, so passing an inline `[a, b, c]` literal does not tear down the GL stack on every render. Values that only feed a uniform (`reveal`, `bloomStrength`, `vignette`, `drift`) are read from a ref inside the draw loop and never appear in a dependency list.\n- Geometries are disposed by hand rather than through `disposeObject` over each scene: the clip-space quad is shared by three scenes, and a traversal would dispose it three times.\n- To extend the chain, add a target and a pass — the structure does not change. The costly mistakes are all in the two rules this file already follows: clear deliberately, and resize rather than reallocate.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThree soft coloured lights drift across a dark angled ramp with a halo that is clearly wider and softer than the lights themselves, corners falling off, and no banding anywhere in the gradient — tilt a laptop screen and the ramp stays smooth. Resize the window continuously: the image tracks the box with no stutter, no flash of black, and the halo keeps the same apparent width. Set `reveal` to 0.5 and only the ramp is visible; sweep it to 1 and the lights come up over the last third. With reduced motion the full composition, bloom included, is present and still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "nav-dot-wave": "# Build: Nav Dot Wave\n\nOne keyframe block and N elements. Each nav item writes its own index into an inline `--i`, and the stylesheet turns that index into an animation delay, so a crest travels along the row. Adding a nav item requires no change to the CSS and no JavaScript at all.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nNav dots that lift in sequence. The index is an inline custom property; CSS derives the delay.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every list item carries `style={{ \"--i\": i }}`. The dot's rule reads `animation-delay: calc(var(--i, 0) * var(--kx-dot-stagger, 60ms))`, so the phase offset of each dot is derived from its position rather than authored per item. The index is data; the timing is a function of it.\n2. The keyframe is deliberately mostly flat: `0%, 60%, 100%` is the resting pose and only `30%` lifts. The dot therefore rests for 40% of every 1400 ms cycle. That long rest is what makes a staggered set read as a single crest travelling along the row instead of a row of dots vibrating in place.\n3. 0% is the resting pose, not the lifted one, and that ordering is load-bearing. A positive `animation-delay` holds an element at its 0% pose until the delay expires, so if 0% were the lift, every dot would sit visibly raised for its share of the stagger on first paint and the row would *settle* into place rather than start from it.\n4. The active item swaps to a second keyframe (`kx-dotwave-active`) with identical geometry but a constant `opacity: 1`. It participates in the wave so the row stays coherent, but it never dims, because a location marker that fades to 45% twice a second is a marker you cannot trust.\n5. The lift is written with the `translate` property, not `transform`, so the active state's `scale: 1.6` composes independently and can carry its own spring transition without either value clobbering the other.\n6. `reverse` negates the stagger to `-60ms`. Negative delays start an animation already partway through its cycle, so the phase gradient inverts and the crest runs right-to-left with no second keyframe.\n7. The CSS ships in a `<style href=\"kinetic-nav-dot-wave\" precedence=\"medium\">` element. React 19 deduplicates and hoists it to the head by `href`, so the rules exist exactly once no matter how many navs are mounted, and nothing has to be added to a global stylesheet.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `durationMs = 1400`\n  One dot's full cycle. Below ~900 ms the lift reads as a nervous twitch; above ~2000 ms the crest is so slow that a user glancing at the nav sees a static row and the effect is paid for but not perceived.\n- `staggerMs = 60`\n  Phase gap between neighbours. The crest travels at one item per 60 ms. Under ~30 ms the dots look simultaneous; over ~120 ms the wave breaks into visibly separate blips because the leading dot has finished its lift before the next begins.\n- `keyframe rest span = 0%, 60%, 100%`\n  The dot is flat for 40% of the cycle. Shrink the rest and adjacent dots overlap enough that the whole row appears raised at once, which is a vibration, not a wave.\n- `peak position = 30%`\n  The lift peaks in the first third and eases back over the next third. Symmetric timing (peak at 50%) makes each dot look like it is hovering rather than being flicked, and the crest loses its direction.\n- `lift = 3`\n  Pixels. A nav dot is 5px; 3px is a lift of over half its diameter, which is legible at a glance. Past ~6px the dots start colliding with the label baseline and the row looks broken rather than animated.\n- `resting opacity = 0.45`\n  Low enough that the peak at 1.0 registers as a change, high enough that an inactive nav item is still readable. Below ~0.3 the dots vanish against a dark surface and the row appears to lose items.\n- `active scale = 1.6`\n  The current-page dot has to win against five siblings that are all moving. Under ~1.3 it is lost in the wave; over ~2 it stops reading as the same kind of object as its neighbours.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NavDotWave(props: { items: readonly { label: string; href?: string }[]; activeIndex?: number; staggerMs?: number; durationMs?: number; lift?: number; size?: number; reverse?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: readonly NavDotWaveItem[]`\n  Nav entries. Each is `{ label, href? }`; the label is also the React key.\n- `activeIndex: number` — default `0`\n  Index of the current page. Its dot grows, takes the accent colour and stops dimming.\n- `staggerMs: number` — default `60`\n  Milliseconds between one dot's phase and the next.\n- `durationMs: number` — default `1400`\n  Full cycle of a single dot, in milliseconds.\n- `lift: number` — default `3`\n  Peak lift in pixels.\n- `size: number` — default `5`\n  Dot diameter in pixels.\n- `reverse: boolean` — default `false`\n  Negates the stagger so the crest runs right-to-left.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the `nav` element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst DOT_WAVE_CSS = `\n/* 0% is the RESTING pose, not the lifted one. That matters: a positive\n   animation-delay holds the element at its 0% pose until the delay expires,\n   so if 0% were the lift, every dot would sit visibly raised for its share of\n   the stagger on first paint and the row would settle rather than start. */\n@keyframes kx-dotwave {\n  0%, 60%, 100% { opacity: 0.45; translate: 0 0; }\n  30%           { opacity: 1;    translate: 0 var(--kx-dot-lift, -3px); }\n}\n\n/* The current item never dims — it is a location marker, and a marker that\n   fades to 45% twice a second is a marker you cannot trust. */\n@keyframes kx-dotwave-active {\n  0%, 60%, 100% { opacity: 1; translate: 0 0; }\n  30%           { opacity: 1; translate: 0 var(--kx-dot-lift, -3px); }\n}\n\n.kx-dotwave-list {\n  display: flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: var(--kx-dot-gap, 1.5rem);\n  list-style: none;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\n.kx-dotwave-link {\n  display: inline-flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 0.5rem;\n  color: var(--color-muted, currentColor);\n  transition: color 200ms linear;\n}\n\n.kx-dotwave-link:hover,\n.kx-dotwave-item[data-active=\"true\"] .kx-dotwave-link {\n  color: var(--color-fg, currentColor);\n}\n\n.kx-dotwave-dot {\n  width: var(--kx-dot-size, 5px);\n  height: var(--kx-dot-size, 5px);\n  border-radius: 9999px;\n  background-color: currentColor;\n  flex: none;\n  scale: 1;\n\n  animation: kx-dotwave var(--kx-dot-duration, 1400ms) ease-in-out infinite;\n  animation-delay: calc(var(--i, 0) * var(--kx-dot-stagger, 60ms));\n\n  /* 'translate' and 'opacity', not 'transform' — the scale below belongs to\n     the active state and has to survive independently of the wave. */\n  will-change: translate, opacity;\n  transition: scale 260ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);\n}\n\n.kx-dotwave-item[data-active=\"true\"] .kx-dotwave-dot {\n  animation-name: kx-dotwave-active;\n  background-color: var(--color-accent, currentColor);\n  scale: 1.6;\n}\n\n/* No motion: the crest is decoration, so it goes. Everything that carries\n   information stays — the current item keeps its accent colour and its larger\n   dot, the rest keep their dimmed one. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-dotwave-dot {\n    animation: none;\n    opacity: 0.45;\n    will-change: auto;\n  }\n  .kx-dotwave-item[data-active=\"true\"] .kx-dotwave-dot {\n    opacity: 1;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport function NavDotWave({\n  items,\n  activeIndex = 0,\n  staggerMs = 60,\n  durationMs = 1400,\n  lift = 3,\n  size = 5,\n  reverse = false,\n  className,\n}: NavDotWaveProps) {\n  const rootStyle: StyleWithVars = {\n    '--kx-dot-stagger': `${reverse ? -staggerMs : staggerMs}ms`,\n    '--kx-dot-duration': `${durationMs}ms`,\n    '--kx-dot-lift': `${-lift}px`,\n    '--kx-dot-size': `${size}px`,\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-nav-dot-wave\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {DOT_WAVE_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <nav className={cn('kx-dotwave', className)} style={rootStyle}>\n        <ul className=\"kx-dotwave-list\">\n          {items.map((item, i) => {\n            // The index is the only per-item data the stylesheet needs. Every\n            // timing decision is derived from it in CSS.\n            const itemStyle: StyleWithVars = { '--i': i };\n            const active = i === activeIndex;\n            return (\n              <li\n                key={item.label}\n                className=\"kx-dotwave-item\"\n                data-active={active ? 'true' : 'false'}\n                style={itemStyle}\n              >\n                <a\n                  href={item.href ?? '#'}\n                  className=\"kx-dotwave-link text-xs tracking-tight\"\n                  aria-current={active ? 'page' : undefined}\n                >\n                  <span className=\"kx-dotwave-dot\" aria-hidden=\"true\" />\n                  {item.label}\n                </a>\n              </li>\n            );\n          })}\n        </ul>\n      </nav>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing imperative to tear down. The stylesheet is a React-managed `<style>` with an `href` key, so React owns its lifetime and deduplicates it across instances; unmounting the nav removes the elements and the animations with them.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block in the component's own stylesheet sets `animation: none` and `will-change: auto` on the dots, drops them to the resting opacity, and holds the active dot at full opacity. The accent colour and the 1.6x active scale stay, so the nav still shows where you are — only the travelling crest is removed.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Putting the lift at 0% of the keyframe. `animation-delay` freezes the element at its 0% pose, so on first paint the whole row sits raised and drops in sequence — the animation appears to run once backwards before starting.\n- Animating `transform` for both the lift and the active scale. The last-declared transform wins, so the active dot either loses its lift or snaps back to scale 1 every cycle. Separate `translate` and `scale` properties compose instead.\n- Driving the stagger from JavaScript with a timer or a per-item inline delay string. It costs a re-render per item, drifts out of phase with the CSS clock, and stops working the moment items are added dynamically.\n- Letting the active dot dim with the rest. It is the only element on the page telling the user where they are; a marker at 45% opacity for most of every cycle is worse than no marker.\n- Leaving `will-change: translate, opacity` on under reduced motion. The animation is gone but the compositor layer is not, so a nav that no longer moves still holds GPU memory on every dot.\n- Removing the animation under reduced motion and removing the active state with it. The accent colour and the larger dot carry information, not decoration — only the crest should go.\n- Keying the list by index while items reorder. The `--i` and the label desynchronise for one frame and a dot appears to jump across the row.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Nothing here is JavaScript-driven: no rAF, no observers, no state. The component's entire runtime cost is one style element and one inline custom property per item, which is why it is safe in a header that mounts on every route.\n- The `--i` pattern generalises. Any staggered CSS animation — list entrances, grid reveals, letter-by-letter text — can take its delay from an index custom property instead of from generated per-item rules or a JavaScript timeline.\n- Set `activeIndex` from your router. `aria-current=\"page\"` is applied to the matching link, so the state is announced as well as drawn.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA crest travels left-to-right along the dots once every 1.4 s and each dot is flat for most of that time. On first paint no dot is raised. The current item's dot is accent-coloured, 1.6x larger, and never dims while its neighbours do. Set `reverse` and the crest runs the other way. With reduced motion nothing moves, but the active dot is still larger and still accented.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "ndc-mouse-bulge": "# Build: In-Shader Mouse Bulge\n\nThe pointer is a screen-space quantity and the mesh is a world-space one, so something has to convert between them. Instead of unprojecting the pointer on the CPU with a raycaster, every vertex projects ITSELF once, compares its own normalised device position against the pointer, and displaces by the result. Each vertex gets an exact distance, the cost is one extra matrix multiply per vertex, and nothing about it depends on the CPU knowing the mesh's real shape.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA soft lens that follows the cursor, measured by projecting every vertex to screen space instead of raycasting.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The vertex shader runs `projectionMatrix * modelViewMatrix * vec4(pos, 1.0)` twice. The first is a probe: dividing its xy by w gives this vertex's own NDC position, which is the only common space the pointer and the geometry both live in.\n2. NDC spans -1..1 on both axes whatever the viewport is, so a raw distance is stretched by the aspect ratio. The x component of the delta is multiplied by `uAspect` before the length is taken, which is what keeps the lens circular on a 16:9 canvas instead of an ellipse 1.78 times too wide.\n3. The weight is `exp(-dot(delta, delta) * falloff)`. A Gaussian never actually reaches zero, so there is no radius at which the deformation visibly stops, and its derivative is continuous, so the sheen has no hard edge either — a smoothstep would give both a visible boundary ring.\n4. The vertex is lifted by `bulge * uLift` and the weight is passed to the fragment shader, which pushes the centred UV outward by `centered * vBulge * uMagnify`. Lift alone is a bump; lift plus UV magnification is a lens, and that is the difference between a dent in the surface and glass sitting on top of it.\n5. The second projection is the one that ships the vertex, evaluated after the displacement the first one made possible. The extra matrix multiply per vertex is the entire cost of the technique.\n6. The strength uniform is damped toward the pointer's active state rather than switched, so the lens fades in on enter and out on leave instead of appearing at, and vanishing from, the last known position.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `falloff = 5`\n  Tightness of the Gaussian in aspect-corrected NDC units, where the full viewport is 2 wide. At 5 the lens is roughly a third of the height across. Below ~2 it swells to cover the whole plane and reads as a global warp; above ~15 it collapses to a pinpoint that the vertex grid cannot resolve smoothly.\n- `lift = 0.5`\n  World units the crown rises, against a plane about 2.5 units tall at a camera distance of 3. Above ~1.2 the crown passes the camera's near plane and the lens punches a hole through itself when the cursor is centred.\n- `magnify = 0.12`\n  UV displacement at the crown. This is what makes it a lens rather than a bump. Above ~0.3 the magnification outruns the geometry, the texture visibly slides across the surface, and the illusion of glass breaks into a smear.\n- `sheen = 0.18`\n  Extra brightness at the crown. Small on purpose: the eye reads a slight highlight as curvature, and anything past ~0.4 reads as a blown-out light source instead.\n- `segments = 128`\n  The bulge is per-vertex, so the mesh density is the resolution of the lens. At 16 the Gaussian resolves into a faceted pyramid chasing the cursor; past ~192 the extra vertices land inside a single pixel of the highlight.\n- `STRENGTH_SMOOTHING = 0.0008`\n  Fraction of the gap left after one second, so the lens fades in over roughly a fifth of a second. Instant switching leaves the lens visible at the last known position for one frame after the cursor leaves, which flickers.\n- `pointer smoothing = 0.0008`\n  Matches the strength smoothing so the lens grows and moves at the same rate. A pointer smoothed much harder than the strength arrives late and the lens appears to be dragged; much softer and it inherits every jitter in the raw pointer stream.\n- `CAMERA_FOV / CAMERA_Z = 45 / 3`\n  Sets how much perspective the lift reads against. At a narrow FOV the raised crown barely changes size and the bulge looks flat; at a wide one the plane's own edges keystone enough to compete with the effect.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NdcMouseBulge(props: { strength?: number; falloff?: number; lift?: number; magnify?: number; sheen?: number; segments?: number; paint?: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, size: { width: number; height: number }) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `strength: number` — default `1`\n  Peak displacement weight. 0 disables the effect.\n- `falloff: number` — default `5`\n  Gaussian tightness. Higher is a smaller, sharper lens.\n- `lift: number` — default `0.5`\n  World units the crown of the bulge rises toward the camera.\n- `magnify: number` — default `0.12`\n  How far the lens magnifies at the crown, in UV units.\n- `sheen: number` — default `0.18`\n  Extra brightness at the crown.\n- `segments: number` — default `128`\n  Plane subdivisions per axis. The resolution of the lens.\n- `paint: (ctx, size) => void`\n  Override the surface. Receives a 2D context for a 1024x1024 canvas.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function NdcMouseBulge({\n  strength = 1,\n  falloff = 5,\n  lift = 0.5,\n  magnify = 0.12,\n  sheen = 0.18,\n  segments = 128,\n  paint,\n  className,\n}: NdcMouseBulgeProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Already in NDC, which is exactly the space the shader compares in — no\n  // conversion, no cached bounding rect to go stale on scroll.\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.0008 });\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const engaged = useRef(0);\n\n  const live = useRef({ strength, falloff, lift, magnify, sheen });\n  live.current = { strength, falloff, lift, magnify, sheen };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(CAMERA_FOV, 1, 0.1, 100);\n    camera.position.z = CAMERA_Z;\n\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.width = TEXTURE_SIZE;\n    canvas.height = TEXTURE_SIZE;\n    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n    if (ctx) (paint ?? paintSurface)(ctx, { width: TEXTURE_SIZE, height: TEXTURE_SIZE });\n\n    const texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas);\n    texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    texture.wrapS = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n    texture.wrapT = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n    texture.anisotropy = Math.min(4, renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy());\n\n    const uniforms: BulgeUniforms = {\n      uTexture: { value: texture },\n      uMouseNdc: { value: new THREE.Vector2(0, 0) },\n      uAspect: { value: 1 },\n      uStrength: { value: 0 },\n      uFalloff: { value: falloff },\n      uLift: { value: lift },\n      uMagnify: { value: magnify },\n      uSheen: { value: sheen },\n    };\n\n    // Dense. The bulge is evaluated per vertex, so the lens can only be as\n    // round as the mesh is fine: at 16 segments the Gaussian resolves into a\n    // faceted pyramid that follows the cursor.\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, 1, segments, segments);\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: ndcMouseBulgeVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: ndcMouseBulgeFragmentShader,\n      uniforms,\n    });\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    // The lift happens after the bounding sphere was computed; leaving culling\n    // on lets three blank a plane that is filling the viewport.\n    mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(mesh);\n\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, mesh, geometry, material, texture, uniforms };\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      scene.remove(mesh);\n      // Inside `uniforms`, where `disposeObject` cannot see it.\n      texture.dispose();\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, segments, paint]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    const aspect = width / height;\n    current.renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    current.renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    current.camera.aspect = aspect;\n    current.camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n\n    // Cover the frustum exactly at z = 0. Derived from the camera, because the\n    // visible extent at this distance has no fixed relationship to the pixel\n    // width of the container.\n    const visibleHeight = 2 * Math.tan((CAMERA_FOV * Math.PI) / 360) * CAMERA_Z;\n    current.mesh.scale.set(visibleHeight * aspect, visibleHeight, 1);\n    // The shader corrects its screen-space distance with this, so a stale\n    // value turns the lens into an ellipse the moment the container reshapes.\n    current.uniforms.uAspect.value = aspect;\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n      const { strength: peak, falloff: tightness, lift: rise, magnify: zoom, sheen: light } =\n        live.current;\n\n      // Fade the whole effect in and out with the pointer rather than snapping\n      // it. Snapping leaves a lens sitting at the last known position for one\n      // frame after the cursor leaves, which flickers.\n      const wanted = pointer.current.active && !reduced ? peak : 0;\n      engaged.current = damp(engaged.current, wanted, STRENGTH_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      current.uniforms.uMouseNdc.value.set(pointer.current.ndcX, pointer.current.ndcY);\n      current.uniforms.uStrength.value = engaged.current;\n      current.uniforms.uFalloff.value = tightness;\n      current.uniforms.uLift.value = rise;\n      current.uniforms.uMagnify.value = zoom;\n      current.uniforms.uSheen.value = light;\n\n      current.renderer.render(current.scene, current.camera);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the mesh from the scene, disposes the `CanvasTexture` explicitly because it sits inside `material.uniforms` beyond `disposeObject`'s reach, runs `disposeObject` over the scene, disposes the geometry and material, then `disposeRenderer` — `setAnimationLoop(null)`, `dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()` — and removes the canvas from the DOM. The stage ref is nulled first so a frame already queued on the shared RAF loop exits before touching anything. `useSmoothPointer` removes its own listeners on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The strength target is held at 0, so no vertex is ever displaced, the UV is never magnified and no sheen appears — the surface is a completely static image that ignores the pointer. The value is still damped to zero rather than assigned, so a lens that is on screen when the preference changes retracts smoothly instead of popping flat.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Comparing raw NDC distances. NDC is -1..1 on both axes regardless of the viewport, so on a 16:9 canvas the falloff is 1.78 times wider than it is tall and the lens is a visible ellipse. The x component of the delta is weighted by the aspect before the length is taken.\n- Raycasting the pointer against the plane instead. It costs a matrix inversion per frame, returns exactly one hit point, and — decisively — it intersects the CPU-side geometry, which this shader has already moved. Once anything displaces vertices, the raycast answers a question about a surface that is not on screen.\n- Projecting once and reusing `gl_Position`. The probe has to happen before the displacement and the final projection has to happen after it, so the two cannot be shared. Displacing after computing `gl_Position` simply has no effect at all.\n- Using a linear or smoothstep falloff. Both reach exactly zero at a finite radius, which draws a visible ring on the surface where the deformation stops. The Gaussian is asymptotic and leaves no boundary.\n- Lifting the vertex without magnifying the UV. The result is a bump, not a lens — the surface moves but the image printed on it does not, so it reads as a dent in a rigid sheet rather than glass laid over it.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` on. The lift happens after the bounding sphere was computed, so three can decide a plane that is filling the viewport is off screen and blank it.\n- Sizing the plane from the container's pixel dimensions. The plane has to cover the frustum at z = 0, which is `2 * tan(fov/2) * distance` tall; a pixel width has no fixed relationship to that and the plane ends up short of the edges or overscaled.\n- Forgetting to update `uAspect` on resize. The lens silently turns elliptical the moment the container reshapes, and because it still follows the cursor correctly the bug looks like a design decision.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` alone on unmount. GPU objects are freed but the WebGL context survives, and Chromium drops the oldest once a renderer process holds about 16. `disposeRenderer` calls `forceContextLoss`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `useSmoothPointer` already exposes `ndcX` / `ndcY`, so the pointer reaches the uniform without a single conversion and without a cached bounding rect that could go stale on scroll.\n- This is the same two-projections-per-vertex trick `cylinder-gallery` uses; there it has to be in-shader, because the CPU geometry is a flat plane and the visible drum exists only on the GPU.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove the cursor over the surface and a round highlight follows it, swelling the lattice cells underneath rather than merely brightening them. The highlight is circular at any container aspect — resize from square to wide and it stays round. It has no visible outer edge or ring where the effect stops. Leave the surface and the lens retracts over about a fifth of a second rather than vanishing. Nothing changes when the cursor moves outside the component. With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the surface is completely inert and the cursor has no effect at all.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "nested-splittext-restore": "# Build: Nested Split With True Restore\n\nSplitting text destroys the markup inside it: a paragraph containing an `<em>` and an `<a>` goes into SplitText as semantic HTML and comes out as a flat pile of divs. This component splits into masked lines that rise into view, preserves each child element's own tag through the split, and on teardown restores the container's `innerHTML` byte-identically to what it was before anything touched it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSplits rich markup into masked lines and restores the original innerHTML byte-for-byte on teardown.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `const originalHTML = el.innerHTML` is captured **before** any pre-processing. This string is the contract — everything else is arranged so it can be reapplied exactly.\n2. The nested pre-pass walks the container's element children. Each child is split to lines on its own with a throwaway `new SplitText(child, { type: \"lines\" })`; the line markup is read while that split is live, the split is reverted, and each line is re-emitted inside a **shallow clone** of the child (`child.cloneNode(false)`) before the child is removed. A shallow clone carries the tag and every attribute and none of the content — `cloneNode(true)` would duplicate the child's entire text into every line.\n3. The container is then split for real with `new SplitText(el, { type, mask: \"lines\", linesClass, aria: \"auto\" })`, and `gsap.from(split.lines, { yPercent: 110, duration: 0.9, stagger: 0.08, ease: \"power3.out\" })` runs inside a `gsap.context` scoped to the element.\n4. The build is deferred behind `document.fonts.ready`, because line boxes measured against the fallback face break in the wrong places and never re-flow when the real font arrives. A `cancelled` flag closes the async gap so a build cannot start after unmount.\n5. Teardown runs in a fixed order: `ctx.revert()` first, so the tween's inline styles are removed while the split nodes still exist; then `split.revert()`; then `if (el.innerHTML !== originalHTML) el.innerHTML = originalHTML`.\n6. That last line is the whole point. GSAP's `revert()` restores the string SplitText itself captured at construction time — which, after a nested pre-pass, is the *intermediate* markup, not the original. The explicit restore is what makes the guarantee real.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `Y = 110`\n  Percent of a line's own height it starts below the mask. Past 100 rather than at it, because `mask: \"lines\"` clips to the line box and descenders on the first frame otherwise peek below the mask edge on tall faces.\n- `DURATION = 0.9`\n  Seconds per line. Long enough that `power3.out` has room for its deceleration; below ~0.5s the ease flattens and the lines read as snapping into place.\n- `STAGGER = 0.08`\n  Seconds between lines. About a tenth of the duration, so five or six lines are always in motion together and the block reads as one rising mass rather than as a queue.\n- `VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.25`\n  Fraction of the block visible before splitting and revealing. A paragraph is tall, so requiring more than about a quarter means it must be nearly centred before anything happens.\n- `cloneNode(false) = false`\n  Shallow, always. `true` copies the child's descendants, so every line of a five-line paragraph contains the whole paragraph and the block renders five times its own height.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NestedSplitRestore(props: { children: ReactNode; type?: string; mask?: boolean; nested?: boolean; y?: number; duration?: number; stagger?: number; delay?: number; startOnView?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Markup to split. Element children keep their own tags and attributes through the pre-pass.\n- `type: string` — default `'lines'`\n  SplitText `type`. Lines are what the mask and the reveal use.\n- `mask: boolean` — default `true`\n  Wrap each line in an overflow-hidden box so it rises out of nothing.\n- `nested: boolean` — default `true`\n  Run the per-child pre-pass. Turn off for a single flat paragraph.\n- `y: number` — default `110`\n  Percent of a line's height it starts below the mask.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.9`\n  Seconds per line.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.08`\n  Seconds between lines.\n- `delay: number` — default `0`\n  Seconds before the first line moves.\n- `startOnView: boolean` — default `true`\n  Split and reveal when scrolled into view rather than on mount.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst Y = 110;\nconst DURATION = 0.9;\nconst STAGGER = 0.08;\nconst VIEW_THRESHOLD = 0.25;\n\n/**\n * Replace each element child of `host` with one shallow clone per visual line.\n * Text nodes sitting directly under `host` are left alone — they have no tag\n * to preserve, so the outer split handles them correctly already.\n */\nfunction flattenChildrenToLines(host: HTMLElement): void {\n  for (const child of Array.from(host.children)) {\n    if (!(child instanceof HTMLElement)) continue;\n\n    const inner = new SplitText(child, { type: 'lines' });\n    // Read the line markup while the split is live, then put the child back\n    // exactly as it was. Reading after revert would read the un-split child.\n    const lines = inner.lines.map((line) => line.innerHTML);\n    inner.revert();\n\n    for (const markup of lines) {\n      // cloneNode(false) — the tag and every attribute, none of the content.\n      // cloneNode(true) would duplicate the child's entire text into each line.\n      const clone = child.cloneNode(false);\n      if (!(clone instanceof HTMLElement)) continue;\n      clone.innerHTML = markup;\n      host.insertBefore(clone, child);\n    }\n    host.removeChild(child);\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * A stable string identity for `children`: the text that will be split, plus\n * the element tags the pre-pass above re-emits around every line.\n *\n * React builds a fresh node object on every parent render for anything that is\n * not a bare string, so naming `children` in a dependency array actually says\n * \"re-run whenever the parent renders, for any reason at all\". Here that is\n * destructive rather than merely wasteful. The cleanup reverts the split and\n * writes `originalHTML` back; the next run re-splits and calls `gsap.from`\n * again; and because `useInView` is `once: true` the gate is still open, so it\n * happens immediately. A reader half way down the paragraph watches it drop\n * back below the mask and rise a second time, and whatever tween was in flight\n * is thrown away with the context that owned it. A sibling toggling a menu is\n * enough to trigger it.\n *\n * Dropping the dependency outright is not the fix either — the split would\n * then describe a paragraph that is no longer on screen. So the node is\n * flattened to the only things the split actually depends on, and that string\n * is the dependency. Same content, same string, no re-run.\n */\nfunction childrenSignature(node: ReactNode): string {\n  if (node === null || node === undefined || typeof node === 'boolean') return '';\n  if (typeof node === 'string' || typeof node === 'number' || typeof node === 'bigint') {\n    return String(node);\n  }\n  if (Array.isArray(node)) {\n    // A separator that cannot occur in text, so ['ab', 'c'] and ['a', 'bc'] do\n    // not collapse onto the same key.\n    return (node as ReactNode[]).map(childrenSignature).join('\\u0001');\n  }\n  if (isValidElement<{ children?: ReactNode }>(node)) {\n    // The tag belongs in the signature: `flattenChildrenToLines` clones it\n    // around each line, so <h2>Same words</h2> and <p>Same words</p> are two\n    // different splits of identical text.\n    const { type } = node;\n    const tag =\n      typeof type === 'string'\n        ? type\n        : (type as { displayName?: string; name?: string }).displayName ||\n          (type as { name?: string }).name ||\n          'component';\n    return `<${tag}>${childrenSignature(node.props.children)}`;\n  }\n  // Iterables, portals and promises have no cheap textual identity. A constant\n  // under-reports a change, which is the safe direction: the alternative is a\n  // fresh value every render, i.e. exactly the bug this function exists to\n  // remove.\n  return '';\n}\n\nexport function NestedSplitRestore({\n  children,\n  type = 'lines',\n  mask = true,\n  nested = true,\n  y = Y,\n  duration = DURATION,\n  stagger = STAGGER,\n  delay = 0,\n  startOnView = true,\n  className,\n}: NestedSplitRestoreProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { inView } = useInView(root, { threshold: VIEW_THRESHOLD, once: true });\n  // Compared by value, not by identity. The node itself still goes into the\n  // tree below — only the effect's notion of \"the content changed\" is derived\n  // from this string. See `childrenSignature` for what listing `children` here\n  // costs.\n  const contentKey = childrenSignature(children);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    // Reduced motion never splits at all. The markup React rendered is the\n    // finished state, and leaving it untouched is both the accessible answer\n    // and the fast one.\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n    if (startOnView && !inView) return;\n\n    gsap.registerPlugin(SplitText);\n\n    // Captured before the pre-pass, not after. This string is the contract.\n    const originalHTML = el.innerHTML;\n\n    let cancelled = false;\n    let split: SplitText | null = null;\n    let ctx: gsap.Context | null = null;\n\n    const build = () => {\n      if (cancelled) return;\n\n      if (nested) flattenChildrenToLines(el);\n\n      const s = new SplitText(el, {\n        type,\n        mask: mask ? 'lines' : undefined,\n        linesClass: 'kinetic-split-line',\n        // SplitText restores the original text to the accessibility tree\n        // itself; without this the reveal is read out one line per node.\n        aria: 'auto',\n      });\n      split = s;\n\n      ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n        gsap.from(s.lines, {\n          yPercent: y,\n          duration,\n          stagger,\n          delay,\n          ease: 'power3.out',\n        });\n      }, el);\n    };\n\n    // Measuring line boxes before the webfont arrives measures the fallback,\n    // so the lines break in the wrong places and never re-flow. See\n    // `font-safe-text-split` for the full resize and late-font lifecycle.\n    void document.fonts.ready.then(build);\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n      // Order matters. The context reverts the tween's inline styles while the\n      // split nodes still exist; reverting the split first would leave those\n      // styles orphaned on nodes GSAP can no longer find.\n      ctx?.revert();\n      split?.revert();\n      // The true restore. Only writes when something actually differs, so the\n      // no-pre-pass case hands React back the very nodes it created.\n      if (el.innerHTML !== originalHTML) el.innerHTML = originalHTML;\n    };\n  }, [\n    contentKey,\n    type,\n    mask,\n    nested,\n    y,\n    duration,\n    stagger,\n    delay,\n    startOnView,\n    inView,\n    reduced,\n  ]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** A `cancelled` flag guards the async `document.fonts.ready` boundary. Teardown then runs `ctx.revert()` (kills the tweens and strips their inline styles while the split nodes still exist), `split.revert()`, and finally an explicit `el.innerHTML = originalHTML` when the two differ — the true restore.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No split happens at all — the effect returns before touching the DOM. The markup React rendered is the finished state, so the text is fully visible and fully semantic on first paint, with every `<em>` and `<a>` intact. This is both the accessible answer and the fast one.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Trusting `revert()` to restore the original markup after a nested pre-pass. SplitText snapshots `innerHTML` at construction, which by then is your intermediate line markup — `revert()` faithfully restores the wrong thing, and the `<em>` and `<a>` are gone for good.\n- Splitting before the webfont loads. Lines are measured against the fallback face, so they break in the wrong places and never re-flow; the mask then clips text that has moved outside its line box. See `font-safe-text-split`.\n- Using `cloneNode(true)` in the pre-pass. Each line inherits the child's full contents and the block explodes to several times its height.\n- Reading the line markup after reverting the inner split. Revert restores the child to its unsplit state, so you read the whole child back and produce one \"line\" containing everything.\n- Reverting the split before the context. GSAP's inline styles are then orphaned on nodes the context can no longer find, so a remount starts from a half-animated state.\n- Letting SplitText's line divs into the accessibility tree. Without `aria: \"auto\"` a screen reader announces one fragment per line, mid-sentence; with it SplitText restores the original text for assistive technology.\n- Splitting at all under reduced motion. There is nothing to gain and a whole DOM tree to rebuild — the markup React rendered is already the finished state.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This is one of the two components that deliberately use GSAP `SplitText`, because the technique *is* SplitText: nested splitting and the shortcomings of its `revert()`. `SplitText` ships inside the main `gsap` package and is free to use; it is imported from `gsap/SplitText` rather than vendored, so this is a use grant and no redistribution is involved. The other text components here split declaratively in JSX so consumers are not forced into GSAP for effects that do not need it.\n- The acceptance criterion is exact and testable: after teardown, `element.innerHTML` is byte-identical to what it was before the split — tags, attributes and whitespace included, not merely visually equivalent.\n- The `nested` prop can be turned off for a single flat paragraph, in which case the pre-pass is skipped and the restore write never fires because the strings already match.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nRecord `element.innerHTML` before mounting, unmount, and compare: the two strings are byte-identical, including the `<em>` and `<a>` tags and their attributes. Visually, lines rise from behind their own mask edge, staggered, with no character visible below the mask on the first frame. A screen reader reads the paragraph as continuous prose rather than as one fragment per line. With reduced motion enabled the paragraph is fully rendered on first paint and the DOM is never modified.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "newsletter-card": "# Build: Newsletter Card\n\nA centred sign-up card: a title, a line of copy and the kit `Input` with a mail icon beside a Subscribe button, under soft coloured streaks. Submitting validates the address inline. A valid address enters a submitting state that lasts at least `submitDelay` ms. The card then shows a success panel with the address and a \"Use a different address\" reset, or puts the failure message on the field. With no `onSubscribe` the delay alone simulates the request.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nEmail sign-up card with inline validation, a submitting state on a cleaned-up timer, success with reset, and an error path.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Four states (`idle`, `submitting`, `success`, `error`) plus an `error` string drive the render. The form has `noValidate` and the field `validationBehavior=\"aria\"`, so the browser never shows its own bubble. `validate(email)` returns a message or null, and the message goes into the kit Input as `isInvalid` plus `errorMessage`, rendered through React Aria's FieldError. Editing the field clears the message and leaves the error state.\n2. On a valid submit the card bumps a run counter and enters `submitting`. It wraps `onSubscribe(email)` so a return, a rejection and a synchronous throw all become a settled value, and starts a `window.setTimeout(submitDelay)` stored in a ref. It waits on `Promise.all` of the two, so a fast response still shows the spinner for the full delay and a slow one holds it longer.\n3. When both settle, the result applies only if the run counter still matches. A reset or unmount bumps the counter and clears the timer, so a late result from an abandoned submission cannot overwrite the current state. Failures go through `messageOf` (Error message, then string, then a fallback) and land on the field.\n4. Focus follows the state. A `focusRef` names the target (\"input\" or \"success\"), and an effect keyed on `[status, error]` focuses it after the new state has rendered: the field after an invalid submit, an error or a reset, and the `tabIndex=-1` success panel after success. While submitting, the field is `isReadOnly` and the button `aria-disabled` rather than disabled, because disabling the focused element would drop focus to `<body>`.\n5. A visually hidden `role=\"status\"` paragraph announces \"Subscribing…\" and \"Subscribed as …\". The success panel enters with a 320ms fade and 6px rise from a hoisted `@keyframes`, applied only under `motion-safe`. The streaks are six blurred rounded bars in accent and chart tones, faded out downward by `mask-b-from-10% mask-b-to-100%`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `submitDelay = 1200ms`\n  The minimum time in the submitting state. Long enough to read the spinner as \"working\" rather than a flicker, and short enough not to feel slow. It is also the full simulated request when there is no `onSubscribe`.\n- `email pattern = /^[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@.]+(\\.[^\\s@.]+)+$/`\n  Catches the typos people actually make (no @, no dot in the domain, stray spaces, `name@.com`) without rejecting valid but unusual addresses. The server stays the real authority.\n- `success entrance = 320ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1), opacity 0 → 1, translateY 6px → 0`\n  A short ease-out rise reads as the form turning into the confirmation, rather than the content being swapped.\n- `form width = max-w-[380px], row from @md (28rem)`\n  Fits the 380px input-and-button row of the landing card. Below 28rem of container width the button moves under the field at full width.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NewsletterCard(props: { title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; placeholder?: string; submitLabel?: ReactNode; onSubscribe?: (email: string) => void | Promise<void>; submitDelay?: number; validate?: (email: string) => string | null; successTitle?: ReactNode; successMessage?: (email: string) => ReactNode; resetLabel?: ReactNode; onStatusChange?: (status: \"idle\" | \"submitting\" | \"success\" | \"error\") => void; decoration?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Get new components in your inbox\"`\n  Card heading (an h3 that labels the section).\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Copy under the title.\n- `placeholder: string` — default `\"you@company.com\"`\n  Field placeholder.\n- `submitLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Subscribe\"`\n  Button text when idle. It reads \"Subscribing\" while submitting.\n- `onSubscribe: (email: string) => void | Promise<void>`\n  Called with the trimmed, validated address. A throw or rejection shows the error state with its message.\n- `submitDelay: number` — default `1200`\n  Minimum time in the submitting state, in ms.\n- `validate: (email: string) => string | null` — default `validateEmail`\n  Returns a message to block submission.\n- `successTitle: ReactNode` — default `\"You're on the list\"`\n  Heading of the success panel.\n- `successMessage: (email: string) => ReactNode`\n  Body of the success panel, given the subscribed address.\n- `resetLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Use a different address\"`\n  Reset button text.\n- `onStatusChange: (status: NewsletterStatus) => void`\n  Fired on every state change.\n- `decoration: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the coloured streaks along the top edge.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function NewsletterCard({\n  title = \"Get new components in your inbox\",\n  description = \"One short email when a component, block or template ships. No digests, no drip sequences, and you can leave any time.\",\n  placeholder = \"you@company.com\",\n  submitLabel = \"Subscribe\",\n  onSubscribe,\n  submitDelay = 1200,\n  validate = validateEmail,\n  successTitle = \"You're on the list\",\n  successMessage = (email) => (\n    <>\n      We'll write to <span className=\"font-medium text-text-primary\">{email}</span> when something new ships.\n    </>\n  ),\n  resetLabel = \"Use a different address\",\n  onStatusChange,\n  decoration = true,\n  className,\n}: NewsletterCardProps) {\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(\"\");\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState<NewsletterStatus>(\"idle\");\n  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [subscribed, setSubscribed] = useState(\"\");\n\n  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\n  const successRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const timerRef = useRef<number | undefined>(undefined);\n  const runRef = useRef(0);\n  const focusRef = useRef<\"input\" | \"success\" | null>(null);\n  const statusChangeRef = useRef(onStatusChange);\n  const titleId = useId();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    statusChangeRef.current = onStatusChange;\n  });\n\n  // Unmount: stop the timer and orphan any pending result.\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      window.clearTimeout(timerRef.current);\n      runRef.current += 1;\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n\n  // Move focus once the next state has rendered, so keyboard users are never left on a removed node.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const target = focusRef.current;\n    focusRef.current = null;\n    if (target === \"input\") inputRef.current?.focus();\n    if (target === \"success\") successRef.current?.focus();\n  }, [status, error]);\n\n  const go = (next: NewsletterStatus) => {\n    setStatus(next);\n    statusChangeRef.current?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const submit = (event: FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {\n    event.preventDefault();\n    if (status === \"submitting\") return;\n    const email = value.trim();\n    const invalid = validate(email);\n    if (invalid) {\n      setError(invalid);\n      if (status !== \"idle\") go(\"idle\");\n      focusRef.current = \"input\";\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const run = ++runRef.current;\n    setError(null);\n    go(\"submitting\");\n\n    let outcome: Promise<unknown>;\n    try {\n      outcome = Promise.resolve(onSubscribe?.(email)).then(\n        () => null,\n        (reason: unknown) => ({ reason }),\n      );\n    } catch (reason) {\n      outcome = Promise.resolve({ reason });\n    }\n    const delay = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n      timerRef.current = window.setTimeout(resolve, Math.max(0, submitDelay));\n    });\n\n    void Promise.all([outcome, delay]).then(([failure]) => {\n      if (runRef.current !== run) return;\n      timerRef.current = undefined;\n      if (failure) {\n        setError(messageOf((failure as { reason: unknown }).reason));\n        focusRef.current = \"input\";\n        go(\"error\");\n      } else {\n        setSubscribed(email);\n        focusRef.current = \"success\";\n        go(\"success\");\n      }\n    });\n  };\n\n  const reset = () => {\n    window.clearTimeout(timerRef.current);\n    runRef.current += 1;\n    setValue(\"\");\n    setError(null);\n    setSubscribed(\"\");\n    focusRef.current = \"input\";\n    go(\"idle\");\n  };\n\n  const submitting = status === \"submitting\";\n  const announcement =\n    status === \"submitting\" ? \"Subscribing…\" : status === \"success\" ? `Subscribed as ${subscribed}.` : \"\";\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** An unmount effect clears the submit timer and bumps the run counter, so a promise still in flight resolves into nothing. A reset does the same. There are no listeners or observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The success entrance is behind `motion-safe:`, so with reduced motion the panel simply appears. The streaks never move, and the opacity change on success transitions only under `motion-safe`. The spinner still turns, because it is the status indicator and not decoration.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Disabling the field or button while submitting. If either has focus, the browser moves focus to `<body>` and a keyboard user has to tab back in from the top of the page. Read-only and `aria-disabled` keep focus where it was, and the submit handler ignores repeats.\n- Letting a stale request finish. If someone resets, or the card unmounts mid-request, the old promise still resolves. Without the run-counter check it would flip a fresh form to success or call setState after unmount.\n- Using `Promise.all([onSubscribe(), delay])` directly. A rejection short-circuits `all`, the error appears before the minimum delay, and a synchronous throw escapes the handler entirely. Settle the call into a value first.\n- Leaving native validation on. `type=\"email\"` with `required` shows the browser bubble on submit, which ignores the theme and does not reach the FieldError slot.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass your own `validate` to add rules such as blocking disposable domains. It receives the trimmed address.\n- The exported `validateEmail` is the default validator, so a custom one can call it and add rules on top.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSubmitting an empty field shows \"Enter your email address.\" under it, keeps focus there and makes no request. Submitting \"abc\" shows the format message, and typing clears it. A valid address disables repeat submits, shows \"Subscribing\" with a spinner for about 1.2s, then shows the success panel naming the address. \"Use a different address\" returns to an empty, focused field. An address that `onSubscribe` rejects shows its message on the field after the delay. Unmounting mid-submit leaves no timer running.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "not-found-page": "# Build: Not Found Page\n\nThe page a site shows when a link leads nowhere. A large gradient error code sits over a small \"Error 404 · /the/path\" line, so the visitor sees what they asked for. Below them come the title, a plain explanation and two ways out: Back home and Browse components. A grid of suggested destination cards follows, and an optional footnote, here machine-readable entry points for agents. Every link reports through `onNavigate`, so a client router can take over.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA 404 block: big error code, the missing path, home and browse actions, suggested page cards and an agent footnote, staggered in.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is an `@container` `<section>` with a centred `max-w-[672px]` column, so the block sizes by its own width, not the viewport. The destination grid goes from one column to two at `@xl`. The code grows from Display 2 to the 4rem Large Title, and the title from Display 4 to Display 3, at the same breakpoint.\n2. The code is decorative (`aria-hidden`) and painted with `bg-linear-to-b from-text-primary to-text-tertiary bg-clip-text text-transparent`. Both stops are theme tokens, so the fade flips with dark mode. The readable \"Error 404\" and the path in `<code>` sit on the caption line under it.\n3. The entry is pure CSS. Each band gets `transition-[opacity,translate] duration-500 ease-out` with `starting:opacity-0 starting:translate-y-2`, so the browser animates from the `@starting-style` values on first render. A step index picks a static `delay-*` class (0 to 500ms): the code, then the caption and title, the description, the actions, the card rows and the footnote.\n4. Destination cards are `<a>` elements in a `<ul>` inside `<nav aria-label=\"Suggested pages\">`. The whole card is the link target, hover lifts the surface to `background-secondary-hover`, and the arrow after the title nudges 2px (mirrored under RTL with `directionalIconClass`).\n5. The actions are kit `ButtonLink`s: primary with a leading back arrow (which flips in RTL) and secondary with a grid icon. The footnote styles any `<code>` and `<a>` in its body through arbitrary child variants, and renders its links as mono underlined entries followed by \"— description\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `column = max-w-[672px], py-12 → py-20 at @3xl`\n  At this width the description stays under about 75 characters a line at the 20px size, and two cards sit side by side at about 320px each.\n- `entry = 500ms ease-out, 8px rise, delays 0, 75, 150, 200, 300, 400, 500ms`\n  Short enough that the page is settled before the visitor has read the title, and the stagger reads top to bottom without feeling like a sequence to wait for.\n- `code size = Display 2 (3rem) → Large Title (4rem) at @xl`\n  The code is the first thing the eye lands on, but it stays smaller than a hero headline, so the title and the ways out keep the weight.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NotFoundPage(props: { code?: string; path?: string; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; homeAction?: { label: string; href: string } | false; browseAction?: { label: string; href: string } | false; destinations?: { title: string; description: string; href: string; icon?: ComponentType }[]; footnote?: { title: string; body?: ReactNode; links?: { label: string; href: string; description?: string }[] }; onNavigate?: (href: string, event: MouseEvent<HTMLAnchorElement>) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `code: string` — default `\"404\"`\n  The big error code, also shown as \"Error {code}\" in the caption.\n- `path: string`\n  The requested path, shown in mono after the code.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Page not found\"`\n  The h1.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  The explanation under the title.\n- `homeAction: { label, href } | false` — default `{ label: \"Back home\", href: \"/\" }`\n  Primary button with a back arrow.\n- `browseAction: { label, href } | false` — default `{ label: \"Browse components\", href: \"/components\" }`\n  Secondary button with a grid icon.\n- `destinations: NotFoundDestination[]` — default `[]`\n  `{ title, description, href, icon? }` cards in a two-column grid.\n- `footnote: NotFoundFootnote`\n  `{ title, body?, links?: { label, href, description? }[] }`, shown under a divider.\n- `onNavigate: (href, event) => void`\n  Called for every link the block renders. `preventDefault()` there to route client-side.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the root section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Entry stagger: each band starts 8px low and transparent, then settles. Static strings so Tailwind sees them. */\nconst STEP_DELAYS = [\"delay-0\", \"delay-75\", \"delay-150\", \"delay-200\", \"delay-300\", \"delay-[400ms]\", \"delay-500\"] as const;\n\nfunction rise(step: number) {\n  return cx(\n    \"transition-[opacity,translate] duration-500 ease-out starting:translate-y-2 starting:opacity-0 motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n    STEP_DELAYS[Math.min(step, STEP_DELAYS.length - 1)],\n  );\n}\n\nfunction DestinationCard({\n  item,\n  step,\n  onNavigate,\n}: {\n  item: NotFoundDestination;\n  step: number;\n  onNavigate?: NotFoundPageProps[\"onNavigate\"];\n}) {\n  const Icon = item.icon;\n  return (\n    <li className={rise(step)}>\n      <a\n        href={item.href}\n        data-destination=\"\"\n        onClick={(event) => onNavigate?.(item.href, event)}\n        className=\"group flex h-full items-start gap-3 rounded-xl border border-border-component-detail-container bg-background-secondary-default p-4 outline-none transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-hover focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n      >\n        {Icon ? (\n          <span className=\"flex size-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs\">\n            <Icon aria-hidden className=\"size-[18px] text-foreground-icon-secondary\" />\n          </span>\n        ) : null}\n        <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1\">\n          <span className=\"flex items-center gap-1 text-headline-semibold text-text-primary\">\n            {item.title}\n            <RiArrowRightLine\n              aria-hidden\n              className={cx(\n                \"size-4 text-foreground-icon-tertiary transition-[translate,color] duration-150 ease-out group-hover:translate-x-0.5 group-hover:text-foreground-icon-secondary motion-reduce:transition-none motion-reduce:group-hover:translate-x-0 rtl:group-hover:-translate-x-0.5\",\n                directionalIconClass(RiArrowRightLine),\n              )}\n            />\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"mt-1 block text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{item.description}</span>\n        </span>\n      </a>\n    </li>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** There are no effects, timers or listeners. The entry is CSS `@starting-style` transitions, and the only handlers are React `onClick` props on the links.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Every band has `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so under prefers-reduced-motion the `@starting-style` values never animate and the page renders settled. The card arrow nudge is removed (`motion-reduce:group-hover:translate-x-0`) and hover colours change instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building the stagger delay with string interpolation (`delay-${n}`). Tailwind only generates classes it can see, so the delays silently vanish. The delays live in a static array.\n- Making the big code the only place the error appears. It is decorative and `aria-hidden`, so screen readers would never hear \"404\". The caption line carries it as text.\n- Returning a 404 page with a 200 status. The block is visual only. In Next.js render it from `not-found.tsx` (or call `notFound()`), so the response status is 404 and crawlers drop the URL.\n- Running the entry with JavaScript on mount. A hydration-timed fade flashes the content in and out on slow devices. `@starting-style` runs on first style resolution, with no effect and no state.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `onNavigate={(href, e) => { e.preventDefault(); router.push(href) }}` for client routing. It covers the actions, the cards and the footnote links. Links inside a custom `footnote.body` are yours to wire.\n- Pass the requested pathname as `path` (for example from `usePathname()`), so visitors can spot a typo.\n- Set `homeAction={false}` or `browseAction={false}` to drop a button, and leave `destinations` empty to hide the grid.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px a gradient \"404\" sits over \"Error 404 · /charts/bubble-chart\", then \"Page not found\", the explanation, and Back home and Browse components buttons. Four cards follow in two columns (Components, Documentation, Installation, MCP server), then the \"Reading this site as an agent\" footnote with four mono links. On load the bands rise in top to bottom. Clicking any link calls `onNavigate` with its href (the demo status reads \"Would open /components\"). Under reduced motion nothing moves. At 360px the cards stack in one column.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "notification": "# Build: Notification\n\nA card for one event (a deploy finished, someone commented, a build failed) with a round status icon or an avatar with presence, a title, timestamp, description, small action buttons and a close button. It can dismiss itself after a set time, showing a 3px countdown bar along its bottom edge that pauses and resumes with the timer. `NotificationViewport` portals a fixed stack to any corner or bottom/top centre, and cards entering or leaving it make the others glide into place on a spring.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDismissible notification card with status icon or avatar, actions and a pausable countdown bar, stacked in a corner viewport.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, motion@^12.43.0, react-aria@^3.53.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Notification` keeps its own `dismissedState`, ORed with the `dismissed` prop, and renders a `motion.div` inside `AnimatePresence onExitComplete={onDismiss}`. Dismissing (close button, timer or `dismissed`) plays the exit (opacity 0, y 8, scale 0.96, `blur(3px)`, 0.18s easeOut) and only then calls `onDismiss`, so the parent removes the item after the card has gone. A ref marks the card dismissed at once: AnimatePresence keeps rendering the exiting element with its old handlers, so a second press of the close button during the exit does nothing instead of firing `onDismissStart` again.\n2. The entrance plays only when `introDelay` is defined: `initial` is opacity 0, y 12, scale 0.97, `blur(4px)`, animating to rest over 0.25s easeOut after `introDelay` seconds. Without it, `initial={false}` renders the card in place.\n3. With `autoDismissDuration`, the countdown bar is a native Web Animation (`element.animate` from `scaleX(1)` to `scaleX(0)`, linear, `fill: \"forwards\"`, delayed by the intro) on a `h-[3px] bg-accent-600` span anchored `origin-left` (`rtl:origin-right`). A second effect owns the timer: `remaining` starts at duration plus intro delay, and each run plays the bar and sets a timeout for `remaining`. On `paused` it pauses the bar, and its cleanup subtracts the elapsed time, so a held countdown resumes where it stopped.\n4. The card is `relative flex items-start gap-3 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border p-4 pe-11` on the primary background with `shadow-dropdown`; `pe-11` reserves room for the `xs` `CloseButton` at `top-3 end-3`. The visual is the avatar (36px Avatar forced to `size-10`, plus a 12px presence dot: lime online, rose busy, grey offline) or a 40px round badge with `icon ?? STATUS_ICON[status]` tinted by status. Actions are small Buttons; without a `variant`, the first is secondary and the rest primary.\n5. The card's `role` defaults to \"alert\" for `status=\"error\"` and \"status\" otherwise, so screen readers announce errors assertively and the rest politely.\n6. `NotificationViewport` waits for hydration (`useSyncExternalStore` returns false on the server), then `createPortal`s a `motion.div layoutRoot` to React Aria's portal container (`useUNSAFE_PortalContext().getContainer`) or `document.body`. The layer is `pointer-events-none fixed z-100 w-[min(400px,calc(100vw-24px))] flex-col gap-3`, positioned 12px from the edges (24px from `sm`). Each child is wrapped in a `pointer-events-auto` `motion.div layout`, keyed by the child's key, inside `AnimatePresence initial={false} mode=\"popLayout\"`, with a layout spring of stiffness 520, damping 42 and mass 0.7.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `layout spring = stiffness 520, damping 42, mass 0.7`\n  Quick and almost without overshoot, so the rest of the stack closes the gap promptly when a card leaves without bouncing.\n- `enter / exit = enter 0.25s: y 12, scale 0.97, blur 4px / exit 0.18s: y 8, scale 0.96, blur 3px`\n  The exit is shorter than the entrance so a dismissed card gets out of the way faster than a new one arrives.\n- `countdown bar = 3px, bg-accent-600, linear`\n  Linear makes the bar's length a true reading of the time left; 3px is visible along the bottom edge without crowding the actions.\n- `viewport width and inset = w-[min(400px,calc(100vw-24px))], 12px inset (24px from sm)`\n  A comfortable width for a title, two-line description and actions, while keeping a 12px gutter on each side of a phone.\n- `z-index = z-100`\n  The overlay layer shared with Modal and Drawer, so notifications sit above page content.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Notification(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"title\" | \"onDrag\" | \"onDragStart\" | \"onDragEnd\" | \"onAnimationStart\" | \"onAnimationEnd\"> & { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; timestamp?: ReactNode; status?: \"neutral\" | \"information\" | \"success\" | \"error\"; icon?: IconComponent; avatar?: Omit<AvatarProps, \"size\"> & { presence?: \"online\" | \"busy\" | \"offline\" }; actions?: { label: ReactNode; onClick?: () => void; variant?: ButtonProps[\"variant\"] }[]; dismissible?: boolean; closeLabel?: string; onDismiss?: () => void; onDismissStart?: () => void; autoDismissDuration?: number; paused?: boolean; dismissed?: boolean; introDelay?: number; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction NotificationViewport(props: HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement> & { children?: ReactNode; position?: NotificationPosition; placement?: \"bottom-left\" | \"bottom-right\" }): ReactPortal | null\n```\n\n- `title / description / timestamp: ReactNode`\n  The card's copy; the timestamp sits after the title.\n- `status: \"neutral\" | \"information\" | \"success\" | \"error\"` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  Badge tint and default icon; \"error\" also makes the card an alert.\n- `icon: IconComponent`\n  Replaces the status icon.\n- `avatar: AvatarProps & { presence?: \"online\" | \"busy\" | \"offline\" }`\n  Shown instead of the icon, with an optional presence dot.\n- `actions: { label; onClick?; variant? }[]`\n  Small buttons under the message; first secondary, others primary by default.\n- `dismissible / closeLabel: boolean / string` — default `true / \"Dismiss notification\"`\n  Show the close button and name it.\n- `autoDismissDuration: number (ms)`\n  Dismiss after this long, with the countdown bar.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Hold the countdown and its bar.\n- `dismissed: boolean` — default `false`\n  Dismiss from outside; the exit plays, then `onDismiss` runs.\n- `introDelay: number (s)`\n  Enables the entrance animation, after this delay.\n- `onDismiss: () => void`\n  Called after the exit animation completes.\n- `onDismissStart: () => void`\n  Called once when the card closes itself (close button or countdown), as the exit starts.\n- `NotificationViewport.position: NotificationPosition` — default `\"bottom-end\"`\n  Corner or centre edge the stack is anchored to.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Notification({\n  title,\n  description,\n  timestamp,\n  status = \"neutral\",\n  icon,\n  avatar,\n  actions,\n  dismissible = true,\n  closeLabel = \"Dismiss notification\",\n  onDismiss,\n  onDismissStart,\n  autoDismissDuration,\n  paused = false,\n  dismissed: dismissedProp = false,\n  introDelay,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  role,\n  ...props\n}: NotificationProps) {\n  const [dismissedState, setDismissed] = useState(false);\n  const dismissed = dismissedProp || dismissedState;\n  // The countdown can fire long after the render that scheduled it, so it\n  // reads the latest callback.\n  const onDismissStartRef = useRef(onDismissStart);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    onDismissStartRef.current = onDismissStart;\n  });\n  // While a card exits, AnimatePresence keeps rendering its last element,\n  // whose close button still holds this handler. The ref makes a second press\n  // (or the countdown) during the exit a no-op instead of a second\n  // onDismissStart.\n  const dismissedRef = useRef(dismissed);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    dismissedRef.current = dismissed;\n  });\n  const dismiss = () => {\n    if (dismissedRef.current) return;\n    dismissedRef.current = true;\n    setDismissed(true);\n    onDismissStartRef.current?.();\n  };\n  const Icon = icon ?? STATUS_ICON[status];\n  const hasIntro = introDelay !== undefined;\n  const introDelayMs = (introDelay ?? 0) * 1000;\n  const timed = Boolean(autoDismissDuration && autoDismissDuration > 0);\n\n  // The countdown bar runs as one native Web Animation so `paused` can hold\n  // and resume it in step with the timer below. (Motion's standalone\n  // animate() on this child cancelled the card's own enter animation.)\n  const barRef = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const bar = useRef<Animation | null>(null);\n  const remaining = useRef((autoDismissDuration ?? 0) + introDelayMs);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const element = barRef.current;\n    if (!timed || !element) return;\n    bar.current = element.animate(\n      [{ transform: \"scaleX(1)\" }, { transform: \"scaleX(0)\" }],\n      { duration: autoDismissDuration, delay: introDelayMs, easing: \"linear\", fill: \"forwards\" },\n    );\n    return () => bar.current?.cancel();\n  }, [timed, autoDismissDuration, introDelayMs]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!timed || dismissed) return;\n    if (paused) {\n      bar.current?.pause();\n      return;\n    }\n    bar.current?.play();\n    const startedAt = performance.now();\n    const timer = window.setTimeout(dismiss, remaining.current);\n    return () => {\n      window.clearTimeout(timer);\n      remaining.current = Math.max(0, remaining.current - (performance.now() - startedAt));\n    };\n  }, [timed, paused, dismissed]);\n\n  return (\n    <AnimatePresence onExitComplete={onDismiss}>\n      {!dismissed ? (\n        <motion.div\n          ref={ref}\n          role={role ?? (status === \"error\" ? \"alert\" : \"status\")}\n          initial={\n            hasIntro\n              ? { opacity: 0, y: 12, scale: 0.97, filter: \"blur(4px)\" }\n              : false\n          }\n          animate={\n            hasIntro\n              ? {\n                  opacity: 1,\n                  y: 0,\n                  scale: 1,\n                  filter: \"blur(0px)\",\n                  transition: {\n                    duration: 0.25,\n                    delay: introDelay,\n                    ease: \"easeOut\",\n                  },\n                }\n              : undefined\n          }\n          exit={{\n            opacity: 0,\n            y: 8,\n            scale: 0.96,\n            filter: \"blur(3px)\",\n            transition: { duration: 0.18, ease: \"easeOut\" },\n          }}\n          className={cx(styles.card, className)}\n          {...props}\n        >\n          {avatar ? (\n            <NotificationAvatarVisual {...avatar} />\n          ) : (\n            <span className={cx(styles.visual, styles.status[status])}>\n              <Icon className={styles.icon} aria-hidden />\n            </span>\n          )}\n\n          <div className={styles.content}>\n            <div className={styles.header}>\n              <p className={styles.title}>{title}</p>\n              {timestamp ? <span className={styles.timestamp}>{timestamp}</span> : null}\n            </div>\n            {description ? <p className={styles.description}>{description}</p> : null}\n\n            {actions?.length ? (\n              <div className={styles.actions}>\n                {actions.map((action, index) => (\n                  <Button\n                    key={index}\n                    size=\"small\"\n                    variant={action.variant ?? (index === 0 ? \"secondary\" : \"primary\")}\n                    onClick={action.onClick}\n                  >\n                    {action.label}\n                  </Button>\n                ))}\n              </div>\n            ) : null}\n          </div>\n\n          {dismissible ? (\n            <CloseButton\n              size=\"xs\"\n              aria-label={closeLabel}\n              onClick={dismiss}\n              className={styles.close}\n            />\n          ) : null}\n\n          {timed ? (\n            <span\n              ref={barRef}\n              aria-hidden\n              className=\"absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 h-[3px] origin-left rtl:origin-right bg-accent-600\"\n            />\n          ) : null}\n        </motion.div>\n      ) : null}\n    </AnimatePresence>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The countdown Web Animation is cancelled and the dismiss timeout cleared by their effect cleanups (the timer cleanup also banks the elapsed time). The viewport portal unmounts with its owner.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not read `prefers-reduced-motion`: the 0.25s entrance, the 0.18s exit and the layout spring run regardless. A host can wrap the app in Motion's `MotionConfig reducedMotion=\"user\"`. The countdown bar also runs, since it shows how much time is left.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not drive the countdown bar with Motion's standalone `animate()`. On this child it cancelled the card's own enter animation; a native Web Animation can be paused and played in step with the timer.\n- Do not remove a card from your list to dismiss it. Set `dismissed` (or let the timer or close button do it) and remove it in `onDismiss`, or it vanishes without its exit animation.\n- Do not leave out `introDelay` and expect an entrance. Without it `initial` is `false`; pass `introDelay={0}` for cards that arrive while the page is open.\n- Do not key the viewport's children by array index. The wrapper falls back to the index when a child has no key, so removing a card from the middle would animate the wrong ones.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `paused` is meant for a stack held by hover or focus; the demo sets it from `onPointerEnter` / `onPointerLeave` on the viewport.\n- `position` accepts logical (`top-start`, `bottom-end`) and physical (`top-left`, `bottom-center`, …) corners; `placement` is a deprecated alias for the two bottom corners.\n- The viewport defaults to `aria-label=\"Notifications\"` and `position=\"bottom-end\"`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nNothing shows until a button is pressed. \"Deploy finished\" brings a green success card into the bottom-right stack with a blue bar shrinking over 5 seconds; hovering the stack holds the bar, leaving resumes it, and at the end the card blurs out while the others glide into place. \"New comment\" shows the LS avatar with a green presence dot and Mark read / Reply buttons, either of which closes the card. \"Build failed\" is announced as an alert. The position control moves the stack to the top or bottom centre, and a fifth card pushes the oldest out with its exit animation.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "notification-bell-badge": "# Build: Notification Bell Badge\n\nThe dashboard header bell: a 36px bordered button with a small red count badge over the glyph. Pressing it opens the kit Popover with the notification list; each row toggles its own read state and \"Mark all as read\" clears them, and the badge, the trigger's accessible name and the \"N unread\" line all come from the same list.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBell button with a red unread badge that opens a notification popover; mark one or all read and the badge follows.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. State is one notifications array (controlled via notifications + onNotificationsChange, or uncontrolled via defaultNotifications); unread is derived as items.filter(!read).length and never stored separately.\n2. BellButton is a plain <button> that forwards its ref and spreads props, styled with the kit buttonStyles base and secondary variant at 36px (or 32px), so it can be the first child of PopoverTrigger.\n3. Its aria-label reads \"Notifications, N unread\"; the visual badge is aria-hidden, absolutely positioned at top-1 start-[18px] and scales from 50% plus fades out when the count hits 0.\n4. While fading out the badge keeps its last non-zero number (a setState-during-render sync that only updates on non-zero counts), so it never flashes an empty red dot.\n5. Each row's toggle button flips between \"Mark as read\" and \"Mark as unread\" and calls setRead(id, !read), mapping a new array; \"Mark all as read\" maps every unread item to read.\n6. Neither control unmounts or becomes disabled after use (the mark-all button turns aria-disabled), so keyboard focus stays inside the popover; the unread line is aria-live so screen readers hear the new count.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `badge position = top-1 start-[18px], h-3.5 min-w-3.5`\n  Sits on the bell glyph's top-right shoulder inside the 36px button, matching the dashboard shot.\n- `badge ring = ring-2 ring-background-primary-default`\n  A 2px ring in the button's own fill cuts the badge out of the glyph outline in both themes.\n- `max = 9`\n  A 14px badge holds two characters; larger counts read 9+.\n- `popover width = 360px (max 100vw - 32px)`\n  Fits a two-line title, a description and the toggle button without wrapping the time row.\n- `list height = max-h-[min(340px,60vh)]`\n  About five rows before the list scrolls, and never taller than a phone viewport allows.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function NotificationBellBadge({ notifications, defaultNotifications, onNotificationsChange, isOpen, defaultOpen, onOpenChange, max, size, label, emptyState, footer, placement, className, popoverClassName }: NotificationBellBadgeProps): JSX.Element\nexport function BellButton({ count, max, size, label, className, ref, ...props }: BellButtonProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `notifications: readonly BellNotification[]`\n  Controlled list: { id, title, description?, time, read?, icon?, tone? }.\n- `defaultNotifications: readonly BellNotification[]` — default `[]`\n  Initial list when uncontrolled.\n- `onNotificationsChange: (next: BellNotification[]) => void`\n  Receives the whole updated list after a mark-one or mark-all change.\n- `isOpen / defaultOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Popover open state, controlled or not.\n- `max: number` — default `9`\n  Counts above this show as max+.\n- `size: 'medium' | 'small'` — default `'medium'`\n  36px or 32px bell.\n- `label: string` — default `'Notifications'`\n  Trigger accessible name base and popover title.\n- `emptyState: ReactNode` — default `'You are all caught up.'`\n  Shown when the list is empty.\n- `footer: ReactNode`\n  Content under the list, e.g. a \"View all\" link.\n- `placement: 'bottom' | 'bottom start' | 'bottom end'` — default `'bottom end'`\n  Popover placement relative to the bell.\n- `BellButton.count: number`\n  Unread count shown in the standalone bell; the badge hides at 0.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function NotificationBellBadge({\n  notifications,\n  defaultNotifications = [],\n  onNotificationsChange,\n  isOpen,\n  defaultOpen,\n  onOpenChange,\n  max = 9,\n  size = 'medium',\n  label = 'Notifications',\n  emptyState = 'You are all caught up.',\n  footer,\n  placement = 'bottom end',\n  className,\n  popoverClassName,\n}: NotificationBellBadgeProps) {\n  const [uncontrolled, setUncontrolled] = useState<readonly BellNotification[]>(defaultNotifications);\n  const items = notifications ?? uncontrolled;\n  const unread = items.filter((item) => !item.read).length;\n\n  const commit = (next: BellNotification[]) => {\n    setUncontrolled(next);\n    onNotificationsChange?.(next);\n  };\n  const setRead = (id: string, read: boolean) =>\n    commit(items.map((item) => (item.id === id ? { ...item, read } : item)));\n  const markAllRead = () => {\n    if (unread === 0) return;\n    commit(items.map((item) => (item.read ? item : { ...item, read: true })));\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <PopoverTrigger isOpen={isOpen} defaultOpen={defaultOpen} onOpenChange={onOpenChange}>\n      <BellButton count={unread} max={max} size={size} label={label} className={className} />\n      <Popover placement={placement} className={cx('w-[360px]', popoverClassName)} dialogClassName=\"gap-1.5 p-1\">\n        <div className=\"flex items-start justify-between gap-3 px-2 pt-1.5 pb-1\">\n          <PopoverHeader\n            title={label}\n            description={<span aria-live=\"polite\">{unread > 0 ? `${unread} unread` : 'No unread notifications'}</span>}\n          />\n          <Button\n            variant=\"ghost\"\n            size=\"xs\"\n            leadingIcon={RiCheckDoubleLine}\n            aria-disabled={unread === 0 || undefined}\n            onClick={markAllRead}\n            className=\"mt-px\"\n          >\n            Mark all as read\n          </Button>\n        </div>\n\n        {items.length === 0 ? (\n          <p className=\"px-2 py-6 text-center text-body-2-regular text-text-tertiary\">{emptyState}</p>\n        ) : (\n          <ul className=\"flex max-h-[min(340px,60vh)] flex-col gap-0.5 overflow-y-auto\" aria-label={label}>\n            {items.map((item) => {\n              const Icon = item.icon ?? RiNotification3Line;\n              return (\n                <li\n                  key={item.id}\n                  className={cx(\n                    'flex items-start gap-3 rounded-xl px-2 py-2.5 transition-colors duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n                    item.read ? 'bg-transparent' : 'bg-background-secondary-default',\n                  )}\n                >\n                  <span\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className={cx('flex size-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full', styles.tone[item.tone ?? DEFAULT_TONE])}\n                  >\n                    <Icon className=\"size-4\" aria-hidden />\n                  </span>\n                  <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n                    <p className={cx('text-body-2-medium', item.read ? 'text-text-secondary' : 'text-text-primary')}>\n                      {item.title}\n                      {item.read ? null : <span className=\"sr-only\"> (unread)</span>}\n                    </p>\n                    {item.description ? <p className=\"text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{item.description}</p> : null}\n                    <p className=\"flex items-center gap-1 text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n                      {item.read ? null : <StatusDot color=\"indigo\" className=\"-ms-[3px]\" />}\n                      {item.time}\n                    </p>\n                  </div>\n                  <button\n                    type=\"button\"\n                    className={styles.toggle}\n                    aria-label={item.read ? `Mark \"${item.title}\" as unread` : `Mark \"${item.title}\" as read`}\n                    title={item.read ? 'Mark as unread' : 'Mark as read'}\n                    onClick={() => setRead(item.id, !item.read)}\n                  >\n                    {item.read ? <RiMailLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4\" /> : <RiCheckLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4\" />}\n                  </button>\n                </li>\n              );\n            })}\n          </ul>\n        )}\n        {footer ? <div className=\"border-t border-separator-border px-2 pt-2 pb-1\">{footer}</div> : null}\n      </Popover>\n    </PopoverTrigger>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing of its own to release: no timers or listeners. The kit PopoverTrigger owns and removes its outside-press listener.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The badge and row highlight change instantly (motion-reduce:transition-none); the kit Popover drops its scale and blur entry and only fades.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not remove the per-row button once a notification is read: focus would drop to <body> mid-popover. Flip its label instead.\n- Do not disable \"Mark all as read\" with the disabled attribute for the same reason; use aria-disabled and no-op the handler.\n- Do not wrap IconButton in a span as the PopoverTrigger child: the trigger must be the focusable element that receives the forwarded ref and press props.\n- Do not keep a separate unread counter in state; derive it from the list or the badge and the list will drift apart.\n\n## Notes\n\n- BellButton is exported on its own for headers that open a different surface (a drawer or a full notification center).\n- Pass isOpen / onOpenChange to control the popover, e.g. to close it after navigating to a notification.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith 5 unread, the bell reads \"Notifications, 5 unread\"; marking one read in the popover makes it \"4 unread\", and \"Mark all as read\" hides the badge and leaves the name \"Notifications\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "notification-center": "# Build: Notification Center\n\nA 430px inbox card: a header with the unread count and \"Mark all read\", a segmented control filtering All / Mentions / System with a count on each tab, and a scrolling list of notification rows. Each row shows an avatar or a tinted status icon, title, timestamp, an unread dot, the description and optional action buttons. Read state is tracked inside the component, so pressing an action or \"Mark all read\" clears the dots without the parent rewriting its data; the tab can be controlled or left uncontrolled.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTabbed activity inbox with unread counts, avatars or status icons, inline action buttons and a mark-all-read control.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Tab state bridges controlled and uncontrolled use: `activeTab = tab ?? internalTab`, and `setTab` only writes `internalTab` when `tab` is undefined before calling `onTabChange`. The `SegmentedControl` (`selectedKeys={[activeTab]}`) reads the first key of the selection set and ignores an empty selection, so a tab can never be deselected.\n2. Read state is a `Set` of ids in local state. `isUnread(item)` is `item.unread === true && !readIds.has(item.id)`; the header shows \"N unread\" or \"No unread notifications\", and \"Mark all read\" (a small ghost Button, disabled at zero) replaces the set with every `unread` id. Pressing any action button adds that row's id before calling `onAction(notificationId, actionId)`.\n3. Tab counts and the visible list are `useMemo`s over `notifications`: All shows everything, Mentions and System filter by `category`. Items with `category: \"activity\"` only appear under All. Each tab label renders its count in a `bg-badge-neutral-background` pill.\n4. `NotificationVisual` renders the `avatar` (Avatar `size=\"lg\"` forced to `size-10`) when present, else a 40px round badge with `icon ?? STATUS_ICON[status]` tinted by `status`: neutral (tertiary background), information, success and error (`bg-notification-*-background` / `text-notification-*-foreground`).\n5. The list sits in a padded well (`p-1.5` around a `rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default` box) whose inner div is `max-h-[516px] overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain`, so long inboxes scroll inside the card without scrolling the page. Rows are `<article>`s with `rounded-notification-card` (10px) on the primary background; the unread dot is an 8px `bg-accent-500` span labelled \"Unread\". With no visible rows it shows an empty state with `RiNotificationOffLine` and `emptyMessage`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card width = w-full max-w-[430px], rounded-3xl`\n  Fits a notification row with two inline buttons and a timestamp on one line, and fits the 440px bell popover in the app shell with its border.\n- `list height = max-h-[516px]`\n  Caps the list so the card stays inside a laptop viewport when it is opened from a header bell; longer inboxes scroll inside it.\n- `row card radius = --radius-notification-card: 10px`\n  Smaller than the 16px (`rounded-2xl`) well around the rows, so the nested corners read as nested inside its 8px padding.\n- `visual size = size-10 (40px), icon size-5`\n  Avatars and status icons share one footprint, so titles line up whatever the row's visual is.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NotificationCenter(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"onChange\"> & { notifications: NotificationCenterItem[]; defaultTab?: \"all\" | \"mentions\" | \"system\"; tab?: \"all\" | \"mentions\" | \"system\"; onTabChange?: (tab: NotificationCenterTab) => void; onAction?: (notificationId: string, actionId: string) => void; title?: string; emptyMessage?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\ntype NotificationCenterItem = { id: string; category: \"mentions\" | \"system\" | \"activity\"; group: string; title: string; description: string; timestamp: string; unread?: boolean; status?: \"neutral\" | \"information\" | \"success\" | \"error\"; icon?: IconComponent; avatar?: Pick<AvatarProps, \"src\" | \"alt\" | \"initials\" | \"color\">; actions?: { id: string; label: string; variant?: ButtonProps[\"variant\"] }[] }\n```\n\n- `notifications: NotificationCenterItem[]`\n  The inbox, rendered in array order.\n- `defaultTab: \"all\" | \"mentions\" | \"system\"` — default `\"all\"`\n  Initial tab when uncontrolled.\n- `tab / onTabChange: NotificationCenterTab / (tab) => void`\n  Controlled tab.\n- `onAction: (notificationId: string, actionId: string) => void`\n  Called when a row's action button is pressed; the row is marked read first.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Notifications\"`\n  Heading and accessible name of the section.\n- `emptyMessage: string` — default `\"You’re all caught up.\"`\n  Shown when the active tab has no rows.\n- `item.status: \"neutral\" | \"information\" | \"success\" | \"error\"` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  Tint and default icon of the round badge when there is no avatar.\n- `item.actions[].variant: ButtonProps[\"variant\"]` — default `\"secondary\"`\n  Button style of an inline action.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function NotificationCenter({\n  notifications,\n  defaultTab = \"all\",\n  tab,\n  onTabChange,\n  onAction,\n  title = \"Notifications\",\n  emptyMessage = \"You’re all caught up.\",\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: NotificationCenterProps) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const [internalTab, setInternalTab] = useState<NotificationCenterTab>(defaultTab);\n  const [readIds, setReadIds] = useState<Set<string>>(() => new Set());\n  const activeTab = tab ?? internalTab;\n\n  const setTab = (nextTab: NotificationCenterTab) => {\n    if (tab === undefined) setInternalTab(nextTab);\n    onTabChange?.(nextTab);\n  };\n\n  const isUnread = (item: NotificationCenterItem) => item.unread === true && !readIds.has(item.id);\n  const unreadCount = notifications.filter(isUnread).length;\n\n  const tabCounts = useMemo(\n    () => ({\n      all: notifications.length,\n      mentions: notifications.filter((item) => item.category === \"mentions\").length,\n      system: notifications.filter((item) => item.category === \"system\").length,\n    }),\n    [notifications],\n  );\n\n  const visibleNotifications = useMemo(\n    () =>\n      activeTab === \"all\"\n        ? notifications\n        : notifications.filter((item) => item.category === activeTab),\n    [activeTab, notifications],\n  );\n\n  const markAllRead = () => {\n    setReadIds(new Set(notifications.filter((item) => item.unread).map((item) => item.id)));\n  };\n\n  return localize((\n    <section\n      ref={ref}\n      aria-label={title}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-full max-w-[430px] flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-3xl border border-border-button-default bg-notification-center-background shadow-dropdown\",\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 p-4 pb-1.5\">\n        <div className=\"flex items-start justify-between gap-4\">\n          <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n            <h2 className=\"text-title-3-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</h2>\n            <p className=\"text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">\n              {unreadCount === 0 ? \"No unread notifications\" : `${unreadCount} unread`}\n            </p>\n          </div>\n          <Button\n            variant=\"ghost\"\n            size=\"small\"\n            onClick={markAllRead}\n            disabled={unreadCount === 0}\n          >\n            Mark all read\n          </Button>\n        </div>\n\n        <SegmentedControl\n          aria-label=\"Notification category\"\n          selectedKeys={[activeTab]}\n          onSelectionChange={(keys) => {\n            const next = [...(keys as Set<string>)][0] as NotificationCenterTab | undefined;\n            if (next) setTab(next);\n          }}\n          className=\"flex w-full\"\n        >\n          {TABS.map(({ id, label }) => (\n            <SegmentedControlItem\n              key={id}\n              id={id}\n              className=\"flex-1\"\n            >\n              <TabLabel label={label} count={tabCounts[id]} />\n            </SegmentedControlItem>\n          ))}\n        </SegmentedControl>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"bg-notification-center-background p-1.5\">\n        <div className=\"overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default\">\n          <div className=\"max-h-[516px] overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain bg-background-secondary-default p-2\">\n            {visibleNotifications.length === 0 ? (\n              <div className=\"flex min-h-64 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-2xl bg-background-primary-default px-6 text-center\">\n                <span className=\"flex size-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-background-secondary-default text-foreground-icon-secondary\">\n                  <RiNotificationOffLine className=\"size-5\" aria-hidden />\n                </span>\n                <p className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{emptyMessage}</p>\n                <p className=\"max-w-64 text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">\n                  New activity will appear here when it arrives.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n            ) : (\n              <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-2\">\n                {visibleNotifications.map((item) => {\n                  const unread = isUnread(item);\n                  return (\n                    <article\n                      key={item.id}\n                      className=\"group/item relative flex gap-3 rounded-notification-card bg-background-primary-default px-3 py-3\"\n                    >\n                      <NotificationVisual item={item} />\n                      <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n                        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 items-start justify-between gap-3\">\n                          <p className=\"min-w-0 text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{item.title}</p>\n                          <div className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2\">\n                            <span className=\"text-caption-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-tertiary\">\n                              {item.timestamp}\n                            </span>\n                            {unread ? (\n                              <span\n                                className=\"size-2 rounded-full bg-accent-500\"\n                                aria-label=\"Unread\"\n                              />\n                            ) : null}\n                          </div>\n                        </div>\n                        <p className=\"text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{item.description}</p>\n                        {item.actions?.length ? (\n                          <div className=\"mt-1.5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\">\n                            {item.actions.map((action) => (\n                              <Button\n                                key={action.id}\n                                size=\"small\"\n                                variant={action.variant ?? \"secondary\"}\n                                onClick={() => {\n                                  setReadIds((current) => new Set(current).add(item.id));\n                                  onAction?.(item.id, action.id);\n                                }}\n                              >\n                                {action.label}\n                              </Button>\n                            ))}\n                          </div>\n                        ) : null}\n                      </div>\n                    </article>\n                  );\n                })}\n              </div>\n            )}\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** None: state only (tab, read ids), no listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component adds no motion of its own; tab and button transitions come from SegmentedControl and Button and follow their reduced-motion rules.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not expect `unread: false` to be written back to your data. Read state lives in the component's `readIds` set; persist it in `onAction` (or a \"mark all\" handler of your own) if it must survive a remount.\n- Do not rely on `group` for headings. It is part of the item type for your own data, but the list renders flat rows in array order; sort the array yourself.\n- Do not pass `tab` without `onTabChange`. A controlled tab never changes on its own, so the segmented control would appear stuck.\n- Do not give an item both `avatar` and `icon` expecting the icon; the avatar wins.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The fixed copy (tab names, \"Mark all read\", the empty-state line) runs through `useTemplateCopy()`'s `localize`, so localized templates translate it.\n- The section is labelled by `title` (`aria-label`), and the h2 shows the same text.\n- Used by the app shell's notification bell, which renders it inside a non-modal popover.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card reads \"4 unread\" with tab counts All 6, Mentions 2, System 3. Choosing System shows the three system rows and \"Viewing System\" in the activity panel. Pressing Retry removes that row's blue dot, drops the count to 3 and logs \"Retry\" beside the card. \"Mark all read\" clears every dot, reads \"No unread notifications\" and disables itself. The list scrolls inside the card when it is taller than 516px.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "number-field": "# Build: Number Field\n\nA number typed or stepped, such as seats, a price or a percentage. It puts Input's field shell (`InputBase`, with the same hover, focus, invalid and disabled states) inside React Aria's `NumberField` and adds minus and plus buttons at the trailing end. React Aria formats and parses the value for the locale, clamps it to `minValue` and `maxValue` on blur, disables a stepper at its limit, and handles arrow keys, Home and End, the scroll wheel and press-and-hold repeat.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nInput's field shell plus minus and plus steppers on React Aria NumberField: locale currency, percent and unit formats, clamped to limits.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `NumberField` renders `AriaNumberField` as `group flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1`. All React Aria number props (`value`, `defaultValue`, `onChange`, `minValue`, `maxValue`, `step`, `formatOptions`, `isWheelDisabled`, `isDisabled`, `isInvalid`) pass straight through. Without a `label` or `aria-label`, the `placeholder` becomes the `aria-label`.\n2. Its render function shows `Label` (with the asterisk when `isRequired`) when `label` is set, then `InputBase` from the Input item, then `HintText` (red when invalid) when `hint` is set. Because `InputBase` renders React Aria's `Group` and `Input`, they pick up `NumberField`'s context: the group gets the field's state for the ring and fill classes, and the input gets the formatted value, `inputMode` and key handling. The input adds `tabular-nums` so digits do not shift width while stepping.\n3. The steppers go in `InputBase`'s `trailingAddon`: a `-my-1 -me-1 flex gap-0.5` span with two React Aria `Button`s, `slot=\"decrement\"` (`RiSubtractLine`) and `slot=\"increment\"` (`RiAddLine`). The slots connect them to the field, so React Aria disables each at its limit, repeats the step while it is held, and keeps focus in the input on press.\n4. Each stepper is `rounded-md` in `text-foreground-icon-secondary`, `size-6` (or `size-5` at `small`) with a 16px icon. Hover and press set `bg-background-tertiary-hover` with a 150ms colour transition; keyboard focus draws `ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring`; disabled switches to the disabled text colour and a `not-allowed` cursor with no hover fill.\n5. `hideSteppers` drops the buttons entirely; Up, Down, Page Up, Page Down, Home and End still step the value from the keyboard.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `stepper size = size-6 (24px) at medium, size-5 (20px) at small; icon size-4 (16px)`\n  Fits inside the 36px or 32px shell without changing its height, and leaves the 16px icon a 4px hover margin on every side.\n- `stepper inset = -my-1 -me-1`\n  Pulls the button pair 4px into the shell's padding, so their hover fill sits closer to the edge while the text keeps Input's inset.\n- `stepper gap = gap-0.5 (2px)`\n  Keeps minus and plus as separate hover targets without reading as two distant controls.\n- `hover transition = transition-colors duration-150 ease-out`\n  The same 150ms as the field shell (`--input-transition-ms`), so a stepper and the ring around it change together.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction NumberField(props: Omit<AriaNumberFieldProps, \"className\" | \"children\"> & { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; tooltip?: boolean | string; placeholder?: string; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; leadingIcon?: IconComponent; hideSteppers?: boolean; className?: string; fieldClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value / defaultValue: number`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled numeric value.\n- `onChange: (value: number) => void`\n  Called with the parsed number, after clamping; `NaN` when cleared.\n- `minValue / maxValue: number`\n  Limits. The value is clamped on blur and the matching stepper disables at each limit.\n- `step: number` — default `1`\n  Amount per stepper press or arrow key.\n- `formatOptions: Intl.NumberFormatOptions`\n  Currency, percent, unit or decimal formatting for the locale.\n- `label / hint: ReactNode`\n  Label above and caption below, as on Input.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px or 32px shell; 24px or 20px steppers.\n- `hideSteppers: boolean` — default `false`\n  Removes the minus and plus buttons.\n- `leadingIcon: ComponentType`\n  20px icon before the value.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function NumberField({\n  label,\n  hint,\n  tooltip,\n  placeholder,\n  size = \"medium\",\n  leadingIcon,\n  hideSteppers = false,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  fieldClassName,\n  ...props\n}: NumberFieldProps) {\n  const stepper = cx(STEPPER, size === \"small\" ? \"size-5\" : \"size-6\");\n\n  return (\n    <AriaNumberField\n      {...props}\n      aria-label={ariaLabel ?? (!label ? placeholder : undefined)}\n      className={cx(\"group flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1\", className)}\n    >\n      {({ isInvalid }) => (\n        <>\n          {label ? (\n            <Label isRequired={props.isRequired} isInvalid={isInvalid} tooltip={tooltip}>\n              {label}\n            </Label>\n          ) : null}\n          <InputBase\n            size={size}\n            placeholder={placeholder}\n            leadingIcon={leadingIcon}\n            fieldClassName={fieldClassName}\n            className=\"tabular-nums\"\n            trailingAddon={\n              hideSteppers ? undefined : (\n                <span className=\"-my-1 -me-1 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-0.5\">\n                  <AriaButton slot=\"decrement\" className={stepper}>\n                    <RiSubtractLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4\" />\n                  </AriaButton>\n                  <AriaButton slot=\"increment\" className={stepper}>\n                    <RiAddLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4\" />\n                  </AriaButton>\n                </span>\n              )\n            }\n          />\n          {hint ? <HintText isInvalid={isInvalid}>{hint}</HintText> : null}\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaNumberField>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component holds no state of its own; React Aria owns the value and the press-and-hold timer.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. Its only motion is the 150ms colour transition on the steppers and the shell, which a global rule that removes transitions makes instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not render steppers as plain `<button>`s with your own `onClick`. Only React Aria `Button`s with `slot=\"increment\"` and `slot=\"decrement\"` get the limit disabling, hold-to-repeat and focus handling from the field.\n- Do not format the value yourself and pass a string. Pass a number and `formatOptions` (for example `{ style: \"currency\", currency: \"USD\" }`); React Aria formats it for display and parses typed text back.\n- Do not forget that `onChange` can give `NaN` when the field is cleared. Guard any total you compute from it.\n- Do not use `step` for a percent field in whole numbers. With `style: \"percent\"`, 10% is the value 0.1, so a 5% step is `step={0.05}`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The field imports `InputBase` from Input, and `Label` and `HintText` from Input's companion files, so install Input alongside it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA \"Seats\" field shows 5 with minus and plus at its end. Pressing plus shows 6; holding it keeps stepping. At the maximum the plus button greys out and stops responding. Typing 80 into a field with a maximum of 50 and tabbing away snaps it to 50. A currency field shows \"$12.00\" and a percent field \"10%\"; Up and Down step them. Hover on a stepper fills it softly; focus on the field draws Input's inset ring.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "on-this-page-toc": "# Build: On This Page TOC\n\nThe table of contents that sits to the right of a docs article. A thin rail runs down the list and a 2px bar slides to the section currently in view, which also turns its label from tertiary to primary. Clicking a label scrolls the article to that section and moves focus there. It watches any scroll container, not only the window, so it works inside panels and previews.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRight-hand \"On this page\" list that tracks the section you are reading with an IntersectionObserver and a sliding active bar.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Items are `{ id, label, depth? }`, where `id` is the id of a section element. One IntersectionObserver is created with `root` set to the `scrollRoot` element (or the viewport when omitted) and observes every section.\n2. The observer's `rootMargin` shrinks the root to an activation band: `-offset px` from the top and `-(1 - activationRatio)` from the bottom (by default the top 30%). Callbacks add or remove ids from an in-band set, and the active item is the last id in TOC order that is in the band, which is the section whose heading most recently scrolled into it. When nothing is in the band, the last active id is kept.\n3. A passive scroll listener on the same root only checks for reaching the end: once `scrollTop + clientHeight` hits `scrollHeight`, the last item wins, because a short final section can never reach the band.\n4. Clicking an item prevents the hash jump, computes the target offset relative to the root with `getBoundingClientRect`, and calls `root.scrollTo` (smooth unless reduced motion). The clicked id is committed at once and observer updates are locked until `scrollend` or a 900ms fallback, so the bar does not stutter through the sections the scroll passes.\n5. The indicator is one absolutely positioned span whose `translateY` and height are copied from the active link's `offsetTop` / `offsetHeight` in a layout effect. A ResizeObserver on the list re-measures when wrapping changes, and the first placement skips the transition so it does not slide in from the top.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `activation band = top 30% of the root (activationRatio 0.3)`\n  A section counts as \"being read\" once its top crosses into the upper part of the view; a band that is too low flips the TOC before the heading is visible.\n- `offset = 16px`\n  Used for both the observer's top margin and the scroll target, so a clicked section lands where the observer already treats it as active.\n- `click lock = until scrollend, max 900ms`\n  Long enough for a smooth scroll across the article; the timeout covers browsers without `scrollend` and clicks on the section already in place.\n- `indicator = 2px bar on a 1px rail, 300ms ease-out`\n  Matches the reference: a light rail with a darker bar beside the active label.\n- `end tolerance = 2px`\n  Fractional scroll positions on high-DPI screens can stop a pixel short of `scrollHeight`.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction OnThisPageToc(props: { items: TocItem[]; scrollRoot?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; activeId?: string | null; defaultActiveId?: string | null; onActiveChange?: (id: string) => void; title?: ReactNode; offset?: number; activationRatio?: number; focusTarget?: boolean; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype TocItem = { id: string; label: string; depth?: 0 | 1 | 2 }\n```\n\n- `items: TocItem[]`\n  Entries in document order; `id` is the section element id and `depth` indents sub-sections.\n- `scrollRoot: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>` — default `window`\n  Scroll container holding the sections. Becomes the observer root and the element scrolled on click.\n- `activeId / defaultActiveId: string | null` — default `first item`\n  Active section, controlled or initial.\n- `onActiveChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when scrolling or a click changes the active section.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"On this page\"`\n  Heading above the list; pass null to hide it.\n- `offset: number` — default `16`\n  Px between the root top and a section after a jump; also the top of the activation band.\n- `activationRatio: number` — default `0.3`\n  Fraction of the root height, from the top, in which a section counts as active.\n- `focusTarget: boolean` — default `true`\n  Moves focus to the section after a jump (adds `tabindex=\"-1\"` if needed).\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the nav.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Scrollspy: the active section is the last one (in TOC order) inside a band\n// from `offset` px below the root's top down to `activationRatio` of its\n// height, i.e. the section whose heading most recently entered the band.\n// Scrolled to the very end, the last section wins even when it is too short\n// to ever reach the band.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const root = scrollRoot?.current ?? null;\n  const order = ids.split(\"|\").filter(Boolean);\n  const targets = order\n    .map((id) => document.getElementById(id))\n    .filter((el): el is HTMLElement => el !== null);\n  if (targets.length === 0 || typeof IntersectionObserver === \"undefined\") return;\n\n  const inBand = new Set<string>();\n  const atEnd = () =>\n    root\n      ? root.scrollTop + root.clientHeight >= root.scrollHeight - 2\n      : window.scrollY + window.innerHeight >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2;\n  const pick = () => {\n    if (lockRef.current) return;\n    const last = order[order.length - 1];\n    const scrolled = (root ? root.scrollTop : window.scrollY) > 0;\n    if (last && scrolled && atEnd()) return commit(last);\n    let current: string | undefined;\n    for (const id of order) if (inBand.has(id)) current = id;\n    if (current) commit(current);\n  };\n\n  const bottomInset = Math.round((1 - activationRatio) * 100);\n  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(\n    (entries) => {\n      for (const entry of entries) {\n        if (entry.isIntersecting) inBand.add(entry.target.id);\n        else inBand.delete(entry.target.id);\n      }\n      pick();\n    },\n    { root, rootMargin: `-${offset}px 0px -${bottomInset}% 0px`, threshold: 0 },\n  );\n  for (const target of targets) observer.observe(target);\n\n  let wasAtEnd = atEnd();\n  const scroller: HTMLElement | Window = root ?? window;\n  const onScroll = () => {\n    const end = atEnd();\n    if (end !== wasAtEnd) {\n      wasAtEnd = end;\n      pick();\n    }\n  };\n  scroller.addEventListener(\"scroll\", onScroll, { passive: true });\n\n  return () => {\n    observer.disconnect();\n    scroller.removeEventListener(\"scroll\", onScroll);\n  };\n}, [ids, scrollRoot, offset, activationRatio, commit]);\n\nconst jumpTo = (event: ReactMouseEvent<HTMLAnchorElement>, id: string) => {\n  const target = document.getElementById(id);\n  if (!target) return;\n  event.preventDefault();\n  const root = scrollRoot?.current ?? null;\n  const behavior: ScrollBehavior = reduced ? \"auto\" : \"smooth\";\n\n  // Hold the clicked item while the smooth scroll passes other sections.\n  releaseRef.current?.();\n  lockRef.current = id;\n  commit(id);\n  const scroller: HTMLElement | Window = root ?? window;\n  const release = () => {\n    lockRef.current = null;\n    scroller.removeEventListener(\"scrollend\", release);\n    window.clearTimeout(timer);\n    releaseRef.current = null;\n  };\n  const timer = window.setTimeout(release, 900);\n  scroller.addEventListener(\"scrollend\", release);\n  releaseRef.current = release;\n\n  if (root) {\n    const top = root.scrollTop + target.getBoundingClientRect().top - root.getBoundingClientRect().top - offset;\n    root.scrollTo({ top, behavior });\n  } else {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: window.scrollY + target.getBoundingClientRect().top - offset, behavior });\n  }\n  if (focusTarget) {\n    if (!target.hasAttribute(\"tabindex\")) target.setAttribute(\"tabindex\", \"-1\");\n    target.focus({ preventScroll: true });\n  }\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The IntersectionObserver is disconnected and the scroll listener removed when the component unmounts or its items, root, offset or ratio change. A pending click lock removes its `scrollend` listener and clears its timeout on release and on unmount. The ResizeObserver lives in `useResizeObserver` and disconnects itself.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With reduced motion the click jump uses `behavior: \"auto\"` (an instant scroll) and the bar and label colour transitions carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so the indicator moves without sliding.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Observing with the default viewport root while the sections live in an inner scroll container. The sections never leave the viewport, so the TOC never changes; pass the container as `root`.\n- Picking the entry with the largest `intersectionRatio`. Tall sections have tiny ratios, so the TOC jumps to short neighbours; track which ids are in the band and choose the last one in document order instead.\n- Letting the observer drive the TOC during a click-initiated smooth scroll. The bar runs through every section in between; lock it to the clicked id until the scroll ends.\n- Using `scrollIntoView` for the jump. It scrolls every ancestor, so the page under an embedded article moves too.\n- Forgetting the end-of-scroll rule. A final section shorter than the band never becomes active and the last TOC item is unreachable by scrolling.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Section ids in the demo are prefixed with `useId` so two copies on one page do not share targets.\n- Prefer the section wrapper as the target over its heading: the wrapper stays intersecting while its body is read.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPreview is active on load with the bar beside it. Scrolling the article moves the bar to Installation, Usage and so on; scrolling to the bottom activates Accessibility. Clicking Tokens scrolls the article so Tokens sits near the top, the Tokens link gets `aria-current=\"location\"`, and the page around the demo does not move.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "orbit-ring-points": "# Build: Single-Draw Orbit Rings\n\nFive concentric orbits of static path dots, each carrying one brighter dot that travels it. Every dot in the system is a vertex of one `THREE.Points` object, so ring count and dot count cost buffer memory but never a second draw call. The outer rings are dimmer than the inner ones, which reads as depth even though the camera is orthographic and nothing is actually further away.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nConcentric rings of dots, one bright marker travelling each ring, all of it in a single draw call.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each dot packs its whole identity into one attribute, `aPointData = vec4(kind, ringIndex, angle, dotRadius)`, where `kind` is 0 for a path dot and 1 for the orbiting marker. `position` is zeroed for every vertex and carries no information at all.\n2. The vertex shader derives the ring radius as `uBaseRadius * scale * uExtent * 0.5` with `scale = 1.0 - (uRingCount - 1.0 - ringIndex) * 0.1`, so rings step 10% apart with the outermost at full radius, and `uExtent = min(width, height)` keeps the structure inside a preview of any aspect.\n3. Path dots sit at `vec2(cos(angle), sin(angle)) * ringRadius`; markers use `angle - PI * 0.5 + uElapsed * uSpeed` instead, so the phase offset baked into the attribute becomes a starting position and the clock does the travelling.\n4. Depth is faked with a focal length that no camera uses: `alpha = 0.15 + 0.65 * FOCAL_LENGTH / (FOCAL_LENGTH + ringRadius)` dims a ring in proportion to its size, so wider rings recede.\n5. `gl_PointSize` is clamped to a 2px floor and the fragment alpha is then divided by the area that clamping invented — `vAlphaScale = unclamped² / clamped²` — so a dot forced twice as wide comes out four times fainter and the field looks identical at 1x and 3x device pixel ratio.\n6. The fragment shader turns each square point sprite into a disc with `1.0 - smoothstep(0.43, 0.5, length(gl_PointCoord - 0.5))` and discards anything fully transparent, so the renderer is created with `antialias: false`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `RING_STEP = 0.1`\n  10% radius difference between adjacent rings. Below ~0.06 the rings visually merge into a band at preview size; above ~0.15 the innermost ring collapses to a dot when ringCount is 5.\n- `FOCAL_LENGTH = 800.0`\n  Not a camera property — the denominator of the alpha falloff. At 800 the outermost ring lands about 30% dimmer than the innermost at typical preview sizes: enough to read as depth, not enough to read as a fade-out. At 200 the outer rings vanish.\n- `MIN_POINT_SIZE = 2.0`\n  Hardware floor. Below roughly 1px most GPUs rasterise nothing at all, so a 1px dot at DPR 1 would simply be missing on some machines rather than faint.\n- `UNCLAMPED_MIN_POINT_SIZE = 1.0`\n  The size the dot *would* have had. It is the numerator of the area correction — without a separate constant there is nothing to compare the clamped size against and the compensation cannot be computed.\n- `disc edge = smoothstep(0.43, 0.5, d)`\n  A soft band 7% of the point radius wide. Narrower and a 2px dot aliases into a square; wider and every dot looks blurred rather than small.\n- `baseRadius = 0.75`\n  Outermost ring diameter as a fraction of the container short side. At 1.0 the ring touches the edge and the marker clips as it passes; 0.75 leaves a margin the marker can travel through.\n- `dotsPerRing = 170`\n  Dense enough that the path reads as a continuous line at 320px wide, sparse enough that the innermost ring is not a solid filled circle.\n- `speed = 0.3`\n  Radians per second — one lap every ~21s. Linear, no easing: a constant angular rate is what makes it read as a mechanism rather than an animation.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction OrbitRingPoints(props: { ringCount?: number; dotsPerRing?: number; speed?: number; baseRadius?: number; dotRadius?: number; markerRadius?: number; opacity?: number; color?: string; markerColor?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `ringCount: number` — default `5`\n  Concentric rings. Each gets exactly one travelling marker.\n- `dotsPerRing: number` — default `170`\n  Static path dots per ring.\n- `speed: number` — default `0.3`\n  Marker angular speed in radians per second.\n- `baseRadius: number` — default `0.75`\n  Outermost ring diameter as a fraction of the container short side.\n- `dotRadius: number` — default `1`\n  Path dot radius in CSS pixels, before pixel-ratio scaling.\n- `markerRadius: number` — default `3`\n  Marker radius in CSS pixels.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied to every dot in the vertex shader.\n- `color: string` — default `'#8a8f98'`\n  Path dot colour, any value `THREE.Color` accepts.\n- `markerColor: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Travelling marker colour.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'orbit-ring-points'`\n  Seeds the angular scatter, so the ring is identical on server and client.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function OrbitRingPoints({\n  ringCount = 5,\n  dotsPerRing = 170,\n  speed = 0.3,\n  baseRadius = 0.75,\n  dotRadius = 1,\n  markerRadius = 3,\n  opacity = 1,\n  color = '#8a8f98',\n  markerColor = '#ffffff',\n  seed = 'orbit-ring-points',\n  className,\n}: OrbitRingPointsProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // `antialias: false` is deliberate: the fragment shader anti-aliases each\n    // disc itself with a smoothstep, so MSAA would pay for a whole extra\n    // sample buffer to solve a problem that is already solved.\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    // Orthographic and 1:1 with CSS pixels, so `gl_PointSize` in the shader is\n    // a real pixel count and the ring radii are real pixel radii.\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0.1, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const perRing = dotsPerRing + 1; // path dots + one marker\n    const total = ringCount * perRing;\n\n    const positions = new Float32Array(total * 3); // stays all zeros\n    const pointData = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n\n    let at = 0;\n    for (let ring = 0; ring < ringCount; ring += 1) {\n      // Sorting the angles is not cosmetic. Points are drawn in buffer order,\n      // and an unsorted ring interleaves near and far dots so the alpha blend\n      // stipples along the path instead of reading as one continuous line.\n      const angles = new Float32Array(dotsPerRing);\n      for (let i = 0; i < dotsPerRing; i += 1) angles[i] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      angles.sort();\n\n      for (let i = 0; i < dotsPerRing; i += 1) {\n        pointData[at] = 0;\n        pointData[at + 1] = ring;\n        pointData[at + 2] = angles[i] ?? 0;\n        pointData[at + 3] = dotRadius;\n        at += 4;\n      }\n\n      pointData[at] = 1;\n      pointData[at + 1] = ring;\n      pointData[at + 2] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      pointData[at + 3] = markerRadius;\n      at += 4;\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aPointData', new THREE.BufferAttribute(pointData, 4));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uExtent: { value: 1 },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uRingCount: { value: ringCount },\n      uSpeed: { value: speed },\n      uBaseRadius: { value: baseRadius },\n      uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(color) },\n      uMarkerColor: { value: new THREE.Color(markerColor) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: ORBIT_RING_POINTS_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: ORBIT_RING_POINTS_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    // Every vertex sits at the origin, so three computes a bounding sphere of\n    // radius zero and culls the entire object the moment the camera is not\n    // looking straight at (0,0,0). Displacement happens in the shader, which\n    // the culler cannot see.\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.left = -w / 2;\n      camera.right = w / 2;\n      camera.top = h / 2;\n      camera.bottom = -h / 2;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n      // Ring radii scale off the SHORT side. Off the height alone, a wide\n      // preview leaves the rings floating in a sea of empty space; off the\n      // width alone, a tall one clips them.\n      uniforms.uExtent.value = Math.min(w, h);\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, uniforms, resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      // Order matters: release the scene graph's GPU objects while the\n      // context is still alive, then hand the context back.\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    ringCount,\n    dotsPerRing,\n    speed,\n    baseRadius,\n    dotRadius,\n    markerRadius,\n    opacity,\n    color,\n    markerColor,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect so a container reflow costs a `setSize` call\n  // rather than a full teardown and rebuild of the buffer.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed), ready && !reduced && !unsupported);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Clears the stage ref first so no queued frame can touch a dead renderer, then `disposeObject(scene)` for the geometry, `material.dispose()`, `disposeRenderer(renderer)` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + zeroing the drawing buffer, and finally removes the canvas from the DOM. The rAF subscription is released by `useRaf` on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF subscription is never opened. One frame is drawn at `uElapsed = 0`, which parks every marker at twelve o'clock on its ring, so the full structure is visible and only the orbiting stops. Resizing still redraws that frame.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` at its default. Every vertex sits at the origin, so three computes a bounding sphere of radius zero; the displacement is invisible to the culler and the entire object disappears the moment the camera is not pointed exactly at (0,0,0).\n- Not sorting the angles within a ring before writing them into the buffer. Points are blended in buffer order with depth testing off, so an unsorted ring stipples — the path visibly breaks into a dotted texture instead of reading as one line.\n- Clamping `gl_PointSize` without correcting the alpha. The clamp is invisible at DPR 3 and doubles the covered area at DPR 1, so the same component looks twice as bright on a cheap monitor as on a retina laptop.\n- Turning on `antialias: true` to smooth the dots. The fragment shader already anti-aliases each disc; MSAA then allocates a multisample buffer the size of the canvas to solve nothing.\n- Rebuilding the renderer on every resize. The buffer is size-independent — only the camera frustum and `uExtent` change — so a container reflow should cost a `setSize` call, not a teardown that drops and re-acquires a GL context.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` and stopping there. It frees GPU objects but leaves the context alive; `disposeRenderer` also calls `forceContextLoss`, without which a grid of previews silently blacks out the oldest one at around sixteen contexts.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The camera is orthographic and spans the canvas 1:1 in CSS pixels, so `gl_PointSize` is a real pixel count and ring radii are real pixel radii. That is what makes the pixel-ratio compensation expressible at all.\n- Ring count and dots per ring are both free at draw time. A hundred rings is one draw call and one buffer upload.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFive rings of dots with one brighter dot travelling each, all at the same angular rate, the outer rings visibly dimmer than the inner ones. Resize the container and the rings rescale without flicker or a dropped context. Zoom the browser to 200% and the dots stay the same apparent brightness rather than getting denser. With reduced motion on, the rings render fully and the markers sit still at the top.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "orders-chart-card": "# Build: Orders Chart Card\n\nA 344px dashboard card that charts twelve months of orders as grouped Recharts bars, last year in neutral grey beside this year in indigo. The header shows the year total with a delta chip and the year-earlier figure. Hovering a month swaps all three for that month, and the number counts to its new value. It shares its frame and header with the revenue chart card and the stat cards, so it drops into the same dashboard row.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dashboard card of monthly orders as paired bars, this year beside last, whose count-up headline follows the hovered month.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One piece of state, `activeIndex`, drives the header. `BarChart`'s `onMouseMove` reads `state.activeTooltipIndex`, and when `isTooltipActive` is true and the index is finite it stores it. `onMouseLeave` resets it to `null`. A `<Tooltip content={() => null}>` keeps Recharts tracking the hovered category and drawing its cursor band (`fill: var(--color-chart-track)`, opacity 0.5) without rendering a tooltip box.\n2. With no month hovered the headline is the sum of `current` over `data` and the comparison is the sum of `previous`. With a month hovered they are that point's two values, and the label becomes the full month name looked up from the short `label` through `MONTHS` and `MONTHS_FULL` (it falls back to the raw label).\n3. `describeDelta` turns the pair into a chip. A zero `previous` gives \"New\" in neutral. Otherwise the percentage change is rounded to one decimal: 0 gives \"0%\" in neutral, a rise gives \"+x%\" in lime and a fall \"-x%\" in rose.\n4. The figure is passed through `useCountUp(headlineValue)`, which eases from the shown value to the new one over 320ms with an ease-out cubic on `requestAnimationFrame`. The `<p>` holding it is keyed by `activeIndex ?? \"total\"`, so each change remounts it and replays `.animate-number-fade` (220ms, opacity 0.35 to 1). The digits are `tabular-nums` and formatted with `toLocaleString(\"en-US\")`.\n5. The chart fills the rest of the card through `ResponsiveContainer` at 100% by 100% inside a `min-h-0 flex-1` box. `barCategoryGap=\"28%\"` and `barGap={3}` keep each month's pair together. The Y axis is fixed at 40px wide with `domain={[0, yMax * 1.1]}`, four ticks and `formatK` labels (\"2.5k\"). The X axis uses `interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"`, so narrow cards drop middle months but keep January and December.\n6. Both bars use `radius={[4, 4, 0, 0]}` and grow in over 450ms on mount. Colours are CSS variables (`--color-chart-neutral`, `--color-chart-9-active`) passed to Recharts as `fill`, so the chart follows the light and dark theme without re-rendering.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card height = h-[344px], gap-6, px-4 pt-4 pb-3`\n  Matches the revenue chart card and the stat-card rows, so chart cards placed side by side line up. The chart takes whatever height the header leaves.\n- `y domain = [0, yMax * 1.1]`\n  The 10% headroom keeps the tallest bar off the top edge. The axis starts at 0 because a bar's length is the value, and a cut axis would exaggerate the gap between years.\n- `bar spacing = barCategoryGap 28%, barGap 3`\n  A 3px gap reads the two bars as one pair, and the 28% gap between months separates pairs clearly, so twelve pairs still fit at about 560px.\n- `count-up and fade = useCountUp 320ms ease-out cubic, number-fade 220ms`\n  Fast enough to keep up with a pointer sweeping across months. The fade restarts on every change and covers the first frames of the count, so the swap never looks like a jump.\n- `bar entrance = animationDuration 450`\n  Long enough to show the bars growing on first paint, short enough to finish before a user reaches for the chart.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction OrdersChartCard(props?: { data?: OrdersPoint[]; title?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype OrdersPoint = { label: string; current: number; previous: number }\nconst ORDERS_DATA: OrdersPoint[]\n```\n\n- `data: OrdersPoint[]` — default `ORDERS_DATA`\n  Twelve points, one per month, each with this year's and last year's orders.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Orders\"`\n  Header label shown while no month is hovered.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section, for example to set a width.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function OrdersChartCard({\n  data = ORDERS_DATA,\n  title = \"Orders\",\n  className,\n}: {\n  /** Twelve points, one per month; defaults to the demo year. */\n  data?: OrdersPoint[];\n  /** Headline label when no month is hovered. */\n  title?: string;\n  className?: string;\n} = {}) {\n  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState<number | null>(null);\n\n  const totalCurrent = data.reduce((sum, point) => sum + point.current, 0);\n  const totalPrevious = data.reduce((sum, point) => sum + point.previous, 0);\n  const hovering = activeIndex !== null && activeIndex < data.length;\n  const point = hovering ? data[activeIndex] : null;\n\n  const headlineValue = point ? point.current : totalCurrent;\n  const comparison = point ? point.previous : totalPrevious;\n  const delta = describeDelta(headlineValue, comparison);\n  const monthIndex = point ? MONTHS.indexOf(point.label) : -1;\n  const label = point ? (monthIndex >= 0 ? MONTHS_FULL[monthIndex] : point.label) : title;\n  const display = useCountUp(headlineValue);\n\n  const yMax = Math.max(...data.map((d) => Math.max(d.current, d.previous)));\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex h-[344px] min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-6 rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default px-4 pt-4 pb-3\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {/* Header: label over the count-up figure and its delta; legend on the right */}\n      <div className=\"flex w-full flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between\">\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          <p className=\"w-full text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">{label}</p>\n          <div className=\"flex w-full items-center gap-2\">\n            <p\n              key={activeIndex ?? \"total\"}\n              className=\"animate-number-fade text-title-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums\"\n            >\n              {display.toLocaleString(\"en-US\")}\n            </p>\n            <Chip variant=\"bold\" color={delta.color}>\n              {delta.label}\n            </Chip>\n          </div>\n          <p className=\"text-body-2-medium text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n            {comparison.toLocaleString(\"en-US\")} {point ? \"a year earlier\" : \"last year\"}\n          </p>\n        </div>\n        <dl className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-4 text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary\">\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n            <span className=\"size-2 rounded-full bg-chart-9-active\" aria-hidden />\n            <dt>This year</dt>\n          </div>\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n            <span className=\"size-2 rounded-full bg-chart-neutral\" aria-hidden />\n            <dt>Last year</dt>\n          </div>\n        </dl>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Chart */}\n      <div className=\"min-h-0 w-full flex-1\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n          <BarChart\n            data={data}\n            margin={{ top: 4, right: 6, bottom: 0, left: 0 }}\n            barCategoryGap=\"28%\"\n            barGap={3}\n            onMouseMove={(state) => {\n              const index = Number(state?.activeTooltipIndex);\n              if (state?.isTooltipActive && Number.isFinite(index)) setActiveIndex(index);\n            }}\n            onMouseLeave={() => setActiveIndex(null)}\n          >\n            <YAxis\n              width={40}\n              domain={[0, yMax * 1.1]}\n              tickCount={4}\n              tickFormatter={formatK}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={{ fontSize: 12, fill: \"var(--color-text-tertiary)\" }}\n            />\n            <XAxis\n              dataKey=\"label\"\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tickMargin={12}\n              interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"\n              tick={{ fontSize: 13, fill: \"var(--color-text-tertiary)\" }}\n            />\n            <Tooltip content={() => null} cursor={{ fill: \"var(--color-chart-track)\", opacity: 0.5 }} />\n            <Bar\n              dataKey=\"previous\"\n              fill=\"var(--color-chart-neutral)\"\n              radius={[4, 4, 0, 0]}\n              isAnimationActive\n              animationDuration={450}\n            />\n            <Bar\n              dataKey=\"current\"\n              fill=\"var(--color-chart-9-active)\"\n              radius={[4, 4, 0, 0]}\n              isAnimationActive\n              animationDuration={450}\n            />\n          </BarChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its pending animation frame when the value changes and on unmount. `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer when the card unmounts. The card adds no listeners of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** There is no reduced-motion branch. The bars grow in over 450ms on mount, the headline counts over 320ms through `useCountUp` and the number fades in over 220ms on each hover change. All three are short and none moves the layout, but they play regardless of the user's setting.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Dropping the `<Tooltip>`. Without it Recharts does not track the active category, `activeTooltipIndex` never arrives in `onMouseMove`, and the headline never follows the pointer. It renders `content={() => null}` on purpose.\n- Putting the card in a parent with no height. The chart is `ResponsiveContainer height=\"100%\"` inside a flex child, and the card sets `h-[344px]` itself. Overriding that with `h-auto` collapses the chart to zero height.\n- Passing more or fewer than twelve points without matching labels. The month names come from looking up `label` in `MONTHS`; a label like \"Q1\" still works but shows as \"Q1\" in the header.\n- Hard-coding hex colours into the `Bar` fills. They are theme variables, so the chart follows the theme with no re-render.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `ORDERS_DATA` and `OrdersPoint` are exported. The default data adds up to 25,162 orders against 20,968 the year before, which the chip shows as \"+20%\".\n- Recharts is forced to `direction: ltr` and its focus outlines are hidden by the theme stylesheet, so the chart reads left to right in an RTL page while the header mirrors.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card is 344px tall with \"Orders\", \"25,162\" (shown at once, with no count on mount), a lime \"+20%\" chip and \"20,968 last year\" in the header, and \"This year\" and \"Last year\" legend dots at the end. Twelve pairs of rounded bars grow in. Hovering a month bands it, the label becomes the full month name, and the number counts to that month's orders with its own delta. Leaving the chart returns to the total.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "page-reveal-overlay": "# Build: Key-Replayed Page Reveal\n\nTwo opaque layers and one CSS keyframe do the whole job of a route transition. The inbound layer carries `key={revealKey}`, so a route change unmounts it and mounts a new one — and a freshly mounted element runs its animation from frame zero, which replays the fade with no state, no library and nothing to reset. The outbound layer fades up to opaque and the navigation commits at 80% of that, while the screen is already covered, so the two halves hand off invisibly without ever referring to each other.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn opaque overlay that fades away on every route change, replayed by nothing more than a changed React key.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The inbound layer is `<div key={revealKey} className=\"kinetic-reveal-in\" />`. Its animation runs `from { opacity: 1 }` to `to { opacity: 0; visibility: hidden }` over 0.55 s on `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)` with `forwards`. Changing `revealKey` is the entire replay mechanism: React sees a different key, discards the node, mounts a new one, and the new node starts its animation at frame zero.\n2. `visibility: hidden` in the `to` frame, held by `forwards`, is what retires the layer. At `opacity: 0` alone a full-viewport opaque surface stays composited for the rest of the page's life; `visibility` removes it from the compositor without unmounting anything.\n3. `usePageCover(coverDuration)` returns `{ covering, cover }`. `cover(commit)` sets `covering` true — which flips `data-covering` on the outbound layer and runs `opacity: 0 -> 1` over `coverDuration` on `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1)` — then, after `coverDuration * 0.8` seconds, calls `setCovering(false)` and `commit()` in the same tick.\n4. Both calls in one tick matters: React batches them into a single commit, so the cover drops to transparent on the exact frame the re-keyed inbound layer mounts at opacity 1. Split them across two ticks and there is one frame of bare destination content visible between the two halves.\n5. The transition property is declared on the `[data-covering=\"true\"]` rule only, never on the base rule, so release is a hard cut rather than a fade. The inbound layer is opaque and stacked above the cover at that instant, so the cut is invisible — and a symmetric fade there would compound two opacities and hold the screen dark past the end of the reveal.\n6. The stylesheet is emitted through React 19's `<style href precedence>` hoisting, which dedupes by `href`. Per-instance values — durations, colour, z-index — ride in CSS custom properties set on the root, because there is only ever one copy of the rule block.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `revealDuration = 0.55`\n  Seconds for the inbound fade. Under ~0.35 s the reveal reads as a flicker of the wrong colour rather than a curtain lifting; over ~0.8 s the destination is legible through a haze for long enough that people start clicking through it.\n- `PAGE_COVER_DURATION = 0.32`\n  Seconds for the outbound cover. Deliberately about half the reveal: leaving should feel decisive and arriving should feel gradual. Exported as a constant so the hook that times the commit and the layer that runs the transition cannot be handed two different numbers.\n- `COMMIT_FRACTION = 0.8`\n  Fraction of the cover the commit waits for. On `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1)` the layer is roughly 96% opaque at 80% of the way through, so the step to fully covered is imperceptible while a fifth of the perceived navigation latency disappears. At 1.0 every click pays a full 0.32 s of dead time; below ~0.6 the destination is visibly swapped behind a translucent sheet.\n- `cover ease = cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1)`\n  Ease-in, no ease-out. The cover must be at its steepest as it approaches opaque, which is what makes 80% look like 100%. An ease-out cover crawls through its last 20% and the early commit becomes visible.\n- `zIndex = 195`\n  Above a custom cursor (150) and a scroll progress bar (120) — both of which must be hidden during a transition — and below a preloader (200), which must be able to sit over a reveal on first load.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PageRevealOverlay(props: { revealKey: string; revealDuration?: number; coverDuration?: number; covering?: boolean; color?: string; zIndex?: number; position?: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction usePageCover(coverDuration?: number): { covering: boolean; cover: (commit: () => void) => void }\n```\n\n- `revealKey: string`\n  Identity of the current page. A change replays the reveal. `usePathname()` in Next.js.\n- `revealDuration: number` — default `0.55`\n  Seconds the inbound cover takes to fade away.\n- `coverDuration: number` — default `0.32`\n  Seconds the outbound cover takes to reach opaque. Pass the same value to `usePageCover`.\n- `covering: boolean` — default `false`\n  Drive from `usePageCover().covering`.\n- `color: string` — default `'#000'`\n  Any CSS colour. Must be opaque, or the page shows through the handoff.\n- `zIndex: number` — default `195`\n  Above the site and any custom cursor, below a preloader.\n- `position: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"` — default `\"fixed\"`\n  `absolute` inside a bounded container; `fixed` for a real page transition.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Seconds the outbound cover takes to reach opaque. Shared default so the hook\n * that times the commit and the layer that runs the transition cannot be given\n * two different numbers.\n */\nexport const PAGE_COVER_DURATION = 0.32;\n\n/**\n * Fraction of the cover the commit waits for. On the `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1,\n * 1)` cover, 80% of the way through the screen is about 96% opaque, so the\n * step to fully covered is imperceptible — and a fifth of the perceived\n * navigation latency is gone. Waiting for 100% is a fifth of a second of dead\n * time on every single click.\n */\nconst COMMIT_FRACTION = 0.8;\n\nconst REVEAL_CSS = `\n@keyframes kinetic-page-reveal {\n  from { opacity: 1; }\n  to   { opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; }\n}\n.kinetic-reveal-root {\n  /* Never eats a click, not even mid-fade. The layer is opaque and covers the\n     whole viewport; one swallowed click on a nav item is a dead site. */\n  pointer-events: none;\n  z-index: var(--kx-reveal-z, 195);\n}\n.kinetic-reveal-layer {\n  position: absolute;\n  inset: 0;\n  background: var(--kx-reveal-color, #000);\n}\n/* Inbound. Starts opaque because it is mounted at the moment the destination\n   is mounted, and holds the end frame with 'forwards' so 'visibility: hidden'\n   retires the layer from compositing instead of leaving an invisible\n   full-screen surface alive for the rest of the page's life. */\n.kinetic-reveal-in {\n  opacity: 1;\n  animation: var(--kx-reveal-duration, 0.55s) cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)\n    forwards kinetic-page-reveal;\n}\n/* Outbound. Idle is hidden as well as transparent, for the same reason. */\n.kinetic-reveal-cover {\n  opacity: 0;\n  visibility: hidden;\n}\n/* The transition is declared on the covering state ONLY, so release is a cut.\n   That is deliberate: at release the re-keyed inbound layer is already sitting\n   at opacity 1 directly above this one, so a second fade here would compound\n   two opacities and hold the screen dark past the end of the reveal. */\n.kinetic-reveal-cover[data-covering='true'] {\n  opacity: 1;\n  visibility: visible;\n  transition: opacity var(--kx-cover-duration, 0.32s)\n    cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1);\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  /* Not 'animation: none' — that would leave the inbound layer parked at its\n     opaque from-frame, permanently covering the site. The layers are removed\n     entirely, and 'usePageCover' commits synchronously, so navigation is a\n     hard cut to the destination with no flash of overlay in either direction. */\n  .kinetic-reveal-layer { display: none; }\n}\n`;\n\nexport interface PageRevealOverlayProps {\n  /**\n   * Identity of the current page. A change replays the reveal. In Next.js App\n   * Router this is `usePathname()`; in the demo it is a local state value.\n   */\n  revealKey: string;\n  /** Seconds the inbound cover takes to fade away. */\n  revealDuration?: number;\n  /** Seconds the outbound cover takes to reach opaque. */\n  coverDuration?: number;\n  /** Drive from `usePageCover().covering`. */\n  covering?: boolean;\n  /** Any CSS colour. Must be opaque, or the page shows through the handoff. */\n  color?: string;\n  /** Above the site, below a preloader. */\n  zIndex?: number;\n  /** `absolute` to demo inside a bounded box; `fixed` for a real page. */\n  position?: 'fixed' | 'absolute';\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function PageRevealOverlay({\n  revealKey,\n  revealDuration = 0.55,\n  coverDuration = PAGE_COVER_DURATION,\n  covering = false,\n  color = '#000',\n  zIndex = 195,\n  position = 'fixed',\n  className,\n}: PageRevealOverlayProps) {\n  return (\n    <>\n      {/* React 19 hoists and dedupes by `href`, so a hundred navigations and\n          any number of instances still emit exactly one rule block. Per\n          instance values ride in custom properties for that reason. */}\n      <style href=\"kinetic-page-reveal-overlay\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {REVEAL_CSS}\n      </style>\n\n      <div\n        aria-hidden\n        className={cn(\n          'kinetic-reveal-root',\n          position === 'fixed' ? 'fixed inset-0' : 'absolute inset-0',\n          className,\n        )}\n        style={\n          {\n            '--kx-reveal-duration': `${revealDuration}s`,\n            '--kx-cover-duration': `${coverDuration}s`,\n            '--kx-reveal-color': color,\n            '--kx-reveal-z': zIndex,\n          } as CSSProperties\n        }\n      >\n        {/* Cover first, reveal second: later in the DOM paints on top, and the\n            inbound layer's opaque first frame is what hides the cover's cut. */}\n        <div\n          className=\"kinetic-reveal-layer kinetic-reveal-cover\"\n          data-covering={covering ? 'true' : 'false'}\n        />\n        <div key={revealKey} className=\"kinetic-reveal-layer kinetic-reveal-in\" />\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface PageCover {\n  /** Feed to `PageRevealOverlay`. */\n  covering: boolean;\n  /** Raise the cover, then run `commit` — your navigation — under it. */\n  cover: (commit: () => void) => void;\n}\n\n/**\n * The outbound half. `commit` is a callback rather than a router call so\n * nothing here depends on a routing library.\n */\nexport function usePageCover(coverDuration = PAGE_COVER_DURATION): PageCover {\n  const [covering, setCovering] = useState(false);\n  const timer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      if (timer.current !== null) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n      timer.current = null;\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n\n  const cover = useCallback(\n    (commit: () => void) => {\n      // Reduced motion: no cover at all, and the commit is synchronous. A\n      // zero-duration cover would still be one frame of opaque colour, which\n      // is a flash — the single most unpleasant thing to show someone who has\n      // asked for less motion.\n      if (reduced) {\n        commit();\n        return;\n      }\n      // A second click while a cover is already up would schedule a second\n      // commit and navigate twice. The live timer is the lock.\n      if (timer.current !== null) return;\n\n      setCovering(true);\n      timer.current = setTimeout(\n        () => {\n          timer.current = null;\n          // Both in one tick, so React batches them into a single commit: the\n          // cover drops to transparent on the exact frame the re-keyed reveal\n          // layer mounts at opacity 1. Split across two ticks and there is one\n          // frame of bare destination content between them.\n          setCovering(false);\n          commit();\n        },\n        coverDuration * COMMIT_FRACTION * 1000,\n      );\n    },\n    [coverDuration, reduced],\n  );\n\n  return { covering, cover };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The only resource is the commit timer, cleared in an unmount effect and nulled so the lock cannot survive a remount. There are no listeners, no rAF and no tweens; the animations are CSS and die with the nodes React removes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Both layers get `display: none`, and `usePageCover` calls the commit synchronously rather than starting a timer. Navigation is a hard cut straight to the destination — no cover, no reveal, and crucially not a zero-duration overlay, which would still be one frame of opaque colour.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating the overlay with a tween and a timeline. There is nothing to tween: the whole reason this is bulletproof is that a remount restarts a CSS animation, so there is no in-flight animation to kill, no state to reset, and a double navigation cannot leave the overlay half-faded.\n- Using `animation: none` for reduced motion. That leaves the inbound layer parked on its opaque `from` frame, permanently covering the site — the failure looks like a blank page. The layers get `display: none` and `usePageCover` commits synchronously instead.\n- Committing the navigation after the cover finishes. A fifth of a second of nothing happening after a click is exactly where a visitor decides the link is broken and clicks again.\n- Omitting `pointer-events: none`. A full-viewport opaque div swallows the click on whatever the visitor aims at next, and mid-transition that is usually another nav item.\n- Letting `cover()` run while a cover is already up. Without the live-timer lock a fast double click schedules two commits and navigates twice, so the visitor lands one page past where they aimed.\n- Leaving the finished inbound layer at `opacity: 0` without `visibility: hidden`. It is still a composited full-viewport surface, and on a page that also runs a WebGL hero it costs a measurable slice of fill rate for the rest of the session.\n- Fading the cover back out symmetrically. Two opaque layers both mid-fade multiply, not add: the screen stays darker for longer and the reveal appears to stall before it starts.\n\n## Notes\n\n- In Next.js App Router: mount once in `app/layout.tsx` with `revealKey={usePathname()}` and `position=\"fixed\"`, then replace nav `<Link>` elements with buttons calling `cover(() => router.push(href))`. Nothing in this file imports `next/navigation` — the commit is a callback so the same component works under React Router or a plain state switch.\n- The overlay colour must be opaque. A translucent colour makes both halves show the wrong page through the handoff, which is the one artefact this technique exists to prevent.\n- The root carries `aria-hidden`: it is a purely decorative surface, and a screen reader announcing it on every navigation would be worse than no transition at all.\n- Browser back and forward change the key without going through `cover()`, so they get the reveal but not the cover. That is correct — a history navigation has already happened by the time your code hears about it, so there is nothing left to cover.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick a nav item: the panel fades to the overlay colour, the destination content swaps while it is covered, and the overlay fades away over roughly half a second. The heading is never visible in an intermediate state, and there is no frame where old and new content are both on screen. Click two nav items in quick succession and you land on the second one, not past it. With reduced motion the content swaps instantly with no coloured frame in either direction.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pagination": "# Build: Pagination\n\nA controlled page navigator: secondary small `Button`s for Previous and Next at either end, and a row of 32px page cells between them that collapses long ranges to first, last, a window around the current page, and \"…\" placeholders. The active page borrows the raised secondary-button surface; the rest are ghost cells. A `ResizeObserver` on the nav switches it to an icon-only, zero-sibling layout when its own container is narrow, so it never clips.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nControlled pager with Previous/Next buttons, 32px page cells and ellipsis collapsing that turns compact below a 420px container.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `paginationRange(current, total, sibling)` builds the item list. If `sibling * 2 + 5` (first, last, current, the siblings on both sides and two dots) is at least `total`, every page is shown. Otherwise it computes `leftSibling = max(current - sibling, 1)` and `rightSibling = min(current + sibling, total)`, and shows left dots only when `leftSibling > 2` and right dots only when `rightSibling < total - 2`.\n2. The three collapsed shapes keep the row a constant length: near the start it is `1…(3 + 2·sibling), …, total`; near the end `1, …, (total − 2 − 2·sibling)…total`; in the middle `1, …, leftSibling…rightSibling, …, total`. With the default `siblingCount` of 1 this is always 7 items, so the Next button does not jump sideways as you page.\n3. `useIsCompact` returns a callback ref for the `<nav>`. When the nav mounts it measures `offsetWidth` at once (during commit, so the first paint already has the right layout), then observes the border box with a `ResizeObserver`; `isCompact` is `width < 420`, and a width of 0 (hidden, not laid out) is ignored so the last layout stays. The ref's cleanup disconnects the observer when the nav unmounts. In compact mode both arrow buttons become `iconOnly` with the label moved to `aria-label`, and `paginationRange` is called with sibling 0, which trims the row to 5 items.\n4. The nav is `flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-2`, so Previous sits flush left, Next flush right and the `<ul>` of cells (`gap-0.5`) floats between them. Previous is disabled on page 1 and Next on the last page, and each calls `onChange(page ∓ 1)`.\n5. Each page is a `<button type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Go to page N\">` in a shared `cell` class: `size-8 rounded-lg text-body-medium`, centred. The current page gets `aria-current=\"page\"` and the secondary-button surface (`border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default text-text-primary shadow-xs`). Others are `text-text-secondary` with `hover:bg-background-secondary-hover hover:text-text-primary`. Dots are `aria-hidden` `<li>`s in `text-text-tertiary`.\n6. The cells deliberately have no transition. Animating background, shadow and colour makes the previously active number visibly fade out on every page change, which reads as a flicker.\n7. With `totalPages <= 1` the component returns `null` after its hooks have run. The whole tree passes through `useTemplateCopy()`, which is the identity unless a `TemplateCopyProvider` supplies a translator for the labels.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `COMPACT_WIDTH = 420px`\n  The full-label, one-sibling layout starts clipping somewhere between 380 and 420px depending on how many digits `totalPages` has. Switching at the upper end of that range means it never clips.\n- `cell = size-8 (32×32), rounded-lg, gap-0.5`\n  32px matches the small secondary `Button` height on either side, so the whole row shares one baseline and one height. The 2px gap keeps hover backgrounds from touching.\n- `item count = siblingCount × 2 + 5 (7 at the default of 1)`\n  First, last, current, the siblings and two dot slots. Keeping the count constant across the three collapsed shapes is what stops the row from changing width while paging.\n- `siblingCount default = 1`\n  One neighbour each side is enough to step through pages by clicking without overfilling a card footer. Compact mode forces 0.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Pagination(props: { page: number; totalPages: number; onChange: (page: number) => void; siblingCount?: number; className?: string; previousLabel?: string; nextLabel?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `page: number`\n  Current page, 1-based. Controlled.\n- `totalPages: number`\n  Number of pages. At 1 or fewer the component renders nothing.\n- `onChange: (page: number) => void`\n  Called with the target page from a cell, Previous or Next.\n- `siblingCount: number` — default `1`\n  Pages shown on each side of the current one. Forced to 0 below a 420px container.\n- `previousLabel / nextLabel: string` — default `\"Previous\" / \"Next\"`\n  Button labels, used as `aria-label` in compact mode.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Pagination\"`\n  Accessible name of the `<nav>` landmark.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst DOTS = \"dots\";\n\nfunction range(start: number, end: number): number[] {\n  return Array.from({ length: end - start + 1 }, (_, i) => start + i);\n}\n\nfunction paginationRange(\n  current: number,\n  total: number,\n  sibling: number,\n): (number | typeof DOTS)[] {\n  // first + last + current + 2*sibling + 2 dots\n  const totalPageNumbers = sibling * 2 + 5;\n  if (totalPageNumbers >= total) return range(1, total);\n\n  const leftSibling = Math.max(current - sibling, 1);\n  const rightSibling = Math.min(current + sibling, total);\n  const showLeftDots = leftSibling > 2;\n  const showRightDots = rightSibling < total - 2;\n\n  if (!showLeftDots && showRightDots) {\n    return [...range(1, 3 + 2 * sibling), DOTS, total];\n  }\n  if (showLeftDots && !showRightDots) {\n    return [1, DOTS, ...range(total - (2 + 2 * sibling), total)];\n  }\n  return [1, DOTS, ...range(leftSibling, rightSibling), DOTS, total];\n}\n\n// No transition on the cells: animating background/shadow/color makes the\n// previously-active number visibly fade out (\"flicker\") on every page change.\nconst cell = \"flex size-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-lg text-body-medium\";\n\n/** Below this nav width, Previous/Next drop their labels and siblingCount\n *  clamps to 0 — the full-label, full-sibling layout starts clipping\n *  somewhere around 380–420px depending on totalPages' digit count. */\nconst COMPACT_WIDTH = 420;\n\nfunction useIsCompact() {\n  const [isCompact, setIsCompact] = useState(false);\n\n  // A callback ref, so the observer follows the nav itself: it attaches when\n  // the nav mounts (also when `totalPages` grows past 1 after a `null` render)\n  // and disconnects when the nav unmounts. A mount-only effect would have run\n  // once while the nav was absent and never observed it.\n  const ref = useCallback((el: HTMLElement | null) => {\n    if (!el) return;\n    const measure = (width: number) => {\n      // 0 means hidden or not laid out (display: none, a closed tab or panel).\n      // Keep the last layout instead of flipping to compact, which would then\n      // paint for a frame when the nav is shown again.\n      if (width > 0) setIsCompact(width < COMPACT_WIDTH);\n    };\n    // Measure now, during commit, so the first paint already has the right\n    // layout. The observer's first callback lands after that paint, so on a\n    // narrow container the full labels would flash before switching.\n    measure(el.offsetWidth);\n    // Border box, like offsetWidth, and unaffected by a scaled ancestor.\n    const observer = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {\n      if (entry) measure(entry.borderBoxSize?.[0]?.inlineSize ?? entry.contentRect.width);\n    });\n    observer.observe(el, { box: \"border-box\" });\n    return () => observer.disconnect();\n  }, []);\n\n  return [ref, isCompact] as const;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The `ResizeObserver` on the nav is disconnected by the callback ref's cleanup whenever the nav unmounts (component unmount, or `totalPages` dropping to 1). There are no timers or animation frames.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates. The page cells have no transitions by design, and the arrow buttons only have the shared button press, which `Button` already removes under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not add `transition-colors` to the page cells. The old active cell then fades out while the new one fades in, which looks like a flicker on every click.\n- Do not decide compact mode only in the `ResizeObserver` callback, and do not let a 0 width count. The first callback lands after the first paint, so a narrow pager flashes its full labels, and a hidden pager (0 wide) would flip to compact and flash that when shown again.\n- Do not switch to compact mode on a viewport media query. The pager lives in cards and table footers, so it observes its own nav width with a `ResizeObserver`.\n- Do not drop the `aria-label` on the icon-only arrows. In compact mode the visible label is gone and the button would otherwise be announced as just \"button\".\n- Do not use the array index as the key for page cells. They are keyed by page number so a cell keeps its identity, and focus, when the window shifts. Only the dots use an index key.\n- Do not treat it as uncontrolled. It renders whatever `page` it is given and only reports clicks through `onChange`, so the parent must store the page.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The observer follows the nav, not the component: if `totalPages` starts at 1 (the pager renders `null`) and grows later, the nav attaches and is measured then; dropping back to 1 disconnects it.\n- The arrow icons come through `Button`, which mirrors directional glyphs in RTL, so Previous and Next point the reading direction automatically.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith page 3 of 10 the row reads Previous, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, …, 10, Next, with 3 raised and bordered. Clicking 4 moves the raised cell instantly with no fade and the row becomes 1, …, 3, 4, 5, …, 10, still seven items. Previous is disabled on page 1 and Next on page 10. Narrowing the container below 420px turns both arrows into icon-only buttons and the row becomes 1, …, 3, …, 10 without clipping. Each page cell announces \"Go to page N\" and the current one has `aria-current=\"page\"`.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "panel-wipe-preloader": "# Build: Staggered Panel Wipe\n\nThe counter, the word list and the exit wipe are three parts of a single `gsap.timeline`, not three independent animations racing each other. The number is tweened on a plain object with `power2.inOut`, so it accelerates and settles instead of ticking linearly — the difference between a loading count and a progress bar wearing a costume.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn eased 0-100 counter, a word list and a five-column wipe, all on one timeline so they cannot drift.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A plain object `{ v: 0 }` is tweened to 100 over `countDuration` seconds. `onUpdate` writes `String(Math.round(count.v)).padStart(3, \"0\")` into a ref'd span. Tweening the object rather than the DOM keeps the eased value in one place and means the number is interpolated by the same engine, on the same clock, as everything else on the timeline.\n2. The words are placed with `tl.set(wordEl, { textContent: w }, i * step)` where `step = countDuration / words.length` — for five words over 1.6 s that is one word every 320 ms. Because they are positioned on the timeline rather than scheduled with `setTimeout`, they stay locked to the counter if the timeline is retimed, paused, or seeked.\n3. When the count lands, the content block leaves on `yPercent: -120, opacity: 0` over 0.5 s with `power3.inOut`. -120 rather than -100 clears the block past its own height plus a margin, so a descender never lingers at the top edge.\n4. The five columns then retract with `scaleY: 0` and `transformOrigin: \"top\"`, 0.9 s of `power4.inOut`, staggered 0.08 s apart, inserted at `-=0.2` so the wipe begins while the content is still leaving. `scaleY` is a compositor-friendly transform; animating `height` here would relayout five elements every frame.\n5. The whole build runs inside `useGsap`, which wraps it in a `gsap.context` scoped to the root and calls `ctx.revert()` on unmount, so a preloader torn down mid-count leaves no inline transforms behind.\n6. Only after the timeline's `onComplete` fires does React set `gone` and return `null`. Unmounting on a timer instead would pull the nodes out from under running tweens.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `countDuration = 1.6`\n  Seconds for 0 to 100. Under ~1.2 s the digits change too fast to read as a count and the word list flickers; over ~2.5 s people have already decided the site is slow.\n- `CONTENT_DURATION = 0.5`\n  Content exit. Long enough to read as a departure rather than a cut, short enough that it is finished before the panels have opened far enough to reveal what is behind it.\n- `PANEL_DURATION = 0.9`\n  Per-column retract. `power4.inOut` spends most of its time near the ends, so a shorter duration collapses the slow start and the whole wipe reads as a single instant cut.\n- `PANEL_STAGGER = 0.08`\n  Gap between columns. At 0 the five columns are one panel; past ~0.15 the last column is still standing after the first has finished, which reads as a stuck element rather than a sequence.\n- `PANEL_OVERLAP = 0.2`\n  Seconds the wipe is pulled back into the content exit. Without the overlap there is a dead beat between the number disappearing and the panels moving, and that pause is where the eye decides something has hung.\n- `padStart(3, \"0\") = 3`\n  Fixed three-digit width. Without it the counter changes character count at 10 and 100 and the number visibly jumps sideways twice mid-tween.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PanelWipePreloader(props: { words?: string[]; panels?: number; countDuration?: number; onDone?: () => void; position?: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `words: string[]` — default `['Assets', 'Type', 'Shaders', 'Layout', 'Ready']`\n  Words shown under the counter, spread evenly across the count.\n- `panels: number` — default `5`\n  Number of collapsing columns.\n- `countDuration: number` — default `1.6`\n  Seconds the counter takes to reach 100.\n- `onDone: () => void`\n  Fires from the timeline's `onComplete`, after the last column has collapsed.\n- `position: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"` — default `\"fixed\"`\n  Use `absolute` inside a bounded container; `fixed` for a real full-page preloader.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function PanelWipePreloader({\n  words = ['Assets', 'Type', 'Shaders', 'Layout', 'Ready'],\n  panels = 5,\n  countDuration = 1.6,\n  onDone,\n  position = 'fixed',\n  className,\n}: PanelWipePreloaderProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const content = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const counter = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const word = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [gone, setGone] = useState(false);\n\n  const done = useRef(onDone);\n  done.current = onDone;\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      const contentEl = content.current;\n      const counterEl = counter.current;\n      const wordEl = word.current;\n      if (!el || !contentEl || !counterEl || !wordEl) return;\n\n      const columns = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-wipe-panel]'));\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        counterEl.textContent = '100';\n        wordEl.textContent = words[words.length - 1] ?? '';\n        setGone(true);\n        done.current?.();\n        return;\n      }\n\n      const count = { v: 0 };\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({\n        onComplete: () => {\n          setGone(true);\n          done.current?.();\n        },\n      });\n\n      tl.to(count, {\n        v: 100,\n        duration: countDuration,\n        ease: 'power2.inOut',\n        onUpdate: () => {\n          counterEl.textContent = String(Math.round(count.v)).padStart(3, '0');\n        },\n      });\n\n      // Positioned on the SAME timeline as the counter, at computed times, so\n      // the words stay locked to the number no matter how the timeline is\n      // retimed, paused or seeked.\n      const step = countDuration / Math.max(words.length, 1);\n      words.forEach((w, i) => {\n        tl.set(wordEl, { textContent: w }, i * step);\n      });\n\n      tl.to(contentEl, {\n        yPercent: -120,\n        opacity: 0,\n        duration: CONTENT_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power3.inOut',\n      });\n\n      if (columns.length > 0) {\n        // transformOrigin top + scaleY 0 retracts each column upward like a\n        // blind. The -0.2 overlap fuses the wipe with the tail of the content\n        // exit so the two read as one gesture.\n        tl.to(\n          columns,\n          {\n            scaleY: 0,\n            transformOrigin: 'top',\n            duration: PANEL_DURATION,\n            ease: 'power4.inOut',\n            stagger: PANEL_STAGGER,\n          },\n          `-=${PANEL_OVERLAP}`,\n        );\n      }\n    },\n    root,\n    [reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` runs the whole build inside a `gsap.context` scoped to the root and calls `ctx.revert()` on unmount, which kills the counter tween, the positioned `set` calls and the panel stagger, and restores every property they touched — including the span's original `textContent`.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No timeline is built at all. The counter is written straight to 100, the last word is set, the component unmounts itself and `onDone` fires on the same tick — so a reduced-motion visitor sees the real page immediately rather than a silent panel that still has to be waited out.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Driving the counter with `setInterval` and the panels with GSAP. Two clocks, and on a loaded main thread the number finishes after the wipe has already covered it.\n- Tweening a linear counter. It is the ease that sells the count — `power2.inOut` slows near 100, which is the shape of real work finishing.\n- Animating panel `height` instead of `scaleY`. Five elements relayouting every frame on a page that is simultaneously mounting its real content is the one place the jank is guaranteed to be visible.\n- Unmounting the preloader on a `setTimeout` sized to match the timeline. The two drift under load; unmount from `onComplete` instead.\n- Leaving the preloader in the tree with `opacity: 0`. It still swallows every click on the page underneath. This returns `null`.\n- Forgetting `transformOrigin: \"top\"`. The default centre origin makes the columns shrink to a band in the middle of the screen instead of retracting like a blind.\n\n## Notes\n\n- In a Next.js App Router project this mounts once in `app/layout.tsx`, above `{children}`, and calls `onDone` to flip a context flag the hero listens to. It is deliberately not keyed to the pathname — a preloader that replays on every navigation is a bug, not a feature.\n- If the real site has an actual load signal (fonts ready, hero image decoded), gate `onDone` on both the timeline and that promise. See `safe-preloader` for the ceiling-and-floor version of that problem.\n- The counter is decorative. The root carries `aria-hidden`, so a screen reader is never read a number climbing to 100.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe number counts 000 to 100 with a visible ease, never changing width. Five words swap under it at even intervals and the last word lands as the number does. The content lifts away and five columns retract upward in sequence, the wipe starting before the content has finished leaving. `onDone` fires once, after the last column. With reduced motion the preloader is gone on the first frame. Unmount mid-count and no transforms or altered text remain.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "patient-info-card": "# Build: Patient Info Card\n\nThe patient summary card from BoardUI's medical profile dashboard, built to the Figma frame's measurements. A centred initials avatar carries a small round \"add photo\" button on its corner, the name sits under it, and four inset rows list date of birth, gender, blood type and GP, each with a leading icon. The content is fixed sample copy. The card is a layout to copy and wire to your own record.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA medical-dashboard profile card: a 66px avatar with an add-photo button, the patient's name and four icon label/value rows.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The card is a fixed-height `section` (`h-[330px]`, `rounded-[20px]`, `bg-background-secondary-default`) laid out as a centred column with `gap-[15px]`, `pt-6` and `px-2.5 pb-2.5`. It stretches to its container with `w-full min-w-0`.\n2. The 15px gap is copied from the Figma absolute positions rather than rounded to a spacing step: avatar top 24 and bottom 90, name at 105, details at 146, as the source comment records.\n3. The kit's `Avatar` at `size=\"lg\"` is enlarged with `size-[66px] text-[24.75px]` and shows initials. A `Button` (`variant=\"secondary\"`, `size=\"xs\"`, `iconOnly`, `RiAddLine`) is absolutely placed at `-top-0.5 -end-0.5` on a `relative` wrapper, so it overlaps the avatar's top corner and moves to the other side in RTL. It is labelled \"Add profile photo\".\n4. The details come from a module-level `DETAILS` array of `{ icon, label, value }`. Each row is a `justify-between` flex row with `rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default px-2.5 py-2`: a 16px icon and the label in `text-body-regular text-text-secondary` on the start side, the value in `text-body-medium text-text-primary` on the end side. Both are `whitespace-nowrap`.\n5. The rows sit in a `flex-1 flex-col gap-2.5` block, so they take the space under the name. The whole tree goes through `useTemplateCopy()`, which returns it unchanged unless a `TemplateCopyProvider` translates the copy.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `vertical gap = gap-[15px]`\n  Figma spaces avatar, name and details 15px apart (90 to 105, 131 to 146). Rounding to 16px would shift the stack by 2px against the design file.\n- `avatar = size-[66px], text-[24.75px]`\n  The Figma avatar is 66px. The initials keep the kit's lg ratio of 18px text on a 36px circle (0.375 of the size), so 66 × 0.375 = 24.75.\n- `card frame = h-[330px], rounded-[20px], pt-6 px-2.5 pb-2.5`\n  Frame 125's height and corner radius. The 24px top padding puts the avatar at Figma's y = 24, and the 10px sides let the inset rows nearly reach the edge.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PatientInfoCard(props?: { className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section, for example to set its width or height.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function PatientInfoCard({ className }: { className?: string } = {}) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  return localize((\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex h-[330px] w-full min-w-0 flex-col items-center gap-[15px] rounded-[20px] bg-background-secondary-default px-2.5 pt-6 pb-2.5\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"relative\">\n        <Avatar size=\"lg\" initials=\"JL\" className=\"size-[66px] text-[24.75px]\" />\n        <Button\n          variant=\"secondary\"\n          size=\"xs\"\n          iconOnly\n          leadingIcon={RiAddLine}\n          aria-label=\"Add profile photo\"\n          className=\"absolute -top-0.5 -end-0.5 rounded-full\"\n        />\n      </div>\n      <p className=\"text-title-2-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary\">Jordan Lee</p>\n      <div className=\"flex w-full flex-1 flex-col gap-2.5\">\n        {DETAILS.map((detail) => (\n          <div\n            key={detail.label}\n            className=\"flex w-full items-center justify-between rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default px-2.5 py-2\"\n          >\n            <div className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n              <detail.icon className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-text-secondary\" aria-hidden />\n              <span className=\"text-body-regular whitespace-nowrap text-text-secondary\">\n                {detail.label}\n              </span>\n            </div>\n            <span className=\"text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary\">{detail.value}</span>\n          </div>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The card is static markup with no effects, timers or listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The card has no animation of its own. The add-photo button uses the kit's `button-press-motion`: a colour transition and a 0.98 press scale. It is small and only follows a direct press.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Letting a long value wrap. Labels and values are `whitespace-nowrap`, and a long GP name in a narrow column will push past the row. Give the card at least about 280px, or truncate the value.\n- Rounding the 15px gaps to `gap-4`. The rhythm is copied from Figma on purpose.\n- Expecting props for the patient. Only `className` is accepted. The name, initials and `DETAILS` rows are hard-coded sample content to replace with your data.\n- Dropping the `aria-label` on the add-photo button. It is icon-only, so the label is its only accessible name.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The add-photo button has no click handler. Wire it to your upload flow.\n- The details use RemixIcon glyphs: asterisk for date of birth, a male figure for gender, a drop for blood type and a stethoscope for the GP.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA 330px card with 20px corners shows a 66px \"JL\" avatar with a small round plus button overlapping its top corner, \"Jordan Lee\" under it and four inset rows: Date of Birth 28 July, 1997; Gender Male; Blood Type A rh+; GP Doctor Mattheus Clarkson. Labels are secondary text on the start side and values primary text on the end side. In RTL the plus button and the row contents mirror.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "patients-table": "# Build: Patients Table\n\nA patient list for a clinic dashboard. A toolbar shows the result count (and how many rows are picked) next to status and condition filters and a rounded search field; the table lists each patient with a selection checkbox, initials avatar, name and age, an inline admission select, a coloured status pill, condition chips, the next appointment with the last visit under it, and three row actions. Selection, sorting and paging run on React Aria's own Table state, with selection kept across pages and filters.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA clinic patient list: initials, age, admission select, status pill, conditions, next and last visit, row selection, sort, filter, pages.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Rows are filtered in a `useMemo` by status, condition and a case-insensitive name search, then copied and sorted when the React Aria `sortDescriptor` names a sortable column: `name` uses `localeCompare`, `nextAppointment` compares ISO strings, and a missing date always sorts last.\n2. The sorted rows are sliced into pages of `pageSize` (default 6). The page index is clamped to the page count so a filter that shrinks the list never leaves an empty page, and every filter, search keystroke and sort change resets to page 1. The kit `Pagination` footer appears only when there is more than one page.\n3. The table uses React Aria `selectionMode=\"multiple\"`: a `Checkbox slot=\"selection\"` in the Patient column header is the select-all-on-this-page box (React Aria makes it indeterminate for a partial page) and one in each Patient cell selects the row. Clicking anywhere else on a row also toggles it.\n4. Selection is one `Set` of patient ids (controlled via `selectedKeys` or uncontrolled via `defaultSelectedKeys`). React Aria only receives the ids on the visible page; its `onSelectionChange` result, `\"all\"` meaning every id on the page, replaces that page's ids in the full set, so picks on other pages and on filtered-out rows survive.\n5. Inline controls are React Aria buttons (the admission `Select` trigger, the chart and edit buttons, the more-menu `DropdownTrigger`), whose presses stop at the button and never toggle the row. Admission changes live in a per-id state map and call `onAdmissionChange`; row actions call `onAction(action, patient)`.\n6. Dates are ISO strings parsed with `parseDate` and printed with an `@internationalized/date` `DateFormatter` (`month: \"short\", day: \"2-digit\", year: \"numeric\"`, UTC), so server and client render the same \"Oct 03, 2026\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pageSize = 6`\n  Default rows per page; the demo uses 5 so the card fits a dashboard panel.\n- `table min width = 1060px`\n  Six columns keep their widths; narrower viewports scroll the table inside its own overflow-x-auto wrapper.\n- `search width = 153px`\n  Matches the kit data table search pill; it grows to fill the row on mobile.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function PatientsTable({ patients, pageSize = 6, selectedKeys, defaultSelectedKeys, onSelectionChange, onAdmissionChange, onAction, conditionOptions, locale = \"en-US\", className, \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Patients\" }: PatientsTableProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `patients: Patient[]`\n  Rows: id, name, optional initials/avatarColor/age, admission, status, conditions, optional lastVisit and nextAppointment ISO dates.\n- `pageSize: number` — default `6`\n  Rows per page.\n- `selectedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Controlled selected patient ids, kept across pages and filters.\n- `defaultSelectedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Initial selection when uncontrolled.\n- `onSelectionChange: (keys: Set<Key>) => void`\n  Called with the full selection after any checkbox or row press.\n- `onAdmissionChange: (id: string, admission: Admission) => void`\n  Called when a row's admission select changes.\n- `onAction: (action: PatientAction, patient: Patient) => void`\n  Chart, edit, book, message or archive from the row actions.\n- `conditionOptions: string[]`\n  Condition filter options; defaults to every condition in the data.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for the date columns and the result count.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the outer section.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Patients\"`\n  Accessible name of the table.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Filter → sort → page. Selection is kept as one Set of ids across every\n// page; React Aria only ever sees the ids that are on the visible page, and\n// its answers are merged back so off-page picks are never lost.\nconst selected = useMemo(\n  () => (selectedKeys !== undefined ? new Set(selectedKeys) : ownSelection),\n  [selectedKeys, ownSelection],\n);\n\nconst rows = useMemo(() => {\n  const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();\n  const filtered = patients.filter(\n    (p) =>\n      (statusFilter === \"all\" || p.status === statusFilter) &&\n      (conditionFilter === \"all\" || p.conditions.includes(conditionFilter)) &&\n      (q === \"\" || p.name.toLowerCase().includes(q)),\n  );\n  if (!sort || !SORTABLE.has(sort.column)) return filtered;\n  const dir = sort.direction === \"ascending\" ? 1 : -1;\n  return [...filtered].sort((a, b) =>\n    sort.column === \"name\"\n      ? a.name.localeCompare(b.name, locale) * dir\n      : compareOptional(a.nextAppointment, b.nextAppointment, dir),\n  );\n}, [patients, query, statusFilter, conditionFilter, sort, locale]);\n\nconst totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(rows.length / pageSize));\nconst currentPage = Math.min(page, totalPages);\nconst pageRows = rows.slice((currentPage - 1) * pageSize, currentPage * pageSize);\nconst pageSelection = new Set<Key>(pageRows.filter((p) => selected.has(p.id)).map((p) => p.id));\n\nconst commitSelection = (next: Set<Key>) => {\n  if (selectedKeys === undefined) setOwnSelection(next);\n  onSelectionChange?.(next);\n};\n\nconst handleSelectionChange = (keys: Selection) => {\n  const pageIds = pageRows.map((p) => p.id);\n  const next = new Set(selected);\n  for (const id of pageIds) next.delete(id);\n  for (const id of keys === \"all\" ? pageIds : keys) next.add(id);\n  commitSelection(next);\n};\n\nconst resetPage = () => setPage(1);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers of its own; the Select, Dropdown and Tooltip release their popovers when unmounted.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves except colour transitions; the sort chevron flip and the row button colour fades are dropped with motion-reduce:transition-none.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not hand React Aria the full selection set: it only knows the rendered page, so `\"all\"` would be read as every patient. Merge its answer back into the page's ids only.\n- Do not use the kit IconButton (a plain <button>) for row actions inside a selectable row; a React Aria Button keeps the press from toggling selection.\n- Do not format dates with `toLocaleDateString` on a `Date` built in local time; parse ISO strings with `parseDate` and format in UTC so SSR and hydration agree.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The condition filter lists every condition found in `patients` unless `conditionOptions` is passed.\n- Age and last visit are optional per patient and are simply omitted when missing; a missing next appointment shows an em dash.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTicking a row checkbox adds it to \"N selected\", the header box selects the whole page, sorting Patient orders names A to Z, the status filter narrows the rows, and choosing a new admission does not change selection.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pause-dont-unmount": "# Build: Pause, Don't Unmount\n\nA rotating stack of slabs mounted twice over: once behind `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">` and once behind a plain conditional, switchable live. Hiding tears the Effects down either way — the WebGL context is released, which is what you want, since contexts are a scarce per-process resource. What Activity keeps is everything React owns: the fiber, the DOM, and every `useState` and `useRef` value inside. So the stack resumes at the angle it left, and the build counter on its fiber survives to be incremented again. Requires React 19.2 or newer.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact 19's Activity hides a WebGL scene without losing its state. Effects still tear down, so the GL context is genuinely released.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">` does three separable things, and conflating them is where the misuse starts: it sets `display: none` on the host children, it destroys the Effects inside — cleanup functions run, in order, exactly as on unmount — and it keeps the fiber alive, so state, refs and DOM nodes are all still there when `mode` goes back to `visible`. Children continue to re-render, at a priority below anything visible.\n2. Because Effects are destroyed, the scene's cleanup runs on every hide: materials and geometry are disposed and `disposeRenderer` calls `renderer.forceContextLoss()`. That is the correct behaviour rather than a shortfall — Chromium hands out roughly sixteen WebGL contexts per renderer process and evicts the oldest past that, so a context parked behind `display: none` is the thing that blanks an unrelated canvas three sections down the page.\n3. The rotation phase lives in a `useRef` inside the hidden subtree, and the build Effect seeds the slab rotations from it BEFORE the first draw. On re-show the scene is correct on frame one; there is no snap from zero up to the resumed angle. Under the `unmount` arm the same ref is a fresh zero because the fiber it lived on was collected.\n4. The build counter is the proof, and it needs both halves to move: it increments inside the Effect, so it only rises if the Effect really re-ran, and it survives between runs only if the fiber was preserved. Under `activity` it climbs 1, 2, 3; under `unmount` it is pinned, because each show starts a new fiber whose counter is new too.\n5. Build cost is measured with `performance.now()` from the top of the Effect to the end of the first completed `render`, so it covers context acquisition, program linking and the initial upload — the work Activity does not save. On a desktop GPU it lands in the tens of milliseconds; the point of the readout is that the number is not zero.\n6. The size hook re-seeds synchronously from `getBoundingClientRect` on every Effect run, and its state is preserved across the hide as well, so the re-shown scene already knows its dimensions and skips the zero-sized first render that a fresh mount has to pass through. That one saved render round-trip is a second, quieter thing the preserved fiber buys.\n7. The renderer creates its own canvas inside the Effect and it is removed in cleanup. A React-rendered `<canvas ref>` cannot work here: with `forceContextLoss()` in cleanup, StrictMode's double-invoke hands the second run a permanently dead element, and Activity turns that from a once-per-mount hazard into a once-per-toggle one.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `GAP = 0.2`\n  Vertical spacing between slabs in world units, at a slab height of 1.15. Tight enough that the group reads as one stack rather than seven separate cards, wide enough that the back-to-front transparent sort has visible separation to work with.\n- `TWIST = 0.24`\n  Radians of rotation between neighbouring slabs — about 14 degrees, so seven slabs fan through 84 degrees, just under a quarter turn. Past a full turn the stack becomes rotationally ambiguous and \"it resumed where it left\" stops being checkable by eye.\n- `count / period = 7 / 24`\n  Seven slabs at 24 seconds a revolution: 15 degrees per second. Slow enough that a hide and a show a second apart leaves a gap the eye can measure against the readout, which is the entire experiment.\n- `CAM_Y / CAM_Z = 2.05 / 4.4`\n  A camera lean of about 25 degrees above the stack. Flatter and the slabs collapse into a single silhouette; steeper and it reads as a plan view where the vertical stacking disappears.\n- `opacity ramp = 0.34 to 0.80`\n  Bottom slab to top. The ramp is what makes the stack legible through itself; a constant alpha turns seven slabs into one muddy plane.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PauseDontUnmount(props: { strategy?: 'activity' | 'unmount'; count?: number; period?: number; color?: string; accent?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `strategy: 'activity' | 'unmount'` — default `'activity'`\n  Which arm the switch starts on.\n- `count: number` — default `7`\n  Slabs in the stack.\n- `period: number` — default `24`\n  Seconds for one full rotation.\n- `color: string` — default `'#5f6873'`\n  Colour of the bottom slab.\n- `accent: string` — default `'#e8e4dc'`\n  Colour of the top slab. The stack lerps between the two.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'pause-dont-unmount'`\n  Seeds the per-slab size jitter.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const el = host.current;\n  if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n  if (!hasWebGL()) {\n    setUnsupported(true);\n    return;\n  }\n\n  // Timed from here to the first completed draw: allocating the context,\n  // linking the programs and uploading the geometry are what re-showing\n  // actually costs, and the number is the honest counterweight to \"Activity\n  // makes hiding free\".\n  const t0 = performance.now();\n\n  // The renderer makes its own canvas inside the effect. Two reasons, and\n  // both bite here: StrictMode double-invokes this effect, so a React-owned\n  // `<canvas ref>` would be handed back after `forceContextLoss()` killed\n  // it; and Activity destroys and re-runs this effect on every show, which\n  // is the same hazard once per toggle instead of once per mount.\n  let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n  try {\n    renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n  } catch {\n    // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n    // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n    // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n    // page with it.\n    setUnsupported(true);\n    return;\n  }\n  renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n  renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n  const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n  const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(FOV, 1, 0.1, 100);\n  camera.position.set(0, CAM_Y, CAM_Z);\n  camera.lookAt(0, 0, 0);\n\n  const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(SLAB_W, SLAB_H);\n  const rng = createRng(seed);\n  const base = new THREE.Color(color);\n  const tint = new THREE.Color(accent);\n\n  const slabs: Slab[] = [];\n  const span = Math.max(count - 1, 1);\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    const t = i / span;\n    const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({\n      color: base.clone().lerp(tint, t),\n      transparent: true,\n      opacity: 0.34 + 0.46 * t,\n      side: THREE.DoubleSide,\n      // The slabs are flat and stacked along Y with nothing between them, so\n      // the transparent pass sorted back to front is already correct. Writing\n      // depth would let the upper slabs clip the lower ones they overhang.\n      depthWrite: false,\n    });\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    const k = 0.66 + rng() * 0.46;\n    mesh.scale.set(k, k, 1);\n    mesh.position.y = (i - span / 2) * GAP;\n    scene.add(mesh);\n    slabs.push({ mesh, material });\n  }\n\n  el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n  renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n  renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n  renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n  const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n    renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n    camera.aspect = w / Math.max(h, 1);\n    camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n    dirty.current = true;\n  };\n  resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n\n  // Seed the scene from the PRESERVED phase before the first draw. Under\n  // Activity this is wherever the stack was when it went away; under a\n  // conditional the ref is new and this reads zero. Doing it before the\n  // timed draw also means the resumed frame is correct on frame one, with\n  // no snap from zero to the real angle.\n  for (let i = 0; i < slabs.length; i += 1) {\n    const slab = slabs[i];\n    if (slab) slab.mesh.rotation.set(-Math.PI / 2, 0, phase.current + i * TWIST);\n  }\n  renderer.render(scene, camera);\n\n  builds.current += 1;\n  buildMs.current = performance.now() - t0;\n\n  stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, slabs, geometry, resize };\n  report(true);\n\n  return () => {\n    stage.current = null;\n    for (const slab of slabs) {\n      scene.remove(slab.mesh);\n      slab.material.dispose();\n    }\n    geometry.dispose();\n    // Not optional. `dispose()` alone leaves the context alive and counting\n    // against the per-process budget; only `forceContextLoss()`, which\n    // `disposeRenderer` calls, actually gives it back. Every hide runs this.\n    disposeRenderer(renderer);\n    renderer.domElement.remove();\n  };\n}, [ready, count, color, accent, seed, report]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The Effect removes each slab and disposes its material, disposes the one shared geometry, calls `disposeRenderer` — which stops the loop, disposes the renderer and calls `forceContextLoss()` — and removes the canvas it appended. This runs on unmount, on a StrictMode double-invoke, and on every Activity hide, so it is exercised constantly rather than once. `useRaf` unsubscribes and the shared loop parks when its last subscriber leaves; the `ResizeObserver` unobserves with its hook. A separate Effect reports the scene as gone on teardown so the readout does not freeze on a stale row.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The stack holds still — the phase is integrated from delta time, so freezing it is a matter of adding nothing — and with nothing marking the frame dirty the renderer draws once per build and then idles. The demonstration survives the loss of motion because it was never really about the motion: the build counter still climbs under `activity` and stays pinned under `unmount`, and the rebuild cost in milliseconds is still charged on every show. Only the resumed rotation angle stops being informative, since both arms sit at zero degrees.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Expecting Activity to keep the WebGL context alive. It does not, by design — Effects are destroyed on hide, so your cleanup runs and the context is released. If you had assumed otherwise and skipped disposal, you leak instead; if you assumed re-showing is free, the rebuild millisecond readout says otherwise.\n- Lifting the state you wanted preserved above the boundary. Anything outside the `<Activity>` was never at risk, and anything the parent owns is not evidence of anything. The value has to live on a fiber inside the boundary or the comparison proves nothing.\n- Changing the element at the boundary position instead of changing `mode`. Swapping `<Activity>{scene}</Activity>` for `{scene}` reconciles as a different type at that slot, so React unmounts the subtree and every preserved value goes with it. Keep the boundary mounted and toggle `mode`.\n- Treating hidden as unrendered. Children keep re-rendering, only at a lower priority, so a parent state update still walks the hidden subtree. Anything expensive in render — not in an Effect — is still paid for while hidden.\n- Assuming a ref into hidden DOM behaves the same as a ref into visible DOM. The DOM nodes themselves survive, but ref attachment is part of the layout-effect phase that the offscreen path tears down and replays, and this component does not depend on the answer either way. Do the work in an Effect, which is re-run on show, rather than caching a node in a closure that outlives the hide.\n- Handing three's renderer a React-rendered `<canvas ref>`. Cleanup calls `forceContextLoss()`, and the next Effect run — from StrictMode or from a re-show — is given the same dead element. Let the renderer build its own canvas in the Effect and remove it in cleanup.\n- Reading the build counter in development and taking the absolute number literally. StrictMode double-invokes Effects on mount, so the first build reads 2, not 1. The claim to check is the shape: climbing under `activity`, pinned under `unmount`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `Activity` is exported from `react` from 19.2 (verified against 19.2.8: `exports.Activity = REACT_ACTIVITY_TYPE`, typed as `ExoticComponent<ActivityProps>` with `mode?: \"hidden\" | \"visible\"`). On anything older the import is undefined at runtime, not a build error, which fails as a blank subtree.\n- The strongest real use is not a canvas at all — it is a tab, a route the user is likely to come back to, or a filter panel: scroll offsets, uncontrolled input values and list virtualisation windows are all preserved for free, and none of them cost a GPU context to rebuild.\n- Pre-rendering the other direction, `mode=\"hidden\"` on a subtree that has never been visible, mounts it at low priority without running Effects. Useful for warming a route, useless for warming a renderer, for the same reason as above.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn `activity`: press hide, wait, press show. The stack picks up at the angle the readout froze at, `builds` has gone up by one, and the rebuild cost is a real number of milliseconds. On `unmount`: the same sequence returns the stack to zero degrees and `builds` never moves. Hide on either arm and the canvas is gone from the DOM inspector under `unmount` but present with `display: none` under `activity`. Neither arm accumulates WebGL contexts — toggle twenty times and the other previews on the page stay drawn.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "percent-slide-carousel": "# Build: Percent Slide Carousel\n\nThe simplest slide carousel that is still correct. A flex track holds one full-width slide per child and translates by `-100% * index`; because a percentage translate resolves against the translated element's own width, and the track is exactly one viewport-box wide, one step is exactly one slide. Nothing is measured, so nothing goes stale on resize.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA flex track translated by -100% per slide. One CSS transition, no measurement, no library.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The viewport box is `overflow: hidden`. Inside it a flex track is `h-full w-full`, and every slide is `w-full shrink-0` — full width of the *track*, not of the flex line, so `count` slides overflow the track by `count - 1` widths instead of being compressed into it.\n2. The track carries `transform: translateX(-${100 * index}%)`. A percentage in `translateX` is relative to the border box of the element being transformed, which is the track, whose width equals the viewport box, which equals one slide. So `-100%` is one slide exactly, with no numbers taken from layout.\n3. That is the whole reason to prefer this over a pixel translate: a pixel offset needs a measured slide width, which needs a resize observer, which needs a re-measure pass that can land a frame late and show a seam. Here a resize changes the meaning of `100%` on the next paint for free.\n4. The transition is `transform 600ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)` on the track only. Interrupting it mid-flight is handled by the compositor: the browser retargets from the current computed transform, so rapid clicks chain smoothly rather than restarting from the last committed index.\n5. Navigation clamps rather than wraps by default. `-100% * index` is a linear function of the index with no modulo in it, so a wrap from the last slide to the first would translate the whole track back across every intermediate slide — a visible rewind. `loop` is offered but honest about that.\n6. Each slide is a `role=\"group\"` with `aria-roledescription=\"slide\"` and an `aria-label` of `n of N`, so a screen reader announces position without the component owning any live region.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 600`\n  Milliseconds for a full slide. Under ~350 ms a full-width traversal reads as a cut rather than a move and the eye loses which direction it went; over ~900 ms a rapid click-through feels blocked because each step is still finishing when the next arrives.\n- `EASE = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  An expo-out curve: ~80% of the distance is covered in the first third of the duration. On a full-viewport traversal that front-loading is what keeps a 600 ms move from feeling slow, while the long flat tail hides the exact moment the slide lands.\n- `translate step = 100%`\n  Locked to the slide width by construction, not by choice. Any other value desynchronises the track from the slides and leaves a permanent sliver of the neighbour visible at rest.\n- `shrink-0 on slides = flex-shrink: 0`\n  Without it flexbox honours the container width and squeezes every slide to `1/count` of the track. The translate still moves a full track-width, so one click jumps `count` slides — the single most common way this pattern breaks.\n- `loop = false`\n  Default off. With a linear percentage translate there is no cheap wrap: going from the last index to 0 animates back through every slide. If you need a true loop, reach for the drum instead.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PercentSlideCarousel(props: { children: ReactNode; duration?: number; index?: number; defaultIndex?: number; onIndexChange?: (index: number) => void; loop?: boolean; className?: string; slideClassName?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One slide per child. Each is wrapped in a full-width, non-shrinking cell.\n- `duration: number` — default `600`\n  Slide transition duration in milliseconds.\n- `index: number`\n  Controlled index. Omit to let the component hold its own state.\n- `defaultIndex: number` — default `0`\n  Starting index when uncontrolled.\n- `onIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Fires with the resolved index after clamping or wrapping.\n- `loop: boolean` — default `false`\n  Wrap past the ends. Off by default because the wrap animates backwards through every slide.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the viewport box.\n- `slideClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every slide cell.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Carousel'`\n  Label for the carousel group.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst trackStyle = {\n  // One slide is one track-width, so the step is exactly 100%. This is the\n  // only carousel arithmetic that needs no numbers from layout at all.\n  transform: `translateX(-${100 * current}%)`,\n  // No transition under reduced motion: the slide still changes, it just\n  // arrives instantly. Cutting the movement is the point; cutting the\n  // navigation would be a bug.\n  transition: reduced ? 'none' : `transform ${duration}ms ${EASE}`,\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. No listeners, no timers, no animation frames, no imperative writes to the DOM; the only state is the current index and the browser owns the transition.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The track transition is set to `none`, so the slide changes on the next paint with no traversal. Navigation, focus order and the announced position are untouched — only the movement between slides is removed.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Omitting `flex-shrink: 0` on the slides. Flexbox compresses them to fit the track, the `-100%` step no longer equals one slide, and the carousel advances by the whole set on the first click.\n- Putting the transition on the slides instead of the track. Only the track moves; a transition on the children costs a style recalculation per slide per frame and animates nothing.\n- Translating in pixels from a measured slide width. It works until the container resizes between the measure and the paint, at which point the track is offset by the difference and every slide sits a few pixels off-centre until the next navigation.\n- Wrapping the index with a modulo and expecting a loop. `-100% * 0` after `-100% * (n - 1)` animates the whole track backwards past every slide; users read it as the carousel malfunctioning, not as a wrap.\n- Marking off-screen slides `aria-hidden` while they still contain focusable children. Tab lands inside a hidden subtree, which is an accessibility violation and, in practice, scrolls the overflow container sideways and desynchronises it from the transform.\n- Forgetting `overflow: hidden` on the viewport box. The overflowing slides remain visible, and inside a page-level container they widen the document and add a horizontal scrollbar.\n- Killing the transition under reduced motion but also killing the index change. The slide must still change — reduced motion removes the traversal, not the navigation.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This is the flat, non-3D member of the carousel trio. `wrapping-drum-carousel` fakes depth with scale and `autoAlpha` over a `gsap.utils.wrap` ring and loops infinitely; `perspective-coverflow-scroller` uses real `perspective()` / `rotateY()` / `translateZ()` driven by a native scroll container. This one has no depth, no wrap and no JavaScript in the motion path at all — reach for it when the content is the point and the carousel is not.\n- Because the motion is a single compositor-friendly transform on one element, it holds 60 fps on hardware where the other two do not. If you are shipping a hero on a low-end phone, this is the one that survives.\n- Controlled and uncontrolled: pass `index` to drive it from outside, omit it and the component keeps its own state. `onIndexChange` fires in both cases.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick next and exactly one slide traverses the box in 600 ms with no neighbour visible at rest. Hold the next button down and the slides chain without restarting from the previous index. Resize the container mid-transition and the resting position stays flush with the box edges. At the last slide the next button is disabled unless `loop` is set. With reduced motion the slide changes instantly and the buttons still work.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "perspective-coverflow-scroller": "# Build: Perspective Coverflow Scroller\n\nA horizontal `overflow-x-auto` container with `scroll-snap-type: x proximity`. Each panel's distance from the centre of the viewport is normalised to roughly ±1 and mapped onto a per-element `perspective() translateZ() rotateY() scale()`, so panels turn their inside edge toward you as they leave the middle. The scroll position itself is never animated by JavaScript — momentum, rubber-banding and keyboard paging are the browser's.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPanels turn away from the centre in real 3D as you scroll a native container — no tween, no loop.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A measure pass caches each panel's layout centre as `panel.offsetLeft + panel.offsetWidth / 2` and sets the track's left and right padding to `(scroller.clientWidth - endPanel.offsetWidth) / 2`, which is exactly enough runway for the first and last panel to reach the middle of the viewport.\n2. On every coalesced scroll frame, the normalised offset is `r = clamp((centre - scrollLeft - half) / half, -1.2, 1.2)` where `half = scroller.clientWidth / 2`. `r = 0` is dead centre, `r = ±1` is one half-viewport out.\n3. That single scalar drives four things: `rotateY = -rotation * r` (negative, so a panel on the right turns its left edge toward you — the flip-book read), `translateZ = -depth * |r|` (always receding), `scale = 1 - scaleFalloff * min(|r|, 1)`, and `opacity = 1 - opacityFalloff * min(|r|, 1.2)`.\n4. `perspective(1300px)` is written inside each panel's own transform rather than as a `perspective` property on the track. A shared parent perspective gives every panel the same vanishing point, so panels near the edges skew as well as rotate; per-element perspective turns each panel about its own centre.\n5. The two clamps differ on purpose — `min(|r|, 1)` for scale and `min(|r|, 1.2)` for opacity — so distant panels keep their proportions while continuing to fade out.\n6. A `ResizeObserver` on the track re-runs the measure pass whenever content size changes (a font swap, a child growing), which is the deterministic replacement for the usual pile of `setTimeout(recompute, 300)` calls.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `perspective = 1300`\n  Per-element perspective distance in px. Under ~700 the foreshortening is strong enough that a 40° turn hides most of the panel; over ~2000 the rotation flattens out and reads as a skew rather than a turn.\n- `rotation = 40`\n  Degrees of `rotateY` at the clamp. 40° is the point where the panel is visibly turned but still legible; past ~55° it is close enough to edge-on that text disappears and antialiasing on the near edge gets ugly.\n- `depth = 130`\n  Pixels of `translateZ`, always negative so off-centre panels recede. Combined with the rotation this is what makes the row read as the inside of a cylinder rather than a fanned deck. At 0 you get a pure hinge; past ~250 the far panels shrink faster than `scaleFalloff` intends and the two cues disagree.\n- `scaleFalloff = 0.12`\n  A panel at one half-width out renders at 0.88. Kept small because `translateZ` is already shrinking it perspectivally — stacking a large explicit scale on top double-counts the distance.\n- `opacityFalloff = 0.35`\n  The edge panels land near 0.58, not 0. They must stay readable, because on a proximity-snap track they are the affordance that tells you there is more to scroll to.\n- `maxOffset = 1.2`\n  Clamp on `r`. Without it, a long track keeps rotating panels past 90° and they flip to their back faces; 1.2 rather than 1.0 leaves a little travel past the half-width mark so the outermost visible panels are not all frozen at an identical angle.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PerspectiveCoverflowScroller(props: { children: ReactNode; perspective?: number; rotation?: number; depth?: number; scaleFalloff?: number; opacityFalloff?: number; maxOffset?: number; initialIndex?: number; className?: string; panelClassName?: string; 'aria-label'?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One panel per child. Each is wrapped in a snap-aligned cell.\n- `perspective: number` — default `1300`\n  Per-element perspective distance in px.\n- `rotation: number` — default `40`\n  Degrees of `rotateY` at the clamp.\n- `depth: number` — default `130`\n  Pixels of `translateZ` at the clamp. Applied negatively, so panels recede.\n- `scaleFalloff: number` — default `0.12`\n  How much a panel shrinks one half-viewport out.\n- `opacityFalloff: number` — default `0.35`\n  How much a panel fades one half-viewport out.\n- `maxOffset: number` — default `1.2`\n  Clamp on the normalised offset, so distant panels stop turning further.\n- `initialIndex: number`\n  Panel centred on mount. Defaults to the middle of the set.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the scroll container.\n- `panelClassName: string`\n  Merged onto each snap cell. Size the panels here.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Gallery'`\n  Label for the scrollable region, which is focusable for keyboard scrolling.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Cache each panel's layout centre and pad the track so the first and last\n * panel can reach the middle of the viewport.\n *\n * `offsetLeft`/`offsetWidth` are *layout* values: unaffected by `scrollLeft`\n * and, critically, unaffected by the transforms this component writes.\n * Reading `getBoundingClientRect()` in the scroll handler instead would feed\n * each panel's own `scale()` back into the next frame's offset.\n */\nconst measure = useCallback(() => {\n  const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n  const track = trackRef.current;\n  if (!scroller || !track) return;\n\n  const panels = panelsRef.current;\n  const first = panels[0];\n  const last = panels[count - 1];\n\n  // Half a viewport minus half a panel. The source of this technique used a\n  // fixed `24vw`, which is wrong for any panel that is not ~52vw wide and\n  // wrong for every container that is not the viewport.\n  if (first) {\n    track.style.paddingLeft = `${Math.max(0, (scroller.clientWidth - first.offsetWidth) / 2)}px`;\n  }\n  if (last) {\n    track.style.paddingRight = `${Math.max(0, (scroller.clientWidth - last.offsetWidth) / 2)}px`;\n  }\n\n  // Read after the padding write: the padding moves every offsetLeft.\n  centresRef.current = panels.map((panel) =>\n    panel ? panel.offsetLeft + panel.offsetWidth / 2 : 0,\n  );\n}, [count]);\n\n/** Write one transform per panel from the current scroll position. */\nconst update = useCallback(() => {\n  const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n  if (!scroller) return;\n\n  const half = scroller.clientWidth / 2 || 1;\n  const scroll = scroller.scrollLeft;\n  const panels = panelsRef.current;\n  const centres = centresRef.current;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < panels.length; i += 1) {\n    const panel = panels[i];\n    const centre = centres[i];\n    if (!panel || centre === undefined) continue;\n\n    if (reduced) {\n      panel.style.transform = '';\n      panel.style.opacity = '';\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    // Signed, normalised distance from the viewport centre: -1 = one half\n    // width to the left, +1 = one half width to the right.\n    const r = clamp((centre - scroll - half) / half, -maxOffset, maxOffset);\n    const away = Math.abs(r);\n\n    // Perspective lives *in the transform*, not on the parent. A shared\n    // parent perspective gives every panel the same vanishing point, so\n    // edge panels skew as well as rotate; per-element perspective turns each\n    // panel about its own centre, which is the coverflow look.\n    panel.style.transform =\n      `perspective(${perspective}px) ` +\n      `translateZ(${(-depth * away).toFixed(1)}px) ` +\n      `rotateY(${(-rotation * r).toFixed(2)}deg) ` +\n      `scale(${(1 - scaleFalloff * Math.min(away, 1)).toFixed(3)})`;\n\n    // Two different clamps on purpose: scale bottoms out at 1, opacity keeps\n    // going to `maxOffset`. Distant panels stay proportionate while fading.\n    panel.style.opacity = (1 - opacityFalloff * Math.min(away, maxOffset)).toFixed(2);\n  }\n}, [reduced, perspective, rotation, depth, scaleFalloff, opacityFalloff, maxOffset]);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n  if (!scroller) return;\n\n  measure();\n\n  // Start centred on a panel, not on the midpoint of the scroll range: with\n  // an even count the midpoint falls in the gap between two panels and the\n  // component opens looking like nothing is selected.\n  if (!centredRef.current && count > 0) {\n    const target = initialIndex ?? Math.floor((count - 1) / 2);\n    const centre = centresRef.current[clamp(Math.round(target), 0, count - 1)];\n    if (centre !== undefined) {\n      scroller.scrollLeft = centre - scroller.clientWidth / 2;\n    }\n    centredRef.current = true;\n  }\n\n  update();\n\n  let frame = 0;\n  const onScroll = () => {\n    // Coalesce: a trackpad fires `scroll` far more often than the compositor\n    // paints, and each handler run writes N transforms.\n    if (frame !== 0) return;\n    frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {\n      frame = 0;\n      update();\n    });\n  };\n\n  scroller.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });\n\n  return () => {\n    scroller.removeEventListener('scroll', onScroll);\n    if (frame !== 0) cancelAnimationFrame(frame);\n  };\n}, [measure, update, count, initialIndex]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the `scroll` listener, cancels any pending `requestAnimationFrame`, and disconnects the track `ResizeObserver`. The shared element observer unregisters the scroller. Inline `transform`/`opacity` written on panels dies with the nodes; no timer or tween outlives the component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The scroller keeps working exactly as it did — it is a real scroll container, and scrolling is user-driven, not animation. What is removed is the 3D: `transform` and `opacity` are cleared on every panel, so the row renders as a flat, fully opaque filmstrip that still snaps by proximity. Nothing moves that the user did not move.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Computing the offset from `getBoundingClientRect()`. The rect reflects the transform this component just wrote, so each panel's `scale()` feeds back into the next frame's offset and the row slowly drifts to a wrong steady state. `offsetLeft`/`offsetWidth` are layout values — untouched by transforms and by `scrollLeft`.\n- Putting `perspective` on the track instead of in each panel's transform. Every panel then shares one vanishing point, so the ones near the edges skew into parallelograms instead of turning.\n- Handling `scroll` without coalescing into a frame. A trackpad fires scroll events considerably faster than the compositor paints, and every run writes N style strings for no visible benefit.\n- Padding the track with a viewport unit like `24vw`. It is correct for exactly one panel width and one container width; inside a card, a modal or a split layout the end panels can never reach the centre and are permanently unselectable.\n- Setting `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory`. Release between two panels and the browser yanks you to one of them, which fights every attempt to scan the row. `proximity` snaps only when you were already close.\n- Starting at `scrollLeft = 0`. The first panel is then hard against the left edge with nothing to its left, so the effect is invisible until the user scrolls. Centring on a panel on mount shows the technique in both directions immediately.\n- Re-centring on every resize. `scrollLeft` is only assigned once, guarded by a ref — otherwise rotating a phone, or any resize during a scroll, teleports the user back to the middle mid-gesture.\n\n## Notes\n\n- One of three deliberately different carousel techniques here, and the distinctions are the point. This one is **true 3D on a native scroll container**: actual `rotateY` and `translateZ`, and no JavaScript animation drives the scroll at all — the handler only reads a position the browser already set. `wrapping-drum-carousel` is **fake 3D** driven by a tweened JS position with `rotationY: 0, z: 0` pinned, and wraps infinitely via `gsap.utils.wrap`. `percent-slide-carousel` is **flat, bounded, one CSS transition per index change**.\n- Choose this one when the set is long, scanning matters more than stepping, and you want native momentum and keyboard support for free. It cannot loop: a native scroll container has real ends.\n- Because the panels rotate in 3D, text inside them is composited on a rotated plane and will look softer than flat text. That is inherent to the technique, not a bug to tune away; keep small type out of the panels or accept it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn mount a panel sits centred and the ones either side are visibly turned toward it. Drag or trackpad-scroll and the turn follows continuously with no lag and no snap-back; release near a panel and it snaps, release between two and it does not. Scroll to either end and the outermost panel reaches the exact centre of the container. Resize the container and the end padding recomputes so it still can.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pinned-horizontal-track": "# Build: Pinned Horizontal Track\n\nA full-height section is pinned by ScrollTrigger while a flex strip inside it translates on x. The pin lasts exactly as many scroll pixels as the strip has to travel, so the mapping is 1:1 and the section releases the moment the last panel lands. The tween and the trigger are handed back to the parent, so reveal systems and drag handles can drive off the same pin instead of building a second one.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA section pins while a strip of panels slides sideways, one scroll pixel per track pixel.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One measurement backs the whole construction: `distanceOf() = max(0, track.scrollWidth - root.clientWidth)`. It is a function, not a captured number, because fonts, images and container resizes all change it after first paint.\n2. `gsap.to(track, { x: () => -distanceOf(), ease: \"none\" })` uses a function-based end value, so `invalidateOnRefresh: true` re-derives the travel on every refresh rather than replaying a stale target.\n3. The ScrollTrigger pins the root with `start: \"top top\"` and `end: () => \"+=\" + distanceOf()`. Because the pin duration and the tween distance come from the same call, one scroll pixel always buys one track pixel.\n4. `scrub: 1` gives the strip one second of catch-up, so a flicked wheel produces a glide rather than a jump; `anticipatePin: 1` lets ScrollTrigger apply the pin a frame early, which removes the single-frame jitter at high scroll speeds.\n5. `document.fonts.ready` triggers `ScrollTrigger.refresh()`. A font swap changes `scrollWidth` after the pin was measured, and without the refresh the pin ends before the track does — the last panel is unreachable.\n6. On build the component calls `onTrack({ animation, trigger, getDistance })`; on teardown it calls `onTrack(null)`, so consumers rebuild rather than holding a reverted tween.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `scrub = 1`\n  Seconds of catch-up between the scrollbar and the strip. `true` (0) locks the track to the scrollbar and inherits every wheel step as a visible stutter; above ~1.5 the track keeps moving long after the wheel stops and reads as broken input.\n- `anticipatePin = 1`\n  Applies the pin one frame of predicted scroll early. At 0, a fast flick pins one frame late and the section visibly slips before locking. Above 1 it pins early enough to look premature.\n- `end = `+=${max(1, distanceOf())}``\n  Pin duration in pixels, equal to the horizontal travel. The `max(1, …)` floor exists because a track narrower than its container yields 0, and an end equal to start makes ScrollTrigger treat the trigger as degenerate and never fire it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PinnedHorizontalTrack(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; scrub?: number; onTrack?: (track: PinnedTrack | null) => void; className?: string; trackClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Panels. The track is a flex row, so give each panel a width and `shrink-0`.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container the trigger reads. Omit to use the document.\n- `scrub: number` — default `1`\n  Seconds of catch-up between scrollbar and strip.\n- `onTrack: (track: PinnedTrack | null) => void`\n  Receives `{ animation, trigger, getDistance }` on build and `null` on teardown.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the pinned root.\n- `trackClassName: string`\n  Merged onto the moving strip.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function PinnedHorizontalTrack({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  scrub = 1,\n  onTrack,\n  className,\n  trackClassName,\n}: PinnedHorizontalTrackProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const track = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const onTrackRef = useRef(onTrack);\n  onTrackRef.current = onTrack;\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    const trackEl = track.current;\n    if (!rootEl || !trackEl || reduced) return;\n\n    // `undefined` means \"the document\" to ScrollTrigger. Passing `null` would\n    // be read as an explicit scroller and throw on the first refresh.\n    const scrollerEl = scroller?.current ?? undefined;\n\n    // The single source of truth. Both the tween's end value and the pin's\n    // duration call it, so a refresh that changes one changes the other.\n    const distanceOf = () => Math.max(0, trackEl.scrollWidth - rootEl.clientWidth);\n\n    let cancelled = false;\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      const animation = gsap.to(trackEl, {\n        // Function-based value: re-evaluated by `invalidateOnRefresh`.\n        x: () => -distanceOf(),\n        ease: 'none',\n        scrollTrigger: {\n          trigger: rootEl,\n          scroller: scrollerEl,\n          pin: true,\n          anticipatePin: 1,\n          scrub,\n          start: 'top top',\n          // 1 px of scroll buys 1 px of track. Expressing the end as a\n          // percentage of viewport height instead makes the mapping depend\n          // on window shape, and the track finishes early on a short screen.\n          end: () => `+=${Math.max(1, distanceOf())}`,\n          invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n        },\n      });\n\n      const trigger = animation.scrollTrigger;\n      if (trigger) {\n        onTrackRef.current?.({ animation, trigger, getDistance: distanceOf });\n      }\n    }, rootEl);\n\n    // The most-forgotten line in scroll-driven layout. A font swap changes\n    // `scrollWidth` after the pin has already been measured, and the last\n    // panel becomes unreachable because the pin ends before the track does.\n    void document.fonts?.ready.then(() => {\n      if (!cancelled) ScrollTrigger.refresh();\n    });\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n      onTrackRef.current?.(null);\n      // revert(), not kill(): it also removes the pin-spacer GSAP injected\n      // into the DOM and restores the inline transform on the strip.\n      ctx.revert();\n    };\n  }, [scroller, scrub, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Calls `onTrack(null)` so consumers drop their reference, then `ctx.revert()`, which kills the tween and the ScrollTrigger, removes the injected pin-spacer, and clears the inline transform from the strip. A pending `document.fonts.ready` refresh is gated behind a `cancelled` flag.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No ScrollTrigger and no pin are created. The root becomes a horizontally scrollable row, so the same panels are still read left to right — under the reader's own control, at the reader's own speed.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Measuring `scrollWidth - clientWidth` once into a variable. Fonts swap after first paint and the number grows; the pin then ends before the track has finished travelling and the final panel can never be reached.\n- Expressing `end` as a percentage of viewport height (`\"+=\" + distance * 100 / innerWidth + \"%\"`). It works on the machine it was tuned on and finishes the track early on a shorter screen, because the mapping now depends on window shape rather than on the travel itself.\n- Passing `scroller: null` when the component sits on the page rather than in a box. ScrollTrigger reads `null` as an explicit scroller element and throws on the first refresh; omit the option instead, which is why the code coalesces to `undefined`.\n- Calling `kill()` instead of `revert()` on unmount. `kill()` leaves the pin-spacer div GSAP injected into the DOM and the inline transform on the strip, so the next mount measures a layout that still contains the last one.\n- Forgetting that a pinned element inside a custom scroller needs that scroller passed explicitly. Without it, ScrollTrigger measures the document, the trigger never becomes active inside the box, and the panels sit motionless.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` no trigger is created at all and the strip becomes a native `overflow-x-auto` row, so every panel stays reachable by thumb, trackpad and keyboard.\n- `gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger)` runs once at module scope behind a `typeof window` guard, so repeated imports are harmless and prerender never touches the plugin.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll down: the section locks, the panels slide left, and the last panel reaches the right edge at exactly the moment the section unpins. Resize the container mid-pin and the track still ends flush. With reduced motion on, the section never pins and the panels can be swiped sideways instead.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "plan-comparison-table": "# Build: Plan Comparison Table\n\nA real `<table>` comparing plans feature by feature. The header row carries each plan name, badge, price and CTA and sticks to the top of the scroll container while you read. Feature rows are grouped; each group has a full-width toggle row that collapses or expands its rows. Cells render a check for `true`, a dash for `false` or a missing value, and plain text for strings. On narrow containers a segmented control picks which single plan column sits beside the labels.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nFeature-by-plan table with a sticky plan header, collapsible groups, check, dash and text cells, and a tinted highlighted column.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The header cells (`th` in `thead`) are `position: sticky` with `top` taken from the `stickyTop` prop and an opaque `bg-background-full`, so rows scroll beneath them. The table uses `border-separate` because sticky cells in a collapsed-border table lose their borders while stuck.\n2. Each group is its own `<tbody>`. Its first row is a `th scope=\"colgroup\"` spanning every column that holds a native button with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` listing the ids of the group rows. Collapsed group ids live in a `Set` in state, seeded from `defaultCollapsed`; a collapsed group renders no feature rows at all.\n3. Expanded rows fade in through `@starting-style` (`starting:opacity-0` with a 200ms opacity transition), so expanding needs no JavaScript animation. The chevron rotates by -90deg when the group is closed.\n4. `ValueCell` maps `true` to a check in a tinted circle with sr-only \"Included\", `false`, `\"\"` or a missing key to a dash with sr-only \"Not included\", and any other string to text.\n5. The block is a size container. From `@3xl` (48rem) every plan column shows; below that the cells of all plans except `focusPlan` get `hidden`, and a `SegmentedControl` above the table (itself `@3xl:hidden`) switches `focusPlan`. `focusPlan` starts at the highlighted plan, or the first plan.\n6. A highlighted plan tints its whole column with the same class on header, body and footer cells; the header tint is drawn on an inner div with rounded top corners and the footer cell has rounded bottom corners, so the column reads as one tall card. Its CTAs use the primary button.\n7. CTAs appear in the sticky header (small) and again in the footer row (xs). A plan with `cta.href` renders `ButtonLink`; without it a `Button` calls `onSelectPlan(plan.id)`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `container breakpoint = @3xl (48rem)`\n  Below about 768px four plan columns plus labels squeeze each cell under 120px; one focused column keeps values readable.\n- `label column = 55% narrow, 32% wide`\n  Labels wrap less than values, so they get the larger share when only one plan is visible.\n- `row fade = 200ms opacity, ease-out, @starting-style`\n  Short enough not to delay reading, and @starting-style animates newly inserted rows without measuring heights.\n- `TINT = accent-50 / accent-950 at 40% in dark`\n  A quiet column wash marks the recommended plan without competing with check marks.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PlanComparisonTable(props: { plans: ComparisonPlan[]; groups: ComparisonGroup[]; defaultCollapsed?: string[]; stickyTop?: number; label?: string; sublabel?: string; onSelectPlan?: (planId: string) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface ComparisonPlan { id: string; name: string; price: string; period?: string; highlighted?: boolean; badge?: string; cta?: { label: string; href?: string } }\ninterface ComparisonGroup { id: string; title: string; icon?: IconComponent; rows: { id: string; label: string; hint?: string; values: Partial<Record<string, boolean | string>> }[] }\n```\n\n- `plans: ComparisonPlan[]`\n  Columns in display order.\n- `groups: ComparisonGroup[]`\n  Feature groups; each row maps plan ids to true, false or text.\n- `defaultCollapsed: string[]` — default `[]`\n  Group ids that start collapsed.\n- `stickyTop: number` — default `0`\n  Pixel offset of the sticky header from the scroll container top.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Compare plans\"`\n  First header cell text and the table caption.\n- `sublabel: string`\n  Second line in the first header cell.\n- `onSelectPlan: (planId: string) => void`\n  Called by CTAs of plans without `cta.href`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction ValueCell({ value }: { value: ComparisonValue | undefined }) {\n  if (value === true) {\n    return (\n      <span className=\"inline-grid size-5 place-items-center rounded-full bg-badge-new-background text-badge-new-text\">\n        <RiCheckLine aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5\" />\n        <span className=\"sr-only\">Included</span>\n      </span>\n    );\n  }\n  if (value === false || value === undefined || value === \"\") {\n    return (\n      <span className=\"inline-flex text-foreground-icon-quaternary\">\n        <RiSubtractLine aria-hidden className=\"size-4\" />\n        <span className=\"sr-only\">Not included</span>\n      </span>\n    );\n  }\n  return <span className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{value}</span>;\n}\n\nexport function PlanComparisonTable({\n  plans,\n  groups,\n  defaultCollapsed = [],\n  stickyTop = 0,\n  label = \"Compare plans\",\n  sublabel,\n  onSelectPlan,\n  className,\n}: PlanComparisonTableProps) {\n  const uid = useId();\n  const [collapsed, setCollapsed] = useState<ReadonlySet<string>>(() => new Set(defaultCollapsed));\n  // On narrow containers only one plan column fits beside the labels; this picks it.\n  const [focusPlan, setFocusPlan] = useState(() => (plans.find((p) => p.highlighted) ?? plans[0])?.id ?? \"\");\n\n  const toggle = (id: string) =>\n    setCollapsed((prev) => {\n      const next = new Set(prev);\n      if (next.has(id)) next.delete(id);\n      else next.add(id);\n      return next;\n    });\n\n  // Every plan column is visible from @3xl; below it only the focused plan's column is.\n  const planCol = (plan: ComparisonPlan) =>\n    cx(plan.id === focusPlan ? \"table-cell\" : \"hidden @3xl:table-cell\", plan.highlighted && TINT);\n\n  const cta = (plan: ComparisonPlan, size: \"small\" | \"xs\") => {\n    if (!plan.cta) return null;\n    const variant = plan.highlighted ? \"primary\" : \"secondary\";\n    const cls = \"w-full rounded-full\";\n    return plan.cta.href ? (\n      <ButtonLink href={plan.cta.href} size={size} variant={variant} className={cls}>\n        {plan.cta.label}\n      </ButtonLink>\n    ) : (\n      <Button size={size} variant={variant} className={cls} onClick={() => onSelectPlan?.(plan.id)}>\n        {plan.cta.label}\n      </Button>\n    );\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className={cx(\"@container w-full\", className)}>\n      {plans.length > 1 && (\n        <div className=\"mb-4 flex justify-center @3xl:hidden\">\n          <SegmentedControl\n            aria-label=\"Plan to compare\"\n            selectedKeys={new Set([focusPlan])}\n            onSelectionChange={(keys) => {\n              const next = [...keys][0];\n              if (typeof next === \"string\") setFocusPlan(next);\n            }}\n          >\n            {plans.map((plan) => (\n              <SegmentedControlItem key={plan.id} id={plan.id}>\n                {plan.name}\n              </SegmentedControlItem>\n            ))}\n          </SegmentedControl>\n        </div>\n      )}\n\n      <table className=\"w-full table-fixed border-separate border-spacing-0 text-left\">\n        <caption className=\"sr-only\">{label}</caption>\n        <thead>\n          <tr>\n            <th\n              scope=\"col\"\n              style={{ top: stickyTop }}\n              className=\"sticky z-10 w-[55%] border-b border-separator-border bg-background-full px-3 pt-5 pb-4 align-bottom @3xl:w-[32%] @3xl:px-4\"\n            >\n              <span className=\"block text-title-3-medium text-text-primary\">{label}</span>\n              {sublabel && <span className=\"mt-1 block text-body-2-regular text-text-tertiary\">{sublabel}</span>}\n            </th>\n            {plans.map((plan) => (\n              <th\n                key={plan.id}\n                scope=\"col\"\n                style={{ top: stickyTop }}\n                className={cx(\n                  \"sticky z-10 border-b border-separator-border bg-background-full p-0 align-bottom\",\n                  plan.id === focusPlan ? \"table-cell\" : \"hidden @3xl:table-cell\",\n                )}\n              >\n                <div className={cx(\"flex h-full flex-col gap-3 rounded-t-2xl px-3 pt-5 pb-4 @3xl:px-4\", plan.highlighted && TINT)}>\n                  <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\">\n                    <span className=\"text-headline-medium text-text-primary\">{plan.name}</span>\n                    {plan.badge && (\n                      <span className=\"rounded-full bg-badge-new-background px-2 py-0.5 text-caption-2-semibold text-badge-new-text\">\n                        {plan.badge}\n                      </span>\n                    )}\n                  </div>\n                  <p className=\"flex items-baseline gap-1\">\n                    <span className=\"text-title-1-medium tracking-tight text-text-primary tabular-nums\">{plan.price}</span>\n                    {plan.period && <span className=\"text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{plan.period}</span>}\n                  </p>\n                  {cta(plan, \"small\")}\n                </div>\n              </th>\n            ))}\n          </tr>\n        </thead>\n\n        {groups.map((group) => {\n          const open = !collapsed.has(group.id);\n          const rowIds = group.rows.map((row) => `${uid}-${group.id}-${row.id}`);\n          const GroupIcon = group.icon;\n          return (\n            <tbody key={group.id}>\n              <tr>\n                <th scope=\"colgroup\" colSpan={plans.length + 1} className=\"px-0 pt-6 pb-1\">\n                  <button\n                    type=\"button\"\n                    aria-expanded={open}\n                    aria-controls={rowIds.join(\" \")}\n                    onClick={() => toggle(group.id)}\n                    className=\"flex w-full cursor-pointer items-center gap-2.5 rounded-xl bg-background-secondary-default px-3 py-2.5 text-left outline-none transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-hover focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring motion-reduce:transition-none @3xl:px-4\"\n                  >\n                    {GroupIcon && <GroupIcon aria-hidden className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" />}\n                    <span className=\"text-body-semibold text-text-primary\">{group.title}</span>\n                    <span className=\"text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">{group.rows.length}</span>\n                    <RiArrowDownSLine\n                      aria-hidden\n                      className={cx(\n                        \"ml-auto size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-tertiary transition-transform duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                        !open && \"-rotate-90\",\n                      )}\n                    />\n                  </button>\n                </th>\n              </tr>\n              {open &&\n                group.rows.map((row, i) => (\n                  <tr\n                    key={row.id}\n                    id={rowIds[i]}\n                    className=\"transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out starting:opacity-0 motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                  >\n                    <th scope=\"row\" className=\"border-b border-separator-border px-3 py-3 align-middle font-normal @3xl:px-4\">\n                      <span className=\"block text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{row.label}</span>\n                      {row.hint && <span className=\"mt-0.5 block text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{row.hint}</span>}\n                    </th>\n                    {plans.map((plan) => (\n                      <td\n                        key={plan.id}\n                        className={cx(\"border-b border-separator-border px-3 py-3 align-middle @3xl:px-4\", planCol(plan))}\n                      >\n                        <ValueCell value={row.values[plan.id]} />\n                      </td>\n                    ))}\n                  </tr>\n                ))}\n            </tbody>\n          );\n        })}\n\n        <tfoot>\n          <tr>\n            <td className=\"px-3 pt-4 pb-5 @3xl:px-4\" />\n            {plans.map((plan) => (\n              <td key={plan.id} className={cx(\"rounded-b-2xl px-3 pt-4 pb-5 @3xl:px-4\", planCol(plan))}>\n                {cta(plan, \"xs\")}\n              </td>\n            ))}\n          </tr>\n        </tfoot>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: the block uses only React state and CSS. No listeners, timers or observers are created.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the row fade, the chevron rotation and the toggle hover colour all use `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so groups open and close instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Wrapping the table in `overflow: auto` or `overflow: hidden` for a horizontal scroll or rounded clip. That ancestor becomes the sticky containing scroller and the header stops sticking; the block switches columns with container queries instead.\n- Using `border-collapse: collapse`. Borders of sticky cells disappear while stuck in Chromium; `border-separate border-spacing-0` keeps them.\n- Collapsing rows with `opacity: 0` or a zero height. They stay in the accessibility tree and screen readers still read them; collapsed rows here are not rendered at all.\n- A transparent sticky header. Rows would show through it; it needs an opaque background, with the highlighted tint on an inner element.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Set `stickyTop` to the height of a fixed site header so the plan header docks beneath it.\n- Values are keyed by plan id, so a plan without an entry in a row shows a dash.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px four plan columns show under a header with names, prices and CTAs; the Team column is tinted with a \"Popular\" pill. Scrolling the page keeps the header row pinned at the top. Clicking the \"Support\" group toggle reveals its three rows and sets `aria-expanded=\"true\"`; clicking \"Components & blocks\" hides its five rows. Checks, dashes and text values (\"3\", \"While subscribed\") render in the right cells. At 360px only one plan column shows and the segmented control switches it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "plan-usage-card": "# Build: Plan Usage Card\n\nA compact usage tile for a subscription: the plan name in the header, one row per limit (a 5-hour window, weekly caps) with its label, when it resets and the share used, a 4px meter under each row, and a footer with the renewal date and an Upgrade button. Meters change colour as they fill so the limit that will stop you first stands out. `bare` drops the surface so the same rows can sit under a context-window card in one \"Agent limits\" tile.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA plan's rolling limits as thin meters with reset times, turning amber then red as they fill, plus renewal date and upgrade.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Clamp each limit's `used` to 0–100 and round it; pick a tone from two thresholds: `accent` below `warnAt` (70), `warning` from 70, `error` from `criticalAt` (90).\n2. Render every row as a react-aria `Meter` with `aria-label` = the limit label and a `valueLabel` that reads \"38% used, Resets in 2 hr 46 min\", so screen readers get the number and the reset together while the visible row splits them into label, muted reset text and a tabular percentage.\n3. Draw the bar with the kit `ProgressTrack` at size `sm` (4px). Its fill paints `bg-current`, so the tone is just a text colour class on the track; the fill slides with a 500ms transform transition when usage changes.\n4. Colour the percentage figure with the same tone once it leaves `accent`, with a 300ms colour transition, so amber and red show in the number as well as the bar.\n5. Render the footer only when `renews` or `onUpgrade` is given: a hairline top border, the renewal text on the left and a primary `xs` Button with a trailing arrow on the right. Omitting `onUpgrade` (e.g. on the top plan) removes the button.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `tone thresholds = warnAt 70, criticalAt 90`\n  Amber leaves time to act before a limit runs out; red at 90 matches the point where one long agent run can end the window.\n- `bar height = ProgressTrack size sm (4px)`\n  Same hairline as the context window bar above it in the Agent limits tile, so the two read as one family.\n- `percent column = w-9, text-end, tabular-nums`\n  A fixed column keeps \"3%\" and \"100%\" right-aligned so the reset texts line up row to row.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PlanUsageCard(props: { plan: ReactNode; limits: PlanLimit[]; title?: ReactNode; renews?: ReactNode; onUpgrade?: () => void; upgradeLabel?: ReactNode; warnAt?: number; criticalAt?: number; bare?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype PlanLimit = { id: string; label: string; used: number; resets?: string }\n```\n\n- `plan: ReactNode`\n  Plan name shown after the title, e.g. \"Max (5x)\".\n- `limits: PlanLimit[]`\n  One meter row per limit: label, percent used (0–100) and an optional reset text.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Plan usage limits\"`\n  Header title before the plan name.\n- `renews: ReactNode`\n  Renewal line in the footer, e.g. \"Renews Dec 1\".\n- `onUpgrade: () => void`\n  Shows the Upgrade button and is called when it is pressed.\n- `upgradeLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Upgrade\"`\n  Upgrade button label.\n- `warnAt: number` — default `70`\n  Percent at which a meter turns amber.\n- `criticalAt: number` — default `90`\n  Percent at which a meter turns red.\n- `bare: boolean` — default `false`\n  Drop the card surface and padding to nest inside another tile.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function PlanUsageCard({\n  plan,\n  limits,\n  title = \"Plan usage limits\",\n  renews,\n  onUpgrade,\n  upgradeLabel = \"Upgrade\",\n  warnAt = 70,\n  criticalAt = 90,\n  bare = false,\n  className,\n}: PlanUsageCardProps) {\n  const toneOf = (pct: number): Tone => (pct >= criticalAt ? \"error\" : pct >= warnAt ? \"warning\" : \"accent\");\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-3\",\n        !bare && \"rounded-[14px] bg-background-secondary-default p-3.5\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <h3 className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5 text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">\n        <span className=\"truncate\">{title}</span>\n        <span aria-hidden>·</span>\n        <span className=\"shrink-0 text-text-primary\">{plan}</span>\n      </h3>\n\n      <ul className=\"flex flex-col gap-3\">\n        {limits.map((limit) => {\n          const pct = Math.round(Math.min(100, Math.max(0, limit.used)));\n          const tone = toneOf(pct);\n          return (\n            <li key={limit.id}>\n              <AriaMeter\n                value={pct}\n                aria-label={limit.label}\n                valueLabel={limit.resets ? `${pct}% used, ${limit.resets}` : `${pct}% used`}\n                className=\"flex flex-col gap-1.5\"\n              >\n                {({ percentage }) => (\n                  <>\n                    <div className=\"flex items-baseline gap-2 text-caption-1-medium\">\n                      <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-text-primary\">{limit.label}</span>\n                      {limit.resets ? (\n                        <span className=\"shrink-0 truncate text-text-tertiary\">{limit.resets}</span>\n                      ) : null}\n                      <span\n                        className={cx(\n                          \"w-9 shrink-0 text-end text-caption-1-semibold tabular-nums transition-colors duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                          figure[tone],\n                        )}\n                      >\n                        {pct}%\n                      </span>\n                    </div>\n                    <ProgressTrack percentage={percentage} size=\"sm\" className={fill[tone]} />\n                  </>\n                )}\n              </AriaMeter>\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n\n      {renews != null || onUpgrade ? (\n        <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-3 border-t border-border-table pt-3\">\n          <span className=\"min-w-0 truncate text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary\">{renews}</span>\n          {onUpgrade ? (\n            <Button variant=\"primary\" size=\"xs\" trailingIcon={RiArrowRightUpLine} onClick={onUpgrade}>\n              {upgradeLabel}\n            </Button>\n          ) : null}\n        </div>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: the component is stateless and its only motion is CSS transitions.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The bar fill and the percentage colour carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so new usage values jump into place without a slide or a colour fade.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building the bar from a bare div with a width style. You lose the meter role and its value text; the RAC Meter gives both for free.\n- Putting the reset time only in the visible row. Screen readers announce the meter value without it unless it is part of `valueLabel`.\n- Signalling the limit state with colour alone. The percentage stays printed next to every bar, so amber and red are reinforcement, not the only cue.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Nest it with `bare` as `children` of the Context Window Card to get the full Agent limits tile, as the demo does.\n- `used` is a percentage on purpose: plan limits are rarely published as token counts, and the reset text carries the units that matter.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo shows \"Plan usage limits · Max (5x)\" with rows at 38%, 3% and 5%. Each \"Send a prompt\" adds 9 points to the 5-hour row: it turns amber at 74% and red at 92%, and at 100% the button disables and the status reads \"5-hour limit reached\". \"Upgrade\" switches the header to Max (20x), quarters every value and removes the button.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pointer-palette-sampling": "# Build: Pointer Palette Sampling\n\nA surface you paint into a 2D canvas, plus a pointer that reads the pixel underneath it and snaps that read to the nearest entry in a named palette. The colour comes out as a CSS custom property on the root and as a throttled callback, so a chip can follow the cursor with no JavaScript per frame. It turns a palette from a legend you read into something you find by pointing.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPoint at the artwork and it names the colour — a clamped pixel read snapped to the nearest palette entry.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: canvas2d\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The canvas is created with `document.createElement` inside the effect and appended to the root, not rendered with a React `ref`. `willReadFrequently` is only honoured on the first `getContext` for a given canvas, so the element and the context have to share one lifecycle — under StrictMode a rendered canvas survives the mount/unmount/remount cycle while the context does not.\n2. On resize the backing store is set to `round(cssSize * min(devicePixelRatio, 2))` and the transform re-applied with `setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0)`, in that order, because assigning `canvas.width` resets both the bitmap and the transform. The consumer's `paint` then draws in CSS pixels.\n3. A `pointermove` converts client coordinates to bitmap coordinates with the canvas's own backing ratio — `canvas.width / rect.width` — rather than `devicePixelRatio`, so browser zoom and any CSS scale on an ancestor stay correct.\n4. A 3x3 box centred on that pixel is clamped to `[0, canvas.width - 1]` and `[0, canvas.height - 1]`, then read in one `getImageData` call. The nine samples are averaged with alpha as the weight, so a dithered or grainy surface produces a stable colour instead of oscillating between two palette entries.\n5. The average and every palette hex are converted sRGB → linear → OKLab, and the match is the smallest squared OKLab distance. Nearest-colour in raw RGB is dominated by the green channel and routinely calls a mid blue \"black\".\n6. The match is written to `--kx-sample-color` on the root immediately, along with `--kx-sample-x` / `--kx-sample-y` in CSS pixels, and emitted through `onSample` on a throttle — position is a cursor and must not lag, colour is information and must not spam.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `willReadFrequently = true`\n  A default 2D context lives in GPU memory, and each `getImageData` forces a synchronous readback that stalls the pipeline — on a pointermove that is a visible drag on the cursor. The flag moves the surface to a software backing: paint is marginally slower, reads are roughly an order of magnitude faster.\n- `SAMPLE_BOX = 3`\n  Odd, so the box centres on the pointer's own pixel. One pixel flickers between neighbouring palette entries over any gradient dither; 3x3 is enough to settle it, and 5x5 starts averaging across a real colour boundary so edges read as a colour that is not on screen.\n- `MAX_DPR = 2`\n  Sampling cost is per read and does not care, but paint cost is per pixel. A 3x display would quadruple the fill work relative to 1.5x for no perceptible gain on a flat-colour artwork.\n- `throttleMs = 50`\n  Matches the source. Fast enough that dragging across the artwork feels continuous, slow enough that a React consumer re-rendering a swatch on every emission stays under one render per three frames.\n- `alpha weight = data[i + 3] / 255`\n  Transparent pixels carry no colour but their RGB is usually zero, so an unweighted mean over a partly-transparent box returns something darker than anything visible. Weighting by alpha, and bailing when the total is zero, keeps the answer to what was actually painted.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PointerPaletteSampling(props: { palette: readonly PaletteEntry[]; paint: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, size: { width: number; height: number; dpr: number }) => void; paintKey?: string | number; onSample?: (entry: PaletteEntry | null) => void; throttleMs?: number; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `palette: readonly PaletteEntry[]`\n  Named colours the read snaps to. `{ name, hex }`, `#rgb` or `#rrggbb`.\n- `paint: (ctx, size) => void`\n  Draws the surface. Context is pre-scaled and cleared, so draw in CSS pixels. Must not draw a cross-origin image.\n- `paintKey: string | number`\n  Bump to force a repaint when the painter's inputs change. The painter itself is read through a ref, so an inline arrow is safe.\n- `onSample: (entry: PaletteEntry | null) => void`\n  Throttled match. `null` when the pointer leaves.\n- `throttleMs: number` — default `50`\n  Minimum gap between emissions.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Overlay content, rendered in a positioned layer above the canvas.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root. Give it a size.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction parseHex(hex: string): readonly [number, number, number] | null {\n  const raw = hex.trim().replace(/^#/, '');\n  const full =\n    raw.length === 3\n      ? raw\n          .split('')\n          .map((c) => c + c)\n          .join('')\n      : raw;\n  if (!/^[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/.test(full)) return null;\n  const n = Number.parseInt(full, 16);\n  return [(n >> 16) & 255, (n >> 8) & 255, n & 255];\n}\n\nfunction srgbToLinear(channel: number): number {\n  const v = channel / 255;\n  return v <= 0.04045 ? v / 12.92 : Math.pow((v + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4);\n}\n\n/**\n * sRGB to OKLab. Nearest-colour in raw RGB is the usual shortcut and it is\n * wrong in a way users notice: RGB distance is dominated by green, so a mid\n * blue reads closer to black than to a lighter blue. OKLab is perceptually\n * uniform, so \"nearest\" means what the eye means.\n */\nfunction rgbToOklab(r: number, g: number, b: number): Oklab {\n  const lr = srgbToLinear(r);\n  const lg = srgbToLinear(g);\n  const lb = srgbToLinear(b);\n\n  const l = Math.cbrt(0.4122214708 * lr + 0.5363325363 * lg + 0.0514459929 * lb);\n  const m = Math.cbrt(0.2119034982 * lr + 0.6806995451 * lg + 0.1073969566 * lb);\n  const s = Math.cbrt(0.0883024619 * lr + 0.2817188376 * lg + 0.6299787005 * lb);\n\n  return [\n    0.2104542553 * l + 0.793617785 * m - 0.0040720468 * s,\n    1.9779984951 * l - 2.428592205 * m + 0.4505937099 * s,\n    0.0259040371 * l + 0.7827717662 * m - 0.808675766 * s,\n  ];\n}\n\nexport function PointerPaletteSampling({\n  palette,\n  paint,\n  paintKey,\n  onSample,\n  throttleMs = 50,\n  children,\n  className,\n}: PointerPaletteSamplingProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(root);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // The canvas is created imperatively rather than rendered with a `ref`.\n  // Under StrictMode a rendered canvas is the same element across the\n  // mount / unmount / remount cycle, so the second mount inherits a context\n  // whose `willReadFrequently` was decided on the first `getContext` — and any\n  // teardown that disposes the context leaves the survivor holding a dead one.\n  // Owning the element per effect run makes the two lifecycles the same.\n  const [surface, setSurface] = useState<{\n    canvas: HTMLCanvasElement;\n    ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D;\n  } | null>(null);\n\n  // Read through refs so an inline painter or handler does not tear the canvas\n  // down on every parent render.\n  const paintRef = useRef(paint);\n  const sampleRef = useRef(onSample);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    paintRef.current = paint;\n    sampleRef.current = onSample;\n  });\n\n  /** Palette in OKLab, computed once. Entries with an unparseable hex drop out. */\n  const swatches = useMemo(() => {\n    const out: { entry: PaletteEntry; lab: Oklab }[] = [];\n    for (const entry of palette) {\n      const rgb = parseHex(entry.hex);\n      if (!rgb) continue;\n      out.push({ entry, lab: rgbToOklab(rgb[0], rgb[1], rgb[2]) });\n    }\n    return out;\n  }, [palette]);\n\n  /* Mount the canvas. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl) return;\n\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');\n    canvas.style.cssText =\n      'position:absolute;inset:0;display:block;width:100%;height:100%';\n\n    // The one call where the flag counts.\n    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d', { willReadFrequently: true });\n    if (!ctx) return;\n\n    rootEl.appendChild(canvas);\n    setSurface({ canvas, ctx });\n\n    return () => {\n      canvas.remove();\n      // Guard the swap: on a StrictMode remount the next effect has already\n      // published its canvas by the time this cleanup runs in some orderings.\n      setSurface((current) => (current?.canvas === canvas ? null : current));\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  /* Size and paint. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!surface || width <= 0 || height <= 0) return;\n    const { canvas, ctx } = surface;\n\n    const dpr = Math.min(\n      typeof window === 'undefined' ? 1 : window.devicePixelRatio || 1,\n      MAX_DPR,\n    );\n    // Assigning width or height resets the bitmap AND the transform, so the\n    // scale has to be re-applied after, never before.\n    canvas.width = Math.max(1, Math.round(width * dpr));\n    canvas.height = Math.max(1, Math.round(height * dpr));\n    ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);\n    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height);\n\n    paintRef.current(ctx, { width, height, dpr });\n  }, [surface, width, height, paintKey]);\n\n  /* Sample. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl || !surface || swatches.length === 0) return;\n    const { canvas, ctx } = surface;\n\n    let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;\n    let pendingIsClear = false;\n\n    /**\n     * The source's throttle, kept verbatim in behaviour: while an emission is\n     * pending, a new colour is DROPPED rather than queued, so the consumer sees\n     * one colour per window instead of a burst at the end of one. The single\n     * exception is that a pending clear may be replaced — re-entering the\n     * canvas within the window cancels the leave instead of flickering to\n     * nothing and back.\n     */\n    const emit = (entry: PaletteEntry | null, isClear: boolean) => {\n      if (timer !== null && !pendingIsClear) return;\n      pendingIsClear = isClear;\n      if (timer !== null) clearTimeout(timer);\n      timer = setTimeout(() => {\n        timer = null;\n        pendingIsClear = false;\n        sampleRef.current?.(entry);\n      }, throttleMs);\n    };\n\n    const sampleAt = (clientX: number, clientY: number): PaletteEntry | null => {\n      const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (rect.width <= 0 || rect.height <= 0) return null;\n\n      // Backing ratio, not devicePixelRatio: this is still correct under\n      // browser zoom and under a CSS transform on any ancestor.\n      const scaleX = canvas.width / rect.width;\n      const scaleY = canvas.height / rect.height;\n\n      const px = Math.round((clientX - rect.left) * scaleX);\n      const py = Math.round((clientY - rect.top) * scaleY);\n      const half = (SAMPLE_BOX - 1) / 2;\n\n      // Clamp to the bitmap. An unclamped box at the right or bottom edge is\n      // padded with transparent black, which pulls the average to black.\n      const x0 = clamp(px - half, 0, canvas.width - 1);\n      const y0 = clamp(py - half, 0, canvas.height - 1);\n      const x1 = clamp(px + half, 0, canvas.width - 1);\n      const y1 = clamp(py + half, 0, canvas.height - 1);\n      const w = x1 - x0 + 1;\n      const h = y1 - y0 + 1;\n      if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) return null;\n\n      const data = ctx.getImageData(x0, y0, w, h).data;\n\n      // Alpha-weighted mean over the box. A single pixel flickers between two\n      // palette entries on any dithered or noisy surface; nine pixels do not.\n      let r = 0;\n      let g = 0;\n      let b = 0;\n      let weight = 0;\n      for (let i = 0; i + 3 < data.length; i += 4) {\n        const a = (data[i + 3] ?? 0) / 255;\n        if (a === 0) continue;\n        r += (data[i] ?? 0) * a;\n        g += (data[i + 1] ?? 0) * a;\n        b += (data[i + 2] ?? 0) * a;\n        weight += a;\n      }\n      // Fully transparent: there is no colour here, and dividing would be NaN.\n      if (weight <= 0) return null;\n\n      const lab = rgbToOklab(r / weight, g / weight, b / weight);\n      let best: PaletteEntry | null = null;\n      let bestDistance = Infinity;\n      for (const swatch of swatches) {\n        const dl = lab[0] - swatch.lab[0];\n        const da = lab[1] - swatch.lab[1];\n        const db = lab[2] - swatch.lab[2];\n        const distance = dl * dl + da * da + db * db;\n        if (distance < bestDistance) {\n          bestDistance = distance;\n          best = swatch.entry;\n        }\n      }\n      return best;\n    };\n\n    const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const entry = sampleAt(event.clientX, event.clientY);\n      if (!entry) return;\n\n      rootEl.dataset.sampling = 'true';\n      rootEl.style.setProperty('--kx-sample-color', entry.hex);\n      // The colour is throttled because it is information; the position is not,\n      // because it is a cursor. Throttling the position makes the readout lag\n      // the pointer by up to a frame budget and feel broken.\n      if (!reduced) {\n        const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n        rootEl.style.setProperty('--kx-sample-x', `${event.clientX - rect.left}px`);\n        rootEl.style.setProperty('--kx-sample-y', `${event.clientY - rect.top}px`);\n      }\n      emit(entry, false);\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      rootEl.dataset.sampling = 'false';\n      emit(null, true);\n    };\n\n    // Pointer events already carry touch; `touch-action: none` on the root\n    // stops a drag over the artwork from scrolling the page out from under it.\n    rootEl.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n    rootEl.addEventListener('pointerdown', onMove);\n    rootEl.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n    rootEl.addEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave);\n\n    return () => {\n      rootEl.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      rootEl.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onMove);\n      rootEl.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      rootEl.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave);\n      if (timer !== null) clearTimeout(timer);\n      timer = null;\n      rootEl.dataset.sampling = 'false';\n    };\n  }, [surface, swatches, throttleMs, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The canvas element is removed from the DOM in the same effect that created it, which drops the bitmap and the context with it. The four pointer listeners are removed, any pending throttle timer is cleared so no emission lands after unmount, and `data-sampling` is reset. The shared ResizeObserver unsubscribes through the hook.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Sampling is not motion, so it keeps working: the colour still updates and `onSample` still fires. What stops is the chase — `--kx-sample-x` and `--kx-sample-y` are left parked at their initial 50% so a readout pinned to those properties stays still and simply recolours, instead of flying around the frame after the cursor.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `getContext(\"2d\")` first and adding `{ willReadFrequently: true }` later. The attributes of a canvas context are fixed at creation; the second call returns the first context and silently ignores the options, so the optimisation appears to be applied and is not.\n- Reading the box unclamped. A pointer on the right or bottom edge produces a rect that overhangs the bitmap — no exception, the overhang is filled with transparent black — so the average darkens and the match snaps to the palette's darkest entry along every border.\n- Letting the clamp collapse the rect. `getImageData` with a zero width or height throws `IndexSizeError`, which is what happens on a 1px-wide canvas if the box is clamped naively to a range instead of to an inclusive index pair.\n- Converting with `devicePixelRatio` instead of the backing ratio. Correct at 100% zoom on a static layout, then off by the zoom factor the moment the user hits Cmd-plus, and off by the scale factor inside any transformed ancestor.\n- Drawing a cross-origin image into the canvas. The canvas is tainted from that draw onward and every `getImageData` throws `SecurityError` — including for the parts of the surface you painted yourself. Paint procedurally, or serve the image same-origin with CORS.\n- Matching in RGB. Euclidean distance in sRGB is not perceptual: green outweighs red and blue roughly six to one, so mid-tones snap to the wrong hue and the palette looks mislabelled rather than the maths looking wrong.\n- Rendering the canvas as JSX with a `ref` and disposing the context in cleanup. Under StrictMode the element is reused across the double-invoke while the effect runs twice, so the surviving mount holds a context the first teardown already reset.\n- Throttling the pointer position along with the colour. The readout then trails the cursor by the throttle window and the whole interaction reads as laggy, even though the sampling itself is instant.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Everything downstream is CSS. `--kx-sample-color` and `--kx-sample-x` / `--kx-sample-y` mean a chip can follow the pointer and recolour itself with no rAF and no React render.\n- `data-sampling` on the root is `true` while the pointer is over the artwork, which is the hook for fading the readout in and out.\n- Palette entries with an unparseable hex are dropped at match time rather than throwing, so one bad token in a design-token export does not take the interaction down.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove the pointer across the artwork: the readout recolours continuously and names a palette entry, with no stutter under a fast drag. Park the cursor exactly on the right or bottom edge — the name must not jump to the darkest colour in the palette. Zoom the browser to 150% and point at the same region; the answer is unchanged. With reduced motion on, the readout stops following the pointer but still renames itself.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pointer-var-gradient": "# Build: Pointer-Variable Gradient\n\nA radial gradient declared once in CSS reads its centre from `--pointer-x` and `--pointer-y`. The pointer handler does nothing but write those two properties as percentages. There is no animation frame, no tween library and no `background` string assembled in JavaScript.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA soft glow tracks the pointer through two CSS custom properties. No rAF, no tween, no string rebuilding.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The gradient — its size, colour, stops and the layer underneath it — is declared once on the element. The only thing JavaScript ever writes is two custom properties, so the style engine invalidates the paint of one gradient rather than re-parsing a `background` shorthand it has already parsed thousands of times.\n2. Values are written as percentages, not pixels. A percentage in a gradient position resolves against the element box, so the glow stays put relative to the panel across a resize with no resize listener and no recomputation — the value that was correct before the resize is still correct after it.\n3. There is deliberately no `requestAnimationFrame`. A custom-property write is not a layout read, so there is nothing to batch away from; the browser already coalesces style changes into the next paint. Wrapping this in a frame loop adds one frame of latency and buys nothing measurable.\n4. There is deliberately no tween. Smoothing the glow would mean owning a value between the pointer and the gradient, which means a frame loop, which is the cost this component exists to avoid. A 38rem glow is so diffuse that a few pixels of jitter are invisible — the smoothing would be paying for something no one can see.\n5. Leaving removes the properties rather than writing the rest position back, so the fallback declared in the inline `style` takes over and the resting position is stated in exactly one place.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `REST_X / REST_Y = '50%' / '38%'`\n  Where the glow sits before any pointer has been seen, and where it returns to on leave. Slightly above centre because a glow that sits dead-centre behind a headline reads as a lighting mistake, while one a little high reads as a source above the frame.\n- `glowWidth / glowHeight = '38rem' / '30rem'`\n  Wider than tall, matching the aspect of most content blocks. Sizing in rem rather than % means the glow keeps its physical size on a small panel instead of shrinking to a hotspot — a percentage-sized gradient in a 320px preview looks like a different effect entirely.\n- `intensity = 0.09`\n  Peak accent opacity at the centre. Under ~0.05 it is indistinguishable from a gradient banding artefact; over ~0.2 it stops being ambient light and becomes a coloured blob that competes with the text it is behind.\n- `transparent 68% = 68%`\n  Where the glow reaches zero. Ending short of 100% keeps the falloff inside the gradient box so the edge is never visible as a circle boundary against the panel edge.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PointerVarGradient(props: { children?: ReactNode; glowWidth?: string; glowHeight?: string; intensity?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content rendered over the gradient.\n- `glowWidth: string` — default `'38rem'`\n  Horizontal radius of the glow, any CSS length.\n- `glowHeight: string` — default `'30rem'`\n  Vertical radius of the glow, any CSS length.\n- `intensity: number` — default `0.09`\n  Peak accent opacity at the glow centre, 0..1.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Where the glow sits before the pointer has ever been seen. */\nconst REST_X = '50%';\nconst REST_Y = '38%';\n\nexport function PointerVarGradient({\n  children,\n  glowWidth = '38rem',\n  glowHeight = '30rem',\n  intensity = 0.09,\n  className,\n}: PointerVarGradientProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n\n    /* Fine pointers only. On touch a `pointermove` exists only during a drag,\n       so the glow would be pinned under the finger that is scrolling the page\n       and then abandoned there on lift — worse than not moving at all. The\n       rest position set in the inline style stays, so touch users still get a\n       composed gradient. */\n    if (!window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) return;\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      /* Percentages, not px. The gradient position resolves against the\n         element box, so a percentage survives a resize with no listener and\n         no recalculation — the glow stays where it was relative to the panel\n         even if the panel changes size between moves. */\n      el.style.setProperty(\n        '--pointer-x',\n        `${((e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width) * 100}%`,\n      );\n      el.style.setProperty(\n        '--pointer-y',\n        `${((e.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * 100}%`,\n      );\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      /* Removing the property rather than writing the rest value back lets the\n         inline `style` fallback take over, so the resting position is declared\n         in exactly one place. */\n      el.style.removeProperty('--pointer-x');\n      el.style.removeProperty('--pointer-y');\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      el.style.removeProperty('--pointer-x');\n      el.style.removeProperty('--pointer-y');\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  /* The gradient definition lives here and only here. The handler above never\n     mentions colours, sizes or stops — it only moves the centre.\n\n     No `transition`: an unregistered custom property is an untyped token, so\n     the browser cannot interpolate it and a transition on the gradient would\n     silently do nothing. Making the leave ease would mean registering the\n     properties with `@property { syntax: '<percentage>' }` in a stylesheet,\n     which is a real option but not a free one. The snap back is acceptable\n     here because the glow at rest is barely above the background. */\n  const style = {\n    '--pointer-x': REST_X,\n    '--pointer-y': REST_Y,\n    backgroundImage: `radial-gradient(${glowWidth} ${glowHeight} at var(--pointer-x) var(--pointer-y), color-mix(in oklab, var(--k-accent) ${Math.round(\n      intensity * 100,\n    )}%, transparent), transparent 68%)`,\n  } as CSSProperties;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the two pointer listeners and removes both custom properties from the element, so a remount starts from the declared rest position rather than inheriting the last pointer location. Nothing else was ever allocated — no frame loop, no tween, no observer.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Unchanged, and intentionally so. Nothing here animates: the gradient centre tracks the pointer one-to-one with no easing, no inertia and no continuing motion after input stops. Suppressing it would remove a hover affordance rather than a motion effect.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Rebuilding the whole `background` string in JavaScript on every move. The browser re-parses every layer, including the static one, and you have moved a CSS declaration into a hot event handler where it will drift out of sync with the stylesheet.\n- Expecting a `transition` on the gradient to smooth the leave. An unregistered custom property is an untyped token and cannot be interpolated, so the transition silently does nothing and there is no warning anywhere. Smoothing requires `@property { syntax: \"<percentage>\"; inherits: false; initial-value: 50% }` in a real stylesheet.\n- Writing pixel values instead of percentages. They are correct only until the element resizes, at which point the glow is offset by however much the box changed and stays wrong until the next pointer move.\n- Wrapping the write in `requestAnimationFrame`. It adds a frame of latency to a write that was never the bottleneck. rAF batching is for handlers that read layout; this one does not.\n- Setting the properties on `document.documentElement` instead of the element. Every subtree that uses the same variable names then responds to a pointer that is nowhere near it, and two instances on one page fight over the same two globals.\n- Reading `getBoundingClientRect()` and caching it. The rect is cheap here because nothing in this component writes layout, so there is no forced reflow to avoid — but a cached rect goes stale on scroll, which is the actual bug people ship trying to optimise this.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch no listener attaches at all: `pointermove` only fires mid-drag, so the glow would follow the finger that is scrolling and be abandoned wherever it lifted. The inline rest position remains, so touch users get a composed static gradient rather than a blank panel.\n- No reduced-motion branch is needed and none is present. There is no animation to reduce — the glow does not move on its own, does not ease, and does not persist after the pointer leaves. It is a hover state that happens to be continuous.\n- The glow colour is `color-mix(in oklab, var(--k-accent) N%, transparent)` so it follows the category accent and the light/dark theme without a second code path.\n- This is the cheapest component in the cursor category. If an effect can be expressed this way it should be, and the interesting question about any pointer effect is why it cannot.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove across the panel and a soft accent glow follows the pointer exactly, with no lag and no easing. Leave and it snaps back above centre. Resize the panel while the pointer is stationary and the glow stays in the same relative position rather than sliding. Open DevTools and confirm the element has exactly two inline custom properties changing and that no `background` or `background-image` value is being rewritten. On touch the glow never moves.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pooled-image-trail": "# Build: Pooled Cursor Image Trail\n\nSweeping the pointer across the panel drops a trail of tiles behind it, each fading up over 220ms, holding, and fading out — total life 520ms. The tiles come from a fixed ring of 22 nodes created once at mount and reused by index, and a new one is only released when the pointer has travelled more than 130px since the last. Those two rules are the component; the tween values are decoration.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTiles drop behind the cursor from a fixed ring of 22 reused nodes, one every 130px of travel.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A ring of `poolSize` absolutely-positioned canvases is rendered once, all with `will-change: transform` so layer promotion has already happened before the first spawn. Nothing is ever created or removed after mount.\n2. `plateCount` gradient plates are painted into offscreen canvases at mount — the registry ships no image assets, so the \"photographs\" are drawn. A spawn is then a single `drawImage` blit, which keeps the gradient and text work off the frame where a tween starts.\n3. On `pointermove` the handler measures `Math.hypot(x - anchor.x, y - anchor.y)` against the last spawn point and returns early below `threshold`. The very first move only seeds the anchor and spawns nothing, so entering from an edge does not drop a tile before any real movement has happened.\n4. A spawn takes slot `spawned % poolSize`, picks a plate with a seeded RNG plus a one-line no-repeat guard (`if (i === last) i = (i + 1) % n`), blits it, then `gsap.killTweensOf(node)` before `gsap.set` reinitialises x, y, rotation, scale 0.92, opacity 0 and `zIndex: spawned`.\n5. `zIndex` is the monotonically increasing spawn counter, so the newest tile always paints over older ones with no stacking logic at all. At ten spawns a second it would take roughly 285,000 years to reach `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`.\n6. The life is one timeline: in over 220ms with `power2.out`, hold 100ms, out over 200ms with `power2.in`. The pool is sized so a slot's 520ms life always expires before the ring wraps back around to it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `threshold = 130`\n  Pixels of travel per tile, and the single most load-bearing number here. A deliberate sweep across a hero yields 8-10 readable tiles; small jitters yield none. Drop it toward 20 and a flick produces a solid smear of overlapping tiles; raise it past ~250 and normal mouse movement never triggers the effect at all.\n- `poolSize = 22`\n  The ring must not wrap while a tile is still visible. At 520ms of life you would have to move 22 x 130 = 2860px in 520ms to catch your own tail — a flick nobody performs by accident.\n- `plateCount = 17`\n  Deliberately coprime with the 22 slots, so the plate-to-slot pairing never settles into a short repeating pattern as the ring advances. Also fewer plates than slots, so the memory cost is bounded independently of pool size.\n- `IN_DURATION / HOLD / OUT_DURATION = 0.22 / 0.1 / 0.2`\n  520ms total. Fast in with `power2.out` (arrives), a beat of stillness so the tile is actually readable, then a `power2.in` exit (leaves). Collapse the hold and the trail reads as a blur instead of as a sequence of pictures.\n- `START_SCALE = 0.92`\n  An 8% growth on entry. Enough to read as arriving, small enough not to pop — at 0.7 it looks like a modal opening.\n- `TILT = 7`\n  Degrees of seeded rotation either way. Perfectly square tiles read as UI panels; a few degrees reads as prints dropped on a table. Past ~12 the corners of successive tiles collide and the trail looks untidy.\n- `PLATE_SCALE = 2`\n  Backing-store multiplier for the plate canvases. Fixed rather than `devicePixelRatio` so the drawn output is byte-identical on every machine and a visual regression is actually visible.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PooledImageTrail(props: { children?: ReactNode; threshold?: number; poolSize?: number; plateCount?: number; tileWidth?: number; tileHeight?: number; seed?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content rendered above the trail layer.\n- `threshold: number` — default `130`\n  Pixels of pointer travel required before another tile is released.\n- `poolSize: number` — default `22`\n  Number of reusable nodes in the ring.\n- `plateCount: number` — default `17`\n  Distinct plates painted at mount. Keep coprime with poolSize.\n- `tileWidth: number` — default `236`\n  Tile width in CSS px.\n- `tileHeight: number` — default `160`\n  Tile height in CSS px.\n- `seed: string` — default `'pooled-image-trail'`\n  Seeds plate choice and tilt so the sequence is deterministic.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst IN_DURATION = 0.22;\nconst HOLD = 0.1;\nconst OUT_DURATION = 0.2;\nconst START_SCALE = 0.92;\nconst TILT = 7;\n\nexport function PooledImageTrail({\n  children,\n  threshold = 130,\n  poolSize = 22,\n  plateCount = 17,\n  tileWidth = 236,\n  tileHeight = 160,\n  seed = 'pooled-image-trail',\n  className,\n}: PooledImageTrailProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const slots = useRef<Array<HTMLCanvasElement | null>>([]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n\n    const nodes: HTMLCanvasElement[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < poolSize; i++) {\n      const node = slots.current[i];\n      if (node) nodes.push(node);\n    }\n    if (nodes.length === 0) return;\n\n    const bw = tileWidth * PLATE_SCALE;\n    const bh = tileHeight * PLATE_SCALE;\n\n    // Paint every plate once, offscreen. A spawn is then a single blit; if the\n    // plate were drawn at spawn time the gradient + text work would land on\n    // the same frame as the tween start, which is the one frame that must not\n    // drop.\n    const plates: HTMLCanvasElement[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < plateCount; i++) {\n      const plate = document.createElement('canvas');\n      plate.width = bw;\n      plate.height = bh;\n      const pctx = plate.getContext('2d');\n      if (!pctx) break;\n      pctx.scale(PLATE_SCALE, PLATE_SCALE);\n      paintPlate(pctx, tileWidth, tileHeight, i);\n      plates.push(plate);\n    }\n    if (plates.length === 0) return;\n\n    const contexts = nodes.map((node) => {\n      node.width = bw;\n      node.height = bh;\n      return node.getContext('2d');\n    });\n\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;\n    const fine = window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches;\n\n    gsap.set(nodes, { xPercent: 0, yPercent: 0, opacity: 0, force3D: true });\n\n    if (reduced || !fine) {\n      // Poster fallback: three plates fanned in the middle, drawn once. The\n      // panel is composed rather than empty, and nothing ever moves.\n      const shown = Math.min(3, nodes.length, plates.length);\n      for (let i = 0; i < shown; i++) {\n        const ctx = contexts[i];\n        const plate = plates[i % plates.length];\n        const node = nodes[i];\n        if (!ctx || !plate || !node) continue;\n        ctx.drawImage(plate, 0, 0);\n        gsap.set(node, {\n          x: el.clientWidth / 2 - tileWidth / 2 + (i - 1) * tileWidth * 0.42,\n          y: el.clientHeight / 2 - tileHeight / 2 + (i - 1) * 14,\n          rotate: (i - 1) * 4,\n          opacity: 1,\n          zIndex: i,\n        });\n      }\n      return () => {\n        gsap.killTweensOf(nodes);\n        gsap.set(nodes, { clearProps: 'all' });\n      };\n    }\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    /** Monotonic. Doubles as the ring index and as the z-index. */\n    let spawned = 0;\n    /** Where the last tile was dropped. `null` until the first move seeds it. */\n    let anchor: { x: number; y: number } | null = null;\n    let lastPlate = -1;\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const x = e.clientX - rect.left;\n      const y = e.clientY - rect.top;\n\n      // First move seeds the anchor and spawns nothing. Otherwise the initial\n      // distance is measured from (0, 0) and entering from the right-hand side\n      // of a wide panel drops a tile immediately, before any real movement.\n      if (anchor === null) {\n        anchor = { x, y };\n        return;\n      }\n      if (Math.hypot(x - anchor.x, y - anchor.y) < threshold) return;\n      anchor = { x, y };\n\n      const slot = spawned % nodes.length;\n      const node = nodes[slot];\n      const ctx = contexts[slot];\n      if (!node || !ctx) return;\n      spawned++;\n\n      // One-line no-repeat guard. It does not attempt a uniform distribution,\n      // because the only thing the eye actually notices is the same picture\n      // twice in a row.\n      let plateIndex = Math.floor(rng() * plates.length);\n      if (plateIndex === lastPlate) plateIndex = (plateIndex + 1) % plates.length;\n      lastPlate = plateIndex;\n      const plate = plates[plateIndex];\n      if (!plate) return;\n\n      ctx.clearRect(0, 0, bw, bh);\n      ctx.drawImage(plate, 0, 0);\n\n      // Kill before reuse. A slot recycled while still fading out would carry\n      // two competing tweens on `opacity` and visibly flicker.\n      gsap.killTweensOf(node);\n      gsap.set(node, {\n        x: x - tileWidth / 2,\n        y: y - tileHeight / 2,\n        rotate: randRange(rng, -TILT, TILT),\n        scale: START_SCALE,\n        opacity: 0,\n        // Monotonic z-index: the newest tile always paints over older ones and\n        // there is no stacking logic to maintain.\n        zIndex: spawned,\n      });\n\n      gsap\n        .timeline()\n        .to(node, { opacity: 1, scale: 1, duration: IN_DURATION, ease: 'power2.out' })\n        .to(node, { opacity: 0, duration: OUT_DURATION, ease: 'power2.in' }, `+=${HOLD}`);\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      gsap.killTweensOf(nodes);\n      gsap.set(nodes, { clearProps: 'all' });\n    };\n  }, [threshold, poolSize, plateCount, tileWidth, tileHeight, seed]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the single `pointermove` listener, calls `gsap.killTweensOf` on every pool node — which also kills the per-spawn timelines, since they only target those nodes — then `gsap.set(nodes, { clearProps: \"all\" })`. The offscreen plate canvases are plain locals and are collected with the closure; nothing was allocated that needs revoking.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No listener attaches and nothing animates. Three plates are drawn once into the first three pool slots, fanned across the centre with a few degrees of rotation each, so the panel is a composed still of what the effect produces rather than an empty rectangle.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Creating an element per spawn. A fast sweep across a wide hero allocates a hundred nodes, each forcing a style recalc, a layer promotion and a paint at the exact moment the main thread is busiest — the trail stutters precisely when it is being used hardest.\n- Spawning on every `pointermove` instead of on distance travelled. Events fire on sub-pixel movement, so a flick produces a solid smear and a slow drift produces a dense clump; \"moved\" and \"moved enough\" are different predicates.\n- Measuring the first move against a zeroed anchor. The distance from (0, 0) to wherever the pointer entered is usually well over the threshold, so a tile fires the instant the pointer crosses the boundary, before any movement has happened.\n- Reusing a slot without `gsap.killTweensOf(node)` first. If the ring wraps onto a tile that is still fading out, two tweens fight over `opacity` and it flickers rather than restarting cleanly.\n- Recomputing z-order from tile ages. A monotonic counter is correct, free, and cannot get out of sync; sorting is a whole class of bug bought for nothing.\n- Painting the gradient plate at spawn time. The gradient fill, the radial sheen and the text layout all land on the same frame as the tween start, which is the one frame in the effect that must not drop. Paint once at mount and blit.\n- Omitting `will-change: transform` from the pool. The browser promotes the tile to its own layer partway through its 220ms entry, so the first frames of every spawn are visibly cheaper-looking than the rest.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch a `pointermove` only exists during a drag, so the trail would appear only under a finger that is already covering it and would then be stranded mid-life on lift. The touch path renders the same static fan as reduced motion.\n- The tiles sit behind the content layer (`z-0` under a `z-10` child wrapper) so headline copy stays readable while the trail passes under it.\n- Plate choice runs on `createRng(seed)`, never `Math.random()`, so the sequence is identical on every load and across a server render.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSweep the pointer slowly across the panel and tiles drop behind it roughly every 130px, each readable for about half a second before fading. Jiggle the pointer in place and nothing spawns. Flick as fast as you can and you get a spaced trail, not a smear, and no tile ever restarts mid-fade with a flicker. Enter the panel from an edge and no tile appears until you have actually moved. On touch or with reduced motion you see three static fanned plates.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "popover": "# Build: Popover\n\nA non-modal surface for a share link, a short form or the explanation behind an info icon, anchored to its trigger with React Aria's `DialogTrigger`, `Popover` and `Dialog`. It positions and flips itself, Escape closes it and focus returns to the trigger. It opens with the menu family's 150ms fade, 0.95 scale and 2px blur, grown from the point nearest the trigger, and can draw Tooltip's caret pointing back at it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nFree-form content anchored to a trigger, with an optional arrow, smart positioning and the menu family's motion, built on React Aria.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `PopoverTrigger` owns the open state (controlled `isOpen` or uncontrolled `defaultOpen`) and keeps two refs in context: `triggerRef` on a `Pressable` wrapped around the first child (so any kit button that forwards its ref works as the trigger) and `popoverRef`, which `Popover` attaches to the surface. The remaining children go through to `AriaDialogTrigger`.\n2. The popover is non-modal (`isNonModal` whenever it sits inside `PopoverTrigger`): React Aria's modal popovers lock page scroll and the reflow shunts sticky layout. Because non-modal popovers lose outside-press dismissal, `useDismissOnOutsidePress` adds a capture-phase `pointerdown` listener on `document` that closes it when a press lands outside both refs.\n3. `useTriggerToggle` watches for a `pointerdown` on the trigger while open and swallows the reopen that would follow, so `onOpenChange` is gated by `allowOpenChange(next)` and pressing the trigger again closes the popover instead of flickering it closed and open.\n4. `Popover` renders `AriaPopover` with `placement` defaulting to \"bottom\", `offset` 10 with the arrow or 8 without, `dir` from `useDirection()`, and the surface classes `z-110 w-80 max-w-[calc(100vw-32px)] rounded-[20px] border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-1.5 shadow-dropdown`.\n5. Motion is CSS on React Aria's state attributes: `origin-(--trigger-anchor-point)` grows the surface from the point nearest the trigger, `transition-[opacity,scale,filter] duration-150 ease-out`, `data-[entering]` / `data-[exiting]` set `opacity-0`, and `motion-safe:` adds `scale-95` and `blur-[2px]`. React Aria keeps the popover mounted until the exit transition finishes.\n6. With `showArrow`, an `OverlayArrow` render prop reads the resolved `placement` and draws the matching SVG path from `TOOLTIP_CARETS` (imported from Tooltip) with `fill-background-primary-default stroke-border-button-default` and a placement-specific 5% black drop shadow, so the caret flips with the popover. The content sits in an `AriaDialog` with `flex flex-col gap-3 p-2.5`; `PopoverHeader` renders the title as `Heading slot=\"title\"` so it names the dialog.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `offset = 8px, or 10px with the arrow`\n  The extra 2px makes room for the caret so its tip, not the surface, sits 8px from the trigger.\n- `width = w-80 (320px), max-w-[calc(100vw-32px)]`\n  A comfortable measure for a sentence or a short form; the cap keeps a 16px gutter on each side on a phone.\n- `motion = 150ms ease-out, scale 0.95, blur 2px`\n  The same values as Select and Dropdown so every anchored surface in the kit opens alike; small scale and blur read as \"growing out of the trigger\" without drifting.\n- `surface = rounded-[20px], p-1.5 outer + p-2.5 inner`\n  The composer permission popover's recipe: the outer 6px padding lets menu-like rows (with `dialogClassName=\"p-0\"`) sit inset with their own radius.\n- `z-index = z-110`\n  Above Modal and Drawer overlays (z-100), so a popover opened from inside a dialog is not hidden behind it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PopoverTrigger(props: { isOpen?: boolean; defaultOpen?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\nfunction Popover(props: Pick<AriaPopoverProps, \"placement\" | \"offset\" | \"crossOffset\" | \"shouldFlip\" | \"containerPadding\" | \"triggerRef\" | \"isOpen\" | \"onOpenChange\"> & { showArrow?: boolean; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string; dialogClassName?: string; children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode) }): JSX.Element\nfunction PopoverHeader(props: { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction PopoverFooter(props: { className?: string; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `showArrow: boolean` — default `false`\n  Draw a caret pointing at the trigger; bumps the offset from 8 to 10px.\n- `placement: Placement` — default `\"bottom\"`\n  Preferred side; React Aria flips it when there is no room.\n- `offset: number` — default `8 (10 with arrow)`\n  Distance from the trigger in pixels.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the surface, e.g. a width other than the default 320px.\n- `dialogClassName: string`\n  Classes on the inner dialog; `p-0` suits menu-like rows that pad themselves.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Accessible name when the content has no PopoverHeader title.\n- `PopoverTrigger.isOpen / onOpenChange: boolean / (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Controlled open state; `defaultOpen` for uncontrolled.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function PopoverTrigger({ isOpen: controlledOpen, defaultOpen = false, onOpenChange, children }: PopoverTriggerProps) {\n  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const [uncontrolledOpen, setUncontrolledOpen] = useState(defaultOpen);\n  const isOpen = controlledOpen ?? uncontrolledOpen;\n  const setOpen = (next: boolean) => {\n    setUncontrolledOpen(next);\n    onOpenChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\n  // Pressing the trigger while open closes the popover instead of reopening it.\n  const allowOpenChange = useTriggerToggle(isOpen, triggerRef);\n\n  const [trigger, ...rest] = Children.toArray(children);\n  return (\n    <PopoverContext.Provider value={{ popoverRef }}>\n      <AriaDialogTrigger isOpen={isOpen} onOpenChange={(next) => allowOpenChange(next) && setOpen(next)}>\n        {isValidElement(trigger) ? (\n          <Pressable ref={triggerRef}>{trigger as ReactElement<Record<string, unknown>, string>}</Pressable>\n        ) : (\n          trigger\n        )}\n        {rest}\n      </AriaDialogTrigger>\n    </PopoverContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface PopoverProps\n  extends Pick<AriaPopoverProps, \"placement\" | \"offset\" | \"crossOffset\" | \"shouldFlip\" | \"containerPadding\" | \"triggerRef\" | \"isOpen\" | \"onOpenChange\"> {\n  /** Draw a caret pointing at the trigger. */\n  showArrow?: boolean;\n  /** Accessible name when the content has no PopoverHeader title. */\n  \"aria-label\"?: string;\n  /** Classes on the surface, e.g. a width (default 320px). */\n  className?: string;\n  /** Classes on the inner dialog; `p-0` suits menu-like rows that pad themselves. */\n  dialogClassName?: string;\n  children: ReactNode | ((renderProps: DialogRenderProps) => ReactNode);\n}\n\nexport function Popover({\n  showArrow = false,\n  placement = \"bottom\",\n  offset,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  dialogClassName,\n  children,\n  ...props\n}: PopoverProps) {\n  const context = useContext(PopoverContext);\n  const direction = useDirection();\n\n  return (\n    <AriaPopover\n      {...props}\n      ref={context?.popoverRef}\n      dir={direction}\n      // Standalone (triggerRef + isOpen) popovers keep React Aria's own\n      // dismissal; inside PopoverTrigger the trigger restores it.\n      isNonModal={context != null}\n      placement={placement}\n      offset={offset ?? (showArrow ? 10 : 8)}\n      className={cx(\n        \"z-110 w-80 max-w-[calc(100vw-32px)]\",\n        \"rounded-[20px] border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-1.5 shadow-dropdown\",\n        \"origin-(--trigger-anchor-point) transition-[opacity,scale,filter] duration-150 ease-out\",\n        \"data-[entering]:opacity-0 data-[exiting]:opacity-0\",\n        \"motion-safe:data-[entering]:scale-95 motion-safe:data-[entering]:blur-[2px]\",\n        \"motion-safe:data-[exiting]:scale-95 motion-safe:data-[exiting]:blur-[2px]\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {showArrow ? (\n        <AriaOverlayArrow>\n          {({ placement: side }) => {\n            const caret = TOOLTIP_CARETS[side as keyof typeof TOOLTIP_CARETS] ?? TOOLTIP_CARETS.top;\n            return (\n              <svg\n                width={caret.width}\n                height={caret.height}\n                viewBox={`0 0 ${caret.width} ${caret.height}`}\n                className=\"block overflow-visible fill-background-primary-default stroke-border-button-default\"\n                style={{ filter: `drop-shadow(${caret.shadow} rgb(0 0 0 / 0.05))` }}\n              >\n                <path d={caret.path} />\n              </svg>\n            );\n          }}\n        </AriaOverlayArrow>\n      ) : null}\n      <AriaDialog aria-label={ariaLabel} className={cx(\"flex flex-col gap-3 p-2.5 outline-none\", dialogClassName)}>\n        {children}\n      </AriaDialog>\n    </AriaPopover>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The two `pointerdown` capture listeners on `document` are added only while the popover is open and removed in their effect cleanups when it closes or unmounts. React Aria restores focus to the trigger on close.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The scale and blur are behind `motion-safe:`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the popover only fades in and out over 150ms.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not switch the popover to modal to get outside-press dismissal. Modal popovers lock page scroll and shift sticky layout; dismissal comes from the capture-phase `pointerdown` listener instead.\n- Do not drop the trigger toggle. Without `useTriggerToggle`, pressing the trigger of an open popover closes it on the outside press and immediately reopens it.\n- Do not put the transform origin at the centre. `origin-(--trigger-anchor-point)` is what makes it grow from the trigger after a flip.\n- Do not hard-code the caret to one side. The arrow must read `placement` from `OverlayArrow` because React Aria can flip the popover when there is no room.\n\n## Notes\n\n- A standalone `Popover` (outside `PopoverTrigger`, positioned with `triggerRef` and `isOpen`) keeps React Aria's own modal dismissal, because `isNonModal` is only set when the context exists.\n- The caret geometry is shared with Tooltip through the exported `TOOLTIP_CARETS` map, so both overlays draw the same arrow.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPressing \"Details\" grows a 320px popover with a caret out of the button over about 150ms; pressing the button again, clicking anywhere outside, or pressing Escape closes it with the reverse motion and focus returns to the button. The page stays scrollable while it is open. The Share popover opens above its button and flips below when there is no room, with \"Copy link\" and \"Done\" inside.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "prescatter-horizontal-gallery": "# Build: Pre-Scatter Horizontal Gallery\n\nA pinned horizontal strip whose media is deliberately off-grid: every item gets a small seeded resting offset, then rides in from ahead of its slot and runs out behind it, rotating through zero on the way past. Two `fromTo` pairs per item — one entry, one exit — measured against the strip's own tween rather than against scroll position, so the choreography is a property of where an item is in the container, not of where the page is.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA pinned strip where each item rests slightly off its slot, flies in from ahead and peels away behind.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The component owns a `PinnedHorizontalTrack` and captures its `onTrack` payload in state. The strip's tween is built in the child's effect and therefore arrives one commit late, which is why it is state and not a ref — the arrival has to re-run the builder.\n2. Every `[data-scatter]` element draws one value from `createRng(seed)`: `rest = (rng() - 0.5) * scatter`, a resting `xPercent` offset. It is applied with `gsap.set` for every item whether or not it animates, so the composition is identical with motion on and off.\n3. The entry is `fromTo(el, { xPercent: rest + sweep, yPercent: dir * lift, rotation: dir * rotation }, { xPercent: rest, yPercent: 0, rotation: 0 })` scrubbed over `left 110%` to `left 65%` — the item lags behind its slot and catches up as it crosses the container.\n4. The exit is the mirror: `fromTo(el, { xPercent: rest, yPercent: 0, rotation: 0 }, { xPercent: rest - sweep, yPercent: -dir * lift, rotation: -dir * rotation })` over `right 35%` to `right -10%`, with `immediateRender: false` so it does not overwrite the entry's from-state at creation.\n5. `dir` alternates by index parity, so odd items throw up and rotate clockwise while even items do the opposite, and the rotation passes continuously through zero between the two ranges.\n6. Both triggers take `containerAnimation: track.animation` and horizontal position keywords, so ScrollTrigger measures each item along the strip's timeline instead of down the page.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `scatter = 32`\n  Total resting spread as a percentage of the item's own width, i.e. ±16%. Below ~10 the grid still reads as a grid and the effect is invisible; above ~50 items collide with their neighbours' captions and it reads as a layout bug rather than a composition.\n- `sweep = 400`\n  Horizontal lag on entry and lead on exit, as a percentage of item width. Four widths is enough that the item is genuinely off-stage at the start of its range, so the catch-up is a travel rather than a nudge. Under ~150 the item is already visible when its tween begins and appears to jerk into place.\n- `lift = 150`\n  Vertical throw as a percentage of item height. At 100 the item starts exactly one height away, so its edge is still touching the frame; 150 clears the frame with margin, which is what makes it read as flying in rather than sliding.\n- `rotation = 40°`\n  Peak tilt. Under ~15° the rotation is mistaken for a rendering artefact; past ~60° the item is legibly upside-down at the extremes and text inside it becomes unreadable during the travel.\n- `ENTER_START / ENTER_END = 'left 110%' / 'left 65%'`\n  The item's leading edge, measured along the container. Starting past 100% means the tween begins before the item is visible, so nothing pops in already moving. Ending at 65% leaves the middle third of the crossing settled.\n- `EXIT_START / EXIT_END = 'right 35%' / 'right -10%'`\n  The trailing edge. Ending below 0% carries the item fully past the left frame before the tween completes, so it never freezes half-visible at the edge. The two ranges cannot overlap for any item narrower than 1.3 containers.\n- `item scrub = true`\n  Locked to the container animation, not a number. These triggers read the strip's progress, and the strip already scrubs with a second of catch-up, so a numeric scrub here smooths an already-smoothed signal and the items swim behind the panels they belong to.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PrescatterHorizontalGallery(props: { children: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; seed?: number | string; scatter?: number; rotation?: number; lift?: number; sweep?: number; scrub?: number; className?: string; trackClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Panels for the strip. Give each a width and `shrink-0`, and mark the media inside `data-scatter`.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container the triggers read. Omit for the document.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'gallery'`\n  Anything hashable. The same seed rebuilds the same composition.\n- `scatter: number` — default `32`\n  Total resting spread as a percentage of item width.\n- `rotation: number` — default `40`\n  Peak tilt in degrees, alternating by index.\n- `lift: number` — default `150`\n  Vertical throw as a percentage of item height.\n- `sweep: number` — default `400`\n  Horizontal lag and lead as a percentage of item width.\n- `scrub: number` — default `1`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the strip, forwarded to the pinned track.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the gallery wrapper.\n- `trackClassName: string`\n  Merged onto the moving strip.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * The item's leading edge crosses the container from just off the right\n * (110%) to two thirds in (65%), and its trailing edge leaves from a third in\n * (35%) to just past the left (-10%). The two ranges cannot overlap for any\n * item narrower than 1.3 containers, so every item gets a settled window in\n * the middle where it is simply itself.\n */\nconst ENTER_START = 'left 110%';\nconst ENTER_END = 'left 65%';\nconst EXIT_START = 'right 35%';\nconst EXIT_END = 'right -10%';\n\nexport function PrescatterHorizontalGallery({\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  seed = 'gallery',\n  scatter = 32,\n  rotation = 40,\n  lift = 150,\n  sweep = 400,\n  scrub = 1,\n  className,\n  trackClassName,\n}: PrescatterHorizontalGalleryProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  // The strip's tween is built in a child's effect, so it arrives one commit\n  // late. State, not a ref: the arrival has to re-run the builder below.\n  const [track, setTrack] = useState<PinnedTrack | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl) return;\n\n    const containerAnimation = track?.animation;\n    const scrollerEl = scroller?.current ?? undefined;\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      const targets = Array.from(rootEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-scatter]'));\n      const rng = createRng(seed);\n\n      targets.forEach((el, i) => {\n        // Drawn for every item whether or not it animates, so adding motion\n        // never shifts the resting composition.\n        const rest = (rng() - 0.5) * scatter;\n        // Parity, not the generator: alternating up/down and cw/ccw is a\n        // rhythm, and a random sign clumps three the same way and looks broken.\n        const dir = i % 2 === 0 ? 1 : -1;\n\n        gsap.set(el, { xPercent: rest });\n        // The resting scatter is layout. Under reduced motion the composition\n        // survives and only the flight is dropped.\n        if (reduced || !containerAnimation) return;\n\n        gsap.fromTo(\n          el,\n          { xPercent: rest + sweep, yPercent: dir * lift, rotation: dir * rotation },\n          {\n            xPercent: rest,\n            yPercent: 0,\n            rotation: 0,\n            ease: 'none',\n            scrollTrigger: {\n              trigger: el,\n              scroller: scrollerEl,\n              containerAnimation,\n              start: ENTER_START,\n              end: ENTER_END,\n              // `true`, not a number. The strip already scrubs with catch-up,\n              // and these triggers read the strip's progress rather than the\n              // scrollbar, so they inherit that smoothing. A second scrub\n              // value smooths the smoothed signal and the items swim.\n              scrub: true,\n            },\n          },\n        );\n\n        gsap.fromTo(\n          el,\n          { xPercent: rest, yPercent: 0, rotation: 0 },\n          {\n            xPercent: rest - sweep,\n            yPercent: -dir * lift,\n            rotation: -dir * rotation,\n            ease: 'none',\n            // The single most important line here. A `fromTo` renders its\n            // from-state at creation, so without this the exit immediately\n            // overwrites the entry's from-state — every item starts on the\n            // grid instead of off-stage, and the entry appears not to exist.\n            immediateRender: false,\n            scrollTrigger: {\n              trigger: el,\n              scroller: scrollerEl,\n              containerAnimation,\n              start: EXIT_START,\n              end: EXIT_END,\n              scrub: true,\n            },\n          },\n        );\n      });\n    }, rootEl);\n\n    return () => ctx.revert();\n  }, [track, scroller, seed, scatter, rotation, lift, sweep, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** One `gsap.context` scoped to the root holds the `set` calls and both tweens per item, so `ctx.revert()` kills every tween, kills every ScrollTrigger, and strips the inline transforms back off the media. It runs on unmount and on every dependency change, including the commit where the strip's tween arrives.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The resting scatter is still applied — it is layout, not motion, and the gallery is supposed to look deliberately off-grid. What is dropped is the flight: no entry or exit triggers are created, so every item sits at its offset from first paint. The pinned track also declines to pin, so the strip becomes a horizontally scrollable row and every item stays reachable.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `gsap.to` for the exit. A `to` records its start values when it is created, which is after the entry has already rendered its from-state — so the exit animates from `xPercent: rest + sweep` and the item never returns to its slot.\n- Using a second `fromTo` for the exit without `immediateRender: false`. A `fromTo` writes its from-state immediately, so the later tween wins and every item starts on the grid: the entry animation exists, runs, and is invisible.\n- Keeping vertical position keywords with a `containerAnimation`. `start: \"top 110%\"` on a horizontal container resolves to a position the element never occupies. No error, no warning, no animation — the items simply arrive pre-placed.\n- Measuring the scatter in pixels off the container width. It has to be recomputed on every ScrollTrigger refresh or the composition creeps as the window resizes; expressed as `xPercent` it resolves against the element and never needs invalidating.\n- `Math.random()` for the offsets. The server and the client disagree at hydration, and the composition is different on every reload, so nobody can tell a regression from a reroll.\n- Building the item triggers before the strip's tween exists. Treating `undefined` as \"no container animation\" builds a full set of vertical triggers on horizontal content and tears them down on the next commit — a visible flash of items that were already in place.\n- Alternating direction from the generator instead of from the index. A random sign lands three items the same way often enough to look like a bug, where parity always reads as rhythm.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Mark the media, not the panel: `data-scatter` on an inner element lets the panel keep the caption on the grid while the image drifts off it.\n- ScrollTrigger forbids `pin` on a trigger that has a `containerAnimation`. The strip's section is pinned by `PinnedHorizontalTrack`; the items riding it are never pinned.\n- Panels need `overflow: visible` for the sweep to be seen. Clipping happens once, at the pinned section's edge.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll into the section: it pins, the strip travels sideways, and each item swings in from ahead of its slot, straightens, holds through the middle of the crossing, then tilts the other way and runs out to the left. Scroll back up and every item reverses along the same path. Note that no two items rest exactly on the grid, and that reloading gives the identical composition. With reduced motion on, the items are already at their offsets and the strip can be swiped by hand.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "preview-code-tabs": "# Build: Preview Code Tabs\n\nThe two-tab frame that sits at the top of a component docs page. A grey pill track holds \"Preview\" and \"Code\" tabs, with a white thumb that slides to the selected tab. Below it, a bordered stage renders the live example centred, or a code block shows the example source with line numbers and copy. The preview stays mounted while the code tab is open, so whatever the reader did to the example survives a look at the code.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDocs Preview / Code switcher: a pill tab track with a sliding thumb over a bordered live stage and a highlighted, copyable source.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. React Aria `Tabs`, `TabList`, `Tab` and `TabPanel` supply the roles, `aria-controls` wiring, roving focus and arrow, Home and End keys. Selection is controlled through `selectedTab` or uncontrolled through `defaultSelectedTab`, and only the two known keys reach `onSelectedTabChange`.\n2. A collection can only contain `Tab` children, so the thumb is a sibling of the `TabList` inside a positioned track. A layout effect reads the selected tab's `offsetLeft`, `offsetTop`, `offsetWidth` and `offsetHeight` and applies them as a translate plus width and height, which a 200ms `ease-out` transition animates.\n3. A `MutationObserver` filtered to `data-selected` re-measures on every selection change, controlled or not. A `ResizeObserver` on the track and each tab re-measures when fonts load or labels change. The state setter returns the previous object when nothing moved, so the observers never cause a render loop.\n4. The preview `TabPanel` uses `shouldForceMount`. While the code tab is selected, React Aria marks it `inert` and the render-prop class adds `hidden`, so it leaves focus order and layout but keeps its React state.\n5. The code tab renders the kit `CodeBlock` (TSX tokenizer, line numbers, wrap toggle, copy) with the `filename` chip and a `codeMaxHeight` cap. The `actions` slot at the end of the tab row takes extra controls such as a copy or open-in-new-tab button.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `thumb transition = 200 ms ease-out on transform, width, height`\n  Fast enough to keep up with arrow-key presses, and slow enough that the eye can follow the thumb between two tabs of different widths.\n- `track / thumb radii = rounded-xl track, p-1, rounded-lg thumb`\n  The thumb sits 4px inside the track, so a 12px outer and 8px inner radius stay concentric.\n- `stage = rounded-[20px], border-button-default, background-full, p-6 to p-10, minHeight 320 (code panel too)`\n  The code panel shares the floor so the page does not jump between tabs. A calm, slightly rounder frame than the cards it shows, with room on every side so a centred example never touches the edge.\n- `codeMaxHeight = 420 px`\n  Long sources scroll inside the panel rather than pushing the rest of the page down when the reader switches tabs.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype PreviewCodeTab = \"preview\" | \"code\";\nfunction PreviewCodeTabs(props: { children: ReactNode; code: string; filename?: string; language?: \"tsx\" | \"plain\"; selectedTab?: PreviewCodeTab; defaultSelectedTab?: PreviewCodeTab; onSelectedTabChange?: (tab: PreviewCodeTab) => void; actions?: ReactNode; previewLabel?: string; codeLabel?: string; \"aria-label\"?: string; minHeight?: number; codeMaxHeight?: number; onCopied?: (code: string) => void; previewClassName?: string; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The live example, centred in the preview stage.\n- `code: string`\n  Source shown on the Code tab and copied by its copy button.\n- `filename: string`\n  Chip label above the code, such as `usage` or `line-chart-card.tsx`.\n- `language: \"tsx\" | \"plain\"` — default `\"tsx\"`\n  Highlight as TSX or print as is.\n- `selectedTab / defaultSelectedTab / onSelectedTabChange: \"preview\" | \"code\"` — default `uncontrolled, \"preview\"`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled tab selection.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Controls at the end of the tab row.\n- `previewLabel / codeLabel: string` — default `\"Preview\" / \"Code\"`\n  Tab labels.\n- `minHeight: number` — default `320`\n  Minimum height of the preview stage, in px.\n- `codeMaxHeight: number` — default `420`\n  Height cap of the code panel, in px.\n- `onCopied: (code: string) => void`\n  Called after the code panel copies.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The thumb is a sibling of the TabList (a collection can only hold Tabs), so\n// it is positioned from the selected tab's offset box inside the track. A\n// ResizeObserver catches font loading and label changes; a MutationObserver\n// on `data-selected` catches selection, controlled or not.\nuseIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {\n  const track = trackRef.current;\n  if (!track) return;\n  const measure = () => {\n    const selected = track.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[role=\"tab\"][data-selected]');\n    if (!selected) return;\n    setThumb((prev) => {\n      const next = {\n        left: selected.offsetLeft,\n        top: selected.offsetTop,\n        width: selected.offsetWidth,\n        height: selected.offsetHeight,\n      };\n      return prev &&\n        prev.left === next.left &&\n        prev.top === next.top &&\n        prev.width === next.width &&\n        prev.height === next.height\n        ? prev\n        : next;\n    });\n  };\n  measure();\n  const ro = new ResizeObserver(measure);\n  ro.observe(track);\n  track.querySelectorAll('[role=\"tab\"]').forEach((tab) => ro.observe(tab));\n  const mo = new MutationObserver(measure);\n  mo.observe(track, { attributes: true, subtree: true, attributeFilter: [\"data-selected\"] });\n  return () => {\n    mo.disconnect();\n    ro.disconnect();\n  };\n}, []);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The layout effect disconnects its `ResizeObserver` and `MutationObserver` on unmount. There are no timers. The code panel copy timer belongs to `CodeBlock` and is cleared there.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only movement is the thumb slide. With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the thumb has `motion-reduce:transition-none` and jumps straight to the selected tab. The tab text colour change stays because it is not motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Putting the thumb inside `TabList`. React Aria builds the tab collection from its children, and anything that is not a `Tab` breaks the collection. The thumb lives next to the list, in the positioned track.\n- Unmounting the preview on tab change. Any state the reader built up in the example (a toggled option, a typed value) would reset every time they glanced at the code. `shouldForceMount` together with `hidden` on `isInert` avoids this.\n- Measuring with `getBoundingClientRect` relative to the viewport. Page scroll and transforms on ancestors would leak into the thumb position. Offsets relative to the track do not have that problem.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Labels are props (`previewLabel`, `codeLabel`), and the list name defaults to \"Example view\".\n- Pass `language=\"plain\"` for examples that are not TSX, such as CSS or shell output.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe Preview tab is selected on load and the white thumb sits under it. Clicking Code, or pressing the Right arrow with the list focused, slides the thumb under Code (200ms, instant with reduced motion), shows the highlighted source and sets `aria-selected=\"true\"` on the Code tab. Returning to Preview shows the example with its state intact. The code panel copy button puts the exact `code` string on the clipboard.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pricing-plan-cards": "# Build: Pricing Plan Cards\n\nA pricing section: an optional icon tile, heading and copy, a Monthly / Yearly segmented control, and a responsive grid of plan cards. Each card is a grey shell holding two white panels (name and description on top, features below) with the price and CTA sitting on the shell between them. Switching the cycle rolls every price to its new per-month figure, strikes through the monthly price and swaps the caption for the yearly saving.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPlan cards with a monthly/yearly switch that rolls the prices, shows yearly savings, highlights one plan and lists features with icons.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The cycle is controlled-or-uncontrolled: `cycle ?? inner`, where `inner` starts at `defaultCycle`. The kit `SegmentedControl` (a React Aria single-select toggle group) feeds `onSelectionChange`, and the handler only accepts the keys \"monthly\" and \"yearly\" before calling `setInner` (when uncontrolled) and `onCycleChange`.\n2. Each plan stores `price.monthly` and the annual total `price.yearly`. `perMonth()` returns the monthly price, or `yearly / 12` on the yearly cycle; `yearlySavings()` is `monthly * 12 - yearly`, clamped at 0. The best saving across paid plans, as a rounded percentage, becomes the \"-21%\" chip inside the Yearly segment.\n3. `useTweenedNumber` rolls the displayed price from where it currently is to the new target over 360ms with an ease-out cubic in a `requestAnimationFrame` loop, writing the in-flight value back to a ref so a toggle mid-roll restarts from what is on screen. The rolling digits are `aria-hidden`; an `sr-only` sibling always holds the final formatted price.\n4. Prices format through two cached `Intl.NumberFormat` instances (whole and two-decimal) built from the fixed `locale` and `currency` props, so the server and client print the same string. A plan with 0 / 0 prices renders `freeLabel` instead of a number.\n5. On the yearly cycle a paid plan with a saving shows the monthly price struck through (with a hidden \"was\" for screen readers) and replaces the second caption line with \"Save €48 a year\" in the success colour. A plan with its own `caption` keeps it on both cycles.\n6. The card grid is driven by a container query (`@container` on the section): one column, two from `@2xl` (42rem) and four from `@5xl` (64rem), so the block reflows by the width it is given rather than the viewport. A highlighted plan gets the primary CTA, an accent ring and its badge pill; the others use the secondary button.\n7. The CTA is a `ButtonLink` when the plan has `cta.href`; otherwise a `Button` that calls `onSelectPlan(plan.id, cycle)`. Features render as a list with the given icon, a check when no icon is given, or a dash plus struck text and an \"Not included:\" prefix for screen readers when `included` is false.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `TWEEN_MS = 360`\n  Long enough to read as the price rolling to its new value, short enough that a quick double toggle never feels laggy.\n- `ease = 1 - (1 - t)^3`\n  Ease-out cubic: the number moves fast first and settles, so the final digits are readable almost immediately.\n- `grid breakpoints = @2xl (42rem) → 2 cols, @5xl (64rem) → 4 cols`\n  Four cards need about 250px each for the feature labels to wrap at most once; container queries keep that true inside narrow hosts.\n- `panel radius = shell rounded-3xl (24px), panels 20px, shell padding 4px`\n  Inner radius = outer radius minus the padding, so the nested panels stay concentric with the shell.\n- `CTA = h-11 rounded-full`\n  Matches the reference: a 44px pill across the full card width, taller than the kit default 36px button.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PricingPlanCards(props: { plans: PricingPlan[]; cycle?: BillingCycle; defaultCycle?: BillingCycle; onCycleChange?: (cycle: BillingCycle) => void; onSelectPlan?: (planId: string, cycle: BillingCycle) => void; currency?: string; locale?: string; freeLabel?: string; heading?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype BillingCycle = \"monthly\" | \"yearly\"\ninterface PricingPlan { id: string; name: string; description: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; price: { monthly: number; yearly: number }; caption?: [ReactNode, ReactNode]; badge?: string; highlighted?: boolean; cta: { label: string; icon?: IconComponent; href?: string }; features: PricingPlanFeature[] }\n```\n\n- `plans: PricingPlan[]`\n  Cards in display order. `price.yearly` is the annual total.\n- `cycle: \"monthly\" | \"yearly\"`\n  Controlled billing cycle.\n- `defaultCycle: \"monthly\" | \"yearly\"` — default `\"monthly\"`\n  Initial cycle when uncontrolled.\n- `onCycleChange: (cycle: BillingCycle) => void`\n  Fires when the segmented control changes.\n- `onSelectPlan: (planId: string, cycle: BillingCycle) => void`\n  Called by CTAs of plans without `cta.href`.\n- `currency: string` — default `\"EUR\"`\n  ISO 4217 currency for every price.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Fixed formatting locale, so SSR and client output match.\n- `freeLabel: string` — default `\"Free\"`\n  Shown instead of a number for 0 / 0 plans.\n- `heading: ReactNode`\n  Section heading.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Copy under the heading.\n- `icon: ComponentType`\n  Glyph drawn in a tile above the heading.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction PlanPrice({\n  plan,\n  cycle,\n  format,\n  freeLabel,\n  reduced,\n}: {\n  plan: PricingPlan;\n  cycle: BillingCycle;\n  format: (n: number) => string;\n  freeLabel: string;\n  reduced: boolean;\n}) {\n  const target = perMonth(plan, cycle);\n  const shown = useTweenedNumber(target, reduced);\n  const free = plan.price.monthly === 0 && plan.price.yearly === 0;\n  const savings = yearlySavings(plan);\n  const wholeOnly = Number.isInteger(target) && Number.isInteger(plan.price.monthly);\n  const display = format(wholeOnly ? Math.round(shown) : Math.round(shown * 100) / 100);\n  const [line1, line2] = plan.caption ?? [\n    \"per month\",\n    cycle === \"yearly\" ? `billed ${format(plan.price.yearly)} yearly` : \"billed monthly\",\n  ];\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex min-h-12 items-center gap-2.5\">\n      {free ? (\n        <span className=\"text-display-4-medium tracking-tight text-text-primary\">{freeLabel}</span>\n      ) : (\n        <span className=\"flex items-baseline gap-1.5\">\n          {cycle === \"yearly\" && savings > 0 && (\n            <s className=\"text-title-1-regular text-text-tertiary tabular-nums decoration-1\">\n              <span className=\"sr-only\">was </span>\n              {format(plan.price.monthly)}\n            </s>\n          )}\n          <span aria-hidden className=\"text-display-4-medium tracking-tight text-text-primary tabular-nums\">\n            {display}\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"sr-only\">{format(target)}</span>\n        </span>\n      )}\n      <span className=\"flex flex-col text-caption-1-regular leading-tight\">\n        <span className=\"text-text-secondary\">{line1}</span>\n        {cycle === \"yearly\" && savings > 0 && !plan.caption ? (\n          <span className=\"text-state-success-text\">Save {format(savings)} a year</span>\n        ) : (\n          <span className=\"text-text-tertiary\">{line2}</span>\n        )}\n      </span>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction PlanCard({\n  plan,\n  cycle,\n  format,\n  freeLabel,\n  reduced,\n  onSelectPlan,\n}: {\n  plan: PricingPlan;\n  cycle: BillingCycle;\n  format: (n: number) => string;\n  freeLabel: string;\n  reduced: boolean;\n  onSelectPlan?: (planId: string, cycle: BillingCycle) => void;\n}) {\n  const titleId = useId();\n  const Icon = plan.icon;\n  const variant = plan.highlighted ? \"primary\" : \"secondary\";\n  const ctaClass = \"h-11 w-full rounded-full text-body-medium\";\n\n  return (\n    <article\n      aria-labelledby={titleId}\n      data-highlighted={plan.highlighted || undefined}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex flex-col rounded-3xl bg-background-secondary-default p-1 ring-1 ring-separator-border\",\n        plan.highlighted && \"ring-accent-200 dark:ring-accent-900\",\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"relative rounded-[20px] bg-background-primary-default px-5 pt-5 pb-5 shadow-xs\">\n        {Icon && <Icon aria-hidden className=\"mb-3 size-5 text-text-primary\" />}\n        {plan.badge && (\n          <span className=\"absolute top-4 right-4 inline-flex items-center rounded-md bg-badge-new-background px-1.5 py-0.5 text-caption-1-medium text-badge-new-text\">\n            <span aria-hidden className=\"absolute -top-1 -left-1 size-1 rounded-full bg-accent-300\" />\n            <span aria-hidden className=\"absolute -right-1 -bottom-1 size-1 rounded-full bg-accent-300\" />\n            {plan.badge}\n          </span>\n        )}\n        <h3 id={titleId} className=\"text-title-2-regular text-text-primary\">\n          {plan.name}\n        </h3>\n        <p className=\"mt-1 text-body-regular text-text-secondary [&_strong]:font-semibold [&_strong]:text-text-primary\">\n          {plan.description}\n        </p>\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-4 px-4 pt-5 pb-5\">\n        <PlanPrice plan={plan} cycle={cycle} format={format} freeLabel={freeLabel} reduced={reduced} />\n        {plan.cta.href ? (\n          <ButtonLink href={plan.cta.href} variant={variant} leadingIcon={plan.cta.icon} className={ctaClass}>\n            {plan.cta.label}\n          </ButtonLink>\n        ) : (\n          <Button\n            variant={variant}\n            leadingIcon={plan.cta.icon}\n            className={ctaClass}\n            aria-describedby={titleId}\n            onClick={() => onSelectPlan?.(plan.id, cycle)}\n          >\n            {plan.cta.label}\n          </Button>\n        )}\n      </div>\n\n      <ul className=\"flex flex-1 flex-col gap-3.5 rounded-[20px] bg-background-primary-default px-5 py-5 shadow-xs\">\n        {plan.features.map((feature, i) => {\n          const included = feature.included !== false;\n          const Glyph = included ? (feature.icon ?? RiCheckLine) : RiSubtractLine;\n          return (\n            <li key={i} className={cx(\"flex items-start gap-2.5 text-body-regular\", included ? \"text-text-secondary\" : \"text-text-tertiary\")}>\n              <Glyph aria-hidden className={cx(\"mt-px size-[18px] shrink-0\", included ? \"text-foreground-icon-tertiary\" : \"text-foreground-icon-quaternary\")} />\n              <span className={cx(!included && \"line-through decoration-1\")}>\n                {!included && <span className=\"sr-only\">Not included: </span>}\n                {feature.label}\n              </span>\n              {feature.action && (\n                <a\n                  href={feature.action.href}\n                  className=\"-my-0.5 ml-0.5 shrink-0 rounded-md bg-background-secondary-default px-1.5 py-0.5 text-caption-1-medium text-text-primary outline-none transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-hover focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                >\n                  {feature.action.label}\n                </a>\n              )}\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n    </article>\n  );\n}\n\nexport function PricingPlanCards({\n  plans,\n  cycle: cycleProp,\n  defaultCycle = \"monthly\",\n  onCycleChange,\n  onSelectPlan,\n  currency = \"EUR\",\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  freeLabel = \"Free\",\n  heading,\n  description,\n  icon: HeadingIcon,\n  className,\n}: PricingPlanCardsProps) {\n  const [inner, setInner] = useState<BillingCycle>(defaultCycle);\n  const cycle = cycleProp ?? inner;\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const format = useMoney(currency, locale);\n\n  const bestPercent = useMemo(() => {\n    let best = 0;\n    for (const p of plans) {\n      if (p.price.monthly > 0) best = Math.max(best, yearlySavings(p) / (p.price.monthly * 12));\n    }\n    return Math.round(best * 100);\n  }, [plans]);\n\n  const setCycle = (next: BillingCycle) => {\n    if (cycleProp === undefined) setInner(next);\n    onCycleChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section className={cx(\"@container flex flex-col items-center\", className)}>\n      {HeadingIcon && (\n        <span className=\"mb-5 grid size-14 place-items-center rounded-2xl bg-background-primary-default shadow-sm ring-1 ring-separator-border\">\n          <HeadingIcon aria-hidden className=\"size-7 text-text-primary\" />\n        </span>\n      )}\n      {heading && (\n        <h2 className=\"max-w-2xl text-center text-display-3-medium tracking-tight text-balance text-text-primary\">{heading}</h2>\n      )}\n      {description && (\n        <p className=\"mt-3 max-w-md text-center text-body-regular text-pretty text-text-secondary\">{description}</p>\n      )}\n\n      <SegmentedControl\n        aria-label=\"Billing cycle\"\n        className=\"mt-6\"\n        selectedKeys={new Set([cycle])}\n        onSelectionChange={(keys) => {\n          const next = [...keys][0];\n          if (next === \"monthly\" || next === \"yearly\") setCycle(next);\n        }}\n      >\n        <SegmentedControlItem id=\"monthly\">Monthly</SegmentedControlItem>\n        <SegmentedControlItem id=\"yearly\">\n          Yearly\n          {bestPercent > 0 && (\n            <span className=\"ml-1.5 rounded-sm bg-badge-new-background px-1 text-caption-2-semibold text-badge-new-text\">\n              -{bestPercent}%\n            </span>\n          )}\n        </SegmentedControlItem>\n      </SegmentedControl>\n\n      <div className=\"mt-8 grid w-full grid-cols-1 gap-4 @2xl:grid-cols-2 @5xl:grid-cols-4\">\n        {plans.map((plan) => (\n          <PlanCard\n            key={plan.id}\n            plan={plan}\n            cycle={cycle}\n            format={format}\n            freeLabel={freeLabel}\n            reduced={reduced}\n            onSelectPlan={onSelectPlan}\n          />\n        ))}\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The price tween cancels its pending `requestAnimationFrame` when the target changes or the card unmounts. There are no other listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the price roll is skipped: `useTweenedNumber` returns the target directly and starts no animation frame. The \"Join now\" pill hover transition is removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Formatting with the browser locale (`toLocaleString()` without a locale). The server and client may print different separators and React reports a hydration mismatch; the `locale` prop pins it.\n- Tweening the accessible text. Screen readers would announce every intermediate number; the rolling figure is `aria-hidden` and an `sr-only` copy carries the final price.\n- Storing the yearly price as a per-month value. Savings and the \"billed €180 yearly\" caption need the annual total; the per-month figure is derived.\n- Using viewport breakpoints for the grid. Inside a narrow container (a docs preview, a modal) four cards would squeeze; the grid uses container queries instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `cycle` and `onCycleChange` to keep the switch in the URL or in shared state; omit both to let the component own it.\n- Rich descriptions are allowed: `<strong>` inside `description` is styled as the primary text colour, matching the reference card copy.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe section renders four cards in a row at 1280px and one column at 360px. Monthly is selected and the Pro card reads €19 per month. Clicking Yearly rolls Pro to €15, strikes €19 through, and the caption reads \"Save €48 a year\"; the Yearly segment carries a \"-21%\" chip. The highlighted Team card has a blue CTA and a \"Most popular\" pill. Clicking a CTA reports the plan and current cycle through `onSelectPlan`. Under reduced motion prices change instantly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "priority-badge": "# Build: Priority Badge\n\nThe priority pill from the project board cards: \"Low\" on blue, \"Medium\" on yellow, \"High\" on orange and \"Urgent\" on rose, drawn from the kit's status tokens so it flips cleanly in dark mode. A small three-bar glyph (one, two or three lit bars, an alert tile for urgent) can ride in front of the label so the level still reads for people who cannot tell the colours apart. Sorting helpers (`PRIORITIES`, `PRIORITY_RANK`) ship with it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nUrgent, high, medium and low ticket pills with a signal-bars glyph, in tinted, plain and icon-only looks.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Four levels map to four status token pairs in one `sortCx` table: urgent → `status-rose`, high → `status-orange`, medium → `status-yellow`, low → `status-blue`, each as `bg-status-*-background text-status-*-text`. The dark sheet redefines those tokens as 60% colour-mixed 950 fills with brighter text, so the pill needs no `dark:` classes.\n2. `PriorityGlyph` is a 16×16 SVG in `currentColor`: three 3px-wide bars at x = 2, 6.5, 11 with heights 4.5, 8 and 11.5, bottom-aligned at y = 14.5. `LIT_BARS` decides how many are lit (low 1, medium 2, high 3) and the rest draw at 30% opacity. Urgent replaces the bars with a 13px rounded tile and an exclamation cut out in `--color-background-primary-default`, so it reads as a hole in light and dark.\n3. The `soft` variant tints the whole pill. The `plain` variant drops the fill, writes the label in `text-text-secondary` and tints only the glyph with the level's text token, for dense lists where five coloured pills in a row get loud.\n4. `iconOnly` hides the visible label but keeps \"<Level> priority\" in an `sr-only` span, so the glyph never becomes an unlabeled image. `label` overrides the visible text for localized copy, and `data-priority` is stamped on the root for styling hooks and tests.\n5. `PRIORITIES` lists the levels highest first and `PRIORITY_RANK` gives weights 4…1, so a board can sort with `PRIORITY_RANK[b] - PRIORITY_RANK[a]` without string comparisons.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `md pill = px-1.5 py-px, 14/20 body-regular, radius 6px`\n  Measured off the board card: a 22px tall pill with the label in regular weight, the same height as the bordered project tag beside it, so the two sit on one line.\n- `sm pill = px-1 py-px, 12/16 caption-1-medium, 12px glyph`\n  For table rows and pickers. The glyph shrinks with the type so it never towers over the label.\n- `unlit bar opacity = 0.3`\n  Enough to show the empty slots (so \"low\" reads as one of three, not just one bar) while staying clearly off in both themes.\n- `PRIORITY_RANK = low 1, medium 2, high 3, urgent 4`\n  Integer weights make the board's sort a subtraction and keep \"urgent above high\" true even if labels are translated.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype Priority = \"urgent\" | \"high\" | \"medium\" | \"low\";\nfunction PriorityBadge(props: Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement>, \"children\"> & { priority: Priority; variant?: \"soft\" | \"plain\"; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; showIcon?: boolean; iconOnly?: boolean; label?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction PriorityGlyph(props: { priority: Priority; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nconst PRIORITIES: readonly Priority[]; const PRIORITY_LABELS: Record<Priority, string>; const PRIORITY_RANK: Record<Priority, number>\n```\n\n- `priority: \"urgent\" | \"high\" | \"medium\" | \"low\"`\n  Level to show. Picks the token pair, glyph and default label.\n- `variant: \"soft\" | \"plain\"` — default `\"soft\"`\n  Tinted pill, or no fill with a tinted glyph and muted label.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  18px caption pill or 22px body pill.\n- `showIcon: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draw the bars glyph before the label. Always on for `plain` and `iconOnly`.\n- `iconOnly: boolean` — default `false`\n  Glyph only; the label moves into an sr-only span.\n- `label: string`\n  Replaces the visible text, e.g. for localized copy.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** 3-bar signal glyph in `currentColor`; unlit bars sit at 30% opacity. */\nexport function PriorityGlyph({ priority, className }: PriorityGlyphProps) {\n  if (priority === \"urgent\") {\n    return (\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-3.5 shrink-0\", className)}>\n        <rect x=\"1.5\" y=\"1.5\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" rx=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\" />\n        <path d=\"M8 4.5v4.25\" stroke=\"var(--color-background-primary-default)\" strokeWidth=\"1.75\" strokeLinecap=\"round\" />\n        <circle cx=\"8\" cy=\"11.25\" r=\"1\" fill=\"var(--color-background-primary-default)\" />\n      </svg>\n    );\n  }\n  const lit = LIT_BARS[priority];\n  return (\n    <svg viewBox=\"0 0 16 16\" aria-hidden className={cx(\"size-3.5 shrink-0\", className)}>\n      {[0, 1, 2].map((i) => (\n        <rect\n          key={i}\n          x={2 + i * 4.5}\n          y={10 - i * 3.5}\n          width=\"3\"\n          height={4.5 + i * 3.5}\n          rx=\"1\"\n          fill=\"currentColor\"\n          opacity={i < lit ? 1 : 0.3}\n        />\n      ))}\n    </svg>\n  );\n}\n\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  base: \"inline-flex shrink-0 items-center whitespace-nowrap rounded-md\",\n  size: {\n    sm: \"gap-0.5 px-1 py-px text-caption-1-medium\",\n    md: \"gap-1 px-1.5 py-px text-body-regular\",\n  },\n  soft: {\n    urgent: \"bg-status-rose-background text-status-rose-text\",\n    high: \"bg-status-orange-background text-status-orange-text\",\n    medium: \"bg-status-yellow-background text-status-yellow-text\",\n    low: \"bg-status-blue-background text-status-blue-text\",\n  },\n  /** Plain: no fill, label in secondary text, only the glyph is tinted. */\n  plainGlyph: {\n    urgent: \"text-status-rose-text\",\n    high: \"text-status-orange-text\",\n    medium: \"text-status-yellow-text\",\n    low: \"text-status-blue-text\",\n  },\n});\n\nexport interface PriorityBadgeProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement>, \"children\"> {\n  priority: Priority;\n  /** `soft` = tinted pill (board cards); `plain` = glyph + muted label (dense lists). */\n  variant?: PriorityBadgeVariant;\n  size?: PriorityBadgeSize;\n  /** Draw the bars glyph before the label. Forced on for `plain`. */\n  showIcon?: boolean;\n  /** Hide the visible label (glyph only). The label stays as the accessible name. */\n  iconOnly?: boolean;\n  /** Override the visible text (e.g. localized copy). */\n  label?: string;\n  ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement>;\n}\n\nexport function PriorityBadge({\n  priority,\n  variant = \"soft\",\n  size = \"md\",\n  showIcon = true,\n  iconOnly = false,\n  label,\n  className,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: PriorityBadgeProps) {\n  const text = label ?? PRIORITY_LABELS[priority];\n  const glyph = showIcon || iconOnly || variant === \"plain\";\n  return (\n    <span\n      ref={ref}\n      data-priority={priority}\n      className={cx(\n        styles.base,\n        styles.size[size],\n        variant === \"soft\" ? styles.soft[priority] : \"px-0 text-text-secondary\",\n        iconOnly && (size === \"sm\" ? \"px-0.5\" : \"px-1\"),\n        className,\n      )}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      {glyph && (\n        <PriorityGlyph\n          priority={priority}\n          className={cx(size === \"sm\" && \"size-3\", variant === \"plain\" && styles.plainGlyph[priority])}\n        />\n      )}\n      {iconOnly ? <span className=\"sr-only\">{`${text} priority`}</span> : text}\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: no state, effects, listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates in the badge itself; it is static markup. The demo's row and picker colour transitions are dropped with `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Hard-coding blue/yellow/orange/rose shades. The status tokens are redefined for dark mode as translucent fills with lighter text; raw shades leave bright pastel blocks on dark cards.\n- Relying on colour alone. Yellow and orange are close for many people; keep `showIcon` on wherever the badge is the only priority cue.\n- Rendering `iconOnly` without a label. The SVG is `aria-hidden`, so the `sr-only` \"<Level> priority\" span is the only thing a screen reader hears.\n- Sorting by label string. \"Urgent\" < \"Low\" alphabetically is meaningless; sort with `PRIORITY_RANK`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The board cards in BoardUI's template show the text-only pill; pass `showIcon={false}` for that look. Kanban Card does exactly that.\n- The urgent tile's cut-out uses the primary surface token, so on a non-white custom surface pass a `className` to the glyph if the hole should match that surface.\n- Below 768px the demo shows only the triage queue so it fits a phone screen; the Levels grid joins it from `md` up.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFour pills render in rose, orange, yellow and blue with labels Urgent, High, Medium, Low; the glyph lights 1, 2 and 3 bars for low, medium and high and shows an alert tile for urgent. In the demo, selecting FE-82 and choosing Urgent changes that row's badge to \"Urgent\" and, with Priority sort on, moves it to the top of the list; switching to Recent reorders by date. In dark mode every pill keeps readable contrast.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pro-access-card": "# Build: Pro Access Card\n\nThe card a docs page shows in place of a paid component. A brushed-metal lock tile, the heading and licence copy sit on top with a \"Pro access\" pill; below, inside a grey inset, the real preview is rendered blurred and desaturated under a \"locked\" chip, and a footer carries the upsell line with a get-access button. When `locked` turns false the blur lifts, the chip fades and the footer swaps to your unlocked content.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPro-locked component placeholder: metallic lock tile, a blurred inert preview, upsell copy and a get-access action that lifts the veil.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Layout: a rounded-3xl bordered section; the header is a flex row of a lock tile (40px, 56px from `sm`) and a title column; the title has a 14rem flex-basis, so in narrow cards the pill wraps under it instead of squeezing the heading. A `bg-background-secondary-default` inset with 4px padding holds the preview stage (rounded-[20px], primary background) and the footer, which reads as two nested surfaces like the docs install panel.\n2. The veil is the preview wrapper itself: while locked it gets `blur-[6px] saturate-50 opacity-60 scale-[1.03]`, `pointer-events-none select-none`, the `inert` attribute and `aria-hidden`, so the preview cannot be focused, clicked, selected or read out. The slight scale pushes the blurred edge outside the clipped stage so no soft halo shows at the corners.\n3. Unlocking sets the wrapper back to `blur-none` and identity scale/opacity with a 500ms `transition-[filter,opacity,scale]`, while the absolutely positioned lock chip runs a 300ms `transition-[opacity,visibility]` to `invisible opacity-0` so it leaves the tab order and the accessibility tree once faded.\n4. The footer is an `aria-live=\"polite\"` region: locked, it shows the title/description and the action (a `ButtonLink` with `actionHref`, else a `Button`); `pending` swaps the icon for a spinner glyph and sets `aria-disabled` (not `disabled`, so focus stays on the button) and `aria-busy` on the card; unlocked, it renders `unlockedFooter`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `veil = blur 6px, saturate 50%, opacity 60%, scale 1.03`\n  Enough to hide detail and colour while the silhouette still sells the component; the scale hides the blur halo at the clipped edge.\n- `unveil = 500ms ease-out (chip 300ms)`\n  The chip is gone before the preview finishes sharpening, so the reveal reads as one motion.\n- `lock tile = 56px (40px below sm), rx 14`\n  Matches the icon tile in the reference header and the card's rounded-3xl rhythm.\n- `inset = p-1 (4px), stage radius 20px`\n  Outer radius 24px minus the 4px inset keeps the nested corners concentric.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ProAccessCard(props: { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; badge?: ReactNode; unlockedBadge?: ReactNode; icon?: ReactNode; preview?: ReactNode; lockedLabel?: ReactNode; locked?: boolean; footerTitle?: ReactNode; footerDescription?: ReactNode; unlockedFooter?: ReactNode; actionLabel?: ReactNode; actionIcon?: IconComponent; actionHref?: string; onAction?: () => void; pending?: boolean; titleAs?: \"h2\" | \"h3\" | \"h4\"; previewClassName?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction ProLockTile(props: { className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Heading, e.g. \"Line Chart Card is a Pro component.\"; labels the section.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Licence copy under the title.\n- `preview: ReactNode`\n  The component preview, veiled while locked.\n- `locked: boolean` — default `true`\n  Blurs and disables the preview and shows the call to action.\n- `pending: boolean` — default `false`\n  Spinner in the action, `aria-disabled`, and `aria-busy` on the card.\n- `onAction / actionHref: () => void / string`\n  Click handler, or a link target that renders the action as an anchor.\n- `actionLabel / actionIcon: ReactNode / IconComponent` — default `\"Get access\" / RiVipCrown2Line`\n  Action text and leading icon.\n- `badge / unlockedBadge: ReactNode` — default `\"Pro access\" / \"Unlocked\"`\n  Pill text for each state.\n- `lockedLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Preview locked\"`\n  Chip over the blurred preview.\n- `footerTitle / footerDescription: ReactNode` — default `\"This component is included in Pro\"`\n  Upsell copy beside the action.\n- `unlockedFooter: ReactNode`\n  Replaces the footer once unlocked.\n- `icon: ReactNode` — default `<ProLockTile />`\n  Header icon.\n- `titleAs: \"h2\" | \"h3\" | \"h4\"` — default `\"h3\"`\n  Heading level for the title.\n- `previewClassName: string` — default `\"p-3 sm:p-6\"`\n  Classes for the preview stage.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The veil: blur + desaturate + inert while locked, so the preview teases\n// the component but cannot be tabbed into, clicked or read out. Unlocking\n// transitions filter, opacity and scale back to identity in one 500ms pass,\n// while the lock chip fades out over 300ms and then turns `invisible`.\nconst veilClass = cx(\n  \"origin-center transition-[filter,opacity,scale] duration-500 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n  locked ? \"pointer-events-none scale-[1.03] opacity-60 blur-[6px] saturate-50 select-none\" : \"blur-none\",\n  previewClassName ?? \"p-3 sm:p-6\",\n);\nconst overlayClass = cx(\n  \"absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center bg-background-primary-default/30\",\n  \"transition-[opacity,visibility] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n  locked ? \"visible opacity-100\" : \"invisible opacity-0\",\n);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component holds no timers or listeners. The demo's simulated activation is a `setTimeout` inside an effect keyed on the status and cleared in its cleanup.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Every transition (veil filter/opacity/scale, chip fade, pill colour) carries `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so the preview snaps between veiled and clear.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Blurring a preview that is still interactive. Without `inert` keyboard users tab into invisible-looking controls and screen readers read the hidden component; add `inert` and `aria-hidden` together.\n- Fading the overlay with opacity only. It stays clickable and announced; pair it with `visibility` in the same transition.\n- Setting `disabled` on the action while pending. The focused button loses focus to `<body>`; use `aria-disabled` and ignore clicks instead.\n- Writing `blur-0` to clear the filter in Tailwind v4: it emits nothing. Use `blur-none`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The card does not own the licence state: pass `locked` from wherever you check it, and `pending` while an activation request is in flight.\n- The lock tile colours are fixed SVG artwork (brushed metal reads the same in both themes); pass `icon` to use your own.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo loads with a blurred revenue chart, an \"Unlock to use this component\" chip, a \"Pro access\" pill and a \"Get Pro\" button; nothing in the preview is focusable. Clicking Get Pro shows \"Activating…\" with a spinner and aria-disabled for about 900ms, then the chart sharpens, the chip fades, the pill reads \"Unlocked\", the footer says the licence is active and focus lands on \"Lock again\". Clicking Lock again restores the veil and the Get Pro button.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "pro-upsell-toast": "# Build: Pro Upsell Toast\n\nThe card that appears when someone reaches for a paid feature, such as installing a Pro template. A 300px white card floats in the corner with a soft wash of blue and pink light across its top, a brushed-metal app tile, a title, one line of copy, a full-width \"Get Pro\" button and a round dismiss button. It is controlled with `isOpen`, rises in from the corner, closes on the dismiss button or Escape and hands focus back to the control that opened it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA floating Upgrade-to-Pro card with a grainy pastel wash, app tile, dismiss and a full-width CTA; it rises in and returns focus on close.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The card is a `motion.div` inside `AnimatePresence`, so it can play an exit before it unmounts. It enters from 16px lower at 96% scale over 340ms on an ease-out curve (0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) with its transform origin at the bottom, and leaves 8px down at 98% over 160ms.\n2. It is a non-modal `role=\"dialog\"` named by its title and described by its copy. When it opens with `autoFocus`, an effect remembers `document.activeElement` and focuses the card itself (`tabIndex={-1}`), so a screen reader reads the title and copy and the next Tab reaches the button. Nothing else on the page is blocked.\n3. When `isOpen` turns false the card is still in the DOM for its exit animation, so the same effect can check whether focus is inside it. If it is, focus goes back to the remembered element; if the user had already moved on, focus is left where it is.\n4. Escape is handled by a `keydown` on the card, not on the window, so it only closes the toast while focus is inside it and never fights a modal or menu elsewhere on the page. The dismiss button and Escape both call `onOpenChange(false)`; the call to action calls `onCta` and leaves closing to the parent.\n5. The wash is inline SVG: five blurred vertical ellipses in pastel blue, violet and pink, a fractal-noise grain at 12% and a gradient mask that fades the lot out by the middle of the card. It is stretched with `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"` and drops to 55% opacity in dark mode so it glows instead of glaring.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card = 300px wide (never wider than the container minus 2rem), radius 24px, 16px padding, 16px from the corner`\n  Wide enough for the title on one line and the copy on two, narrow enough to leave the page usable beside it on a laptop.\n- `enter = 340ms, y 16 → 0, scale 0.96 → 1, ease (0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`\n  A fast start with a long settle reads as the card arriving from the corner without bouncing; the small scale keeps the motion near the corner it grows from.\n- `exit = 160ms, y +8, scale 0.98, ease-in`\n  Leaving is half the length of arriving, so dismissing feels immediate and the page underneath is usable again at once.\n- `wash = blur 12, grain 12%, faded to transparent by 100% of a 112px band; 55% in dark mode`\n  Enough colour to mark the card as special, kept above the text so the copy sits on plain white.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ProUpsellToast(props: ProUpsellToastProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ProUpsellToastProps { isOpen: boolean; onOpenChange?: (isOpen: boolean) => void; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; ctaLabel?: ReactNode; onCta?: () => void; icon?: ReactNode; autoFocus?: boolean; className?: string }\n```\n\n- `isOpen: boolean`\n  Shows the card. It animates in and out on change.\n- `onOpenChange: (isOpen: boolean) => void`\n  Called with false by the dismiss button and by Escape.\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"This template is part of Pro\"`\n  Dialog title.\n- `description: ReactNode` — default `\"Buy once and install the full source with the CLI.\"`\n  One line under the title; also the dialog description.\n- `ctaLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Get Pro\"`\n  Label of the full-width button.\n- `onCta: () => void`\n  Called by the button. Close the card yourself if the action leaves the page state.\n- `icon: ReactNode`\n  Replaces the default metal Pro tile.\n- `autoFocus: boolean` — default `true`\n  Focus the card when it opens and return focus when it closes.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card; the default placement is `fixed end-4 bottom-4 z-50`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ProUpsellToast({\n  isOpen,\n  onOpenChange,\n  title = \"This template is part of Pro\",\n  description = \"Buy once and install the full source with the CLI.\",\n  ctaLabel = \"Get Pro\",\n  onCta,\n  icon,\n  autoFocus = true,\n  className,\n}: ProUpsellToastProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const cardRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const returnTo = useRef<HTMLElement | null>(null);\n  const titleId = useId();\n  const descriptionId = useId();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (isOpen) {\n      if (!autoFocus) return;\n      const active = document.activeElement;\n      returnTo.current = active instanceof HTMLElement ? active : null;\n      cardRef.current?.focus({ preventScroll: true });\n      return;\n    }\n    // The card is still mounted while it animates out, so this sees where\n    // focus is. Only pull it back if it was inside the card.\n    if (cardRef.current?.contains(document.activeElement)) returnTo.current?.focus({ preventScroll: true });\n    returnTo.current = null;\n  }, [isOpen, autoFocus]);\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (event.key !== \"Escape\") return;\n    event.stopPropagation();\n    onOpenChange?.(false);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <AnimatePresence>\n      {isOpen ? (\n        <motion.div\n          key=\"pro-upsell-toast\"\n          ref={cardRef}\n          role=\"dialog\"\n          aria-labelledby={titleId}\n          aria-describedby={descriptionId}\n          tabIndex={-1}\n          onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n          initial={reduced ? { opacity: 0 } : { opacity: 0, y: 16, scale: 0.96 }}\n          animate={{\n            opacity: 1,\n            y: 0,\n            scale: 1,\n            transition: reduced ? { duration: 0.15 } : { duration: 0.34, ease: ENTER_EASE },\n          }}\n          exit={\n            reduced\n              ? { opacity: 0, transition: { duration: 0.12 } }\n              : { opacity: 0, y: 8, scale: 0.98, transition: { duration: 0.16, ease: EXIT_EASE } }\n          }\n          className={cx(\n            \"fixed end-4 bottom-4 z-50 w-[min(300px,calc(100%-2rem))] origin-bottom overflow-hidden\",\n            \"rounded-3xl bg-background-primary-default p-4 shadow-lg ring-1 ring-border-ai-profile-card outline-none\",\n            \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n            className,\n          )}\n        >\n          <ProWash className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-28 w-full dark:opacity-55\" />\n\n          <div className=\"relative flex flex-col\">\n            <span className=\"mb-4 flex\">{icon ?? <ProTile />}</span>\n            <h2 id={titleId} className=\"pe-8 text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n              {title}\n            </h2>\n            <p id={descriptionId} className=\"mt-1 text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary\">\n              {description}\n            </p>\n            <Button variant=\"primary\" size=\"medium\" className=\"mt-4 w-full justify-center\" onClick={onCta}>\n              {ctaLabel}\n            </Button>\n          </div>\n\n          <CloseButton\n            size=\"sm\"\n            aria-label=\"Dismiss\"\n            onClick={() => onOpenChange?.(false)}\n            className=\"absolute end-3 top-3 dark:bg-background-quaternary-default\"\n          />\n        </motion.div>\n      ) : null}\n    </AnimatePresence>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** There are no timers or global listeners. The Escape handler is a React `onKeyDown` on the card, the focus effect only reads and moves focus, and motion stops the exit animation if the component unmounts mid-way.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the card does not move or scale: it fades in over 150ms and out over 120ms. The wash and tile are static in every mode.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Clearing the remembered element or skipping the focus return when the parent closes the toast. Keyboard users are left on `<body>` at the top of the page. The effect returns focus whenever the card closes with focus inside it, whoever closed it.\n- Listening for Escape on `window`. It would close the toast while a modal or a select popover is open, and one keypress would close both. The listener lives on the card.\n- Making it a modal or trapping focus. An upsell is optional; a trap turns a suggestion into a wall. Use `autoFocus={false}` when the card appears on its own, so it does not steal focus from someone typing.\n- Placing it `fixed` inside a transformed parent. The parent becomes the containing block and the card lands in the wrong place. Render it near the root, or pass `absolute` to pin it inside a positioned frame as the demo does.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `icon` to swap the default tile for your product mark, and `title`, `description` and `ctaLabel` for your own plan.\n- For several toasts at once use the kit Toaster; this card is a single, specific prompt.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nNothing floats at first. \"Install template\" opens a 300px card in the bottom-right corner of the frame that rises in with a blue-and-pink grainy glow across its top, a metal tile, \"This template is part of Pro\", \"Buy once and install the full source with the CLI.\" and a blue \"Get Pro\" button. Focus lands on the card; Tab reaches Get Pro and then the round dismiss button. Escape or the dismiss button lowers it away and the status reads \"Dismissed\", with focus back on \"Install template\". Get Pro closes it and the status reads \"Checkout opened\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "profile-cover-header": "# Build: Profile Cover Header\n\nThe card at the top of a profile page. A wide cover band of soft gradient artwork runs across the top, an 80px initials avatar sits half over its bottom edge, and below it come the name, the handle with small badges, inline follower counts and an underline tab strip. Visitors get Share and a Follow toggle; the owner gets Share and Edit.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nProfile identity card: gradient cover, overlapping initials avatar, name, handle and badges, Follow or Edit actions and underline tabs.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is a `section` labelled by the name heading, with a 16px radius, a 1px `border-ai-profile-card` border and `overflow-hidden`, so the cover is clipped to the top corners without its own radius.\n2. The cover band is 112px tall on phones and 160px from `sm` up. By default it holds `ProfileCoverArt`: an inline SVG (viewBox 680x170, `xMidYMid slice`) with a sky gradient, a radial sun halo, three blurred haze ellipses, a 24px sun and two rows of rounded forms. Its gradient ids come from `useId`, so several headers can share a page. Pass `cover` to put any node there instead.\n3. Under the cover, one flex row puts the avatar on the left and the actions on the right. The kit Avatar is enlarged to 80px with 28px initials and pulled up with `-mt-10`, so half of it overlaps the cover; a 4px ring in the card background cuts it out of the artwork. The actions sit 12px below the cover edge.\n4. Follow is a toggle button with `aria-pressed`. It works controlled (`isFollowing` + `onFollowingChange`) or uncontrolled (`defaultFollowing`): the click computes the next value, stores it only when uncontrolled, and always reports it. The button swaps from a primary \"Follow\" with a user-add icon to a secondary \"Following\" with a user-check icon, at a 104px minimum width from `sm` up so the row does not jump. Below `sm` the Share button keeps its icon and hides its label (its `aria-label` stays \"Share\"), so avatar and both actions fit one row at 360px.\n5. With `isOwner` the Follow toggle is replaced by an Edit button; Share appears whenever `onShare` is passed. Name (20px medium), handle (16px secondary), tone-coloured badges (11px semibold) and an optional bio and `dl` of stats stack below with 4px gaps.\n6. Tabs use the kit Tabs (react-aria): arrow keys move between tabs and each has a `TabPanel` filled by `renderPanel(id)`. The strip sits in an `overflow-x-auto` wrapper with `min-w-max`, so four tabs with counts scroll sideways on a phone instead of wrapping.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `cover height = 112px (h-28) below sm, 160px (h-40) from sm`\n  Wide enough to read as a banner on a laptop; on a phone it shrinks so the name and actions stay above the fold.\n- `avatar = 80px, -40px top margin, 4px ring, 28px initials`\n  Exactly half the avatar overlaps the cover, the classic profile silhouette; the ring keeps the circle readable over any artwork.\n- `follow button = min-width 104px from sm; Share label hidden below sm`\n  \"Following\" is wider than \"Follow\"; the minimum stops the Share button shifting when the state flips. On phones the icon-only Share leaves room for the wider label without wrapping.\n- `card padding = 16px sides, 16px panel padding`\n  Lines the avatar, name and tab labels up on one left edge.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ProfileCoverHeader(props: ProfileCoverHeaderProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ProfileCoverHeaderProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>, \"children\"> { name: string; handle: string; initials?: string; badges?: ProfileBadge[]; bio?: ReactNode; stats?: ProfileStat[]; cover?: ReactNode; coverTone?: ProfileCoverTone; isOwner?: boolean; isFollowing?: boolean; defaultFollowing?: boolean; onFollowingChange?: (isFollowing: boolean) => void; onShare?: () => void; onEdit?: () => void; tabs?: ProfileTab[]; selectedTab?: string; defaultSelectedTab?: string; onTabChange?: (id: string) => void; renderPanel?: (id: string) => ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }\n```\n\n- `name: string`\n  Display name, rendered as the h2 that labels the section.\n- `handle: string`\n  Handle shown under the name, e.g. \"@miraok\".\n- `initials: string` — default `first letter of name`\n  Avatar initials.\n- `badges: { label: ReactNode; tone?: \"neutral\" | \"purple\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" }[]` — default `[]`\n  Small pills after the handle, such as PRO.\n- `bio: ReactNode`\n  Optional one or two lines under the handle.\n- `stats: { label: string; value: ReactNode }[]` — default `[]`\n  Inline counts, such as followers and following.\n- `cover: ReactNode`\n  Replaces the built-in cover artwork.\n- `coverTone: \"sky\" | \"dusk\" | \"aurora\"` — default `\"sky\"`\n  Palette of the built-in artwork.\n- `isOwner: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows Edit instead of the Follow toggle.\n- `isFollowing: boolean`\n  Controlled follow state.\n- `defaultFollowing: boolean` — default `false`\n  Initial follow state when uncontrolled.\n- `onFollowingChange: (isFollowing: boolean) => void`\n  Called with the next state on every toggle.\n- `onShare: () => void`\n  Shows the Share button and handles its click.\n- `onEdit: () => void`\n  Click handler of Edit in the owner view.\n- `tabs: { id: string; label: ReactNode; count?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent }[]` — default `[]`\n  Underline tabs under the identity block.\n- `selectedTab: string`\n  Controlled selected tab id.\n- `defaultSelectedTab: string` — default `first tab`\n  Initial tab when uncontrolled.\n- `onTabChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the selected tab changes.\n- `renderPanel: (id: string) => ReactNode`\n  Content of each tab panel.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Follow works controlled (`isFollowing`) or uncontrolled (`defaultFollowing`).\nconst [innerFollowing, setInnerFollowing] = useState(defaultFollowing);\nconst following = isFollowing ?? innerFollowing;\nconst toggleFollow = () => {\n  const next = !following;\n  if (isFollowing === undefined) setInnerFollowing(next);\n  onFollowingChange?.(next);\n};\nconst avatarText = (initials ?? name.trim().charAt(0)).toUpperCase();\n\nconst actions = (\n  <div className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center justify-end gap-2\">\n    {onShare && (\n      <Button\n        variant=\"secondary\"\n        size=\"small\"\n        leadingIcon={RiUpload2Line}\n        aria-label=\"Share\"\n        onClick={onShare}\n      >\n        <span className=\"max-sm:hidden\">Share</span>\n      </Button>\n    )}\n    {isOwner ? (\n      <Button variant=\"secondary\" size=\"small\" leadingIcon={RiEditBoxLine} onClick={onEdit}>\n        Edit\n      </Button>\n    ) : (\n      <Button\n        variant={following ? \"secondary\" : \"primary\"}\n        size=\"small\"\n        aria-pressed={following}\n        leadingIcon={following ? RiUserFollowLine : RiUserAddLine}\n        onClick={toggleFollow}\n        className=\"sm:min-w-[104px]\"\n      >\n        {following ? \"Following\" : \"Follow\"}\n      </Button>\n    )}\n  </div>\n);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component owns no timers, listeners or observers. The tab strip uses the kit TabList, which disconnects its own resize and mutation observers on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates on its own. The only motion is the kit tab underline sliding and the buttons changing colour, both short CSS transitions; the artwork is static. In dark mode the cover is dimmed to 85% brightness so it does not glare.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Giving the cover its own top radius instead of clipping it with the card. At fractional zoom the two curves drift apart and a hairline of background shows. Clip with `overflow-hidden` on the card.\n- Hard-coding SVG gradient ids. Two headers on one page then share the first gradient and the second cover renders the wrong tone. The art builds its ids from `useId`.\n- Toggling the visible label without exposing the state. Screen readers hear a new label but no relationship to the old one; `aria-pressed` tells them it is the same toggle, now on.\n- Letting the tab strip wrap on narrow screens. The underline is measured from the selected tab and lands under the wrong row; scroll the strip instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Swap the artwork tone with `coverTone` (\"sky\", \"dusk\", \"aurora\") or pass a photo or your own SVG as `cover`.\n- Follower counts are plain `stats`; update them in `onFollowingChange` as the demo does.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA card shows a blue sky cover, an 80px \"M\" avatar overlapping its bottom edge, \"Mira Okafor\", \"@miraok\" with a PRO pill, \"1,284 followers\" and four tabs. Clicking Follow turns it into a pressed secondary \"Following\" button and the count reads 1,285; clicking again reverts. Switching the view to Owner swaps Follow for Edit. Arrow keys move between tabs and the panel below changes; at 360px wide the tab strip scrolls instead of wrapping.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "profile-stat-tiles": "# Build: Profile Stat Tiles\n\nThe contributions block of an AI profile: a caption, a large headline figure with a purple trend chip, and a row of small grey tiles such as lifetime tokens, peak tokens, longest task and top streak. Each tile shows its value, its label and an optional up or down change. The headline rolls to new values, and the tiles can become toggle buttons so one metric can drive another view.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA rolling headline figure with a trend chip over a row of grey stat tiles (value, label, trend) that can act as toggles.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The headline value runs through the kit `useCountUp` hook (480ms, ease-out cubic via requestAnimationFrame), so switching the dataset rolls the number instead of snapping. The rolling text is `aria-hidden`; a visually hidden copy carries the final formatted value, so screen readers never hear the intermediate numbers.\n2. The default formatter is an `Intl.NumberFormat` for whole US dollars; pass `formatValue` for tokens, hours or anything else. Trends are formatted by `formatTrend`: one decimal at most, a leading plus for gains and a true minus sign (U+2212) for losses.\n3. The headline trend is a kit Chip at caption size: purple for gains, rose for losses, neutral for zero. Inside tiles the trend is smaller: a 12px arrow icon and the unsigned percentage (`formatTrend(trend, false)`) in the same purple or rose text, with a visually hidden \"up\" or \"down\" so the direction is not conveyed by colour and icon alone.\n4. Tiles sit in a `role=\"group\"` grid: two columns on phones and `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(132px, 1fr))` from `sm`, so four tiles share one row on a card and fold cleanly when the card narrows. Each tile is a 10px-radius block on `background-secondary` with 10px padding, the value in 14px medium and the label in 13px secondary text.\n5. Selection is opt-in: when `onSelectionChange` or `selectedId` is passed, the tiles render as `button`s with `aria-pressed`; clicking the selected one clears it. The selected tile lifts to the primary background with a 1px ring and a small shadow. It works controlled (`selectedId`) or uncontrolled (`defaultSelectedId`).\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `count-up = 480ms, ease-out cubic`\n  Long enough to read as a change of dataset, short enough to finish before the eye has moved on to the tiles.\n- `tile grid = 2 columns below sm, auto-fit minmax(132px, 1fr) from sm, 8px gap`\n  132px is the narrowest width that keeps \"Lifetime tokens\" and \"562.7M ↑ 8.1%\" on single lines.\n- `tile = 10px radius, 10px padding, 14px value, 13px label`\n  Small, quiet tiles that sit under a 24px headline without competing with it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ProfileStatTiles(props: ProfileStatTilesProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ProfileStatTilesProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>, \"children\" | \"onSelect\"> { label?: ReactNode; value?: number; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; trend?: number; tiles: StatTile[]; selectedId?: string | null; defaultSelectedId?: string | null; onSelectionChange?: (id: string | null) => void; tilesLabel?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }\ninterface StatTile { id: string; label: string; value: ReactNode; trend?: number }\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Caption above the headline.\n- `value: number`\n  Headline number; rolls when it changes. Omit to show only the tiles.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `whole US dollars`\n  Formats the headline.\n- `trend: number`\n  Headline percent change, shown as a chip.\n- `tiles: StatTile[]`\n  The tiles: id, label, value and optional trend.\n- `selectedId: string | null`\n  Controlled selected tile. Passing it makes tiles toggle buttons.\n- `defaultSelectedId: string | null` — default `null`\n  Initial selection when uncontrolled.\n- `onSelectionChange: (id: string | null) => void`\n  Called with the tile id, or null when the selected tile is clicked again.\n- `tilesLabel: string` — default `\"Stats\"`\n  Accessible name of the tile group.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Tiles are toggle buttons only when someone listens or controls them.\nconst selectable = onSelectionChange !== undefined || selectedId !== undefined;\nconst [innerSelected, setInnerSelected] = useState<string | null>(defaultSelectedId);\nconst current = selectedId !== undefined ? selectedId : innerSelected;\nconst toggle = (id: string) => {\n  const next = current === id ? null : id;\n  if (selectedId === undefined) setInnerSelected(next);\n  onSelectionChange?.(next);\n};\n\nconst tileBase =\n  \"flex min-w-0 flex-col items-start gap-0.5 rounded-[10px] px-2.5 py-2.5 text-start transition-[background-color,box-shadow] duration-150 ease-out\";\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The count-up hook cancels its requestAnimationFrame when the value changes again or the component unmounts. There are no other timers or listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the headline shows its final value immediately instead of rolling. Tile hover and selection keep their 150ms colour change, which does not move anything.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Announcing the rolling number. A live headline that changes every frame floods a screen reader; the animated text is hidden and only the final value is exposed.\n- Showing direction by colour alone. Purple and rose are hard to tell apart for many people; the arrow icon and the hidden \"up\" or \"down\" carry the meaning.\n- Rendering tiles as buttons when nothing listens to them. They would be focus stops that do nothing; tiles are plain blocks unless selection is wired up.\n- Using a hyphen for negative trends. \"-3.2%\" looks lighter than \"+3.2%\" and may break onto a new line; the U+2212 minus sign is the width of a plus.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Omit `value` to render only the tiles, for example inside another card that already has its own headline.\n- Wire `onSelectionChange` to a heatmap or chart to filter it by the chosen metric.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Contributions this year\", \"$7,462\" with a purple \"+14.8%\" chip and four tiles: 9B Lifetime tokens, 562.7M Peak tokens, 12h 54m Longest task (rose, down 3.2%) and 62 days Top streak. Clicking a tile presses it and the hint names that metric; clicking it again clears it. Choosing 2024 rolls the headline to $5,954 with a rose \"−2.5%\" chip and updates every tile. At 360px wide the tiles form two columns.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "progress": "# Build: Progress\n\nHow far along a task is, as a rounded rail or a small ring. Both are React Aria `ProgressBar`s, so they are announced with their label and a locale-formatted value. The bar's fill moves by `translate` and eases to each new value over 500ms; the indeterminate bar sweeps a band of light (crisp core, tight glow, wide bloom) along the rail, and the indeterminate ring is the Spinner's orbiting light.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nProgress bar and ring, determinate or indeterminate, with an eased translate fill and a layered light sweep, on React Aria.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `ProgressTrack` draws the rail: `relative w-full rounded-full bg-border-button-default text-accent-500` at `h-1`, `h-1.5` or `h-2` for sm / md / lg. The fill colour is `currentColor`, so a text class recolours it.\n2. Determinate: inside an `overflow-hidden rounded-full` clip, a full-width `bg-current` span is shifted by `translate: -(100 - p)% 0` (positive in RTL, read from `useDirection()`), with `p` clamped to 0..100. `transition-[translate] duration-500` with `cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)` eases each change on the compositor instead of animating `width`.\n3. Indeterminate (`Sweep`): a wrapper clipped with `[clip-path:inset(-8px_0_round_9999px)]` keeps the light inside the rail's length but lets it bleed 8px past its thickness. A `w-2/5` band runs `bui-progress-sweep` (translate -100% to 250%) over 1.6s `cubic-bezier(0.65,0,0.35,1)`, reversed in RTL.\n4. The band holds three right-anchored `bg-linear-to-r from-transparent to-current` layers: a bloom (`-inset-y-[5px] right-[10px]`, opacity 30%, `blur-[5px]`), a glow (`-inset-y-[1.5px] right-[4px] left-[25%]`, opacity 60%, `blur-[2px]`) and a crisp core (`right-0 left-[35%]`). Pulling the soft layers back from the right edge is what keeps the sharp head in front.\n5. `ProgressBar` wraps `AriaProgressBar` and uses its render props `{ percentage, valueText }`: a label row (the kit `Label`, truncated, and `valueText` in `tabular-nums`), the track, and an optional caption description. The value label shows by default only when there is a label and the bar is determinate.\n6. `ProgressCircle` maps sm / md / lg to box 16 / 24 / 32 and line 2 / 2.5 / 3. It draws a `-rotate-90` SVG with a `stroke-border-button-default` track circle and a `currentColor` arc whose `strokeDashoffset = circumference × (1 - p/100)`, transitioned over 500ms. Indeterminate, it renders `SpinnerGlyph` at sm / lg / xl.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `track heights = sm h-1 (4px), md h-1.5 (6px), lg h-2 (8px)`\n  Md is the default rail of the agent-limits card; sm fits inside dense rows and lg reads as a primary step indicator.\n- `fill easing = 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) on translate`\n  A strong ease-out lands each new value softly. Moving by `translate` keeps the change on the compositor; animating `width` relayouts every frame.\n- `sweep = band w-2/5, 1.6s cubic-bezier(0.65,0,0.35,1), translate -100% to 250%; 4s under reduced motion`\n  250% of a 40% band carries it fully past the rail's end, so the band leaves before it re-enters on the left.\n- `sweep bleed = clip-path inset(-8px 0 round 9999px); bloom -5px, glow -1.5px`\n  The glow and bloom are taller than the 6px rail. Clipping only horizontally lets the light halo above and below while it still stops at the rail ends.\n- `ring sizes = sm 16/2, md 24/2.5, lg 32/3 (box/line px); indeterminate uses SpinnerGlyph sm / lg / xl`\n  Ring and spinner share box sizes, so a ring can swap between determinate and indeterminate in place without moving its neighbours.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype ProgressSize = \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"\ninterface ProgressBarProps extends Omit<AriaProgressBarProps, \"children\" | \"className\"> { label?: ReactNode; showValueLabel?: boolean; description?: ReactNode; size?: ProgressSize; className?: string }\nfunction ProgressBar(props: ProgressBarProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ProgressTrackProps { percentage: number; size?: ProgressSize; isIndeterminate?: boolean; className?: string }\nfunction ProgressTrack(props: ProgressTrackProps): JSX.Element\ninterface ProgressCircleProps extends Omit<AriaProgressBarProps, \"children\" | \"className\"> { size?: ProgressSize; className?: string }\nfunction ProgressCircle(props: ProgressCircleProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: number` — default `0`\n  Current value between `minValue` and `maxValue` (React Aria).\n- `isIndeterminate: boolean` — default `false`\n  Show the light sweep (bar) or the orbiting spinner (ring) instead of a value.\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Bar only. Label above the rail; also turns the value label on by default.\n- `showValueLabel: boolean`\n  Bar only. Show the formatted value at the end of the label row. Defaults to on when there is a label and a value.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Bar only. Helper text under the rail.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Bar: 4 / 6 / 8px rail. Ring: 16 / 24 / 32px box.\n- `className: string`\n  Bar: classes on the root. Ring: arc colour via a text class (default accent).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** The sweeping band of light for an indeterminate bar. */\nfunction Sweep() {\n  return (\n    // The clip keeps the light inside the rail's length but lets the glow\n    // and bloom bleed past its thickness.\n    <span aria-hidden className=\"absolute inset-0 [clip-path:inset(-8px_0_round_9999px)]\">\n      <span\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute inset-y-0 left-0 w-2/5\",\n          \"animate-[bui-progress-sweep_1.6s_cubic-bezier(0.65,0,0.35,1)_infinite]\",\n          \"rtl:[animation-direction:reverse] motion-reduce:[animation-duration:4s]\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <span className=\"absolute inset-0 rtl:-scale-x-100\">\n          <span className=\"absolute -inset-y-[5px] right-[10px] left-0 rounded-full bg-linear-to-r from-transparent to-current opacity-30 blur-[5px]\" />\n          <span className=\"absolute -inset-y-[1.5px] right-[4px] left-[25%] rounded-full bg-linear-to-r from-transparent to-current opacity-60 blur-[2px]\" />\n          <span className=\"absolute inset-y-0 right-0 left-[35%] rounded-full bg-linear-to-r from-transparent to-current\" />\n        </span>\n      </span>\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface ProgressTrackProps {\n  /** 0 to 100. */\n  percentage: number;\n  size?: ProgressSize;\n  /** Draw the indeterminate sweep instead of a fill. */\n  isIndeterminate?: boolean;\n  /** Colour of the fill, from the text colour. Default accent. */\n  className?: string;\n}\n\n/** The rail and its fill, shared by ProgressBar and Meter. */\nexport function ProgressTrack({ percentage, size = \"md\", isIndeterminate = false, className }: ProgressTrackProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  return (\n    // The rail is File Upload's ring track colour, which keeps an edge on\n    // every surface in both themes (chart-track matches dark cards).\n    <div className={cx(\"relative w-full rounded-full bg-border-button-default text-accent-500\", styles.track[size], className)}>\n      {isIndeterminate ? (\n        <Sweep />\n      ) : (\n        <span className=\"absolute inset-0 overflow-hidden rounded-full\">\n          <span\n            className={cx(\"absolute inset-0 rounded-full bg-current transition-[translate] duration-500 motion-reduce:transition-none\", EASE)}\n            style={{ translate: `${(direction === \"rtl\" ? 1 : -1) * (100 - Math.min(100, Math.max(0, percentage)))}% 0` }}\n          />\n        </span>\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. Motion is CSS transitions and keyframes; there are no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Fill and ring transitions are removed (`motion-reduce:transition-none`), so values jump straight to their new position. The indeterminate sweep keeps running but slows from 1.6s to 4s, and the indeterminate ring inherits Spinner's slower 2.4s orbit, because a stopped loader reads as a hang.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not animate the fill's `width`. The component translates a full-width span inside an `overflow-hidden` clip; width transitions cause layout on every frame.\n- Do not forget RTL. The fill offset flips sign with `useDirection()` and the sweep reverses; a hard-coded negative translate fills from the wrong edge in Arabic or Hebrew.\n- Do not replace the sweep's `clip-path` with `overflow: hidden`. It would cut the glow off at the rail's 6px thickness.\n- Do not render a ring or an unlabelled bar without `aria-label`. React Aria warns and screen readers announce an unnamed progress bar.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `ProgressTrack` is exported and shared with Meter, which reuses the same rail and fill.\n- Values are React Aria's: `value`, `minValue` (0), `maxValue` (100) and `formatOptions`; `valueText` is formatted for the locale.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n`<ProgressBar label=\"Uploading\" value={64} />` shows \"Uploading\" with \"64%\" on the right and a 6px rail filled 64% in the accent colour; raising the value to 90 slides the fill smoothly over half a second. An indeterminate bar shows a glowing band crossing the rail left to right every 1.6s, with its glow spilling slightly above and below the rail but never past its ends. Rings show a clockwise arc from 12 o'clock proportional to the value, and an indeterminate ring shows the spinner. Each exposes `role=\"progressbar\"` with its name.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "project-board": "# Build: Project Board\n\nThe Project Board template as one working page. Five columns (Backlog, To do, In progress, Review, Done) show count against a work-in-progress limit; cards drag between and within columns with an insertion line, open a side panel with editable status, priority, project, assignees, a token usage chart and comments, and the header carries an activity inbox, sort, filter, a panel toggle and New ticket.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA full kanban page: drag tickets across five columns, open a side panel to edit them, create, sort and filter, with an activity inbox.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every ticket lives in one ordered array; a column is that array filtered by `stage`, then filtered by the active `BoardFilter` and optionally sorted for display. Counts in the column header use the unfiltered stage list, so `3/8` stays true while filters hide cards.\n2. `moveTicket(id, stage, beforeId)` removes the ticket, restamps its stage (moving into Done stamps a completion day, moving out clears it) and splices it back in front of `beforeId`, or after the last ticket already in the target stage when `beforeId` is null. Drag, the Alt+Arrow keys, the panel's Status select and the column menu all go through it.\n3. Drag and drop is native HTML5. A card sets `text/plain` to its id on `dragstart`. Each card's `dragover` compares the pointer to its vertical midpoint and reports `{stage, beforeId}` (itself, or the next card); the column's own `dragover` only claims the end slot when the pointer first enters it. `drop` reads the id and calls `moveTicket`; `dragend` clears the indicator either way.\n4. Each card is an `article` with a stretched title button (`after:absolute after:inset-0`), so the whole card opens the detail panel while the hit area stays one real button. Alt+ArrowLeft/Right on that button moves the ticket one stage and an `aria-live` region announces it.\n5. The side panel shows `TicketPanel` (keyed by ticket id so the title draft resets) or, with nothing open, a board overview of column load and total tokens. Every edit appends an entry to the activity log that feeds the Inbox popover; closing the popover marks entries read.\n6. Sidebar rows are `href=\"#\"` links; one `onClickCapture` on the root maps the link's `aria-label` to a `DASHBOARD_NAV` key and switches the view in state. Other keys show a placeholder with \"Back to Project board\".\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `column width = 268px`\n  Fits \"KEY > epic\" plus a three-avatar stack on one line; the board scrolls sideways when the side panel takes 332px.\n- `WIP limits = 8 / 5 / 4 / 3 / 6`\n  Backlog to Done. Going over turns the count rose so overload is visible at a glance.\n- `TODAY = 15 (Sep)`\n  Fixed day stamped on created tickets and on tickets moved into Done, so renders never read the clock.\n- `drop midpoint = rect.top + rect.height / 2`\n  Upper half inserts before the hovered card, lower half after it, matching where the line is drawn.\n- `activity cap = 30 entries`\n  Keeps the inbox list short and the state small during long sessions.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ProjectBoard(props: { tickets?: Ticket[]; defaultNotifications?: BellNotification[]; defaultView?: string; defaultPanelOpen?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `tickets: Ticket[]` — default `BOARD_TICKETS`\n  Tickets in board order (13 samples). Moves, edits, comments and deletes change a local copy.\n- `defaultNotifications: BellNotification[]` — default `BOARD_NOTIFICATIONS`\n  Initial bell contents; unread items drive the badge count.\n- `defaultView: string` — default `\"project-board\"`\n  Sidebar key shown first. Any other key shows the placeholder view.\n- `defaultPanelOpen: boolean` — default `false`\n  Show the side panel (board overview) on first render. Opening a ticket always opens it.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The sidebar rows are `href=\"#\"` links: one capture listener turns any of\n// them into a local view switch so the host URL never changes.\nconst onNavClickCapture = (event: MouseEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n  if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;\n  const link = event.target.closest('a[href=\"#\"]');\n  if (!link) return;\n  event.preventDefault();\n  const item = DASHBOARD_NAV.find((entry) => entry.label === link.getAttribute(\"aria-label\"));\n  if (item) setView(item.key);\n};\n\n// One ordered array holds every ticket. Moving = remove it, restamp its\n// stage (Done gets a completion day), then splice it back in front of\n// `beforeId`, or after the last ticket already in the target stage.\nconst moveTicket = (id: string, stage: StageId, beforeId: string | null) => {\n  const ticket = tickets.find((t) => t.id === id);\n  if (!ticket) return;\n  if (beforeId === id) {\n    if (ticket.stage === stage) return;\n    beforeId = null;\n  }\n  setTickets((list) => {\n    const current = list.find((t) => t.id === id);\n    if (!current) return list;\n    const rest = list.filter((t) => t.id !== id);\n    let at = beforeId ? rest.findIndex((t) => t.id === beforeId) : -1;\n    if (at < 0) {\n      at = rest.length;\n      for (let i = rest.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        if (rest[i]?.stage === stage) {\n          at = i + 1;\n          break;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    return [...rest.slice(0, at), withStage(current, stage), ...rest.slice(at)];\n  });\n  const label = stageOf(stage).label;\n  const message = ticket.stage === stage ? `Reordered ${id} in ${label}` : `${id} moved to ${label}`;\n  if (ticket.stage !== stage) log(`Jordan moved ${id} to ${label}`);\n  setAnnouncement(message);\n};\n\nconst onDropTicket = (id: string) => {\n  const target = drop;\n  setDragId(null);\n  setDrop(null);\n  if (target) moveTicket(id, target.stage, target.beforeId);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, observers or global listeners: drag state lives in React state set by element drag events and is cleared on `dragend` and `drop`. Overlays and the Toaster clean up their own listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The ticket entry fade, token bar height and column-load width transitions all carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so changes apply instantly. Dragging uses the browser's own drag image and has no added animation.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `preventDefault` only on the column. Cards sit on top of it, so each card must also cancel `dragover` (and stop it bubbling) or the drop is refused over cards.\n- Clearing the drop target on every `dragleave`. The event fires when the pointer crosses into a child card; ignore it while `relatedTarget` is still inside the column.\n- Counting the visible cards for the WIP limit. Filters would make a full column look under its limit; count the stage before filtering.\n- Reordering inside a sorted view. With a sort active the display order is derived, so dropping still moves stage but the manual order only shows again under \"Manual order\".\n- Wrapping a `PopoverTrigger` child in a plain `span`. The trigger must be an element that forwards its ref and props (a native `button` here) or the popover cannot anchor.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Composed from kit items: `DashboardSidebar`, `DashboardPageHeader`, `NotificationBellBadge`, `AvatarStack`, `PriorityBadge`, `Dropdown`, `Popover`, `Modal`, `Select`, `Input`, `Textarea`, `Checkbox` and `Toaster`. The board, panel and toolbar are this item's own files.\n- New tickets slide in with a CSS `@starting-style` fade and lift (`starting:opacity-0 starting:translate-y-1`), switched off under `motion-reduce`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280x800 the sidebar shows Project board selected and the board shows Backlog 3/8, To do 3/5, In progress 2/4, Review 1/3 and Done 4/6. Dragging FE-76 onto Done makes Review 0/3 and Done 5/6 with \"Completed 15 Sep\" on the card. New ticket with a title adds it to the top of Backlog (4/8) and toasts. Clicking a card opens its panel; changing Status moves the card; posting a comment adds it to the list. Filter by Urgent leaves only DS-31 visible. Clicking Calendar in the sidebar shows the placeholder and \"Back to Project board\" returns.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "props-table": "# Build: Props Table\n\nA documentation table for a component API: prop name in a grey code chip, the TypeScript type in the purple command colour, the default in muted code and a plain-language description. Required and deprecated props get small tinted tags. When the slot it sits in is narrower than 42rem, the same table re-flows into one bordered card per prop with Type and Default labels.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAPI props table with code-styled names, types and defaults; long unions collapse, and narrow slots stack each prop into a card.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is an `@container`; every layout switch uses `@2xl:` container variants, so the table reacts to the width of the column it lives in (a sidebar, a modal, a phone) rather than the viewport.\n2. Mobile-first, the `<table>`, `<tbody>` and `<tr>` are `block` / `flex flex-col` and each row is a rounded bordered card; from `@2xl` they return to `table`, `table-row-group` and `table-row` with a top separator instead of a card border. Explicit `role=\"table|rowgroup|row|columnheader|rowheader|cell\"` attributes keep the semantics that `display` overrides strip in some engines.\n3. The header row is `sr-only` in card mode (`@2xl:not-sr-only` brings it back) and each cell carries its own `aria-hidden` label (\"Type\", \"Default\") that is `@2xl:hidden`, so sighted users get labels in cards while screen readers keep the column headers.\n4. A string `type` is split by `splitUnion`, which walks the string and only splits on `|` at depth 0 outside quotes (tracking `([{<` and ignoring the `>` of `=>`). Unions with more than `maxUnionMembers` members show the first ones plus a \"+N more\" button with `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls`; the expanded rows are kept in a `Set` keyed by prop name.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `stack breakpoint = @2xl (42rem / 672px container width)`\n  Below this the four columns squeeze descriptions into 2-3 words per line; the card layout reads better.\n- `maxUnionMembers = 4 (demo uses 3)`\n  Four literal members fit one line of a 30% column; longer unions wrap into a wall of code.\n- `column widths = 22% / 30% / 14% / rest`\n  Types need the most room after descriptions; defaults are short literals.\n- `cell padding = px-4 py-3, header py-2.5`\n  Matches the 16px inset of the docs preview cards and keeps rows at a ~44px minimum.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction PropsTable(props: { rows: PropsTableRow[]; caption?: string; maxUnionMembers?: number; labels?: PropsTableLabels; idPrefix?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype PropsTableRow = { name: string; type: string | ReactNode; default?: string | ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; required?: boolean; deprecated?: boolean }\nfunction splitUnion(type: string): string[]\n```\n\n- `rows: PropsTableRow[]`\n  One entry per prop: name, type, optional default, description, required and deprecated flags.\n- `caption: string`\n  Visually hidden `<caption>` naming the table.\n- `maxUnionMembers: number` — default `4`\n  Unions longer than this collapse behind a \"+N more\" toggle; 0 never collapses.\n- `labels: PropsTableLabels` — default `{ name: \"Prop\", type: \"Type\", default: \"Default\", description: \"Description\", ... }`\n  Header and tag wording, for localisation.\n- `idPrefix: string`\n  Gives each row an id of `<idPrefix>-<name>` for deep links.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the container element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Splits a type string on `|` at nesting depth 0, so\n * `\"a\" | Array<\"b\" | \"c\"> | (() => void)` yields three members.\n */\nexport function splitUnion(type: string): string[] {\n  const parts: string[] = [];\n  let depth = 0;\n  let quote: string | null = null;\n  let start = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < type.length; i++) {\n    const ch = type[i];\n    if (quote) {\n      if (ch === quote && type[i - 1] !== \"\\\\\") quote = null;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (ch === '\"' || ch === \"'\" || ch === \"`\") quote = ch;\n    else if (ch === \"(\" || ch === \"[\" || ch === \"{\" || ch === \"<\") depth++;\n    else if (ch === \")\" || ch === \"]\" || ch === \"}\" || (ch === \">\" && type[i - 1] !== \"=\")) depth = Math.max(0, depth - 1);\n    else if (ch === \"|\" && depth === 0) {\n      parts.push(type.slice(start, i).trim());\n      start = i + 1;\n    }\n  }\n  parts.push(type.slice(start).trim());\n  return parts.filter(Boolean);\n}\n\nfunction TypeCell({\n  type,\n  max,\n  expanded,\n  onToggle,\n  controlsId,\n}: {\n  type: PropsTableRow[\"type\"];\n  max: number;\n  expanded: boolean;\n  onToggle: () => void;\n  controlsId: string;\n}) {\n  if (typeof type !== \"string\") return <>{type}</>;\n  const members = splitUnion(type);\n  const collapsible = max > 0 && members.length > max;\n  const shown = collapsible && !expanded ? members.slice(0, max) : members;\n  return (\n    <span className=\"inline-flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-1 gap-y-0.5\">\n      <code id={controlsId} className=\"font-mono text-caption-1-regular break-words text-docs-command-accent\">\n        {shown.map((member, i) => (\n          <span key={`${member}-${i}`}>\n            {i > 0 ? <span className=\"text-text-tertiary\"> | </span> : null}\n            {member}\n          </span>\n        ))}\n      </code>\n      {collapsible ? (\n        <button\n          type=\"button\"\n          aria-expanded={expanded}\n          aria-controls={controlsId}\n          onClick={onToggle}\n          className={cx(\n            \"cursor-pointer rounded-md px-1 text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary outline-none\",\n            \"transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-hover hover:text-text-primary motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n          )}\n        >\n          {expanded ? \"Show less\" : `+${members.length - max} more`}\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n\nconst CELL_LABEL = \"w-20 shrink-0 text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary @2xl:hidden\";\n\nexport function PropsTable({\n  rows,\n  caption,\n  maxUnionMembers = 4,\n  labels: labelsProp,\n  idPrefix,\n  className,\n}: PropsTableProps) {\n  const labels = { ...DEFAULT_LABELS, ...labelsProp };\n  const baseId = useId();\n  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState<ReadonlySet<string>>(() => new Set());\n  const toggle = (name: string) =>\n    setExpanded((prev) => {\n      const next = new Set(prev);\n      if (next.has(name)) next.delete(name);\n      else next.add(name);\n      return next;\n    });\n\n  // Explicit ARIA roles keep table semantics while the narrow layout sets\n  // every element to block/flex (display overrides strip implicit roles in\n  // some engines). The layout switch is a container query, so it follows the\n  // width of the slot the table sits in, not the viewport.\n  return (\n    <div className={cx(\"@container w-full\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"@2xl:overflow-hidden @2xl:rounded-2xl @2xl:border @2xl:border-border-component-detail-container @2xl:bg-background-primary-default\">\n        <table role=\"table\" className=\"block w-full border-collapse text-start @2xl:table\">\n          {caption ? <caption className=\"sr-only\">{caption}</caption> : null}\n          <thead role=\"rowgroup\" className=\"sr-only @2xl:not-sr-only @2xl:table-header-group\">\n            <tr role=\"row\" className=\"@2xl:bg-background-secondary-default\">\n              {[\n                [labels.name, \"w-[22%]\"],\n                [labels.type, \"w-[30%]\"],\n                [labels.default, \"w-[14%]\"],\n                [labels.description, \"\"],\n              ].map(([label, width]) => (\n                <th\n                  key={label}\n                  role=\"columnheader\"\n                  scope=\"col\"\n                  className={cx(\"px-4 py-2.5 text-start align-middle text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary\", width)}\n                >\n                  {label}\n                </th>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          </thead>\n          <tbody role=\"rowgroup\" className=\"flex flex-col gap-2 @2xl:table-row-group\">\n            {rows.map((row) => {\n              const typeId = `${baseId}-${row.name}-type`;\n              return (\n                <tr\n                  key={row.name}\n                  role=\"row\"\n                  id={idPrefix ? `${idPrefix}-${row.name}` : undefined}\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"flex scroll-mt-20 flex-col gap-2 rounded-2xl border border-border-component-detail-container bg-background-primary-default p-4\",\n                    \"@2xl:table-row @2xl:rounded-none @2xl:border-0 @2xl:border-t @2xl:border-border-table @2xl:bg-transparent @2xl:p-0\",\n                    \"@2xl:target:bg-background-secondary-default\",\n                  )}\n                >\n                  <th role=\"rowheader\" scope=\"row\" className=\"block text-start font-normal @2xl:table-cell @2xl:px-4 @2xl:py-3 @2xl:align-top\">\n                    <span className=\"inline-flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1.5\">\n                      <code\n                        className={cx(\n                          \"rounded-md bg-background-secondary-default px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-caption-1-medium text-text-primary\",\n                          row.deprecated && \"text-text-tertiary line-through\",\n                        )}\n                      >\n                        {row.name}\n                      </code>\n                      {row.required ? (\n                        <span className=\"rounded-sm bg-status-rose-background px-1 text-caption-2-medium text-status-rose-text\">\n                          {labels.required}\n                        </span>\n                      ) : null}\n                      {row.deprecated ? (\n                        <span className=\"rounded-sm bg-status-yellow-background px-1 text-caption-2-medium text-status-yellow-text\">\n                          {labels.deprecated}\n                        </span>\n                      ) : null}\n                    </span>\n                  </th>\n                  <td role=\"cell\" className=\"flex gap-3 @2xl:table-cell @2xl:px-4 @2xl:py-3 @2xl:align-top\">\n                    <span aria-hidden className={CELL_LABEL}>\n                      {labels.type}\n                    </span>\n                    <TypeCell\n                      type={row.type}\n                      max={maxUnionMembers}\n                      expanded={expanded.has(row.name)}\n                      onToggle={() => toggle(row.name)}\n                      controlsId={typeId}\n                    />\n                  </td>\n                  <td role=\"cell\" className=\"flex gap-3 @2xl:table-cell @2xl:px-4 @2xl:py-3 @2xl:align-top\">\n                    <span aria-hidden className={CELL_LABEL}>\n                      {labels.default}\n                    </span>\n                    {row.default === undefined ? (\n                      <span className=\"text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n                        <span aria-hidden>—</span>\n                        <span className=\"sr-only\">{labels.none}</span>\n                      </span>\n                    ) : typeof row.default === \"string\" ? (\n                      <code className=\"font-mono text-caption-1-regular break-words text-text-secondary\">{row.default}</code>\n                    ) : (\n                      row.default\n                    )}\n                  </td>\n                  <td\n                    role=\"cell\"\n                    className={cx(\n                      \"block text-body-regular text-text-secondary @2xl:table-cell @2xl:border-0 @2xl:px-4 @2xl:py-3 @2xl:align-top\",\n                      row.description ? \"border-t border-border-table pt-3\" : \"hidden\",\n                    )}\n                  >\n                    {row.description}\n                  </td>\n                </tr>\n              );\n            })}\n          </tbody>\n        </table>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release: no timers, listeners or observers. Container queries are pure CSS.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only the \"+N more\" button has a 150ms colour transition, removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none`. The layout switch is instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using viewport breakpoints (`md:`) for the stack switch. A props table in a narrow docs column on a wide screen would stay a cramped table; a container query follows the slot.\n- Setting `display: block` on table elements without explicit ARIA roles. Some browsers then expose a flat list of text with no row or column relationships.\n- Splitting union types on every `|`. Pipes inside generics, tuples, object types or string literals belong to one member.\n- Labelling card cells with visible text that screen readers also hear next to the real column header. Mark the inline labels `aria-hidden`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass a ReactNode as `type` or `default` to skip union splitting and render your own markup (links to type docs, for example).\n- With `idPrefix`, each row gets `id=\"<prefix>-<name>\"` and `scroll-mt-20`, and a `:target` row is highlighted in table mode.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt full width the demo shows a bordered table with Prop, Type, Default and Description headers and six LineChartCard rows; `data` carries a \"required\" tag and `curve` a struck-through name with \"deprecated\". The `range` type shows three members and \"+3 more\"; clicking it reveals all six and the button reads \"Show less\" with aria-expanded=\"true\". Switching to ChartTooltip swaps to four rows. Choosing Narrow constrains the slot to 360px and the same rows become separate cards with \"Type\" and \"Default\" labels while the header row disappears visually.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "proximity-snap-track": "# Build: Proximity Snap Track\n\nA scrollable rail whose items may be any width. `scroll-snap-type: x proximity` snaps to an item only when the scroll comes to rest near one, so free scanning stays free. `scroll-snap-stop: always` caps a single fling at one item, so a hard swipe cannot launch the user past six cards. No JavaScript runs during the scroll.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA horizontal rail of variable-width cards that snaps only when you stop near one, and never skips past two at once.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The container is `overflow-x: auto` with `scroll-snap-type: x proximity`. `proximity` means the browser only adjusts the resting position when the natural end of the scroll already falls close to a snap point; anywhere else, it leaves the position exactly where the user left it.\n2. Each item declares `scroll-snap-align: start` (or `center`) — this is what makes it a snap point at all — and `scroll-snap-stop: always`, which forbids the browser from passing more than one snap point per gesture no matter how much momentum the fling carried.\n3. `scroll-padding-inline` is set alongside `padding-inline` to the same gutter. They are different properties: `padding` insets the content, `scroll-padding` insets the *snapport* — the rectangle a snap position is measured against. With padding alone, a `start`-aligned item comes to rest flush against the container border and the inset the design asked for vanishes at every rest position.\n4. `overscroll-behavior-x: contain` stops a horizontal fling that reaches the end of the rail from chaining to an ancestor scroller or triggering the browser's back-navigation gesture.\n5. `scroll-behavior` is `smooth` normally and `auto` under reduced motion, which affects programmatic and keyboard-initiated scrolls. The snap adjustment itself is the browser's own and already respects the OS setting.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `snapType = 'proximity'`\n  The whole argument of this component. With `mandatory`, the browser must always come to rest on a snap point, so on a rail of variable-width items it is physically impossible to stop between two of them — every attempt to park somewhere gets yanked. Worse, on a long rail `mandatory` plus a short flick can leave the user unable to reach a position at all. `proximity` snaps when it helps and gets out of the way when it does not.\n- `snapStop = 'always'`\n  Without it, a hard fling on a trackpad passes every snap point and lands on whichever item the momentum ran out over — which on a rail of 20 cards means the user has no way to advance by exactly one. `always` turns each item into a gate.\n- `snapAlign = 'start'`\n  Correct default for variable-width items: their left edges line up at rest, which reads as a column of starts. `center` is right only when items are uniform, because with mixed widths a centred rest position puts every item's left edge somewhere different.\n- `gutter = 16`\n  Set on both `padding-inline` and `scroll-padding-inline`. The two must match or the resting inset and the visual inset disagree by exactly this many pixels, which looks like a rounding bug.\n- `gap = 12`\n  Large enough that two adjacent cards read as separate snap targets, small enough that the next card is clearly a continuation of the row rather than a separate section.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ProximitySnapTrack(props: { children: ReactNode; snapType?: 'proximity' | 'mandatory'; snapAlign?: 'start' | 'center'; snapStop?: 'normal' | 'always'; gutter?: number; gap?: number; className?: string; itemClassName?: string; 'aria-label'?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One item per child. Widths may differ.\n- `snapType: 'proximity' | 'mandatory'` — default `'proximity'`\n  Snap strictness. `mandatory` is exposed so the difference can be felt, not because it is usually right here.\n- `snapAlign: 'start' | 'center'` — default `'start'`\n  Which edge of an item lines up with the snapport.\n- `snapStop: 'normal' | 'always'` — default `'always'`\n  `always` caps one gesture at one item.\n- `gutter: number` — default `16`\n  Inset in px, applied to both `padding-inline` and `scroll-padding-inline`.\n- `gap: number` — default `12`\n  Gap between items in px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the scroll container.\n- `itemClassName: string`\n  Merged onto each item cell.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Track'`\n  Label for the focusable scroll region.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst trackStyle: CSSProperties = {\n  scrollSnapType: `x ${snapType}`,\n  // `scroll-padding` is what a snap position is measured against, and it is\n  // a separate property from `padding`. With padding alone, a `start`-aligned\n  // item snaps flush to the container's border and the inset the design asked\n  // for silently disappears at every rest position.\n  scrollPaddingInline: `${gutter}px`,\n  paddingInline: `${gutter}px`,\n  gap: `${gap}px`,\n  // Programmatic scrolls (anchor jumps, `scrollIntoView`, arrow keys in some\n  // engines) animate unless told not to. Snapping itself is not affected —\n  // it is the browser's own transition and honours the OS setting already.\n  scrollBehavior: reduced ? 'auto' : 'smooth',\n};\n\nconst itemStyle: CSSProperties = {\n  scrollSnapAlign: snapAlign,\n  scrollSnapStop: snapStop,\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release. All behaviour is declarative CSS on nodes React owns; the only subscription is `useReducedMotion`, which removes its own `matchMedia` listener on unmount. No listeners, no timers, no rAF.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rail behaves identically — it is a scroll container, and scrolling is the user moving, not the interface animating. The one change is `scroll-behavior: auto` instead of `smooth`, so keyboard and programmatic scrolls jump rather than glide. The snap adjustment is the browser's own transition and already follows the OS setting without any help.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory` on a track with variable-width items. It makes it impossible to rest between items: every release is pulled to a snap point, so the user cannot park where they want, and on a long rail with wide items they end up fighting the browser for the whole length of the track. `proximity` is the correct choice whenever the items are not uniform or the track is long.\n- Setting `padding-inline` without `scroll-padding-inline`. Every rest position then puts the item flush against the container edge, so the gutter appears while scrolling and disappears the instant you let go.\n- Omitting `scroll-snap-stop: always`. A single hard swipe lands eight cards along, which makes stepping by one impossible and makes the rail feel slippery rather than mechanical.\n- Hiding the scrollbar without making the container focusable. The rail becomes invisible to keyboard users and to anyone who was relying on the scrollbar as the affordance. `tabIndex={0}` and a `role`/`aria-label` restore arrow-key scrolling, and Chrome stops warning about a non-focusable scroll container.\n- Leaving `overscroll-behavior-x` at `auto`. Flinging past the end chains into the page or, in Safari and Chrome, triggers the swipe-to-go-back gesture and the user loses the page.\n- Reaching for a JS carousel because \"the design has arrows\". A snap track already has momentum, rubber-banding, keyboard support and accessible scroll semantics; arrows are one `scrollBy` call on top, not a reason to replace the mechanism.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Snap is a layout-level contract, not an animation. Everything here runs on the compositor and continues to work when the main thread is blocked, which is the reason to prefer it over a JS track whenever the interaction is genuinely \"a scrollable row\".\n- Items are `shrink-0`. Without it, flex would compress them to fit the container and the track would never overflow, so there would be nothing to snap.\n- `scroll-snap-stop` is ignored during programmatic scrolls in every engine — it only constrains user gestures. Arrows implemented with `scrollTo` can therefore still jump several items at once by design.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the rail slowly and stop between two cards — it stays where you left it. Stop just past a card's edge and it eases onto that card. Fling as hard as you can and it advances exactly one card. Tab to the rail and press the arrow keys: it scrolls and snaps the same way. At every rest position the leading card sits inset from the container edge by the gutter, not flush against it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "questionnaire": "# Build: Questionnaire\n\nA chat-thread card that walks through a few questions, one at a time. Multiple-select questions are checkbox rows; single-select questions number their rows, and a click or the digit key picks and moves on after a short beat so the choice is seen landing. An optional Other row takes free text. The footer has a pill per question for jumping around plus Previous and Next, which reads Done on the last step. Questions slide in the direction of travel while the card eases to the new height.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe questions an agent asks before it plans: one per step, checkbox or numbered rows, a free-text Other and step pills.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Answers are keyed by question id as { values, other? }: values keep option order, and other exists only while the Other row is selected. Typed text is kept in a separate drafts map so unticking Other and ticking it again restores it.\n2. answers and step are each controllable: the prop wins when given, otherwise internal state holds it, and the change callback fires either way.\n3. A single-select pick writes { values: [value] } and schedules goTo(step + 1) after advanceDelay; the timeout is cleared by any other navigation and on unmount. When the pick moves the card on, focus lands on the next question's first row.\n4. goTo records the direction (+1 or -1, mirrored in RTL); AnimatePresence in popLayout mode hands it to both the entering and the exiting panel so they slide opposite ways at once.\n5. The exiting panel is popped out of flow, so a shared ResizeObserver on the panel wrapper reports the new question's height right away, and an outer motion.div animates its height to it with overflow hidden.\n6. One keydown handler on the card maps 1-9 to rows (the last digit focuses the Other input) and ArrowUp/ArrowDown to moving focus between rows; it ignores typing inside the input, where Enter sends the free text.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `advanceDelay = 180ms`\n  Long enough to see the row and its key turn selected, short enough that picking feels like one action.\n- `slide = 28px, 320ms, cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1), 2px blur`\n  A short travel distance keeps the two overlapping panels readable while they cross.\n- `height = 300ms on the same curve`\n  Finishes with the slide so the footer settles once.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\n<Questionnaire questions={[{ id: \"permission\", select: \"single\", question: \"How should the permission pill behave?\", options: [{ value: \"lock\", label: \"Lock it\" }], other: true }]} onComplete={(answers) => plan(answers)} onDismiss={() => setOpen(false)} />\n```\n\n- `questions: readonly QuestionnaireQuestion[]`\n  Each has id, question, options ({ value, label, description? }), optional other (true or { label, placeholder }), select and stepLabel.\n- `select: \"single\" | \"multiple\"` — default `\"multiple\"`\n  Default mode for questions that do not set their own.\n- `advanceDelay: number` — default `180`\n  Milliseconds before a single-select pick moves to the next question.\n- `answers: QuestionnaireAnswers`\n  Controlled answers keyed by question id.\n- `defaultAnswers: QuestionnaireAnswers` — default `{}`\n  Initial answers when uncontrolled.\n- `onAnswersChange: (answers: QuestionnaireAnswers) => void`\n  Fires on every pick, untick or keystroke in Other.\n- `step: number`\n  Controlled index of the visible question.\n- `defaultStep: number` — default `0`\n  Initial question when uncontrolled.\n- `onStepChange: (step: number) => void`\n  Fires when Previous, Next, a pill or an auto-advance changes the question.\n- `onComplete: (answers: QuestionnaireAnswers) => void`\n  Fires when Done is pressed on the last question, or Enter is pressed in its Other field.\n- `onDismiss: () => void`\n  Shows the corner dismiss button when set.\n- `labels: QuestionnaireLabels`\n  previous, next, complete, other, otherPlaceholder, dismiss and steps.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Questions\"`\n  Accessible name of the card.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Questionnaire({\n  questions,\n  select = 'multiple',\n  advanceDelay = 180,\n  answers: answersProp,\n  defaultAnswers,\n  onAnswersChange,\n  step: stepProp,\n  defaultStep = 0,\n  onStepChange,\n  onComplete,\n  onDismiss,\n  labels = {},\n  'aria-label': ariaLabel = 'Questions',\n  className,\n}: QuestionnaireProps) {\n  const uid = useId();\n  const reduce = useReducedMotion();\n  const rtl = useDirection() === 'rtl';\n  const [answers, setAnswers] = useControlled<QuestionnaireAnswers>(answersProp, defaultAnswers ?? {}, onAnswersChange);\n  const [rawStep, setStep] = useControlled<number>(stepProp, defaultStep, onStepChange);\n  const last = Math.max(questions.length - 1, 0);\n  const step = Math.min(Math.max(rawStep, 0), last);\n  const q = questions[step];\n\n  // Direction of travel: +1 forward, -1 back. Read by both the entering and the exiting panel.\n  const [dir, setDir] = useState(1);\n  // Free text survives unticking Other, so ticking it again restores what was typed.\n  const [drafts, setDrafts] = useState<Record<string, string>>({});\n  const rootRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const bodyRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const { height } = useResizeObserver(bodyRef);\n  const timer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\n  const focusAfter = useRef(false);\n\n  const clearTimer = () => {\n    if (timer.current !== null) clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    timer.current = null;\n  };\n  useEffect(() => clearTimer, []);\n\n  const goTo = (next: number, refocus = false) => {\n    clearTimer();\n    const target = Math.min(Math.max(next, 0), last);\n    if (target === step) return;\n    setDir(target > step ? 1 : -1);\n    focusAfter.current = refocus;\n    setStep(target);\n  };\n\n  // A pick that moves the card on unmounts the focused row; land focus on the next question's first row.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!focusAfter.current) return;\n    focusAfter.current = false;\n    rootRef.current?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`[data-step=\"${step}\"] [data-q-row]`)?.focus({ preventScroll: true });\n  }, [step]);\n\n  if (!q) return null;\n  const mode = q.select ?? select;\n  const other = otherConfig(q, labels);\n  const answer = answers[q.id] ?? EMPTY;\n  const otherOn = answer.other !== undefined;\n  const isLast = step === last;\n\n  const write = (next: QuestionnaireAnswer) => setAnswers({ ...answers, [q.id]: next });\n\n  const toggle = (value: string) => {\n    const on = answer.values.includes(value);\n    const values = q.options.map((o) => o.value).filter((v) => (v === value ? !on : answer.values.includes(v)));\n    write({ ...answer, values });\n  };\n\n  const pick = (value: string) => {\n    write({ values: [value] });\n    clearTimer();\n    if (isLast) return;\n    const from = step;\n    timer.current = setTimeout(() => goTo(from + 1, true), advanceDelay);\n  };\n\n  const setOtherText = (text: string) => {\n    setDrafts((d) => ({ ...d, [q.id]: text }));\n    const filled = text.trim().length > 0;\n    if (mode === 'single') write(filled ? { values: [], other: text } : { values: [] });\n    else write(filled ? { ...answer, other: text } : { values: answer.values });\n  };\n\n  const toggleOther = () => {\n    if (otherOn) write({ values: answer.values });\n    else write({ ...answer, other: drafts[q.id] ?? '' });\n  };\n\n  const advance = () => {\n    if (isLast) {\n      clearTimer();\n      onComplete?.(answers);\n    } else goTo(step + 1);\n  };\n\n  const otherInputId = `${uid}-other`;\n  const rowCount = q.options.length + (other ? 1 : 0);\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: ReactKeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if (event.altKey || event.ctrlKey || event.metaKey) return;\n    const target = event.target as HTMLElement;\n    if (target.tagName === 'INPUT' || target.tagName === 'TEXTAREA') return;\n    if (event.key === 'ArrowDown' || event.key === 'ArrowUp') {\n      const rows = Array.from(\n        rootRef.current?.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(`[data-step=\"${step}\"] [data-q-row]`) ?? [],\n      );\n      const at = rows.indexOf(target);\n      if (at < 0) return;\n      event.preventDefault();\n      rows[(at + (event.key === 'ArrowDown' ? 1 : -1) + rows.length) % rows.length]?.focus();\n      return;\n    }\n    if (!/^[1-9]$/.test(event.key)) return;\n    const index = Number(event.key) - 1;\n    if (index >= rowCount) return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    const option = q.options[index];\n    if (option) {\n      if (mode === 'single') pick(option.value);\n      else toggle(option.value);\n    } else {\n      document.getElementById(otherInputId)?.focus();\n    }\n  };\n\n  const shift = reduce ? 0 : rtl ? -28 : 28;\n  const variants: Variants = {\n    enter: (d: number) => ({ x: d * shift, opacity: 0, filter: reduce ? 'none' : 'blur(2px)' }),\n    center: { x: 0, opacity: 1, filter: reduce ? 'none' : 'blur(0px)' },\n    exit: (d: number) => ({ x: -d * shift, opacity: 0, filter: reduce ? 'none' : 'blur(2px)' }),\n  };\n  const slide = reduce ? { duration: 0.12 } : { duration: 0.32, ease: EASE_OUT };\n\n  return (\n    <div\n      ref={rootRef}\n      role=\"group\"\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n      className={cx(\n        'relative w-full rounded-2xl bg-background-primary-default p-3 shadow-sm ring-1 ring-border-button-default',\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {onDismiss ? (\n        <CloseButton aria-label={labels.dismiss ?? 'Dismiss questions'} onClick={onDismiss} className=\"absolute end-3 top-3 z-10\" />\n      ) : null}\n\n      <motion.div\n        initial={false}\n        animate={{ height: height > 0 ? height : 'auto' }}\n        transition={reduce ? { duration: 0 } : { duration: 0.3, ease: EASE_OUT }}\n        className=\"overflow-hidden\"\n      >\n        <div ref={bodyRef} className=\"relative\">\n          <AnimatePresence initial={false} mode=\"popLayout\" custom={dir}>\n            <motion.div\n              key={step}\n              data-step={step}\n              custom={dir}\n              variants={variants}\n              initial=\"enter\"\n              animate=\"center\"\n              exit=\"exit\"\n              transition={slide}\n              className=\"flex w-full flex-col gap-2\"\n            >\n              <p id={`${uid}-q${step}`} className={cx('px-0.5 pb-0.5 text-body-medium text-text-primary', onDismiss && 'pe-8')}>\n                {q.question}\n              </p>\n              <div\n                role={mode === 'single' ? 'radiogroup' : 'group'}\n                aria-labelledby={`${uid}-q${step}`}\n                className=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"\n              >\n                {q.options.map((option, i) => {\n                  const on = answer.values.includes(option.value);\n                  const id = `${uid}-${step}-${i}`;\n                  return (\n                    <button\n                      key={option.value}\n                      type=\"button\"\n                      data-q-row\n                      role={mode === 'single' ? 'radio' : 'checkbox'}\n                      aria-checked={on}\n                      aria-labelledby={`${id}-l`}\n                      aria-describedby={option.description ? `${id}-d` : undefined}\n                      aria-keyshortcuts={String(i + 1)}\n                      onClick={() => (mode === 'single' ? pick(option.value) : toggle(option.value))}\n                      className={cx(\n                        ROW,\n                        on ? 'border-border-button-hover bg-background-primary-hover' : 'border-border-button-default',\n                      )}\n                    >\n                      <RowText id={id} label={option.label} description={option.description} />\n                      {mode === 'single' ? <NumberKey n={i + 1} on={on} /> : tick(on)}\n                    </button>\n                  );\n                })}\n\n                {other ? (\n                  <div\n                    onClick={(event) => {\n                      if ((event.target as HTMLElement).closest('input,button')) return;\n                      document.getElementById(otherInputId)?.focus();\n                    }}\n                    className={cx(\n                      ROW,\n                      'cursor-text focus-within:ring-2 focus-within:ring-inset focus-within:ring-border-focus-ring',\n                      otherOn ? 'border-border-button-hover bg-background-primary-hover' : 'border-border-button-default',\n                    )}\n                  >\n                    <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col\">\n                      <label htmlFor={otherInputId} className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n                        {other.label}\n                      </label>\n                      <input\n                        id={otherInputId}\n                        data-q-row\n                        value={drafts[q.id] ?? answer.other ?? ''}\n                        placeholder={other.placeholder}\n                        onChange={(event) => setOtherText(event.target.value)}\n                        onKeyDown={(event) => {\n                          if (event.key !== 'Enter') return;\n                          event.preventDefault();\n                          if (mode === 'single' && !otherOn) return;\n                          advance();\n                        }}\n                        aria-keyshortcuts={String(rowCount)}\n                        className=\"w-full min-w-0 bg-transparent p-0 text-body-regular text-text-primary outline-none placeholder:text-text-placeholder\"\n                      />\n                    </span>\n                    {mode === 'single' ? (\n                      <NumberKey n={rowCount} on={otherOn} />\n                    ) : (\n                      <button\n                        type=\"button\"\n                        role=\"checkbox\"\n                        aria-checked={otherOn}\n                        aria-label={other.label}\n                        onClick={toggleOther}\n                        className=\"rounded-sm outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2\"\n                      >\n                        {tick(otherOn)}\n                      </button>\n                    )}\n                  </div>\n                ) : null}\n              </div>\n            </motion.div>\n          </AnimatePresence>\n        </div>\n      </motion.div>\n\n      <div className=\"mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-1 gap-y-2\">\n        <div role=\"group\" aria-label={labels.steps ?? 'Steps'} className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1\">\n          {questions.map((item, i) => (\n            <button\n              key={item.id}\n              type=\"button\"\n              aria-current={i === step ? 'step' : undefined}\n              onClick={() => goTo(i)}\n              className={cx(\n                'rounded-full px-2 py-1 text-body-regular outline-none',\n                'transition-colors duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n                'focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring',\n                i === step\n                  ? 'bg-status-blue-background text-status-blue-text'\n                  : 'text-text-secondary hover:bg-background-primary-hover hover:text-text-primary',\n              )}\n            >\n              {item.stepLabel ?? `Step ${i + 1}`}\n            </button>\n          ))}\n        </div>\n        <div className=\"ms-auto flex items-center gap-2\">\n          <Button variant=\"secondary\" size=\"small\" disabled={step === 0} onClick={() => goTo(step - 1)}>\n            {labels.previous ?? 'Previous'}\n          </Button>\n          <Button variant=\"primary\" size=\"small\" onClick={advance}>\n            {isLast ? (labels.complete ?? 'Done') : (labels.next ?? 'Next')}\n          </Button>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      <span aria-live=\"polite\" className=\"sr-only\">\n        {`Question ${step + 1} of ${questions.length}`}\n      </span>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The pending auto-advance timeout is cleared on every navigation and on unmount; the height observer is released on unmount. Keyboard handling is a React handler on the card, so no global listeners are attached.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Questions swap with a 120ms crossfade instead of the slide and blur, the card height changes instantly, and row colour transitions are off. Single-select picks still wait advanceDelay so the choice is visible.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Give every question a stable, unique id: answers and drafts are stored by it.\n- A pick on the last single-select question does not complete on its own; Done fires onComplete, so a stray click cannot send the answers.\n- When you control step, keep it inside 0..questions.length - 1; out-of-range values are clamped for display only.\n- Digit keys only work while focus is inside the card, so they never steal keys from the rest of the page.\n\n## Notes\n\n- select on a question overrides the card-level select, so one card can mix checkbox and numbered questions.\n- labels rewords Previous, Next, Done, the Other row, the dismiss button and the steps group.\n- onComplete receives the answers as they stand; questions left unanswered are absent or have empty values.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTicking two rows on step 1 sets both checkboxes and Next moves to step 2 with the card sliding left; pressing 2 there highlights Queue the change and moves to step 3 on its own; Previous returns with the pick still highlighted; typing in Other ticks it; the pills jump straight to any step; Done on step 4 shows the summary, Edit answers comes back with every answer intact and Start over clears them.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "quick-search-button": "# Build: Quick Search Button\n\nThe search entry point for an app or docs header. It looks like a small search field (magnifier, \"Search...\" and a ⌘ K key hint) but is a button. Clicking it, or pressing ⌘K / Ctrl+K anywhere on the page, opens the existing CommandPalette with whatever sections and items you pass as children; running an item closes the palette and reports its id.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA \"Search...\" field-style trigger with a ⌘K hint that opens the kit command palette, and a global ⌘K / Ctrl+K shortcut that toggles it.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The trigger is a native `button` with `aria-haspopup=\"dialog\"`, `aria-expanded` and `aria-keyshortcuts` (\"Meta+K\" on Apple platforms, \"Control+K\" elsewhere). The key hint is two kit `Kbd` elements marked `aria-hidden`, since the shortcut is already exposed through `aria-keyshortcuts`.\n2. Open state lives here and is passed to `CommandPalette` as `isOpen` / `onOpenChange`, with the palette's own shortcut turned off (`shortcut={false}`), so exactly one keyboard listener exists. The query is controlled too (`inputValue` / `onInputChange`) and cleared on every open, because the palette only resets it when it opens itself.\n3. A single `keydown` listener on `window`, added in an effect and removed in its cleanup, accepts ⌘ or Ctrl plus `shortcutKey` (default \"k\"), ignores Alt, Shift and auto-repeat, calls `preventDefault` so the browser address-bar shortcut does not fire, and toggles the palette.\n4. The ⌘ versus Ctrl glyph comes from `useSyncExternalStore` with a server snapshot of \"Apple\", so server HTML and the hydration pass agree and Windows or Linux visitors see \"Ctrl\" right after hydration with no mismatch warning.\n5. Everything inside the palette (filtering, arrow-key navigation, Escape, focus return to the trigger, the modal scrim) is the kit CommandPalette built on React Aria Autocomplete and Menu.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `shortcutKey = \"k\"`\n  ⌘K / Ctrl+K is the convention people already try first for search and command menus.\n- `trigger width = w-full, max 16rem`\n  Wide enough to read as a field in a header, and it shrinks with a truncating label in tight bars.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction QuickSearchButton(props: { label?: string; shortcutKey?: string; placeholder?: string; paletteLabel?: string; onAction?: (key: Key) => void; children: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: string` — default `\"Search...\"`\n  Trigger text.\n- `shortcutKey: string` — default `\"k\"`\n  Letter pressed with ⌘ or Ctrl to toggle the palette; also shown in the key hint.\n- `placeholder: string`\n  Palette input placeholder; the palette default is used when omitted.\n- `paletteLabel: string` — default `\"Search\"`\n  Accessible name of the palette dialog.\n- `onAction: (key: Key) => void`\n  Called with the id of the item that was run.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  CommandPaletteSection and CommandPaletteItem elements.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the trigger button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function QuickSearchButton({\n  label = \"Search...\",\n  shortcutKey = \"k\",\n  placeholder,\n  paletteLabel = \"Search\",\n  onAction,\n  children,\n  className,\n}: QuickSearchButtonProps) {\n  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);\n  // The query is controlled here: the palette only clears it when it opens itself, and here it is opened from outside.\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState(\"\");\n  // Server and first hydration render use the Mac glyph; the real platform lands right after without a mismatch.\n  const apple = useSyncExternalStore(noop, isApple, () => true);\n  const key = shortcutKey.toLowerCase();\n\n  // One global listener for ⌘K / Ctrl+K. It toggles, so the same chord closes an open palette.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {\n      if (!(event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey) || event.altKey || event.shiftKey) return;\n      if (event.key.toLowerCase() !== key || event.repeat) return;\n      event.preventDefault();\n      setQuery(\"\");\n      setOpen((current) => !current);\n    };\n    window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n  }, [key]);\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        onClick={() => {\n          setQuery(\"\");\n          setOpen(true);\n        }}\n        aria-haspopup=\"dialog\"\n        aria-expanded={open}\n        aria-keyshortcuts={`${apple ? \"Meta\" : \"Control\"}+${key.toUpperCase()}`}\n        className={cx(\n          \"group inline-flex h-9 w-full max-w-64 min-w-0 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 rounded-xl bg-background-primary-default pr-1.5 pl-3 text-start shadow-xs ring-1 ring-separator-border outline-none\",\n          \"transition-[background-color,box-shadow] duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-default focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          className,\n        )}\n      >\n        <RiSearchLine\n          aria-hidden\n          className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-tertiary transition-colors group-hover:text-foreground-icon-secondary motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n        />\n        <span className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-body-2-regular text-text-tertiary transition-colors group-hover:text-text-secondary motion-reduce:transition-none\">\n          {label}\n        </span>\n        <span aria-hidden className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-0.5\">\n          <Kbd className=\"min-w-5\">{apple ? \"⌘\" : \"Ctrl\"}</Kbd>\n          <Kbd className=\"min-w-5\">{key.toUpperCase()}</Kbd>\n        </span>\n      </button>\n\n      <CommandPalette\n        isOpen={open}\n        onOpenChange={setOpen}\n        shortcut={false}\n        aria-label={paletteLabel}\n        placeholder={placeholder}\n        inputValue={query}\n        onInputChange={setQuery}\n        onAction={onAction}\n      >\n        {children}\n      </CommandPalette>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The window `keydown` listener is removed when the component unmounts or `shortcutKey` changes. The palette is closed state, not a portal left behind.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The trigger's hover colour transitions use `motion-reduce:transition-none`. The palette's open and close motion is handled by the kit CommandPalette.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving the palette's built-in shortcut on while adding your own. Both listeners toggle on the same keypress and the palette opens and closes in one event; this component passes `shortcut={false}`.\n- Reading `navigator` during render to choose ⌘ or Ctrl. The server has no navigator and the markup mismatches on hydration; `useSyncExternalStore` with a server snapshot avoids it.\n- Controlling `isOpen` but not the query. The palette clears its own query only when it opens itself, so a palette opened from outside would come back showing the last search; the query is reset here on each open.\n- Forgetting `preventDefault` on Ctrl+K. In Chrome and Firefox on Windows and Linux it focuses the browser search bar instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Children are ordinary `CommandPaletteSection` / `CommandPaletteItem` elements, so items can have icons, descriptions and shortcut hints.\n- Mount one per page. Two instances would each register the global shortcut.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe demo header shows a \"Search...\" field with a ⌘ K hint and no dialog open. Clicking it opens the palette with Pages and Actions sections and focus in the input. Typing \"pri\" leaves only Pricing; Enter runs it, closes the palette, returns focus to the trigger and the demo shows \"Ran: Pricing\". Pressing Ctrl+K (or ⌘K) opens it again with an empty query and pressing it a second time closes it. Escape also closes it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "quickto-cursor-preview": "# Build: quickTo Cursor Preview\n\nHovering a row fades in a preview panel that then trails the pointer with a full second of catch-up, so it reads as a heavy object being dragged rather than a tooltip pinned to the cursor. Position comes from exactly two `gsap.quickTo` setters created once at mount; the hovered row is the only thing that ever enters React state.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA preview panel drifts a full second behind the pointer, driven by two reused quickTo tweens.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `gsap.quickTo(box, \"x\", { duration, ease })` returns a setter backed by ONE tween whose end value is mutated in place. A `pointermove` handler firing 60-120 times a second therefore allocates nothing after the first frame, where the `gsap.to()` equivalent allocates and garbage-collects a tween object per event and leaves each new tween fighting the last for the same property.\n2. Hovered row is React state because it changes a handful of times a second and drives real DOM structure (text colour, stroke, offset). Panel position is never state: it changes on every pointer event, and a re-render per move would cost more than the animation it is driving.\n3. `duration: 1` is roughly five times a normal cursor follow, and that is the entire design. Under `power3` the panel covers most of the gap early and then creeps, so it always reads as heading for the pointer without ever arriving — which is what makes it feel like mass rather than lag.\n4. The first move after entry calls `xTo(x, x)` — quickTo's second argument is the tween's START value, so the panel is teleported rather than eased. Without it, re-entering the list sends the panel sailing diagonally across the whole component over a full second before it becomes useful.\n5. Position is centred with `xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50` set once at mount, so `x`/`y` are raw pointer coordinates and no offset arithmetic appears in the hot handler.\n6. Every preview is a gradient plate painted into an offscreen canvas at mount — the registry ships no image assets. Switching rows is one `drawImage` blit, so the gradient, band and text work never lands on the frame where the fade-in starts.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 1`\n  Seconds of catch-up, and the whole character of the component. At 0.2 it is an ordinary tooltip that happens to be smooth; at 1 it is an object with weight. Past ~1.6 the panel is still crossing the list when the pointer has already left it, and the effect reads as broken rather than heavy.\n- `ease: 'power3' = power3 (in-out)`\n  In-out, not `.out`. Because the end value is remutated on every event, the tween is permanently restarting from its own midpoint; the symmetric curve means direction changes are absorbed smoothly instead of snapping to a new outward ramp each time.\n- `FADE_DURATION = 0.35`\n  Fade and scale on row change. Long enough to register as a cross-fade rather than a cut, short enough that dragging down a list of eight rows does not leave the panel permanently mid-transition.\n- `scale 0.94 -> 1 = 0.94`\n  A 6% growth on appear. Paired with opacity it reads as arriving from behind the page; without it the fade alone looks like a stuck render.\n- `DOCK_INSET = 20`\n  Pixels of margin when the panel docks to the right edge on touch or reduced motion. Clamped against `panelWidth / 2` so a panel wider than its container still lands fully inside rather than half off-screen.\n- `PLATE_SCALE = 2`\n  Backing-store multiplier for the plate canvases. Fixed rather than `devicePixelRatio` so the drawn output is identical on every machine and a visual regression is actually visible in a screenshot diff.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction QuickToCursorPreview(props: { items: PreviewItem[]; duration?: number; panelWidth?: number; panelHeight?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: PreviewItem[]`\n  Rows to render. `{ id, title, meta? }`; one plate is painted per item.\n- `duration: number` — default `1`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the follow. The defining value of the effect.\n- `panelWidth: number` — default `260`\n  Preview panel width in CSS px.\n- `panelHeight: number` — default `180`\n  Preview panel height in CSS px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst PLATE_SCALE = 2;\nconst FADE_DURATION = 0.35;\nconst DOCK_INSET = 20;\n\n/**\n * True when the panel must NOT follow: no fine pointer (a `pointermove` on\n * touch only exists during a drag, so the panel would live under the finger\n * that is dragging it), or reduced motion (a full second of drift is precisely\n * what that user opted out of). One predicate, so the two effects below can\n * never disagree about which mode the component is in.\n */\nfunction docked(): boolean {\n  return (\n    !window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches ||\n    window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches\n  );\n}\n\nexport function QuickToCursorPreview({\n  items,\n  duration = 1,\n  panelWidth = 260,\n  panelHeight = 180,\n  className,\n}: QuickToCursorPreviewProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const panel = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const surface = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n  const plates = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement[]>([]);\n\n  /* Hovered row is React state because it changes a handful of times a\n     second. Panel position is never state — it changes 120 times a second and\n     a re-render per pointermove would dwarf the animation itself. */\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState<number | null>(null);\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(root);\n\n  const count = items.length;\n\n  /* Paint every plate once. Drawing at hover time would put a gradient fill,\n     four band rects and a text layout on the same frame as the fade-in. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const bw = panelWidth * PLATE_SCALE;\n    const bh = panelHeight * PLATE_SCALE;\n    const built: HTMLCanvasElement[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n      const plate = document.createElement('canvas');\n      plate.width = bw;\n      plate.height = bh;\n      const ctx = plate.getContext('2d');\n      if (!ctx) break;\n      ctx.scale(PLATE_SCALE, PLATE_SCALE);\n      paintPlate(ctx, panelWidth, panelHeight, i);\n      built.push(plate);\n    }\n    plates.current = built;\n\n    const canvas = surface.current;\n    if (canvas) {\n      canvas.width = bw;\n      canvas.height = bh;\n    }\n    return () => {\n      plates.current = [];\n    };\n  }, [count, panelWidth, panelHeight]);\n\n  /* Centre the panel on its own coordinates ONCE, at mount. Doing it with a\n     negative margin or `left: -130px` instead means the offset and the\n     transform fight, and the panel sits half a width off. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const box = panel.current;\n    if (!box) return;\n    gsap.set(box, { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, autoAlpha: 0, scale: 0.94 });\n    return () => {\n      gsap.killTweensOf(box);\n      gsap.set(box, { clearProps: 'all' });\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  /* With no fine pointer — or with reduced motion, where a second of drift is\n     exactly what the user opted out of — the panel docks to the right edge and\n     only cross-fades as rows change. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const box = panel.current;\n    if (!box || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n    if (!docked()) return;\n    gsap.set(box, {\n      x: Math.max(width - panelWidth / 2 - DOCK_INSET, panelWidth / 2),\n      y: height / 2,\n    });\n  }, [width, height, panelWidth]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const box = panel.current;\n    if (!el || !box || docked()) return;\n\n    // ONE tween per property, reused for the lifetime of the component.\n    const xTo = gsap.quickTo(box, 'x', { duration, ease: 'power3' });\n    const yTo = gsap.quickTo(box, 'y', { duration, ease: 'power3' });\n    let seeded = false;\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      const x = e.clientX - rect.left;\n      const y = e.clientY - rect.top;\n      if (seeded) {\n        xTo(x);\n        yTo(y);\n        return;\n      }\n      // Second argument is quickTo's `start` value: it snaps rather than\n      // easing. Without it the panel sails in from wherever it was left,\n      // taking a full second to cross the list on every re-entry.\n      seeded = true;\n      xTo(x, x);\n      yTo(y, y);\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      seeded = false;\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n    /* Deliberately NOT clearing props here. `xPercent`, `yPercent` and\n       `autoAlpha` belong to the mount effect; wiping them whenever `duration`\n       changes would un-centre the panel and pop it to full opacity mid-hover.\n       Teardown of those lives in the effect that set them. */\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      gsap.killTweensOf(box);\n    };\n  }, [duration]);\n\n  /* Visibility and content follow the hovered row, not the pointer. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const box = panel.current;\n    const canvas = surface.current;\n    if (!box || !canvas) return;\n\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;\n\n    if (hovered !== null) {\n      const plate = plates.current[hovered];\n      const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n      if (plate && ctx) {\n        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\n        ctx.drawImage(plate, 0, 0);\n      }\n    }\n\n    gsap.to(box, {\n      autoAlpha: hovered === null ? 0 : 1,\n      scale: hovered === null ? 0.94 : 1,\n      duration: reduced ? 0 : FADE_DURATION,\n      ease: 'power3.out',\n      // Only the fade is overwritten; the quickTo tweens own x and y and must\n      // keep running underneath, which is exactly what 'auto' guarantees.\n      overwrite: 'auto',\n    });\n  }, [hovered]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The follow effect removes both listeners and calls `gsap.killTweensOf(box)`, which disposes the two quickTo tweens. The mount effect separately kills tweens and runs `clearProps: \"all\"`, restoring the centring transform and opacity it set. The offscreen plate canvases are dropped by nulling the ref array; they are plain canvases, never object URLs, so there is nothing to revoke.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No `pointermove` listener attaches. The panel docks to the right edge, vertically centred, and hovering a row swaps its plate with a zero-duration cross-fade. The preview still works; it simply never moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `gsap.to()` in the `pointermove` handler. It allocates a tween per event and every new one competes with the still-running previous tween for `x`, so the panel judders under fast movement — exactly when the follow is most visible.\n- Putting the panel position in React state. Every pointer event becomes a render of the whole list; the follow gets slower the more rows you add, which is a scaling failure with no obvious cause at the call site.\n- Omitting the start value on the first call after entry. The panel eases in from wherever it was abandoned, so re-entering the list gives a full second of a panel flying across the layout before it means anything.\n- Centring with a negative margin or `left: -130px` as well as `xPercent: -50`. The two offsets compose and the panel sits half a width away from the pointer — a bug that looks like a follow-lag problem and is not one.\n- Running `clearProps: \"all\"` in the cleanup of an effect that only owns `x` and `y`. Any dependency change then wipes the `xPercent`/`yPercent` centring and the `autoAlpha` state owned by a different effect, and the panel jumps or flashes to full opacity mid-hover. Each effect must tear down only what it set.\n- Using `overwrite: true` on the fade tween. It kills the quickTo tweens that own `x` and `y`, and the panel freezes in place the moment you move between rows. `overwrite: \"auto\"` only claims the properties the fade actually animates.\n- Transitioning `all` on the row text. The panel and rows share a stacking context, and a blanket transition catches `transform`, which then fights whatever else is animating it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch there is no hover and a `pointermove` only exists mid-drag, so the panel would live directly under the finger dragging it. The touch path docks the panel to the right edge and cross-fades on row change instead, which keeps the preview useful without pretending there is a cursor.\n- Reduced motion takes the same docked path — a second of drift is precisely what that user opted out of — and the cross-fade duration drops to 0.\n- Both branches read the same `docked()` predicate, so the follow effect and the dock effect can never disagree about which mode the component is in.\n- The panel is `aria-hidden` and `pointer-events-none`: it is decoration for the row title, which remains the accessible content.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover a row and the panel fades up over ~350ms while scaling from 0.94. Move the pointer and it follows with about a second of lag, always heading toward the pointer and never quite catching it. Move fast between rows and the image cross-fades while the follow keeps running underneath — the panel never freezes. Leave and re-enter the list and the panel appears at the pointer, not sailing in from the far side. On touch or with reduced motion the panel sits docked at the right edge and only its contents change.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radar-chart-card": "# Build: Radar Chart Card\n\nA grey dashboard card that plots six months of visitors as a Recharts radar on a hexagonal polar grid, with the range total counted up in the header next to a chip comparing it with the range before, and a white tile per month underneath. A calendar pill swaps between H1 2024, H2 2024 and the full year. Hovering an axis pulses a dot on that vertex, opens a tooltip with the value and its share, and switches the header to that month. Four looks are built in: a filled polygon, a light fill with vertex dots, an outline only, and a fill with a centre score disc that turns rose when the value it shows is low.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA \"Visitors\" card with a lime radar on a hexagon grid; hovering an axis pulses its vertex and counts the header to that month.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `RadarChart` draws a `PolarGrid` with `gridType=\"polygon\"` in `--color-chart-track`, a `PolarAngleAxis` keyed on the month label for the outer labels, and a hidden `PolarRadiusAxis` (no ticks, no line) with its domain set to `[0, \"auto\"]` so the polygon always grows from the centre.\n2. Hover state comes from Recharts' own store. `RadarActiveAxis` is a child of the chart that reads `useIsTooltipActive()` and `useActiveTooltipLabel()` and passes the active label (or null) up in an effect. Reading the store, rather than the chart's `onMouseMove` payload, means the header is right on the very first pointer event over the chart and follows the chart's arrow-key focus too.\n3. The hovered label is looked up in the current range by name, so after a range switch a stale label simply finds nothing and the header falls back to the total. With an axis active the header shows that axis value and hides the chip; at rest it shows the sum against `previous` through `describeDelta`.\n4. The `Radar` keeps one 2px outline in the tone's `-active` colour and changes only `fillOpacity` per look: 0.6 filled, 0.2 dots, 0 lines, 0.35 score. The dots look adds 3.5px vertex dots ringed in the card colour; every look uses the shared `PulseDot` as `activeDot`, which pings unless motion is reduced.\n5. The score disc is an absolutely centred HTML circle over the plot. The chart margins match on opposite sides (12px top and bottom, 40px left and right), which keeps the polar centre at the middle of the plot box, so `top-1/2 left-1/2` with a translate lands on it. It shows the axis average at rest and the hovered value on hover, and its text and border switch to the rose status colour below `lowThreshold`.\n6. The tooltip uses a dashed radial cursor and renders `ChartTooltipCard` from `activeIndex` (mapped from `null` to -1 first) with the value and its share of the range total.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `outer radius = 78% of the plot half-size, 280px plot height`\n  The radius is a share of the smaller plot side, so on a wide card the 280px height sets it and on a 360px screen the narrowed width does, leaving room for \"February\" and \"September\" at the side vertices.\n- `fill per look = filled 0.6, dots 0.2, lines 0, score 0.35`\n  The solid look matches the preview; the dots look needs a lighter fill so the dots carry the shape; the score look stays light enough for the disc to read on top.\n- `chart margin = 12px top and bottom, 40px left and right`\n  Matching opposite margins keep the polar centre at the middle of the box, where the score disc is positioned with plain CSS; the wider sides shrink the radius only when the card is narrow, so the side labels are never cut off.\n- `low threshold = 200`\n  With the default data, January (186) is the only month under it, so hovering it shows the rose disc while the 237 average stays neutral.\n- `motion = polygon 500ms, tooltip 160ms, disc colour 200ms, count-up 320ms`\n  Quick enough to sweep round six axes, and all of it is off under reduced motion.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadarChartCard(props?: RadarChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype RadarChartCardProps = { title?: string; ranges?: RadarRange[]; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; variant?: \"filled\" | \"dots\" | \"lines\" | \"score\"; tone?: ChartTone; lowThreshold?: number; rangeControl?: \"segmented\" | \"select\"; hideTiles?: boolean; className?: string }\ntype RadarRange = { id: string; label: string; data: { label: string; value: number }[]; previous?: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Visitors\"`\n  Header label and series name.\n- `ranges: RadarRange[]` — default `RADAR_RANGES`\n  Data sets for the pill, one point per axis clockwise from the top; `previous` feeds the chip.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled range id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial range when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the chosen range id.\n- `variant: \"filled\" | \"dots\" | \"lines\" | \"score\"` — default `\"filled\"`\n  Solid polygon, light fill with vertex dots, outline only, or fill with a centre score disc.\n- `tone: ChartTone` — default `2`\n  Series hue; the outline, dots and active dot use its `-active` step.\n- `lowThreshold: number` — default `200`\n  Score look only: the disc turns rose below this value.\n- `rangeControl: \"segmented\" | \"select\"` — default `\"select\"`\n  Calendar pill with a listbox, or a segmented switch.\n- `hideTiles: boolean` — default `false`\n  Hide the per-axis tiles under the chart.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RadarChartCard({\n  title = \"Visitors\",\n  ranges = RADAR_RANGES,\n  range,\n  defaultRange,\n  onRangeChange,\n  variant = \"filled\",\n  tone = 2,\n  lowThreshold = 200,\n  rangeControl = \"select\",\n  hideTiles = false,\n  className,\n}: RadarChartCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [innerRange, setInnerRange] = useState(defaultRange ?? ranges[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const rangeId = range ?? innerRange;\n  const active = ranges.find((r) => r.id === rangeId) ?? ranges[0];\n  const data = active?.data ?? [];\n  const [hoveredLabel, setHoveredLabel] = useState<string | null>(null);\n\n  const total = data.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.value, 0);\n  const hoveredIndex = hoveredLabel === null ? -1 : data.findIndex((p) => p.label === hoveredLabel);\n  const hovered = data[hoveredIndex];\n\n  // At rest the header is the range total against the range before; on an axis it is that axis alone.\n  const headline = hovered ? hovered.value : total;\n  const delta = hovered ? null : describeDelta(total, active?.previous);\n  const average = data.length > 0 ? total / data.length : 0;\n  const score = hovered ? hovered.value : average;\n  const low = score < lowThreshold;\n\n  const selectRange = (id: string) => {\n    if (range === undefined) setInnerRange(id);\n    onRangeChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section aria-label={`${title} radar chart`} className={cx(CHART_CARD_FRAME, \"gap-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex items-start justify-between gap-3 px-2 pt-2\">\n        <ChartHeadline\n          label={hovered ? `${title} · ${hovered.label}` : title}\n          value={headline}\n          delta={delta}\n          swapKey={`${rangeId}-${hovered?.label ?? \"total\"}`}\n          reducedMotion={reduced}\n        />\n        {ranges.length > 1 ? (\n          <ChartPeriodControl\n            options={ranges}\n            value={rangeId}\n            onChange={selectRange}\n            variant={rangeControl}\n            label={`${title} range`}\n            className=\"shrink-0\"\n          />\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"relative h-[280px] w-full\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" initialDimension={{ width: 420, height: 280 }}>\n          <RadarChart data={data} margin={RADAR_MARGIN} outerRadius={RADAR_OUTER_RADIUS}>\n            <RadarActiveAxis onChange={setHoveredLabel} />\n            <PolarGrid gridType=\"polygon\" stroke={GRID_STROKE} />\n            <PolarRadiusAxis domain={[0, \"auto\"]} tick={false} axisLine={false} />\n            <PolarAngleAxis dataKey=\"label\" tick={AXIS_TICK} tickLine={false} />\n            <Tooltip\n              cursor={CHART_CURSOR}\n              offset={16}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={160}\n              wrapperStyle={{ outline: \"none\", zIndex: 10 }}\n              content={({ active: isActive, activeIndex }) => {\n                const at = data[activeIndex == null ? -1 : Number(activeIndex)];\n                if (!isActive || !at) return null;\n                return (\n                  <ChartTooltipCard\n                    title={at.label}\n                    rows={[\n                      { key: \"value\", label: title, tone, value: formatNumber(at.value) },\n                      { key: \"share\", label: \"Share\", value: `${formatNumber((at.value / (total || 1)) * 100, 1)}%` },\n                    ]}\n                  />\n                );\n              }}\n            />\n            <Radar\n              dataKey=\"value\"\n              name={title}\n              fill={toneColor(tone)}\n              fillOpacity={VARIANT_FILL[variant]}\n              stroke={toneActiveColor(tone)}\n              strokeWidth={2}\n              strokeLinejoin=\"round\"\n              dot={\n                variant === \"dots\"\n                  ? {\n                      r: 3.5,\n                      fill: toneActiveColor(tone),\n                      fillOpacity: 1,\n                      stroke: \"var(--color-background-secondary-default)\",\n                      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n                    }\n                  : false\n              }\n              activeDot={<PulseDot color={toneActiveColor(tone)} pulse={!reduced} />}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={500}\n            />\n          </RadarChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n\n        {variant === \"score\" ? (\n          <div\n            aria-hidden\n            className={cx(\n              \"pointer-events-none absolute top-1/2 left-1/2 flex size-14 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-full border-2 bg-background-primary-default transition-colors duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n              low ? \"text-status-rose-text\" : \"text-text-primary\",\n            )}\n            style={{ borderColor: low ? \"var(--color-status-rose-text)\" : toneActiveColor(tone) }}\n          >\n            <span className=\"text-body-medium tabular-nums\">{formatNumber(score)}</span>\n            <span className=\"max-w-12 truncate text-[10px] leading-3 text-text-tertiary\">\n              {hovered ? hovered.label : \"avg\"}\n            </span>\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      {hideTiles ? null : (\n        <ChartStatTiles\n          columns={3}\n          tiles={data.map((p) => ({ key: p.label, label: p.label, value: formatNumber(p.value) }))}\n        />\n      )}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `RadarActiveAxis` only runs an effect that calls the setter, `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame on target change and unmount, `useReducedMotion` unsubscribes its media-query listener, `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer, and the kit `Select` tears down its popover when closed. The card adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` turns off `isAnimationActive` on the Radar and the Tooltip, the header shows its value without counting, the active dot drops its ping, and the number fade and disc colour transition are disabled with `motion-reduce:` variants.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Driving the header from the chart `onMouseMove` payload alone. In testing, a single pointer event that entered the chart opened the tooltip but left that payload inactive, so the header stayed on the total; the store hooks update on the same event.\n- Using different margins on opposite sides with the centred disc. The polar centre follows the plot area, not the box, and the disc would sit off the hub.\n- Reading `activeIndex` with `Number()` directly. Recharts passes `null` when inactive and `Number(null)` is 0, which would pin the tooltip to January.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `RADAR_RANGES`, `RADAR_MARGIN`, `RADAR_OUTER_RADIUS`, `RadarActiveAxis` and the `RadarRange`, `RadarAxisPoint` and `RadarVariant` types are exported; Radar Comparison Card reuses them. H1 2024 totals 1,424 against 1,354 (\"+5.2%\"), H2 2024 1,598 (\"+12.2%\") and 2024 3,022 against 2,780 (\"+8.7%\").\n- The full-year range uses two-month buckets so every range keeps six axes and six tiles.\n- The frame, header, range pill, tooltip card, tiles and pulse dot come from `line-chart-card-parts.tsx`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Visitors\", \"1,424\" with a lime \"+5.2%\" chip, an \"H1 2024\" pill, a lime filled hexagon radar labelled January to June on a grey polygon grid, and six tiles January 186, February 305, March 237, April 273, May 209 and June 214. Hovering an axis pulses its vertex, opens a tooltip with the value and share, and changes the header to \"Visitors · <month>\" and that value. Picking \"H2 2024\" shows \"1,598\" and \"+12.2%\". The score look shows a disc reading 237 that turns rose on January.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radar-comparison-card": "# Build: Radar Comparison Card\n\nA grey \"Visitors\" dashboard card that overlays two outline-only Recharts radars, Desktop in lime and Mobile in blue, on one hexagonal grid. The header counts up the lead series total with a chip against the range before, a calendar pill swaps between H1 2024, H2 2024 and the full year, and a tile per axis sits underneath. The legend under the chart is live: it shows each series total at rest and its value on the hovered axis, and every entry is a toggle button that hides or shows its outline, with the last visible series locked on.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDesktop and Mobile as lime and blue radar outlines on one grid, with a live legend whose entries toggle each series on and off.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each series is a `Radar` with `fillOpacity={0}` and a 2px outline in its tone's `-active` colour, so overlapping shapes stay readable. The range data is stored as `{ label, values: { desktop, mobile } }` and spread into flat chart rows, one `dataKey` per series key.\n2. Hiding a series sets the Radar `hide` prop instead of unmounting it. The hidden `PolarRadiusAxis` gets a fixed domain of `[0, max]`, where `max` is taken over every series in the range, so turning one outline off never rescales the other.\n3. The legend is a list of native buttons with `aria-pressed`. A click builds the next key list in series order and, when `visible` is uncontrolled, stores it; `onVisibleChange` gets it either way. If the clicked series is the only one shown, the click is ignored and the button carries `aria-disabled` and a title saying why.\n4. Hover state comes from Recharts' store through `RadarActiveAxis` from Radar Chart Card, so it is right on the first pointer event and follows the chart's arrow-key focus. With an axis active, the header shows the lead series value for it and hides the chip, and every legend entry swaps its total for its value on that axis.\n5. The lead series is the first visible one. The header total, the chip (`describeDelta` against `previous[lead]`) and the tiles all follow it, and the tiles carry its colour square so it is clear which series they show.\n6. The tooltip lists only visible series, each with its colour dot, and every Radar uses the shared `PulseDot` as `activeDot`, so each visible outline pulses on the hovered axis.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `radius domain = [0, largest value of any series in the range]`\n  A shared, fixed scale keeps side-by-side reads honest and stops the remaining outline jumping when one is hidden.\n- `outline = 2px stroke, fill opacity 0`\n  Outline-only shapes let the smaller series show through the larger one, as in the preview.\n- `hidden legend entry = 50% opacity, hollow square`\n  Shows the series is off while keeping its value readable and the button easy to find again.\n- `plot size = 260px high, 78% outer radius, margins 12px top and bottom and 40px left and right`\n  The same geometry as Radar Chart Card, a little shorter to make room for the legend row.\n- `motion = outlines 500ms, tooltip 160ms, legend fade 150ms, count-up 320ms`\n  Quick enough to sweep round six axes, and all of it is off under reduced motion.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadarComparisonCard(props?: RadarComparisonCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype RadarComparisonCardProps = { title?: string; series?: RadarSeries[]; ranges?: RadarComparisonRange[]; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; visible?: string[]; defaultVisible?: string[]; onVisibleChange?: (keys: string[]) => void; rangeControl?: \"segmented\" | \"select\"; hideTiles?: boolean; className?: string }\ntype RadarSeries = { key: string; label: string; tone: ChartTone }\ntype RadarComparisonRange = { id: string; label: string; data: { label: string; values: Record<string, number> }[]; previous?: Record<string, number> }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Visitors\"`\n  Header label.\n- `series: RadarSeries[]` — default `RADAR_COMPARISON_SERIES`\n  Series keys, legend labels and tones; the first visible one leads the header and tiles.\n- `ranges: RadarComparisonRange[]` — default `RADAR_COMPARISON_RANGES`\n  Data sets for the pill, one point per axis with a value per series key; `previous` feeds the chip.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled range id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial range when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the chosen range id.\n- `visible: string[]`\n  Controlled list of visible series keys.\n- `defaultVisible: string[]` — default `every series`\n  Initial visible keys when uncontrolled.\n- `onVisibleChange: (keys: string[]) => void`\n  Called with the next visible keys after a legend click.\n- `rangeControl: \"segmented\" | \"select\"` — default `\"select\"`\n  Calendar pill with a listbox, or a segmented switch.\n- `hideTiles: boolean` — default `false`\n  Hide the per-axis tiles under the chart.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RadarComparisonCard({\n  title = \"Visitors\",\n  series = RADAR_COMPARISON_SERIES,\n  ranges = RADAR_COMPARISON_RANGES,\n  range,\n  defaultRange,\n  onRangeChange,\n  visible,\n  defaultVisible,\n  onVisibleChange,\n  rangeControl = \"select\",\n  hideTiles = false,\n  className,\n}: RadarComparisonCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [innerRange, setInnerRange] = useState(defaultRange ?? ranges[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const [innerVisible, setInnerVisible] = useState<string[]>(defaultVisible ?? series.map((s) => s.key));\n  const [hoveredLabel, setHoveredLabel] = useState<string | null>(null);\n\n  const rangeId = range ?? innerRange;\n  const active = ranges.find((r) => r.id === rangeId) ?? ranges[0];\n  const data = active?.data ?? [];\n  const rows = data.map((p) => ({ label: p.label, ...p.values }));\n\n  // Never render an empty chart: if nothing valid is visible, the first series stays on.\n  const visibleKeys = visible ?? innerVisible;\n  const picked = series.filter((s) => visibleKeys.includes(s.key));\n  const shown = picked.length > 0 ? picked : series.slice(0, 1);\n  const isShown = (key: string) => shown.some((s) => s.key === key);\n  const lead = shown[0];\n\n  const valueOf = (p: RadarComparisonPoint | undefined, key: string) => p?.values[key] ?? 0;\n  const totalOf = (key: string) => data.reduce((sum, p) => sum + valueOf(p, key), 0);\n  // One radius scale for every series, so hiding one never rescales the other.\n  const max = Math.max(0, ...data.flatMap((p) => series.map((s) => valueOf(p, s.key))));\n\n  const hovered = hoveredLabel === null ? undefined : data.find((p) => p.label === hoveredLabel);\n  const leadKey = lead?.key ?? \"\";\n  const leadTotal = totalOf(leadKey);\n  const leadPrevious = active?.previous?.[leadKey];\n  const headline = hovered ? valueOf(hovered, leadKey) : leadTotal;\n  const delta = hovered || leadPrevious === undefined ? null : describeDelta(leadTotal, leadPrevious);\n\n  const selectRange = (id: string) => {\n    if (range === undefined) setInnerRange(id);\n    onRangeChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  const toggle = (key: string) => {\n    const on = isShown(key);\n    if (on && shown.length <= 1) return;\n    const next = series.filter((s) => (s.key === key ? !on : isShown(s.key))).map((s) => s.key);\n    if (visible === undefined) setInnerVisible(next);\n    onVisibleChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section aria-label={`${title} radar comparison`} className={cx(CHART_CARD_FRAME, \"gap-3\", className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex items-start justify-between gap-3 px-2 pt-2\">\n        <ChartHeadline\n          label={hovered ? `${title} · ${hovered.label}` : title}\n          value={headline}\n          delta={delta}\n          swapKey={`${rangeId}-${leadKey}-${hovered?.label ?? \"total\"}`}\n          reducedMotion={reduced}\n        />\n        {ranges.length > 1 ? (\n          <ChartPeriodControl\n            options={ranges}\n            value={rangeId}\n            onChange={selectRange}\n            variant={rangeControl}\n            label={`${title} range`}\n            className=\"shrink-0\"\n          />\n        ) : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"h-[260px] w-full\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" initialDimension={{ width: 420, height: 260 }}>\n          <RadarChart data={rows} margin={RADAR_MARGIN} outerRadius={RADAR_OUTER_RADIUS}>\n            <RadarActiveAxis onChange={setHoveredLabel} />\n            <PolarGrid gridType=\"polygon\" stroke={GRID_STROKE} />\n            <PolarRadiusAxis domain={[0, max || 1]} tick={false} axisLine={false} />\n            <PolarAngleAxis dataKey=\"label\" tick={AXIS_TICK} tickLine={false} />\n            <Tooltip\n              cursor={CHART_CURSOR}\n              offset={16}\n              isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n              animationDuration={160}\n              wrapperStyle={{ outline: \"none\", zIndex: 10 }}\n              content={({ active: isActive, activeIndex }) => {\n                const at = data[activeIndex == null ? -1 : Number(activeIndex)];\n                if (!isActive || !at) return null;\n                return (\n                  <ChartTooltipCard\n                    title={at.label}\n                    rows={shown.map((s) => ({ key: s.key, label: s.label, tone: s.tone, value: formatNumber(valueOf(at, s.key)) }))}\n                  />\n                );\n              }}\n            />\n            {series.map((s) => (\n              <Radar\n                key={s.key}\n                dataKey={s.key}\n                name={s.label}\n                hide={!isShown(s.key)}\n                fillOpacity={0}\n                stroke={toneActiveColor(s.tone)}\n                strokeWidth={2}\n                strokeLinejoin=\"round\"\n                dot={false}\n                activeDot={<PulseDot color={toneActiveColor(s.tone)} pulse={!reduced} />}\n                isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n                animationDuration={500}\n              />\n            ))}\n          </RadarChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n\n      <ul aria-label={`${title} series`} className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 px-2\">\n        {series.map((s) => {\n          const on = isShown(s.key);\n          const locked = on && shown.length <= 1;\n          return (\n            <li key={s.key}>\n              <button\n                type=\"button\"\n                aria-pressed={on}\n                aria-disabled={locked || undefined}\n                title={locked ? \"At least one series stays visible\" : undefined}\n                onClick={() => toggle(s.key)}\n                className={cx(\n                  \"flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md px-1.5 py-0.5 text-body-2-regular text-text-secondary outline-none transition-opacity duration-150 ease-out focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                  on ? \"opacity-100\" : \"opacity-50\",\n                  locked ? \"cursor-default\" : \"cursor-pointer hover:bg-background-primary-hover\",\n                )}\n              >\n                <span\n                  aria-hidden\n                  className={cx(\"size-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-[3px]\", on ? TONE_DOT[s.tone] : \"ring-1 ring-current ring-inset\")}\n                />\n                {s.label}\n                <span className=\"text-body-2-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums\">\n                  {formatNumber(hovered ? valueOf(hovered, s.key) : totalOf(s.key))}\n                </span>\n              </button>\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n\n      {hideTiles ? null : (\n        <ChartStatTiles\n          columns={3}\n          tiles={data.map((p) => ({ key: p.label, label: p.label, value: formatNumber(valueOf(p, leadKey)), tone: lead?.tone }))}\n        />\n      )}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `RadarActiveAxis` only runs an effect that calls the setter, `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame on target change and unmount, `useReducedMotion` unsubscribes its media-query listener, `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer, and the kit `Select` tears down its popover when closed. The card adds no listeners or timers of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` turns off `isAnimationActive` on every Radar and the Tooltip, the header shows its value without counting, the active dots drop their ping, and the legend fade is disabled with a `motion-reduce:` variant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving the radius domain on \"auto\". Recharts then scales to the visible data only, so hiding Desktop makes Mobile balloon to fill the grid and the comparison is lost.\n- Unmounting a hidden Radar instead of using `hide`. The series order, colours and the tooltip rows then shift as series come and go.\n- Letting every series be turned off. An empty grid has no lead series for the header or tiles, so the last visible entry is locked.\n- Reading `activeIndex` with `Number()` directly. Recharts passes `null` when inactive and `Number(null)` is 0, which would pin the tooltip to January.\n\n## Notes\n\n- H1 2024 is Desktop 1,424 against 1,354 (\"+5.2%\") and Mobile 860 against 800 (\"+7.5%\"); H2 2024 is 1,598 (\"+12.2%\") and 1,100 (\"+27.9%\"); 2024 is 3,022 (\"+8.7%\") and 1,960 (\"+15.3%\") in two-month buckets.\n- Imports `RADAR_MARGIN`, `RADAR_OUTER_RADIUS` and `RadarActiveAxis` from `radar-chart-card.tsx`, and the frame, header, range pill, tooltip card, tiles and pulse dot from `line-chart-card-parts.tsx`.\n- With Desktop hidden, Mobile becomes the lead series: the header shows \"860\" with \"+7.5%\" and the tiles show the Mobile values.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Visitors\", \"1,424\" with a lime \"+5.2%\" chip and an \"H1 2024\" pill, a lime and a blue radar outline on a grey hexagon grid labelled January to June, a legend reading \"Desktop 1,424\" and \"Mobile 860\", and six tiles January 186 to June 214. Hovering an axis pulses both vertices, opens a tooltip with both values and switches the header and legend to that month. Clicking \"Desktop\" hides the lime outline, and the header reads \"860\" with \"+7.5%\"; clicking \"Mobile\" then does nothing. Picking \"H2 2024\" shows \"1,598\" and \"+12.2%\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radial-chart-card": "# Build: Radial Chart Card\n\nA dashboard card that draws one rounded arc per item over its own track ring, smallest item innermost, every arc starting at twelve o'clock and running clockwise. The arcs sweep in on mount and to their new lengths on every period or look change. Hovering, focusing or tapping a ring darkens it one tone, fades the others, floats a tooltip on the arc's tip and swaps the count-up headline to that item. Four looks come from one prop: plain rings, names set along each arc, rings over a circular grid, and a single gauge of the total against a goal with the percentage in the centre.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nConcentric radial bars in the dashboard card shell, with a tooltip on the arc tip, a count-up headline that follows hover, and stat tiles.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The chart is an inline SVG drawn 1:1 in a 216px square. `layoutRings` puts the outermost ring flush with the edge (radius 108 − 2 − stroke/2) and steps inwards by the pitch (stroke + gap, 8 + 4 for rings). If the rings would come closer than 26px to the centre, the pitch shrinks to fit.\n2. Each arc is a `<circle>` rotated −90° about the centre, with `pathLength=100` and `strokeLinecap=\"round\"`. Its dash is just `fraction × 100`, so no arc maths is needed. The fraction is `value / max`, where max defaults to the largest item ÷ 0.9, so the biggest ring sweeps 324°.\n3. The fractions go through `useTweenedValues`: a 520ms ease-out cubic tween that starts from 0 on mount and from the current sweeps on a new period or look. So the arcs grow, shrink and re-proportion in one motion.\n4. Each ring is a `<g role=\"button\" tabIndex=0>` holding the track, the arc, an optional name and a transparent hit circle as wide as the pitch, so the gaps between rings still hover. Pointer enter and focus set the active ring, click or Enter/Space toggle a pin (`aria-pressed`), and leaving the square clears the hover.\n5. The active arc switches to the darker `--color-chart-N-active` tone. The other arcs fade to 0.3 opacity over 200ms, and so do the stat tiles (to 0.45). A keyboard focus also draws a focus-ring halo under that ring.\n6. The tooltip is a plain absolutely positioned div. It sits on the arc's tip, at `(C + r·cos θ, C + r·sin θ)` with θ = fraction × 2π − π/2. Its x is clamped 52px inside the square so it never clips, and it is lifted 10px above the tip.\n7. The `labels` look writes each name in 8px white type on a `<textPath>` that follows a closed circle path starting at twelve o'clock. A name only shows once its arc is longer than the text (about 4.9px per character plus 10px of padding and the stroke), so short arcs never spill their label.\n8. `gauge` folds the items into one ring of their total against the range's `goal` (or `max`), with the percentage and a caption in the centre. Its tiles show the total, the goal and what is still to go.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SIZE = 216px square, drawn 1:1`\n  Matches the preview's ring diameter, and keeps tooltip positions in plain CSS px with no viewBox scaling.\n- `LOOKS = rings 8/4, labels 12/2, grid 8/4 without track, gauge 16 (stroke/gap px)`\n  Five rings fit with a 50px hole on the plain look. The labelled look needs a 12px band for 8px type.\n- `HEADROOM = 0.9`\n  The largest item sweeps 324° instead of a closed circle, so every ring still shows where it ends.\n- `MIN_HOLE = 26px`\n  With many items the pitch shrinks so the innermost ring never collapses into the centre.\n- `TIP_INSET = 52px`\n  Keeps the roughly 110px-wide tooltip inside the chart square at the ring's far left or right.\n- `tween = 520ms ease-out cubic (useTweenedValues)`\n  Sweeps are tweened as fractions, so a period or look change starts from wherever the arcs are.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadialChartCard(props: { title?: string; variant?: \"rings\" | \"labels\" | \"grid\" | \"gauge\"; ranges?: RadialRange[]; items?: RadialItem[]; delta?: number; max?: number; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; tiles?: boolean; showCenter?: boolean; gaugeCaption?: string; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype RadialItem = { label: string; value: number; color?: ChartColor }\ntype RadialRange = { id: string; label: string; items: RadialItem[]; delta?: number; goal?: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Visitors\"`\n  Header label while nothing is active. Also the gauge's item name and the card's accessible name.\n- `variant: \"rings\" | \"labels\" | \"grid\" | \"gauge\"` — default `\"rings\"`\n  Plain rings, names along each arc, rings over a circular grid, or one total against a goal.\n- `ranges: RadialRange[]` — default `RADIAL_RANGES`\n  Periods with their own items, delta and goal. Two or more show the period select.\n- `items: RadialItem[]`\n  A single item list, innermost first. Overrides `ranges` and hides the select.\n- `delta: number`\n  Headline chip percentage when `items` is used.\n- `max: number` — default `largest ÷ 0.9 (rings), range goal (gauge)`\n  The value of a full circle.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the period select changes.\n- `tiles: boolean` — default `true`\n  Stat tiles under the chart, three per row; hovering one focuses its ring.\n- `showCenter: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows the active value (or the total) in the hole of the ring looks.\n- `gaugeCaption: string` — default `\"of goal\"`\n  Caption under the gauge's centre percentage.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `en-US integer`\n  Formats the headline, tooltip, tiles and the accessible labels.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * One ring: track, arc, optional name on a text path, and a transparent hit\n * stroke as wide as the ring pitch so the gaps between rings still hover.\n * The arc is a circle rotated to twelve o'clock with `pathLength=100`, so its\n * dash is simply the tweened fraction × 100 and round caps close it off.\n */\nfunction RingView({\n  item,\n  index,\n  ring,\n  fraction,\n  color,\n  variant,\n  active,\n  pinned,\n  focusVisible,\n  label,\n  pathId,\n  onTarget,\n  onFocusVisible,\n  onTogglePin,\n}: RingViewProps) {\n  const tone = CHART_COLORS[color];\n  const isActive = active === index;\n  const dimmed = active !== null && !isActive;\n  const look = LOOKS[variant];\n  const dash = clamp01(fraction) * 100;\n  const arcLength = clamp01(fraction) * 2 * Math.PI * ring.r;\n  const showName =\n    variant === \"labels\" && arcLength >= item.label.length * LABEL_CHAR + LABEL_OFFSET * 2 + ring.stroke;\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<SVGGElement>) => {\n    if (event.key !== \"Enter\" && event.key !== \" \") return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    onTogglePin();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <g\n      role=\"button\"\n      tabIndex={0}\n      aria-label={label}\n      aria-pressed={pinned}\n      data-ring={item.label}\n      className=\"cursor-pointer outline-none\"\n      onPointerEnter={() => onTarget(index)}\n      onFocus={(event) => {\n        onTarget(index);\n        onFocusVisible(event.currentTarget.matches(\":focus-visible\") ? index : null);\n      }}\n      onBlur={() => {\n        onTarget(null);\n        onFocusVisible(null);\n      }}\n      onClick={onTogglePin}\n      onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n    >\n      {focusVisible ? (\n        <circle\n          cx={C}\n          cy={C}\n          r={ring.r}\n          fill=\"none\"\n          stroke=\"var(--color-border-focus-ring)\"\n          strokeWidth={ring.stroke + Math.min(4, ring.pitch - ring.stroke + 2)}\n        />\n      ) : null}\n      {look.track ? (\n        <circle cx={C} cy={C} r={ring.r} fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--color-chart-track)\" strokeWidth={ring.stroke} />\n      ) : null}\n      {dash > 0 ? (\n        <circle\n          cx={C}\n          cy={C}\n          r={ring.r}\n          fill=\"none\"\n          stroke={isActive ? tone.active : tone.fill}\n          strokeWidth={ring.stroke}\n          strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n          pathLength={100}\n          strokeDasharray={`${dash} 100`}\n          transform={`rotate(-90 ${C} ${C})`}\n          opacity={dimmed ? 0.3 : 1}\n          className=\"transition-[stroke,opacity] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n        />\n      ) : null}\n      {variant === \"labels\" ? (\n        <>\n          <path id={pathId} d={circlePath(ring.r)} fill=\"none\" stroke=\"none\" />\n          <text\n            aria-hidden\n            fontSize={LABEL_FONT}\n            fontWeight={600}\n            fill=\"#fff\"\n            dominantBaseline=\"central\"\n            opacity={showName ? (dimmed ? 0.5 : 1) : 0}\n            className=\"pointer-events-none select-none transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n          >\n            <textPath href={`#${pathId}`} startOffset={LABEL_OFFSET}>\n              {item.label}\n            </textPath>\n          </text>\n        </>\n      ) : null}\n      <circle\n        cx={C}\n        cy={C}\n        r={ring.r}\n        fill=\"none\"\n        stroke=\"transparent\"\n        strokeWidth={ring.pitch}\n        pointerEvents=\"stroke\"\n      />\n    </g>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The sweep tween and the headline's `useCountUp` cancel their animation frame on every new target and on unmount. Hover, focus and pin are plain React state on SVG elements, with no document listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useTweenedValues` is disabled, so the arcs render at their final sweeps and jump on a change. The stroke/opacity fades on arcs and names use `motion-reduce:transition-none`. The headline count runs over 1ms, and the number fade is off.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Drawing arcs with SVG `A` commands. A full or near-full sweep then needs special cases, because start and end coincide. A rotated circle with `pathLength=100` and a dash covers 0–100% with one formula.\n- Hit-testing only the visible stroke. The 4px gaps between rings then flicker the hover off as the pointer crosses them. A transparent stroke as wide as the pitch makes the whole band one target.\n- Showing ring names regardless of arc length. The name of a short arc then runs on past its end over the bare track. Hide it until the arc is longer than the text.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `RADIAL_RANGES`, `RadialItem`, `RadialRange` and `RadialVariant` are exported. Pass `items` (and `delta`) for one static list without the period select.\n- `showCenter` writes the active item's value (or the total) in the hole of the ring looks.\n- The frame, header, period select and stat tiles come from `funnel-chart-card-shell`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Visitors\", \"925\", a lime \"+5.2%\" chip and a \"Last 7 days\" select. Five rings follow, from the inside out: Other (lime), Edge (blue), Firefox (purple), Safari (pink) and Chrome (yellow), with Chrome sweeping about 324°. Five tiles sit three per row, the last row stretched. Hovering the Chrome ring fades the others, shows a tooltip \"Chrome 275 30%\" at its tip, and the header reads \"Chrome\", \"275\" and \"30% of total\". The Labels look writes each name along its arc. The Gauge look shows one ring with \"62%\" over \"of goal\" and tiles for Visitors 925, Goal 1,500 and To go 575. \"Last 30 days\" sweeps the arcs to the new values, and the headline counts to 4,005.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radial-mask-spotlight": "# Build: Radial Mask Spotlight\n\nA stacked layer is masked by a radial gradient whose centre follows the pointer, so content is lit rather than uncovered. The mask string is rewritten once per animation frame from a damped pointer position, and both the prefixed and unprefixed `mask-image` properties are set.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA layer masked to a soft disc follows the pointer, with a flat core that makes it read as a beam.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The gradient stops are the design: solid black to 30% of the radius, then a ramp to transparent at 72%. That flat core gives the beam a lit region with an actual size. A gradient that begins falling off at 0% has no lit area at all — just a bright point that fades, which the eye reads as blur rather than as light.\n2. A px radius is used rather than a percentage. A percentage radius in a radial gradient resolves against the element box, so the same declaration produces a beam of a different physical size in every panel it is dropped into, and the effect stops being reusable.\n3. Position comes from `useSmoothPointer` with a very low smoothing factor, so the beam has a small amount of drag. `damp` is frame-rate independent — the smoothing constant is the fraction of the gap left after one full second — so the same value produces the same feel at 60Hz and 144Hz rather than the beam arriving twice as fast on a fast display.\n4. The mask is written in a `useRaf` callback, not in the pointer handler. Setting `mask-image` invalidates paint for the layer, and a fast pointer delivers two or three events per frame, so a per-event write does the same expensive work several times to produce one visible result.\n5. The layer is seeded with a mask parked two radii off-panel, so the first painted frame — before rAF has run once — cannot show the layer unmasked. Nothing is written until `pointer.active` is true, which is what stops the beam flashing at the hook's centre-parked seed position on mount.\n6. Both `maskImage` and `WebkitMaskImage` are set. This is not defensive habit: released Safari versions mishandle gradient masks without the prefix, and the failure mode is the layer being fully visible or fully invisible rather than slightly wrong.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `radius = 220`\n  Beam radius in px, giving a 132px solid core and a 92px falloff. Below ~120 the core is too small to hold a line of text and the effect becomes a magnifier; above ~350 in a small panel the falloff never completes inside the box and the beam reads as an uneven wash.\n- `CORE_STOP = 0.3`\n  Where the solid region ends. This is the single number that separates \"a light\" from \"a smudge\". At 0 there is no lit area, only a peak; past ~0.5 the edge becomes hard enough that it reads as a cut-out hole instead of illumination.\n- `EDGE_STOP = 0.72`\n  Where the falloff reaches zero. Short of 100% so the gradient completes inside its own radius and the beam never shows a visible circular boundary at the edge of the declared circle.\n- `smoothing = 0.0006`\n  Fraction of the remaining distance still uncovered after one second — so the beam is effectively caught up within about 100ms. Enough drag to give the light some weight, not enough for the beam to visibly trail behind the thing you are pointing at, which would make it useless for reading.\n- `FADE_MS = 260`\n  Enter and leave fade on the whole layer. Long enough that the beam does not blink on and off as the pointer crosses the edge, short enough that leaving does not leave a light hanging in an unhovered panel.\n- `rest mask offset = -radius * 2`\n  Where the seeded mask sits before the first frame. Two radii guarantees no part of the falloff is inside the box at any panel size, so nothing is lit before the pointer has been seen.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadialMaskSpotlight(props: { children: ReactNode; radius?: number; smoothing?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content of the lit layer. Should have a transparent background.\n- `radius: number` — default `220`\n  Beam radius in CSS px. Core is 30% of this, falloff completes at 72%.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0006`\n  Fraction of the gap remaining after one second. Lower is tighter.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Where the solid core ends, as a fraction of `radius`. */\nconst CORE_STOP = 0.3;\n/** Where the falloff reaches full transparency, as a fraction of `radius`. */\nconst EDGE_STOP = 0.72;\nconst FADE_MS = 260;\n\nexport function RadialMaskSpotlight({\n  children,\n  radius = 220,\n  smoothing = 0.0006,\n  className,\n}: RadialMaskSpotlightProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const lit = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const enabled = useRef(false);\n  const visible = useRef(false);\n\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({\n    target: root,\n    smoothing,\n    // Touch is explicitly off: this component is fine-pointer only, and\n    // letting touch drags drive the mask would move the beam under the finger.\n    touch: false,\n  });\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    const layer = lit.current;\n    if (!el || !layer) return;\n\n    const fine = window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches;\n    const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;\n\n    if (!fine) {\n      /* No pointer to track. Rather than leaving a masked layer invisible, the\n         mask is removed entirely so the lit layer simply shows in full — the\n         content stays readable and nothing is hidden behind an effect that\n         cannot be operated. */\n      layer.style.maskImage = '';\n      layer.style.webkitMaskImage = '';\n      layer.style.opacity = '1';\n      return;\n    }\n\n    enabled.current = !reduced;\n\n    if (reduced) {\n      /* Reduced motion keeps the spotlight — it is a hover affordance, not\n         gratuitous movement — but pins it. A beam that chases the pointer with\n         inertia is the part that was objected to, so the mask is written once,\n         parked, and never updated again. */\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      writeMask(layer, rect.width * 0.5, rect.height * 0.45, radius);\n      layer.style.opacity = '1';\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const onEnter = () => {\n      visible.current = true;\n      layer.style.opacity = '1';\n    };\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      visible.current = false;\n      layer.style.opacity = '0';\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointerenter', onEnter);\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerenter', onEnter);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      enabled.current = false;\n      layer.style.maskImage = '';\n      layer.style.webkitMaskImage = '';\n      layer.style.opacity = '';\n    };\n  }, [radius]);\n\n  useRaf(() => {\n    const layer = lit.current;\n    if (!layer || !enabled.current) return;\n    const p = pointer.current;\n    // `active` is false until a real pointer has been seen. Writing before\n    // that would place the beam at the hook's centre-parked seed value, which\n    // flashes a spotlight the user never asked for.\n    if (!p.active) return;\n    writeMask(layer, p.x, p.y, radius);\n  });\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={root} className={cn('relative isolate overflow-hidden', className)}>\n      <div\n        ref={lit}\n        className=\"absolute inset-0\"\n        style={{\n          opacity: 0,\n          transition: `opacity ${FADE_MS}ms ease-out`,\n          // Seeded off-panel so the very first painted frame cannot show an\n          // unmasked layer before rAF runs.\n          maskImage: restMask(radius),\n          WebkitMaskImage: restMask(radius),\n        }}\n      >\n        {children}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction maskFor(x: number, y: number, radius: number): string {\n  /* `circle <radius>px at <x>px <y>px` — an explicit px radius rather than a\n     percentage, because a percentage radius in a radial gradient resolves\n     against the box and would make the beam a different physical size on\n     every panel it is dropped into. */\n  return `radial-gradient(circle ${radius}px at ${x}px ${y}px, #000 0%, #000 ${\n    CORE_STOP * 100\n  }%, transparent ${EDGE_STOP * 100}%)`;\n}\n\nfunction restMask(radius: number): string {\n  return maskFor(-radius * 2, -radius * 2, radius);\n}\n\nfunction writeMask(layer: HTMLElement, x: number, y: number, radius: number) {\n  const value = maskFor(x, y, radius);\n  /* Both spellings. Safari has shipped unprefixed `mask-image` for years but\n     still mishandles gradient masks without `-webkit-` in enough released\n     versions that dropping the prefix means the layer is either fully visible\n     or fully gone on those browsers — a total failure, not a degraded one. */\n  layer.style.maskImage = value;\n  layer.style.webkitMaskImage = value;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the enter and leave listeners, flips the enabled flag so any in-flight frame is a no-op, and clears the inline `maskImage`, `WebkitMaskImage` and `opacity`. `useRaf` and `useSmoothPointer` unsubscribe themselves. Nothing was allocated beyond the two strings written per frame.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The spotlight is still drawn — it is a hover affordance and removing it would hide content — but it never moves. The mask is written once at 50% across and 45% down, the rAF loop is left disabled, and no pointer listeners attach.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- A linear falloff from 0%. Without a flat core there is no lit region, just a bright centre that fades continuously, and the result reads as a blurred blob rather than a light. This is the most common way this effect is built and the most common reason it looks cheap.\n- Setting only `maskImage`. Released Safari versions mishandle gradient masks without `-webkit-mask-image`, and the failure is total — the layer is either always visible or never visible — so it does not look like a mask bug when reported.\n- Writing the mask in the `pointermove` handler. Every write invalidates paint for the layer, and a fast pointer produces several events per frame, so the same paint is thrown away two or three times before anything reaches the screen.\n- A percentage radius. `circle 30% at ...` resolves against the element box, so the beam is a different size in every container and the constant that looked right in the design mock is wrong everywhere else.\n- Writing the mask before the pointer has actually been seen. `useSmoothPointer` parks at the element centre so the first movement eases out sensibly, but that seed is not a real position — treating it as one flashes a spotlight in the middle of the panel on mount.\n- Masking a layer containing interactive elements. `mask-image` does not clip hit testing, so links in the dark region are still clickable while being invisible. Either keep the masked layer non-interactive or accept that the mask is purely decorative.\n- Putting the effect on a layer with its own background. The mask reveals the layer, and a masked opaque background is a visible disc of colour, not a light. The lit layer needs a transparent background so the beam shows content, not a shape.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch the mask is removed entirely and the lit layer shows in full rather than staying hidden — an effect that cannot be operated must not be allowed to hide content. `useSmoothPointer` is also configured with `touch: false` so a scroll drag can never move the beam.\n- Reduced motion keeps the spotlight but pins it: the mask is written once, centred slightly above middle, and never updated. The affordance survives; the chasing does not.\n- Scoped to its own container and measured against its rect, so several instances can appear on one page without interfering.\n- The masked layer is decorative by construction. If it holds links, remember that a mask hides paint but not hit testing.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove across the panel and a soft round beam follows the pointer with a slight drag, lighting the content under it. The centre of the beam is evenly lit rather than brightest at one point — cover the middle third with a finger and the light should look flat, not peaked. Enter and leave and the whole layer fades over about a quarter of a second rather than blinking. On first load, before you move, nothing is lit. Check in Safari that the beam appears at all. On touch the layer is fully visible with no mask.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radial-ripple-dot-cloud": "# Build: Radial Ripple Dot Cloud\n\nSeveral thousand dots in one draw call, with a wave travelling outward from a point left of centre. The displacement is a sine in radius minus a phase in time, attenuated exponentially, so the field is violent near the source and nearly still at the rim — which is what gives the motion an origin rather than making it a screensaver. Dot density along the radius is a hand-placed cubic B-spline, so the rings and gaps in the layout are drawn rather than derived from a formula.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dot field with a travelling wave running out of one off-centre point, its density drawn by hand as a B-spline.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Rest positions are baked once, in field units, into an `aOrigin` attribute: the field spans ±16/9 horizontally and ±1 vertically. The draw-time uniform `uCover = max(1, aspect / (16/9))` scales that field to cover the camera, so a resize writes one float instead of rebuilding a buffer.\n2. Density along the radius comes from a uniform cubic B-spline over eight hand-placed control points — five spans — evaluated in JS at bake time. `bsplineWeights(t)` returns the four basis values `[(1-t)³, 4-6t²+3t³, 1+3t+3t²-3t³, t³] / 6` and `sampleRamp` blends control points `i..i+3`. The curve is C2 and does not pass through its control points, which is what lets a hand-placed sequence stay smooth.\n3. Positions are chosen by rejection sampling: a seeded candidate uniform over the field rectangle is kept with probability `ramp(dist / maxRadius)`, where `maxRadius` is the distance from the ripple source to the farthest field corner. That is the only way to realise an arbitrary drawn radial density from a uniform PRNG without inverting the curve analytically.\n4. The same ramp value is baked into `aScale` (0.55 + 0.75d) and `aAlpha` (0.3 + 0.7d), so a sparse band is also a dim band. Two independent curves would let them disagree and the field would read as noise with holes in it.\n5. The vertex shader computes `dist = length(origin - uCenter)`, `wave = sin(dist * uFrequency - uElapsed * uSpeed)` — a travelling wave, because the time term is subtracted from the radius term — and `attenuated = wave * exp(-dist * uDecay)`, then displaces each dot along its own outward direction by `attenuated * uAmplitude`.\n6. The direction is guarded: `dir = dist > 1e-5 ? delta / dist : vec2(0.0)`, because normalising a zero vector at the exact source yields NaN and one NaN vertex takes the whole point with it.\n7. `gl_PointSize` is floored at 2 device pixels and the alpha is divided by the area that flooring invented, so the cloud has the same density on a 1x monitor as on a 3x phone. The fragment shader cuts a disc with `1 - smoothstep(0.42, 0.5, d)` and tints toward `uCrestColor` by the signed wave, so only the outgoing crest lights up.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `FIELD_HALF_W = 16 / 9`\n  Half-width of the baked field, with the field 2 units tall. Baking at one fixed aspect is what makes a resize a single uniform write; 16:9 is the aspect most containers are near, so `uCover` is usually 1 and nothing is scaled at all.\n- `DENSITY_RAMP = [1, 1, 0.34, 0.28, 0.92, 0.5, 0, -0.15]`\n  Eight control points, so five spans. Sampled it reads 0.89 / 0.44 / 0.40 / 0.74 / 0.49 / 0.06 — dense core, gap, secondary band at ~60% radius, empty rim. The last point is negative deliberately: a control point is not a value, and pulling it below zero is what makes the tail actually reach zero instead of settling around 0.15.\n- `centerX = 0.4`\n  Ripple source at 40% of the width rather than 50%. A centred radial wave is bilaterally symmetric and the eye reads symmetry as a screensaver; a source off the axis reads as an impact somewhere in the scene.\n- `frequency = 6.5 rad per field unit`\n  Wavelength 2π/6.5 ≈ 0.97 units, about half the field height, so roughly two and a half crests fit between the source and the far corner. Past ~12 the rings alias against the dot spacing and the field boils; below ~3 there is only one crest and no ripple to read.\n- `speed = 2.4 rad per second`\n  Crest speed is speed / frequency = 0.37 field units per second, so a crest crosses the field in about six seconds. Fast enough to be motion, slow enough that the eye can follow a single ring outward.\n- `decay = 0.85 per field unit`\n  Amplitude is 43% at one unit from the source and 13% at the far corner, which gives the wave a source without a visible boundary. A linear falloff reaches exactly zero at a fixed radius and draws a hard circle there.\n- `amplitude = 0.045 field units`\n  2.25% of the field height — around 25px on a 1100px-tall field, in the same range as the 18px the technique was observed at. Past ~0.12 dots cross each other and the cloud shears instead of rippling.\n- `size = 2.6 CSS pixels`\n  Base diameter, scaled 0.55..1.3 per dot. Under ~2 the hardware point-size floor kicks in for most dots and the alpha compensation is doing all the work; over ~5 the dense core fills solid and the ripple stops being legible.\n- `count = 6000`\n  One interleaved-free buffer, one draw call. At this size the field is vertex-bound rather than fill-bound, and 6000 is roughly where the core reads as continuous without the gaps needing more dots to hide them.\n- `MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_DOT = 24`\n  Rejection-sampling budget. The mean of the ramp is about 0.45, so ~2.2 attempts per kept dot; a 24x budget absorbs a much sparser custom curve and still terminates rather than spinning forever if someone authors a ramp that is zero everywhere.\n- `STATIC_ELAPSED = 3.4 s`\n  The frame drawn under reduced motion. Far enough in that two crests have left the source, so the still frame shows the ripple structure rather than the undisturbed lattice.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadialRippleDotCloud(props: { count?: number; size?: number; frequency?: number; speed?: number; decay?: number; amplitude?: number; centerX?: number; centerY?: number; color?: string; crestColor?: string; opacity?: number; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `6000`\n  Dots kept after density rejection. One draw call regardless.\n- `size: number` — default `2.6`\n  Base dot diameter in CSS pixels, before the per-dot density scale.\n- `frequency: number` — default `6.5`\n  Radians of wave per field unit; wavelength is 2π / frequency.\n- `speed: number` — default `2.4`\n  Radians of phase per second. Crest speed is speed / frequency.\n- `decay: number` — default `0.85`\n  Exponential attenuation per field unit of radius.\n- `amplitude: number` — default `0.045`\n  Peak displacement in field units, where the field is 2 units tall.\n- `centerX: number` — default `0.4`\n  Ripple source across the field, 0..1. Deliberately not centred.\n- `centerY: number` — default `0.5`\n  Ripple source down the field, 0..1.\n- `color: string` — default `'#5d6b7a'`\n  Dot colour in the troughs.\n- `crestColor: string` — default `'#eaf2ff'`\n  Dot colour on an outgoing crest.\n- `opacity: number` — default `1`\n  Multiplier applied after the per-dot area compensation.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'radial-ripple-dot-cloud'`\n  Seeds rejection sampling, so the layout is stable across reloads and matches the server.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Uniform cubic B-spline basis at parameter `t` within one span. */\nfunction bsplineWeights(t: number): [number, number, number, number] {\n  const t2 = t * t;\n  const t3 = t2 * t;\n  return [\n    (1 - 3 * t + 3 * t2 - t3) / 6,\n    (4 - 6 * t2 + 3 * t3) / 6,\n    (1 + 3 * t + 3 * t2 - 3 * t3) / 6,\n    t3 / 6,\n  ];\n}\n\n/**\n * Sample the density curve at `u` in 0..1. `n` control points give `n - 3`\n * spans; the curve is C2 and does NOT pass through its control points, which\n * is the property that lets a hand-placed sequence stay smooth.\n */\nfunction sampleRamp(ramp: readonly number[], u: number): number {\n  const spans = ramp.length - 3;\n  if (spans < 1) return clamp(ramp[0] ?? 0, 0, 1);\n  const s = clamp(u, 0, 1) * spans;\n  const span = Math.min(Math.floor(s), spans - 1);\n  const weights = bsplineWeights(s - span);\n  let total = 0;\n  for (let k = 0; k < 4; k += 1) {\n    total += (ramp[span + k] ?? 0) * (weights[k] ?? 0);\n  }\n  return clamp(total, 0, 1);\n}\n\nexport function RadialRippleDotCloud({\n  count = 6000,\n  size = 2.6,\n  frequency = 6.5,\n  speed = 2.4,\n  decay = 0.85,\n  amplitude = 0.045,\n  centerX = 0.4,\n  centerY = 0.5,\n  color = '#5d6b7a',\n  crestColor = '#eaf2ff',\n  opacity = 1,\n  seed = 'radial-ripple-dot-cloud',\n  className,\n}: RadialRippleDotCloudProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size2 = useRef({ width, height });\n  size2.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0.1, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    // Field-unit centre of the ripple. Baked alongside the origins so the\n    // normalised radius each dot stores is measured from the same point the\n    // shader will use.\n    const cx = (centerX * 2 - 1) * FIELD_HALF_W;\n    const cy = 1 - centerY * 2;\n\n    /* The farthest corner from the source. Normalising radius by this is what\n       makes the density ramp aspect-independent: the tail of the curve lands on\n       the corner of the field rather than at some arbitrary unit distance. */\n    const maxRadius = Math.max(\n      Math.hypot(FIELD_HALF_W - cx, 1 - cy),\n      Math.hypot(FIELD_HALF_W + cx, 1 - cy),\n      Math.hypot(FIELD_HALF_W - cx, 1 + cy),\n      Math.hypot(FIELD_HALF_W + cx, 1 + cy),\n    );\n\n    /* Rejection sampling against the density curve. Candidates are uniform over\n       the field rectangle and kept with probability `ramp(r)`, which is the only\n       way to get an arbitrary hand-drawn radial density out of a uniform PRNG\n       without inverting the curve analytically. Seeded, so the layout is\n       identical on the server, on the client, and on every reload. */\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const wanted = Math.max(1, Math.round(count));\n    const origins: number[] = [];\n    const scales: number[] = [];\n    const alphas: number[] = [];\n\n    let attempts = 0;\n    const budget = wanted * MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_DOT;\n    while (origins.length < wanted * 2 && attempts < budget) {\n      attempts += 1;\n      const x = (rng() * 2 - 1) * FIELD_HALF_W;\n      const y = rng() * 2 - 1;\n      const density = sampleRamp(DENSITY_RAMP, Math.hypot(x - cx, y - cy) / maxRadius);\n      if (rng() > density) continue;\n      origins.push(x, y);\n      // The ramp is reused for weight as well as for density, so a sparse band\n      // is also a dim one. Two curves would let them disagree and the field\n      // would read as noise with holes in it.\n      scales.push(0.55 + 0.75 * density);\n      alphas.push(0.3 + 0.7 * density);\n    }\n\n    const kept = scales.length;\n    const positions = new Float32Array(kept * 3); // zeros; the shader positions\n    const originAttr = new Float32Array(kept * 2);\n    const scaleAttr = new Float32Array(kept);\n    const alphaAttr = new Float32Array(kept);\n    for (let i = 0; i < kept; i += 1) {\n      originAttr[i * 2] = origins[i * 2] ?? 0;\n      originAttr[i * 2 + 1] = origins[i * 2 + 1] ?? 0;\n      scaleAttr[i] = scales[i] ?? 1;\n      alphaAttr[i] = alphas[i] ?? 1;\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aOrigin', new THREE.BufferAttribute(originAttr, 2));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aScale', new THREE.BufferAttribute(scaleAttr, 1));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aAlpha', new THREE.BufferAttribute(alphaAttr, 1));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uCover: { value: 1 },\n      uCenter: { value: new THREE.Vector2(cx, cy) },\n      uFrequency: { value: frequency },\n      uSpeed: { value: speed },\n      uDecay: { value: decay },\n      uAmplitude: { value: amplitude },\n      uSize: { value: size },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uColor: { value: new THREE.Color(color) },\n      uCrestColor: { value: new THREE.Color(crestColor) },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: RADIAL_RIPPLE_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: RADIAL_RIPPLE_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      // Additive, because overlapping dots in the dense core should read as\n      // brighter rather than as a flat plate of the same colour.\n      blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    // Every vertex is displaced in the shader, so three's bounding sphere is\n    // wrong by construction and culling against it can blank the whole cloud.\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) return;\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      const aspect = w / h;\n      camera.left = -aspect;\n      camera.right = aspect;\n      camera.top = 1;\n      camera.bottom = -1;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n\n      /* Cover, not contain. Past 16:9 the baked field is narrower than the\n         camera, so it is scaled up until it reaches both edges; the alternative\n         is a visible vertical seam where the dots stop. The centre is scaled by\n         the same factor so the ripple stays put relative to the field. */\n      const cover = Math.max(1, aspect / FIELD_HALF_W);\n      uniforms.uCover.value = cover;\n      uniforms.uCenter.value.set(cx * cover, cy * cover);\n      // Re-read: moving a window between displays changes devicePixelRatio\n      // without changing the CSS size, and dot sizes are specified in CSS px.\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    resize(size2.current.width, size2.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, draw };\n    draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    count,\n    size,\n    frequency,\n    speed,\n    decay,\n    amplitude,\n    centerX,\n    centerY,\n    color,\n    crestColor,\n    opacity,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    // A resize clears the drawing buffer, so the frozen frame has to be\n    // redrawn or a reduced-motion visitor sees an empty box after any reflow.\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.draw(STATIC_ELAPSED);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed),\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref, walks the scene with `disposeObject` to release the geometry and its four attribute buffers, disposes the ShaderMaterial explicitly, then calls `disposeRenderer` — `dispose` plus `forceContextLoss` plus a zeroed drawing buffer — and removes the canvas. The canvas is created inside the effect and appended imperatively rather than being owned by a React ref, so a StrictMode double-mount builds a fresh context instead of handing back the one that was just killed.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component reads `useReducedMotion` itself and opens no rAF subscription. One frame is drawn at `uElapsed = 3.4`, by which point two crests have left the source — so the still image shows the ripple structure and the drawn density bands, not the undisturbed lattice. The layout, the colours and the crest tinting are all present; only the travel stops. The frame is re-drawn on resize because a canvas resize clears the buffer.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing `sin(dist * frequency) * sin(elapsed * speed)`. That is a standing wave: the rings pulse in place and never travel. Subtracting the phase from the radius inside one sine is what makes crests move outward — and adding it instead sends them inward, which reads as a drain rather than an impact.\n- Normalising the offset vector without guarding the source. At `dist == 0` the division produces NaN, the vertex is discarded by the rasteriser, and on some drivers the whole primitive goes with it — an intermittently missing dot that is very hard to attribute.\n- Attenuating the radius instead of the wave, or using a linear falloff. Linear reaches zero at a fixed radius and draws a visible circle across the field; the exponential never quite reaches zero and has no edge.\n- Rebuilding the position buffer on resize. It reallocates hundreds of kilobytes during a window drag and, if the layout is re-sampled, re-randomises every dot mid-motion. Baking in field units and scaling by one `uCover` uniform avoids both.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` at its default. Every vertex is displaced in the shader, so three's bounding sphere — computed from the undisplaced attribute — is wrong, and the whole cloud can vanish when the camera or the container changes.\n- Using `Math.random()` for the layout. The server and the client disagree, so React logs a hydration mismatch, and the composition a designer approved is not the one the next visitor sees.\n- Treating the B-spline control points as points on the curve. The curve does not pass through them — the value at the start of the first span is `(P0 + 4P1 + P2) / 6`. Authoring the ramp as if it were interpolating produces a flatter, wrong curve and hours of confusion.\n- Driving dot brightness from a second curve independent of the density curve. Where they disagree the field reads as random noise with holes in it rather than as drawn bands.\n- Forgetting that a resize clears the drawing buffer. Under reduced motion nothing redraws afterwards, so the component is blank after any reflow unless the frozen frame is re-rendered.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The camera is orthographic, ±aspect by ±1, so field units are directly comparable to the container height: 1 unit is half the visible height.\n- `uCover` implements cover, not contain. Past 16:9 the baked field would otherwise stop short of the container edge and leave a visible vertical seam where the dots end.\n- The ripple runs on absolute elapsed time from the shared rAF clock, so several instances on one page stay in phase with each other.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nRings of dots visibly travel outward from a point left of centre, roughly two crests visible at a time, brightening as each crest passes and dimming behind it. The rim of the field barely moves. The layout has obvious drawn structure — a dense core, a gap, a second band — and is identical after a reload and after resizing the window. With reduced motion the field renders in full and holds a frozen mid-ripple frame.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radio": "# Build: Radio\n\nA `RadioGroup` and `Radio` on React Aria, plus the bare `RadioDot` glyph they draw. The dot is three stacked circles: a bordered white surface, the accent gradient surface on top of it, and a small white centre. Selecting cross-fades the two surfaces over 200ms while the centre scales up from nothing, which gives a gradient fill a smooth transition even though CSS cannot animate gradients.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact Aria radio group with a gradient selected dot that cross-fades in while the white centre scales up, in two sizes.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `RadioGroup` spreads its props onto `AriaRadioGroup` (single selection, arrow-key roving between radios, form `name` and `value`, label association) and lays the radios out as `flex flex-col gap-2`. `className` accepts a string or React Aria's state function.\n2. `Radio` renders `AriaRadio`, a `<label>` around a visually hidden `<input type=\"radio\">`, as `group inline-flex items-center select-none gap-2`. Its children render function passes `isSelected` and `isFocusVisible` to `RadioDot`, then renders `children` (when not null or undefined) in the size's label type in `text-text-primary`. Disabled adds `cursor-not-allowed opacity-50`.\n3. `RadioDot` is a `relative rounded-full` span marked `aria-hidden`, sized by `dotStyles[size]`. Layer one is the default surface (`border-border-checkbox-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs`). Layer two is the selected surface: `bg-gradient-to-b from-accent-500 to-accent-600` with an inset 1px accent ring and an inset 2px white top highlight at 25% opacity. Only one of them is at full opacity at a time, and both have `transition-opacity duration-200`.\n4. Layer three is the inner dot (`bg-control-indicator-background`, white), positioned with `absolute top-1/2 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2` rather than flex centring, because fractional sizes such as 5px can land on half pixels under flex. It transitions `scale` and `opacity` over 200ms between `scale-0 opacity-0` and `scale-100 opacity-100`.\n5. Focus draws `ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-2` on the dot only while React Aria reports `isFocusVisible`, so a mouse click does not show a ring.\n6. `RadioDot` is exported on its own for menu rows that already manage selection (for example a dropdown acting as a radio group). It has no role or focus, so it can sit inside a menu item without nesting one interactive control in another.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `md = size-4 dot (16px), size-1.5 centre (6px), text-body-medium`\n  The Figma radio for forms. The 6px centre leaves a 5px gradient ring around it at 16px, which reads as a ring instead of a filled disc.\n- `sm = size-3.5 dot (14px), 5px centre, 0.875px border, text-body-2-medium`\n  The AI chat model picker (node 4035:6925). Every stroke is scaled by 14/16 (0.875) so the small dot looks like the medium one shrunk, not thinner.\n- `crossfade = 200ms opacity on both surfaces, 200ms scale + opacity on the centre`\n  Gradient backgrounds cannot transition, so the motion is carried by opacity on stacked layers. All three layers share one duration so the fill and the centre arrive together.\n- `selected inset shadow = inset 0 0 0 1px accent-500, inset 0 2px 0 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.25)`\n  The first is a crisp edge where a border would shift the layout; the second is the top highlight that matches the primary button's gradient.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadioGroup(props: Omit<AriaRadioGroupProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction Radio(props: Omit<AriaRadioProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction RadioDot(props: { selected?: boolean; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; focusVisible?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `RadioGroup.value / defaultValue: string`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled selected value, from React Aria.\n- `RadioGroup.onChange: (value: string) => void`\n  Called with the newly selected radio's `value`.\n- `RadioGroup.aria-label: string`\n  Accessible name when the group has no visible label.\n- `Radio.value: string`\n  Value this radio contributes to the group.\n- `Radio.size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  16px dot with body label, or 14px dot with the 13px body-2 label.\n- `Radio.isDisabled: boolean`\n  Half opacity, not-allowed cursor, skipped by arrow keys.\n- `RadioDot.selected: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows the gradient surface and the white centre.\n- `RadioDot.size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"sm\"`\n  Same size table as `Radio`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RadioDot({ selected = false, size = \"sm\", focusVisible = false, className }: RadioDotProps) {\n  const s = dotStyles[size];\n  return (\n    <span\n      aria-hidden\n      className={cx(\n        \"relative flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full\",\n        s.dot,\n        focusVisible && \"ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-2\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {/* Default surface */}\n      <span\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute inset-0 rounded-full border-border-checkbox-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs\",\n          \"transition-opacity duration-200 ease\",\n          s.border,\n          selected && \"opacity-0\",\n        )}\n      />\n      {/* Selected gradient surface */}\n      <span\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute inset-0 rounded-full\",\n          \"transition-opacity duration-200 ease\",\n          s.selected,\n          !selected && \"opacity-0\",\n        )}\n      />\n      {/* Inner dot: absolutely centered (flex centering of fractional sizes can\n          land on subpixels) and no drop shadow — a downward shadow makes the\n          dot read as sitting below center. */}\n      <span\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute top-1/2 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full bg-control-indicator-background\",\n          \"transition-[scale,opacity] duration-200 ease\",\n          s.inner,\n          selected ? \"scale-100 opacity-100\" : \"scale-0 opacity-0\",\n        )}\n      />\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component holds no state, listeners or timers of its own; React Aria owns the group state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. The 200ms opacity crossfade and the centre scale are CSS transitions, so a global rule that removes transitions makes the dot switch instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not swap the gradient with a conditional `background-image` on one element. It snaps. The crossfade needs the two surfaces as separate layers.\n- Do not centre the inner dot with flexbox. At 5px it can land on a half pixel and look off-centre. Keep the absolute `top-1/2 left-1/2` plus translate.\n- Do not give the inner dot a drop shadow. A downward shadow makes it look like it sits below the centre of the ring.\n- Do not put a `Radio` inside a menu item. Use `RadioDot`, which is presentational, and let the menu own selection and focus.\n- Do not leave `RadioGroup` without a visible `label` or an `aria-label`. React Aria warns about a radio group with no accessible name.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `RadioDot` defaults to `size=\"sm\"` (it is mostly used in compact menus), while `Radio` defaults to `size=\"md\"`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThree radios stack vertically with Weekly selected: its dot is a blue gradient with a white centre. Clicking Monthly fades Weekly back to a white bordered circle while Monthly's gradient fades in and its centre grows from nothing, in about 200ms. Arrow keys move the selection within the group. Tab shows a focus ring around the dot, a mouse click does not. The small size renders a slightly smaller dot and label with the same look.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "radio-card": "# Build: Radio Card\n\nThe radio counterpart of Checkbox Card: a 10px-radius bordered card with a title, an optional description and the 16px `RadioDot` on the trailing edge. It is a React Aria `Radio`, so it must sit in a `RadioGroup`, which gives single selection and arrow-key movement between cards. Selecting a card cross-fades its dot to the accent gradient while the white centre grows in.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBordered option card with title, description and the kit radio dot on the right; inside a RadioGroup a click anywhere selects it.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `RadioCard` renders `AriaRadio` directly. React Aria makes it a `<label>` around a visually hidden `<input type=\"radio\">`, so the whole card is the click target and the card reads its selection, focus and disabled state from the surrounding `RadioGroup` context. All Aria radio props (`value`, `isDisabled`, `autoFocus`) pass through.\n2. The card class is computed from the render state: `flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-2lg border border-border-button-default py-3 pe-5 ps-4 select-none` with `transition-colors duration-150`. Hovered and not disabled gives `bg-background-primary-hover`, otherwise `bg-background-primary-default`. Disabled adds `cursor-not-allowed opacity-50`.\n3. The text column (`flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5`) holds the title in `text-body-medium text-text-primary` and, when `description` is not null or undefined, a line in `text-body-regular text-text-secondary`. Both truncate to one line.\n4. The trailing slot (`shrink-0 py-1`) renders `<RadioDot size=\"md\" selected={state.isSelected} focusVisible={state.isFocusVisible} />`, the same glyph `Radio` uses: a bordered white disc and an accent gradient disc cross-fading over 200ms, with a 6px white centre that scales from 0 to 1. The focus ring appears on the dot for keyboard focus only.\n5. Because the cards are radios in one group, Tab enters the group once (on the selected card) and the arrow keys move both focus and selection between cards, which is the standard radio pattern.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `padding = ps-4 pe-5 py-3 (16 / 20 / 12px)`\n  The Figma checkbox item spec (node 3793:2966), reused so radio and checkbox cards are interchangeable in one settings page. The wider trailing padding gives the dot breathing room in its corner.\n- `dot size = RadioDot size=\"md\" (16px, 6px centre)`\n  Fixed to medium so the dot matches the 16px checkbox box in Checkbox Card. It is not a prop.\n- `radius = rounded-2lg (10px)`\n  The kit's radius for card-level controls, shared with Checkbox Card and Switch Card.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RadioCard(props: Omit<AriaRadioProps, \"children\"> & { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: string`\n  Value this card contributes to the surrounding `RadioGroup`.\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Main label in body medium. Also the accessible name of the radio.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Optional secondary line, truncated to one line.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Half opacity, not-allowed cursor, skipped by arrow keys.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RadioCard({ className, title, description, ref, ...props }: RadioCardProps) {\n  return (\n    <AriaRadio\n      ref={ref}\n      {...props}\n      className={(state) =>\n        cx(\n          \"group flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-2lg border border-border-button-default py-3 pe-5 ps-4 select-none\",\n          \"transition-colors duration-150 ease\",\n          state.isHovered && !state.isDisabled\n            ? \"bg-background-primary-hover\"\n            : \"bg-background-primary-default\",\n          state.isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\" : \"cursor-pointer\",\n          typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {(state) => (\n        <>\n          <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n            <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</span>\n            {description !== undefined && description !== null && (\n              <span className=\"truncate text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{description}</span>\n            )}\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center py-1\">\n            <RadioDot size=\"md\" selected={state.isSelected} focusVisible={state.isFocusVisible} />\n          </span>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaRadio>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The card holds no state, listeners or timers; the `RadioGroup` owns the value.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The card fades its background over 150ms and the dot cross-fades over 200ms, all as CSS transitions. The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`, so a global rule that removes transitions makes the states switch instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not render a `RadioCard` outside a `RadioGroup`. React Aria's `Radio` reads its state from the group context and fails without it.\n- Do not put links or buttons inside `title` or `description`. The card is a `<label>`, so clicks inside it select the radio.\n- Do not highlight the selected card by changing its border. The kit keeps the border neutral and lets the dot carry the state, which keeps the card the same in both themes.\n- Do not forget the group's accessible name (`aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`). React Aria warns about an unnamed radio group.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Use the kit's `RadioGroup` from the Radio item (it is a plain `flex-col gap-2` wrapper around React Aria's group) to stack the cards.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThree plan cards in a `RadioGroup` show one selected dot. Clicking anywhere on another card, including its description, moves the selection there: the old dot fades back to white and the new one fills with the gradient while its white centre grows in. With a card focused, the arrow keys move the selection between cards. Hover tints a card's background. A disabled card is at half opacity and cannot be selected.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "realtime-cube-envmap": "# Build: Realtime Cube EnvMap\n\nA `CubeCamera` sits inside the reflector and renders the six directions around it every other frame; that cube texture is assigned straight onto the material as its `envMap`. Because the panels supplying the reflection are orbiting, the highlights crawl across the surface instead of being painted on. No HDRI, no loader, no asset — the environment is scene geometry.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA metal knot reflecting panels that are actually orbiting it, captured six faces at a time every other frame.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `WebGLCubeRenderTarget(resolution, { generateMipmaps: true, minFilter: LinearMipmapLinearFilter })` backs a `CubeCamera(0.1, 60, target)` positioned at the reflector, and `cubeTarget.texture` is assigned to `material.envMap` once at build time — the texture object never changes identity, only its contents.\n2. The environment is built from geometry: an inside-out `SphereGeometry(16)` with `side: BackSide` carrying a vertical gradient `CanvasTexture`, plus `panelCount` `PlaneGeometry` emitters on a ring of radius `panelRadius`, each `lookAt(0,0,0)` and lifted by `sin(i * 2.39996) * 0.9` so the reflection has vertical structure to move through.\n3. Panels alternate bright and near-black via `color.setHSL(hue + i*26, i%2 ? 0.4 : 0.66, i%2 ? 0.08 : 0.62)`. The dark ones are load-bearing: a uniformly bright environment gives a mirror nothing to contrast against and the metal reads as matte plastic.\n4. Each frame the ring and the reflector are rotated from `elapsed`, then — only on frames where `frames % refreshEvery === 0` — the reflector is set `visible = false`, `cubeCamera.update(renderer, scene)` runs, and it is set visible again before the single visible `renderer.render`.\n5. Resize lives in its own effect: it sets renderer size, sets `camera.aspect`, moves the camera to `BASE_DISTANCE / Math.min(1, aspect)` so a portrait container does not clip the panel ring off both sides, and forces one capture plus one redraw so a reflow never leaves a stale or black frame on screen.\n6. Teardown disposes the shell texture, both geometries, every panel material, the reflector material, then `cubeTarget.dispose()` — the cube target is not in the scene graph, so `disposeObject` never reaches it — and finally `disposeRenderer`, which is what calls `forceContextLoss()`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `refreshEvery = 2`\n  Cube captures per rendered frame. Six faces is six scene renders against the visible pass's one, so this is 6/7 of the component's cost. An env map is sampled by direction, not position, so at these rotation speeds a one-frame-old capture is not observable; at 4 or more the highlights visibly step.\n- `resolution = 256`\n  Cube face size. Cost is 6 x resolution² fragments plus a mip chain — about 2 MB of GPU memory. At 128 the panel edges alias into the reflection as jagged bands; above 512 nothing improves because roughness immediately blurs the detail away.\n- `roughness = 0.08`\n  Not 0. A perfect mirror shows the panels as hard-edged rectangles and every seam in the cube map becomes visible; a slight roughness selects a lower mip and softens exactly those seams. Above ~0.3 the reflection stops being readable as a scene at all.\n- `envMapIntensity = 1.4`\n  Reflected light multiplier. At 1 a dark environment leaves the object nearly invisible against the shell; above ~2 the bright panels clip to flat white and the curvature information in the highlight is lost.\n- `panelRadius = 4.2`\n  Ring radius against a ~1.4-unit reflector. Closer and the panels enter the camera frustum and read as walls rather than as light sources; further and they subtend too small an angle to produce a highlight worth reflecting.\n- `BASE_DISTANCE = 7.2`\n  Camera distance at aspect >= 1, chosen against a 34 degree field of view so the reflector fills about two thirds of the frame height. Divided by `min(1, aspect)` on narrower containers.\n- `STATIC_POSE = 0.9`\n  The `elapsed` value used for the reduced-motion still and for post-resize redraws. Not 0: at zero the knot is axis-aligned and the panels sit on the cardinal directions, which reads as a technical diagram instead of a lit object.\n- `lift stagger = sin(i * 2.39996) * 0.9`\n  2.39996 rad is the golden angle, so successive panels never repeat a height for any panel count. A constant height puts every panel in one band and the reflection has nothing to travel through vertically.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RealtimeCubeEnvmap(props: { shape?: 'knot' | 'icosahedron'; panelCount?: number; panelRadius?: number; spinSpeed?: number; objectSpin?: number; resolution?: number; refreshEvery?: number; roughness?: number; metalness?: number; envMapIntensity?: number; hue?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `shape: 'knot' | 'icosahedron'` — default `'knot'`\n  Reflector silhouette. The knot has more curvature and so more reflection per pixel.\n- `panelCount: number` — default `7`\n  Emitting panels on the ring. Odd counts keep bright and dark panels from pairing off across the object.\n- `panelRadius: number` — default `4.2`\n  Ring radius in world units.\n- `spinSpeed: number` — default `0.18`\n  Panel ring rotation, radians per second. This is what makes the reflection move.\n- `objectSpin: number` — default `0.32`\n  Reflector rotation about Y, radians per second. X spins at 0.55 of this.\n- `resolution: number` — default `256`\n  Cube face size in pixels. Cost is six faces plus mips.\n- `refreshEvery: number` — default `2`\n  Capture the cube map every Nth rendered frame. 1 captures every frame.\n- `roughness: number` — default `0.08`\n  Surface roughness, which selects the env map mip level.\n- `metalness: number` — default `1`\n  1 is a mirror; 0 is diffuse and lit only by the env map.\n- `envMapIntensity: number` — default `1.4`\n  Multiplier on reflected light.\n- `hue: number` — default `208`\n  Base hue in degrees for the panels and the shell gradient.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RealtimeCubeEnvmap({\n  shape = 'knot',\n  panelCount = 7,\n  panelRadius = 4.2,\n  spinSpeed = 0.18,\n  objectSpin = 0.32,\n  resolution = 256,\n  refreshEvery = 2,\n  roughness = 0.08,\n  metalness = 1,\n  envMapIntensity = 1.4,\n  hue = 208,\n  className,\n}: RealtimeCubeEnvmapProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: false });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(FIELD_OF_VIEW, 1, 0.1, 60);\n    camera.position.set(0, 0.35, BASE_DISTANCE);\n    camera.lookAt(0, 0, 0);\n\n    // The shell is the environment's floor and sky in one object: a sphere\n    // turned inside out. It is what the cube camera sees in every direction\n    // the panels do not cover, so without it the reflection has large holes of\n    // clear-colour black and the object reads as unlit rather than dark.\n    const shellTexture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(makeShellTexture(hue));\n    shellTexture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    const shellGeometry = new THREE.SphereGeometry(16, 32, 16);\n    const shellMaterial = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({\n      map: shellTexture,\n      side: THREE.BackSide,\n    });\n    scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(shellGeometry, shellMaterial));\n\n    // Panels. Their arrangement is a pure function of the index — there is no\n    // random scatter here, so there is no seed to keep in step between the\n    // server render and the client one.\n    const ring = new THREE.Group();\n    const panelGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1.5, 3.4);\n    const panelMaterials: THREE.MeshBasicMaterial[] = [];\n    const count = Math.max(2, Math.round(panelCount));\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ side: THREE.DoubleSide });\n      // Alternating bright and near-black panels. An environment that is\n      // uniformly bright gives a mirror nothing to contrast against and the\n      // metal reads as matte plastic; the dark panels are what draw the\n      // specular edges.\n      const bright = i % 2 === 0;\n      material.color.setHSL(\n        ((hue + i * 26) % 360) / 360,\n        bright ? 0.66 : 0.4,\n        bright ? 0.62 : 0.08,\n      );\n      panelMaterials.push(material);\n\n      const panel = new THREE.Mesh(panelGeometry, material);\n      const angle = (i / count) * Math.PI * 2;\n      // Golden-angle stagger on the height, so the panels do not sit in a\n      // single band and the reflection has vertical structure to move through.\n      const lift = Math.sin(i * 2.39996) * 0.9;\n      panel.position.set(\n        Math.sin(angle) * panelRadius,\n        lift,\n        Math.cos(angle) * panelRadius,\n      );\n      panel.lookAt(0, 0, 0);\n      ring.add(panel);\n    }\n    scene.add(ring);\n\n    const reflectorGeometry =\n      shape === 'knot'\n        ? new THREE.TorusKnotGeometry(0.92, 0.31, 220, 32)\n        : new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.35, 2);\n\n    // Mipmaps are not optional here. `roughness` selects a mip level of the\n    // env map, so a cube target without them samples level 0 at every\n    // roughness and the surface stays a hard mirror no matter what you pass.\n    const cubeTarget = new THREE.WebGLCubeRenderTarget(Math.max(16, Math.round(resolution)), {\n      generateMipmaps: true,\n      minFilter: THREE.LinearMipmapLinearFilter,\n    });\n    const cubeCamera = new THREE.CubeCamera(0.1, 60, cubeTarget);\n    // Co-located with the reflector: an env map is sampled by direction, so\n    // the capture point is the only position information it carries. Offset it\n    // and the reflections belong to a different object.\n    cubeCamera.position.set(0, 0, 0);\n    scene.add(cubeCamera);\n\n    const material = new THREE.MeshPhysicalMaterial({\n      metalness,\n      roughness,\n      envMapIntensity,\n      // Assigned to the material rather than `scene.environment` on purpose.\n      // A live capture belongs to one object at one point in space; sharing it\n      // scene-wide would give every reflector the same viewpoint.\n      envMap: cubeTarget.texture,\n      color: 0xffffff,\n    });\n    const reflector = new THREE.Mesh(reflectorGeometry, material);\n    scene.add(reflector);\n\n    const throttle = Math.max(1, Math.round(refreshEvery));\n    let frames = 0;\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number, forceCapture: boolean) => {\n      ring.rotation.y = elapsed * spinSpeed;\n      reflector.rotation.y = elapsed * objectSpin;\n      reflector.rotation.x = elapsed * objectSpin * 0.55;\n\n      frames += 1;\n      if (forceCapture || frames % throttle === 0) {\n        // Hide, capture, show — in that order, inside one frame. Skipping the\n        // hide is the classic version of this bug: the object captures itself\n        // and within a second the surface is a recursive tunnel.\n        reflector.visible = false;\n        cubeCamera.update(renderer, scene);\n        reflector.visible = true;\n      }\n\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      if (w < 1 || h < 1) return;\n      const aspect = w / h;\n      renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.aspect = aspect;\n      // Pull the camera back on narrow containers. A fixed distance frames the\n      // object on height alone, so a portrait preview clips the panel ring off\n      // both sides and the reflections lose their source.\n      camera.position.z = BASE_DISTANCE / Math.min(1, aspect);\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, cubeTarget, resize, draw };\n    // First frame forces a capture: with an empty cube map a metal surface is\n    // pure black, and on a throttled refresh that black would be on screen for\n    // `refreshEvery` frames.\n    draw(STATIC_POSE, true);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      scene.remove(cubeCamera);\n      // Order matters: free the scene graph's GPU objects while the context is\n      // still alive, then hand the context itself back.\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      shellTexture.dispose();\n      shellGeometry.dispose();\n      shellMaterial.dispose();\n      panelGeometry.dispose();\n      for (const panelMaterial of panelMaterials) panelMaterial.dispose();\n      reflectorGeometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      // The cube target is not in the scene graph, so nothing above reaches\n      // it. It holds six faces plus their mip chain — at 256px that is about\n      // 2 MB of GPU memory per instance.\n      cubeTarget.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    shape,\n    panelCount,\n    panelRadius,\n    spinSpeed,\n    objectSpin,\n    resolution,\n    refreshEvery,\n    roughness,\n    metalness,\n    envMapIntensity,\n    hue,\n  ]);\n\n  // Resize in its own effect: a container reflow costs a `setSize` and one\n  // redraw rather than a rebuild of the geometry and the cube target.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current) return;\n    current.resize(width, height);\n    current.draw(STATIC_POSE, true);\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.draw(elapsed, false),\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `disposeObject(scene)` walks the graph, then the shell canvas texture, shell and panel geometries, every panel material and the reflector material are disposed explicitly, then `cubeTarget.dispose()` releases the six faces and their mip chain, then `disposeRenderer(renderer)` calls `dispose()` and `forceContextLoss()` and zeroes the backing store, and the canvas node is removed. The `CubeCamera` is removed from the scene first so nothing holds a reference to a disposed target.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF subscription is never made, and the scene is drawn once at `STATIC_POSE` with a forced cube capture — a fully reflective still with the panels off the cardinal axes. Reflections, gradient shell and material are all exactly as they are in motion; only the rotation is absent. Resizing redraws the same pose rather than resuming.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Capturing with the reflector visible. It photographs its own previous frame, the next capture includes that photograph, and within about a second the surface is a recursive tunnel of dark shells. Hide it, capture, show it — inside the same frame, so it is never absent from the visible render.\n- Creating the cube target without mipmaps. `roughness` picks a mip level of the env map; with only level 0 available the surface stays a hard mirror at every roughness value and the prop appears to do nothing.\n- Refreshing the cube map every frame because it looks like the honest thing to do. That is seven scene renders per displayed frame, and it is the difference between a preview grid that holds 60fps and one that does not.\n- Rendering the first frame without forcing a capture. An unwritten cube map is black, a metal surface reflecting black is black, and on a throttled refresh that black sits on screen for `refreshEvery` frames as a visible flash.\n- Building the environment out of bright panels only. Metal is read entirely from the contrast between light and dark in its surroundings; with no dark panels the highlights have no edges and the material looks like painted plastic.\n- Letting `disposeObject(scene)` stand in for cube-target cleanup. The target is not a child of anything, so nothing traverses it, and six faces plus mips leak per mount — which under StrictMode double-invocation is two leaks per page load.\n- Leaving the camera distance fixed. Framing on height alone means a portrait container crops the panel ring away entirely, and the object loses the thing it is reflecting.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No lights in the scene. `metalness: 1` with `roughness` near 0 is lit purely by its environment, so a light would contribute almost nothing while adding a uniform to every shader compile.\n- `transmission` is deliberately not exposed. Three renders a separate full-scene pass per transmissive object, which on top of six cube faces triples the frame cost for an effect the cube map already implies.\n- The panel arrangement is a pure function of the index, so there is no RNG and nothing to seed — server and client markup cannot disagree.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe bright bands on the object crawl across its surface continuously while it turns, and the same colours are visible on the panels orbiting behind it. There is no dark recursive tunnel anywhere on the surface at any moment. Raising `roughness` toward 0.3 progressively blurs the reflection instead of leaving it a hard mirror. Resize the container and the object stays fully in frame in both landscape and portrait. Mount and unmount a dozen instances and none of the earlier ones go black.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "recent-hires-card": "# Build: Recent Hires Card\n\nA grey dashboard card listing the newest people to join, newest start date first. The header pairs the title with a team switcher (a kit Dropdown) and shows the headline count with an optional \"View all\" link. Hires render as white tiles in a grid that goes from one to two columns once the card itself is wider than 28rem, with Previous / Next buttons paging through them. Team and page each work controlled or uncontrolled.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA recent hires card: team switcher, headline count and a paged grid of tiles with initials, status, start date and role.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Resolve `team` and `page` through a small controllable-state helper: a defined prop wins, otherwise local state seeded by `defaultTeam` / `defaultPage` holds the value, and the change callback fires either way.\n2. Filter `hires` by `team` (null keeps every team) and sort by `startDate` descending with `CalendarDate.compare`, memoised on `[hires, team]`.\n3. Compute `pageCount = max(1, ceil(visible.length / pageSize))` and clamp the requested page into `[1, pageCount]` before slicing, so a team switch that shrinks the list never leaves the grid on an empty page. Choosing a team also resets the page to 1 and closes the menu.\n4. Each tile computes `today.compare(startDate)` (days since start) and passes it to `formatRelative`, which defaults to English captions: \"Joined today\", \"Joined yesterday\", \"N days ago\", \"A week ago\", \"N weeks ago\", then months; negative values read \"Starts tomorrow\" / \"Starts in N days\".\n5. Format the start date with a memoised `DateFormatter(locale, { month: \"short\", day: \"numeric\", year: \"numeric\", timeZone: \"UTC\" })` over `startDate.toDate(\"UTC\")`, so the server and the browser print the same day whatever the viewer's time zone.\n6. Key the `<ul>` on `team-page`, so each page mounts fresh tiles; the tiles enter with a 200ms `starting:` opacity + 4px rise, and `motion-reduce:transition-none` removes it.\n7. Announce the visible range (\"Showing 5–8 of 12\") through an `sr-only` `aria-live=\"polite\"` paragraph, and disable Previous / Next at the ends.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pageSize = 4`\n  Two rows of the two-column grid, the density of the reference card; the card stays short enough to sit beside a chart.\n- `two-column breakpoint = @md (container 28rem)`\n  A container query, not a viewport one, so the card reflows by its own width when it sits in a narrow dashboard column.\n- `tile enter = 200ms ease-out, opacity 0 → 1, translate-y 4px → 0`\n  Short enough to read as a page change rather than a show; it runs only on mount through @starting-style.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype Hire = { id: string; name: string; role: string; startDate: CalendarDate; status: \"active\" | \"onboarding\" | \"pending\"; team?: string; initials?: string; avatarColor?: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\" }\nfunction RecentHiresCard(props: RecentHiresCardProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `hires: Hire[]`\n  Every hire; the card filters, sorts and pages them.\n- `today: CalendarDate`\n  Reference day for the relative caption under each name.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Recent hires\"`\n  Header label and the section's accessible name.\n- `total: number | ((visible: Hire[]) => number)`\n  Headline number. Defaults to the number of hires in the selected team.\n- `teams: { value: string | null; label: string }[]`\n  Team switcher options. A `null` value means every team. Omit to hide the switcher.\n- `team / defaultTeam / onTeamChange: string | null` — default `null`\n  Selected team, controlled or uncontrolled.\n- `pageSize: number` — default `4`\n  Tiles per page.\n- `page / defaultPage / onPageChange: number` — default `1`\n  One-based page, controlled or uncontrolled. Out-of-range values are clamped.\n- `viewAllHref: string`\n  Renders \"View all\" as a link.\n- `onViewAll: () => void`\n  Renders \"View all\" as a button when no href is given.\n- `viewAllLabel: string` — default `\"View all\"`\n  Link text.\n- `statusLabels: Partial<Record<HireStatus, string>>`\n  Overrides the status captions.\n- `formatRelative: (daysSinceStart: number) => string` — default `relativeStart`\n  Replaces the English relative caption; negative values are future starts.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for the start date and the headline number.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Filter by team, newest start first, then slice the page. The page is\n// clamped rather than trusted, so a team switch that shrinks the list can\n// never leave the grid on an empty page.\nconst visible = useMemo(\n  () =>\n    hires\n      .filter((h) => team == null || h.team === team)\n      .sort((a, b) => b.startDate.compare(a.startDate)),\n  [hires, team],\n);\nconst pageCount = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(visible.length / pageSize));\nconst current = Math.min(Math.max(1, page), pageCount);\nconst start = (current - 1) * pageSize;\nconst slice = visible.slice(start, start + pageSize);\nconst headline = typeof total === \"function\" ? total(visible) : (total ?? visible.length);\nconst teamLabel = teams?.find((t) => t.value === team)?.label ?? teams?.[0]?.label;\n\nconst chooseTeam = (next: string | null) => {\n  setTeam(next);\n  setPage(1);\n  setMenuOpen(false);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. There are no timers, listeners or observers; the dropdown popover is managed by react-aria and unmounts with the card.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 200ms tile enter and the chevron flip. Both carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` pages swap instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Pass `today` from a fixed value (for example `parseDate(\"2026-11-05\")` or a server-computed date). Calling `today(getLocalTimeZone())` during render can differ between server and client and cause a hydration mismatch.\n- Give each hire a stable `id`. The tile key uses it, and duplicates break the enter transition and React reconciliation.\n- In controlled mode, update `team` and `page` together: choosing a team calls `onTeamChange` and then `onPageChange(1)`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The avatar colour comes from a hash of the name over the kit Avatar colours (blue, lime, pink, neutral) unless `avatarColor` is set, so a person keeps one colour across renders.\n- Without `teams`, the switcher is hidden. Without `viewAllHref` or `onViewAll`, the \"View all\" link is hidden.\n- Status labels default to \"Active\", \"Onboarding\" and \"Starts soon\"; override them with `statusLabels`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows the title, the team switcher and the headline count, with four tiles newest first. Each tile has an initials avatar, the name, a relative caption, a status dot with its label, the start date and a role pill. Next and Previous page through the list and disable at the ends. Choosing a team from the menu filters the tiles, updates the count and returns to page 1. Escape closes the menu. Below 28rem of card width the grid is a single column. Light and dark both read correctly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "rekey-caption-replay": "# Build: Re-Key Caption Replay\n\nA wrapper that replays a staggered entrance every time its `entryKey` changes. It does it by being a different node: React unmounts the old caption and mounts a new one, and a freshly mounted element starts its CSS animation at frame zero. No forced reflow to restart the animation, no tween to kill, and a fast prev/next cannot leave the block half-entered because the half-entered node no longer exists.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA caption block whose staggered entrance replays on every index change, restarted by a changed React key.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The children are wrapped in a single `<div key={entryKey}>`. Changing `entryKey` makes React discard that subtree and mount a fresh one; a new element runs its `animation` from frame zero, which is the entire replay mechanism. The key goes on the wrapper, not on each child — keyed individually, only the children whose own identity changed would replay, which for a caption is usually none of them.\n2. One keyframe, `kinetic-rekey-in`, runs `opacity: 0` and `translateY(var(--kx-rekey-from))` to `opacity: 1` and `translateY(0)` over 0.5 s on `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`. Because the offset is a custom property rather than a literal, flipping its sign on the root turns a rise into a drop — both directions from one set of keyframes.\n3. The stagger is a generated ladder of `:nth-child(n) { animation-delay: calc(var(--kx-rekey-stagger) * (n - 1)) }` rules, built once at module scope for the first eight children. A ninth-and-beyond rule pins the tail to the eighth delay, so an overlong block degrades to \"the tail arrives together\" rather than \"the tail arrives first at delay zero\".\n4. `animation-fill-mode: both` is what makes the delay usable. Without it a delayed line is painted at its normal full-opacity style for the length of its own delay, then snaps to the from-frame to begin animating — every line flashes before it enters.\n5. The stylesheet is emitted once through React 19's `<style href precedence>` hoisting, so duration, stagger and offset are read from CSS custom properties set on the wrapper. Ten captions on a page share one rule block and differ only in their variables.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `duration = 0.5`\n  Seconds per line. With the 0.06 s stagger, a four-line block finishes in 0.68 s — under the ~0.7 s where a caption change starts to feel like waiting. Halve it and the ease has no room to decelerate, so the rise reads as a jump.\n- `distance = 14`\n  Pixels travelled. Roughly one line of leading at caption size: enough for the eye to register direction, small enough that the text is never far from where it lands. Past ~30 px the block reads as flying in from off-panel.\n- `stagger = 0.06`\n  Seconds between lines. At 0 the block is one object; past ~0.12 s the metadata is still arriving after the title has settled, which reads as a slow network rather than a sequence.\n- `ease = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  An out-quart. It covers about 80% of the distance in the first third of the duration, so the line appears to arrive fast and then settle — which is what hides the fact that nothing is actually decelerating.\n- `MAX_STAGGERED_LINES = 8`\n  How many nth-child rules are generated. Eight covers eyebrow, title, two metadata rows and a counter with room spare. The number exists because CSS cannot do arithmetic on the child index, so the ladder has to be finite.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RekeyCaptionReplay(props: { entryKey: string | number; children: ReactNode; duration?: number; distance?: number; stagger?: number; direction?: \"up\" | \"down\"; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `entryKey: string | number`\n  Identity of the current caption. Any change replays the entrance.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Direct children each get their own delay, in source order.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.5`\n  Seconds one line's entrance lasts.\n- `distance: number` — default `14`\n  Pixels a line travels. Signed by `direction`.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.06`\n  Seconds between successive lines. 0 makes the block arrive as one piece.\n- `direction: \"up\" | \"down\"` — default `\"up\"`\n  `up` enters from below; `down` enters from above, for a step backward.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the keyed wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * How many direct children get their own delay. Eight covers eyebrow, title,\n * two metadata rows and a counter with room to spare; children past the eighth\n * are pinned to the eighth's delay rather than falling back to zero, so an\n * overlong block degrades to \"the tail arrives together\" instead of \"the tail\n * arrives first\".\n */\nconst MAX_STAGGERED_LINES = 8;\n\nconst STAGGER_RULES = [\n  ...Array.from(\n    { length: MAX_STAGGERED_LINES },\n    (_, i) =>\n      `.kinetic-rekey > :nth-child(${i + 1}) { animation-delay: calc(var(--kx-rekey-stagger, 0.06s) * ${i}); }`,\n  ),\n  `.kinetic-rekey > :nth-child(n + ${MAX_STAGGERED_LINES + 1}) { animation-delay: calc(var(--kx-rekey-stagger, 0.06s) * ${MAX_STAGGERED_LINES - 1}); }`,\n].join('\\n');\n\nconst REKEY_CSS = `\n@keyframes kinetic-rekey-in {\n  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(var(--kx-rekey-from, 14px)); }\n  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }\n}\n/* One keyframe covers both directions: the offset is a custom property, so\n   flipping its sign on the root turns a rise into a drop without a second\n   set of keyframes to keep in sync. */\n.kinetic-rekey > * {\n  animation: var(--kx-rekey-duration, 0.5s) cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)\n    both kinetic-rekey-in;\n}\n${STAGGER_RULES}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  /* The still state IS the destination: full opacity, no offset. Dropping the\n     animation re-exposes those as the base values, which is why there is\n     nothing to declare here beyond 'none'. A zero-duration animation would\n     instead hold whatever 'both' resolved and still fire animationend. */\n  .kinetic-rekey > * { animation: none; }\n}\n`;\n\nexport interface RekeyCaptionReplayProps {\n  /**\n   * Identity of the current caption. Any change replays the entrance — in a\n   * slideshow this is the slide index, in a filter it is the active facet.\n   */\n  entryKey: string | number;\n  /** Direct children each get their own delay, in source order. */\n  children: ReactNode;\n  /** Seconds one line's entrance lasts. */\n  duration?: number;\n  /** Pixels a line travels. Signed by `direction`. */\n  distance?: number;\n  /** Seconds between successive lines. 0 makes the block arrive as one piece. */\n  stagger?: number;\n  /**\n   * `up` enters from below — the default, and correct for advancing forward.\n   * `down` enters from above, which is what makes a step backward read as a\n   * step backward rather than as another step forward.\n   */\n  direction?: 'up' | 'down';\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function RekeyCaptionReplay({\n  entryKey,\n  children,\n  duration = 0.5,\n  distance = 14,\n  stagger = 0.06,\n  direction = 'up',\n  className,\n}: RekeyCaptionReplayProps) {\n  const from = direction === 'up' ? distance : -distance;\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-rekey-caption-replay\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {REKEY_CSS}\n      </style>\n\n      {/* The key is on this wrapper, so every child is a new node on every\n          change and every child animation restarts. Putting the key on the\n          children individually would replay only the ones whose own identity\n          changed, which for a caption is usually none of them. */}\n      <div\n        key={entryKey}\n        className={cn('kinetic-rekey', className)}\n        style={\n          {\n            '--kx-rekey-duration': `${duration}s`,\n            '--kx-rekey-stagger': `${stagger}s`,\n            '--kx-rekey-from': `${from}px`,\n          } as CSSProperties\n        }\n      >\n        {children}\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. There are no listeners, no timers, no rAF and no tweens — the animation is CSS and the nodes running it are removed by React itself on every key change.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `animation: none` on the children, which re-exposes their base styles — full opacity, no offset. The caption is fully rendered on the first frame after the key change, in its final position. This is the one case where dropping the animation is the correct still state rather than a cop-out, because the from-frame is the only thing the animation adds.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Restarting the animation by toggling a class off and on. The browser coalesces both mutations into one style recalculation and the animation never restarts, which is why that approach always ends up with a `void el.offsetWidth` forced reflow wedged between the two lines.\n- Tweening the caption with an animation library instead. A fast prev/next leaves two opposing tweens on the same node and the text jitters between them; a remount cannot have that problem because the contended node is gone.\n- Omitting `animation-fill-mode: both`. Every delayed line paints at full opacity for the length of its delay, then snaps to invisible to start animating. The block flickers, and it flickers worse the larger the stagger.\n- Putting the key on the individual lines. Their identities usually do not change between captions, so React reuses the nodes and nothing replays — the most common way this technique silently does nothing.\n- Wrapping anything focusable or stateful. The remount destroys DOM state and moves focus to the body: an input inside this loses its caret and its value on every key change. This is for display content only.\n- Animating both directions with a second keyframe block. The two drift apart the first time someone tunes the offset. One keyframe reading a signed custom property cannot.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pairs with a counter badge and prev/next controls to make a slideshow caption, which is where the technique was observed. The same wrapper works for a filter result header, a hovered-row detail panel, or a step label in a multi-step form summary.\n- Direction is a prop rather than something derived internally, because only the caller knows whether an index change was forward, backward, or a jump from a thumbnail strip.\n- For the media beside the caption, a CSS `transition` on the background beats a tween: an index change is a discrete event with one start and one end state, which is exactly what a transition is for.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nStep forward and the eyebrow, title and metadata rise into place one after another, roughly 60 ms apart. Step backward and they drop in from above instead. Click next four times as fast as you can and every caption plays its full entrance from the start with no jitter or stuck line. With reduced motion the new caption is simply there, complete, on the first frame.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "relaxed-seeded-scatter": "# Build: Relaxed Seeded Scatter\n\nHalf the points sit on an exact lattice and half are seeded noise; fifteen relaxation sweeps then push the noisy ones apart until no neighbour pair is closer than 40% of the mean spacing. The result keeps the liveliness of random placement without the clusters that read as a bug. The visualiser leaves a dashed ghost at each point's pre-relaxation angle, so the displacement is legible, and prints the smallest gap before and after.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSeeded angles around a ring, pushed apart until nothing collides. Ghost ticks mark where they started.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `floor(count * evenRatio)` points are written at `(i / fixedCount) * 2PI` and flagged fixed; the remainder get `rng() * 2PI` from `createRng(seed)`, a mulberry32 stream. The raw array is copied before anything moves so the two states can be compared and interpolated.\n2. The target gap is `(2PI / count) * 0.4` — 40% of the mean spacing on the full ring, not on the random subset, so the floor means the same thing regardless of how many points are pinned.\n3. Each sweep sorts the nodes by `mod(angle, 2PI)`, then walks consecutive pairs measuring `gap = mod(next.angle - current.angle, 2PI)`. Because `mod` always measures the short way round, the pair that straddles zero needs no special case.\n4. When a gap is under target, each free endpoint is moved by `(target - gap) * 0.5` away from the other; if one endpoint is pinned to the lattice the free one absorbs the whole `(target - gap)` instead, or points pile up against the lattice and never separate.\n5. Angles are stored unwrapped — a point pushed below zero keeps a small negative value rather than jumping to just under 2PI — so the component can interpolate `raw -> relaxed` linearly and never take the long way round. Only the sort and the gap measurement wrap.\n6. The visualiser eases that interpolation over 1.4 s with `easeOutQuart` on the shared rAF loop, writing the `transform` attribute of one `<g>` per point. The SVG uses a `-50 -50 100 100` viewBox, so it resizes with its container and needs no ResizeObserver.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `RELAX_PASSES = 15`\n  Each sweep removes about half the remaining overlap, so 15 leaves 2^-15 of the initial error — fully converged for any count up to ~64. At 3 passes the tightest pairs are still visibly paired; past ~20 nothing measurable changes and you are paying O(passes · n log n) for nothing.\n- `MIN_GAP_RATIO = 0.4`\n  Floor on the neighbour gap as a fraction of the mean, so `targetGap = 0.4 * 2PI / count`. Below ~0.25 clusters survive and the scatter still reads as clumped. Above ~0.6 the relaxation cannot satisfy every pair at once, so it oscillates between passes and converges toward the lattice — which throws away the randomness you asked for.\n- `EVEN_RATIO = 0.5`\n  Fraction pinned to the exact lattice. At 0 the composition has no skeleton and the eye finds no structure; at 1 it is a clock face. Half and half is the mix that reads as deliberate but not mechanical.\n- `PUSH_SHARE = 0.5`\n  Each free neighbour absorbs half the deficit, so a pair separates by exactly the deficit per sweep. At 1.0 both move the full amount, overshoot, and the pair oscillates instead of settling.\n- `MORPH_DURATION = 1.4`\n  Seconds for the raw-to-relaxed sweep. Long enough to follow individual points leaving their ghosts; under ~0.6 s it reads as a single cut and the comparison is lost.\n- `seed = 'kinetic'`\n  Any fixed value works — what matters is that it is fixed. `hashSeed` turns the string into a 32-bit integer, so a named seed per instance gives every ring its own reproducible composition.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction relaxedRingAngles(count: number, seed: number | string, options?: { passes?: number; minGapRatio?: number; evenRatio?: number; pushShare?: number }): RelaxedRing;\nfunction RelaxedSeededScatter(props: { count?: number; seed?: number | string; passes?: number; minGapRatio?: number; evenRatio?: number; morph?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `24`\n  Points on the ring.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'kinetic'`\n  Fixed seed. Strings are hashed with `hashSeed`.\n- `passes: number` — default `15`\n  Relaxation sweeps.\n- `minGapRatio: number` — default `0.4`\n  Minimum neighbour gap as a fraction of the mean spacing.\n- `evenRatio: number` — default `0.5`\n  Fraction of points pinned to an exact lattice.\n- `morph: boolean` — default `true`\n  Ease from the raw angles to the relaxed ones on first view.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Sweeps. Each halves the residual overlap; 15 is fully converged for n<=64. */\nconst RELAX_PASSES = 15;\n/** Floor on the neighbour gap, as a fraction of the mean gap. */\nconst MIN_GAP_RATIO = 0.4;\n/** Half the points sit on an exact lattice and never move. */\nconst EVEN_RATIO = 0.5;\n/** Each free neighbour absorbs half the deficit. */\nconst PUSH_SHARE = 0.5;\n\ntype Node = { id: number; angle: number; fixed: boolean };\n\n/** Smallest neighbour gap on the circle, and how many pairs are under `target`. */\nfunction ringStats(values: ArrayLike<number>, target: number) {\n  const n = values.length;\n  if (n < 2) return { minGap: TAU, crowded: 0 };\n  const sorted = Array.from(values, (v) => mod(v, TAU)).sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  let minGap = TAU;\n  let crowded = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const a = sorted[i] ?? 0;\n    const b = sorted[(i + 1) % n] ?? 0;\n    const gap = i === n - 1 ? b + TAU - a : b - a;\n    if (gap < minGap) minGap = gap;\n    if (gap < target) crowded++;\n  }\n  return { minGap, crowded };\n}\n\nexport function relaxedRingAngles(\n  count: number,\n  seed: number | string,\n  options: RelaxedRingOptions = {},\n): RelaxedRing {\n  const {\n    passes = RELAX_PASSES,\n    minGapRatio = MIN_GAP_RATIO,\n    evenRatio = EVEN_RATIO,\n    pushShare = PUSH_SHARE,\n  } = options;\n\n  const n = Math.max(0, Math.floor(count));\n  const angles = new Float32Array(n);\n  const raw = new Float32Array(n);\n  const targetGap = n > 0 ? (TAU / n) * minGapRatio : 0;\n  const fixedCount = Math.floor(n * evenRatio);\n\n  if (n === 0) {\n    return { angles, raw, minGap: 0, rawMinGap: 0, crowded: 0, rawCrowded: 0, targetGap, fixedCount };\n  }\n\n  // Half order: an exact lattice, so the composition has a skeleton.\n  for (let i = 0; i < fixedCount; i++) angles[i] = (i / fixedCount) * TAU;\n  // Half chaos: seeded, never Math.random().\n  const rng = createRng(seed);\n  for (let i = fixedCount; i < n; i++) angles[i] = rng() * TAU;\n  raw.set(angles);\n\n  const nodes: Node[] = Array.from({ length: n }, (unused, id) => ({\n    id,\n    angle: angles[id] ?? 0,\n    fixed: id < fixedCount,\n  }));\n\n  for (let pass = 0; pass < passes && n > 1; pass++) {\n    // Re-sort every sweep. A push can carry a point across zero, which\n    // re-orders the ring; a gap computed from stale neighbours pushes the\n    // wrong point and leaves a permanent clump at the wrap.\n    nodes.sort((a, b) => mod(a.angle, TAU) - mod(b.angle, TAU));\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      const a = nodes[i];\n      const b = nodes[(i + 1) % n];\n      if (!a || !b || a === b) continue;\n\n      // mod() measures the gap the short way round, so the pair that straddles\n      // zero needs no special case.\n      const gap = mod(b.angle - a.angle, TAU);\n      if (gap >= targetGap) continue;\n\n      const deficit = (targetGap - gap) * pushShare;\n      if (a.fixed && b.fixed) continue;\n      // A pinned neighbour cannot absorb its share, so the free one takes all\n      // of it. Otherwise points pile up against the lattice and never separate.\n      if (a.fixed) b.angle += deficit * 2;\n      else if (b.fixed) a.angle -= deficit * 2;\n      else {\n        a.angle -= deficit;\n        b.angle += deficit;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (const node of nodes) angles[node.id] = node.angle;\n\n  const after = ringStats(angles, targetGap);\n  const before = ringStats(raw, targetGap);\n\n  return {\n    angles,\n    raw,\n    minGap: after.minGap,\n    rawMinGap: before.minGap,\n    crowded: after.crowded,\n    rawCrowded: before.crowded,\n    targetGap,\n    fixedCount,\n  };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes on unmount and the shared frame is cancelled when the last subscriber leaves; the loop also unsubscribes itself the moment the morph completes, so a settled ring costs nothing. `useInView` unobserves the root. No timers, no listeners, no retained typed arrays beyond the memo.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The relaxed angles render immediately with no sweep. The dashed ghosts at the raw angles stay, so the before/after comparison the component exists to make is fully readable without anything moving, and the gap readout is unchanged.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- `Math.random()` instead of a seeded generator. The server renders one arrangement and the client another, React reports a hydration mismatch and re-renders the subtree, and the layout is different on every reload so no visual diff is ever reviewable.\n- Not re-sorting between sweeps. A push can carry a point across zero, which re-orders the ring; the next sweep then measures the gap to a point that is no longer the neighbour, pushes the wrong one, and leaves a permanent clump sitting exactly on the wrap.\n- Measuring the gap as `next - current`. For the pair that straddles zero that is a large negative number, which is always below the target, so the relaxation shoves those two apart on every sweep forever and one point walks all the way around the ring.\n- Splitting the deficit evenly when one neighbour is pinned. The free point only ever gets half the correction it needs, so it converges to half the required gap and stays visibly stuck to the lattice tick.\n- Wrapping the angles into `[0, 2PI)` on the way out. The interpolation from `raw` to `angles` then sends any point that crossed zero the long way round the ring, and one tick sweeps a full circle during the settle while the rest move a few degrees.\n- Rebuilding the ring on every render. `relaxedRingAngles` is O(passes · n log n) and allocates; without `useMemo` keyed on the inputs, every parent render re-runs 15 sweeps and re-allocates two typed arrays.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Relaxing over the full ring with the lattice pinned is a deliberate deviation from the version this was taken from, which relaxed only the random subset against itself. That version lets a random point land exactly on a lattice point, which is the one collision the technique is supposed to prevent.\n- `relaxedRingAngles` is the shipped part. Feed the angles into whatever you are placing — CSS `rotate()`, an instanced attribute, a `THREE.Vector3` per item — the visualiser is only here to show what the numbers do.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn first scroll into view the solid ticks slide off their dashed ghosts and stop; every accent tick stays exactly on its ghost because the lattice is pinned. The \"min gap\" readout shows a struck-through small number followed by a larger one at or above the target. Reload ten times and the arrangement is byte-identical. Set `evenRatio` to 0 and every tick moves.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "repo-tree-nav": "# Build: Repo Tree Nav\n\nA file tree for agent and code-review sidebars. Nested folders open and close with the pointer, Enter or the arrow keys; files show an icon picked from their extension, green/red added and removed line counts and an A/M/D/R status letter. Only files can be selected. A collapsed folder rolls up the counts of every changed file inside it, so the totals never disappear when a branch is closed.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nRepository file tree with folder expand/collapse, file-type icons, single file selection and +/− line counts per file.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Rendered on the kit TreeView (React Aria Tree): treegrid semantics, Up/Down/Home/End, typeahead, Right/Left to expand, collapse or jump to the parent, and the animated row height on expand.\n2. Folder ids go into disabledKeys with disabledBehavior=\"selection\": folders stay focusable and expandable but can never become the selection, and a press on a folder row runs its onAction, which toggles that id in the expanded set.\n3. Files are selected through React Aria single selection (aria-selected), mapped back to a node through an id lookup built in the same memoised pass that collects folder ids.\n4. The same pass stores summarizeChanges(children) per folder; a folder shows that roll-up only while collapsed, when its files are hidden.\n5. Both selection (selectedId/defaultSelectedId/onSelect) and expansion (expandedIds/defaultExpandedIds/onExpandedChange) are controlled-or-uncontrolled.\n6. Counts are drawn aria-hidden and repeated in an sr-only phrase (\"3 changed files, 156 additions, 23 deletions\"), and the status letter has an sr-only word.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `row height = 32px (size=\"sm\")`\n  Matches dense sidebar lists; md (36px) is available for roomier panels.\n- `status colours = status-lime / status-orange / status-rose / status-blue`\n  Theme tokens with separate dark values, so added, modified, deleted and renamed stay legible on both surfaces.\n- `status chip = 16px, caption-2 semibold`\n  One letter fits without widening the trailing column, keeping counts aligned.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RepoTreeNav(props: { nodes: RepoTreeNode[]; selectedId?: string | null; defaultSelectedId?: string | null; onSelect?: (id: string, node: RepoTreeNode) => void; expandedIds?: Iterable<string>; defaultExpandedIds?: Iterable<string>; onExpandedChange?: (ids: Set<string>) => void; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; showGuides?: boolean; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `nodes: RepoTreeNode[]`\n  { id, name, children?, additions?, deletions?, status? }; a node with children is a folder.\n- `selectedId: string | null`\n  Controlled selected file id.\n- `defaultSelectedId: string | null` — default `null`\n  Initial selected file when uncontrolled.\n- `onSelect: (id: string, node: RepoTreeNode) => void`\n  Called when a different file is chosen.\n- `expandedIds: Iterable<string>`\n  Controlled expanded folder ids.\n- `defaultExpandedIds: Iterable<string>` — default `[]`\n  Initially expanded folders when uncontrolled.\n- `onExpandedChange: (ids: Set<string>) => void`\n  Called with the full expanded set after any toggle.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"sm\"`\n  Row density.\n- `showGuides: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draw the branch guide lines beside nested rows.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Files\"`\n  Accessible name of the tree.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes for the tree surface.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// One pass over the tree: folder ids (never selectable, so a press on a\n// folder row runs its toggle action instead of selecting it), a lookup for\n// onSelect, and rolled-up counts shown on folders while they are collapsed.\nconst { folderIds, byId, totals } = useMemo(() => {\n  const folderIds: string[] = [];\n  const byId = new Map<string, RepoTreeNode>();\n  const totals = new Map<string, RepoChangeSummary>();\n  const visit = (node: RepoTreeNode) => {\n    byId.set(node.id, node);\n    if (!node.children) return;\n    folderIds.push(node.id);\n    node.children.forEach(visit);\n    totals.set(node.id, summarizeChanges(node.children));\n  };\n  nodes.forEach(visit);\n  return { folderIds, byId, totals };\n}, [nodes]);\n\nconst setExpanded = (next: Set<string>) => {\n  setUncontrolledExpanded(next);\n  onExpandedChange?.(next);\n};\n\nconst toggleFolder = (id: string) => {\n  const next = new Set(expanded);\n  if (next.has(id)) next.delete(id);\n  else next.add(id);\n  setExpanded(next);\n};\n\nconst select = (keys: Selection) => {\n  if (keys === \"all\") return;\n  const [key] = Array.from(keys as Set<Key>);\n  const node = key == null ? undefined : byId.get(String(key));\n  if (!node || isFolder(node) || node.id === selected) return;\n  setUncontrolledSelected(node.id);\n  onSelect?.(node.id, node);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners of its own; expansion animations belong to the TreeView rows and stop when they unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The kit TreeView reads prefers-reduced-motion and drops the height/blur expand animation to an instant swap; hover and chevron transitions use motion-reduce:transition-none.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- With selectionMode=\"single\", React Aria turns a row press into selection; without disabledKeys + disabledBehavior=\"selection\" a folder click would select the folder instead of opening it.\n- Folder detection uses children !== undefined, so pass children: [] for an empty folder or it renders as a file.\n- Ids must be unique across the whole tree, not per folder; full paths are the easy choice.\n\n## Notes\n\n- summarizeChanges(nodes) is exported for headers such as \"9 files +156 −23\", and fileIconFor(name) for matching icons elsewhere.\n- The tree grows with its content; wrap it in an overflow-y-auto container to scroll.\n- Inside a sidebar that already has a surface, pass className=\"rounded-none border-0 bg-transparent p-0\" to drop the tree card; row hover and selection still read on a primary background.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking a folder (or Enter / Right Arrow on it) shows its children and hides its roll-up count; clicking again or Left Arrow collapses it; clicking a file marks it aria-selected and fires onSelect once; folders never become selected; each changed file shows its non-zero +N / −M counts and its status letter.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "revenue-chart-card": "# Build: Revenue Chart Card\n\nA 344px dashboard card that charts twelve months of revenue with Recharts. This year is a solid line over a gradient-filled area, and last year is a dashed grey line behind it, so the gap between them is what the eye reads. The header shows the year total in dollars with a delta chip and last year's figure. Hovering a month swaps them for that month, counts the number to it and marks the point with a ringed dot.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dashboard card that draws a year of revenue as a filled area over last year's dashed line, with a hover-following count-up headline.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The chart is a `ComposedChart` with three series drawn in order: a dashed `Line` for `previous` (`strokeDasharray=\"5 5\"`, no dots), an `Area` for `current` with no stroke and a gradient fill, and a solid 2.5px `Line` for `current` on top. All three use `type=\"monotone\"` and grow in over 450ms.\n2. The area gradient is a vertical `linearGradient` in the chart's `<defs>`, from `--color-chart-2` at 0.35 opacity to 0. Its id comes from `useId()`, so two cards on one page never share or override each other's gradient.\n3. `onMouseMove` reads `state.activeTooltipIndex` and stores it in `activeIndex` when `isTooltipActive` is true and the index is finite. `onMouseLeave` clears it. A `<Tooltip content={() => null}>` keeps Recharts tracking the hovered month and draws a dashed vertical cursor (`--color-chart-cursor`, `4 4`) with no tooltip box.\n4. The current line's `activeDot` is a custom `ActiveDot`: a 7px circle at 0.25 opacity under a 4px solid circle with a 2px stroke in the card background colour, so the marker reads as a ring cut out of the line. It returns `null` until Recharts passes coordinates.\n5. The header shows the sum of `current` against the sum of `previous`, or the hovered point's pair, with the full month name as its label. `describeDelta` gives \"New\" when the comparison is 0, \"0%\" when the one-decimal change rounds to zero, otherwise \"+x%\" in lime or \"-x%\" in rose. The headline goes through `useCountUp(Math.round(headlineValue))` (320ms ease-out cubic) inside a `<p>` keyed by `activeIndex ?? \"total\"`, so `.animate-number-fade` replays on every swap.\n6. Money is formatted with `toLocaleString(\"en-US\")` at zero decimals behind a \"$\". The Y axis is 44px wide with `domain={[0, yMax * 1.1]}`, four ticks and `formatK` labels (\"$15k\"). The X axis keeps its first and last months at narrow widths through `interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `area gradient = chart-2 at stopOpacity 0.35 → 0, top to bottom`\n  Enough fill to show the area as volume without hiding the dashed last-year line behind it. Fading to 0 at the axis keeps the bottom of the chart quiet.\n- `line weights = current 2.5px solid, previous 2px dashed 5 5`\n  The heavier solid line is this year and the lighter dashed one is the reference. The difference still reads in greyscale and for colour-blind viewers.\n- `active dot = r 7 halo at 0.25 opacity, r 4 core with a 2px background-colour stroke`\n  The halo makes the hovered point findable at a glance. The background-coloured stroke separates the core from the line it sits on in both themes.\n- `y domain and axis width = [0, yMax * 1.1], width 44`\n  10% headroom keeps the peak off the top edge. 44px fits \"$15k\" at 12px, 4px more than the orders card needs for \"2.5k\".\n- `count-up and fade = useCountUp 320ms, number-fade 220ms`\n  Quick enough to track a pointer sweeping across months. The target is rounded to whole dollars before counting, which matches the zero-decimal money format.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RevenueChartCard(props?: { data?: RevenuePoint[]; title?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype RevenuePoint = { label: string; current: number; previous: number }\nconst REVENUE_DATA: RevenuePoint[]\n```\n\n- `data: RevenuePoint[]` — default `REVENUE_DATA`\n  Twelve points, one per month, each with this year's and last year's revenue.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Revenue\"`\n  Header label shown while no month is hovered.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section, for example to set a width.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RevenueChartCard({\n  data = REVENUE_DATA,\n  title = \"Revenue\",\n  className,\n}: {\n  /** Twelve points, one per month; defaults to the demo year. */\n  data?: RevenuePoint[];\n  /** Headline label when no month is hovered. */\n  title?: string;\n  className?: string;\n} = {}) {\n  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState<number | null>(null);\n  const gradientId = useId();\n\n  const totalCurrent = data.reduce((sum, point) => sum + point.current, 0);\n  const totalPrevious = data.reduce((sum, point) => sum + point.previous, 0);\n  const hovering = activeIndex !== null && activeIndex < data.length;\n  const point = hovering ? data[activeIndex] : null;\n\n  const headlineValue = point ? point.current : totalCurrent;\n  const comparison = point ? point.previous : totalPrevious;\n  const delta = describeDelta(headlineValue, comparison);\n  const monthIndex = point ? MONTHS.indexOf(point.label) : -1;\n  const label = point ? (monthIndex >= 0 ? MONTHS_FULL[monthIndex] : point.label) : title;\n  const display = useCountUp(Math.round(headlineValue));\n\n  const yMax = Math.max(...data.map((d) => Math.max(d.current, d.previous)));\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex h-[344px] min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-6 rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default px-4 pt-4 pb-3\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {/* Header: label over the count-up figure and its delta; legend on the right */}\n      <div className=\"flex w-full flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between\">\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          <p className=\"w-full text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">{label}</p>\n          <div className=\"flex w-full items-center gap-2\">\n            <p\n              key={activeIndex ?? \"total\"}\n              className=\"animate-number-fade text-title-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums\"\n            >\n              ${formatMoney(display)}\n            </p>\n            <Chip variant=\"bold\" color={delta.color}>\n              {delta.label}\n            </Chip>\n          </div>\n          <p className=\"text-body-2-medium text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n            ${formatMoney(comparison)} {point ? \"a year earlier\" : \"last year\"}\n          </p>\n        </div>\n        <dl className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center gap-4 text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary\">\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n            <span className=\"size-2 rounded-full bg-chart-2-active\" aria-hidden />\n            <dt>This year</dt>\n          </div>\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n            <span className=\"size-2 rounded-full bg-chart-neutral\" aria-hidden />\n            <dt>Last year</dt>\n          </div>\n        </dl>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Chart */}\n      <div className=\"min-h-0 w-full flex-1\">\n        <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n          <ComposedChart\n            data={data}\n            margin={{ top: 4, right: 6, bottom: 0, left: 0 }}\n            onMouseMove={(state) => {\n              const index = Number(state?.activeTooltipIndex);\n              if (state?.isTooltipActive && Number.isFinite(index)) setActiveIndex(index);\n            }}\n            onMouseLeave={() => setActiveIndex(null)}\n          >\n            <defs>\n              <linearGradient id={gradientId} x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\">\n                <stop offset=\"0%\" stopColor=\"var(--color-chart-2)\" stopOpacity={0.35} />\n                <stop offset=\"100%\" stopColor=\"var(--color-chart-2)\" stopOpacity={0} />\n              </linearGradient>\n            </defs>\n            <YAxis\n              width={44}\n              domain={[0, yMax * 1.1]}\n              tickCount={4}\n              tickFormatter={formatK}\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tick={{ fontSize: 12, fill: \"var(--color-text-tertiary)\" }}\n            />\n            <XAxis\n              dataKey=\"label\"\n              tickLine={false}\n              axisLine={false}\n              tickMargin={12}\n              interval=\"preserveStartEnd\"\n              tick={{ fontSize: 13, fill: \"var(--color-text-tertiary)\" }}\n            />\n            <Tooltip\n              content={() => null}\n              cursor={{ stroke: \"var(--color-chart-cursor)\", strokeWidth: 1, strokeDasharray: \"4 4\" }}\n            />\n            <Line\n              type=\"monotone\"\n              dataKey=\"previous\"\n              stroke=\"var(--color-chart-neutral)\"\n              strokeWidth={2}\n              strokeDasharray=\"5 5\"\n              dot={false}\n              activeDot={false}\n              isAnimationActive\n              animationDuration={450}\n            />\n            <Area\n              type=\"monotone\"\n              dataKey=\"current\"\n              stroke=\"none\"\n              fill={`url(#${gradientId})`}\n              isAnimationActive\n              animationDuration={450}\n            />\n            <Line\n              type=\"monotone\"\n              dataKey=\"current\"\n              stroke=\"var(--color-chart-2-active)\"\n              strokeWidth={2.5}\n              dot={false}\n              activeDot={<ActiveDot />}\n              isAnimationActive\n              animationDuration={450}\n            />\n          </ComposedChart>\n        </ResponsiveContainer>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useCountUp` cancels its pending animation frame when the target changes and on unmount. `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its resize observer on unmount. The card adds no listeners of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** There is no reduced-motion branch. The lines and area draw in over 450ms on mount, the headline counts over 320ms and the number fades in over 220ms on each hover change. All are short and none shifts the layout, but they play regardless of the user's setting.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using a fixed gradient id such as \"revenueFill\". Two cards on one page would both point at the first definition, and changing one card's colour would recolour the other. The id comes from `useId()`.\n- Removing the `<Tooltip>` because it renders nothing. Without it Recharts does not track the active index, so the headline and the active dot never follow the pointer.\n- Drawing the area after the solid line. Series paint in source order, and the area would cover the line's lower half. The order is dashed line, area, solid line.\n- Expecting cents in the headline. The value is rounded before `useCountUp` and the money format uses `maximumFractionDigits: 0`, so December's $14,703.92 shows as \"$14,704\". Change both if your currency needs decimals.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `REVENUE_DATA` and `RevenuePoint` are exported. The default data adds up to $152,313.92 against $131,304 the year before, which the header shows as \"$152,314\" and \"+16%\".\n- The card shares its frame, header and delta logic with the orders chart card, so the two sit in one dashboard row at the same height.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card is 344px tall with \"Revenue\", \"$152,314\", a lime \"+16%\" chip and \"$131,304 last year\" in the header. A lime line over a fading lime area rises above a dashed grey line. Hovering a month draws a dashed vertical cursor, puts a ringed dot on the lime line, names the full month and counts the headline to that month's revenue with its own delta. Leaving the chart returns to the total.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "rotary-drag-carousel": "# Build: Rotary Drag Carousel\n\nItems sit at fixed stations around a circle whose hub is below the frame, so only the top arc is ever visible. The gesture is measured in angle rather than pixels: `atan2(y - cy, x - cx)`, differenced through a shortest-signed wrap, so a hand sweeping the top of the wheel turns it by the amount it actually travelled around the hub. Release and it keeps going at the speed it left at, then stops with a station facing up — one motion, no corrective hop.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA wheel of cards you grab and spin. Angular drag, projected inertia, and it always lands on a station.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Layout is a pure function of one number. For wheel angle `A`, station `i` sits at screen angle `theta = A + i * (360 / count) - 90`, at `(cx + R cos theta, cy + R sin theta)`, rotated by the shortest signed distance to the top, `away = wrapRange(theta + 90, -180, 180)`. `A` is unbounded and may be -4000 without consequence, because `away` is the only value that ever needs wrapping.\n2. The radius is floored at `1.25 x containerHeight`, which forces the hub `cy = topInset * height + R` strictly below the box. That is a correctness constraint, not a look: `atan2` about a point the user can touch converts a two-pixel wobble near the hub into a hundred degrees of spin.\n3. Each `pointermove` computes the pointer's own angle about the hub and adds `wrapRange(current - previous, -180, 180)` to `A`. The wrap is what keeps a drag across the 180° seam from reading as a 359° jerk backwards.\n4. Release velocity is measured over a rolling ~70 ms window of `(angle, time)` rather than off the final delta. Because a stationary finger produces no `pointermove` events, the window never advances while the hand is still, and the measured speed decays toward zero on its own — \"drag, pause, lift\" therefore does not throw.\n5. Inertia without a plugin: `power3.out` is `p(t) = 1 - (1 - t)^3`, so `p'(0) = 3`. A tween of duration `D` covering `delta` degrees departs at `3 * delta / D` deg/s, so setting `delta = v * D / 3` makes a plain `gsap.to` continue the gesture at exactly the speed the hand left.\n6. That projected landing angle is rounded to a multiple of the station spacing *before* the tween is created, so the throw and the snap are the same tween. Snapping afterwards is a second animation starting from rest — the small hop that gives away a hand-rolled dial.\n7. Pointer capture is taken on `pointerdown`, and the release path is one idempotent `finish` guarded on the stored `pointerId`, wired to `pointerup`, `pointercancel` and `lostpointercapture` alike. A drag that ends off-screen still lands.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MIN_RADIUS_HEIGHT_RATIO = 1.25`\n  Radius floor as a multiple of container height, so the hub is always below the visible box. At 1.0 the hub lands exactly on the bottom edge and a press there divides by a near-zero radius; below 1.0 it is reachable and the wheel spins wildly for a pointer that barely moved.\n- `POWER3_OUT_SLOPE = 3`\n  The initial derivative of `power3.out`, and the divisor in `delta = v * D / 3`. It is exact, not tuned: change the ease and this number changes with it — `power2.out` needs 2, `power4.out` needs 4. Leave it at 3 with a different ease and the throw either undershoots or overshoots by a constant factor.\n- `throwDuration = 1.1`\n  Seconds of inertial travel. Under ~0.6 s the wheel stops so abruptly that a fast fling feels like it hit a wall; over ~1.6 s the tail is so slow it reads as broken rather than as momentum, and it delays the settle callback past the point anyone is still watching.\n- `maxThrow = 260`\n  Degrees one throw may carry, applied to the projected distance before snapping. Without a cap, a 900 deg/s fling projects 330° and the wheel makes most of a revolution from a flick — past about 270° the user loses track of which way it went and which station is coming.\n- `VELOCITY_WINDOW = 0.07`\n  Seconds of gesture history the release speed is averaged over. Shorter and it is effectively the last delta, which is noisy at 120 Hz and jitters the throw distance between identical gestures; much longer and a deliberate slow-down before release is averaged away, so the wheel throws when the user meant to place it.\n- `MAX_VELOCITY = 900`\n  Degrees per second the measured speed is clamped to. A pointer that jumps (a tab switch mid-drag, a synthetic event, a coalesced move across the hub) can report thousands of deg/s; clamping keeps one bad sample from spinning the wheel several turns.\n- `DRAG_THRESHOLD = 5`\n  Pixels of travel before a press becomes a drag. Below ~3 the hand tremor in a normal tap counts as a drag, so every tap is swallowed as a gesture; above ~10 a small deliberate nudge of the wheel does nothing at all.\n- `CULL_DEG = 96`\n  Stations more than this far from the top are `visibility: hidden`. Just past 90° they are behind the horizontal through the hub and outside the box in every supported aspect ratio, so painting them is pure cost; culling at exactly 90° makes the boundary coincide with the fade reaching its floor and items visibly wink out.\n- `radiusRatio = 0.78`\n  Radius as a fraction of container width, before the height floor. Larger flattens the arc until the wheel reads as a straight row; much smaller pulls the hub up toward the box and the visible arc bends so hard that only three stations fit.\n- `fade = 0.85`\n  Opacity lost per 90° off the top. The station one step out stays clearly legible, which is the affordance that says the wheel turns; at 1.0 the neighbours reach zero exactly at the cull angle and the wheel looks like it holds one card.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RotaryDragCarousel(props: { children: ReactNode; radiusRatio?: number; topInsetRatio?: number; snapDeg?: number; throwDuration?: number; maxThrow?: number; fade?: number; scaleFalloff?: number; initialIndex?: number; className?: string; itemClassName?: string; onIndexChange?: (index: number) => void; 'aria-label'?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One item per child. Each gets a station on the wheel.\n- `radiusRatio: number` — default `0.78`\n  Wheel radius as a fraction of container width, floored against height so the hub stays below the box.\n- `topInsetRatio: number` — default `0.08`\n  Where the top of the wheel sits, as a fraction of container height.\n- `snapDeg: number`\n  Degrees the wheel snaps to. Defaults to one station, `360 / count`.\n- `throwDuration: number` — default `1.1`\n  Seconds of inertial travel after release.\n- `maxThrow: number` — default `260`\n  Hard cap on how far one throw may carry, in degrees.\n- `fade: number` — default `0.85`\n  Opacity lost per 90° away from the top station.\n- `scaleFalloff: number` — default `0.22`\n  Scale lost at 90° away from the top station.\n- `initialIndex: number` — default `0`\n  Station facing up on mount. Seeded once, never re-applied.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the drag surface.\n- `itemClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every station. Size the items here.\n- `onIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Fires with the wrapped index of the station that lands facing up.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Carousel'`\n  Label for the focusable wheel, which also takes arrow keys.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Write one transform per station from the current wheel angle. A pure\n * function of `angleRef.current` and the box size — nothing accumulates in\n * here, so interrupting the throw tween mid-flight cannot desynchronise it.\n */\nconst layout = useCallback(() => {\n  const host = hostRef.current;\n  if (!host || count === 0) return;\n\n  const width = host.clientWidth;\n  const height = host.clientHeight;\n  if (width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n\n  const radius = Math.max(radiusRatio * width, height * MIN_RADIUS_HEIGHT_RATIO);\n  const cx = width / 2;\n  const cy = topInsetRatio * height + radius;\n  const angle = angleRef.current;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    const station = stationsRef.current[i];\n    if (!station) continue;\n\n    // Station i's screen angle. `UP_DEG` puts station 0 at the top when the\n    // wheel is at 0, so the maths and the visual agree at rest.\n    const theta = angle + i * step + UP_DEG;\n    // Shortest signed distance to the top, in (-180, 180]. The wheel is a\n    // real circle, so this is the only place wrapping is needed at all —\n    // `angle` itself may be -4000 without consequence.\n    const away = wrapRange(theta - UP_DEG, -180, 180);\n    const distance = Math.abs(away);\n\n    if (distance > CULL_DEG) {\n      station.style.visibility = 'hidden';\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    const rad = theta * DEG_TO_RAD;\n    const x = cx + radius * Math.cos(rad);\n    const y = cy + radius * Math.sin(rad);\n    const t = Math.min(distance / 90, 1);\n\n    station.style.visibility = 'visible';\n    // Counter-rotating by `-away` would keep the cards upright; leaving the\n    // rotation in is what makes it read as one rigid wheel rather than a row\n    // of cards travelling along an arc.\n    station.style.transform =\n      `translate(-50%, -50%) translate(${x.toFixed(2)}px, ${y.toFixed(2)}px) ` +\n      `rotate(${away.toFixed(2)}deg) ` +\n      `scale(${(1 - scaleFalloff * t).toFixed(3)})`;\n    station.style.opacity = clamp(1 - fade * t, 0, 1).toFixed(3);\n    station.style.zIndex = String(100 - Math.round(distance));\n  }\n}, [count, step, radiusRatio, topInsetRatio, fade, scaleFalloff]);\n\n/** Angle at which station `index` faces up: `angle + index * step = 0`. */\nconst angleForIndex = useCallback((index: number) => -index * step, [step]);\n\nconst report = useCallback(() => {\n  if (count === 0) return;\n  // `angle = -i * step` puts station i up, so `i = -angle / step`.\n  const index = wrapIndex(Math.round(-angleRef.current / step), count);\n  if (index === reportedRef.current) return;\n  reportedRef.current = index;\n  notify.current?.(index);\n}, [count, step]);\n\n/**\n * Land the wheel. `velocity` is degrees per second at the moment of release;\n * pass 0 for a keyboard step or a tap.\n */\nconst settle = useCallback(\n  (velocity: number) => {\n    const projected =\n      angleRef.current +\n      clamp(\n        (velocity * throwDuration) / POWER3_OUT_SLOPE,\n        -maxThrow,\n        maxThrow,\n      );\n    // Round to a station BEFORE tweening. Snapping afterwards is a second\n    // animation that starts from rest, which is the little hop that gives\n    // away every hand-rolled dial.\n    const landing = Math.round(projected / snap) * snap;\n\n    gsap.killTweensOf(angleRef);\n\n    if (reduced || Math.abs(landing - angleRef.current) < 0.01) {\n      angleRef.current = landing;\n      layout();\n      report();\n      return;\n    }\n\n    gsap.to(angleRef, {\n      current: landing,\n      duration: throwDuration,\n      ease: 'power3.out',\n      overwrite: true,\n      onUpdate: layout,\n      onComplete: report,\n    });\n  },\n  [throwDuration, maxThrow, snap, reduced, layout, report],\n);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const host = hostRef.current;\n  if (!host) return;\n\n  let pointerId: number | null = null;\n  let lastPointerAngle = 0;\n  let pressX = 0;\n  let pressY = 0;\n  let moved = false;\n  let swallowClick = false;\n  let swallowTimer: number | null = null;\n\n  // The gate expires. A throw released over a gap between cards, or one the\n  // browser cancels, produces no click at all — and a gate left armed eats\n  // the visitor's next real click, however long afterwards it lands.\n  const disarmSwallow = () => {\n    swallowClick = false;\n    if (swallowTimer !== null) {\n      window.clearTimeout(swallowTimer);\n      swallowTimer = null;\n    }\n  };\n  /** Rolling window: the wheel angle and clock at the start of the window. */\n  let sampleAngle = 0;\n  let sampleTime = 0;\n\n  const now = () => performance.now() / 1000;\n\n  const pointerAngle = (clientX: number, clientY: number) => {\n    const rect = host.getBoundingClientRect();\n    const radius = Math.max(\n      radiusRatio * rect.width,\n      rect.height * MIN_RADIUS_HEIGHT_RATIO,\n    );\n    const cx = rect.left + rect.width / 2;\n    const cy = rect.top + topInsetRatio * rect.height + radius;\n    return Math.atan2(clientY - cy, clientX - cx) / DEG_TO_RAD;\n  };\n\n  const onPointerDown = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n    // One pointer owns the wheel. A second finger arriving mid-throw would\n    // otherwise reset the origin angle and the wheel would jump.\n    if (pointerId !== null || !e.isPrimary) return;\n    if (e.pointerType === 'mouse' && e.button !== 0) return;\n\n    // A new press means the previous gesture is over and whatever click it\n    // was owed never came.\n    disarmSwallow();\n    pointerId = e.pointerId;\n    pressX = e.clientX;\n    pressY = e.clientY;\n    moved = false;\n    lastPointerAngle = pointerAngle(e.clientX, e.clientY);\n    sampleAngle = angleRef.current;\n    sampleTime = now();\n\n    // Capture on press, not once the threshold is crossed: by then the\n    // pointer may already have left the element, and `pointermove` would\n    // have stopped arriving.\n    host.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);\n    gsap.killTweensOf(angleRef);\n  };\n\n  const onPointerMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n    if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n\n    const current = pointerAngle(e.clientX, e.clientY);\n    // Shortest signed step. Without the wrap, dragging across the 180°\n    // seam reads as a 359° jerk in the wrong direction.\n    const delta = wrapRange(current - lastPointerAngle, -180, 180);\n    lastPointerAngle = current;\n\n    if (!moved) {\n      if (Math.hypot(e.clientX - pressX, e.clientY - pressY) < DRAG_THRESHOLD) return;\n      moved = true;\n    }\n\n    angleRef.current += delta;\n    layout();\n\n    // Retire the velocity window once it is full. Measuring over a window\n    // rather than off the last delta is what stops \"drag, pause, lift\" from\n    // throwing the wheel: with no movement there are no events, the window\n    // never advances, and the measured speed decays toward zero on its own.\n    const t = now();\n    if (t - sampleTime > VELOCITY_WINDOW) {\n      sampleAngle = angleRef.current;\n      sampleTime = t;\n    }\n  };\n\n  /**\n   * Idempotent. Called from `pointerup`, `pointercancel` and\n   * `lostpointercapture`, all three of which can be the last event a gesture\n   * produces, and at least one of which will always arrive — including when\n   * the pointer is released outside the window, or the OS takes it away for\n   * a gesture of its own. Guarding on `pointerId` makes the extra calls\n   * no-ops instead of a double throw.\n   */\n  const finish = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n    if (pointerId !== e.pointerId) return;\n    const id = pointerId;\n    pointerId = null;\n\n    const elapsed = now() - sampleTime;\n    const velocity =\n      moved && elapsed > 0\n        ? clamp((angleRef.current - sampleAngle) / elapsed, -MAX_VELOCITY, MAX_VELOCITY)\n        : 0;\n\n    disarmSwallow();\n    if (moved) {\n      swallowClick = true;\n      swallowTimer = window.setTimeout(disarmSwallow, SWALLOW_TIMEOUT_MS);\n    }\n    moved = false;\n\n    if (host.hasPointerCapture(id)) host.releasePointerCapture(id);\n    settle(velocity);\n  };\n\n  // Capture phase, ahead of any child's own handler. A throw ends with a\n  // `click` on whatever happens to be under the pointer, so without this the\n  // gesture that spins the wheel also activates a card.\n  const onClick = (e: MouseEvent) => {\n    if (!swallowClick) return;\n\n    // Enter or Space on a focused card dispatches a click with `detail === 0`\n    // and no pointer sequence behind it. Nothing was thrown, and swallowing\n    // it is what makes the wheel keyboard-inoperable.\n    if (e.detail === 0) return;\n\n    disarmSwallow();\n    e.preventDefault();\n    e.stopPropagation();\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {\n    const direction =\n      e.key === 'ArrowRight' || e.key === 'ArrowDown'\n        ? -1\n        : e.key === 'ArrowLeft' || e.key === 'ArrowUp'\n          ? 1\n          : 0;\n    if (direction === 0) return;\n    e.preventDefault();\n    gsap.killTweensOf(angleRef);\n    angleRef.current = Math.round(angleRef.current / snap) * snap + direction * snap;\n    settle(0);\n  };\n\n  host.addEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n  host.addEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n  host.addEventListener('pointerup', finish);\n  host.addEventListener('pointercancel', finish);\n  host.addEventListener('lostpointercapture', finish);\n  host.addEventListener('click', onClick, true);\n  host.addEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);\n\n  return () => {\n    host.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onPointerDown);\n    host.removeEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove);\n    host.removeEventListener('pointerup', finish);\n    host.removeEventListener('pointercancel', finish);\n    host.removeEventListener('lostpointercapture', finish);\n    host.removeEventListener('click', onClick, true);\n    // Or the timer outlives the effect and a remount starts life armed.\n    disarmSwallow();\n    host.removeEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);\n    if (pointerId !== null && host.hasPointerCapture(pointerId)) {\n      host.releasePointerCapture(pointerId);\n    }\n    pointerId = null;\n  };\n}, [layout, settle, snap, radiusRatio, topInsetRatio]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes `pointerdown`, `pointermove`, `pointerup`, `pointercancel`, `lostpointercapture`, the capture-phase `click` and `keydown`; releases pointer capture if a gesture is still live at unmount; and kills the throw tween on the angle ref via a separate effect so an in-flight `gsap.to` cannot call `onUpdate` against detached nodes. The shared resize observer unregisters. Nothing but inline styles on the stations remains, and those die with the nodes.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Dragging is unchanged — it is the user's own motion, tracked one-to-one, and removing it would remove the component. What goes away is everything the component would have animated by itself: the inertial throw is not created, so release rounds the current angle to the nearest station and assigns it in one frame, and an arrow key moves one station instantly. The wheel never travels further than the hand did.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Driving the rotation from `deltaX` instead of from an angle. A linear delta turns the wheel by the same amount wherever you grab it, so a drag near the left edge feels geared wrong and a drag straight down the middle does nothing at all. Angular input is what makes it feel like a physical wheel.\n- Differencing raw `atan2` output. Crossing the ±180° seam gives a delta of about -359° instead of +1°, and the wheel snaps a full turn backwards once per revolution. Every angle difference here goes through `wrapRange(d, -180, 180)`.\n- Putting the hub inside the container. Near the hub the pointer's angular change per pixel goes to infinity, so a press within a few pixels of it and a one-pixel jitter spins the wheel a hundred degrees. The radius floor makes that geometrically impossible rather than guarding it after the fact.\n- Taking the release velocity from the last `pointermove`. Drag, hold still for half a second, lift, and the wheel flies off at the speed from before the pause, because the last event recorded was the last time the hand moved. A rolling time window measured against `performance.now()` at release decays to zero on its own.\n- Snapping after the inertial tween instead of before it. The throw finishes, comes to rest between two stations, and a second tween hops it into place — two eases back to back, and it is instantly recognisable as fake momentum.\n- Calling `setPointerCapture` only after the drag threshold is crossed. By then a fast gesture has already left the element, `pointermove` has stopped arriving, and the wheel sticks at the angle it had when the pointer crossed the boundary.\n- Handling only `pointerup`. Release outside the window, let the OS claim the gesture, or have the element re-parented mid-drag and no `pointerup` arrives; the wheel stays captured and frozen mid-throw. `pointercancel` and `lostpointercapture` both route to the same idempotent finish, guarded on the stored `pointerId` so the duplicate is a no-op rather than a second throw.\n- Letting the click through. A throw ends with a `click` dispatched on whatever card is under the pointer, so spinning the wheel also opens a project. The click is swallowed in the capture phase, once, only when the gesture actually moved.\n- Reaching for `InertiaPlugin`. It is a Club GreenSock plugin — not redistributable, and not installable from a public registry — so a component that depends on it fails to build for most consumers. The `v * D / 3` projection reproduces the throw with the free core.\n- Re-seeding `initialIndex` on resize. Rotate a phone mid-gesture and the wheel teleports back to its opening station. The seed is guarded by a ref and runs exactly once.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The counter-rotation is deliberately absent: each card is rotated *with* the wheel by `away`, so the whole thing reads as one rigid disc. Cancelling that rotation keeps labels upright but turns it into a row of cards sliding along an arc, which is a different and much weaker effect.\n- The snap grid is a multiple of `360 / count`, so a station always lands facing up. The source of this technique snapped to a fixed 10°, which only coincides with item positions when `360 / count` is itself a multiple of 10 — with 7 or 9 items it lands between cards.\n- Arrow keys step one station and route through the same settle path, so keyboard and pointer cannot disagree about where the wheel rests.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nGrab anywhere in the box and drag in an arc: the wheel follows your hand around the hub, and a drag near the left or right edge turns it less than the same pixel distance across the top. Fling and release — it keeps going, decelerates, and comes to rest with one card exactly upright, in a single continuous motion with no hop at the end. Drag, hold still for a second, then lift: it settles to the nearest station without throwing. Start a drag, move the pointer off the window, and release outside — the wheel still lands rather than freezing. Release over a card and no click reaches it; tap the same card without moving and the click goes through. Arrow keys step one card at a time.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "rotating-word-hero": "# Build: Rotating Word Hero\n\nThe landing page opener: an announcement pill, a two-line headline whose key word cycles through a list (\"for Finance / Healthcare / Agentic Interface\"), a subcopy paragraph, a CTA row and a \"built with\" badges row. Each swap slides the old word up through a blur while the next rises from below, and the word slot animates its width so the centred line re-balances smoothly. A dotted rule under the word signals that it changes. `RotatingWord` is exported on its own for headlines laid out differently.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLanding hero whose key word slides and blurs through a list while the line glides to each new width, with CTAs and a stack badges row.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Slot sizing: inside the word slot sits an in-flow, invisible `inline-block` copy of the current word. It gives the slot its line height and baseline, and a ResizeObserver on it reports its border-box inline size. That number becomes the slot's explicit `width`, which has `transition-[width] duration-500 ease-out`, so the rest of the centred line glides instead of jumping. The first measurement replaces `auto` and therefore does not animate.\n2. Word swap: the visible word is a `motion.span` keyed by index inside `AnimatePresence initial={false}`, absolutely positioned at the slot's top-left. Enter is `y 45% → 0, opacity 0 → 1, blur 8px → 0`; exit is `y 0 → -45%` with the same fade and blur, 550ms on `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`. Both run at once, so the words cross mid-slot.\n3. Rotation clock: one `setInterval` advances the index every `interval` ms (2600 by default). The effect only runs while `running` is true: more than one word, not `paused`, no reduced-motion preference, not hovered, tab visible (`useVisibility`) and slot on screen (`useInView`). Any of these flipping clears the interval, so a hidden tab never queues swaps.\n4. Measurement uses ResizeObserver layout sizes, never `getBoundingClientRect`, because the hero is often rendered inside a CSS-scaled preview and a rect would report the scaled width.\n5. Accessibility: the animated layers are `aria-hidden`; a `sr-only` span holds the first word, so assistive tech reads one stable headline (\"React Design System for Finance Interface\") rather than a sentence that mutates every few seconds. Hovering the word pauses it for reading.\n6. Layout: the `<section>` is an `@container/hero`; the headline steps from display-4 to display-2 at `@xl` and to the 64px large title at `@4xl`, so a narrow column gets a smaller heading even on a wide window. The optional glow is four blurred token-coloured bars behind the headline, masked off toward the bottom.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `interval = 2600ms`\n  Long enough to read a word and the line it completes, short enough that a visitor sees at least two swaps before scrolling.\n- `swap = 550ms, y ±45%, blur 8px`\n  Under half the slot height keeps both words partly in the slot while they cross, and the blur hides the overlap.\n- `width transition = 500ms ease-out`\n  Slightly shorter than the swap so the line settles before the incoming word fully sharpens.\n- `dot rule = 0.15em × 0.1em tiles, 0.032em dots`\n  Em units scale the dotted underline with every headline size step.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction RotatingWordHero(props: { words: string[]; before?: ReactNode; after?: ReactNode; announcement?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; actions?: ReactNode; badges?: { label: string; version?: string; icon?: ComponentType }[]; interval?: number; paused?: boolean; glow?: boolean; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction RotatingWord(props: { words: string[]; interval?: number; paused?: boolean; underline?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction HeroAnnouncement(props: { tag?: string; href?: string; onClick?: MouseEventHandler; children: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `words: string[]`\n  Words cycled in the headline slot. One word renders static.\n- `before / after: ReactNode`\n  Headline text around the rotating word.\n- `interval: number` — default `2600`\n  Milliseconds per word.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Holds the current word.\n- `announcement: ReactNode`\n  Pill above the headline, usually a `HeroAnnouncement`.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Subcopy under the headline (max 620px).\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  CTA row, e.g. a primary and a secondary kit Button.\n- `badges: HeroBadgeItem[]` — default `[]`\n  `{ label, version?, icon? }` items separated by dots.\n- `glow: boolean` — default `true`\n  Soft accent light bars behind the headline.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Content below the badges row, e.g. a product shot.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * A word that swaps in place: the outgoing word slides up and blurs out while\n * the next rises from below. An in-flow invisible copy of the current word\n * gives the slot its height and baseline; a ResizeObserver on that copy feeds\n * an explicit width, so the surrounding line glides to the new length instead\n * of jumping. The rotation stops under reduced motion, while hovered, off\n * screen and in a hidden tab.\n */\nexport function RotatingWord({ words, interval = 2600, paused = false, underline = true, className }: RotatingWordProps) {\n  const rootRef = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const sizerRef = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);\n  const [width, setWidth] = useState<number | undefined>(undefined);\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(false);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(rootRef);\n\n  const count = words.length;\n  const word = words[index % Math.max(count, 1)] ?? \"\";\n  const running = count > 1 && !paused && !reduced && !hovered && visible && inView;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!running) return;\n    const id = window.setInterval(() => setIndex((i) => (i + 1) % count), interval);\n    return () => window.clearInterval(id);\n  }, [running, interval, count]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const sizer = sizerRef.current;\n    if (!sizer || typeof ResizeObserver === \"undefined\") return;\n    // Layout size, not getBoundingClientRect: the hero may sit inside a scaled preview.\n    const ro = new ResizeObserver((entries) => {\n      const box = entries[0]?.borderBoxSize?.[0];\n      setWidth(box ? box.inlineSize : sizer.offsetWidth);\n    });\n    ro.observe(sizer);\n    return () => ro.disconnect();\n  }, []);\n\n  return (\n    <span\n      ref={rootRef}\n      data-word={word}\n      onPointerEnter={() => setHovered(true)}\n      onPointerLeave={() => setHovered(false)}\n      className={cx(\n        \"relative inline-block whitespace-nowrap\",\n        !reduced && \"transition-[width] duration-500 ease-out\",\n        className,\n      )}\n      style={width === undefined ? undefined : { width }}\n    >\n      <span className=\"sr-only\">{words[0]}</span>\n      <span ref={sizerRef} aria-hidden=\"true\" className=\"invisible inline-block\">\n        {word}\n      </span>\n      <span aria-hidden=\"true\" className=\"absolute inset-0\">\n        <AnimatePresence initial={false}>\n          <motion.span\n            key={`${index}-${word}`}\n            className=\"absolute top-0 left-0 inline-block\"\n            initial={reduced ? false : { y: \"45%\", opacity: 0, filter: \"blur(8px)\" }}\n            animate={{ y: \"0%\", opacity: 1, filter: \"blur(0px)\" }}\n            exit={reduced ? { opacity: 0, transition: { duration: 0 } } : { y: \"-45%\", opacity: 0, filter: \"blur(8px)\" }}\n            transition={{ duration: 0.55, ease: EASE_OUT }}\n          >\n            {word}\n          </motion.span>\n        </AnimatePresence>\n      </span>\n      {underline ? (\n        <span\n          aria-hidden=\"true\"\n          className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 -bottom-[0.08em] h-[0.1em] bg-[radial-gradient(circle,currentColor_0.032em,transparent_0.042em)] bg-size-[0.15em_0.1em] bg-repeat-x text-text-placeholder opacity-70\"\n        />\n      ) : null}\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The interval is cleared whenever the rotation stops or the hero unmounts; the ResizeObserver is disconnected on unmount. useInView and useVisibility unsubscribe their shared observers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rotation stops entirely and the headline shows its current word. The width transition is removed, and the word renders without enter/exit motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Measuring an inline `<span>`: ResizeObserver reports 0x0 for non-replaced inline elements. The sizer must be `inline-block`.\n- Letting the slot width follow its content: CSS cannot transition `width: auto`, so the line snaps. Feed a measured pixel width instead.\n- Leaving the interval running in a background tab: when the tab returns, the browser fires the backlog and the word flickers through several swaps.\n- Putting `aria-live` on the word. Screen readers then announce a new word every 2.6 seconds; keep the animated part hidden and expose one stable word.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `paused` to hold the word, e.g. while a modal is open over the hero.\n- The `before` slot accepts any node; the demo wraps \"React Design System\" in a block span to force the two-line split.\n- `HeroAnnouncement` renders an anchor when given `href`, otherwise a static pill.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe headline reads \"React Design System / for Finance Interface\" with a dotted rule under Finance. About every 2.6s the word slides up and blurs out while the next (Healthcare, Agentic, …) rises in, and \"Interface\" glides to its new position. Hovering the word pauses it. With reduced motion the word never changes. Light and dark both show the pill, CTAs and four badges.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "row-actions": "# Build: Row Actions\n\nThe action cluster from the BoardUI finance table rows: a 32px bordered kebab that opens the kit Dropdown with Edit, Duplicate and Delete. Delete never fires straight away; it opens the kit AlertDialog with a row-specific title and focus on Cancel. With inline, delete and edit also appear as 32px icon buttons in front of the kebab, as in the reference, and fold into the kebab on phones. The component owns no row data: it calls onEdit, onDuplicate and onDelete and the table updates itself.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nKebab menu at the end of a table row with Edit, Duplicate and a red Delete that asks first through the kit AlertDialog.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The kebab is the kit DropdownTrigger styled with the exported buttonStyles (secondary, small, icon-only), so it matches the inline kit Buttons beside it; while the menu is open it keeps the pressed background and border.\n2. The menu is the kit Dropdown (non-modal React Aria popover with outside-press dismissal) narrowed to w-48 with p-1.5 and denser px-2 py-1.5 rows; only actions with a handler are rendered, and Delete sits under a divider in text-error-primary with a tertiary-error hover.\n3. Choosing a row first focuses the kebab, then closes the panel and runs the action. Any dialog opened next (the caller's edit form, the confirm) therefore records the kebab as its restore target instead of a menu row that is about to unmount.\n4. Delete, from the menu or the inline trash button, only sets confirmOpen. The kit AlertDialog (role=alertdialog, no backdrop dismissal, focus on Cancel) calls onDelete on confirm, and an async onDelete keeps it open with a busy Delete button until the promise settles.\n5. Inline buttons carry max-sm:hidden, so below 640px the row keeps a single kebab that still holds every action.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `button size = 32px (kit Button small icon-only), gap-2`\n  Matches the finance table's trash / edit / kebab squares and keeps a comfortable touch target.\n- `menu width = w-48, p-1.5`\n  Three short verbs do not need the 266px kit default; the narrow panel stays inside the row on phones.\n- `placement = 'bottom end', offset 4`\n  The kebab is the last cell, so the panel grows leftwards into the table instead of off the edge.\n- `danger row = text-error-primary, bg-background-tertiary-error on hover`\n  Marks the destructive verb in both themes before the confirm even appears.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function RowActions({ name, onEdit, onDuplicate, onDelete, inline, confirmTitle, confirmDescription, confirmLabel, placement, className }: RowActionsProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `name: string`\n  Row name used in the kebab, inline and dialog labels.\n- `onEdit: () => void`\n  Adds Edit to the menu (and the inline pencil).\n- `onDuplicate: () => void`\n  Adds Duplicate to the menu.\n- `onDelete: () => void | Promise<unknown>`\n  Adds Delete and runs after confirmation; a promise keeps the dialog busy.\n- `inline: boolean` — default `false`\n  Show delete and edit as icon buttons before the kebab from 640px up.\n- `confirmTitle: ReactNode` — default ``Delete ${name}?``\n  Confirm dialog title.\n- `confirmDescription: ReactNode`\n  Confirm dialog body; defaults to a generic cannot-undo line.\n- `confirmLabel: ReactNode` — default `'Delete'`\n  Label of the danger button.\n- `placement: 'bottom end' | 'bottom start' | 'top end' | 'top start'` — default `'bottom end'`\n  Menu placement relative to the kebab.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function RowActions({\n  name,\n  onEdit,\n  onDuplicate,\n  onDelete,\n  inline = false,\n  confirmTitle,\n  confirmDescription,\n  confirmLabel = 'Delete',\n  placement = 'bottom end',\n  className,\n}: RowActionsProps) {\n  const rootRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [menuOpen, setMenuOpen] = useState(false);\n  const [confirmOpen, setConfirmOpen] = useState(false);\n\n  // Park focus on the kebab before the panel unmounts: whatever opens next\n  // (the caller's edit form, the confirm dialog) then records the kebab as\n  // the element to restore focus to, instead of a detached menu row.\n  const run = (action: (() => void) | undefined) => {\n    rootRef.current?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-row-actions-trigger]')?.focus();\n    setMenuOpen(false);\n    action?.();\n  };\n  const askDelete = () => setConfirmOpen(true);\n  const hasMenuAbove = Boolean(onEdit || onDuplicate);\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={rootRef} className={cx('inline-flex items-center gap-2', className)}>\n      {inline && onDelete ? (\n        <Button\n          variant=\"secondary\"\n          size=\"small\"\n          iconOnly\n          leadingIcon={RiDeleteBinLine}\n          aria-label={`Delete ${name}`}\n          onClick={askDelete}\n          className=\"max-sm:hidden\"\n        />\n      ) : null}\n      {inline && onEdit ? (\n        <Button\n          variant=\"secondary\"\n          size=\"small\"\n          iconOnly\n          leadingIcon={RiPencilLine}\n          aria-label={`Edit ${name}`}\n          onClick={onEdit}\n          className=\"max-sm:hidden\"\n        />\n      ) : null}\n\n      <Dropdown isOpen={menuOpen} onOpenChange={setMenuOpen}>\n        <DropdownTrigger data-row-actions-trigger aria-label={`Actions for ${name}`} className={cx(KEBAB, menuOpen && KEBAB_OPEN)}>\n          <RiMore2Fill aria-hidden className={buttonStyles.icon.small} />\n        </DropdownTrigger>\n        <DropdownPopover aria-label={`Actions for ${name}`} placement={placement} className=\"w-48 p-1.5\" dialogClassName=\"gap-0.5\">\n          {onEdit ? (\n            <DropdownItem onSelect={() => run(onEdit)} className=\"px-2 py-1.5\">\n              <ItemLabel icon={RiPencilLine}>Edit</ItemLabel>\n            </DropdownItem>\n          ) : null}\n          {onDuplicate ? (\n            <DropdownItem onSelect={() => run(onDuplicate)} className=\"px-2 py-1.5\">\n              <ItemLabel icon={RiFileCopyLine}>Duplicate</ItemLabel>\n            </DropdownItem>\n          ) : null}\n          {onDelete ? (\n            <>\n              {hasMenuAbove ? <DropdownDivider className=\"-mx-1.5 my-1\" /> : null}\n              <DropdownItem onSelect={() => run(askDelete)} className={cx('px-2 py-1.5', DANGER_ITEM)}>\n                <ItemLabel icon={RiDeleteBinLine} danger>\n                  Delete\n                </ItemLabel>\n              </DropdownItem>\n            </>\n          ) : null}\n        </DropdownPopover>\n      </Dropdown>\n\n      {onDelete ? (\n        <AlertDialog\n          isOpen={confirmOpen}\n          onOpenChange={setConfirmOpen}\n          icon={RiDeleteBinLine}\n          title={confirmTitle ?? `Delete ${name}?`}\n          description={confirmDescription ?? 'This row will be removed from the table. You can’t undo this.'}\n          confirmLabel={confirmLabel}\n          onConfirm={onDelete}\n        />\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners of its own. The kit Dropdown removes its outside-press listener on close and unmount, and the modal releases its scroll lock when it unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The kit Dropdown drops its fade, scale and blur under reduced motion and the kit AlertDialog keeps only its fade; RowActions adds no motion of its own.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Deleting the row unmounts the focused controls and the open dialog with it; move focus to a neighbouring row (the demo uses its kebab) or focus drops to <body>.\n- Do not pass a ref to DropdownTrigger: it would replace the Dropdown's own trigger ref and break outside-press and toggle handling. RowActions finds the kebab through data-row-actions-trigger instead.\n- Give every row a distinct name; the labels (\"Actions for Ana Carter\", \"Delete Ana Carter?\") are the only thing that tells repeated buttons apart.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Leave out onDuplicate (or onEdit) to drop that row from the menu; without onDelete there is no divider and no dialog.\n- Pass confirmDescription with the row's details (\"The $640 payment from Jun 12 ...\") so the confirm says exactly what goes away.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOpening \"Actions for Mia Chen\" shows Edit, Duplicate and Delete; Duplicate inserts \"Mia Chen (copy)\" below it and the count reads 6 payments; Delete asks \"Delete Mia Chen?\" with focus on Cancel, Cancel keeps the row, Delete removes it, the count drops and focus lands on the next row's kebab.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "safe-preloader": "# Build: Preloader That Cannot Hang\n\nEvery hand-rolled preloader eventually ships the same bug: a signal that never arrives — a 404 on a font, a decode that fails on iOS, an image behind a dead CDN — and the site is a permanent opaque rectangle nobody can see through or tab past. This inverts the contract. A hard ceiling guarantees the page is visible by a fixed time and the load signal only ever makes it sooner; a floor stops a warm cache turning the overlay into a two-frame flash; a session flag keeps it an introduction rather than a toll booth.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn intro overlay with a hard ceiling, a minimum visible time and a once-per-session flag, so a stalled load cannot trap the page.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A three-state phase machine — `holding`, `leaving`, `gone` — drives the render. It starts at `holding` on both server and client so the markup matches, and the session check runs in a `useLayoutEffect`, before paint, so a returning visitor never sees a single frame of the overlay.\n2. THE CEILING: `setTimeout(reveal, ceilingMs)` is armed in an effect on mount, before anything subscribes to a load signal, and nothing clears it except a successful reveal or unmount. This is not a fallback — it is the actual guarantee, and the load signal is the optimisation.\n3. THE FLOOR: when the signal does arrive, the remaining hold is `Math.max(0, floorMs - (performance.now() - started))` and the reveal is deferred by that much. Measured from mount, not from the signal, so the floor overlaps the load instead of being added to it.\n4. The signal is `ready` when the prop is passed, otherwise the browser's own: `document.readyState === \"complete\"` is checked first and only then is a one-shot `load` listener attached, because if the document has already finished the event has been and gone and a listener alone would wait forever.\n5. ONCE: on reveal, `sessionStorage[sessionKey] = \"1\"`, wrapped in try/catch because `setItem` throws in Safari private browsing and a preloader is not worth taking the page down for. Both the read and the write are guarded.\n6. `reveal()` guards on a ref, not on state, so the ceiling and the floor firing in the same tick still reveal exactly once. It sets `leaving` — a CSS opacity transition on `[data-leaving=\"true\"]` — and schedules the actual removal `fadeMs` later, so the node is never pulled out of the tree mid-transition. `onDone` fires from that same timer, after the overlay is gone.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `ceilingMs = 4000`\n  The reveal deadline. Long enough that a genuinely slow first paint on a cold 3G connection is not cut off mid-load, short enough that a user staring at a broken overlay gives up after it rather than before. Past ~6s people reload, which restarts the same stall.\n- `floorMs = 700`\n  Minimum visible time. On a warm cache the signal resolves in about 40ms; without a floor the overlay appears and vanishes inside two frames, which reads as a rendering fault rather than an introduction. Below ~400ms the fade has no room to be perceived as deliberate.\n- `fadeMs = 420`\n  Fade-out duration, passed to CSS as `--k-safe-fade` and used as the removal delay so the two can never disagree. Long enough that the page underneath is already composited and visible through the fade; past ~700ms the user is looking at content they cannot yet click.\n- `fade easing = cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  Heavily front-loaded, so most of the opacity is gone in the first third and the perceived wait is shorter than the measured one. A linear fade of the same length feels about twice as long.\n- `scan bar = 1.4s cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1) infinite`\n  A determinate progress bar would be a lie — nothing here knows a byte count. This is an indeterminate scan whose cycle is slow enough not to read as urgency and fast enough to prove the page is not frozen. The in-out curve keeps the ends from clipping at the container edges.\n- `sessionKey = 'k-preloader'`\n  Session, not local, storage. Local storage would mean the introduction plays once ever, so a returning visitor months later gets no reveal and QA can never see it again without clearing site data.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SafePreloader(props: { children?: ReactNode; ready?: boolean; ceilingMs?: number; floorMs?: number; fadeMs?: number; sessionKey?: string; once?: boolean; onDone?: () => void; position?: 'fixed' | 'absolute'; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Overlay content. Defaults to a label and an indeterminate scan bar.\n- `ready: boolean`\n  The load signal. Leave undefined to use the browser's own `load` event and `readyState`.\n- `ceilingMs: number` — default `4000`\n  Hard ceiling. The reveal happens by this time whether or not the signal ever arrives.\n- `floorMs: number` — default `700`\n  Minimum time the overlay stays up, measured from mount.\n- `fadeMs: number` — default `420`\n  Fade-out duration in ms. Also the delay before the node is removed.\n- `sessionKey: string` — default `'k-preloader'`\n  sessionStorage key used by the once-per-session gate.\n- `once: boolean` — default `true`\n  Set false to replay on every load, for development.\n- `onDone: () => void`\n  Fires after the overlay has finished leaving and unmounted.\n- `position: 'fixed' | 'absolute'` — default `'fixed'`\n  Absolute lets the overlay be scoped to a container instead of the viewport.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the overlay via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst reveal = useCallback(() => {\n  if (revealed.current) return;\n  revealed.current = true;\n  clearTimers();\n  if (once) markShown(sessionKey);\n  setPhase('leaving');\n  // The node is removed only after the fade has actually run, so nothing is\n  // pulled out of the tree mid-transition.\n  timers.current.push(\n    setTimeout(\n      () => {\n        setPhase('gone');\n        done.current?.();\n      },\n      reduced ? 0 : fadeMs,\n    ),\n  );\n}, [clearTimers, fadeMs, once, reduced, sessionKey]);\n\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\n  if (once && wasShown(sessionKey)) {\n    revealed.current = true;\n    setPhase('gone');\n    return;\n  }\n  started.current = performance.now();\n}, [once, sessionKey]);\n\n// THE CEILING. Armed on mount, before any load signal is subscribed to, and\n// never cancelled by anything except a successful reveal. If the signal\n// never comes, this is what saves the page.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (revealed.current) return;\n  const id = setTimeout(reveal, ceilingMs);\n  timers.current.push(id);\n  return () => clearTimeout(id);\n}, [ceilingMs, reveal]);\n\n// THE FLOOR. When the signal arrives, wait out whatever is left of the\n// minimum so a warm cache does not produce a two-frame black flash.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (revealed.current) return;\n\n  const settle = () => {\n    const elapsed = performance.now() - started.current;\n    const remaining = Math.max(0, (reduced ? 0 : floorMs) - elapsed);\n    const id = setTimeout(reveal, remaining);\n    timers.current.push(id);\n  };\n\n  if (ready !== undefined) {\n    if (ready) settle();\n    return;\n  }\n\n  // No `ready` prop: use the browser's own signal. If the document has\n  // already finished loading, the event has been and gone — check first.\n  if (document.readyState === 'complete') {\n    settle();\n    return;\n  }\n  window.addEventListener('load', settle, { once: true });\n  return () => window.removeEventListener('load', settle);\n}, [floorMs, ready, reduced, reveal]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Every timeout is pushed into a ref array and cleared on unmount, the ceiling effect clears its own id, and the one-shot `load` listener is removed by its effect. Because the removal timer is cleared, `setPhase` cannot run on a dead component and `onDone` cannot fire for a page that has already gone. No rAF, no observers, no global state beyond the session flag.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The overlay still appears and the ceiling still applies — withholding content is a behaviour, not an animation, so it is not skipped. What goes away is the motion: the floor collapses to 0 so nothing is held longer than it must be, the removal delay drops to 0 and the CSS transition to 1ms, and the scanning bar becomes a static full-width rule via `animation: none; transform: scaleX(1)` rather than a pulsing one. The result is a brief still plate, not a zero-duration version of the animated one.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Having no ceiling at all. One 404 on a font or a decode that fails only on iOS, and the overlay stays up forever: the content is not visible, not scrollable, and not reachable by keyboard, and it happens to a fraction of users you will never hear from.\n- Clearing the ceiling when the load signal is subscribed to, or when it fires. Whatever the signal hands off to — a floor timer, a promise chain, a decode — can itself stall, and the one thing that guaranteed a reveal has already been thrown away.\n- Attaching a `load` listener without checking `document.readyState` first. On a warm cache or after a client-side navigation the event has already fired, so the listener never runs; the ceiling silently does all the work and you only discover it the day someone shortens the ceiling.\n- Shipping without a floor. The overlay appears and vanishes inside two frames on a fast connection, producing a black flash on every load that reads as a broken paint and is nearly impossible to reproduce on a throttled dev machine.\n- Starting the floor clock when the signal arrives instead of at mount. The floor is then added to the load time rather than overlapping it, so a 2s load becomes a 2.7s hold for no benefit.\n- Using state for the reveal-once guard. The ceiling and the floor can both fire before React re-renders, so `reveal` runs twice and the second call re-arms the fade on a node that is already leaving. A ref flips synchronously and cannot be raced.\n- Unmounting the overlay in the same commit that starts the fade. The node leaves the tree before the transition can run a single frame, so the overlay simply disappears and the fade you wrote never happens.\n- Reading `sessionStorage` in `useEffect` instead of `useLayoutEffect`. The overlay gets one painted frame before the effect runs, so a returning visitor sees a flash of it on every single navigation.\n- Initialising state from `sessionStorage` during render. The server has no session storage, so it renders `holding` while the client renders `gone`, and React logs a hydration mismatch and re-renders the tree.\n- Calling `sessionStorage.setItem` unguarded. It throws in Safari private browsing, and an unhandled throw inside the reveal path leaves the overlay up permanently — the exact failure the component exists to prevent.\n- Replaying it on every visit. A preloader is an introduction; on the fourth arrival in the same session it is a toll booth, and the fix is one flag rather than a shorter animation.\n- Leaving timers pending on unmount. The removal timer fires after the route has changed, so `setPhase` runs on a dead component and `onDone` fires for a page that is no longer mounted.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `position` defaults to `fixed` for real page use and can be switched to `absolute` so the overlay can be demonstrated, or used, inside a bounded container.\n- The overlay is `role=\"status\"` with `aria-live=\"polite\"`, so a screen reader announces the loading state once rather than reading a decorative bar, and the announcement is not interrupted by the reveal.\n- `once={false}` exists for development and for the demo. In production the default keeps the overlay to one appearance per tab session.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nSimulate a signal that never arrives and the page is nonetheless visible and interactive at the ceiling. Reload with a warm cache and the overlay is visible for at least the floor — never a single-frame flash. Reload again in the same tab and no overlay is painted at all, not even one frame. Watch the reveal and the overlay fades rather than vanishing, with the content already composited underneath. Navigate away mid-hold and no state update warning appears and `onDone` never fires. With reduced motion on, the plate appears static and leaves immediately.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "sankey-chart-card": "# Build: Sankey Chart Card\n\nA Sankey diagram in the BoardUI chart card shell. The layout is computed in the component: columns by link depth, nodes stacked to one shared scale, ribbons ordered so they only cross where the data does. Each ribbon takes its source node's colour, sources are labelled with name and value on the left, sinks with their share on the right. Hovering or focusing a node, or hovering a ribbon, lights the whole path through it, dims everything else and swaps the headline for that node or flow.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSource-to-sink flows as tinted SVG ribbons in a dashboard card: hover a node or ribbon to isolate its path while the headline follows.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Links reference nodes by name or index. Unknown references, self-links and non-positive values are dropped. Depths are relaxed across the links (a node sits one column right of its deepest source), with the passes capped at the node count so a cycle cannot loop forever.\n2. A node's value is the larger of its inflow and outflow. Each column needs `(height - 14px * (n - 1)) / sum` px per unit; the smallest of those is used for every column, so equal hours are equal heights everywhere. Each column is then centred vertically.\n3. Ribbons leave a source stacked in the vertical order of their targets and arrive at a target stacked in the order of their sources. Each is a filled path: two cubic Béziers whose control points sit at the horizontal midpoint, so the band keeps its thickness at both ends and eases between them.\n4. The chart width comes from `useResizeObserver`. The label gutters are `clamp(14% of width, 72, 96)` on the left and `clamp(26%, 104, 168)` on the right. Below 440px the labels drop to 12px names and the sink labels stack the share under the name.\n5. Hover is `{ kind: \"node\" | \"link\", index }`. `litPath` walks upstream and downstream from the node (or from the ribbon's two ends), collecting links and nodes. Lit ribbons go to 0.65 fill-opacity, the rest to 0.1 (0.4 at rest), and unlit nodes and labels fade over 200ms.\n6. The labels are absolutely positioned `<button>`s carrying a full description (\"Focus: 32h, 37% of total. to Browsing 9h, …\"), so keyboard and screen-reader users get every flow. Focus lights the same path as hover.\n7. A clipPath rect sweeps from the first column to the right edge over 800ms (ease-out cubic) on mount and on every period change. `useSweep` resets to 0 during render when the key changes, so no frame shows the old ribbons at full width.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `NODE_W = 12px, rx 3`\n  Wide enough to read as a bar and to hover, narrow enough that the ribbons carry the picture.\n- `NODE_GAP = 14px`\n  At the default 340px height with seven sinks, this leaves 256px for 86h (about 3px per hour) and room for a two-line label beside a 7h node.\n- `RIBBON = rest 0.4, lit 0.65, dim 0.1 fill-opacity`\n  At rest overlaps stay readable; on hover the lit path stands out and the rest almost disappears without vanishing.\n- `SWEEP_MS = 800ms ease-out cubic`\n  The sweep reads as flow from sources to sinks. A new period redraws instead of morphing, because node order and counts can change between ranges.\n- `COMPACT_W = 440px`\n  Below it, \"Everything else · 13%\" no longer fits the right gutter on one line, so the share moves under a 12px name.\n- `chartHeight = 340px`\n  Matches the preview's proportion with seven sinks.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SankeyChartCard(props: { title?: string; ranges?: FlowRange[]; nodes?: FlowNode[]; links?: FlowLink[]; delta?: number; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; sourceCaption?: string; targetCaption?: string; chartHeight?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype FlowNode = { name: string; color?: ChartColor }\ntype FlowLink = { source: string | number; target: string | number; value: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Tracked time\"`\n  Header label while nothing is hovered; also the card's accessible name.\n- `ranges: FlowRange[]` — default `TRACKED_TIME_RANGES`\n  Periods with their own nodes, links and optional delta/headline. Two or more show the period select.\n- `nodes: FlowNode[]`\n  Static nodes; used with `links` instead of `ranges`.\n- `links: FlowLink[]`\n  Static links `{ source, target, value }`, by node name or index.\n- `delta: number`\n  Headline chip percentage for static data.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the period select changes.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `whole hours, e.g. \"86h\"`\n  Formats the headline, the source labels and the screen-reader descriptions.\n- `sourceCaption: string` — default `\"Tracked time\"`\n  Caption under the left column.\n- `targetCaption: string` — default `\"Share of tracked time\"`\n  Caption under the right column.\n- `chartHeight: number` — default `340`\n  Chart height in px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Columns come from link depth (a node sits one column right of its deepest\n * source). Every column is stacked to the same px-per-unit scale, the\n * tightest one any column allows, then centred. Ribbons leave a node in the\n * vertical order of their targets and arrive in the order of their sources,\n * so they only cross where the data makes them.\n */\nfunction layoutSankey(\n  nodesIn: readonly FlowNode[],\n  linksIn: readonly FlowLink[],\n  box: { width: number; height: number; left: number; right: number },\n): Layout {\n  const byName = new Map(nodesIn.map((node, index) => [node.name, index] as const));\n  const resolve = (ref: string | number) =>\n    typeof ref === \"number\" ? (ref >= 0 && ref < nodesIn.length ? ref : -1) : (byName.get(ref) ?? -1);\n  const links = linksIn\n    .map((link) => ({ source: resolve(link.source), target: resolve(link.target), value: link.value }))\n    .filter((link) => link.source >= 0 && link.target >= 0 && link.source !== link.target && link.value > 0);\n\n  const count = nodesIn.length;\n  const depth = new Array<number>(count).fill(0);\n  // Relax depths; capping the passes at the node count keeps a cycle from looping forever.\n  for (let pass = 0; pass < count; pass++) {\n    let changed = false;\n    for (const link of links) {\n      const next = (depth[link.source] ?? 0) + 1;\n      if (next > (depth[link.target] ?? 0) && next < count) {\n        depth[link.target] = next;\n        changed = true;\n      }\n    }\n    if (!changed) break;\n  }\n  const maxDepth = Math.max(0, ...depth);\n\n  const inSum = new Array<number>(count).fill(0);\n  const outSum = new Array<number>(count).fill(0);\n  for (const link of links) {\n    outSum[link.source] = (outSum[link.source] ?? 0) + link.value;\n    inSum[link.target] = (inSum[link.target] ?? 0) + link.value;\n  }\n  const values = nodesIn.map((_, i) => Math.max(inSum[i] ?? 0, outSum[i] ?? 0));\n\n  const columns: number[][] = Array.from({ length: maxDepth + 1 }, () => []);\n  depth.forEach((d, i) => columns[d]?.push(i));\n\n  let scale = Infinity;\n  for (const column of columns) {\n    const sum = column.reduce((acc, i) => acc + (values[i] ?? 0), 0);\n    if (sum > 0) scale = Math.min(scale, (box.height - NODE_GAP * (column.length - 1)) / sum);\n  }\n  if (!Number.isFinite(scale) || scale < 0) scale = 0;\n\n  const span = Math.max(0, box.width - box.left - box.right - NODE_W);\n  let firstOrdinal = 0;\n  const nodes: LaidNode[] = nodesIn.map((node, index) => {\n    const d = depth[index] ?? 0;\n    const colorKey = node.color ?? (d === 0 ? chartColorAt(firstOrdinal++) : null);\n    return {\n      index,\n      name: node.name,\n      colorKey,\n      fill: colorKey ? CHART_COLORS[colorKey].fill : NEUTRAL_FILL,\n      depth: d,\n      value: values[index] ?? 0,\n      x: box.left + (maxDepth > 0 ? (d / maxDepth) * span : 0),\n      y0: 0,\n      y1: 0,\n    };\n  });\n\n  for (const column of columns) {\n    const used =\n      column.reduce((acc, i) => acc + (values[i] ?? 0) * scale, 0) + NODE_GAP * Math.max(0, column.length - 1);\n    let y = (box.height - used) / 2;\n    for (const i of column) {\n      const node = nodes[i];\n      if (!node) continue;\n      node.y0 = y;\n      node.y1 = y + node.value * scale;\n      y = node.y1 + NODE_GAP;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const top = (i: number) => nodes[i]?.y0 ?? 0;\n  const outOffset = nodes.map((node) => node.y0);\n  const inOffset = nodes.map((node) => node.y0);\n  const sy = new Array<number>(links.length).fill(0);\n  const ty = new Array<number>(links.length).fill(0);\n  const order = links.map((_, i) => i);\n  for (const i of [...order].sort((a, b) => top(links[a]!.target) - top(links[b]!.target) || a - b)) {\n    const link = links[i]!;\n    sy[i] = outOffset[link.source] ?? 0;\n    outOffset[link.source] = sy[i]! + link.value * scale;\n  }\n  for (const i of [...order].sort((a, b) => top(links[a]!.source) - top(links[b]!.source) || a - b)) {\n    const link = links[i]!;\n    ty[i] = inOffset[link.target] ?? 0;\n    inOffset[link.target] = ty[i]! + link.value * scale;\n  }\n\n  const laidLinks: LaidLink[] = links.map((link, index) => {\n    const from = nodes[link.source]!;\n    const to = nodes[link.target]!;\n    const h = link.value * scale;\n    const x0 = r2(from.x + NODE_W);\n    const x1 = r2(to.x);\n    const xm = r2((x0 + x1) / 2);\n    const s0 = r2(sy[index] ?? 0);\n    const s1 = r2((sy[index] ?? 0) + h);\n    const t0 = r2(ty[index] ?? 0);\n    const t1 = r2((ty[index] ?? 0) + h);\n    return {\n      index,\n      source: link.source,\n      target: link.target,\n      value: link.value,\n      fill: from.fill,\n      d: `M${x0} ${s0}C${xm} ${s0} ${xm} ${t0} ${x1} ${t0}V${t1}C${xm} ${t1} ${xm} ${s1} ${x0} ${s1}Z`,\n    };\n  });\n\n  const total = nodes.filter((node) => node.depth === 0).reduce((acc, node) => acc + node.value, 0);\n  return { nodes, links: laidLinks, maxDepth, total };\n}\n\n/** Everything upstream and downstream of the hovered node, or of the hovered ribbon's ends. */\nfunction litPath(hover: Hover, layout: Layout) {\n  if (!hover) return null;\n  const nodes = new Set<number>();\n  const links = new Set<number>();\n  const walk = (start: number, dir: \"up\" | \"down\") => {\n    const queue = [start];\n    while (queue.length) {\n      const at = queue.shift()!;\n      for (const link of layout.links) {\n        const from = dir === \"down\" ? link.source : link.target;\n        const to = dir === \"down\" ? link.target : link.source;\n        if (from !== at || links.has(link.index)) continue;\n        links.add(link.index);\n        nodes.add(to);\n        queue.push(to);\n      }\n    }\n  };\n  if (hover.kind === \"node\") {\n    nodes.add(hover.index);\n    walk(hover.index, \"up\");\n    walk(hover.index, \"down\");\n  } else {\n    const link = layout.links[hover.index];\n    if (!link) return null;\n    links.add(link.index);\n    nodes.add(link.source).add(link.target);\n    walk(link.source, \"up\");\n    walk(link.target, \"down\");\n  }\n  return { nodes, links };\n}\n\n/** 0 to 1 eased progress that restarts whenever `key` changes; 1 throughout when disabled. */\nfunction useSweep(key: string, disabled: boolean) {\n  const [progress, setProgress] = useState(0);\n  const [lastKey, setLastKey] = useState(key);\n  if (lastKey !== key) {\n    setLastKey(key);\n    setProgress(0);\n  }\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (disabled) return;\n    const start = performance.now();\n    let raf = 0;\n    const tick = (now: number) => {\n      // rAF's timestamp is the frame's start, which can precede the effect's\n      // performance.now() after a long commit; unclamped, t < 0 gives a\n      // negative clip width.\n      const t = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (now - start) / SWEEP_MS));\n      setProgress(easeOutCubic(t));\n      if (t < 1) raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    };\n    raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(raf);\n  }, [key, disabled]);\n  return disabled ? 1 : progress;\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The sweep's and the count-up's animation frames are cancelled on every restart and on unmount. `useResizeObserver` unobserves the chart box on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useSweep` is disabled, so the ribbons are drawn in full at once on mount and on a period change. The headline count runs over 1ms, and the opacity transitions and number fade are removed with `motion-reduce:` variants.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Stroking a centre line with `stroke-width` for ribbons. The stroke keeps its width perpendicular to the curve, so on a slope the band visibly thins and the ends no longer match the node slices. Filled ribbons keep exact heights at both ends.\n- Scaling each column to fill the height on its own. A column with fewer nodes would get a bigger px-per-unit than its neighbours, and ribbons would change thickness between columns.\n- Sorting a node's outgoing ribbons by value instead of by target position. Ribbons then cross right next to the node and the diagram turns to noise.\n- Leaving the old ribbons visible for a frame when the period changes. `useSweep` resets its progress during render, not in an effect.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `nodes` and `links` for one static data set (no period select), or `ranges` for a select. `TRACKED_TIME_RANGES`, `FlowNode`, `FlowLink` and `FlowRange` are exported.\n- More than two columns work: middle nodes are labelled on their right with their share of the total. Labels of very small neighbouring nodes can overlap; raise `chartHeight` for dense columns.\n- The frame, header and period select come from `funnel-chart-card-shell`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card reads \"Tracked time\" and \"86h\" with a \"This week\" select. Sources Focus 32h (emerald), Meetings 18h (purple), Breaks 12h (yellow), Admin 14h (blue) and Learning 10h (pink) sit on the left. On the right are grey sinks Browsing · 22%, Writing · 18%, Messaging · 13%, Productivity · 12%, Email · 8%, Video calls · 14% and Everything else · 13%. Hovering Focus dims the other sources' ribbons and the header shows \"Focus\", \"32h\" and \"37% of total\". Hovering a ribbon shows e.g. \"Focus → Browsing\". Choosing \"Last week\" redraws the ribbons with a sweep, and the headline becomes 79h.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "scatter-chart-card": "# Build: Scatter Chart Card\n\nA grey dashboard card that plots one dot per record against two measures with a Recharts `ScatterChart`, one `Scatter` per series in its own chart token. When any point carries a `z`, the dots become bubbles sized by it. Hovering a dot, or focusing the plot and pressing the arrow keys, puts that record in the count-up headline with its gap to the average, dims the other series and floats a small readout over the dot. White tiles under the plot give each series' average, and a period pill swaps the dataset through a dropdown.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dashboard scatter card: records grouped into coloured series, sized by a third measure, with a hover headline, readout and average tiles.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Derive a model from the active series in one `useMemo`: x ticks are `niceTicks(xMax, 4)` and y ticks `niceTicks(yMax, 3)`, where `niceStep` rounds `max / count` up to two significant digits in fives (533 → 550, 24.3 → 25). The last tick becomes the axis domain, so the top grid line and the highest dot share a scale. The model also holds the z range, the overall and per-series averages of `y`, and a keyboard order of every record sorted by x then y.\n2. Render `ScatterChart` with fixed geometry: margin `{ top: 20, right: 12, bottom: 0, left: 0 }`, a 40px `YAxis`, a 26px `XAxis` and a 208px plot box. Ticks are 12px in `--color-text-tertiary`, the y ticks run through `Intl.NumberFormat` compact notation (\"1.1K\"), and a dashed `CartesianGrid` in `--color-chart-track` draws both directions. `accessibilityLayer` is off because the wrapper is the single tab stop.\n3. Each `Scatter` gets a closure `shape` that draws a `<g>` holding the dot: the radius is 5 for plain dots or lerped 6 → 14 across the z range for bubbles. Fill is the series token at 0.85 opacity with a 1.25px ring in its `-active` shade. The active record gets a halo at radius + 5 and 0.3 opacity, and its ring turns into a 2px stroke in the card colour. Every other series drops to opacity 0.22 through a 150ms opacity transition. `onMouseEnter`/`onMouseLeave` on the group set and clear `active`.\n4. The wrapper div repeats Recharts' geometry to place HTML over the plot: `left = 40 + x / xMax * (width - 52)` and `top = 20 + (1 - y / yMax) * 162`, with `width` from `useResizeObserver`. The readout sits above the dot at radius + 10px and flips its horizontal anchor within 90px of either edge. It shows the record name, then the x and y measures under the axis captions.\n5. The wrapper is `tabIndex=0` with `role=\"group\"` and an aria-label that lists each series average. Arrow Right/Up and Left/Down step through the sorted records, Home and End jump to the ends, and Escape or blur clears the selection.\n6. The header shows `title` and the overall average, or \"Record · Series\" and that record's y. The value runs through `useCountUp` (bypassed under reduced motion) inside a `<p>` keyed by the active record, so `.animate-number-fade` replays. The chip shows the period `change`, or the record's percent gap to the average with a \"vs avg\" suffix, in lime when the value is zero or above and in rose below zero.\n7. When `ranges` is passed, the pill is a kit `Dropdown`. Selecting an item updates the range (controlled through `range`/`onRangeChange`, otherwise held in state), clears the active record and closes the menu. Without `ranges` the pill is a static span showing `periodLabel`. Hovering a tile or a legend row sets `hoveredSeries`, which dims the other series the same way an active dot does.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `plot geometry = height 208, margin top 20 / right 12, YAxis 40, XAxis 26`\n  The readout and the keyboard focus are placed in HTML from these numbers. If any of them changes in only one place, the readout drifts off its dot. The 20px top margin leaves room for the largest bubble's halo (14 + 5px) above the top grid line.\n- `bubble radius = 6 → 14px over the z range; 5px plain dots`\n  Below 6px a bubble reads as a plain dot, and above 14px the top-plan bubbles overlap their neighbours on a 576px card.\n- `dim opacity = 0.22 over 150ms`\n  Low enough that the active series reads as the only one on the plot, high enough that the other series still show where they sit.\n- `tick counts = x 4 intervals, y 3 intervals, nice step in fives at two significant digits`\n  Gives 0 / 25 / 50 / 75 / 100 and 0 / 550 / 1.1K / 1.7K for the default data, so the labels stay short at 12px.\n- `default averages = Starter 270, Growth 783, Scale 1,388; overall 744`\n  The demo data is chosen so the tiles show these figures. The headline is the mean of all 17 records, not of the three series averages.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ScatterChartCard(props?: ScatterChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype ScatterPoint = { x: number; y: number; z?: number; label?: string }\ntype ScatterSeries = { label: string; points: ScatterPoint[]; color?: string }\ntype ScatterRange = { id: string; label: string; series: ScatterSeries[]; change?: number }\nconst SCATTER_SERIES: ScatterSeries[]\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Revenue per account\"`\n  Header label while no record is active.\n- `series: ScatterSeries[]` — default `SCATTER_SERIES`\n  Records grouped into series. Ignored when `ranges` is passed.\n- `change: number` — default `6.8`\n  Percent change shown in the chip when `ranges` is not used.\n- `periodLabel: string` — default `\"This quarter\"`\n  Text of the static period pill. Pass an empty string to hide it.\n- `ranges: ScatterRange[]`\n  Periods to choose from. The pill becomes a dropdown and each range brings its own series and change.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled active range id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range`\n  Uncontrolled starting range id.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the id picked from the period menu.\n- `axisLabels: [string, string]` — default `[\"Sessions\", \"Revenue\"]`\n  Captions for the x and y measures, shown under the plot and in the readout.\n- `format: (value: number) => string` — default `rounded en-US number`\n  Formats y values in the headline, tiles and readout.\n- `formatX: (value: number) => string` — default `rounded en-US number`\n  Formats x values in the readout.\n- `bubble: boolean` — default `true when any point has z`\n  Size dots by `z`. False forces 5px dots.\n- `tiles: boolean` — default `true`\n  Average tiles under the plot. False shows a centred one-line legend.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ScatterChartCard({\n  title = \"Revenue per account\",\n  series: seriesProp = SCATTER_SERIES,\n  change: changeProp = 6.8,\n  periodLabel = \"This quarter\",\n  ranges,\n  range: rangeProp,\n  defaultRange,\n  onRangeChange,\n  axisLabels = [\"Sessions\", \"Revenue\"],\n  format = defaultFormat,\n  formatX = defaultFormat,\n  bubble,\n  tiles = true,\n  className,\n}: ScatterChartCardProps = {}) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const labelId = useId();\n  const plotRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const { width } = useResizeObserver(plotRef);\n\n  const [innerRange, setInnerRange] = useState(defaultRange ?? ranges?.[0]?.id);\n  const [menuOpen, setMenuOpen] = useState(false);\n  const rangeId = rangeProp ?? innerRange;\n  const activeRange = ranges?.find((r) => r.id === rangeId) ?? ranges?.[0];\n  const series = activeRange?.series ?? seriesProp;\n  const change = activeRange ? activeRange.change : changeProp;\n\n  const [active, setActive] = useState<Active>(null);\n  const [hoveredSeries, setHoveredSeries] = useState<number | null>(null);\n\n  const model = useMemo(() => {\n    const all = series.flatMap((s) => s.points);\n    const xMaxRaw = Math.max(1, ...all.map((p) => p.x));\n    const yMaxRaw = Math.max(1, ...all.map((p) => p.y));\n    const zs = all.map((p) => p.z).filter((z): z is number => z !== undefined);\n    const xTicks = niceTicks(xMaxRaw, 4);\n    const yTicks = niceTicks(yMaxRaw, 3);\n    const averages = series.map((s) =>\n      s.points.length ? s.points.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.y, 0) / s.points.length : 0,\n    );\n    // Keyboard order: every record left to right, then bottom to top.\n    const order = series\n      .flatMap((s, si) => s.points.map((p, pi) => ({ s: si, p: pi, x: p.x, y: p.y })))\n      .sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x || a.y - b.y);\n    return {\n      xMax: xTicks[xTicks.length - 1] ?? xMaxRaw,\n      yMax: yTicks[yTicks.length - 1] ?? yMaxRaw,\n      xTicks,\n      yTicks,\n      zMin: zs.length ? Math.min(...zs) : 0,\n      zMax: zs.length ? Math.max(...zs) : 0,\n      hasZ: zs.length > 0,\n      overall: all.length ? all.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.y, 0) / all.length : 0,\n      averages,\n      order,\n    };\n  }, [series]);\n\n  const useBubbles = bubble ?? model.hasZ;\n  const radiusOf = (point: ScatterPoint) => {\n    if (!useBubbles || point.z === undefined || model.zMax === model.zMin) return useBubbles ? 9 : DOT_RADIUS;\n    const t = (point.z - model.zMin) / (model.zMax - model.zMin);\n    return BUBBLE_RADIUS[0] + t * (BUBBLE_RADIUS[1] - BUBBLE_RADIUS[0]);\n  };\n\n  const activePoint = active ? series[active.s]?.points[active.p] : undefined;\n  const activeSeries = activePoint && active ? active.s : hoveredSeries;\n\n  const headlineValue = activePoint ? activePoint.y : model.overall;\n  const counted = useCountUp(Math.round(headlineValue));\n  const shown = reduced ? Math.round(headlineValue) : counted;\n  const pointDelta =\n    activePoint && model.overall > 0 ? Math.round(((activePoint.y - model.overall) / model.overall) * 1000) / 10 : null;\n  const chipValue = activePoint ? pointDelta : change;\n  const colorAt = (i: number) => {\n    const custom = series[i]?.color;\n    const slot = PALETTE[i % PALETTE.length] ?? PALETTE[0];\n    return custom ? { fill: custom, ring: custom } : slot;\n  };\n\n  // Pixel centre of a record, from the same geometry Recharts lays out.\n  const plotLeft = MARGIN.left + Y_AXIS_WIDTH;\n  const plotWidth = Math.max(0, width - plotLeft - MARGIN.right);\n  const plotHeight = CHART_HEIGHT - MARGIN.top - MARGIN.bottom - X_AXIS_HEIGHT;\n  const positionOf = (point: ScatterPoint) => ({\n    left: plotLeft + (point.x / model.xMax) * plotWidth,\n    top: MARGIN.top + (1 - point.y / model.yMax) * plotHeight,\n  });\n\n  const step = (key: string) => {\n    const { order } = model;\n    if (!order.length) return;\n    const at = active ? order.findIndex((o) => o.s === active.s && o.p === active.p) : -1;\n    let next = at;\n    if (key === \"ArrowRight\" || key === \"ArrowUp\") next = at < 0 ? 0 : Math.min(order.length - 1, at + 1);\n    else if (key === \"ArrowLeft\" || key === \"ArrowDown\") next = at < 0 ? order.length - 1 : Math.max(0, at - 1);\n    else if (key === \"Home\") next = 0;\n    else if (key === \"End\") next = order.length - 1;\n    const target = order[next];\n    if (target) setActive({ s: target.s, p: target.p });\n  };\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n    if ([\"ArrowRight\", \"ArrowLeft\", \"ArrowUp\", \"ArrowDown\", \"Home\", \"End\"].includes(event.key)) {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      step(event.key);\n    } else if (event.key === \"Escape\" && active) {\n      setActive(null);\n    }\n  };\n\n  const renderDot = (si: number) =>\n    function dot({ cx: x, cy: y, payload }: ShapeProps) {\n      if (x === undefined || y === undefined || !payload) return <g />;\n      const isActive = active?.s === si && active.p === payload.pointIndex;\n      const dimmed = activeSeries !== null && activeSeries !== si;\n      const { fill, ring } = colorAt(si);\n      const r = radiusOf(payload);\n      return (\n        <g\n          className=\"cursor-pointer transition-opacity duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n          style={{ opacity: dimmed ? 0.22 : 1 }}\n          onMouseEnter={() => setActive({ s: si, p: payload.pointIndex })}\n          onMouseLeave={() => setActive(null)}\n        >\n          {isActive && <circle cx={x} cy={y} r={r + 5} fill={fill} opacity={0.3} />}\n          <circle\n            cx={x}\n            cy={y}\n            r={r}\n            fill={fill}\n            fillOpacity={useBubbles ? 0.85 : 1}\n            stroke={isActive ? \"var(--color-background-secondary-default)\" : ring}\n            strokeWidth={isActive ? 2 : 1.25}\n          />\n        </g>\n      );\n    };\n\n  const pill = (\n    <>\n      <RiCalendarLine className=\"size-4 shrink-0 text-text-secondary\" aria-hidden />\n      <span className=\"whitespace-nowrap\">{activeRange?.label ?? periodLabel}</span>\n    </>\n  );\n  const pillClass =\n    \"inline-flex h-8 shrink-0 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default px-2.5 text-body-medium text-text-primary\";\n\n  const tooltipPos = activePoint && width > 0 ? positionOf(activePoint) : null;\n  const tooltipLift = activePoint ? radiusOf(activePoint) + 10 : 0;\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      aria-labelledby={labelId}\n      className={cx(\"flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-3 rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default p-2\", className)}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 px-2 pt-2\">\n        {/* Header: title or active record, count-up value, change chip; period pill on the right */}\n        <div className=\"flex items-start justify-between gap-3\">\n          <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n            <p id={labelId} className=\"text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">\n              {activePoint && active\n                ? `${activePoint.label ?? \"Record\"} · ${series[active.s]?.label ?? \"\"}`\n                : title}\n            </p>\n            <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n              <p\n                key={active ? `${active.s}-${active.p}` : \"all\"}\n                className=\"animate-number-fade text-title-1-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums motion-reduce:animate-none\"\n              >\n                {format(shown)}\n              </p>\n              {chipValue !== undefined && chipValue !== null && (\n                <Chip variant=\"bold\" color={chipValue >= 0 ? \"lime\" : \"rose\"} className=\"tabular-nums\">\n                  {chipValue > 0 ? \"+\" : \"\"}\n                  {chipValue}%{activePoint ? \" vs avg\" : \"\"}\n                </Chip>\n              )}\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          {ranges && ranges.length > 0 ? (\n            <Dropdown isOpen={menuOpen} onOpenChange={setMenuOpen}>\n              <DropdownTrigger aria-label={`Period: ${activeRange?.label ?? \"\"}`} className={cx(pillClass, \"hover:bg-background-secondary-hover\")}>\n                {pill}\n                <RiArrowDownSLine\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"size-4 shrink-0 text-text-secondary transition-transform duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                    menuOpen && \"rotate-180\",\n                  )}\n                  aria-hidden\n                />\n              </DropdownTrigger>\n              <DropdownPopover aria-label=\"Period\" placement=\"bottom end\" className=\"w-44\">\n                {ranges.map((r) => (\n                  <DropdownItem\n                    key={r.id}\n                    selected={r.id === activeRange?.id}\n                    onSelect={() => {\n                      setInnerRange(r.id);\n                      onRangeChange?.(r.id);\n                      setActive(null);\n                      setMenuOpen(false);\n                    }}\n                  >\n                    {r.label}\n                  </DropdownItem>\n                ))}\n              </DropdownPopover>\n            </Dropdown>\n          ) : periodLabel ? (\n            <span className={pillClass}>{pill}</span>\n          ) : null}\n        </div>\n\n        {/* Plot: Recharts draws axes, grid and dots; the readout is HTML over it */}\n        <div\n          ref={plotRef}\n          tabIndex={0}\n          role=\"group\"\n          aria-roledescription=\"scatter chart\"\n          aria-label={`${title}: ${series.map((s, i) => `${s.label} average ${format(model.averages[i] ?? 0)}`).join(\", \")}. Arrow keys step through records.`}\n          onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n          onBlur={() => setActive(null)}\n          className=\"relative w-full rounded-lg outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\"\n          style={{ height: CHART_HEIGHT }}\n        >\n          <ResponsiveContainer width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\">\n            <ScatterChart margin={MARGIN} accessibilityLayer={false}>\n              <CartesianGrid stroke=\"var(--color-chart-track)\" strokeDasharray=\"3 3\" />\n              <XAxis\n                type=\"number\"\n                dataKey=\"x\"\n                domain={[0, model.xMax]}\n                ticks={model.xTicks}\n                height={X_AXIS_HEIGHT}\n                tickLine={false}\n                axisLine={false}\n                tickMargin={8}\n                tick={{ fontSize: 12, fill: \"var(--color-text-tertiary)\" }}\n              />\n              <YAxis\n                type=\"number\"\n                dataKey=\"y\"\n                domain={[0, model.yMax]}\n                ticks={model.yTicks}\n                width={Y_AXIS_WIDTH}\n                tickLine={false}\n                axisLine={false}\n                tickMargin={6}\n                tickFormatter={(n: number) => compact.format(n)}\n                tick={{ fontSize: 12, fill: \"var(--color-text-tertiary)\" }}\n              />\n              {series.map((s, si) => (\n                <Scatter\n                  key={s.label}\n                  name={s.label}\n                  data={s.points.map((p, pointIndex) => ({ ...p, pointIndex }))}\n                  shape={renderDot(si)}\n                  isAnimationActive={!reduced}\n                  animationDuration={450}\n                />\n              ))}\n            </ScatterChart>\n          </ResponsiveContainer>\n\n          {activePoint && active && tooltipPos && (\n            <div\n              role=\"presentation\"\n              className=\"pointer-events-none absolute z-10 flex min-w-32 flex-col gap-1 rounded-lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default px-2.5 py-2 shadow-dropdown\"\n              style={{\n                left: tooltipPos.left,\n                top: tooltipPos.top - tooltipLift,\n                transform: `translate(${tooltipPos.left > width - 90 ? \"-90%\" : tooltipPos.left < 90 ? \"-10%\" : \"-50%\"}, -100%)`,\n              }}\n            >\n              <span className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5 text-body-2-medium text-text-primary\">\n                <span className=\"size-2 shrink-0 rounded-sm\" style={{ backgroundColor: colorAt(active.s).fill }} aria-hidden />\n                {activePoint.label ?? series[active.s]?.label}\n              </span>\n              <span className=\"flex justify-between gap-3 text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">\n                {axisLabels[0]}\n                <span className=\"text-text-primary\">{formatX(activePoint.x)}</span>\n              </span>\n              <span className=\"flex justify-between gap-3 text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary tabular-nums\">\n                {axisLabels[1]}\n                <span className=\"text-text-primary\">{format(activePoint.y)}</span>\n              </span>\n            </div>\n          )}\n        </div>\n\n        {/* Axis captions under the plot: y measure left, x measure right */}\n        <div className=\"flex justify-between text-body-2-medium text-text-tertiary\">\n          <span>{axisLabels[1]}</span>\n          <span>{axisLabels[0]}</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Per-series averages: tiles, or a single centred legend line */}\n      {tiles ? (\n        <ul className=\"grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(6rem,1fr))] gap-1.5\">\n          {series.map((s, i) => (\n            <li\n              key={s.label}\n              onMouseEnter={() => setHoveredSeries(i)}\n              onMouseLeave={() => setHoveredSeries(null)}\n              className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5 rounded-xl bg-background-primary-default px-2.5 py-2\"\n            >\n              <span className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5 text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">\n                <span className=\"size-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-[3px]\" style={{ backgroundColor: colorAt(i).fill }} aria-hidden />\n                <span className=\"truncate\">{s.label} · avg</span>\n              </span>\n              <span className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums\">{format(model.averages[i] ?? 0)}</span>\n            </li>\n          ))}\n        </ul>\n      ) : (\n        <ul className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-4 gap-y-1 px-2 pb-2\">\n          {series.map((s, i) => (\n            <li\n              key={s.label}\n              onMouseEnter={() => setHoveredSeries(i)}\n              onMouseLeave={() => setHoveredSeries(null)}\n              className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5 text-body-medium text-text-secondary\"\n            >\n              <span className=\"size-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-[3px]\" style={{ backgroundColor: colorAt(i).fill }} aria-hidden />\n              {s.label}\n              <span className=\"text-text-primary tabular-nums\">{format(model.averages[i] ?? 0)}</span>\n            </li>\n          ))}\n        </ul>\n      )}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useResizeObserver` unregisters the plot wrapper from the shared observer on unmount. `useCountUp` cancels its animation frame when the target changes and on unmount. `ResponsiveContainer` disconnects its own observer. The dropdown's outside-press listener is removed when the menu closes. Pointer and key handlers are React props.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the scatter entrance animation is off (`isAnimationActive={false}`), the headline shows its value with no count-up, and `.animate-number-fade` is removed with `motion-reduce:animate-none`. The series dimming and the chevron rotation change instantly through `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Letting Recharts pick the axis domains. Its automatic domain can end above the last tick, and the readout's position, computed from `xMax` and `yMax`, would no longer line up with the dots. The domains are fixed to the last nice tick.\n- Leaving `accessibilityLayer` on. The SVG becomes a second tab stop whose arrow keys move a tooltip that is not rendered, next to the wrapper's own record navigation.\n- Averaging the series averages for the headline. Series have different sizes, so the overall figure is the mean of every record.\n- Drawing the readout inside the SVG. Text in SVG cannot wrap or size itself to its content. The readout is an HTML layer over the plot with `pointer-events: none`, so it never steals the hover from the dot under it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `SCATTER_SERIES` and the `ScatterPoint`, `ScatterSeries` and `ScatterRange` types are exported. A series `color` takes any CSS colour and is used for the fill, the ring and the swatch.\n- Pass `tiles={false}` for the compact legend line, and `bubble={false}` for even dots when the data carries `z`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Revenue per account\", \"744\" and a lime \"+6.8%\" chip, with a \"This quarter\" pill. The plot has lime, blue and purple bubbles on a dashed grid with ticks 0–100 and 0–1.7K, \"Revenue\" and \"Sessions\" captions, and tiles reading Starter · avg 270, Growth · avg 783 and Scale · avg 1,388. Hovering the top-right bubble changes the header to \"Quill · Scale\" and \"1,640\", dims the lime and blue dots and shows a readout with Sessions 98. Focusing the plot and pressing End does the same. Picking \"This year\" from the pill redraws the plot with larger values and a \"+18.2%\" chip.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "scroll-deform-wave": "# Build: Scroll Deformation Wave\n\nSigned, smoothed scroll velocity drives a single scalar, and that scalar scales two sine displacements in the vertex shader. Because the velocity keeps its sign, scrolling up bends the surface the opposite way from scrolling down, which is the direction cue that makes the effect read as momentum rather than as noise. Everything else — the wave shape, the shading, the crop — is a pure function of that one number.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn image that ripples with scroll speed and bends the other way on the way back, from two sine terms.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The depth term is `pos.z += sin(position.y * frequency + phase) * deform * amplitude`, and the shear term is `pos.y += sin(position.x * frequency) * deform * shear`. Both are sampled from `position` — the original flat attribute — not from the running `pos`.\n2. That distinction is the whole technique. Sampling `position` fixes the wave to the picture, so a given pixel always sits at the same point on the sine and the image appears to flex. Sampling the already-displaced `pos` would make each term feed the next, so the wave travels through the surface and reads as a glitch.\n3. The phase constant breaks the symmetry. Without it, `sin(y * f)` is odd about the plane's centre line and the top and bottom halves mirror each other exactly, which looks like a crease rather than a wave.\n4. The slope, not the height, drives the shading: `vBend = cos(position.y * frequency + phase) * deform` is the analytic derivative of the depth term, so crests brighten and troughs darken with one cosine and no recomputed vertex normals.\n5. `useScrollVelocity` supplies a two-stage smoothed signal — a low-pass on the raw delta, then a damped approach — and the component damps once more on top of it with a 0.0025 smoothing factor, so the ripple eases out for a beat after the scroll stops instead of snapping flat.\n6. The fragment shader cover-fits the source into the quad and divides the sampled window by an overscan factor, which is what keeps the displaced vertices from sampling past the texture edge at the crest of every wave.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `amplitude = 0.07`\n  World units of depth at full deflection, against a plane roughly 2.5 units tall. Below ~0.02 the ripple is lost in the perspective foreshortening; above ~0.2 the crests swing far enough toward the camera to cross the near plane and punch holes in the image.\n- `shear = 0.04`\n  Deliberately about half the amplitude. Its only job is to break the perfectly horizontal banding so the surface reads as cloth; raise it toward the amplitude and the two waves beat against each other into a visible moire.\n- `frequency = 3`\n  Radians per world unit, so roughly one and a half crests across the visible plane. Below ~1.5 there is less than a full wave on screen and the image just tilts; above ~8 the crests fall closer together than the vertex spacing and the wave aliases into flicker.\n- `phase = 100`\n  An arbitrary large offset whose only purpose is to be nowhere near a multiple of pi. At phase 0 the sine is odd about the plane's centre, the halves mirror, and the ripple looks like a fold down the middle.\n- `maxDeform = 1.5`\n  Ceiling on the signed scalar, so a trackpad flick cannot drive the displacement past the point where the surface self-intersects. Everything downstream multiplies by this, so it is the one number that bounds the entire effect.\n- `reference = 900 px/s`\n  Scroll speed that counts as full deflection. The hook defaults to 1500, which is tuned for whole-page parallax; a component-sized scroller rarely reaches it, so the wave would never appear.\n- `DEFORM_SMOOTHING = 0.0025`\n  Fraction of the gap remaining after one second. Slower and the wave lags visibly behind the scroll; much faster and it snaps flat the instant scrolling stops, losing the ease-out that makes it feel like momentum.\n- `overscan = 1.12`\n  Twelve percent crop held in reserve. At exactly 1.0 the displaced vertices sample past the edge of the texture at every crest and the clamped border pixel smears across the top and bottom of the image.\n- `segments = 96`\n  The wave is evaluated per vertex, so subdivision is its resolution. Below ~32 the sine renders as a polyline and the shading term steps in visible bands; above ~160 the vertices land inside a single pixel and only cost transform time.\n- `shade = 0.22`\n  How far the slope term swings brightness. At 0 the ripple is nearly invisible on a busy image; above ~0.5 the crests blow out to white and read as a lighting bug rather than as a surface.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ScrollDeformWave(props: { scrollTarget?: HTMLElement | null; amplitude?: number; shear?: number; frequency?: number; phase?: number; maxDeform?: number; reference?: number; shade?: number; overscan?: number; segments?: number; paint?: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, size: { width: number; height: number }) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `scrollTarget: HTMLElement | null` — default `null`\n  Element whose scrollTop drives the wave. null reads window.scrollY.\n- `amplitude: number` — default `0.07`\n  Depth of the ripple in world units at full deflection.\n- `shear: number` — default `0.04`\n  Vertical shear, which breaks up the horizontal banding.\n- `frequency: number` — default `3`\n  Spatial frequency of the sine, in radians per world unit.\n- `phase: number` — default `100`\n  Phase offset, so the wave is not symmetric about the plane centre.\n- `maxDeform: number` — default `1.5`\n  Clamp on the signed deform scalar.\n- `reference: number` — default `900`\n  Pixels per second of scroll that count as full deflection.\n- `shade: number` — default `0.22`\n  How far the slope term brightens crests and darkens troughs.\n- `overscan: number` — default `1.12`\n  Crop held in reserve so displaced vertices never sample past the edge.\n- `segments: number` — default `96`\n  Plane subdivisions per axis. The resolution of the wave.\n- `paint: (ctx, size) => void`\n  Override the image. Receives a 2D context for a 1024x1024 canvas.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ScrollDeformWave({\n  scrollTarget = null,\n  amplitude = 0.07,\n  shear = 0.04,\n  frequency = 3,\n  phase = 100,\n  maxDeform = 1.5,\n  reference = 900,\n  shade = 0.22,\n  overscan = 1.12,\n  segments = 96,\n  paint,\n  className,\n}: ScrollDeformWaveProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Signed and already smoothed. Raw scroll deltas are spiky enough that\n  // feeding them straight into a geometry parameter produces visible stutter.\n  const scroll = useScrollVelocity({ reference, target: scrollTarget });\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const deform = useRef(0);\n\n  const live = useRef({ amplitude, shear, frequency, phase, maxDeform, shade, overscan });\n  live.current = { amplitude, shear, frequency, phase, maxDeform, shade, overscan };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(CAMERA_FOV, 1, 0.1, 100);\n    camera.position.z = CAMERA_Z;\n\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.width = TEXTURE_WIDTH;\n    canvas.height = TEXTURE_HEIGHT;\n    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n    if (ctx) (paint ?? paintImage)(ctx, { width: TEXTURE_WIDTH, height: TEXTURE_HEIGHT });\n\n    const texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas);\n    texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    texture.wrapS = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n    texture.wrapT = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n    texture.anisotropy = Math.min(4, renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy());\n\n    const uniforms: WaveUniforms = {\n      uTexture: { value: texture },\n      uTextureAspect: { value: TEXTURE_WIDTH / TEXTURE_HEIGHT },\n      uPlaneAspect: { value: 1 },\n      uOverscan: { value: overscan },\n      uShade: { value: shade },\n      uDeform: { value: 0 },\n      uFrequency: { value: frequency },\n      uPhase: { value: phase },\n      uAmplitude: { value: amplitude },\n      uShear: { value: shear },\n    };\n\n    // A unit plane, rescaled on resize rather than rebuilt. The wave is\n    // measured in world units, so the plane keeps a fixed subdivision and the\n    // mesh scale carries the aspect.\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, 1, segments, segments);\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: scrollDeformWaveVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: scrollDeformWaveFragmentShader,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n    });\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    // The bend happens after the bounding sphere was computed, so a plane at\n    // the edge of the frustum blinks out mid-ripple if this stays on.\n    mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(mesh);\n\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, mesh, geometry, material, texture, uniforms };\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      scene.remove(mesh);\n      // Lives inside `uniforms`, which `disposeObject` never walks.\n      texture.dispose();\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, segments, paint]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || width < 1 || height < 1) return;\n    const aspect = width / height;\n    current.renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    current.renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    current.camera.aspect = aspect;\n    current.camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n\n    // Cover the frustum exactly at z = 0, in world units derived from the\n    // camera — not from `window.innerWidth`, which has no fixed relationship\n    // to the visible extent at this distance.\n    const visibleHeight = 2 * Math.tan((CAMERA_FOV * Math.PI) / 360) * CAMERA_Z;\n    current.mesh.scale.set(visibleHeight * aspect, visibleHeight, 1);\n    // The shader fits the texture into the QUAD, so this is the quad's aspect.\n    current.uniforms.uPlaneAspect.value = aspect;\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n      const {\n        amplitude: depth,\n        shear: skew,\n        frequency: freq,\n        phase: offset,\n        maxDeform: limit,\n        shade: light,\n        overscan: zoom,\n      } = live.current;\n\n      // Signed on purpose. Taking the magnitude would make scrolling up and\n      // scrolling down produce an identical wave, and the direction cue — the\n      // surface bending the other way — is most of the effect.\n      const target = reduced ? 0 : clamp(scroll.current.normalized, -1, 1) * limit;\n      deform.current = damp(deform.current, target, DEFORM_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      current.uniforms.uDeform.value = deform.current;\n      current.uniforms.uAmplitude.value = depth;\n      current.uniforms.uShear.value = skew;\n      current.uniforms.uFrequency.value = freq;\n      current.uniforms.uPhase.value = offset;\n      current.uniforms.uShade.value = light;\n      current.uniforms.uOverscan.value = zoom;\n\n      current.renderer.render(current.scene, current.camera);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the mesh from the scene, disposes the `CanvasTexture` by hand because it lives inside `material.uniforms` where `disposeObject` does not look, runs `disposeObject` over the scene, disposes the geometry and the material, then `disposeRenderer` for `setAnimationLoop(null)` / `dispose()` / `forceContextLoss()`, and removes the canvas element. The stage ref is nulled first so a queued frame is a no-op. `useScrollVelocity` unsubscribes with the shared RAF loop it was registered on.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The deform scalar is forced to 0 regardless of scroll velocity, and it is still damped there, so a component that is mid-ripple when the preference changes eases flat rather than snapping. The image then stays perfectly still while scrolling — the cover fit and the resize handling continue to work, but no vertex is ever displaced and no shading swing occurs.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sampling the wave from the running `pos` instead of the original `position`. Each term then feeds the next, the ripple travels through the surface as it deforms rather than staying pinned to the image, and the effect looks like a rendering fault instead of the photograph flexing.\n- Taking the magnitude of the velocity. `abs(velocity)` makes scrolling up and scrolling down produce an identical wave, which throws away the direction cue that carries most of the effect. The scalar stays signed all the way into the uniform.\n- Using a phase of 0. `sin(y * f)` is odd about the plane's centre, so the top and bottom halves mirror each other exactly and the ripple reads as a crease down the middle rather than a wave.\n- Feeding raw scroll deltas into the uniform. A wheel emits discrete steps and a trackpad emits bursts; either drives the geometry into visible stutter. The signal is low-passed, damped, and damped again before it reaches the shader.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` on. The bounding sphere was computed for the flat plane, so when the component sits at the edge of the viewport three culls it against geometry the shader has already moved and the image blinks out mid-ripple.\n- Fitting the plane with `window.innerWidth`. The plane has to cover the camera's frustum at z = 0, which is `2 * tan(fov/2) * distance` tall — a pixel width has no fixed relationship to that, so the image ends up letterboxed or overscaled depending on the viewport.\n- Skipping the overscan. Displaced vertices sample outside [0,1] at the crest of every wave, and the texture's clamped border smears along the top and bottom edges exactly when the wave is at its most visible.\n- Passing a ref to `scrollTarget`. The hook takes an `HTMLElement | null`, not a ref object, so a scroller inside the component has to be captured with a callback ref into state before it can be handed over — a plain ref would still be null on the render that matters.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` alone. That frees GPU objects and leaves the context alive; a page with several WebGL components then hits Chromium's per-process cap and the oldest canvases go blank. `disposeRenderer` calls `forceContextLoss`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `scrollTarget` defaults to `null`, which reads `window.scrollY`. Pass the scrolling element when the component lives inside its own `overflow-y-auto` container, as the demo does.\n- The plane is a unit quad rescaled on resize rather than rebuilt, so a container resize never reallocates a 96x96 vertex buffer.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the container and the image ripples in horizontal bands, with the crests catching a little more light than the troughs. Scroll the other way and the bands bow the opposite direction. Stop and the surface eases flat over about half a second rather than snapping. Flick hard and the ripple deepens but never folds through itself or shows a smeared edge. The image keeps its own proportions at any container aspect, and scrolling the page while the component is off screen costs nothing because the loop is gated on visibility. With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the image is completely still at every scroll speed.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "scroll-scrubbed-video": "# Build: Scroll-Scrubbed Video\n\nThe hard part of a scroll-scrubbed hero is never the playback — it is the mapping and the throttle. Scroll becomes a smoothed progress value that snaps when it arrives; progress becomes a quantised frame index; the frame is only ever requested when that index changes; and the loop unsubscribes once the two agree. This component ships all four and draws its frames procedurally onto a canvas, so the technique can be read without a video file in the way.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe scroll-to-frame mapping a scrubbed hero actually needs: smoothing, a snap, a frame quantiser and a loop that parks itself.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Progress is `(hostTop - sectionTop) / (total - hostHeight)` against a manufactured range, read from live rects each frame. The range height is written on mount and on resize as well as from the loop, so a section still below the fold already occupies its full height instead of shifting the page when it arrives.\n2. The playhead follows with `damp(current, target, smoothing, dt)`, the per-second form of the familiar `current += (target - current) * 0.12`. `perFrameToSmoothing(0.12)` performs that conversion, so the feel is preserved and the settle no longer runs at double speed on a 144 Hz display.\n3. A snap threshold ends the approach. Exponential smoothing is asymptotic: without `if (|target - current| < 0.0002) current = target` the value never arrives, and any loop that waits for arrival runs forever.\n4. The frame index is `round(progress * (frames - 1))`, and the draw call is guarded by `index !== drawnIndex`. This is the same guard as the `Math.abs(video.currentTime - t) > 0.003` test in a real player: asking the decoder to seek to the frame it is already showing is what makes a scrubbed video judder.\n5. The loop parks itself. Once the value has snapped, `useRaf` is disabled and the subscription dropped; a scroll listener on the container wakes it. A settled hero therefore costs nothing, rather than 60 wasted frames a second over a still image.\n6. Work is gated on `useInView` with `rootMargin: 200px`, so the first frame is painted just before the section enters rather than just after — the difference between arriving on a picture and arriving on a blank box.\n7. The canvas backing store is sized in device pixels and the element in CSS pixels, with the ratio clamped at 2. Assigning `width`/`height` also clears the canvas, so the resize path forces a redraw by resetting the drawn index to -1.\n8. Smoothed progress is published as `--scrub-progress` on the section, so a scrubber bar can move continuously in CSS while the frame behind it advances in steps.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `frames = 96`\n  Steps the range quantises to. Around three seconds of 30 fps footage. Fewer than ~48 and the stepping is visible as stutter; many more and consecutive frames differ by less than the smoothing can resolve, so decode work is spent on frames nobody perceives.\n- `smoothing = perFrameToSmoothing(0.12)`\n  The classic `0.12` per frame at 60 Hz expressed per second (~0.0004). It absorbs wheel steps without the playhead feeling detached from the gesture. The conversion is the point: the raw 0.12 would settle more than twice as fast at 144 Hz.\n- `SNAP = 0.0002`\n  Progress delta below which the playhead is considered arrived. Two ten-thousandths of the range is well under one frame at any sane frame count, so snapping is invisible — and it is what allows the loop to terminate at all.\n- `rootMargin = 200px`\n  Starts work just before the section enters. Enough lead time to decode and paint the first frame at typical scroll speeds, small enough that scrolling past does not warm up work that is never seen.\n- `viewports = 2`\n  Scroll travel as a multiple of the scroller height. Two screens over ~96 frames is roughly one frame per 15 px of scroll, which reads as a continuous shot rather than a flipbook.\n- `MAX_DPR = 2`\n  Backing-store cap. Past 2x the extra pixels are invisible on the phones that report 3x and 4x, but the per-frame fill cost scales with their square.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ScrollScrubbedVideo(props: { frames?: number; draw?: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, frame: ScrubFrame) => void; viewports?: number; smoothing?: number; children?: ReactNode; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `frames: number` — default `96`\n  Discrete frames the scroll range quantises to.\n- `draw: (ctx, frame: ScrubFrame) => void`\n  Called only when the frame index changes or after a resize. Replace with a `currentTime` write for real footage.\n- `viewports: number` — default `2`\n  Scroll travel as a multiple of the scroller height.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `perFrameToSmoothing(0.12)`\n  Fraction of the progress gap left after one second.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Overlaid on the canvas — captions, a scrubber, a title.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container. Omit to auto-detect.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ScrollScrubbedVideo({\n  frames = 96,\n  draw = defaultDraw,\n  viewports = 2,\n  smoothing = DEFAULT_SMOOTHING,\n  children,\n  scroller,\n  className,\n}: ScrollScrubbedVideoProps) {\n  const section = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const pin = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const canvas = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n\n  const current = useRef(0);\n  const drawnIndex = useRef(-1);\n  const lastProgress = useRef('');\n  const lastHeight = useRef(0);\n  const palette = useRef<ScrubPalette | null>(null);\n  const drawRef = useRef(draw);\n  drawRef.current = draw;\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const size = useResizeObserver(pin);\n  // Work starts 200 px before the section enters, so the first frame is\n  // already painted when it arrives rather than after it.\n  const { inView } = useInView(section, { rootMargin: '200px' });\n\n  // A self-parking loop. Once the smoothed value has reached the target there\n  // is nothing left to compute, so the subscription is dropped entirely; a\n  // scroll on the container wakes it. Without this, a settled hero keeps a\n  // core busy re-deriving the same frame index sixty times a second.\n  const [awake, setAwake] = useState(true);\n\n  const render = (index: number, progress: number) => {\n    const el = canvas.current;\n    const ctx = el?.getContext('2d');\n    if (!el || !ctx || size.width === 0 || size.height === 0) return;\n    if (!palette.current) palette.current = readPalette(el);\n\n    drawnIndex.current = index;\n    ctx.save();\n    const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, MAX_DPR);\n    ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);\n    drawRef.current(ctx, {\n      progress,\n      index,\n      frames,\n      width: size.width,\n      height: size.height,\n      palette: palette.current,\n    });\n    ctx.restore();\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = canvas.current;\n    if (!el || size.width === 0 || size.height === 0) return;\n    // The backing store is sized in device pixels and the element in CSS\n    // pixels; assigning width/height also clears it, so a redraw is forced.\n    const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, MAX_DPR);\n    el.width = Math.round(size.width * dpr);\n    el.height = Math.round(size.height * dpr);\n    palette.current = readPalette(el);\n    drawnIndex.current = -1;\n    setAwake(true);\n  }, [size.width, size.height]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const sectionEl = section.current;\n    if (!sectionEl) return;\n    const host = scroller?.current ?? nearestScroller(sectionEl);\n    const target = host === document.documentElement ? window : host;\n    const wake = () => setAwake(true);\n    target.addEventListener('scroll', wake, { passive: true });\n    return () => target.removeEventListener('scroll', wake);\n  }, [scroller]);\n\n  // The manufactured range is layout, so it is written on mount and on resize\n  // rather than only from the loop — a section still below the fold must\n  // already occupy its full height, or entering it shifts everything below.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const sync = () => {\n      const sectionEl = section.current;\n      const pinEl = pin.current;\n      if (!sectionEl || !pinEl) return;\n      const host = scroller?.current ?? nearestScroller(sectionEl);\n      const hostHeight =\n        host === document.documentElement ? window.innerHeight : host.clientHeight;\n      if (hostHeight === 0) return;\n      const total = Math.round(hostHeight * (reduced ? 1 : 1 + viewports));\n      if (total === lastHeight.current) return;\n      lastHeight.current = total;\n      sectionEl.style.height = `${total}px`;\n      pinEl.style.height = `${hostHeight}px`;\n      setAwake(true);\n    };\n    sync();\n    window.addEventListener('resize', sync);\n    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', sync);\n  }, [reduced, viewports, scroller]);\n\n  useRaf((dt) => {\n    const sectionEl = section.current;\n    const pinEl = pin.current;\n    if (!sectionEl || !pinEl) return;\n\n    const host = scroller?.current ?? nearestScroller(sectionEl);\n    const hostIsRoot = host === document.documentElement;\n    const hostTop = hostIsRoot ? 0 : host.getBoundingClientRect().top;\n    const hostHeight = hostIsRoot ? window.innerHeight : host.clientHeight;\n    if (hostHeight === 0) return;\n\n    const total = Math.round(hostHeight * (reduced ? 1 : 1 + viewports));\n    if (total !== lastHeight.current) {\n      lastHeight.current = total;\n      sectionEl.style.height = `${total}px`;\n      pinEl.style.height = `${hostHeight}px`;\n    }\n\n    const travel = total - hostHeight;\n    const target = reduced\n      ? 0.5\n      : travel > 0\n        ? clamp((hostTop - sectionEl.getBoundingClientRect().top) / travel, 0, 1)\n        : 0;\n\n    // Smooth, then snap. Without the snap the value approaches the target\n    // asymptotically and the loop can never decide it has arrived.\n    current.current = reduced ? target : damp(current.current, target, smoothing, dt);\n    let settled = false;\n    if (Math.abs(target - current.current) < SNAP) {\n      current.current = target;\n      settled = true;\n    }\n\n    const text = current.current.toFixed(4);\n    if (text !== lastProgress.current) {\n      lastProgress.current = text;\n      sectionEl.style.setProperty('--scrub-progress', text);\n    }\n\n    // The seek throttle. A real player writes `video.currentTime` here, and\n    // only when the frame actually changed — re-seeking to the frame already\n    // displayed makes the decoder stall and the playhead judder.\n    const index = clamp(Math.round(current.current * (frames - 1)), 0, frames - 1);\n    if (index !== drawnIndex.current) render(index, current.current);\n\n    if (settled) setAwake(false);\n  }, awake && inView);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes on unmount and again whenever the loop parks. The scroll and resize listeners are removed with their effects, `useInView` unobserves through its pool, and `useResizeObserver` disconnects. The canvas holds no timers and no decoded resources.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The manufactured range collapses to a single screen and the playhead is pinned to the middle frame with no smoothing, so the section becomes a still image with its caption rather than several screens of scroll that scrub something. The loop parks immediately and never redraws.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Writing `video.currentTime` on every frame. The decoder is asked to seek to the position it is already at dozens of times a second, and on Safari in particular the playhead visibly stalls and jumps. Quantise to a frame and guard the assignment.\n- Smoothing without a snap. The playhead approaches the target forever, the \"is it settled\" test never passes, and a loop written to park itself never does.\n- Using a per-frame smoothing constant. The scrub lags noticeably further behind the scroll at 60 Hz than at 144 Hz, and there is no single value that feels right on both.\n- Encoding the source with long GOPs. Seeking then lands on the nearest keyframe and decodes forward, which is why a scrub built on a normal web encode stutters no matter how good the JavaScript is — an all-intra encode is a prerequisite, not an optimisation.\n- Running the loop while the section is off screen. The whole point of a scrubbed hero is that it costs nothing until it is on screen; without an IntersectionObserver gate it costs a decode loop for the entire page lifetime.\n- Sizing the canvas in CSS only. The backing store stays at its default 300x150 and the image is stretched — the resize path must assign `width` and `height` in device pixels.\n- Ignoring the device pixel ratio cap. A 4x phone quadruples the fill area for pixels nobody can see, and the scrub drops to single-digit frame rates.\n- Leaving the manufactured range in place under reduced motion. The user gets several screens of scroll during which nothing at all changes.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No video file is shipped. `draw(ctx, frame)` renders each frame procedurally so the mapping, the quantiser and the throttle can be read on their own. A real implementation replaces the body of that call with a single assignment — `video.currentTime = frame.progress * (video.duration - 0.05)` — keeping the surrounding `index !== drawnIndex` guard exactly as it is, and draws the video element instead of the canvas.\n- The other things a production version needs, none of which are part of the mapping: an all-intra encode (`-g 1`), several responsive encodes selected by width with the playhead position preserved across the swap, and a decode prime on Safari — `play()` followed immediately by `pause()` — because a video that has never played will not seek.\n- The `frames` value should match the source: with a video, use `duration * fps` so one index step is one real frame.\n- The default draw reads its colours from the `--k-*` custom properties on the canvas, so the placeholder footage follows the theme.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScrolling advances the frame counter smoothly and reverses exactly. Stopping settles within a few frames, after which a performance profile shows no rAF activity until the next scroll. Scrolling the section off screen stops all work. Resizing redraws at the correct resolution with no stretching. The settle takes the same wall-clock time at 60 and 144 Hz. With reduced motion on, one static frame is shown and the section is one screen tall.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "scrub-char-color-reveal": "# Build: Scrubbed Character Colour Reveal\n\nA paragraph sits at 16% strength and resolves character by character as it crosses the viewport, with the reveal position pinned to the scrollbar rather than to a clock. Scroll back up and the characters ghost out again in reverse, because a scrubbed timeline is a function of position, not a playback. The stagger and durations are ratios of the scroll range rather than seconds, so the pacing per character is unchanged whether the paragraph is thirty words or three hundred.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA dim paragraph resolves character by character as it crosses the viewport, tied to scroll position not a clock.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The text is split in JSX — `text.split(\" \")` for word boxes, `Array.from(word)` for one `inline-block` span per character — so React owns every node and nothing mutates the tree behind its back.\n2. One `gsap.to(chars, { opacity: 1, stagger, duration, ease: \"power1.out\", scrollTrigger: {...} })` does the whole reveal. GSAP builds the stagger internally as a single tween whose total length is `duration + (n - 1) * stagger`.\n3. ScrollTrigger maps that total length onto the distance between `start` and `end`, which is why `stagger: 0.1` and `duration: 0.35` are ratios, not seconds: each character begins 0.1/total of the way through the scroll range and takes 0.35/total to resolve. Change the paragraph length and the pacing per character is identical; change the scroll range and everything rescales together.\n4. The resting opacity is written as a JSX inline style on each character, so the pre-scroll frame — including the server render and the no-JS render — is already the dim state, and GSAP has a real DOM value to tween away from.\n5. The paragraph carries `aria-label={text}` and the character spans are inside an `aria-hidden` wrapper, so assistive technology reads one paragraph rather than two hundred single letters.\n6. `ease: \"power1.out\"` applies *within* each character's own resolve, not across the stagger. The stagger itself remains linear against scroll, which is what keeps the sweep pinned to the scrollbar.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `DIM = 0.16`\n  Resting strength as opacity on `currentColor`. Below ~0.1 the unread text is illegible and the block reads as a loading skeleton; above ~0.3 the contrast between resolved and unresolved is too weak for the sweep to be visible while scrolling.\n- `STAGGER = 0.1`\n  Timeline units between adjacent characters. Combined with `CHAR_DURATION` it sets the width of the resolve front: roughly 3-4 characters are mid-transition at any moment. Larger and the front narrows to one character at a time, which reads as a typewriter rather than a wash.\n- `CHAR_DURATION = 0.35`\n  3.5x the stagger, so several characters are always resolving together. Equal to the stagger it becomes a hard edge sweeping the paragraph; much larger and the front is so soft the reveal has no direction.\n- `SCRUB = 0.75`\n  Seconds of catch-up. `scrub: true` welds the reveal to the scrollbar exactly, which on a trackpad exposes every jitter in the input. Three quarters of a second smooths it without ever feeling like the text is animating on its own.\n- `START / END = 'top 78%' / 'bottom 55%'`\n  The reveal begins when the paragraph top is a little above the fold and completes when its bottom passes just past the middle. Ending at `bottom top` means the last characters only resolve as the paragraph leaves the screen, so the reader never sees the finished text.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ScrubCharColorReveal(props: { text: string; dim?: number; stagger?: number; charDuration?: number; scrub?: number; start?: string; end?: string; scroller?: Element | null; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `text: string`\n  Body copy. Split to words then characters in JSX.\n- `dim: number` — default `0.16`\n  Resting strength of an unrevealed character, 0-1.\n- `stagger: number` — default `0.1`\n  Timeline units between adjacent characters.\n- `charDuration: number` — default `0.35`\n  Timeline units for one character to resolve.\n- `scrub: number` — default `0.75`\n  Seconds of catch-up between the scrollbar and the reveal.\n- `start: string` — default `'top 78%'`\n  ScrollTrigger `start`.\n- `end: string` — default `'bottom 55%'`\n  ScrollTrigger `end`.\n- `scroller: Element | null` — default `null`\n  Scroll container, when the text is not in the document scroller.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the paragraph via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst DIM = 0.16;\nconst STAGGER = 0.1;\nconst CHAR_DURATION = 0.35;\nconst SCRUB = 0.75;\nconst START = 'top 78%';\nconst END = 'bottom 55%';\n\nexport function ScrubCharColorReveal({\n  text,\n  dim = DIM,\n  stagger = STAGGER,\n  charDuration = CHAR_DURATION,\n  scrub = SCRUB,\n  start = START,\n  end = END,\n  scroller = null,\n  className,\n}: ScrubCharColorRevealProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLParagraphElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = root.current;\n    if (!el || reduced) return;\n\n    gsap.registerPlugin(ScrollTrigger);\n\n    const chars = Array.from(el.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-char]'));\n    if (chars.length === 0) return;\n\n    const ctx = gsap.context(() => {\n      gsap.to(chars, {\n        opacity: 1,\n        duration: charDuration,\n        ease: 'power1.out',\n        stagger,\n        scrollTrigger: {\n          trigger: el,\n          scroller: scroller ?? undefined,\n          start,\n          end,\n          scrub,\n        },\n      });\n    }, el);\n\n    return () => ctx.revert();\n  }, [text, dim, stagger, charDuration, scrub, start, end, scroller, reduced]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `ctx.revert()` on the `gsap.context` scoped to the paragraph. That kills the staggered tween, disposes the ScrollTrigger instance so it stops measuring a detached node, and strips the inline `opacity` GSAP wrote — leaving React's own inline dim value intact.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The resting opacity collapses to 1 in the JSX, so the paragraph renders fully resolved, and the effect returns before registering ScrollTrigger. The text is never left dim waiting for a scroll that will not drive it.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Splitting text before the webfont has loaded. Character boxes are measured against the fallback face, so widths and line breaks are wrong and never re-flow when the real font arrives — see `font-safe-text-split` for the `document.fonts.ready` gate and the debounced re-split this needs at scale.\n- Treating `stagger` and `duration` as seconds. Under `scrub` they are proportions of the scroll range; a value tuned as \"0.35 seconds\" behaves completely differently the moment the trigger distance changes.\n- Building a separate tween per character. That is one ScrollTrigger per character on a paragraph of two hundred, and refresh cost becomes visible on resize. A single staggered tween is one trigger.\n- Leaving the character spans exposed to assistive technology. A screen reader announces every letter individually — `aria-label` on the paragraph plus `aria-hidden` on the spans is not optional here.\n- Setting the resting opacity from `gsap.set` in the effect. The first paint then shows the paragraph at full strength before dimming it, which is a visible flash on every load.\n- Splitting straight to characters with no word boxes. Line breaks land inside words and the paragraph becomes unreadable at narrow widths.\n- Forgetting `scroller` when the paragraph lives in an `overflow-y-auto` panel. ScrollTrigger measures the window instead and the reveal fires at the wrong scroll position or not at all.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The split is declarative JSX, deliberately: GSAP `SplitText` is reserved for the two components whose subject *is* SplitText (`nested-splittext-restore`, `font-safe-text-split`). Here a React split gives the same per-character targets with no DOM mutation and no extra dependency for the consumer.\n- Opacity on `currentColor` rather than a literal colour tween, so the component is correct in any theme without a per-context variant.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the paragraph up through the viewport: characters brighten in a soft front sweeping left to right and top to bottom, and the front position tracks the scrollbar rather than a clock. Scroll back up and it reverses. Turn on a screen reader and the paragraph is announced once as whole sentences, not letter by letter. With reduced motion enabled the paragraph is at full strength on first paint.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "scrub-timeline-spine": "# Build: Scrub Timeline Spine\n\nA vertical rule that fills with scroll while entries alternate either side of it. Entries rest at 50% opacity rather than 0, so the animation adds emphasis to content that is already readable, and each one reverses as it leaves — so scrolling back up replays the sequence instead of showing a column of finished animations.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA rule that fills as you scroll, with entries alternating either side and reversing on the way back.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The rule is two elements: a faint full-height track, and a fill of the same height scaled with `gsap.fromTo(fill, { scaleY: 0 }, { scaleY: 1, ease: \"none\" })` on a `scrub: 0.6` trigger running `top 55%` → `bottom 75%`.\n2. `transformOrigin: \"top center\"` is passed to the tween, not just set in CSS. GSAP writes its own transform string, and without it the fill grows from the centre and the rule appears to unroll in both directions at once.\n3. Every entry gets its own trigger with `toggleActions: \"play reverse play reverse\"` — the four slots are onEnter, onLeave, onEnterBack, onLeaveBack. Reversing on leave is what makes the column scrubbable upward as well as downward.\n4. Entries animate `fromTo({ opacity: 0.5, scale: 0.9 }, { opacity: 1, scale: 1, duration: 0.6, ease: \"power2.out\" })`. The rest state is 0.5, not 0, so nothing is gated behind a scroll event.\n5. `transformOrigin` per entry follows the side it hangs off — `left center` on the left column, `right center` on the right — so each card grows outward from the spine. A centre origin makes both columns creep toward the middle and the rule looks squeezed.\n6. Alternation is decided from the measured container width. Below `alternateAbove` px, entries stop alternating and stack to the right of a left-hand rule, and every origin becomes `left center`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `restOpacity = 0.5`\n  The resting state of an entry. Zero is the obvious choice and the wrong one: it makes the animation a gate on the content, so a failed script or a skipped trigger leaves a blank column, and a screen reader meets text that is visually absent. At 0.5 the animation is emphasis, not access.\n- `restScale = 0.9`\n  Paired with the opacity change so the entry gains presence rather than just brightness. Below ~0.85 the text visibly reflows as it scales and becomes hard to read mid-transition.\n- `scrub = 0.6`\n  Catch-up on the fill only. The rule keeps advancing for about half a second after the wheel stops, which reads as the timeline settling. `true` makes it feel welded to the mouse.\n- `fillStart / fillEnd = 'top 55%' / 'bottom 75%'`\n  The fill begins once the section is past halfway up the scroller and completes before its bottom clears. Ending at `bottom top` instead would leave the rule still filling while the last entry is already being read, which makes the last item feel unreached.\n- `entryDuration = 0.6`\n  Not scrubbed — this one plays on its own clock, so it must be short enough to finish before the next entry enters at normal scrolling speed.\n- `alternateAbove = 420`\n  Container width in px below which a half-width column cannot hold a title and a line of body. Measured from the element, not the window, because the spine is usually inside an article column narrower than the page.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ScrubTimelineSpine(props: { entries: { label: string; title: string; body?: string }[]; scrub?: number; restOpacity?: number; restScale?: number; entryDuration?: number; fillStart?: string; fillEnd?: string; entryStart?: string; alternateAbove?: number; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `entries: { label: string; title: string; body?: string }[]`\n  Ordered entries. Rendered as an `<ol>` either side of the rule.\n- `scrub: number` — default `0.6`\n  Seconds of catch-up on the fill.\n- `restOpacity: number` — default `0.5`\n  Resting opacity of an inactive entry. Do not set 0.\n- `restScale: number` — default `0.9`\n  Resting scale of an inactive entry.\n- `entryDuration: number` — default `0.6`\n  Seconds for one entry to reach full emphasis.\n- `fillStart: string` — default `'top 55%'`\n  ScrollTrigger start for the fill.\n- `fillEnd: string` — default `'bottom 75%'`\n  ScrollTrigger end for the fill.\n- `entryStart: string` — default `'top 78%'`\n  ScrollTrigger start for each entry.\n- `alternateAbove: number` — default `420`\n  Container width in px above which entries alternate sides.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ScrubTimelineSpine({\n  entries,\n  scrub = 0.6,\n  restOpacity = 0.5,\n  restScale = 0.9,\n  entryDuration = 0.6,\n  fillStart = 'top 55%',\n  fillEnd = 'bottom 75%',\n  entryStart = 'top 78%',\n  alternateAbove = 420,\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: ScrubTimelineSpineProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const fill = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { width } = useResizeObserver(root);\n  const alternate = width >= alternateAbove;\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const container = root.current;\n      const line = fill.current;\n      if (!container || !line) return;\n\n      const items = Array.from(container.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-spine-entry]'));\n      const scroller = scrollerRef?.current ?? undefined;\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        gsap.set(line, { scaleY: 1 });\n        gsap.set(items, { opacity: 1, scale: 1 });\n        return;\n      }\n\n      gsap.fromTo(\n        line,\n        { scaleY: 0 },\n        {\n          scaleY: 1,\n          ease: 'none',\n          // transformOrigin is set in CSS too, but GSAP writes its own\n          // transform string; without stating it here the fill grows from the\n          // centre and the rule appears to unroll in both directions.\n          transformOrigin: 'top center',\n          scrollTrigger: {\n            trigger: container,\n            scroller,\n            // Starts filling once the section is just past halfway up and\n            // completes before its bottom leaves — so the rule is never still\n            // filling while the last entry is already being read.\n            start: fillStart,\n            end: fillEnd,\n            scrub,\n            invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n          },\n        },\n      );\n\n      items.forEach((item, i) => {\n        gsap.fromTo(\n          item,\n          { opacity: restOpacity, scale: restScale },\n          {\n            opacity: 1,\n            scale: 1,\n            duration: entryDuration,\n            ease: 'power2.out',\n            // Scale from the edge the entry hangs off, so it grows outward\n            // from the spine. A centre origin pulls both columns toward the\n            // middle and the rule looks like it is being squeezed.\n            transformOrigin: alternate && i % 2 === 1 ? 'right center' : 'left center',\n            scrollTrigger: {\n              trigger: item,\n              scroller,\n              start: entryStart,\n              // onEnter, onLeave, onEnterBack, onLeaveBack. Reversing on the\n              // way out is what makes the column scrubbable upward as well as\n              // downward.\n              toggleActions: 'play reverse play reverse',\n            },\n          },\n        );\n      });\n    },\n    root,\n    [\n      scrub,\n      restOpacity,\n      restScale,\n      entryDuration,\n      fillStart,\n      fillEnd,\n      entryStart,\n      alternate,\n      reduced,\n      entries.length,\n    ],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` reverts the `gsap.context`, killing the fill tween, all per-entry tweens and all of their ScrollTriggers, and restoring the inline transform and opacity. This matters more than usual here because the alternation flip rebuilds every trigger on resize: a surviving trigger would keep writing a right-edge `transform-origin` to a card now laid out on the left.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The fill is set to `scaleY: 1` and every entry to full opacity and scale during the first effect, with no triggers created. The spine renders as a completed timeline — the rule is drawn, every entry is emphasised, and nothing changes with scroll.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- `toggleActions: \"play none none none\"`. The default-feeling choice, and it means scrolling back up shows a column of already-completed animations with no motion left in them. Reversing on leave is what makes the direction of travel legible.\n- Resting entries at `opacity: 0`. The animation becomes a gate: any trigger that fails to fire — a mis-set scroller, a refresh during a resize, a JS error elsewhere — leaves the content permanently invisible while still occupying layout and still being read by assistive technology.\n- Setting `transform-origin` only in CSS. GSAP writes a complete transform and its own `transform-origin`, so the CSS value is overwritten and the fill grows from the middle of the rule instead of the top.\n- A single centre `transform-origin` for both columns. Cards then scale toward the spine, so the whole layout appears to pinch inward as you scroll instead of each entry growing outward from the rule.\n- Scaling the same element that is the track. The fill needs to be a second element; scaling the visible track means the \"remaining\" portion disappears and the reader loses the sense of how much is left.\n- One shared ScrollTrigger for all entries. The emphasis then depends on the section's progress rather than each entry's own position, so on a tall screen three entries brighten at once and none of them reads as current.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The fill uses the accent token, so it inherits the category accent when placed inside a `data-category` subtree.\n- Entries are a real `<ol>`, so the sequence is conveyed structurally and not only by the drawn rule.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll down: the accent rule fills from the top and each entry brightens and grows outward from its own side of the rule as it arrives. Scroll back up and each entry dims again as it leaves the top, and the rule retreats. Stop mid-section and the fill continues for about half a second. Disable JavaScript and every entry is still readable at half opacity. Narrow the container and the entries stack to the right of a left-hand rule.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "seamless-marquee": "# Build: Pixels-Per-Second Marquee\n\nA looping strip of content whose speed is expressed in pixels per second and whose duration is derived from it, never the other way round. A fixed `duration` means a 900px strip crawls while a 4000px strip sprints, so two marquees on the same page visibly disagree about how fast scrolling is — and adding one word to either changes its speed. The loop distance is exactly one content set plus its gap, so the wrap frame is pixel-identical to the frame before it and the seam is invisible.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA marquee whose duration is derived from measured width and a px/second speed, so every strip moves at the same rate.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `ResizeObserver` on the first content copy gives `copyWidth`; the repeating unit is `setWidth = copyWidth + gap`, because the gap that follows a copy is part of the unit — omit it and the rhythm at the seam is one gap tighter than everywhere else, which reads as a stutter once per cycle.\n2. Duration is `setWidth / speed` — seconds = px / (px per second). Every marquee on the site then moves at the same rate regardless of how much content is in it.\n3. The number of copies is `Math.max(2, Math.ceil(rootWidth / setWidth) + 1)`, from a second `ResizeObserver` on the container. The `+1` covers the gap that the translation itself opens up; with the exact ceiling a blank wedge appears at the right edge on the last frame of every cycle.\n4. The tween is `gsap.fromTo(track, { x: 0 }, { x: -setWidth, duration, ease: \"none\", repeat: -1 })`. `ease: \"none\"` is mandatory — any easing makes the strip accelerate and decelerate once per cycle, which turns the seam into a visible pulse.\n5. Pausing lives in a *separate* effect that calls `tween.paused(!(visible && inView))`, driven by `useVisibility` (tab hidden) and `useInView` (scrolled away). Rebuilding the tween on a visibility flip would snap the strip back to x = 0.\n6. Hover ramps the timeScale rather than toggling paused: `gsap.to(tween, { timeScale: 0 | 1, duration: 0.35, overwrite: true })`. The tween object is itself a valid GSAP target, so the strip glides to a stop instead of stopping dead.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SPEED = 60`\n  Pixels per second. Around 60px/s a 12px word takes a fifth of a second to cross its own width — readable while moving. Past ~120px/s the copy is decorative texture; below ~30px/s viewers assume it is stuck.\n- `GAP = 48`\n  Pixels between items and between the last item and the next copy. It must be identical in both places or the seam is visibly a different rhythm from the interior. Below ~24px adjacent items read as one phrase.\n- `HOVER_RAMP = 0.35`\n  Seconds to ramp `timeScale` between 1 and 0. Zero (a hard pause) reads as a broken animation; past ~0.6s the strip keeps drifting long enough that a user trying to read an item has to chase it.\n- `copies = max(2, ceil(rootWidth / setWidth) + 1)`\n  The floor of 2 covers content wider than the container, where the ceiling alone yields 1 and there is nothing to wrap into. The `+1` covers the width the translation itself vacates.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SeamlessMarquee(props: { children: ReactNode; speed?: number; direction?: 'left' | 'right'; gap?: number; pauseOnHover?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One content set. Cloned as many times as the container width requires.\n- `speed: number` — default `60`\n  Pixels per second. Identical for every marquee whatever its width.\n- `direction: 'left' | 'right'` — default `'left'`\n  Travel direction.\n- `gap: number` — default `48`\n  Pixels between items and between copies.\n- `pauseOnHover: boolean` — default `true`\n  Ramp `timeScale` to 0 on pointer enter and back to 1 on leave.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the clipping container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst SPEED = 60;\nconst GAP = 48;\nconst HOVER_RAMP = 0.35;\n\nexport function SeamlessMarquee({\n  children,\n  speed = SPEED,\n  direction = 'left',\n  gap = GAP,\n  pauseOnHover = true,\n  className,\n}: SeamlessMarqueeProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const track = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const copy = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const tween = useRef<gsap.core.Tween | null>(null);\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(root);\n  const { width: rootWidth } = useResizeObserver(root);\n  const { width: copyWidth } = useResizeObserver(copy);\n\n  // The loop distance is one copy *plus* the gap that follows it — the gap is\n  // part of the repeating unit. Leave it out and the rhythm of the seam is one\n  // gap tighter than the rhythm everywhere else, which reads as a stutter.\n  const setWidth = copyWidth + gap;\n  const measured = copyWidth > 0;\n  const copies =\n    reduced || !measured ? 1 : Math.max(2, Math.ceil(rootWidth / setWidth) + 1);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = track.current;\n    if (!el || reduced || !measured) return;\n\n    const duration = setWidth / speed;\n    const t = gsap.fromTo(\n      el,\n      { x: direction === 'left' ? 0 : -setWidth },\n      {\n        x: direction === 'left' ? -setWidth : 0,\n        duration,\n        ease: 'none',\n        repeat: -1,\n      },\n    );\n    tween.current = t;\n\n    return () => {\n      tween.current = null;\n      // Kill the hover timeScale tweens too — they target the tween object\n      // itself, so killTweensOf(el) would not reach them.\n      gsap.killTweensOf(t);\n      t.kill();\n      gsap.set(el, { clearProps: 'transform' });\n    };\n  }, [setWidth, measured, speed, direction, reduced]);\n\n  // Separate effect so a visibility flip pauses in place instead of rebuilding\n  // the tween, which would snap the strip back to its start position.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const t = tween.current;\n    if (!t) return;\n    t.paused(!(visible && inView));\n  }, [visible, inView, setWidth, measured, speed, direction, reduced]);\n\n  const ramp = useCallback(\n    (to: number) => {\n      const t = tween.current;\n      if (!pauseOnHover || !t) return;\n      gsap.to(t, { timeScale: to, duration: HOVER_RAMP, overwrite: true });\n    },\n    [pauseOnHover],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The build effect clears the tween ref, calls `gsap.killTweensOf(tween)` to catch in-flight hover `timeScale` ramps that target the tween object, then `tween.kill()`, then `gsap.set(track, { clearProps: \"transform\" })`. Both `ResizeObserver` instances are disconnected by their hooks.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Renders a single, un-cloned content set with no tween created at all. The content is fully visible and static — the strip is not left mid-translation and nothing is clipped out of view.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Specifying `duration` instead of a px/second speed. Two marquees on one page then move at different rates, and editing the copy silently changes the speed of the one you edited.\n- Translating by the copy width and forgetting the gap. The seam arrives one gap early every cycle — a small, regular hitch that is very hard to diagnose from the code.\n- Hardcoding the clone count at 2. On a wide viewport with short content the strip runs out and a blank band sweeps through once per cycle.\n- Any ease other than `none`. The strip speeds up and slows down once per loop, so the seam that is geometrically invisible becomes obvious from the velocity change alone.\n- Rebuilding the tween when visibility or hover changes. The `fromTo` restarts at x = 0 and the strip teleports backwards.\n- Killing only `killTweensOf(trackElement)` on unmount. The hover ramps target the *tween object*, not the element, so they survive and keep writing `timeScale` to a dead tween — `gsap.killTweensOf(t)` before `t.kill()` is what catches them.\n- Leaving the cloned copies exposed to assistive technology. A screen reader reads the same six items three times; every clone past the first is `aria-hidden`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Reduced motion renders exactly one copy with no tween and no transform, so the content sits statically at the left edge and remains fully readable.\n- No text splitting is involved — the marquee moves whole content sets, so no split library dependency is imposed on consumers.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWatch the seam for a full cycle: no jump, no blank wedge, no change in spacing as one copy hands over to the next. Put two marquees with very different content lengths side by side and both move at visibly the same speed. Hover and the strip glides to a stop over about a third of a second rather than halting. Switch browser tabs and back, and the strip resumes from where it was rather than restarting. With reduced motion the content is static and readable.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "search-field": "# Build: Search Field\n\nA query field for filtering a list or searching a table. It puts Input's field shell inside React Aria's `SearchField`, rounds it into a pill by default, and adds a search icon at the start and a small clear button at the end that scales and fades in once there is text. `isLoading` cross-fades the icon into Spinner's orbiting light in the same 20px box. Escape clears the text and Enter calls `onSubmit`.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nPill or rounded search input on React Aria SearchField: Escape clears, a clear button scales in with text, and the icon fades to a spinner.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `SearchField` renders `AriaSearchField` as `group flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1` and always passes it a `value`: the controlled `value` when given, otherwise its own `uncontrolledValue` state. Its `change` function updates that state and calls `onChange`, so the clear button can work in both modes. With no `label` or `aria-label`, the `placeholder` becomes the `aria-label`.\n2. The render function shows `Label` when `label` is set, then Input's `InputBase` with `fieldClassName` set to `rounded-full` for `shape=\"pill\"` (the default; `rounded` keeps Input's 10px corners), then `HintText` when `hint` is set. The input hides the browser's own WebKit cancel button so only the kit's clear button shows.\n3. The `leadingAddon` is one `aria-hidden` 20px box holding two absolutely positioned layers: the `icon` (default `RiSearchLine`) and `SpinnerGlyph size=\"sm\"`. Both transition `opacity` and `scale` over 200ms. When `isLoading` the icon goes to `scale-75 opacity-0` and the spinner comes in; otherwise the spinner is faded out and its spin is paused with `[animation-play-state:paused]` on its children, so it stays mounted for the fade but does no work.\n4. The `trailingAddon` is a `CloseButton size=\"2xs\"` (16px) on `bg-background-tertiary-hover`. While the field is empty or disabled it is `pointer-events-none scale-50 opacity-0` and `aria-hidden`; once there is text it transitions to `scale-100 opacity-100` over 150ms. It has `tabIndex={-1}`, because Escape already clears from the keyboard.\n5. Clicking the clear button runs `clear`: `change(\"\")`, `onClear`, then focus back to the input. Its `onMouseDown` calls `preventDefault`, so the input never loses focus while the pointer is down. React Aria's own Escape handling clears through the same `change` and `onClear`.\n6. The clear button's accessible name goes through `useTemplateCopy`, which returns the string unchanged unless a template copy provider is present, so the default \"Clear search\" can be localised.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `icon box = size-5 (20px), ms-1`\n  The icon and the spinner share one box and cross-fade in place, so the text never moves when loading starts. A leading addon drops the shell's start padding to 4px (`ps-1`), so the 4px margin brings the icon back to the 8px inset of a plain field.\n- `icon / spinner fade = 200ms opacity + scale, hidden at scale-75`\n  A short shrink while fading reads as one glyph turning into the other rather than two overlapping.\n- `clear button = CloseButton 2xs (16px), 150ms opacity + scale from scale-50`\n  Small enough to sit inside the 36px shell. Scaling in from half size makes it appear as the first character is typed without a jump in the layout, since it always occupies its slot.\n- `shape = pill (rounded-full) by default, rounded (Input's rounded-2lg) as the option`\n  The pill marks the field as search at a glance; `rounded` keeps Input's corners so it lines up with other fields in a form.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SearchField(props: Omit<AriaSearchFieldProps, \"className\" | \"children\"> & { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; placeholder?: string; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; shape?: \"pill\" | \"rounded\"; isLoading?: boolean; icon?: IconComponent; clearLabel?: string; className?: string; fieldClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value / defaultValue / onChange: string / (value: string) => void`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled query.\n- `onSubmit: (value: string) => void`\n  Called on Enter, from React Aria.\n- `onClear: () => void`\n  Called when Escape or the clear button empties the field.\n- `shape: \"pill\" | \"rounded\"` — default `\"pill\"`\n  Fully rounded, or Input's 10px corners.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px or 32px shell.\n- `isLoading: boolean` — default `false`\n  Cross-fades the icon into a spinner.\n- `icon: ComponentType` — default `RiSearchLine`\n  Replaces the search icon.\n- `clearLabel: string` — default `\"Clear search\"`\n  Accessible name of the clear button.\n- `label / hint: ReactNode`\n  Label above and caption below, as on Input.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SearchField({\n  label,\n  hint,\n  placeholder,\n  size = \"medium\",\n  shape = \"pill\",\n  isLoading = false,\n  icon: Icon = RiSearchLine,\n  clearLabel = \"Clear search\",\n  value: controlledValue,\n  defaultValue = \"\",\n  onChange,\n  onClear,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  fieldClassName,\n  ...props\n}: SearchFieldProps) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\n  const [uncontrolledValue, setUncontrolledValue] = useState(defaultValue);\n  const value = controlledValue ?? uncontrolledValue;\n\n  const change = (next: string) => {\n    setUncontrolledValue(next);\n    onChange?.(next);\n  };\n\n  const clear = () => {\n    change(\"\");\n    onClear?.();\n    inputRef.current?.focus();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <AriaSearchField\n      {...props}\n      value={value}\n      onChange={change}\n      onClear={onClear}\n      aria-label={ariaLabel ?? (!label ? placeholder : undefined)}\n      className={cx(\"group flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1\", className)}\n    >\n      {({ isEmpty, isDisabled, isInvalid }) => (\n        <>\n          {label ? (\n            <Label isRequired={props.isRequired} isInvalid={isInvalid}>\n              {label}\n            </Label>\n          ) : null}\n          <InputBase\n            ref={inputRef}\n            size={size}\n            placeholder={placeholder}\n            fieldClassName={cx(shape === \"pill\" && \"rounded-full\", fieldClassName)}\n            className=\"[&::-webkit-search-cancel-button]:hidden\"\n            leadingAddon={\n              // The icon and the light share one 20px box and cross-fade.\n              <span aria-hidden className=\"relative ms-1 size-5 shrink-0\">\n                <Icon\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"absolute inset-0 size-5 transition-[opacity,scale] duration-200 ease-out\",\n                    isLoading && \"scale-75 opacity-0\",\n                  )}\n                />\n                <span\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center transition-[opacity,scale] duration-200 ease-out\",\n                    // Hidden, the light stays mounted for the fade but stops turning.\n                    !isLoading && \"scale-75 opacity-0 [&_*]:[animation-play-state:paused]\",\n                  )}\n                >\n                  <SpinnerGlyph size=\"sm\" />\n                </span>\n              </span>\n            }\n            trailingAddon={\n              <CloseButton\n                size=\"2xs\"\n                aria-label={localize(clearLabel)}\n                aria-hidden={isEmpty || undefined}\n                tabIndex={-1}\n                disabled={isDisabled}\n                // Keep focus in the input while the pointer clears it.\n                onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}\n                onClick={clear}\n                className={cx(\n                  \"me-0.5 bg-background-tertiary-hover transition-[opacity,scale,color] duration-150 ease-out\",\n                  isEmpty || isDisabled ? \"pointer-events-none scale-50 opacity-0\" : \"scale-100 opacity-100\",\n                )}\n              />\n            }\n          />\n          {hint ? <HintText isInvalid={isInvalid}>{hint}</HintText> : null}\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaSearchField>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component keeps one piece of state and a ref; the spinner's CSS animation is paused while hidden.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The fades are CSS transitions and the component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion` itself. Spinner's glyph does: under `motion-reduce` its spin slows from 900ms to 2400ms per turn.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not unmount the spinner when not loading. It stays mounted at `opacity-0` with its animation paused so it can fade in and out; unmounting would make it pop.\n- Do not put the clear button in the tab order. Escape already clears from the keyboard, and a tabbable 16px button would add an extra stop on every search.\n- Do not remove the `onMouseDown` `preventDefault` on the clear button. Without it, pressing the button blurs the input first, which closes any results popover tied to focus.\n- Do not leave the field with no `label`, `aria-label` or string `placeholder`. Nothing would name it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The field imports `InputBase` from Input, `Label` and `HintText` from Input's companions, `CloseButton` from Close Button and `SpinnerGlyph` from Spinner, so install those items alongside it.\n- The clear button always occupies its slot, even when hidden, so the text area width does not change when text is typed.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAn empty pill field shows a search icon and no clear button. Typing a letter scales the clear button in at the end; clicking it empties the field and keeps the caret in it. Escape also clears it. While `isLoading` is true the search icon shrinks away and a spinning light takes its place, then swaps back when loading ends. With `shape=\"rounded\"` the corners match Input. Tab never lands on the clear button.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "segmented-control": "# Build: Segmented Control\n\nA single-select segmented control on React Aria's ToggleButtonGroup, so arrow keys move focus, Space or Enter selects, and the selection can never be emptied. Instead of each segment painting its own background, one absolutely positioned thumb is measured off the selected button and slides between segments over 200ms. A `plain` variant drops the track and the thumb and marks the selection with text weight and colour alone.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA pill switcher for Weekly / Monthly / Yearly choices: one white thumb, measured from the DOM, slides under the selected segment.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `SegmentedControl` renders an `AriaToggleButtonGroup` with `selectionMode=\"single\"` and `disallowEmptySelection` fixed, then spreads the caller's props (`selectedKeys`, `defaultSelectedKeys`, `onSelectionChange`, `aria-label`, ...). The `solid` track is `inline-flex gap-0.5 rounded-2lg p-1` on `bg-segmented-control-background`.\n2. In a layout effect (a plain effect on the server) it queries the group for the `[data-selected]` button that React Aria marks and stores its `offsetLeft`, `offsetTop`, `offsetWidth` and `offsetHeight`. A 0px width (the control is hidden or not laid out yet) or no selection stores no thumb, and equal measurements keep the previous state so observer callbacks cannot re-render in a loop. Offsets are relative to the group because the group is `relative`, so the thumb needs no `getBoundingClientRect` arithmetic.\n3. The thumb is a single `aria-hidden` span at `left-0 top-0`, placed with `transform: translate(left, top)` and sized with explicit `width` / `height`, with `transition-[transform,width,height] duration-200`. Moving between segments of different widths therefore slides and resizes in one motion.\n4. Re-measuring is event driven, never polled: a ResizeObserver watches the group and every button (labels can change width when fonts load or text changes), and a MutationObserver on the subtree re-observes new buttons and re-measures when `data-selected` or the children change. Both disconnect on cleanup; the effect re-runs when the document direction flips.\n5. Each `SegmentedControlItem` is a `relative z-10` React Aria `ToggleButton` above the thumb: `px-2.5 py-1 rounded-md`, `text-body-medium text-text-primary` when selected and `text-body-regular text-text-secondary` otherwise, with a 200ms colour transition, a focus-visible ring and 50% opacity when disabled. Until the thumb exists (the server render, or a hidden control) a context flag gives the selected segment the thumb's own `bg-segmented-control-selected-background shadow-2xs`, so first paint already shows the white pill; once the thumb is measured the segment drops it and the thumb takes over and slides.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `thumb transition = 200ms on transform, width and height`\n  Long enough to read as one object moving to the new choice, short enough that rapid clicking never lags behind the selection.\n- `track = p-1, gap-0.5, rounded-2lg (10px); segments rounded-md (6px)`\n  From the Figma switch (node 3731:3096). A 4px inset with a 6px inner radius keeps the thumb's corners concentric with the 10px track.\n- `segment padding = px-2.5 py-1`\n  Compact enough to sit in a card header next to a title without dominating it.\n- `thumb shadow = shadow-2xs: 0 1px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.16)`\n  A single-pixel bottom edge lifts the white thumb off the light-grey track without a blurred shadow.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SegmentedControl(props: Omit<ToggleButtonGroupProps, \"selectionMode\" | \"disallowEmptySelection\"> & { variant?: \"solid\" | \"plain\"; children?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction SegmentedControlItem(props: Omit<ToggleButtonProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `selectedKeys / defaultSelectedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Controlled or initial selection; holds exactly one item id.\n- `onSelectionChange: (keys: Set<Key>) => void`\n  Fires with the new one-key set.\n- `variant: \"solid\" | \"plain\"` — default `\"solid\"`\n  `plain` drops the track background and the thumb.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Names the group for assistive technology.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  On the group or a single `SegmentedControlItem`; disabled items drop to 50% opacity.\n- `id: Key`\n  SegmentedControlItem only. The key reported in the selection set.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SegmentedControl({ className, children, variant = \"solid\", ref, ...props }: SegmentedControlProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const innerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [thumb, setThumb] = useState<Thumb | null>(null);\n\n  useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {\n    const el = innerRef.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    const measure = () => {\n      const selected = el.querySelector<HTMLElement>(\"[data-selected]\");\n      // Nothing selected, or a 0px box (display: none, not laid out yet):\n      // drop the thumb so the segment's own fill shows. The ResizeObserver\n      // measures again once the segment has a size.\n      const next =\n        selected && selected.offsetWidth > 0\n          ? {\n              left: selected.offsetLeft,\n              top: selected.offsetTop,\n              width: selected.offsetWidth,\n              height: selected.offsetHeight,\n            }\n          : null;\n      // Skip equal values so observer callbacks never re-render in a loop.\n      setThumb((prev) =>\n        prev && next && prev.left === next.left && prev.top === next.top && prev.width === next.width && prev.height === next.height\n          ? prev\n          : next,\n      );\n    };\n    measure();\n    const ro = new ResizeObserver(measure);\n    ro.observe(el);\n    const observeItems = () => el.querySelectorAll(\"button\").forEach(item => ro.observe(item));\n    observeItems();\n    const mo = new MutationObserver(() => { observeItems(); measure(); });\n    mo.observe(el, { attributes: true, childList: true, characterData: true, subtree: true, attributeFilter: [\"data-selected\"] });\n    return () => {\n      mo.disconnect();\n      ro.disconnect();\n    };\n  }, [direction]);\n\n  const setRefs = (node: HTMLDivElement | null) => {\n    innerRef.current = node;\n    if (typeof ref === \"function\") ref(node);\n    else if (ref) (ref as { current: HTMLDivElement | null }).current = node;\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <AriaToggleButtonGroup\n      ref={setRefs}\n      selectionMode=\"single\"\n      disallowEmptySelection\n      {...props}\n      className={(state) =>\n        cx(\n          \"relative inline-flex items-start gap-0.5 rounded-2lg\",\n          variant === \"solid\" && \"bg-segmented-control-background p-1\",\n          typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {variant === \"solid\" && thumb && (\n        <span\n          aria-hidden\n          className=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 rounded-md bg-segmented-control-selected-background shadow-2xs transition-[transform,width,height] duration-200 ease\"\n          style={{\n            transform: `translate(${thumb.left}px, ${thumb.top}px)`,\n            width: thumb.width,\n            height: thumb.height,\n          }}\n        />\n      )}\n      <SelectedFillContext.Provider value={variant === \"solid\" && !thumb}>{children}</SelectedFillContext.Provider>\n    </AriaToggleButtonGroup>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The layout effect disconnects its ResizeObserver and MutationObserver when the control unmounts or the direction changes. No timers or window listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not branch on `prefers-reduced-motion`. The only motion is the 200ms thumb slide plus colour fades on the labels, and the selected state is also carried by text weight and colour, so turning transitions off loses nothing.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not give each selected segment its own background once the thumb is measured. The point is a single thumb that travels; per-segment backgrounds jump with no motion. The segment fill is only the stand-in before the first measurement.\n- Do not measure with `getBoundingClientRect` against the viewport. The thumb is positioned inside the `relative` group, so `offsetLeft` / `offsetTop` are already in the right space and survive page scroll.\n- Do not measure only once on mount. Web fonts, translated labels and a direction flip all change segment widths; without the ResizeObserver and MutationObserver the thumb drifts off its segment.\n- Do not pass `selectionMode` or `disallowEmptySelection`. Both are fixed by the component and omitted from its props; a segmented control that can be emptied leaves the thumb parked on nothing.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Selection uses React Aria's key sets: `selectedKeys={new Set([\"monthly\"])}` with `onSelectionChange={(keys) => ...}`, where each item's `id` is its key.\n- Give the group an `aria-label` (\"Period\", \"View\"); React Aria renders it as `role=\"radiogroup\"` with radio items in single-selection mode.\n- The thumb renders only after the first measurement. Before that (on the server, or while the control is hidden) the selected segment paints the same white fill itself, so server HTML and first paint match the measured look.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA grey pill track holds Weekly, Monthly and Yearly with a white thumb under the selected one. Clicking another segment slides and resizes the thumb to it in about 200ms while its label turns medium weight and primary colour. Arrow keys move focus between segments and Space selects; clicking the selected segment keeps it selected. The `plain` variant shows no track or thumb, only the weight and colour change, and a disabled item is dimmed and cannot be chosen.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "select": "# Build: Select\n\nReact Aria's `Select` with BoardUI's trigger and menu chrome: a 10px-radius bordered button whose 16px chevron turns 180° while open, and a 266px popover that shares the Dropdown recipe (radius 2xl, shadow-dropdown, 150ms fade, scale and blur). The popover is deliberately non-modal so opening a select never locks page scroll, and the component restores the outside-press dismissal and trigger toggle that React Aria drops in that mode. Rows take any content, so a status dot, avatar or flag sits beside the text.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA React Aria select: bordered trigger with a turning chevron, non-modal popover on the shared menu surface, free-form rows.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Open state is controlled locally (`isOpen` in `useState`) and passed to `AriaSelect`. The `AriaPopover` is `isNonModal` with `offset={4}`, which skips React Aria's scroll lock: that lock sets `overflow: hidden` on `<html>` and, under an `h-full` root, makes the page jump each time a select opens.\n2. `isNonModal` also turns off React Aria's outside-press dismissal, so `useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, close, [triggerRef, popoverRef])` adds a capture-phase `pointerdown` listener on `document` while open and closes when the press lands outside both refs.\n3. Pressing the trigger while open makes React Aria call `onOpenChange(false)` then `onOpenChange(true)` in the same press. `useTriggerToggle` watches for that pointerdown and swallows the reopen, so `onOpenChange={(o) => allowOpenChange(o) && setIsOpen(o)}` turns the trigger into a true toggle.\n4. The trigger is an `AriaButton` holding `AriaSelectValue` (the selected row's own content, or `renderValue`) and `ChevronDownSmall`, which gets `rotate-180` from the render-prop `isOpen` and animates with `transition-transform duration-200`. The button itself transitions background, border, shadow, padding and font size over 200ms, so hover and a `size` switch both ease.\n5. The popover combines `MENU_POPOVER_WIDTH` (266px), `MENU_POPOVER_SURFACE` and `p-2`. The surface's `data-[entering]` and `data-[exiting]` classes run opacity 0, `scale-95` and `blur-[2px]` over 150ms ease-out, with `transform-origin` taken from `data-placement`. Inside, an `AriaListBox` scrolls past `max-h-[240px]`.\n6. `size` goes down to the rows through `SelectSizeContext`. `SelectItem` reads it to pick `text-body-medium` (md) or `px-2 py-1.5 text-body-2-medium` (sm), and applies `MENU_ITEM_ACTIVE` when the row is focused or selected.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover offset = 4px`\n  The gap between trigger and menu. It is enough to show the trigger border and shadow, and close enough that the menu reads as belonging to that trigger.\n- `menu width = w-[266px] (MENU_POPOVER_WIDTH)`\n  Shared with Dropdown so every menu in the kit has the same width. Pass `popoverClassName` to narrow it, for example to the trigger width in a filter bar.\n- `list height = max-h-[240px]`\n  About six rows at 36px plus 4px gaps. Longer lists scroll inside the popover instead of running past the viewport.\n- `trigger padding = md px-2.5 py-2, gap-1.5 / sm px-[7px] py-1, gap-1`\n  From Figma: md gives a 38px trigger that lines up with Input, and sm gives a 28px trigger for compact table toolbars.\n- `motion = chevron 200ms rotate; popover 150ms opacity/scale-95/blur-2px`\n  The menu is quicker than the chevron so the list is readable by the time the chevron settles. The 2px blur hides the scale step on the first frame.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Select<T extends object>(props: Omit<AriaSelectProps<T>, \"children\"> & { label?: ReactNode; className?: string; triggerClassName?: string; popoverClassName?: string; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; children: ReactNode; items?: Iterable<T>; renderValue?: ReactNode | ((values: AriaSelectValueRenderProps<T>) => ReactNode); ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element; function SelectItem(props: Omit<AriaListBoxItemProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Visible label above the trigger. React Aria wires it as the accessible name. Without it, pass `aria-label`.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Trigger padding and type size. Rows read the same size through context.\n- `placeholder: string`\n  React Aria's placeholder, shown in the trigger until something is selected.\n- `value / defaultValue / onChange: Key | null`\n  React Aria's selection value, controlled or uncontrolled. The older `selectedKey` props still work but are deprecated in React Aria.\n- `renderValue: ReactNode | ((values) => ReactNode)`\n  Custom trigger content. Falls back to the selected row's children.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the root, for example a fixed width.\n- `triggerClassName: string`\n  Classes on the trigger button.\n- `popoverClassName: string`\n  Classes on the popover, for example a narrower width.\n- `items: Iterable<T>`\n  Optional dynamic collection, rendered with a children function inside the listbox.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  `SelectItem` rows, each with an `id` and a `textValue`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Select<T extends object>({\n  label,\n  className,\n  triggerClassName,\n  popoverClassName,\n  size = \"md\",\n  children,\n  items,\n  renderValue,\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: SelectProps<T>) {\n  // The popover is `isNonModal`: react-aria's modal scroll lock puts\n  // `overflow: hidden` on <html>, which — with this app's `h-full` root —\n  // collapses the page scroll position and visibly yanks the sticky\n  // sidebar upward every time a select opens. Non-modal skips the lock,\n  // but also disables react-aria's outside-press dismissal (`isDismissable`\n  // is hard-coupled to `!isNonModal` in usePopover), so open state is\n  // controlled here and dismissal restored manually — the same fix as the\n  // date-picker family (see utils/use-dismiss-on-outside-press.ts).\n  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setIsOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\n  // Pressing the trigger while open closes the popover instead of reopening\n  const allowOpenChange = useTriggerToggle(isOpen, triggerRef);\n\n  return (\n    <AriaSelect\n      ref={ref}\n      {...props}\n      isOpen={isOpen}\n      onOpenChange={(o) => allowOpenChange(o) && setIsOpen(o)}\n      className={cx(\"group flex flex-col\", label ? \"gap-1\" : undefined, className)}\n    >\n      {({ isOpen, isRequired }) => (\n        <>\n          {label ? <Label isRequired={isRequired}>{label}</Label> : null}\n          <AriaButton\n            ref={triggerRef}\n            className={cx(\n              \"flex w-full cursor-pointer items-center justify-between rounded-2lg\",\n              \"border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs\",\n              \"text-text-primary\",\n              \"transition-[background-color,border-color,box-shadow,padding,font-size] duration-200 ease\",\n              \"hover:bg-background-primary-hover hover:border-border-button-hover\",\n              \"outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n              \"disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:bg-background-primary-disabled disabled:text-text-tertiary disabled:shadow-none\",\n              size === \"sm\"\n                ? \"gap-1 px-[7px] py-1 text-body-2-medium\"\n                : \"gap-1.5 px-2.5 py-2 text-body-medium\",\n              triggerClassName,\n            )}\n          >\n            <AriaSelectValue\n              className={cx(\n                \"flex min-w-0 items-center truncate\",\n                size === \"sm\" ? \"gap-1\" : \"gap-[5px]\",\n              )}\n            >\n              {renderValue as never /* RAC accepts node or render-prop */}\n            </AriaSelectValue>\n            <ChevronDownSmall\n              className={cx(\n                \"shrink-0 text-text-secondary transition-transform duration-200 ease\",\n                size === \"sm\" ? \"size-3.5\" : \"size-4\",\n                isOpen && \"rotate-180\",\n              )}\n            />\n          </AriaButton>\n          <AriaPopover\n            ref={popoverRef}\n            isNonModal\n            offset={4}\n            className={cx(\n              MENU_POPOVER_WIDTH,\n              MENU_POPOVER_SURFACE,\n              // Listbox rows already space themselves 4px apart, so the\n              // surface sits a touch tighter than the action-menu Dropdown.\n              \"p-2\",\n              popoverClassName,\n            )}\n          >\n            <AriaListBox\n              items={items}\n              className={cx(MENU_ITEMS_CONTAINER, \"max-h-[240px] overflow-auto\")}\n            >\n              <SelectSizeContext.Provider value={size}>{children}</SelectSizeContext.Provider>\n            </AriaListBox>\n          </AriaPopover>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaSelect>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The outside-press listener is added in an effect only while the popover is open and removed in that effect's cleanup, both on close and on unmount. `useTriggerToggle` clears its 400ms safety timeout the same way. React Aria owns the popover portal and removes it on close.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The chevron rotation, the trigger colour transitions and the popover fade, scale and blur are CSS transitions of 150–200ms with no JavaScript tween. They do not change position on screen, so they run under `prefers-reduced-motion` too. Add `motion-reduce:transition-none` through `popoverClassName` and `triggerClassName` to remove them.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving out both `label` and `aria-label`. React Aria then logs an accessibility warning and screen readers announce an unnamed button. Pass one of them.\n- Removing `isNonModal` to get dismissal for free. The modal popover's scroll lock returns and the page jumps under an `h-full` root on every open.\n- Giving a `SelectItem` rich children with no `textValue`. Typeahead and the accessible name then fall back to an empty string, so pass `textValue` whenever children are not plain text.\n- Passing `isOpen` or `onOpenChange` from outside. The component controls open state itself, and the spread comes before its own props, so outside values are overwritten.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The trigger value is the selected row's own content, so a row with a status dot shows the dot in the trigger too. Use `renderValue` when the trigger needs a more compact form, such as a flag and a dial code.\n- Like the other BoardUI controls, `className` and `triggerClassName` merge through tailwind-merge, so `w-[240px]` replaces the default width cleanly.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking the trigger opens a 266px menu 4px below it with a short fade and scale, and the chevron turns 180°. Picking a row closes the menu and shows that row's content, status dot included, in the trigger. Clicking the trigger again, clicking outside, or pressing Escape closes the menu, and the page does not scroll or jump. Arrow keys move the highlight, and typing a letter jumps to the matching `textValue`. No accessibility warning appears in the console when `label` or `aria-label` is passed.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "self-parking-raf": "# Build: Self-Parking RAF\n\nAn eased value never arrives. Closing 12% of the remaining gap each frame closes it 100% never — the distance decays asymptotically and `current !== target` stays true for the life of the page, so a loop conditioned on that comparison never stops. One line fixes it: below a threshold, assign the target outright. The equality test can now succeed, the loop reaches its own stopping condition, and it unsubscribes itself. The frame callback exists only while something is genuinely moving.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn epsilon snap lets a damped value reach its target exactly, so the frame loop hits its exit test and dies.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The damped step is `damp(current, target, smoothing, dt)`, which is `current + (target - current) * (1 - smoothing^dt)`. The exponent is what makes it frame-rate independent: the naked `* 0.12` from the source converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz, so the same code feels different on different hardware and visibly speeds up whenever the browser drops frames.\n2. After the step, `Math.abs(target - next) < epsilon` assigns `next = target` exactly. This is the entire technique. Without it the loop's exit condition — `next === target` — is unreachable, because exponential decay produces 1e-8, then 1e-9, then a denormal, and never a clean zero.\n3. Parking is expressed as React state, not as a local flag: `setRunning(false)` drops the `useRaf` subscription, and when the last subscriber leaves the shared loop calls `cancelAnimationFrame` and stops scheduling frames at all. The saving is the whole callback plus the paint it would have dirtied, not merely the arithmetic.\n4. Waking is driven by a `target` change in an effect, guarded by `value.current === target`. The guard is load-bearing: without it any unrelated re-render that happens to pass the same target restarts a loop that had already parked, which is how \"self-parking\" quietly degrades back into an always-on loop.\n5. Because `perFrameToSmoothing(0.12)` is `0.88^60 ≈ 0.00047`, the value retains 0.047% of its gap after a second — so the settle time is a few hundred milliseconds and the epsilon is crossed within roughly twenty frames rather than being an escape hatch from a genuinely long tail.\n6. A hidden tab and reduced motion both take the same short path: `park()` assigns the target, emits one `onFrame`, and never starts a loop. No observer means no reason to interpolate, and the value stays correct either way.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `per-frame rate = 0.12`\n  Fraction of the remaining gap closed per frame at 60fps, converted through `perFrameToSmoothing`. It settles in about 300 ms — fast enough that a scrubber feels attached to the click, slow enough to read as motion. Above ~0.3 the damping is barely visible and the loop may as well be a jump; below ~0.05 the tail is long enough that a user beats it with the next click.\n- `EPSILON = 0.0002`\n  Snap threshold, in the units of the value. For the 0-1 normalised scrub position of the source this is a fifth of a pixel on a 1000 px track — invisible. Set it to 0 and the loop never parks, which is the exact bug this component exists to fix. Set it far too large and the last visible fraction of the travel is skipped as a jump.\n- `MAX_DT = 0.1`\n  Inherited from `useRaf`. A tab returning from the background delivers a multi-second delta; unclamped, `smoothing^dt` underflows to 0, the damp closes the entire gap in one frame, and the \"settle\" is a teleport.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useSelfParkingRaf(target: number, options: { smoothing?: number; epsilon?: number; onFrame: (value: number) => void }): { running: boolean; value: RefObject<number>; park: () => void }\n```\n\n- `target: number`\n  The value to settle toward. Changing it wakes the loop; it is the only trigger.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `perFrameToSmoothing(0.12)`\n  Fraction of the gap remaining after one second. Smaller is snappier.\n- `epsilon: number` — default `0.0002`\n  Distance below which the value snaps to the target and the loop parks. Must match the unit of the value.\n- `onFrame: (value: number) => void`\n  Called with the damped value every frame while running, and once when it parks. For DOM writes.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * `0.12` of the gap per frame, measured at 60fps, expressed as the retention\n * factor `damp` wants. Using the raw 0.12 with a per-frame multiply would run\n * twice as fast on a 120 Hz display; this converges in the same wall-clock\n * time on any refresh rate.\n */\nconst SMOOTHING = perFrameToSmoothing(0.12);\n/** The snap threshold. This single line is what allows the loop to end. */\nconst EPSILON = 0.0002;\n\n/**\n * A damped value that runs a frame loop only while it is moving.\n *\n * `target` is a plain prop: changing it wakes the loop, and the loop parks\n * itself once it arrives. There is no `start`, and there is no\n * `requestAnimationFrame` running when the value is at rest.\n */\nexport function useSelfParkingRaf(\n  target: number,\n  options: SelfParkingOptions,\n): SelfParkingValue {\n  const { smoothing = SMOOTHING, epsilon = EPSILON } = options;\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n\n  const value = useRef(target);\n  const [running, setRunning] = useState(false);\n\n  const config = useRef({ smoothing, epsilon, target, options });\n  config.current = { smoothing, epsilon, target, options };\n\n  const park = useCallback(() => {\n    const cfg = config.current;\n    value.current = cfg.target;\n    cfg.options.onFrame(value.current);\n    setRunning(false);\n  }, []);\n\n  // Waking the loop is the only thing a target change does. The guard matters:\n  // without it, any unrelated re-render that happens to pass the same target\n  // restarts a loop that had already parked.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (value.current === target) return;\n    // Nothing to interpolate: reduced motion wants the end state, and a hidden\n    // tab has no observer, so both arrive in one write instead of a loop.\n    if (reduced || !visible) {\n      park();\n      return;\n    }\n    setRunning(true);\n  }, [target, reduced, visible, park]);\n\n  useRaf((dt) => {\n    const cfg = config.current;\n    let next = damp(value.current, cfg.target, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n\n    // The whole technique. Asymptotic approach means `next` would otherwise\n    // never equal `cfg.target`, so the exit test below could never pass.\n    if (Math.abs(cfg.target - next) < cfg.epsilon) next = cfg.target;\n\n    value.current = next;\n    cfg.options.onFrame(next);\n\n    // Parking flips `running`, which drops the `useRaf` subscription. When the\n    // last subscriber goes, the shared loop cancels its frame entirely.\n    if (next === cfg.target) setRunning(false);\n  }, running);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` removes the subscription when `running` goes false or the component unmounts, and cancels the shared frame when it was the last subscriber — so an unmount mid-settle leaves nothing scheduled. The visibility and media-query listeners belong to `useVisibility` and `useReducedMotion`, which detach their own. No timers and no direct `requestAnimationFrame` handles are held.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No loop is started at all. A target change calls `park()`, which assigns the target and emits a single `onFrame`, so the value lands on its final position in one write. That is the still, correct state rather than a zero-duration animation: `onFrame` still fires, so the DOM the consumer writes is identical to the end of a full settle, and `running` never goes true.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Omitting the epsilon snap. The value approaches the target forever, the exit test never passes, and one hover or one scrub leaves a rAF running for the rest of the session. It is invisible in a demo and shows up as a battery complaint.\n- Expressing epsilon in the wrong unit. The threshold is compared against the value, so 0.0002 is correct for a 0-1 progress value and absurd for a pixel offset — at pixel scale it costs dozens of extra frames to cross, and the loop appears not to park.\n- Keeping the loop alive to \"avoid the cost of restarting it\". Subscribing to a shared rAF is a `Set.add`; a frame callback plus the paint it dirties is orders of magnitude more. Restarting is free, running is not.\n- Using `current += (target - current) * 0.12` directly. Frame-rate dependent: the same settle takes 300 ms at 60 Hz and 150 ms at 120 Hz, and speeds up mid-motion whenever a long task drops frames — the animation gets faster precisely when the page is struggling.\n- Waking the loop without comparing against the current value. Every re-render that re-supplies the same target restarts the loop, which parks on its first frame and restarts on the next render — a loop that thrashes instead of resting.\n- Calling `setState` from `onFrame`. That re-renders on every frame of the settle, which costs far more than the loop being parked ever saved. `onFrame` is for DOM writes.\n- Reading the returned `value` ref during render. It changes between renders without notifying React, so the rendered output and the ref disagree; read it inside a frame callback or an event handler only.\n- Forgetting that the loop is asleep when the target is set imperatively. Anything that mutates the underlying source without changing the `target` prop leaves the value stale, because nothing is polling — the trade for not running a loop is that changes must be declared.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The natural pairing is with an invalidate-driven renderer: a WebGL scene that renders only on demand plus a value that only moves on demand gives a page that is genuinely idle at rest, not merely cheap.\n- The `park()` escape hatch exists for handoffs — a drag that takes over the value, or a route change that should not animate the old position. It emits one final `onFrame` so the DOM is left in the correct end state rather than mid-travel.\n- `running` is exposed because parking is otherwise unobservable, and a component that claims to park should be able to prove it. The demo prints it beside a frame count that freezes.\n- For several values settling together, one hook per value is fine — they all share the single `useRaf` loop, and the last one to park is the one that stops it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick a timecode. The bar and the readout race to it and stop, and the status line goes from \"rAF live\" to \"rAF parked\" with the frame count frozen at roughly twenty — not climbing. Leave it alone for a minute: the count does not move. Open DevTools performance and record while idle after a seek; there are no animation frames. Click the timecode that is already active and nothing wakes. Switch tabs mid-settle and come back — the bar is already at its target with no catch-up sweep. With reduced motion on, a seek moves the bar in one step, the count reads 1, and the status never leaves \"parked\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "settings-modal": "# Build: Settings Modal\n\nA controlled `SettingsModal` (`isOpen` / `onClose`) portals an 871×614 panel with a grouped section rail on the left and a scrolling page on the right. It ships four pages built from shared row primitives: General (a plan card with a WebGL \"burning flag\" artwork, pull-request and notification preferences), Profile (fields that save on Enter or blur and trigger a \"Saved\" toast), Tools (MCP servers by scope) and Storage (a sortable, filterable, paginated file table with uploads). The open and close are the same transition run in reverse. The DOM is kept mounted until the close has finished.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA controlled settings dialog with a section rail and General, Profile, Tools and Storage pages. It scales in from 85% with a light blur.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria@^3.53.1, react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @internationalized/date@^3.12.4\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Opening runs in two phases. `mounted` puts the portal in the DOM at its hidden pose (`scale-[0.85] opacity-0 blur-[4px]`), and a double `requestAnimationFrame` flips `visible` one frame after that pose has been committed. Without the second frame the browser merges both class sets into one style recalc and skips the transition. Closing clears `visible` straight away and only unmounts after a 320ms timer, so the 300ms exit plays in full.\n2. The backdrop and the panel transition separately. The backdrop is a `bg-black/70` `<button>` (`tabIndex={-1}`, clicking it closes) that fades its opacity over 300ms. The wrapper carries `transform-gpu transition-[opacity,transform,filter]` on `cubic-bezier(0.32,0.72,0,1)`, a fast start that settles softly. The \"Saved\" toast lives in this same wrapper, outside the panel's clip, so it scales with the panel while sitting across its bottom edge.\n3. The portal target is `useUNSAFE_PortalContext().getContainer?.() ?? document.body`. Hosts that clip fixed children, such as a scaled preview frame, can hand it their own container. Opening focuses the panel (`role=\"dialog\"`, `aria-modal`, `tabIndex={-1}`), and a document `keydown` listener closes it on Escape. That listener only exists while `isOpen` is true.\n4. The rail renders `NAV_GROUPS` as Settings, Desktop app and Customize. Only entries that carry a `page` navigate. The others (Appearance, Billing, Plugins…) are inert rows that complete the layout. Marketplace only appears when a `marketplace` node is passed. Each open resets the page to `defaultPage`. Switching pages resets the scroll fade, an `h-10` gradient over the top of the scroller that fades in over 200ms once `scrollTop > 0`.\n5. The Saved toast is a three-phase state machine: `hidden → shown → leaving → hidden`. Each phase has its own offset: it rises in from 12px below its resting spot, stays for 2000ms, then drifts 10px up while fading and blurring. After 220ms it is quietly reset to the bottom start, so the next save rises in again instead of falling back down. A new save within the window restarts the timer.\n6. General's plan art is `PlanArtFlame`. It is a fullscreen-quad fragment shader that ripples the artwork like a flag, with the most movement at the edges, and burns it away with 4-octave fbm noise behind an ember rim and three layers of rising flakes. The pointer hotspot is eased toward the cursor every frame. With no `planArtSrc` it burns built-in blobs painted from theme colour tokens. If WebGL is unavailable it falls back to a static DOM blur.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `enter / exit pose = scale 0.85, opacity 0, blur 4px → 1, 1, 0 over 300ms`\n  An 85% start reads as the dialog coming forward rather than popping. The blur was 8px until the close stuttered. Re-filtering an 8px blur over the whole 871×614 panel every frame of the scale-down was the cost, and 4px with GPU promotion stays smooth.\n- `unmount delay = 320ms`\n  20ms longer than the 300ms transition, so the last frame of the exit lands before the portal is removed. If it is equal or shorter, the panel can vanish one frame early on a busy main thread.\n- `panel size = 871 × 614 px, capped at calc(100vw-32px) × calc(100dvh-32px)`\n  Taken from the Figma frame. The caps keep a 16px gutter on small screens, and the rail falls back to icons only (`w-14`) below `sm` so the page still has room.\n- `saved toast timing = 2000ms shown, 200ms transitions, 220ms reset`\n  Two seconds is enough to notice and still gone before the next edit. The reset waits 20ms past the fade so the jump back to the start offset happens at opacity 0 and is never seen.\n- `storage table = 1,262 rows (mulberry32 seed 26), 6 per page, 400ms row enter / 225ms row exit`\n  A seeded PRNG generates the same inventory on the server and the client, so there is no hydration mismatch. The exit is shorter than the enter because removing a row should feel immediate, while a new row needs time to be noticed.\n- `flame pointer easing = position 0.12, strength 0.07 per frame`\n  Position follows quickly enough that the heat clearly tracks the cursor. Strength eases about half as fast, so the burn swells and cools gradually instead of switching on and off at the canvas edge.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype SettingsPage = \"general\" | \"profile\" | \"storage\" | \"tools\" | \"marketplace\"\nfunction SettingsModal(props: { isOpen: boolean; onClose: () => void; defaultPage?: SettingsPage; planArtSrc?: string; marketplace?: ReactNode }): ReactPortal | null\n```\n\n- `isOpen: boolean`\n  Controlled open state. Flipping it to false plays the exit and unmounts 320ms later.\n- `onClose: () => void`\n  Called by the backdrop, the close button and Escape. The parent sets `isOpen` back to false.\n- `defaultPage: SettingsPage` — default `\"general\"`\n  Page shown each time the dialog opens. Read on the open transition only.\n- `planArtSrc: string`\n  Image burned by the plan card's WebGL artwork. Omit it to burn the built-in token-coloured blobs.\n- `marketplace: ReactNode`\n  Content for an optional Marketplace page. The rail entry is hidden unless this is provided.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Mount/visible two-phase state so both the enter and the exit play the\n// full fade + blur + scale transition before the DOM goes away.\nconst [mounted, setMounted] = useState(false);\nconst [visible, setVisible] = useState(false);\nconst unmountTimer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\nconst panelRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n\n// \"Saved\" toast — floats half-out of the panel's bottom edge for ~2s\n// whenever a profile field commits a change (Enter / blur). Three phases\n// so the motion is directional: it rises in from below (\"hidden\" starting\n// offset) and keeps drifting upward while fading out (\"leaving\" offset).\nconst [savedPhase, setSavedPhase] = useState<\"hidden\" | \"shown\" | \"leaving\">(\"hidden\");\nconst savedTimer = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null>(null);\nconst showSavedToast = () => {\n  if (savedTimer.current) clearTimeout(savedTimer.current);\n  setSavedPhase(\"shown\");\n  savedTimer.current = setTimeout(() => {\n    setSavedPhase(\"leaving\");\n    // Reset to the below-the-edge start once the fade-out finished, so the\n    // next save rises in from the bottom again (invisible: opacity 0 → 0).\n    savedTimer.current = setTimeout(() => setSavedPhase(\"hidden\"), 220);\n  }, 2000);\n};\n\n// Top fade over the scrolling page so rows dissolve under the title row\n// instead of cutting sharply (same recipe as the medical alerts feed).\nconst [contentScrolled, setContentScrolled] = useState(false);\n\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (isOpen) {\n    if (unmountTimer.current) clearTimeout(unmountTimer.current);\n    setPage(defaultPage);\n    setMounted(true);\n    // Double rAF: the panel must commit its hidden state before the\n    // transition to visible starts, or the browser skips the animation.\n    requestAnimationFrame(() => requestAnimationFrame(() => setVisible(true)));\n  } else {\n    setVisible(false);\n    setSavedPhase(\"hidden\");\n    unmountTimer.current = setTimeout(() => setMounted(false), 320);\n  }\n  return () => {\n    if (unmountTimer.current) clearTimeout(unmountTimer.current);\n    if (savedTimer.current) clearTimeout(savedTimer.current);\n  };\n  // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps -- defaultPage only matters at the open transition\n}, [isOpen]);\n\n// Escape closes; focus moves into the dialog on open.\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (!isOpen) return;\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {\n    if (event.key === \"Escape\") onClose();\n  };\n  document.addEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n  panelRef.current?.focus();\n  return () => document.removeEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n}, [isOpen, onClose]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Closing clears the Saved-toast timers and schedules the unmount timer. Unmounting clears both. The Escape listener is removed whenever `isOpen` goes false. `PlanArtFlame` cancels its rAF, removes its window `pointermove` listener, calls `WEBGL_lose_context.loseContext()` and removes its canvas. The canvas is created inside the effect, so StrictMode's re-run gets a fresh context rather than the dead one. The Storage page clears its pending row-delete and just-added timers on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Not handled in JavaScript. The open and close transitions, the Saved toast and the scroll fade all run at full duration under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, and the plan card's WebGL flame keeps animating. Only the Storage row enter and exit animations are cut to 1ms by the theme sheet. To honour the setting, add `motion-reduce:transition-none` to the wrapper and backdrop, and gate `PlanArtFlame`'s rAF loop on the media query, drawing one frame instead.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Setting `visible` in the same commit as `mounted`. The panel never renders its hidden pose, so the open snaps in. The double rAF is not a superstition.\n- Using `overflow-hidden` on the panel instead of `overflow-clip`. A hidden-overflow box is still programmatically scrollable, so focusing a switch's visually hidden input in a row clipped by the inner scroller scrolls the whole panel and shifts the modal with no way back. `clip` forbids scrolling outright.\n- Passing an inline arrow as `onClose` and expecting the Escape listener to stay put. The effect depends on `onClose`, so a new function every render re-binds the listener and re-focuses the panel. Memoise it if the parent re-renders while the dialog is open.\n- Rendering the modal inside a transformed or `overflow-hidden` ancestor without a portal container. `fixed inset-0` becomes relative to the transformed ancestor and the dialog is clipped to it. Provide an `UNSAFE_PortalProvider` container or let it fall back to `document.body`.\n- Opening many settings dialogs at once in a gallery. Each General page creates its own WebGL context, and Chromium silently drops the oldest one at about 16.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The rows (`SettingsCard`, `SettingsRow`, `SettingsSectionLabel`, `SettingsValueField`) live in `settings-rows.tsx`, so a new page can be added with the same rhythm: 52px rows, a 202px value column and a hairline between rows.\n- The Storage page uses data-URI SVGs from `settings-storage-icons.ts` in place of BoardUI's `/public` artwork, so the item works without static assets.\n- `SettingsProfile` uses `@internationalized/date` for its birth-date picker. That is why the date package is a direct dependency even though the modal shell never touches dates.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nNothing is on screen until the trigger is clicked. Opening scales the dialog up from about 85% while it fades in and sharpens, behind a 70% black backdrop. Clicking the backdrop, the round close button or pressing Escape plays the same motion in reverse, and the DOM is gone afterwards. Rail items with pages switch the content and highlight the selected row. Scrolling a page fades in a gradient under the title. On General, the plan card's artwork ripples and burns at the edges, and the burn intensifies near the cursor. On Profile, changing a name and pressing Enter shows a \"Saved\" pill that rises in across the bottom edge and drifts up and away after about 2 seconds. On Storage, sorting, filtering, paging, selecting and deleting rows all work, and deleted rows collapse upward.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "sidebar": "# Build: Sidebar\n\nA floating `<aside>` with an account switcher at the top, Quick Search, the primary nav and, at the bottom, Support, Settings and a team card. One button folds it from 260px to a 60px rail. The panel width animates, and every label, badge and shortcut hint blurs and narrows away while the icons stay where they were. The account and team cards open React Aria popovers beside the rail, and Settings opens the kit's `SettingsModal`.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA floating dashboard sidebar that folds from 260px to a 60px icon rail, with ⌘L nav search, team and account menus and a settings entry.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Collapsing animates two things on the same 300ms ease-in-out clock. The aside's `width` goes from 260px to 60px, and each text slot is wrapped in `Collapsible`, which moves `max-width` from full to 0, `opacity` from 1 to 0 and `filter` from blur(0) to blur(3px). The icons are never re-centred, so they stay pinned to the left edge while the panel closes over them.\n2. Each row is an `<a>` with `aria-label`, `aria-current` on the selected row and a `title` tooltip only while collapsed. The selected row is filled with a top-to-bottom accent gradient and `shadow-nav-selected`, and its badge switches to the primary colour. Rows passed through `items` are filtered against the search query by a separate `NavRows` component, so the closures over the query and `collapsed` stay out of the sidebar body.\n3. Quick Search has three ways in: the button, ⌘L or Ctrl+L, and expanding the rail first if it is collapsed. All of them set `searchActive`. The field replaces the button with a 2px inset ring and its input is focused one animation frame later, once it exists. A document `click` listener closes the search when a click lands outside the field. Escape clears it and puts focus back on the trigger on the next frame. Support and Settings are filtered too, and \"No results\" appears only when nothing in either list matches.\n4. The scroller is wider than the rail by 16px (`-m-2 p-2 w-[calc(100%+16px)]`). A vertical `overflow-y: auto` box also clips horizontally, so without that extra room the selected row's ring, the focus rings and the account trigger's 6px hover pill would be cut off at the rail edge. The negative margin gives the space back, so nothing moves.\n5. The account and team cards are React Aria `DialogTrigger` + `Popover` pairs, 265px wide and offset 8px. The account menu opens to the right and aligned to the top of its trigger, and the team menu to the right and aligned to the bottom. In RTL they open to the left, and below 640px they drop under the trigger. Both animate 150ms ease-out from `data-[entering]` / `data-[exiting]` states at opacity 0, scale 0.95 and blur 2px. React Aria handles focus containment, Escape and outside-click dismissal.\n6. Expanding the rail sets `suppressHover` on the account trigger. Its hover pill is drawn by a `::before` with `-inset-x-1.5 -inset-y-[5px]`. Without the flag, the newly expanded trigger would slide under a pointer that has not moved and light its pill. The flag is cleared on the trigger's next `pointerleave`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `rail widths = 260px expanded, 60px collapsed (px 11, py 12)`\n  The collapsed width is 1px border + 11px padding on each side, which leaves exactly 36px, the width of a `w-9` icon row. So icons sit centred in the rail without being moved, and the selected fill fits the column with no clipping.\n- `collapse timing = 300ms ease-in-out, label blur 3px`\n  Width, label fade and icon flip share one duration, so the panel edge and the disappearing text arrive together. A 3px blur is enough to soften the text as it is squeezed, so it never reads as being cut off.\n- `popover motion = 150ms ease-out, scale 0.95, blur 2px, offset 8px`\n  Menus are opened often and should feel instant, so they run at half the rail's duration. A 0.95 scale from the corner nearest the trigger (`origin-top-left` / `origin-bottom-left`) makes the panel read as coming out of the card.\n- `search shortcut = ⌘L / Ctrl+L on window`\n  Matches the Kbd hint on the button. The handler calls `preventDefault`, so while the sidebar is mounted the shortcut opens the nav search instead of focusing the browser's address bar.\n- `mobile breakpoint = (max-width: 639px)`\n  Below Tailwind's `sm`, the sidebar can span the whole viewport, and a 265px popover placed to its right would render off-screen. Both menus switch to `bottom start` under that query.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ninterface DashboardNavItem { key: string; label: string; icon: IconComponent; href?: string; badge?: string | number }\nconst DASHBOARD_NAV: DashboardNavItem[]\nfunction DashboardSidebar(props?: { mobile?: boolean; onClose?: () => void; fluid?: boolean; showThemeToggle?: boolean; selected?: DashboardNavKey; items?: DashboardNavItem[]; flat?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction DashboardTeamMenu(props: { collapsed?: boolean; className?: string; showThemeToggle?: boolean }): JSX.Element\nfunction DashboardUserMenu(props: { collapsed?: boolean; suppressHover?: boolean; onHoverSuppressionEnd?: () => void; avatarClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `selected: string` — default `\"home\"`\n  Key of the row drawn in the filled accent state.\n- `items: DashboardNavItem[]` — default `DASHBOARD_NAV`\n  Primary navigation rows: key, label, icon component, optional href and badge.\n- `showThemeToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Shows the light/dark segmented control above the footer. It toggles the document theme, so hide it when the sidebar is used as artwork.\n- `fluid: boolean` — default `false`\n  Expanded width fills the container below `lg` instead of a fixed 260px. The collapsed rail stays 60px.\n- `mobile: boolean` — default `false`\n  Drawer mode: always expanded, with a close button in place of the collapse toggle.\n- `onClose: () => void`\n  Called by the mobile close button.\n- `flat: boolean` — default `false`\n  Removes the border, radius and shadow and paints the full background.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the aside.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Collapsible text/badge slot: blurs + fades + shrinks away when the rail\n * closes, and blurs back in on expand. The icons/rows themselves stay pinned in\n * place — only these label/badge slots animate — so nothing jumps to center.\n */\nfunction Collapsible({ collapsed, children, className }: { collapsed: boolean; children: ReactNode; className?: string }) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  return localize((\n    <span\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex min-w-0 items-center overflow-hidden transition-[max-width,opacity,filter] duration-300 ease-in-out\",\n        // Expanded, the cap is the row itself: a fixed cap (it was 160px)\n        // clipped any label wider than it, \"Components and Blocks\" included.\n        collapsed ? \"max-w-0 opacity-0 blur-[3px]\" : \"max-w-full opacity-100 blur-none\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {children}\n    </span>\n  ));\n}\n\nfunction NavItem({\n  icon: Icon,\n  label,\n  badge,\n  isSelected = false,\n  collapsed = false,\n  href = \"#\",\n  onClick,\n}: {\n  icon: IconComponent;\n  label: string;\n  badge?: ReactNode;\n  isSelected?: boolean;\n  collapsed?: boolean;\n  href?: string;\n  /** Action rows (e.g. Settings → modal) intercept the navigation. */\n  onClick?: () => void;\n}) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  return localize((\n    <a\n      href={href}\n      onClick={\n        onClick\n          ? (event) => {\n              event.preventDefault();\n              onClick();\n            }\n          : undefined\n      }\n      aria-current={isSelected ? \"page\" : undefined}\n      aria-label={label}\n      title={collapsed ? label : undefined}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex items-center justify-between overflow-hidden rounded-2lg p-2\",\n        \"transition-[width,background-color] duration-300 ease-in-out\",\n        collapsed ? \"w-9\" : \"w-full\",\n        isSelected\n          ? \"bg-linear-to-b from-accent-500 to-accent-600 shadow-nav-selected\"\n          : \"hover:bg-background-secondary-hover\",\n      )}\n    >\n      <span className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-2\">\n        <Icon\n          className={cx(\"size-5 shrink-0\", isSelected ? \"text-white\" : \"text-foreground-icon-secondary\")}\n          aria-hidden\n        />\n        <Collapsible collapsed={collapsed}>\n          <span\n            className={cx(\n              \"text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap\",\n              isSelected ? \"text-white\" : \"text-text-secondary\",\n            )}\n          >\n            {label}\n          </span>\n        </Collapsible>\n      </span>\n      {badge && <Collapsible collapsed={collapsed}>{badge}</Collapsible>}\n    </a>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Every listener is scoped to an effect: the window `keydown` for ⌘L (always), the document `click` for outside-click (only while search is active) and the rAF that focuses the search input (cancelled if search closes first). Each is removed on unmount or when its condition ends. The account menu removes its `matchMedia` change listener, and the team menu reads the same query through `useSyncExternalStore`, which unsubscribes itself. React Aria tears down the popovers' focus scope and dismiss handlers when they close.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Not handled. The rail width, label blur, search field and popover transitions run at full duration under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, and none of the three files uses `motion-reduce:` variants. The motion is short (150 to 300ms) and never loops, but to honour the setting add `motion-reduce:transition-none` to the aside, `Collapsible` and the two popovers. The theme toggle, when shown, already skips its view-transition reveal under reduced motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Clipping the nav scroller to the rail's own box. `overflow-y: auto` forces `overflow-x` to clip too, and the selected ring, the focus rings and the account trigger's hover pill all extend past the rows. Keep the padded, negatively-margined scroller.\n- Centring icons when the rail collapses (`justify-center` on the rows). The icons then jump sideways while the width animates. Only the label slots should change. The 60px rail is sized so the left-aligned icons already end up centred.\n- Animating labels with `width` or `display`. `display: none` cannot transition, and a fixed `max-width` cap (an earlier version used 160px) truncates long labels such as \"AI Image Generation\". The slot animates `max-width` between `full` and 0.\n- Rendering two sidebars on one page. Each registers its own window ⌘L listener, so a single shortcut opens search in both.\n- Rendering the sidebar inside a parent without a height. The aside is `h-full` and its nav scrolls inside it, so a parent with no height gives the nav nothing to scroll in and the footer is pushed below the fold.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `items` replaces the primary rows (the default is `DASHBOARD_NAV`), and `selected` takes any row `key`. Rows without an `href` fall back to `#`, so a host app that routes client-side should pass real paths.\n- `flat` drops the floating panel treatment for a sidebar revealed beneath mobile content. `mobile` swaps the collapse button for a close button, and with `flat` it turns search into an expanding pill next to the account trigger.\n- Settings is an action row. Its click handler calls `preventDefault` and opens `SettingsModal`, so the settings dialog comes with the sidebar as a registry dependency.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe sidebar fills the page height beside the content, with Home selected and its 152 badge. Clicking the fold icon narrows the panel to a 60px rail as labels, badges and the ⌘L hint blur away, and the icons do not move sideways. Clicking it again restores everything. Pressing ⌘L or Ctrl+L, or clicking Quick Search, opens a ringed search field (expanding the rail if needed). Typing filters the rows, \"zzz\" shows \"No results\", and Escape or a click outside closes it. Clicking the Jordan Lee trigger opens an account list to the right that blurs and scales in, and clicking the Acme team card opens the team menu to the right, aligned with the card's bottom edge. Escape closes either. Settings opens the settings dialog, which is closed until then.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "site-footer": "# Build: Site Footer\n\nThe footer that closes a marketing site. The brand mark and tagline sit on the left, with social buttons and one call to action on the right. Up to six link columns follow, each with an optional \"View all (N) →\" link. The bottom bar holds the Language Select, the kit theme toggle, a centre slot (the demo uses the accent swatches companion) and the copyright and legal links. Every link reports through `onLinkClick`, so a client router can take over.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nMarketing footer: logo, tagline and socials, six link columns with View all counts, then a language, theme, swatch and legal bar.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The root is an `@container` `<footer>` with a top border. Inside, a `max-w-[1152px]` column has three bands: the brand row, a `<nav aria-label=\"Footer\">` grid, and the bottom bar. Layout follows the footer's own width, not the viewport: the brand row splits left and right from `@3xl`, and the grid goes from 2 columns to 3 at `@xl` and 6 at `@5xl`.\n2. Socials branch on `count`. Without one, a social renders the kit `IconLinkButton` (36px square) named by `label`. With one, it renders a secondary `ButtonLink` with the icon and the count as visible text, named \"GitHub stars, 2.4k\". All socials open in a new tab with `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`.\n3. Each column is an uppercase caption `h2` over a `ul` of links. A `badge` renders a status-blue pill after the label. `viewAll` adds a medium-weight link with a tertiary \"(N)\" and an arrow that nudges 2px on hover and flips under RTL.\n4. The bottom bar is a one-column grid that becomes `grid-cols-[1fr_auto_1fr]` from `@3xl`. That keeps the centre slot centred whatever the widths of the language and theme controls on the left and the legal text on the right. `language` takes `LanguageSelect` props (or `false`) and `themeToggle` renders the kit `ThemeToggle` as a small segmented control.\n5. `AccentSwatches` (companion) is a `role=\"radiogroup\"` of `role=\"radio\"` buttons with a roving tab stop. Only the checked dot has `tabIndex=0`. Arrow keys (mirrored under RTL), Home and End move focus and select. Each dot is SVG artwork: a linear gradient through its stops, a white radial highlight at (35%, 30%) and a 12% black hairline.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `content width = max-w-[1152px], px-0 from a 1200px container`\n  This matches the landing page grid, so the footer columns line up with the sections above. Below 1200px, 16 or 24px gutters return.\n- `grid breakpoints = 2 cols, @xl (36rem) 3 cols, @5xl (64rem) 6 cols`\n  Six columns of about 170px need 64rem before \"Marketing Dashboard\" fits on one line. Three columns keep two rows readable on tablets.\n- `link rhythm = gap-3 between Body 1 links, mt-4 under the heading`\n  A roughly 33px row pitch makes a 9-link column about 300px tall, so all six columns end on the same baseline band.\n- `swatch = 24px dot, gap-1.5, ring-2 + ring-offset-1 when checked, hover scale 1.1 over 150ms`\n  The ring offset leaves a gap in the bar colour between the dot and the ring, so the checked state reads on every hue, including the pale spectrum dot.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SiteFooter(props: { logo?: ReactNode; brandLabel?: string; brandHref?: string; tagline?: ReactNode; socials?: FooterSocial[]; cta?: ReactNode; columns: FooterColumn[]; language?: false | LanguageSelectProps; themeToggle?: boolean; bottomCenter?: ReactNode; copyright?: ReactNode; legal?: FooterLink[]; onLinkClick?: (href: string, event: MouseEvent<HTMLAnchorElement>) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `columns: FooterColumn[]`\n  `{ title, links: { label, href, badge?, external? }[], viewAll?: { href, count?, label? } }` per column.\n- `logo: ReactNode`\n  Brand mark, wrapped in a link to `brandHref`.\n- `brandLabel: string` — default `\"Home\"`\n  Accessible name of the logo link.\n- `brandHref: string` — default `\"/\"`\n  Logo link target.\n- `tagline: ReactNode`\n  Paragraph under the logo, at most 430px wide.\n- `socials: FooterSocial[]` — default `[]`\n  `{ label, href, icon, count? }`. With `count` it renders a labelled button, otherwise an icon button.\n- `cta: ReactNode`\n  Action after the socials, e.g. a \"Get Pro\" `ButtonLink`.\n- `language: false | LanguageSelectProps` — default `{}`\n  Props for the bottom-bar Language Select, or false to hide it.\n- `themeToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the kit segmented ThemeToggle.\n- `bottomCenter: ReactNode`\n  Centre slot of the bottom bar, e.g. `<AccentSwatches />`.\n- `copyright: ReactNode`\n  Leading text of the legal line.\n- `legal: FooterLink[]` — default `[]`\n  Links after the copyright, separated by middle dots.\n- `onLinkClick: (href, event) => void`\n  Called for every footer link. `preventDefault()` there to route client-side.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SiteFooter({\n  logo,\n  brandLabel = \"Home\",\n  brandHref = \"/\",\n  tagline,\n  socials = [],\n  cta,\n  columns,\n  language = {},\n  themeToggle = true,\n  bottomCenter,\n  copyright,\n  legal = [],\n  onLinkClick,\n  className,\n}: SiteFooterProps) {\n  const click = (href: string) => (event: MouseEvent<HTMLAnchorElement>) => onLinkClick?.(href, event);\n\n  return (\n    <footer className={cx(styles.root, className)}>\n      <div className={styles.inner}>\n        <div className={styles.top}>\n          <div className=\"min-w-0\">\n            {logo ? (\n              <a\n                href={brandHref}\n                aria-label={brandLabel}\n                onClick={click(brandHref)}\n                className={cx(styles.link, \"inline-flex\")}\n              >\n                {logo}\n              </a>\n            ) : null}\n            {tagline ? <p className={styles.tagline}>{tagline}</p> : null}\n          </div>\n\n          {socials.length > 0 || cta ? (\n            <div className={styles.actions}>\n              {socials.map((social) =>\n                social.count !== undefined ? (\n                  <ButtonLink\n                    key={social.label}\n                    href={social.href}\n                    variant=\"secondary\"\n                    leadingIcon={social.icon}\n                    aria-label={countLabel(social)}\n                    onClick={click(social.href)}\n                    {...externalProps(true)}\n                  >\n                    {social.count}\n                  </ButtonLink>\n                ) : (\n                  <IconLinkButton\n                    key={social.label}\n                    href={social.href}\n                    icon={social.icon}\n                    aria-label={social.label}\n                    onClick={click(social.href)}\n                    {...externalProps(true)}\n                  />\n                ),\n              )}\n              {cta}\n            </div>\n          ) : null}\n        </div>\n\n        <nav aria-label=\"Footer\" className={styles.grid}>\n          {columns.map((column) => (\n            <div key={column.title} className=\"min-w-0\">\n              <h2 className={styles.heading}>{column.title}</h2>\n              <ul className={styles.list}>\n                {column.links.map((link) => (\n                  <li key={link.href}>\n                    <a\n                      href={link.href}\n                      onClick={click(link.href)}\n                      className={styles.link}\n                      {...externalProps(link.external)}\n                    >\n                      {link.label}\n                      {link.badge ? <span className={styles.badge}>{link.badge}</span> : null}\n                    </a>\n                  </li>\n                ))}\n                {column.viewAll ? (\n                  <li>\n                    <a\n                      href={column.viewAll.href}\n                      onClick={click(column.viewAll.href)}\n                      className={cx(styles.link, styles.viewAll)}\n                    >\n                      {column.viewAll.label ?? \"View all\"}\n                      {column.viewAll.count !== undefined ? (\n                        <span className=\"text-body-regular text-text-tertiary\">({column.viewAll.count})</span>\n                      ) : null}\n                      <RiArrowRightLine\n                        aria-hidden\n                        className=\"size-4 transition-transform duration-150 ease-out group-hover/view:translate-x-0.5 motion-reduce:transition-none motion-reduce:group-hover/view:translate-x-0 rtl:-scale-x-100\"\n                      />\n                    </a>\n                  </li>\n                ) : null}\n              </ul>\n            </div>\n          ))}\n        </nav>\n\n        <div className={styles.bottom}>\n          <div className={styles.bottomStart}>\n            {language !== false ? <LanguageSelect {...language} /> : null}\n            {themeToggle ? <ThemeToggle appearance=\"segmented\" size=\"small\" /> : null}\n          </div>\n          <div className={styles.bottomCenter}>{bottomCenter}</div>\n          {copyright || legal.length > 0 ? (\n            <p className={styles.legal}>\n              {copyright ? <span>{copyright}</span> : null}\n              {legal.map((link, i) => (\n                <span key={link.href} className=\"inline-flex items-center gap-2\">\n                  {copyright || i > 0 ? <span aria-hidden>·</span> : null}\n                  <a\n                    href={link.href}\n                    onClick={click(link.href)}\n                    className={styles.legalLink}\n                    {...externalProps(link.external)}\n                  >\n                    {link.label}\n                  </a>\n                </span>\n              ))}\n            </p>\n          ) : null}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </footer>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** There are no effects, timers or listeners. The swatch keyboard handler is a React prop on the radio group. The Language Select and ThemeToggle own and clean up their own listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** All motion is hover feedback: link colour fades, the View all arrow nudge and the swatch scale. They are CSS transitions with `motion-reduce:` variants that remove the transition and the transform. The theme toggle keeps the kit behaviour.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using viewport breakpoints. A footer placed in a narrower shell, such as a docs layout with a sidebar, would still get six columns and overflow. Container queries size it by its own width.\n- Repeating the same SVG gradient `id` in two marks or swatches. Browsers resolve `url(#id)` to the first match, so a later swatch can paint with another's gradient. Ids come from `useId()`.\n- Giving every swatch `tabIndex=0`. Tab would then stop seven times in the bottom bar. A radio group has one tab stop and uses the arrow keys inside.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `onLinkClick={(href, e) => { e.preventDefault(); router.push(href) }}` for client-side routing. External links (`external: true`) can be skipped in that handler.\n- The theme toggle is the kit `ThemeToggle`. It writes the document theme and persists it, so set `themeToggle={false}` when the host page already has one.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px the footer shows the logo and tagline on the left and X, Discord, a \"2.4k\" GitHub button and Get Pro on the right. Six columns follow, Components and Charts ending in \"View all (96) →\" and \"View all (18) →\", and Affiliate carries an \"Earn 40%\" pill. The bottom bar reads, left to right: the English select and the sun/moon toggle, seven centred colour dots, then \"© 2026 Tessera Labs · Privacy · Terms · License\". Clicking a link calls `onLinkClick` with its href. Tabbing reaches the swatches once, and ArrowRight checks the next dot. At 360px the columns become two and the bottom bar stacks.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "site-header": "# Build: Site Header\n\nThe landing page's top bar: a brand lockup, a row of nav links where any link with `items` becomes a menu button, and an action cluster of search pill, social icons, GitHub star count, a free slot and the kit ThemeToggle. It responds to its own width through a container query, so the same header collapses to a menu button that opens the kit Drawer whenever its container is under 896px, whatever the window size.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nMarketing header with a menu link, a ⌘K search pill, a GitHub star pill and the theme toggle, folding into a drawer in narrow containers.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The `<header>` is an `@container/site-header`. Every responsive switch is a container variant (`@4xl/site-header:` = 56rem = 896px): the desktop `<nav>`, the socials/GitHub/actions/theme cluster and the full search pill appear at that width, and the menu IconButton hides at it. Media queries are never used, so a header placed in a narrow column or a scaled preview still picks the right layout.\n2. Links with an `items` array render through `NavMenu`, which wraps the kit `Dropdown`. Open state lives in the header (`openMenu` holds the label of the one open menu), so opening a second menu closes the first. The trigger is a React Aria button (Enter, Space and Escape for free); its chevron rotates 180deg on open; the panel holds plain `<a>` rows, so destinations keep native link behaviour, and picking a row calls `onNavigate` then closes the panel.\n3. Current page: `isCurrentLink` marks a link current when its `href`, or any row of its menu, equals `currentHref`. Current anchors get `aria-current=\"page\"`, which the shared link class styles as primary text, and current menu rows take the dropdown hover fill.\n4. The search trigger is a `<button>` that calls `onSearch`. The shortcut chip is display only (it sets `aria-keyshortcuts=\"Meta+K\"` for the default label); the key binding belongs to whatever the search opens, e.g. the kit CommandPalette, whose `shortcut=\"k\"` already toggles it. Below 896px the pill shrinks to a 40px icon button.\n5. The narrow layout puts the same data in a `DrawerTrigger` + `Drawer placement=\"end\" size=\"sm\"`: plain links become full-width menu rows, each menu becomes a labelled group of its rows, and the GitHub pill, actions and a ThemeToggle sit under a divider. Every row calls `close()` from the Drawer render prop after `onNavigate`, so navigating dismisses the sheet.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `bar height = h-[72px]`\n  Matches the reference: a 40px control row (search pill, GitHub pill, theme toggle) with 16px of air above and below.\n- `collapse width = @4xl (896px container)`\n  Below it the four links plus a 220px search pill, two socials, the GitHub pill, Get Pro and the toggle no longer fit on one line at 1280px max width.\n- `search pill = w-[220px] h-10 rounded-full`\n  Wide enough for \"Quick Search\" plus the ⌘K chip without truncation; 40px matches the toggle and GitHub pill so the cluster shares one baseline.\n- `menu panel = w-[320px]`\n  Room for a 32px icon tile plus a one-line description; the default 266px menu width wraps the descriptions.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SiteHeader(props: { brand?: ReactNode; brandHref?: string; links?: SiteHeaderLink[]; currentHref?: string; onNavigate?: (href: string, event: MouseEvent<HTMLAnchorElement>) => void; onSearch?: () => void; searchLabel?: string; searchShortcut?: string; socials?: SiteHeaderSocial[]; githubHref?: string; githubStars?: number; actions?: ReactNode; showThemeToggle?: boolean; sticky?: boolean; menuLabel?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction SiteHeaderLogo(props: { name?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `links: SiteHeaderLink[]` — default `[]`\n  `{ label, href?, items? }`. A link with `items` (`{ label, href, description?, icon? }`) becomes a menu button.\n- `currentHref: string`\n  Marks the matching link or menu row with `aria-current=\"page\"`.\n- `onNavigate: (href, event) => void`\n  Called for every link and row; prevent the default for client routing.\n- `onSearch: () => void`\n  Search trigger press. Omit it to hide the trigger.\n- `searchLabel: string` — default `\"Quick Search\"`\n  Pill text and the trigger's accessible name.\n- `searchShortcut: string` — default `\"⌘K\"`\n  Shortcut chip in the pill. Display only.\n- `socials: SiteHeaderSocial[]` — default `[]`\n  Icon links (`{ label, href, icon }`) before the GitHub pill.\n- `githubHref / githubStars: string / number`\n  GitHub pill target and compact-formatted star count.\n- `actions: ReactNode`\n  Extra controls before the theme toggle; repeated in the drawer.\n- `showThemeToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Renders the kit ThemeToggle (segmented) on the end edge and in the drawer.\n- `sticky: boolean` — default `false`\n  Pins the bar to the top of its scroll container.\n- `brand / brandHref: ReactNode / string` — default `SiteHeaderLogo / \"/\"`\n  Lockup on the start edge and its link.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** A link counts as current when it, or any row of its menu, matches the page. */\nfunction isCurrentLink(link: SiteHeaderLink, currentHref: string | undefined) {\n  if (currentHref === undefined) return false;\n  return link.href === currentHref || (link.items ?? []).some((item) => item.href === currentHref);\n}\n\n/**\n * A nav link with rows: a real menu button (Enter, Space, Escape, outside\n * press all handled by the kit Dropdown) whose panel holds plain anchors, so\n * middle-click and \"open in new tab\" still work on every destination. The\n * chevron flips with the open state; picking a row closes the panel.\n */\nfunction NavMenu({\n  link,\n  items,\n  currentHref,\n  isOpen,\n  onOpenChange,\n  onNavigate,\n}: {\n  link: SiteHeaderLink;\n  items: SiteHeaderMenuItem[];\n  currentHref: string | undefined;\n  isOpen: boolean;\n  onOpenChange: (open: boolean) => void;\n  onNavigate: SiteHeaderProps[\"onNavigate\"];\n}) {\n  return (\n    <Dropdown isOpen={isOpen} onOpenChange={onOpenChange}>\n      <DropdownTrigger className={cx(linkClass, \"cursor-pointer\", isCurrentLink(link, currentHref) && \"text-text-primary\")}>\n        {link.label}\n        <RiArrowDownSLine\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            \"size-4 transition-transform duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            isOpen && \"rotate-180\",\n          )}\n        />\n      </DropdownTrigger>\n      <DropdownPopover aria-label={link.label} className=\"w-[320px]\">\n        {items.map((item) => (\n          <MenuRow\n            key={item.href}\n            item={item}\n            current={item.href === currentHref}\n            onClick={(event) => {\n              onNavigate?.(item.href, event);\n              onOpenChange(false);\n            }}\n          />\n        ))}\n      </DropdownPopover>\n    </Dropdown>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing of its own: no listeners, timers or effects. The Dropdown and Drawer clean up their overlays and outside-press listeners when they close or unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The menu chevron's rotation transition is removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none`; the kit Dropdown and Drawer apply their own reduced-motion fades. Colour hover transitions stay because they do not move anything.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `md:` / `lg:` media variants for the collapse. The header then follows the window, not its container, and shows the desktop row inside a narrow column (or the mobile row in a scaled preview).\n- Binding ⌘K inside the header as well as in the search it opens. Two listeners on the same key open and immediately close the palette; keep the chip display-only.\n- Rendering menu rows as buttons that set `location`. Rows are anchors on purpose, so middle-click, copy link and open-in-new-tab work.\n- Forgetting `event.preventDefault()` in `onNavigate` when routing on the client: the anchor then performs a full navigation as well.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The ThemeToggle is the kit's own: it switches the whole document's theme and persists the choice, so two headers on one page stay in sync.\n- `SiteHeaderLogo` is exported for reuse in footers; pass any node as `brand` to replace it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px the bar shows the logo, Components with a chevron, Templates, Docs, Blog, a Quick Search pill with ⌘K, two social icons, a GitHub pill reading 963, Get Pro and the theme toggle. Clicking Components opens a panel of three rows; choosing Charts closes it and marks Components current. Escape closes the panel. Clicking the search pill opens the command palette. In a 390px container only the logo, a search icon and a menu button show; the menu button opens an end drawer listing every link, and choosing one closes the drawer.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "skeleton": "# Build: Skeleton\n\nPlaceholders in the shape of the content on its way: rectangles, text lines and circles tinted from the text colour at 8%, so they show on any surface in both themes. A sheen sweeps each block in three layers (a crisp 2px line, a 72px glow and a 240px bloom). Given children, a Skeleton lays the real content out invisibly to take its exact size, then cross-fades the block away while the content sharpens in from a 4px blur.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nContent-shaped placeholders with a layered light sheen that size themselves to real content and blur it in once loaded.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Paint every block with `bui-skeleton block bg-text-primary/8` plus a radius per shape: `rounded-xl` (rect), `rounded-md` (text) or `rounded-full` (circle). The 8% text-colour tint is what keeps the block visible on light and dark cards alike.\n2. Draw the sheen in CSS on `.bui-skeleton::after` (in the kit theme sheet): three right-anchored `linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, <colour>)` layers sized 2px, 72px and 240px, so nothing soft runs ahead of the crisp leading line. `.bui-skeleton` sets `overflow: hidden; isolation: isolate` and leaves positioning to the component's `relative` / `absolute` classes.\n3. Animate the pseudo-element with `bui-skeleton-sheen`: `translate` from -100% to 100% over the first 60% of a 2.2s `cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)` cycle, then rest off-block until the loop restarts. `:dir(rtl)` flips it with `scale: -1 1` and `animation-direction: reverse`. Dark mode lowers the three layer alphas from 0.9 / 0.55 / 0.35 to 0.2 / 0.08 / 0.04.\n4. Without children, render one `<span aria-hidden>` block sized by the caller's `className` (`h-4 w-40`, `size-10`).\n5. With children, render a `relative block` wrapper holding two layers: the real content in a `<span>` that is `opacity-0 motion-safe:blur-[4px]`, `inert` and `aria-hidden` until `isLoaded`, and an `absolute inset-0` skeleton block on top. When `isLoaded` flips, the content goes to `opacity-100 blur-[0px]` and the block to `opacity-0`, both via `transition-[opacity,filter] duration-300 ease-out`.\n6. `SkeletonText` maps `lines` to `text`-shaped Skeletons in a `flex flex-col gap-2.5` column, each `lineClassName` tall (default `h-3.5`), with the last line at `w-3/5` when there is more than one line.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `block tint = bg-text-primary/8`\n  Derived from the text colour rather than a fixed grey, so the placeholder sits at the same contrast on white and dark cards and flips with the theme for free.\n- `sheen layers = 2px line, 72px glow, 240px bloom; light alpha 0.9 / 0.55 / 0.35, dark 0.2 / 0.08 / 0.04`\n  A sharp leading edge with a soft tail reads as light passing over the surface. On dark surfaces the same alphas would flash, so they drop sharply.\n- `sweep timing = 2.2s cycle, sweep occupies 0 to 60%, cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)`\n  The rest period between sweeps is what keeps a page full of skeletons calm instead of a constant shimmer.\n- `reveal = 300ms ease-out on opacity and filter; content enters from blur(4px)`\n  Long enough to read as a cross-fade, short enough that loaded data is not held back. The blur hides the layout snap of text replacing a flat block.\n- `SkeletonText = gap-2.5 (10px), h-3.5 lines, last line w-3/5`\n  A 14px bar with 10px gaps approximates Body 1 text at a 24px rhythm; the short last line is what makes a block read as a paragraph.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype SkeletonShape = \"rect\" | \"text\" | \"circle\"\ninterface SkeletonProps { shape?: SkeletonShape; isLoaded?: boolean; className?: string; style?: CSSProperties; children?: ReactNode }\nfunction Skeleton(props: SkeletonProps): JSX.Element\ninterface SkeletonTextProps { lines?: number; lineClassName?: string; className?: string }\nfunction SkeletonText(props: SkeletonTextProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `shape: \"rect\" | \"text\" | \"circle\"` — default `\"rect\"`\n  Corner radius: 12px, 6px or fully round.\n- `isLoaded: boolean` — default `false`\n  With children: fade the block out and blur the content in.\n- `className: string`\n  Size and placement, e.g. `h-4 w-40` or `size-10`.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Real content to size the placeholder from and reveal when loaded.\n- `lines (SkeletonText): number` — default `3`\n  Number of lines; the last runs 60% wide.\n- `lineClassName (SkeletonText): string` — default `\"h-3.5\"`\n  Line height, matched to the text it stands in for.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  block: \"bui-skeleton block bg-text-primary/8\",\n  shape: {\n    rect: \"rounded-xl\",\n    text: \"rounded-md\",\n    circle: \"rounded-full\",\n  },\n});\n\nconst FADE = \"transition-[opacity,filter] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\";\n\nexport interface SkeletonProps {\n  /** `rect` (radius 12, default), `text` (radius 6), or `circle`. */\n  shape?: SkeletonShape;\n  /** With children: show them once true, blurring them in. */\n  isLoaded?: boolean;\n  /** Size and placement, e.g. `h-4 w-40` or `size-10`. */\n  className?: string;\n  style?: CSSProperties;\n  /** Real content to measure the placeholder from and reveal when loaded. */\n  children?: ReactNode;\n}\n\nexport function Skeleton({ shape = \"rect\", isLoaded = false, className, style, children }: SkeletonProps) {\n  if (children === undefined) {\n    return <span aria-hidden className={cx(styles.block, \"relative\", styles.shape[shape], className)} style={style} />;\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <span className={cx(\"relative block\", className)} style={style}>\n      <span\n        aria-hidden={!isLoaded || undefined}\n        inert={!isLoaded}\n        className={cx(\"block\", FADE, isLoaded ? \"opacity-100 blur-[0px]\" : \"opacity-0 motion-safe:blur-[4px]\")}\n      >\n        {children}\n      </span>\n      <span\n        aria-hidden\n        className={cx(styles.block, styles.shape[shape], \"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0\", FADE, isLoaded && \"opacity-0\")}\n      />\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. All motion is CSS animation on a pseudo-element and CSS transitions; there are no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The sweep is removed (`::after { display: none }`) and each block instead breathes with `bui-skeleton-pulse`, a 2s ease-in-out opacity dip to 0.55. The reveal drops its transition and skips the blur (`motion-reduce:transition-none`, `motion-safe:blur-[4px]`), so loaded content simply appears.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not give the `.bui-skeleton` class its own `position` in unlayered CSS. It would beat Tailwind's layered `relative` / `absolute` utilities and break the overlay form.\n- Do not render the loaded content outside the Skeleton and swap it in. The children form measures the placeholder from the real content, which is what prevents layout shift.\n- Do not forget `aria-busy` on the region that is loading. Skeletons are `aria-hidden`, so without it assistive tech gets no loading signal at all.\n- Do not tint the block with a fixed grey such as `bg-neutral-200`. It vanishes on dark cards; the 8% text tint does not.\n- Do not leave hidden content focusable. The component sets `inert` on it until `isLoaded`; a custom version must too, or Tab lands on invisible buttons.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The sheen and its keyframes live in the theme sheet (`.bui-skeleton`), not in the component, so it needs the kit stylesheet to animate.\n- With children, size and placement classes go on the wrapper; the overlay block inherits the wrapper's box.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA card of skeletons (a 36px circle, two text bars, two text lines and a 64px rect) shows a soft sheen crossing each block left to right roughly every 2.2s, with a pause between sweeps, in both light and dark mode. Wrapping real content in `<Skeleton isLoaded={false}>` gives a block exactly the size of that content; switching to `isLoaded` cross-fades the block out over 300ms while the content un-blurs, with no layout shift. Under reduced motion the blocks pulse instead of sweeping.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "slab-page-transition": "# Build: Slab Page Transition\n\nThe outgoing page slides one screen height up and out while a solid slab rises from below to take its place. The route commits under the slab, the slab holds for a beat with the destination name on it, then the slab keeps travelling up and out while the new page follows it in from below. Nothing reverses direction at any point, which is the whole difference between a slab and a curtain: a curtain that drops and lifts reads as an interruption, a slab that passes through reads as travel.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn opaque slab rises through the frame, the route changes under it, and it keeps going — nothing ever reverses.\n\n- Category: transition\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. One paused GSAP timeline, played from zero on every navigation, covers the whole gesture. `to(content, { y: -H })` and `fromTo(slab, { y: +H }, { y: 0 })` both start at 0 and run for `duration`; a `.call()` at `duration` fires the caller's commit; `set(content, { y: +H })` on that same frame teleports the page below the frame; then `to(slab, { y: -H })` and `to(content, { y: 0 })` both start at `duration + hold`. Total length is `2 * duration + hold`.\n2. The midpoint commit and the content teleport happen while the slab is exactly covering the frame, so neither is ever visible. That is the only reason the page can jump from -H to +H without a flash: there is an opaque surface in front of it for the whole of that frame.\n3. Each half eases `power3.inOut` on its own rather than one tween covering the full 2H. An `inOut` across the whole travel is at its FASTEST in the middle — precisely where the slab has to look like it stopped. Two `inOut` halves meet at zero velocity, so the slab lands, holds, and leaves.\n4. Travel is measured in pixels per run — `window.innerHeight` when `position=\"fixed\"`, `root.getBoundingClientRect().height` when `\"absolute\"` — through GSAP function-based values. `yPercent: -100` would be 100% of the CONTENT element's own height, which on a long page is the whole document, so the page would fly thousands of pixels instead of one screen.\n5. `tl.invalidate().play(0)` starts each run. `invalidate()` re-evaluates every function-based value, so a resized container or a flipped direction is picked up without rebuilding the timeline; without it the slab travels whatever height the box had when the component mounted.\n6. Direction is a sign read from a ref inside those function values, and the ref is written in `run()` before `play(0)` rather than during render. `play(0)` renders the timeline synchronously, so a direction that only landed on the next React commit would be read one frame late and the two halves would disagree about which way is out.\n7. The slab's parked position — `transform: translateY(100%)` — is declared in an injected stylesheet, not set from an effect. Effects run after the first paint, so a slab parked from JS is one frame of full-frame colour on mount. GSAP's inline transform outranks the rule while a transition runs, and when `useGsap` reverts it strips that inline value, which re-parks the slab with no extra code.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SLAB_DURATION = 0.8`\n  Seconds for ONE half; the full gesture is twice this plus the hold. The source ran 1 s per half and 0.8 keeps the weight while taking 0.4 s off the round trip. Under ~0.5 s the slab stops reading as a physical object and becomes a colour flash; over ~1.1 s the hold in the middle turns into a wait and people click a second nav item.\n- `SLAB_HOLD = 0.12`\n  Seconds the slab sits still at full cover. Two jobs: it is the beat in which the label is legible, and it is the margin in which React commits the destination — the commit fires at the start of the hold, so a page whose first layout costs two or three frames still finishes laying out before the slab moves off it. At 0 a heavy destination visibly settles while it is sliding in.\n- `SLAB_EASE = 'power3.inOut'`\n  Applied to each half separately. `inOut` is the only family that starts and ends at zero velocity, which is what makes the midpoint a stop rather than a peak. `power3` specifically: `power2` does not hold still long enough at the ends for the hold to read as deliberate, and `power4` spends so long near stationary that the 0.8 s feels like 1.2 s.\n- `zIndex = 190`\n  Above a custom cursor (150) and a scroll progress bar (120), both of which must be hidden during a transition, and below a preloader (200), which has to be able to sit over a slab on first load.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SlabPageTransition(props: { controller: SlabNavigation; children: ReactNode; duration?: number; hold?: number; direction?: \"up\" | \"down\"; color?: string; labelClassName?: string; position?: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"; zIndex?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction useSlabNavigation(): { host: RefObject<SlabRunner | null>; navigate: (commit: () => void, options?: { label?: string; direction?: \"up\" | \"down\" }) => void }\n```\n\n- `controller: SlabNavigation`\n  From `useSlabNavigation()`. The component registers its runner on it.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The current page. Swapped by the `commit` you hand to `navigate`.\n- `duration: number` — default `0.8`\n  Seconds for ONE half. The full gesture is `2 * duration + hold`.\n- `hold: number` — default `0.12`\n  Seconds the slab holds at full cover, between the halves.\n- `direction: \"up\" | \"down\"` — default `\"up\"`\n  Default travel. Overridable per navigation via the options argument.\n- `color: string` — default `'#000'`\n  Any CSS colour. Must be opaque, or the swap shows through the slab.\n- `labelClassName: string`\n  Applied to the label span, so the caller owns its typography.\n- `position: \"fixed\" | \"absolute\"` — default `\"fixed\"`\n  `absolute` inside a bounded container; `fixed` for a real page transition.\n- `zIndex: number` — default `190`\n  Above the site and any custom cursor, below a preloader.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Seconds for one half. The source ran 1 s per half; 0.8 keeps the weight and\n * takes 0.4 s off the round trip. Under ~0.5 s the slab stops reading as a\n * physical object and becomes a colour flash; over ~1.1 s the hold in the\n * middle turns into a wait, and people click a second nav item.\n */\nexport const SLAB_DURATION = 0.8;\n\n/**\n * Seconds the slab sits still at full cover, between the two halves. Two jobs:\n * it is the beat in which the slab's label is legible, and it is the margin in\n * which React commits the destination. The commit fires at the start of the\n * hold, so a destination whose first layout costs two or three frames still\n * finishes laying out before the slab starts moving off it. At 0 a heavy page\n * visibly settles while it is sliding in.\n */\nexport const SLAB_HOLD = 0.12;\n\n/** Both halves. `inOut` on each is what makes the midpoint a stop, not a peak. */\nconst SLAB_EASE = 'power3.inOut';\n\nconst SLAB_CSS = `\n.kx-slab-panel {\n  /* An opaque full-frame surface must never eat a click, not even while it is\n     covering. Mid-transition the pointer is usually already aimed at the next\n     nav item. */\n  pointer-events: none;\n  z-index: var(--kx-slab-z, 190);\n  background: var(--kx-slab-color, #000);\n  display: flex;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n  overflow: hidden;\n}\n/* The parked position lives in CSS, not in an inline style and not in a\n   'gsap.set' inside an effect. Effects run after the first paint, so a slab\n   parked from JS is one frame of full-frame colour on mount. GSAP's inline\n   'transform' outranks these rules while a transition runs, and when\n   'useGsap' reverts it strips that inline value — which parks the slab again\n   with no extra code. */\n.kx-slab-park-up { transform: translateY(100%); }\n.kx-slab-park-down { transform: translateY(-100%); }\n`;\n\nexport interface SlabNavigateOptions {\n  /** Written into the slab, legible during the hold. */\n  label?: string;\n  /** Overrides the component's `direction` for this one navigation. */\n  direction?: 'up' | 'down';\n}\n\n/** What the component registers on the controller. Internal. */\nexport interface SlabRunner {\n  run: (commit: () => void, options?: SlabNavigateOptions) => void;\n}\n\nexport interface SlabNavigation {\n  /** Pass straight to `<SlabPageTransition controller>`. Do not read it. */\n  host: RefObject<SlabRunner | null>;\n  /**\n   * Raise the slab, run `commit` — your navigation — under it, then lower the\n   * page in behind it.\n   */\n  navigate: (commit: () => void, options?: SlabNavigateOptions) => void;\n}\n\n/**\n * The caller's half. `commit` is a callback rather than a router call, so\n * nothing here depends on a routing library.\n */\nexport function useSlabNavigation(): SlabNavigation {\n  const host = useRef<SlabRunner | null>(null);\n\n  const navigate = useCallback(\n    (commit: () => void, options?: SlabNavigateOptions) => {\n      const runner = host.current;\n      // No mounted component — the caller wired the hook up but rendered the\n      // transition conditionally, or this fired during the first commit.\n      // Navigate anyway. A transition that swallows navigations is worse than\n      // no transition.\n      if (!runner) {\n        commit();\n        return;\n      }\n      runner.run(commit, options);\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n\n  return useMemo(() => ({ host, navigate }), [navigate]);\n}\n\nexport interface SlabPageTransitionProps {\n  /** From `useSlabNavigation()`. */\n  controller: SlabNavigation;\n  /** The current page. Swapped by the `commit` you hand to `navigate`. */\n  children: ReactNode;\n  /** Seconds for ONE half. The full gesture is `2 * duration + hold`. */\n  duration?: number;\n  /** Seconds the slab holds at full cover between the halves. */\n  hold?: number;\n  /**\n   * Default travel. `up` sends everything upward; `down` mirrors it, which is\n   * what a back navigation wants. Overridable per navigation.\n   */\n  direction?: 'up' | 'down';\n  /** Any CSS colour. Must be opaque, or the swap shows through the slab. */\n  color?: string;\n  /** Applied to the label span so the caller owns its type. */\n  labelClassName?: string;\n  /** `absolute` to demo inside a bounded box; `fixed` for a real page. */\n  position?: 'fixed' | 'absolute';\n  /** Above the site, below a preloader. */\n  zIndex?: number;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function SlabPageTransition({\n  controller,\n  children,\n  duration = SLAB_DURATION,\n  hold = SLAB_HOLD,\n  direction = 'up',\n  color = '#000',\n  labelClassName,\n  position = 'fixed',\n  zIndex = 190,\n  className,\n}: SlabPageTransitionProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const content = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const slab = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const labelEl = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n\n  const timeline = useRef<ReturnType<typeof gsap.timeline> | null>(null);\n  const pending = useRef<(() => void) | null>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // Read inside the tweens' function-based values, so flipping direction\n  // between navigations needs no rebuild — and therefore no `revert()`, which\n  // would strip the transform GSAP is mid-way through writing. Set in `run`,\n  // never during render: `play(0)` renders the timeline synchronously, so a\n  // value that only lands on the next React commit would be read one frame\n  // too late and the two halves would disagree about which way is out.\n  const dir = useRef<'up' | 'down'>(direction);\n  const defaultDir = useRef(direction);\n  defaultDir.current = direction;\n\n  // Built once and held paused. `direction` is deliberately NOT a dependency:\n  // `useGsap` reverts on every dependency change, and revert removes the\n  // inline transform, so a rebuild during a transition drops the slab back to\n  // its CSS park mid-cover and the swap is visible.\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const rootEl = root.current;\n      const contentEl = content.current;\n      const slabEl = slab.current;\n      if (!rootEl || !contentEl || !slabEl) return;\n\n      // Travel in pixels, measured per run. `yPercent: -100` would be 100% of\n      // the CONTENT's own height — on a long page that is the whole document,\n      // so the page would fly thousands of pixels instead of one screen. The\n      // original wrote `y: '-100vh'` for exactly this reason; measuring the\n      // real box also gets the bounded case right, where one screen is the\n      // container and not the viewport.\n      const travel = () =>\n        position === 'fixed'\n          ? window.innerHeight\n          : rootEl.getBoundingClientRect().height;\n      const sign = () => (dir.current === 'down' ? -1 : 1);\n\n      const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });\n\n      tl.to(contentEl, { y: () => -travel() * sign(), duration, ease: SLAB_EASE }, 0)\n        .fromTo(\n          slabEl,\n          { y: () => travel() * sign() },\n          { y: 0, duration, ease: SLAB_EASE },\n          0,\n        )\n        // Midpoint. The slab is exactly covering the frame for the whole of\n        // this and the `set` below, so neither is ever seen.\n        .add(() => {\n          const commit = pending.current;\n          pending.current = null;\n          commit?.();\n        })\n        .set(contentEl, { y: () => travel() * sign() })\n        .to(\n          slabEl,\n          { y: () => -travel() * sign(), duration, ease: SLAB_EASE },\n          duration + hold,\n        )\n        .to(contentEl, { y: 0, duration, ease: SLAB_EASE }, duration + hold)\n        // Re-park the slab below the frame at the very end, so `progress(0)`\n        // and `progress(1)` describe the same rest state and a second run\n        // starts from a known place rather than from wherever it stopped.\n        .set(slabEl, { y: () => travel() * sign() });\n\n      timeline.current = tl;\n\n      return () => {\n        timeline.current = null;\n        // A dependency change reverts and kills this timeline. If it happened\n        // mid-transition the commit had not fired yet, so fire it here: the\n        // visitor clicked a link and must end up on that page whatever the\n        // animation did.\n        const commit = pending.current;\n        pending.current = null;\n        commit?.();\n      };\n    },\n    root,\n    [duration, hold, position],\n  );\n\n  const run = useCallback(\n    (commit: () => void, options?: SlabNavigateOptions) => {\n      const tl = timeline.current;\n      const span = labelEl.current;\n      if (span) span.textContent = options?.label ?? '';\n      dir.current = options?.direction ?? defaultDir.current;\n\n      // Reduced motion: no slab and a synchronous commit. Not a zero-duration\n      // slab — that is still one frame of full-frame colour, which is the\n      // single most unpleasant thing to show someone who asked for less\n      // motion. `pause(0)` suppresses callbacks, so the midpoint `.call()`\n      // cannot fire a second commit.\n      if (!tl || reduced) {\n        tl?.pause(0);\n        commit();\n        return;\n      }\n      // A second click while the slab is in flight would queue a second commit\n      // and land the visitor one page past where they aimed.\n      if (tl.isActive()) return;\n\n      pending.current = commit;\n      // `invalidate()` before replaying re-evaluates every function-based\n      // value: the box may have been resized, and `direction` may have\n      // flipped, since the timeline was built. Without it the slab travels the\n      // height the container had on mount.\n      tl.invalidate().play(0);\n    },\n    [reduced],\n  );\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const host = controller.host;\n    host.current = { run };\n    return () => {\n      if (host.current?.run === run) host.current = null;\n    };\n  }, [controller, run]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useGsap` reverts the `gsap.context`, which kills the timeline and strips every inline transform it wrote — restoring the slab to the parked position its stylesheet rule specifies and the page to `y: 0`. The cleanup also flushes any commit that had not fired yet, so a prop change mid-transition cannot lose a navigation. The runner registration is removed from the controller on unmount, and it is removed only if it is still the current one, so a remount cannot null out the live handle.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The timeline is never played. `navigate()` calls the commit synchronously and `tl.pause(0)` resets the slab to its rest position — deliberately not a zero-duration slab, which is still one frame of full-frame colour. `pause()` suppresses callbacks, so the midpoint `.call()` cannot fire a second commit. Navigation is a hard cut straight to the destination in both directions.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `yPercent: -100` on the page wrapper. Percentage transforms resolve against the element's OWN height, so on a 6000 px page the wrapper travels 6000 px in 0.8 s and the screen just goes blank a frame in. The travel has to be one screen — measured in pixels, or written as the original did with an explicit `vh` unit.\n- Reversing the slab instead of driving it through. `tl.reverse()` after the commit sends the slab back down the way it came, and the eye reads the whole thing as a mistake being undone rather than as a move. The second half is a separate forward tween to `-H`.\n- One `power3.inOut` across the full 2H travel. That ease is at maximum speed at its own midpoint, which is exactly the frame the route changes on, so the slab appears to accelerate straight through the cover and the label is unreadable.\n- Putting the direction flag in the `useGsap` dependency list. Every dependency change reverts the context, and `revert()` strips the inline transform GSAP is mid-way through writing — so flipping direction during a transition drops the slab back to its CSS park while it is meant to be covering, and the content swap is visible. Direction is read from a ref by function-based values instead.\n- Parking the slab with `gsap.set` in an effect. Effects run after the first paint, so the first frame of the page is a full-frame slab. The rest position belongs in a stylesheet; GSAP overrides it from the inline style the moment it is needed.\n- Setting the slab label through state. `setLabel()` inside the navigate handler re-renders the subtree React is about to hand to GSAP on the exact frame the timeline starts. The label is one `textContent` write on a ref, and the slab is off-frame when it happens.\n- Letting a second click start a second run. Without the `tl.isActive()` guard a double click queues two commits and the visitor lands one page past where they aimed.\n- Dropping the commit when the timeline is destroyed. If a prop change reverts the context mid-transition, the `.call()` never fires and the route never changes — the visitor clicked a link and stayed put. The `useGsap` cleanup flushes any pending commit.\n- Leaving the slab clickable. A full-frame opaque div swallows the pointer for the length of the transition, and mid-transition the pointer is usually already aimed at the next nav item. It carries `pointer-events: none` and `aria-hidden`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- In Next.js App Router: mount once in `app/layout.tsx` with `position=\"fixed\"`, then replace nav `<Link>` elements with buttons calling `navigate(() => router.push(href), { label })`. Nothing in this file imports `next/navigation` — the commit is a callback, so the same component works under React Router or a plain `useState` page switch.\n- The content wrapper stays in normal flow. A transform moves an element without taking it out of flow, so the page keeps its own height and the document still scrolls — which an `absolute inset-0` wrapper would break on any real page.\n- The colour must be opaque. A translucent slab shows the content teleport at the midpoint, which is the single artefact this technique exists to hide.\n- Browser back and forward change the route without going through `navigate()`, so they get no slab. That is correct: by the time your code hears about a history navigation it has already happened, and there is nothing left to cover.\n- Set `direction: \"down\"` per navigation for anything that reads as going back — an up-nav click, a breadcrumb, a close button — and the site acquires a sense of which way the visitor is travelling for the cost of one ternary.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClick a nav item: the page slides up out of frame while a coloured slab rises from below, the slab pauses at full cover with the destination name on it, then continues up and out while the new page follows it in from below. The slab never travels downward on a forward navigation, and there is no frame in which old and new content are both visible. Click a nav item to the left of the current one and the whole gesture mirrors — everything travels down. Click two items in quick succession and you land on the first one you clicked, not past it. Resize the preview and navigate again: the slab still travels exactly one frame height. With reduced motion the content swaps instantly with no coloured frame in either direction.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "sleep-score-card": "# Build: Sleep Score Card\n\nA grey dashboard card headed \"Sleep score\" with a rating word (\"Excellent\") and a week pill with arrows. Below sits a ring split into one coloured arc per metric (Duration, Bedtime, Interruptions), each owning a share of the lap equal to its points available and filled by its score, with the total in the middle. A white panel lists the metrics with their detail and score; hovering or focusing a row (or an arc) dims the other arcs and puts that metric's score in the centre, and stepping through weeks glides the arcs to the new values.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA segmented SVG ring that sums sleep sub-scores to a total out of 100, with a week switcher and metric rows that focus their arc.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Each metric owns a slot of the ring sized `max / sum(max) × 100` percent (50, 30 and 20 by default, so the total reads out of 100), laid clockwise from twelve o'clock in row order. Its arc fills `score / max` of that slot.\n2. Every arc is a circle with `pathLength={100}` rotated -90°, drawn with `stroke-dasharray: length 100` and `stroke-dashoffset: -start` over a full `stroke-chart-track` circle. Round caps reach half a stroke past each dash end, so the dash is trimmed by one cap on each side, `cap = (stroke / 2) / (2πr) × 100`, plus a 4% visible gap, and starts a half-gap plus a cap into its slot.\n3. The dash length, offset and opacity are inline CSS properties with a 700ms expo-out `transition-[stroke-dasharray,stroke-dashoffset,opacity]`. On mount every arc has length 0 and opacity 0; two animation frames later the real values apply, so the arcs grow in. Changing week only changes the numbers, so the same transition glides each arc to its new length.\n4. The week pill steps an index into `weeks` (controlled via `weekIndex` / `onWeekIndexChange`, or uncontrolled starting at the latest week); the arrows disable at either end. The rating word comes from `rate(total)` (Excellent ≥ 90, Good ≥ 75, Fair ≥ 60, else Poor) and is keyed by week so it fades in on change.\n5. Focus is `preview ?? pinned`: pointer enter on a row or an arc (hit-tested on the stroke) and keyboard focus on a row set the preview; clicking a row toggles the pinned metric. Other arcs drop to 25% and other rows to 50%, and the centre swaps the total for the focused score with \"of max\" underneath, in a polite live region.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SIZE / STROKE = 108px / 12px`\n  A chunky band on a small ring, as in the reference; the stroke scales with `size`.\n- `GAP = 4 (ring percent)`\n  About 15° of track shows between arcs after the caps are trimmed, enough to read the segments as separate scores.\n- `arc transition = 700ms, cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  Fast start and soft landing, so a week change reads instantly but the arcs settle gently.\n- `max split = 50 / 30 / 20`\n  Duration matters most, so it owns half the ring; the three maxima sum to 100 so the centre total is a percentage-style score.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SleepScoreCard(props?: SleepScoreCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype SleepMetric = { id: string; label: string; detail: string; score: number; max: number; color: string }\ntype SleepWeek = { id: string; label: string; metrics: SleepMetric[] }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Sleep score\"`\n  Header label.\n- `weeks: SleepWeek[]` — default `SLEEP_WEEKS`\n  Weeks the pill steps through, oldest first.\n- `weekIndex: number`\n  Controlled index into `weeks`.\n- `defaultWeekIndex: number` — default `weeks.length - 1`\n  Uncontrolled starting week.\n- `onWeekIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Called when an arrow changes week.\n- `rate: (score: number) => string` — default `sleepRating`\n  Turns the total into the headline word.\n- `size: number` — default `108`\n  Ring diameter in px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Every metric owns a slot of the lap sized by its `max`; its arc fills score/max of that slot.\n// Round caps poke out half a stroke past each dash end, so the dash is trimmed by a cap on both\n// sides plus the visible GAP. With pathLength 100 the cap length in ring percent is\n// (stroke / 2) / circumference * 100.\nconst cap = ((stroke / 2) / (2 * Math.PI * r)) * 100;\nlet cursor = 0;\nconst segments = metrics.map((m) => {\n  const slot = totalMax > 0 ? (m.max / totalMax) * 100 : 0;\n  const fill = m.max > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, m.score / m.max)) : 0;\n  const start = cursor + GAP / 2 + cap;\n  const length = Math.max(0, slot * fill - GAP - 2 * cap);\n  cursor += slot;\n  const shown = drawn || reduced;\n  return { m, start, length: shown ? length : 0, visible: shown && length > 0 };\n});\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The mount effect cancels both pending animation frames on unmount. Everything else is CSS transitions and React event props.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `useReducedMotion` renders the arcs at their final lengths from the first frame and `motion-reduce:transition-none` removes the draw and week-change transitions, so arcs jump to new values. The rating and centre fades and the row dimming are also instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Forgetting the round caps. A dash of exactly the slot length overlaps its neighbour by a full stroke width; trim one cap from each end before subtracting the visible gap.\n- Setting the dash values as SVG attributes and expecting a CSS transition. Put `strokeDasharray` and `strokeDashoffset` in `style` so the browser interpolates them.\n- Starting from length 0 with round caps leaves a dot at each arc's start; the arcs also start at opacity 0 and fade in with the draw.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Exports `SLEEP_WEEKS` (six sample weeks, oldest first), `sleepRating` and the `SleepMetric` / `SleepWeek` types. A metric is `{ id, label, detail, score, max, color }`; any number of metrics works and the slots follow their maxima.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe default card reads \"Sleep score\", \"Excellent\", \"29 Jun - 5 Jul\" and 98 in the centre, with purple, pink and blue arcs that grow in clockwise from the top over a grey track, and rows \"Duration: 7h 50m 49/50\", \"Bedtime: 20m earlier 29/30\" and \"Interruptions: 5m wake up 20/20\". The next arrow is disabled. Hovering the Bedtime row dims the other arcs and rows and shows 29 \"of 30\" in the centre. Clicking the previous arrow shows \"22 Jun - 28 Jun\", \"Good\" and 83, and the arcs shorten smoothly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "slider": "# Build: Slider\n\nOne shared `SliderBase` drawn over React Aria's `Slider`, exported as `Slider` (one thumb) and `RangeSlider` (two). React Aria owns the value model, pointer dragging, keyboard steps, hidden range inputs, RTL and thumb collision. The component owns the look: a 6px rail, an accent gradient fill between the thumbs, 20px thumbs that grow while dragged, and an exact-value bubble with a pointer that lifts while its thumb is active.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSingle and min/max sliders on React Aria: a gradient fill, thumbs that grow while dragged, and a value bubble above each thumb.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `AriaSlider` gets the consumer's props plus a forced `orientation=\"horizontal\"`. Its render prop exposes `state`, and `isRange` is simply `state.values.length > 1`.\n2. The fill is a positioned span. Its `insetInlineStart` is `state.getThumbPercent(0)` for a range or 0 for a single slider, and its `insetInlineEnd` is `1 - getThumbPercent(last)`. Logical insets make the fill grow from the right in RTL with no extra code.\n3. Each value in `state.values` renders an `AriaSliderThumb` with `index`. Its `aria-label` comes from `thumbLabels[index]` (default \"Value\", or \"Minimum\" and \"Maximum\"), so each thumb is named for what it changes and not by its position.\n4. Thumb states come from React Aria's render props. Hover gives `scale-105` and a darker border. Dragging gives `scale-110`, an accent border, an accent-tinted shadow and `z-20`, so the active thumb sits above the other where they overlap. All of it eases over 150ms via `transition-[scale,box-shadow,border-color]`.\n5. With `showTooltip` (the default), each thumb holds a bubble at `bottom-[calc(100%+9px)]` that shows `formatValue(value, index)`, or `state.getThumbValueLabel(index)` when there is no formatter. A rotated 8px square draws its pointer. While the thumb is dragged or focused the bubble rises 2px (`-translate-y-0.5`) and picks up `shadow-dropdown`. The track adds `mt-8` so the bubble has room above it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `track = h-8 hit area, h-1.5 rail`\n  The visible rail is 6px, but the track element is 32px tall, so a click slightly above or below the rail still moves the thumb.\n- `thumb = size-5 (20px) with an 8px accent dot; scale 1.05 hover, 1.1 drag`\n  Twenty pixels is the Figma size and a comfortable pointer target. The grow-on-drag is small enough to keep the thumb centred on the value and still visible under a finger.\n- `bubble offset = bottom-[calc(100%+9px)], h-7, lift -2px`\n  Nine pixels clears the thumb's scaled size plus the pointer. The 2px lift shows which bubble belongs to the thumb you are holding.\n- `transition = 150ms ease`\n  Short enough that the thumb still feels attached to the pointer while scale and shadow change underneath it.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Slider(props: Omit<AriaSliderProps<number>, \"children\" | \"className\" | \"orientation\"> & { label?: ReactNode; showTooltip?: boolean; formatValue?: (value: number, index: number) => ReactNode; className?: string; thumbLabel?: string }): JSX.Element; function RangeSlider(props: Omit<AriaSliderProps<number[]>, \"children\" | \"className\" | \"orientation\"> & { label?: ReactNode; showTooltip?: boolean; formatValue?: (value: number, index: number) => ReactNode; className?: string; thumbLabels?: [string, string] }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Visible label above the track, wired to every thumb.\n- `value / defaultValue / onChange: number | number[]`\n  React Aria value props. `Slider` takes a number, `RangeSlider` a two-item array.\n- `onChangeEnd: (value) => void`\n  Fires once when a drag or key press ends. Use it for saving.\n- `minValue / maxValue / step: number` — default `0 / 100 / 1`\n  Range and step size.\n- `showTooltip: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the value bubble above each thumb.\n- `formatValue: (value: number, index: number) => ReactNode`\n  Visual text for the bubble only.\n- `thumbLabel: string` — default `\"Value\"`\n  `Slider` only: accessible name for the thumb.\n- `thumbLabels: [string, string]` — default `[\"Minimum\", \"Maximum\"]`\n  `RangeSlider` only: accessible names for the two thumbs.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Dims to 50% and shows a not-allowed cursor.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the root column.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction SliderBase<T extends SliderValue>({\n  className,\n  label,\n  showTooltip = true,\n  formatValue,\n  thumbLabels,\n  ...props\n}: SliderBaseInternalProps<T>) {\n  return (\n    <AriaSlider\n      {...props}\n      orientation=\"horizontal\"\n      className={({ isDisabled }) =>\n        cx(\n          \"group flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-2\",\n          isDisabled && \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\",\n          className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {({ state }) => {\n        const isRange = state.values.length > 1;\n        const startPercent = isRange ? state.getThumbPercent(0) : 0;\n        const endPercent = state.getThumbPercent(state.values.length - 1);\n\n        return (\n          <>\n            {label != null && (\n              <AriaLabel className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary\">\n                {label}\n              </AriaLabel>\n            )}\n\n            <AriaSliderTrack\n              className={cx(\n                \"relative h-8 w-full touch-none\",\n                showTooltip && \"mt-8\",\n              )}\n            >\n              {({ isHovered }) => (\n                <>\n                  <span\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className={cx(\n                      \"absolute top-1/2 end-0 start-0 h-1.5 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full\",\n                      \"bg-background-tertiary-default shadow-[inset_0_1px_1px_rgb(0_0_0/0.06)]\",\n                      \"transition-colors duration-150 ease\",\n                      isHovered && \"bg-background-tertiary-hover\",\n                    )}\n                  />\n                  <span\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className={cx(\n                      \"absolute top-1/2 h-1.5 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full\",\n                      \"bg-linear-to-r from-accent-500 to-accent-600\",\n                      \"shadow-[inset_0_1px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.24),0_1px_2px_rgb(4_80_226/0.16)]\",\n                    )}\n                    style={{\n                      insetInlineStart: `${startPercent * 100}%`,\n                      insetInlineEnd: `${(1 - endPercent) * 100}%`,\n                    }}\n                  />\n\n                  {state.values.map((value, index) => {\n                    const valueLabel =\n                      formatValue?.(value, index) ??\n                      state.getThumbValueLabel(index);\n\n                    return (\n                      <AriaSliderThumb\n                        key={index}\n                        index={index}\n                        aria-label={\n                          thumbLabels[index] ??\n                          (isRange ? `Value ${index + 1}` : \"Value\")\n                        }\n                        className={({\n                          isDragging,\n                          isHovered: isThumbHovered,\n                          isFocusVisible,\n                          isDisabled,\n                        }) =>\n                          cx(\n                            \"top-1/2 size-5 rounded-full border border-border-button-default outline-none\",\n                            \"flex items-center justify-center bg-control-indicator-background\",\n                            \"shadow-[0_2px_4px_rgb(0_0_0/0.10),inset_0_1px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.8)]\",\n                            \"transition-[scale,box-shadow,border-color] duration-150 ease\",\n                            isThumbHovered && !isDragging && \"scale-105 border-border-button-hover\",\n                            isDragging && \"z-20 scale-110 border-accent-500 shadow-[0_3px_8px_color-mix(in_srgb,var(--color-accent-600)_22%,transparent)]\",\n                            isFocusVisible && \"ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-2\",\n                            isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed\" : \"cursor-grab active:cursor-grabbing\",\n                          )\n                        }\n                      >\n                        {({ isDragging, isFocused }) => (\n                          <>\n                            {showTooltip && (\n                              <span\n                                aria-hidden\n                                className={cx(\n                                  \"pointer-events-none absolute bottom-[calc(100%+9px)] left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2\",\n                                  \"flex h-7 min-w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-border-button-default\",\n                                  \"bg-background-primary-default px-2 text-caption-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary shadow-xs\",\n                                  \"transition-[translate,box-shadow] duration-150 ease\",\n                                  (isDragging || isFocused) && \"-translate-y-0.5 shadow-dropdown\",\n                                )}\n                              >\n                                {valueLabel}\n                                <span\n                                  aria-hidden\n                                  className=\"absolute -bottom-[4.5px] left-1/2 size-2 -translate-x-1/2 rotate-45 border-r border-b border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default\"\n                                />\n                              </span>\n                            )}\n                            <span\n                              aria-hidden\n                              className={cx(\n                                \"size-2 rounded-full bg-linear-to-b from-accent-500 to-accent-600\",\n                                \"shadow-[inset_0_1px_0_rgb(255_255_255/0.24)] transition-[filter] duration-150 ease\",\n                                (isDragging || isFocused) && \"brightness-110\",\n                              )}\n                            />\n                          </>\n                        )}\n                      </AriaSliderThumb>\n                    );\n                  })}\n                </>\n              )}\n            </AriaSliderTrack>\n          </>\n        );\n      }}\n    </AriaSlider>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No effects or listeners of its own. React Aria attaches its pointer move and up listeners on drag start and removes them when the drag ends or the slider unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The thumb follows the pointer directly, with no tween, so dragging is the same under any setting. The hover and drag scale, shadow and bubble lift are 150ms CSS transitions of a few pixels. They are kept under `prefers-reduced-motion` because they report state, and `motion-reduce:transition-none` on the root removes them.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving out both `label` and `aria-label`. React Aria logs a warning and the group is unnamed. `thumbLabel` names only the thumb, not the slider.\n- Setting `showTooltip={false}` and expecting the space above to stay. The track drops its `mt-8`, so any layout that depended on that height moves up.\n- Returning a long string from `formatValue`. The bubble is `whitespace-nowrap`, so wide text spreads sideways and can collide with the other thumb's bubble when the values are close.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `RangeSlider` keeps the thumbs from crossing because React Aria clamps each one to its neighbour. `minValue`, `maxValue`, `step` and `formatOptions` pass straight through.\n- The value bubble is `aria-hidden`. Screen readers hear React Aria's value text from the hidden range input, so use `formatOptions` when the spoken value should be formatted too (currency, percent).\n\n## Definition of done\n\nDragging the volume thumb moves the bubble and fill with it, the thumb grows slightly and its border turns accent, and the summary line updates on every frame. The range thumbs cannot cross, the fill always runs between them, and the dragged thumb sits above the other where they overlap. Tab focuses a thumb and lifts its bubble, and the arrow keys step by 100 on the budget. In RTL the fill grows from the right. No accessibility warning appears in the console.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "slow-float-idle": "# Build: Slow Idle Float\n\nThe ambient motion you put on a hero image, a floating card or a badge so the page does not feel frozen. Fourteen pixels over five seconds is under the threshold at which the eye tracks it as movement — you read it as presence. Because it animates `translate` rather than `transform`, the element can still carry a hover scale or a tilt without the two fighting.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA 14px rise and fall over 5 seconds, on the independent translate property so hover transforms still work.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A two-pose keyframe: `0%, 100% { translate: 0 0 }` and `50% { translate: var(--kx-float-x) var(--kx-float-y) }`. Both endpoints of the round trip are the resting position, so the loop has no seam and the element always returns to exactly its layout position.\n2. The animated property is `translate`, not `transform`. They are separate properties that compose in the order translate, rotate, scale, transform — so a consumer can put `hover:scale-105` (a `transform`) on the same element and both apply. An animation on `transform` would instead override any transition on `transform` for its entire duration, and the hover would appear dead.\n3. Direction comes from custom properties rather than separate keyframe blocks: `axis=\"y\"` writes `--kx-float-y: -14px` and leaves X at 0, `axis=\"x\"` does the reverse. One keyframe block covers both axes and any diagonal a consumer wants to set by hand.\n4. `phase` is applied as a negative `animation-delay` — `-durationMs * phase`. A negative delay means the animation is treated as having already been running for that long, so an element starts mid-flight on its first painted frame. A positive delay would make it sit dead still until its turn came round, which is exactly what makes staggered idle animations look broken on load.\n5. `ease-in-out` is what makes it buoyant. The element decelerates into both extremes and hangs there; `linear` gives constant velocity and reads as a mechanical lift.\n6. `will-change: translate` names the property that is actually animating. `will-change: transform` — the reflex — hints at a property this component never touches.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `distance = 14px`\n  Peak travel. Average speed works out at roughly 11px per second, which is below the rate at which the eye starts tracking an object instead of just registering it. Under about 6px the motion is invisible on a 1x display; over about 24px it reads as a balloon on a string and pulls focus from the content.\n- `durationMs = 5000`\n  The round trip. Faster than about 3s and it becomes a pulse that competes with real feedback in the UI; slower than about 8s and the element appears to have simply drifted out of position rather than to be floating.\n- `timing function = ease-in-out`\n  Buoyancy comes from the hold at the two extremes. `linear` moves at constant velocity, which reads as a machine; `ease-out` alone makes the return leg abrupt and the loop develops a visible tick at 100%.\n- `phase sign = negative`\n  Negative delay starts the animation partway through its first cycle instead of postponing it. With a positive delay a staggered group is motionless for the first phase*duration milliseconds after mount — on a five-second cycle with four items that is several seconds of a page that looks frozen.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SlowFloatIdle(props: { children: ReactNode; distance?: number; durationMs?: number; axis?: 'x' | 'y'; phase?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  What floats. Wrapped in a block-level element that owns the `translate`.\n- `distance: number` — default `14`\n  Peak travel in pixels, reached at the midpoint of the cycle.\n- `durationMs: number` — default `5000`\n  Full round trip in milliseconds.\n- `axis: 'x' | 'y'` — default `'y'`\n  Direction of travel. `y` rises then falls; `x` drifts right then back.\n- `phase: number` — default `0`\n  Start position in the cycle, 0-1. Applied as a negative `animation-delay`.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`. Safe to use `transform`-based utilities here — the float does not use `transform`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst FLOAT_CSS = `\n@keyframes kx-float-idle {\n  0%, 100% { translate: 0 0; }\n  50%      { translate: var(--kx-float-x, 0px) var(--kx-float-y, -14px); }\n}\n\n.kx-float {\n  display: block;\n  animation: kx-float-idle var(--kx-float-duration, 5s) ease-in-out infinite;\n  animation-delay: var(--kx-float-delay, 0s);\n  /* Names the property that is actually animating. 'will-change: transform'\n     is the reflex here and it hints at the wrong property entirely. */\n  will-change: translate;\n}\n\n/* No motion: the element rests at its natural layout position, not at the\n   midpoint of the float. The hint is released too — a promoted layer for an\n   animation that is not running is pure GPU memory. */\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kx-float {\n    animation: none;\n    will-change: auto;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport function SlowFloatIdle({\n  children,\n  distance = 14,\n  durationMs = 5000,\n  axis = 'y',\n  phase = 0,\n  className,\n}: SlowFloatIdleProps) {\n  const style: StyleWithVars = {\n    '--kx-float-duration': `${durationMs}ms`,\n    // Negative: the animation is treated as having already run for this long,\n    // so a set of floats is out of phase from the very first frame.\n    '--kx-float-delay': `${-durationMs * phase}ms`,\n    // Rise, not fall: negative Y is up. X drifts right on the positive side.\n    '--kx-float-x': axis === 'x' ? `${distance}px` : '0px',\n    '--kx-float-y': axis === 'y' ? `${-distance}px` : '0px',\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-slow-float-idle\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {FLOAT_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <div className={cn('kx-float', className)} style={style}>\n        {children}\n      </div>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. No listeners, no timers, no animation frames. Unmounting removes the element and its CSS animation; React removes the hoisted `<style>` when the last instance unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block sets `animation: none` and `will-change: auto`. The element rests at its natural layout position — not at the top of the float — so nothing shifts and no gap appears where the animation used to be.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Animating `transform` instead of `translate`. Animations beat transitions on the same property for as long as they run, so a `transition: transform` hover on the same element never has a visible effect — the hover looks broken and the cause is a keyframe elsewhere in the file.\n- Animating `top` or `margin-top` for the float. Those are layout properties: the ancestor reflows on every frame and any sibling laid out after it jitters. Only `translate`, `transform`, `opacity` and `filter` stay off the layout path.\n- Staggering a group with positive `animation-delay`. Every element holds at its 0% pose until its delay expires, so the group starts as a static row and comes alive one item at a time — most visible right after a page load, which is the worst possible moment.\n- Setting `will-change: transform` for an animation on `translate`. The hint names a property that never changes; the browser may still promote the layer for other reasons, so the mistake produces no error and no benefit.\n- Leaving `will-change` on when reduced motion is active. The element keeps a dedicated compositor layer for an animation that has been switched off — cost with no motion in exchange.\n- Parking the element at the midpoint of the float for reduced motion. The float exists to add life, not to reposition anything; freezing it 14px above its layout position leaves a permanent, unexplained gap under the element.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The independent `translate`, `rotate` and `scale` properties need Chrome 104+, Safari 14.1+ and Firefox 72+. In an older engine the declaration is dropped and the element simply does not float — the layout is unaffected.\n- The wrapper is a `div` with `display: block`. To float an inline element, pass `className=\"inline-block\"`; a floated `display: inline` box would have nothing to translate.\n- For a group, give each instance a different `phase` — 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 for four items. Equal phases make the group move as one rigid object, which is more noticeable than no motion at all.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe element rises about 14px, hangs, and settles back over five seconds with no visible tick at the loop point. Add a `hover:scale-110` to the same element and the scale still works while the float continues. Give three instances phases 0, 0.33 and 0.66 and all three are already at different heights on the first painted frame. With reduced motion on, the element sits exactly where the layout puts it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "social-button": "# Build: Social Button\n\nA \"Continue with …\" button where the brand is named rather than passed as artwork: `brand=\"github\"` pulls the glyph, the brand colour and the label from one table, so a stack of providers cannot drift out of sync. Three appearances share one shape: `colorful` fills with a gradient derived from the brand colour, `black` is solid high-contrast, and `white` is the outlined secondary surface that shows each provider's real multi-colour mark. Passing `href` renders an anchor, because starting OAuth is a navigation.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSign-in button for 24 named providers: one brand prop supplies logo, colour and label, in colourful, black or outlined styles.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `SOCIAL_PROVIDERS` maps 24 providers to `{ label, brand, viewBox, path }`. The glyphs are monochrome single paths painted with `currentColor` (Simple Icons, CC0, on a 24 grid; Bootstrap Icons, MIT, on a 16 grid for LinkedIn, Slack, Microsoft and Amazon). Each keeps its own `viewBox`, and the button scales whichever grid it gets into the same 18 or 16px box.\n2. For `appearance=\"white\"` the button looks up `SOCIAL_COLOR_LOGOS` instead: real multi-path marks with their own fills (gilbarbara/logos, CC0), injected with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` from a build-time constant. Amazon has no plain mark upstream, so it falls back to the monochrome glyph tinted with its brand colour. Apple, GitHub and X are drawn near-black, so in dark mode `dark:[&_path]:fill-white` repaints them white.\n3. `colorful` builds its gradient inline per brand: `linear-gradient(180deg, fill, oklch(from fill calc(l − 0.04) calc(c + 0.01) h))`. The second stop is the brand colour 4% darker and slightly more saturated, which is the same step the kit's primary button takes from blue-500 to blue-600. Hover and press adjust `brightness` (1.06 and 0.95) instead of swapping gradients.\n4. A brand whose colour is a CSS variable (Google is `var(--color-accent-500)`) skips the inline gradient and takes `bg-button-primary`, so it is exactly the kit's primary button, including its hover gradient layer, rather than an approximation of it.\n5. Sizes copy `Button` to the pixel (36px with a 10px radius, 32px with 8px) and add a fixed width (300 or 250px) so a column of providers lines up without a wrapper; `fullWidth` swaps that for `w-full`. With `iconOnly` the label is hidden and the element receives `aria-label=\"Continue with <Label>\"` so it is never announced unlabelled.\n6. `brand=\"custom\"` takes `config: { icon, label, color }` for anything not built in. The label defaults to \"SSO\" and the colour to black.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `gradient end stop = oklch(from fill calc(l - 0.04) calc(c + 0.01) h)`\n  Darkening in OKLCH keeps the hue stable. Mixing toward black in sRGB would wash out saturated brands such as Spotify green or Twitch purple and turn them muddy at the bottom edge.\n- `hover and press brightness = 1.06 / 0.95`\n  A per-brand hover gradient would need 24 more colours. A brightness filter gives every brand a consistent, subtle lift and dip from the one colour it already has.\n- `fixed widths = 300px (medium) / 250px (small)`\n  \"Continue with Microsoft\" is the longest built-in label and fits at both sizes. A fixed width lets a stack of providers align without each caller adding a wrapper.\n- `glyph sizes = 18px / 16px`\n  One step below Button's 20 / 18px icons. A filled brand mark is denser than a line icon and reads heavier at the same size.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SocialButton(props: SocialButtonProps | SocialButtonLinkProps): JSX.Element\n// shared: { brand: SocialProvider | \"custom\"; config?: { icon: ReactNode; label?: string; color?: string }; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; appearance?: \"colorful\" | \"black\" | \"white\"; iconOnly?: boolean; fullWidth?: boolean; children?: ReactNode; className?: string }\n// SocialButtonProps: Omit<ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>, \"children\" | \"color\"> & { href?: never; ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement> }\n// SocialButtonLinkProps: Omit<AnchorHTMLAttributes<HTMLAnchorElement>, \"children\" | \"color\"> & { href: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLAnchorElement> }\nconst SOCIAL_PROVIDERS: Record<SocialProvider, { label: string; brand: string; viewBox: string; path: string }>\n```\n\n- `brand: SocialProvider | \"custom\"`\n  Required. One of 24 providers (google, apple, github, gitlab, microsoft, x, facebook, linkedin, discord, slack, figma, notion, dropbox, spotify, twitch, reddit, tiktok, instagram, telegram, whatsapp, amazon, bitbucket, auth0, okta) or `custom`.\n- `appearance: \"colorful\" | \"black\" | \"white\"` — default `\"colorful\"`\n  Brand-colour gradient with a white glyph, solid black, or the outlined surface with the full-colour mark.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px tall and 300px wide, or 32px and 250px.\n- `iconOnly: boolean` — default `false`\n  Square logo-only button. The label moves to `aria-label`.\n- `fullWidth: boolean` — default `false`\n  Fill the container instead of the fixed width.\n- `config: { icon: ReactNode; label?: string; color?: string }`\n  Required for `brand=\"custom\"`, ignored otherwise.\n- `href: string`\n  Renders an `<a>` instead of a `<button>`, for OAuth redirects.\n- `children: ReactNode` — default `\"Continue with <Label>\"`\n  Overrides the visible label.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SocialButton({ href, ...props }: SocialButtonProps | SocialButtonLinkProps) {\n  const {\n    brand,\n    config,\n    size = \"medium\",\n    appearance = \"colorful\",\n    iconOnly = false,\n    fullWidth = false,\n    children,\n    className,\n    ref,\n    ...rest\n  } = props as SocialButtonBaseProps & {\n    ref?: Ref<HTMLButtonElement & HTMLAnchorElement>;\n  } & Record<string, unknown>;\n\n  const custom = brand === \"custom\";\n  const meta = custom ? undefined : SOCIAL_PROVIDERS[brand as SocialProvider];\n  const label = custom ? (config?.label ?? \"SSO\") : meta!.label;\n  const fill = custom ? (config?.color ?? \"#000000\") : meta!.brand;\n\n  // The outlined treatment is where the real multi-colour mark belongs: on a\n  // brand or black fill it would fight the background. Not every provider has\n  // one upstream (see social-color-logos.ts), so a missing mark falls back to\n  // the monochrome glyph tinted with the brand colour.\n  const colorLogo =\n    appearance === \"white\" && !custom\n      ? SOCIAL_COLOR_LOGOS[brand as SocialProvider]\n      : undefined;\n\n  const glyph = custom ? (\n    <span className={cx(styles.glyph[size], \"inline-flex items-center justify-center\")}>\n      {config?.icon}\n    </span>\n  ) : colorLogo ? (\n    <svg\n      viewBox={colorLogo.viewBox}\n      aria-hidden\n      className={cx(\n        styles.glyph[size],\n        DARK_INVERTED_BRANDS.has(brand) && \"dark:[&_path]:fill-white\",\n      )}\n      // The mark carries its own fills, so it goes in as markup. This is a\n      // build-time constant from this repo, never caller- or user-supplied.\n      dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: colorLogo.body }}\n    />\n  ) : (\n    <svg\n      viewBox={meta!.viewBox}\n      aria-hidden\n      className={styles.glyph[size]}\n      // On the filled appearances the glyph rides the white label; outlined\n      // falls back to the brand colour so it is not a grey smudge.\n      style={appearance === \"white\" ? { color: meta!.brand } : undefined}\n    >\n      <path d={meta!.path} fill=\"currentColor\" />\n    </svg>\n  );\n\n  const content = (\n    <>\n      {glyph}\n      {iconOnly ? null : <span className=\"truncate\">{children ?? `Continue with ${label}`}</span>}\n    </>\n  );\n\n  // A brand whose colour is a system token (Google) hands the fill straight\n  // to `bg-button-primary`, so it is the primary button, gradient, hover and\n  // active included, rather than an approximation of it. Every other brand\n  // gets that gradient's shape derived from its own colour: down in\n  // lightness and up slightly in chroma, which is what blue-500 → blue-600\n  // does. Mixing toward black instead would wash the second stop out.\n  const systemFill = appearance === \"colorful\" && fill.startsWith(\"var(\");\n\n  const classes = cx(\n    styles.base,\n    iconOnly ? styles.iconOnlySize[size] : styles.size[size],\n    styles.appearance[appearance],\n    // `bg-button-primary` carries its own hover and active gradients; the\n    // derived brands need the brightness shift instead.\n    systemFill\n      ? \"bg-button-primary\"\n      : appearance === \"colorful\" && \"hover:brightness-[1.06] active:brightness-95\",\n    fullWidth && !iconOnly && \"w-full\",\n    className,\n  );\n\n  // The visible label already names the brand; when it is hidden the element\n  // needs that name back, or the control announces as unlabelled.\n  const accessibleName = iconOnly ? `Continue with ${label}` : undefined;\n\n  const inlineStyle =\n    appearance === \"colorful\" && !systemFill\n      ? {\n          backgroundImage: `linear-gradient(180deg, ${fill} 0%, oklch(from ${fill} calc(l - 0.04) calc(c + 0.01) h) 100%)`,\n        }\n      : undefined;\n\n  if (href !== undefined) {\n    return (\n      <a\n        ref={ref as Ref<HTMLAnchorElement>}\n        href={href}\n        aria-label={accessibleName}\n        className={classes}\n        style={inlineStyle}\n        {...(rest as AnchorHTMLAttributes<HTMLAnchorElement>)}\n      >\n        {content}\n      </a>\n    );\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <button\n      ref={ref as Ref<HTMLButtonElement>}\n      type=\"button\"\n      aria-label={accessibleName}\n      className={classes}\n      style={inlineStyle}\n      {...(rest as ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>)}\n    >\n      {content}\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The press scale from `button-press-motion` is removed under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. Hover still brightens or changes the fill, because that colour feedback carries meaning and does not move anything.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Passing a `style` prop. It is spread after the computed inline style, so it replaces the brand gradient and the colourful button turns transparent with white text.\n- Relying on the colourful gradient in older browsers. Relative colour syntax (`oklch(from …)`) is invalid where unsupported, which drops the whole `background-image`. Use `appearance=\"white\"` or `black` if you must support those engines.\n- Writing the label yourself as \"Sign in with Google\" in only some buttons. Leave `children` empty and every provider gets the same \"Continue with <Label>\" wording.\n- Using `brand=\"custom\"` without `config`. The button then renders \"Continue with SSO\" on a black fill with no logo.\n- Shipping without checking each provider's brand guidelines. Google and Apple in particular publish rules on wording, colour and clear space for sign-in buttons.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The outlined `white` appearance with full-colour marks is what most sign-in screens use. `colorful` suits a single prominent provider; `black` suits monochrome layouts.\n- `SOCIAL_PROVIDERS` and the `SocialProvider` type are re-exported, so a settings page can list connected accounts with the same glyphs and labels.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe outlined stack shows Google's four-colour G, Apple and GitHub marks that turn white in dark mode, and three equal-width rows reading \"Continue with …\". The filled column shows Google in the kit's exact primary-button blue, Discord in its own purple, slightly darker at the bottom edge, and Apple in solid black. Hover brightens Discord without a colour jump. Icon-only buttons are 36px squares, and a screen reader announces \"Continue with GitHub\". At 360px wide nothing overflows the frame.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "speedometer-chart-card": "# Build: Speedometer Chart Card\n\nA dashboard card with a half gauge that opens at the bottom. The `segments` look stacks one round-capped segment per item around a gray track, with a small gap between neighbours, and writes the primary item's share in the centre (\"47%\" over \"Desktop\"). The segments sweep in from the left end on mount and re-proportion on a period change. Hovering, focusing or tapping a segment darkens it one tone, fades the others, swaps the count-up headline to that item and moves the centre to its share; a click or Enter makes it the primary. The `needle` look draws one value arc in the item's colour over a tick scale, with a needle that swings to every new value, the value under the hub and min/max labels at the arc ends.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA 220° half gauge in the dashboard card shell: stacked segments with the main share in the centre, or a value arc and needle.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The gauge is an inline SVG drawn 1:1 in a 280 × 212 box, centred on (140, 134) with a 110px radius. Angles run clockwise from twelve o'clock, from −110° to +110° (a 220° sweep), and `polar(θ, r)` maps them to `(140 + r·sin θ, 134 − r·cos θ)`. `arcPath` writes one SVG `A` command between two angles, with the large-arc flag set past 180°.\n2. For `segments`, the items become cumulative boundaries (0..1 of the total) that go through `useTweenedValues`, a 520ms ease-out cubic tween from 0 on mount and from the current boundaries on a period change. `layoutSegments` turns them into angles: a 6px gap (6/110 rad) between neighbours, scaled by the last boundary so the gaps open as the gauge sweeps in, and the rest of the sweep shared by value.\n3. Each segment path is inset by the round cap's half-width (14/110 rad) at both ends, so the caps land exactly on the segment's extent and the gaps stay clean. The gray track is inset the same way, so no track shows past the first and last segments. A segment too short for its two caps becomes a dot centred on its extent, as wide as the extent (at least 4px).\n4. Each segment is a `<g role=\"button\" tabIndex=0 aria-pressed>`. Pointer enter and focus set the active item, leaving the gauge or blurring clears it, and click or Enter/Space call `onPrimaryChange` (or set the uncontrolled primary). The active segment switches to the darker `--color-chart-N-active` tone, the others fade to 0.35 opacity over 200ms, and a keyboard focus draws a focus-ring halo under the segment.\n5. The centre is an HTML overlay over the SVG with `aria-live=\"polite\"`. It shows the share of the hovered item, or else the primary item, in `text-display-4`, and remounts on every change so the shell's number fade plays. The header shows the total and the delta chip at rest; on hover it shows the item, its value and \"x% of total\".\n6. For `needle`, the value is the `value` prop or else the shown item's share in percent, mapped to 0..1 of `min`..`max`. The fraction goes through `useTweenedValues` over 700ms. The value arc runs from the left end to the needle angle in the shown item's colour, and the needle polygon is drawn pointing up and rotated about the hub by that angle, so the arc and the needle move together. Eleven ticks mark every 10% (the 0, 50 and 100% ticks are longer), and the min/max labels sit under the arc ends.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `W × H = 280 × 212px, centre (140, 134), radius 110`\n  Drawn 1:1 so the centre overlay is placed in plain CSS px with no viewBox scaling, and the arc ends and their labels fit under the hub.\n- `START / SWEEP = −110° / 220°`\n  Opens the gauge at the bottom like the preview, with room for the centre value inside the arc.\n- `SEG_STROKE / NEEDLE_STROKE = 28px / 16px`\n  Thick segments read as a stacked gauge; the needle look uses a thinner band so the ticks and needle stay the focus.\n- `GAP_PX = 6px`\n  A visible break between round-capped neighbours. It is scaled by the sweep progress so it does not show before the gauge fills.\n- `NEEDLE_MS = 700ms ease-out cubic`\n  Slower than the 520ms segment tween, so the needle reads as a swing rather than a jump.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SpeedometerChartCard(props: { title?: string; variant?: \"segments\" | \"needle\"; ranges?: GaugeRange[]; items?: GaugeItem[]; delta?: number; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; primary?: number; defaultPrimary?: number; onPrimaryChange?: (index: number) => void; value?: number; min?: number; max?: number; formatGaugeValue?: (value: number) => string; gaugeLabel?: string; tiles?: boolean; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype GaugeItem = { label: string; value: number; color?: ChartColor }\ntype GaugeRange = { id: string; label: string; items: GaugeItem[]; delta?: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Visitors\"`\n  Header label while nothing is hovered, and the card's accessible name.\n- `variant: \"segments\" | \"needle\"` — default `\"segments\"`\n  Stacked segments with the primary share in the centre, or one value arc with a needle.\n- `ranges: GaugeRange[]` — default `SPEEDOMETER_RANGES`\n  Periods with their own items and delta. Two or more show the period select.\n- `items: GaugeItem[]`\n  A single item list. Overrides `ranges` and hides the select.\n- `delta: number`\n  Headline chip percentage when `items` is used.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the period select changes.\n- `primary: number`\n  Controlled index of the item the centre and the needle report while nothing is hovered.\n- `defaultPrimary: number` — default `0`\n  Initial primary index when uncontrolled.\n- `onPrimaryChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Called when a segment is clicked or pressed with Enter/Space.\n- `value: number`\n  Needle value. Defaults to the shown item's share of the total in percent.\n- `min: number` — default `0`\n  Needle scale start.\n- `max: number` — default `100`\n  Needle scale end.\n- `formatGaugeValue: (value: number) => string` — default ``${Math.round(v)}%``\n  Formats the centre value and the min/max labels.\n- `gaugeLabel: string` — default `shown item's name`\n  Caption under the centre value.\n- `tiles: boolean` — default `true`\n  Stat tiles under the gauge, three per row; hovering one focuses its segment.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `en-US integer`\n  Formats the headline, tiles and the segments' accessible labels.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * One stacked segment. Its visible extent is [from, to]; the path is inset\n * by the cap's half-width at both ends so the round caps land exactly on the\n * extent and neighbours keep a clean gap. Too short for its caps, it becomes\n * a dot as wide as the extent allows, centred on it.\n */\nfunction SegmentView({\n  item,\n  index,\n  segment,\n  color,\n  active,\n  primary,\n  focusVisible,\n  label,\n  onTarget,\n  onFocusVisible,\n  onSelect,\n}: SegmentViewProps) {\n  const span = segment.to - segment.from;\n  if (span <= 0.0001) return null;\n  const tone = CHART_COLORS[color];\n  const isActive = active === index;\n  const dimmed = active !== null && !isActive;\n  const cap = SEG_STROKE / 2 / R;\n  const fits = span > cap * 2;\n  const mid = (segment.from + segment.to) / 2;\n  const d = fits ? arcPath(segment.from + cap, segment.to - cap, R) : arcPath(mid, mid, R);\n  const width = fits ? SEG_STROKE : Math.max(4, span * R);\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<SVGGElement>) => {\n    if (event.key !== \"Enter\" && event.key !== \" \") return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    onSelect();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <g\n      role=\"button\"\n      tabIndex={0}\n      aria-label={label}\n      aria-pressed={primary}\n      data-segment={item.label}\n      className=\"cursor-pointer outline-none\"\n      onPointerEnter={() => onTarget(index)}\n      onFocus={(event) => {\n        onTarget(index);\n        onFocusVisible(event.currentTarget.matches(\":focus-visible\") ? index : null);\n      }}\n      onBlur={() => {\n        onTarget(null);\n        onFocusVisible(null);\n      }}\n      onClick={onSelect}\n      onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n    >\n      {focusVisible ? (\n        <path d={d} fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--color-border-focus-ring)\" strokeWidth={width + 6} strokeLinecap=\"round\" />\n      ) : null}\n      <path\n        d={d}\n        fill=\"none\"\n        stroke={isActive ? tone.active : tone.fill}\n        strokeWidth={width}\n        strokeLinecap=\"round\"\n        opacity={dimmed ? 0.35 : 1}\n        className=\"transition-[stroke,opacity] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n      />\n    </g>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The segment and needle tweens and the headline's count-up cancel their animation frame on every new target and on unmount. Hover, focus and the primary are plain React state on SVG elements, with no document listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useTweenedValues` is disabled for both looks, so the segments render at their final extents and the needle sits at its value, jumping on a change. The stroke/opacity fades use `motion-reduce:transition-none`, and the centre number fade is off (`motion-reduce:animate-none`).\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Drawing round-capped segments edge to edge. The caps then overlap the neighbours by half the stroke on each side. Inset each path by the cap's half-width so the visible extent is exactly the segment.\n- Opening the gaps from the first frame. On mount every segment then starts as a dot at its gap position. Scaling the gap with the last boundary makes the whole gauge sweep in from one end.\n- Rotating the needle with a CSS transition while the arc is tweened in JS. The two then drift apart mid-swing. Drive both from the same tweened fraction.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `SPEEDOMETER_RANGES`, `GaugeItem`, `GaugeRange` and `SpeedometerVariant` are exported. Pass `items` (and `delta`) for one static list without the period select.\n- Pass `value` with `min`/`max` to use the needle look as a plain gauge for any number, and `formatGaugeValue` for the units.\n- The frame, header, period select and stat tiles come from `funnel-chart-card-shell`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Visitors\", \"2,500\", a lime \"+5.2%\" chip and a \"Last 7 days\" select. Under it is a half gauge with lime, blue, purple and pink segments (Desktop, Mobile, Tablet, TV) separated by small gaps, with \"47%\" over \"Desktop\" in the centre, and four tiles: Desktop 1,180, Mobile 620, Tablet 380 and TV 320. Hovering the Mobile tile fades the other segments and tiles, the header reads \"Mobile\", \"620\" and \"25% of total\", and the centre reads \"25%\" over \"Mobile\". In the Needle look the needle and a lime arc point at 47%, \"0%\" and \"100%\" sit under the arc ends, and picking Mobile swings the needle back to 25%. \"Last 30 days\" re-proportions the segments, and the headline counts to 10,000.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "spinner": "# Build: Spinner\n\nA spinner for work of unknown length, drawn as a comet of light: the same arc at three scales (a crisp line with a rounded head, a tight glow and a wide bloom), each softer layer shorter and a few degrees behind, over a faint full-circle track. Everything paints in `currentColor`, so a text class sets the colour. `Spinner` is announced as an indeterminate React Aria progress bar labelled \"Loading\"; `SpinnerGlyph` is the bare visual.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn orbiting light in three layers (crisp arc, tight glow, wide bloom) in five sizes and any text colour, announced as loading.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Look up the box and line width from `SIZES`: xs 12/1.5, sm 16/2, md 20/2, lg 24/2.5, xl 32/3 px. Render a `relative inline-flex shrink-0 text-accent-500` span at that box size.\n2. If `track` is on, draw the full circle as an `absolute inset-0 rounded-full border-current opacity-15` span with `borderWidth: line`.\n3. Each `Arc` paints `conic-gradient(transparent ${360 - arc}deg, currentColor 360deg)` so the arc ends at 12 o'clock, then masks it to a ring with a `radial-gradient` that is opaque between `center ± width/2` (with `center = box/2 - line/2`). Its wrapper is grown by `bleed = max(0, outer + blur*2 - box/2)` on every side so the blur never clips, then gets `filter: blur()`, `opacity` and `rotate: -lag deg`.\n4. Stack three arcs inside one `animate-spin [animation-duration:900ms]` layer: bloom (width line×4, blur line×1.6, opacity 0.32, 110°, 22° behind), glow (width line×2, blur line×0.6, opacity 0.6, 200°, 9° behind) and crisp (width line, no blur, opacity 1, 290°, 0°). The shorter, lagging soft layers make the glow gather just behind the sharp head.\n5. Finish the head with a `line × line` `rounded-full bg-current` dot at `left-1/2 top-0` (`translate: -50% 0`), the leading end of the crisp arc.\n6. `Spinner` wraps the glyph in React Aria's `<ProgressBar isIndeterminate aria-label=\"Loading\">`, giving it `role=\"progressbar\"`. With `aria-hidden` it returns the bare `SpinnerGlyph`, for when a parent control already announces the wait.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `SIZES = xs 12/1.5, sm 16/2, md 20/2, lg 24/2.5, xl 32/3 (box/line px)`\n  Sm and md match the kit's 16px and 20px icon slots, so a spinner can replace a button icon without changing the button height. Line width grows slower than the box so small spinners do not turn into solid discs.\n- `layers = bloom 4×line, blur 1.6×line, 0.32, 110°, lag 22° / glow 2×line, blur 0.6×line, 0.6, 200°, lag 9° / crisp 1×line, 1, 290°, lag 0°`\n  Shorter and later soft layers keep the bright head in front and the glow trailing like a comet's tail. Scaling everything by `line` keeps the look identical from 12 to 32px.\n- `orbit = 900ms per turn; 2400ms under reduced motion`\n  Under a second reads as active work. The reduced-motion orbit slows rather than stops, because a frozen spinner reads as a hang.\n- `track = opacity-15 border in currentColor`\n  Faint enough to sit behind the light, but it completes the circle so the 290° arc never reads as a broken ring.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype SpinnerSize = \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\" | \"xl\"\ninterface SpinnerProps { size?: SpinnerSize; track?: boolean; \"aria-label\"?: string; \"aria-hidden\"?: boolean; className?: string }\nfunction SpinnerGlyph(props: Omit<SpinnerProps, \"aria-label\" | \"aria-hidden\">): JSX.Element\nfunction Spinner(props: SpinnerProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `size: \"xs\" | \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\" | \"xl\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  12, 16, 20, 24 or 32px box with a matching line width.\n- `track: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draw the faint full circle under the light.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Loading\"`\n  Accessible name of the progress bar.\n- `aria-hidden: boolean`\n  Render only the glyph, with no progress-bar role, when a parent already announces the wait.\n- `className: string`\n  Colour via a text class (default `text-accent-500`) and extra layout classes.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Box size and line width in px. */\nconst SIZES: Record<SpinnerSize, { box: number; line: number }> = {\n  xs: { box: 12, line: 1.5 },\n  sm: { box: 16, line: 2 },\n  md: { box: 20, line: 2 },\n  lg: { box: 24, line: 2.5 },\n  xl: { box: 32, line: 3 },\n};\n\n/** One scale of the light: a conic arc masked to a ring, blurred by its wrapper. */\nfunction Arc({\n  box,\n  line,\n  width,\n  blur,\n  opacity,\n  arc,\n  lag,\n}: {\n  box: number;\n  line: number;\n  /** Ring thickness, px. */\n  width: number;\n  blur: number;\n  opacity: number;\n  /** Arc length in degrees; the head sits at 12 o'clock before the turn. */\n  arc: number;\n  /** Degrees this layer trails the crisp head. */\n  lag: number;\n}) {\n  const center = box / 2 - line / 2;\n  const inner = center - width / 2;\n  const outer = center + width / 2;\n  // The wrapper is grown to hold the ring plus its blur, so nothing clips.\n  const bleed = Math.max(0, outer + blur * 2 - box / 2);\n  const ring = `radial-gradient(circle, transparent ${inner}px, #000 ${inner + 0.5}px, #000 ${outer - 0.5}px, transparent ${outer}px)`;\n  const style: CSSProperties = {\n    inset: -bleed,\n    filter: blur > 0 ? `blur(${blur}px)` : undefined,\n    opacity,\n    rotate: `${-lag}deg`,\n  };\n  return (\n    <span aria-hidden className=\"absolute\" style={style}>\n      <span\n        className=\"absolute inset-0\"\n        style={{\n          background: `conic-gradient(transparent ${360 - arc}deg, currentColor 360deg)`,\n          maskImage: ring,\n          WebkitMaskImage: ring,\n        }}\n      />\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n\nexport interface SpinnerProps {\n  /** 12, 16, 20 (default), 24, or 32px. */\n  size?: SpinnerSize;\n  /** Draw the faint full circle under the light. Default true. */\n  track?: boolean;\n  /** Accessible name. Default \"Loading\". */\n  \"aria-label\"?: string;\n  /** Hide it from assistive technology when a parent already announces the wait. */\n  \"aria-hidden\"?: boolean;\n  /** Colour comes from the text colour, e.g. `text-white`. Default accent. */\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function SpinnerGlyph({ size = \"md\", track = true, className }: Omit<SpinnerProps, \"aria-label\" | \"aria-hidden\">) {\n  const { box, line } = SIZES[size];\n  return (\n    <span\n      aria-hidden\n      className={cx(\"relative inline-flex shrink-0 text-accent-500\", className)}\n      style={{ width: box, height: box }}\n    >\n      {track ? (\n        <span className=\"absolute inset-0 rounded-full border-current opacity-15\" style={{ borderWidth: line }} />\n      ) : null}\n      <span className=\"absolute inset-0 animate-spin [animation-duration:900ms] motion-reduce:[animation-duration:2400ms]\">\n        <Arc box={box} line={line} width={line * 4} blur={line * 1.6} opacity={0.32} arc={110} lag={22} />\n        <Arc box={box} line={line} width={line * 2} blur={line * 0.6} opacity={0.6} arc={200} lag={9} />\n        <Arc box={box} line={line} width={line} blur={0} opacity={1} arc={290} lag={0} />\n        {/* The crisp line's rounded head, at the arc's leading end. */}\n        <span\n          className=\"absolute left-1/2 top-0 rounded-full bg-current\"\n          style={{ width: line, height: line, translate: \"-50% 0\" }}\n        />\n      </span>\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The orbit is a CSS animation; there are no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The orbit keeps turning but slows from 900ms to 2400ms per revolution (`motion-reduce:[animation-duration:2400ms]`), since a stopped spinner would read as a frozen app.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not rotate each arc separately. All three share one spinning parent; their offsets come from a static `rotate: -lag`, so the comet shape never drifts apart.\n- Do not set the wrapper to `overflow: hidden` or trim the `bleed` inset. The blurred layers extend past the box and would be cut into a square.\n- Do not colour it with `fill` or `stroke`. Everything paints in `currentColor`; set a text colour such as `text-white` instead.\n- Do not leave both a busy button label and the spinner announced. Pass `aria-hidden` to the spinner inside a pending button so screen readers do not hear \"Loading\" twice.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The default colour is `text-accent-500`; override it with any `text-*` class.\n- `SpinnerGlyph` is exported for places that need the visual without the progress-bar role.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA md spinner renders a 20px accent ring with a bright arc and rounded head turning clockwise about once every 0.9s, with a soft glow trailing behind the head and a faint full circle underneath. `className=\"text-white\"` turns every layer white. The default spinner exposes `role=\"progressbar\"` named \"Loading\" with no value; with `aria-hidden` no progressbar role is rendered. Nothing is clipped at any of the five sizes.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "spring-lattice-field": "# Build: Spring Lattice Field\n\nA grid that behaves like a stretched sheet. A coarse lattice of damped springs runs on the CPU, is packed into an RGBA8 texture once per frame, and the fragment shader reads that texture with LINEAR filtering to displace its own sample coordinate. The pointer pushes cells outward and drags them along with its velocity; diffusion between neighbours turns each push into a wave that spreads and settles.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA CPU damped-spring lattice uploaded as a texture each frame, displacing a procedural grid the cursor can push around.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The lattice is `round(width / cellSize)` by `round(height / cellSize)` cells, sized in CSS pixels rather than device pixels so the simulation is identical on a retina display instead of running four times as many cells.\n2. Each step, cells within `radius` of the pointer receive two impulses: a radial one along `(cell - pointer) / |cell - pointer|` scaled by `radialStrength`, and a flow one along the pointer's own velocity scaled by `flowStrength`. Both are weighted by `t*t*(3-2t)` where `t = 1 - dist/radius` — a smoothstep falloff, C1 at both ends, so the disturbance has no visible rim.\n3. Then `vel = (vel - disp * returnStiffness) * velocityDamping` and `nextDisp = disp + vel + (neighbourAverage - disp) * diffusion`. The diffusion term is what makes it a sheet rather than a field of independent springs: a local push propagates outward as a wave instead of staying put.\n4. The integration writes into a scratch buffer, not in place. In place, the +x and +y neighbours would already hold the new value when a cell reads them, and the wave would visibly drift toward the lattice origin.\n5. Displacement is packed into the texture as `d / DISP_MAX * 0.5 + 0.5` in RG, with cell speed in B. Speed, not displacement, drives the glow — so the field lights up while it is moving and goes dark once it settles, which is what reads as elastic rather than as a permanent dent.\n6. The shader adds `disp / gridDimensions` to its uv before drawing an `fwidth`-normalised grid, so lines stay one pixel wide even where the field is stretching them, and `u_density` is set to the lattice row count so the drawn lines land on cell boundaries.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `STEP = 1 / 60`\n  Fixed simulation timestep. Every other physics constant is expressed per step — `velocityDamping = 0.93` only means \"keep 93% per 1/60 s\" if the step is always 1/60 s. Integrating per frame instead makes the field stiffer on a 60 Hz laptop than on a 120 Hz phone.\n- `MAX_SUBSTEPS = 4`\n  Catch-up ceiling. Returning to a backgrounded tab otherwise tries to simulate the whole elapsed time in one frame and the page locks up for seconds. The leftover accumulator is discarded when the ceiling is hit, or the debt compounds and the simulation runs saturated forever.\n- `returnStiffness = 0.018`\n  Spring constant per step. Above ~0.05 the lattice snaps back before the wave can spread and it reads as rubber sheeting; below ~0.006 disturbances take several seconds to clear and the grid looks permanently dented.\n- `velocityDamping = 0.93`\n  Velocity retained per step. At 1.0 the lattice never loses energy and rings forever; below ~0.85 the push is absorbed within a few cells and nothing propagates.\n- `diffusion = 0.22`\n  Fraction of the way each cell moves toward its neighbours' average per step. This is the wave speed. Past 0.25 the explicit scheme goes unstable and the lattice fills with checkerboard noise; near 0 the cells decouple and the grid dimples pointwise.\n- `radius = 5.5`\n  Pointer influence, in cells. Expressed in cells rather than pixels so the physics is unchanged at any `cellSize`. Under ~3 the cursor pokes a hole; past ~10 it lifts the whole panel and the motion loses its centre.\n- `radialStrength = 0.02`\n  Sustained outward push per step. Equilibrium displacement is roughly `radialStrength / returnStiffness` ≈ 1.1 cells — enough for the grid to visibly part around a resting cursor without approaching the encode range.\n- `flowStrength = 0.08`\n  Drag along the pointer velocity. A transient impulse peaks at about `impulse / (1 - velocityDamping)` ≈ 14x, so this is roughly ten times smaller than the radial constant for a comparable displacement. Raise it and fast swipes clip the encode range.\n- `MAX_POINTER_SPEED = 45`\n  Cells per second. A pointer that teleports across the element — tab switch, window drag, the first event after a long pause — otherwise injects one enormous impulse and the whole lattice snaps in a single step.\n- `DISP_MAX = 2.5`\n  Encode range in cells. Displacement is clamped to it before packing into 8 bits; an unclamped value wraps the byte and the cell flips to the opposite sign, which reads as a hard pop exactly where the motion is strongest.\n- `cellSize = 18`\n  CSS pixels per cell. Cost is quadratic: at 6 a 1440x900 panel is 36,000 cells and the step stops fitting in a frame. Above ~30 the lattice is coarse enough that LINEAR interpolation between cells becomes visible as flat facets.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SpringLatticeField(props: { cellSize?: number; radius?: number; radialStrength?: number; flowStrength?: number; ink?: readonly [number, number, number]; line?: readonly [number, number, number]; glow?: readonly [number, number, number]; maxDpr?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `cellSize: number` — default `18`\n  CSS pixels per lattice cell. Cost is quadratic in this.\n- `radius: number` — default `5.5`\n  Pointer influence radius, in lattice cells.\n- `radialStrength: number` — default `0.02`\n  Outward push per fixed step, in cells/step².\n- `flowStrength: number` — default `0.08`\n  How strongly pointer velocity drags cells along with it.\n- `ink: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.035, 0.038, 0.048]`\n  Base field colour, linear 0..1 RGB.\n- `line: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.16, 0.17, 0.2]`\n  Grid line colour, added on top of ink.\n- `glow: readonly [number, number, number]` — default `[0.35, 0.62, 0.95]`\n  Colour added in proportion to cell speed.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `2`\n  Upper bound on device pixel ratio.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TRIANGLE = new Float32Array([-1, -1, 3, -1, -1, 3]);\nconst MAX_DPR = 2;\n\n/* The simulation runs on a fixed timestep. Every constant below is tuned per\n   STEP — `velocityDamping = 0.93` means \"keep 93% of the velocity after 1/60 of\n   a second\", and it only means that if the step is always 1/60 of a second. A\n   per-frame integration would make the field stiffer on a 60 Hz laptop than on\n   a 120 Hz phone, which is the exact failure mode the frame-rate independence\n   rule exists to prevent. Everything here is in LATTICE CELLS, not pixels, so\n   the physics is identical at any cellSize. */\nconst STEP = 1 / 60;\n/* Ceiling on catch-up steps per frame. Without it, returning to a backgrounded\n   tab tries to simulate the whole elapsed time in one frame and the page hangs;\n   with it, the field simply resumes from where it was. */\nconst MAX_SUBSTEPS = 4;\n\nconst RETURN_STIFFNESS = 0.018;\nconst VELOCITY_DAMPING = 0.93;\nconst DIFFUSION = 0.22;\n/* Pointer speed cap, cells/second. A pointer teleporting across the element\n   (tab switch, window drag) otherwise injects one enormous impulse and the\n   lattice snaps past the encode range in a single step. */\nconst MAX_POINTER_SPEED = 45;\n/* Encode range in cells. Displacement is packed into 8 bits as\n   `d / DISP_MAX * 0.5 + 0.5`, so anything beyond this must be clamped — an\n   unclamped value wraps the byte and the cell flips to the opposite sign,\n   which reads as a hard pop. */\nconst DISP_MAX = 2.5;\n/* Maps cell speed (cells/step) into the 0..1 glow channel. */\nconst ENERGY_SCALE = 6;\n\nconst MIN_CELLS = 8;\nconst MAX_CELLS = 80;\n\ninterface Lattice {\n  cols: number;\n  rows: number;\n  /** Interleaved x,y displacement per cell, in cells. */\n  disp: Float32Array;\n  /** Interleaved x,y velocity per cell, in cells/step. */\n  vel: Float32Array;\n  /** Scratch buffer — diffusion must read the whole previous state. */\n  next: Float32Array;\n  /** RGBA8 upload staging. */\n  pixels: Uint8Array;\n}\n\nfunction createLattice(cols: number, rows: number): Lattice {\n  const cells = cols * rows;\n  return {\n    cols,\n    rows,\n    disp: new Float32Array(cells * 2),\n    vel: new Float32Array(cells * 2),\n    next: new Float32Array(cells * 2),\n    pixels: new Uint8Array(cells * 4),\n  };\n}\n\n/* One fixed-timestep step. Pointer position is in cell coordinates; pointer\n   velocity is in cells/second and is converted here, so the caller does not\n   have to know the step length. */\nfunction stepLattice(\n  lattice: Lattice,\n  pointerX: number,\n  pointerY: number,\n  pointerVX: number,\n  pointerVY: number,\n  active: boolean,\n  radius: number,\n  radialStrength: number,\n  flowStrength: number,\n): void {\n  const { cols, rows, disp, vel, next } = lattice;\n\n  if (active) {\n    // Only the cells inside the radius can be touched, so bound the loop\n    // instead of testing every cell. At radius 5.5 that is ~120 cells out of\n    // a couple of thousand.\n    const minX = Math.max(0, Math.floor(pointerX - radius));\n    const maxX = Math.min(cols - 1, Math.ceil(pointerX + radius));\n    const minY = Math.max(0, Math.floor(pointerY - radius));\n    const maxY = Math.min(rows - 1, Math.ceil(pointerY + radius));\n    const flowX = pointerVX * STEP * flowStrength;\n    const flowY = pointerVY * STEP * flowStrength;\n\n    for (let y = minY; y <= maxY; y++) {\n      for (let x = minX; x <= maxX; x++) {\n        const dx = x - pointerX;\n        const dy = y - pointerY;\n        const dist = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);\n        if (dist > radius) continue;\n\n        /* Smoothstep falloff, not linear. A linear falloff has a corner at the\n           radius: cells cross the boundary with non-zero force and the edge of\n           the disturbance is a visible circle. `t*t*(3-2t)` is C1 at both ends,\n           so the influence dies out with zero slope and no rim appears. */\n        const t = 1 - dist / radius;\n        const shaped = t * t * (3 - 2 * t);\n\n        // At dist 0 the direction is undefined; pushing nothing is correct.\n        const inv = dist > 1e-4 ? 1 / dist : 0;\n        const i = (y * cols + x) * 2;\n        vel[i] = (vel[i] ?? 0) + dx * inv * shaped * radialStrength + flowX * shaped;\n        vel[i + 1] =\n          (vel[i + 1] ?? 0) + dy * inv * shaped * radialStrength + flowY * shaped;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Spring toward rest, then bleed energy. Damping applied AFTER the spring\n  // term keeps the system unconditionally stable at these constants.\n  for (let i = 0; i < vel.length; i++) {\n    vel[i] = ((vel[i] ?? 0) - (disp[i] ?? 0) * RETURN_STIFFNESS) * VELOCITY_DAMPING;\n  }\n\n  /* Integrate and diffuse in one pass into `next`. Diffusion is what makes the\n     lattice behave like a sheet rather than a field of independent springs:\n     each cell is pulled `DIFFUSION` of the way toward its four neighbours'\n     average, so a local push propagates outward as a wave. Writing in place\n     would make the result depend on iteration order — the +x and +y neighbours\n     would already be updated, and the wave would drift toward the origin. */\n  for (let y = 0; y < rows; y++) {\n    for (let x = 0; x < cols; x++) {\n      const i = (y * cols + x) * 2;\n      for (let c = 0; c < 2; c++) {\n        const self = disp[i + c] ?? 0;\n        // Edges mirror themselves: a free boundary. Clamping them to zero\n        // instead pins the border and every wave reflects off it.\n        const left = x > 0 ? disp[i - 2 + c] ?? self : self;\n        const right = x < cols - 1 ? disp[i + 2 + c] ?? self : self;\n        const up = y > 0 ? disp[i - cols * 2 + c] ?? self : self;\n        const down = y < rows - 1 ? disp[i + cols * 2 + c] ?? self : self;\n        const avg = (left + right + up + down) * 0.25;\n        next[i + c] = self + (vel[i + c] ?? 0) + (avg - self) * DIFFUSION;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  disp.set(next);\n}\n\n/** Pack displacement into RG and speed into B. Returns total energy. */\nfunction encodeLattice(lattice: Lattice): number {\n  const { disp, vel, pixels } = lattice;\n  const cells = lattice.cols * lattice.rows;\n  let total = 0;\n\n  for (let c = 0; c < cells; c++) {\n    const i = c * 2;\n    const dx = disp[i] ?? 0;\n    const dy = disp[i + 1] ?? 0;\n    const vx = vel[i] ?? 0;\n    const vy = vel[i + 1] ?? 0;\n    const speed = Math.sqrt(vx * vx + vy * vy);\n    total += speed;\n\n    const o = c * 4;\n    pixels[o] = encodeSigned(dx);\n    pixels[o + 1] = encodeSigned(dy);\n    pixels[o + 2] = Math.min(255, Math.round(speed * ENERGY_SCALE * 255));\n    pixels[o + 3] = 255;\n  }\n\n  return total;\n}\n\nfunction encodeSigned(value: number): number {\n  const n = value / DISP_MAX;\n  const clamped = n < -1 ? -1 : n > 1 ? 1 : n;\n  return Math.round((clamped * 0.5 + 0.5) * 255);\n}\n\ninterface Pass {\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext;\n  program: WebGLProgram;\n  vao: WebGLVertexArrayObject;\n  buffer: WebGLBuffer;\n  texture: WebGLTexture;\n  locations: Map<string, WebGLUniformLocation | null>;\n  /** Allocated texture dimensions, so resize can tell reallocation from upload. */\n  texCols: number;\n  texRows: number;\n}\n\nfunction compileShader(\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext,\n  type: number,\n  source: string,\n): WebGLShader | null {\n  const shader = gl.createShader(type);\n  if (!shader) return null;\n  gl.shaderSource(shader, source);\n  gl.compileShader(shader);\n  if (!gl.getShaderParameter(shader, gl.COMPILE_STATUS)) {\n    console.error('[spring-lattice-field]', gl.getShaderInfoLog(shader));\n    gl.deleteShader(shader);\n    return null;\n  }\n  return shader;\n}\n\nfunction createPass(gl: WebGL2RenderingContext): Pass | null {\n  const vert = compileShader(gl, gl.VERTEX_SHADER, SPRING_LATTICE_VERT);\n  const frag = compileShader(gl, gl.FRAGMENT_SHADER, SPRING_LATTICE_FRAG);\n  const program = gl.createProgram();\n  if (!vert || !frag || !program) {\n    if (vert) gl.deleteShader(vert);\n    if (frag) gl.deleteShader(frag);\n    if (program) gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.attachShader(program, vert);\n  gl.attachShader(program, frag);\n  gl.linkProgram(program);\n  gl.deleteShader(vert);\n  gl.deleteShader(frag);\n\n  if (!gl.getProgramParameter(program, gl.LINK_STATUS)) {\n    console.error('[spring-lattice-field]', gl.getProgramInfoLog(program));\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  const vao = gl.createVertexArray();\n  const buffer = gl.createBuffer();\n  const texture = gl.createTexture();\n  if (!vao || !buffer || !texture) {\n    gl.deleteProgram(program);\n    if (vao) gl.deleteVertexArray(vao);\n    if (buffer) gl.deleteBuffer(buffer);\n    if (texture) gl.deleteTexture(texture);\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  gl.bindVertexArray(vao);\n  gl.bindBuffer(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, buffer);\n  gl.bufferData(gl.ARRAY_BUFFER, TRIANGLE, gl.STATIC_DRAW);\n  const attrib = gl.getAttribLocation(program, 'a_pos');\n  if (attrib >= 0) {\n    gl.enableVertexAttribArray(attrib);\n    gl.vertexAttribPointer(attrib, 2, gl.FLOAT, false, 0, 0);\n  }\n  gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n\n  gl.activeTexture(gl.TEXTURE0);\n  gl.bindTexture(gl.TEXTURE_2D, texture);\n  /* LINEAR is load-bearing, not a default: it is what turns a 40x24 simulation\n     into a smooth field across two million fragments. CLAMP_TO_EDGE because\n     REPEAT would sample the opposite edge of the lattice at the border and\n     stamp a seam there. */\n  gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER, gl.LINEAR);\n  gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER, gl.LINEAR);\n  gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_WRAP_S, gl.CLAMP_TO_EDGE);\n  gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_WRAP_T, gl.CLAMP_TO_EDGE);\n\n  return {\n    gl,\n    program,\n    vao,\n    buffer,\n    texture,\n    locations: new Map(),\n    texCols: 0,\n    texRows: 0,\n  };\n}\n\nfunction uniformLocation(pass: Pass, name: string): WebGLUniformLocation | null {\n  const cached = pass.locations.get(name);\n  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;\n  const found = pass.gl.getUniformLocation(pass.program, name);\n  pass.locations.set(name, found);\n  return found;\n}\n\nfunction setVec3(\n  gl: WebGL2RenderingContext,\n  location: WebGLUniformLocation | null,\n  v: readonly [number, number, number],\n): void {\n  if (location === null) return;\n  gl.uniform3f(location, v[0], v[1], v[2]);\n}\n\nfunction disposePass(pass: Pass): void {\n  const { gl } = pass;\n  gl.deleteVertexArray(pass.vao);\n  gl.deleteBuffer(pass.buffer);\n  gl.deleteTexture(pass.texture);\n  gl.deleteProgram(pass.program);\n  loseContext(gl);\n}\n\nexport function SpringLatticeField({\n  cellSize = 18,\n  radius = 5.5,\n  radialStrength = 0.02,\n  flowStrength = 0.08,\n  ink = [0.035, 0.038, 0.048],\n  line = [0.16, 0.17, 0.2],\n  glow = [0.35, 0.62, 0.95],\n  maxDpr = MAX_DPR,\n  className,\n}: SpringLatticeFieldProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const canvasRef = useRef<HTMLCanvasElement>(null);\n  const passRef = useRef<Pass | null>(null);\n  const latticeRef = useRef<Lattice | null>(null);\n  const accumulator = useRef(0);\n  const prevPointer = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  // Light smoothing only. Heavy smoothing would blunt the velocity signal the\n  // flow push is built from, and the lattice would stop reacting to flicks.\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.004 });\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n\n  const params = useRef({ radius, radialStrength, flowStrength, ink, line, glow });\n  params.current = { radius, radialStrength, flowStrength, ink, line, glow };\n\n  const drawRef = useRef<() => void>(() => {});\n  drawRef.current = () => {\n    const pass = passRef.current;\n    const lattice = latticeRef.current;\n    if (!pass || !lattice) return;\n    const { gl } = pass;\n    const p = params.current;\n\n    gl.useProgram(pass.program);\n    gl.activeTexture(gl.TEXTURE0);\n    gl.bindTexture(gl.TEXTURE_2D, pass.texture);\n\n    if (pass.texCols !== lattice.cols || pass.texRows !== lattice.rows) {\n      gl.texImage2D(\n        gl.TEXTURE_2D,\n        0,\n        gl.RGBA8,\n        lattice.cols,\n        lattice.rows,\n        0,\n        gl.RGBA,\n        gl.UNSIGNED_BYTE,\n        lattice.pixels,\n      );\n      pass.texCols = lattice.cols;\n      pass.texRows = lattice.rows;\n    } else {\n      // texSubImage2D, not texImage2D: re-specifying the texture every frame\n      // makes the driver re-validate and often reallocate it, which shows up as\n      // a steadily climbing GPU memory graph.\n      gl.texSubImage2D(\n        gl.TEXTURE_2D,\n        0,\n        0,\n        0,\n        lattice.cols,\n        lattice.rows,\n        gl.RGBA,\n        gl.UNSIGNED_BYTE,\n        lattice.pixels,\n      );\n    }\n\n    const uField = uniformLocation(pass, 'u_field');\n    if (uField !== null) gl.uniform1i(uField, 0);\n\n    const uRes = uniformLocation(pass, 'u_resolution');\n    if (uRes !== null) {\n      gl.uniform2f(uRes, gl.drawingBufferWidth, gl.drawingBufferHeight);\n    }\n    const uGrid = uniformLocation(pass, 'u_grid');\n    if (uGrid !== null) gl.uniform2f(uGrid, lattice.cols, lattice.rows);\n    const uDisp = uniformLocation(pass, 'u_dispMax');\n    if (uDisp !== null) gl.uniform1f(uDisp, DISP_MAX);\n    /* Grid density is tied to the lattice row count so the drawn lines land on\n       cell boundaries. Decoupling them makes the displacement look like it is\n       sliding underneath the grid rather than moving it. */\n    const uDensity = uniformLocation(pass, 'u_density');\n    if (uDensity !== null) gl.uniform1f(uDensity, lattice.rows);\n\n    setVec3(gl, uniformLocation(pass, 'u_ink'), p.ink);\n    setVec3(gl, uniformLocation(pass, 'u_line'), p.line);\n    setVec3(gl, uniformLocation(pass, 'u_glow'), p.glow);\n\n    gl.bindVertexArray(pass.vao);\n    gl.drawArrays(gl.TRIANGLES, 0, 3);\n    gl.bindVertexArray(null);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // Built here rather than rendered by React, because cleanup hands the\n    // context back through `WEBGL_lose_context` and a canvas that has been\n    // through that never yields another one — `getContext` returns the same\n    // lost object. StrictMode's double invoke re-gave React's element to the\n    // second run, whose `createPass` then compiled against a dead context and\n    // demoted the component to its fallback for good.\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.className = 'block h-full w-full';\n\n    const gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', {\n      antialias: false,\n      alpha: false,\n      depth: false,\n      stencil: false,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n    });\n    if (!gl) {\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const pass = createPass(gl);\n    if (!pass) {\n      loseContext(gl);\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    el.append(canvas);\n    canvasRef.current = canvas;\n    passRef.current = pass;\n    setSupported(true);\n\n    // Left unhandled the lattice simply vanishes — a blank canvas that never\n    // recovers and never falls back — so a loss demotes the component to its\n    // static stand-in. `preventDefault` keeps a later restore possible.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      // Detached too: the fallback renders as a sibling in normal flow, and a\n      // canvas left in place would sit on top of it.\n      canvas.remove();\n      setSupported(false);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      passRef.current = null;\n      canvasRef.current = null;\n      disposePass(pass);\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  // Resize rebuilds the lattice and redraws. Under reduced motion this is the\n  // only draw that ever happens, and it shows the field at rest.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const canvas = canvasRef.current;\n    const pass = passRef.current;\n    if (!canvas || !pass || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n\n    const dpr = pixelRatio(maxDpr);\n    const w = Math.max(1, Math.round(width * dpr));\n    const h = Math.max(1, Math.round(height * dpr));\n    if (canvas.width !== w || canvas.height !== h) {\n      canvas.width = w;\n      canvas.height = h;\n    }\n    pass.gl.viewport(0, 0, w, h);\n\n    // Lattice resolution is in CSS pixels, deliberately: the simulation should\n    // feel the same on a retina display, not run four times as many cells.\n    const cols = Math.min(MAX_CELLS, Math.max(MIN_CELLS, Math.round(width / cellSize)));\n    const rows = Math.min(MAX_CELLS, Math.max(MIN_CELLS, Math.round(height / cellSize)));\n    const current = latticeRef.current;\n    if (!current || current.cols !== cols || current.rows !== rows) {\n      latticeRef.current = createLattice(cols, rows);\n      encodeLattice(latticeRef.current);\n      prevPointer.current = null;\n      accumulator.current = 0;\n    }\n\n    drawRef.current();\n  }, [width, height, cellSize, maxDpr, supported]);\n\n  const running = supported && visible && !reduced && width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useRaf((dt) => {\n    const lattice = latticeRef.current;\n    if (!lattice) return;\n    const p = params.current;\n    const state = pointer.current;\n\n    const px = state.nx * lattice.cols;\n    const py = state.ny * lattice.rows;\n\n    // Velocity from the change in position, in cells/second, then capped.\n    let vx = 0;\n    let vy = 0;\n    const prev = prevPointer.current;\n    if (prev && dt > 0) {\n      vx = (px - prev.x) / dt;\n      vy = (py - prev.y) / dt;\n      const speed = Math.sqrt(vx * vx + vy * vy);\n      if (speed > MAX_POINTER_SPEED) {\n        const k = MAX_POINTER_SPEED / speed;\n        vx *= k;\n        vy *= k;\n      }\n    }\n    prevPointer.current = { x: px, y: py };\n\n    accumulator.current += dt;\n    let steps = 0;\n    while (accumulator.current >= STEP && steps < MAX_SUBSTEPS) {\n      stepLattice(\n        lattice,\n        px,\n        py,\n        vx,\n        vy,\n        state.active,\n        p.radius,\n        p.radialStrength,\n        p.flowStrength,\n      );\n      accumulator.current -= STEP;\n      steps++;\n    }\n    // Hit the ceiling: throw the backlog away rather than carrying a debt that\n    // can never be paid off, which would leave the simulation permanently\n    // saturated and running MAX_SUBSTEPS every frame.\n    if (steps === MAX_SUBSTEPS) accumulator.current = 0;\n\n    if (steps > 0) {\n      encodeLattice(lattice);\n      drawRef.current();\n    }\n  }, running);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Deletes the VAO, vertex buffer, field texture and program, then `loseContext(gl)`. The typed arrays are dropped with the lattice ref. The rAF subscription, resize observer and pointer listener unsubscribe with their hooks.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No rAF subscription, so the lattice never steps and the pointer never disturbs it. One frame is drawn on mount and on resize showing the field at rest: the full grid, the vignette, no displacement and no glow. The component is a static grid, not a blank panel.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Integrating per frame instead of on a fixed accumulator. The constants above are tuned per 1/60 s; run them per frame and the same component is noticeably stiffer at 60 fps than at 120, and unstable under a frame spike. This is the one place the tuned constants and the frame-rate independence rule collide, and the accumulator is the resolution.\n- Carrying the accumulator forward after hitting `MAX_SUBSTEPS`. The debt can never be repaid, so the simulation runs four substeps every frame from then on and quietly costs four times what it should.\n- Diffusing in place. Cells read their +x and +y neighbours after those neighbours have already been updated, so the wave propagates faster in one diagonal than the other and visibly drifts toward the lattice origin.\n- Setting the field texture to NEAREST. The lattice itself becomes visible as hard blocky steps — at `cellSize` 18 that is a grid of 18 px squares sliding under the drawn grid.\n- Calling `texImage2D` every frame instead of `texSubImage2D`. Re-specifying the texture makes the driver re-validate and frequently reallocate it; the symptom is a GPU memory graph that climbs for as long as the page is open.\n- Failing to clamp before encoding. Displacement past `DISP_MAX` wraps the unsigned byte, so a cell being pushed hard right suddenly reads as pushed hard left, and the field pops.\n- Sizing the lattice in device pixels. A retina display quietly runs four times the cells for identical visual output, and the component drops frames only on the machines its author is least likely to be testing on.\n- Heavy pointer smoothing. The flow push is built from the derivative of the pointer position, and over-smoothing flattens that derivative — the lattice stops responding to flicks while still responding to slow drags, which reads as lag rather than as smoothing.\n- Leaving the context alive on unmount. Raw GL has no `dispose()`; the VAO, buffer, texture and program are deleted explicitly and `loseContext(gl)` hands the context back, or the page starts blanking its oldest canvases after roughly sixteen mounts.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The simulation could run on the GPU with ping-pong framebuffers, and at this lattice size it would be slower overall: two extra render targets, a readback whenever anything else needs the field state, and float-texture support to feature detect. A few thousand cells on the CPU cost under 0.2 ms and stay inspectable in a debugger.\n- Resizing rebuilds the lattice from rest. Interpolating the old state into the new dimensions is possible but the disturbance is gone within a second of a resize anyway.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nRest the cursor on the field and the grid parts around it by roughly one cell and holds there. Move it and a wave spreads outward through the lines and settles within about a second. Swipe fast and the lines are dragged along the swipe direction, glowing while they move and going dark once they stop. Grid lines stay one pixel wide even where they are most stretched. Throttle the CPU to 6x and the wave still travels at the same speed, just at fewer frames.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "spring-to-linear-easing": "# Build: Spring To CSS linear()\n\nA spring is a differential equation, and the usual way to run one on the web is a JavaScript loop that integrates it every frame — on the main thread, where a long task or a garbage collection makes it visibly hitch. `linear()` takes a list of output values and interpolates between them, and those values are not clamped to [0, 1], so a dense enough sample of a spring *is* the spring, overshoot included. Solve it once at author time, emit the samples, and the animation runs on the compositor where nothing on the main thread can reach it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSolves a spring once, samples it into a CSS linear() string, and runs the whole bounce on the compositor.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The motion is the second-order system `d'' + 2*zeta*w0*d' + w0^2*d = 0` on the *remaining* distance `d`, with `d(0) = 1` and `d'(0) = -velocity`. The natural frequency is `w0 = sqrt(stiffness / mass)` and the damping ratio is `zeta = damping / (2 * sqrt(stiffness * mass))`.\n2. Each damping regime has its own closed form. Underdamped (`zeta < 1`) is `exp(-zeta*w0*t) * (cos(wd*t) + b*sin(wd*t))` with `wd = w0*sqrt(1 - zeta^2)` and `b = (zeta*w0 - v0) / wd`. Critically damped (`zeta = 1`) collapses to `exp(-w0*t) * (1 + (w0 - v0)*t)`. Overdamped (`zeta > 1`) is a sum of two real exponentials with roots `-zeta*w0 ± w0*sqrt(zeta^2 - 1)`. Picking the wrong branch is silent until `sqrt(1 - zeta^2)` goes imaginary and every sample is NaN.\n3. `bounce` is expressed against the critical damping coefficient `2*sqrt(stiffness*mass)` rather than as an absolute damping value, which is what makes `bounce: 0` mean exactly \"no overshoot\" at any stiffness. Without that normalisation the same damping number is bouncy at high stiffness and sluggish at low.\n4. Duration is not a parameter — it is solved. A 240 Hz scan walks the solution until the spring is both close enough (`|d| < restDelta`) and slow enough (`|d'| < restSpeed`), and the first `t` that satisfies both becomes the duration. Both tests are needed: an underdamped spring crosses the target at full speed on every oscillation, so a distance-only test declares it settled half a bounce in.\n5. The solved duration is divided by `resolutionMs` to get a stop count, capped at 200, and the solution is evaluated at each stop. Samples are `1 - d(t)` — progress, not remaining distance — and are deliberately left unclamped, because values above 1 are the overshoot and clamping them is precisely how a spring degrades into an ease-out.\n6. The last sample is forced to exactly 1. The scan stopped within `restDelta` of the target, so it would otherwise be ~0.999 and every transition would end with a visible sub-pixel snap as the browser jumps from the final easing output to the real end value.\n7. `fallback` is a monotonic `cubic-bezier()` meant to be declared on the line *before* the `linear()` one. An engine that cannot parse `linear()` drops that declaration during parsing and the earlier one survives — the ordinary CSS cascade, with no feature query.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `MAX_STOPS = 200`\n  Upper bound on samples. The compositor shows one value per frame, so stops closer together than a frame are bytes with no visible effect — 200 stops across a 1.6s spring is one every 8 ms, already finer than a 120 Hz frame. It also bounds the emitted string: a slow overdamped spring at 10 ms resolution would otherwise run past a thousand stops.\n- `resolutionMs = 10`\n  Milliseconds between samples. Below ~6 ms the string grows with no visual change even at 165 Hz; past ~20 ms the linear segments between stops become long enough that the interpolation reads as faceted rather than smooth, most visibly around the first peak where curvature is highest.\n- `SCAN_STEP = 1 / 240`\n  Integration step for the settle scan — four samples per 60 Hz frame. Coarser steps can step straight over the narrow window where an underdamped spring is simultaneously near the target and slow, which sends the duration to `maxDurationMs`.\n- `MIN_RATIO = 0.05`\n  Floor on the damping ratio. At zeta = 0 the spring is undamped and never satisfies the rest test, so the scan runs to `maxDurationMs` and emits a 200-stop string of a curve that oscillates forever. `bounce` is clamped to `1 - MIN_RATIO` for the same reason.\n- `restDelta = 0.005`\n  Distance, as a fraction of the total, that counts as arrived. At 0.005 a 400 px move stops 2 px out, under the forced final sample, so the snap is invisible. An order of magnitude tighter roughly doubles the duration of a bouncy spring for motion nobody can see.\n- `restSpeed = 0.1`\n  Speed, in fractions per second, that counts as stopped. Paired with `restDelta` so a fast pass through the target is not mistaken for arrival.\n- `precision = 4`\n  Decimal places per sample. Four is sub-pixel on any realistic distance (0.0001 of 2000 px is 0.2 px) and keeps a 200-stop string near 1.4 KB. Full float precision triples the byte count for nothing.\n- `maxDurationMs = 10000`\n  Hard ceiling so a pathological configuration returns a long easing rather than hanging the scan loop.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction springToLinear(spring?: { stiffness?: number; damping?: number; mass?: number; bounce?: number; velocity?: number; restDelta?: number; restSpeed?: number }, options?: { resolutionMs?: number; maxDurationMs?: number; precision?: number }): { easing: string; fallback: string; durationMs: number; samples: readonly number[] }\nfunction useSpringEasing(spring?: SpringConfig, options?: LinearEasingOptions): SpringEasing\n```\n\n- `stiffness: number` — default `100`\n  Restoring force per unit displacement. Higher is faster and tighter.\n- `damping: number` — default `10`\n  Resistance proportional to velocity. Ignored when `bounce` is given.\n- `mass: number` — default `1`\n  Inertia. Raising it lowers the natural frequency and lengthens the settle.\n- `bounce: number`\n  Overshoot 0-1, relative to critical damping. 0 is monotonic, 0.5 is lively. Overrides `damping`.\n- `velocity: number` — default `0`\n  Initial velocity in fractions of the distance per second, for handing off from a gesture.\n- `restDelta: number` — default `0.005`\n  Remaining distance, as a fraction, that counts as arrived.\n- `restSpeed: number` — default `0.1`\n  Speed, in fractions per second, that counts as stopped.\n- `resolutionMs: number` — default `10`\n  Milliseconds between emitted stops.\n- `maxDurationMs: number` — default `10000`\n  Ceiling on the solved duration.\n- `precision: number` — default `4`\n  Decimal places per sample.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Upper bound on samples. The compositor can only show one value per frame, so\n * stops spaced closer than a frame are bytes with no visible effect: 200 stops\n * across a 1.6s spring is one every 8ms, already finer than a 120Hz frame.\n */\nconst MAX_STOPS = 200;\n/** Integration step for the settle scan. 240Hz — four samples per 60Hz frame. */\nconst SCAN_STEP = 1 / 240;\n/** A damping ratio of 0 never settles; this is the floor. */\nconst MIN_RATIO = 0.05;\n\n/** Remaining distance and its derivative, at time `t` seconds. */\ntype Solver = (t: number) => readonly [distance: number, velocity: number];\n\n/**\n * Closed-form solution of `d'' + 2*zeta*w0*d' + w0^2*d = 0` with `d(0) = 1`\n * and `d'(0) = -velocity`, i.e. the *remaining* distance to the target. All\n * three damping regimes have different solutions and picking the wrong branch\n * is silent: an overdamped spring evaluated with the underdamped formula\n * returns NaN the moment `sqrt(1 - zeta^2)` goes imaginary.\n */\nfunction makeSolver(w0: number, zeta: number, v0: number): Solver {\n  if (zeta < 1) {\n    const wd = w0 * Math.sqrt(1 - zeta * zeta);\n    const b = (zeta * w0 - v0) / wd;\n    return (t) => {\n      const decay = Math.exp(-zeta * w0 * t);\n      const cos = Math.cos(wd * t);\n      const sin = Math.sin(wd * t);\n      const d = decay * (cos + b * sin);\n      const dp = decay * (-zeta * w0 * (cos + b * sin) + (-wd * sin + b * wd * cos));\n      return [d, dp];\n    };\n  }\n\n  if (zeta === 1) {\n    const a = w0 - v0;\n    return (t) => {\n      const decay = Math.exp(-w0 * t);\n      return [decay * (1 + a * t), decay * (a - w0 * (1 + a * t))];\n    };\n  }\n\n  const root = w0 * Math.sqrt(zeta * zeta - 1);\n  const r1 = -zeta * w0 + root;\n  const r2 = -zeta * w0 - root;\n  const c1 = (-v0 - r2) / (r1 - r2);\n  const c2 = 1 - c1;\n  return (t) => {\n    const e1 = Math.exp(r1 * t);\n    const e2 = Math.exp(r2 * t);\n    return [c1 * e1 + c2 * e2, c1 * r1 * e1 + c2 * r2 * e2];\n  };\n}\n\nexport function springToLinear(\n  spring: SpringConfig = {},\n  options: LinearEasingOptions = {},\n): SpringEasing {\n  const {\n    stiffness = 100,\n    mass = 1,\n    velocity = 0,\n    restDelta = 0.005,\n    restSpeed = 0.1,\n  } = spring;\n  const { resolutionMs = 10, maxDurationMs = 10000, precision = 4 } = options;\n\n  const w0 = Math.sqrt(stiffness / mass);\n  // The critical damping coefficient. `bounce` is expressed against it, which\n  // is what makes \"bounce: 0\" mean exactly \"no overshoot\" for any stiffness.\n  const critical = 2 * Math.sqrt(stiffness * mass);\n  const damping =\n    spring.bounce === undefined\n      ? (spring.damping ?? 10)\n      : critical * (1 - clamp(spring.bounce, 0, 1 - MIN_RATIO));\n  const zeta = Math.max(damping / critical, MIN_RATIO);\n\n  const solve = makeSolver(w0, zeta, velocity);\n\n  // Scan for the first moment the spring is both close enough and slow enough.\n  // Distance alone is not sufficient: an underdamped spring passes through the\n  // target at full speed on every oscillation, and a distance-only test would\n  // declare it settled on the first crossing, half a bounce in.\n  const maxSeconds = maxDurationMs / 1000;\n  let settled = maxSeconds;\n  for (let t = 0; t <= maxSeconds; t += SCAN_STEP) {\n    const [d, dp] = solve(t);\n    if (Math.abs(d) < restDelta && Math.abs(dp) < restSpeed) {\n      settled = t;\n      break;\n    }\n  }\n  const durationMs = Math.max(Math.ceil(settled * 1000), 1);\n\n  const stops = clamp(Math.round(durationMs / resolutionMs), 2, MAX_STOPS);\n  const factor = 10 ** precision;\n  const samples: number[] = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < stops; i++) {\n    const t = (i / (stops - 1)) * (durationMs / 1000);\n    // Progress, not remaining distance. NOT clamped: values above 1 are the\n    // overshoot, and clamping them is exactly how a spring becomes an ease-out.\n    samples.push(Math.round((1 - solve(t)[0]) * factor) / factor);\n  }\n  // The scan stops within `restDelta` of the target, so the final sample is\n  // ~0.999. Left alone, every transition ends with a sub-pixel snap as the\n  // browser jumps from the last easing output to the real end value.\n  samples[stops - 1] = 1;\n\n  return {\n    easing: `linear(${samples.join(', ')})`,\n    // Declared first so a browser without linear() support keeps it: the\n    // unparseable longhand is dropped, the previous declaration survives.\n    fallback: CSS_EASING.outQuart,\n    durationMs,\n    samples,\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * `springToLinear` with memoisation and a reduced-motion variant.\n *\n * Reduced motion does not shorten the animation to nothing — it removes the\n * oscillation. The damping ratio is forced to 1 and the initial velocity to 0,\n * which keeps the spring's stiffness and therefore its pace, and produces a\n * monotonic curve that arrives once and stays.\n */\nexport function useSpringEasing(\n  spring: SpringConfig = {},\n  options: LinearEasingOptions = {},\n): SpringEasing {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { stiffness, damping, mass, bounce, velocity, restDelta, restSpeed } = spring;\n  const { resolutionMs, maxDurationMs, precision } = options;\n\n  return useMemo(\n    () =>\n      springToLinear(\n        reduced\n          ? { stiffness, mass, bounce: 0, velocity: 0, restDelta, restSpeed }\n          : { stiffness, damping, mass, bounce, velocity, restDelta, restSpeed },\n        { resolutionMs, maxDurationMs, precision },\n      ),\n    [\n      reduced,\n      stiffness,\n      damping,\n      mass,\n      bounce,\n      velocity,\n      restDelta,\n      restSpeed,\n      resolutionMs,\n      maxDurationMs,\n      precision,\n    ],\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: `springToLinear` is a pure function and `useSpringEasing` only calls it inside `useMemo`. No timers, listeners, or animation frames are created. The reduced-motion subscription belongs to `useReducedMotion`, which removes its own media-query listener on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useSpringEasing` recomputes with `bounce: 0` and `velocity: 0`, forcing the damping ratio to exactly 1. That removes the oscillation — the trigger for vestibular discomfort — while keeping the stiffness and therefore the pace, so the motion stays the same length and simply arrives once instead of settling. It is not shortened to zero: the curve is still a real ease, just monotonic, and the demo's peak readout drops to 100% to show it.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Clamping the samples to [0, 1]. This is the one mistake that destroys the whole technique: the overshoot lives in the values above 1, and a clamped spring is an ease-out with extra bytes. It looks like the maths is wrong when the maths is fine.\n- Reading the raw solution as progress. The closed form gives *remaining distance*, which starts at 1 and decays to 0; the easing needs `1 - d`. Emitting `d` directly produces an animation that runs backwards and every reviewer misdiagnoses it as an inverted easing.\n- Using one formula for all damping ratios. `sqrt(1 - zeta^2)` is imaginary above zeta = 1, so an overdamped spring evaluated with the underdamped branch yields NaN, and `linear(0, NaN, NaN)` fails to parse — the transition silently reverts to `ease` with nothing in the console.\n- Testing only distance for the settle time. An underdamped spring passes exactly through the target on every swing, so the scan stops on the first crossing and the emitted curve is cut off mid-bounce.\n- Leaving the final sample at its solved value. It is ~0.999, and the browser jumps from there to the real end value in one frame — a visible tick at the end of every transition that reads as a rendering bug.\n- Passing the `linear()` string through a CSS custom property. An unsupported value inside `var()` is invalid at computed-value time, which resolves the property to its *initial* value (`ease`) instead of falling back to the previous declaration. That silently defeats the two-declaration fallback in exactly the browsers that need it.\n- Declaring the fallback after `linear()`. Later declarations win, so a supporting browser also uses the cubic-bezier and the spring never appears anywhere.\n- Setting a duration by hand. Duration is an output of the spring, not an input; overriding it rescales the curve in time so a stiff spring animates slowly with a stiff shape, which looks wrong without looking obviously wrong.\n- Recomputing the easing every render. The scan runs up to 2400 solver evaluations, so an unmemoised call inside a frequently-rendering component is measurable main-thread work to produce a string that never changes.\n- Using this for anything interruptible. A `linear()` easing is a fixed curve of fixed length; a spring that must absorb a new target mid-flight, with its current velocity carried over, still needs a real per-frame integrator.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Deterministic and pure, so it is safe to precompute at build time and inline the string into a stylesheet — the runtime cost then drops to zero. `springToLinear` is exported separately from the hook for that reason.\n- Works for any animatable property, including ones a JavaScript spring handles badly: `linear()` on a `background-color` transition springs the colour without touching the main thread at all.\n- A `linear()` easing applies to Web Animations and CSS animations identically. Passing `easing` as the `easing` option of `element.animate()` gets the same compositor handoff.\n- The overshoot means the property leaves the 0-1 range, so a spring on `scale` momentarily exceeds the target scale. Anything clipped by an `overflow: hidden` parent should be sized with that headroom in mind — the demo's peak readout is the exact multiplier.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPress Run. The three squares cross their tracks and each one visibly passes its end position and settles back — Loose the most, Snap barely. The plotted curve beside each row rises above the dashed 100% line by the amount stated in the readout. Scroll or drag hard during the run and the motion does not hitch, because it is not on the main thread. Read the emitted string: it starts at 0, contains values greater than 1, and ends at exactly 1. Turn reduced motion on and re-run: every curve is monotonic, no square passes its end position, every peak reads 100%, and the durations are still hundreds of milliseconds rather than zero.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "stacked-card-fan": "# Build: Stacked Card Fan\n\nCards stacked on top of each other with a slight fan: the front card sits upright in the middle and the others peek out to its left and right, each tilted, lowered, shrunk and dimmed a little more the further back it is. Hovering or keyboard-focusing the stack spreads the fan so more of each card shows; clicking the front card, or pressing an arrow key, sends it round the back and brings the next one forward, and clicking a card behind brings that card forward directly. A \"See all\" pill under the stack spreads the cards almost flat for a full view.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA fanned stack of tilted cards: hover spreads the fan, clicking or arrow keys bring the next card forward, and a pill lays them all out.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria@^3.53.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Slots: each card gets a signed circular distance `d` from the front card, `((i − active + ⌊n/2⌋) mod n) − ⌊n/2⌋`, so five cards are ordered 0, +1, +2, −2, −1 around the front. Cycling shifts every `d` by one; the card leaving one end wraps to the other end at the back of the pile, where the higher cards hide the crossing.\n2. One transform per card, written once as CSS variables: `translateX(-50%) translateX(d·gap) translateY(|d|·drop) rotate(d·tilt) scale(1 − |d|·shrink)`. `--d` and `--ad` are set per card; `--spread`, `--tilt`, `--drop`, `--shrink` come from `data-state` (rest, peek, expanded) on the stage. Changing either recomputes the transform and a single 500ms `transform` transition animates it, so hover, cycle and expand share one path with no JS animation loop.\n3. Fit: the wrapper is a size container. Card width is `min(cardWidth, 80cqi)` and height keeps the aspect ratio. The gap is `max(12px, min(cardW · spread, (100cqi − cardW) / (2·⌊n/2⌋)))`, so the outermost card never passes the container edge, however wide the state asks it to spread. Expanded lays the cards in a row instead, which also needs them smaller: the container width from the kit `useResizeObserver` gives `scale = width / (card · (1 + (n − 1) · 0.86))` and a gap of `0.86 · card · scale`, passed in as `--base` and `--gap`. Scale is a plain number, and CSS cannot yet divide lengths into one in every browser.\n4. Depth: `z-index` is `n − |d|`, and a token-coloured veil inside each card (`bg-background-primary-default` at `0.24·|d|` opacity, capped at 0.6) dims cards behind. A veil rather than card opacity keeps cards behind from showing through each other in dark mode.\n5. Input: the stage is a focusable `role=\"group\"` with `aria-roledescription=\"card stack\"`. Arrow keys, Enter and Space cycle; Home and End jump. react-aria `useHover` (mouse only, so touch never sticks in peek) and `useFocusRing` (keyboard focus only) drive the peek state. Cards behind are `aria-hidden`, and a polite live region announces the new front card (\"Tokens, 1 of 5\").\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `rest / peek spread = 0.30 / 0.46 × card width`\n  Rest shows a sliver of each card; peek shows their headings.\n- `expanded step = 0.86 × scaled card width`\n  Cards sit in a row with 14% overlap, scaled so the whole row fits the container.\n- `tilt = 5° / 8° / 1.5° per slot`\n  The fan opens wider on hover; flat-ish when expanded so content reads.\n- `drop, shrink = 10px / 16px / 4px per slot; 4% per slot (0 expanded)`\n  Cards behind sit lower and smaller, which reads as depth without a 3D perspective.\n- `transition = 500ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`\n  A fast start and long settle makes the cycle feel like a flick.\n- `card size = 300 × 260px, 80cqi max`\n  Default shown in the reference; shrinks together with the aspect ratio below about 400px.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction StackedCardFan(props: { items: { id: string; label: string; content: ReactNode }[]; activeIndex?: number; defaultActiveIndex?: number; onActiveChange?: (index: number) => void; expanded?: boolean; defaultExpanded?: boolean; onExpandedChange?: (expanded: boolean) => void; showToggle?: boolean; expandLabel?: string; collapseLabel?: string; cardWidth?: number; cardHeight?: number; fade?: boolean; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: StackedCardFanItem[]`\n  `{ id, label, content }`; `label` is announced when the card comes forward.\n- `activeIndex: number`\n  Controlled front card.\n- `defaultActiveIndex: number` — default `0`\n  Initial front card.\n- `onActiveChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Called when the front card changes.\n- `expanded: boolean`\n  Controlled expanded state.\n- `defaultExpanded: boolean` — default `false`\n  Initial expanded state.\n- `showToggle: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the See all / Stack pill.\n- `expandLabel: string` — default `\"See all {n}\"`\n  Pill label while stacked.\n- `collapseLabel: string` — default `\"Stack cards\"`\n  Pill label while expanded.\n- `cardWidth: number` — default `300`\n  Card width in px.\n- `cardHeight: number` — default `260`\n  Card height in px.\n- `fade: boolean` — default `false`\n  Mask the bottom of the stack into the page.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Signed circular distance from the front card: for five cards and front 0,\n * the order is 0, 1, 2, -2, -1. The front card sits at 0, the rest alternate\n * to its right (+) and left (-), and cycling the front card makes the one\n * leaving the far end slip round behind the stack to the other end.\n */\nfunction slotOf(index: number, active: number, count: number) {\n  const half = Math.floor(count / 2);\n  return ((((index - active + half) % count) + count) % count) - half;\n}\n\n/**\n * Every card has one transform made of CSS variables: `--d` (its slot) and\n * `--ad` (|slot|) are set per card, while `--spread`, `--tilt`, `--drop`,\n * `--shrink` are set by the stack state (rest, peek on hover/focus, expanded). A change of\n * either re-computes the transform and a single CSS transition animates it, so\n * hover, cycling and expanding all share one code path with no JS animation.\n * The horizontal gap is capped by the container so the outer cards never\n * leave it: half * gap + card / 2 <= 50cqi.\n */\nconst CARD_TRANSFORM =\n  \"translateX(-50%) translateX(calc(var(--d) * var(--gap))) translateY(calc(var(--ad) * var(--drop))) rotate(calc(var(--d) * var(--tilt))) scale(calc(var(--base) * (1 - var(--ad) * var(--shrink))))\";\n\nconst GAP = \"max(12px, min(calc(var(--card-w) * var(--spread)), calc((100cqi - var(--card-w)) / var(--span))))\";\n\n/** Each expanded card advances this fraction of a card width past the one before (14% overlap). */\nconst EXPANDED_STEP = 0.86;\n\n/**\n * Expanded lays every card out in a row, so cards must also shrink: the scale\n * that makes card + (n - 1) * step * card fit the measured width. Scale is a\n * plain number, which CSS cannot derive from lengths everywhere yet, so it is\n * computed here from the container's layout width.\n */\nfunction expandedLayout(width: number, cardWidth: number, count: number) {\n  const card = Math.min(cardWidth, width * 0.8);\n  const scale = Math.min(1, width / (card * (1 + (count - 1) * EXPANDED_STEP)));\n  return { scale, gap: card * scale * EXPANDED_STEP };\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Motion is a CSS transition with no loop to stop. The shared ResizeObserver unobserves the wrapper on unmount, and react-aria removes its hover and focus listeners itself.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Card transforms and the dimming veil change instantly (`motion-reduce:transition-none`); hover, cycling and expanding still work. The step spinner in the sample faces stops.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `inert` on the cards behind: inert elements are skipped by hit testing, so clicks on them fall through and they cannot be brought forward. Use `aria-hidden` and keep the card faces non-interactive.\n- Declaring `--gap` on an element above the stage: unregistered custom properties substitute their `var()` references where they are declared, so a `--gap` that reads `--spread` must sit on the element that sets `--spread`.\n- Fading cards behind with `opacity` on the whole card: they become see-through and stack into a muddle in dark mode. Dim with an opaque-coloured veil instead.\n- Driving peek from `onMouseEnter`: a tap on touch devices fires it and never the leave, leaving the fan open. react-aria `useHover` ignores emulated mouse events.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `stacked-card-fan-cards.tsx` holds five agent-dashboard card faces (tokens chart, activity calendar, requests bars, usage meters, step list) and `AGENT_DASHBOARD_CARDS`, the array the demo and the CTA Section use.\n- With `fade` the lower part of the stack is masked into the page, as in a hero visual.\n- Active card and expanded state are both controllable (`activeIndex`/`onActiveChange`, `expanded`/`onExpandedChange`).\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFive cards rest in a slight fan with the Tokens chart upright in front. Hovering spreads them and shows each heading; clicking the front card sends it round the back and brings September forward; clicking a card behind brings it forward; the arrow keys do the same with a visible focus ring. See all five lays the cards out nearly flat and Stack cards gathers them. At 360px the cards shrink and the fan stays inside the frame. Light and dark both read.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "stage-bars-card": "# Build: Stage Bars Card\n\nA dashboard card that lists funnel stages as rounded pills over the chart track: name on the left, value and share of the top stage on the right, a 14px icon at the start of each pill. The pills grow to their shares on mount and on every period change. Hovering, focusing or tapping a stage fades the others and swaps the count-up headline to that stage with its step conversion. With `stacked`, each pill splits into its breakdown parts with a legend, and hovering a part (or arrowing through it) shows that part's value and its share of the stage.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA funnel as rounded stage pills with value and share, optional stacked parts with a legend, stat tiles and a headline that follows hover.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The rows live in one CSS grid, `max-content | minmax(0,1fr) | max-content`, and every row is a `<button>` spanning it with `grid-cols-subgrid`. So the name column sizes to the longest stage name and all pills start on the same line.\n2. Each pill is an absolutely positioned span inside a `bg-chart-track` track. Its width is `share * 100%`, where share is `value / top` tweened by `useTweenedValues` (520ms ease-out cubic, from 0 on mount and from the current shares on a new period). With an icon, the pill keeps a 20px minimum so the icon always sits on colour.\n3. Stacked, the pill becomes a flex row with a 2px gap. Each part is `flex-basis: 0` with `flex-grow` set to its value, so the parts always fill exactly the pill. When a new period changes the mix, `flex-grow` moves through a 300ms CSS transition.\n4. The legend is built from the parts' labels in first-seen order. Each series takes the next palette colour (lime, blue, purple, …) unless a part sets `color`. In mono, the series step down in opacity (1, 0.75, 0.5, floor 0.4) instead.\n5. The target is `{ stage, segment | null }`. Pointer enter and focus on a row set the stage; pointer enter on a part sets the part, and leaving it goes back to the stage. On a focused stacked row, ArrowRight/ArrowLeft walk through the parts and back to the whole stage. Click toggles a pin (`aria-pressed`) that holds the stage when nothing is hovered.\n6. The header shows the title, the top value and the delta chip at rest. A stage shows its name, its value and \"58% of Sign-up\" (step conversion) or \"Top of funnel\". A part shows \"Sign-up · Paid\", its value and \"32% of Sign-up\". The number counts up in a `<p>` keyed by the target, so the fade replays on every swap.\n7. Inactive rows and tiles fade to 0.4 and 0.45 opacity over 200ms. Within a hovered stacked row the other parts fade to 0.35, and the active fill switches to the darker `--color-chart-N-active` tone (text-primary in mono).\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pill = 20px tall (h-5), rounded-full, rows 28px + 4px gap`\n  Matches the preview's pitch: six stages fit in about 190px, with room for a 14px icon and a hit area tall enough to hover.\n- `ICON_PILL_MIN = 20px`\n  A 3% stage would otherwise be a 7px sliver with its icon over the track; on the dark track the near-black icon would vanish.\n- `tween = 520ms ease-out cubic, from 0 on mount`\n  Shares are tweened rather than widths in px, so resizing the card never animates and a new period starts from wherever the bars are.\n- `part transition = flex-grow, background-color and opacity over 300ms`\n  Re-proportioning the parts at the same pace as the bar width keeps a period change reading as one motion.\n- `monoOpacity = 1 − 0.25 × series index, floor 0.4`\n  With one ink, stacked parts still need to read as different series; three steps stay apart on both themes.\n- `tiles = 3 per row, 120px minimum`\n  Six stages make two even rows, as in the preview; a 360px card wraps to two per row.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction StageBarsCard(props: { title?: string; ranges?: StageRange[]; stages?: BarStage[]; delta?: number; range?: string; defaultRange?: string; onRangeChange?: (id: string) => void; stacked?: boolean; mono?: boolean; showIcons?: boolean; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype BarStage = { label: string; value: number; color?: ChartColor; icon?: ReactNode; segments?: StageSegment[] }\ntype StageSegment = { label: string; value: number; color?: ChartColor }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Pipeline\"`\n  Header label while nothing is active; also the card's accessible name.\n- `ranges: StageRange[]` — default `PIPELINE_RANGES`\n  Periods with their own stages, delta and optional headline. Two or more show the period select.\n- `stages: BarStage[]`\n  A single stage list, top first. Overrides `ranges` and hides the select.\n- `delta: number`\n  Headline chip percentage when `stages` is used.\n- `range: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultRange: string` — default `first range id`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onRangeChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the period select changes.\n- `stacked: boolean` — default `false`\n  Splits each pill into its stage's `segments` and shows a legend. Tile swatches are hidden, since colour then means the part.\n- `mono: boolean` — default `false`\n  One ink for every pill (secondary text colour, primary when active) and no tile swatches.\n- `showIcons: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draws each stage's `icon` at the left end inside its pill.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `en-US integer`\n  Formats the headline, row values, tiles and the accessible labels.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * One stage: a subgrid row of [name | pill on the track | value + share].\n * The pill's width is the tweened share of the top stage. Stacked, it is a\n * flex row of parts whose `flex-grow` is their value, so the parts always fill\n * exactly the pill and re-proportion with a CSS transition on a new period.\n */\nfunction StageRow({\n  stage,\n  index,\n  share,\n  topLabel,\n  topValue,\n  series,\n  mono,\n  showIcons,\n  active,\n  pinned,\n  formatValue,\n  onTarget,\n  onTogglePin,\n}: StageRowProps) {\n  const parts = series.length > 0 ? partsOf(stage) : [];\n  const isActive = active?.stage === index;\n  const activePart = isActive ? (active?.segment ?? null) : null;\n  const color = CHART_COLORS[chartColorAt(index, stage.color)];\n  const solid = mono ? (isActive ? \"var(--color-text-primary)\" : MONO_FILL) : isActive ? color.active : color.fill;\n  const icon = showIcons && stage.icon ? stage.icon : null;\n  const percent = pct(stage.value, topValue);\n\n  const onKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) => {\n    if (parts.length === 0 || (event.key !== \"ArrowRight\" && event.key !== \"ArrowLeft\")) return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n    const last = parts.length - 1;\n    // Right walks 0..last then back to the whole stage; Left walks the other way.\n    const next =\n      event.key === \"ArrowRight\"\n        ? activePart === null ? 0 : activePart < last ? activePart + 1 : null\n        : activePart === null ? last : activePart > 0 ? activePart - 1 : null;\n    onTarget({ stage: index, segment: next });\n  };\n\n  const breakdown = parts.map((p) => `${p.label} ${formatValue(p.value)}`).join(\", \");\n\n  return (\n    <button\n      type=\"button\"\n      aria-pressed={pinned}\n      aria-label={`${stage.label}: ${formatValue(stage.value)}, ${percent}% of ${topLabel}${breakdown ? `. ${breakdown}` : \"\"}`}\n      aria-keyshortcuts={parts.length > 0 ? \"ArrowLeft ArrowRight\" : undefined}\n      onPointerEnter={() => onTarget({ stage: index, segment: null })}\n      onFocus={() => onTarget({ stage: index, segment: null })}\n      onBlur={() => onTarget(null)}\n      onClick={onTogglePin}\n      onKeyDown={onKeyDown}\n      className={cx(\n        \"col-span-3 grid cursor-pointer grid-cols-subgrid items-center rounded-md py-1 text-left outline-none\",\n        \"transition-opacity duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n        \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n        active !== null && !isActive && \"opacity-40\",\n      )}\n    >\n      <span className=\"text-right text-body-regular whitespace-nowrap text-text-secondary\">{stage.label}</span>\n      <span className=\"relative h-5 min-w-0 rounded-full bg-chart-track\">\n        <span\n          className=\"absolute inset-y-0 left-0 flex gap-0.5 overflow-hidden rounded-full\"\n          style={{\n            width: `${Math.max(0, share) * 100}%`,\n            minWidth: icon && stage.value > 0 ? ICON_PILL_MIN : undefined,\n          }}\n        >\n          {parts.length > 0 ? (\n            parts.map((part, partIndex) => {\n              const seriesIndex = series.findIndex((s) => s.label === part.label);\n              const tone = CHART_COLORS[series[seriesIndex]?.color ?? \"lime\"];\n              const lit = activePart === partIndex;\n              return (\n                <span\n                  key={part.label}\n                  data-part={part.label}\n                  onPointerEnter={() => onTarget({ stage: index, segment: partIndex })}\n                  onPointerLeave={() => onTarget({ stage: index, segment: null })}\n                  className=\"h-full min-w-0 basis-0 transition-[flex-grow,background-color,opacity] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n                  style={{\n                    flexGrow: part.value,\n                    backgroundColor: mono ? (lit ? \"var(--color-text-primary)\" : MONO_FILL) : lit ? tone.active : tone.fill,\n                    opacity: activePart !== null && !lit ? 0.35 : mono ? monoOpacity(seriesIndex) : 1,\n                  }}\n                />\n              );\n            })\n          ) : (\n            <span\n              className=\"h-full flex-1 transition-[background-color] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n              style={{ backgroundColor: solid }}\n            />\n          )}\n        </span>\n        {icon ? (\n          <span\n            aria-hidden\n            className=\"pointer-events-none absolute top-1/2 left-[3px] flex size-3.5 -translate-y-1/2 items-center justify-center text-neutral-950\"\n          >\n            {icon}\n          </span>\n        ) : null}\n      </span>\n      <span className=\"flex items-baseline justify-end gap-1.5 tabular-nums\">\n        <span className=\"text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{formatValue(stage.value)}</span>\n        <span className=\"min-w-[4ch] text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">{percent}%</span>\n      </span>\n    </button>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The width tween and `useCountUp` cancel their animation frame on every new target and on unmount. Hover, pin and keyboard targets are plain React state; there are no listeners outside React.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `useTweenedValues` is disabled, so the pills render at their final widths on mount and jump on a period change. The parts' flex-grow/colour/opacity transitions and the row fades are removed with `motion-reduce:transition-none`. The headline count runs over 1ms, and the number fade is off.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sizing stacked parts as percentages of the track. Their sum then drifts from the pill width as soon as the 2px gaps or the icon minimum come in. Flex-grow by value always fills the pill exactly.\n- Putting the label, bar and numbers in separate flex rows. Each row then sizes its own name column and the pills start at different x positions. A shared grid with subgrid rows keeps one column for every row.\n- Making stacked parts separate tab stops. Six stages × three parts is 18 stops; arrow keys on the focused stage reach the same detail with one stop per row.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `PIPELINE_RANGES`, `BarStage`, `StageSegment` and `StageRange` are exported. Pass `stages` (and `delta`) for one static list without the period select.\n- Stages share the funnel's shape (`label`, `value`, `color`) plus `icon` and `segments`, so the same data can drive the Funnel Chart Card.\n- The frame, header, period select and stat tiles come from `funnel-chart-card-shell`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Pipeline\", \"1,180\" and a lime \"+2.4%\" chip. Six rows follow: Visits 1,180 100%, Sign-up 790 67%, Active 460 39%, Pro 250 21%, Team 120 10% and Enterprise 40 3%, with lime, blue, purple, pink, yellow and emerald pills and icons. Six tiles sit three per row. Hovering Active fades the other rows and the header reads \"Active\", \"460\" and \"58% of Sign-up\". With \"By channel\", each pill splits into three parts with an Organic / Paid / Referral legend. Hovering Sign-up's second part reads \"Sign-up · Paid\", \"253\" and \"32% of Sign-up\". \"Last 30 days\" grows the pills and the headline counts to 4,820.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "stat-cards": "# Build: Stat Cards\n\nThe KPI row at the top of a dashboard, driven by an array of `Stat` objects. `variant=\"plain\"` is a compact 132px card with an icon tile, a label, the value and a delta `Chip`. `variant=\"footer\"` adds a tinted gradient icon tile, an optional info tooltip, a display-size value and an inner footer band with a comparison caption and a delta pill. The grid reflows from one or two columns up to four, and `count` and `columns` cut it down for narrower hosts.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA responsive row of KPI cards in two looks: a compact tile with a delta chip, or a gradient icon tile over a caption band and delta pill.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `StatCards` picks `stats`, or the demo set that matches the variant (`DEFAULT_STATS` or `DEFAULT_FOOTER_STATS`), slices it to `count` and renders one card per entry keyed by `label`. The wrapper is a `grid w-full gap-4`.\n2. Columns are set per variant. `columns={1}` is always `grid-cols-1`. Otherwise plain cards start at `grid-cols-2` and footer cards at `grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2`, because a display-size value does not fit two-up on a phone. With `columns={4}` plain cards go four-up at `lg` and footer cards at `xl`.\n3. Delta colour is data, not maths. Each stat carries `deltaColor: \"lime\" | \"rose\" | \"neutral\"`, and `DELTA_STYLES` maps it to a glyph (`RiArrowUpCircleFill`, `RiArrowDownCircleFill`, `RiIndeterminateCircleFill`), a text colour and a pill background. A rise that is bad news, such as refunds going up, can therefore read rose.\n4. The plain card is a `section` with `h-[132px]`, `justify-between`, `rounded-2xl`, `bg-background-secondary-default` and `p-4`. The icon sits in a `bg-stat-card-icon-background` tile, the value is `text-title-1-medium`, and the delta is a `Chip variant=\"bold\"` in the stat's colour. The value and chip row is `flex-wrap`, so a narrow card drops the chip below the number instead of overflowing.\n5. The footer card is a `p-2` section in three parts. A header holds a 40px `rounded-2lg` icon tile filled with `bg-linear-to-b` from the `TILE_TONES[tone]` stops (blue by default), with a white glyph. The body holds a truncated label over a `text-display-4-medium tabular-nums` value. The band is an inner tile (`bg-background-inner-default shadow-card rounded-2lg`, `mt-auto`) holding the caption (default \"From last month\") and the `DeltaPill`.\n6. When `hint` is set, `StatHint` renders a bare 20px info button inside React Aria's `TooltipTrigger` (200ms delay) wrapped in `Focusable`. The open state is controlled, so the tooltip opens on hover and focus as usual and the button's `onClick` also toggles it, because a glyph that small gets tapped. The button is labelled \"About {label}\".\n7. Every subcomponent returns its JSX through `useTemplateCopy()`. With no `TemplateCopyProvider` this returns the node unchanged. With one, labels, captions and hints are translated while values and IDs are left alone.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `plain card height = h-[132px]`\n  Fixed so a row of cards lines up whether or not a chip wraps. `justify-between` pins the icon to the top and the label and value to the bottom, so the row reads as one band across the dashboard.\n- `footer icon tile = size-10 rounded-2lg, gradient from-*-500 to-*-600 (orange and sky use 400 to 500)`\n  A top-to-bottom two-step gradient gives the tile a lit edge without an image. The tones are fixed Tailwind palette stops rather than theme tokens, so the tile reads the same on light and dark surfaces.\n- `tooltip delay = 200ms`\n  Long enough that sweeping the pointer across a row of cards does not flash four tooltips, short enough that a deliberate hover feels immediate. A click skips the delay altogether.\n- `breakpoints = plain lg:grid-cols-4, footer sm:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-4`\n  The footer card's `display-4` value needs roughly a third more width than the plain card's `title-1`, so it reaches four-up one breakpoint later and stays single-column below `sm`.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction StatCards(props?: { variant?: \"plain\" | \"footer\"; stats?: Stat[]; count?: number; columns?: 1 | 2 | 4; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype Stat = { icon: IconComponent; label: string; value: string; delta: string; deltaColor: \"lime\" | \"rose\" | \"neutral\"; tone?: StatTone; caption?: string; hint?: string }\ntype StatTone = \"blue\" | \"orange\" | \"purple\" | \"pink\" | \"sky\" | \"emerald\"\n```\n\n- `variant: \"plain\" | \"footer\"` — default `\"plain\"`\n  Compact tile with a delta chip, or the gradient-tile card with a caption band and delta pill.\n- `stats: Stat[]`\n  Cards to render. Defaults to demo metrics that match the variant.\n- `count: number`\n  Render only the first `count` stats.\n- `columns: 1 | 2 | 4` — default `4`\n  Column count at the widest breakpoint. `2` stays two-up, `1` pins a single column at every width.\n- `Stat.deltaColor: \"lime\" | \"rose\" | \"neutral\"`\n  Picks the chip or pill colour and the up, down or flat glyph.\n- `Stat.tone: StatTone` — default `\"blue\"`\n  Footer variant only: the icon tile gradient.\n- `Stat.hint: string`\n  Footer variant only: adds an info button whose tooltip shows this text.\n- `Stat.caption: string` — default `\"From last month\"`\n  Footer variant only: the comparison caption in the band.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction FooterStatCard({ stat }: { stat: Stat }) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  return localize((\n    <section className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col rounded-2xl bg-background-secondary-default p-2\">\n      {/* Icon tile + optional info glyph, both hanging from the same top inset */}\n      <div className=\"flex w-full items-start justify-between gap-2.5 p-2\">\n        <span\n          className={cx(\n            \"flex size-10 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-2lg bg-linear-to-b\",\n            TILE_TONES[stat.tone ?? \"blue\"],\n          )}\n        >\n          <stat.icon className=\"size-5 shrink-0 text-white\" aria-hidden />\n        </span>\n        {stat.hint && <StatHint label={stat.label} hint={stat.hint} />}\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Label sits directly over the number, as on the plain cards */}\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-0.5 px-2 pt-2.5 pb-3.5\">\n        <p className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">{stat.label}</p>\n        <p className=\"text-display-4-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums\">\n          {stat.value}\n        </p>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Footer band: comparison caption + delta pill on an inner tile */}\n      <div className=\"mt-auto flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-2 rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default py-1.5 pe-1.5 ps-2.5 shadow-card\">\n        <p className=\"truncate text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">\n          {stat.caption ?? \"From last month\"}\n        </p>\n        <DeltaPill delta={stat.delta} deltaColor={stat.deltaColor} />\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  ));\n}\n\nexport function StatCards({\n  variant = \"plain\",\n  stats,\n  count,\n  columns = 4,\n  className,\n}: {\n  variant?: StatCardsVariant;\n  /** KPI cards to render; defaults to demo metrics matching the variant. */\n  stats?: Stat[];\n  /** How many KPI cards to render (from the start of the list). */\n  count?: number;\n  /** Columns at the widest breakpoint - 2 keeps the grid two-up for\n   *  narrower hosts (docs previews, split layouts), 1 pins a single\n   *  column at every width. */\n  columns?: 1 | 2 | 4;\n  className?: string;\n} = {}) {\n  const localize = useTemplateCopy();\n  const items =\n    stats ?? (variant === \"footer\" ? DEFAULT_FOOTER_STATS : DEFAULT_STATS);\n  return localize((\n    <div\n      className={cx(\n        \"grid w-full gap-4\",\n        // The footer cards carry a display-size value, so they go one per\n        // row on phones where the plain cards still fit two up.\n        columns === 1\n          ? \"grid-cols-1\"\n          : variant === \"footer\"\n            ? \"grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2\"\n            : \"grid-cols-2\",\n        columns === 4 && (variant === \"footer\" ? \"xl:grid-cols-4\" : \"lg:grid-cols-4\"),\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {items.slice(0, count ?? items.length).map((stat) =>\n        variant === \"footer\" ? (\n          <FooterStatCard key={stat.label} stat={stat} />\n        ) : (\n          <PlainStatCard key={stat.label} stat={stat} />\n        ),\n      )}\n    </div>\n  ));\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up in the cards. The tooltip is a React Aria overlay, and React Aria removes its portal and listeners when it closes or unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The cards do not move. The only motion is the tooltip's 200ms blur and scale-in, a CSS transition on React Aria's `data-entering` and `data-exiting` states. It is short and does not travel, so it is kept under reduced motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Deriving the delta colour from the sign of the number. Whether a change is good depends on the metric. Pass `deltaColor` with the data, as the default refunds card does.\n- Removing `Focusable` around the info button. React Aria's `TooltipTrigger` hands its hover and focus handlers to its child through a context, and a plain `<button>` does not read that context, so neither hover nor focus would open the tooltip.\n- Making the tooltip hover-only. The info glyph is 20px and touch users tap it, which is why `onClick` toggles the controlled `open` state as well.\n- Giving cards a fixed width so the values line up. The grid sizes them, and the values use `tabular-nums`. A fixed width clips \"$152,313.92\" at the `display-4` size.\n- Keying cards by array index. They are keyed by `label`, so a reordered `stats` array keeps tooltip state with the right card. Labels must be unique.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `count` slices from the start of the list. Together with `columns={1}` it makes a single-card preview or sidebar summary.\n- The tone palette (`blue`, `orange`, `purple`, `pink`, `sky`, `emerald`) only affects the footer variant. The plain variant always uses the neutral `stat-card-icon-background` tile.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe footer variant shows a blue revenue card and an orange orders card side by side, each with a white glyph on a gradient tile, a large tabular value and a band reading \"From last month\" with a lime up-arrow pill. Hovering or clicking the info glyph opens a tooltip with the hint after about 200ms. The plain variant shows four 132px cards whose chips are lime, rose and neutral. Below `sm` the footer cards stack in one column while the plain cards stay two-up.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "status-dot": "# Build: Status Dot\n\nTwo nested spans: a 12px round halo in a tinted status colour and a 6px solid dot centred inside it. It is the marker the kit puts in front of a purchase status (\"Completed\", \"Waiting\", \"Processing\") in select options and data-table cells. It is decorative by design, so the text beside it carries the meaning.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA 12px status indicator: a solid 6px dot centred on a tinted halo, in green, yellow and indigo, for selects and table cells.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Keep the colour classes in one `sortCx` map with three keys: `base` (`inline-flex size-3 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full`), `halo` (one semantic token per colour) and `dot` (one palette class per colour).\n2. Render an outer `<span>` with `base` plus `styles.halo[color]`. `size-3` makes it 12px and `shrink-0` stops a flex row (a select option, a table cell) from squeezing the circle into an oval.\n3. Render an inner `<span className=\"size-1.5 rounded-full\">` with `styles.dot[color]`: a 6px solid dot, centred by the outer flex box, painted with the plain palette steps `bg-green-500`, `bg-yellow-500` and `bg-indigo-500`.\n4. Paint the halo with the semantic tokens `bg-status-dot-{green,yellow,indigo}-halo`. Light maps them to green-100, yellow-200 and indigo-100. Dark maps them to the 800 step mixed to 40% with `transparent`, so only the halo dims while the centre dot stays solid.\n5. Set `aria-hidden` on the outer span and spread the remaining props after it, so the dot is skipped by screen readers unless a caller explicitly overrides it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `sizes = halo size-3 (12px), dot size-1.5 (6px)`\n  From the Figma status-dropdown dots (node 3731:3266). A 3px ring of halo around the dot is wide enough to read as a glow at body-text size without growing past the 12px icon slot of a select option.\n- `light halos = green-100, yellow-200, indigo-100`\n  Yellow is one step darker than the other two because yellow-100 nearly vanishes on a white surface.\n- `dark halos = color-mix(in srgb, <hue>-800 40%, transparent)`\n  A translucent dark halo sits on any dark surface (row hover, selected option) without a visible light disc around the dot.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ninterface StatusDotProps extends HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement> { color?: \"green\" | \"yellow\" | \"indigo\"; ref?: Ref<HTMLSpanElement> }\nfunction StatusDot(props: StatusDotProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `color: \"green\" | \"yellow\" | \"indigo\"` — default `\"green\"`\n  Picks the halo token and the solid dot colour together.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer halo span through `tailwind-merge`.\n- `ref: Ref<HTMLSpanElement>`\n  Forwarded to the outer span (React 19 ref-as-prop).\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst styles = sortCx({\n  base: \"inline-flex size-3 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full\",\n  halo: {\n    green: \"bg-status-dot-green-halo\",\n    yellow: \"bg-status-dot-yellow-halo\",\n    indigo: \"bg-status-dot-indigo-halo\",\n  },\n  dot: {\n    green: \"bg-green-500\",\n    yellow: \"bg-yellow-500\",\n    indigo: \"bg-indigo-500\",\n  },\n});\n\nexport function StatusDot({ color = \"green\", className, ref, ...props }: StatusDotProps) {\n  return (\n    <span\n      ref={ref}\n      aria-hidden\n      className={cx(styles.base, styles.halo[color], className)}\n      {...props}\n    >\n      <span className={cx(\"size-1.5 rounded-full\", styles.dot[color])} />\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component is stateless markup with no listeners, timers or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing animates, so the dot renders identically under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not use the dot as the only signal of a status. It is `aria-hidden`, so always render the status label next to it.\n- Do not remove `shrink-0`. In a tight flex row the halo collapses into an ellipse.\n- Do not paint the halo with a raw palette class such as `bg-green-100`. The semantic token is what switches to the translucent dark-mode halo.\n- Do not add a colour that is not in both maps. `styles.halo[color]` and `styles.dot[color]` are indexed by the same key, and a missing key renders an invisible dot.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Used inside the kit by `select` (option markers) and `data-table` (the purchase-status column).\n- Only three colours exist because the source design defines only three status pairs. Add a fourth by adding a halo token to both themes and a key to both maps.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nEach of the three colours renders a 12px circle with a 6px solid dot exactly centred in it. The halo is a pale tint of the dot in light mode and a translucent dark tint in dark mode, while the dot itself keeps the same 500-step colour. The outer span has `aria-hidden`, and the circle stays round inside a narrow flex row.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "status-select-cell": "# Build: Status Select Cell\n\nThe coloured status badge from BoardUI table rows (\"Recovering\", \"Under observation\", \"Critical\") turned into its own editor. It is a React Aria Select whose trigger wears the kit Chip colours, so the cell still reads as a static pill; hovering or focusing reveals a chevron, and the list shows each status as its own pill with a check on the current one. Arrow keys, type-ahead, Enter and Escape come from the listbox.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTable status pill in status-* colours that is also a select: hover reveals a chevron, press or Enter opens the list to change it.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Value is controlled (value + onChange) or uncontrolled (defaultValue); the current option is looked up in options by id and its color picks the bg/text pair from the kit CHIP_COLORS map (status-*-background / status-*-text tokens).\n2. A React Aria Select wraps an AriaButton styled as the pill (rounded-md, py-0.5, ps-1.5, text-body-medium) whose SelectValue renders only the option label, so the trigger's accessible name is \"<label> <aria-label>\", e.g. \"Critical Status for Livia Saris\".\n3. A ChevronDownSmall sits at the end of the pill collapsed to width 0 and opacity 0; on data-hovered or data-focus-visible (and always on hover:none touch screens or while open) it grows to 14px wide at 0.7 opacity, 2px from the label and pulled 4px into the end padding, and rotates 180deg while open.\n4. The Popover is isNonModal like the kit Select, so it never locks <html> scroll; the kit useDismissOnOutsidePress and useTriggerToggle hooks restore outside-press closing and trigger toggling with a controlled isOpen.\n5. Each ListBoxItem renders the status pill and an optional description as React Aria Text slots (label / description, so the option is named by its label and described by the line), plus a RiCheckLine that is visible only when isSelected; choosing the current status is ignored, any other calls onChange(id) and the listbox returns focus to the pill.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover width = w-60 (240px), offset 6px`\n  Fits the longest pill (\"Under observation\") plus a one-line description and the check column.\n- `chevron = w-0 -> w-3.5 (ms-0.5, -me-1), opacity 0 -> 0.7`\n  At rest the pill is as tight as a static badge; on hover the chevron signals \"editable\" without turning every row into a form field.\n- `hover ring = ring-1 ring-current/25`\n  Derived from the pill's own text colour, so the hover outline matches every status tone in light and dark.\n- `focus ring = ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-1`\n  The kit focus treatment; the offset keeps it legible against tinted pills.\n- `list height = max-h-[280px]`\n  Five statuses with descriptions fit without scrolling; longer workflows scroll inside the surface.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function StatusSelectCell<K extends string = string>({ options, value, defaultValue, onChange, \"aria-label\", size, chevron, placeholder, isDisabled, placement, className, popoverClassName }: StatusSelectCellProps<K>): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `options: readonly StatusOption<K>[]`\n  Each { id, label, color: ChipColor, description? }.\n- `value / defaultValue: K`\n  Selected status id, controlled or initial.\n- `onChange: (value: K) => void`\n  Called when a different status is picked.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `'Status'`\n  Accessible name; include the row, e.g. \"Status for Ana Carter\".\n- `size: 'md' | 'sm'` — default `'md'`\n  Body (14px) or caption (12px) pill text.\n- `chevron: 'hover' | 'always' | 'none'` — default `'hover'`\n  When the dropdown chevron is shown.\n- `placeholder: ReactNode` — default `'Set status'`\n  Shown in a dashed pill when nothing matches.\n- `isDisabled: boolean` — default `false`\n  Renders the pill at 60% opacity and blocks opening.\n- `placement: 'bottom start' | 'bottom end' | 'top start' | 'top end'` — default `'bottom start'`\n  Popover placement relative to the pill.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function StatusSelectCell<K extends string = string>({\n  options,\n  value,\n  defaultValue,\n  onChange,\n  'aria-label': ariaLabel = 'Status',\n  size = 'md',\n  chevron = 'hover',\n  placeholder = 'Set status',\n  isDisabled,\n  placement = 'bottom start',\n  className,\n  popoverClassName,\n}: StatusSelectCellProps<K>) {\n  const [inner, setInner] = useState<K | undefined>(defaultValue);\n  const current = value ?? inner;\n  const option = options.find((o) => o.id === current);\n\n  // Same non-modal recipe as the kit Select: no scroll lock on <html>, with\n  // outside-press dismissal and trigger toggling restored by the kit hooks.\n  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);\n  const popoverRef = useRef<HTMLElement>(null);\n  const refs = useMemo(() => [triggerRef, popoverRef], []);\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\n  const close = useCallback(() => setIsOpen(false), []);\n  useDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, close, refs);\n  const allowOpenChange = useTriggerToggle(isOpen, triggerRef);\n\n  const select = (key: Key | null) => {\n    if (key === null) return;\n    const next = options.find((o) => o.id === String(key));\n    if (!next || next.id === current) return;\n    setInner(next.id);\n    onChange?.(next.id);\n  };\n\n  const showChevron = chevron !== 'none';\n\n  return (\n    <AriaSelect\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      selectedKey={current ?? null}\n      onSelectionChange={select}\n      isOpen={isOpen}\n      onOpenChange={(open) => allowOpenChange(open) && setIsOpen(open)}\n      isDisabled={isDisabled}\n      className={cx('inline-flex max-w-full', className)}\n    >\n      <AriaButton\n        ref={triggerRef}\n        data-status={current}\n        className={cx(\n          'group/status inline-flex max-w-full cursor-pointer items-center whitespace-nowrap outline-none',\n          'transition-[box-shadow,background-color] duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n          'data-[hovered]:ring-1 data-[hovered]:ring-current/25',\n          'data-[focus-visible]:ring-2 data-[focus-visible]:ring-border-focus-ring data-[focus-visible]:ring-offset-1 data-[focus-visible]:ring-offset-background-primary-default',\n          'data-[disabled]:cursor-not-allowed data-[disabled]:opacity-60',\n          PILL_SIZE[size],\n          option ? CHIP_COLORS[option.color] : 'border border-dashed border-border-button-default text-text-tertiary',\n          isOpen && 'ring-1 ring-current/25',\n        )}\n      >\n        <AriaSelectValue className=\"truncate\">{() => (option ? option.label : placeholder)}</AriaSelectValue>\n        {showChevron ? (\n          <ChevronDownSmall\n            className={cx(\n              'h-3.5 shrink-0 transition-[width,margin,opacity,rotate] duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n              chevron === 'always' || isOpen ? CHEVRON_SHOWN : CHEVRON_ON_HOVER,\n              isOpen && 'rotate-180',\n            )}\n          />\n        ) : null}\n      </AriaButton>\n      <AriaPopover\n        ref={popoverRef}\n        isNonModal\n        offset={6}\n        placement={placement}\n        className={cx(\n          MENU_POPOVER_SURFACE,\n          'w-60 p-1.5',\n          'motion-reduce:transition-opacity motion-reduce:data-[entering]:scale-100 motion-reduce:data-[entering]:blur-none',\n          'motion-reduce:data-[exiting]:scale-100 motion-reduce:data-[exiting]:blur-none',\n          popoverClassName,\n        )}\n      >\n        <AriaListBox items={options} className={cx(MENU_ITEMS_CONTAINER, 'max-h-[280px] overflow-auto')}>\n          {(item) => (\n            <AriaListBoxItem\n              id={item.id}\n              textValue={item.label}\n              className={({ isFocused }) => cx(MENU_ITEM, 'items-start gap-2 p-1.5', isFocused && MENU_ITEM_ACTIVE)}\n            >\n              {({ isSelected }) => (\n                <>\n                  <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col items-start gap-1\">\n                    <AriaText\n                      slot=\"label\"\n                      elementType=\"span\"\n                      className={cx('rounded-md px-1.5 py-0.5', size === 'sm' ? 'text-caption-1-medium' : 'text-body-medium', CHIP_COLORS[item.color])}\n                    >\n                      {item.label}\n                    </AriaText>\n                    {item.description ? (\n                      <AriaText slot=\"description\" elementType=\"span\" className=\"ps-0.5 text-caption-1-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n                        {item.description}\n                      </AriaText>\n                    ) : null}\n                  </span>\n                  <RiCheckLine\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className={cx('mt-1 size-4 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-primary', isSelected ? 'opacity-100' : 'opacity-0')}\n                  />\n                </>\n              )}\n            </AriaListBoxItem>\n          )}\n        </AriaListBox>\n      </AriaPopover>\n    </AriaSelect>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Its only listeners are the kit hooks' capture-phase pointerdown handlers, added while open and removed on close or unmount; the toggle hook's 400ms safety timeout only clears a ref.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Ring and chevron transitions are removed (motion-reduce:transition-none) and the popover drops its scale and blur entry, keeping only the opacity fade.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not render the label as plain children of the trigger without SelectValue: React Aria labels the button with the SelectValue id, and the value would drop out of the accessible name.\n- Pass a row-specific aria-label (\"Status for John Clarkson\"); a column of identical \"Status\" names is useless to screen-reader users.\n- If the popover is made modal instead, React Aria puts overflow:hidden on <html> while open, which jumps pages that scroll the root element.\n\n## Notes\n\n- size=\"sm\" uses caption text for dense tables; chevron=\"always\" suits touch-first layouts, chevron=\"none\" a pure badge look.\n- Options without a match render a dashed placeholder pill (\"Set status\").\n\n## Definition of done\n\nTabbing to \"Critical Status for Livia Saris\" and pressing Enter opens a list of five status pills with a check on Critical; typing \"S\" moves to \"Stable\" and Enter closes it, the pill turns lime and reads Stable, the Critical count drops to 0 and Stable rises to 3.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "step-end-caret": "# Build: Step-End Blinking Caret\n\nA bar caret that blinks the way a terminal cursor does: fully on, then fully off, with nothing in between. The switch is `animation-timing-function: step-end`, which holds each keyframe value until the next one rather than interpolating toward it. The keyframe distribution is deliberately uneven so the rhythm reads as alive rather than as a metronome, and the whole thing is CSS — no JavaScript runs after mount.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA text caret that snaps on and off instead of fading, using step-end timing and an uneven blink rhythm.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A single `@keyframes` rule sets `opacity: 1` at 0%, 70% and 100%, and `opacity: 0` at 20% and 50%. With `step-end`, each value is held until the following keyframe is reached, so opacity is only ever exactly 1 or exactly 0.\n2. The shorthand `animation: var(--kx-caret-period, 1.05s) step-end infinite kinetic-caret-blink` puts the period in a custom property, so the `period` prop varies the speed through an inline `style` without generating a new class or a new keyframe rule.\n3. The bar itself is an `inline-block` span with `background-color: currentColor`, so it inherits the colour of the text it sits in, and `vertical-align: -0.14em` to seat it on the baseline of the surrounding type rather than the box.\n4. The `paused` state sets `animation: none; opacity: 1` rather than `animation-play-state: paused` — pausing freezes on whichever frame the clock happened to reach, which is a coin flip between a visible caret and an invisible one.\n5. The stylesheet is emitted through React 19's `<style href precedence>` hoisting, so the rule is deduplicated by `href` and a hundred carets on a page produce exactly one rule in the document.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `period = 1.05`\n  Seconds per full on-off-on-off cycle. Terminal defaults sit near 1s; below ~0.6s it reads as an error indicator, above ~1.6s the reader stops associating the blink with the text it follows.\n- `keyframe stops = 0/20/50/70/100%`\n  On 0-20, off 20-50, on 50-70, off 70-100. An even 50/50 square wave reads as a metronome. The long-on / short-off asymmetry is what makes it read as something alive rather than as a timer.\n- `width = 2`\n  Pixels. 1px disappears at typical body sizes on a low-DPI display; past 3px it stops being a caret and starts being a block cursor, which implies overtype mode.\n- `vertical-align = -0.14em`\n  Seats the bar on the text baseline. At 0 the caret floats above the descender line and looks like a stray rule; in `em` it tracks the type size, so the same caret is correct at 14px and at 72px.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction StepEndCaret(props: { width?: number; height?: string; period?: number; paused?: boolean; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `width: number` — default `2`\n  Bar width in pixels.\n- `height: string` — default `'1em'`\n  Any CSS length. `em` keeps it locked to the surrounding type size.\n- `period: number` — default `1.05`\n  Seconds for one full on-off-on-off cycle.\n- `paused: boolean` — default `false`\n  Hold the caret solid — what a terminal does while keys are down.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the bar via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst CARET_CSS = `\n@keyframes kinetic-caret-blink {\n  0%, 70%, 100% { opacity: 1; }\n  20%, 50%      { opacity: 0; }\n}\n.kinetic-caret {\n  display: inline-block;\n  background-color: currentColor;\n  vertical-align: -0.14em;\n  animation: var(--kx-caret-period, 1.05s) step-end infinite kinetic-caret-blink;\n}\n/* Paused means solid, not frozen. 'animation-play-state: paused' stops on\n   whatever frame it happened to be on, which is a 50% chance of an invisible\n   caret. Dropping the animation entirely re-exposes the base opacity. */\n.kinetic-caret[data-paused='true'] {\n  animation: none;\n  opacity: 1;\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  .kinetic-caret {\n    animation: none;\n    opacity: 1;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport interface StepEndCaretProps {\n  /** Bar width in px. */\n  width?: number;\n  /** Any CSS length. `em` keeps it locked to the type size around it. */\n  height?: string;\n  /** Seconds for one on-off-on-off cycle. */\n  period?: number;\n  /** Hold the caret solid — what a real terminal does while keys are down. */\n  paused?: boolean;\n  className?: string;\n}\n\nexport function StepEndCaret({\n  width = 2,\n  height = '1em',\n  period = 1.05,\n  paused = false,\n  className,\n}: StepEndCaretProps) {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-step-end-caret\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {CARET_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <span\n        aria-hidden\n        data-paused={paused ? 'true' : 'false'}\n        className={cn('kinetic-caret', className)}\n        style={\n          {\n            width,\n            height,\n            '--kx-caret-period': `${period}s`,\n          } as CSSProperties\n        }\n      />\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. There is no effect, no timer and no listener; unmounting removes the span, and React's stylesheet hoisting handles the shared rule.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block inside the component stylesheet sets `animation: none; opacity: 1`. The caret remains fully visible and correctly positioned — it simply stops blinking. It is never left in the off state.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Leaving the default `ease` timing function. The opacity interpolates and the caret pulses like a glow, which reads as decoration; a cursor must be unambiguously present or absent.\n- Using `animation-play-state: paused` for the held state. It stops on an arbitrary frame, so roughly half the time the \"solid\" caret is invisible and the user thinks the field lost focus.\n- A symmetric 50/50 blink. It reads as mechanical timekeeping rather than as a cursor waiting for input.\n- Hardcoding the caret colour. `background-color: currentColor` is what lets the same component sit inside inverted, accented or muted text without a variant per context.\n- Emitting the keyframes per instance in a styled-component or inline `<style>` without a precedence key. Ten carets means ten identical rule sets the style engine has to resolve.\n- Exposing the caret to assistive technology. It is `aria-hidden` — a screen reader announcing a decorative bar between every phrase is noise.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No JavaScript executes after mount: no rAF loop, no timer, no listener, nothing to clean up. The component is a span and a stylesheet.\n- Designed to be composed — `asymmetric-typewriter` renders this component and drives its `paused` prop from its own typing state.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe bar is either fully opaque or fully invisible at every moment — screenshot it repeatedly and no intermediate opacity appears. Set `paused` and it goes solid immediately rather than freezing wherever it was. Render ten carets and inspect the document: one `kinetic-step-end-caret` stylesheet. Enable reduced motion and the caret is present and solid.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "stepped-film-grain": "# Build: Stepped Film Grain\n\nA full-screen transparent grain layer for stacking over hero art, video, or flat colour. The pixels are an ordinary hash, and everything that makes it look like film rather than television snow is in the clock: the noise offset is quantised to twelve changes per second, and the frames in between are not drawn at all. Grain cells are sized in CSS pixels, so the overlay weighs the same on a laptop and on a 3x phone.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA transparent grain overlay whose pattern jumps twelve times a second instead of every frame, so it reads as film.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `PlaneGeometry(2, 2)` is written straight to clip space — `gl_Position = vec4(position.xy, 0.0, 1.0)` — so there are no matrices and no camera transform, only a fragment shader over the whole canvas.\n2. Each fragment picks a lattice cell with `cell = floor(gl_FragCoord.xy / uPixelRatio / uCellSize) + uOffset`, dividing by the pixel ratio so a cell is a fixed number of CSS pixels rather than of device pixels.\n3. `cell = mod(cell, uTile)` wraps the lattice at 170 cells, which keeps the hash argument small enough for float precision and turns the offset into a jump anywhere inside a finite, tiling field.\n4. The value is `fract(sin(dot(cell, vec2(12.9898, 78.233))) * 43758.5453)`, shaped into signed grain by `g = (n - 0.5) * uContrast + uLift`. The lift is film base fog: it raises the mean so the layer opens crushed blacks instead of only speckling them.\n5. One normal-blended draw carries both directions: the colour is `vec3(step(0.0, g))` — white above the lift, black below — and the alpha is `abs(g) * uOpacity`, with anything under 0.0035 discarded.\n6. The rAF callback computes `index = floor(elapsed * stepsPerSecond) % steps` and returns immediately if the index is unchanged, so the GPU sees twelve draws a second regardless of display refresh rate. On a step it reads `uOffset` from a seeded table of integer jumps and renders once.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `stepsPerSecond = 12`\n  The filmic/snow dial. Below about 8 the pattern visibly ticks and reads as a stutter; past about 18 the eye fuses the steps into continuous shimmer and it is television snow again. 12 is 24 fps film with each pattern held for two frames.\n- `steps = 24`\n  Table length. At 12 steps a second this is a 2-second cycle before the sequence repeats — long enough that nothing breathes on a one-second beat, and the whole table is 48 floats.\n- `tile = 170`\n  Cells before the lattice wraps. Large enough that the repeat is imperceptible in unstructured noise; small enough that `dot(cell, vec2(12.9898, 78.233))` stays around 15 000, where `fract(sin(x) * 43758)` still has usable precision.\n- `contrast = 0.24`\n  Peak-to-peak swing before opacity, giving a signed range of ±0.12. Past ~0.4 the grain competes with the image it sits on; below ~0.1 it disappears once the browser composites it at 8 bits.\n- `lift = 0.095`\n  Just under half the contrast, so roughly 80% of samples land above zero. That asymmetry is what makes it read as an emulsion over the image rather than as symmetric static in front of it.\n- `opacity = 0.4`\n  Peak alpha works out at 0.4 x 0.215 ≈ 0.086. Above ~0.6 the layer stops being texture and starts being a visible veil over type.\n- `cellSize = 1 CSS pixel`\n  One cell per CSS pixel is the finest grain that is still DPR-invariant. Larger values give coarser, pushed-film grain; smaller values ask for detail below a CSS pixel that the backing store cannot hold.\n- `maxDpr = 1`\n  A grain cell is a whole CSS pixel, so a 2x backing store draws four device pixels of identical value per cell — four times the fill rate for a bit-identical image. This is the one full-screen shader that should not be rendered at device resolution.\n- `ALPHA_EPSILON = 0.0035`\n  Just under 1/255. Below it an 8-bit compositor shows nothing, but blending still costs a framebuffer read-modify-write — and on a lifted, low-contrast field a large share of pixels land there.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SteppedFilmGrain(props: { opacity?: number; contrast?: number; lift?: number; cellSize?: number; tile?: number; stepsPerSecond?: number; steps?: number; maxDpr?: number; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.4`\n  Overall strength, multiplied onto the grain magnitude.\n- `contrast: number` — default `0.24`\n  Peak-to-peak swing of the noise before opacity.\n- `lift: number` — default `0.095`\n  Mean added to the noise — base fog, which biases the layer toward lightening.\n- `cellSize: number` — default `1`\n  Size of one grain cell in CSS pixels. Clamped to a 0.25 floor.\n- `tile: number` — default `170`\n  Cells before the lattice repeats. Clamped to a floor of 8.\n- `stepsPerSecond: number` — default `12`\n  How many times a second the pattern changes. Also the draw rate.\n- `steps: number` — default `24`\n  Entries in the seeded offset table; the cycle length is steps / stepsPerSecond seconds.\n- `maxDpr: number` — default `1`\n  Backing-store cap. 1 is deliberate; see the constant.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'stepped-film-grain'`\n  Seeds the offset table so the sequence is stable across reloads and matches the server.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SteppedFilmGrain({\n  opacity = 0.4,\n  contrast = 0.24,\n  lift = 0.095,\n  cellSize = 1,\n  tile = 170,\n  stepsPerSecond = 12,\n  steps = 24,\n  maxDpr = 1,\n  seed = 'stepped-film-grain',\n  className,\n}: SteppedFilmGrainProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  const stepCount = Math.max(1, Math.round(steps));\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n        // Nothing here has a geometric edge, so MSAA would allocate a\n        // multisample buffer the size of the canvas and resolve it every draw\n        // for no visible difference.\n        antialias: false,\n        alpha: true,\n        depth: false,\n        stencil: false,\n        powerPreference: 'low-power',\n      });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    // The quad is authored in clip space, so the camera exists only because\n    // three's render signature requires one.\n    const camera = new THREE.Camera();\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.getPixelRatio() },\n      uOffset: { value: new THREE.Vector2() },\n      uCellSize: { value: Math.max(cellSize, 0.25) },\n      uTile: { value: Math.max(tile, 8) },\n      uContrast: { value: contrast },\n      uLift: { value: lift },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: STEPPED_FILM_GRAIN_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: STEPPED_FILM_GRAIN_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      // Normal blending, not additive: the shader emits black as well as white\n      // and additive could only lighten, which turns grain into haze.\n      blending: THREE.NormalBlending,\n    });\n\n    const quad = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    quad.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(quad);\n\n    /* The offset table. Seeded, not `Math.random`, so the sequence is identical\n       on the server, on the client, and on every reload — and integer, so each\n       jump lands exactly on the cell lattice. A non-integer offset resamples\n       cell boundaries and the grain appears to slide a fraction of a cell,\n       which is the crawl this component exists to avoid. */\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const wrap = uniforms.uTile.value;\n    const offsets: Array<[number, number]> = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < stepCount; i += 1) {\n      offsets.push([Math.floor(rng() * wrap), Math.floor(rng() * wrap)]);\n    }\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    let lastIndex = -1;\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) return;\n      renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(maxDpr));\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      // Re-read rather than assume: dragging the window between a retina and a\n      // non-retina display changes devicePixelRatio without changing the CSS\n      // size, and a stale value rescales every grain cell.\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n      // Resizing a canvas clears its drawing buffer, and this component only\n      // draws on a step change — without a redraw here the overlay would be\n      // blank until the next step, up to 1/stepsPerSecond later.\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    const step = (index: number) => {\n      if (index === lastIndex) return;\n      lastIndex = index;\n      const pair = offsets[index] ?? offsets[0];\n      if (pair) uniforms.uOffset.value.set(pair[0], pair[1]);\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, step };\n    step(0);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [ready, opacity, contrast, lift, cellSize, tile, stepCount, maxDpr, seed]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  useRaf((_dt, elapsed) => {\n    // Quantised time, not time. Everything filmic about this component is the\n    // fact that this number changes twelve times a second and the draw call\n    // is skipped in between.\n    const index = Math.floor(elapsed * stepsPerSecond) % stepCount;\n    stage.current?.step(index);\n  }, ready && visible && !reduced && !unsupported);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage ref, disposes the quad geometry and the ShaderMaterial, calls `disposeRenderer` for `dispose` + `forceContextLoss` + a zeroed drawing buffer, then removes the canvas from the DOM. `useRaf` releases its own frame subscription, and there are no listeners of the component's own — sizing comes from the shared ResizeObserver and visibility from the shared document listener.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component reads `useReducedMotion` itself. No rAF subscription is opened, and the grain is drawn once at table entry 0 — the texture is fully present and correct, it simply never advances. That is the right degradation for grain: it is a surface quality, not a motion, and removing it entirely would change how the artwork beneath it reads.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Advancing the noise every frame. It is the single thing that separates film grain from TV static, and it also makes the effect frame-rate dependent — the same overlay churns twice as fast on a 120 Hz display. Quantising the offset fixes both at once.\n- Sliding the offset by a constant per step instead of jumping to table entries. A constant increment makes the whole field crawl in one direction, and the eye locks onto and tracks that direction; uncorrelated jumps have nowhere to track.\n- Using a fractional offset. Non-integer values move the lattice off the pixel grid, so cell boundaries resample and the grain shimmers within each step — the artefact the stepping was supposed to remove.\n- Declaring `precision mediump float` out of habit. The hash argument reaches ~15 000, which a 16-bit float holds to three digits, so adjacent cells return the same value and the grain becomes flat blocks tens of pixels wide. This shader needs highp.\n- Sizing grain in device pixels. `gl_FragCoord` is a device-pixel coordinate, so grain authored without dividing by the pixel ratio is three times finer and effectively invisible on the phone, while looking correct on the machine it was tuned on.\n- Rendering the overlay at the device pixel ratio. A cell is one CSS pixel of constant value; a 2x buffer costs four times the fragments and produces the identical image.\n- Forgetting that resizing a canvas clears its drawing buffer. A component that only draws on a step change goes blank for up to 1/stepsPerSecond after every resize, which during a window drag is a visible flicker — the resize path redraws explicitly.\n- Using additive blending so the grain can only lighten. The result is haze rather than grain; emitting black or white with the magnitude in alpha gets both directions out of one normal-blended draw.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The host is `pointer-events-none` and `aria-hidden`. It is a decorative layer that must never intercept a click on the content underneath it.\n- If WebGL is unavailable the component renders an empty box. A missing grain layer is a correct degradation — the content beneath is untouched.\n- Stack it as the last child of a relatively-positioned container with `absolute inset-0`. It composites through the browser, so no blend mode is required, though `mix-blend-mode: overlay` is a reasonable variation over photography.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA fine speckle over the content that visibly holds still for a beat and then changes, roughly twelve times a second — count it by eye and it should read as a flicker, not a fizz. Zoom the browser from 100% to 200% and the grain gets larger with the layout rather than staying pinned to device pixels. Resize the window and the overlay never blanks. With reduced motion the grain is still there and completely static.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "steps-card": "# Build: Steps Card\n\nA grey dashboard card headed \"Steps\" with the week total (\"31,600 total steps\") and a week pill with arrows. Below are seven rounded grey tracks, Monday to Sunday, each filled from the bottom with a teal bar for that day's steps. The bars grow in on mount and glide to new heights when the week changes, while the total counts to the new figure. Hovering or focusing a bar outlines it, deepens its teal and rolls the headline to that day's steps (\"6,340 steps on Thu\").\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSeven daily step bars with a week switcher, a total that counts up, and an outline on the hovered bar that rolls the headline to that day.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. All weeks share one scale: `max`, or by default the busiest day across every week rounded up to the next 1,000 (8,000 for the sample data). A fill's height is `steps / scale` as a percentage of its track, so switching weeks compares like with like instead of re-stretching each week to its own peak.\n2. Each fill is an absolutely positioned span at the bottom of its track with an inline `height` percentage and a 600ms expo-out `height` transition, delayed 40ms per bar from Monday so the row grows left to right. On mount every height is 0; two animation frames later the real heights apply, so the transition draws them in. A week change only changes the heights, so the same transition moves them.\n3. The headline number is `useCountUp(target, 600)` from the shared hooks. The target is 0 until the bars are drawn, then the hovered day's steps or else the week total; the hook eases from whatever is on screen to the new target, so hovering across bars or changing week mid-roll never jumps. The unit (\"total steps\" or \"steps on Thu\") is a keyed span, so `.animate-number-fade` replays when it changes.\n4. Bars are buttons in a group with a roving tabindex: Arrow Left and Right move focus, Home and End jump to Monday and Sunday. Pointer enter and focus set the active bar and pointer leave of the row or blur clears it. The active track gets a 2px teal outline, offset 2px and faded in over 150ms, and its fill switches to `chart-1-active`; keyboard focus uses the focus-ring colour instead.\n5. The card is a container (`@container`). Under 420px the unit drops below the number so the header never collides with the week pill, and from 480px the bar gap opens from 8px to 30px.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `chartHeight = 200px`\n  Tall enough that a 1,000-step difference is 25px, which the eye reads at a glance.\n- `GROW_MS / STAGGER = 600ms / 40ms`\n  The whole row settles in under a second, and a short stagger reads as a sweep without making Sunday wait.\n- `scale rounding = up to the next 1,000`\n  The busiest day nearly fills its track but never touches the top, so no bar looks clipped.\n- `count-up = 600ms ease-out cubic`\n  Same length as the bar growth, so the number and the bars land together.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction StepsCard(props?: StepsCardProps): JSX.Element\ntype StepsWeek = { id: string; label: string; days: number[] }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Steps\"`\n  Header label.\n- `totalLabel: string` — default `\"total steps\"`\n  Unit after the week total.\n- `weeks: StepsWeek[]` — default `STEPS_WEEKS`\n  Weeks the pill steps through, oldest first.\n- `weekIndex: number`\n  Controlled index into `weeks`.\n- `defaultWeekIndex: number` — default `weeks.length - 1`\n  Uncontrolled starting week.\n- `onWeekIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Called when an arrow changes week.\n- `max: number` — default `busiest day rounded up to 1,000`\n  Top of the shared bar scale.\n- `chartHeight: number` — default `200`\n  Height of the bar tracks in px.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// One fixed scale for every week, so switching weeks compares like with like.\nconst scale = useMemo(() => {\n  if (maxProp && maxProp > 0) return maxProp;\n  const peak = Math.max(0, ...weeks.flatMap((w) => w.days));\n  return Math.max(1000, Math.ceil(peak / 1000) * 1000);\n}, [weeks, maxProp]);\nconst live = drawn || reduced;\nconst total = days.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);\nconst activeSteps = active !== null ? days[active] : undefined;\n// The headline rolls to the hovered day's steps, back to the week total, or up from 0 on mount.\nconst target = live ? (activeSteps ?? total) : 0;\nconst counted = useCountUp(target, 600);\nconst shown = reduced ? target : counted;\nconst pct = (n: number) => (live ? Math.min(100, Math.max(0, (n / scale) * 100)) : 0);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The mount effect cancels both pending animation frames on unmount, and `useCountUp` cancels its frame whenever the target changes or the card unmounts. Pointer, focus and key handling are React event props.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, the bars render at their final heights from the first frame and `motion-reduce:transition-none` makes week changes and hover outlines instant. The headline shows the target value directly instead of counting, and the unit no longer fades.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Scaling each week to its own peak. Every week then looks equally busy; a shared scale is what makes the switcher useful.\n- Animating the bars with `scaleY`. It squashes the rounded corners while it grows; transitioning `height` keeps the 10px radius intact, and seven elements are cheap to lay out.\n- Rolling the number with a fresh tween that starts from the old target. If the pointer crosses bars mid-roll the number jumps; easing from the value on screen keeps it continuous.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Exports `STEPS_WEEKS` (six sample weeks, oldest first) and the `StepsWeek` type `{ id, label, days }`, where `days` holds seven counts starting on Monday.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe default card reads \"Steps\", \"31,600 total steps\" (counting up from 0 on load) and \"29 Jun - 5 Jul\", with the next arrow disabled. Seven teal bars grow left to right in grey tracks: Friday is the tallest at about nine tenths of its track and Wednesday the shortest. Hovering Thursday outlines its track in teal and the headline rolls to \"6,340 steps on Thu\"; leaving the row rolls it back to 31,600. Clicking the previous arrow shows \"22 Jun - 28 Jun\", the bars move to their new heights and the total counts to 32,010.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "stroke-to-fill-hover": "# Build: Stroke-To-Fill Hover Rows\n\nA list of large type where every row is an outline with a ghost of fill, and the hovered row materialises: the stroke drops to zero width, the fill goes to full `currentColor`, and the row steps 12px to the right while its siblings recede. The row does not light up, it becomes solid. Everything is CSS, so nothing but the compositor runs on hover.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn index of outlined type where the hovered row fills in solid, drops its stroke, and steps sideways.\n\n- Category: text\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Two properties carry the effect, and they are separate on purpose: `-webkit-text-stroke-width` goes 1px to 0, and `-webkit-text-fill-color` goes from a ghost of `currentColor` to `currentColor` itself.\n2. `-webkit-text-fill-color` overrides `color` for the glyph interior only, which is what lets the outline stay at `currentColor` while the inside is nearly transparent. Doing this with `color` alone is impossible — `color` is also what `currentColor` resolves to, so fading the fill fades the stroke with it and the row just dims.\n3. The ghost is `color-mix(in oklch, currentColor var(--kx-sf-ghost, 16%), transparent)` rather than a literal `rgba`. It inherits the surrounding text colour, so one component is correct on a dark page and a light one, and mixing in oklch keeps the 16% step perceptually even instead of clumping toward black the way sRGB alpha does.\n4. The three tunables are custom properties set on the `<ul>` from props (`--kx-sf-stroke`, `--kx-sf-ghost`, `--kx-sf-nudge`), so per-instance configuration needs no extra class and no extra rule.\n5. Sibling dimming is scoped to the list's own hover: `.kinetic-sf-list:hover .kinetic-sf-row:not(:hover) { opacity: 0.45 }`. Scoping to the list is what makes the resting state \"all rows equal\" rather than \"all rows dimmed\".\n6. Every hover rule is duplicated for `:focus-visible`. Each row is a real `<a>` or `<button>` inside an `<li>`, so keyboard traversal produces the same state change — without it a keyboard user tabbing ten identical outlines has no idea where they are.\n7. The stylesheet is emitted once through React 19's `<style href precedence>` hoisting, so any number of lists on a page share one rule set.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `STROKE_WIDTH = 1`\n  Pixels of outline at rest. At the 28-72px type this component targets, 1px is a hairline that still reads as a complete letterform; 2px starts to close up counters in tighter faces and the row looks bold rather than outlined.\n- `GHOST = 16`\n  Percent of `currentColor` mixed into the resting fill. At 0 the glyph interiors are fully transparent and the type dissolves into whatever is behind it; past ~30% the resting and hovered states are too close for the transition to register.\n- `NUDGE = 12`\n  Pixels the hovered row steps right. Enough to be unmistakable next to a static neighbour; past ~24px the row visibly leaves the left margin of the list and the alignment looks broken.\n- `transition duration / curve = 380ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`\n  A strong ease-out. The row arrives quickly and settles, which matches the pointer having already stopped. A symmetric ease at this duration lags the cursor noticeably on a fast pass down a ten-row list.\n- `sibling opacity = 0.45`\n  Enough separation that the hovered row is unambiguous, while the others stay legible — dimming to 0.2 turns a browsable index into a spotlight and hides the thing the user is scanning for.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction StrokeToFillRows(props: { items: StrokeToFillItem[]; strokeWidth?: number; ghost?: number; nudge?: number; className?: string; onSelect?: (item: StrokeToFillItem, index: number) => void }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: StrokeToFillItem[]`\n  Rows. Each has a `label`, an optional `meta` node for right-hand metadata, and an optional `href`.\n- `strokeWidth: number` — default `1`\n  Outline thickness in px at rest.\n- `ghost: number` — default `16`\n  Resting fill as a percentage of `currentColor`.\n- `nudge: number` — default `12`\n  Pixels the hovered row steps right.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the `<ul>` via `cn`.\n- `onSelect: (item: StrokeToFillItem, index: number) => void`\n  Called when a row without an `href` is activated.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst ROWS_CSS = `\n.kinetic-sf-title {\n  -webkit-text-stroke: var(--kx-sf-stroke, 1px) currentColor;\n  -webkit-text-fill-color: color-mix(in oklch, currentColor var(--kx-sf-ghost, 16%), transparent);\n  transition:\n    -webkit-text-fill-color 380ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),\n    -webkit-text-stroke-width 380ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),\n    transform 380ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);\n  transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0);\n}\n/* :focus-visible alongside :hover, always. A keyboard user tabbing an index\n   of ten rows otherwise sees ten identical outlines and no current position. */\n.kinetic-sf-row:hover .kinetic-sf-title,\n.kinetic-sf-row:focus-visible .kinetic-sf-title {\n  -webkit-text-fill-color: currentColor;\n  -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px;\n  transform: translate3d(var(--kx-sf-nudge, 12px), 0, 0);\n}\n/* Everything that is not the hovered row recedes. Scoped to the list's own\n   hover so the resting state is all rows equal, not all rows dimmed. */\n.kinetic-sf-list:hover .kinetic-sf-row:not(:hover) {\n  opacity: 0.45;\n}\n.kinetic-sf-row {\n  transition: opacity 380ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);\n}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n  /* The stroke-to-fill swap is a state change, not motion, and it stays. The\n     12 px lateral travel is motion, and it goes. */\n  .kinetic-sf-row:hover .kinetic-sf-title,\n  .kinetic-sf-row:focus-visible .kinetic-sf-title {\n    transform: none;\n  }\n}\n`;\n\nexport interface StrokeToFillItem {\n  label: string;\n  /** Right-hand metadata: a year, a category, a count. */\n  meta?: ReactNode;\n  href?: string;\n}\n\nexport interface StrokeToFillRowsProps {\n  items: StrokeToFillItem[];\n  /** Outline thickness in px at rest. */\n  strokeWidth?: number;\n  /** Resting fill, as a percentage of `currentColor`. */\n  ghost?: number;\n  /** Pixels the hovered row steps right. */\n  nudge?: number;\n  className?: string;\n  onSelect?: (item: StrokeToFillItem, index: number) => void;\n}\n\nconst STROKE_WIDTH = 1;\nconst GHOST = 16;\nconst NUDGE = 12;\n\nexport function StrokeToFillRows({\n  items,\n  strokeWidth = STROKE_WIDTH,\n  ghost = GHOST,\n  nudge = NUDGE,\n  className,\n  onSelect,\n}: StrokeToFillRowsProps) {\n  return (\n    <>\n      <style href=\"kinetic-stroke-to-fill\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {ROWS_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <ul\n        className={cn('kinetic-sf-list w-full', className)}\n        style={\n          {\n            '--kx-sf-stroke': `${strokeWidth}px`,\n            '--kx-sf-ghost': `${ghost}%`,\n            '--kx-sf-nudge': `${nudge}px`,\n          } as CSSProperties\n        }\n      >\n        {items.map((item, i) => {\n          const inner = (\n            <>\n              <span className=\"kinetic-sf-title block text-[clamp(28px,5.5vw,72px)] leading-[1.06] tracking-[-0.03em]\">\n                {item.label}\n              </span>\n              {item.meta ? (\n                <span className=\"meta shrink-0 pt-[0.35em]\">{item.meta}</span>\n              ) : null}\n            </>\n          );\n\n          return (\n            <li key={`${item.label}-${i}`} className=\"border-b border-line\">\n              {item.href ? (\n                <a\n                  href={item.href}\n                  className=\"kinetic-sf-row flex w-full items-start justify-between gap-6 py-[0.35em] text-left outline-none\"\n                >\n                  {inner}\n                </a>\n              ) : (\n                <button\n                  type=\"button\"\n                  onClick={() => onSelect?.(item, i)}\n                  className=\"kinetic-sf-row flex w-full items-start justify-between gap-6 py-[0.35em] text-left outline-none\"\n                >\n                  {inner}\n                </button>\n              )}\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: no effect, no listener, no timer. Unmounting removes the list; React's `<style precedence>` hoisting owns the shared rule set.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The stroke-to-fill swap is a state change, not motion, so it is kept in full — the hovered row still fills solid and its siblings still recede. Only the 12px lateral travel is removed, via `transform: none` in a `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` block. The rows are never left unreadable.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Trying to build this with `color` alone. Fading the fill fades the stroke with it because `currentColor` resolves to the same value, so the row dims instead of filling.\n- Transitioning the `-webkit-text-stroke` shorthand. Shorthands are not independently interpolable here; transition `-webkit-text-stroke-width` explicitly or the stroke snaps off in one frame while the fill glides.\n- Hardcoding the resting fill as `rgba(255,255,255,0.16)`. The component is then wrong the moment it lands on a light background, and wrong again in an inverted section of the same page.\n- Mixing the ghost in sRGB. The intermediate steps clump toward black, so the resting fill on a mid-tone accent looks muddy rather than faint.\n- Dimming siblings without scoping to `.kinetic-sf-list:hover`. Every row is then permanently at 0.45 and the \"recede\" has nothing to recede from.\n- Styling `:hover` and forgetting `:focus-visible`. Keyboard users get ten identical outlines and no indication of the current row, which is a genuine accessibility failure and not just a polish gap.\n- Animating `left` or `margin-left` for the nudge. Both trigger layout on every row of a large-type index; `translate3d` stays on the compositor.\n\n## Notes\n\n- No text splitting and no motion library: this is a state change on a whole row, so imposing a splitter or GSAP on the consumer for a CSS-cost effect would be pure overhead.\n- `-webkit-text-stroke` is unprefixed-less by history but supported in every current engine including Firefox and Safari. There is no standards-track alternative that composites the same way.\n- Rows render as `<a>` when `href` is given and as `<button>` otherwise, so the list is keyboard-traversable either way without a `tabIndex` hack.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHover a row: the outline thins to nothing as the glyph interiors fill to full colour, and the row slides 12px right while the others fade back. Tab through with a keyboard and the same state appears on the focused row. Flip the page between light and dark themes and the resting ghost fill is still faintly visible in both, without editing the component. With reduced motion enabled the fill still happens and the row does not move.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "superellipse-text-avoidance": "# Build: Superellipse Text Avoidance\n\nThe headline is real DOM on top of an instanced particle field, and the field is pushed out of a 6-norm superellipse fitted to the headline's measured rect. Interior particles are remapped into a band hugging the shape rather than clamped onto it, so the hole has a bright rim instead of a pile-up. The same avoidance function runs a second time at exponent 2 for the damped pointer, because a disc is a superellipse with a lower exponent.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA particle field that flows around the headline, held off by a 6-norm superellipse fitted to the measured text box.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: ogl@^1.0.11\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The avoidance region is `pow(pow(|nx|, e) + pow(|ny|, e), 1/e) < 1` in the rect's own normalised space. At e = 2 that is an ellipse, at e = 6 a squircle, at e = 20 effectively a box. Six is the useful one: a circle around a wide headline clears a large empty halo past the ends of the line, and a box has four corner discontinuities that crease the field along straight lines.\n2. Interior radii are REMAPPED, not clamped. `mapped = 1 + whip * min(r, 1 + whip) / (1 + whip) + max(r - (1 + whip), 0)` compresses [0, 1 + whip] into [1, 1 + whip] and is the identity beyond it. Clamping instead maps a whole area onto a curve, so every interior particle lands on the outline and the shape is drawn as one infinitely dense line with an empty interior behind it.\n3. The displacement is `half * (n / max(r, 1e-3)) * (mapped - r)` — divided by a floor rather than normalized. A particle at the exact centre has no direction and `normalize()` returns NaN there, which propagates into `gl_Position` and drops the whole instance rather than one component. A lattice puts particles exactly on both axes on the first frame, so this case is reached immediately, not never.\n4. `pow()` is undefined for a negative base and for zero raised to a non-positive power, and a symmetric field hits both. The norm takes `abs()` first and adds 1e-5, which costs nothing and removes an entire class of driver-dependent black frames.\n5. The rect is measured, never passed in. `getBoundingClientRect()` on the text node and on the host produce four uv numbers; a ResizeObserver on the text node plus `document.fonts.ready` keeps them in step through a rewrap, a font swap and a container resize. A hand-tuned rect is wrong on the first webfont load and wrong again on the first narrow viewport.\n6. The text rect is deliberately NOT aspect-corrected, because it was measured in element space and has to stay there or it stops agreeing with the letters. The pointer disc IS aspect-corrected, because it is a shape in screen space and an uncorrected one is a visible ellipse on any non-square container. The two live in the same shader and want opposite treatment.\n7. Displaced particles brighten and grow slightly, tinted toward a third colour. Without it the field is just a hole and the exponent is invisible; with it the squircle is legible as a rim, which is the only on-screen evidence that the shape is not a box.\n8. The scatter is a jittered lattice, then shuffled. Uniform random has clumps and voids at this population, and once the avoidance opens a real hole the accidental voids read as more holes. The shuffle matters because the instanced count is a prefix of the buffers — in lattice order a smaller count would draw only the top rows.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `exponent = 6`\n  The p-norm. Below ~4 the shape is round enough that the clear zone bulges past the ends of the line; above ~10 the corners are sharp enough to crease the field visibly, which is the axis-aligned-box failure the superellipse exists to avoid.\n- `WHIP_DISC = 0.3`\n  Width of the band the interior is compressed into, in normalised units — the whole feel of the effect. Small values whip particles out hard and stack them into a bright outline; past ~0.6 the field bends around from so far away that the text no longer reads as the cause.\n- `margin = 0.06`\n  Clear ring around the text, in units of container HEIGHT, divided by the aspect ratio for x so the gap is the same number of pixels on all four sides. Adding one uv figure to both halves pads a wide container far more at the ends of the line than above it.\n- `pointerRadius / pointerWhip = 0.16 / 0.45`\n  The second field, at exponent 2. Softer than the text rect on purpose: the pointer is a passing influence and a hard-edged hole following the cursor competes with the headline it is supposed to frame.\n- `smoothing = 0.0008`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second — an ~90ms tail. The pointer hole has a visible edge, unlike a glow, so lag reads as broken rather than as smooth.\n- `CELL_JITTER = 0.42`\n  Displacement inside a lattice cell, as a fraction of the cell. Zero is a visible grid; past ~0.5 neighbouring cells overlap and the stratification that made the scatter even in the first place is gone.\n- `density = 900`\n  Particles per megapixel, so the same value reads right in a 320px card and a 2000px hero. Too sparse and the rim is a dotted line rather than a shape.\n- `MAX_DPR = 1.5`\n  Additive fill is the whole cost and it scales with the square of the ratio. These are soft round dots with no fine detail, so a third sample per axis resolves nothing and costs 78% more fragments.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SuperellipseTextAvoidance(props: { children?: ReactNode; density?: number; maxCount?: number; sizeRange?: readonly [number, number]; exponent?: number; margin?: number; whip?: number; pointerRadius?: number; pointerWhip?: number; smoothing?: number; opacity?: number; colorA?: string; colorB?: string; glowColor?: string; background?: string; seed?: number | string; className?: string; textClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The headline. Rendered above the field and measured for the avoidance rect.\n- `density: number` — default `900`\n  Particles per megapixel of container area.\n- `maxCount: number` — default `1600`\n  Population ceiling, and the size the buffers are allocated at.\n- `sizeRange: readonly [number, number]` — default `[1.4, 4.4]`\n  Particle size in CSS pixels, biased toward the small end.\n- `exponent: number` — default `6`\n  Superellipse exponent. 2 is a circle, 6 a squircle, high values a box.\n- `margin: number` — default `0.06`\n  Clear ring around the text, in units of container height.\n- `whip: number` — default `0.3`\n  Width of the band the interior is compressed into.\n- `pointerRadius: number` — default `0.16`\n  Pointer avoidance radius in container-height units. 0 disables it.\n- `pointerWhip: number` — default `0.45`\n  Whip band for the pointer disc.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0008`\n  Fraction of the pointer gap still remaining after one second.\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.92`\n  Overall alpha multiplier.\n- `colorA: string` — default `'#9ec6ff'`\n  First tint. Any value ogl's Color accepts.\n- `colorB: string` — default `'#eef2fb'`\n  Second tint; each particle mixes between the two.\n- `glowColor: string` — default `'#ffd9a0'`\n  Tint for displaced particles, which is what makes the shape legible.\n- `background: string` — default `'#07080d'`\n  Opaque clear colour the additive field is composited onto.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'superellipse-text-avoidance'`\n  Seeds the lattice jitter, sizes, phases and tints.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n- `textClassName: string`\n  Merged onto the measured text layer.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Floor on the population. Below this the field reads as a few stray specks. */\nconst MIN_COUNT = 260;\n/**\n * Width of the band interior particles are compressed into, in normalised\n * superellipse units. The catalogue's WHIP_DISC. Small values throw particles\n * out hard and stack them on the outline; large ones bend the field around from\n * a distance and the text stops looking like the cause.\n */\nconst WHIP_DISC = 0.3;\n/** Fraction of the presence gap left after a second. ~200ms in and out. */\nconst PRESENCE_SMOOTHING = 0.007;\n/** Size distribution exponent. Above 1 crowds the population into small sizes. */\nconst SIZE_BIAS = 1.7;\n/** Fill scales with dpr squared, and these are soft dots with no fine detail. */\nconst MAX_DPR = 1.5;\n/** Lattice bounds. Wider than 0..1 so drift never bares the edges. */\nconst HOME_MIN = -0.05;\nconst HOME_MAX = 1.05;\n/** Jitter as a fraction of a lattice cell. Past ~0.5 cells overlap and clump. */\nconst CELL_JITTER = 0.42;\n/** The reduced-motion still: one frame with the pointer parked off to one side. */\nconst STILL_ELAPSED = 4.2;\nconst STILL_POINTER: readonly [number, number] = [0.3, 0.34];\n\nexport function SuperellipseTextAvoidance({\n  children,\n  density = 900,\n  maxCount = 1600,\n  sizeRange = [1.4, 4.4],\n  exponent = 6,\n  margin = 0.06,\n  whip = WHIP_DISC,\n  pointerRadius = 0.16,\n  pointerWhip = 0.45,\n  smoothing = 0.0008,\n  opacity = 0.92,\n  colorA = '#9ec6ff',\n  colorB = '#eef2fb',\n  glowColor = '#ffd9a0',\n  background = '#07080d',\n  seed = 'superellipse-text-avoidance',\n  className,\n  textClassName,\n}: SuperellipseTextAvoidanceProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const text = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const textSize = useResizeObserver(text);\n  const size = useRef({ width, height });\n  size.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const reducedRef = useRef(reduced);\n  reducedRef.current = reduced;\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1;\n\n  const [minSize = 1.4, maxSize = 4.4] = sizeRange;\n\n  /* Raw pointer target in field space, or null until a real move — which is not\n     the same as a pointer resting at the bottom-left corner. */\n  const target = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const inside = useRef(false);\n  const follow = useRef({ x: 0.5, y: 0.5 });\n  const presence = useRef(0);\n\n  /* Live numbers the loop reads. Changing one must not rebuild the renderer:\n     Chromium drops the OLDEST WebGL context past roughly 16 per process, so a\n     rebuild per prop change can blank an unrelated canvas on the same page. */\n  const live = useRef({ exponent, margin, whip, pointerRadius, pointerWhip, smoothing, opacity });\n  live.current = { exponent, margin, whip, pointerRadius, pointerWhip, smoothing, opacity };\n\n  /**\n   * Reads the text box out of the DOM and writes it into `uRect` as uv centre\n   * plus padded half-extents. No React state: a measurement that re-renders is\n   * a measurement that can re-measure itself in a loop.\n   */\n  const measure = () => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    const hostEl = host.current;\n    const textEl = text.current;\n    if (!s || !hostEl || !textEl) return;\n\n    const hostBox = hostEl.getBoundingClientRect();\n    if (hostBox.width < 1 || hostBox.height < 1) return;\n    const textBox = textEl.getBoundingClientRect();\n\n    if (textBox.width < 1 || textBox.height < 1) {\n      // No children, or the text has not laid out yet. Presence 0 rather than a\n      // zero-size rect, which would be a singularity at the centre of the field.\n      s.uniforms.uRectPresence.value = 0;\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const aspect = hostBox.width / hostBox.height;\n    /* The margin is specified in HEIGHT units and divided by the aspect ratio\n       for x, so the clear ring is the same number of pixels on all four sides.\n       Adding the same uv figure to both halves pads a wide container far more\n       at the ends of the line than above and below it. */\n    const padX = live.current.margin / Math.max(aspect, 0.0001);\n    const padY = live.current.margin;\n\n    s.uniforms.uRect.value[0] = (textBox.left - hostBox.left + textBox.width * 0.5) / hostBox.width;\n    // Flipped: field y is up, client y is down.\n    s.uniforms.uRect.value[1] =\n      1 - (textBox.top - hostBox.top + textBox.height * 0.5) / hostBox.height;\n    s.uniforms.uRect.value[2] = textBox.width * 0.5 / hostBox.width + padX;\n    s.uniforms.uRect.value[3] = textBox.height * 0.5 / hostBox.height + padY;\n    s.uniforms.uRectPresence.value = 1;\n\n    // The live loop picks this up next frame; a paused one has to be told.\n    if (reducedRef.current) s.draw(STILL_ELAPSED);\n  };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    /* ogl creates its own canvas, and it is appended and removed per effect\n       run. Reusing one React-owned canvas breaks under StrictMode: the first\n       cleanup force-loses the context, and `getContext` on that element\n       afterwards hands back the dead one rather than a fresh context — the\n       panel is then permanently blank in development and fine in production. */\n    const renderer = new Renderer({\n      dpr: pixelRatio(MAX_DPR),\n      // Opaque: the pass is additive, and additive onto a transparent canvas\n      // would have to brighten the page behind it, which the GPU cannot see.\n      alpha: false,\n      // Nothing occludes anything, so a depth buffer is memory plus a clear.\n      depth: false,\n      antialias: false,\n      powerPreference: 'low-power',\n    });\n\n    const gl = renderer.gl;\n    const canvas = gl.canvas;\n\n    const bg = new Color(background);\n    gl.clearColor(bg.r, bg.g, bg.b, 1);\n\n    const quad = new Float32Array([-0.5, -0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5, 0.5, -0.5, 0.5]);\n    const index = new Uint16Array([0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 3]);\n\n    const total = Math.max(MIN_COUNT, Math.round(maxCount));\n    const homes = new Float32Array(total * 2);\n    const params = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n    const seeds = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n\n    /* A STRATIFIED scatter, not `rng()` twice. Uniform random has clumps and\n       voids at this population, and once the avoidance opens a real hole those\n       accidental voids read as more holes — the shape of the field stops being\n       legible. A jittered lattice is even everywhere and still looks organic.\n       Cells are then SHUFFLED, because the instanced count is a prefix of these\n       buffers: in lattice order a smaller count would draw the top rows only. */\n    const aspect = Math.max(size.current.width, 1) / Math.max(size.current.height, 1);\n    const cols = Math.max(2, Math.round(Math.sqrt(total * aspect)));\n    const rows = Math.max(2, Math.ceil(total / cols));\n    const cells: Array<[number, number]> = [];\n    for (let r = 0; r < rows; r += 1) {\n      for (let c = 0; c < cols; c += 1) {\n        cells.push([(c + 0.5) / cols, (r + 0.5) / rows]);\n      }\n    }\n    shuffle(rng, cells);\n\n    const spanX = HOME_MAX - HOME_MIN;\n    const spanY = HOME_MAX - HOME_MIN;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i += 1) {\n      const cell = cells[i % cells.length] ?? [0.5, 0.5];\n      const [cellX = 0.5, cellY = 0.5] = cell;\n      homes[i * 2] = HOME_MIN + (cellX + randRange(rng, -CELL_JITTER, CELL_JITTER) / cols) * spanX;\n      homes[i * 2 + 1] =\n        HOME_MIN + (cellY + randRange(rng, -CELL_JITTER, CELL_JITTER) / rows) * spanY;\n\n      // Biased small: a linear distribution puts a quarter of the population in\n      // the largest quarter of the range and the field reads as gravel.\n      const sizeT = Math.pow(rng(), SIZE_BIAS);\n      params[i * 4] = Math.max(0.5, minSize) + Math.max(maxSize - minSize, 0.25) * sizeT;\n      // Drift radius and rate. Small, because this field is a texture the text\n      // sits in — a fast one competes with the headline for attention.\n      params[i * 4 + 1] = randRange(rng, 0.002, 0.014);\n      params[i * 4 + 2] = (rng() < 0.5 ? -1 : 1) * randRange(rng, 0.08, 0.32);\n      // Bigger particles are dimmer, so total light stays roughly constant.\n      params[i * 4 + 3] = 0.32 + 0.5 * (1 - sizeT);\n\n      seeds[i * 4] = rng() * Math.PI * 2;\n      seeds[i * 4 + 1] = randRange(rng, 0.3, 1);\n      seeds[i * 4 + 2] = rng();\n      seeds[i * 4 + 3] = 0;\n    }\n\n    const geometry = new Geometry(gl, {\n      index: { data: index },\n      position: { size: 2, data: quad },\n      aHome: { size: 2, data: homes, instanced: 1 },\n      aParams: { size: 4, data: params, instanced: 1 },\n      aSeed: { size: 4, data: seeds, instanced: 1 },\n    });\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uResolution: { value: [1, 1] },\n      uPixelRatio: { value: renderer.dpr },\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uRect: { value: [0.5, 0.5, 0.001, 0.001] },\n      uRectPresence: { value: 0 },\n      uExponent: { value: clamp(exponent, 2, 16) },\n      uWhip: { value: Math.max(whip, 0.02) },\n      uPointer: { value: [0.5, 0.5] },\n      uPointerPresence: { value: 0 },\n      uPointerRadius: { value: Math.max(pointerRadius, 0) },\n      uPointerWhip: { value: Math.max(pointerWhip, 0.02) },\n      uColorA: { value: new Color(colorA) },\n      uColorB: { value: new Color(colorB) },\n      uGlowColor: { value: new Color(glowColor) },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n    };\n\n    const program = new Program(gl, {\n      vertex: SUPERELLIPSE_TEXT_AVOIDANCE_VERT,\n      fragment: SUPERELLIPSE_TEXT_AVOIDANCE_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      // ogl culls BACK faces by default and the quad's winding is not\n      // guaranteed to face the way the driver expects. A culled particle is not\n      // dim — it is absent.\n      cullFace: false,\n    });\n    // Additive, overriding the source-over that `transparent: true` selected.\n    // Order independent, so a thousand overlapping instances need no sort.\n    program.setBlendFunc(gl.ONE, gl.ONE);\n\n    const mesh = new Mesh(gl, { geometry, program });\n\n    el.insertBefore(canvas, el.firstChild);\n    canvas.style.display = 'block';\n\n    /* Listeners on the CANVAS, not the window. A document-level handler\n       outlives the renderer it was written for, and the rect it measures is not\n       the rect being drawn into — after a scroll the hole sits at an offset\n       from the cursor. Passive, because nothing here calls preventDefault and a\n       non-passive move handler makes the compositor wait for JS on every event. */\n    const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (rect.width === 0 || rect.height === 0) return;\n      target.current = {\n        x: (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width,\n        y: 1 - (event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height,\n      };\n      inside.current = true;\n    };\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      // Position is left where it is. Tweening it back to the centre drags a\n      // visible furrow across the field on the way out.\n      inside.current = false;\n    };\n\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave, { passive: true });\n    canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave, { passive: true });\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      renderer.setSize(w, h);\n      // `setSize` writes literal px into the canvas's inline style, which wins\n      // over any class. Re-asserting percentages keeps it fluid between resize\n      // notifications.\n      canvas.style.width = '100%';\n      canvas.style.height = '100%';\n\n      uniforms.uResolution.value = [gl.drawingBufferWidth, gl.drawingBufferHeight];\n      uniforms.uPixelRatio.value = renderer.dpr;\n\n      // Population from area, not a fixed number: the same density reads right\n      // in a 320px card and a 2000px hero. The buffers already hold `total`\n      // particles in shuffled order, so a prefix of them is still spatially\n      // even — no rebuild, no reallocation, no new GPU memory.\n      const megapixels = (w * h) / 1_000_000;\n      const count = clamp(Math.round(megapixels * density), MIN_COUNT, total);\n      geometry.setInstancedCount(count);\n    };\n\n    const draw = (elapsed: number) => {\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      renderer.render({ scene: mesh });\n    };\n\n    resize(size.current.width, size.current.height);\n    stage.current = { renderer, geometry, program, mesh, uniforms, resize, draw };\n    measure();\n    draw(0);\n\n    /* A webfont swap changes the text box after the first measurement, and\n       nothing about a font swap resizes the element's border box on every\n       engine — so the ResizeObserver alone is not enough. */\n    let cancelled = false;\n    if (typeof document !== 'undefined' && 'fonts' in document) {\n      void document.fonts.ready.then(() => {\n        if (!cancelled) measure();\n      });\n    }\n\n    // Nothing repaints a lost context of its own accord: unhandled, the panel\n    // sits blank behind the text indefinitely with no fallback, so the loss is\n    // folded into the unsupported path. `preventDefault` keeps a restore legal.\n    const onLost = (event: Event): void => {\n      event.preventDefault();\n      setUnsupported(true);\n    };\n    canvas.addEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true;\n      stage.current = null;\n      canvas.removeEventListener('webglcontextlost', onLost);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      canvas.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onLeave);\n      // ogl has no `dispose()`: `remove()` on the geometry deletes its VAOs and\n      // every buffer, `remove()` on the program deletes the shaders and the\n      // program, and only then is the context worth handing back. Dropping the\n      // context is the part that matters — the per-process budget is about\n      // sixteen, and exceeding it kills someone else's oldest context.\n      geometry.remove();\n      program.remove();\n      loseContext(gl);\n      canvas.width = 0;\n      canvas.height = 0;\n      canvas.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, density, maxCount, minSize, maxSize, seed]);\n\n  // Colours go into the live uniforms rather than being effect deps, so\n  // recolouring never costs a context.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s) return;\n    s.uniforms.uColorA.value.set(colorA);\n    s.uniforms.uColorB.value.set(colorB);\n    s.uniforms.uGlowColor.value.set(glowColor);\n    const bg = new Color(background);\n    s.renderer.gl.clearColor(bg.r, bg.g, bg.b, 1);\n  }, [colorA, colorB, glowColor, background]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect: a reflow costs a `setSize`, an instanced-count\n  // change and a re-measure, never a buffer rebuild.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    measure();\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  // The text box changes on its own too — wrapping to a new line, a font swap,\n  // or different children entirely.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    measure();\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [textSize.width, textSize.height, margin, children]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt, elapsed) => {\n      const s = stage.current;\n      if (!s) return;\n      const cfg = live.current;\n\n      const aim = target.current;\n      if (aim) {\n        follow.current.x = damp(follow.current.x, aim.x, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n        follow.current.y = damp(follow.current.y, aim.y, cfg.smoothing, dt);\n      }\n      presence.current = damp(\n        presence.current,\n        inside.current && aim !== null ? 1 : 0,\n        PRESENCE_SMOOTHING,\n        dt,\n      );\n\n      const u = s.uniforms;\n      // Mutated in place. Assigning a fresh tuple allocates an array a frame and\n      // defeats ogl's redundant-uniform cache, which compares by length first\n      // and then element by element against the array it kept.\n      u.uPointer.value[0] = follow.current.x;\n      u.uPointer.value[1] = follow.current.y;\n      u.uPointerPresence.value = presence.current;\n      u.uPointerRadius.value = Math.max(cfg.pointerRadius, 0);\n      u.uPointerWhip.value = Math.max(cfg.pointerWhip, 0.02);\n      u.uExponent.value = clamp(cfg.exponent, 2, 16);\n      u.uWhip.value = Math.max(cfg.whip, 0.02);\n      u.uOpacity.value = cfg.opacity;\n\n      s.draw(elapsed);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n\n  /* Reduced motion is a composed still, not an empty box: one frame with the\n     text rect fully avoided and the pointer parked off to one side, so the\n     shape of the field is exactly what a moving version would show. The\n     listeners stay attached and their target is never consumed. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = stage.current;\n    if (!s || !reduced || !ready) return;\n    const [px = 0.3, py = 0.34] = STILL_POINTER;\n    s.uniforms.uPointer.value[0] = px;\n    s.uniforms.uPointer.value[1] = py;\n    s.uniforms.uPointerPresence.value = 1;\n    s.draw(STILL_ELAPSED);\n  }, [reduced, ready, width, height]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the three pointer listeners from the canvas, cancels the pending `document.fonts.ready` callback with a flag, nulls the stage ref so an in-flight frame cannot touch a dead renderer, then `geometry.remove()` (deletes the VAOs and every buffer), `program.remove()` (deletes the shaders and the program), `loseContext(gl)`, zeroes the canvas dimensions and removes it from the DOM. ogl has no `dispose()`, and dropping the context is the part that matters — the per-process budget is about sixteen, and exceeding it kills the oldest, which belongs to someone else.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The loop never starts. One frame is drawn with the text rect fully avoided and the pointer parked off to one side at full presence, so the still shows exactly the shape a moving version produces — the drift is the only thing lost. Re-measuring after a font swap redraws that single frame rather than starting a loop. The listeners stay attached but their target is never consumed.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `normalize()` for the push direction. It is NaN at the origin, and NaN in `gl_Position` discards the entire primitive — so the bug looks like missing particles, not like a maths error.\n- `pow()` with a negative base or a zero base. Both are undefined in GLSL, both are reached on the axes of a symmetric field, and the result is driver-dependent: fine on the machine it was written on, black on someone else's.\n- Clamping interior particles onto the shell instead of remapping their radius. Every particle inside lands exactly on the outline, so the shape is one dense curve with a dead interior behind it — visibly a stencil rather than a field flowing around something.\n- Hard-coding the text rect, or deriving it from a character count. It is wrong the moment a webfont swaps, the line rewraps, or the container narrows, and the hole drifts off the words while still looking deliberate.\n- Relying on the ResizeObserver alone for the text box. A font swap does not necessarily change the element's border box on every engine, so the measurement has to be repeated after `document.fonts.ready` as well.\n- Aspect-correcting the text rect. It was measured in element space; correcting it clears a region visibly narrower than the headline on a wide container, which is worse than not avoiding at all.\n- Forgetting to aspect-correct the POINTER disc. Exactly the opposite case — a screen-space shape left uncorrected is an obvious ellipse on anything but a square container.\n- Writing pointer events straight into `uPointer`. A high-rate trackpad delivers a staircase, and after a stalled frame it is a jump that drags the hole across the field in one step. The listener records a target; the loop damps toward it.\n- Damping with a per-frame constant (`x += (t - x) * 0.1`) instead of `damp(..., dt)`. The follow is visibly tighter at 120Hz than at 60Hz, so the feel is tuned to whichever display it was authored on.\n- Scattering with `rng()` twice. The clumps and voids of a uniform random field are indistinguishable from more avoidance holes once the real one exists. A jittered lattice, shuffled so the instanced-count prefix is still spatially even, costs the same.\n- Leaving ogl's default BACK-face culling on. The quad winding is not guaranteed to face the way the driver expects, and a culled particle is not dim — it is absent, so the field renders empty on some machines and fine on others.\n- Owning the canvas in React while calling `loseContext()` on cleanup. Under `reactStrictMode` the effect runs twice and the second `getContext` on that element returns the dead context: blank in development, fine in production.\n- Letting the text layer swallow pointer events. It sits above the canvas, so without `pointer-events: none` the pointer hole disappears whenever the cursor crosses the headline — precisely where the effect is most visible.\n- Re-measuring through React state. A measurement that triggers a render can trigger another measurement; the rect is written straight into the uniform instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `children` is the headline and is rendered as real DOM above the field, so it is selectable, searchable and styled with ordinary classes. The component only reads its box.\n- The text layer is `pointer-events: none` so the canvas underneath keeps receiving moves. Anything interactive belongs outside it.\n- The same `avoid()` function serves both fields; the pointer is just exponent 2 with a wider whip. Adding a third avoided region is one more call, not one more code path.\n- Blending is additive with an opaque clear colour, so a thousand overlapping instances need no depth sort and the whole field is one draw call. The fragment output is premultiplied because the destination is only ever added to.\n- Population is derived from container area and selected with `setInstancedCount` out of buffers allocated once at `maxCount`, so a resize changes the count without touching GPU memory.\n- The scatter runs on `createRng(seed)`, never `Math.random()`, so the field is identical on every load and a visual regression is a real regression.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe headline sits in a clear zone whose shape is a squircle, not a circle and not a rectangle: the gap above and below the line is the same as the gap at its ends, and the corners are rounded without being round. A brighter rim traces that shape. Resize the container until the headline rewraps to two lines — the clear zone re-fits within a frame, with no visible sweep from the old rect. Move the pointer in and a second, softer hole follows it with a short even lag that is identical on a 60Hz and a 120Hz display; the hole stays circular on a wide container and does not disappear when the pointer crosses the headline. No particle sits inside the clear zone and none pile into a single dense outline. Reload twice: the field is identical. With reduced motion the panel is one composed still with the same shape.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "support-cta-card": "# Build: Support CTA Card\n\nThe contact strip that closes a FAQ. It shows the people who answer as overlapping kit `Avatar`s, then a title and one line of copy, then a single secondary action that is a link when given `href` and a button when given `onAction`. A short accent beam drifts back and forth along the top border. The row stacks below 36rem of container width.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA \"Still have a question?\" card: stacked initials avatars, a line of copy, one contact action and an accent beam on the top border.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The first `maxAvatars` people render as `size=\"md\"` (32px) kit Avatars with 12px initials in a `-space-x-1.5` row. Each has a `ring-2 ring-background-primary-default`, so every circle is cut out of the one behind it in the card colour. Anyone past the limit becomes one neutral \"+N\" avatar. Neutral avatars use `bg-background-quaternary-default`, because in dark mode the kit neutral avatar is the same colour as the card. The row is a single `role=\"img\"` with an accessible label such as \"Answered by Ada Park, Lin Okafor, Sam Ruiz and 2 more\", and the individual avatars are `aria-hidden`.\n2. The action branches on `href`. With a link it renders the kit `ButtonLink`, targets `_blank` by default and adds `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` when it does. Without one it renders the kit `Button` wired to `onAction`. Both use the secondary variant and take the optional `actionIcon` as `leadingIcon`.\n3. The beam is an `aria-hidden` layer at `-inset-px`: it covers the border box and clips to the card radius through `rounded-[inherit]`. Inside, a track a quarter of the card wide holds a 1px `via-accent-500` gradient line and a blurred `via-accent-400/25` glow under it. A hoisted `<style href precedence>` keyframe moves the track from `translateX(-100%)` to `translateX(400%)`, from just past the left edge to just past the right, over 7s, `infinite alternate`.\n4. The root is an `@container`, so the row follows the card width: `flex-col items-start` below `@xl` (36rem) and `flex-row items-center` from it. That is why the same component works as a full-width strip and in a 360px sidebar.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `beam sweep = 7s cubic-bezier(0.45, 0, 0.55, 1), infinite alternate`\n  A symmetric ease-in-out with `alternate` makes the beam slow down at each end and drift back instead of jumping to the start. 7s keeps it ambient rather than attention-seeking.\n- `beam track = w-1/4, translateX(-100%) → translateX(400%)`\n  Percent translations are relative to the track, so -100% to 400% of a quarter-width track starts just outside the left edge and ends just outside the right edge.\n- `avatar overlap = -space-x-1.5 (6px) on 32px circles, 12px initials, ring-2 in the card colour`\n  A 6px overlap with 12px initials leaves the ring just clear of each pair of letters, so no initial is cut. The ring has to match the card surface (`background-primary-default`) in both themes, or the cut-outs show as outlines.\n- `stack breakpoint = @xl (36rem of container width)`\n  Below about 576px the action button squeezes the copy into three lines, so it moves under the copy instead.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SupportCtaCard(props: { title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; people?: { name: string; initials: string; color?: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\" }[]; maxAvatars?: number; actionLabel?: ReactNode; actionIcon?: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>; href?: string; target?: string; onAction?: () => void; beam?: boolean; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode` — default `\"Still have a question?\"`\n  Card heading.\n- `description: ReactNode` — default `\"We answer in the open, usually the same day.\"`\n  One line under the title.\n- `people: SupportPerson[]` — default `[]`\n  `{ name, initials, color? }` for each person who answers. With an empty list there is no avatar row.\n- `maxAvatars: number` — default `3`\n  How many avatars to show before collapsing the rest into \"+N\".\n- `actionLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Get in touch\"`\n  Action text.\n- `actionIcon: ComponentType`\n  Leading icon component for the action, e.g. `RiDiscordFill`.\n- `href: string`\n  Renders the action as a link.\n- `target: string` — default `\"_blank\"`\n  Link target when `href` is set. `_blank` adds `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`.\n- `onAction: () => void`\n  Click handler when the action is a button (no `href`).\n- `beam: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the drifting accent beam on the top border.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4` — default `3`\n  Heading level of the title.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SupportCtaCard({\n  title = \"Still have a question?\",\n  description = \"We answer in the open, usually the same day.\",\n  people = [],\n  maxAvatars = 3,\n  actionLabel = \"Get in touch\",\n  actionIcon,\n  href,\n  target,\n  onAction,\n  beam = true,\n  headingLevel = 3,\n  className,\n}: SupportCtaCardProps) {\n  const shown = people.slice(0, Math.max(0, maxAvatars));\n  const extra = people.length - shown.length;\n  const Heading = HEADINGS[headingLevel];\n  const linkTarget = target ?? \"_blank\";\n\n  return (\n    <div className={cx(styles.card, className)}>\n      {beam ? (\n        <>\n          <style href=\"kinetic-support-cta-card\" precedence=\"medium\">\n            {BEAM_CSS}\n          </style>\n          <div aria-hidden className={styles.beamLayer}>\n            <div className={styles.beamTrack}>\n              <span className={styles.beamGlow} />\n              <span className={styles.beamLine} />\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </>\n      ) : null}\n\n      <div className={styles.row}>\n        {shown.length > 0 ? (\n          <div role=\"img\" aria-label={listNames(shown.map((p) => p.name), extra)} className={styles.avatars}>\n            {shown.map((person) => (\n              <Avatar\n                key={person.name}\n                size=\"md\"\n                color={person.color ?? \"neutral\"}\n                initials={person.initials}\n                aria-hidden\n                className={cx(styles.avatar, (person.color ?? \"neutral\") === \"neutral\" && styles.neutral)}\n              />\n            ))}\n            {extra > 0 ? <Avatar size=\"md\" initials={`+${extra}`} aria-hidden className={cx(styles.avatar, styles.neutral)} /> : null}\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n\n        <div className={styles.copy}>\n          <Heading className={styles.title}>{title}</Heading>\n          {description ? <p className={styles.description}>{description}</p> : null}\n        </div>\n\n        {href ? (\n          <ButtonLink\n            href={href}\n            target={linkTarget}\n            rel={linkTarget === \"_blank\" ? \"noopener noreferrer\" : undefined}\n            variant=\"secondary\"\n            leadingIcon={actionIcon}\n            className=\"shrink-0\"\n          >\n            {actionLabel}\n          </ButtonLink>\n        ) : (\n          <Button variant=\"secondary\" leadingIcon={actionIcon} onClick={onAction} className=\"shrink-0\">\n            {actionLabel}\n          </Button>\n        )}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The beam is a CSS animation, and there are no effects, timers or listeners. The hoisted `<style>` is deduplicated by its `href`, so many cards share one stylesheet.\n\n**Reduced motion.** A `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` rule in the hoisted stylesheet removes the beam animation and parks the track at `translateX(150%)`, its centred position. The beam stays as a still highlight on the border.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Clipping the beam with `overflow-hidden` on the card itself. That clips at the padding box, so the beam sits one pixel under the border and any focus ring that reaches past the card is cut off. A separate `-inset-px` layer with `rounded-[inherit]` puts the beam on the border without clipping the content.\n- Giving every avatar its own label. A screen reader then reads five separate images. One `role=\"img\"` with a combined name says it once.\n- Opening `_blank` links without `rel=\"noopener\"`. The opened page gets `window.opener` and can navigate the original tab.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Set `beam={false}` when several cards sit together and only one should draw the eye, as the demo does for its second card.\n- Initials are passed in rather than derived from names, so names with particles or non-Latin scripts get the initials you choose.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt full width the avatars, the copy and the action sit in one row, and a short blue beam drifts along the top border and back. In a 360px container the action drops under the copy. Five people with `maxAvatars` 3 show three initials and \"+2\". Clicking the action calls `onAction`. With `href` it is a link opening in a new tab. With reduced motion the beam sits still in the middle of the border.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "surface-sampled-particles": "# Build: Area-Weighted Surface Particles\n\nTurns any object — one primitive or a whole loaded model — into a particle shell whose density is uniform over the surface rather than over the mesh list. One cumulative distribution is built over the world-space area of every triangle in the object and sampled by binary search, so a face a thousand times larger than its neighbour is picked a thousand times more often. The shell then assembles bottom-up on a per-particle window, breathes along its own sampled normals, and is ringed by orbit lines whose radii are measured from the cloud instead of hard-coded.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAny mesh becomes a particle shell of even density, assembling bottom-up, with orbit rings measured off the sampled silhouette.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Every mesh under the source object is traversed and each triangle is read in WORLD space via `matrixWorld`. `n = (b - a) × (c - a)` gives area as `|n| / 2` and the face normal as `n / |n|` in one operation; the running area total is written into a `Float64Array` cumulative table, one entry per triangle.\n2. To place a particle, `rng() * totalArea` is looked up in that table by binary search. This is inverse-transform sampling over area, which is where per-mesh weighting comes from for free — the alternative, `count / meshCount` points per mesh, gives a dense speckle on the small parts and a sparse haze on the large ones, and no tuning fixes it because the error is structural.\n3. The point inside the chosen triangle uses barycentric coordinates by reflection: draw `u, v` in the unit square and if `u + v > 1` replace them with `1 - u, 1 - v`, folding the far half back across the diagonal. `p = a + (b - a) * u + (c - a) * v` is then exactly uniform over the triangle.\n4. The cloud is centred on its bounding-box centre and divided by its farthest sample, so the shell always has bounding-sphere radius 1. Camera distance follows from the frustum rather than from taste: `d = 1 / sin(min(vFovHalf, atan(tan(vFovHalf) * aspect))) * 1.14` fits the sphere in whichever axis is tighter.\n5. Assembly order is the particle's own normalised target height, so `form = smoothstep(order * 0.45, order * 0.45 + 0.55, uForm)` builds the shell bottom-up — the object is constructed rather than faded in. `uForm` is damped toward 1 with `damp(form, 1, 0.05, dt)`, so the build takes about the same two seconds at 60 and at 120 Hz.\n6. `eff = uScatter * (1 - form)` collapses the pre-assembly cloud into the arrival: each particle is pushed out along `normalize(aRand.xyz - 0.5 + 0.0001)` by `(0.6 + aRand.x * 2.2)` radii plus a slow per-axis sinusoid, and both fade to nothing exactly as that particle's own window closes.\n7. Point size is perspective-corrected in the vertex shader: `uSize * jitter * uSizeScale / max(0.1, -mv.z)`, where `uSizeScale` is `pixelRatio * cameraDistance`. That makes `uSize` a literal CSS-pixel diameter for a particle at the centre of the shell, rather than the tuned constant this term usually is.\n8. Orbit rings are measured, not authored: the cloud is cut into `ringCount` horizontal bands, the radii `sqrt(x² + z²)` within each band are sorted, and the 92nd percentile times 1.06 is the ring radius. Bands with fewer than 12 samples are skipped. All rings go into one buffer as `LineSegments`, so the set is one draw call.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `count = 9000`\n  Particles, one buffer, one draw call. Enough that the silhouette of a torus knot reads as a solid surface at preview size; the cost is fill rather than vertex count, and doubling it doubles the blended pixel area rather than the detail.\n- `size / maxSize = 2.4 / 22 CSS px`\n  Diameter at the centre of the shell, and the ceiling after 1/z growth. The clamp is not cosmetic: a particle that drifts close to the near plane would otherwise ask for a point size in the thousands, which drivers answer by silently capping — or by dropping the draw call.\n- `size jitter = 0.55 + aRand.w * 1.35`\n  A 3.4x spread. With every particle the same size the cloud reads as a halftone screen; much wider and the largest sprites stop looking like members of the same population.\n- `STAGGER / WINDOW = 0.45 / 0.55`\n  They sum to exactly 1.0, so the last particle to start is also the one that finishes as `uForm` reaches 1 — no dead time at either end of the build. Raising STAGGER past ~0.6 leaves the base fully assembled while the top has not begun, which reads as two separate animations.\n- `FORM_SMOOTHING = 0.05`\n  Fraction of the assembly gap remaining after one second, which with the stagger gives a build of roughly two seconds. This is a `damp` smoothing value, not a per-frame lerp — the per-frame form of the same curve converges twice as fast at 120 Hz.\n- `FORM_EPSILON = 0.9995`\n  `damp` is asymptotic and never arrives, but the last stagger window needs `uForm` to reach exactly 1.0 or the topmost particles stay a hair short of their target forever. Past this threshold it snaps.\n- `puff = 0.02 of the shell radius`\n  Breathing amplitude along the sampled normal. At 2% the surface undulates while staying a surface; past roughly 6% the shell loses its silhouette and reads as a cloud that happens to be shaped like the object.\n- `scatter = 0.9`\n  Pre-assembly spread in shell radii, giving a cloud extending to about 2.6 radii. Small enough that the unformed state is still recognisably centred on the object; much larger and the particles start their travel off-screen, so the build begins with an empty frame.\n- `RING_PERCENTILE = 0.92`\n  Which measured radius in a band becomes the ring. The maximum is dragged outward by a single stray sample on a spike; the mean sinks inside the silhouette and the ring cuts through the shell.\n- `MIN_BAND_SAMPLES / MIN_RING_RADIUS = 12 / 0.08`\n  A band with almost nothing in it is a gap in the model, and a ring there would hang in empty air; a measured radius near zero is a dot rather than a ring. Both are skipped, which is why the ring count is a maximum and not a promise.\n- `FIT_PADDING = 1.14`\n  Slack between the unit sphere and the frustum. It has to cover the breathing, the ring margin and the fact that a sprite is drawn centred on its vertex, so a particle exactly on the silhouette still needs half its own width of room.\n- `TILT = 0.2 rad`\n  Static tilt applied on a parent group. Without it the measured rings project to straight lines and read as scan lines rather than orbits; much past 0.4 the top band occludes the shell.\n- `RING_SEGMENTS = 128`\n  Vertices per ring. Roughly where a hairline circle stops showing flats at 600px wide, and cheap enough that four rings are still 1024 line vertices in a single buffer.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SurfaceSampledParticles(props: { source?: () => THREE.Object3D | null; count?: number; size?: number; maxSize?: number; spin?: number; puff?: number; scatter?: number; ringCount?: number; ringOpacity?: number; opacity?: number; colorBase?: string; colorTip?: string; ringColor?: string; fov?: number; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `source: () => THREE.Object3D | null`\n  Builds the object to sample. Measured on the CPU, never added to the scene, disposed once sampling finishes. Held in a ref, so changing it does not itself trigger a rebuild.\n- `count: number` — default `9000`\n  Particle count. One buffer, one draw call; cost is linear in fill.\n- `size: number` — default `2.4`\n  Particle diameter in CSS pixels for a particle at the centre of the shell.\n- `maxSize: number` — default `22`\n  Ceiling in CSS pixels on the perspective-grown size.\n- `spin: number` — default `0.16`\n  Turntable speed in radians per second.\n- `puff: number` — default `0.02`\n  Breathing amplitude along the sampled normal, as a fraction of the shell radius.\n- `scatter: number` — default `0.9`\n  How far the pre-assembly cloud spreads, in shell radii.\n- `ringCount: number` — default `4`\n  Horizontal bands to measure orbit rings from. Bands that are too empty are skipped, so this is a maximum. 0 disables the rings.\n- `ringOpacity: number` — default `0.3`\n  Ring opacity once the shell has assembled; it ramps with the build.\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.9`\n  Overall alpha multiplier applied in the vertex shader.\n- `colorBase: string` — default `'#6b7783'`\n  Tint at the base of the shell.\n- `colorTip: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Tint at the top of the shell.\n- `ringColor: string` — default `'#8d9aa6'`\n  Ring colour.\n- `fov: number` — default `32`\n  Vertical field of view in degrees. Camera distance and the perspective size term are both derived from it.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'surface-sampled-particles'`\n  Seeds the sampling and the per-particle randoms, so the same shell is produced on the server and on every reload.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Sample `count` points over the total world-space surface area of everything\n * under `root`, then centre and scale the result so its bounding sphere has\n * radius 1.\n */\nfunction sampleSurface(root: THREE.Object3D, count: number, rng: Rng): Shell | null {\n  // World matrices, not local ones: a mesh scaled 10x has 100x the area, and\n  // sampling in local space would weight it as though it did not.\n  root.updateMatrixWorld(true);\n\n  const meshes: THREE.Mesh[] = [];\n  root.traverse((obj) => {\n    const mesh = obj as THREE.Mesh;\n    if (mesh.isMesh && mesh.geometry) meshes.push(mesh);\n  });\n\n  let capacity = 0;\n  for (const mesh of meshes) {\n    const position = mesh.geometry.getAttribute('position');\n    if (!position) continue;\n    const index = mesh.geometry.getIndex();\n    capacity += Math.floor((index ? index.count : position.count) / 3);\n  }\n  if (capacity === 0) return null;\n\n  const tri = new Float32Array(capacity * 9);\n  const triNormal = new Float32Array(capacity * 3);\n  // Float64 for the running total. At tens of thousands of triangles a Float32\n  // accumulator loses the smallest ones entirely — they land inside the\n  // rounding error of the sum and become unreachable by any sample.\n  const cumulative = new Float64Array(capacity);\n\n  const a = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const b = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const c = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const ab = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const ac = new THREE.Vector3();\n  const n = new THREE.Vector3();\n\n  let triCount = 0;\n  let total = 0;\n\n  for (const mesh of meshes) {\n    const position = mesh.geometry.getAttribute('position');\n    if (!position) continue;\n    const index = mesh.geometry.getIndex();\n    const faces = Math.floor((index ? index.count : position.count) / 3);\n\n    for (let f = 0; f < faces; f += 1) {\n      const i0 = index ? index.getX(f * 3) : f * 3;\n      const i1 = index ? index.getX(f * 3 + 1) : f * 3 + 1;\n      const i2 = index ? index.getX(f * 3 + 2) : f * 3 + 2;\n\n      a.fromBufferAttribute(position, i0).applyMatrix4(mesh.matrixWorld);\n      b.fromBufferAttribute(position, i1).applyMatrix4(mesh.matrixWorld);\n      c.fromBufferAttribute(position, i2).applyMatrix4(mesh.matrixWorld);\n\n      ab.subVectors(b, a);\n      ac.subVectors(c, a);\n      n.crossVectors(ab, ac);\n      // Half the cross product's magnitude is the triangle's area, and the\n      // cross product's direction is its normal — one operation for both.\n      const area = n.length() * 0.5;\n      // Degenerate triangles are common in generated and exported geometry.\n      // Keeping them would put zero-width steps in the distribution and, worse,\n      // normalising a zero-length normal yields NaN.\n      if (!(area > 0)) continue;\n      n.divideScalar(area * 2);\n\n      const t9 = triCount * 9;\n      tri[t9] = a.x;\n      tri[t9 + 1] = a.y;\n      tri[t9 + 2] = a.z;\n      tri[t9 + 3] = ab.x;\n      tri[t9 + 4] = ab.y;\n      tri[t9 + 5] = ab.z;\n      tri[t9 + 6] = ac.x;\n      tri[t9 + 7] = ac.y;\n      tri[t9 + 8] = ac.z;\n\n      const t3 = triCount * 3;\n      triNormal[t3] = n.x;\n      triNormal[t3 + 1] = n.y;\n      triNormal[t3 + 2] = n.z;\n\n      total += area;\n      cumulative[triCount] = total;\n      triCount += 1;\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (triCount === 0 || total <= 0) return null;\n\n  const positions = new Float32Array(count * 3);\n  const normals = new Float32Array(count * 3);\n  const rands = new Float32Array(count * 4);\n  const orders = new Float32Array(count);\n\n  let minX = Infinity;\n  let minY = Infinity;\n  let minZ = Infinity;\n  let maxX = -Infinity;\n  let maxY = -Infinity;\n  let maxZ = -Infinity;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    // Inverse-transform sampling: a uniform draw across the cumulative area\n    // picks each triangle in proportion to its own area, which is exactly the\n    // per-mesh weighting the naive loop gets wrong.\n    const t = lowerBound(cumulative, triCount, rng() * total);\n    const t9 = t * 9;\n    const t3 = t * 3;\n\n    // Uniform barycentric coordinates by reflection: draw a point in the unit\n    // square and fold the far half back across the diagonal. Cheaper than the\n    // sqrt form and exactly uniform, where using u and v raw would pile\n    // samples into one corner of every triangle.\n    let u = rng();\n    let v = rng();\n    if (u + v > 1) {\n      u = 1 - u;\n      v = 1 - v;\n    }\n\n    const px = (tri[t9] ?? 0) + (tri[t9 + 3] ?? 0) * u + (tri[t9 + 6] ?? 0) * v;\n    const py = (tri[t9 + 1] ?? 0) + (tri[t9 + 4] ?? 0) * u + (tri[t9 + 7] ?? 0) * v;\n    const pz = (tri[t9 + 2] ?? 0) + (tri[t9 + 5] ?? 0) * u + (tri[t9 + 8] ?? 0) * v;\n\n    positions[i * 3] = px;\n    positions[i * 3 + 1] = py;\n    positions[i * 3 + 2] = pz;\n\n    normals[i * 3] = triNormal[t3] ?? 0;\n    normals[i * 3 + 1] = triNormal[t3 + 1] ?? 0;\n    normals[i * 3 + 2] = triNormal[t3 + 2] ?? 1;\n\n    rands[i * 4] = rng();\n    rands[i * 4 + 1] = rng();\n    rands[i * 4 + 2] = rng();\n    rands[i * 4 + 3] = rng();\n\n    if (px < minX) minX = px;\n    if (py < minY) minY = py;\n    if (pz < minZ) minZ = pz;\n    if (px > maxX) maxX = px;\n    if (py > maxY) maxY = py;\n    if (pz > maxZ) maxZ = pz;\n  }\n\n  // Fit to a unit bounding sphere around the box centre. Normalising here is\n  // what lets `puff`, `scatter` and the camera distance be authored once and\n  // stay correct for a source of any scale — including a model in metres.\n  const cx = (minX + maxX) * 0.5;\n  const cy = (minY + maxY) * 0.5;\n  const cz = (minZ + maxZ) * 0.5;\n\n  let maxDist = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    const dx = (positions[i * 3] ?? 0) - cx;\n    const dy = (positions[i * 3 + 1] ?? 0) - cy;\n    const dz = (positions[i * 3 + 2] ?? 0) - cz;\n    const d = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz);\n    if (d > maxDist) maxDist = d;\n  }\n  const scale = maxDist > 0 ? SHELL_RADIUS / maxDist : 1;\n\n  let shellMinY = Infinity;\n  let shellMaxY = -Infinity;\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    const x = ((positions[i * 3] ?? 0) - cx) * scale;\n    const y = ((positions[i * 3 + 1] ?? 0) - cy) * scale;\n    const z = ((positions[i * 3 + 2] ?? 0) - cz) * scale;\n    positions[i * 3] = x;\n    positions[i * 3 + 1] = y;\n    positions[i * 3 + 2] = z;\n    if (y < shellMinY) shellMinY = y;\n    if (y > shellMaxY) shellMaxY = y;\n  }\n\n  // Assembly order is the particle's own target height, so the shell builds\n  // bottom-up. Deriving order from the destination rather than from the buffer\n  // index is what makes the build read as construction instead of as noise\n  // resolving.\n  const span = Math.max(1e-6, shellMaxY - shellMinY);\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    orders[i] = ((positions[i * 3 + 1] ?? 0) - shellMinY) / span;\n  }\n\n  return { positions, normals, rands, orders, minY: shellMinY, maxY: shellMaxY };\n}\n\n/**\n * Measure the cloud's silhouette radius in `bands` horizontal slices. The ring\n * radii come out of the geometry that was actually sampled, so swapping the\n * source does not need a new set of hand-tuned numbers.\n */\nfunction measureRings(shell: Shell, bands: number, count: number): Ring[] {\n  if (bands <= 0) return [];\n  const span = shell.maxY - shell.minY;\n  if (!(span > 0)) return [];\n\n  const rings: Ring[] = [];\n  for (let band = 0; band < bands; band += 1) {\n    const y0 = shell.minY + (span * band) / bands;\n    const y1 = shell.minY + (span * (band + 1)) / bands;\n\n    const radii: number[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      const y = shell.positions[i * 3 + 1] ?? 0;\n      if (y < y0 || y >= y1) continue;\n      const x = shell.positions[i * 3] ?? 0;\n      const z = shell.positions[i * 3 + 2] ?? 0;\n      radii.push(Math.sqrt(x * x + z * z));\n    }\n    if (radii.length < MIN_BAND_SAMPLES) continue;\n\n    radii.sort((p, q) => p - q);\n    const at = Math.min(radii.length - 1, Math.floor(RING_PERCENTILE * (radii.length - 1)));\n    const radius = (radii[at] ?? 0) * RING_MARGIN;\n    if (radius < MIN_RING_RADIUS) continue;\n\n    rings.push({ y: (y0 + y1) * 0.5, radius });\n  }\n  return rings;\n}\n\n/** All rings in one buffer, so the whole set is one draw call. */\nfunction buildRingGeometry(rings: Ring[]): THREE.BufferGeometry {\n  const vertices = new Float32Array(rings.length * RING_SEGMENTS * 2 * 3);\n  let at = 0;\n  for (const ring of rings) {\n    for (let s = 0; s < RING_SEGMENTS; s += 1) {\n      const a0 = (s / RING_SEGMENTS) * Math.PI * 2;\n      const a1 = ((s + 1) / RING_SEGMENTS) * Math.PI * 2;\n      vertices[at] = Math.cos(a0) * ring.radius;\n      vertices[at + 1] = ring.y;\n      vertices[at + 2] = Math.sin(a0) * ring.radius;\n      vertices[at + 3] = Math.cos(a1) * ring.radius;\n      vertices[at + 4] = ring.y;\n      vertices[at + 5] = Math.sin(a1) * ring.radius;\n      at += 6;\n    }\n  }\n  const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n  geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(vertices, 3));\n  return geometry;\n}\n\nexport function SurfaceSampledParticles({\n  source,\n  count = 9000,\n  size = 2.4,\n  maxSize = 22,\n  spin = 0.16,\n  puff = 0.02,\n  scatter = 0.9,\n  ringCount = 4,\n  ringOpacity = 0.3,\n  opacity = 0.9,\n  colorBase = '#6b7783',\n  colorTip = '#ffffff',\n  ringColor = '#8d9aa6',\n  fov = 32,\n  seed = 'surface-sampled-particles',\n  className,\n}: SurfaceSampledParticlesProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const sourceRef = useRef(source);\n  sourceRef.current = source;\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const box = useRef({ width, height });\n  box.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Mirrored into a ref for the same reason `source` is: the build effect below\n  // has to know whether to hand back a still frame or a live one, but making\n  // `reduced` a dependency of it would re-sample the entire surface and rebuild\n  // the WebGL context every time the OS setting is toggled. The ref is read,\n  // never depended on.\n  const reducedRef = useRef(reduced);\n  reducedRef.current = reduced;\n\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // The source object exists only to be measured. It is never added to the\n    // scene, so nothing about it is ever uploaded to the GPU, and it is\n    // disposed the moment sampling is done.\n    const specimen = sourceRef.current?.() ?? buildDefaultSpecimen();\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const total = Math.max(1, Math.round(count));\n    const shell = sampleSurface(specimen, total, rng);\n    disposeObject(specimen);\n    if (!shell) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n    const dpr = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(fov, 1, 0.1, 100);\n\n    // Tilt outside, turntable inside: composing both on one Euler makes the\n    // spin axis tilt with the object, which wobbles instead of turning.\n    const tilt = new THREE.Group();\n    tilt.rotation.x = TILT;\n    const turntable = new THREE.Group();\n    tilt.add(turntable);\n    scene.add(tilt);\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(shell.positions, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aNormal', new THREE.BufferAttribute(shell.normals, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aRand', new THREE.BufferAttribute(shell.rands, 4));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aOrder', new THREE.BufferAttribute(shell.orders, 1));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uForm: { value: 0 },\n      uScatter: { value: scatter },\n      uPuff: { value: puff },\n      uSize: { value: size },\n      uMaxSize: { value: maxSize * dpr },\n      uSizeScale: { value: dpr },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uColorBase: { value: new THREE.Color(colorBase) },\n      uColorTip: { value: new THREE.Color(colorTip) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: SURFACE_SAMPLED_PARTICLES_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: SURFACE_SAMPLED_PARTICLES_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      // No depth at all. Sorting nine thousand points per frame on the CPU is\n      // not affordable, and it is not needed: the samples are in random buffer\n      // order, so an unsorted alpha blend averages out into even density\n      // rather than into visible layers.\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      blending: THREE.NormalBlending,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    // Displacement happens in the shader, where the frustum culler cannot see\n    // it. The bounding sphere three computes from the rest positions is right\n    // for the assembled shell and much too small for the scattered one.\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    turntable.add(points);\n\n    const rings = measureRings(shell, Math.max(0, Math.round(ringCount)), total);\n    let ringMaterial: THREE.LineBasicMaterial | null = null;\n    if (rings.length > 0) {\n      ringMaterial = new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({\n        color: new THREE.Color(ringColor),\n        transparent: true,\n        opacity: 0,\n        depthTest: false,\n        depthWrite: false,\n      });\n      const ringLines = new THREE.LineSegments(buildRingGeometry(rings), ringMaterial);\n      ringLines.frustumCulled = false;\n      turntable.add(ringLines);\n    }\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    let form = 0;\n    let angle = 0;\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) return;\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.aspect = w / h;\n\n      // Fit the unit sphere in BOTH axes. Vertical fov alone leaves a tall\n      // preview cropping the shell; the horizontal half-angle is the binding\n      // constraint whenever the container is narrower than it is tall.\n      const vHalf = (camera.fov * Math.PI) / 360;\n      const hHalf = Math.atan(Math.tan(vHalf) * camera.aspect);\n      const distance = (SHELL_RADIUS / Math.sin(Math.min(vHalf, hHalf))) * FIT_PADDING;\n      camera.position.set(0, 0, distance);\n      camera.far = distance + SHELL_RADIUS * 8;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n\n      // Camera distance is half of the perspective size term, so it has to be\n      // republished whenever the fit changes — otherwise particles keep the\n      // pixel size of the previous layout.\n      uniforms.uSizeScale.value = renderer.getPixelRatio() * distance;\n      uniforms.uMaxSize.value = maxSize * renderer.getPixelRatio();\n    };\n\n    const render = () => {\n      uniforms.uForm.value = form;\n      turntable.rotation.y = angle;\n      if (ringMaterial) ringMaterial.opacity = ringOpacity * form;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    const step = (dt: number, elapsed: number) => {\n      // Frame-rate independent: a per-frame constant would build the shell\n      // twice as fast on a 120 Hz display.\n      form = form >= FORM_EPSILON ? 1 : damp(form, 1, FORM_SMOOTHING, dt);\n      angle += dt * spin;\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      render();\n    };\n\n    const still = () => {\n      form = 1;\n      angle = STATIC_ANGLE;\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = STATIC_ELAPSED;\n      render();\n    };\n\n    resize(box.current.width, box.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, step, still };\n    // The first frame a reduced-motion visitor gets has to be the still one.\n    // `render()` draws whatever `form` happens to be, and `form` starts at 0 —\n    // the fully scattered cloud, with the rings at `ringOpacity * 0`, i.e.\n    // invisible — while `useRaf` below is disabled under reduced motion, so\n    // that first frame would also be the last one they ever see. The resize\n    // effect further down does call `still()`, but only when the observed size\n    // or `reduced` itself changes; every rebuild driven by this effect's own\n    // dependencies (a new `count`, a different colour, a new `seed`) happens at\n    // a size that has not moved, so nothing reaches it and the shell silently\n    // stops assembling.\n    if (reducedRef.current) still();\n    else render();\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      ringMaterial?.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    count,\n    size,\n    maxSize,\n    spin,\n    puff,\n    scatter,\n    ringCount,\n    ringOpacity,\n    opacity,\n    colorBase,\n    colorTip,\n    ringColor,\n    fov,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect: a container reflow costs a `setSize` and a\n  // camera refit, never a re-sample of the surface.\n  //\n  // `reduced` is a dependency, not an accident. It covers the two cases the\n  // build effect cannot: the OS setting being switched to `reduce` mid-session\n  // — `useReducedMotion` subscribes to the media query, so this really does\n  // change after mount — and a resize while it is on, where the refit above\n  // moves the camera but nothing would otherwise draw a frame through it.\n  // `still()` only assigns fixed values and renders once, so running it again\n  // when it is already the current frame costs a draw and changes nothing.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.still();\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.step(dt, elapsed),\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The source object is disposed immediately after sampling, before the renderer even exists. On unmount the stage ref is nulled so an in-flight frame cannot draw into a dying context, `disposeObject(scene)` disposes the point geometry, the ring geometry and their materials, the point material and the ring material are disposed explicitly as well, then `disposeRenderer` performs `dispose()` + `forceContextLoss()` + a zeroed drawing buffer — `dispose()` alone leaves the context alive — and the canvas node is removed. `useRaf`, `useInView`, `useVisibility` and `useResizeObserver` release their own subscriptions.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No rAF subscription is opened and one still frame is drawn with `uForm` forced to 1 and the turntable at 0.6 rad — a three-quarter view of the fully assembled shell with its rings at full opacity, not the unformed cloud. The shell, the density, the depth cue from perspective point sizing and the measured rings are all present; the assembly, the breathing and the rotation are what stop. A resize redraws the same still.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Sampling a fixed number of points per mesh. It is the obvious loop and it is wrong: density then tracks the mesh list rather than the surface, so a model whose parts differ in size gets a dense speckle on the small ones and a haze on the large ones. One cumulative table over all triangles of all meshes is the fix, and it is no more code.\n- Building the area table in local space. A mesh scaled 10x has 100x the surface area; weighting by untransformed triangles gives that part a hundredth of the density it should have, and the bug only shows on models that use node transforms.\n- Accumulating the running area total in a `Float32Array`. At tens of thousands of triangles the smallest ones land inside the rounding error of the sum, their cumulative entries stop increasing, and they become unreachable by any sample — whole thin features silently receive no particles.\n- Keeping degenerate triangles. Exported and generated geometry is full of zero-area faces; they contribute zero-width steps to the distribution and, worse, normalising a zero-length cross product yields NaN. One NaN vertex position takes visible neighbours with it on some drivers.\n- Using `u` and `v` raw as barycentric weights. Points then pile into one corner of every triangle, and at high triangle counts the cloud acquires a visible directional grain along the tessellation. Reflecting the far half of the unit square across the diagonal costs one comparison and is exactly uniform.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` on. Every displacement happens in the vertex shader, so the bounding sphere three computes from the rest positions is right for the assembled shell and far too small for the scattered one — the whole cloud blinks out during the part of the animation where it is largest.\n- Making `gl_PointSize` constant. Point size does not fall off with distance on its own, so a 3D cloud with a fixed size has no depth at all — it looks like a flat stencil of the object. Dividing by view-space depth is the entire perspective cue, and `max(0.1, -mv.z)` is a required guard, not a nicety: a particle crossing the near plane otherwise asks for an infinite size and some drivers drop the draw.\n- Caching the perspective size term at construction. Camera distance is derived from the container aspect ratio, so it changes on every reflow; a stale `uSizeScale` leaves particles at the pixel size of the previous layout.\n- Depth-testing a translucent point cloud. With `depthWrite` on, nine thousand sprites occlude each other in buffer order and the cloud tears into hard layers; sorting them per frame on the CPU is unaffordable. Because the samples are already in random order, no depth and an unsorted blend average out into even density.\n- Hard-coding ring radii for the model you happen to be sampling. They stop matching the moment the source changes, and nothing in the code says why the numbers were what they were. Measuring a percentile of the cloud's own radius per band is the same three lines and survives a new source.\n- Adding the source object to the scene so it can be sampled. It never needs to be rendered — it is a CPU-side measuring stick. Leaving it in the graph uploads every one of its triangles to the GPU and draws a solid mesh through the middle of the particles.\n- Making the `source` callback an effect dependency. An inline arrow changes identity on every parent render, so the entire surface is re-sampled — tens of thousands of triangles and a full buffer upload — on any unrelated state change upstream. It is held in a ref instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The technique is the same one used to scatter particles over a loaded GLB; the built-in specimen exists so the component ships with no asset. It is four primitives with deliberately mismatched areas — a torus knot, a small icosahedral finial, an open cylinder stem and a lathed base — which is precisely the case area weighting exists to handle.\n- Pass `source` to sample something else: return the `scene` of a loaded model, or any procedural `Object3D`. It is measured, normalised to a unit bounding sphere and then disposed, so a source authored in metres and one authored in unit space both frame identically.\n- Sampling costs one pass over every triangle plus a binary search per particle — a few milliseconds for 9000 particles over 10k triangles — and it happens once per rebuild, never per frame.\n- Two draw calls: the whole point cloud, and every measured ring in one `LineSegments` buffer. The rings do not depth-test, so their far halves cross in front of the shell; at hairline width and 0.3 opacity that reads as an orbit diagram rather than as an error.\n- The rAF subscription is gated on `useInView` and `useVisibility`, so a scrolled-away or backgrounded instance stops paying for fill. The assembly resumes from wherever it froze rather than restarting.\n- three constructs its own canvas, which is appended and removed per effect run. Reusing a React-owned `<canvas ref>` is unsafe here: cleanup calls `forceContextLoss()`, and under React 19 StrictMode the remount would hand the effect back the dead context, leaving the component permanently black.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn entering view the shell builds from the base upward over about two seconds and holds, then turns slowly. Density is even everywhere: the small finial and the large knot carry the same particles-per-square-millimetre, with no dense speckle on the small parts. Particles nearer the camera are visibly larger than those on the far side, and none of them pop out of existence as the cloud turns. The rings hug the silhouette — they widen where the form widens — and do not cut through the middle of it. Resize the container from wide to narrow: the shell stays fully in frame and particles keep the same apparent size rather than growing or shrinking. With reduced motion a fully assembled three-quarter view renders immediately and holds still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "switch": "# Build: Switch\n\nA skeuomorphic on/off switch built on React Aria `Switch`. Off, the track is a flat neutral surface; on, it fills with the accent gradient plus an inset ring and top highlight. The white thumb slides across over 200ms and carries a tiny embossed chip in its centre that turns from white to blue. The visual lives in an exported `SwitchTrack`, so Switch Card renders exactly the same control.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact Aria toggle with a gradient track, a white thumb that slides 200ms, and an embossed chip that turns blue when on; 3 sizes, 2 shapes.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Switch` renders `AriaSwitch`, a `<label>` around a visually hidden `<input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"switch\">`, as `group inline-flex items-center gap-2 select-none`. Its children render function passes the state to `<SwitchTrack state={state} size={size} shape={shape} />` and renders `children` (when not null or false) as a `text-body-medium text-text-primary` label. The cursor is `not-allowed` when disabled.\n2. Every dimension comes from the `switchSizes` table, keyed by `sm`, `md` and `lg`: track size, track and thumb radius per shape, the on-state shadow, thumb size, the thumb's resting `offset`, its `travel`, and the chip size, border and shadow. `shape` only chooses between the `pill` and `rectangle` radii in each tier.\n3. The track is a `relative` `aria-hidden` span with `transition-colors duration-200`. Off: `bg-background-tertiary-default`. On: `bg-linear-to-b from-accent-500 to-accent-600` plus the tier's `onShadow`, an inset white top highlight at 25% and an inset accent-500 hairline ring. At `lg` that shadow is exactly the shared `shadow-checkbox-selected` token; `md` and `sm` scale the highlight to 1.5px and 1px and the ring to 0.75px and 0.5px.\n4. The thumb is absolutely positioned at the tier's `offset` (for example `start-[3px] top-[3px]` at md) with a near-white vertical gradient (`from-control-indicator-background from-[43.837%] to-control-indicator-background-subtle`) and a soft contact shadow. When selected it gets the tier's `travel` class (`translate-x-[18px] rtl:-translate-x-[18px]` at md) and `transition-transform duration-200` slides it. Positioning uses the logical `start-*` and the travel flips sign under `rtl:`, so the switch mirrors in RTL.\n5. Inside the thumb, the chip is a bottom-to-top gradient (`bg-linear-to-t`) rather than a flipped element, so it stays pixel-snapped and its own drop shadow points down. Off it uses `switch-off-chip-start/end` with a half-opacity button border; on it uses `switch-on-chip-start/end` with an `accent-600` border.\n6. Disabled dims only the track (`opacity-50`). Keyboard focus draws `ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-2` on the track while `isFocusVisible` is true.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `track sizes = sm 28×16 · md 42×24 · lg 56×32 px`\n  1:1 with the Figma \"Toogles\" frame (node 3856:3807). Thumb = track height minus twice the padding: 12, 18 and 24px.\n- `travel = sm 12px · md 18px · lg 24px`\n  Track width minus thumb minus both paddings (28 − 12 − 4, 42 − 18 − 6, 56 − 24 − 8). Any other value leaves the thumb short of, or overlapping, the far edge.\n- `duration = 200ms on the track colour and the thumb transform`\n  Both run on one clock so the fill and the thumb arrive together. Short enough that a quick double toggle never shows the thumb lagging behind the track colour.\n- `thumb gradient stop = from-[43.837%]`\n  Copied from the Figma gradient: the top 44% of the thumb is solid white before it falls off to neutral-100, which is what makes it read as a rounded physical knob.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Switch(props: Omit<AriaSwitchProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; shape?: \"pill\" | \"rectangle\"; ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction SwitchTrack(props: { state: { isSelected: boolean; isDisabled: boolean; isFocusVisible: boolean }; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; shape?: \"pill\" | \"rectangle\" }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `isSelected / defaultSelected: boolean`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled on state, from React Aria.\n- `onChange: (isSelected: boolean) => void`\n  Called when the switch is toggled.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  28×16, 42×24 or 56×32 track.\n- `shape: \"pill\" | \"rectangle\"` — default `\"pill\"`\n  Fully rounded, or a squarer track and thumb with small radii.\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  Optional label after the track in body medium. Without it, pass `aria-label`.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Dims the track to 50% and ignores input.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SwitchTrack({\n  state,\n  size = \"md\",\n  shape = \"pill\",\n}: {\n  state: SwitchVisualState;\n  size?: SwitchSize;\n  shape?: SwitchShape;\n}) {\n  const s = switchSizes[size];\n\n  return (\n    <span\n      aria-hidden\n      className={cx(\n        \"relative shrink-0 transition-colors duration-200 ease\",\n        s.track,\n        s.trackRadius[shape],\n        state.isSelected\n          ? cx(\"bg-linear-to-b from-accent-500 to-accent-600\", s.onShadow)\n          : \"bg-background-tertiary-default\",\n        state.isDisabled && \"opacity-50\",\n        state.isFocusVisible && \"ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring ring-offset-2\",\n      )}\n    >\n      {/* Thumb */}\n      <span\n        className={cx(\n          \"absolute flex items-center justify-center\",\n          \"bg-linear-to-b from-control-indicator-background from-[43.837%] to-control-indicator-background-subtle\",\n          \"shadow-[0_3px_3px_0_rgb(0_0_0/0.03),0_0.75px_0_0_rgb(0_0_0/0.05)]\",\n          \"transition-transform duration-200 ease\",\n          s.thumb,\n          s.thumbRadius[shape],\n          s.offset,\n          state.isSelected && s.travel,\n        )}\n      >\n        {/* Embossed inner chip. Gradient runs bottom→top (`to-t`) — equivalent\n            to the Figma layer's vertical flip — but WITHOUT a transform, so the\n            chip stays pixel-snapped/centred (no blurry 1px shift at sub-pixel\n            sizes) and its drop shadow points down instead of up. */}\n        <span\n          className={cx(\n            \"border-solid bg-linear-to-t from-[43.837%]\",\n            s.chip,\n            s.chipRadius[shape],\n            state.isSelected\n              ? \"border-accent-600 from-switch-on-chip-start to-switch-on-chip-end\"\n              : \"border-border-button-default/50 from-switch-off-chip-start to-switch-off-chip-end\",\n          )}\n        />\n      </span>\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The component holds no state, listeners or timers of its own; React Aria owns the toggle state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. The thumb slide and the track colour change are 200ms CSS transitions, so a global rule that removes transitions makes the thumb jump straight to the other end.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not animate the thumb with `left`. It is placed once with `start-*` and moved with `translate-x`, which stays on the compositor and mirrors with `rtl:-translate-x-*`.\n- Do not flip the chip with `rotate-180` or `-scale-y-100` to reverse its gradient. A transform at sub-pixel sizes blurs it and points its shadow upward. Use the reversed gradient direction.\n- Do not mix travel values across tiers. Each size's `travel` equals its own track width minus thumb and padding, so changing a track width means recomputing it.\n- Do not ship a switch with no label and no `aria-label`. React Aria warns, and screen readers would announce only \"switch\".\n\n## Notes\n\n- `SwitchTrack` and `switchSizes` are exported, so another control (Switch Card, a menu row) can render the identical visual with its own input.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAn off switch shows a flat grey track with the white thumb at the start. Clicking it, or pressing Space when focused, slides the thumb to the end in about 200ms while the track fills with the blue gradient and the chip in the thumb turns blue. Clicking again reverses both. All three sizes and both shapes keep the thumb inset by the same proportion at both ends. In RTL the thumb starts on the right and slides left. Tab shows a focus ring around the track.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "switch-card": "# Build: Switch Card\n\nThe switch counterpart of Checkbox Card: a 12px-radius bordered row with an optional 24px icon, a title, an optional description and the kit `SwitchTrack` on the trailing edge. The whole card is the React Aria `Switch` label, so a click anywhere toggles it and the thumb slides across in 200ms. Size and shape pass straight through to the track.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nBordered settings row with an optional icon, title and description; a click anywhere on the card flips the kit switch on its trailing edge.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `SwitchCard` renders `AriaSwitch` directly. React Aria makes it a `<label>` around a visually hidden `<input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"switch\">`, so the entire card is the hit area and Space toggles it when focused. All Aria switch props (`isSelected`, `defaultSelected`, `onChange`, `isDisabled`, `name`) pass through.\n2. The card class is computed from the render state: `group flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-xl border border-border-button-default py-3 pe-5 ps-4 select-none` with `transition-colors duration-150`. Hovered and not disabled gives `bg-background-primary-hover`, otherwise `bg-background-primary-default`. Disabled adds `cursor-not-allowed opacity-50`. A `className` function is called with the same state and merged last.\n3. The leading block is `flex min-w-0 items-center gap-3`: the optional `icon` component rendered at `size-6` in `text-foreground-icon-secondary` with `aria-hidden`, then a `flex-col gap-0.5` column with the title in `text-body-medium text-text-primary` and, when `description` is not null or undefined, a line in `text-body-regular text-text-secondary`. Both lines truncate.\n4. The trailing slot (`flex shrink-0 items-center`) renders `<SwitchTrack state={state} size={size} shape={shape} />` from the Switch item with the card's own render state, so selection, disabled dimming and the keyboard focus ring all show on the track exactly as on a plain `Switch`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `padding = ps-4 pe-5 py-3 (16 / 20 / 12px)`\n  The same chrome as Checkbox Card, so the two can share a settings page. The extra 4px on the trailing side gives the 42px track room from the border.\n- `radius = rounded-xl (12px)`\n  The one deliberate difference from Checkbox Card, which uses rounded-2lg (10px); the source header calls it out as \"mirrors CheckboxCard, but with rounded-xl (12px) corners\".\n- `hover fade = transition-colors duration-150`\n  Same hover fade as Checkbox Card and Radio Card, so every selectable card in the kit tints at one speed. The track itself keeps its own 200ms transition.\n- `icon = size-6 (24px), text-foreground-icon-secondary`\n  Matches the 24×24 Remix Icon grid. The component takes the icon component itself (`RiMailLine`), not an element, so it controls size and colour.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction SwitchCard(props: Omit<AriaSwitchProps, \"children\"> & { title: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; icon?: ComponentType<{ className?: string; \"aria-hidden\"?: boolean | \"true\" | \"false\" }>; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"; shape?: \"pill\" | \"rectangle\"; ref?: Ref<HTMLLabelElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: ReactNode`\n  Main label in body medium. Also the accessible name of the switch.\n- `description: ReactNode`\n  Optional secondary line, truncated to one line.\n- `icon: ComponentType`\n  Optional leading 24px icon component, rendered `aria-hidden`.\n- `isSelected / defaultSelected: boolean`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled on state, from React Aria.\n- `onChange: (isSelected: boolean) => void`\n  Called when the card is toggled.\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Track size, passed to `SwitchTrack`.\n- `shape: \"pill\" | \"rectangle\"` — default `\"pill\"`\n  Track shape, passed to `SwitchTrack`.\n- `isDisabled: boolean`\n  Half opacity, not-allowed cursor, ignores input.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function SwitchCard({\n  className,\n  title,\n  description,\n  icon: Icon,\n  size = \"md\",\n  shape = \"pill\",\n  ref,\n  ...props\n}: SwitchCardProps) {\n  return (\n    <AriaSwitch\n      ref={ref}\n      {...props}\n      className={(state) =>\n        cx(\n          \"group flex w-full items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-xl border border-border-button-default py-3 pe-5 ps-4 select-none\",\n          \"transition-colors duration-150 ease\",\n          state.isHovered && !state.isDisabled\n            ? \"bg-background-primary-hover\"\n            : \"bg-background-primary-default\",\n          state.isDisabled ? \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\" : \"cursor-pointer\",\n          typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {(state) => (\n        <>\n          <span className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-3\">\n            {Icon && <Icon className=\"size-6 shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary\" aria-hidden />}\n            <span className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-col gap-0.5\">\n              <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{title}</span>\n              {description !== undefined && description !== null && (\n                <span className=\"truncate text-body-regular text-text-secondary\">{description}</span>\n              )}\n            </span>\n          </span>\n          <span className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center\">\n            <SwitchTrack state={state} size={size} shape={shape} />\n          </span>\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaSwitch>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The card holds no state, listeners or timers of its own; React Aria owns the toggle state.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. The 150ms card tint and the 200ms track and thumb are CSS transitions, so a global rule that removes transitions makes them switch instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not pass an element to `icon` (`<RiMailLine />`). It expects the component (`RiMailLine`) and renders it itself.\n- Do not put links or buttons in `title` or `description`. The card is a `<label>`, so clicks inside it toggle the switch.\n- Do not add a separate `Switch` inside the card. The trailing track is already the visual for this card's own input; a second one would nest two controls.\n- Do not give the card a fixed height. The description truncates to one line, but the title and description stack, so a fixed height clips the second line.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The track comes from `registry/ui/switch.tsx` (`SwitchTrack`), so install the Switch item alongside this one.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA stack of setting cards each shows an icon, a title, a one-line description and a switch on the right. Clicking anywhere on a card, including its description, slides its thumb across and fills the track blue in about 200ms; clicking again reverses it. Hover tints the card. Tab focuses the card and shows the ring on the track; Space toggles it. A disabled card is at half opacity and ignores clicks.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "table": "# Build: Table\n\nThe static table primitive behind BoardUI's dashboard tables. Only the root is wrapped: `Table` adds a horizontal scroll container and a density flag around React Aria's `Table`. `TableHeader`, `TableColumn`, `TableBody`, `TableRow` and `TableCell` are React Aria's collection components re-exported untouched, because wrapping them would break React Aria's collection building. All visual styling (header band, row separators, cell padding, focus ring) lives in CSS scoped to `.bui-table`, so the markup stays real `<table>`, `<th>`, `<tr>` and `<td>` with keyboard navigation for free.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSemantic table on React Aria with a scroll container, an md/sm density flag and dashboard styling from one scoped stylesheet.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Wrap the table in `<div className=\"w-full overflow-x-auto\">` (plus `containerClassName`), so a wide table scrolls sideways inside its card instead of stretching the page.\n2. Render `AriaTable` with every React Aria prop passed through (`aria-label`, `selectionMode`, `sortDescriptor`, …), the forwarded `ref`, and `className = cx(\"bui-table\", size === \"sm\" && \"bui-table-sm\", className)`.\n3. Re-export `TableHeader`, `Column as TableColumn`, `TableBody`, `Row as TableRow` and `Cell as TableCell` from react-aria-components unchanged, so React Aria can still introspect the collection.\n4. In the theme sheet, `.bui-table` is `w-full border-collapse text-start`. Header cells get `px-3 py-2.5 text-body-medium text-text-tertiary whitespace-nowrap`, the secondary background and a separator border above and below; body cells get `px-3 py-2.5 text-body-medium text-text-primary`.\n5. Rows are separated by `tbody tr:not(:last-child) { border-bottom }` only, so the last row never doubles up with the container edge or a pagination footer. `tr[data-focus-visible]` draws an inset 2px focus ring.\n6. `.bui-table-sm` drops cells to `px-2.5 py-1.5 text-body-2-medium`; padding and font-size carry a 200ms ease transition, so switching density animates.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `cell padding = md px-3 py-2.5 (12 / 10px); sm px-2.5 py-1.5 (10 / 6px)`\n  The two dashboard densities. Both are set on th and td together, so header and body stay aligned.\n- `density transition = padding 200ms ease, font-size 200ms ease`\n  Toggling `size` reflows the rows smoothly instead of snapping.\n- `separators = 1px solid var(--color-separator-border)`\n  One token for the header band edges and the between-row rules, set with plain CSS because `@apply` drops border and background on row-group cells.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype TableSize = \"sm\" | \"md\"\ninterface TableProps extends Omit<AriaTableProps, \"className\"> { size?: TableSize; className?: string; containerClassName?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLTableElement> }\nfunction Table(props: TableProps): JSX.Element\nexport { TableHeader, Column as TableColumn, TableBody, Row as TableRow, Cell as TableCell } from \"react-aria-components\"\n```\n\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  Row density. `sm` adds `bui-table-sm` for tighter padding and the smaller body type.\n- `aria-label: string`\n  Accessible name for the table (React Aria). Required unless `aria-labelledby` is set.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the `<table>` after `bui-table`.\n- `containerClassName: string`\n  Class for the `overflow-x-auto` scroll wrapper.\n- `ref: Ref<HTMLTableElement>`\n  Forwarded to the `<table>` element.\n- `...props: AriaTableProps`\n  Selection, sorting and every other React Aria Table prop.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Table({ size = \"md\", className, containerClassName, ref, ...props }: TableProps) {\n  return (\n    <div className={cx(\"w-full overflow-x-auto\", containerClassName)}>\n      <AriaTable\n        ref={ref}\n        {...props}\n        className={cx(\"bui-table\", size === \"sm\" && \"bui-table-sm\", className)}\n      />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport {\n  TableHeader,\n  Column as TableColumn,\n  TableBody,\n  Row as TableRow,\n  Cell as TableCell,\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The wrapper is stateless; React Aria owns focus and selection state and removes its own listeners on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 200ms padding and font-size transition when `size` changes, plus a 150ms colour transition on rows. Neither is gated by prefers-reduced-motion; both are short, non-spatial reflows. Add `motion-reduce:transition-none` through `className` if a product needs them off.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not wrap `TableColumn`, `TableRow` or `TableCell` in your own components. React Aria builds the collection by introspecting these exact elements; restyle through `.bui-table` CSS or `className` instead.\n- Do not forget `aria-label` (or `aria-labelledby`) on `Table`. Without one the grid has no accessible name.\n- Mark exactly one column `isRowHeader`, usually the name column, so screen readers announce each row by it.\n- Do not put the header background or borders on `<thead>`. With `border-collapse` the browser does not paint them on the row group, which is why the sheet puts them on `th`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Sorting, filtering and pagination are not built in. Pair the primitive with @tanstack/react-table (or your own state) and pass `sortDescriptor` / `onSortChange` through to React Aria.\n- The component file's doc comment mentions h-16 / h-12 rows and a `data-size` attribute; the shipped code uses the `bui-table-sm` class and padding-driven heights instead.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA `<Table aria-label=\"Team members\">` with a header row and four body rows renders a real `<table>` inside a horizontally scrolling div. Header cells sit on the secondary background between two hairlines in tertiary text; body rows are separated by hairlines with none under the last row. Tabbing into the table and pressing the arrow keys moves a visible inset ring between rows. Switching `size` to \"sm\" tightens every cell from 12/10px to 10/6px padding over 200ms. At 360px the table scrolls sideways inside its container without widening the page.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "table-filter-bar": "# Build: Table Filter Bar\n\nThe filter row that sits on top of a BoardUI table: a \"Total Results\" count on the left, then one kit Select per filter (\"All prices\", \"All products\", \"All regions\") and a pill SearchField. Every active filter and a non-empty search become removable chips with a Clear all button. The bar never touches rows: it reports values and the query, the caller filters and passes back the result count, which is announced politely.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nStrip above a data table: \"All ...\" select filters, a pill search, removable active-filter chips, Clear all and a live result count.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. State is a FilterValues record (filter id -> option id, a missing key means \"All\") plus a query string; both are controlled (values / query) or uncontrolled (defaultValues / defaultQuery) and every change is reported through onValuesChange / onQueryChange.\n2. Each filter renders a kit Select whose first SelectItem has the sentinel id __all__ and the allLabel text; selectedKey is values[id] ?? __all__, and choosing __all__ deletes the key instead of storing it.\n3. Active chips are derived on render: filters.flatMap finds the option matching values[filter.id]; a trimmed non-empty query adds a \"Search: ...\" chip. The chip row (role=group \"Active filters\") only mounts while something is active.\n4. Chip remove buttons and Clear all set a refocus ref before updating state; an effect after the re-render moves focus to the next [data-filter-chip-remove] button or, if none is left, to the search input, so focus never falls to <body>.\n5. resultCount renders under totalLabel inside an aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic paragraph so assistive tech hears \"4 orders\" after each change; omit the prop to hide the count block.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `ALL sentinel = '__all__'`\n  Keeps \"All ...\" a real listbox option so the trigger shows its label, while the stored values stay free of reset entries.\n- `search width = w-full, sm:w-[200px]`\n  Full row on phones; a fixed 200px pill beside the selects from 640px up, as in the finance table header.\n- `layout breakpoint = lg:flex-row`\n  Below 1024px the count stacks above the controls so three selects plus search can wrap without squeezing the count.\n- `chip remove target = size-5 rounded-full, 14px icon`\n  A round 20px hit area inside a caption chip, with hover and focus-ring states from the kit tokens.\n- `gap = gap-2.5 controls, gap-2 chips`\n  10px between bordered selects matches the reference spacing; chips sit slightly tighter.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function TableFilterBar({ filters, values, defaultValues, onValuesChange, query, defaultQuery, onQueryChange, searchPlaceholder, resultCount, resultLabel, totalLabel, showActiveFilters, clearLabel, className }: TableFilterBarProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `filters: readonly FilterDefinition[]`\n  Each { id, label, allLabel, options: { id, label }[] } renders one select.\n- `values / defaultValues: Record<string, string>` — default `{}`\n  Selected option per filter id, controlled or initial.\n- `onValuesChange: (values: FilterValues) => void`\n  Called with the whole record after any select, chip or Clear all change.\n- `query / defaultQuery: string` — default `''`\n  Search text, controlled or initial.\n- `onQueryChange: (query: string) => void`\n  Called on each keystroke and on clear.\n- `searchPlaceholder: string` — default `'Search'`\n  Placeholder and accessible name of the search field.\n- `resultCount: number`\n  Rows left after filtering; omit to hide the count block.\n- `resultLabel: string` — default `'results'`\n  Noun after the count.\n- `totalLabel: ReactNode` — default `'Total Results'`\n  Caption above the count.\n- `showActiveFilters: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the removable chip row while anything is active.\n- `clearLabel: string` — default `'Clear all'`\n  Label of the reset button.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function TableFilterBar({\n  filters,\n  values,\n  defaultValues = EMPTY,\n  onValuesChange,\n  query,\n  defaultQuery = '',\n  onQueryChange,\n  searchPlaceholder = 'Search',\n  resultCount,\n  resultLabel = 'results',\n  totalLabel = 'Total Results',\n  showActiveFilters = true,\n  clearLabel = 'Clear all',\n  className,\n}: TableFilterBarProps) {\n  const rootRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [innerValues, setInnerValues] = useState<FilterValues>(defaultValues);\n  const [innerQuery, setInnerQuery] = useState(defaultQuery);\n  const current = values ?? innerValues;\n  const search = query ?? innerQuery;\n\n  const setValues = (next: FilterValues) => {\n    setInnerValues(next);\n    onValuesChange?.(next);\n  };\n  const setSearch = (next: string) => {\n    setInnerQuery(next);\n    onQueryChange?.(next);\n  };\n  const setFilter = (filterId: string, optionId: string | null) => {\n    const { [filterId]: _removed, ...rest } = current;\n    setValues(optionId === null ? rest : { ...rest, [filterId]: optionId });\n  };\n\n  // Removing a chip unmounts the focused button; after the re-render, hand\n  // focus to the next chip, else to the search input.\n  const refocus = useRef(false);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!refocus.current) return;\n    refocus.current = false;\n    const root = rootRef.current;\n    (root?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-filter-chip-remove]') ?? root?.querySelector<HTMLElement>('input'))?.focus();\n  });\n\n  const active = filters.flatMap((filter) => {\n    const option = filter.options.find((o) => o.id === current[filter.id]);\n    return option ? [{ filter, option }] : [];\n  });\n  const trimmed = search.trim();\n  const hasActive = active.length > 0 || trimmed.length > 0;\n\n  const clearAll = () => {\n    refocus.current = true;\n    setValues(EMPTY);\n    setSearch('');\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={rootRef} className={cx('flex w-full flex-col gap-3', className)}>\n      <div className=\"flex flex-col gap-3 lg:flex-row lg:items-center lg:justify-between\">\n        {resultCount !== undefined ? (\n          <div className=\"flex shrink-0 flex-col justify-center\">\n            <p className=\"text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-tertiary\">{totalLabel}</p>\n            <p aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic className=\"text-body-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums\">\n              {resultCount.toLocaleString()} {resultLabel}\n            </p>\n          </div>\n        ) : null}\n        <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2.5 lg:justify-end\">\n          {filters.map((filter) => (\n            <Select\n              key={filter.id}\n              aria-label={`Filter by ${filter.label.toLowerCase()}`}\n              className=\"shrink-0\"\n              popoverClassName=\"w-auto min-w-44\"\n              selectedKey={current[filter.id] ?? ALL}\n              onSelectionChange={(key: Key | null) => setFilter(filter.id, key === null || key === ALL ? null : String(key))}\n            >\n              <SelectItem id={ALL} textValue={filter.allLabel}>\n                {filter.allLabel}\n              </SelectItem>\n              {filter.options.map((option) => (\n                <SelectItem key={option.id} id={option.id} textValue={option.label}>\n                  {option.label}\n                </SelectItem>\n              ))}\n            </Select>\n          ))}\n          <SearchField\n            aria-label={searchPlaceholder}\n            placeholder={searchPlaceholder}\n            value={search}\n            onChange={setSearch}\n            className=\"w-full sm:w-[200px]\"\n          />\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      {showActiveFilters && hasActive ? (\n        <div role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Active filters\" className=\"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2\">\n          {active.map(({ filter, option }) => (\n            <Chip key={filter.id} color=\"soft\" variant=\"caption\" className=\"gap-1 rounded-full py-0.5 ps-2.5 pe-0.5\">\n              <span>\n                {filter.label}: <span className=\"text-text-primary\">{option.label}</span>\n              </span>\n              <button\n                type=\"button\"\n                data-filter-chip-remove\n                aria-label={`Remove ${filter.label} filter: ${option.label}`}\n                onClick={() => {\n                  refocus.current = true;\n                  setFilter(filter.id, null);\n                }}\n                className={cx(\n                  'inline-flex size-5 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full text-foreground-icon-tertiary outline-none',\n                  'transition-colors duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n                  'hover:bg-background-tertiary-hover hover:text-foreground-icon-primary focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring',\n                )}\n              >\n                <RiCloseLine aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5\" />\n              </button>\n            </Chip>\n          ))}\n          {trimmed ? (\n            <Chip color=\"soft\" variant=\"caption\" className=\"gap-1 rounded-full py-0.5 ps-2.5 pe-0.5\">\n              <span>\n                Search: <span className=\"text-text-primary\">&ldquo;{trimmed}&rdquo;</span>\n              </span>\n              <button\n                type=\"button\"\n                data-filter-chip-remove\n                aria-label={`Clear search: ${trimmed}`}\n                onClick={() => {\n                  refocus.current = true;\n                  setSearch('');\n                }}\n                className={cx(\n                  'inline-flex size-5 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-full text-foreground-icon-tertiary outline-none',\n                  'transition-colors duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n                  'hover:bg-background-tertiary-hover hover:text-foreground-icon-primary focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring',\n                )}\n              >\n                <RiCloseLine aria-hidden className=\"size-3.5\" />\n              </button>\n            </Chip>\n          ) : null}\n          <Button variant=\"ghost\" size=\"xs\" leadingIcon={RiFilterOffLine} onClick={clearAll}>\n            {clearLabel}\n          </Button>\n        </div>\n      ) : null}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers or listeners of its own; the refocus effect only reads a ref. The kit Select owns its outside-press listener.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only colour transitions are used (motion-reduce:transition-none on the chip remove buttons); the kit Select popover drops its scale entry under reduced motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not store the \"All\" choice as a value: chips and filtering would treat __all__ as an active filter. Delete the key instead.\n- Removing a chip unmounts the focused button; without the refocus effect keyboard focus drops to <body>.\n- The bar does not filter anything itself; pass the post-filter length as resultCount or the live count will be stale.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Set showActiveFilters={false} when the selects alone are enough, e.g. in a narrow card.\n- Option and filter labels feed both the chips and the accessible names (\"Filter by product\", \"Remove Product filter: Laptop\").\n\n## Definition of done\n\nChoosing \"Laptop\" in \"All products\" narrows the demo list to 3 orders, shows a \"Product: Laptop\" chip and announces \"3 orders\"; typing \"clark\" in search narrows it to 1 order; Clear all removes every chip, resets the selects to \"All ...\" and restores 10 orders.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tabs": "# Build: Tabs\n\nTwo tab styles from one kit. The underline tabs wrap React Aria `Tabs`, so arrow keys, roving focus and tab/panel wiring come for free, and a single absolutely positioned 2px bar glides to whichever tab is selected. The pill switcher is a plain row of toggle buttons for local view state, with a shared rounded thumb that slides under the active pill and overshoots slightly before it settles.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact Aria underline tabs with a measured 2px indicator that slides between tabs, plus a pill switcher whose thumb settles with a spring.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Tabs`, `TabList`, `Tab` and `TabPanel` spread their props onto the React Aria primitives, which handle roving tabindex, arrow-key navigation, automatic activation and the `aria-controls` link between a tab and its panel. Every `className` accepts a string or React Aria's state function and is merged through `cx`. `Tabs` is a `flex-col gap-4` stack that flips to `flex-row` when `orientation` is vertical.\n2. `TabList` wraps the Aria list in a `relative w-full` div. The list itself is `flex items-center gap-1 border-b border-separator-border`, the 1px baseline every tab sits on. The selected tab is not underlined by its own border: an `aria-hidden` span (`absolute bottom-0 left-0 h-0.5 bg-accent-600`) is drawn once and moved.\n3. A layout effect (`useLayoutEffect` on the client, `useEffect` on the server to avoid the SSR warning) queries `[role='tab'][data-selected]` and stores its `offsetLeft` and `offsetWidth`. The bar is rendered with `transform: translateX(left px)` and `width`, and `transition-[transform,width] duration-200` animates both, so it slides and resizes in one motion between tabs of different widths.\n4. Measurement re-runs on three triggers: a `ResizeObserver` on the strip and on every tab (labels and web fonts change tab widths without resizing the strip), and a `MutationObserver` on the subtree filtered to `data-selected`, which fires when React Aria moves the selection. The MutationObserver also re-observes tabs when children change. The effect depends on `useDirection()`, so switching between LTR and RTL re-measures from the new layout.\n5. `Tab` renders the label in `text-body-medium text-accent-600` when selected and `text-body-regular text-text-primary` otherwise, with an optional 16px leading `icon` that inherits the colour. A `count` badge (`rounded-sm px-1 py-px text-caption-1-medium`) uses `bg-tab-count-selected-background text-accent-600` when selected, and `bg-black/10 text-text-primary opacity-50` when not.\n6. `PillTabList` is a `role=\"group\"` row (`relative inline-flex gap-1`). It finds the `[data-pill-selected]` button and stores its `left`, `top`, `width`, `height` and variant (read from `data-pill-variant`), then paints one thumb with `translate(left, top)` plus width and height, transitioned over 300ms on `cubic-bezier(0.34,1.2,0.64,1)`. The same Resize/Mutation observer pair keeps it aligned.\n7. `PillTab` is a `<button type=\"button\" aria-pressed>` at `z-10` above the thumb. Variant `blue` is `rounded-full` with an accent-50 thumb and `text-accent-500` label. Variant `gray` is `rounded-2lg` with `bg-background-tertiary-default`. An unselected pill fades in its own hover layer (`opacity-0` to `group-hover:opacity-100`, 200ms), so hover never moves the shared thumb.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `underline = h-0.5 (2px), 200ms, transform + width`\n  Figma draws a 2px blue rule over the 1px baseline. Transitioning only `transform` and `width` keeps the slide on the compositor-friendly path, and 200ms is short enough that rapid arrow-key presses never leave the bar lagging two tabs behind.\n- `pill thumb easing = 300ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.2, 0.64, 1)`\n  The second control point at 1.2 overshoots by a few percent, which reads as a small spring settle without a JS spring. It is longer than the underline because the thumb moves a filled surface and the overshoot needs time to read.\n- `tab padding = px-2.5 py-2, gap-2.5 label to count`\n  10px by 8px and the 10px label-to-count gap are the Figma tab spec (node 3793:2942). The indicator width is measured from the tab box, so changing padding also changes how wide the underline is.\n- `pill padding = px-2 py-[5px], icon size-5`\n  8px by 5px around a 20px icon and 20px line height gives a 30px pill, the height of the AI chat panel switcher it came from.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Tabs(props: AriaTabsProps & { ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction TabList<T extends object>(props: AriaTabListProps<T> & { ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction Tab(props: Omit<AriaTabProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; icon?: IconComponent; count?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction TabPanel(props: AriaTabPanelProps & { ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction PillTabList(props: HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement> & { ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction PillTab(props: { variant?: \"blue\" | \"gray\"; icon?: IconComponent; isSelected: boolean; onSelect: () => void; children: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `Tabs.selectedKey / defaultSelectedKey: Key`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled selection, straight from React Aria. Pair with `onSelectionChange`.\n- `Tabs.orientation: \"horizontal\" | \"vertical\"` — default `\"horizontal\"`\n  Vertical switches the outer stack to `flex-row`. The underline measures `offsetLeft` only, so it is designed for horizontal strips.\n- `TabList.aria-label: string`\n  Accessible name for the tab list. Required when there is no visible heading.\n- `Tab.id: Key`\n  Key that matches the `id` of the `TabPanel` it controls.\n- `Tab.icon: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>`\n  16px leading icon component. Inherits the label colour.\n- `Tab.count: ReactNode`\n  Trailing badge, accent-tinted when the tab is selected and muted otherwise. Omitted when null.\n- `PillTab.variant: \"blue\" | \"gray\"` — default `\"blue\"`\n  Blue is fully rounded with an accent thumb; gray is a 10px radius with a neutral thumb.\n- `PillTab.isSelected / onSelect: boolean / () => void`\n  Controlled selection. The selected pill carries `data-pill-selected`, which the list measures.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\ntype Underline = { left: number; width: number };\n\nexport function TabList<T extends object>({ className, ref, ...props }: TabListProps<T>) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  const wrapperRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [underline, setUnderline] = useState<Underline | null>(null);\n\n  useIsomorphicLayoutEffect(() => {\n    const el = wrapperRef.current;\n    if (!el) return;\n    const measure = () => {\n      const selected = el.querySelector<HTMLElement>(\"[role='tab'][data-selected]\");\n      if (selected) {\n        setUnderline({ left: selected.offsetLeft, width: selected.offsetWidth });\n      }\n    };\n    measure();\n    const ro = new ResizeObserver(measure);\n    ro.observe(el);\n    // Labels and fonts can change width without resizing the full tab strip.\n    const observeTabs = () => el.querySelectorAll(\"[role='tab']\").forEach(tab => ro.observe(tab));\n    observeTabs();\n    const mo = new MutationObserver(() => { observeTabs(); measure(); });\n    mo.observe(el, { attributes: true, childList: true, characterData: true, subtree: true, attributeFilter: [\"data-selected\"] });\n    return () => {\n      mo.disconnect();\n      ro.disconnect();\n    };\n  }, [direction]);\n\n  return (\n    <div ref={wrapperRef} className=\"relative w-full\">\n      <AriaTabList\n        ref={ref}\n        {...props}\n        className={(state) =>\n          cx(\n            \"flex w-full items-center gap-1 border-b border-separator-border\",\n            typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n          )\n        }\n      />\n      {underline && (\n        <span\n          aria-hidden\n          className=\"pointer-events-none absolute bottom-0 left-0 h-0.5 bg-accent-600 transition-[transform,width] duration-200 ease\"\n          style={{\n            transform: `translateX(${underline.left}px)`,\n            width: underline.width,\n          }}\n        />\n      )}\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Each list disconnects its `ResizeObserver` and `MutationObserver` in the layout effect cleanup, on unmount and whenever the direction changes. There are no timers or animation frames.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion` itself. The underline (200ms) and pill thumb (300ms) slides and the colour fades are plain CSS transitions, so a global reduced-motion rule that zeroes transition durations makes them jump straight to the new tab.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not draw the active underline with a border on each `Tab`. It cannot animate between tabs, and it doubles up with the strip's own `border-b`. The single measured span is what slides.\n- Do not measure in a plain `useEffect`. The first paint would show the bar at its old position, or missing, for one frame. The isomorphic layout effect measures before paint.\n- Do not drop the `ResizeObserver` on the individual tabs. A web font that loads after mount widens the labels without resizing the strip, and the underline is then left short of the text.\n- Do not use `PillTab` for routed content panels. It is a toggle button with `aria-pressed`, not a `role=\"tab\"`, so it has no arrow-key navigation and no panel association. Use the React Aria `Tabs` for that.\n- Do not forget `aria-label` on `TabList`. React Aria warns at runtime when a tab list has no accessible name.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `PillTab` is controlled only: it takes `isSelected` and `onSelect`, and the parent owns which one is active. Several pills can be selected at once, but the thumb only follows the first `[data-pill-selected]` it finds.\n- The thumb picks up the variant of the selected pill, so a row can mix blue and gray pills and the surface colour and radius switch as the selection moves.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAn underline strip with three tabs shows a 2px accent bar under the selected tab only. Click or arrow-key to another tab and the bar slides and resizes to the new tab in about 200ms, while the label turns accent and medium weight. Count badges are tinted on the selected tab and faded on the others. In a pill row, selecting another pill slides one shared thumb under it with a small overshoot, and hovering an unselected pill tints only that pill.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tag-group": "# Build: Tag Group\n\nA set of tags to filter by, pick from or prune: topics, labels on a ticket, recipients. `TagGroup` wraps React Aria's `TagGroup` and `TagList` with the kit Label, hint text and an optional empty state; `Tag` wraps React Aria's `Tag` in Chip's colour pairs at 28px, or 24px with `size=\"small\"`. Give the group a `selectionMode` and tags toggle, a selected tag filling with the accent colour; give it `onRemove` and every tag grows a small close button, while Backspace or Delete removes the focused tag. Because it is a React Aria grid, Tab enters once and the arrow keys move between tags.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSelectable or removable tags in Chip's colours, with grid keyboard navigation and an empty state, on React Aria's TagGroup.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `TagGroup` renders `AriaTagGroup` (`flex flex-col items-start gap-1.5`) with every React Aria prop passed through, then the kit `Label`, an `AriaTagList` (`flex flex-wrap gap-1.5`) and an optional `HintText`.\n2. The list takes `items` for data-driven tags, and `renderEmptyState` is set only when `emptyState` is given, rendering it as `text-body-regular text-text-tertiary`.\n3. The group's `size` goes into a `SizeContext`, so each `Tag` reads it without a prop: medium is `h-7 px-2 text-body-medium`, small is `h-6 px-1.5 text-caption-1-medium`.\n4. `Tag` styles itself from React Aria's render props. `isSelected` swaps the Chip colour for `bg-accent-500 text-white`; a selectable, unselected tag on hover darkens (`bg-background-tertiary-hover` for neutral, `brightness-95` for the colours); `isFocusVisible` adds a 2px focus ring; `isDisabled` drops to 50%.\n5. When `allowsRemoving` is true (the group has `onRemove`), the tag trims its end padding and renders an `AriaButton slot=\"remove\"` with `RiCloseLine` at 60% opacity, lifting to 100% with a `bg-current/15` wash on hover.\n6. `textValue` defaults to the children when they are a plain string, so typeahead and screen readers get a label without extra props.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `tag height = medium h-7 (28px) px-2; small h-6 (24px) px-1.5`\n  Matches Chip, so a tag and a static chip in the same row line up.\n- `selected fill = bg-accent-500 text-white`\n  One accent fill for every selected tag, whatever its resting colour, so selection reads the same across a coloured set.\n- `transition = background-color, color, box-shadow, filter over 150ms ease-out`\n  Covers the selection fill, the hover brightness and the focus ring without animating layout.\n- `remove button = size-5 (size-4 small), opacity 0.6 → 1 on hover`\n  Quiet until pointed at, so a row of removable tags still reads as labels first.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype TagSize = \"medium\" | \"small\"\ninterface TagGroupProps<T extends object> extends Omit<AriaTagGroupProps, \"className\" | \"children\"> { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; emptyState?: ReactNode; size?: TagSize; items?: Iterable<T>; children: ReactNode | ((item: T) => ReactNode); className?: string }\ninterface TagProps extends Omit<AriaTagProps, \"className\" | \"children\"> { color?: ChipColor; icon?: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>; className?: string; children: ReactNode }\nfunction TagGroup<T extends object>(props: TagGroupProps<T>): JSX.Element\nfunction Tag(props: TagProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  TagGroup: visible label above the tags.\n- `hint: ReactNode`\n  TagGroup: helper text under the tags.\n- `emptyState: ReactNode`\n  TagGroup: shown in place of the tags when the list is empty.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  TagGroup: 28px or 24px tags.\n- `items: Iterable<T>`\n  TagGroup: data for a children render function.\n- `selectionMode: \"none\" | \"single\" | \"multiple\"` — default `\"none\"`\n  TagGroup: lets tags toggle (React Aria).\n- `selectedKeys / defaultSelectedKeys: Iterable<Key> | \"all\"`\n  TagGroup: controlled or uncontrolled selection (React Aria).\n- `onRemove: (keys: Set<Key>) => void`\n  TagGroup: turns on remove buttons and Backspace/Delete (React Aria).\n- `color: ChipColor` — default `\"neutral\"`\n  Tag: one of Chip's colour pairs.\n- `icon: ComponentType`\n  Tag: Remix Icon before the label.\n- `id: Key`\n  Tag: key used for selection and removal.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Tag({ color = \"neutral\", icon: Icon, className, children, ...props }: TagProps) {\n  const size = useContext(SizeContext);\n\n  return (\n    <AriaTag\n      {...props}\n      textValue={props.textValue ?? (typeof children === \"string\" ? children : undefined)}\n      className={({ isSelected, selectionMode, allowsRemoving, isHovered, isFocusVisible, isDisabled }) => {\n        const allowsSelection = selectionMode !== \"none\";\n        return cx(\n          \"inline-flex max-w-full items-center gap-1 rounded-lg outline-none\",\n          \"transition-[background-color,color,box-shadow,filter] duration-150 ease-out\",\n          size === \"small\" ? \"h-6 px-1.5 text-caption-1-medium\" : \"h-7 px-2 text-body-medium\",\n          allowsRemoving && (size === \"small\" ? \"pe-0.5\" : \"pe-1\"),\n          isSelected ? \"bg-accent-500 text-white\" : CHIP_COLORS[color],\n          allowsSelection ? \"cursor-pointer\" : \"cursor-default\",\n          allowsSelection && !isSelected && isHovered && (color === \"neutral\" ? \"bg-background-tertiary-hover\" : \"brightness-95\"),\n          isFocusVisible && \"ring-2 ring-border-focus-ring\",\n          isDisabled && \"cursor-not-allowed opacity-50\",\n          className,\n        );\n      }}\n    >\n      {({ allowsRemoving }) => (\n        <>\n          {Icon ? <Icon aria-hidden className={cx(\"shrink-0\", size === \"small\" ? \"size-3.5\" : \"size-4\")} /> : null}\n          <span className=\"truncate\">{children}</span>\n          {allowsRemoving ? (\n            <AriaButton\n              slot=\"remove\"\n              className={cx(\n                \"flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-md opacity-60 outline-none\",\n                \"transition-[opacity,background-color] duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-current/15 hover:opacity-100\",\n                size === \"small\" ? \"size-4\" : \"size-5\",\n              )}\n            >\n              <RiCloseLine aria-hidden className={size === \"small\" ? \"size-3\" : \"size-3.5\"} />\n            </AriaButton>\n          ) : null}\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaTag>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. React Aria owns focus, selection and keyboard handling; the component adds no listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only colour, shadow and filter transition, over 150ms, and nothing moves. There is no reduced-motion branch because there is no spatial motion to remove.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Give the group a `label` or an `aria-label`. React Aria's TagGroup is a grid and needs an accessible name.\n- When tags are not plain strings (an icon plus text in a fragment, a formatted value), pass `textValue` so typeahead and screen readers have text.\n- Removing is your state change. `onRemove` hands you the keys; filter them out of your list, or nothing disappears.\n- With `items`, give each item an `id` (or a `key`) so React Aria can track it.\n- Do not nest your own buttons or links in a tag. The only control inside is the remove button React Aria wires up.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Colours come straight from Chip's exported `CHIP_COLORS`, so tags and chips stay one palette.\n- Selection is React Aria's: `selectionMode` \"single\" or \"multiple\" with `selectedKeys` / `defaultSelectedKeys` and `onSelectionChange`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA \"Topics\" group with `selectionMode=\"multiple\"` and Design and Engineering selected shows two accent-filled tags among neutral ones. Clicking Marketing fills it; Tab focuses the group once and the arrow keys walk the tags with a visible ring. A group with `onRemove` shows a faint close icon in each tag that brightens on hover; clicking it, or pressing Delete on a focused tag, calls `onRemove` with that key. When the last tag is removed the empty state text appears in its place. `size=\"small\"` renders 24px tags.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "task-list": "# Build: Task List\n\nRenders what an agent did as a list of tasks, each a clickable header with a nested list of steps that can carry file chips. Every header and every step is one reveal unit paced by the Agent Log ticker, so the log grows row by row with a blur-in and a curved tree guide. A running task shimmers its title; once its last step lands it switches to doneTitle and, with collapseOnComplete, folds away while the header stays clickable.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA streaming agent log: task headers and their steps blur in one unit at a time down a curved guide, then each task settles and folds.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. countTaskUnits sums 1 + steps.length per task; useRevealTicker from agent-log returns how many units have landed, from its own timer (startDelay, then stepInterval) or from the controlled revealed prop.\n2. One pass over the tasks hands each its slice: the header is visible once revealed passes its start offset, shown = clamp(revealed - start - 1, 0, steps.length) steps are rendered, and done means all of its units are in.\n3. Headers mount as motion.li with the shared UNIT_INITIAL/UNIT_ANIMATE blur-and-height reveal; steps are agent-log LogRow items, each drawing its own curved branch, and a GuideBridge drops 6px from the 16px header glyph to the trunk at ms-2.\n4. Open state is overrides[id] ?? !autoClosed, where autoClosed is done (collapseOnComplete true) or the whole run being complete (\"all\"); the region collapses with a grid-template-rows 1fr to 0fr transition and is inert while closed.\n5. Until the last unit lands a WorkingRow (Agent Thinking stars plus elapsed timer) sits at the tail; onComplete fires once through the ticker when revealed reaches the total.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `startDelay = 320ms`\n  Keeps the first row from landing on the same frame as the user turn that started the run.\n- `stepInterval = 850ms (demo 700ms)`\n  One unit per beat reads as work happening; faster reads as a list being printed.\n- `GuideBridge height = 6px`\n  Header row is 28px (20px line + 4px padding each side) and the 16px glyph ends at 22px, so 6px joins it to the nested trunk.\n- `collapse transition = 300ms ease-out`\n  Folding a task should finish before the next unit lands at 700-850ms intervals.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\n<TaskList key={turnId} tasks={tasks} startDelay={320} stepInterval={850} collapseOnComplete onComplete={() => setPhase(\"answered\")} />\n```\n\n- `tasks: readonly TaskItem[]`\n  Tasks with id, title, optional doneTitle and icon, and steps ({ label, chips? }).\n- `run: boolean` — default `true`\n  Pauses and resumes the internal ticker.\n- `startDelay: number` — default `320`\n  Milliseconds before the first unit lands.\n- `stepInterval: number` — default `850`\n  Milliseconds between units.\n- `revealed: number`\n  Units landed, when driven from real events; disables the timer.\n- `collapseOnComplete: boolean | \"all\"` — default `false`\n  Fold each task when its steps land, or all tasks when the run completes.\n- `working: string | false` — default `\"Working\"`\n  Tail indicator label while running; false hides it.\n- `onComplete: () => void`\n  Fires once when the final unit lands.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Agent tasks\"`\n  Accessible name of the log.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Walk the tasks once, handing each its slice of the revealed units: the\n// first unit shows the header, the rest land its steps in order. A task is\n// done once all of its units are in, which settles its title and, with\n// collapseOnComplete, folds it (or waits for the whole run with \"all\").\nlet offset = 0;\nconst blocks = tasks.map((task) => {\n  const start = offset;\n  offset += taskUnits(task);\n  const visible = revealed > start;\n  const shown = Math.max(0, Math.min(task.steps.length, revealed - start - 1));\n  const done = revealed >= start + taskUnits(task);\n  const autoClosed =\n    collapseOnComplete === \"all\" ? complete : collapseOnComplete ? done : false;\n  const open = overrides[task.id] ?? !autoClosed;\n  return { task, visible, shown, done, open };\n});\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The ticker clears its pending timeout whenever revealed, run or pacing changes and on unmount; the Working row timer interval is cleared on unmount. No other listeners are attached.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Rows and headers appear without the blur, lift or height animation, the guide is drawn immediately, the title shimmer and star twinkle stop, and folding happens instantly. Units still arrive on the same schedule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Key the list on the turn: changing tasks in place keeps the revealed count, so a fresh run needs a new key.\n- Appending tasks to a finished list resumes the ticker and fires onComplete again when the new units land.\n- Give every task a stable id; open overrides are stored by id.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Chip icons are ReactNodes sized to 14px by the chip; colour them with theme tokens.\n- Pass working={false} to drop the tail indicator, or a string to relabel it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe log starts with \"Searching project files\" shimmering and its steps landing one by one with file chips; when the fourth step lands the title reads \"Found project files\" and the task folds; after the last task the Working row disappears and onComplete shows the reply. Clicking a folded header sets aria-expanded to true and shows its steps; Add task appends a follow-up that streams in; Replay restarts from the first task.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tech-badges-row": "# Build: Tech Badges Row\n\nThe row of technologies under a landing-page hero: a small glyph tile, the name and a muted version per entry, separated by dots. Glyphs are simple monogram tiles drawn inline (a rounded square and one or two letters in a tone), never brand logos, so any stack can be listed. Hovering or tabbing onto a badge lifts it onto a bordered surface and opens the kit Tooltip with the name, version and a one-line description. An `icon` variant drops the labels for tight spaces and lets the tooltip carry the name.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA \"built with\" strip of monogram glyphs, names and versions; each badge lifts on hover or focus and names itself in a tooltip.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Render a `ul` (`aria-label` \"Built with\") of `li`s that wrap and centre; in the `label` variant a dot separator precedes every badge after the first, hidden below 640px where the row wraps.\n2. Each badge is a `span role=\"img\"` with `aria-label` \"<name> <version>\" inside react-aria `Focusable`, which gives it a tab stop and lets the kit `TooltipTrigger` attach hover, focus and `aria-describedby` to a non-button element.\n3. Draw the glyph as a 20×20 SVG: a tile in the badge `tone` (rx 5.5), a 1px white inner stroke at 18% so dark tiles still read on a dark page, and the monogram centred with `dy=\"0.36em\"` — 11px for one letter, 8.5px with tighter tracking for two.\n4. The lift is CSS: on `hover:` and `focus-visible:` the badge translates up 2px (4px for icon tiles) and gains `bg-background-primary-default`, a `border-button-default` border and `shadow-md`, over 200ms ease-out. `motion-reduce` keeps the surface change and cancels the translate and transition.\n5. The kit `Tooltip` (placement top, 8px offset, no delay) shows the name and version in semibold caption type, with the description on a second, secondary line.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `lift = -2px label · -4px icon, 200ms ease-out`\n  Enough to read as picked up without the row looking jumpy when the pointer sweeps across it.\n- `glyph = 16px in the label row, 20px on a 40px tile`\n  The label glyph matches the cap height of 13px body-2 text; the icon tile stays a comfortable 40px pointer target.\n- `monogram size = 11px (1 letter) · 8.5px, -0.3 tracking (2 letters)`\n  Two-letter monograms must fit the 20-unit tile with side bearing to spare.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TechBadgesRow(props: { badges?: TechBadge[]; variant?: \"label\" | \"icon\"; label?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype TechBadge = { id: string; name: string; version?: string; monogram: string; tone: string; description?: string }\n```\n\n- `badges: TechBadge[]` — default `DEFAULT_TECH_BADGES`\n  Entries: id, name, optional version, a 1–2 letter monogram, a tile tone and an optional tooltip description.\n- `variant: \"label\" | \"icon\"` — default `\"label\"`\n  `label` shows glyph, name and version with dot separators; `icon` shows 40px glyph tiles only.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Built with\"`\n  Accessible name of the list.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the list.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** A rounded tile with a 1px inner highlight and the monogram centred on it. */\nfunction MonogramGlyph({ monogram, tone, className }: { monogram: string; tone: string; className?: string }) {\n  const small = monogram.length > 1;\n  return (\n    <svg viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" aria-hidden className={cx(\"shrink-0\", className)}>\n      <rect width=\"20\" height=\"20\" rx=\"5.5\" fill={tone} />\n      <rect x=\"0.5\" y=\"0.5\" width=\"19\" height=\"19\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ffffff\" strokeOpacity=\"0.18\" />\n      <text\n        x=\"10\"\n        y=\"10\"\n        dy=\"0.36em\"\n        textAnchor=\"middle\"\n        fill=\"#ffffff\"\n        fontSize={small ? 8.5 : 11}\n        fontWeight={700}\n        letterSpacing={small ? -0.3 : 0}\n        style={{ fontFamily: \"inherit\" }}\n      >\n        {monogram}\n      </text>\n    </svg>\n  );\n}\n\n/*\n * The lift: on hover or focus-visible the badge rises 2px (4px for icon\n * tiles) and picks up a surface, border and shadow, over 200ms ease-out.\n * Under reduced motion the surface still appears but nothing moves.\n */\nconst LIFT =\n  \"transition-[translate,background-color,border-color,box-shadow] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none \" +\n  \"hover:shadow-md hover:bg-background-primary-default hover:border-border-button-default \" +\n  \"focus-visible:shadow-md focus-visible:bg-background-primary-default focus-visible:border-border-button-default \" +\n  \"outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\";\n\nfunction Badge({ badge, variant }: { badge: TechBadge; variant: \"label\" | \"icon\" }) {\n  const full = badge.version ? `${badge.name} ${badge.version}` : badge.name;\n  return (\n    <TooltipTrigger>\n      <Focusable>\n        <span\n          role=\"img\"\n          aria-label={full}\n          className={cx(\n            \"inline-flex cursor-default items-center border select-none\",\n            LIFT,\n            variant === \"label\"\n              ? \"gap-1.5 rounded-full border-transparent px-2.5 py-1 hover:-translate-y-0.5 focus-visible:-translate-y-0.5 motion-reduce:hover:translate-y-0 motion-reduce:focus-visible:translate-y-0\"\n              : \"size-10 justify-center rounded-xl border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-xs hover:-translate-y-1 focus-visible:-translate-y-1 motion-reduce:hover:translate-y-0 motion-reduce:focus-visible:translate-y-0\",\n          )}\n        >\n          <MonogramGlyph monogram={badge.monogram} tone={badge.tone} className={variant === \"label\" ? \"size-4\" : \"size-5\"} />\n          {variant === \"label\" && (\n            <>\n              <span className=\"text-body-2-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary\">{badge.name}</span>\n              {badge.version && (\n                <span className=\"text-caption-1-regular whitespace-nowrap text-text-tertiary\">v{badge.version}</span>\n              )}\n            </>\n          )}\n        </span>\n      </Focusable>\n      <Tooltip placement=\"top\" offset={8}>\n        <span className=\"text-caption-1-semibold\">{full}</span>\n        {badge.description && <span className=\"block text-caption-2-regular text-text-secondary\">{badge.description}</span>}\n      </Tooltip>\n    </TooltipTrigger>\n  );\n}\n\nexport function TechBadgesRow({\n  badges = DEFAULT_TECH_BADGES,\n  variant = \"label\",\n  label = \"Built with\",\n  className,\n}: TechBadgesRowProps) {\n  return (\n    <ul\n      aria-label={label}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center\",\n        variant === \"label\" ? \"gap-x-1 gap-y-2\" : \"gap-2\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {badges.map((b, i) => (\n        <li key={b.id} className=\"flex items-center gap-1\">\n          {variant === \"label\" && i > 0 && (\n            <span aria-hidden className=\"hidden px-1 text-text-disabled sm:inline\">\n              ·\n            </span>\n          )}\n          <Badge badge={b} variant={variant} />\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: hover lift is CSS and react-aria owns the tooltip timers and listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Badges still gain their surface, border and shadow on hover and focus, but `motion-reduce` cancels the translate and the transition, so nothing moves; the kit Tooltip appears without its scale and blur.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Shipping traced brand logos as glyphs: they carry trademark rules and break the row's visual rhythm. Monogram tiles in a tone are neutral and extensible.\n- Putting tooltips on plain non-focusable spans: keyboard and screen-reader users never get them. `Focusable` plus an `img` role makes each badge reachable and described.\n- Relying on the tooltip for the name in the `icon` variant without an `aria-label`: tooltips are descriptions, the badge itself still needs a name.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `DEFAULT_TECH_BADGES` covers Next.js, React, Tailwind CSS, TypeScript, Motion and React Aria; pass `badges` to add or replace entries.\n- Tones are SVG fills, so any CSS colour works; the white inner stroke keeps near-black tiles visible in dark mode.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe row reads \"Next.js v16 · React v19.2 · Tailwind CSS v4 · TypeScript v5.9 · Motion v12 · React Aria v1.17\", each with a monogram tile. Hovering \"React\" lifts that badge 2px and opens a tooltip reading \"React 19.2 / UI runtime\". Tabbing onto a badge does the same. In the icon card, hovering the \"TT\" tile shows \"TanStack Table 8.21\".\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "template-showcase-tabs": "# Build: Template Showcase Tabs\n\nThe \"templates\" section of a product landing page: a centred header (Pro pill, title, copy, CTA) over a rounded card. The card holds a horizontally scrollable row of icon tabs with an action slot on the right (e.g. \"Live preview\"), and a large preview well where each tab shows a miniature app layout drawn with plain divs. A soft blue pill slides under the selected tab and the previews crossfade instead of popping.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLanding section with a framed card of template tabs: a pill slides to the chosen tab and miniature app layouts crossfade below it.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Tabs: react-aria-components `Tabs` / `TabList` / `Tab` / `TabPanel`, so arrow keys, Home/End, roving focus and the tab/tabpanel wiring come for free. Selection is controlled internally (or by `selectedKey`) so the component always knows which tab to measure.\n2. Sliding pill: after every selection change a layout effect finds `[role=tab][data-selected]` and stores its `offsetLeft` / `offsetWidth`. The pill is an absolutely positioned span inside the scrolling strip, placed with `translate` and `width` and transitioned over 300ms. Offset metrics are layout values, so the pill stays aligned inside CSS-scaled previews where `getBoundingClientRect` would not. A ResizeObserver on the strip and on each tab re-measures when fonts load or labels change.\n3. Crossfade: every `TabPanel` uses `shouldForceMount`, and all panels share one grid cell (`col-start-1 row-start-1`). react-aria marks unselected panels `inert` and `data-inert`; `data-inert:` turns them `invisible`, `opacity-0` and `scale-[0.985]` with a 300ms transition on opacity, scale and visibility. The outgoing preview fades while the incoming one fades in on top of it, and the inert ones are out of the tab order and the accessibility tree.\n4. Overflow: the strip scrolls sideways with its scrollbar hidden. A scroll listener records whether there is content before or after the viewport and adds `mask-l-from` / `mask-r-from` edge fades only on those sides. When selection moves to a tab that is clipped, the strip scrolls just enough (24px margin) to reveal it with `scrollTo`, never `scrollIntoView`, so the page itself does not jump.\n5. Previews are wrapped in `role=\"img\"` with an `aria-label` of \"<label> template preview\": they are pictures of an app, not working UI.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pill transition = 300ms cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)`\n  Fast start, long settle: the pill arrives before the crossfade finishes, which reads as cause then effect.\n- `crossfade = 300ms ease-out, scale 0.985 → 1`\n  A tiny scale keeps the change noticeable when two previews share a similar layout.\n- `edge fade = mask from calc(100% − 3rem)`\n  Signals more tabs offscreen without hiding the label under the action button.\n- `preview height = 460px / 540px @3xl`\n  Tall enough for a sidebar app layout to read; all panels share it so the card never changes height on switch.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TemplateShowcaseTabs(props: { items: { id: string; label: string; icon?: ComponentType<{ className?: string }>; preview: ReactNode }[]; selectedKey?: string; defaultSelectedKey?: string; onSelectionChange?: (id: string) => void; action?: ReactNode | ((item) => ReactNode); \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction TemplateShowcase(props: { eyebrow?: ReactNode; title?: ReactNode; description?: ReactNode; actions?: ReactNode; children: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `items: TemplateShowcaseItem[]`\n  `{ id, label, icon?, preview }` per tab; `preview` is the miniature layout.\n- `selectedKey: string`\n  Controlled selected tab id.\n- `defaultSelectedKey: string` — default `items[0].id`\n  Initial tab when uncontrolled.\n- `onSelectionChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the tab changes.\n- `action: ReactNode | (item) => ReactNode`\n  Right side of the tab bar.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Templates\"`\n  Label for the tab list.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * A framed card: a scrollable row of tabs over a preview area. The selected tab\n * sits on a pill that slides between tabs (measured with offsetLeft/offsetWidth,\n * which ignore CSS transforms, so it stays correct inside scaled previews).\n * Every panel stays mounted in one grid cell; unselected ones carry `inert`\n * from react-aria, and `data-inert` fades them out, so switching is a crossfade.\n */\nexport function TemplateShowcaseTabs({\n  items,\n  selectedKey,\n  defaultSelectedKey,\n  onSelectionChange,\n  action,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Templates\",\n  className,\n}: TemplateShowcaseTabsProps) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [internal, setInternal] = useState<string | undefined>(defaultSelectedKey ?? items[0]?.id);\n  const selected = selectedKey ?? internal;\n  const current = items.find((item) => item.id === selected) ?? items[0];\n\n  const scrollerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const [pill, setPill] = useState<Pill | null>(null);\n  const [edges, setEdges] = useState({ start: false, end: false });\n  const firstRun = useRef(true);\n\n  useIsoLayoutEffect(() => {\n    const scroller = scrollerRef.current;\n    if (!scroller) return;\n    const measure = () => {\n      const tab = scroller.querySelector<HTMLElement>(\"[role='tab'][data-selected]\");\n      if (tab) setPill({ left: tab.offsetLeft, width: tab.offsetWidth });\n      const max = scroller.scrollWidth - scroller.clientWidth;\n      const at = Math.abs(scroller.scrollLeft);\n      setEdges({ start: at > 1, end: max - at > 1 });\n    };\n    measure();\n\n    // Bring the newly selected tab fully into view without touching the page scroll.\n    const tab = scroller.querySelector<HTMLElement>(\"[role='tab'][data-selected]\");\n    if (tab && !firstRun.current) {\n      const pad = 24;\n      const left = tab.offsetLeft;\n      const right = left + tab.offsetWidth;\n      let next: number | null = null;\n      if (left - pad < scroller.scrollLeft) next = left - pad;\n      else if (right + pad > scroller.scrollLeft + scroller.clientWidth) next = right + pad - scroller.clientWidth;\n      if (next !== null) scroller.scrollTo({ left: next, behavior: reduced ? \"auto\" : \"smooth\" });\n    }\n    firstRun.current = false;\n\n    const ro = new ResizeObserver(measure);\n    ro.observe(scroller);\n    scroller.querySelectorAll(\"[role='tab']\").forEach((el) => ro.observe(el));\n    scroller.addEventListener(\"scroll\", measure, { passive: true });\n    return () => {\n      ro.disconnect();\n      scroller.removeEventListener(\"scroll\", measure);\n    };\n  }, [selected, items.length, reduced]);\n\n  const handleChange = (key: Key) => {\n    const id = String(key);\n    if (selectedKey === undefined) setInternal(id);\n    onSelectionChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  const actionNode = typeof action === \"function\" ? (current ? action(current) : null) : action;\n\n  return (\n    <Tabs\n      selectedKey={selected}\n      onSelectionChange={handleChange}\n      className={cx(\n        \"@container/showcase flex w-full flex-col gap-2 rounded-[28px] border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-2 shadow-xs\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <div className=\"flex items-center gap-2\">\n        <div\n          ref={scrollerRef}\n          className={cx(\n            \"relative min-w-0 flex-1 overflow-x-auto [scrollbar-width:none] [&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden\",\n            edges.start && \"mask-l-from-[calc(100%-3rem)]\",\n            edges.end && \"mask-r-from-[calc(100%-3rem)]\",\n          )}\n        >\n          {pill ? (\n            <span\n              aria-hidden=\"true\"\n              className={cx(\n                \"pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 left-0 rounded-2xl bg-pill-tab-blue-selected-background\",\n                !reduced && \"transition-[translate,width] duration-300 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)]\",\n              )}\n              style={{ width: pill.width, translate: `${pill.left}px 0` }}\n            />\n          ) : null}\n          <TabList aria-label={ariaLabel} className=\"relative flex w-max items-center gap-0.5\">\n            {items.map(({ id, label, icon: Icon }) => (\n              <Tab\n                key={id}\n                id={id}\n                className={cx(\n                  \"group/tab relative flex h-10 shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2 rounded-2xl px-3.5 text-headline-regular whitespace-nowrap outline-none\",\n                  \"text-text-secondary transition-colors duration-150 ease-out\",\n                  \"data-hovered:text-text-primary not-data-selected:data-hovered:bg-pill-tab-blue-hover-background\",\n                  \"data-selected:text-accent-500\",\n                  \"data-focus-visible:ring-2 data-focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring data-focus-visible:ring-inset\",\n                )}\n              >\n                {Icon ? (\n                  <Icon className=\"size-[18px] shrink-0 text-foreground-icon-secondary group-data-selected/tab:text-accent-500\" />\n                ) : null}\n                {label}\n              </Tab>\n            ))}\n          </TabList>\n        </div>\n        {actionNode ? <div className=\"flex shrink-0 items-center pe-1\">{actionNode}</div> : null}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"@container/preview grid overflow-hidden rounded-[20px] border border-border-button-default bg-background-secondary-default\">\n        {items.map((item) => (\n          <TabPanel\n            key={item.id}\n            id={item.id}\n            shouldForceMount\n            className={cx(\n              \"col-start-1 row-start-1 h-[460px] outline-none @3xl/showcase:h-[540px]\",\n              \"data-inert:invisible data-inert:opacity-0 data-inert:scale-[0.985]\",\n              !reduced && \"transition-[opacity,scale,visibility] duration-300 ease-out\",\n              \"data-focus-visible:ring-2 data-focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring data-focus-visible:ring-inset\",\n            )}\n          >\n            <div role=\"img\" aria-label={`${item.label} template preview`} className=\"h-full w-full\">\n              {item.preview}\n            </div>\n          </TabPanel>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n    </Tabs>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The layout effect disconnects its ResizeObserver and removes the strip scroll listener on every re-run and on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The pill jumps to the selected tab and panels switch without the fade or scale (both transitions are omitted); the tab strip scrolls with `behavior: \"auto\"`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Measuring tabs with `getBoundingClientRect` inside a scaled preview: the pill lands in the wrong place by the scale factor. Use offset metrics.\n- Rendering only the selected panel: nothing exists to fade out, so the swap pops. Force-mount the panels and fade by `data-inert`.\n- Fading with opacity alone: invisible panels are still on top for hit testing and still measured as \"visible\" by tests. Transition `visibility` with the opacity.\n- Calling `scrollIntoView` on the selected tab also scrolls the page vertically when the card is partly offscreen; set the strip's `scrollLeft` instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The demo draws seven template previews (agentic chat, image generation, kanban board, marketing, contributor profile, healthcare, analytics) from divs, tokens and a few kit parts (Avatar, Chip). Inside the preview, `@container/preview` queries hide the sidebar and aside on narrow screens.\n- `action` accepts a function of the selected item, so a \"Live preview\" button can label or link itself per template.\n- `TemplateShowcase` is the optional centred header wrapper; the tabs card works on its own.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAt 1280px the card shows seven tabs with Agentic chat on the blue pill and a chat app preview below. Clicking Project board slides the pill there and crossfades to a four-column kanban; arrow keys move between tabs the same way. The Live preview button label follows the tab. At 390px the strip scrolls sideways with an edge fade, selecting a clipped tab scrolls it into view, and the previews drop their sidebars.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "testimonial-carousel": "# Build: Testimonial Carousel\n\nA landing-page testimonial strip: a pill label, then a horizontal track where the active quote sits centred and its neighbours peek in at the edges, faded out by a mask. Each slide shows an initials avatar, the name, \"role at company\" with an optional link and handle, and a large quote. Below sit previous / next buttons, a row of dots whose active dot widens into a pill that fills as the autoplay timer runs, and a button to stop autoplay.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA centred quote carousel with faded neighbours, initials avatars, prev / next, progress dots and autoplay that pauses on hover and focus.\n\n- Category: block\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Slides sit in one flex track, each `flex-basis: var(--slide)`. The section is a size container and sets `--slide` to 86% below `@2xl` and 60% from it. The track is translated by `calc((100% - var(--slide)) / 2 - var(--slide) * index)`, which centres the active slide and leaves the neighbours peeking in on both sides. Percentages resolve against the track, which is exactly as wide as the viewport.\n2. The viewport clips with `overflow-hidden` and fades both edges with Tailwind `mask-x-from-80% mask-x-to-100%` (92% when narrow), so neighbours dissolve rather than end at a hard edge. Inactive slides also drop to 40% opacity and get `inert` plus `aria-hidden`, which keeps their links out of the tab order.\n3. The autoplay timer is the active dot itself: a Web Animation grows the dot fill from `scale: 0 1` to `scale: 1 1` over `interval` ms, and its `onfinish` advances to the next slide. Pausing calls `animation.pause()`, so the remaining time is kept and the fill visibly stops.\n4. Hover (`pointerenter` / `pointerleave` on the section) and focus inside the section (`focus`, and `blur` whose `relatedTarget` falls outside) both pause it. The stop button turns autoplay off entirely; `prefers-reduced-motion` turns it off and hides that button.\n5. Navigation wraps with `((next % count) + count) % count`. Each change calls `onIndexChange`. The track uses `aria-live=\"off\"` while autoplay is running and `\"polite\"` otherwise, so manual changes are announced and timed ones are not.\n6. The section carries `aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"`; each slide is a `figure` with `role=\"group\"`, `aria-roledescription=\"slide\"` and an \"n of N\" label. Dots are buttons labelled \"Show testimonial n\" with `aria-current` on the active one.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `--slide = 60% from @2xl, 86% below`\n  Wide: 20% of each neighbour shows, enough to read as a carousel without competing with the active quote. Narrow: the quote needs nearly the full width, so only a sliver peeks in.\n- `SLIDE_MS = 520`\n  Long enough for the eye to follow a full-width slide, with an ease-out-expo curve `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)` so it settles quickly.\n- `interval = 6000`\n  Roughly the time needed to read a two-line quote twice. WCAG 2.2.2 asks for a way to pause anything that moves for over 5 seconds, which the hover, focus and stop controls provide.\n- `edge mask = mask-x-from-80% (92% narrow)`\n  The fade spans the neighbour peek exactly, so neighbour text fades out towards the edge and the active slide stays fully opaque.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TestimonialCarousel(props: { items: Testimonial[]; label?: ReactNode; \"aria-label\"?: string; interval?: number; defaultIndex?: number; onIndexChange?: (index: number) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ninterface Testimonial { id: string; quote: ReactNode; name: string; role: string; company?: string; companyHref?: string; handle?: string; initials?: string; avatarColor?: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\" }\n```\n\n- `items: Testimonial[]`\n  Quotes in order.\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Pill text above the track.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Testimonials\"`\n  Accessible name of the carousel section.\n- `interval: number` — default `6000`\n  Autoplay time per quote in ms; 0 disables autoplay.\n- `defaultIndex: number` — default `0`\n  Initially active quote (clamped to the item range).\n- `onIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Fires on every change, manual or timed.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the section.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function TestimonialCarousel({\n  items,\n  label,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Testimonials\",\n  interval = 6000,\n  defaultIndex = 0,\n  onIndexChange,\n  className,\n}: TestimonialCarouselProps) {\n  const uid = useId();\n  const count = items.length;\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [index, setIndex] = useState(() => Math.min(Math.max(defaultIndex, 0), Math.max(count - 1, 0)));\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(false);\n  const [focused, setFocused] = useState(false);\n  const [stopped, setStopped] = useState(false);\n  const fillRef = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);\n  const animRef = useRef<Animation | null>(null);\n\n  const go = useCallback(\n    (next: number) => {\n      if (count === 0) return;\n      const wrapped = ((next % count) + count) % count;\n      setIndex(wrapped);\n      onIndexChange?.(wrapped);\n    },\n    [count, onIndexChange],\n  );\n\n  // Autoplay is never on under reduced motion, with one item, or after the user stops it.\n  const autoplay = interval > 0 && count > 1 && !reduced && !stopped;\n  const paused = hovered || focused;\n\n  // The active dot's fill IS the timer: a Web Animation that grows across `interval`\n  // and advances the slide when it finishes. Pausing the animation pauses the timer\n  // with its remaining time intact, so hover never resets the countdown.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const fill = fillRef.current;\n    if (!autoplay || !fill || typeof fill.animate !== \"function\") return;\n    const anim = fill.animate([{ scale: \"0 1\" }, { scale: \"1 1\" }], {\n      duration: interval,\n      easing: \"linear\",\n      fill: \"forwards\",\n    });\n    anim.onfinish = () => go(index + 1);\n    animRef.current = anim;\n    return () => {\n      anim.onfinish = null;\n      anim.cancel();\n      animRef.current = null;\n    };\n  }, [autoplay, interval, index, go]);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const anim = animRef.current;\n    if (!anim) return;\n    if (paused) anim.pause();\n    else if (anim.playState === \"paused\") anim.play();\n  }, [paused, index, autoplay]);\n\n  const onBlur = (event: FocusEvent<HTMLElement>) => {\n    if (!event.currentTarget.contains(event.relatedTarget as Node | null)) setFocused(false);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      aria-roledescription=\"carousel\"\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      onPointerEnter={() => setHovered(true)}\n      onPointerLeave={() => setHovered(false)}\n      onFocus={() => setFocused(true)}\n      onBlur={onBlur}\n      className={cx(\"@container flex w-full flex-col items-center\", className)}\n    >\n      {label && (\n        <span className=\"rounded-full bg-background-primary-default px-3.5 py-1.5 text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary shadow-xs ring-1 ring-separator-border\">\n          {label}\n        </span>\n      )}\n\n      <div className={cx(\"mt-8 w-full overflow-hidden mask-x-from-92% mask-x-to-100% @2xl:mask-x-from-80%\", SLIDE_WIDTH)}>\n        <div\n          aria-live={autoplay && !paused ? \"off\" : \"polite\"}\n          className=\"flex w-full transition-transform ease-[cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1)] motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n          style={{\n            // Percentages resolve against the track, which is exactly as wide as the viewport.\n            transform: `translateX(calc((100% - var(--slide)) / 2 - var(--slide) * ${index}))`,\n            transitionDuration: `${SLIDE_MS}ms`,\n          }}\n        >\n          {items.map((item, i) => {\n            const active = i === index;\n            return (\n              <figure\n                key={item.id}\n                id={`${uid}-slide-${i}`}\n                role=\"group\"\n                aria-roledescription=\"slide\"\n                aria-label={`${i + 1} of ${count}`}\n                aria-hidden={!active}\n                inert={!active}\n                className={cx(\n                  \"flex shrink-0 basis-(--slide) flex-col items-center px-4 text-center transition-opacity duration-500 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none @container\",\n                  active ? \"opacity-100\" : \"opacity-40\",\n                )}\n              >\n                <figcaption className=\"flex items-center gap-3 text-left\">\n                  <Avatar\n                    size=\"lg\"\n                    color={item.avatarColor ?? \"neutral\"}\n                    initials={item.initials ?? initialsOf(item.name)}\n                    className=\"size-11 text-headline-semibold\"\n                  />\n                  <span className=\"flex flex-col\">\n                    <span className=\"text-body-semibold text-text-primary\">{item.name}</span>\n                    <span className=\"text-body-2-regular text-text-tertiary\">\n                      {item.role}\n                      {item.company && (\n                        <>\n                          {\" at \"}\n                          {item.companyHref ? (\n                            <a\n                              href={item.companyHref}\n                              className=\"text-text-secondary underline decoration-separator-border-strong underline-offset-2 hover:text-text-primary\"\n                            >\n                              {item.company}\n                            </a>\n                          ) : (\n                            <span className=\"text-text-secondary\">{item.company}</span>\n                          )}\n                        </>\n                      )}\n                      {item.handle && <span className=\"text-text-tertiary\">{` · ${item.handle}`}</span>}\n                    </span>\n                  </span>\n                </figcaption>\n                <blockquote className=\"mt-6 max-w-3xl text-title-2-medium tracking-tight text-balance text-text-primary @lg:text-title-1-medium\">\n                  <p>{item.quote}</p>\n                </blockquote>\n              </figure>\n            );\n          })}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      {count > 1 && (\n        <div className=\"mt-8 flex items-center gap-3\">\n          <IconButton size=\"small\" icon={RiArrowLeftSLine} aria-label=\"Previous testimonial\" onClick={() => go(index - 1)} className=\"rounded-full\" />\n          <div className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5\">\n            {items.map((item, i) => {\n              const active = i === index;\n              return (\n                <button\n                  key={item.id}\n                  type=\"button\"\n                  aria-label={`Show testimonial ${i + 1}`}\n                  aria-controls={`${uid}-slide-${i}`}\n                  aria-current={active ? \"true\" : undefined}\n                  onClick={() => go(i)}\n                  className=\"group grid h-6 cursor-pointer place-items-center rounded-full outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\"\n                >\n                  <span\n                    className={cx(\n                      \"relative block h-1.5 overflow-hidden rounded-full transition-[width,background-color] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                      active ? \"w-8 bg-background-quaternary-default\" : \"w-1.5 bg-background-tertiary-hover group-hover:bg-foreground-icon-quaternary\",\n                    )}\n                  >\n                    {active && (\n                      <span\n                        ref={fillRef}\n                        aria-hidden\n                        className={cx(\"absolute inset-0 origin-left rounded-full bg-text-primary\", autoplay && \"scale-x-0\")}\n                      />\n                    )}\n                  </span>\n                </button>\n              );\n            })}\n          </div>\n          <IconButton size=\"small\" icon={RiArrowRightSLine} aria-label=\"Next testimonial\" onClick={() => go(index + 1)} className=\"rounded-full\" />\n          {interval > 0 && !reduced && (\n            <IconButton\n              size=\"small\"\n              icon={stopped ? RiPlayLine : RiPauseLine}\n              aria-label={stopped ? \"Start autoplay\" : \"Stop autoplay\"}\n              onClick={() => setStopped((s) => !s)}\n              className=\"ml-1 rounded-full\"\n            />\n          )}\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The dot-fill Web Animation is cancelled and its `onfinish` cleared whenever the slide, interval or autoplay state changes and on unmount, so no timer survives the component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` autoplay never starts and the stop button is not rendered. The track slide, slide fade and dot width changes use `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so prev / next and dots switch instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Restarting a `setTimeout` on every hover leave. The countdown resets and a reader who keeps nudging the mouse never sees the next quote; pausing the animation keeps the remaining time.\n- Animating `transform` on an element that also has a Tailwind v4 `scale-x-*` class. v4 writes the separate `scale` property, which multiplies with `transform`, so the fill would stay at 0; the animation targets `scale` instead.\n- Leaving off-screen slides focusable. Tabbing would land on a hidden company link and scroll the track; `inert` removes them.\n- Announcing every timed slide change. `aria-live` stays `off` while autoplay runs.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass `interval={0}` for a manual-only carousel; the dots then show a solid active pill.\n- Quotes are `ReactNode`, so a highlighted phrase can be wrapped in `<strong>`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nFour quotes load with the first centred and the next one faded at the right edge. The first dot is a wide pill that fills over six seconds, then the track slides to quote two. Hovering the section stops the fill where it is and no slide change happens until the pointer leaves. Next / previous wrap around the ends; clicking dot 3 jumps to quote 3 and sets `aria-current` on it. The stop button freezes autoplay and turns into a play button. With reduced motion nothing advances on its own.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "text-target-particle-glyphs": "# Build: Text-Target Particle Glyphs\n\nDraws a word once into an offscreen 2D canvas with a font the page has already loaded, collects the inked pixels of that raster, and hands them out as destinations to a drifting particle cloud. The stagger is keyed to each destination's own horizontal position, so the word assembles left to right — it is written rather than faded in — and then dissolves right to left. Destinations are stored in glyph-box units and scaled by one uniform, so a container resize costs two uniform writes rather than a re-raster. Nothing ships but code: no glyph atlas, no texture, no font file.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA particle cloud gathers into a word rasterised from a font already on the page, written left to right, then lets it go.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The word is measured once at a 100px probe size and then drawn at `1024 * 0.92 * 100 / probeWidth`, capped at 66% of the raster height. `measureText` is linear in font size, so a single measurement gives the exact fit that a binary search would converge to, for any string and any family.\n2. One pass over the `getImageData` buffer collects the index of every pixel whose alpha exceeds 120 into an `Int32Array`, and tracks the min and max x and y as it goes. That array is the coverage list; the min/max are the ink box. Rejection sampling — guess a pixel, keep it if it is inked — is the usual approach and is much worse: text covers roughly 5% of its box, so the average particle needs twenty guesses and the worst case is unbounded.\n3. A particle picks `pixels[floor(rng() * pixels.length)]`, unpacks it as `x = i % width, y = (i - x) / width`, adds a sub-pixel jitter, and normalises against the INK box rather than the canvas: `u = (x + rng() - minX) / boxW`. The destination is stored as `vec2(u - 0.5, (0.5 - v) * boxH / boxW)` — glyph-box units, with the aspect ratio folded into y.\n4. Because the destination carries its own aspect, one `uWordWidth` uniform scales both axes in the vertex shader and the letterforms cannot be stretched. Layout is derived from the camera: `viewH = 2 * 6 * tan(fov/2)`, `viewW = viewH * aspect`, and `wordWidth = min(viewW * 0.86, viewH * 0.72 / glyphAspect)` — the width bound for the usual case, the height bound for a short wide frame.\n5. The morph is `form = smoothstep(order * 0.45, order * 0.45 + 0.55, uMorph)` with `order = clamp(aTarget.x + 0.5, 0, 1)`. Order comes from the DESTINATION, not the buffer index: index order dissolves the word into place everywhere at once, destination order writes it left to right, and only the second one reads as language. `pos = mix(home, glyph, form)`.\n6. The resting cloud is a seeded uniform scatter in 0..1 per axis, mapped through `uViewSize` to the visible frustum, so it fills whatever the container is. Drift amplitude is `mix(uHomeDrift * viewH, uGlyphDrift * wordWidth, form)`: a loose cloud at rest and a barely-breathing letterform once written, from one interpolated amplitude rather than two motion states that would need cross-fading.\n7. Travel bows outward via `sin(form * PI)`, which is zero at both ends and peaks at the halfway point, displacing each particle along its own fixed angle. Without it fourteen thousand points run down fourteen thousand straight lines and the eye reads a lerp; with it the transit reads as a swarm.\n8. Point size is perspective-corrected: `uSize * jitter * uSizeScale / max(0.1, -mv.z)` with `uSizeScale = pixelRatio * 6`, the camera's distance to the word plane, which makes `uSize` a literal CSS-pixel diameter for a landed particle. `uSize` is then scaled by `clamp(containerWidth / 880, 0.55, 1.15)`, because the word always spans the same fraction of the frame and its strokes are therefore thinner in CSS pixels on a small card.\n9. Left unattended the component runs a cycle: damp toward 1, snap at 0.9995, hold, damp toward 0, hold, repeat. Supplying `progress` overrides the whole state machine and makes the word a scrubbable function of scroll — damped rather than assigned, so a scroll value that jumps between frames does not teleport the letters.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `raster size = 1024 x 320`\n  The mask is the only description of the letterforms there is. A stroke narrower than about three pixels samples into a dotted line, and at 1024 across even a ten-character word keeps five or six pixels in its stems. Doubling it quadruples the single `getImageData` pass for detail that fourteen thousand particles cannot resolve.\n- `RASTER_FILL / RASTER_CAP = 0.92 / 0.66`\n  The text is fitted to 92% of the raster width, or to 66% of its height if that binds first. The width margin absorbs italic overhang and side bearings that `measureText` under-reports; the height cap is what stops a two-letter word from being fitted so large that its ascenders are clipped off the top of the canvas.\n- `ALPHA_THRESHOLD = 120`\n  Alpha above which a pixel counts as inked. Anti-aliasing puts a one-pixel skirt of low alpha around every stroke; including it thickens the word by two pixels everywhere and rounds off the corners, which is precisely the crispness the raster was for. Much above 160 and hairline serifs stop registering at all.\n- `MIN_COVERAGE_PIXELS = 1200`\n  Below this the raster is a failure rather than a short word — an unloaded font, an empty string, a canvas with no 2D context. Rendering it anyway crushes every particle onto a handful of pixels, which looks like a bug rather than like text, so the target is rejected and the cloud simply keeps drifting.\n- `STAGGER / WINDOW = 0.45 / 0.55`\n  They sum to exactly 1.0, so the last letter starts as late as possible and still finishes exactly as `uMorph` reaches 1 — no dead time at either end of the write. Below about 0.25 the word arrives all at once and the left-to-right reading is lost; above 0.6 the first letters sit finished for most of the animation.\n- `WRITE_SMOOTHING / DISSOLVE_SMOOTHING = 0.05 / 0.012`\n  Fraction of the gap remaining after one second, so roughly a two-second write and a faster dissolve. Asymmetric on purpose: the word has already been read by the time it leaves, and an exit as slow as the entrance makes the cycle feel stalled. Both are `damp` smoothings, not per-frame lerps — the per-frame form of the same curve runs twice as fast at 120 Hz.\n- `MORPH_EPSILON = 0.9995`\n  `damp` is asymptotic and never arrives, but the rightmost stagger window needs `uMorph` to reach exactly 1.0 or the last letter stays a hair short of its target forever. Past this threshold it snaps, and the same threshold mirrored at 0.0005 ends the dissolve.\n- `fill / FILL_HEIGHT = 0.86 / 0.72`\n  The word takes 86% of the visible width, or 72% of the visible height if the frame is short and wide. Both bounds are needed: width alone clips a tall word in a letterbox container, height alone leaves a short word floating in a tall one.\n- `CAMERA_DISTANCE = 6 world units`\n  Fixed, with the word scaled to fit rather than the camera moved. Distance is half of the perspective size term, so moving it would change every particle's pixel size on every reflow; holding it fixed means only `uWordWidth` and `uViewSize` change on resize.\n- `REFERENCE_WIDTH clamp = 880px, 0.55x to 1.15x`\n  The container width the default `size` was authored at. Since the word always spans the same fraction of the frame, its stroke thickness in CSS pixels is proportional to container width; particles that do not shrink with it fill the counters of the letters solid on a small card. The clamp stops the ramp from vanishing on a thumbnail or bloating on a 4K hero.\n- `count = 14000`\n  Enough that the strokes of a seven-character word read as continuous marks rather than as a dotted outline. Text needs more than a silhouette does, because the eye is checking letterforms it already knows against a template; the cost is fill, not vertex count.\n- `size / maxSize = 2.2 / 18 CSS px`\n  Landed diameter before the 0.6x-1.8x jitter, and the ceiling after 1/z growth. The clamp is not cosmetic: a particle drifting near the near plane would otherwise ask for a point size in the thousands, which drivers answer by capping silently or by dropping the draw call.\n- `swarm = 0.05 of the word width`\n  Peak mid-flight bow. Around 5% the transit looks like particles finding their place; past roughly 15% the cloud detours so far that the word appears to explode before it forms, and below 2% the straight-line lerp shows through.\n- `homeDrift / glyphDrift = 0.05 of view height / 0.0016 of word width`\n  A thirty-fold difference in amplitude, which is what separates \"a cloud\" from \"a word that is alive\". Give the written state more than about 0.005 and the letterforms visibly wobble, which reads as an unstable render rather than as breathing.\n- `THICKNESS = 0.02 of the word width`\n  Depth spread of the written word. Non-zero so perspective sizing has something to act on and the letters are made of particles at different depths rather than of a flat decal; small enough that the word still reads as typeset on a plane.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TextTargetParticleGlyphs(props: { text?: string; fontFamily?: string; fontWeight?: string | number; count?: number; size?: number; maxSize?: number; fill?: number; progress?: number; loop?: boolean; holdWritten?: number; holdScattered?: number; homeSpread?: number; homeDrift?: number; glyphDrift?: number; swarm?: number; opacity?: number; colorCloud?: string; colorGlyph?: string; fov?: number; seed?: number | string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `text: string` — default `'KINETIC'`\n  The word to write. One line — a newline is not laid out.\n- `fontFamily: string`\n  CSS font family list for the raster. Defaults to the host element's computed family, so the word uses a font the page has already loaded.\n- `fontWeight: string | number` — default `700`\n  CSS font weight for the raster. Heavier strokes hold more particles and read better at small sizes.\n- `count: number` — default `14000`\n  Particle count. One buffer, one draw call; cost is linear in fill.\n- `size: number` — default `2.2`\n  Particle diameter in CSS pixels for a landed particle, before the per-particle jitter and the container-width ramp.\n- `maxSize: number` — default `18`\n  Ceiling in CSS pixels on the perspective-grown size.\n- `fill: number` — default `0.86`\n  Fraction of the visible width the written word spans, unless the height bound binds first.\n- `progress: number`\n  Authoritative morph from 0 (scattered) to 1 (written). Supply it to drive the word from scroll or a timeline; it is held in a ref, so a per-frame value does not rebuild anything.\n- `loop: boolean` — default `true`\n  Write, hold, dissolve, repeat. Ignored while `progress` is supplied; `false` writes once and keeps the word.\n- `holdWritten: number` — default `2.4`\n  Seconds the finished word is held before it dissolves.\n- `holdScattered: number` — default `1.1`\n  Seconds the cloud drifts before the next write.\n- `homeSpread: number` — default `1.15`\n  Resting cloud size as a multiple of the visible frame. Above 1 so there is no empty margin at the edges.\n- `homeDrift: number` — default `0.05`\n  Resting drift amplitude, as a fraction of the visible height.\n- `glyphDrift: number` — default `0.0016`\n  Residual drift once written, as a fraction of the word width. Keeps the letterforms alive without wobbling them.\n- `swarm: number` — default `0.05`\n  Peak mid-flight bow, as a fraction of the word width. Zero at both ends of the morph.\n- `opacity: number` — default `0.95`\n  Overall alpha multiplier applied in the vertex shader.\n- `colorCloud: string` — default `'#5d6875'`\n  Tint of the scattered cloud; particles cross to `colorGlyph` as they land.\n- `colorGlyph: string` — default `'#ffffff'`\n  Tint of the written word.\n- `fov: number` — default `34`\n  Vertical field of view in degrees. The visible extent, and therefore the word width, is derived from it.\n- `seed: number | string` — default `'text-target-particle-glyphs'`\n  Seeds the home scatter, the per-particle randoms and the destination draw, so the same cloud is produced on the server and on every reload.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the host element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Draw `text` into an offscreen canvas with a font already on the page and\n * collect every inked pixel. Returns `null` when the raster is unusable rather\n * than handing back a target that would render as a half-word.\n */\nfunction rasteriseGlyphs(text: string, weight: string | number, family: string): GlyphMask | null {\n  const line = text.trim();\n  if (!line) return null;\n\n  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n  canvas.width = RASTER_WIDTH;\n  canvas.height = RASTER_HEIGHT;\n  // `willReadFrequently` moves the canvas to a CPU backing store. There is\n  // exactly one `getImageData` here, but on a GPU-backed canvas that single\n  // read is a pipeline stall of several milliseconds.\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d', { willReadFrequently: true });\n  if (!ctx) return null;\n\n  const release = () => {\n    canvas.width = 0;\n    canvas.height = 0;\n  };\n\n  // Measure at a probe size and scale: `measureText` is linear in font size, so\n  // one measurement gives the exact fit that a search would converge to.\n  ctx.font = `${weight} ${PROBE_SIZE}px ${family}`;\n  const probe = ctx.measureText(line).width;\n  if (!(probe > 0)) {\n    release();\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  const fontSize = Math.min(\n    (RASTER_WIDTH * RASTER_FILL * PROBE_SIZE) / probe,\n    RASTER_HEIGHT * RASTER_CAP,\n  );\n  ctx.font = `${weight} ${fontSize}px ${family}`;\n  ctx.textAlign = 'center';\n  ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';\n  ctx.fillStyle = '#ffffff';\n  ctx.fillText(line, RASTER_WIDTH / 2, RASTER_HEIGHT / 2);\n\n  const data = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, RASTER_WIDTH, RASTER_HEIGHT).data;\n\n  // Allocate for the worst case and slice down. Growing a plain array to tens\n  // of thousands of entries reallocates repeatedly inside the hot pass.\n  const scratch = new Int32Array(RASTER_WIDTH * RASTER_HEIGHT);\n  let covered = 0;\n  let minX = RASTER_WIDTH;\n  let minY = RASTER_HEIGHT;\n  let maxX = -1;\n  let maxY = -1;\n\n  for (let y = 0; y < RASTER_HEIGHT; y += 1) {\n    for (let x = 0; x < RASTER_WIDTH; x += 1) {\n      const p = y * RASTER_WIDTH + x;\n      if ((data[p * 4 + 3] ?? 0) <= ALPHA_THRESHOLD) continue;\n      scratch[covered] = p;\n      covered += 1;\n      if (x < minX) minX = x;\n      if (x > maxX) maxX = x;\n      if (y < minY) minY = y;\n      if (y > maxY) maxY = y;\n    }\n  }\n\n  release();\n  if (covered < MIN_COVERAGE_PIXELS || maxX <= minX || maxY <= minY) return null;\n\n  // The measured ink box, not the canvas box. Ascenders, descenders and side\n  // bearings differ per string; fitting to the canvas would leave the word\n  // floating off-centre by whatever margin this particular string happens to\n  // have.\n  return { pixels: scratch.slice(0, covered), width: RASTER_WIDTH, minX, minY, maxX, maxY };\n}\n\n/**\n * Draw `count` destinations uniformly from the coverage list, in glyph-box\n * units: x spans -0.5..0.5 and y carries the box's aspect ratio so a single\n * width uniform scales both axes.\n */\nfunction sampleGlyphTargets(mask: GlyphMask, count: number, rng: Rng): Float32Array {\n  const targets = new Float32Array(count * 2);\n  const boxW = mask.maxX - mask.minX;\n  const boxH = mask.maxY - mask.minY;\n  const aspect = boxH / boxW;\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n    const at = Math.min(mask.pixels.length - 1, Math.floor(rng() * mask.pixels.length));\n    const packed = mask.pixels[at] ?? 0;\n    const px = packed % mask.width;\n    const py = (packed - px) / mask.width;\n\n    // Sub-pixel jitter. Without it every particle sits on an integer pixel\n    // centre and the word shows the raster lattice as soon as the container is\n    // wide enough for a pixel to cover more than a particle.\n    const u = (px + rng() - mask.minX) / boxW;\n    const v = (py + rng() - mask.minY) / boxH;\n\n    targets[i * 2] = u - 0.5;\n    targets[i * 2 + 1] = (0.5 - v) * aspect;\n  }\n  return targets;\n}\n\n/** True when the family is not resolvable yet, so the raster must be redone later. */\nfunction fontPending(spec: string): boolean {\n  if (typeof document === 'undefined' || !document.fonts) return false;\n  try {\n    return !document.fonts.check(spec);\n  } catch {\n    return false;\n  }\n}\n\nexport function TextTargetParticleGlyphs({\n  text = 'KINETIC',\n  fontFamily,\n  fontWeight = 700,\n  count = 14000,\n  size = 2.2,\n  maxSize = 18,\n  fill = 0.86,\n  progress,\n  loop = true,\n  holdWritten = 2.4,\n  holdScattered = 1.1,\n  homeSpread = 1.15,\n  homeDrift = 0.05,\n  glyphDrift = 0.0016,\n  swarm = 0.05,\n  opacity = 0.95,\n  colorCloud = '#5d6875',\n  colorGlyph = '#ffffff',\n  fov = 34,\n  seed = 'text-target-particle-glyphs',\n  className,\n}: TextTargetParticleGlyphsProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n  const [unsupported, setUnsupported] = useState(false);\n\n  const progressRef = useRef(progress);\n  progressRef.current = progress;\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const box = useRef({ width, height });\n  box.current = { width, height };\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  // Mirrored into a ref for the same reason `progress` is: the build effect\n  // below has to know whether to hand back a still frame or a live one, but\n  // making `reduced` a dependency of it would destroy the WebGL context and\n  // re-raster the word every time the OS setting is toggled. The ref is read,\n  // never depended on.\n  const reducedRef = useRef(reduced);\n  reducedRef.current = reduced;\n\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '10%' });\n  const ready = width > 0 && height > 0;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    if (!hasWebGL()) {\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const total = Math.max(1, Math.round(count));\n    // A font already on the page, not one this component ships. The computed\n    // family is whatever the host inherits, so the word matches its context.\n    const family = fontFamily || window.getComputedStyle(el).fontFamily || 'sans-serif';\n    const probeSpec = `${fontWeight} ${PROBE_SIZE}px ${family}`;\n\n    // The renderer owns its canvas and it is appended here, per effect run.\n    // Under StrictMode a React-owned `<canvas ref>` survives the double invoke\n    // while the context does not, and the second mount is handed a context that\n    // cleanup already killed.\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setUnsupported(true);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setClearAlpha(0);\n    const dpr = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(fov, 1, 0.1, CAMERA_DISTANCE * 4);\n    camera.position.z = CAMERA_DISTANCE;\n\n    const rng = createRng(seed);\n    const home = new Float32Array(total * 3);\n    const rands = new Float32Array(total * 4);\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i += 1) {\n      home[i * 3] = rng();\n      home[i * 3 + 1] = rng();\n      home[i * 3 + 2] = rng();\n      rands[i * 4] = rng();\n      rands[i * 4 + 1] = rng();\n      rands[i * 4 + 2] = rng();\n      rands[i * 4 + 3] = rng();\n    }\n\n    const mask = rasteriseGlyphs(text, fontWeight, family);\n    let glyphAspect = mask ? (mask.maxY - mask.minY) / (mask.maxX - mask.minX) : 1;\n    let hasTarget = mask !== null;\n\n    // One destination buffer for the life of the effect. The font-load reraster\n    // writes through it in place rather than allocating a second one.\n    const targetData = new Float32Array(total * 2);\n    if (mask) targetData.set(sampleGlyphTargets(mask, total, createRng(seed)));\n    const targetAttr = new THREE.BufferAttribute(targetData, 2);\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();\n    geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(home, 3));\n    geometry.setAttribute('aTarget', targetAttr);\n    geometry.setAttribute('aRand', new THREE.BufferAttribute(rands, 4));\n\n    const uniforms: Uniforms = {\n      uElapsed: { value: 0 },\n      uMorph: { value: 0 },\n      uViewSize: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1, 1) },\n      uHomeDepth: { value: 1 },\n      uWordWidth: { value: 1 },\n      uThickness: { value: THICKNESS },\n      uHomeDrift: { value: homeDrift },\n      uGlyphDrift: { value: glyphDrift },\n      uSwarm: { value: swarm },\n      uSize: { value: size },\n      uMaxSize: { value: maxSize * dpr },\n      uSizeScale: { value: dpr * CAMERA_DISTANCE },\n      uOpacity: { value: opacity },\n      uColorCloud: { value: new THREE.Color(colorCloud) },\n      uColorGlyph: { value: new THREE.Color(colorGlyph) },\n    };\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: TEXT_TARGET_PARTICLE_GLYPHS_VERT,\n      fragmentShader: TEXT_TARGET_PARTICLE_GLYPHS_FRAG,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      // No depth at all. Sorting fourteen thousand points per frame on the CPU\n      // is not affordable, and it is not needed: the coverage list is drawn\n      // from at random, so buffer order is uncorrelated with depth and the\n      // unsorted blend averages into even density rather than into layers.\n      depthTest: false,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      blending: THREE.NormalBlending,\n    });\n\n    const points = new THREE.Points(geometry, material);\n    // Every position in this component is computed in the vertex shader, where\n    // the frustum culler cannot see it. The bounding sphere three derives from\n    // the raw attribute — a unit cube of 0..1 values — has nothing to do with\n    // where the particles end up, and the whole cloud vanishes at some angles.\n    points.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(points);\n\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n\n    let morph = 0;\n    let goal = 1;\n    let hold = 0;\n\n    const fit = (w: number) => {\n      // Visible extent of the frame at the word's depth. Everything the shader\n      // scales by comes from here, so the layout is derived from the camera\n      // rather than guessed in world units and re-guessed per breakpoint.\n      const vHalf = (camera.fov * Math.PI) / 360;\n      const viewH = 2 * CAMERA_DISTANCE * Math.tan(vHalf);\n      const viewW = viewH * camera.aspect;\n\n      // Bound the word on both axes: width for the usual case, height for a\n      // short wide frame where a tall word would otherwise run off the top.\n      const wordWidth = Math.min(viewW * fill, (viewH * FILL_HEIGHT) / Math.max(0.05, glyphAspect));\n\n      uniforms.uViewSize.value.set(viewW * homeSpread, viewH * homeSpread);\n      uniforms.uHomeDepth.value = viewH * HOME_DEPTH;\n      uniforms.uWordWidth.value = wordWidth;\n      uniforms.uThickness.value = wordWidth * THICKNESS;\n\n      const ratio = renderer.getPixelRatio();\n      uniforms.uSizeScale.value = ratio * CAMERA_DISTANCE;\n      uniforms.uMaxSize.value = maxSize * ratio;\n      uniforms.uSize.value = size * clamp(w / REFERENCE_WIDTH, MIN_SIZE_SCALE, MAX_SIZE_SCALE);\n    };\n\n    const resize = (w: number, h: number) => {\n      if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) return;\n      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      camera.aspect = w / h;\n      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n      fit(w);\n    };\n\n    const render = () => {\n      uniforms.uMorph.value = morph;\n      renderer.render(scene, camera);\n    };\n\n    const step = (dt: number, elapsed: number) => {\n      const scrub = progressRef.current;\n      if (!hasTarget) {\n        // No usable raster: the cloud drifts and nothing is ever written. Half a\n        // word is worse than no word.\n        morph = 0;\n      } else if (scrub !== undefined) {\n        // Damped rather than assigned, so a scroll value that jumps a hundred\n        // pixels between frames does not teleport the letters.\n        morph = damp(morph, clamp(scrub, 0, 1), SCRUB_SMOOTHING, dt);\n      } else if (hold > 0) {\n        hold -= dt;\n      } else {\n        morph = damp(morph, goal, goal === 1 ? WRITE_SMOOTHING : DISSOLVE_SMOOTHING, dt);\n        if (goal === 1 && morph >= MORPH_EPSILON) {\n          morph = 1;\n          if (loop) {\n            hold = holdWritten;\n            goal = 0;\n          }\n        } else if (goal === 0 && morph <= 1 - MORPH_EPSILON) {\n          morph = 0;\n          hold = holdScattered;\n          goal = 1;\n        }\n      }\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = elapsed;\n      render();\n    };\n\n    const still = () => {\n      morph = hasTarget ? STATIC_MORPH : 0;\n      uniforms.uElapsed.value = STATIC_ELAPSED;\n      render();\n    };\n\n    resize(box.current.width, box.current.height);\n    stage.current = { resize, step, still };\n    // The first frame a reduced-motion visitor gets has to be the still one.\n    // `render()` draws whatever `morph` happens to be, and `morph` starts at 0\n    // — the scattered cloud, no word in it — while `useRaf` below is disabled\n    // under reduced motion, so that first frame would also be the last one they\n    // ever see. The resize effect further down does call `still()`, but only\n    // when the observed size or `reduced` itself changes; every rebuild driven\n    // by this effect's own dependencies (a new `text`, a different colour, a new\n    // `count`) happens at a size that has not moved, so nothing reaches it and\n    // the word silently stops being drawn.\n    if (reducedRef.current) still();\n    else render();\n\n    // A webfont that is still loading measures and rasters as the fallback\n    // family, so the particles would land on the wrong letterforms and stay\n    // there. Redo the raster once the page's fonts settle, reusing the seed so\n    // the cloud does not reshuffle — only the destinations change.\n    let live = true;\n    if (fontPending(probeSpec)) {\n      document.fonts.ready\n        .then(() => {\n          if (!live) return;\n          const next = rasteriseGlyphs(text, fontWeight, family);\n          if (!next) return;\n          hasTarget = true;\n          glyphAspect = (next.maxY - next.minY) / (next.maxX - next.minX);\n          targetData.set(sampleGlyphTargets(next, total, createRng(seed)));\n          targetAttr.needsUpdate = true;\n          fit(box.current.width);\n          // Redraw the still rather than the current frame. When the first\n          // raster failed because the webfont had not resolved yet, `still()`\n          // has already run with `hasTarget` false and pinned `morph` to 0;\n          // `render()` alone would faithfully redraw that 0 and leave a\n          // reduced-motion visitor staring at a scattered cloud forever, since\n          // the RAF loop that would otherwise write the word is switched off.\n          if (reducedRef.current) still();\n          else render();\n        })\n        .catch(() => undefined);\n    }\n\n    return () => {\n      live = false;\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n  }, [\n    ready,\n    text,\n    fontFamily,\n    fontWeight,\n    count,\n    size,\n    maxSize,\n    fill,\n    loop,\n    holdWritten,\n    holdScattered,\n    homeSpread,\n    homeDrift,\n    glyphDrift,\n    swarm,\n    opacity,\n    colorCloud,\n    colorGlyph,\n    fov,\n    seed,\n  ]);\n\n  // Resize is its own effect: a container reflow costs a `setSize` and a refit,\n  // never a re-raster of the text or a re-upload of the destinations.\n  //\n  // `reduced` is a dependency, not an accident. It covers the two cases the\n  // build effect cannot: the OS setting being switched to `reduce` mid-session\n  // — `useReducedMotion` subscribes to the media query, so this really does\n  // change after mount — and a resize while it is on, where the refit above\n  // changes the projection but nothing would otherwise draw a frame through it.\n  // `still()` only assigns fixed values and renders once, so running it again\n  // when it is already the current frame costs a draw and changes nothing.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    stage.current?.resize(width, height);\n    if (reduced) stage.current?.still();\n  }, [width, height, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt, elapsed) => stage.current?.step(dt, elapsed),\n    ready && inView && visible && !reduced && !unsupported,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The offscreen 2D canvas has its width and height zeroed as soon as the coverage pass is done, releasing the backing store before the WebGL context is even created. A `live` flag is cleared on unmount so a `document.fonts.ready` promise that resolves after teardown cannot write into a disposed attribute. The stage ref is nulled so an in-flight frame cannot draw into a dying context, `disposeObject(scene)` disposes the point geometry and its attributes, the material is disposed explicitly as well, then `disposeRenderer` performs `dispose()` + `forceContextLoss()` + a zeroed drawing buffer — `dispose()` alone leaves the context alive — and the canvas node is removed. `useRaf`, `useInView`, `useVisibility` and `useResizeObserver` release their own subscriptions.\n\n**Reduced motion.** No rAF subscription is opened and one still frame is drawn with `uMorph` forced to 1 and `uElapsed` at 0 — the word fully written and completely still, not the scattered cloud. The typography, the particle grain, the depth cue from perspective point sizing and the colour of the written state are all present; the writing, the drift and the dissolve cycle are what stop. A resize redraws the same still. If the raster was rejected there is nothing to write, so the still shows the motionless cloud instead.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Rejection-sampling the raster. Guessing a pixel and keeping it if it is inked is the first idea and it scales badly: glyph coverage is around 5% of the box, so the expected cost is twenty draws per particle and the worst case has no bound — a long word in a light weight can spin for a noticeable fraction of a second. One pass into a coverage list is O(pixels) once and O(1) per particle.\n- Rasterising before the webfont has loaded. `measureText` and `fillText` silently fall back to another family, so the particles land on the wrong letterforms and stay there for the life of the page — and it only reproduces on a cold cache, which is why it ships. Check `document.fonts.check` and redo the raster on `document.fonts.ready`.\n- Normalising destinations against the canvas box instead of the measured ink box. Ascenders, descenders and side bearings differ per string, so the word ends up floating off-centre by whatever margin that particular string happens to have, and the offset changes when the copy changes.\n- Storing destinations in world units. They then depend on the camera, so every container resize means re-rasterising the text, re-sampling every particle and re-uploading the buffer — during a drag-resize, once per frame. Normalised units plus one scale uniform make the same resize two uniform writes.\n- Scaling x and y by separate uniforms. It is the natural way to write it and it stretches the letterforms at one container width and squashes them at another. Folding the ink box aspect into the stored y and scaling both axes by a single width makes the distortion impossible to introduce.\n- Ordering the stagger by buffer index. The word then resolves out of noise everywhere at once, which is a cross-fade with extra steps. Ordering by the destination's own x costs one `clamp` in the shader and turns the same animation into writing.\n- Landing every particle on an integer pixel centre. Without sub-pixel jitter the raster lattice is visible as soon as the container is wide enough for one mask pixel to cover more than one particle, and the word acquires a faint corduroy texture.\n- Rendering whatever the raster produced. An empty string, a font that never loaded, a zero-size canvas: all of them return a nearly empty mask, and mapping fourteen thousand particles onto forty pixels looks like a crash. Reject the target below a coverage floor and leave the cloud drifting instead.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` on. Every position in this component is computed in the vertex shader, so the bounding sphere three derives from the attribute — a unit cube of 0..1 values — has nothing to do with where the particles are, and the whole cloud vanishes at some container aspect ratios.\n- Making `gl_PointSize` constant. Point size does not fall off with distance on its own, so the cloud has no depth cue at all and the word looks like a flat stencil. Dividing by view-space depth is the whole effect, and `max(0.1, -mv.z)` is a required guard, not a nicety.\n- Depth-testing a translucent point cloud. With `depthWrite` on, fourteen thousand sprites occlude each other in buffer order and the strokes tear into hard layers; sorting per frame on the CPU is unaffordable. Because the coverage list is drawn from at random, buffer order is uncorrelated with depth and an unsorted blend averages into even density.\n- Passing a scroll-driven `progress` as an effect dependency. The value changes every frame, so the text would be re-rasterised and every buffer rebuilt sixty times a second. It is held in a ref and read inside the frame callback.\n- Calling `getImageData` on a GPU-backed canvas. There is exactly one read here, but without `willReadFrequently` that single read stalls the pipeline for several milliseconds during mount, on the same frame as the WebGL context creation.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The word is rasterised from a font that is already on the page: `fontFamily` defaults to the host element's computed family, so the particles adopt whatever the surrounding type is. No glyph atlas, texture or font file is shipped with the component, and nothing under `registry/` is a binary.\n- One line only. A newline is not laid out — multi-line text needs one `fillText` per line, with the coverage pass and the ink box spanning all of them.\n- The raster runs once per rebuild, plus once more if the font was still loading. It is a `fillText`, one `getImageData` over 327,680 pixels and one sampling loop — low single-digit milliseconds — and the canvas has its width and height zeroed straight afterwards to release the backing store.\n- The font-load reraster reuses the same seed, so the cloud does not reshuffle when the real family arrives: only the destinations change, and any particle already in flight simply continues to a slightly different address.\n- Supply `progress` to drive the word from scroll, a timeline or an audio envelope; leave it out and the component writes, holds, dissolves and rewrites on its own. `loop: false` writes once and keeps the word.\n- One draw call and one buffer set for the whole effect. The cost is fill: fourteen thousand blended sprites, heaviest at the moment the word is written and its particles overlap inside the strokes.\n- three constructs its own canvas, which is appended and removed per effect run. Reusing a React-owned `<canvas ref>` is unsafe here: cleanup calls `forceContextLoss()`, and under React 19 StrictMode the remount would hand the effect back the dead context, leaving the component permanently black.\n- The rAF subscription is gated on `useInView` and `useVisibility`, so a scrolled-away or backgrounded instance stops paying for fill and resumes its cycle from wherever it froze.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn entering view the cloud gathers into the word from left to right over about two seconds — watch the first letter finish while the last is still travelling — holds it, then dissolves right to left and repeats. The letterforms are legible and match the page's own font, with no gaps in the stems and no visible pixel lattice inside a stroke. Particles nearer the camera are larger, and none of them pop out of existence at any container aspect ratio. Resize from wide to narrow: the word stays fully inside the frame, keeps its proportions rather than stretching, and the particles get smaller with it instead of filling the counters solid. Hard-reload with the cache disabled on a page using a webfont: the word must end up in the real face, not the fallback. Set `text=\"\"` and the cloud keeps drifting rather than collapsing into a blob. With reduced motion the finished word renders immediately and holds still.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "textarea": "# Build: Textarea\n\nA React Aria text area that reuses Input's `TextField`, `Label` and `HintText`, so it lines up with an Input in the same form and shares its hover, focus, disabled and invalid states. With `autoResize` it measures its content on every input and grows from `rows` lines up to `maxRows`, then scrolls. Without it, the field rests at `rows` lines with a vertical resize handle. `showCount` adds a counter under the field, as `12/280` when `maxLength` is set.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nMultiline sibling of Input on the same filled shell: grows a line at a time up to maxRows, or keeps a resize handle; optional counter.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `Textarea` renders Input's `TextField` (so `TextFieldContext` carries `size` and class overrides), then `Label` when `label` is set, then `TextareaBase`, then a footer row (`flex justify-between gap-3`) with `HintText` and the counter when `hint` or `showCount` is present. Like Input, an unlabelled field falls back to its string `placeholder` for `aria-label`.\n2. `TextareaBase` renders `AriaGroup` with the same class logic as Input's shell: `rounded-2lg bg-background-tertiary-default`, a transparent `ring-2 ring-inset`, `ring-border-button-hover` on hover, `ring-border-button-active` on focus within, the disabled and the red invalid fills, all transitioning over `var(--input-transition-ms)`. Inside, `AriaTextArea` has `p-0 px-1` and `text-body-regular`, so the 8px shell plus 4px gives the same 12px text inset as Input.\n3. Sizing lives in `fit()`. Without `autoResize` it clears any inline `height`, `maxHeight` and `overflowY`. With it, it reads the computed `line-height` (20px fallback), sets `height` to `0px`, reads `scrollHeight`, then sets `height` to `min(max(scrollHeight, rows × line), maxRows × line)`, sets `maxHeight` to the ceiling and `overflowY` to `auto` only when the content exceeds it. Collapsing to 0 first is what lets the field shrink as text is deleted.\n4. `fit` runs from the textarea's `onInput` (for keystrokes in an uncontrolled field, which do not re-render) and from a `useLayoutEffect` with no dependency list, which catches every other change: a controlled parent, a form reset, autofill. Both run before paint, so the height never lags a frame.\n5. The counter needs the value, which React Aria's render props do not provide. A controlled field reads `value.length`; an uncontrolled one keeps `typedLength` in state, updated in `onChange` only when `showCount` is on. It renders as `ms-auto text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary tabular-nums`, red when invalid. `maxLength` is passed to the `<textarea>`, so the browser enforces it.\n6. `resize` maps to `resize-y` (default `vertical`) or `resize-none`, and is forced to `none` while `autoResize` is on, so the handle never fights the measured height.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `rows = 3 (default)`\n  The resting height, and also the floor when `autoResize` is on, so an empty composer never collapses to one line.\n- `FALLBACK_LINE_HEIGHT = 20`\n  The 14/20 line box of `text-body-regular`, used only when the computed `line-height` cannot be parsed (for example before styles resolve).\n- `text inset = shell p-2 (8px) + textarea px-1 (4px) = 12px; small px-1.5 gives 10px`\n  Exactly Input's inset, so a stacked Input and Textarea share one text edge.\n- `--input-transition-ms = 150ms`\n  The shell's ring and fill transitions read the same theme variable as Input.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Textarea(props: Omit<TextFieldProps, \"children\"> & { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; tooltip?: boolean | string; placeholder?: string; maxLength?: number; showCount?: boolean; rows?: number; autoResize?: boolean; maxRows?: number; resize?: \"none\" | \"vertical\"; fieldClassName?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLTextAreaElement>; groupRef?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `label: ReactNode`\n  Visible label above the field.\n- `hint: ReactNode`\n  Caption under the field; red while invalid.\n- `value / defaultValue / onChange: string / (value: string) => void`\n  Controlled or uncontrolled value, from React Aria.\n- `rows: number` — default `3`\n  Resting height in lines; the floor with `autoResize`.\n- `autoResize: boolean` — default `false`\n  Grow with the content instead of scrolling at `rows`.\n- `maxRows: number`\n  Ceiling for `autoResize`; past it the field scrolls.\n- `resize: \"none\" | \"vertical\"` — default `\"vertical\"`\n  Native resize handle. Ignored while `autoResize` is on.\n- `maxLength: number`\n  Hard limit enforced by the browser; also the counter's denominator.\n- `showCount: boolean` — default `false`\n  Shows `count` or `count/maxLength` under the field.\n- `isRequired / isInvalid / isDisabled: boolean`\n  Same states as Input: asterisk, red shell, grey shell.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Measure from zero so the field shrinks back as well as grows. Reading the\n// computed line box keeps the floor and ceiling honest when a consumer\n// overrides the type scale through `className`.\nconst fit = () => {\n  const field = innerRef.current;\n  if (!field) return;\n  if (!autoResize) {\n    field.style.height = \"\";\n    field.style.maxHeight = \"\";\n    field.style.overflowY = \"\";\n    return;\n  }\n  const line =\n    parseFloat(window.getComputedStyle(field).lineHeight) || FALLBACK_LINE_HEIGHT;\n  const ceiling = maxRows ? maxRows * line : Infinity;\n  field.style.height = \"0px\";\n  const next = Math.max(field.scrollHeight, rows * line);\n  field.style.height = `${Math.min(next, ceiling)}px`;\n  field.style.maxHeight = maxRows ? `${ceiling}px` : \"\";\n  field.style.overflowY = next > ceiling ? \"auto\" : \"hidden\";\n};\n\n// No dependency list: typing on an uncontrolled field re-renders nothing,\n// so `onInput` handles keystrokes and this catches every other reason the\n// value moved (a controlled parent, a form reset, an autofill).\nuseLayoutEffect(fit);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. The layout effect only writes inline styles on the textarea and registers no listeners or timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The component does not check `prefers-reduced-motion`. The height change is instant, not animated; the only transition is the 150ms ring and fill on the shell, which a global rule that removes transitions makes instant.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not add vertical padding to the `<textarea>` itself. `fit()` compares `scrollHeight`, which includes padding, against `rows` and `maxRows` times the line height, so padding throws both limits off. Pad the shell instead.\n- Do not give the auto-resize `useLayoutEffect` a dependency list. It runs after every render on purpose, so it catches value changes that do not come from typing in this field: a controlled parent, a form reset, autofill.\n- Do not expect a resize handle with `autoResize`. It is forced to `resize-none` so a manual drag cannot fight the measured height.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The textarea imports `TextField`, `TextFieldContext`, `Label` and `HintText` from the Input item, so install Input alongside it.\n- Textarea has no `errorMessage` prop; while `isInvalid` is set, the `hint` itself turns red and becomes the error message slot.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAn auto-resizing field with `rows={2}` and `maxRows={5}` starts two lines tall. Typing past the end of a line grows it by one line at a time with no visible lag; at five lines it stops growing and scrolls. Deleting text shrinks it back to two lines. The counter under it reads `37/280` and updates on every keystroke. A field without `autoResize` stays at its rows and shows a vertical resize handle. Hover and focus draw the same inset ring as Input.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "theme-toggle": "# Build: Theme Toggle\n\nA manual light and dark control in four skins (a sidebar switch row, a sun and moon segmented pill, a sidebar-surface pill and a glass pill for hero navs) plus a collapsed icon button. Choosing a theme starts a View Transition: the browser snapshots the old page, the theme class flips synchronously, and the new page is revealed through a blurred circular mask that expands from the pointer to past the farthest corner of the viewport. Theme state is read from the `<html>` class with `useSyncExternalStore`, so every toggle on the page, and the host site's own toggle, stay in sync.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLight and dark switch that reveals the new theme in a soft circle growing from the click point, using the View Transition API.\n\n- Category: action\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `applyTheme(theme)` is the single writer: it toggles `.dark` and `.light` on `<html>`, stores the choice under `kinetic-theme` in `localStorage` (inside a `try`, so blocked storage does not break the control) and dispatches a `kinetic:theme-change` event.\n2. `useThemeMode()` is `useSyncExternalStore` over the `<html>` class. Its `subscribe` listens three ways: a `MutationObserver` on the `class` attribute (catches any other code flipping the theme), the custom event, and the `storage` event (another tab changed the theme, which is re-applied here without persisting). The server snapshot is `\"light\"`, so hydration never mismatches.\n3. `applyThemeWithTransition` computes the origin (the pointer position, or the control's centre for keyboard clicks, which report `clientX` and `clientY` of 0), clamps it to the viewport, and takes `radius = hypot(max(x, W − x), max(y, H − y))`, the distance to the farthest corner.\n4. It then injects a `<style>` that gives `::view-transition-new(root)` a mask: an SVG data URL of a white circle (r = 42 in a 100 box) softened by `feGaussianBlur stdDeviation 2`. A keyframe grows `mask-size` from 0 to `radius × 2.5` while keeping the mask centred on the origin, over 820ms with `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`. The theme sheet turns off the default crossfade and stacks new above old.\n5. `startViewTransition(() => flushSync(() => applyTheme(theme)))` matters. React must commit the new theme synchronously inside the callback, or the browser captures the \"after\" snapshot before React has re-rendered and the circle reveals the old palette. `.theme-transitioning` on `<html>` sets `transition: none` everywhere for the duration so no component is caught mid-fade.\n6. A module-level `themeTransitionRunning` flag drops clicks while a reveal is in flight, and `finally` removes the style tag and the class. When the API is missing or the user prefers reduced motion, the theme changes instantly instead.\n7. The segmented skins are a `role=\"group\"` labelled \"Theme\" with two `aria-pressed` buttons and an `aria-hidden` thumb that slides 36px (26px when small) with a 200ms transform, flipped with `rtl:` variants. The default skin is a React Aria `Switch` labelled \"Dark mode\" that records the pointer position on `pointerdown`, because its `onChange` carries no coordinates.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `THEME_TRANSITION_DURATION = 820ms`\n  Long enough for the circle edge to be seen travelling across a full-screen dashboard, and short enough that the theme feels changed rather than loading. Overridable per instance with `transitionDuration`.\n- `THEME_TRANSITION_EASING = cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`\n  A strong ease-out: the circle leaves the click point fast, so the response feels immediate, then decelerates as it sweeps the far corners, where nothing is being looked at.\n- `final mask size = radius × 2.5`\n  The mask circle has radius 42 in a 100 box, so at 2.5 × radius its visible radius is 1.05 × the distance to the farthest corner. The extra 5% lets the blurred edge clear the corner fully before the transition ends.\n- `mask blur = feGaussianBlur stdDeviation 2 (of 100)`\n  Softens the reveal edge in proportion to the circle, so it stays a gentle gradient at any viewport size instead of a hard wipe line.\n- `THEME_STORAGE_KEY = \"kinetic-theme\"`\n  Shared with the host site's header toggle and pre-hydration script, so both controls read and write one preference and the page loads in the right theme without a flash.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ThemeToggle(props: { collapsed?: boolean; appearance?: \"sidebar\" | \"segmented\" | \"sidebar-segmented\" | \"glass-segmented\"; className?: string; size?: \"small\" | \"medium\"; transitionDuration?: number }): JSX.Element\nfunction applyTheme(theme: ThemeMode, options?: { persist?: boolean }): void\nfunction applyThemeWithTransition(theme: ThemeMode, options?: { origin?: { x: number; y: number } | null; element?: HTMLElement | null; duration?: number }): Promise<void>\nfunction useThemeMode(): ThemeMode\ntype ThemeMode = \"light\" | \"dark\"\nconst THEME_STORAGE_KEY = \"kinetic-theme\"\nconst THEME_CHANGE_EVENT = \"kinetic:theme-change\"\n```\n\n- `appearance: \"sidebar\" | \"segmented\" | \"sidebar-segmented\" | \"glass-segmented\"` — default `\"sidebar\"`\n  A \"Dark mode\" switch row, a sun and moon pill on the secondary surface, the same pill on the sidebar surface, or a transparent pill with literal black and white for a hero nav.\n- `size: \"small\" | \"medium\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  Segmented skins only: 24px segments (fits a dropdown row) or 32px. Ignored by `glass-segmented`.\n- `collapsed: boolean` — default `false`\n  With the default `sidebar` appearance, render a single 36px icon button for a collapsed sidebar rail.\n- `transitionDuration: number` — default `820`\n  Duration of the circular reveal in milliseconds.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer element of whichever skin renders.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nfunction installThemeTransitionStyle(\n  { x, y }: ThemeTransitionOrigin,\n  radius: number,\n  duration: number,\n) {\n  document.getElementById(THEME_TRANSITION_STYLE_ID)?.remove();\n\n  const mask = createBlurCircleMask();\n  const finalMaskSize = radius * 2.5;\n  const finalMaskX = x - finalMaskSize / 2;\n  const finalMaskY = y - finalMaskSize / 2;\n  const style = document.createElement(\"style\");\n  style.id = THEME_TRANSITION_STYLE_ID;\n  style.textContent = `\n    ::view-transition-new(root) {\n      -webkit-mask-image: ${mask};\n      mask-image: ${mask};\n      -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;\n      mask-repeat: no-repeat;\n      animation: boardui-theme-mask-reveal ${duration}ms ${THEME_TRANSITION_EASING} both;\n      transform-origin: ${x}px ${y}px;\n      will-change: -webkit-mask-size, -webkit-mask-position, mask-size, mask-position;\n    }\n\n    @keyframes boardui-theme-mask-reveal {\n      from {\n        -webkit-mask-position: ${x}px ${y}px;\n        mask-position: ${x}px ${y}px;\n        -webkit-mask-size: 0px 0px;\n        mask-size: 0px 0px;\n      }\n      to {\n        -webkit-mask-position: ${finalMaskX}px ${finalMaskY}px;\n        mask-position: ${finalMaskX}px ${finalMaskY}px;\n        -webkit-mask-size: ${finalMaskSize}px ${finalMaskSize}px;\n        mask-size: ${finalMaskSize}px ${finalMaskSize}px;\n      }\n    }\n  `;\n  document.head.appendChild(style);\n  return style;\n}\n\n/** null when nothing is stored, so mounting never overrides the page's default. */\nfunction storedTheme(): ThemeMode | null {\n  if (typeof window === \"undefined\") return null;\n  try {\n    const value = window.localStorage.getItem(THEME_STORAGE_KEY);\n    return value === \"dark\" || value === \"light\" ? value : null;\n  } catch {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\nexport function applyTheme(theme: ThemeMode, { persist = true }: { persist?: boolean } = {}) {\n  if (typeof document === \"undefined\") return;\n  document.documentElement.classList.toggle(\"dark\", theme === \"dark\");\n  // Kinetic is dark on :root and keys its light theme off `.light`.\n  document.documentElement.classList.toggle(\"light\", theme === \"light\");\n  if (persist) {\n    try {\n      window.localStorage.setItem(THEME_STORAGE_KEY, theme);\n    } catch {\n      // The control remains usable when storage is blocked or unavailable.\n    }\n  }\n  window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(THEME_CHANGE_EVENT, { detail: theme }));\n}\n\nfunction elementCenter(element: HTMLElement | null): ThemeTransitionOrigin {\n  if (!element) {\n    return { x: window.innerWidth / 2, y: window.innerHeight / 2 };\n  }\n  const rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();\n  return { x: rect.left + rect.width / 2, y: rect.top + rect.height / 2 };\n}\n\nfunction normalizedOrigin(\n  origin: ThemeTransitionOrigin | null,\n  element: HTMLElement | null,\n): ThemeTransitionOrigin {\n  const fallback = elementCenter(element);\n  const x = origin?.x ?? fallback.x;\n  const y = origin?.y ?? fallback.y;\n  return {\n    x: Math.min(Math.max(x, 0), window.innerWidth),\n    y: Math.min(Math.max(y, 0), window.innerHeight),\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * Reveals the next theme from the interaction point using the View Transition\n * API. Keyboard activation starts at the control's center. Unsupported\n * browsers and reduced-motion users receive the same immediate theme change.\n */\nexport async function applyThemeWithTransition(\n  theme: ThemeMode,\n  {\n    origin = null,\n    element = null,\n    duration = THEME_TRANSITION_DURATION,\n  }: {\n    origin?: ThemeTransitionOrigin | null;\n    element?: HTMLElement | null;\n    duration?: number;\n  } = {},\n) {\n  if (typeof document === \"undefined\" || currentTheme() === theme) return;\n\n  const transitionDocument = document as ViewTransitionDocument;\n  const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia(\"(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\").matches;\n  if (!transitionDocument.startViewTransition || reduceMotion) {\n    applyTheme(theme);\n    return;\n  }\n  if (themeTransitionRunning) return;\n\n  const { x, y } = normalizedOrigin(origin, element);\n  const radius = Math.hypot(\n    Math.max(x, window.innerWidth - x),\n    Math.max(y, window.innerHeight - y),\n  );\n\n  themeTransitionRunning = true;\n  document.documentElement.classList.add(\"theme-transitioning\");\n  const transitionStyle = installThemeTransitionStyle({ x, y }, radius, duration);\n  try {\n    const transition = transitionDocument.startViewTransition(() => {\n      flushSync(() => applyTheme(theme));\n    });\n\n    await transition.ready;\n    await transition.finished;\n  } catch {\n    // If the browser aborts a transition mid-flight, the theme still changes.\n    if (currentTheme() !== theme) applyTheme(theme);\n  } finally {\n    transitionStyle.remove();\n    document.documentElement.classList.remove(\"theme-transitioning\");\n    themeTransitionRunning = false;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The `useSyncExternalStore` subscription disconnects its `MutationObserver` and removes its `kinetic:theme-change` and `storage` listeners on unmount. Each transition removes its injected `<style>` and the `.theme-transitioning` class in a `finally`, so an aborted transition leaves nothing behind.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the View Transition is skipped and the theme changes instantly. The segmented thumb still slides over 200ms because it is a small, local indicator of the selected option.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `applyTheme` inside `startViewTransition` without `flushSync`. The browser takes the new snapshot before React commits, so the circle reveals the old theme and the page then snaps.\n- Leaving colour transitions running during the reveal. Without `.theme-transitioning` (transition: none) the captured \"new\" frame shows components halfway through fading, and the circle exposes a blend of both palettes.\n- Using a `clip-path` circle with a hard edge instead of the blurred mask. It works, but reads as a wipe; the softness is the effect.\n- Mounting the control without the pre-hydration theme script. The mount effect re-applies the stored theme, but only after first paint, so users with a stored preference see a one-frame flash.\n- Treating clicks at (0, 0) as real coordinates. Keyboard activation reports `clientX = clientY = 0`, which would start every keyboard toggle from the top-left corner. The component falls back to the control's centre.\n- Reading the operating-system theme as well. This control is manual on purpose; mixing in `prefers-color-scheme` makes the toggle appear to do nothing for users whose OS already matches.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The `.dark` and `.light` class names and the storage key are wired to Kinetic's theme script. In another project, change `applyTheme` and the key to match your own theme switch.\n- Clicking the toggle in this demo changes the theme of the whole site, because it is the same preference the header toggle uses.\n- The default `sidebar` skin depends on the kit's Switch item for its track, which is installed alongside.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking the moon in the segmented pill slides the thumb and reveals the dark theme in a soft-edged circle that grows from the cursor until it covers the screen, with no flash or crossfade. Clicking the sun reverses it from the new click point. Activating with the keyboard starts the circle from the control's centre. All toggles in the demo, and the site header toggle, change together. With reduced motion turned on the theme switches instantly. Rapid double clicks do not stack two reveals.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "throttled-scrub-state": "# Build: Throttled Scrub To State\n\nThe bridge between a continuous scroll signal and discrete React state. A scrubbed ScrollTrigger calls back on every frame the scroll position moves; this component gates those ticks on elapsed time, on distance travelled, and on the animation frame, so the tree below it renders a few times per scroll instead of a hundred. The boundary frames always get through, which is what stops a fast flick from leaving the state stranded at 0.93.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA scrubbed trigger fires 120x a second; a time, distance and frame gate turns that into a handful of React renders.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A single `ScrollTrigger.create` with `scrub` runs `onUpdate`, which increments a tick counter in a ref and then decides whether that tick is worth a React render. Refs are used for everything except the committed state, because a ref write does not render.\n2. Gate 1 is time: `performance.now() - lastAt < interval` returns early. A monotonic clock rather than `Date.now()`, so a system clock correction cannot produce a negative elapsed time and latch the gate shut.\n3. Gate 2 is distance: `Math.abs(p - lastProgress) < minDelta` returns early. A slow drag passes the time gate continuously while moving a fraction of a percent, and that is a render for a change no one can see.\n4. Gate 3 is the frame: the passing value is parked in a `pending` ref and a single `requestAnimationFrame` is scheduled if one is not already outstanding. ScrollTrigger fires per scroll event, not per paint, so several ticks can clear both other gates inside one frame — coalescing makes that one render.\n5. The override: when `p <= 0 || p >= 1` the time and distance gates are skipped entirely, because there is no later tick to correct a dropped endpoint. A second guard compares against the last committed value so that sitting at the end of the range does not re-commit on every tick.\n6. The committed state carries `commits` and `ticks` alongside `progress`, so the ratio between callbacks and renders is visible in the UI rather than only in a profiler.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `interval = 100`\n  Milliseconds between commits — about 10 renders a second against roughly 120 ticks. Around 16ms the gate stops doing anything useful because it approximates one render per frame; past ~200ms the discrete steps are large enough that a reader notices the value lagging behind the scroll.\n- `minDelta = 0.02`\n  2% of the trigger range. Below about 0.005 a slow drag commits on almost every time window; above ~0.05 short triggers can complete their whole range in two or three commits and the readout jumps.\n- `scrub = 0.4`\n  Under half a second of catch-up. Long enough to smooth a jittery wheel into a continuous ramp, short enough that the committed value still feels attached to the hand. `true` welds it to the wheel and every scroll spike becomes a tick.\n- `boundary test = p <= 0 || p >= 1`\n  Inclusive, not strict. ScrollTrigger reports exactly 0 and exactly 1 at the ends, so `< 0` and `> 1` never match and the override silently does nothing.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction ThrottledScrubState(props: { children: (state: { progress: number; commits: number; ticks: number }) => ReactNode; interval?: number; minDelta?: number; scrub?: number | boolean; scrollerRef?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: (state: ScrubState) => ReactNode`\n  Render prop, called only on committed states.\n- `interval: number` — default `100`\n  Minimum milliseconds between commits.\n- `minDelta: number` — default `0.02`\n  Minimum change in progress before a commit is worth making.\n- `scrub: number | boolean` — default `0.4`\n  Seconds of catch-up behind the scroll position.\n- `scrollerRef: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  The scroll container that owns the scroll position. Omit for the document.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function ThrottledScrubState({\n  children,\n  interval = 100,\n  minDelta = 0.02,\n  scrub = 0.4,\n  scrollerRef,\n  className,\n}: ThrottledScrubStateProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // The only React state in the component. Everything else is a ref, because\n  // a ref write does not render.\n  const [state, setState] = useState<ScrubState>({ progress: 0, commits: 0, ticks: 0 });\n\n  const ticks = useRef(0);\n  const commits = useRef(0);\n  const lastAt = useRef(0);\n  const lastProgress = useRef(0);\n  const pending = useRef<number | null>(null);\n  const frame = useRef(0);\n\n  useGsap(\n    gsap,\n    () => {\n      const el = root.current;\n      if (!el) return;\n\n      // Gate 3: coalesce. Several ticks can pass all the other gates inside\n      // one frame (ScrollTrigger fires per scroll event, not per paint), and\n      // three setStates in a frame is three renders for one visible change.\n      const flush = () => {\n        frame.current = 0;\n        const value = pending.current;\n        if (value === null) return;\n        pending.current = null;\n        commits.current += 1;\n        setState({ progress: value, commits: commits.current, ticks: ticks.current });\n      };\n\n      const trigger = ScrollTrigger.create({\n        trigger: el,\n        scroller: scrollerRef?.current ?? undefined,\n        start: 'top bottom',\n        end: 'bottom top',\n        // Reduced motion welds the readout to the wheel: the value still\n        // tracks the scroll the reader is performing, but nothing continues\n        // to move after they stop.\n        scrub: reduced ? true : scrub,\n        invalidateOnRefresh: true,\n        onUpdate: (self) => {\n          const p = self.progress;\n          ticks.current += 1;\n\n          // The override. The ends of the range are the two values that must\n          // never be dropped, because there is no later tick to correct them.\n          const boundary = p <= 0 || p >= 1;\n\n          if (boundary) {\n            // ...but do not re-commit the same boundary on every one of the\n            // dozens of ticks that arrive while sitting at the end.\n            if (p === lastProgress.current) return;\n          } else {\n            // Gate 1: time. `performance.now()` and not `Date.now()` — a\n            // monotonic clock, so an NTP correction cannot make the elapsed\n            // time negative and freeze the gate shut.\n            if (performance.now() - lastAt.current < interval) return;\n            // Gate 2: distance. A slow drag passes the time gate constantly\n            // while moving a fraction of a percent, which is a render for a\n            // change nobody can see.\n            if (Math.abs(p - lastProgress.current) < minDelta) return;\n          }\n\n          lastAt.current = performance.now();\n          lastProgress.current = p;\n          pending.current = p;\n          if (frame.current === 0) frame.current = requestAnimationFrame(flush);\n        },\n      });\n\n      return () => {\n        // Order matters: kill the source first, then drop the frame it may\n        // have just scheduled. A flush that survives teardown calls setState\n        // on an unmounted tree.\n        trigger.kill();\n        if (frame.current !== 0) {\n          cancelAnimationFrame(frame.current);\n          frame.current = 0;\n        }\n        pending.current = null;\n      };\n    },\n    root,\n    [interval, minDelta, scrub, scrollerRef, reduced],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The context cleanup kills the ScrollTrigger first and only then cancels any outstanding animation frame, and nulls the pending value. Reversing the order leaves a window in which the still-live trigger schedules a frame that is never cancelled, and that flush calls `setState` on an unmounted tree — thrown from inside a rAF callback, so the stack trace names no component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The trigger stays, because the value tracks a scroll the reader is performing rather than animating on its own, but `scrub` becomes `true` — welded to the scroll position with no catch-up easing, so nothing continues to move after the reader stops. The gate is unchanged.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Calling `setState` directly from `onUpdate`. On a trackpad flick that is 100+ renders a second of the entire subtree; the symptom is that scrolling is smooth on an empty page and drops to single-digit frame rates as soon as a real list is rendered below the component.\n- Throttling without the boundary override. Release a fast flick and the final tick lands inside the cooldown window, so the state stops at whatever value last got through — a progress bar that never quite reaches 100% and a \"complete\" branch that never runs.\n- Re-committing the boundary on every tick. Once progress is pinned at 1, every subsequent tick passes the override, so idling at the end of the range renders continuously — the opposite of the intent.\n- Skipping the rAF coalescer. ScrollTrigger fires per scroll event, so two or three ticks can pass the time and distance gates within a single frame and React renders three times for one painted change.\n- Cancelling the frame before killing the trigger. The trigger can schedule a new frame between the two calls, and that flush lands after unmount — `setState` on a dead tree, thrown from a rAF callback with no component in the stack trace.\n- Storing tick and commit counts in state. They are diagnostics about renders; putting them in state makes every diagnostic a render and the measurement becomes the thing being measured.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The continuous signal is still available: anything that needs per-frame precision should be written imperatively from `onUpdate`, and only the coarse state should come through the gate.\n- The render prop receives the committed state only, so consumers cannot accidentally subscribe to the raw tick rate.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nScroll the panel and watch the two counters: ticks climbs continuously while commits climbs in steps, at roughly a tenth the rate. Flick hard to the very bottom and the progress readout reads exactly 100%, not 93%. Hold still at the bottom and the commit counter stops increasing. Move the scroller by one or two pixels at a time and no commit happens until the distance threshold is crossed.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "time-field": "# Build: Time Field\n\nReact Aria's `TimeField` dressed in the kit's Input shell: the same tertiary surface, hover and focus rings, invalid and disabled states, with a clock icon at the start. Hour, minute, optional second and AM/PM are separate segments; typing fills one and jumps to the next, arrows step the focused one, and the focused segment fills with the accent colour.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA time of day typed segment by segment in the kit's input shell, with 12 or 24 hour cycles, seconds, limits and an accent focus.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `AriaTimeField` is the root (`group flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1`) and uses its render prop to read `isInvalid`, which it passes to the kit `Label` (with `isRequired` and the optional info `tooltip`) and to `HintText` under the field.\n2. The field is `AriaDateInput` whose `className` is a function of React Aria's state: it calls Input's exported `inputFieldClassName(state, size)`, so hover, focus-within, disabled and invalid rings are literally Input's. Each `segment` renders an `AriaDateSegment` with one shared class string.\n3. The shell is switched from flex to `block whitespace-nowrap`. React Aria wraps the digits in bidi isolates, which only order inline content, so inline layout keeps an RTL page reading 9:00 rather than 00:9. The shell also takes `text-body-regular` so its own line box stays 36px tall.\n4. Segments style themselves from data attributes: `data-[placeholder]` is tertiary text, `data-[type=literal]` (the colon and space) loses its padding and goes tertiary, `data-[focused]` fills `bg-accent-500 text-white`, all with a 100ms colour transition. `tabular-nums` keeps the field from shifting width as digits change, and `caret-transparent` hides the caret inside the filled segment.\n5. The clock icon is positioned absolutely at `start-2`, vertically centred, after the input in the DOM, so it can use `peer-data-[invalid]` and `peer-data-[disabled]` to recolour itself from the input's state. The input pads its start by 34px with an icon and 12px without, matching Input's text inset; `icon={null}` removes it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `field height = 36px medium (p-2) / 32px small (h-8)`\n  Inherited from Input's size map, so a time field sits flush in a row with text inputs and medium buttons.\n- `start padding = ps-[34px] with icon, ps-3 without`\n  An 8px inset, the 20px icon and a 6px gap put the first digit at 34px, the same x as text in an Input with a leading icon.\n- `segment focus = bg-accent-500 text-white, rounded-md, px-0.5, 100ms`\n  A filled block, not a ring, because segments are a few pixels apart and a ring would collide with the colon.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TimeField<T extends TimeValue>(props: Omit<AriaTimeFieldProps<T>, \"className\" | \"children\"> & { label?: ReactNode; hint?: ReactNode; tooltip?: boolean | string; size?: \"medium\" | \"small\"; icon?: ComponentType<{ className?: string; \"aria-hidden\"?: boolean | \"true\" | \"false\" }> | null; className?: string; fieldClassName?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value / defaultValue: TimeValue | null`\n  A `Time` from `@internationalized/date`, controlled or initial.\n- `onChange: (value: TimeValue | null) => void`\n  Fires as segments are typed or stepped.\n- `hourCycle: 12 | 24`\n  Forces the AM/PM or 24-hour layout instead of the locale default.\n- `granularity: \"hour\" | \"minute\" | \"second\"` — default `\"minute\"`\n  The smallest segment shown.\n- `minValue / maxValue: TimeValue`\n  Out-of-range values mark the field invalid.\n- `label / hint: ReactNode`\n  Kit Label above, HintText below; both turn red when invalid.\n- `size: \"medium\" | \"small\"` — default `\"medium\"`\n  36px or 32px shell.\n- `icon: IconComponent | null` — default `RiTimeLine`\n  Leading glyph; `null` hides it and tightens the padding.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function TimeField<T extends TimeValue>({\n  label,\n  hint,\n  tooltip,\n  size = \"medium\",\n  icon: Icon = RiTimeLine,\n  className,\n  fieldClassName,\n  ...props\n}: TimeFieldProps<T>) {\n  return (\n    <AriaTimeField {...props} className={cx(\"group flex w-full flex-col items-start gap-1\", className)}>\n      {({ isInvalid }) => (\n        <>\n          {label ? (\n            <Label isRequired={props.isRequired} isInvalid={isInvalid} tooltip={tooltip}>\n              {label}\n            </Label>\n          ) : null}\n          <div className=\"relative w-full\">\n            <AriaDateInput\n              className={(state) =>\n                cx(\n                  inputFieldClassName(state, size),\n                  // Inline, not flex: React Aria wraps the digits in bidi\n                  // isolates, which only order inline content, so RTL times\n                  // keep reading 9:00 rather than 00:9.\n                  // The shell's own line height sets the line box, so it\n                  // takes the segments' text style to stay 36px tall.\n                  \"peer block cursor-text whitespace-nowrap text-body-regular\",\n                  // Line the first segment up with Input's text: after the\n                  // icon at 34px, or at 12px without one.\n                  Icon ? \"ps-[34px]\" : \"ps-3\",\n                  fieldClassName,\n                )\n              }\n            >\n              {(segment) => <AriaDateSegment segment={segment} className={SEGMENT} />}\n            </AriaDateInput>\n            {Icon ? (\n              <Icon\n                aria-hidden\n                className={cx(\n                  \"pointer-events-none absolute start-2 top-1/2 size-5 -translate-y-1/2 text-foreground-icon-tertiary\",\n                  \"peer-data-[invalid]:text-foreground-icon-error peer-data-[disabled]:text-input-disabled-foreground\",\n                )}\n              />\n            ) : null}\n          </div>\n          {hint ? <HintText isInvalid={isInvalid}>{hint}</HintText> : null}\n        </>\n      )}\n    </AriaTimeField>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up. State lives in React Aria's time field hook; no timers or global listeners.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Only 100ms colour transitions on the segments and Input's ring transition; nothing moves, so nothing changes under `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not make the segment container `flex`. Flex items ignore the bidi isolates React Aria inserts, and RTL locales then show minutes before hours.\n- Do not pass a JS `Date` or a string. Values are `Time` (or `CalendarDateTime` / `ZonedDateTime`) from `@internationalized/date`, e.g. `new Time(9, 30)`.\n- Do not rely on the locale for the hour cycle when the copy assumes one. Set `hourCycle={12}` or `{24}` explicitly; otherwise a 24-hour locale drops the AM/PM segment.\n- Do not render it without `label` or `aria-label`. The segments are spinbuttons that take their name from the field label.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `granularity=\"second\"` adds a seconds segment; `minValue` / `maxValue` mark out-of-range values invalid, and with `validationBehavior=\"aria\"` the red state and the hint show as soon as the value leaves the range.\n- `size=\"small\"` gives the 32px shell; `fieldClassName` adds classes to the shell, `className` to the whole field.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA field labelled \"Opens\" shows \"9:00 AM\" next to a clock icon. Clicking the hour fills it with the accent colour; typing 1 then 0 sets 10 and focus moves to the minutes; Up and Down step the focused segment and wrap. Tab leaves the field and the shell's focus ring clears. With `minValue` 9:00 and `maxValue` 17:30 and `validationBehavior=\"aria\"`, stepping to 6:00 PM turns the shell, icon and hint red. In an RTL locale the time still reads hour-first.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "toast": "# Build: Toast\n\nFire-and-forget notifications callable from any event handler: `toast.success(\"Changes saved\")`, `toast.error(...)`, or `toast(\"Exporting…\", { duration: Infinity })` followed by `toast.update(id, ...)`. A single `<Toaster />` near the root draws them as Notification cards in a NotificationViewport, so toasts share its status icons, countdown bar, enter and exit motion and the spring that closes gaps in the stack. It shows at most three at a time, pauses every countdown while the pointer or keyboard focus is inside the stack, and announces new toasts through a polite live region.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nImperative toast() with success, error, update and dismiss, drawn by one Toaster as a capped, pausable stack of Notification cards.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The store is module state: a `toasts` array of records (`{ ...options, id, title, dismissed, key }`), an id counter starting at 1, a card-key counter and a `Set` of listeners. A generated id skips any id a caller has already given a toast in the store. `show()` replaces the showing (not dismissed) record that has the same id, keeping its `key`, or appends a new record with a new key, then notifies the listeners and returns the id. When the id only belongs to a toast that is leaving, the new toast is appended as a fresh card at the end of the stack and the leaving record stays until its exit finishes, so that card still plays out and its `onDismiss` still fires.\n2. `toast()` calls `show`; `toast.success`, `toast.error` and `toast.info` fix `status` to \"success\", \"error\" and \"information\". `toast.update(id, options)` finds the record if it is still showing (not dismissed), merges the new options over it (the title too, when given) and calls `show` with the same id. `toast.dismiss(id?)` only sets `dismissed` on one record, or on all of them without an id: the card plays its exit, and its `onDismiss` then calls `remove(key)`, which drops that card's record (never a newer toast that has taken the same id) and runs the toast's own `onDismiss`. A card that closes itself, from its close button or countdown, calls `toast.dismiss(id)` through `onDismissStart`, so the store knows it is leaving.\n3. `Toaster` reads the array with `useSyncExternalStore`. Each Toaster registers a `useId()` in a module-level `owners` list on mount and leaves it on unmount; only `owners[0]` renders (the server snapshot is `false`), so a second Toaster renders nothing and takes over if the first unmounts.\n4. An effect enforces `limit` (default 3): when more records are live than the limit, the oldest ones are marked `dismissed`, so they leave with their exit animation rather than disappearing. A card already leaving is not live, so closing one and firing another does not push out an extra toast.\n5. The owner renders a `NotificationViewport` with `role=\"region\"`, `aria-live=\"polite\"`, `aria-relevant=\"additions text\"` and `z-130` (merged over the viewport's `z-100`). Each record becomes a `Notification` keyed `${key}:${timeout}`, with `introDelay={0}` for the entrance, `autoDismissDuration` set only when the duration is finite and above zero, `dismissed` from the record and every action's `onClick` wrapped to call `toast.dismiss(id)` after it. Because the duration is part of the key, an update with a new duration remounts the card and restarts its countdown.\n6. `paused` is `hovered || focused`, from pointer enter and leave on the stack and from focus and a blur whose `relatedTarget` is outside it. A toast removed from under the pointer or focus fires neither leave nor blur, so when the stack becomes empty both flags are reset during render, and while `focused` is set, every change to the stack checks on the next frame that focus is still inside it.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `limit = 3`\n  Enough to see a short burst of toasts together without the stack climbing up the screen; older ones exit to make room.\n- `duration = 5000ms`\n  Long enough to read a title and a one-line description; `Infinity` (or any non-positive value) keeps a toast until it is closed.\n- `z-index = z-130`\n  Above the modal and drawer layer (z-100), popovers and menus (z-110) and tooltips (z-120), so a toast fired from inside any overlay is never hidden behind it.\n- `card key = `${key}:${timeout}``\n  Keeps a card mounted through an update that leaves the duration alone, while a new duration remounts it so the countdown starts again from the full time. The internal key, not the id, keeps ids 1 and \"1\" apart.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype ToastId = string | number\ninterface ToastOptions { id?: ToastId; description?: ReactNode; status?: NotificationStatus; icon?: IconComponent; avatar?: NotificationAvatar; actions?: NotificationAction[]; duration?: number; dismissible?: boolean; onDismiss?: () => void }\nfunction toast(title: ReactNode, options?: ToastOptions): ToastId\ntoast.success | toast.error | toast.info: (title: ReactNode, options?: Omit<ToastOptions, \"status\">) => ToastId\ntoast.update: (id: ToastId, options: ToastOptions & { title?: ReactNode }) => void\ntoast.dismiss: (id?: ToastId) => void\nfunction Toaster(props: { position?: NotificationPosition; limit?: number; duration?: number; \"aria-label\"?: string; closeLabel?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `Toaster.position: NotificationPosition` — default `\"bottom-end\"`\n  Edge or corner the stack is anchored to.\n- `Toaster.limit: number` — default `3`\n  How many toasts show at once; older ones exit as new ones arrive.\n- `Toaster.duration: number (ms)` — default `5000`\n  Countdown for toasts that do not set their own.\n- `Toaster.aria-label / closeLabel: string` — default `\"Notifications\" / the card's default`\n  Names of the region and of each close button.\n- `id: ToastId`\n  Reuse an id to replace a toast that is still showing.\n- `description / status / icon / avatar: ReactNode / NotificationStatus / IconComponent / NotificationAvatar`\n  Passed straight to the Notification card.\n- `actions: NotificationAction[]`\n  Small buttons; pressing one runs its `onClick` and dismisses the toast.\n- `duration: number (ms)` — default `the Toaster's`\n  `Infinity`, 0 or less keeps the toast until closed.\n- `dismissible: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the close button.\n- `onDismiss: () => void`\n  Called once the toast has left the screen and the store.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Toaster({\n  position = \"bottom-end\",\n  limit = 3,\n  duration = 5000,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel = \"Notifications\",\n  closeLabel,\n  className,\n}: ToasterProps) {\n  const ownerId = useId();\n  const items = useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () => toasts, () => toasts);\n  const isOwner = useSyncExternalStore(subscribeOwners, () => owners[0] === ownerId, () => false);\n  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(false);\n  const [focused, setFocused] = useState(false);\n  const viewport = useRef<HTMLElement | null>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    owners = [...owners, ownerId];\n    ownerListeners.forEach((listener) => listener());\n    return () => {\n      owners = owners.filter((owner) => owner !== ownerId);\n      ownerListeners.forEach((listener) => listener());\n    };\n  }, [ownerId]);\n\n  // Toasts that were already leaving when this Toaster took over (the last\n  // one unmounted mid-exit, or none was mounted) have no card to finish an\n  // exit, so they are removed here and their onDismiss fires. Once per\n  // takeover; the ref keeps a StrictMode re-run from sweeping toasts the\n  // limit below has just started to dismiss.\n  const swept = useRef(false);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!isOwner) {\n      swept.current = false;\n      return;\n    }\n    if (swept.current) return;\n    swept.current = true;\n    for (const item of toasts) if (item.dismissed) remove(item.key);\n  }, [isOwner]);\n\n  // Past the limit, the oldest toasts play their exit and make room.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!isOwner) return;\n    const active = items.filter((item) => !item.dismissed);\n    if (active.length > limit) {\n      const overflow = new Set(active.slice(0, active.length - limit).map((item) => item.id));\n      toasts = toasts.map((item) => (overflow.has(item.id) ? { ...item, dismissed: true } : item));\n      emit();\n    }\n  }, [items, limit, isOwner]);\n\n  // A toast removed from under the pointer or focus fires no leave or blur,\n  // so an emptied stack resets both, or the next toast would start paused.\n  const empty = items.length === 0;\n  const [wasEmpty, setWasEmpty] = useState(empty);\n  if (empty !== wasEmpty) {\n    setWasEmpty(empty);\n    if (empty) {\n      setHovered(false);\n      setFocused(false);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // A card removed while it held focus (its close button, say) fires no\n  // blur either. Once the stack has changed, check where focus went, or the\n  // remaining countdowns would stay paused.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!focused) return;\n    const frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {\n      if (!viewport.current?.contains(document.activeElement)) setFocused(false);\n    });\n    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(frame);\n  }, [items, focused]);\n\n  if (!isOwner) return null;\n\n  return (\n    <NotificationViewport\n      position={position}\n      aria-label={ariaLabel}\n      role=\"region\"\n      aria-live=\"polite\"\n      aria-relevant=\"additions text\"\n      className={cx(\"z-130\", className)}\n      onPointerEnter={() => setHovered(true)}\n      onPointerLeave={() => setHovered(false)}\n      onFocus={(event) => {\n        viewport.current = event.currentTarget;\n        setFocused(true);\n      }}\n      onBlur={(event) => {\n        if (!event.currentTarget.contains(event.relatedTarget as Node | null)) setFocused(false);\n      }}\n    >\n      {items.map((item) => {\n        const timeout = item.duration ?? duration;\n        return (\n          <Notification\n            // A new duration remounts the card, so its countdown restarts.\n            key={`${item.key}:${timeout}`}\n            title={item.title}\n            description={item.description}\n            status={item.status}\n            icon={item.icon}\n            avatar={item.avatar}\n            dismissible={item.dismissible ?? true}\n            closeLabel={closeLabel}\n            actions={item.actions?.map((action) => ({\n              ...action,\n              onClick: () => {\n                action.onClick?.();\n                dismissCard(item.key);\n              },\n            }))}\n            autoDismissDuration={Number.isFinite(timeout) && timeout > 0 ? timeout : undefined}\n            paused={hovered || focused}\n            dismissed={item.dismissed}\n            introDelay={0}\n            onDismissStart={() => dismissCard(item.key)}\n            onDismiss={() => remove(item.key)}\n          />\n        );\n      })}\n    </NotificationViewport>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** An unmounting Toaster unsubscribes from the store and removes itself from `owners`, notifying the other Toasters so the next one takes over drawing the stack. Each card's countdown and timer are cleaned up by Notification. The demo clears its pending export timer on unmount and dismisses that toast.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Toasts use the Notification card's motion, which does not read `prefers-reduced-motion`: the 0.25s entrance, the 0.18s exit and the layout spring run regardless, and a host can wrap the app in Motion's `MotionConfig reducedMotion=\"user\"`. The demo's loading spinner turns only under `motion-safe:`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not combine `duration: Infinity` with `dismissible: false` unless your code later calls `toast.update` or `toast.dismiss` for that id. With no timer and no close button, nothing else can remove it.\n- Do not pass `duration: 0` to close a toast immediately. Only a finite duration above zero starts a countdown, so 0 keeps the toast until it is closed; use `toast.dismiss(id)`.\n- Do not call `toast()` during render. It updates the store synchronously, which re-renders the Toaster while another component is rendering; call it from event handlers or effects.\n- Do not expect `toast.update` to bring back a toast that is leaving. It returns without doing anything once the record is dismissed or gone; call `toast()` with the same id to show it again.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Toasts live in module state, not in the Toaster. A record is removed by its card's `onDismiss`, after the exit animation. A toast dismissed while no Toaster is mounted (or whose Toaster unmounted mid-exit) has no card to finish, so it stays in the store until a Toaster takes over, which removes it at once and fires its `onDismiss`.\n- Pressing any action also dismisses its toast, so actions do not need to close it themselves.\n- `toast.update` leaves the toast's existing options unless they are overridden; pass `icon: undefined` to go back to the status icon after a custom one.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nNothing shows until a button is pressed. \"Save\" brings a \"Changes saved\" success card into the bottom-end corner with a countdown bar. \"Archive\" adds an information card with an Undo button; Undo closes it and fires \"Project restored\". \"Upload\" adds an error card, announced as an alert. \"Export\" shows a spinning \"Exporting report…\" card with no close button or bar, which about 1.8s later becomes \"Report exported\" with a 4s countdown. A fourth toast pushes the oldest out with its exit animation, hovering or focusing the stack holds every bar, and \"Dismiss all\" clears the stack.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tokens-chart-card": "# Build: Tokens Chart Card\n\nThe usage card of an AI profile. A grey card shows \"Tokens\", the period total such as \"667.7M tokens\" with a purple trend chip, and a full-bleed chart of daily usage: a straight-jointed purple line over a faint purple area. Days with no usage leave the line and run along the baseline as a dashed grey segment. A segmented switch changes the period, and hovering or arrowing through the chart reads out single days.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nDaily token usage as a sharp purple line over a soft area, idle days dashed on the baseline, with a period switch and day scrubbing.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: recharts@^3.10.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Recharts `ComposedChart` with hidden axes inside a `ResponsiveContainer`, flush with the card edges. The area uses `type=\"linear\"` with a miter join, so peaks stay sharp like the reference rather than smoothed into hills. The fill is a vertical gradient of `--color-chart-5` from 28% to 2% opacity, its id from `useId`.\n2. Each day is split into two series. `line` holds the value on busy days and on zero days that touch a busy day; `idle` holds 0 on zero days only. Recharts breaks a series at `null`, so the solid line drops to the baseline at the start of an idle run, the dashed `idle` line carries the run, and the solid line climbs out at its end.\n3. The chart wrapper is a `role=\"slider\"` over the day index. The mouse sets the active day from `activeTooltipIndex` (the Tooltip renders nothing); the keyboard uses ArrowLeft and ArrowRight, Home, End and Escape. `aria-valuetext` reads \"Dec 3: 24.1M tokens\" or the period total, and Recharts' own accessibility layer is turned off so there is one focus stop, not two.\n4. The active day is drawn with a dashed `ReferenceLine` and a `ReferenceDot` ringed in the card colour, both driven by state, so mouse and keyboard look identical. While a day is active the caption shows its date, the headline its value, and the trend chip hides.\n5. The period total rolls with the kit `useCountUp` hook (480ms) when the period changes; the rolling text is `aria-hidden` with a visually hidden copy of the final value. The period switch is the kit SegmentedControl; the card works controlled (`period`) or uncontrolled (`defaultPeriod`).\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `chart height = 176px (h-44), 8px top margin, y domain 0 to 112% of the max`\n  The tallest peak keeps a little air under the header, and the baseline sits just above the date captions.\n- `line = 1.75px, linear, miter join; area 28% to 2% chart-5`\n  Thin and sharp so 90 days still read as individual days; the faint area gives the line weight without hiding the dashed baseline.\n- `idle segment = 1.25px dashed 4 4 in text-tertiary`\n  Quieter than the data line but darker than the grid colours, so a gap reads as \"no runs\" and not as missing data.\n- `count-up = 480ms`\n  Long enough to read as a new period, short enough to finish before the line has drawn in.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TokensChartCard(props: TokensChartCardProps): JSX.Element\ninterface TokensChartCardProps extends Omit<HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>, \"children\"> { title?: string; periods: TokenPeriod[]; period?: string; defaultPeriod?: string; onPeriodChange?: (id: string) => void; unit?: string; endLabel?: string; formatValue?: (value: number) => string; ref?: Ref<HTMLElement> }\ninterface TokenPeriod { id: string; label: string; points: { label: string; value: number }[]; trend?: number }\n```\n\n- `title: string` — default `\"Tokens\"`\n  Caption above the headline when no day is active.\n- `periods: TokenPeriod[]`\n  Each period: id, switch label, daily points and an optional trend.\n- `period: string`\n  Controlled period id.\n- `defaultPeriod: string` — default `first period`\n  Initial period when uncontrolled.\n- `onPeriodChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called when the switch changes period.\n- `unit: string` — default `\"tokens\"`\n  Word after the figure and in the chart label.\n- `endLabel: string` — default `\"Today\"`\n  Caption under the last day.\n- `formatValue: (value: number) => string` — default `compact, e.g. \"667.7M\"`\n  Formats totals and day values.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Split each day into the solid series and the dashed idle series. A zero day\n// stays on the solid line only where it meets a busy day, so the line drops\n// to the baseline and the dashed run takes over between idle days.\nconst rows = useMemo<ChartRow[]>(\n  () =>\n    points.map((p, i) => {\n      const prev = points[i - 1]?.value ?? 0;\n      const next = points[i + 1]?.value ?? 0;\n      const idle = p.value === 0;\n      return {\n        key: i,\n        value: p.value,\n        line: !idle || prev > 0 || next > 0 ? p.value : null,\n        idle: idle ? 0 : null,\n      };\n    }),\n  [points],\n);\n\nconst selectPeriod = (id: string) => {\n  setActiveIndex(null);\n  if (period === undefined) setInnerPeriod(id);\n  onPeriodChange?.(id);\n};\n\n// The chart is a slider over days: arrows step, Home/End jump, Escape resets.\nconst onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {\n  const last = points.length - 1;\n  if (last < 0) return;\n  let next: number | null | undefined;\n  if (e.key === \"ArrowLeft\") next = activeIndex === null ? last : Math.max(0, activeIndex - 1);\n  else if (e.key === \"ArrowRight\") next = activeIndex === null ? 0 : Math.min(last, activeIndex + 1);\n  else if (e.key === \"Home\") next = 0;\n  else if (e.key === \"End\") next = last;\n  else if (e.key === \"Escape\") next = null;\n  if (next === undefined) return;\n  e.preventDefault();\n  setActiveIndex(next);\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** ResponsiveContainer disconnects its resize observer on unmount, and the count-up hook cancels its animation frame. The card adds no timers or listeners of its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the line and area appear without the Recharts draw-in animation, and the headline shows the new total immediately instead of rolling. The segmented switch thumb keeps its short slide from the kit.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Plotting idle days as normal zeros. The line then dives into a long trough that looks like a crash; splitting into a solid and a dashed series says \"nothing ran\" instead.\n- Using `monotone` curves for spiky daily data. Smoothing overshoots below zero around sharp peaks and hides the day-to-day rhythm; use straight segments.\n- Leaving Recharts' accessibility layer on next to your own keyboard handling. The SVG becomes a second, silent focus stop with its own tooltip state.\n- Animating the area on every hover. Only data changes should animate; the active day is drawn with reference marks, which never re-run the line animation.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Pass one period to hide the switch. Feed `onPeriodChange` to fetch a longer range on demand.\n- Change `unit` and `formatValue` to chart requests, images or minutes instead of tokens.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card shows \"Tokens\", \"667.7M tokens\" with a purple \"+9.4%\" chip and a purple line that drops to a dashed baseline for eight idle days. Hovering a day shows its date and value with a dot and a dashed cursor; focusing the chart and pressing ArrowLeft selects the last day (Dec 14, 33.8M tokens). Choosing 7D shows \"198.1M tokens\" and a rose \"−4.3%\" chip; 90D shows a denser line. Light and dark both read well, and at 360px the switch wraps under the headline.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tooltip": "# Build: Tooltip\n\nA small white card that names or explains its trigger, dressed like the kit's cards and dropdowns: 1px button border, 8px radius, dropdown shadow and primary text. Behaviour is React Aria's `TooltipTrigger` and `Tooltip` (hover and focus open it, Escape closes it, it flips on collision and wires `aria-describedby`), and the surface blurs and scales in over 200ms. The caret is one hand-drawn SVG path per side, not a rotated triangle, so it always meets the body with no gap.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nLight-surface tooltip built on React Aria, with a hand-drawn caret per side and a quick blur-and-scale in and out.\n\n- Category: overlay\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `TooltipTrigger` is React Aria's `TooltipTrigger` with `delay` and `closeDelay` both defaulting to 0, so the tooltip opens and starts closing immediately. Its single child must be focusable (a React Aria `Button`, or a plain button wrapped in `Focusable`).\n2. `Tooltip` renders `AriaTooltip` with `offset` 10 and `dir` from `useDirection()`. Its className is a function of state so a consumer function className is still honoured, and the surface is `pointer-events-none z-120 max-w-[240px] select-none rounded-lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default text-text-primary shadow-dropdown`. `pointer-events-none` means moving off the trigger always closes it and the copy cannot be selected by accident.\n3. Two size tiers: `sm` is `px-2.5 py-1.5 text-caption-1-medium` (12px), `md` is `px-3 py-2 text-body-medium rounded-2lg` (14px, 10px radius).\n4. Motion is one `transition duration-200 ease-out` on scale, filter and opacity, switched off by `motion-reduce:transition-none`. React Aria stamps `data-entering` on mount and removes it, and holds the element while `data-exiting` is set, so `data-[entering]:scale-90 opacity-0 blur-[4px]` and the matching `data-[exiting]` classes play the same transition in and out.\n5. With `showArrow` (default true), `OverlayArrow`'s render prop gives the resolved `placement`, which picks an entry in `TOOLTIP_CARETS`: top and bottom are 12x7, left and right are 7x12, each with its own path left open on the edge touching the body (no `Z`) so only the two outer edges are stroked. The SVG is filled `fill-background-primary-default`, stroked `stroke-border-button-default`, and given a `drop-shadow(... rgb(0 0 0 / 0.05))` that falls downward (or past the tip for bottom) to match the body's shadow.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `caret boxes = 12x7 (top, bottom) / 7x12 (left, right)`\n  Rotating one 12x7 SVG with CSS changes its visual footprint but not its layout box, so `OverlayArrow` would leave a ~2.5px gap on the left and right sides. A per-side path with the true width and height has no gap.\n- `offset = 10px`\n  Room for the 7px caret plus a small gap between its tip and the trigger.\n- `motion = 200ms ease-out, scale 0.9, blur 4px, opacity 0`\n  Fast enough not to delay reading, with the blur hiding the scaled first frames; the same values reverse on exit because React Aria waits on the transition.\n- `delay / closeDelay = 0 / 0`\n  BoardUI opens and begins closing immediately. A consumer can raise `delay` (the demo toolbar uses 300ms) where tooltips should not fire on a quick sweep.\n- `max width = 240px`\n  Tooltips hold a label or a short sentence; a cap forces long copy to wrap into a readable block rather than a wide strip.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction Tooltip(props: Omit<AriaTooltipProps, \"children\"> & { children?: ReactNode; size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; showArrow?: boolean }): JSX.Element\nfunction TooltipTrigger(props: ComponentProps<typeof AriaTooltipTrigger>): JSX.Element\nconst TOOLTIP_CARETS: Record<\"top\" | \"bottom\" | \"left\" | \"right\", { width: number; height: number; path: string; shadow: string }>\n```\n\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"sm\"`\n  sm is 12px Caption 1 with 10x6 padding; md is 14px Body 1 with 12x8 padding and a 10px radius.\n- `showArrow: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draw the caret pointing at the trigger.\n- `placement: Placement` — default `\"top\" (React Aria)`\n  Preferred side; flips on collision and the caret follows.\n- `offset: number` — default `10`\n  Distance from the trigger in pixels.\n- `TooltipTrigger.delay: number` — default `0`\n  Milliseconds of hover before the tooltip opens.\n- `TooltipTrigger.closeDelay: number` — default `0`\n  Milliseconds before it starts closing after the pointer leaves.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function Tooltip({ children, className, size = \"sm\", showArrow = true, offset = 10, ...props }: TooltipProps) {\n  const direction = useDirection();\n  return (\n    <AriaTooltip\n      dir={direction}\n      offset={offset}\n      {...props}\n      className={(state) =>\n        cx(\n          \"pointer-events-none z-120 max-w-[240px] select-none rounded-lg border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default text-text-primary shadow-dropdown\",\n          sizes[size],\n          // Blur + scale in/out. react-aria stamps `data-entering` on mount then\n          // removes it (transition to base), and holds the element mounted while\n          // `data-exiting` is set so the same transition plays in reverse. The\n          // `transition` utility covers scale, filter (blur) and opacity. Under\n          // reduced motion there is no transition, so the tooltip appears and\n          // leaves at once (react-aria finds no running animation to wait for).\n          \"transition duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n          \"data-[entering]:scale-90 data-[entering]:opacity-0 data-[entering]:blur-[4px]\",\n          \"data-[exiting]:scale-90 data-[exiting]:opacity-0 data-[exiting]:blur-[4px]\",\n          typeof className === \"function\" ? className(state) : className,\n        )\n      }\n    >\n      {showArrow && (\n        <AriaOverlayArrow>\n          {({ placement }) => {\n            // `placement` includes react-aria's `\"center\"` (for other overlay\n            // types); this tooltip only ever resolves to one of our 4 sides.\n            const caret = TOOLTIP_CARETS[placement as keyof typeof TOOLTIP_CARETS] ?? TOOLTIP_CARETS.top;\n            return (\n              <svg\n                width={caret.width}\n                height={caret.height}\n                viewBox={`0 0 ${caret.width} ${caret.height}`}\n                className=\"block overflow-visible fill-background-primary-default stroke-border-button-default\"\n                style={{ filter: `drop-shadow(${caret.shadow} rgb(0 0 0 / 0.05))` }}\n              >\n                <path d={caret.path} />\n              </svg>\n            );\n          }}\n        </AriaOverlayArrow>\n      )}\n      {children}\n    </AriaTooltip>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing of its own. React Aria removes the tooltip after its exit transition and clears its hover and focus timers when the trigger unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, `motion-reduce:transition-none` removes the 200ms scale, blur and fade: the tooltip appears and leaves at once, and react-aria unmounts it straight away because no animation is running.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not rotate a single caret SVG with `transform` for the left and right sides. Transforms do not affect layout, so the arrow's box stays 12x7 and leaves a visible gap against the body.\n- Do not make the tooltip interactive (links, buttons). It is `pointer-events-none` by design; use a Popover for content the reader must click.\n- Do not pass a non-focusable trigger. A plain `<button>` must be wrapped in React Aria's `Focusable` (or be a React Aria `Button`) for hover and focus to reach the trigger.\n- Do not close the caret path with `Z`. The edge touching the body would get a stroke and draw a line across the join.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `TOOLTIP_CARETS` is exported and reused by Popover so both overlays draw identical arrows.\n- The `placement` from `OverlayArrow` can be React Aria's \"center\" for other overlay types; the lookup falls back to the top caret.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nHovering or tabbing to \"Left\" shows a white tooltip to the left of the button with a caret that touches the body with no gap; moving the pointer off closes it with a quick blur-out, and Escape closes it while focused. Each of the four buttons shows its caret pointing back at it. In the toolbar, hovering an icon button for 300ms shows its label in the larger size.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "transactions-table": "# Build: Transactions Table\n\nA bordered card holding a toolbar (result count, status filter, search), the kit Table and the kit Pagination. Each row shows a category icon tile and merchant, the account with a status dot, a coloured category chip, the date, a status pill with a dot, and a signed amount chip that turns lime for money in. Sorting runs through React Aria's sortDescriptor, so headers carry aria-sort and sort from the keyboard too.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA finance transactions table with sortable headers, category and status pills, signed currency amounts, search and pagination.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Hold the sort as a React Aria `SortDescriptor` (controlled through `sortDescriptor`/`onSortChange`, otherwise local state seeded with newest date first) and pass it to the kit `Table`; columns with `allowsSorting` toggle it on click or Enter and get `aria-sort` for free.\n2. Derive the rows with `sortTransactions`: filter by status, then by a case-insensitive query on merchant and category label, then sort with a per-column comparator (ISO date strings compare chronologically, statuses by workflow rank) times -1 for descending, falling back to the id so ties keep a stable order.\n3. Slice the sorted rows into pages of `pageSize`; clamp the stored page to the page count instead of resetting it in an effect, and send every sort, filter or search change back to page 1.\n4. Format amounts once per locale with `Intl.NumberFormat(locale, { style: \"currency\", currency, signDisplay: \"exceptZero\" })`, so money in reads \"+$6,200.00\" and money out \"-$135.42\"; dates go through `parseDate(iso).toDate(\"UTC\")` and a UTC `Intl.DateTimeFormat`, so server and client print the same day.\n5. Render the header sort chevron from the column render prop: tertiary and faded when unsorted, secondary when active, rotated 180 degrees for ascending. The table sits in the kit Table's `overflow-x-auto` wrapper with a 760px minimum width, so narrow screens scroll the table and not the page.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `pageSize = 6 (demo uses 5)`\n  Six 52px rows plus toolbar, header and pager keep the card under one dashboard fold.\n- `table min width = 760px`\n  Below this the six columns start wrapping; the kit Table wrapper scrolls horizontally instead.\n- `default sort = { column: \"date\", direction: \"descending\" }`\n  A ledger reads newest first.\n- `status rank = completed 0, pending 1, failed 2`\n  Status sorts by workflow order rather than alphabetically.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype Transaction = { id: string; name: string; category: TransactionCategory; account: \"checking\" | \"savings\" | \"credit\"; date: string; amount: number; status: \"completed\" | \"pending\" | \"failed\" }\nfunction TransactionsTable(props: TransactionsTableProps): JSX.Element\nfunction sortTransactions(rows: Transaction[], sort: SortDescriptor, status?: TransactionStatus | \"all\", query?: string): Transaction[]\n```\n\n- `transactions: Transaction[]`\n  All rows; filtering, sorting and paging happen inside.\n- `pageSize: number` — default `6`\n  Rows per page.\n- `sortDescriptor / defaultSortDescriptor / onSortChange: SortDescriptor` — default `{ column: \"date\", direction: \"descending\" }`\n  Sort state, controlled or uncontrolled. Columns: name, category, date, status, amount.\n- `onPageChange: (page: number) => void`\n  Fires with the 1-based page on every page change, including resets to page 1.\n- `showToolbar: boolean` — default `true`\n  Shows the status filter and the search field.\n- `currency: string` — default `\"USD\"`\n  ISO 4217 code for the amount column.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Locale for amounts, dates and the result count.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Transactions\"`\n  Accessible name of the card and the table.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Comparator per sortable column. Dates are ISO strings, so a plain string\n *  compare is chronological; statuses sort by workflow rank, not by label. */\nfunction compare(a: Transaction, b: Transaction, column: Key): number {\n  switch (column) {\n    case \"name\":\n      return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);\n    case \"category\":\n      return CATEGORY[a.category].label.localeCompare(CATEGORY[b.category].label);\n    case \"date\":\n      return a.date < b.date ? -1 : a.date > b.date ? 1 : 0;\n    case \"status\":\n      return STATUS[a.status].rank - STATUS[b.status].rank;\n    case \"amount\":\n      return a.amount - b.amount;\n    default:\n      return 0;\n  }\n}\n\n/** Filter by status and query, then sort. Ties fall back to the id so the\n *  order (and therefore each page) is stable between renders. */\nexport function sortTransactions(\n  rows: Transaction[],\n  sort: SortDescriptor,\n  status: TransactionStatus | \"all\" = \"all\",\n  query = \"\",\n): Transaction[] {\n  const q = query.trim().toLowerCase();\n  const sign = sort.direction === \"descending\" ? -1 : 1;\n  return rows\n    .filter((t) => status === \"all\" || t.status === status)\n    .filter((t) => !q || t.name.toLowerCase().includes(q) || CATEGORY[t.category].label.toLowerCase().includes(q))\n    .sort((a, b) => sign * compare(a, b, sort.column) || a.id.localeCompare(b.id));\n}\n\nexport function TransactionsTable({\n  transactions,\n  pageSize = 6,\n  sortDescriptor,\n  defaultSortDescriptor = DEFAULT_SORT,\n  onSortChange,\n  onPageChange,\n  showToolbar = true,\n  currency = \"USD\",\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  label = \"Transactions\",\n  className,\n}: TransactionsTableProps) {\n  const [localSort, setLocalSort] = useState<SortDescriptor>(defaultSortDescriptor);\n  const sort = sortDescriptor ?? localSort;\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState<TransactionStatus | \"all\">(\"all\");\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState(\"\");\n  const [rawPage, setRawPage] = useState(1);\n\n  const money = useMemo(\n    () => new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, { style: \"currency\", currency, signDisplay: \"exceptZero\" }),\n    [locale, currency],\n  );\n  const day = useMemo(\n    () => new Intl.DateTimeFormat(locale, { month: \"short\", day: \"2-digit\", year: \"numeric\", timeZone: \"UTC\" }),\n    [locale],\n  );\n\n  const rows = useMemo(\n    () => sortTransactions(transactions, sort, status, query),\n    [transactions, sort, status, query],\n  );\n  const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(rows.length / pageSize));\n  // Clamp rather than store: a filter that shrinks the list never strands\n  // the table on an empty page.\n  const page = Math.min(rawPage, totalPages);\n  const visible = rows.slice((page - 1) * pageSize, page * pageSize);\n\n  const goTo = (next: number) => {\n    setRawPage(next);\n    onPageChange?.(next);\n  };\n  const handleSort = (next: SortDescriptor) => {\n    if (sortDescriptor === undefined) setLocalSort(next);\n    onSortChange?.(next);\n    goTo(1);\n  };\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The component adds no timers or listeners of its own. The kit Pagination disconnects its ResizeObserver on unmount, and the Select popover is closed by React Aria.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The only motion is the 150ms rotation of the sort chevron and the row hover colour; the chevron carries `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so it flips instantly under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not format dates with a local-time Date. A YYYY-MM-DD string parsed in local time can land on the previous day west of UTC and mismatch server output during hydration; format in UTC.\n- Keep the tie-breaker on the id. Array.prototype.sort is stable, but rows that compare equal would otherwise depend on the input order and shuffle between pages after a filter.\n- React Aria collection components (Column, Row, Cell) cannot be wrapped in custom components; the sort label is rendered through the Column render prop instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Failed transactions keep their amount but strike it through at 60% opacity, so they read as not counted.\n- Set `showToolbar={false}` to drop the status filter and search when the table sits under an external filter bar.\n- `sortTransactions` is exported for server-side or test use with the same ordering.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith the demo data the card reads \"18 transactions\" and rows are newest first. Clicking \"Amount\" sorts ascending (largest outflow first) and a second click flips to descending with \"+$6,200.00\" on top; the header gets aria-sort. Choosing \"Failed\" in the status filter shows 2 rows and hides the pager; typing \"sub\" in the search shows only subscription rows. Positive amounts are lime chips with a plus sign, negative ones grey with a minus. At 360px wide the table scrolls horizontally inside the card while the page does not. Light and dark both read correctly.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tree-view": "# Build: Tree View\n\nA nested folder tree on React Aria `Tree`, which supplies the treegrid roles, expansion and selection state, typeahead and locale-aware arrow keys. On top of it the kit draws connected trunk lines with a rounded elbow into each child, swaps the folder icon to an open folder when a branch is expanded, and replaces the collection renderer so rows unfold with a height, fade and blur animation instead of popping in.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nReact Aria file tree with curved guide lines, folder icons that open, and rows that unfold and blur in as branches expand.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, motion@^12.43.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `TreeView` renders `AriaTree` with `selectionMode` defaulting to `\"single\"`, a `data-size` and `data-show-guides` attribute, and a bordered `rounded-3xl p-2` card. It also provides a custom renderer through `CollectionRendererContext`: `TREE_VIEW_RENDERER` is `DefaultCollectionRenderer` with `CollectionRoot` swapped for `AnimatedTreeCollection`. The swap only happens when the parent renderer is the default and the tree has no `dragAndDropHooks`, so virtualizers and drag-and-drop keep working.\n2. `AnimatedTreeCollection` reads `TreeStateContext` and maps the flattened collection (skipping `content` nodes) into `AnimatedTreeRow`s inside `<AnimatePresence initial={false}>`, keyed by node key. Each row is a `motion.div` that animates from `height: 0, opacity: 0, y: -4, blur(4px)` to `height: auto`, 1px vertical padding, `opacity: 1, y: 0, blur(0)` over 0.28s with ease `[0.22, 1, 0.36, 1]`, and reverses on exit. Because of `initial={false}`, rows present on first render do not animate.\n3. An exiting row has already left React Aria's collection, so `AnimatedTreeRow` keeps the last `state` it saw while `useIsPresent()` was true and re-provides it through `TreeStateContext`. That lets the row keep rendering its label and guides while it collapses. It is marked `inert`, `aria-hidden` and `data-tree-view-exiting`, so it cannot be focused or announced.\n4. `TreeViewItem` indents with `paddingInlineStart: calc(0.5rem + (level − 1) × 1.25rem)` and uses `min-h-9` rows (`min-h-8` and `text-body-2-regular` when the tree is `size=\"sm\"`). Hover, press, keyboard focus and selection all paint `MENU_ITEM_ACTIVE` (`bg-dropdown-item-hover-background`), and focus adds an inset 2px ring. Branches get a `slot=\"chevron\"` Aria button whose `RiArrowRightSLine` rotates 90° when open over 150ms. Leaves get a spacer (`w-5` at the root, `w-2` when nested).\n5. The icon defaults to `RiFolder3Line` / `RiFolderOpenLine` for branches and `RiFileLine` for leaves, at `size-4 opacity-50`. `icon={null}` hides it, `expandedIcon` overrides the open state and `iconClassName` tints it.\n6. `TreeGuides` draws the lines in an `aria-hidden` absolute layer. It walks up `parentKey` and adds a full-height 1px trunk for every ancestor level that still has a `nextKey` sibling, so lines run unbroken through expanded subtrees. A nested row then draws its own elbow: a vertical stub to 6px above centre, then an SVG `M0.5 0 Q0.5 6 6.5 6 H…` quadratic curve into the row (12px wide for branches, 16px for leaves). It adds a tail below when the row has a next sibling, and a bridge down from the icon when the row itself is expanded. Each axis sits at `calc(1.125rem + depth × 1.25rem)`.\n7. A capture-phase `onKeyDownCapture` on a `display: contents` wrapper completes Right Arrow (Left Arrow in RTL) on an already-expanded row: it finds the next row in DOM order with a deeper `aria-level`, skips disabled and exiting rows, and focuses it. React Aria handles expanding closed branches and every other key.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `indent step = 1.25rem (20px) per level, from a 0.5rem base`\n  The guide axes use the same 1.25rem step offset by 1.125rem, which centres each trunk under its parent's chevron. Change one and the lines stop lining up with the icons.\n- `row animation = 0.28s, ease [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1], y −4px, blur 4px`\n  A strong ease-out lets the height open quickly and settle softly. The small upward offset and blur make children look as if they slide out from under the parent rather than being inserted.\n- `row heights = min-h-9 (36px) md, min-h-8 (32px) sm`\n  Matches the kit's menu item heights, so a tree in a sidebar lines up with menus and nav items next to it.\n- `elbow = quadratic curve, 6px radius; branch 12px wide, leaf 16px`\n  Branches have a 20px chevron button before the icon and leaves only a spacer, so the elbow length differs to land right at each icon.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TreeView<T extends object>(props: AriaTreeProps<T> & { size?: \"sm\" | \"md\"; showGuides?: boolean; onKeyDownCapture?: KeyboardEventHandler<HTMLDivElement>; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction TreeViewItem<T extends object = object>(props: Omit<AriaTreeItemProps<T>, \"textValue\" | \"children\"> & { label: string; textValue?: string; icon?: IconComponent | null; expandedIcon?: IconComponent; iconClassName?: string; trailingContent?: ReactNode; children?: ReactNode; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nexport { Collection as TreeViewCollection } from \"react-aria-components\"\n```\n\n- `size: \"sm\" | \"md\"` — default `\"md\"`\n  36px or 32px rows. Small also drops the label to the 13px body-2 style.\n- `showGuides: boolean` — default `true`\n  Draws trunk lines and curved branches beside nested rows.\n- `selectionMode: \"none\" | \"single\" | \"multiple\"` — default `\"single\"`\n  React Aria selection mode. Selected rows use the same tint as hover.\n- `defaultExpandedKeys / expandedKeys: Iterable<Key>`\n  Uncontrolled or controlled set of open branches.\n- `TreeViewItem.label: string`\n  Visible label, also the default `textValue` for typeahead and accessible naming.\n- `TreeViewItem.icon: IconComponent | null`\n  Defaults to folder for branches and file for leaves. `null` hides it.\n- `TreeViewItem.iconClassName: string`\n  Extra classes for the icon, typically a colour such as `text-accent-700`.\n- `TreeViewItem.trailingContent: ReactNode`\n  Metadata at the end of the row in caption type, such as a size or count.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst TREE_VIEW_RENDERER = {\n  ...DefaultCollectionRenderer,\n  CollectionRoot: AnimatedTreeCollection,\n};\n\nfunction AnimatedTreeCollection({ collection, ...props }: ComponentProps<typeof DefaultCollectionRenderer.CollectionRoot>) {\n  const state = useContext(TreeStateContext);\n  if (!state) return <DefaultCollectionRenderer.CollectionRoot collection={collection} {...props} />;\n\n  return (\n    <AnimatePresence initial={false}>\n      {Array.from(collection).filter((node) => node.type !== \"content\").map((node) => (\n        <AnimatedTreeRow key={node.key} state={state}>\n          {node.render?.(node)}\n        </AnimatedTreeRow>\n      ))}\n    </AnimatePresence>\n  );\n}\n\ntype TreeState = NonNullable<ContextType<typeof TreeStateContext>>;\n\nfunction TreeGuides({ state, id, level, expanded, branchWidth, direction }: {\n  state: TreeState;\n  id: Key;\n  level: number;\n  expanded: boolean;\n  branchWidth: number;\n  direction: \"ltr\" | \"rtl\";\n}) {\n  const node = state.collection.getItem(id);\n  if (!node) return null;\n\n  // A descendant carries an outer trunk only when its ancestor has a sibling\n  // still to come. This keeps the line connected through an expanded subtree,\n  // while the final child's elbow closes that level of the tree.\n  const continuingLevels: number[] = [];\n  let ancestor = node.parentKey != null ? state.collection.getItem(node.parentKey) : null;\n  let ancestorLevel = level - 1;\n  while (ancestor) {\n    if (ancestor.type === \"item\") {\n      if (ancestorLevel > 1 && ancestor.nextKey != null) continuingLevels.push(ancestorLevel - 2);\n      ancestorLevel--;\n    }\n    ancestor = ancestor.parentKey != null ? state.collection.getItem(ancestor.parentKey) : null;\n  }\n\n  const axis = (depth: number) => `calc(1.125rem + ${depth} * 1.25rem)`;\n\n  return (\n    <span aria-hidden data-tree-view-guides className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 text-foreground-icon-quaternary group-data-[show-guides=false]/tree-view:hidden\">\n      {continuingLevels.map((depth) => (\n        <span key={depth} data-tree-view-trunk className=\"absolute -inset-y-px w-px bg-current\" style={{ insetInlineStart: axis(depth) }} />\n      ))}\n      {level > 1 && (\n        <span data-tree-view-branch className=\"absolute -inset-y-px\" style={{ insetInlineStart: axis(level - 2), width: branchWidth }}>\n          <span className=\"absolute start-0 top-0 w-px bg-current\" style={{ bottom: \"calc(50% + 6px)\" }} />\n          <svg\n            width={branchWidth}\n            height=\"7\"\n            viewBox={`0 0 ${branchWidth} 7`}\n            fill=\"none\"\n            className={cx(\"absolute start-0\", direction === \"rtl\" && \"-scale-x-100\")}\n            style={{ top: \"calc(50% - 6px)\" }}\n          >\n            <path d={`M0.5 0 Q0.5 6 6.5 6 H${branchWidth - 0.5}`} stroke=\"currentColor\" strokeWidth=\"1\" />\n          </svg>\n          {node.nextKey != null && (\n            <span data-tree-view-tail className=\"absolute start-0 bottom-0 w-px bg-current\" style={{ top: \"calc(50% - 6px)\" }} />\n          )}\n        </span>\n      )}\n      {expanded && (\n        <span data-tree-view-bridge className=\"absolute -bottom-px w-px bg-current\" style={{ insetInlineStart: axis(level - 1), top: \"calc(50% + 8px)\" }} />\n      )}\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n\nfunction AnimatedTreeRow({ state, children }: { state: TreeState; children: ReactNode }) {\n  const isPresent = useIsPresent();\n  const reduceMotion = useReducedMotion();\n  const [lastPresentState, setLastPresentState] = useState(state);\n  if (isPresent && lastPresentState !== state) setLastPresentState(state);\n\n  // Preserve the row's last collection while it fades out. The live tree has\n  // already removed it from keyboard navigation; the retained visual is inert.\n  return (\n    <motion.div\n      role=\"presentation\"\n      className=\"relative min-w-0 shrink-0 overflow-hidden\"\n      data-tree-view-exiting={!isPresent || undefined}\n      aria-hidden={!isPresent || undefined}\n      inert={!isPresent}\n      initial={reduceMotion ? false : { height: 0, paddingTop: 0, paddingBottom: 0, opacity: 0, y: -4, filter: \"blur(4px)\" }}\n      animate={{ height: \"auto\", paddingTop: 1, paddingBottom: 1, opacity: 1, y: 0, filter: \"blur(0px)\" }}\n      exit={{ height: 0, paddingTop: 0, paddingBottom: 0, opacity: 0, y: reduceMotion ? 0 : -4, filter: reduceMotion ? \"blur(0px)\" : \"blur(4px)\" }}\n      transition={{ duration: reduceMotion ? 0 : 0.28, ease: [0.22, 1, 0.36, 1] }}\n    >\n      <TreeStateContext.Provider value={isPresent ? state : lastPresentState}>\n        {children}\n      </TreeStateContext.Provider>\n    </motion.div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to release manually. `AnimatePresence` unmounts exiting rows when their exit animation finishes, and motion stops any running animation when the tree unmounts. There are no listeners outside React.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With `useReducedMotion()` true the rows skip the entry animation (`initial={false}`), exit with no offset or blur, and the transition duration becomes 0, so branches open and close instantly. The chevron rotation and colour transitions have `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not animate rows with CSS `height` transitions on the Aria items. A collapsed child is removed from the collection immediately, so there is nothing left to animate. The exit only works because `AnimatePresence` keeps the motion wrapper and the row keeps its last tree state.\n- Do not leave exiting rows focusable. Without `inert` and `aria-hidden`, arrow keys and screen readers would land on rows that are already gone from the tree.\n- Do not reuse ids across the tree. React Aria keys expansion, selection and the guide walk on `id`, so duplicates confuse all three.\n- Do not forget `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` on `TreeView`. React Aria warns when a tree has no accessible name.\n- Do not force the custom renderer when using drag and drop or a virtualizer. The component already falls back to the parent renderer in those cases.\n\n## Notes\n\n- For data-driven trees, pass `items` and render recursively with the re-exported `TreeViewCollection` (React Aria `Collection`).\n- Set `showGuides={false}` to hide every line with one `group-data-[show-guides=false]` rule, leaving plain indentation.\n- The tree grows with its content and does not scroll itself. Wrap it in a scroll container if it lives in a fixed-height sidebar.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWith `src` and `components` expanded, nested rows are indented 20px per level and joined to their parent by thin lines with rounded elbows. The last child's elbow closes its level with no tail. Collapsing a folder makes its children shrink, fade and blur out over about 0.3s while the open-folder icon switches back to a closed folder and the chevron rotates back. Expanding animates them in. Clicking a row selects it with a tinted background. Arrow keys move between rows, Right on a closed folder opens it and Right on an open folder moves to its first child.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "trend-badge": "# Build: Trend Badge\n\nThe small change pill beside the big numbers on the BoardUI dashboard KPI cards (\"+5.3%\", \"-2.1%\", \"0.00%\"). The direction comes from the sign of the value after rounding, so a tiny change never shows as a red \"-0.0%\". Up is lime and down is rose, swapped with invert for metrics where lower is better. A single arrow rotates between up, sideways and down, so a live value turns it smoothly instead of swapping icons. Screen readers hear \"Up 5.3% vs last month\" instead of a bare \"+5.3\".\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nUp, down or flat change pill for dashboard KPIs: one rotating arrow, a signed percent, lime, rose or grey, in three sizes.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The value is rounded to the shown decimals first; the sign of that rounded number picks up, down or flat (direction overrides it), and a rounded -0 is folded to 0.\n2. Tone reuses the kit Chip colours: CHIP_COLORS.lime for good, CHIP_COLORS.rose for bad and CHIP_COLORS.neutral (tertiary background, secondary text) for flat; invert flips which direction counts as good.\n3. The glyph is always RiArrowUpLine, rotated 0, 90 (mirrored under rtl) or 180 degrees with transition-[rotate], and the pill fades its colours with transition-colors, both 200 to 300ms ease-out and off under motion-reduce.\n4. Intl.NumberFormat with signDisplay exceptZero prints \"+5.3%\" and \"0.0%\"; that visible text is aria-hidden and an sr-only span reads the direction word, the unsigned percent and the optional context.\n5. A fixed locale (en-US by default) keeps the server and client text identical, so the badge hydrates cleanly.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `sizes = sm h-5 / md h-6 / lg h-7`\n  sm fits table cells, md sits beside body text and lg matches the 28px pills next to the KPI numbers.\n- `radius = rounded-md (sm, md), rounded-lg (lg)`\n  The reference pills are squared-off rectangles, not full capsules.\n- `rotate = 0 / 90 / 180deg, 300ms ease-out`\n  One glyph that turns reads as the same metric changing, not a new icon popping in.\n- `decimals = 1 by default`\n  Matches \"+5.3%\"; pass 2 for \"0.00%\" as on the Orders card.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function TrendBadge({ value, direction, invert, decimals, size, icon, context, locale, className }: TrendBadgeProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `value: number`\n  Change in percent, e.g. 5.3 or -2.1.\n- `direction: 'up' | 'down' | 'flat'`\n  Overrides the direction taken from the rounded value.\n- `invert: boolean` — default `false`\n  Down is good: down turns lime and up rose.\n- `decimals: number` — default `1`\n  Fraction digits shown and used for rounding.\n- `size: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'` — default `'md'`\n  Pill height, padding and type size.\n- `icon: boolean` — default `true`\n  Show the rotating arrow.\n- `context: string`\n  Appended to the screen-reader text, e.g. \"vs last month\".\n- `locale: string` — default `'en-US'`\n  Number formatting locale.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function TrendBadge({\n  value,\n  direction,\n  invert = false,\n  decimals = 1,\n  size = 'md',\n  icon = true,\n  context,\n  locale = 'en-US',\n  className,\n}: TrendBadgeProps) {\n  // Round first, so -0.04 at one decimal reads \"0.0%\" and counts as flat\n  // instead of showing a red \"-0.0%\".\n  const factor = 10 ** decimals;\n  const rounded = Math.round(value * factor) / factor || 0;\n  const dir: TrendDirection = direction ?? (rounded > 0 ? 'up' : rounded < 0 ? 'down' : 'flat');\n  const tone = dir === 'flat' ? TONE.flat : (dir === 'up') !== invert ? TONE.good : TONE.bad;\n\n  const format = (n: number, signDisplay: 'exceptZero' | 'never') =>\n    new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, { minimumFractionDigits: decimals, maximumFractionDigits: decimals, signDisplay }).format(n);\n  const s = SIZE[size];\n\n  return (\n    <span\n      data-direction={dir}\n      className={cx(\n        'inline-flex shrink-0 items-center whitespace-nowrap tabular-nums',\n        'transition-colors duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n        s.pill,\n        tone,\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {icon ? (\n        <RiArrowUpLine\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            'shrink-0 transition-[rotate] duration-300 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none',\n            s.icon,\n            ROTATE[dir],\n          )}\n        />\n      ) : null}\n      <span aria-hidden>{format(rounded, 'exceptZero')}%</span>\n      <span className=\"sr-only\">\n        {`${WORD[dir]} ${format(Math.abs(rounded), 'never')}%${context ? ` ${context}` : ''}`}\n      </span>\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Stateless span: no timers, listeners or effects.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Under prefers-reduced-motion the arrow snaps to its new angle and the colours switch at once; nothing else moves.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Pass the change in percent (5.3), not as a ratio (0.053); the badge only formats, it does not multiply.\n- Without context the screen-reader text is just \"Up 5.3%\"; add context (\"vs last month\") when the comparison period is not read out nearby.\n- For cost, churn or latency set invert, or a drop shows rose although it is good news.\n\n## Notes\n\n- icon={false} gives the arrowless pills of the reference KPI cards; the arrow is on by default for tables and lists.\n- It renders a plain span with no hooks, so it works in server components.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe KPI cards show +5.3% lime, -2.1% rose and 0.00% grey; stepping the conversion change from +1.0% down through 0.0% to -0.5% turns its arrow from up to sideways to down and its colour from lime to grey to rose; the churn badge reads -0.6% in lime.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "tweened-cursor-uniform": "# Build: Tweened Cursor Uniform\n\nA full-quad fragment shader draws a procedural grid and pulls its UVs toward a lens centred on the pointer. The lens position is not assigned into the uniform — GSAP tweens it onto a plain object, and the render loop copies that object across each frame, so the distortion trails the cursor and then comes fully to rest under it. Presence is a second, independent tween, so leaving the container dims the lens where it stands rather than dragging it away.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA shader grid with a lens under the pointer, whose position is tweened into the uniform so it settles instead of chasing.\n\n- Category: cursor\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1, gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. A `pointermove` handler converts client coordinates into NDC against the live container rect: `x = ((e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width) * 2 - 1` and `y = -(((e.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * 2 - 1)`, y negated because NDC counts upward and CSS counts downward.\n2. That NDC pair is tweened onto a plain `{ x, y }` object with `gsap.to(pos, { x, y, duration: 0.6, ease: \"power3.out\", overwrite: true })`. This is the smoothing. Nothing else damps: the uniform copy in the rAF loop is a straight assignment.\n3. The contrast with `useSmoothPointer` is the point. `damp` closes a fixed fraction of the remaining gap per second — always moving, always behind, asymptotic. A tween has a duration and an end, so `power3.out` decelerates the lens into a hard stop exactly under the cursor when the hand stops.\n4. Presence is a separate scalar tweened to 1 over 0.4s on move and back to 0 over 0.6s on `pointerleave`, multiplied into the falloff in the shader. Splitting it from position is what makes the lens fade out in place instead of sliding off toward a parked coordinate.\n5. The fragment shader works in aspect-corrected centre space, computes a Gaussian `fall = exp(-d² / r²)` from the cursor, and displaces the sampling point with `warped = p - normalize(p - c) * fall * uStrength * uAmount` before the grid function reads it — so the grid compresses toward the cursor like glass rather than smearing.\n6. Anti-aliasing of the grid lines is derived from `uResolution` (`w = widthPx * cells / res`) instead of `fwidth`, so the same source compiles on a WebGL1 context with no derivatives extension pragma.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PARKED_NDC = 999`\n  Initial value of the tweened cursor object. Far outside the -1..1 NDC range, so `exp(-d²/r²)` underflows to exactly 0 and the very first frame is guaranteed lens-free. Initialising at (0,0) puts a full-strength bulge dead centre before the pointer has ever moved.\n- `followDuration = 0.6`\n  Seconds for the lens to reach the pointer. This is long for a cursor effect and deliberately so — the lens reads as heavy glass being dragged. Under ~0.25 it is welded to the pointer and the trailing quality disappears.\n- `PRESENCE_IN / PRESENCE_OUT = 0.4 / 0.6`\n  Asymmetric on purpose. Arrival must beat the eye to the pointer; departure is slower so the lens reads as dissipating rather than being switched off.\n- `radius = 0.28`\n  Gaussian sigma in centre space, where the short edge spans 1.0. So the lens core is a bit over a quarter of the container height. Past ~0.45 the falloff no longer reaches zero inside the frame and the whole grid warps.\n- `amount = 0.22`\n  Peak UV displacement toward the cursor, in centre-space units. Above ~0.35 the pull exceeds the local grid spacing and lines fold back through each other, which reads as a rendering fault.\n- `pixelRatio cap = 2`\n  Fill-rate is quadratic in DPR. On a 3x display an uncapped full-container fragment shader costs 2.25x more per frame for no visible gain.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TweenedCursorUniform(props: { followDuration?: number; radius?: number; amount?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `followDuration: number` — default `0.6`\n  Seconds for the lens to catch the pointer.\n- `radius: number` — default `0.28`\n  Gaussian sigma of the lens, in centre-space units where the short edge is 1.\n- `amount: number` — default `0.22`\n  Peak UV displacement toward the cursor at full strength.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nconst FOLLOW_EASE = 'power3.out';\nconst PRESENCE_IN = 0.4;\nconst PRESENCE_OUT = 0.6;\n/** Far outside NDC, so `exp(-d²/r²)` underflows to 0 before the first move. */\nconst PARKED_NDC = 999;\n\nexport function TweenedCursorUniform({\n  followDuration = 0.6,\n  radius = 0.28,\n  amount = 0.22,\n  className,\n}: TweenedCursorUniformProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const api = useRef<Scene | null>(null);\n\n  /* The two tween targets. Plain objects, not uniforms: GSAP writes numbers\n     onto them and the loop copies them across once per frame. Tweening the\n     uniform's `.value` vector directly works too, but this keeps the tween\n     alive across a material rebuild. */\n  const smooth = useRef({ x: PARKED_NDC, y: PARKED_NDC });\n  const presence = useRef({ value: 0 });\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { rootMargin: '128px' });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n\n  /* Build once. Never in the resize effect — recreating a WebGLRenderer on\n     every resize leaks contexts, and Chromium kills the OLDEST live context\n     when the ~16-per-process ceiling is hit, so an unrelated canvas\n     elsewhere on the page goes black. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !hasWebGL()) return;\n\n    /* three owns its canvas and it is appended here, per effect run. Handing\n       the constructor a React-rendered `<canvas ref>` was the bug: cleanup\n       calls `forceContextLoss()`, and `getContext` on an element that has been\n       through that returns the same dead context, so the next construction\n       threw straight into the catch below and the component stayed blank. The\n       preview host hides components with `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">` — effects\n       unmount, the DOM does not — so that cycle is ordinary scrolling here,\n       not just StrictMode in development. */\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({\n        antialias: false,\n        alpha: false,\n        powerPreference: 'low-power',\n      });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio(2));\n    renderer.domElement.className = 'block h-full w-full';\n    el.append(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader,\n      fragmentShader,\n      uniforms: {\n        uResolution: { value: new THREE.Vector2(1, 1) },\n        uCursor: { value: new THREE.Vector2(PARKED_NDC, PARKED_NDC) },\n        uStrength: { value: 0 },\n        uTime: { value: 0 },\n        uRadius: { value: radius },\n        uAmount: { value: amount },\n        uInk: { value: new THREE.Color(0.08, 0.09, 0.11) },\n        uPaper: { value: new THREE.Color(0.04, 0.04, 0.05) },\n        uAccent: { value: new THREE.Color(0.95, 0.55, 0.2) },\n      },\n    });\n\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material));\n\n    // A 2x2 plane under an identity-ish ortho camera is a full-screen quad\n    // with no projection maths and no camera to keep in sync on resize.\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1);\n\n    api.current = { renderer, camera, scene, geometry, material };\n\n    return () => {\n      api.current = null;\n      // `dispose()` alone frees GPU objects but leaves the context alive;\n      // only `forceContextLoss()` hands it back, which is what this does.\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // Deliberately empty. `radius` and `amount` are uniform values and used to\n    // sit here, which meant changing either tore the renderer down and built a\n    // whole new context for what is one float. The effect below writes them\n    // onto the live material instead.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, []);\n\n  /* Uniform values, not construction inputs. Written straight onto the live\n     material so a prop change costs one assignment rather than a context. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = api.current;\n    if (!s) return;\n    const r = s.material.uniforms.uRadius;\n    if (r) r.value = radius;\n    const a = s.material.uniforms.uAmount;\n    if (a) a.value = amount;\n  }, [radius, amount]);\n\n  /* Size in its own effect so a resize never touches the renderer's lifetime. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = api.current;\n    if (!s || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n    s.renderer.setSize(width, height, false);\n    const res = s.material.uniforms.uResolution;\n    if (res) (res.value as THREE.Vector2).set(width, height);\n    // Render once so a resize while paused still repaints, rather than\n    // stretching the previous framebuffer until the pointer moves again.\n    s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n  }, [width, height]);\n\n  /* Pointer -> tween. The tween is the smoothing; nothing else damps. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || reduced || !hasWebGL()) return;\n    // A coarse pointer only produces events during a drag, so the lens would\n    // pop into existence under a finger and vanish on release.\n    if (!window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) return;\n\n    const pos = smooth.current;\n    const pres = presence.current;\n\n    const onMove = (e: PointerEvent) => {\n      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();\n      if (rect.width === 0 || rect.height === 0) return;\n      const x = ((e.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width) * 2 - 1;\n      const y = -(((e.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * 2 - 1);\n\n      // `overwrite: true` (not 'auto'): each move must kill the whole previous\n      // tween, including the half of it that has not started easing yet.\n      gsap.to(pos, { x, y, duration: followDuration, ease: FOLLOW_EASE, overwrite: true });\n      gsap.to(pres, { value: 1, duration: PRESENCE_IN, ease: 'power2.out', overwrite: 'auto' });\n    };\n\n    const onLeave = () => {\n      // Fade presence only. Leaving position alone means the lens dims where\n      // it stands; tweening it to a corner instead drags a visible streak.\n      gsap.to(pres, { value: 0, duration: PRESENCE_OUT, ease: 'power2.out', overwrite: 'auto' });\n    };\n\n    el.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove, { passive: true });\n    el.addEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);\n      el.removeEventListener('pointerleave', onLeave);\n      gsap.killTweensOf([pos, pres]);\n    };\n  }, [followDuration, reduced]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (_dt, elapsed) => {\n      const s = api.current;\n      if (!s) return;\n      const u = s.material.uniforms;\n      const cursor = u.uCursor;\n      const strength = u.uStrength;\n      const time = u.uTime;\n      if (cursor) (cursor.value as THREE.Vector2).set(smooth.current.x, smooth.current.y);\n      if (strength) strength.value = presence.current.value;\n      if (time) time.value = elapsed;\n      s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n    },\n    inView && visible && !reduced,\n  );\n\n  /* Reduced motion still gets a picture — a single frame with the lens parked\n     off-centre — rather than an empty box. One render, then nothing. */\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const s = api.current;\n    if (!s || !reduced || width === 0 || height === 0) return;\n    const cursor = s.material.uniforms.uCursor;\n    const strength = s.material.uniforms.uStrength;\n    if (cursor) (cursor.value as THREE.Vector2).set(-0.25, 0.15);\n    if (strength) strength.value = 1;\n    s.renderer.render(s.scene, s.camera);\n  }, [reduced, width, height]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes both pointer listeners and kills the tweens on the two plain objects. The build effect nulls its handle, calls `disposeRenderer` (which stops the animation loop, disposes, forces context loss, and zeroes the backing store), then disposes the geometry and the material. The rAF subscription is released by `useRaf`.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The rAF loop and the pointer listeners never attach. A single frame is rendered with the lens parked slightly off-centre at full strength, so the component is a static composed image of the effect rather than an empty box. It repaints once on resize and never again.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Assigning the pointer straight into the uniform and smoothing in the shader instead. The shader has no memory between frames without a render target, so the usual workaround is to lerp a JS variable per frame with a fixed alpha — which is frame-rate dependent and converges twice as fast at 120Hz.\n- Tweening position and presence together. On `pointerleave` the lens then slides toward its parked coordinate at full strength, dragging a bright streak across the whole canvas on the way out.\n- Leaving the tweened cursor at (0, 0) on mount. A full-strength bulge sits dead centre from the first painted frame until the user moves, which looks like a baked-in artefact rather than an interaction.\n- Using `overwrite: \"auto\"` on the position tween. Auto only retires *rendered* conflicts; a burst of moves inside one frame can leave the earlier tween partly live. `overwrite: true` kills the previous tween outright, which is what you want when every event supersedes the last.\n- Recreating the `WebGLRenderer` inside the resize effect. Each resize leaks a context, and Chromium silently kills the oldest of its ~16 per-process contexts, so an unrelated canvas elsewhere on the page goes black.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` and stopping there. That frees GPU objects but not the context itself; `forceContextLoss()` — what `disposeRenderer` does — is the call that actually hands it back.\n- Not repainting after a resize while the loop is paused. The framebuffer keeps the old dimensions and the browser stretches the last frame, so an off-screen preview reappears visibly smeared.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Gated on `(pointer: fine)`. On touch the listeners never attach, so the canvas renders the idle grid with the lens parked outside NDC — a tap cannot pop a bulge under the finger and leave it there on release.\n- Guarded with `hasWebGL()`: on a blocklisted GPU or with hardware acceleration off, nothing is constructed and the container is simply an empty dark panel rather than a permanently blank canvas.\n- The loop is gated on `useInView` plus `useVisibility`, so an off-screen or backgrounded instance costs zero GPU time.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove across the panel and the grid compresses under a soft circular lens that arrives about half a second behind the pointer, then settles precisely under it when you stop. Leave the panel and the lens fades out where it stands without sliding. Resize the container and the lens stays circular rather than becoming an ellipse. Unmount and the WebGL context count in about:gpu goes back down.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "typewriter-install": "# Build: Typewriter Install\n\nThe install line of a landing page: a small card with package-manager tabs on top and a monospace command below that appears one character at a time behind an accent caret, then idles with the caret blinking. Switching tabs swaps the command and types it again from the start; the copy button copies the whole command at any moment and confirms with a check. Screen readers get the full command at once, and reduced motion shows it fully typed.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn install command that types itself behind a caret; pnpm, npm, yarn and bun tabs restart the typing and a copy button takes the full line.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Render a react-aria `Tabs` with one `Tab` and one `TabPanel` per command (`installCommands(pkg)` builds pnpm add / npm install / yarn add / bun add). Only the selected panel is mounted, so a tab change unmounts the old line and mounts a fresh one: the typing restarts without any reset logic.\n2. Inside the panel, `TypedLine` runs a `setTimeout` chain: after `startDelay` (320ms) it bumps a character count, then schedules the next one with `charDelay` — `speed` (42ms) times a deterministic ±20% jitter from the index, ×2.4 after a space and ×1.6 after `@` or `/`. The effect cleanup clears the pending timer.\n3. Stack two layers in one grid cell: the full command `invisible` underneath (it fixes the final width and wrapping, so the card never grows) and `text.slice(0, count)` plus the caret on top. Both are `aria-hidden`; an `sr-only` copy of the full command is what assistive tech reads.\n4. The caret is a 2px accent bar held solid while typing; once the count reaches the end it gets `data-idle=\"true\"` and a step-end blink keyframe (on 55%, off 45% of 1.06s), emitted once through React 19 `<style href precedence>`.\n5. The copy button calls `navigator.clipboard.writeText` with the selected command, crossfades the copy icon into a success check for 1.6s and announces \"Copied to clipboard\" through a `role=\"status\"` region; the reset timer is cleared on re-click and unmount.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `speed = 42ms per character, ±20% jitter`\n  A 24-character command lands in about a second: slow enough to read as typing, fast enough that nobody waits to copy.\n- `word beat = ×2.4 after a space, ×1.6 after @ or /`\n  People type in words; a pause between words is what makes uniform per-key timing stop looking mechanical.\n- `startDelay = 320ms`\n  Shows the empty prompt and caret for a beat after a tab change, so the restart is visible as a restart.\n- `copied state = 1.6s`\n  Long enough to see the check after the pointer leaves the button, short enough that a second copy gets fresh feedback.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction TypewriterInstall(props: { packageName?: string; commands?: InstallCommand[]; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (id: string) => void; speed?: number; startDelay?: number; prompt?: string; label?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype InstallCommand = { id: string; label: string; command: string }\n```\n\n- `packageName: string` — default `\"motion\"`\n  Package used to build the default pnpm, npm, yarn and bun commands.\n- `commands: InstallCommand[]`\n  Replaces the default commands: { id, label, command } per tab.\n- `value: string`\n  Controlled selected tab id.\n- `defaultValue: string` — default `commands[0].id`\n  Initial tab id when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the newly selected tab id.\n- `speed: number` — default `42`\n  Base milliseconds per character.\n- `startDelay: number` — default `320`\n  Milliseconds before the first character after mount or a tab change.\n- `prompt: string` — default `\"$\"`\n  Glyph shown before the command.\n- `label: string` — default `\"Package manager\"`\n  Accessible name of the tab list.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Delay before character `i`. Deterministic, so server and client agree and\n * every replay has the same rhythm: ±20% around `speed` from the index, a\n * longer beat after a space (the eye reads word by word) and after `@`/`/`.\n */\nfunction charDelay(text: string, i: number, speed: number) {\n  const jitter = 0.8 + ((i * 37) % 9) / 20;\n  const prev = text[i - 1];\n  const beat = prev === \" \" ? 2.4 : prev === \"@\" || prev === \"/\" ? 1.6 : 1;\n  return speed * jitter * beat;\n}\n\n/** One typed line. Mounted per tab, so a tab change restarts it. */\nfunction TypedLine({ text, speed, startDelay, prompt }: { text: string; speed: number; startDelay: number; prompt: string }) {\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (reduced) return;\n    let i = 0;\n    let timer = window.setTimeout(function step() {\n      i += 1;\n      setCount(i);\n      if (i < text.length) timer = window.setTimeout(step, charDelay(text, i, speed));\n    }, startDelay);\n    return () => window.clearTimeout(timer);\n  }, [text, speed, startDelay, reduced]);\n\n  // Derived, not stored: reduced motion shows the whole line on the same\n  // render the preference is known, with no typing frame in between.\n  const shown = reduced ? text.length : Math.min(count, text.length);\n  const done = shown >= text.length;\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"flex min-w-0 items-baseline gap-2.5 font-mono text-body-2-regular\">\n      <span aria-hidden className=\"shrink-0 select-none text-text-tertiary\">\n        {prompt}\n      </span>\n      {/* The full line sits invisibly underneath to hold the final width and\n          wrap, so the card never grows while the line types. */}\n      <span className=\"grid min-w-0 flex-1 break-all\">\n        <span aria-hidden className=\"invisible col-start-1 row-start-1\">\n          {text}\n          <span className=\"inline-block w-[2px]\" />\n        </span>\n        <span aria-hidden className=\"col-start-1 row-start-1 text-text-primary\">\n          {text.slice(0, shown)}\n          <span\n            data-idle={done ? \"true\" : \"false\"}\n            data-typewriter-caret=\"\"\n            className=\"ms-px inline-block h-[1em] w-[2px] rounded-full bg-accent-500 align-[-0.14em]\"\n          />\n        </span>\n        <span className=\"sr-only\">{text}</span>\n      </span>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\n/** Copies the whole command; the icon swaps to a check for 1.6s. */\nfunction CopyCommandButton({ text }: { text: string }) {\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState<\"idle\" | \"copied\" | \"failed\">(\"idle\");\n  const timer = useRef<number | undefined>(undefined);\n\n  useEffect(() => () => window.clearTimeout(timer.current), []);\n\n  const copy = async () => {\n    window.clearTimeout(timer.current);\n    try {\n      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);\n      setStatus(\"copied\");\n    } catch {\n      setStatus(\"failed\");\n    }\n    timer.current = window.setTimeout(() => setStatus(\"idle\"), COPIED_MS);\n  };\n\n  const copied = status === \"copied\";\n  return (\n    <>\n      <button\n        type=\"button\"\n        onClick={copy}\n        aria-label={copied ? \"Copied\" : \"Copy command\"}\n        className={cx(\n          \"relative -my-1 flex size-8 shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center justify-center rounded-lg text-foreground-icon-secondary\",\n          \"transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:bg-background-secondary-default hover:text-foreground-icon-primary\",\n          \"outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n          \"active:bg-background-secondary-hover\",\n        )}\n      >\n        <RiFileCopyLine\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            \"absolute size-4 transition-[opacity,scale] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            copied ? \"scale-50 opacity-0\" : \"scale-100 opacity-100\",\n          )}\n        />\n        <RiCheckLine\n          aria-hidden\n          className={cx(\n            \"absolute size-4 text-state-success-text transition-[opacity,scale] duration-200 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n            copied ? \"scale-100 opacity-100\" : \"scale-50 opacity-0\",\n          )}\n        />\n      </button>\n      <span role=\"status\" className=\"sr-only\">\n        {status === \"copied\" ? \"Copied to clipboard\" : status === \"failed\" ? \"Copy failed, select the command instead\" : \"\"}\n      </span>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n\nexport function TypewriterInstall({\n  packageName = \"motion\",\n  commands,\n  value,\n  defaultValue,\n  onChange,\n  speed = 42,\n  startDelay = 320,\n  prompt = \"$\",\n  label = \"Package manager\",\n  className,\n}: TypewriterInstallProps) {\n  const list = commands ?? installCommands(packageName);\n  const [inner, setInner] = useState(defaultValue ?? list[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const selected = value ?? inner;\n  const current = list.find((c) => c.id === selected) ?? list[0];\n\n  const select = (key: Key) => {\n    const id = String(key);\n    setInner(id);\n    onChange?.(id);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <Tabs\n      selectedKey={current?.id}\n      onSelectionChange={select}\n      className={cx(\n        \"w-full max-w-[560px] overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default shadow-card\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <style href=\"kinetic-typewriter-install\" precedence=\"medium\">\n        {CARET_CSS}\n      </style>\n      <div className=\"flex items-center border-b border-border-button-default px-2 py-1.5\">\n        <TabList aria-label={label} className=\"flex items-center gap-0.5\">\n          {list.map((c) => (\n            <Tab\n              key={c.id}\n              id={c.id}\n              className={cx(\n                \"cursor-pointer rounded-md px-2.5 py-1 font-mono text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary outline-none\",\n                \"transition-colors duration-150 ease-out hover:text-text-primary\",\n                \"data-selected:bg-background-secondary-default data-selected:text-text-primary\",\n                \"data-focus-visible:ring-2 data-focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\",\n              )}\n            >\n              {c.label}\n            </Tab>\n          ))}\n        </TabList>\n      </div>\n      <div className=\"flex items-center gap-3 px-4 py-3.5\">\n        {list.map((c) => (\n          <TabPanel key={c.id} id={c.id} className=\"min-w-0 flex-1 rounded-sm outline-none data-focus-visible:ring-2 data-focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring\">\n            <TypedLine text={c.command} speed={speed} startDelay={startDelay} prompt={prompt} />\n          </TabPanel>\n        ))}\n        {current && <CopyCommandButton text={current.command} />}\n      </div>\n    </Tabs>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The typing effect clears its pending timeout on unmount, tab change or prop change; the copy button clears its reset timeout on unmount and before each new copy.\n\n**Reduced motion.** With prefers-reduced-motion the timer chain never starts and the full command renders on the first client render; the caret stays solid without blinking and the copy icon swap has no transition.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Copying the typed slice instead of the full command: a click halfway through would put half a command on the clipboard.\n- Letting the typed text drive layout. Without the invisible full-width layer the card grows a line at a time on narrow screens as the text wraps.\n- Pausing the caret blink with `animation-play-state: paused` — it can freeze on the invisible frame. Drop the animation while typing instead.\n- Exposing the typing to screen readers. Each character change in a live tree is noise; hide the animated layer and give them the whole line once.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `commands` replaces the defaults for anything that is not a package install, e.g. a CLI `add` command.\n- Clicking the already-selected tab does not retype; the panel only remounts when the selection changes.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nOn load the pnpm tab is selected and \"pnpm add @remixicon/react\" types out in about a second, then the caret blinks. Clicking \"npm\" shows \"npm install @remixicon/react\" typing from the start and the caption reads \"Package manager: npm\". Clicking \"Copy command\" turns the icon into a check and the button name into \"Copied\". With reduced motion the full command shows immediately.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "typography": "# Build: Typography\n\nA docs specimen for the kit composite text utilities (`text-{family}-{weight}`). Each family gets a heading with its size, line height and tracking, a one-line usage note, and a bordered card with one row per weight: the sample sentence on the left, and the class name and weight on the right. The numbers are not typed into the component. They are read with `getComputedStyle` from the rendered rows, so the specimen always shows what the theme CSS resolves to. Clicking a class name copies it.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nType-scale specimen of the 13 kit text families in four weights, with size and line height read from the live CSS and copyable class names.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `TYPE_SCALE` lists the 13 families from Large Title to Caption 2. Each has its four class names written out in full (`text-body-medium`, not `text-body-${w}`), because Tailwind only emits utilities it finds as literal strings in the source.\n2. `TypeFamilySection` renders one `<li>` per requested weight. The sample `<p>` gets the composite class and a callback ref stored in `sampleRefs[i]`.\n3. After mount, and again whenever the family or the weight list changes, an effect runs `readTypeMetrics` on every sample element. It turns `fontSize`, `lineHeight`, `letterSpacing` and `fontWeight` into numbers and stores them in state. A window `resize` listener re-reads them, because zoom and responsive root sizes arrive that way.\n4. `formatFamilyMetrics` builds the header string from the measured rows, for example `18px / 24–26px` for Title 3. It shows a range when the weights have different line heights, and adds `· tracking 0.15px` when letter spacing is not zero. Each row shows its own measured weight number next to the weight name.\n5. `TokenCopy` is a mono button holding the class name. It copies through `useCopyToClipboard` and shows a lime check for 1.4s. `Typography` stacks one section per family id with a 40px gap.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `families = 13 × 4 weights = 52 utilities`\n  This mirrors the `@theme` block in boardui-theme.css. A family missing from `TYPE_SCALE` would never be emitted, because its class names would appear nowhere in the source.\n- `row = px-4 py-4, 1px border-button-default between rows, rounded-2xl card`\n  This matches the reference card. The divider and corner radius are the same ones the code block and install box use.\n- `metric header = font-mono caption-1, text-tertiary`\n  Measured numbers read as data next to the title-2 family name, the same as the reference \"64px / 80px\".\n- `copy reset = 1400 ms`\n  The check only needs to confirm the click, and many class names get copied in a row.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\ntype TypeWeight = \"regular\" | \"medium\" | \"semibold\" | \"bold\";\nfunction Typography(props: { families?: readonly string[]; weights?: readonly TypeWeight[]; sample?: string; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; className?: string; ref?: Ref<HTMLDivElement> }): JSX.Element\nfunction TypeFamilySection(props: { family: TypeFamily; weights?: readonly TypeWeight[]; sample?: string; headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction readTypeMetrics(element: Element): { fontSize: number; lineHeight: number; letterSpacing: number; fontWeight: number }\nconst TYPE_SCALE: readonly TypeFamily[]\n```\n\n- `families: readonly string[]` — default `all 13`\n  Family ids to show (`large-title`, `display-1` … `caption-2`), kept in scale order.\n- `weights: readonly TypeWeight[]` — default `[\"regular\", \"medium\", \"semibold\", \"bold\"]`\n  Weights shown in each family card.\n- `sample: string` — default `\"The five boxing wizards jump quickly.\"`\n  Sentence rendered in every row.\n- `headingLevel: 2 | 3 | 4` — default `3`\n  Heading level of each family name.\n- `className: string`\n  Extra classes on the outer stack.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Metrics come from getComputedStyle on the rendered sample, not from a\n// table in this file, so the specimen always shows what the theme CSS\n// really resolves to (including a rem change or an overridden token).\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const read = () =>\n    setMetrics(\n      weightKey.split(\",\").map((_, i) => {\n        const el = sampleRefs.current[i];\n        return el ? readTypeMetrics(el) : null;\n      }),\n    );\n  read();\n  // Page zoom and responsive root sizes arrive as a resize; re-read then.\n  window.addEventListener(\"resize\", read);\n  return () => window.removeEventListener(\"resize\", read);\n}, [family.id, weightKey]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Each family section adds one window `resize` listener in its effect and removes it on unmount or before the next run. The copy reset timer in `useCopyToClipboard` is cleared on unmount.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. Only the copy button hover colour transitions, and it has `motion-reduce:transition-none`.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Building class names with template strings (`text-${family}-${weight}`). Tailwind never sees them, the utilities are not generated, and every row renders in the inherited body size.\n- Measuring in render or during SSR. `getComputedStyle` only exists after layout. Measuring in an effect means the server and the first client render both show a placeholder, so hydration still matches.\n- Trusting a hard-coded metrics table. Title 3 uses a 24px line height for regular and 26px for heavier weights, and a table drifts as soon as a token changes. Reading the CSS keeps the specimen correct.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `readTypeMetrics` and `formatFamilyMetrics` are exported for other token docs, for example a size badge next to a heading in a design review.\n- Pass `families={[\"body\", \"body-2\"]}` and `weights={[\"regular\", \"medium\"]}` to show a focused subset in a component page.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nRendering `<Typography families={[\"title-3\"]} />` shows a \"Title 3\" heading followed by `18px / 24–26px`, and four rows whose samples are visibly 18px regular, medium, semibold and bold. The rows are labelled `text-title-3-regular` … `text-title-3-bold` with \"Regular · 400\" … \"Bold · 700\". Caption 1 shows `12px / 16px · tracking 0.15px`. Clicking a class name puts that exact string on the clipboard and shows a check. Changing `sample` updates every row, and switching families re-measures the header.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "users-access-card": "# Build: Users Access Card\n\nThe \"Users with access\" panel of a workspace settings page. Each member row shows a tinted initials avatar, name, email and a compact role select, with a round × button to remove them; the owner row is locked and shows its role as text. An invite form at the bottom checks the email (empty, malformed, already a member), derives a name from the address and appends an \"Invited\" row with the chosen role. Every change is announced through a polite live region.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nWorkspace members with initials, email and a role select per row, a remove button, and an invite field that validates before adding a row.\n\n- Category: form\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Hold the list controlled-or-uncontrolled: local state seeds from `defaultUsers`, `users` wins when passed, and every change goes through `commit(next)` which sets local state and calls `onUsersChange(next)`.\n2. Render each member as an `li` with the kit `Avatar` (initials from the first letters of the first two words, `aria-hidden`), name plus optional `Badge` \"Invited\", the email, then either the role as text (`locked`) or a kit `Select` (`aria-label` \"Role for <name>\") and a kit `CloseButton` (24px) \"Remove <name>\".\n3. On invite submit, `preventDefault`, trim and lowercase the draft, and test in order: empty, the `EMAIL` regex, and duplicates (case-insensitive). A failure sets the kit `Input`'s `isInvalid` + `errorMessage` and refocuses the field; typing clears the error.\n4. A valid address becomes a row: `nameFromEmail` splits the local part on `.`, `_` and `-` (dropping a `+tag`) and title-cases it, the colour cycles blue, lime, pink, neutral by list length, `invited: true` adds the badge, and the draft is cleared.\n5. Removing stores the next non-locked neighbour (after, then before) in a ref; an effect keyed on the list focuses that row's remove button, or the invite field when none is left, so keyboard users never lose focus to `<body>`.\n6. New rows fade and slide 4px down into place with a CSS `@starting-style` transition (`starting:opacity-0 starting:-translate-y-1`, 200ms ease-out), off under `motion-reduce`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `EMAIL = /^[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@]+\\.[^\\s@]{2,}$/`\n  Catches the common typos (missing @, missing TLD, spaces) without rejecting real addresses the way a strict RFC pattern would.\n- `role select width = 104px`\n  Fits the longest default label (\"Editor\"/\"Viewer\") plus the chevron; locked rows span 140px (select + 12px gap + 24px close button) with 8px start padding so \"Owner\" lines up with the select labels.\n- `row entry = 200ms, translate-y -4px → 0, opacity 0 → 1`\n  Long enough to show where the invited row landed, short enough not to delay a second invite.\n- `invite row breakpoint = @sm container query`\n  Below ~384px card width the field takes its own line and the role select and Invite button share the next one.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction UsersAccessCard(props: { users?: AccessUser[]; defaultUsers?: AccessUser[]; onUsersChange?: (users: AccessUser[]) => void; roles?: AccessRole[]; defaultInviteRole?: string; title?: string; invitePlaceholder?: string; inviteLabel?: ReactNode; className?: string }): JSX.Element\ntype AccessUser = { id: string; name: string; email: string; role: string; initials?: string; color?: \"neutral\" | \"blue\" | \"lime\" | \"pink\"; locked?: boolean; invited?: boolean }\n```\n\n- `users: AccessUser[]`\n  Controlled member list.\n- `defaultUsers: AccessUser[]` — default `[]`\n  Initial members when uncontrolled.\n- `onUsersChange: (users: AccessUser[]) => void`\n  Called with the new list after a role change, removal or invite.\n- `roles: AccessRole[]` — default `Admin, Editor, Viewer`\n  Options of every role select ({ id, label }).\n- `defaultInviteRole: string` — default `roles[1].id`\n  Role preselected in the invite form.\n- `title: string` — default `\"Users with access\"`\n  Card heading and the list's accessible name.\n- `invitePlaceholder: string` — default `\"Invite by email\"`\n  Placeholder of the invite field.\n- `inviteLabel: ReactNode` — default `\"Invite\"`\n  Label of the submit button.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function UsersAccessCard({\n  users,\n  defaultUsers = [],\n  onUsersChange,\n  roles = DEFAULT_ACCESS_ROLES,\n  defaultInviteRole,\n  title = \"Users with access\",\n  invitePlaceholder = \"Invite by email\",\n  inviteLabel = \"Invite\",\n  className,\n}: UsersAccessCardProps) {\n  const [own, setOwn] = useState(defaultUsers);\n  const list = users ?? own;\n  const [draft, setDraft] = useState(\"\");\n  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);\n  const [inviteRole, setInviteRole] = useState(defaultInviteRole ?? roles[1]?.id ?? roles[0]?.id ?? \"\");\n  const [announcement, setAnnouncement] = useState(\"\");\n\n  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\n  const removeRefs = useRef(new Map<string, HTMLButtonElement>());\n  // After a removal, focus lands on the next row's remove button (or the\n  // invite field) once the new list has rendered.\n  const pendingFocus = useRef<string | null>(null);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const id = pendingFocus.current;\n    if (id === null) return;\n    pendingFocus.current = null;\n    (removeRefs.current.get(id) ?? inputRef.current)?.focus();\n  }, [list]);\n\n  const commit = (next: AccessUser[]) => {\n    setOwn(next);\n    onUsersChange?.(next);\n  };\n  const roleLabel = (id: string) => roles.find((r) => r.id === id)?.label ?? id;\n\n  const invite = (e: FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const email = draft.trim().toLowerCase();\n    let problem: string | null = null;\n    if (!email) problem = \"Enter an email address.\";\n    else if (!EMAIL.test(email)) problem = \"Enter a valid email, like name@company.com.\";\n    else if (list.some((u) => u.email.toLowerCase() === email)) problem = `${email} already has access.`;\n    if (problem) {\n      setError(problem);\n      inputRef.current?.focus();\n      return;\n    }\n    const name = nameFromEmail(email);\n    commit([\n      ...list,\n      {\n        id: `invite:${email}`,\n        name,\n        email,\n        role: inviteRole,\n        color: COLORS[list.length % COLORS.length],\n        invited: true,\n      },\n    ]);\n    setDraft(\"\");\n    setAnnouncement(`Invited ${name} as ${roleLabel(inviteRole)}.`);\n  };\n\n  const remove = (id: string) => {\n    const i = list.findIndex((u) => u.id === id);\n    const gone = list[i];\n    if (!gone) return;\n    const next = list.filter((u) => u.id !== id);\n    const neighbour = [...next.slice(i), ...next.slice(0, i).reverse()].find((u) => !u.locked);\n    pendingFocus.current = neighbour?.id ?? \"\";\n    commit(next);\n    setAnnouncement(`Removed ${gone.name}.`);\n  };\n\n  const setRole = (id: string, role: string) => {\n    commit(list.map((u) => (u.id === id ? { ...u, role } : u)));\n    const who = list.find((u) => u.id === id);\n    if (who) setAnnouncement(`${who.name} is now ${roleLabel(role)}.`);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      className={cx(\n        \"@container flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col rounded-2xl border border-border-button-default bg-background-primary-default p-1.5 shadow-xs\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      <header className=\"flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 px-2.5 pt-1.5 pb-1\">\n        <h3 className=\"text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">{title}</h3>\n        <span className=\"text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary tabular-nums\">\n          {list.length} {list.length === 1 ? \"person\" : \"people\"}\n        </span>\n      </header>\n\n      <ul aria-label={title} className=\"flex flex-col\">\n        {list.map((u) => (\n          <li\n            key={u.id}\n            className=\"flex items-center gap-3 rounded-2lg px-2.5 py-2 transition-[opacity,translate] duration-200 ease-out starting:-translate-y-1 starting:opacity-0 motion-reduce:transition-none\"\n          >\n            <Avatar size=\"md\" color={u.color ?? \"neutral\"} initials={u.initials ?? initialsOf(u.name)} aria-hidden />\n            <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col\">\n              <span className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5\">\n                <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary\">{u.name}</span>\n                {u.invited ? <Badge className=\"shrink-0\">Invited</Badge> : null}\n              </span>\n              <span className=\"truncate text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary\">{u.email}</span>\n            </div>\n            {u.locked ? (\n              <span className=\"w-[140px] shrink-0 ps-2 text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary\">\n                {roleLabel(u.role)}\n              </span>\n            ) : (\n              <>\n                <Select\n                  aria-label={`Role for ${u.name}`}\n                  size=\"sm\"\n                  value={u.role}\n                  onChange={(k) => k != null && setRole(u.id, String(k))}\n                  className=\"w-[104px] shrink-0\"\n                  popoverClassName=\"w-40\"\n                >\n                  {roles.map((r) => (\n                    <SelectItem key={r.id} id={r.id} textValue={r.label}>\n                      {r.label}\n                    </SelectItem>\n                  ))}\n                </Select>\n                <CloseButton\n                  ref={(el) => {\n                    if (el) removeRefs.current.set(u.id, el);\n                    else removeRefs.current.delete(u.id);\n                  }}\n                  size=\"sm\"\n                  aria-label={`Remove ${u.name}`}\n                  onClick={() => remove(u.id)}\n                />\n              </>\n            )}\n          </li>\n        ))}\n      </ul>\n\n      <form\n        noValidate\n        onSubmit={invite}\n        className=\"mt-1 grid grid-cols-[1fr_auto] items-start gap-2 border-t border-border-button-default px-2.5 pt-3 pb-2 @sm:grid-cols-[1fr_auto_auto]\"\n      >\n        <Input\n          ref={inputRef}\n          aria-label=\"Invite by email\"\n          type=\"email\"\n          size=\"small\"\n          leadingIcon={RiMailLine}\n          placeholder={invitePlaceholder}\n          value={draft}\n          onChange={(v) => {\n            setDraft(v);\n            setError(null);\n          }}\n          isInvalid={error !== null}\n          errorMessage={error}\n          validationBehavior=\"aria\"\n          className=\"col-span-2 min-w-0 @sm:col-span-1\"\n        />\n        <Select\n          aria-label=\"Role for new member\"\n          size=\"sm\"\n          value={inviteRole}\n          onChange={(k) => k != null && setInviteRole(String(k))}\n          className=\"w-[104px] @sm:mt-px\"\n          popoverClassName=\"w-40\"\n        >\n          {roles.map((r) => (\n            <SelectItem key={r.id} id={r.id} textValue={r.label}>\n              {r.label}\n            </SelectItem>\n          ))}\n        </Select>\n        <Button type=\"submit\" variant=\"primary\" size=\"small\" className=\"justify-self-end\">\n          {inviteLabel}\n        </Button>\n      </form>\n\n      <p className=\"sr-only\" aria-live=\"polite\">\n        {announcement}\n      </p>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nothing to clean up: no timers, listeners or observers. The remove-button ref map deletes an entry when its button unmounts.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Rows carry `motion-reduce:transition-none`, so an invited row appears in place without the fade and slide; the kit Select popover follows its own reduced-motion rule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Letting focus fall to <body> after removing a row: the button that had focus is gone, so focus must move to a neighbour explicitly after the re-render.\n- Using native constraint validation on the email input: the browser bubble ignores the kit styling and fires before the duplicate check. Use `noValidate` and `validationBehavior=\"aria\"` and show your own message.\n- Comparing emails case-sensitively, which lets \"Jon.Park@\" and \"jon.park@\" both in.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `locked` accepts any role label (e.g. \"Owner\") that is not in `roles`; it is shown as text.\n- The demo is controlled and counts admins under the card, so a role change updates the caption.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe card lists 4 people with Ava Lindqvist as a locked Owner. Submitting an empty invite shows \"Enter an email address.\"; \"bad@x\" shows the valid-email message; \"jon.park@example.com\" says it already has access. Inviting \"dana.kim@example.com\" appends \"Dana Kim\" with an Invited badge and the count becomes 5 people. Clicking \"Remove Jon Park\" drops the row and moves focus to the next remove button; changing a role through its select updates the admin count under the card.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "uv-cover-fit": "# Build: Cover-Fit UV Maths\n\nA shader has no box to crop, so `object-fit: cover` has to happen on the texture coordinates before the sample. The fit is a pure function of two numbers — the texture aspect and the quad aspect — which means the quad can be reshaped on every frame and the crop simply follows, with no re-upload and no CPU work. The frame here cycles from tall to wide continuously, because a cover fit only ever tested at one aspect ratio has not been tested.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nobject-fit: cover for a textured quad, solved in UV space so the crop follows a frame that reshapes every frame.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The sampled window starts at the full texture, `scale = vec2(1.0)`, and is only ever narrowed. If `textureAspect > planeAspect` the texture has surplus width, so `scale.x = planeAspect / textureAspect`; otherwise it has surplus height and `scale.y = textureAspect / planeAspect`. Exactly one axis shrinks and the other reaches both edges of the image.\n2. The leftover slack is `1 - scale`, and multiplying it by the focal point places that slack: `offset = (1.0 - scale) * uFocal`. At a focal of 0.5 the sampled window is `[(1-s)/2, (1+s)/2]` — centred — while 0 pins the crop to the left or top edge and 1 to the right or bottom.\n3. The final coordinate is `vUv * scale + offset`, which is guaranteed to stay inside [0,1] because scale is never greater than 1 and offset is exactly the remaining room. Nothing has to be clamped and no branch depends on the fragment.\n4. Overscan divides the scale further, shrinking the window past a strict fit. That reserve is what a vertex displacement elsewhere in a pipeline eats into: with no reserve, a wave or a bulge pushes a coordinate past the sampled region and the texture's clamped border smears along the edge.\n5. The frame is driven by an eased triangle wave with a hold at each end, and its scale is fitted to the viewport by the contain rule — `frameAspect > maxW / maxH ? width-limited : height-limited` — so the tall end of the cycle stays fully on screen rather than running off the top and bottom.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `minAspect / maxAspect = 0.62 / 2.3`\n  The range the frame sweeps. Both ends have to sit clearly on opposite sides of the source's own 16:9 so the shader is seen switching which axis it crops; a range that stays on one side of the texture aspect never exercises the other branch at all.\n- `period / hold = 8 s / 0.12`\n  Twelve percent of the cycle parked at each extreme. Without a hold the frame is only ever momentarily at its widest and the eye never gets to check the crop; past ~0.25 the component spends more time still than moving.\n- `fill = 0.84`\n  Fraction of the container the frame occupies at its largest. At 1.0 the frame is flush with the edges and its reshaping is invisible because there is no background to reshape against.\n- `overscan = 1`\n  Exactly 1 is a strict fit and the right default when nothing displaces the geometry. Raise it — 1.12 is the value `cylinder-gallery` uses — only when vertices move after the UV is computed; every increment past 1 is crop permanently thrown away.\n- `focal = [0.5, 0.5]`\n  Centres the slack on both axes. 0 or 1 pins the crop to an edge, which is what a subject-aware crop wants; anything outside [0,1] pushes the window off the texture and samples the clamped border.\n- `texture size = 1200 x 675`\n  A deliberately 16:9 source, so both branches of the fit are exercised as the frame sweeps past that ratio. The absolute size only sets sampling quality.\n- `grid cell = width / 16`\n  Square cells measured in source pixels. They are the test instrument: under a correct cover fit they stay square at every frame aspect, and the moment the maths degrades into a stretch they visibly become rectangles.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction UvCoverFit(props: { minAspect?: number; maxAspect?: number; period?: number; hold?: number; fill?: number; overscan?: number; focal?: [number, number]; paint?: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, size: { width: number; height: number }) => void; textureSize?: { width: number; height: number }; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `minAspect: number` — default `0.62`\n  Narrowest frame aspect in the cycle (w / h).\n- `maxAspect: number` — default `2.3`\n  Widest frame aspect in the cycle (w / h).\n- `period: number` — default `8`\n  Seconds for a full narrow-wide-narrow cycle, holds included.\n- `hold: number` — default `0.12`\n  Fraction of the cycle spent parked at each extreme.\n- `fill: number` — default `0.84`\n  Fraction of the container the frame fills at its largest.\n- `overscan: number` — default `1`\n  Extra zoom on the crop. 1 fits exactly; above 1 keeps margin in reserve.\n- `focal: [number, number]` — default `[0.5, 0.5]`\n  Where the crop slack goes, per axis. 0.5 centres it.\n- `paint: (ctx, size) => void`\n  Override the source image. Receives a 2D context for the source canvas.\n- `textureSize: { width: number; height: number }` — default `{ width: 1200, height: 675 }`\n  Source canvas size. Its ratio is the texture aspect the shader fits.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function UvCoverFit({\n  minAspect = 0.62,\n  maxAspect = 2.3,\n  period = 8,\n  hold = 0.12,\n  fill = 0.84,\n  overscan = 1,\n  focal = [0.5, 0.5],\n  paint,\n  textureSize = DEFAULT_TEXTURE,\n  className,\n}: UvCoverFitProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const elapsed = useRef(0);\n  const viewport = useRef({ width: 1, height: 1 });\n\n  const live = useRef({ minAspect, maxAspect, period, hold, fill, overscan, focal });\n  live.current = { minAspect, maxAspect, period, hold, fill, overscan, focal };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n  const textureWidth = textureSize.width;\n  const textureHeight = textureSize.height;\n\n  /* Sizing is a callback rather than effect-body code because it has to be\n     reachable from BOTH effects, which fire on independent triggers. The\n     camera is constructed at +/-1 and `viewport` starts at 1x1, and the only\n     thing that replaced either was the effect keyed on the observed width and\n     height. So any rebuild without an accompanying size change left the ortho\n     bounds square while the container was not — the exact viewport stretch\n     this component exists to disprove — and left the frame sized against a\n     1x1 world inside a camera spanning aspect*2 by 2, i.e. a sliver. The\n     build effect re-runs on `textureSize` and `paint`, and a caller passing\n     an inline `paint` arrow rebuilds the stage on every parent render, so\n     that state persisted until the user happened to resize the window.\n     Everything below is derived purely from w and h, so calling it from both\n     paths is idempotent. */\n  const fitViewport = useCallback((w: number, h: number) => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || w < 1 || h < 1) return;\n    const aspect = w / h;\n    current.renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    current.renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n    // Ortho bounds track the container aspect so a world unit is the same\n    // number of pixels on both axes. Leave them at +/-1 and the quad is\n    // stretched by the viewport, which would fake the exact bug this\n    // component exists to avoid.\n    current.camera.left = -aspect;\n    current.camera.right = aspect;\n    current.camera.top = 1;\n    current.camera.bottom = -1;\n    current.camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n    viewport.current = { width: aspect * 2, height: 2 };\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    // Orthographic, because nothing here is about perspective. World units map\n    // one-to-one to the frame: the quad's scale IS its aspect ratio, with no\n    // frustum arithmetic in between.\n    const camera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 10);\n    camera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.width = textureWidth;\n    canvas.height = textureHeight;\n    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n    if (ctx) (paint ?? paintSource)(ctx, { width: textureWidth, height: textureHeight });\n\n    const texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas);\n    texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    // Clamp, not repeat. If a rounding error ever pushes a UV a hair past 1,\n    // repeat wraps the far edge of the image into view as a bright seam;\n    // clamp smears the border pixel instead, which is invisible.\n    texture.wrapS = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n    texture.wrapT = THREE.ClampToEdgeWrapping;\n    texture.anisotropy = Math.min(4, renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy());\n\n    const uniforms: CoverUniforms = {\n      uTexture: { value: texture },\n      uTextureAspect: { value: textureWidth / textureHeight },\n      uPlaneAspect: { value: 1 },\n      uOverscan: { value: overscan },\n      uFocal: { value: new THREE.Vector2(focal[0], focal[1]) },\n    };\n\n    // One quad, two triangles. The fit is per-fragment ratio arithmetic, so\n    // subdividing would buy nothing at all.\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, 1);\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: uvCoverFitVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: uvCoverFitFragmentShader,\n      uniforms,\n    });\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    scene.add(mesh);\n\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, mesh, geometry, material, texture, uniforms };\n\n    // Fit before the first frame. `ready` guarantees the observed box is real,\n    // and waiting for the resize effect to do it is a bet that the box will\n    // change size again — on a stage that is built once and never reflowed,\n    // that bet never pays out and every frame is drawn against a 1x1 world.\n    fitViewport(width, height);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      scene.remove(mesh);\n      // Inside `uniforms`, so `disposeObject` will never reach it.\n      texture.dispose();\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, textureWidth, textureHeight, paint]);\n\n  // Reflow costs a re-fit, never a rebuild. The guard against a stage that\n  // does not exist yet now lives inside `fitViewport`.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fitViewport(width, height);\n  }, [fitViewport, width, height]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n      const {\n        minAspect: low,\n        maxAspect: high,\n        period: cycle,\n        hold: pause,\n        fill: coverage,\n        overscan: zoom,\n        focal: focalPoint,\n      } = live.current;\n\n      if (!reduced) elapsed.current += dt;\n      const phase = reduced ? 0.5 : cyclePhase(elapsed.current, Math.max(cycle, 1), pause);\n      const frameAspect = lerp(low, high, phase);\n\n      // Contain the frame inside the viewport at the requested aspect. Fitting\n      // on width alone means the tall end of the cycle runs off the top and\n      // bottom, which hides half the thing being demonstrated.\n      const available = viewport.current;\n      const maxW = available.width * coverage;\n      const maxH = available.height * coverage;\n      const frameW = frameAspect > maxW / maxH ? maxW : maxH * frameAspect;\n      const frameH = frameAspect > maxW / maxH ? maxW / frameAspect : maxH;\n\n      current.mesh.scale.set(frameW, frameH, 1);\n      // The uniform is the QUAD's aspect, not the canvas's. Passing the\n      // container aspect here is the classic bug: the crop then ignores the\n      // frame and only tracks the browser window.\n      current.uniforms.uPlaneAspect.value = frameAspect;\n      current.uniforms.uOverscan.value = zoom;\n      current.uniforms.uFocal.value.set(focalPoint[0], focalPoint[1]);\n\n      current.renderer.render(current.scene, current.camera);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the quad from the scene, disposes the `CanvasTexture` explicitly because it sits inside `material.uniforms` where `disposeObject` cannot reach it, runs `disposeObject` over the scene, disposes the geometry and material, then calls `disposeRenderer` — `setAnimationLoop(null)`, `dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()` — and removes the canvas from the DOM. The stage ref is nulled before any of it, so a frame already queued by the shared RAF loop returns immediately instead of rendering into a dead context.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The aspect cycle does not advance: elapsed time stops accumulating and the phase is pinned to 0.5, so the frame holds at the midpoint between `minAspect` and `maxAspect` and never reshapes. The cover fit itself still runs every frame and still responds correctly to container resizes — what is removed is the autonomous animation, not the behaviour.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Scaling the short axis up rather than the long axis down. `scale.y = planeAspect / textureAspect` when that ratio exceeds 1 pushes the sampled window past the edge of the texture, and with the default `RepeatWrapping` the far side of the image wraps into view as a bright seam across the frame.\n- Passing the container's aspect ratio as `uPlaneAspect`. The uniform has to be the aspect of the QUAD being drawn; hand it the canvas aspect instead and the crop stops tracking the frame entirely and only responds to browser-window resizes.\n- Leaving the orthographic camera at the default -1..1 bounds. A world unit is then a different number of pixels horizontally and vertically, so the quad is stretched by the viewport — faking the exact distortion this component exists to prevent. The bounds track the container aspect.\n- Applying the offset before the scale. `(vUv + offset) * scale` shrinks the offset along with the coordinate, so the crop drifts toward the origin as the aspect mismatch grows instead of staying centred.\n- Leaving `wrapS` / `wrapT` at `RepeatWrapping`. A rounding error of one ulp past 1.0 then wraps to the opposite edge of the image and shows as a one-pixel bright line; `ClampToEdgeWrapping` smears the border pixel instead, which is invisible.\n- Testing with a smooth gradient. Every fit looks correct on a gradient. A square grid and a true circle are what make a stretch obvious — the cells go rectangular and the circle goes elliptical.\n- Fitting the frame on width alone. The tall end of the aspect cycle then runs off the top and bottom of the container, hiding half of what is being demonstrated. The frame is contained on whichever axis is tighter.\n- Omitting `#include <colorspace_fragment>` from the `ShaderMaterial`. three only injects output conversion into its own materials, so raw linear values ship into an sRGB framebuffer and the quad renders visibly washed out.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` alone on unmount. It frees GPU objects but leaves the WebGL context alive, and Chromium silently kills the oldest context once a renderer process holds about 16 of them. `disposeRenderer` calls `forceContextLoss`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The fit is per-fragment ratio arithmetic, so the quad is a single unsubdivided `PlaneGeometry(1, 1)`. `cylinder-gallery` runs the same maths per vertex instead, because there it feeds a bend that needs the result before rasterisation.\n- The camera is orthographic so that world units map straight onto the frame: the mesh's scale IS its aspect ratio, with no frustum arithmetic sitting between the two.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe frame reshapes continuously from tall to wide and back, pausing briefly at each extreme. Throughout, the grid cells stay square and the circle stays circular — nothing ever stretches. As the frame passes the source's own 16:9 ratio you can see the crop switch from trimming the top and bottom to trimming the sides, with the centre of the image staying put. Resize the container and the frame stays fully inside it at both ends of its cycle. With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the frame holds at one aspect and does not animate, and the image inside it is still correctly cropped.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "velocity-afterimage-pass": "# Build: Velocity Afterimage Pass\n\nA motion trail that stores no history. Two render targets take turns: one holds everything that has happened, the other receives that accumulation multiplied by a decay factor with the current frame composited over it, and next frame they swap roles. Because the trail is a texture rather than a list of past positions, its length is one float — so it can be driven straight from how fast the marks are moving and change continuously without allocating anything. Fast movement smears; slow movement is perfectly crisp.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nMarks that smear into a tail when the pointer moves fast and stay crisp when it slows, from two targets swapping roles.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Three half-float targets are allocated at container size x DPR x `resolutionScale`: `frame` receives only this frame's marks, and `ping`/`pong` alternate as the accumulation. Reading and writing one target in a single pass is undefined in WebGL, which is the only reason there are two.\n2. Pass 1 clears `frame` and renders the content scene into it — one lead mark plus `satellites` orbiters, all sharing a single 1x1 `PlaneGeometry` and one 256px canvas-drawn radial falloff texture, so the whole cluster is a handful of draw calls and no per-frame allocation.\n3. The lead follows the pointer through `damp(lead.x, pointer.ndcX * aspect, 0.0004, dt)`, and the satellites sit at `lead + (cos, sin)(i/n * 2PI + elapsed * 1.5) * 0.3` around it. The x target is multiplied by aspect because the orthographic frustum is widened to `[-aspect, aspect]` while staying `[-1, 1]` tall.\n4. Trail length is measured from the *mark*, not the cursor: `speed = hypot(lead - previousLead) / dt`, normalised by `reference`, clamped to 0..1 and scaled by `maxDamp`. That target is then itself run through `damp(..., 0.003, dt)`, so the tail extends and retracts smoothly instead of popping to full length on the first fast frame.\n5. Pass 2 renders a fullscreen quad into whichever target is not the current history, with `uCurrent = frame.texture`, `uHistory = ping.texture` and `uDamp` set to the smoothed speed. The fragment is `float alive = step(uCutoff, history.a); history *= uDamp * alive; gl_FragColor = max(current, history);` — `max` keeps the head at full strength and lets the tail sit underneath it.\n6. Pass 3 blits the accumulation to the canvas through a stock `MeshBasicMaterial`, so three performs output colour conversion exactly once and the blend pass stays in linear space. Then `ping` and `pong` are swapped: what was just written becomes next frame's history.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `maxDamp = 0.9`\n  Per-frame decay factor at full speed. Must stay below 1: at exactly 1 nothing ever decays and the trail becomes a permanent painted streak across the panel. Above ~0.95 the tail outlives the gesture by seconds and reads as a smudged framebuffer rather than motion.\n- `cutoff = 0.06`\n  Alpha below which `step()` zeroes a pixel outright. Geometric decay never actually reaches zero — at 0.9 a pixel still holds 0.5% of its value a hundred frames later, and on a half-float target that faint ghost of the very first frame is baked in permanently. Below ~0.02 the ghost is visible on a dark surface; above ~0.15 the tail ends in a hard edge instead of fading.\n- `reference = 1.6`\n  World units per second that count as full speed. The frustum is 2 units tall, so this is roughly 0.8 container heights per second — a brisk but ordinary hand movement. Set it much higher and only a violent flick produces any trail at all.\n- `LEAD_SMOOTHING = 0.0004`\n  Fraction of the gap to the pointer still remaining after one second. Tight enough that the lead feels attached to the cursor, loose enough that the mark keeps travelling for a frame or two after the hand stops — which is what gives the tail something to decay from.\n- `DAMP_SMOOTHING = 0.003`\n  Smoothing on the decay factor itself, deliberately looser than the position smoothing. If the trail length tracked speed instantly, a single dropped frame would spike `dt`, spike the measured speed, and flash a full-length tail out of a stationary mark.\n- `ORBIT_RADIUS / ORBIT_SPEED = 0.3 / 1.5`\n  Satellites orbit 0.3 world units out at 1.5 rad/s. The radius has to exceed the lead's own 0.34 half-size or the satellites never clear the lead's falloff and the cluster reads as one blob; the speed is what produces a curved tail rather than three parallel straight ones.\n- `SPRITE_SIZE = 256`\n  One canvas-drawn radial gradient shared by every mark. 256px is above the on-screen size of the largest mark at DPR 2, so it never magnifies into visible steps, and it is small enough that the upload is a single sub-millisecond cost at build time.\n- `target type / filter = HalfFloatType, NearestFilter`\n  The accumulation is multiplied by a fraction every frame. On the default 8-bit unsigned byte the result quantises into stair-stepped bands down the length of the trail. Nearest rather than linear because the blend samples texel-for-texel from a target of identical size — bilinear filtering would smear the trail sideways a little more on each of the hundreds of passes a pixel survives.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction VelocityAfterimagePass(props: { maxDamp?: number; cutoff?: number; reference?: number; satellites?: number; hue?: number; resolutionScale?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `maxDamp: number` — default `0.9`\n  Longest trail, as the per-frame decay factor. Must stay below 1.\n- `cutoff: number` — default `0.06`\n  Alpha below which a pixel is cut to nothing instead of decaying forever.\n- `reference: number` — default `1.6`\n  Mark travel in world units per second that counts as full speed.\n- `satellites: number` — default `3`\n  Marks orbiting the lead. 0 leaves a single mark.\n- `hue: number` — default `196`\n  Base hue in degrees. Satellites are offset 26 degrees apart from it.\n- `resolutionScale: number` — default `1`\n  Render target scale relative to the canvas. Below 1 trades tail sharpness for fill rate.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the root container via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function VelocityAfterimagePass({\n  maxDamp = 0.9,\n  cutoff = 0.06,\n  reference = 1.6,\n  satellites = 3,\n  hue = 196,\n  resolutionScale = 1,\n  className,\n}: VelocityAfterimagePassProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.0005 });\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const elapsed = useRef(0);\n  const dampNow = useRef(0);\n  const last = useRef<{ x: number; y: number } | null>(null);\n  const lead = useRef({ x: 0, y: 0 });\n\n  const live = useRef({ maxDamp, cutoff, reference });\n  live.current = { maxDamp, cutoff, reference };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  /* Sizing is a callback rather than effect-body code because it has to be\n     reachable from BOTH effects, which fire on independent triggers. The build\n     effect sizes the render targets itself, but it constructs `contentCamera`\n     at +/-1 and nothing else ever widened it — that lived only in the effect\n     keyed on the observed width and height. So any rebuild without an\n     accompanying size change left the camera square inside a non-square\n     canvas: the marks render as ellipses, and because the raf loop reads the\n     aspect back off `contentCamera.right` the lead mark also tracks the\n     pointer at the wrong horizontal scale, drifting further from the cursor\n     the further it travels. The build effect re-runs on `satellites`, `hue`\n     and `resolutionScale`, so that state persisted until the user happened to\n     resize the window. Everything below is derived purely from w and h, and\n     `WebGLRenderTarget.setSize` no-ops on unchanged dimensions, so calling it\n     from both paths is idempotent. */\n  const fitStage = useCallback(\n    (w: number, h: number) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current || w < 1 || h < 1) return;\n      const dpr = pixelRatio();\n      const aspect = w / h;\n      current.renderer.setPixelRatio(dpr);\n      current.renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n      current.contentCamera.left = -aspect;\n      current.contentCamera.right = aspect;\n      current.contentCamera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n\n      const bufferW = Math.max(2, Math.round(w * dpr * resolutionScale));\n      const bufferH = Math.max(2, Math.round(h * dpr * resolutionScale));\n      for (const target of [current.frame, current.ping, current.pong]) {\n        target.setSize(bufferW, bufferH);\n        // setSize reallocates the texture, so whatever the history held is now\n        // undefined memory. Without an explicit clear, the first frame after a\n        // resize can composite garbage that then decays for a second.\n        current.renderer.setRenderTarget(target);\n        current.renderer.clear();\n      }\n      current.renderer.setRenderTarget(null);\n    },\n    [resolutionScale],\n  );\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: false, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    const dpr = pixelRatio();\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(dpr);\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const bufferW = Math.max(2, Math.round(el.clientWidth * dpr * resolutionScale));\n    const bufferH = Math.max(2, Math.round(el.clientHeight * dpr * resolutionScale));\n\n    // Three targets: one for this frame's marks, two that swap as history.\n    // Reading and writing the same target in one pass is undefined behaviour\n    // in WebGL and shows up as tearing or a black frame depending on driver.\n    const frame = makeTarget(bufferW, bufferH);\n    const ping = makeTarget(bufferW, bufferH);\n    const pong = makeTarget(bufferW, bufferH);\n\n    const sprite = new THREE.CanvasTexture(makeSprite());\n    sprite.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n\n    const content = new THREE.Scene();\n    const contentCamera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 10);\n    contentCamera.position.z = 1;\n\n    const markGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(1, 1);\n    const markMaterials: THREE.MeshBasicMaterial[] = [];\n\n    const makeMark = (size: number, lightness: number, shift: number) => {\n      const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({\n        map: sprite,\n        transparent: true,\n        depthWrite: false,\n        depthTest: false,\n      });\n      material.color.setHSL(((hue + shift) % 360) / 360, 0.72, lightness);\n      markMaterials.push(material);\n      const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(markGeometry, material);\n      mesh.scale.setScalar(size);\n      content.add(mesh);\n      return mesh;\n    };\n\n    const leadMesh = makeMark(LEAD_SIZE, 0.62, 0);\n    const orbiters: THREE.Mesh[] = [];\n    for (let i = 0; i < Math.max(satellites, 0); i++) {\n      orbiters.push(makeMark(SATELLITE_SIZE, 0.5, (i + 1) * 26));\n    }\n\n    // Fullscreen quad, reused by both post passes. PlaneGeometry(2, 2) exactly\n    // fills a -1..1 orthographic frustum with no scaling in between.\n    const quadGeometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2);\n    const quadCamera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1);\n\n    const blendUniforms: BlendUniforms = {\n      uCurrent: { value: frame.texture },\n      uHistory: { value: ping.texture },\n      uDamp: { value: 0 },\n      uCutoff: { value: cutoff },\n    };\n    const blendMaterial = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: velocityAfterimageVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: velocityAfterimageFragmentShader,\n      uniforms: blendUniforms,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      depthTest: false,\n    });\n    const quadScene = new THREE.Scene();\n    quadScene.add(new THREE.Mesh(quadGeometry, blendMaterial));\n\n    // The final blit uses a stock material precisely so three applies its own\n    // output colour-space conversion here, and only here.\n    const screenMaterial = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({\n      map: pong.texture,\n      transparent: true,\n      depthWrite: false,\n      depthTest: false,\n    });\n    const screenScene = new THREE.Scene();\n    screenScene.add(new THREE.Mesh(quadGeometry, screenMaterial));\n\n    const stageValue: Stage = {\n      renderer,\n      content,\n      contentCamera,\n      quadScene,\n      quadCamera,\n      screenScene,\n      frame,\n      ping,\n      pong,\n      blendUniforms,\n      screenMaterial,\n      lead: leadMesh,\n      orbiters,\n      sprite,\n      quadGeometry,\n      markGeometry,\n      blendMaterial,\n      markMaterials,\n    };\n    stage.current = stageValue;\n\n    // Fit before the first frame. `ready` guarantees the observed box is real,\n    // and waiting for the resize effect to widen the camera is a bet that the\n    // box will change size again — on a stage that is built once and never\n    // reflowed, that bet never pays out. The clear this performs is wanted\n    // here too: the three targets are freshly allocated and their contents are\n    // undefined until something writes them.\n    fitStage(width, height);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      disposeObject(content);\n      disposeObject(quadScene);\n      disposeObject(screenScene);\n      sprite.dispose();\n      quadGeometry.dispose();\n      markGeometry.dispose();\n      blendMaterial.dispose();\n      screenMaterial.dispose();\n      for (const material of markMaterials) material.dispose();\n      // Render targets are not part of any scene graph, so nothing walks them.\n      // Each one holds a full-resolution half-float texture and a framebuffer.\n      frame.dispose();\n      ping.dispose();\n      pong.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, satellites, hue, resolutionScale]);\n\n  // Reflow costs a re-fit, never a rebuild. `resolutionScale` stays covered\n  // because `fitStage` closes over it, so a new scale gives a new identity.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fitStage(width, height);\n  }, [fitStage, width, height]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n      const { maxDamp: longest, cutoff: floor, reference: full } = live.current;\n\n      if (!reduced) elapsed.current += dt;\n\n      const aspect = current.contentCamera.right;\n      const targetX = pointer.current.active ? pointer.current.ndcX * aspect : 0;\n      const targetY = pointer.current.active ? pointer.current.ndcY : 0;\n      lead.current.x = damp(lead.current.x, targetX, LEAD_SMOOTHING, dt);\n      lead.current.y = damp(lead.current.y, targetY, LEAD_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      // Speed of the mark itself, not of the raw pointer. The mark is what is\n      // being smeared, so a trail measured from the cursor would start before\n      // the mark moves and keep going after it has stopped.\n      const previous = last.current;\n      const speed =\n        previous && dt > 0\n          ? Math.hypot(lead.current.x - previous.x, lead.current.y - previous.y) / dt\n          : 0;\n      last.current = { x: lead.current.x, y: lead.current.y };\n\n      const wanted = reduced ? 0 : clamp(speed / Math.max(full, 0.0001), 0, 1) * longest;\n      dampNow.current = damp(dampNow.current, wanted, DAMP_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      current.lead.position.set(lead.current.x, lead.current.y, 0);\n      for (let i = 0; i < current.orbiters.length; i++) {\n        const orbiter = current.orbiters[i];\n        if (!orbiter) continue;\n        const phase = (i / current.orbiters.length) * Math.PI * 2 + elapsed.current * ORBIT_SPEED;\n        orbiter.position.set(\n          lead.current.x + Math.cos(phase) * ORBIT_RADIUS,\n          lead.current.y + Math.sin(phase) * ORBIT_RADIUS,\n          0,\n        );\n      }\n\n      // Pass 1 — this frame's marks alone, into their own target.\n      current.renderer.setRenderTarget(current.frame);\n      current.renderer.clear();\n      current.renderer.render(current.content, current.contentCamera);\n\n      // Pass 2 — decayed history plus this frame, into the target that is not\n      // currently the history. Reading and writing one target in a single pass\n      // is undefined in WebGL, which is the entire reason for the swap.\n      current.blendUniforms.uCurrent.value = current.frame.texture;\n      current.blendUniforms.uHistory.value = current.ping.texture;\n      current.blendUniforms.uDamp.value = dampNow.current;\n      current.blendUniforms.uCutoff.value = floor;\n      current.renderer.setRenderTarget(current.pong);\n      current.renderer.render(current.quadScene, current.quadCamera);\n\n      // Pass 3 — the accumulation to the canvas, through a stock material so\n      // three performs output colour conversion exactly once.\n      current.renderer.setRenderTarget(null);\n      current.screenMaterial.map = current.pong.texture;\n      current.renderer.render(current.screenScene, current.quadCamera);\n\n      // Swap. The target just written becomes next frame's history.\n      const swap = current.ping;\n      current.ping = current.pong;\n      current.pong = swap;\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Nulls the stage handle first so an in-flight frame cannot touch a half-disposed renderer, then `disposeObject` on all three scenes, disposes the sprite texture, both geometries, the blend, screen and every mark material, then explicitly disposes `frame`, `ping` and `pong` — no scene graph reaches them — then `disposeRenderer` (which forces context loss, not just `dispose()`) and removes the canvas element. The rAF subscription is released by `useRaf`.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The orbit clock is not advanced and the decay factor is pinned to 0, so no accumulation survives a frame: the panel shows the crisp mark cluster with the satellites holding their starting angles and no tail behind them, however fast the pointer moves. The marks still follow the pointer, since that is direct response to input rather than self-driven motion.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Blending a target into itself. It is the obvious shape — one history target, read it and write it — and it is undefined behaviour in WebGL: depending on driver you get tearing, a black frame, or a result that looks right on your machine and fails on someone else's.\n- Leaving the targets at the default `UnsignedByteType`. Repeated multiplication by 0.9 on 8 bits per channel quantises the tail into visible stair-stepped bands, and the banding is worst on exactly the slow gradients the effect is made of.\n- Using `LinearFilter` on the ping-pong pair. Each pass smears the accumulation by a fraction of a texel, and a pixel survives hundreds of passes, so the trail visibly fattens and drifts diagonally away from the path the pointer actually took.\n- Decaying with no floor. `history *= 0.9` is a geometric series that never reaches zero, so a permanent ghost of the first frames stays welded into the buffer and the panel is never clean again. The `step(uCutoff, history.a)` factor cuts it instead.\n- Compositing with `mix()` or additive blending instead of `max()`. `mix` dims the current frame in proportion to the trail behind it, so the head goes dark exactly when it is moving fastest; additive blows out to white wherever the path crosses itself.\n- Applying output colour conversion in the blend pass. Its result goes into another render target that stays in linear working space, so converting there and again on the final blit brightens the accumulation on every pass until the whole panel is white within a second.\n- Calling `setSize` on the targets after a resize and rendering straight away. `setSize` reallocates the texture, so the history now points at undefined memory, and the first composited frame is garbage that then decays for a full second.\n- Deriving the trail from raw pointer speed. The mark is what is being smeared, and it lags the cursor — so a cursor-driven trail starts before the mark moves and keeps growing after it has stopped.\n- Smoothing the decay factor with a per-frame constant (`d += (target - d) * 0.1`). That converges twice as fast at 120Hz, so the same hand speed produces a visibly shorter trail on a high-refresh display.\n- Disposing the scene graph and stopping there. Render targets belong to no scene, so nothing walks them — each holds a full-resolution half-float texture plus a framebuffer, and three of them at DPR 2 is tens of megabytes retained per unmount.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Guarded with `hasWebGL()`: where a context cannot be acquired the component renders a single static radial gradient at the configured hue instead of an empty canvas.\n- The loop is gated on `useInView` plus `useVisibility`, so an off-screen or backgrounded instance costs no GPU time at all — which matters more here than elsewhere, because this effect pays for three full-resolution passes per frame.\n- `resolutionScale` below 1 trades tail sharpness for fill rate. Because the trail is low-frequency by nature, 0.6 is close to free visually and cuts the post-processing cost to roughly a third.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nMove the pointer slowly across the panel and the marks stay crisp with no tail. Flick across it and a smooth curved tail follows the cluster, fading to nothing rather than to a faint permanent ghost. Stop moving and the tail retracts over about a third of a second, leaving the surface clean. Resize the container mid-gesture and no block of garbage appears. Scroll the panel out of view and GPU work stops; unmount and the WebGL context count in about:gpu drops.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "velocity-deform-cards": "# Build: Velocity-Deformed Cards\n\nScroll position says where the page is; scroll velocity says how hard the user is pushing it, and that is the input that gives a list a material. Cards skew and stretch along the direction of travel, trail behind by an amount proportional to speed, and ease back to rest when you stop — the ease-out being a property of the filter rather than a keyframe.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nCards skew, stretch and trail in proportion to scroll speed, then settle on their own with no easing curve anywhere.\n\n- Category: scroll\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Velocity comes from `useScrollVelocity`, which filters twice: a one-pole low-pass on the raw per-frame delta so wheel steps stop ringing, then a damped approach toward that filtered value. The second stage is what produces the settle — a step change in speed leaves the filter as a curve, so no easing is ever authored.\n2. Both stages use `damp(current, target, smoothing, dt)`, where `smoothing` is the fraction of the gap left after one second. The deformation therefore looks identical on a 60 Hz laptop and a 144 Hz monitor, which the per-frame form (`v += (target - v) * 0.1`) does not.\n3. The normalised velocity drives four values per card: trailing translate, skew, stretch along the travel axis, and a squash across it at half the stretch, so the card roughly preserves its area. Without the squash the deformation reads as a scaling bug rather than as inertia.\n4. Each card scales its share by a `data-deform` depth. Identical depths make the grid move as one rigid sheet; varied depths make it bend, which is the entire difference between \"the page is skewing\" and \"these cards have weight\".\n5. Every transform is composed into a string, rounded, and compared with what was written last frame. A stationary list therefore performs zero style writes — at rest all cards produce the same string, and the loop costs one comparison each.\n6. Card elements are collected once per `children` change instead of per frame. A `querySelectorAll` inside a rAF loop is a forced style recalculation 60 times a second for a list that changed once.\n7. Only `transform` is animated, so the work stays on the compositor. Velocity is held in a ref and never enters React state — a value that changes every frame must not cause a render.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `maxSkew = 5`\n  Degrees at full velocity. Around 5 the cards read as flexing; past ~10 the text inside becomes visibly sheared and the effect turns into a distraction the user actively fights.\n- `maxLag = 28`\n  Pixels a card trails at full velocity. Enough to open a visible gap against the container edge, small enough that a card never appears to belong to a different row.\n- `maxStretch = 0.14`\n  Fractional stretch along the travel axis. Fourteen percent is at the edge of what reads as elastic rather than broken; the cross-axis squash is half of it, which keeps the area roughly constant.\n- `reference = 1400`\n  Pixels per second that count as full deflection. A firm trackpad flick peaks around 1200-1800 px/s, so a normal gesture uses most of the range without a hard flick pinning it.\n- `smoothing = 0.0002`\n  Fraction of the velocity gap left after one second — a settle of roughly a third of a second. Faster and the cards twitch on every wheel step; slower and they keep moving after the scrollbar has visibly stopped, which reads as lag rather than as weight.\n- `per-card depth = 0.55 – 1`\n  The spread used in the demo. Below about 0.4 a card barely participates and looks broken next to its neighbours; a uniform 1 removes the bend that makes the grid feel like fabric.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction VelocityDeformCards(props: { children: ReactNode; maxSkew?: number; maxLag?: number; maxStretch?: number; reference?: number; smoothing?: number; scroller?: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  The grid contents. Elements carrying `data-deform` are the ones deformed.\n- `maxSkew: number` — default `5`\n  Degrees of skew at full velocity.\n- `maxLag: number` — default `28`\n  Pixels a card trails at full velocity.\n- `maxStretch: number` — default `0.14`\n  Stretch along the travel axis at full velocity.\n- `reference: number` — default `1400`\n  Velocity in px/s that counts as full deflection.\n- `smoothing: number` — default `0.0002`\n  Fraction of the velocity gap left after one second.\n- `scroller: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>`\n  Scroll container. Omit to auto-detect.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper — the grid definition usually lives here.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function VelocityDeformCards({\n  children,\n  maxSkew = 5,\n  maxLag = 28,\n  maxStretch = 0.14,\n  reference = 1400,\n  smoothing = 0.0002,\n  scroller,\n  className,\n}: VelocityDeformCardsProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const cards = useRef<HTMLElement[]>([]);\n  const written = useRef<string[]>([]);\n  const lastVelocity = useRef('');\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  // The hook wants an element, not a ref, so the container is resolved once\n  // after mount — `document.documentElement` is the window-scroll case and is\n  // read through `scrollTop` there too, which is correct on every engine.\n  const [host, setHost] = useState<HTMLElement | null>(null);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl) return;\n    setHost(scroller?.current ?? nearestScroller(rootEl));\n  }, [scroller]);\n\n  const velocity = useScrollVelocity({\n    reference,\n    smoothing,\n    // `host` is null until the effect above runs, which means it is null for\n    // the whole of the server render — and null is already the answer there.\n    // Short-circuiting on it is what keeps `document` out of the render path:\n    // reading it unguarded threw a ReferenceError on the server, and a client\n    // component still renders there.\n    target: host === null || host === document.documentElement ? null : host,\n  });\n\n  // Cached rather than queried per frame: a `querySelectorAll` inside the rAF\n  // loop is a forced style recalc 60 times a second for a list that changed\n  // once.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl) return;\n    cards.current = Array.from(rootEl.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-deform]'));\n    written.current = cards.current.map(() => '');\n  }, [children]);\n\n  useRaf((_dt) => {\n    const rootEl = root.current;\n    if (!rootEl) return;\n\n    if (reduced) {\n      // One pass to clear, then nothing. The guard below keeps it to one pass.\n      for (let i = 0; i < cards.current.length; i += 1) {\n        if (written.current[i] === '') continue;\n        written.current[i] = '';\n        const card = cards.current[i];\n        if (card) card.style.transform = '';\n      }\n      return;\n    }\n\n    // -1..1, already low-passed twice. Positive is scrolling down.\n    const v = velocity.current.normalized;\n    const magnitude = Math.abs(v);\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < cards.current.length; i += 1) {\n      const card = cards.current[i];\n      if (!card) continue;\n\n      // Per-card depth is the whole effect: identical values make the list\n      // move as one rigid sheet, varied values make it bend like cloth.\n      const raw = Number.parseFloat(card.dataset.deform ?? '');\n      const depth = Number.isFinite(raw) ? raw : 1;\n\n      const lag = -v * maxLag * depth;\n      const skew = -v * maxSkew * depth;\n      const stretch = 1 + magnitude * maxStretch * depth;\n      // Squash across the travel axis by half the stretch: the card keeps\n      // roughly its area, which is what stops the deformation reading as a\n      // scaling bug.\n      const squash = 1 - magnitude * maxStretch * depth * 0.5;\n\n      const transform =\n        `translate3d(0, ${lag.toFixed(2)}px, 0) ` +\n        `skewY(${skew.toFixed(2)}deg) ` +\n        `scale(${squash.toFixed(4)}, ${stretch.toFixed(4)})`;\n\n      // At rest every card produces the same string as last frame, so an idle\n      // list costs one comparison per card and zero style invalidations.\n      if (written.current[i] === transform) continue;\n      written.current[i] = transform;\n      card.style.transform = transform;\n    }\n\n    // Published for anything that would rather react in CSS than in JS —\n    // guarded, so a stationary list stops writing to the DOM entirely.\n    const text = v.toFixed(3);\n    if (text !== lastVelocity.current) {\n      lastVelocity.current = text;\n      rootEl.style.setProperty('--scroll-velocity', text);\n    }\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useRaf` unsubscribes from the shared loop on unmount, and `useScrollVelocity` does the same. There are no listeners, observers or timers to remove; the only state is refs and one element resolved after mount, and every transform is written on nodes React owns.\n\n**Reduced motion.** When `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` matches, one pass clears every transform and the loop then writes nothing at all. The cards keep their layout, spacing and hover states, so the grid is intact — it simply does not flex. Toggling the OS setting takes effect on the next frame without a reload.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Feeding raw `scrollY` deltas straight into the transform. A trackpad emits bursts and a mouse wheel emits steps, so the cards strobe instead of flexing — the two filter stages exist precisely for this.\n- Smoothing with a per-frame constant. The settle is more than twice as fast on a 144 Hz display, and every tuning pass that fixes one refresh rate breaks the other.\n- Stretching without squashing. The cards visibly gain area at speed, which the eye reads as a rendering fault rather than as elasticity.\n- Giving every card the same depth. The whole grid then skews as one plane, which looks like the viewport is broken rather than like the cards have mass.\n- Putting velocity in React state. That is a re-render per frame, and the reconciliation cost alone is enough to lower the frame rate that the velocity is measured against — the effect degrades itself.\n- Writing transforms unconditionally. Idle frames still cost a style invalidation and a composite for each card, on a list where nothing moved.\n- Querying the cards inside the loop. `querySelectorAll` per frame forces style resolution 60 times a second for a list whose contents changed once.\n- Animating `top`, `margin` or `height` for the trail instead of `transform`. Every frame relayouts the whole grid, and the deformation becomes a slideshow on any laptop.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The scroll container is auto-detected by walking ancestors for a scrollable `overflow-y`; pass `scroller` when it is ambiguous. `useScrollVelocity` takes an element rather than a ref, so the container is resolved once after mount.\n- The normalised velocity is also published as `--scroll-velocity` on the root, so a border colour or a blur can react in CSS without another JS subscriber.\n- Set `will-change: transform` on the cards, not on the container: the container never moves, and promoting a large scrolling element wastes memory for nothing.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nA fast flick skews and stretches the cards in the direction of travel by different amounts per column, and they settle within about a third of a second after the scroll stops, with no overshoot. Text stays readable throughout. At rest, a paint-flashing or style-recalc profile shows no per-frame work. The settle takes the same wall-clock time at 60 and 144 Hz. With reduced motion on, nothing deforms.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "vertex-cylinder-bend": "# Build: Vertex Shader Cylinder Bend\n\nThe bend on its own, with no gallery attached. A flat `PlaneGeometry` is fed to a vertex shader that reads each vertex's x coordinate as an arc parameter and re-emits it on the surface of a cylinder. Because curvature is a uniform rather than a mesh, the same buffer is flat, bowed or nearly closed between two frames — no `CylinderGeometry`, no rebuild, no re-upload.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA flat plane wrapped around a cylinder on the GPU, with the curvature living in a uniform you can animate.\n\n- Category: webgl\n- Runtime cost: webgl\n- Dependencies: three@^0.185.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Curl is defined as radians of bend per world unit of plane width, so the radius is its reciprocal: `radius = 1.0 / curl`. A 3-unit plane at curl 0.62 wraps 1.86 rad of a circle of radius 1.61, and the arc length is still exactly 3 units — the banner bends without appearing to shrink.\n2. `angle = uSpin + position.x * curl`, then `pos.x = radius * sin(angle)` and `pos.z = radius * cos(angle) - radius`. The trailing `- radius` pins the arc's midpoint at z = 0 so tightening the curl bends the ends away instead of lunging the whole banner at the camera.\n3. The vertical bow is `pos.y = sin(position.y * k) / k`, which tends to `position.y` as k tends to 0 and becomes a full sine bow at k = 1. Normalising by k is what keeps the plane the same height at every bow depth.\n4. The surface normal is available in closed form: differentiating the position gives tangents `(cos a, 0, -sin a)` and `(0, 1, 0)`, whose cross product is `(sin a, 0, cos a)`. Its z component — `cos(angle)` — is how squarely the patch faces the camera, so the shading term costs one cosine and no `normalMatrix`.\n5. On resize the camera is moved rather than the plane resized: `distance = max(width / fit / (2 tan(fov/2) aspect), height / fit / (2 tan(fov/2)))`. The plane's world width is the yardstick `curl` is measured in, so it must never change with the viewport.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `PLANE_WIDTH / PLANE_HEIGHT = 3 / 1.4`\n  Fixed world units on purpose. Curl is radians per world unit, so a plane that resized with the viewport would bend through a different total angle on every device — the same `curl` prop would mean something different on a phone.\n- `curl = 0.62`\n  Times the 3-unit width, this is 1.86 rad — about 106 degrees of arc. Under ~0.25 the bend is indistinguishable from a perspective skew; over ~1.6 the far edges pass 90 degrees from the camera, present zero pixels of width, and the banner appears to have been cut off.\n- `curlSwing = 0.22`\n  Amplitude of the idle breathing. Large enough that the curvature is visibly a live value rather than a baked mesh; large enough to pass 1.6 total and the ends would clip away at the peak of every cycle.\n- `barrel = 0.3`\n  k in `sin(k*y)/k`. At 0 the top and bottom edges are dead straight and the surface reads as a rolled poster; past ~1.2 the bow overtakes the curl and the plane looks like a saddle rather than a cylinder.\n- `segments = 96`\n  Subdivisions across the bend — the resolution of the curve, since it is evaluated per vertex. Below ~24 the arc becomes a visible polyline and the shading term steps in bands; above ~192 the extra vertices land inside a single pixel.\n- `PlaneGeometry y-segments = 16`\n  The cylinder bend is a function of x alone, so rows would be pure waste — except the barrel bow, which needs enough rows to curve rather than kink at the midline.\n- `FIT = 0.86`\n  Fraction of the frustum the banner fills. At 1.0 the receding edges touch the viewport border and the curl has nowhere to read against; below ~0.7 the component is mostly empty space.\n- `shade = 0.55`\n  How far the facing term is allowed to darken the edges. At 0 the bend reads as a flat trapezoid — a perspective mistake rather than a curve. At 1 the edges go fully black and the banner appears to end early.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction VertexCylinderBend(props: { curl?: number; curlSwing?: number; parallax?: number; barrel?: number; shade?: number; segments?: number; period?: number; paint?: (ctx: CanvasRenderingContext2D, size: { width: number; height: number }) => void; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `curl: number` — default `0.62`\n  Radians of bend per world unit of plane width. 0 is flat.\n- `curlSwing: number` — default `0.22`\n  Peak extra curl added by the idle breathing cycle.\n- `parallax: number` — default `0.34`\n  Radians the banner rocks around the drum axis at full pointer travel.\n- `barrel: number` — default `0.3`\n  Vertical bow. 0 leaves the edges straight, ~1 is a full sine.\n- `shade: number` — default `0.55`\n  How far the facing term darkens the receding edges.\n- `segments: number` — default `96`\n  Subdivisions across the bend. This is the resolution of the curve.\n- `period: number` — default `9`\n  Seconds per breathing cycle.\n- `paint: (ctx, size) => void`\n  Override the banner face. Receives a 2D context for a 1024x512 canvas.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the wrapper via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function VertexCylinderBend({\n  curl = 0.62,\n  curlSwing = 0.22,\n  parallax = 0.34,\n  barrel = 0.3,\n  shade = 0.55,\n  segments = 96,\n  period = 9,\n  paint,\n  className,\n}: VertexCylinderBendProps) {\n  const host = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const stage = useRef<Stage | null>(null);\n\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(host);\n  const { inView } = useInView(host, { threshold: 0 });\n  const visible = useVisibility();\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const pointer = useSmoothPointer({ target: host, smoothing: 0.002 });\n\n  const [supported, setSupported] = useState(true);\n  useEffect(() => setSupported(hasWebGL()), []);\n\n  const elapsed = useRef(0);\n  const spin = useRef(0);\n  const curlNow = useRef(curl);\n\n  const live = useRef({ curl, curlSwing, parallax, barrel, shade, period });\n  live.current = { curl, curlSwing, parallax, barrel, shade, period };\n\n  const ready = width > 1 && height > 1 && supported;\n\n  /* Fitting the camera is a callback rather than effect-body code because it\n     has to be reachable from BOTH effects, which fire on independent triggers.\n     The camera is constructed with a placeholder aspect of 1 and the default\n     position z of 0 — inside the plane — and everything that corrects that\n     lived only in the effect keyed on the observed width and height. So any\n     rebuild without an accompanying size change left a camera sitting in the\n     middle of its own geometry: the build effect re-runs on `segments` and\n     `paint`, and a caller passing an inline `paint` arrow rebuilds the stage\n     on every parent render, at which point the banner stayed broken until the\n     user happened to resize the window. Everything below is derived purely\n     from w and h, so calling it from both paths is idempotent. */\n  const fitCamera = useCallback((w: number, h: number) => {\n    const current = stage.current;\n    if (!current || w < 1 || h < 1) return;\n    const aspect = w / h;\n    current.renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    current.renderer.setSize(w, h, false);\n    current.camera.aspect = aspect;\n\n    // Contain-fit by moving the camera, not by resizing the plane: the plane's\n    // world width is what `curl` is measured against, so it has to stay fixed.\n    // Take the larger of the two required distances or the banner is cropped\n    // on whichever axis is tighter.\n    const tanHalf = Math.tan((CAMERA_FOV * Math.PI) / 360);\n    const forWidth = PLANE_WIDTH / FIT / (2 * tanHalf * aspect);\n    const forHeight = PLANE_HEIGHT / FIT / (2 * tanHalf);\n    current.camera.position.z = Math.max(forWidth, forHeight);\n    current.camera.updateProjectionMatrix();\n  }, []);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = host.current;\n    if (!el || !ready) return;\n\n    let renderer: THREE.WebGLRenderer;\n    try {\n      renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });\n    } catch {\n      // A context can still be refused between the probe and the construction —\n      // the tab may have hit the browser's live-context ceiling in between. three\n      // throws rather than returning null, and an uncaught throw here takes the\n      // page with it.\n      setSupported(false);\n      return;\n    }\n    renderer.setPixelRatio(pixelRatio());\n    renderer.setSize(el.clientWidth, el.clientHeight, false);\n    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);\n    renderer.domElement.style.display = 'block';\n    renderer.domElement.style.width = '100%';\n    renderer.domElement.style.height = '100%';\n    el.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n\n    const scene = new THREE.Scene();\n    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(CAMERA_FOV, 1, 0.1, 100);\n\n    const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');\n    canvas.width = TEXTURE_WIDTH;\n    canvas.height = TEXTURE_HEIGHT;\n    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');\n    if (ctx) (paint ?? paintBanner)(ctx, { width: TEXTURE_WIDTH, height: TEXTURE_HEIGHT });\n\n    const texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas);\n    texture.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;\n    texture.anisotropy = Math.min(4, renderer.capabilities.getMaxAnisotropy());\n\n    const uniforms: BendUniforms = {\n      uTexture: { value: texture },\n      uCurl: { value: curl },\n      uSpin: { value: 0 },\n      uBarrel: { value: barrel },\n      uShade: { value: shade },\n    };\n\n    // Segments only along x. The bend is a function of x alone, so vertical\n    // subdivision buys nothing but vertices — except the barrel bow, which\n    // needs a handful of rows to curve at all.\n    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(PLANE_WIDTH, PLANE_HEIGHT, segments, 16);\n    const material = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({\n      vertexShader: vertexCylinderBendVertexShader,\n      fragmentShader: vertexCylinderBendFragmentShader,\n      uniforms,\n      transparent: true,\n      // The bend turns the far edges away from the camera, so without\n      // DoubleSide a hard curl punches holes through the ends of the banner.\n      side: THREE.DoubleSide,\n    });\n\n    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);\n    // The CPU bounding sphere describes a flat plane that the shader has\n    // already moved. Culling against it blanks the banner at shallow angles.\n    mesh.frustumCulled = false;\n    scene.add(mesh);\n\n    stage.current = { renderer, scene, camera, geometry, material, texture, uniforms };\n\n    // Fit before the first frame. `ready` guarantees the observed box is real,\n    // and waiting for the resize effect to do it is a bet that the box will\n    // change size again — on a stage that is built once and never reflowed,\n    // that bet never pays out and the first frame is also the last wrong one.\n    fitCamera(width, height);\n\n    return () => {\n      stage.current = null;\n      scene.remove(mesh);\n      // The texture is inside `uniforms`, where disposeObject cannot see it.\n      texture.dispose();\n      disposeObject(scene);\n      geometry.dispose();\n      material.dispose();\n      disposeRenderer(renderer);\n      renderer.domElement.remove();\n    };\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, [ready, segments, paint]);\n\n  // Reflow costs a re-fit, never a rebuild. The guard against a stage that\n  // does not exist yet now lives inside `fitCamera`.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    fitCamera(width, height);\n  }, [fitCamera, width, height]);\n\n  useRaf(\n    (dt) => {\n      const current = stage.current;\n      if (!current) return;\n      const { curl: base, curlSwing: swing, parallax: sway, barrel: bow, shade: dark, period: cycle } =\n        live.current;\n\n      elapsed.current += dt;\n\n      // Breathing curl. The whole reason the bend lives in a uniform is that\n      // it can move; holding it at one value hides the technique.\n      const target = reduced ? base : base + swing * Math.sin((elapsed.current * Math.PI * 2) / Math.max(cycle, 0.5));\n      curlNow.current = damp(curlNow.current, target, CURL_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      // Pointer rocks the banner around the drum axis. This survives reduced\n      // motion because it is a direct response to input, not autonomous motion.\n      // ndcX, not nx: ndc is -1..1 about the centre, so the banner rocks\n      // symmetrically. nx is 0..1, which would bias every rock to one side.\n      const aim = pointer.current.active ? pointer.current.ndcX * sway : 0;\n      spin.current = damp(spin.current, aim, SPIN_SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n      current.uniforms.uCurl.value = curlNow.current;\n      current.uniforms.uSpin.value = spin.current;\n      current.uniforms.uBarrel.value = bow;\n      current.uniforms.uShade.value = dark;\n      current.renderer.render(current.scene, current.camera);\n    },\n    ready && inView && visible,\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the mesh from the scene, disposes the `CanvasTexture` explicitly (it lives inside `material.uniforms`, out of `disposeObject`'s reach), runs `disposeObject` over the scene, disposes the geometry and material, then `disposeRenderer` for `setAnimationLoop(null)` / `dispose()` / `forceContextLoss()`, and removes the canvas from the DOM. The stage ref is nulled first, so any frame already queued returns immediately instead of touching a lost context.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The breathing cycle is dropped: `uCurl` holds at the `curl` prop and never oscillates, so nothing moves on its own. The pointer rock is kept, because it is a direct one-to-one response to the user's own input rather than autonomous motion, and removing it would leave a component that ignores the cursor entirely.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Dividing by `curl` without a guard. The flat case is curl 0, which is an infinite radius: `1.0 / 0.0` in GLSL yields infinity, every vertex becomes NaN, and the mesh vanishes with no error anywhere. The shader clamps to 0.0001 and approaches flat instead.\n- Omitting the `- radius` term from `pos.z`. The arc is then centred on the cylinder's axis rather than on the origin, so raising the curl walks the entire banner toward the camera and it appears to lunge every time the curvature changes.\n- Bowing with a bare `sin(position.y)`. That silently rescales the plane — a 1.4-tall plane becomes 1.29 tall — so animating the bow makes the banner pulse in size. Normalising by k (`sin(k*y)/k`) keeps the height fixed at every depth.\n- Leaving `frustumCulled` on. The bounding sphere describes the flat plane the shader has already moved, so at a hard curl three decides the banner is off screen and blanks it while it is plainly in view.\n- Keeping the default `FrontSide`. The bend turns the far edges past 90 degrees from the camera, and single-sided faces punch clean holes through the ends of the banner at high curl.\n- Resizing the plane geometry to fit the container. `curl` is radians per world unit, so a viewport-sized plane bends through a different total angle on every device and the prop stops meaning anything. Move the camera instead — and fit on the larger of the two required distances, or the banner is cropped on whichever axis is tighter.\n- Texturing it with a smooth gradient. The bend is then almost invisible, because there is no structure for the foreshortening to compress. Evenly spaced vertical rules bunch toward the receding edges and make the curvature legible.\n- Calling `renderer.dispose()` and stopping there. That frees GPU objects but keeps the WebGL context; Chromium caps a renderer process at roughly 16 and silently kills the oldest. `disposeRenderer` calls `forceContextLoss`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This is the primitive that `cylinder-gallery` instantiates nineteen times. Isolated here so the arc maths can be read without the layout, focus and picking code around it.\n- The normal is derived analytically rather than by recomputing vertex normals on the CPU, so `geometry.computeVertexNormals()` is never called and the buffer stays untouched after upload.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe banner is visibly a section of a cylinder, not a skewed rectangle: the vertical rules bunch together toward both ends and the ends are darker than the middle. Over about nine seconds the curvature tightens and relaxes and the banner never changes size while it does. Move the cursor left and right and the whole arc rocks around its axis, following with a slight lag. Resize the container from wide to narrow and the banner stays fully in frame with the same amount of bend. With `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the curvature is frozen but the cursor rock still works.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "vitals-chart-card": "# Build: Vitals Chart Card\n\nOne vital sign over a short period, drawn like the medical profile's steps card: a column per reading, each a full-height grey track filled from the bottom. A dashed band marks the normal range, readings outside it turn pink, and an inner tile under the chart spells the range out next to an in-range count. Hovering, focusing or selecting a column swaps the headline for that reading; a \"range\" variant floats each bar from a low to a high value for blood pressure.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA weekly vital-sign card: track-and-fill columns, a dashed normal-range band, pink out-of-range bars and a period stepper.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The value axis is `domain` or, when omitted, the min and max of every value, low and normal-range bound padded by 25% of the spread. `pct(v)` maps a value into 0-100% of the plot height and clamps it.\n2. Each reading is a `<button>` track (`bg-chart-track`, 10px radius) in a CSS grid of `repeat(n, minmax(0, 1fr))` columns, positioned `absolute inset-0` inside the flex-1 plot box. Its fill is an absolutely positioned span with `bottom` 0 (bars) or `pct(low)` (range) and `height` `pct(value) - bottom`, so a new period animates through a 500ms CSS transition of `height` and `bottom`.\n3. `vitalStatus` compares `value` with `normalRange` (and, when `value` is normal, `low` with `lowNormalRange`) and returns low, normal or high. Normal fills use the `color` prop's chart token, anything else uses `bg-chart-3`, and the dashed `bg-chart-7/10` band (one per range) is laid over the tracks with the same `pct` mapping.\n4. The active column is the hovered one, else the selected one. Clicking toggles selection (controlled `selectedIndex` or uncontrolled `defaultSelectedIndex`), other fills dim to 40%, and the headline re-keys so the kit `animate-number-fade` replays with that day's value, detail label and a Normal, High or Low chip.\n5. Columns use a roving tabindex: Arrow keys, Home and End move focus and preview the reading (a focus that matches `:focus-visible` previews, a mouse focus does not), Enter or Space toggles it, and Escape clears the selection.\n6. With no active column the headline shows the `summary` (average, latest, max or min; range readings print as value/low) and the chip reads \"n/total in range\", lime when every reading is normal and orange otherwise. The optional `period` prop renders a bordered stepper whose arrows call `onPrevious` and `onNext`.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `card min height = 330px`\n  Matches the medical profile row of cards, so it lines up with Patient Info Card.\n- `domain padding = 25% of the data spread`\n  Keeps the tallest bar and the normal band clear of the top and bottom of the track.\n- `fill transition = 500ms ease-out`\n  Long enough to read the change when stepping periods, short enough not to delay hovering.\n- `dimmed fill opacity = 40%`\n  Keeps the other readings visible while the active one is read.\n- `track radius = 10px`\n  The rounded column shape of the steps chart.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nexport function VitalsChartCard({ title, unit, icon, readings, normalRange, lowNormalRange, domain, variant = \"bars\", color = \"teal\", summary = \"average\", decimals = 0, period, selectedIndex, defaultSelectedIndex = null, onSelectedIndexChange, className }: VitalsChartCardProps): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `title: string`\n  Card label, e.g. \"Heart rate\".\n- `unit: string`\n  Unit printed after the headline and in the range hint.\n- `icon: ComponentType`\n  Optional icon before the title.\n- `readings: VitalReading[]`\n  One column per reading: label, optional detail, value and optional low.\n- `normalRange: readonly [number, number]`\n  Inclusive normal range for value; drawn as the dashed band.\n- `lowNormalRange: readonly [number, number]`\n  Inclusive normal range for low in the range variant; draws a second band.\n- `domain: readonly [number, number]`\n  Value axis floor and ceiling; derived from the data when omitted.\n- `variant: \"bars\" | \"range\"` — default `\"bars\"`\n  Fill from the floor, or float from low to value.\n- `color: \"teal\" | \"lime\" | \"sky\" | \"purple\" | \"blue\" | \"emerald\" | \"indigo\"` — default `\"teal\"`\n  Chart token for in-range fills.\n- `summary: \"average\" | \"latest\" | \"max\" | \"min\"` — default `\"average\"`\n  Headline when no column is active.\n- `decimals: number` — default `0`\n  Fraction digits for printed values.\n- `period: VitalsPeriod`\n  Label plus onPrevious/onNext and disabled flags for the header stepper.\n- `selectedIndex: number | null`\n  Controlled selected column.\n- `defaultSelectedIndex: number | null` — default `null`\n  Initial selected column when uncontrolled.\n- `onSelectedIndexChange: (index: number | null) => void`\n  Called when a column is selected or cleared.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the card.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function VitalsChartCard({\n  title,\n  unit,\n  icon: Icon,\n  readings,\n  normalRange,\n  lowNormalRange,\n  domain,\n  variant = \"bars\",\n  color = \"teal\",\n  summary = \"average\",\n  decimals = 0,\n  period,\n  selectedIndex,\n  defaultSelectedIndex = null,\n  onSelectedIndexChange,\n  className,\n}: VitalsChartCardProps) {\n  const [innerSelected, setInnerSelected] = useState<number | null>(defaultSelectedIndex);\n  const [hoverIndex, setHoverIndex] = useState<number | null>(null);\n  const [focusIndex, setFocusIndex] = useState(Math.max(0, readings.length - 1));\n  const barRefs = useRef<(HTMLButtonElement | null)[]>([]);\n\n  const isRange = variant === \"range\";\n  const selectedRaw = selectedIndex !== undefined ? selectedIndex : innerSelected;\n  const selected = selectedRaw !== null && selectedRaw < readings.length ? selectedRaw : null;\n  const hovered = hoverIndex !== null && hoverIndex < readings.length ? hoverIndex : null;\n  const activeIndex = hovered ?? selected;\n  const active = activeIndex !== null ? readings[activeIndex] : undefined;\n  const rovingIndex = Math.min(focusIndex, Math.max(0, readings.length - 1));\n\n  const select = (index: number | null) => {\n    if (selectedIndex === undefined) setInnerSelected(index);\n    onSelectedIndexChange?.(index);\n  };\n\n  const [d0, d1] = domain ?? autoDomain(readings, normalRange, lowNormalRange);\n  const span = d1 - d0 || 1;\n  const pct = (v: number) => clampPct(((v - d0) / span) * 100);\n\n  const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\", {\n    minimumFractionDigits: decimals,\n    maximumFractionDigits: decimals,\n  });\n  const print = (r: Pick<VitalReading, \"value\" | \"low\">) =>\n    isRange && r.low !== undefined ? `${fmt.format(r.value)}/${fmt.format(r.low)}` : fmt.format(r.value);\n\n  // Headline when nothing is active: the summary of the whole period.\n  let summaryReading: Pick<VitalReading, \"value\" | \"low\"> | undefined;\n  if (readings.length > 0) {\n    if (summary === \"latest\") summaryReading = readings[readings.length - 1];\n    else if (summary === \"max\") summaryReading = readings.reduce((a, b) => (b.value > a.value ? b : a));\n    else if (summary === \"min\") summaryReading = readings.reduce((a, b) => (b.value < a.value ? b : a));\n    else {\n      const mean = (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((s, x) => s + x, 0) / xs.length;\n      const lows = readings.flatMap((r) => (r.low === undefined ? [] : [r.low]));\n      summaryReading = {\n        value: mean(readings.map((r) => r.value)),\n        low: lows.length > 0 ? mean(lows) : undefined,\n      };\n    }\n  }\n\n  const statuses = readings.map((r) => vitalStatus(r, normalRange, lowNormalRange));\n  const inRange = statuses.filter((s) => s === \"normal\").length;\n  const activeStatus = activeIndex !== null ? statuses[activeIndex] : undefined;\n  const chip = activeStatus\n    ? STATUS_CHIP[activeStatus]\n    : {\n        label: `${inRange}/${readings.length} in range`,\n        color: inRange === readings.length ? (\"lime\" as const) : (\"orange\" as const),\n      };\n\n  const headline = active ?? summaryReading;\n  const caption = active\n    ? (active.detail ?? active.label)\n    : `${SUMMARY_LABEL[summary]}${period ? ` · ${period.label}` : \"\"}`;\n\n  const rangeText = (r: readonly [number, number]) => `${fmt.format(r[0])}–${fmt.format(r[1])}`;\n  const normalText =\n    isRange && lowNormalRange\n      ? `${rangeText(normalRange)}/${rangeText(lowNormalRange)}`\n      : `${rangeText(normalRange)} ${unit}`;\n\n  const bands = [normalRange, ...(isRange && lowNormalRange ? [lowNormalRange] : [])];\n\n  const moveFocus = (index: number) => {\n    const next = (index + readings.length) % readings.length;\n    setFocusIndex(next);\n    setHoverIndex(next);\n    barRefs.current[next]?.focus();\n  };\n\n  const onBarKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLButtonElement>, index: number) => {\n    if (event.key === \"ArrowRight\" || event.key === \"ArrowUp\") moveFocus(index + 1);\n    else if (event.key === \"ArrowLeft\" || event.key === \"ArrowDown\") moveFocus(index - 1);\n    else if (event.key === \"Home\") moveFocus(0);\n    else if (event.key === \"End\") moveFocus(readings.length - 1);\n    else if (event.key === \"Escape\" && selected !== null) select(null);\n    else return;\n    event.preventDefault();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      aria-label={title}\n      className={cx(\n        \"flex min-h-[330px] w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-4 rounded-[20px] bg-background-secondary-default px-4 pt-4 pb-3\",\n        className,\n      )}\n    >\n      {/* Header: label, headline figure + unit, caption; period stepper on the right */}\n      <div className=\"flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-x-3 gap-y-2\">\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 flex-[1_1_9rem] flex-col gap-0.5\">\n          <p className=\"flex items-center gap-1.5 text-body-medium text-text-secondary\">\n            {Icon ? <Icon className=\"size-4 shrink-0\" aria-hidden /> : null}\n            <span className=\"truncate\">{title}</span>\n          </p>\n          <p className=\"flex items-baseline gap-1.5\">\n            <span\n              key={activeIndex ?? \"summary\"}\n              className=\"animate-number-fade text-title-1-medium whitespace-nowrap text-text-primary tabular-nums motion-reduce:animate-none\"\n              data-vital-value=\"\"\n            >\n              {headline ? print(headline) : \"–\"}\n            </span>\n            <span className=\"text-body-2-medium text-text-secondary\">{unit}</span>\n          </p>\n          <p className=\"truncate text-caption-1-medium text-text-tertiary\" data-vital-caption=\"\">\n            {caption}\n          </p>\n        </div>\n        {period ? <PeriodStepper period={period} title={title} /> : null}\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Plot: one track per reading, fills from the floor (bars) or floats low → value (range) */}\n      <div className=\"flex min-h-[140px] flex-1 flex-col gap-2\">\n        <div className=\"relative min-h-0 flex-1\">\n          <div\n            role=\"group\"\n            aria-label={`${title} readings`}\n            className=\"absolute inset-0 grid gap-2\"\n            style={{ gridTemplateColumns: `repeat(${Math.max(1, readings.length)}, minmax(0, 1fr))` }}\n            onPointerLeave={() => setHoverIndex(null)}\n          >\n            {readings.map((reading, index) => {\n              const status = statuses[index] ?? \"normal\";\n              const bottom = isRange && reading.low !== undefined ? pct(reading.low) : 0;\n              const height = Math.max(pct(reading.value) - bottom, 0);\n              const isSelected = selected === index;\n              const dimmed = activeIndex !== null && activeIndex !== index;\n              return (\n                <button\n                  key={`${reading.label}-${index}`}\n                  ref={(node) => {\n                    barRefs.current[index] = node;\n                  }}\n                  type=\"button\"\n                  tabIndex={index === rovingIndex ? 0 : -1}\n                  aria-pressed={isSelected}\n                  aria-label={`${reading.detail ?? reading.label}: ${print(reading)} ${unit}, ${STATUS_CHIP[status].label.toLowerCase()}`}\n                  className={cx(\n                    \"relative h-full min-w-0 cursor-pointer overflow-hidden rounded-[10px] bg-chart-track outline-none\",\n                    \"focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-border-focus-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-background-secondary-default\",\n                    isSelected && \"ring-2 ring-text-primary ring-offset-2 ring-offset-background-secondary-default\",\n                  )}\n                  onPointerEnter={() => setHoverIndex(index)}\n                  onFocus={(event) => {\n                    setFocusIndex(index);\n                    if (event.currentTarget.matches(\":focus-visible\")) setHoverIndex(index);\n                  }}\n                  onBlur={() => setHoverIndex((current) => (current === index ? null : current))}\n                  onKeyDown={(event) => onBarKeyDown(event, index)}\n                  onClick={() => select(isSelected ? null : index)}\n                >\n                  <span\n                    aria-hidden\n                    data-status={status}\n                    className={cx(\n                      \"absolute inset-x-0 min-h-1.5 rounded-[10px] transition-[height,bottom,opacity] duration-500 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                      status === \"normal\" ? FILL[color] : OUT_OF_RANGE_FILL,\n                      dimmed && \"opacity-40\",\n                    )}\n                    style={{ bottom: `${bottom}%`, height: `${height}%` }}\n                  />\n                </button>\n              );\n            })}\n          </div>\n          {bands.map((band, i) => (\n            <div\n              key={i}\n              aria-hidden\n              className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 border-y border-dashed border-chart-7-active bg-chart-7/10\"\n              style={{ bottom: `${pct(band[0])}%`, height: `${Math.max(pct(band[1]) - pct(band[0]), 0)}%` }}\n            />\n          ))}\n        </div>\n        <div\n          aria-hidden\n          className=\"grid gap-2\"\n          style={{ gridTemplateColumns: `repeat(${Math.max(1, readings.length)}, minmax(0, 1fr))` }}\n        >\n          {readings.map((reading, index) => (\n            <span\n              key={`${reading.label}-${index}`}\n              className={cx(\n                \"truncate text-center text-caption-1-medium transition-colors duration-150 ease-out motion-reduce:transition-none\",\n                activeIndex === index ? \"text-text-primary\" : \"text-text-tertiary\",\n              )}\n            >\n              {reading.label}\n            </span>\n          ))}\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      {/* Normal-range hint */}\n      <div className=\"flex items-center justify-between gap-2 rounded-2lg bg-background-inner-default px-2.5 py-2\">\n        <div className=\"flex min-w-0 items-center gap-1.5\">\n          <span\n            aria-hidden\n            className=\"h-2.5 w-3.5 shrink-0 rounded-[3px] border border-dashed border-chart-7-active bg-chart-7/15\"\n          />\n          <span className=\"text-body-regular whitespace-nowrap text-text-secondary\">Normal</span>\n          <span className=\"truncate text-body-medium text-text-primary tabular-nums\">{normalText}</span>\n        </div>\n        <Chip variant=\"caption\" color={chip.color} className=\"shrink-0\" data-vital-status=\"\">\n          {chip.label}\n        </Chip>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** No timers, listeners or observers; hover state lives in React state and the column refs are plain arrays.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The fill height transition, the label colour fade and the headline fade are removed with motion-reduce:transition-none and motion-reduce:animate-none; values change instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Do not use pink (`chart-3`) as the card colour: it is reserved for out-of-range readings, so the `color` prop excludes it.\n- Do not let a mouse focus preview the column; only keyboard focus (`:focus-visible`) should, or the headline sticks after the pointer leaves.\n- Do not size the column grid with h-full inside a flex-1 box whose parent only has a min-height: the percentage resolves to auto and the tracks collapse to zero. Position the grid absolute inset-0 inside the relative plot box instead.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Values print with `Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-US\")` and `decimals` fraction digits; range readings print as \"118/76\", and the range variant's hint drops the unit (\"90–120/60–80\") so it fits a third-width card.\n- The period stepper is display-only: the parent owns which readings are shown and passes new `readings` plus the period label.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking a column sets aria-pressed and shows that day's value, detail and status chip in the header; Escape clears it; the previous-period arrow swaps the readings and the label, and readings outside normalRange render with the pink fill.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "web-audio-scheduler": "# Build: Web Audio Scheduling Kit\n\nTwo clocks, one job each. `AudioContext.currentTime` is a sample counter and never drifts, but it cannot wake you up; `setInterval` can wake you up, but only when the main thread is free. The scheduler runs a 90 ms timer whose only responsibility is to ask which beats fall inside the next 300 ms and hand each one an exact audio time. The timer can fire late, early, or twice in a row and the notes still land in the same place.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nA lookahead sequencer: a coarse timer queues beats onto the sample-accurate audio clock, so tempo never drifts.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `nextTime` holds the audio-clock time of the next unqueued beat, initialised to `ctx.currentTime + START_LEAD` when `start()` runs. Every timer tick loops `while (nextTime < ctx.currentTime + lookahead)`, calls `onBeat({ time: nextTime, beat, step, ctx })`, and advances `nextTime += 60 / bpm`.\n2. The advance accumulates from the previous beat time, never from `ctx.currentTime`. Re-basing on the clock each pass folds the timer's jitter into the tempo, and a sequence that re-bases runs measurably sharp inside a minute.\n3. It is a `while` and not an `if` because one tick may have to cover several beats — the timer was throttled, the tempo is faster than the interval, or the tab just came back to the foreground.\n4. `scheduleTone` builds an oscillator and a gain node per beat, ramps `gain` from its peak to 0.001 over `decay`, stops the oscillator `TAIL` seconds after the ramp ends, and disconnects both nodes in `onended` so the graph does not accumulate dead nodes.\n5. The visual step index comes from a second, independent pass: a rAF loop reads `ctx.currentTime` once per frame and pops every queued beat whose time has passed, so the highlighted step is the step you can hear rather than the step that was most recently queued 300 ms ago.\n6. `phase` is derived in the same loop as `1 - (nextQueuedBeat.time - now) / secondsPerBeat`, clamped to 0-1, and written to a ref rather than to state — a playhead needs a value every frame and state would re-render the whole sequencer 60 times a second.\n7. On `visibilitychange` to hidden the lookahead widens to 1.5s, because a hidden tab clamps `setInterval` to roughly one call per second and a 0.3s window would leave gaps of missed beats.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `intervalMs = 90`\n  How often the scheduler wakes. It only has to beat the lookahead window, not the tempo — at 96 bpm a beat is 625 ms, so the timer gets about seven chances to queue each one. Dropping to ~10 ms burns main-thread time for nothing; going past ~250 ms leaves too few chances inside a 300 ms window.\n- `lookahead = 0.3`\n  Seconds queued in advance. Must exceed the worst-case gap between timer calls, which under main-thread contention is several times `intervalMs` — 0.3s over a 0.09s interval is a 3x margin. Larger windows make a tempo or pattern change take longer to take effect, because beats already queued cannot be un-queued.\n- `HIDDEN_LOOKAHEAD = 1.5`\n  Seconds queued while the tab is hidden. Chrome and Firefox clamp background timers to about one call per second, so the window has to be over 1s or the sequence develops audible holes; 1.5s leaves margin over the clamp.\n- `START_LEAD = 0.08`\n  Seconds before the first beat. Covers constructing the context, resuming it, and the first `drain()` — all of which happen inside the click handler. Scheduling the downbeat at exactly `currentTime` means its attack is already partly in the past.\n- `SILENCE = 0.001`\n  The ramp target, -60 dB. `exponentialRampToValueAtTime` is undefined at 0 and throws a RangeError; ramping to a literal 0 is the single most common Web Audio mistake.\n- `TAIL = 0.05`\n  Seconds between the end of the decay ramp and `osc.stop()`. Stopping exactly at the end of the ramp truncates the waveform mid-cycle, which is an audible click on a triangle or square wave.\n- `gain default = 0.14`\n  Peak per tone. Three simultaneous rows sum to 0.42, which leaves headroom for their overlapping tails; at 0.4 each the same chord clips the destination.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useWebAudioScheduler(options?: { bpm?: number; steps?: number; lookahead?: number; intervalMs?: number; onBeat?: (beat: { time: number; beat: number; step: number; ctx: AudioContext }) => void }): { start: () => void; stop: () => void; running: boolean; step: number | null; phase: RefObject<number> }\nfunction scheduleTone(ctx: AudioContext, options: { frequency: number; at: number; gain?: number; decay?: number; type?: OscillatorType }): void\n```\n\n- `bpm: number` — default `96`\n  Tempo. One beat is `60 / bpm` seconds on the audio clock.\n- `steps: number` — default `8`\n  Beats per bar. Only affects the wrapped `step` index handed to `onBeat`.\n- `lookahead: number` — default `0.3`\n  Seconds of beats queued in advance. Must exceed the worst-case timer gap.\n- `intervalMs: number` — default `90`\n  How often the scheduling timer runs. Independent of the tempo.\n- `onBeat: (beat: ScheduledBeat) => void`\n  Called once per beat, `lookahead` seconds early. Schedule audio against `beat.time`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** `exponentialRampToValueAtTime` is undefined at 0, so silence is -60 dB. */\nconst SILENCE = 0.001;\n/** Seconds after the ramp before `stop()`, so the cut is inaudible. */\nconst TAIL = 0.05;\n/** Seconds before the first beat, absorbing the cost of `start()` itself. */\nconst START_LEAD = 0.08;\n/** Lookahead used while the tab is hidden, in seconds. */\nconst HIDDEN_LOOKAHEAD = 1.5;\n\ninterface QueuedBeat {\n  time: number;\n  step: number;\n}\n\n/**\n * One decaying tone, scheduled and self-disposing.\n *\n * The oscillator and its gain node disconnect themselves in `onended`. A\n * sequencer that omits that leaks two nodes per beat — at 120 bpm that is\n * 7200 dead nodes an hour, all still attached to the destination.\n */\nexport function scheduleTone(ctx: AudioContext, options: ToneOptions): void {\n  const { frequency, at, gain = 0.14, decay = 0.28, type = 'triangle' } = options;\n\n  const osc = ctx.createOscillator();\n  const env = ctx.createGain();\n  osc.type = type;\n  osc.frequency.setValueAtTime(frequency, at);\n  env.gain.setValueAtTime(gain, at);\n  env.gain.exponentialRampToValueAtTime(SILENCE, at + decay);\n  osc.connect(env);\n  env.connect(ctx.destination);\n  osc.start(at);\n  osc.stop(at + decay + TAIL);\n  osc.onended = () => {\n    osc.disconnect();\n    env.disconnect();\n  };\n}\n\nexport function useWebAudioScheduler(\n  options: UseWebAudioSchedulerOptions = {},\n): WebAudioScheduler {\n  const { bpm = 96, steps = 8, lookahead = 0.3, intervalMs = 90 } = options;\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const [running, setRunning] = useState(false);\n  const [step, setStep] = useState<number | null>(null);\n  const phase = useRef(0);\n\n  const ctxRef = useRef<AudioContext | null>(null);\n  const timer = useRef<number | null>(null);\n  const queue = useRef<QueuedBeat[]>([]);\n  /** Audio time of the next beat to be scheduled. */\n  const nextTime = useRef(0);\n  /** Monotonic beat counter, so `step` survives a change of `steps`. */\n  const counter = useRef(0);\n  /** Widened while the tab is hidden, where timers are clamped to ~1 Hz. */\n  const window_ = useRef(lookahead);\n\n  const config = useRef({ bpm, steps, lookahead, intervalMs, reduced, options });\n  config.current = { bpm, steps, lookahead, intervalMs, reduced, options };\n\n  /** Queue every beat that falls inside the lookahead window. */\n  const drain = useCallback(() => {\n    const ctx = ctxRef.current;\n    if (!ctx) return;\n    const cfg = config.current;\n    const secondsPerBeat = 60 / cfg.bpm;\n    const horizon = ctx.currentTime + window_.current;\n\n    // A `while`, not an `if`: one call may have to cover several beats when the\n    // timer was throttled, and a fast tempo can put two beats in one window.\n    while (nextTime.current < horizon) {\n      const beat = counter.current;\n      const at = nextTime.current;\n      const index = wrapIndex(beat, cfg.steps);\n\n      cfg.options.onBeat?.({ time: at, beat, step: index, ctx });\n      queue.current.push({ time: at, step: index });\n\n      // Accumulate from the previous beat time, never from `currentTime`.\n      // Re-basing on the clock each pass folds the timer's jitter into the\n      // tempo and the sequence drifts sharp within a minute.\n      nextTime.current = at + secondsPerBeat;\n      counter.current = beat + 1;\n    }\n  }, []);\n\n  const stop = useCallback(() => {\n    if (timer.current !== null) {\n      clearInterval(timer.current);\n      timer.current = null;\n    }\n    queue.current.length = 0;\n    phase.current = 0;\n    setRunning(false);\n    setStep(null);\n  }, []);\n\n  const start = useCallback(() => {\n    if (timer.current !== null) return;\n    const cfg = config.current;\n\n    let ctx = ctxRef.current;\n    if (!ctx) {\n      // Built inside the gesture handler that called `start`. Anywhere else\n      // and the autoplay policy hands back a suspended context whose notes\n      // are dropped silently.\n      ctx = new AudioContext();\n      ctxRef.current = ctx;\n    }\n    if (ctx.state === 'suspended') void ctx.resume();\n\n    counter.current = 0;\n    queue.current.length = 0;\n    window_.current = document.visibilityState === 'hidden' ? HIDDEN_LOOKAHEAD : cfg.lookahead;\n    nextTime.current = ctx.currentTime + START_LEAD;\n\n    setRunning(true);\n    drain();\n    timer.current = window.setInterval(drain, cfg.intervalMs);\n  }, [drain]);\n\n  // The visual clock. Reading `ctx.currentTime` once per frame and popping the\n  // beats that have passed keeps the highlight on the note you can hear; a\n  // separate rAF counter of its own would slide out of step over a few bars.\n  useRaf(() => {\n    const ctx = ctxRef.current;\n    if (!ctx) return;\n    const now = ctx.currentTime;\n    const q = queue.current;\n\n    let current: QueuedBeat | null = null;\n    while (q.length > 0) {\n      const head = q[0];\n      if (head === undefined || head.time > now) break;\n      q.shift();\n      current = head;\n    }\n    if (current) setStep(current.step);\n\n    if (config.current.reduced) {\n      phase.current = 0;\n      return;\n    }\n    const secondsPerBeat = 60 / config.current.bpm;\n    const head = q[0];\n    // Time until the next queued beat, turned into progress through this one.\n    phase.current =\n      head === undefined ? 0 : clamp(1 - (head.time - now) / secondsPerBeat, 0, 1);\n  }, running);\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!running) return;\n    // A hidden tab clamps `setInterval` to roughly one call per second, so a\n    // 0.3s window drops entire bars. Widening the window instead of stopping\n    // means the bar plays through unbroken while the tab is in the background.\n    const onVisibility = () => {\n      window_.current =\n        document.visibilityState === 'hidden' ? HIDDEN_LOOKAHEAD : config.current.lookahead;\n      drain();\n    };\n    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);\n    return () => document.removeEventListener('visibilitychange', onVisibility);\n  }, [running, drain]);\n\n  useEffect(\n    () => () => {\n      if (timer.current !== null) clearInterval(timer.current);\n      timer.current = null;\n      queue.current.length = 0;\n      const ctx = ctxRef.current;\n      ctxRef.current = null;\n      // close() cancels every note already scheduled in the future. Without\n      // it a bar keeps playing after the component has gone.\n      if (ctx && ctx.state !== 'closed') void ctx.close();\n    },\n    [],\n  );\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The unmount cleanup clears the scheduling interval, empties the pending-beat queue, and calls `ctx.close()`, which cancels every note already committed to the audio thread. The `visibilitychange` listener is removed with its effect, and the rAF subscription is released by `useRaf` when `running` goes false. Each tone disconnects its own oscillator and gain node in `onended`.\n\n**Reduced motion.** `step` still advances — a step index is information about the sound, not decoration — but `phase` is pinned to 0, so a playhead bound to it jumps from step to step in place of sliding continuously between them. The demo's playhead therefore renders as a static line on the current step.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Driving the sequence with one `setTimeout` per note. Every note then inherits main-thread jitter, and a single 80 ms layout pass puts that note 80 ms off the beat — clearly audible at any tempo.\n- Advancing the beat time from `ctx.currentTime` instead of from the previous beat time. The timer never fires exactly on schedule, so each re-base rounds the interval down and the tempo creeps upward; a sequence started against a metronome is noticeably ahead within a minute.\n- Using `if` instead of `while` in the drain. When the tab returns to the foreground, or the tempo is faster than the timer interval, one tick has several beats to queue — an `if` queues one and silently drops the rest.\n- Setting the lookahead smaller than the worst-case timer gap. The window is the entire safety margin: at a 0.05s lookahead a single long frame means the beat is already in the past when the timer finally runs, and `osc.start(pastTime)` plays immediately, so the note is late *and* off-grid.\n- Constructing the `AudioContext` at mount. The autoplay policy hands back a `suspended` context, `osc.start(at)` neither throws nor sounds, and the failure looks like a silent bug with a clean console.\n- Ramping the gain to 0. `exponentialRampToValueAtTime(0, t)` throws — the target must be strictly positive.\n- Leaving each beat's oscillator and gain connected. At 120 bpm that is two dead nodes a beat, 7200 an hour, all still attached to the destination; CPU climbs and the output eventually distorts.\n- Counting the visual step with its own rAF counter rather than reading `ctx.currentTime`. The two clocks tick at different rates, so the highlight slides out of step with the sound over a few bars — the classic \"my metronome light is not on the beat\" bug.\n- Highlighting the step at the moment it was *queued*. That is `lookahead` seconds before it sounds, so the playhead runs a third of a second ahead of the audio and looks broken.\n- Publishing the beat phase as React state. Sixty re-renders a second of the whole sequencer to move one line; a ref plus a rAF write to a CSS custom property costs nothing.\n- Never calling `ctx.close()`. Notes already queued keep playing after the component unmounts, and Chrome throws once about six contexts exist in one document.\n\n## Notes\n\n- A change to the pattern takes effect at most `lookahead` seconds later, because beats inside the window are already committed to the audio thread. That is inherent to lookahead scheduling, not a defect — shrink the window if you need a tighter response and accept the reduced timing margin.\n- `onBeat` receives the context so the caller schedules its own nodes; the hook deliberately does not own the sound. Sequencing and synthesis are separate concerns and pairing them makes both untestable.\n- For sub-millisecond scheduling with no main-thread involvement at all, the same shape moves into an `AudioWorklet` that posts beat messages. The lookahead pattern is identical; only the timer changes.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nPress Play: the highlight lands on a cell at the same instant you hear that cell, and stays in step after a minute of playing. Toggle a cell while it runs and the change is audible on the next pass. Switch to another tab for ten seconds and come back — the pattern is still on the grid with no gap or double-trigger. Press Stop and the step counter clears with no trailing note. Unmount while playing and nothing keeps sounding. With reduced motion on, the playhead line sits still on the current step instead of sweeping.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "web-search": "# Build: Web Search\n\nShows where an agent looked while it researched. Headings open a level and the searches under them land one row at a time on the Agent Log guide; a step with sources branches once more into a Sources row whose site marks overlap like a stack, each with its title and domain on hover. The row expands into a list of titles and domains, past six marks the rest collapse into a count, and per-step dwell makes slow searches hold longer than cached ones.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe research half of an agent log: each query lands on a curved guide, and a step that found something branches into overlapping site marks.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: motion@^12.43.0, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0, react-aria-components@^1.22.1\n\n## How it works\n\n1. Steps flatten into reveal units: one per step plus one for its Sources row. useRevealTicker from agent-log counts them, paced by a delayFor callback: startDelay first, sourceBeat between a step and its Sources row, then that step's dwell (or stepInterval) before the next step.\n2. A memoised tree groups plain steps under the heading before them, with each node's unit offset; a node renders once revealed passes its offset, and its Sources row once revealed passes offset + 1.\n3. The step the trail is holding on is the one owning unit revealed - 1, and it shimmers through ShimmerText until the next unit lands.\n4. Top-level rows mount as motion.li with the shared blur-and-height reveal; nested steps and Sources rows are agent-log LogRow items with their own curved branch, and a GuideBridge drops 6px from each 16px glyph to the nested trunk at ms-2.\n5. Site marks reuse the social-button glyph set: the path is drawn in the brand colour, black brands take text-primary so they invert with the theme, and anything else gets a globe. Each mark is a react-aria Focusable trigger for the kit Tooltip, rendered as a link when the source has an href.\n6. The Sources list collapses with a grid-template-rows 1fr to 0fr transition and is inert while closed.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `startDelay = 320ms`\n  The first row should not land on the same frame as the user turn that started the search.\n- `sourceBeat = 450ms`\n  Long enough to read the step before its sources branch off, short enough that the two still read as one find.\n- `stepInterval = 850ms (demo 800ms)`\n  Default hold for a step with no dwell; dwell overrides it per step.\n- `maxMarks = 6`\n  Six overlapping 20px marks fit beside \"Sources\" at 360px; the rest become a +N count.\n- `mark overlap = 20px bordered tiles, -6px overlap`\n  Each tile carries its own border and fill, so the stack reads as separate sites on any surface in either theme.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\n<WebSearch key={turnId} steps={[{ label: \"Ran 3 searches\", icon: RiSearchLine, heading: true }, { label: \"Searched Reddit for\", query: \"design tokens\", brand: \"reddit\", meta: \"5 results\", dwell: 1400, sources }]} onComplete={() => reply()} />\n```\n\n- `steps: readonly WebSearchStep[]`\n  Steps with label, optional query, icon or brand, heading, meta, dwell and sources ({ title, domain, brand?, href? }).\n- `run: boolean` — default `true`\n  Pauses and resumes the internal timer.\n- `startDelay: number` — default `320`\n  Milliseconds before the first row lands.\n- `stepInterval: number` — default `850`\n  Hold on a step that has no dwell, in milliseconds.\n- `sourceBeat: number` — default `450`\n  Milliseconds between a step and its Sources row.\n- `revealed: number`\n  Rows landed, when driven from real events; disables the timer.\n- `maxMarks: number` — default `6`\n  Marks drawn before the rest collapse into a count.\n- `working: string | false` — default `\"Working\"`\n  Tail indicator label while running; false hides it.\n- `onComplete: () => void`\n  Fires once when the last row lands.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Web search\"`\n  Accessible name of the log.\n- `className: string`\n  Classes on the root.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// Reveal order: every step is one row and a step with sources is two, the\n// step then its Sources row. Pacing is per row: the Sources row lands a beat\n// after its step, and the trail then holds for that step's dwell (or the\n// interval) before the next step, so slow searches look slow.\nconst units = useMemo(() => {\n  const out: { step: number; sources: boolean }[] = [];\n  steps.forEach((step, i) => {\n    out.push({ step: i, sources: false });\n    if (searchStepUnits(step) === 2) out.push({ step: i, sources: true });\n  });\n  return out;\n}, [steps]);\n\nconst delayFor = useCallback(\n  (index: number) => {\n    if (index === 0) return startDelay;\n    if (units[index]?.sources) return sourceBeat;\n    const prev = units[index - 1];\n    const step = prev ? steps[prev.step] : undefined;\n    return step?.dwell ?? stepInterval;\n  },\n  [units, steps, startDelay, stepInterval, sourceBeat],\n);\n\n// Headings open a level; plain steps after one nest under it, and plain\n// steps before any heading sit at the top level.\nconst tree = useMemo(() => {\n  const top: SearchNode[] = [];\n  let parent: SearchNode | null = null;\n  let offset = 0;\n  steps.forEach((step, index) => {\n    const node: SearchNode = { index, step, start: offset, children: [] };\n    offset += searchStepUnits(step);\n    if (step.heading) {\n      top.push(node);\n      parent = node;\n    } else if (parent) {\n      parent.children.push(node);\n    } else {\n      top.push(node);\n    }\n  });\n  return top;\n}, [steps]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The ticker clears its pending timeout whenever the revealed count, run flag or pacing changes and on unmount; the Working row clears its timer on unmount; tooltips are owned by react-aria and close with their trigger.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Rows appear without the blur, lift or height animation, the guide is drawn at once, the shimmer and star twinkle stop, marks no longer lift on hover, and the Sources list opens and closes instantly. Rows still arrive on the same schedule.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Key the component on the turn: a new question needs a new key, or the reveal count carries over.\n- Pass steps as a stable array; the pacing callback depends on it and a new array every render restarts the pending timer.\n- A step with sources counts as two units when you drive revealed yourself.\n- The marks sit inside the log's clipping edge, so their tooltips portal out through the kit Tooltip rather than rendering inline.\n\n## Notes\n\n- brand accepts any of the 24 social-button providers; pass href on a source to make its mark and list entry links.\n- Pass working={false} to hide the tail indicator or a string to relabel it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nAfter the user turn, \"Ran 3 searches\" lands, then the first search shimmers and its Sources row branches off with five marks; the Reddit search shows six marks and +2; the X search lands last, the Working row leaves and the answer appears. Clicking Sources sets aria-expanded to true and lists titles with domains; clicking again folds it; sending the message again replays the trail from the top.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "week-view-grid": "# Build: Week View Grid\n\nA week calendar in the template style: a grey frame holding seven white rounded day columns, a 52px hour gutter and a sticky header with the weekday and date, today in an accent pill. Timed events are absolutely positioned from their start and duration with the kit event chip; events that overlap share the column in equal slices. All-day events span their days in a row under the header. A red now-line and time pill mark the fixed `now` you pass in. The body scrolls inside its own box, opening at `scrollToHour`, and on narrow screens it scrolls sideways with the gutter pinned. `renderEvent` lets each chip be wrapped, for example in an EventDetailPopover.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSeven-day time grid: hour gutter, day columns, events placed by start and duration, overlaps side by side, a fixed now-line.\n\n- Category: display\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: @internationalized/date@^3.12.4\n\n## How it works\n\n1. The seven days come from `startOfWeek(week, locale, firstDayOfWeek)`, so en-US starts on Sunday unless you override it. One CSS grid template, `52px repeat(7, minmax(0, 1fr))`, is shared by the header, the all-day row and the body, so the columns line up.\n2. `layoutDayEvents` clips each timed event to the day with a UTC day difference (minutes from midnight, negative before the day) and the visible hours, then sorts by start, longer first. It walks the list once: an event that starts after the cluster has ended closes it; inside a cluster the event takes the first column that is free, or opens a new one. Every member of a cluster then gets `columns` equal to the cluster width.\n3. Each block is `top = (start − startHour·60) × hourHeight / 60`, height its duration in px (at least 22px; blocks under 40px switch to the one-line compact chip so a 15 minute stand-up still shows its title and time), left `col / cols` of the column plus 2px and width `1 / cols` minus 4px. Overlap is judged on the drawn height, so padded short events never cover the next one.\n4. The header row is `position: sticky; top: 0` inside the scroll box and the gutter cells are `sticky; left: 0`, both on the frame colour, so the hours and day names stay put in either scroll direction. Hour lines are absolutely placed 1px borders behind the events.\n5. When `now` falls in the week and inside the hour range, its column gets a 2px red line with a dot at the start edge and the gutter shows the time in a red pill at the same offset. A single effect sets `scrollTop` to `scrollToHour` on mount.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `hourHeight = 48px`\n  Half an hour is 24px, enough for a one-line title; a full working day (8–18) fits a 480px box.\n- `minimum block height = 22px`\n  The compact chip height, so 15 minute events stay legible and clickable.\n- `compact threshold = 40px`\n  Under this the full chip cannot fit a title line and the time line, so short blocks render the one-line compact chip.\n- `gutter = 52px`\n  Holds \"10:42\" in the now pill and \"11 PM\" in 12-hour mode without wrapping.\n- `min width = 640px`\n  Below this the seven columns get too narrow for titles, so the body scrolls sideways instead.\n- `scrollToHour = 8`\n  Opens on the working day instead of midnight.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction WeekViewGrid(props: { events: CalendarEvent[]; week: CalendarDate; firstDayOfWeek?: DayOfWeek; startHour?: number; endHour?: number; hourHeight?: number; scrollToHour?: number; now?: CalendarDateTime; height?: number; selectedEventId?: string | null; onEventClick?: (event: CalendarEvent) => void; renderEvent?: (event: CalendarEvent, chip: ReactElement) => ReactNode; locale?: string; hourCycle?: 12 | 24; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element\nfunction layoutDayEvents(events: CalendarEvent[], day: CalendarDate, options: { startHour: number; endHour: number; hourHeight: number; minHeight: number }): WeekEventLayout[]\n```\n\n- `events: CalendarEvent[]`\n  Timed and all-day events; any outside the week are ignored.\n- `week: CalendarDate`\n  Any day of the week to show.\n- `firstDayOfWeek: 'sun' | 'mon' | … | 'sat'` — default `locale`\n  First column.\n- `startHour / endHour: number` — default `0 / 24`\n  Visible hour range.\n- `hourHeight: number` — default `48`\n  Pixels per hour.\n- `scrollToHour: number` — default `8`\n  Hour scrolled to the top on mount.\n- `now: CalendarDateTime`\n  Draws the now-line and highlights today. Never read from the clock inside.\n- `height: number` — default `520`\n  Height of the scrolling box in px.\n- `selectedEventId: string | null`\n  Rings the matching chip.\n- `onEventClick: (event) => void`\n  Called when a chip is pressed.\n- `renderEvent: (event, chip) => ReactNode`\n  Wrap each chip, for example in an EventDetailPopover.\n- `hourCycle: 12 | 24` — default `24`\n  Hour labels and chip times.\n- `locale: string` — default `\"en-US\"`\n  Weekday names, first day of week, times.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/**\n * Places one day's timed events. Events are clipped to the day and the hour\n * range, sorted by start (longer first), and walked once: an event that starts\n * after everything so far has ended opens a new cluster; inside a cluster each\n * event takes the first column whose last event has ended, or a new column.\n * Every event in a cluster then shares its width by the cluster's column count.\n */\nexport function layoutDayEvents(events: CalendarEvent[], day: CalendarDate, options: LayoutOptions): WeekEventLayout[] {\n  const { startHour, endHour, hourHeight, minHeight } = options;\n  const perMinute = hourHeight / 60;\n  const from = startHour * 60;\n  const to = endHour * 60;\n  const total = (to - from) * perMinute;\n\n  const items = events\n    .filter((event) => !event.allDay)\n    .map((event) => ({\n      event,\n      start: Math.max(minutesInto(day, event.start), from),\n      end: Math.min(minutesInto(day, event.end), to),\n    }))\n    .filter((item) => item.end > item.start)\n    .sort((a, b) => a.start - b.start || b.end - a.end);\n\n  const placed: WeekEventLayout[] = [];\n  let cluster: WeekEventLayout[] = [];\n  let columnEnds: number[] = [];\n  let clusterEnd = -Infinity;\n  const closeCluster = () => {\n    for (const item of cluster) item.columns = columnEnds.length;\n    cluster = [];\n    columnEnds = [];\n  };\n\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const height = Math.max((item.end - item.start) * perMinute, minHeight);\n    const top = Math.min((item.start - from) * perMinute, total - height);\n    // Overlap is judged on the drawn block, so short events padded to\n    // minHeight never cover the next one.\n    const drawnEnd = item.start + height / perMinute;\n    if (item.start >= clusterEnd) closeCluster();\n    let column = columnEnds.findIndex((end) => end <= item.start);\n    if (column === -1) {\n      column = columnEnds.length;\n      columnEnds.push(drawnEnd);\n    } else {\n      columnEnds[column] = drawnEnd;\n    }\n    const layout = { event: item.event, top, height, column, columns: 1 };\n    cluster.push(layout);\n    placed.push(layout);\n    clusterEnd = Math.max(clusterEnd, drawnEnd);\n  }\n  closeCluster();\n  return placed;\n}\n\nexport interface WeekViewGridProps {\n  events: CalendarEvent[];\n  /** Any day in the week to show. */\n  week: CalendarDate;\n  /** First column; defaults to the locale's first day of the week (Sunday in en-US). */\n  firstDayOfWeek?: DayOfWeek;\n  /** First and last hour rows. Default 0 and 24. */\n  startHour?: number;\n  endHour?: number;\n  /** Pixel height of one hour. Default 48. */\n  hourHeight?: number;\n  /** Hour scrolled to the top on mount. Default 8. */\n  scrollToHour?: number;\n  /** Draws the now-line and highlights today. Pass a fixed value; the grid never reads the clock. */\n  now?: CalendarDateTime;\n  /** Height of the scrolling area in px. Default 520. */\n  height?: number;\n  selectedEventId?: string | null;\n  onEventClick?: (event: CalendarEvent) => void;\n  /** Wrap each chip, for example in an EventDetailPopover. */\n  renderEvent?: (event: CalendarEvent, chip: ReactElement) => ReactNode;\n  locale?: string;\n  hourCycle?: HourCycle;\n  \"aria-label\"?: string;\n  className?: string;\n  ref?: Ref<HTMLElement>;\n}\n\nconst GRID_COLUMNS = \"grid grid-cols-[52px_repeat(7,minmax(0,1fr))] gap-x-1.5\";\n/** Below this height (px) a block has no room for the time line, so it uses the compact chip. */\nconst SHORT_BLOCK = 40;\n/**\n * Slices of an overlap cluster are narrow: tighter padding and a one-line\n * caption-size title with an ellipsis, so \"Planning\" reads \"Plann…\" instead of\n * wrapping mid-word to \"Plannin / g\". The popover shows the full title.\n */\nconst NARROW_CHIP =\n  \"ps-1 pe-0.5 [&>span:first-child]:block [&>span:first-child]:truncate [&>span:first-child]:text-caption-1-medium\";\n\nexport function WeekViewGrid({\n  events,\n  week,\n  firstDayOfWeek,\n  startHour = 0,\n  endHour = 24,\n  hourHeight = 48,\n  scrollToHour = 8,\n  now,\n  height = 520,\n  selectedEventId = null,\n  onEventClick,\n  renderEvent,\n  locale = \"en-US\",\n  hourCycle = 24,\n  \"aria-label\": ariaLabel,\n  className,\n  ref,\n}: WeekViewGridProps) {\n  const scrollRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const first = startOfWeek(week, locale, firstDayOfWeek);\n  const days = Array.from({ length: 7 }, (_, i) => first.add({ days: i }));\n  const last = days[6] ?? first;\n  const hours = Array.from({ length: Math.max(endHour - startHour, 1) }, (_, i) => startHour + i);\n  const bodyHeight = hours.length * hourHeight;\n  const todayIndex = now ? daysBetween(now, first) : -1;\n  const nowMinutes = now ? now.hour * 60 + now.minute : 0;\n  const showNow = todayIndex >= 0 && todayIndex < 7 && nowMinutes >= startHour * 60 && nowMinutes <= endHour * 60;\n  const nowTop = ((nowMinutes - startHour * 60) * hourHeight) / 60;\n\n  // Scroll the working hours into view once the grid mounts (and when the\n  // target hour changes). Plain scrollTop: no listener, nothing to clean up.\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = scrollRef.current;\n    // 12px of slack keeps the target hour's label clear of the sticky header.\n    if (el) el.scrollTop = Math.max(0, (scrollToHour - startHour) * hourHeight - 12);\n  }, [scrollToHour, startHour, hourHeight]);\n\n  const allDay = events\n    .filter((event) => event.allDay)\n    .map((event) => ({\n      event,\n      from: Math.max(daysBetween(event.start, first), 0),\n      to: Math.min(daysBetween(lastDayOf(event), first), 6),\n    }))\n    .filter((span) => span.to >= span.from && span.from <= 6 && span.to >= 0)\n    .sort((a, b) => a.from - b.from || b.to - a.to);\n\n  const chipFor = (event: CalendarEvent, variant: \"compact\" | \"full\", chipClassName?: string) => {\n    const chip = (\n      <CalendarEventChip\n        variant={variant}\n        className={chipClassName}\n        title={event.title}\n        color={event.color}\n        start={event.start}\n        end={event.end}\n        allDay={event.allDay}\n        selected={selectedEventId === event.id}\n        locale={locale}\n        hourCycle={hourCycle}\n        onClick={onEventClick ? () => onEventClick(event) : undefined}\n      />\n    );\n    return renderEvent ? renderEvent(event, chip) : chip;\n  };\n\n  const label =\n    ariaLabel ??\n    `Week of ${formatDay(first, locale, { month: \"long\", day: \"numeric\" })} – ${formatDay(last, locale, {\n      month: \"long\",\n      day: \"numeric\",\n      year: \"numeric\",\n    })}`;\n\n  return (\n    <section\n      ref={ref}\n      aria-label={label}\n      className={cx(\"flex w-full flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-[20px] bg-background-secondary-default\", className)}\n    >\n      <div ref={scrollRef} className=\"relative overflow-auto overscroll-contain\" style={{ height }}>\n        <div className=\"min-w-[640px] px-2 pb-2\">\n          <div className=\"sticky top-0 z-20 bg-background-secondary-default pt-2 pb-1.5\">\n            <div className={GRID_COLUMNS}>\n              <div aria-hidden className=\"sticky left-0 z-10 bg-background-secondary-default\" />\n              {days.map((day, i) => {\n                const isToday = i === todayIndex;\n                return (\n                  <div key={day.toString()} aria-hidden className=\"flex flex-col items-center gap-0.5 py-0.5\">\n                    <span className=\"text-caption-1-medium text-text-secondary\">\n                      {formatDay(day, locale, { weekday: \"short\" })}\n                    </span>\n                    <span\n                      className={cx(\n                        \"flex size-7 items-center justify-center rounded-full text-body-2-medium tabular-nums\",\n                        isToday ? \"bg-accent-500 text-text-white\" : \"text-text-primary\",\n                      )}\n                    >\n                      {day.day}\n                    </span>\n                  </div>\n                );\n              })}\n            </div>\n            {allDay.length > 0 ? (\n              <div className={cx(GRID_COLUMNS, \"mt-1.5\")}>\n                <div className=\"sticky left-0 z-10 flex items-start justify-end bg-background-secondary-default pe-2 pt-1 text-caption-2-medium text-text-tertiary\">\n                  All day\n                </div>\n                <ul className=\"col-span-7 grid grid-flow-row-dense grid-cols-7 gap-x-1.5 gap-y-1\">\n                  {allDay.map(({ event, from, to }) => (\n                    <li key={event.id} className=\"min-w-0\" style={{ gridColumn: `${from + 1} / ${to + 2}` }}>\n                      {chipFor(event, \"compact\")}\n                    </li>\n                  ))}\n                </ul>\n              </div>\n            ) : null}\n          </div>\n\n          <div className={GRID_COLUMNS} style={{ height: bodyHeight }}>\n            <div aria-hidden className=\"sticky left-0 z-10 bg-background-secondary-default\">\n              {hours.slice(1).map((hour, i) => (\n                <span\n                  key={hour}\n                  className=\"absolute end-2 -translate-y-1/2 text-caption-2-regular tabular-nums text-text-tertiary\"\n                  style={{ top: (i + 1) * hourHeight }}\n                >\n                  {formatEventTime({ hour, minute: 0 }, { locale, hourCycle })}\n                </span>\n              ))}\n              {showNow && now ? (\n                <span\n                  className=\"absolute end-1 z-[1] -translate-y-1/2 rounded-[5px] bg-text-error-primary px-1 text-caption-2-semibold tabular-nums text-text-white\"\n                  style={{ top: nowTop }}\n                >\n                  {formatEventTime(now, { locale, hourCycle })}\n                </span>\n              ) : null}\n            </div>\n\n            {days.map((day, i) => (\n              <div\n                key={day.toString()}\n                role=\"group\"\n                aria-label={`${formatDay(day, locale, { weekday: \"long\", month: \"long\", day: \"numeric\" })}${i === todayIndex ? \", today\" : \"\"}`}\n                className=\"relative rounded-xl bg-background-primary-default\"\n              >\n                <div aria-hidden className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-0\">\n                  {hours.slice(1).map((hour, h) => (\n                    <div\n                      key={hour}\n                      className=\"absolute inset-x-0 border-t border-border-table dark:border-border-button-group\"\n                      style={{ top: (h + 1) * hourHeight }}\n                    />\n                  ))}\n                </div>\n                {layoutDayEvents(events, day, { startHour, endHour, hourHeight, minHeight: 22 }).map((item) => {\n                  const style: CSSProperties = {\n                    top: item.top,\n                    height: item.height,\n                    insetInlineStart: `calc(${(item.column / item.columns) * 100}% + 2px)`,\n                    width: `calc(${100 / item.columns}% - 4px)`,\n                  };\n                  return (\n                    // Blocks too short for title and time fall back to the one-line chip;\n                    // in a narrow slice it drops the time so the title keeps the room.\n                    <div key={item.event.id} className=\"absolute z-[1] flex\" style={style}>\n                      {item.height < SHORT_BLOCK\n                        ? chipFor(item.event, \"compact\", cx(\"h-full\", item.columns > 1 && \"px-1 [&>span+span]:hidden\"))\n                        : chipFor(item.event, \"full\", item.columns > 1 ? NARROW_CHIP : undefined)}\n                    </div>\n                  );\n                })}\n                {showNow && i === todayIndex ? (\n                  <div\n                    aria-hidden\n                    className=\"pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 z-[2] h-0.5 -translate-y-1/2 bg-text-error-primary\"\n                    style={{ top: nowTop }}\n                  >\n                    <span className=\"absolute -start-1 top-1/2 size-2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full bg-text-error-primary\" />\n                  </div>\n                ) : null}\n              </div>\n            ))}\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** One mount effect assigns `scrollTop` and returns nothing; there are no listeners, timers or observers. Formatters are cached in a module-level map.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Nothing moves. The grid opens already scrolled to `scrollToHour` with an instant `scrollTop`, never a smooth scroll; the only transition is the kit chip's 150ms hover brightness and selection ring, which changes no position or size.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reading the clock for the now-line during render. Server and client disagree by the time they hydrate; take `now` as a prop and update it from a timer in the caller if it should move.\n- Measuring overlap on raw times when short events are padded to a minimum height. Two back-to-back 10 minute events would draw on top of each other; compare the drawn end instead.\n- Wrapping chips in a popover inside the scroll box. React Aria closes an open popover when an ancestor of its trigger scrolls, which is the right behaviour but means scrolling the grid dismisses it.\n- Giving each day its own grid. Columns drift as content changes; share one grid template for header, all-day row and body.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `layoutDayEvents` is exported for custom day or resource views.\n- An event that ends exactly at midnight belongs to the day it started for the all-day row, so a one-day all-day event can be stored as midnight to midnight.\n- Pass `startHour` / `endHour` to show only working hours; events outside are clipped to the edges.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nThe week of August 16 2026 opens scrolled to 08:00 with Sun 16 to Sat 22 in the header and 20 in an accent pill. Planning and Design crit on Monday sit side by side at half width; Retro, Demo day and Wrap-up on Friday split three ways where they overlap. Payday spans Wednesday and Holidays spans Friday and Saturday in the all-day row. A red line and a \"10:42\" pill mark Thursday. Clicking Stand-up opens its details popover; Delete removes the block, lowers the count and offers Undo. At 360px the body scrolls sideways with the hour gutter pinned.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "wheel-accumulator-stepper": "# Build: Wheel Accumulator Stepper\n\n`wheel` is four input devices wearing one event name. A mouse notch is a single event carrying 100 units; a trackpad swipe is forty events of four units each, followed by a synthesised momentum tail that keeps arriving for about a second after the fingers lift; and `deltaMode` decides whether those numbers are pixels, lines, or whole pages. This hook scales the delta by `deltaMode`, sums it, and emits one step per 90 normalised pixels — then latches, and re-arms only when the incoming magnitude stops decaying, which is the one signal that separates a fresh flick from the tail of the last one.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nWheel input normalised by deltaMode and accumulated to a threshold, so one flick of any device moves exactly one step.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `wheelDelta(e, axis)` picks the axis — `Math.abs(deltaX) > Math.abs(deltaY) ? deltaX : deltaY` for `both`, so a sideways two-finger swipe and a mouse wheel drive the same widget — then multiplies by the scale `deltaMode` implies: 1 for pixel mode, 40 for line mode, 800 for page mode. Everything downstream counts pixels.\n2. A signed accumulator sums the normalised deltas. While `Math.abs(units) >= step` it subtracts one signed `step` and calls `onStep(direction, detail)`, capped at `maxPerEvent` iterations. One mouse notch (100 units) clears the 90-unit threshold on its own; a trackpad swipe clears it after roughly a dozen events. No device detection anywhere.\n3. After any step the accumulator is zeroed rather than left holding the remainder, and a latch closes. `peak` records the magnitude of the event that fired the step.\n4. While latched, an event whose magnitude is below `peak` is absorbed: no accumulation, no step, but `preventDefault` still runs so the page does not scroll underneath the widget. The first event whose magnitude reaches `peak` re-arms. macOS momentum is monotonically decreasing, so a tail can never reach its own peak and is swallowed entirely; the next notch of a mouse wheel has an identical magnitude and re-arms immediately; a second deliberate flick exceeds it on its first event.\n5. Two further resets bypass the latch: a gap greater than `gestureGapMs` since the previous accepted event, which means the gesture and its tail are finished, and a change of sign, since no momentum tail reverses direction.\n6. `e.ctrlKey` events return before `preventDefault` and before accumulation — that combination is pinch-zoom on a trackpad and page zoom on a mouse, and swallowing it removes the only way to enlarge the page.\n7. The listener is registered `{ passive: !preventDefault }`, so it is non-passive exactly when it intends to cancel. The live accumulator is exposed as a `RefObject`, not state, so a gauge can read it every frame without re-rendering the widget forty times per swipe.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `STEP = 90`\n  Normalised pixels per step. It has to sit below one mouse notch so a single notch always moves exactly one item — Chrome reports 100 per notch and legacy browsers 120, so anything above 100 makes a mouse feel broken. Going much below ~60 puts it inside the range of a small trackpad twitch and the widget steps when the user meant to scroll past it.\n- `LINE_UNITS = 40`\n  Pixels assumed per line for `deltaMode === 1`. Firefox reports line mode on several platforms, where `deltaY` is typically 3 — 3 x 40 = 120 units, which matches what the same physical notch reports as pixels in Chrome. Using 1 here (i.e. not normalising at all) makes the stepper need thirty notches per step in Firefox and one in Chrome.\n- `PAGE_UNITS = 800`\n  Pixels assumed per page for `deltaMode === 2`. Roughly one viewport. The exact figure barely matters because any page-mode event is far past the threshold; what matters is that it is not 1, which would make a full-page wheel event register as almost no movement.\n- `GESTURE_GAP_MS = 140`\n  Silence that ends a gesture. Momentum tail events arrive at frame rate, so ~8-17 ms apart; sustained finger input is similar. 140 ms is far above both and far below the ~250 ms a person needs to lift, reposition, and flick again, so it never splits one gesture in two and never merges two.\n- `MAX_PER_EVENT = 1`\n  Steps emitted from a single event. One event should mean at most one step, and with the latch in place the only thing this guards is a single enormous delta — a page-mode wheel worth 800 units, or a synthetic event from an automation script, either of which would otherwise advance eight items in one frame. Raise it to 2 or 3 if a violent flick should travel further; the demo keeps it at 1.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction useWheelStepper(ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>, options: { step?: number; axis?: 'both' | 'x' | 'y'; maxPerEvent?: number; gestureGapMs?: number; preventDefault?: boolean; enabled?: boolean; onStep: (direction: 1 | -1, detail: { index: number; delta: number; deltaMode: number; event: WheelEvent }) => void }): { units: RefObject<number>; latched: RefObject<boolean> }\nfunction wheelDelta(e: WheelEvent, axis?: 'both' | 'x' | 'y'): number\nfunction wheelUnitScale(deltaMode: number): number\n```\n\n- `step: number` — default `90`\n  Normalised pixels of travel per emitted step. Must stay below one mouse notch (100).\n- `axis: 'both' | 'x' | 'y'` — default `'both'`\n  `both` uses whichever of `deltaX` / `deltaY` dominates, so horizontal and vertical input both work.\n- `maxPerEvent: number` — default `1`\n  Ceiling on steps emitted from one event. Guards a single page-mode delta worth 800 units.\n- `gestureGapMs: number` — default `140`\n  Silence that ends the gesture and clears the latch. Above frame interval, below a human reflick.\n- `preventDefault: boolean` — default `true`\n  Cancel the page scroll on every accepted event. Also decides whether the listener is passive.\n- `enabled: boolean` — default `true`\n  Detaches the listener when false — for a widget that only owns the wheel while open.\n- `onStep: (direction: 1 | -1, detail: WheelStepDetail) => void`\n  Called once per step, synchronously in the wheel handler. `+1` is down or right.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Normalised pixels per step. */\nconst STEP = 90;\n/** Pixels per line for `deltaMode === 1`. */\nconst LINE_UNITS = 40;\n/** Pixels per page for `deltaMode === 2`. */\nconst PAGE_UNITS = 800;\n/** Milliseconds of silence that end a gesture. */\nconst GESTURE_GAP_MS = 140;\n/** Steps allowed out of one event. */\nconst MAX_PER_EVENT = 1;\n\n/** The pixel scale implied by `deltaMode`. Unknown modes are treated as pixels. */\nexport function wheelUnitScale(deltaMode: number): number {\n  if (deltaMode === 1) return LINE_UNITS;\n  if (deltaMode === 2) return PAGE_UNITS;\n  return 1;\n}\n\n/**\n * One wheel event as normalised pixels on the chosen axis.\n *\n * `'both'` takes whichever axis dominates, so a two-finger sideways swipe and\n * a mouse wheel drive the same widget and the user never has to discover\n * which axis it wanted.\n */\nexport function wheelDelta(e: WheelEvent, axis: WheelAxis = 'both'): number {\n  const raw =\n    axis === 'x'\n      ? e.deltaX\n      : axis === 'y'\n        ? e.deltaY\n        : Math.abs(e.deltaX) > Math.abs(e.deltaY)\n          ? e.deltaX\n          : e.deltaY;\n  return raw * wheelUnitScale(e.deltaMode);\n}\n\n/**\n * Turn continuous wheel input into discrete steps, one per physical gesture.\n *\n * Attaches to `ref`, so the widget owns the wheel only while the pointer is\n * over it. Nothing is published as state: `onStep` fires on the event and the\n * live accumulator is a ref.\n */\nexport function useWheelStepper(\n  ref: RefObject<HTMLElement | null>,\n  options: UseWheelStepperOptions,\n): WheelStepperState {\n  const {\n    step = STEP,\n    axis = 'both',\n    maxPerEvent = MAX_PER_EVENT,\n    gestureGapMs = GESTURE_GAP_MS,\n    preventDefault = true,\n    enabled = true,\n  } = options;\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n\n  const units = useRef(0);\n  const latched = useRef(false);\n\n  const config = useRef({ step, axis, maxPerEvent, gestureGapMs, reduced, options });\n  config.current = { step, axis, maxPerEvent, gestureGapMs, reduced, options };\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const el = ref.current;\n    if (!el || !enabled) return;\n\n    /** `timeStamp` of the previous accepted event, for the gesture gap. */\n    let lastTime = 0;\n    /** Largest magnitude seen since the latch closed. */\n    let peak = 0;\n\n    const onWheel = (e: WheelEvent) => {\n      // ctrl+wheel is pinch-zoom on a trackpad and page zoom on a mouse.\n      // Swallowing it takes away the user's only way to enlarge the page, so\n      // it is passed through untouched — no preventDefault, no accumulation.\n      if (e.ctrlKey) return;\n\n      const cfg = config.current;\n      const delta = wheelDelta(e, cfg.axis);\n      // A pure cross-axis event on a single-axis widget, or the zero-delta\n      // event some drivers emit at the end of a gesture. Neither is input.\n      if (delta === 0) return;\n\n      // Before the latch check, not after: while the widget is absorbing a\n      // momentum tail the page must not scroll underneath it instead.\n      if (preventDefault) e.preventDefault();\n\n      const magnitude = Math.abs(delta);\n      const gap = e.timeStamp - lastTime;\n      lastTime = e.timeStamp;\n\n      // Silence long enough to outlast a momentum tail's frame interval means\n      // the previous gesture is over in every sense. Start clean.\n      if (gap > cfg.gestureGapMs) {\n        units.current = 0;\n        latched.current = false;\n      }\n\n      // A reversal is always deliberate — no momentum tail changes sign — so\n      // it clears both the latch and any units banked in the other direction.\n      if (units.current !== 0 && Math.sign(delta) !== Math.sign(units.current)) {\n        units.current = 0;\n        latched.current = false;\n      }\n\n      if (latched.current) {\n        // The whole technique. A tail decays, so it never reaches the peak it\n        // had when the step fired; a new flick or the next wheel notch does,\n        // on its first event. Magnitude alone cannot tell them apart and a\n        // fixed cooldown either eats the second flick or leaks the tail.\n        if (magnitude < peak) return;\n        latched.current = false;\n      }\n\n      units.current += delta;\n\n      // Reduced motion caps the burst at one step regardless of the option:\n      // travelling several items from one flick is the motion-heavy part.\n      const limit = cfg.reduced ? 1 : Math.max(1, cfg.maxPerEvent);\n      let fired = 0;\n\n      while (Math.abs(units.current) >= cfg.step && fired < limit) {\n        const direction: 1 | -1 = units.current > 0 ? 1 : -1;\n        units.current -= cfg.step * direction;\n        cfg.options.onStep(direction, {\n          index: fired,\n          delta,\n          deltaMode: e.deltaMode,\n          event: e,\n        });\n        fired += 1;\n      }\n\n      if (fired > 0) {\n        // Zeroed, not left with the remainder: a flick's overshoot belongs to\n        // the flick that is now finished, and banking it means the next\n        // gesture starts part-way to a step and fires early.\n        units.current = 0;\n        latched.current = true;\n        peak = magnitude;\n      }\n    };\n\n    // Non-passive only when it actually calls preventDefault. Registering\n    // non-passive unconditionally opts the element out of the scrolling fast\n    // path for nothing; calling preventDefault inside a passive listener is\n    // ignored with a console error, which is the same bug from the other end.\n    el.addEventListener('wheel', onWheel, { passive: !preventDefault });\n    return () => {\n      el.removeEventListener('wheel', onWheel);\n      units.current = 0;\n      latched.current = false;\n    };\n  }, [ref, enabled, preventDefault]);\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The effect removes the `wheel` listener with the same capture flag it was added with, and zeroes both the accumulator and the latch so a remount does not inherit half a gesture. There is no timer, no rAF, and no pointer capture to release. Re-running under React 19 StrictMode is harmless: the second effect attaches a second listener only after the first has removed its own.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Steps still fire — a step is the user's input, not decoration, and suppressing it would leave the widget unresponsive. What changes is the burst: the per-event step count is clamped to 1 regardless of `maxPerEvent`, so a violent flick advances one item instead of travelling several in a single frame. Whatever the consumer animates in response to a step is where a reduced-motion transition belongs.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reading `deltaY` without reading `deltaMode`. The same physical notch is `deltaY: 100` in pixel mode and `deltaY: 3` in line mode, so a threshold tuned in Chrome needs about thirty notches per step in a line-mode browser. The bug never reproduces on the developer's machine.\n- Stepping on every wheel event. A trackpad emits thirty to forty events per swipe, so a carousel jumps thirty slides for one gesture — the single most common wheel bug and the reason the accumulator exists at all.\n- Accumulating with no latch. The accumulator fixes the burst but not the tail: macOS keeps synthesising decaying wheel events for about a second after the fingers lift, and a hard flick has enough left in that tail for three or four more thresholds. The carousel takes one step under your fingers and then keeps going on its own.\n- Latching with a fixed cooldown instead. A cooldown long enough to outlast the tail (roughly 600-1000 ms) also eats the second of two deliberate flicks, so the widget feels dead when hurried; a cooldown short enough to keep double flicks alive lets the loud part of the tail through. Decay is the only signal that separates the two cases.\n- Comparing each tail event against the previous event rather than against the peak. Tail magnitudes jitter, so `39 >= 38` looks like new input and re-arms mid-tail, which is exactly the extra step the latch was added to prevent. Comparing against the peak since the latch closed cannot be fooled by jitter.\n- Carrying the leftover units past a step. Zeroing looks lossy but is correct: the overshoot belongs to the gesture that just ended, and banking 80 of 90 units means the next flick fires after a single event of travel.\n- Skipping `preventDefault` while the latch is absorbing the tail. The widget correctly refuses to step, and the page scrolls instead — so the carousel holds still and the whole section slides away underneath it.\n- Registering the listener non-passive when it never cancels. That opts the element out of the browser's scrolling fast path for nothing. The mirror-image bug is calling `preventDefault` inside a passive listener, where it is silently ignored with a console error and the page scrolls anyway.\n- Swallowing `ctrl`+wheel. That is pinch-zoom on every trackpad and page zoom on every mouse; intercepting it means a low-vision user cannot enlarge the page while the pointer is anywhere over the widget.\n- Publishing the accumulator as React state. Forty state updates per swipe re-render the whole widget to move a 40 px gauge; a ref plus one CSS custom property write costs nothing and cannot tear.\n- Attaching to `window` instead of the element. The widget then steps while the pointer is somewhere else entirely, and a non-passive window-level wheel listener degrades scrolling for the whole document.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The latch makes one gesture mean one step, which is right for a carousel, a chapter list, or anything with discrete positions. For a continuous value — a zoom level, a scrub position — drop the latch and use the normalised delta directly; the `deltaMode` scaling is the part you still need.\n- `onStep` is called synchronously inside the wheel handler, so it runs before the frame that would have scrolled. Keep it to a state update or a ref write; layout reads there cost a forced synchronous reflow on every notch.\n- A trackpad in a browser that supports it will also fire `gesturestart` / `gesturechange` for pinch, which is a separate event stream. This hook deliberately ignores it rather than trying to reconstruct pinch from `ctrl`+wheel.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nWheel over the panel with a mouse: every notch moves exactly one item, and a fast spin moves one per notch with no skipping. Flick hard on a trackpad and let go: the index advances exactly once and then holds still while the readout shows the tail arriving and being absorbed. Flick twice in quick succession and it advances twice. Push slowly and continuously and the gauge fills to the threshold before each step, so the accumulation is visible. Reverse direction mid-gesture and it steps back without first having to unwind the units already banked. Hold ctrl and wheel: the browser zooms, and the index does not move. Scroll the outer page with the pointer just outside the panel and the panel ignores it.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "workspace-switcher": "# Build: Workspace Switcher\n\nA workspace switcher for the head of a sidebar. The trigger is a full-width row showing the current workspace mark, name and plan with an up/down chevron. It opens a non-modal menu listing every workspace (mark, name, plan · detail) with a check on the current one, plus an optional \"Create workspace\" action in its own section. Choosing a workspace closes the menu and the trigger contents cross in with a short starting-style entrance.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nSidebar-top workspace menu: mark, name and plan on the trigger, a checked list of workspaces and a create-workspace row.\n\n- Category: navigation\n- Runtime cost: css\n- Dependencies: react-aria-components@^1.22.1, @remixicon/react@^4.9.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. React Aria MenuTrigger + Menu supply the menu semantics: arrow keys, Home/End, typeahead, Enter/Space to choose and Escape to close with focus back on the trigger.\n2. The workspace rows live in a MenuSection with selectionMode=\"single\", disallowEmptySelection and selectedKeys=[current], so they render as menuitemradio with aria-checked and the check icon reads isSelected from the item render props.\n3. The create row sits in a second MenuSection with no selection mode and uses onAction, so it can never become the selected workspace.\n4. The Popover is isNonModal so the page never scroll-locks; useDismissOnOutsidePress restores close-on-outside-press and useTriggerToggle swallows the spurious reopen when the open trigger is pressed again.\n5. Value and open state are each controlled-or-uncontrolled: value/defaultValue/onChange and isOpen/onOpenChange.\n6. The trigger mark and label are keyed by workspace id, so a switch remounts them and their @starting-style classes (starting:opacity-0, starting:translate-y-1, starting:scale-75) play a 200ms entrance.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `popover width = 280px`\n  Room for a 32px mark plus a name and \"plan · members\" line without truncating typical names.\n- `offset = 6px`\n  Clears the trigger hover fill so the menu reads as attached, not overlapping.\n- `entrance = 200ms ease-out, translate-y 4px / scale 0.75`\n  Long enough to notice the switch, short enough not to delay reading the new name.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction WorkspaceSwitcher(props: { workspaces: Workspace[]; value?: string; defaultValue?: string; onChange?: (id: string) => void; onCreateWorkspace?: () => void; createLabel?: string; sectionLabel?: string; isOpen?: boolean; onOpenChange?: (open: boolean) => void; \"aria-label\"?: string; className?: string }): JSX.Element | null\n```\n\n- `workspaces: Workspace[]`\n  { id, name, plan, detail?, initials?, tone? } for every workspace; tone is the kit Avatar colour.\n- `value: string`\n  Controlled selected workspace id.\n- `defaultValue: string` — default `workspaces[0].id`\n  Initial id when uncontrolled.\n- `onChange: (id: string) => void`\n  Called with the newly chosen id (not when re-choosing the current one).\n- `onCreateWorkspace: () => void`\n  Shows the create row and runs when it is chosen.\n- `createLabel: string` — default `\"Create workspace\"`\n  Label of the create row.\n- `sectionLabel: string` — default `\"Workspaces\"`\n  Heading above the list, also the menu aria-label.\n- `isOpen: boolean`\n  Controlled open state.\n- `onOpenChange: (open: boolean) => void`\n  Open state change callback.\n- `aria-label: string` — default `\"Switch workspace\"`\n  Trigger name prefix; the current workspace name is appended.\n- `className: string`\n  Extra classes for the trigger.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n// The popover is non-modal (no scroll lock on <html>), which also turns off\n// React Aria's outside-press dismissal: restore it, and make a press on the\n// trigger while open close the menu instead of reopening it.\nuseDismissOnOutsidePress(isOpen, () => setOpen(false), [triggerRef, popoverRef]);\nconst allowOpenChange = useTriggerToggle(isOpen, triggerRef);\n\nconst select = (keys: Selection) => {\n  if (keys === \"all\") return;\n  const [next] = Array.from(keys as Set<Key>);\n  if (next == null || String(next) === selectedId) return;\n  setUncontrolledValue(String(next));\n  onChange?.(String(next));\n};\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** The outside-press and trigger-toggle hooks add document pointerdown listeners only while open and remove them on close or unmount; there are no timers.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The popover keeps its opacity fade from the kit surface but drops nothing else that moves; the trigger entrance, hover fills and check fade all use motion-reduce:transition-none, so a switch swaps contents instantly.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Put selection on the MenuSection, not the Menu: a Menu-level selectionMode would turn the create row into a selectable radio.\n- isNonModal popovers lose React Aria outside-press dismissal; without useDismissOnOutsidePress the menu stays open when clicking the page.\n- Key the trigger contents by id, not by name, or two workspaces with equal names will not replay the entrance.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The trigger accessible name is \"<aria-label>: <current name>\", so screen readers announce the current workspace before opening.\n- Pass onCreateWorkspace to show the create row; omit it for a pick-only switcher.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nClicking the trigger opens a menu of workspaces with exactly one checked; choosing another closes the menu and the trigger shows the new name and plan; Escape closes with focus on the trigger; arrow keys move between rows; \"Create workspace\" runs the callback and is never checked.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "wrapped-ring-offset": "# Build: Shortest Signed Ring Offset\n\nA ring of items laid out from `wrappedOffset(i, position, count)` — the shortest signed distance around the circle — with the naive `i - position` printed beside each one so the two can be compared while the ring turns. The dots move continuously through the seam; the raw numbers leap by a whole count at it. This is the single function that makes a finite array behave like an infinite one, and the only honest way to teach it is to show the version it replaces failing.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nTen dots on a ring, each showing its wrapped offset and the raw index difference it replaces.\n\n- Category: primitive\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: none beyond React\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `wrappedOffset(i, position, count)` computes `mod(i - position, count)` and then subtracts `count` when the result exceeds `count / 2`, producing a signed distance in `(-count/2, count/2]`. Negative means the item is behind the read head.\n2. Each item is placed at `angle = (offset / count) * 2PI - PI/2`, so offset 0 sits under the notch at twelve o'clock and the ring is symmetric about it. Because a ±count jump in the offset is exactly one full turn, the dot lands on the same pixel and the seam is invisible.\n3. Focus is `gaussian(|offset|, 0.45)` — `exp(-(0.45·d)²)` — driving scale from 0.74 to 1.20 and opacity from 0.30 to 1.00. A continuous falloff with no thresholds is what makes the ring read as analogue rather than stepped.\n4. Dragging converts the pointer to an angle about the ring centre with `atan2`, then advances the target position by `shortestAngle(previous, current) / 2PI * count`. `shortestAngle` is required here for the same reason `wrappedOffset` is required for the layout: `atan2` flips from +PI to -PI at nine o'clock.\n5. The ring position is eased with `damp(position, target, 0.0015, dt)` on the shared rAF loop, and every frame writes only `transform`, `opacity` and `z-index` — no layout reads, because width and height come from a ResizeObserver rather than `clientWidth`.\n6. React re-renders only when `wrapIndex(Math.round(position), count)` changes, which is a couple of times a second; the sixty-times-a-second work is plain style writes on refs.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `count = 10`\n  Items on the ring, and therefore the wrap threshold at count / 2 = 5. Ten keeps every raw label at most two characters wide, so the pills fit on a 320 px preview without colliding; above ~16 the labels overlap tangentially and the comparison becomes unreadable.\n- `FOCUS_FALLOFF = 0.45`\n  Gaussian falloff on |offset|. Yields 1.00 / 0.82 / 0.44 / 0.16 / 0.04 at distances 0-4, so roughly four items read as \"near\" on a ten-item ring. At 1.0 only the centre item is lit and the ring looks broken; at 0.2 nothing falls off and there is no focus at all.\n- `SMOOTHING = 0.0015`\n  Fraction of the remaining gap left after one second. Fast enough that the ring feels attached to the pointer, slow enough that releasing mid-drag reads as momentum rather than a cut. This is `damp`'s smoothing argument, not a per-frame lerp constant — a per-frame constant converges twice as fast at 120 Hz.\n- `RADIUS_RATIO = 0.36`\n  Ring radius as a fraction of the shorter container side. Above ~0.42 the 36 px pills clip the edges of a 16:10 frame; below ~0.30 adjacent pills touch.\n- `IDLE_DRIFT = 0.22`\n  Ring positions per second before the first interaction, so the seam sweeps past roughly every 45 s and the component demonstrates itself without being touched. Faster than ~0.6 and the raw labels change too quickly to read.\n- `threshold (useDrag) = 3`\n  Pixels of travel before a press becomes a rotation. Lower than the 8 px a carousel wants, because this ring has no click target to protect — it only has to reject hand tremor.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction WrappedRingOffset(props: { count?: number; drift?: number; className?: string }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `count: number` — default `10`\n  Items on the ring. The wrap threshold is count / 2.\n- `drift: number` — default `0.22`\n  Ring positions per second before the first interaction. 0 to sit still.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the outer element via `cn`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\n/** Fraction of the gap to the target that survives one second. */\nconst SMOOTHING = 0.0015;\n/** Gaussian falloff on |offset|. At 0.45: 1.00 / 0.82 / 0.44 / 0.16 / 0.04. */\nconst FOCUS_FALLOFF = 0.45;\nconst RADIUS_RATIO = 0.36;\nconst IDLE_DRIFT = 0.22;\n\nexport function WrappedRingOffset({\n  count = 10,\n  drift = IDLE_DRIFT,\n  className,\n}: WrappedRingOffsetProps) {\n  const root = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const nodes = useRef<(HTMLLIElement | null)[]>([]);\n\n  /** Where the ring is being pulled to. Fractional and unbounded. */\n  const target = useRef(0);\n  /** Where the ring actually is. Eased toward `target`. */\n  const position = useRef(0);\n  /** Suppresses the idle drift once a human has touched it. */\n  const engaged = useRef(false);\n  const grabAngle = useRef(0);\n  const shown = useRef(0);\n\n  const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const { width, height } = useResizeObserver(root);\n\n  /** Pointer angle around the ring centre, measured fresh: the page scrolls. */\n  const angleAt = useCallback((clientX: number, clientY: number) => {\n    const box = root.current?.getBoundingClientRect();\n    if (!box) return 0;\n    return Math.atan2(clientY - (box.top + box.height / 2), clientX - (box.left + box.width / 2));\n  }, []);\n\n  useDrag(root, {\n    threshold: 3,\n    onDragStart: (s) => {\n      engaged.current = true;\n      grabAngle.current = angleAt(s.clientX, s.clientY);\n    },\n    onDrag: (s) => {\n      const angle = angleAt(s.clientX, s.clientY);\n      // shortestAngle, not `angle - grabAngle`: atan2 flips from +PI to -PI at\n      // nine o'clock, and the raw difference there is a full turn of garbage\n      // that spins the ring backwards every time the pointer crosses it.\n      const step = (shortestAngle(grabAngle.current, angle) / TAU) * count;\n      target.current -= step;\n      grabAngle.current = angle;\n    },\n  });\n\n  useRaf((dt) => {\n    if (!engaged.current && !reduced) target.current += drift * dt;\n\n    // Reduced motion still moves the ring — it just does not ease into place.\n    position.current = reduced\n      ? target.current\n      : damp(position.current, target.current, SMOOTHING, dt);\n\n    const p = position.current;\n    const radius = Math.min(width, height) * RADIUS_RATIO;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n      const el = nodes.current[i];\n      if (!el) continue;\n\n      // The one line the whole component exists to demonstrate. `offset` is\n      // continuous in `p` even across the seam, because the ±count jump maps\n      // to exactly one turn of the circle and lands on the same place.\n      const offset = wrappedOffset(i, p, count);\n      const angle = (offset / count) * TAU - Math.PI / 2;\n      const focus = gaussian(Math.abs(offset), FOCUS_FALLOFF);\n\n      const x = Math.cos(angle) * radius;\n      const y = Math.sin(angle) * radius;\n      // translate3d places the box origin, translate(-50%,-50%) re-centres it,\n      // scale runs about that centre. One string, one composited property.\n      el.style.transform = `translate3d(${x.toFixed(2)}px, ${y.toFixed(2)}px, 0) translate(-50%, -50%) scale(${(0.74 + focus * 0.46).toFixed(3)})`;\n      el.style.opacity = (0.3 + focus * 0.7).toFixed(3);\n      el.style.zIndex = String(10 + Math.round(focus * 40));\n    }\n\n    // Labels are integers, so they only need a render when the rounded ring\n    // position changes — a couple of times a second, not sixty.\n    const next = wrapIndex(Math.round(p), count);\n    if (next !== shown.current) {\n      shown.current = next;\n      setIndex(next);\n    }\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** `useDrag` removes its five pointer listeners and releases any outstanding pointer capture; `useRaf` unsubscribes and cancels the shared frame when it was the last subscriber; `useResizeObserver` unobserves the root. No transforms are left behind because the nodes unmount with the component.\n\n**Reduced motion.** The idle drift never starts and the `damp` easing is bypassed, so the ring tracks the drag one-to-one and stops dead on release. Dragging and the arrow keys still work, and the labels still update — the maths is the content here, so removing it would remove the component.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Using `i - position` for layout. The ring still turns, but item 0 renders nine slots away from item 9 instead of one slot back, so the last item snaps across the entire ring every time the position passes zero. That snap is the seam, and it is the whole reason this function exists.\n- Using `%` instead of a true modulo. JavaScript's `%` returns a negative result for negative operands, so scrolling backwards past zero produces offsets outside `[0, count)` and half the ring inverts. `mod(n, m) = ((n % m) + m) % m` is the fix.\n- Comparing `offset > count / 2` with `>=`. On an even count that puts the antipodal item at `-count/2` instead of `+count/2`, and the item directly opposite the read head flickers between the two on alternate frames.\n- Taking `angle - previousAngle` when converting pointer motion to rotation. `atan2` wraps from +PI to -PI at nine o'clock, so the raw difference there is a full turn and the ring spins backwards once per lap. `shortestAngle` wraps the difference into `(-PI, PI]`.\n- Reading `element.clientWidth` inside the rAF callback to compute the radius. The same loop has already written transforms, so every read forces a synchronous reflow — sixty per second, and it only shows up as jank once there are a few of these on a page. The size comes from a ResizeObserver instead.\n- Driving the per-item transforms through React state. Sixty commits a second for ten nodes is an order of magnitude more work than writing `style.transform` on refs, and it makes the labels — which change twice a second — re-render at frame rate for nothing.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `wrappedOffset` is the same three lines behind an infinite marquee, a drum carousel, a cylinder of image planes, and any \"closest N to the playhead\" query. Pair it with a scroll library set to `infinite: true` and the sequence has no beginning and no end.\n- The idle drift stops permanently at the first drag or arrow key. An ambient motion that resumes on top of a user's input reads as the component fighting them.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nLeave it alone: the ring drifts and, as each item passes the six o'clock antipode, its struck-through raw label flips sign by the full count while the dot itself does not move. Drag the ring in a full circle past nine o'clock and it keeps turning in the same direction. Press ArrowRight and the position readout increments by one. Resize the frame and the ring re-radiuses without the dots drifting off-centre.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n",
  "wrapping-drum-carousel": "# Build: Wrapping Drum Carousel\n\nCards sit stacked at the centre of the stage and are pushed outward by their signed distance from a fractional carousel position. That distance is wrapped into a ring, so the sequence has no first or last card and the position variable can drift forever. The drum reads as three-dimensional but every transform is flat: `rotationY` and `z` are pinned to zero on purpose.\n\nBuild this as a React component in my project. Match my existing conventions —\nfile layout, styling system, and naming — rather than the ones implied below.\nEverything after this line is specification, not code to copy verbatim.\n\n## What it does\n\nAn endless carousel that reads as a rotating drum with no 3D at all — only x, scale and fading opacity.\n\n- Category: layout\n- Runtime cost: dom\n- Dependencies: gsap@^3.15.0\n\n## How it works\n\n1. `gsap.utils.wrap(-count / 2, count / 2)` turns `i - pos` into a signed ring offset in `(-count/2, count/2]`. For six cards at `pos = 0`, card 5 resolves to `-1` and renders one slot to the *left* of centre instead of five slots right — this single call is what makes a finite array behave like an endless one.\n2. Horizontal placement is `x = offset * step` where `step = min(spacingRatio * stage.clientWidth, maxSpacing)`. Spacing is measured off the stage element, not `window.innerWidth`, so a preview in a grid cell lays out correctly when the grid reflows rather than only when the viewport does.\n3. Depth is faked by two curves with deliberately different clamps: `scale = 1 - scaleFalloff * min(distance, 1)` bottoms out one slot from centre, while `autoAlpha = clamp(0, 1, 1.15 - 0.75 * distance)` keeps falling and reaches zero at distance 1.533. Cards therefore fade out *before* they shrink far enough to look wrong, which reads as recession into fog rather than into a vanishing point.\n4. `rotationY: 0` and `z: 0` are written on every `gsap.set`. They are not decoration — they stop an ancestor `perspective` or `transform-style: preserve-3d` from turning a deliberately flat drum into a half-broken 3D one that skews at the edges.\n5. `zIndex = 100 - round(10 * distance)` is stepped, not continuous, so cards at similar distances do not flicker past each other as `pos` crosses an integer.\n6. Drag comes from `useDrag`, which handles pointer capture, an 8 px dead zone and the capture-phase click swallow. `onDrag` divides `deltaX` by the current spacing so the drum tracks the finger 1:1, and `onDragEnd` snaps to `Math.round(pos)` with a 0.7 s `power3.out` tween whose `onUpdate` re-runs the same layout function.\n7. The wheel handler accumulates `deltaX` and fires one step per 90 units, capped at 3 steps per event, then discards the remainder — an inertial trackpad flick otherwise keeps queuing steps after the fingers lift.\n\n## Constants — use these values\n\nThese are tuned. Each one has a reason; do not round them off or replace them\nwith values that \"look cleaner\".\n\n- `spacingRatio = 0.46`\n  Card spacing as a fraction of stage width. At 0.46 the neighbours are half-visible at the edges, which is what tells the user there is more to come. Below ~0.3 the cards overlap into a stack; above ~0.6 the neighbours leave the stage and the drum looks like a single card.\n- `maxSpacing = 560`\n  Upper bound in px. Without it a 3440 px monitor computes 1582 px of spacing and the neighbours are simply gone; the ratio has to stop scaling at roughly the width of one comfortable reading column.\n- `scaleFalloff = 0.2`\n  Neighbours render at 0.8. Less than ~0.12 and the depth cue disappears; more than ~0.3 and the off-centre cards look like a different component rather than the same one further away.\n- `ALPHA_INTERCEPT / ALPHA_SLOPE = 1.15 / 0.75`\n  Alpha is `1.15 - 0.75 * distance`. The intercept above 1 keeps the first ~0.2 of travel at full opacity so the centre card does not start dimming the instant you touch it; the slope puts the zero crossing at distance 1.533, comfortably past where scale clamps.\n- `SNAP_DURATION = 0.7`\n  Seconds for the release snap. Under ~0.45 the settle is abrupt enough that the drum reads as stepped; over ~0.9 a second drag arrives before the first has landed.\n- `WHEEL_STEP = 90`\n  Accumulated wheel units per index step. Matches roughly one detent on a mouse wheel and one deliberate trackpad swipe; at 40 a single flick advances four cards.\n- `MAX_WHEEL_STEPS = 3`\n  Ceiling on how many steps one wheel event may fire. A high-resolution trackpad can deliver a `deltaX` of 400+ in one event, which without the cap jumps five cards from a gesture the user read as one.\n- `DRAG_THRESHOLD = 8`\n  Pixels before a press counts as a drag. Below ~4 a click on a touchpad registers as a drag and the click swallow eats the tap; above ~12 short deliberate flicks are ignored.\n\n## API\n\n```ts\nfunction WrappingDrumCarousel(props: { children: ReactNode; spacingRatio?: number; maxSpacing?: number; scaleFalloff?: number; className?: string; cardClassName?: string; onIndexChange?: (index: number) => void }): JSX.Element\n```\n\n- `children: ReactNode`\n  One card per child. Each is wrapped in a positioned cell the component controls.\n- `spacingRatio: number` — default `0.46`\n  Card spacing as a fraction of the stage width.\n- `maxSpacing: number` — default `560`\n  Ceiling on spacing in pixels, so wide screens do not fling the neighbours off-stage.\n- `scaleFalloff: number` — default `0.2`\n  How much a card shrinks by one slot out from centre.\n- `className: string`\n  Merged onto the stage element.\n- `cardClassName: string`\n  Merged onto every positioned cell. This is where you size the cards.\n- `onIndexChange: (index: number) => void`\n  Fires on every snap with the wrapped index, always in `0..count-1`.\n\n## Reference implementation of the core\n\nThis is the part that is hard to get right. Adapt it; do not treat the rest of\nthe component as fixed.\n\n```tsx\nexport function WrappingDrumCarousel({\n  children,\n  spacingRatio = 0.46,\n  maxSpacing = 560,\n  scaleFalloff = 0.2,\n  className,\n  cardClassName,\n  onIndexChange,\n}: WrappingDrumCarouselProps) {\n  const stageRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);\n  const cardsRef = useRef<Array<HTMLDivElement | null>>([]);\n  /** Fractional carousel position. Tweened by the snap, driven raw by drag. */\n  const posRef = useRef({ pos: 0 });\n  /** Integer target index. Unwrapped — it counts up forever. */\n  const indexRef = useRef(0);\n  /** Last computed spacing in px. Drag divides by this for 1:1 tracking. */\n  const stepRef = useRef(1);\n\n  const reduced = useReducedMotion();\n  const size = useResizeObserver(stageRef);\n\n  const items = Children.toArray(children);\n  const count = items.length;\n\n  const notify = useRef(onIndexChange);\n  notify.current = onIndexChange;\n\n  /**\n   * Lay every card out for the current `pos`. Called on mount, on resize,\n   * on every frame of the snap tween, and on every pointermove during a drag.\n   * It is a pure function of `pos` — there is no accumulated state in here,\n   * which is why interrupting the tween mid-flight cannot desynchronise it.\n   */\n  const layout = useCallback(() => {\n    const stage = stageRef.current;\n    if (!stage || count === 0) return;\n\n    const step = Math.min(spacingRatio * stage.clientWidth, maxSpacing);\n    stepRef.current = step > 0 ? step : 1;\n\n    // Signed ring offset in (-count/2, count/2]. The whole illusion is here.\n    const ring = gsap.utils.wrap(-count / 2, count / 2);\n    const pos = posRef.current.pos;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n      const card = cardsRef.current[i];\n      if (!card) continue;\n\n      const offset = ring(i - pos);\n      const distance = Math.abs(offset);\n\n      gsap.set(card, {\n        xPercent: -50,\n        yPercent: -50,\n        x: offset * step,\n        // Explicitly flat. Without these two, a `transform-style: preserve-3d`\n        // or a stray `rotateY` on an ancestor turns a deliberately 2D drum\n        // into a half-broken 3D one that skews at the edges.\n        rotationY: 0,\n        z: 0,\n        // Scale bottoms out at one slot out; alpha keeps falling and hits 0 at\n        // distance 1.533. Cards therefore fade into fog rather than shrink to\n        // a vanishing point — that mismatch is what sells the cylinder.\n        scale: 1 - scaleFalloff * Math.min(distance, 1),\n        autoAlpha: gsap.utils.clamp(0, 1, ALPHA_INTERCEPT - ALPHA_SLOPE * distance),\n        zIndex: 100 - Math.round(10 * distance),\n        transformOrigin: '50% 50%',\n      });\n    }\n  }, [count, spacingRatio, maxSpacing, scaleFalloff]);\n\n  /** Tween `pos` to an integer index and re-lay out on every frame. */\n  const snapTo = useCallback(\n    (index: number) => {\n      indexRef.current = index;\n      notify.current?.(wrapIndex(index, count));\n\n      if (reduced) {\n        // Not \"duration 0\" via the tween: a zero-duration gsap.to does not\n        // guarantee an onUpdate call, so the layout would never run.\n        posRef.current.pos = index;\n        layout();\n        return;\n      }\n\n      gsap.to(posRef.current, {\n        pos: index,\n        duration: SNAP_DURATION,\n        ease: 'power3.out',\n        overwrite: true,\n        onUpdate: layout,\n      });\n    },\n    [count, layout, reduced],\n  );\n\n  useDrag(stageRef, {\n    threshold: DRAG_THRESHOLD,\n    onDragStart: () => {\n      // Kill the snap first or it keeps writing `pos` underneath the drag and\n      // the cards visibly fight the finger.\n      gsap.killTweensOf(posRef.current);\n    },\n    onDrag: (state) => {\n      posRef.current.pos -= state.deltaX / stepRef.current;\n      layout();\n    },\n    onDragEnd: (_state, moved) => {\n      if (moved) snapTo(Math.round(posRef.current.pos));\n    },\n  });\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\nThese are not optional. A version of this component that skips any of them is\nnot finished.\n\n**Cleanup.** Removes the wheel listener, kills the tween on the internal position object, kills any tween on the card elements and clears every property GSAP wrote with `clearProps: \"all\"`. `useDrag` releases pointer capture and removes its own five listeners. Ordering matters: tweens are killed before props are cleared, or an in-flight tween writes the transform straight back.\n\n**Reduced motion.** Drag still tracks the finger 1:1 — direct manipulation is not motion the user did not ask for. What changes is the release: instead of a 0.7 s `power3.out` tween, `pos` is assigned the target index and the layout runs once, so the drum arrives in a single frame. Wheel steps land instantly for the same reason.\n\n**Frame-rate independence.** Any smoothing must be a function of delta time.\n`x += (target - x) * 0.1` converges twice as fast at 120 Hz as at 60 Hz; use\n`x = target + (x - target) * Math.pow(smoothing, dt)` instead, where\n`smoothing` is the fraction of the gap remaining after one second.\n\n**Strict mode.** Assume effects run twice in development. Setup must be\nidempotent and teardown must fully undo it.\n\n## Known pitfalls — avoid these specifically\n\n- Reaching for `rotationY` and `translateZ` to get the drum look. This technique is flat on purpose — real 3D needs a shared or per-element perspective decision, correct `transform-style` on every ancestor, and it renders text on a rotated plane, which is where the blurring comes from. Write `rotationY: 0, z: 0` explicitly so an ancestor cannot opt you into 3D by accident.\n- Accumulating position instead of recomputing it. If the layout adds a delta to each card's current transform, interrupting the snap tween mid-flight leaves the cards permanently offset from `pos`. Layout must be a pure function of `pos` and nothing else.\n- Using `opacity` instead of `autoAlpha`. At `opacity: 0` the wrapped-around cards are still hit-testable, so a click intended for the centre card lands on an invisible one stacked on top of it. `autoAlpha` also sets `visibility: hidden`, which removes them from hit-testing.\n- Forgetting to kill the snap tween on `onDragStart`. The tween keeps writing `pos` underneath the drag and the cards visibly stutter against the finger.\n- Calling `preventDefault` on every wheel event. A vertical trackpad scroll over the carousel then does nothing and the page appears frozen. Only the horizontal component is claimed here — `Math.abs(deltaX) > Math.abs(deltaY)` gates the whole handler.\n- Registering the wheel listener as passive. `preventDefault` is silently ignored on a passive listener and Chrome logs an \"Unable to preventDefault inside passive event listener\" warning on every event.\n- Computing spacing from `window.innerWidth`. The carousel is then correct on load and wrong the moment it sits inside anything narrower than the viewport — a sidebar, a modal, a preview grid.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This is one of three deliberately different carousel techniques in the registry, and they must not be collapsed into one. This one is **fake 3D driven by a JS position variable**: `gsap.utils.wrap` maps a continuous, unbounded index onto the item count, and depth is scale plus `autoAlpha` only. `perspective-coverflow-scroller` is **true 3D driven by a native scroll container** — real `rotateY`/`translateZ`, no JS loop. `percent-slide-carousel` is **flat percentage translation with a CSS transition** and no wrapping at all.\n- Choose this one when the sequence must be endless in both directions and the item count is small enough that every item can stay mounted. It cannot virtualise: every card is laid out on every frame of a drag.\n- `touch-action: pan-y` on the stage tells the browser that horizontal gestures belong to the carousel and vertical ones to the page. Without it, dragging on a phone either scrolls the page or forces you to `preventDefault` every `touchmove`, which fights the browser instead of cooperating with it.\n\n## Definition of done\n\nDrag left past the last card and the first one arrives from the right without a gap or a jump. Release mid-way and it settles onto the nearest card. Neighbours are visibly smaller and dimmer, and inspecting a card shows a `matrix()` transform with no 3D component. Scroll the page vertically with the cursor over the carousel and the page scrolls normally; swipe horizontally and the drum steps one card.\n\nWhen you are finished, tell me which of the requirements above you implemented\nand where, so I can check them.\n"
};
